// point-descs.js — per-point event descriptions for the Empire Explorer (explorer page only)

const POINT_DESCS = {
  "Abbasid Caliphate|750": "Abbasids defeat Umayyads at Battle of the Great Zab. Al-Saffah inherits a vast empire but faces fractured loyalty, Shi'a betrayal, independent power brokers, and an Umayyad rival in Spain.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|762": "Al-Mansur founds Baghdad (762), executes rival Abu Muslim, centralizes Abbasid rule. Suppresses Alid revolt; welcomes Persian bureaucrats. Empire consolidates but loyalty remains enforced, not organic.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|786": "Harun al-Rashid's reign opens the Islamic Golden Age. House of Wisdom established; Baghdad becomes the world's leading cultural hub. Defeats Byzantium (806); exchanges embassies with Charlemagne and Tang China.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|800": "Peak of Abbasid power: Baghdad is the world's most populous city, House of Wisdom flourishes, and Harun controls territory from Central Asia to North Africa. Widely regarded as the dynasty's apex.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|820": "Al-Ma'mun's reign is the intellectual zenith. Al-Khwarizmi invents algebra; observatories built; Earth's circumference calculated. Empire militarily strong, financially robust, and politically cohesive under direct caliphal authority.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|836": "Al-Mu'tasim creates a Turkic slave army and relocates to Samarra after troops clash with Baghdad citizens. Empire still powerful, but caliphal independence is now structurally compromised by military dependence.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|850": "Turkish commanders effectively control the caliph. Al-Mutawakkil's assassination is being plotted. Provincial autonomy grows under Tahirids and Aghlabids. Largest territorial extent but sovereignty is already hollowed out.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|861": "Al-Mutawakkil murdered by Turkish officers. 'Anarchy at Samarra' (861–870) sees four caliphs violently removed in nine years. Tax collection collapses, provinces exploit chaos, and Baghdad itself is besieged.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|870": "Post-Anarchy caliphate severely weakened. Zanj slave rebellion (869–883) devastates Mesopotamia; Tulunids, Saffarids, and others functionally independent. Caliphate struggles to hold Iraq while hemorrhaging revenue and territory.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|900": "Al-Mu'tadid moves capital back to Baghdad and reconquers parts of Syria. Genuine but partial recovery — Samanids, Saffarids, and Hamdanids still hold vast regions. Caliphate controls only ~25% of peak territory.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|945": "Shi'a Buyid dynasty occupies Baghdad; caliph becomes a powerless figurehead. All political and military authority transfers to Buyid emirs. Medieval historians consider this the lowest ebb of the caliphate.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|950": "Five years into Buyid occupation, the caliph exercises zero political authority. Tax revenues belong to the Buyids; the caliphate exists only as a religious title with no territorial or administrative substance.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1055": "Sunni Seljuk Turks expel the Buyids and restore ceremonial Sunni caliphal prestige. Caliph grants Tughril the title 'Sultan.' As Seljuk power fragments, later caliphs begin tentatively reasserting limited independence.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1150": "Al-Muqtafi defends Baghdad against Seljuk siege (1157) and expands southward. Al-Mustanjid declares independence from Seljuks (1165) — the first genuine caliphal autonomy in over 200 years. Still regional, not an empire.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1200": "Al-Nasir's 45-year reign rebuilds real military and political authority in Iraq. Reorganizes civic institutions, plays regional powers against each other diplomatically. Britannica calls him the key figure of the late Abbasid revival.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1258": "Mongols under Hulagu Khan sack Baghdad (Feb 10, 1258). Caliph al-Musta'sim executed; hundreds of thousands killed; House of Wisdom destroyed; irrigation systems smashed. Total annihilation of the caliphal state.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1261": "Mamluk Sultan Baybars installs a surviving Abbasid as caliph in Cairo for political legitimacy. Caliphs hold no power — no army, no finances, no territory. Authority strictly ceremonial, used to validate Mamluk rule.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1350": "Cairo caliphs are 90-year figureheads, installed and discarded by Mamluk sultans at will. Diminishing religious credibility as Ottomans build rival Islamic legitimacy. The institution persists with barely any symbolic relevance.",
  "Abbasid Caliphate|1517": "Ottoman Sultan Selim I conquers Egypt; last Abbasid caliph surrenders authority. Caliphal title reportedly transferred to the Ottomans. End of seven-and-a-half centuries of Abbasid history.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-559": "Cyrus II becomes King of Anshan, still a Median vassal. Small but growing kingdom in southwestern Iran. Real imperial power lies entirely ahead; score reflects the very beginning of the imperial trajectory.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-550": "Cyrus defeats Median king Astyages; Achaemenid Persia inherits the entire Median empire spanning modern Iran, Azerbaijan, and much of Anatolia. Dramatic leap in power, but peak is still far ahead.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-546": "Cyrus defeats Croesus of Lydia at Thymbra; Sardis captured; Ionian Greek cities submit. Empire now spans Asia Minor to Central Asia, gaining first European-adjacent territories and Mediterranean access.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-539": "Cyrus captures Babylon without significant resistance; issues the Cyrus Cylinder declaring religious tolerance. Empire spans from the Aegean to the Indus with the ancient world's wealthiest city absorbed.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-530": "Cyrus II dies fighting the Massagetae; Cambyses II inherits an empire stretching from Egypt's border to the Indus. Full satrapal administrative system and Egypt's incorporation still lie ahead.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-525": "Cambyses defeats Pharaoh Psamtik III at Pelusium; Egypt incorporated as the 27th Dynasty. Empire spans three continents — from Thrace to the Nile's First Cataract to the Indus. Largest extent by any single ruler to this point.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-522": "Cambyses dies; usurper seizes the throne; simultaneous revolts erupt across nine provinces. Darius kills the usurper but must fight to hold every province. Empire at its most vulnerable since founding.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-520": "Darius I suppresses all revolts in one year, fights 19 battles, captures nine rebel kings, then reorganizes 20 satrapies, builds Persepolis and Susa, formalizes the Royal Road, and standardizes coinage.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-513": "Darius campaigns into Europe: subjugates Thrace, forces Macedon to submit. Empire reaches maximum territorial extent — from the Indus to the Danube, from the Aral Sea to the Nile.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-500": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — 5.5 million km², ~44% of world population, three continents. Satrapal system mature, Royal Road operational, Persepolis under construction. Maximum territory, administrative coherence, cultural output, and economic vitality converge.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-493": "Darius suppresses the Ionian Revolt (499–493 BCE); Asia Minor reintegrated; Miletus sacked. Empire near peak, now planning punitive expedition against Athens and Eretria.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-490": "Persian punitive expedition lands at Marathon; Eretria destroyed but Athenians win the battle. A minor tactical setback — no territory lost; Darius immediately plans a full-scale invasion.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-480": "Xerxes wins Thermopylae, sacks Athens, loses the naval Battle of Salamis. Land army destroyed at Plataea (479 BCE). Persia loses all European territory except Thrace and abandons westward expansion permanently.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-479": "Persian army annihilated at Plataea; fleet destroyed at Mycale. Delian League formed to pursue Persia. Asian heartland, Egypt, Babylon, and eastern satrapies all remain fully intact.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-465": "Xerxes I assassinated by royal bodyguard commander; Artaxerxes I consolidates after court intrigue. Territory unchanged; Greek ambitions abandoned but Asian, African, and eastern extent fully intact.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-450": "Peace of Callias (~449 BCE): Persia withdraws fleet from the Aegean and acknowledges Greek autonomy in Ionia. Western ambitions permanently curtailed; Asian and African domains intact.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-404": "Egypt revolts at Darius II's death; native dynasties rule independently for 61 years (404–343 BCE). Loss of the empire's wealthiest province marks the structural beginning of the terminal decline phase.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-401": "Cyrus the Younger raises Greek mercenaries to challenge Artaxerxes II; killed at Cunaxa. Greeks' subsequent march through the Persian heartland with impunity publicly exposes the empire's deep structural fragility.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-400": "Artaxerxes II exploits the Corinthian War; King's Peace (387 BCE) restores Ionian cities to Persian sovereignty. Diplomatic success partially compensates for military weakness, but Egypt remains free.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-370": "Great Satraps' Revolt (~372–362 BCE): multiple western satraps rebel simultaneously; Egypt supports them. Revolt suppressed through force and bribery, but empire can no longer rely on its provincial governors.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-343": "Artaxerxes III reconquers Egypt after two failed attempts, ending 61 years of native rule. Most capable ruler since Darius I — but his 338 BCE assassination triggers the final collapse.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-338": "Artaxerxes III poisoned by court eunuch; puppet kings installed. Philip II crushes Greece at Chaeronea. Last capable ruler lost to assassination while the greatest external threat completes conquest of Greece.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-334": "Alexander III crosses the Hellespont; wins the Battle of Granicus; western Anatolia falls. Empire still controls its Asian heartland but is in existential struggle against the world's most professional army.",
  "Achaemenid Empire|-330": "Alexander reaches Persepolis and burns the palace. Darius III flees and is stabbed to death by his own satrap Bessus. Last legitimate Achaemenid king dead; empire extinguished and absorbed into Alexander's realm.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1415": "Sabr ad-Din III returns from exile and founds Adal Sultanate at Zeila. Dynasty reconstituted after Ethiopian defeat and years in Yemen, but controls only its coastal base with no capacity for offensive operations against Ethiopia.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1443": "Sultan Badlay launches aggressive campaigns into Dawaro, overrunning southeastern Ethiopian provinces. Achieves greatest territorial gains in pre-Ahmad Adal history. Sultanate transforms from coastal trading city into genuine regional military power.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1445": "Battle of Gomit: Emperor Zara Yaqob's army defeats Adal and kills Badlay. Ethiopia launches sustained counteroffensive. Most capable pre-Ahmad sultan dead, army broken, Ethiopia actively pressing its advantage.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1450": "Post-Gomit recovery under Shams al-Din. Basic military capacity restored but state remains defensive, focused on internal consolidation rather than expansion. Lacks leadership for sustained offensive operations against Ethiopia.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1490": "Muhammad Ashar ad-Din organizes annual military expeditions into the Ethiopian highlands over three decades. Adal returns to offensive footing, maintaining constant frontier pressure, though without achieving major territorial gains.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1518": "Muhammad Ashar ad-Din assassinated; five sultans succeed within two years. Effective governance paralyzed. Power struggle between traditional Walashma dynasty and rising religious-military leadership reaches acute crisis point.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1527": "Ahmad ibn Ibrahim seizes de facto control as Imam, drawing on religious rather than dynastic legitimacy. Military reorganized with Ottoman-supplied firearms. State transformed from civil war nadir but not yet battle-tested against Ethiopia.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1529": "Battle of Shimbra Kure: Ahmad routs a much larger Ethiopian force through superior firearms discipline. Military equilibrium that defined the region for a century is shattered; Adal proves it can defeat Ethiopia in open battle.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1531": "Battle of Amba Sel: Ahmad defeats Ethiopia, captures imperial regalia, puts Emperor Dawit II to permanent flight. Adal forces fan across the highlands looting monasteries and coercing populations. Territorial maximum approaching.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1535": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — roughly three-quarters of Ethiopia under Adal occupation. Emperor Galawdewos reduced to guerrilla resistance; royal family exiled. Maximum territorial conquest, unrestricted military initiative, no effective Ethiopian conventional resistance.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1543": "Battle of Wayna Daga: Portuguese musketeers and Emperor Galawdewos's army defeat and kill Ahmad ibn Ibrahim. His death triggers immediate coalition collapse — tribal and Ottoman contingents withdraw; Ethiopia rapidly retakes the highlands.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1550": "Nur ibn Mujahid rebuilds Adal, fortifies Harar with the Jugol wall, and organizes annual campaigns against Ethiopia. Partial recovery from post-Ahmad collapse, but can no longer project force across three-quarters of Ethiopia.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1559": "Nur invades Ethiopia and defeats and kills Emperor Gelawdewos at the Battle of Fatagar — the last Adal military victory against Ethiopia. Genuine resurgence, but Adal still controls only Harar and its immediate environs.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1565": "Oromo migrations devastate Adal's lowland territories; Nur's army defeated at Hazalo. Harar's walls protect the city but surrounding territory is lost. Adal hemmed in by Oromo from the west and Ethiopian pressure from the north.",
  "Adal Sultanate|1577": "Emperor Sarsa Dengel defeats and executes Sultan Muhammad V; Muhammad ibn Nasir's final campaign crushed at the Battle of Webi River. Adal fragments into city-state remnants, permanently ending it as a military power.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1729": "Nader Qoli expels Afghan occupiers from Isfahan, pushes back Ottomans, and controls Iran as the power behind the Safavid throne. Iranian territorial integrity restored, but the formal empire and peak conquests still lie ahead.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1736": "Nader deposes the last Safavid and crowns himself Shah on the Mughan plain. All foreign occupiers expelled; Russia cedes Caspian lands; 375,000-strong army assembled. India and Central Asia remain unconquered.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1739": "Battle of Karnal crushes a Mughal army six times larger in under three hours. Delhi sacked; 20,000–30,000 killed in one day. Nader departs with the Peacock Throne, Koh-i-Noor, and wealth so vast he cancels Iranian taxes for three years.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1741": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Empire spans Iran, Caucasus, Afghanistan, Central Asia, parts of Iraq, Turkey, Arabia, and the Indian subcontinent. Iran's greatest territorial extent since the Sasanians. Nader adopts the title Shahanshah, the world's most powerful ruler.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1744": "Territory intact but internal rot accelerates. Crushing taxation, paranoia, and cruelty mount — Nader blinds his own son. Revolts spread across the empire, yet the army and territorial extent remain fundamentally intact.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1747": "Nader Shah assassinated by his own officers. Collapse immediate: Ahmad Shah Abdali founds the Durrani Empire; Georgia and Caucasian khanates declare independence. Afsharid state reduced to Khorasan within weeks.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1748": "Fratricidal succession war: Adil Shah and Ibrahim Shah destroy each other; 13-year-old Shahrokh installed in Mashhad. Zand dynasty rises in the west; Durranis consolidate the east. Bare dynastic survival amid complete institutional collapse.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1760": "Shahrokh Shah rules Khorasan as a blind figurehead, virtually a vassal of the Durrani Empire. Encyclopaedia Iranica states the post-1747 Afsharid 'state' is just the history of Khorasan — no wider imperial connotation remains.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1780": "Shahrokh maintains nominal rule of Mashhad amid periodic Durrani invasions. His coins read 'Iran has regained its youth' — a poignant aspiration from a blind prince in a single city with no real power.",
  "Afsharid Empire|1796": "Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar takes Mashhad; Shahrokh tortured to reveal hidden treasures and dies of injuries. Last Afsharid ruler dead, dynasty extinguished, territory absorbed into the new Qajar Empire.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2334": "Sargon overthrows King Ur-Zababa of Kish, defeats Lugal-zage-si of Uruk, and brings Sumerian city-states under Akkadian control. Founds the city of Akkad. Powerful locally, but vast conquests still entirely ahead.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2310": "Sargon fights 34 battles to consolidate Mesopotamia, conquers Syria and Canaan, installs his daughter Enheduanna — history's first named author — as high priestess at Ur. Postal system and standardized administration established across 65+ cities.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2300": "Sargon's empire spans all Mesopotamia with Elam as a vassal; trade reaches the Persian Gulf and Indus Valley. Administrative reforms in place, but late-reign revolts reveal structural fragility.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2279": "Rimush inherits and faces immediate mass rebellions from Sumer and Elam simultaneously. Suppresses them with extreme violence, killing tens of thousands. Military success maintained by coercion; Rimush assassinated by his own courtiers.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2270": "Manishtushu campaigns into Elam and the Persian Gulf, encourages trade, and builds temples. More stable than Rimush but fundamentally continuationist rather than expansionist. He too is reportedly assassinated.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2254": "Naram-Sin faces the Great Revolt — 'the four quarters together revolted.' Wins nine battles in one year, captures all rebel kings. Citizens petition for his deification; he adopts the title 'King of the Four Quarters.' Near peak.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2240": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Naram-Sin's empire spans Anatolia to Arabia, inner Iran to the Mediterranean. Conquers Ebla, Armanum, and Magan. First Mesopotamian king deified in his own lifetime. Maximum territory, divine kingship, and cultural efflorescence.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2218": "Naram-Sin dies after 36 years; Shar-Kali-Sharri inherits an intact empire, builds temples, and maintains governors. Growing Gutian raids and Amorite incursions place increasing pressure on an overextended empire.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2200": "Late Shar-Kali-Sharri: city-states like Lagash achieve independence; Gutian incursions intensify; 4.2-kiloyear drought triggers crop failures and famine. Central authority still functions but rapidly deteriorating.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2193": "Shar-Kali-Sharri dies; four kings succeed in rapid chaos — the Sumerian King List asks 'Who was king? Who was not king?' City-states declare independence; Gutians control central Babylonia. Empire nominally exists, effectively collapsed.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2170": "Dudu and Shu-Durul restore minimal order around Akkad city only. Territory shrunk back to the immediate city region. Gutians control former Akkadian lands. Kings of a city-state, not an empire.",
  "Akkadian Empire|-2154": "Shu-Durul falls to the Gutians. The city of Akkad is destroyed and never reoccupied — its location remains unknown today. A dark age ensues until the Third Dynasty of Ur (~2112 BCE). Empire ceases to exist.",
  "Aksumite Empire|100": "First historical attestation: the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea names Aksum and king Zoskales. Port of Adulis is a major hub for ivory, gold, and tortoiseshell. Aksum has displaced Kush as Rome's primary African trading partner.",
  "Aksumite Empire|200": "King Gedara sends military expeditions to Arabia, gaining control of western Yemen including Tihama, Najran, and Zafar. Monumentum Adulitanum records conquests into Ethiopia's interior, northeastern Sudan, and the Arabian Peninsula. Commercially dominant.",
  "Aksumite Empire|270": "King Endubis introduces Aksumite coinage — the first African state outside Roman control to mint its own currency. Coins struck in gold, silver, and bronze in Greek and Ge'ez. Mani names Aksum as co-equal with Rome, Persia, and China.",
  "Aksumite Empire|320": "King Ezana accedes to one of the world's great powers. Red Sea trade flourishing, Adulis port network at its height, coinage well-established. His mentor Frumentius is guiding him toward Christianity. Absolute peak lies just ahead.",
  "Aksumite Empire|350": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Ezana declares Christianity as state religion; becomes the first nation to mint coins bearing the cross. Conquers and sacks the Kingdom of Kush (Meroë). The Ezana Stone records this era in three languages. Peak of religious prestige, territory, and international recognition.",
  "Aksumite Empire|500": "Institutional stability and continued prosperity. Aksumite coins circulate as far as India. Christian identity and Byzantine diplomatic ties maintained. King Kaleb's transformative Yemen campaign is still ~20 years away; empire strong but not actively expanding.",
  "Aksumite Empire|520": "King Kaleb launches a Byzantine-backed invasion of Yemen, kills the Himyarite king Dhu Nuwas, and installs a Christian viceroy. Empire reaches its largest-ever extent (~2.5 million km²). Historian Munro-Hay calls this Aksum's 'swan song as a great power.'",
  "Aksumite Empire|575": "Sasanian Persians permanently expel Aksum from Yemen (~575–578 CE), installing a Persian governor. Aksum loses control of the Red Sea's eastern shore and the trade revenues that were its economic lifeblood. Irreversible strategic defeat.",
  "Aksumite Empire|600": "Despite losing Yemen, Aksum's highland Ethiopian-Eritrean core remains intact. Coin minting continues; Byzantine relations hold; Christianity deeply embedded. Declining but not collapsing — Muslim control of the Red Sea is still a generation away.",
  "Aksumite Empire|800": "Muslim Arab powers control the Red Sea, cutting off Aksumite commerce entirely. Coin minting ceases in the early 7th century. The city of Aksum shrinks dramatically; catastrophic soil erosion accelerates after 700 CE. Kingdom hollowed out economically.",
  "Aksumite Empire|900": "Capital relocated from Aksum to Kubar. Vastly reduced territory, no foreign trade, no coins, no monumental building. Functionally terminated even before its formal political end — a residual institution bearing no resemblance to Ezana's superpower.",
  "Aksumite Empire|940": "Last king Dil Na'od defeated by his own general Mara Takla Haymanot, who founds the Zagwe dynasty. Aksumite political dynasty ends, though Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity — tracing its lineage to Ezana — continues as one of the world's oldest Christian institutions.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1121": "Ibn Tumart proclaims himself the Mahdi among the Masmuda Berbers of the High Atlas, denouncing the Almoravids as impious. Controls only a few mountain villages; ideology and tribal following exist, but no urban centers or territorial power.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1124": "Abd al-Mu'min builds the fortified ribat of Tinmel, institutionalizing the Almohad hierarchy beyond a preacher's following. The Almoravids remain overwhelmingly dominant; the first pitched battle against them (1130) will end in catastrophic defeat.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1130": "Ibn Tumart dies after the disastrous Battle of al-Buhayra. Abd al-Mu'min conceals the death for two years to prevent disintegration, then emerges to claim leadership. Mountain base and ideological cohesion survive; founder dead, major battle lost.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1139": "Abd al-Mu'min goes on the offensive, expands into the Rif mountains, and founds a citadel at Taza — the first Almohad urban outpost beyond the Atlas heartland. Transitioned from defensive survival to territorial expansion, but Almoravid state still intact.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1147": "Abd al-Mu'min captures Marrakesh, kills the last Almoravid ruler, and declares himself Caliph — the first such title in the western Islamic world since the Umayyads. Almohads enter Al-Andalus; Córdoba falls (1148). Eastern Maghreb not yet subjugated.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1159": "Abd al-Mu'min completes conquest of the entire Maghreb, including Norman-held Mahdia (Tunisia). The greatest unification of North Africa since the Arab conquests. Both the North African theater and most of Iberia now under Almohad dominance.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1163": "Abd al-Mu'min dies after 30+ years of rule; his son Abu Yaqub Yusuf I inherits a consolidated empire spanning the entire Maghreb and most of Al-Andalus. Britannica notes both Yusuf I and al-Mansur 'raised western Islam to the zenith of its power.'",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1170": "Yusuf I secures all of Islamic Iberia; moves the capital to Seville; begins construction of the Giralda minaret. First time all of Al-Andalus AND the entire Maghreb are simultaneously under a single ruler. Military zenith under al-Mansur still ahead.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1184": "Yaqub al-Mansur accedes — Britannica explicitly identifies his reign (1184–1199) as the dynasty's zenith. Empire fully intact across North Africa and Muslim Iberia. Patronizes Averroes and Ibn Tufayl. Military prestige not yet at apex; Alarcos still ahead.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1195": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — al-Mansur crushes Castile at Alarcos, raids the Tagus valley and the walls of Toledo. Takes the epithet 'Made Victorious by God.' Builds the Hassan Tower, Kasbah of the Udayas, and Koutoubia Mosque. Maximum power across all dimensions.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1200": "Al-Mansur dies (January 1199); son al-Nasir inherits maximum territorial extent but is no warrior. Engages in no Iberian campaigning until 1211, allowing Christian kingdoms to consolidate and ally. Territory maximal but institutional brittleness now visible.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1212": "Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa: a crusading coalition of Castile, Aragon, Navarre, and Portugal crushes the Almohad army. Al-Nasir flees; dies the following year in despair. Britannica calls it 'a crushing defeat' that significantly hastened the dynasty's decline.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1224": "Prolonged succession crisis after weak Yusuf II: Abd al-Wahid I murdered after six months; two simultaneous rival caliphs. Iberian governors defect, surrendering cities to Christian kingdoms for local autonomy. Authority fragmenting at all levels.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1229": "Double catastrophe: Hafsids declare independence in Ifriqiya; Caliph al-Mamun surrenders Iberian cities to Castile for mercenaries and shockingly renounces Ibn Tumart's Mahdi claim — the caliphate's ideological foundation. Territorial, ideological, and political integrity all simultaneously breached.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1236": "Zayyanids seize Tlemcen in western Algeria; Ferdinand III captures Córdoba — the historic capital of Al-Andalus. Caliphate now controls only Morocco in Africa plus increasingly isolated Andalusian cities. Losses accelerating but a functioning Moroccan state still operates.",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1250": "Seville falls (1248); Muslim Iberia reduced to the isolated Nasrid emirate of Granada. Marinids progressively control northern Morocco. The reigning caliph governs a Moroccan rump state described by Britannica as existing 'at the mercy of another emerging Berber power.'",
  "Almohad Caliphate|1269": "Last caliph Idris al-Wathiq, reduced to holding Marrakesh alone, is murdered by a slave (1269). The Marinids seize the city immediately. The empire that had once stretched from the Atlantic to Tunisia and from the Sahara to the Tagus is extinguished.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1040": "Abdallah ibn Yasin arrives in the Sahara and founds the Almoravid movement among the Lamtuna tribe. Controls no cities or trade routes — just assembled its first followers committed to strict Islamic law and conquest.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1056": "Almoravids conquer Aoudaghost and seize Sijilmasa — the northern terminus of the trans-Saharan gold trade. A transformative economic threshold crossed. Almoravid gold dinars will circulate from England to Egypt. Ibn Yasin killed in 1059 fighting the Barghawata.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1070": "Yusuf ibn Tashfin founds Marrakesh (~1062) and subdues most of Morocco; Fez falls in 1075; expansion into Algeria underway. Empire split into northern (Yusuf) and southern (Abu Bakr) branches. Regional power achieved; Iberian Peninsula not yet entered.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1086": "Battle of Sagrajas: Yusuf's Saharan cavalry shatters Alfonso VI of Castile's army, halting the Reconquista. However, Yusuf returns to Africa — not a single Andalusian city yet under direct Almoravid rule. Iberian entry begun with a spectacular victory; full conquest still ahead.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1097": "All Muslim Spain under Almoravid rule after systematic deposing of taifa kings (1090–1094). First unified Muslim government of Iberia since 1031. Yusuf assumes title Amir al-Muslimin. Abu Bakr dead (1087); empire reunified. El Cid dies 1099; Valencia falls to Almoravids 1102.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1100": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — All of northwestern Africa to Algiers, all of Iberia south of the Tagus, Balearic Islands added under Ali ibn Yusuf (1106). Empire stretches ~3,000 km from Zaragoza to the Senegal River. Almoravid gold dinars praised across the Mediterranean for purity.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1113": "Ali ibn Yusuf wins the Battle of Ucles (1108); Alfonso VI's only son killed. Andalusian civilization flows into Marrakesh. Empire at near-maximum territorial extent. But structural weaknesses emerge: dual-front military burden strains the small Berber elite; founding austerity visibly eroding.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1130": "Zaragoza lost to Aragon (1118); Ibn Tumart's Almohad revolt launched (~1121); Ali ibn Yusuf consistently fails to destroy the Almohads. Empire still holds major territories but is losing on two fronts to opponents exploiting the same reformist ideology that built Almoravid power.",
  "Almoravid Empire|1138": "Ali ibn Yusuf defeated by Alfonso VII (1138); catastrophic defeat at Ourique prompts Afonso I to proclaim the Kingdom of Portugal (1139); Lisbon falls (1147). Ali dies 1143; his son Tashfin falls from a cliff fleeing defeat near Oran (1146).",
  "Almoravid Empire|1147": "Almohad forces storm Marrakesh; last ruler Ishaq ibn Ali killed; Almoravid palace destroyed in deliberate symbolic erasure. Simultaneously, Lisbon falls to Portugal. Dynasty physically extinct — last ruler dead, capital destroyed, Iberian position collapsed.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1670": "Osei Tutu I assumes leadership of Kumasi chiefdom and begins unifying fragmented Akan states. Okomfo Anokye conjures the Golden Stool, the spiritual symbol binding all Asante clans. Confederation forming, but the empire still pays tribute to the powerful Denkyira kingdom.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1700": "Asante confederation militarily capable and preparing for war, but formally still a subject polity under Denkyira suzerainty. Golden Stool ceremony recently performed. On the eve of its defining moment — the Battle of Feyiase is one year away.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1701": "Battle of Feyiase: Asante defeats and kills the Denkyirahene, seizes access to the Dutch fort at Elmina and European firearms trade. Osei Tutu installed as Asantehene; Kotoko Council established; Okomfo Anokye's Seventy-Seven Laws promulgated. Empire formally founded.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1717": "Osei Tutu I killed in battle against Akyem on the Pra River. Aowin sacks Kumasi, carrying off ~20,000 captives. Empire in genuine crisis and brief fragmentation. Institutional foundations — Golden Stool, Kotoko Council, constitutional framework — hold and prevent total collapse.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1730": "Opoku Ware I conquers Bono-Tekyiman, Gonja, Dagomba, Gyaman, and defeats Akyem. Dutch factors report Asante armies of 50,000 men. Administrative centralization accelerates. Asante becomes West Africa's pre-eminent regional power under its greatest territorial expansionist.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1750": "Britannica states Asante 'reached its fullest extent in the interior' under Opoku Ware (r. ~1720–1750). Controls vast swathes of modern Ghana's interior, major gold trade routes, and extensive tribute networks. A genuine regional hegemon; further coastal expansion still to come.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1765": "Osei Kwadwo introduces meritocratic appointment of officials based on ability rather than birth — transforming Asante from an aristocratic confederation into a sophisticated bureaucratic polity. Military command reorganized; empire at a plateau of institutional maturity and territorial stability.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1800": "Osei Bonsu inherits a formidable empire and invades Fante territory (1806–1807), besieging Cape Coast Castle before a British diplomatic settlement concedes Asante coastal claims. Controls virtually all of modern Ghana's gold fields and trade routes. One campaign from its zenith.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1817": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Osei Bonsu secures direct coastal access, controls virtually all of present-day Ghana. British diplomatic mission concludes the Treaty of 1817, explicitly recognizing the Asantehene's authority. Thomas Bowdich describes Kumasi's paved roads, architectural grandeur, and enormous gold treasury.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1824": "Battle of Nsamankow: Asante annihilates a British expeditionary force; Governor-General MacCarthy killed; his skull kept as a royal trophy. The empire's single greatest military triumph against Europeans. Osei Bonsu dies the same day — simultaneous peak victory and loss of the greatest king.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1826": "Battle of Katamansu: British-led coalition with Congreve rockets inflicts Asante's first defeat against European forces. Treaty of 1831 follows; Asante waives coastal claims and accepts the River Pra as the de facto southern border. Still dominant in the interior; checked at the coast.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1850": "Kwaku Dua I's long stable reign (1834–1867): internal consolidation, flourishing gold and kola trade, peace with Britain maintained. No expansion but regionally hegemonic. Growing British coastal infrastructure and Fante alliances represent a slowly tightening strategic constraint.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1874": "Third Anglo-Ashanti War: General Wolseley occupies and burns Kumasi for one day; palace looted. Treaty of Fomena demands 50,000-ounce gold indemnity, renunciation of all territory south of the Pra, and abolition of the slave trade. Triggers immediate secessionist wars among tributary states.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1883": "Mensah Bonsu leads partial recovery, reasserting authority over some breakaway states and rebuilding the treasury and army. Core territory intact. But Britain's Gold Coast colony is firmly established, French and German colonial powers are expanding from neighboring territories.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1896": "British expedition marches on Kumasi unopposed; Governor Maxwell demands the 1874 indemnity. Asantehene Prempeh I, unable or unwilling to pay, is arrested and exiled to the Seychelles. Empire forced to sign a treaty of British protection. Nominal existence but political sovereignty lost.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1900": "War of the Golden Stool: Queen Mother Yaa Asantewaa leads the last major armed resistance against British colonial rule in West Africa after Governor Hodgson demands the sacred Golden Stool. Nine-month revolt; Asante besieges Fort Kumasi. Yaa Asantewaa eventually captured and exiled.",
  "Ashanti Empire|1902": "Asante formally annexed as a British Crown Colony on 1 January 1902. The Golden Stool — never surrendered — remains hidden. Prempeh I in exile until 1924. The Asante Kingdom survives today as a constitutionally protected traditional state within Ghana, with a ceremonial Asantehene.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-2025": "Puzur-Ashur I founds independent Assur after the collapse of Ur III. Tiny city-state centered on the god Ashur's temple-hill. Kings styled as 'governor of Ashur,' not king. No military empire, no territory beyond the immediate hinterland. Fragile independence just emerging from foreign domination.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1950": "Karum Kanesh trade network at its height: thousands of cuneiform tablets document Assyrian merchants importing tin and textiles, trading them for Anatolian silver. An estimated 25 tons of silver and 100,000 textiles annually. Commercial hegemony spanning hundreds of kilometers, not yet military power.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1900": "Assur at peak of Old Assyrian commercial dominance, with trading colonies (karums) operating across Anatolia. Mixed constitution — city assembly, elected limmu official, council of elders — gives Assur a remarkably democratic character. Prosperous and regionally influential, but power derived from commerce, not conquest.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1808": "Amorite conqueror Shamshi-Adad I seizes Assur; styles himself 'King of the Universe' — first Assyrian ruler to claim absolute kingship. Moves capital to Shubat-Enlil. Abrupt shift from trading city to incipient territorial power, though the kingdom was still being assembled.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1800": "Shamshi-Adad I controls most of northern Mesopotamia including Nineveh, Arrapha, Erbil, and Mari on the Euphrates. Erects a victory stele on the Mediterranean shore; receives tribute from Persian Gulf to the 'Upper Sea.' Appoints sons as viceroys. Dominant power in northern Mesopotamia.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1700": "After Shamshi-Adad's death (~1776 BCE), his kingdom rapidly unravels. Zimri-Lim retakes Mari; Hammurabi destroys the kingdom. Assur passes through Eshnunna, Elamite, and Babylonian control. Bel-bani founds the Adaside dynasty, restoring local stability but not the expansive kingdom.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1595": "Hittite sack of Babylon creates a power vacuum; the Mitanni kingdom rises and dominates northern Mesopotamia. Assur caught between competing powers with little autonomy. Adaside dynasty continues under increasing Mitanni pressure. Survived but wielded minimal independent power.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1400": "Assur formally under Mitanni vassalage; Adaside kings stripped of independent foreign policy. City of Assur, dynasty, and temples all continue to function — humiliated and powerless, but institutional identity and continuity preserved.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1363": "Ashur-uballit I seizes the opportunity of Hittite destruction of Mitanni to assert independence and extend control over Nineveh and Arbela. First Assyrian king to style himself 'king of the land of Ashur' — shift from city-state to territorial kingdom.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1307": "Adad-nirari I defeats the Mitanni remnants and pushes to the Euphrates, bordering the Hittite Empire. Royal inscriptions require 32 lines for his titles alone. World History Encyclopedia credits him with 'a foundation for empire upon which his successors would build.'",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1300": "Shalmaneser I finally destroys Mitanni, absorbs their culture, and establishes systematic deportation of conquered peoples — a defining Assyrian imperial practice for centuries. Territory now substantial across northern Mesopotamia. Middle Assyrian Empire firmly established as a great power.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1250": "Tukulti-Ninurta I ascends the throne; wins Battle of Nihriya (~1237 BCE), claiming 28,800 Hittite prisoners, marking the beginning of the end of Hittite influence in northern Mesopotamia. Vast, largely secure empire at a transitional moment between very powerful and maximum Middle Assyrian extent.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1225": "Tukulti-Ninurta I defeats Babylon, treads on the Kassite king's neck as a footstool, sacks the city, and carries off the statue of Marduk. First native Mesopotamian to rule Babylon; first Assyrian king to claim the title 'King of Kings.' Empire stretches to the Persian Gulf.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1115": "Tiglath-pileser I launches a dramatic revival after Bronze Age Collapse — campaigns to Lake Van, the Mediterranean coast, and against Aramaean groups. A genuine recovery arresting Assyrian decline, but mass Aramaean migrations will overwhelm the empire's territories after his death.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-1050": "After Ashur-bel-kala's death, Aramaean tribal migrations overwhelm Upper Mesopotamia and Assyrian control contracts dramatically. Administrative record thins sharply. Assyria still exists as a polity but controls little beyond its heartland and faces existential pressure.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-935": "Ashur-Dan II conducts punitive raids to clear Aramaean tribes, rebuilds government offices in all provinces, and provides ploughs to farmers. Britannica describes 'a marked economic and organisational upturn.' Institutional recovery has begun; not yet re-emerged as a military power.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-912": "Last year of Ashur-Dan II. Active institutional rebuilding and successful border raids underway. Statehood intact and leadership improving matters. The dynasty is about to launch one of history's most spectacular imperial revivals.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-911": "Adad-nirari II marks the formal beginning of the Neo-Assyrian period. Subjugates Aramaean, Neo-Hittite, and Hurrian populations; twice defeats Babylonia and annexes territory north of the Diyala River. State in early-phase revival — stronger than the nadir, vast expansion still ahead.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-883": "Ashurnasirpal II transfers the capital from Assur to the newly built Nimrud; campaigns across the Zagros, Anatolia, and to the Mediterranean, receiving tribute from Phoenician cities. Significant expansion beyond the heartland; greatest gains still to come.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-850": "Shalmaneser III campaigns to the Mediterranean coast, receives tribute from Tyre and Sidon, defeats Urartu. At Battle of Qarqar (853 BCE) faces a coalition of twelve kings including Ahab of Israel. Substantial empire reaching the Mediterranean, but civil war after his death will bring stagnation.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-782": "Following Adad-nirari III's death, powerful generals rather than kings wield real power. General Shamshi-ilu wins major battles but omits his king from victory inscriptions. Internal rebellions in Ashur, Arrapkha, and Guzana. Powerful empire with territorial holdings maintained but expansion completely halted.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-745": "Tiglath-pileser III ends the 'age of the magnates,' more than doubles Assyrian territory, and vasalizes the Levant to the Egyptian border. Conquers Babylonia (729 BCE). Fundamentally reforms the army, provincial administration, and resettlement policy. Transforms Assyria into a true world empire.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-722": "Sargon II founds the Sargonid dynasty; conquers Israel (722 BCE) and deports its population; defeats Urartu; forces Pharaoh Osorkon IV to pay tribute; founds the new capital Dur-Sharrukin. Near-peak military and administrative capacity; final push to include Egypt one generation away.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-700": "Sennacherib builds the 'Palace without Rival' at Nineveh; besieges Jerusalem and receives tribute from Hezekiah (701 BCE); commits total destruction of Babylon (689 BCE). Vast empire from Anatolia to the Persian Gulf. Egypt not yet conquered — that territorial maximum belongs to Esarhaddon (671 BCE).",
  "Assyrian Empire|-671": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Esarhaddon conquers Egypt (671 BCE), sacks Memphis, and installs a puppet pharaoh. First Mesopotamian ruler to claim 'king of Egypt.' Empire spans from Egypt and Cyprus in the west to the Iranian plateau — the largest empire the world had ever seen.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-650": "Ashurbanipal sacks Thebes (663 BCE), suppresses his brother's great rebellion, and destroys Elam (~647 BCE). Builds the Library of Nineveh with 30,000+ cuneiform tablets. Egypt is being lost, but the army is still formidable and Ashurbanipal is at the height of his personal power.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-630": "Late Ashurbanipal reign is 'obscure' (Britannica). Egypt has slipped from Assyrian control under Psamtik I. Court eunuchs have displaced experienced generals. Structural vulnerabilities of the overextended empire becoming apparent. Midpoint between peak power and collapse.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-627": "Ashurbanipal dies after 38 years — 'last great king of Assyria.' Succession disputes immediately erupt. Nabopolassar seizes Babylon (626 BCE), founding the Neo-Babylonian Empire. Medes consolidate power to the east. Empire still powerful militarily but succession crisis opens the terminal phase.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-614": "Medes under Cyaxares sack Ashur — Assyria's original capital and supreme religious center, home to the god Ashur's principal temple. Devastating blow to morale and legitimacy. Nabopolassar concludes alliance with Cyaxares, cementing the coalition that will deliver the final blows.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-612": "Chaldean-Median coalition breaches Nineveh; Nimrud already destroyed. Administrative and ideological infrastructure completely collapsed. Bodies of Assyrian soldiers found defending Nineveh's gates. Last king Sin-shar-ishkun likely dies in the fall. A remnant holds Harran; the state is effectively over.",
  "Assyrian Empire|-609": "Babylonians and Medes capture Harran. A combined Assyrian-Egyptian attempt to retake it fails. Ashur-uballit II, last claimant to the Assyrian throne, disappears from history. The Assyrian political entity is utterly extinguished — capital cities destroyed, last king lost to history.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1867": "Austro-Hungarian Compromise (Ausgleich) establishes the Dual Monarchy after defeats in 1859 and 1866. Franz Joseph grants Hungary full internal autonomy in exchange for unified foreign and defence policy. A genuine act of political creativity that stabilizes the empire at ~35 million people.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1870": "Compromise structure fully operational; both parliaments functioning. Economy described as comparable to France or the USA in 1870. Dual Alliance with Germany (1879) still ahead. Stabilizing empire in the early phase of mature development — past the 1866 crisis but not yet at peak growth.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1878": "Congress of Berlin: Foreign Minister Andrássy forces Russia to reduce demands of the Treaty of San Stefano. Austria-Hungary authorized to occupy Bosnia-Herzegovina — a major territorial addition. The empire's greatest diplomatic success before WWI. Dual Alliance with Germany signed the following year (1879).",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1880": "Post-1873 crash recovery underway. Bosnia-Herzegovina under administration with heavy infrastructure investment. Triple Alliance (Austria-Hungary, Germany, Italy) formed in 1882. Three-fourths of railway lines nationalized; sustained economic growth continuing. Well-established empire in stable development.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1895": "GNP nearly doubled from 1870–1913; fin-de-siècle Vienna is one of the most culturally vibrant cities in Europe — Freud, Klimt, Mahler, Herzl all active. 47 million people, 6th largest GDP in the world. One step below absolute maximum.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1900": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Austro-Hungarian GDP ranked 6th in the world; population reaches 53 million by 1913 — third most populous European state. Vienna, Prague, and Budapest are globally significant cultural capitals. Largest political state in Central Europe. Maximum across all key dimensions simultaneously.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1908": "Bosnia-Herzegovina formally annexed — maximum territorial extent — a total diplomatic victory. But constitutional crisis of 1904–1907 was genuinely destabilizing, and the Bosnian Crisis permanently damaged relations with Serbia, Italy, and Russia, hardening the alliances that lead directly to 1914.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1914": "Archduke Franz Ferdinand assassinated in Sarajevo; Austria-Hungary's ultimatum to Serbia triggers WWI. Empire still at full territorial extent and has a functional military — hence 5. But it has just committed to the miscalculation that will kill the empire within four years.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1916": "Brusilov Offensive inflicts 600,000–1,000,000 Austro-Hungarian casualties — the army never fully recovers. Franz Joseph dies after 68 years on the throne. Karl I inherits a dying empire, immediately begins seeking a separate peace. Food crisis already acute and worsening to starvation by 1917.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1917": "General starvation in Cisleithania; increasing strikes in factories and uprisings in the army. Karl I reconvenes the Imperial Parliament and offers federalization, but nationalities reject his overtures. Empire still formally exists but disintegrating from within. Gradual institutional collapse accelerating.",
  "Austro-Hungarian Empire|1918": "Hungarian Parliament votes to terminate the union with Austria (17 October 1918). Battle of Vittorio Veneto breaks the army on the Italian front. Karl I relinquishes authority on 11–13 November. Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, and Poland declared. The Dual Monarchy ceases to exist entirely.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1171": "Saladin abolishes the Fatimid Caliphate and realigns Egypt with the Sunni Abbasid caliphate, founding the Ayyubid dynasty. Position is fragile: nominally answers to the Zengid ruler Nur al-Din in Damascus, no territory beyond Egypt, and army rebuilding after Fatimid resistance.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1174": "Nur al-Din dies, releasing Saladin from his principal constraint. He conquers Yemen, takes Damascus, and begins absorbing Zengid principalities. Now an independent sultan with Egypt, Yemen, and Damascus under his control, but has not yet unified the Muslim world against the Crusaders.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1183": "Aleppo and Mosul brought under control; Crusader states now encircled on all sides. Saladin controls more territory and resources than all the Crusader states combined. The Battle of Hattin is structurally set up — Jerusalem and the Crusader interior remain in Frankish hands.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1187": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Battle of Hattin annihilates the combined Crusader army; Acre, Beirut, Sidon, Nablus, Jaffa, and Ascalon all fall within three months. Jerusalem surrenders on 2 October 1187 after 88 years of Crusader rule. Empire spans Egypt, Syria, Levant, Mesopotamia, Hejaz, Yemen, and Nubia.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1192": "Third Crusade fought to a draw; Treaty of Jaffa restores a Crusader coastal strip but preserves Muslim control of Jerusalem. Saladin loses Acre but crucially holds the holy city. Empire still spans Egypt–Syria–Yemen–Mesopotamia, but Crusader coastal presence is restored.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1193": "Saladin dies in Damascus, reportedly having given away so much wealth there was nothing left for his burial. Death immediately triggers succession war: al-Afdal takes Damascus, al-Aziz takes Egypt, other princes take various provinces. Empire's territorial extent still large but political coherence collapsed.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1200": "Al-Adil I defeats rival nephews and proclaims himself Sultan, reunifying the realm after seven years of civil war. Rules wisely for nearly two decades, extending control into al-Jazira and Anatolia. Territorially intact and diplomatically stable, but structurally more fragile than under Saladin.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1210": "Al-Adil's stable reign continues; empire at consolidated equilibrium. Deliberately diplomatic stance avoids provoking new Crusades. Trade with Italian city-states flourishes; Cairo Citadel expanded; son al-Kamil develops as a capable administrator. As large and well-governed as it will ever be after Saladin.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1218": "Al-Adil dies upon hearing Crusaders had landed in Egypt — opening of the Fifth Crusade. Al-Kamil inherits under siege, immediately facing both a Crusader assault on Damietta and an internal coup attempt by the Hakkari Kurdish regiment.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1220": "Al-Kamil defeats the Fifth Crusade: Damietta recaptured, Crusader army trapped and forced to retreat with magnanimous terms. Empire's core secure. However, political fragmentation that will resume after his death is already latent in his brothers' ambitions in Palestine and Syria.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1229": "Jerusalem ceded diplomatically to Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II — al-Kamil needing support against his own Ayyubid brothers. The Islamic world is scandalized; the qadi of Jerusalem protests. Dramatically damages the Ayyubids' founding claim as jihadist protectors of Jerusalem.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1238": "Al-Kamil dies — Encyclopedia.com calls him the 'last powerful ruler' of the dynasty. His sons al-Salih Ayyub and al-Adil II immediately contest Egypt and Damascus. Jerusalem recovered (the Frederick II treaty having expired), but the empire is immediately in civil war.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1240": "Multiple Ayyubid princes contest Egypt, Damascus, and other principalities simultaneously. Al-Salih Ayyub uses Khwarazmian mercenaries as shock troops. Khwarazmians sack Jerusalem in 1244. Yemen and the Hejaz already lost to local dynasties. The realm mired in inter-Ayyubid civil war.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1247": "As-Salih Ayyub subdues most of Syria by 1247, with only Aleppo remaining independent. The last partially-unified empire — still controlling Egypt and most of Syria — but Yemen, the Hejaz, and parts of Mesopotamia already lost. Strategically weaker and ideologically exhausted than any prior generation.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1250": "As-Salih Ayyub dies during the Seventh Crusade (Louis IX's invasion of Egypt). His Mamluk slave troops murder his son and successor Turanshah. His widow Shajar al-Durr briefly rules, then is pressured to marry Mamluk general Aybak. Ayyubid power in Egypt effectively ended.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1258": "Mongols sack Baghdad (1258) and murder the Abbasid Caliph. The last significant Ayyubid ruler, an-Nasir Yusuf of Aleppo, desperately seeks alliances but finds no coherent response. Rump Syrian principalities whose nominal independence is days from extinction.",
  "Ayyubid Empire|1260": "Mongol forces sack Aleppo and overrun the Ayyubid principalities of Syria. An-Nasir Yusuf captured and executed. The Battle of Ain Jalut sees the Mamluks — not the Ayyubids — repel the Mongols, who then absorb Syria. The empire as a political entity is extinct.",
  "Aztec Empire|1325": "Mexica people settle on a small swampy island in Lake Texcoco, guided by the prophesied omen of an eagle devouring a serpent. The weakest and most marginalized group in the Valley of Mexico — subordinate to the Tepanec city of Azcapotzalco as mercenaries and tribute-payers.",
  "Aztec Empire|1375": "First Mexica royal dynasty established under Acamapichtli (~1376). Mexica serve as skilled mercenaries and build chinampas that dramatically increase agricultural output. Still formal tributaries of Azcapotzalco — they pay taxes, send soldiers, and have no independent foreign policy. Building foundations, not projecting power.",
  "Aztec Empire|1427": "Azcapotzalco succession crisis: Tezozomoc's son Maxtla seizes power and allegedly orchestrates the murder of the Mexica ruler Chimalpopoca. Itzcoatl elected Mexica Huey Tlatoani; exiled Nezahualcoyotl recruits coalition against Maxtla. Mexica on the eve of liberation — active belligerents, not passive tributaries.",
  "Aztec Empire|1428": "Triple Alliance (Tenochtitlan, Texcoco, Tlacopan) defeats Azcapotzalco. Itzcoatl and Tlacaelel institute sweeping reforms, rewriting Mexica history to create an ideological foundation for empire. Armies begin subduing city-states across the Valley of Mexico — the tribute-extraction system is established. Empire born.",
  "Aztec Empire|1450": "Moctezuma I conquers Oaxaca (1445) and expands east to the Gulf Coast — the empire stretches from sea to sea for the first time. A catastrophic four-year drought then triggers famine; Moctezuma initiates the Flower Wars as a permanent institution to secure war captives.",
  "Aztec Empire|1470": "Moctezuma I dies (1469); grandson Axayacatl inherits 38+ tribute provinces. He conquers the sister city-state of Tlatelolco (1473). In 1479, his army of ~24,000 is routed by the Purépecha (Tarascan) Empire — the worst military defeat in Aztec history.",
  "Aztec Empire|1490": "Ahuitzotl, eighth Huey Tlatoani, conquers southward to Xoconusco (modern Chiapas) and the Isthmus of Tehuantepec. Reopens the expanded Templo Mayor (1487) in a ceremony of mass sacrifice. Pushes the Pacific and Gulf coasts as tributary zones. Greatest territorial footprint in Aztec history.",
  "Aztec Empire|1502": "Moctezuma II inherits the most powerful and extensive empire in Aztec history. He reorganizes imperial administration, abolishes social mobility from commoner to nobility, and continues military expansion as far south as Chiapas. Administrative peak of a fully mature, centralized imperial machine.",
  "Aztec Empire|1519": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Cortés arrives in November 1519; Moctezuma II receives him as an honored guest. Tenochtitlan houses 200,000–400,000 people — far bigger than any European city. Triple Alliance controls ~80,000 sq miles, 38 tribute provinces from the Pacific to the Gulf.",
  "Aztec Empire|1520": "La Noche Triste (30 June / 1 July 1520): Aztecs drive Cortés and his men from Tenochtitlan — a genuine military victory. But the Spaniards leave behind smallpox, killing over 50% of the population including the new emperor Cuitlahuac. Cortés retreats to Tlaxcala to rebuild.",
  "Aztec Empire|1521": "Cortés besieges Tenochtitlan with ~100,000 warriors — the vast majority former Aztec tributaries. The 93-day siege ends 13 August 1521; Emperor Cuauhtémoc is captured while fleeing by canoe. Cortés razes the city and begins building Mexico City on its ruins.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1894": "Sumuabum appropriates Babylon from Kazallu, declares it a state, conquers Dilbat and Kish, and begins constructing a defensive wall. Critically, Sumuabum is never titled 'king' in any inscription. Hemmed in on all sides by Elam, Isin, Larsa, Eshnunna, and Shamshi-Adad.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1860": "Under Sumu-la-El and Sabium, Babylon establishes itself as a recognized player in the multi-polar Mesopotamian arena. City walls completed; military victories won; regional sphere of influence established. A contemporary dispatch records Babylon as comparable to Larsa, Eshnunna, and Yamhad — one of several comparable city-states.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1800": "Pre-Hammurabi Babylon; Sin-muballit's generation holds the line.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1792": "Hammurabi ascends the throne. The Mari dispatch records Babylon as commanding roughly 10–15 allied kings — comparable to what Larsa, Eshnunna, and Yamhad could mobilize. His first ~20 years consist overwhelmingly of domestic activities: constructing thrones, digging canals, fortifying borders. A middling city-state.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1764": "Hammurabi defeats the Elamite coalition (1764 BCE), then immediately turns on Larsa. Rim-Sin — long Babylon's dominant rival — is defeated, brought to Babylon in a cage, and Larsa's vast territory absorbed.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1760": "Hammurabi sacks and destroys Mari (1761 BCE) rather than absorbing it, then marches on Ashur. Babylon now controls territory stretching from Harran in the northwest to the Persian Gulf. Near-total Mesopotamian dominance — just short of the absolute peak; Eshnunna not yet destroyed.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1750": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Eshnunna finally destroyed by damming its water supply; Hammurabi undisputed master of all Mesopotamia. Rules from Harran and Aleppo to the Persian Gulf. Code of Hammurabi promulgated: 282 laws inscribed on a 2.25-meter diorite stele.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1749": "Samsu-iluna inherits the full empire; Rim-Sin II raises a pan-Sumerian rebellion across 26 southern cities. Samsu-iluna defeats this rebellion, pulling down the walls of Ur, Uruk, and Isin — but all records from these cities cease entirely after year 10.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1740": "Ilum-ma-ili founds the Sealand Dynasty after defeating Samsu-iluna; a native Assyrian governor expels Babylonian influence from Assur; Elam sacks the now-undefended Uruk. Babylon still controls the central strip but has lost both southern and northern extensions. Empire collapsed to a regional middling power.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1700": "Abi-Eshuh makes a determined but futile attempt to recapture the Sealand Dynasty. Babylonia has shrunk to the small and relatively weak nation it was upon its founding — the unequivocal verdict of every modern source. Subsequent kings confine themselves to building projects within Babylon itself.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1650": "Ammi-Ditana and Ammi-Saduqa produce building inscriptions and religious dedications — the activities of rulers with no prospect of territorial expansion. Ammi-Saduqa is best known for astronomical observations (the Venus Tablet). Meanwhile, Kassite groups from the Zagros Mountains accumulate pressure on Babylonian borders.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1600": "Samsu-Ditana rules over a small, economically weakened Babylonia under growing Kassite pressure. Hittite king Mursili I has begun his devastating raid down the Euphrates — 800 km from Aleppo to Babylon. City and dynasty still exist at 1600 BCE, but the sack is now imminent.",
  "Babylonian Empire|-1595": "Mursili I of the Hittite Empire sacks Babylon, carrying off the statues of Marduk and his consort Zarpanitu. Samsu-Ditana, the last Amorite king, killed or disappeared.",
  "Benin Empire|1180": "The Edo people establish Ubini (later Benin City) under the Ogiso ('Kings of the Sky') dynasty. A hierarchical cluster of villages with an emerging monarchy — not yet an empire or consolidated kingdom.",
  "Benin Empire|1260": "Oba Ewedo moves the royal palace, renames the state 'Ubini' (land of inexhaustible resources), implements a palace bureaucracy, and expands territorial influence. The first genuine centralization of royal authority over local chiefs. But the Oba still competes with powerful hereditary uzama chiefs.",
  "Benin Empire|1280": "Oba Oguola commissions the first phase of Benin's legendary earthwork walls — recognized as the largest earthworks carried out prior to the mechanical era — and introduces bronze-casting to the kingdom, beginning the tradition that will produce the famous Benin Bronzes.",
  "Benin Empire|1300": "Benin heavily involved in trade and the arts using copper, bronze, and brass. The Benin Bronzes tradition is growing.",
  "Benin Empire|1400": "Late pre-imperial Obas consolidating authority but have not yet launched territorial expansion. The uzama chiefs still exercise significant checks on royal power.",
  "Benin Empire|1440": "Ewuare the Great seizes the throne, restructures government completely, removes uzama chiefs' power to appoint the Oba, rebuilds Benin City with new walls and nine fortified gates, and launches military campaigns conquering Idah, Owo, and Akure.",
  "Benin Empire|1473": "Ewuare dies having transformed Benin into a genuine empire. In the very same year, first Portuguese contacts are initiated. The empire is fully operational — centralized bureaucratic monarchy, disciplined army, organized craft guilds, and world-class bronze, ivory, and iron artworks.",
  "Benin Empire|1480": "Oba Ozolua the Conqueror accedes; renowned for reportedly winning over 200 battles. Portuguese explorer João Afonso de Aveiro arrives (1485), establishing formal diplomatic relations.",
  "Benin Empire|1500": "Oba Esigie (1504–1550) defeats an Igala invasion and expands east; Benin gains political ascendancy over much of mid-western Nigeria. Establishes the position of Iyoba (Queen Mother); sends ambassadors to Portugal; maintains a Portuguese-language school in Edo. Brass imports boom, fueling the artistic golden age.",
  "Benin Empire|1560": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Orhogbua brings the empire to its greatest geographical extent, stretching beyond the Niger River to the east and to modern-day Ghana to the west. Establishes the military garrison of Eko (Lagos). Contemporary European accounts describe Benin fielding 180,000 men.",
  "Benin Empire|1600": "Ehengbuda (1578–1606), last of the five great warrior kings, dies. After his reign the empire gradually shrank, losing control over territories in the west. Trade with Portuguese and Dutch continues, artistic guilds are active — but the era of expansion is definitively over.",
  "Benin Empire|1650": "Between 1606 and 1700, six Obas rule, several with short and disputed reigns. A succession crisis and drop in cloth export value are documented. Territory continues to contract in the west while progressively weaker central authority leads to ongoing territorial erosion at the fringes.",
  "Benin Empire|1700": "Oba Ewuakpe presides over a near-catastrophic civil war; the palace is besieged and the monarchy nearly collapses entirely. Benin 'temporarily declined after 1700 after a civil war, then partially recovered' (Wikipedia).",
  "Benin Empire|1750": "Akenzua I and Eresoyen oversee partial stabilization and recovery. Trade in ivory, palm oil, and pepper with Portuguese and Dutch merchants continues. From 1730 onward, the slave trade becomes an increasing revenue source.",
  "Benin Empire|1800": "Britannica states the kingdom was 'weakened by violent succession struggles, some of which erupted into civil wars.' Weaker Obas sequester themselves in their palaces and indiscriminately grant aristocratic titles. Britain's Slave Trade Act (1807) removes a key revenue stream. Opening 19th-century conditions are severe.",
  "Benin Empire|1850": "Under Oba Adolo (1848–1888), Benin maintains its core territories but faces increasing pressure from British commercial interests. Kingdom adapts by concentrating on palm oil and restricting goods that can leave Benin. The 1880s–90s see escalating British demands for a protectorate treaty.",
  "Benin Empire|1897": "Following the killing of a British delegation, Admiral Rawson's force conquers and burns Benin City. Oba Ovonramwen is exiled to Calabar, where he dies in 1914. Around 3,000 bronze, ivory, and iron artworks looted and distributed to museums worldwide.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1822": "Prince Regent Pedro proclaims independence on 7 September 1822; acclaimed Emperor Pedro I on 12 October. British support smooths recognition. Faces the Confederation of the Equator (1824) in the northeast and Portuguese loyalists in some provinces.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1831": "Pedro I abdicates in favor of his five-year-old son and departs for Europe. The nine-year Regency (1831–1840) is characterized by serial rebellions: the Cabanagem in the Amazon kills ~30,000–40,000; the Balaiada in Maranhão; the Ragamuffin War in Rio Grande do Sul establishes a short-lived republic.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1840": "Pedro II, age 14, is declared of age to end the chaotic Regency. He proves to be an unusually capable monarch — personally opposed to slavery, committed to education and the sciences.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1850": "Internal rebellions ended (Ragamuffin War concluded 1845). Coffee surpasses sugar as Brazil's main export. The Eusébio de Queirós Law (1850) effectively ends the transatlantic slave trade. Brazil recognized diplomatically by all major European powers. Pedro II governs confidently with a meritocratic noble system.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1860": "High-water mark of Brazilian stability and prosperity before the Paraguayan War. Brazil wins the Platine War (1851–1852), asserting regional dominance over the River Plate. Coffee economy generating enormous revenues; navy modernizing with ironclads. Pedro II personally visits Europe and the United States, projecting enlightened monarchy.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1870": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — End of the Paraguayan War: Brazil bears the overwhelming burden of the deadliest interstate war in Latin American history and emerges victorious. Brazilian military decisively the strongest in South America. Maximum territorial security, military prestige, economic strength, and political stability all coincide.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1875": "Post-war consolidation; still strong but first reform tensions visible. Abolitionist movement growing; Law of the Free Womb (1871) satisfies neither slaveholders nor abolitionists. Military develops corporate identity and political ambitions distinct from civil government. Pedro II firmly in control but political coalitions beginning to fray.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1885": "Empire faces convergence of structural pressures: abolitionist movement politically dominant; Pedro II personally sympathetic, creating a fatal split with the slaveholding landed elite. Military increasingly receptive to Republican ideas. Republican Party growing. Heir apparent Princess Isabel deemed unacceptable by the ruling classes.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1888": "Princess Isabel signs the Lei Áurea (13 May 1888) abolishing slavery without compensation — Brazil becomes the last country in the Americas to end slavery. Morally correct but politically catastrophic: the slaveholding class, the monarchy's social backbone for 66 years, immediately withdraws its support.",
  "Brazilian Empire|1889": "General Deodoro da Fonseca leads a coup on 15 November 1889 with minimal planning and almost no public mobilization. Pedro II offers no resistance; he is exiled to Europe two days later and dies in Paris in 1891. The First Brazilian Republic is proclaimed.",
  "British Empire|1583": "Sir Humphrey Gilbert formally claims Newfoundland under Royal Charter — England's first overseas colony. No settlers were left behind; Gilbert was lost at sea on the return voyage; Walter Raleigh's subsequent Roanoke Colony (1585) would also fail. An aspirational claim, contested and without permanent substance.",
  "British Empire|1607": "Virginia Company establishes James Fort — the first permanent English foothold in the Americas. Two-thirds of Jamestown colonists die in the 'Starving Time' of 1609–10. East India Company (founded 1600) controls no territory in India.",
  "British Empire|1650": "England has established colonies in Virginia, Maryland, and Barbados (1627) and is competing for Caribbean islands. Navigation Acts of 1651 channel colonial trade through English ships.",
  "British Empire|1707": "Acts of Union unite England and Scotland into the Kingdom of Great Britain: single Parliament, unified currency, single internal market. The fiscal-military state — funded national debt, Bank of England (1694), professional army and navy — is now fully functional.",
  "British Empire|1713": "Treaty of Utrecht delivers Britain's largest territorial gains to date: Gibraltar and Minorca (strategic Mediterranean bases), Newfoundland, Nova Scotia, Hudson Bay, and the asiento — exclusive right to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America. Gibraltar becomes a permanent naval base controlling Atlantic-Mediterranean access.",
  "British Empire|1757": "Battle of Plassey (23 June 1757): Robert Clive and the East India Company defeat the Nawab of Bengal, gaining effective control of Bengal — the wealthiest province of the Mughal Empire.",
  "British Empire|1763": "Treaty of Paris ends the Seven Years' War. France cedes all of Canada and territory east of the Mississippi; Spain cedes Florida; Britain gains decisive commercial dominance in India. France's overseas empire is destroyed. Britain commands the Atlantic trade and is unchallenged at sea.",
  "British Empire|1783": "Treaty of Paris recognises American independence after Britain's defeat in the Revolutionary War. Britain loses its most populous overseas possession at a cost of £236.4 million. Yet recovery is rapid: Britain retains Canada, the Caribbean, India, and begins colonising Australia (Botany Bay, 1788).",
  "British Empire|1815": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Napoleon's final defeat leaves Britain without a serious international rival. Britain gains Malta, the Cape of Good Hope, and Ceylon. The Royal Navy is superior to any other two navies combined.",
  "British Empire|1850": "Britain produces approximately half of the world's iron and cotton cloth, and ~40% of its manufactured goods. The Great Exhibition of 1851 displays British industrial and imperial confidence. Free trade policy (adopted ~1846) allows Britain to dominate global commerce without formal annexation of every market.",
  "British Empire|1858": "Indian Rebellion of 1857–58 — the most serious challenge to British rule in the 19th century. Government of India Act 1858 dissolves the East India Company, transfers all powers to the Crown, and establishes the British Raj under a Viceroy.",
  "British Empire|1876": "Royal Titles Act confers 'Empress of India' on Queen Victoria. The Berlin Conference (1884–85) formalizes European partition of Africa, with Britain acquiring the largest share. Britain controls Suez (1875).",
  "British Empire|1900": "Second Boer War (1899–1902) exposes limits of British imperial power: a swift victory against Boer farmers drags on nearly three years, requiring 450,000 troops and using brutal concentration camps. Germany's industrial output challenges Britain's, and the Kaiser's naval building programme signals a looming rival.",
  "British Empire|1918": "Treaty of Versailles gives Britain control of Palestine, Transjordan, Iraq, and Tanganyika. By 1920, territorial maximum of 35.5 million km² is reached.",
  "British Empire|1931": "Statute of Westminster (11 December 1931) grants full legislative independence to the Dominions (Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, Irish Free State, Newfoundland). Empire transitioning into a looser voluntary association. The Great Depression exposes economic vulnerability — pound taken off the gold standard in 1931.",
  "British Empire|1945": "Britain emerges victorious from WWII but financially broken: Lend-Lease ends abruptly in August 1945 leaving dollar debts not repaid until 2006. Japan's occupation of Malaya, Singapore, Burma, and Hong Kong shattered the myth of British invincibility in Asia.",
  "British Empire|1947": "India and Pakistan become independent on 14–15 August 1947, in a partition killing 200,000–2 million people and displacing 10–20 million.",
  "British Empire|1956": "Egypt's Nasser nationalises the Suez Canal; Britain, France, and Israel mount a secret invasion. US President Eisenhower forces Britain to withdraw by threatening to block IMF support. PM Eden, humiliated, resigns in January 1957.",
  "British Empire|1960": "1960 is the 'Year of Africa': 17 African nations gain independence, including Nigeria. PM Macmillan's 'Wind of Change' speech formally acknowledges the inevitability of African decolonisation. Over the 1960s, Britain's entire African colonial empire dissolves. The 'East of Suez' defence commitment is abandoned by 1971.",
  "British Empire|1997": "At midnight on 30 June / 1 July 1997, the UK hands sovereignty over Hong Kong to the People's Republic of China — widely understood as the closing ceremony of the British Empire. Britain retains 14 small Overseas Territories, all self-governing and voluntary.",
  "Byzantine Empire|330": "Constantine I dedicates Constantinople on 11 May 330 — J. B. Bury called it 'a permanent division between the Eastern and Western halves of the Empire.' Introduces the gold solidus coin, which remains the world's most stable currency for 700 years.",
  "Byzantine Empire|395": "Theodosius I dies; the empire is permanently split, the Eastern half going to his son Arcadius. The Eastern half is better positioned than the West: stronger urban culture, greater financial resources, and the ability to pay off barbarian pressure.",
  "Byzantine Empire|476": "Western Rome falls — Romulus Augustus deposed by Odoacer. The Eastern Empire was already de facto independent since 395, but now stands as the sole Roman empire claiming the full inheritance of 500 years of Roman civilization.",
  "Byzantine Empire|527": "Justinian I takes sole power; reconquest program — recovering Africa, Italy, and Spain from barbarian kingdoms — will begin in 533 CE. Corpus Juris Civilis initiated almost immediately. Constantinople is the Mediterranean world's largest city, with half a million inhabitants.",
  "Byzantine Empire|541": "Justinian's reconquests substantially complete: North Africa taken 533–534, much of Italy recovered by 540, Ravenna captured. Corpus Juris Civilis complete. Hagia Sophia (completed 537 CE) stands as the world's largest building. Near-peak territorial achievement.",
  "Byzantine Empire|565": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Justinian I dies having achieved an empire stretching from southern Spain to Syria, from the Danube to the Nile. The entire Mediterranean coastline under Byzantine control. Corpus Juris Civilis codified all Roman law.",
  "Byzantine Empire|600": "Emperor Maurice (582–602) manages multiple simultaneous crises: Avars and Slavs devastating the Balkans; expensive Sasanian Persian campaigns. Nevertheless maintains the empire's grip on its richest provinces — Egypt, Syria, and North Africa.",
  "Byzantine Empire|610": "Heraclius deposes Phocas and inherits an empire in profound crisis: Persian forces under Khosrow II had already advanced deep into Byzantine territory. By 615 CE, Persians will occupy Egypt, Syria, and Palestine — roughly half the empire's fiscal revenue.",
  "Byzantine Empire|642": "The Arab conquest moves with stunning speed: the Battle of Yarmouk (636 CE) is decisive; Jerusalem surrenders to Caliph Umar in 637 CE. Egypt — the empire's wealthiest province, the grain supply of Constantinople — is conquered between 639 and 642 CE.",
  "Byzantine Empire|700": "Egypt lost 58 years ago; Syria lost 62 years ago; the Exarchate of Africa (Carthage) falls to the Umayyads in 698 CE. The first Arab siege of Constantinople (674–678 CE) barely repelled.",
  "Byzantine Empire|718": "Umayyad Caliphate mounts the most massive assault on Constantinople: 80,000 troops and 1,800 ships under Maslama.",
  "Byzantine Empire|867": "Basil I inaugurates the Macedonian dynasty, historians' 'Golden Age' of Byzantium. Empire stabilized; the themes (military-agricultural units) defensive structure allows emperors to plan wars of reconquest. His son Leo VI compiles the Basilika, the Tactica, and the Book of the Eparch.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1000": "Basil II presides over the most powerful Byzantine state since Justinian.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1071": "Battle of Manzikert (26 August 1071): Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes is defeated and captured by Seljuk Sultan Alp Arslan — the first Byzantine emperor ever taken prisoner by an Islamic power. Romanos returns home, deposed, blinded, and dies.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1143": "The Komnenian dynasty (1081–1185) represents the most remarkable recovery in Byzantine history. Manuel I (1143–1180) presides over what Wikipedia terms 'one of the peaks in Byzantine prosperity and cultural life.' Venice-sponsored trade gives Constantinople 60,000 Latin residents.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1204": "Crusaders and Venetian merchants divert the Fourth Crusade and storm Constantinople on 13 April 1204. The sack is systematic and brutal: churches looted, Hagia Sophia desecrated, libraries destroyed. A Latin Empire is established under Baldwin of Flanders. Byzantine survivors establish three successor states.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1261": "Michael VIII Palaiologos recaptures Constantinople from the Latin Empire — the Hagia Sophia restored to Orthodox worship.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1350": "A devastating civil war (1341–1347) 'delivered a mortal blow' to remaining Byzantine power. The Black Death struck Constantinople in 1347. By 1350 CE, the Ottomans had crossed into Europe (1354) and the empire is reduced to Constantinople plus northern Greece, Thrace, and the Peloponnese.",
  "Byzantine Empire|1453": "Sultan Mehmed II's forces breach the Theodosian Walls on 29 May 1453 after a 53-day siege. Constantine XI Palaiologos dies fighting on the walls. The last Byzantine lands — Mistra and Trebizond — fall in 1460 and 1461 respectively.",
  "Carolingian Empire|768": "Pepin the Short dies; realm divided between Charlemagne and his younger brother Carloman. Charlemagne receives outer coastal territories while Carloman receives the richer inner heartlands. The two brothers refuse to cooperate even when Pope Stephen III requests military aid against the Lombards.",
  "Carolingian Empire|771": "Carloman dies unexpectedly at age 20 on 4 December 771. Charlemagne immediately seizes his brother's territories, ignoring the claims of Carloman's infant sons. Now sole ruler of the unified Frankish realm. The beginning of real Carolingian imperial potential, though no external conquests have yet occurred.",
  "Carolingian Empire|774": "Pope Adrian I appeals to Charlemagne against Lombard aggression; Charlemagne crosses the Alps, besieges Pavia, and deposes King Desiderius in June 774. Crowns himself 'King of the Lombards.' Confirms the Donation of Pepin, establishing the Papal States and cementing the Frankish-papal alliance.",
  "Carolingian Empire|778": "Military expansion in full drive but Battle of Roncevaux Pass sees Charlemagne's rearguard ambushed by Basques; rearguard commander Roland is killed. The Spanish campaign yields nothing. The Saxon front requires brutal annual campaigns and mass deportations (Massacre of Verden, 782, executes 4,500 Saxons).",
  "Carolingian Empire|800": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Pope Leo III crowns Charlemagne Imperator Romanorum in St. Peter's Basilica on 25 December 800. Empire spans from the Pyrenees to the Elbe, from the North Sea to central Italy.",
  "Carolingian Empire|814": "Charlemagne dies; Louis the Pious inherits an intact, administratively coherent empire. But problems are already visible: Louis exiles Charlemagne's trusted advisers and must provide kingdoms for three sons — Lothair, Pepin, and Louis the German — whose competing claims will ultimately destroy the empire's unity.",
  "Carolingian Empire|830": "First civil war erupts: Frankish army mutinies in February 830; Lothair and Pepin side with rebellious nobles; Louis is deposed and imprisoned.",
  "Carolingian Empire|840": "Louis the Pious dies on 20 June 840. Eldest son Lothair immediately claims the entire empire, disregarding agreed partitions; Louis the German and Charles the Bald fight for their inheritances.",
  "Carolingian Empire|843": "Treaty of Verdun (10 August 843) divides the empire into three kingdoms: West Francia (Charles the Bald), East Francia (Louis the German), and Middle Francia (Lothair I).",
  "Carolingian Empire|870": "After Lothair I's death (855), Middle Francia itself is partitioned three ways. When Lothair II dies in 869, his uncles immediately divide Lotharingia between themselves at the Treaty of Meerssen (870).",
  "Carolingian Empire|884": "Charles III ('the Fat') inherits the kingdoms of his dying relatives until he briefly rules all Carolingian territories. The reunification is hollow: in 885–886, Vikings besiege Paris and Charles pays tribute to withdraw — perceived at court as cowardly and incompetent.",
  "Carolingian Empire|888": "Charles the Fat dies on 13 January 888. The empire immediately fragments: Arnulf retains Carinthia; Count Odo of Paris is elected king of West Francia — the first non-Carolingian king; Ranulf II takes Aquitaine; Berengar takes Italy; Rudolph I takes Upper Burgundy.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1225": "Khanate does not yet exist as an independent entity. Genghis Khan is alive and the Mongol Empire is a single unified state. Chagatai received his appanage only upon Genghis's death on August 25, 1227.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1227": "Chagatai Khan formally receives his appanage after Genghis Khan's death: roughly the former Qara Khitai Empire including Transoxiana, the Ili River valley, Kashgaria, the Tarim Basin, and Semirechye.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1250": "Eight years of chaos after Chagatai's death (1242): Karakorum installs and removes puppet rulers at will. The cities of Transoxiana remain directly administered by Mahmud Yalavach's dynasty, answering to Karakorum. The Chagataid khans have no independent foreign policy, army command, or revenue base.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1260": "The Toluid Civil War (Kublai vs. Ariq Böke) creates a power vacuum. Alghu rebels against Ariq Böke, independently seizes territories, gains control of the Great Khan's Transoxianan administrators for the first time, and drives off Kaidu's early incursions.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1300": "At its height in the late 13th century, the khanate extended from the Amu Darya to the Altai Mountains. However, the Chagataid khans are Kaidu's appointees. Duwa (r.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1307": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Kaidu dies in 1301. Duwa immediately throws off allegiance to Kaidu's son Chapar; both Yuan dynasty and Duwa attack Chapar, forcing him to surrender all of Kaidu's former territory to Duwa in 1306.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1320": "Kebek Khan institutes standardized coinage — small silver coins so widely used they are called 'kebeks,' ancestor of the Russian kopek — and establishes a permanent sedentary capital at Qarshi in southern Uzbekistan.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1334": "Tarmashirin converts to Islam — the first Chagataid khan to fully embrace it — moves his court to Transoxiana, and raids deep into the Delhi Sultanate reaching Delhi itself.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1340": "After Tarmashirin's death (1334), a series of short-reigning, powerless khans are installed in rapid succession. The eastern tribes become effectively autonomous.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1363": "Tughlugh Timur's son Ilyas Khoja is expelled from Transoxiana by Timur and Amir Husayn. The western Chagataid line now serves as Timur's legitimizing figureheads — 'political prisoners in Samarkand' (Wikipedia).",
  "Chagatai Empire|1370": "The eastern Chagatai khanate (Moghulistan) continues under Chagatayid rulers centered on the Tarim Basin and Ili River steppe — but the Dughlat tribal chiefs install and control the khans. Timur repeatedly invades but cannot subjugate its nomadic inhabitants.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1400": "Under Uvais Khan and Esen Buqa (1428–1462), the Moghulistan court is at war with Oirats and raids the Timurid Empire. The khanate still controls the Tarim Basin and benefits from overland trade, but lacks any capacity to project power regionally.",
  "Chagatai Empire|1500": "Around 1462, following the death of Esen Buqa, Moghulistan splits into the Turpan Khanate and the Yarkent Khanate. By 1500, neither successor is recognizable as the Chagatai Khanate — they are smaller regional polities, though still Chagatayid in dynastic descent.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-626": "In 626 BCE, Nabopolassar exploited the post-Ashurbanipal power vacuum to seize Babylon, repel an Assyrian counterattack, and claim the throne on November 22/23 — the first native Babylonian kingship in over a century. Yet the kingdom remained existentially threatened: Assyria's army was intact, loyalties fluid, and survival uncertain for nearly a decade.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-620": "By 620 BCE, Nabopolassar had secured full control of Babylonia — Uruk and Nippur firmly held, all Assyrian outposts eliminated, aided by a timely internal Assyrian revolt in 622 BCE. He turned outward, initiating building programs in Babylon and expanding irrigation infrastructure. No longer a rebel clinging to power, but a consolidated king — sovereign within his core, not yet beyond it.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-612": "In 612 BCE, Nabopolassar and the Median king Cyaxares besieged and sacked Nineveh after three months, killing the Assyrian king Sinsharishkun and shattering the ancient world's greatest empire. Babylon claimed Mesopotamia and the Levant. Yet Egypt under Necho II physically held the Levant, and a remnant Assyrian king still clung to Harran with Egyptian backing — western consolidation remained unfinished.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-609": "In 609 BCE, Nabopolassar — now fighting alongside his son Nebuchadnezzar — crushed the last Assyrian king Ashur-uballit II and his Egyptian allies at Harran, extinguishing the Neo-Assyrian Empire entirely. Yet Egypt retained firm control of the Levant: Necho II had swept through Palestine and Phoenicia unopposed, killing Judah's king Josiah at Megiddo. Babylon ruled Mesopotamia; Egypt held the west.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-605": "In 605 BCE, crown prince Nebuchadnezzar annihilated Pharaoh Necho II's army at Carchemish, ending Egyptian presence in the Levant and securing Babylonian control of Syria and Palestine. Nabopolassar died shortly after; Nebuchadnezzar raced back to claim the throne. Babylon now stood as the uncontested supreme power of the ancient Near East — no rival remained in Mesopotamia or the Levant.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-601": "In 601 BCE, Nebuchadnezzar invaded Egypt and failed — both armies suffering heavy casualties, forcing him to spend the following year rebuilding his chariot forces. The defeat had immediate political consequences: Levantine vassals, including Judah under Jehoiakim, defected back toward Egypt. The empire's core power was unchanged, but its military prestige was damaged and its western flank destabilized.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-600": "At 600 BCE, Babylon was dominant but not yet at its peak. The failed Egyptian campaign had triggered western rebellions; Nebuchadnezzar spent 600/599 BCE rebuilding his military and suppressing instability. The Levant was secured in principle after Carchemish but remained administratively unintegrated — Judah's defection toward Egypt would soon force a direct Jerusalem campaign. Strong and dominant, but pre-zenith.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-597": "In 597 BCE, Nebuchadnezzar besieged Jerusalem, accepted Jehoiachin's surrender, and deported Judah's elite — craftsmen, soldiers, and nobles — to Babylonia in the first wave of the Babylonian captivity. Following campaigns against Ashkelon, Egypt, and Arab tribes, the Levant was now demonstrably under Babylonian control. Military prestige fully restored, the empire stood on the cusp of its peak.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-587": "In 587/586 BCE, Nebuchadnezzar destroyed Jerusalem after an 18-month siege — razing Solomon's Temple, burning the city, and deporting its population, erasing Judah as a state. Simultaneously, a 13-year siege of Tyre began. Babylon's territory spanned the Persian Gulf to Egypt's borders; the Ishtar Gate, Etemenanki, and palace expansions marked its cultural zenith. Militarily, territorially, and politically — the empire was at its absolute peak.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-580": "At 580 BCE, Babylon was at the height of its power — building programs in full execution, trade networks thriving, no military rival, and Nebuchadnezzar with nearly two decades of reign remaining. The Tyre siege continued at cost, and Egypt remained an unresolved border tension, but the empire was otherwise unchallenged across every dimension.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-573": "Around 573 BCE, Tyre surrendered after a 13-year siege — delivering political submission and tribute, though the Tyrians reportedly evacuated much of their wealth by sea. The Levantine coast was now fully integrated into Babylon's sphere. The prolonged siege had been costly in men and material, Nebuchadnezzar was deep into his reign, and territorial expansion had reached its ceiling.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-562": "Nebuchadnezzar died in 562 BCE, leaving an empire still formidable — full treasury, experienced army, intact vassal network, Babylon at its greatest architectural extent. But his death immediately exposed the empire's fatal weakness: everything had rested on one exceptional ruler. His successor Amel-Marduk was already controversial, and the institutional structure proved incapable of producing a second strong king.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-560": "Amel-Marduk was assassinated after two years by his brother-in-law Neriglissar — a capable general but connected to the royal line only through marriage. The Chaldean dynastic bloodline of Nabopolassar was effectively extinct. The empire remained functionally operational, but dynastic illegitimacy had taken hold — four rulers in six years signaled a fundamental collapse of political succession.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-556": "Labashi-Marduk was killed after nine months; Nabonidus — outside the royal bloodline entirely — seized power. Three regime changes in six years. The empire remained intact, but Nabopolassar's legitimate dynasty was gone, and Nabonidus's priorities would alienate both the priesthood and the military.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-552": "Around 552 BCE, Nabonidus abandoned Babylon for Tayma in Arabia for nearly a decade. The New Year's Festival — requiring the king's physical presence — was cancelled repeatedly, shattering religious legitimacy. Belshazzar governed as co-regent but unconvincingly. The empire held its shape; its governance had not.",
  "Chaldean Empire|-539": "In October 539 BCE, Persian forces defeated Babylon at Opis, then entered the city itself without resistance — the priesthood, alienated by Nabonidus's neglect of the Marduk cult, offered no defense. Nabonidus was captured; the Neo-Babylonian Empire ceased to exist. With its fall ended Mesopotamian political independence entirely — a continuous civilizational tradition stretching back to Sumer, permanently extinguished.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1600": "In c. 1600, Suleiman Solong founds the Keira dynasty in the Marrah highlands, introducing the hakura land-grant system and beginning Islamisation of the royal court. The state is territorially confined, institutionally basic, and still working to subdue surrounding groups including the Baggara Arabs. Legitimate and structured, but tiny and fragile.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1650": "By 1650, Solong and his successors expand north beyond the Marrah Mountains. The Darb El Arba'in trade route to Egypt grows active, generating revenue. Educated Islamic scholars imported from Bornu and Bagirmi strengthen administration. Wadai is now a tributary. Meaningful growth, but still a regional — not major — Sahelian power.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1700": "Under Ahmed Bukr (c. 1682–1722), Islam becomes the official state religion. The Darb El Arba'in flourishes, bringing significant wealth; literate Islamic immigrants deepen governance and law. Bukr personally commissions slave raids southward. Wadai remains tributary. A mature, prosperous mid-sized Islamic sultanate with functioning economy and regional significance.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1730": "After Bukr's death, his decree that each son rule in turn ignites an intermittent civil war lasting until 1785/86. Simultaneously, Wadai — a former tributary — asserts independence; repeated Darfuri campaigns to reduce it fail. The sultanate remains territorially intact and economically functional, but is politically destabilised for over half a century.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1750": "By 1750, the succession dispute is ~30 years old with no resolution until 1785/86. The civil war is intermittent — trade revenues from the Darb El Arba'in continue and the sultanate functions. However, resources consumed by dynastic competition prevent further expansion. Not near collapse, but significantly below its later peak coherence.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1786": "The succession civil war concludes c. 1785/86. Under Sultan Mohammed Tayrab (r. c. 1752–1785), the sultanate annexes Kordofan — transformationally doubling the empire's size and opening direct Nile Valley trade access. Al-Tūnisī describes a sophisticated, thriving court. Internal crisis resolved; empire poised for its final flourishing under Mohammed al-Fadl.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1800": "Under Mohammed al-Fadl (c. 1803–1838), the sultanate reaches its documented zenith. By c. 1800–1812, the empire stretches from Darfur to Kordofan to the western White Nile — the size of present-day Nigeria. Al-Tūnisī records a sophisticated, wealthy court with active diplomatic ties across the Sahel and Nile Valley. The Darb El Arba'in carries thousands of pilgrims and merchants annually.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1821": "In 1821, Muhammad Ali of Egypt conquers the Funj Sultanate and Kordofan — Darfur's eastern buffer. A Darfuri counterattack is routed near Bara on 19 August 1821. North–south trade routes begin bypassing Darfur via Egypt, triggering economic recession. The sultanate retains its heartland and nominal independence, but faces a vastly more powerful neighbour.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1850": "After Mohammed al-Fadl's death c. 1838, his successors face deepening structural decline: Kordofan lost permanently in 1821, trade revenues severed, Egyptian-controlled slave markets outcompeting Darfuri raiders, and the Fur elite divided by succession disputes. The final sultan Ibrahim faces overstretched resources when Al-Zubayr eventually moves against him. Trajectory is clearly downward.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1874": "On 24 October 1874, Al-Zubayr Rahma Mansur — a Sudanese slave-trading warlord with tacit Egyptian backing — decisively defeats Sultan Ibrahim, who is killed in battle. His uncle Hassab Allah continues resistance in the Marrah Mountains but is captured in 1875 and removed to Cairo. The formal sultanate has ceased to exist; only fragmented mountain resistance survived, briefly.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1884": "By December 1883, Mahdist forces drive out Turco-Egyptian administration; Rudolf von Slatin, Egyptian governor of Darfur, surrenders. Darfur is incorporated into the Mahdist State, which forcibly relocates western tribal warriors to Omdurman, sparking rebellions. No Keira political authority exists — the dynasty survives in exile but holds no territory or governance whatsoever.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1898": "After the British defeat the Mahdist state at Omdurman in September 1898, Ali Dinar — grandson of Mohammed al-Fadl, formerly a Mahdist prisoner — returns to el-Fashir. Recognised by the Anglo-Egyptian Sudan government in 1899 for £500 annual tribute, he reconstructs the palace, regrants hakura titles, and repels encroaching Arab nomads. Restored in form, but circumscribed as a tribute-paying client.",
  "Darfur Sultanate|1916": "During WWI, Ali Dinar's relations with Anglo-Egyptian Sudan deteriorate under Ottoman Pan-Islamic influence and the Libyan Sanūsīya movement; he pledges allegiance to the Ottoman Empire. The British launch an expedition; Ali Dinar's army is defeated at the Battle of Birinjīya near al-Fashir. He is killed shortly after, fleeing into the Marrah Mountains. Darfur is formally annexed into Anglo-Egyptian Sudan. The Keira sultanate ceases to exist in any form.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1206": "In 1206, Aibak declares independence in Delhi having governed it as viceroy since 1192. Rival claimants contest his authority, Rajput rulers resist in every direction, and administration rests entirely on personal loyalty of Turkish slave-officers. He dies in 1210, leaving institutions barely begun — real military capacity, but too contested and institutionally thin.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1211": "From 1211, Iltutmish eliminates rivals — defeating Yildiz at Tarain (1215), suppressing Qabacha in Sindh, crushing Rajput resistance. He introduces the Iqtadari revenue system, the silver tanka, and the Council of Forty. Yet his early reign remains principally a war for survival; the institutional edifice is being laid, not yet realised.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1229": "In February 1229, Abbasid Caliph Al-Mustansir grants Iltutmish the titles Sultan of Hindustan and Deputy of the Faithful — making Delhi the only recognised Muslim sovereign state in South Asia. He completes the Qutb Minar, issues standardised coinage, builds India's first Islamic mausoleum (1231), and wisely denies asylum to the fleeing Khwarezm Shah, averting Mongol wrath.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1266": "In 1266, Balban — de facto regent for 25 years — officially assumes the sultanate. He dismantles the Council of Forty, introduces Persian court prostration ceremonies to elevate royal mystique, builds a spy network, and personally campaigns to pacify Mewat, the Doab, and Bengal. The sultanate under Balban is stable, feared, and approaching the threshold of imperial power.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1300": "By 1300, Alauddin Khalji — who murdered his uncle to seize power in 1296 — has conquered Gujarat (1299, sacking Somnath), repelled two Mongol invasions, and introduced four state-run markets with fixed prices and direct troop payment replacing the iqta system. Ranthambhor falls in 1301. Roughly 55–60% of eventual territorial maximum, but already at near-peak military sophistication.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1311": "By 1311, Malik Kafur's Deccan campaigns extract submission from Hoysala, Warangal, and the Pandya treasury at Madurai. The sultanate controls Gujarat, Rajputana, Devagiri, Warangal, Dwarasamudra, and Madurai. Alauddin's army numbers ~475,000; his market system stabilises grain prices through famine. This is the Khalji administrative and military apex — just short of the Tughlaq territorial peak still to come.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1320": "Alauddin dies January 1316. Malik Kafur seizes power briefly; a six-year-old is installed; three more sultans follow through coups. Khusrau Khan murders the last Khalji in 1320 but is himself killed months later by Ghazi Malik — who renames himself Ghiyath al-Din Tughlaq. The territorial core survives intact, but institutional coherence is badly shaken.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1330": "Under Muhammad bin Tughlaq (r. 1325–1351), the sultanate reaches its greatest ever extent by 1330 — Punjab to Madurai, including Bengal, Malwa, Gujarat, and the Deccan, covering over 3 million km². His army numbers ~370,000. He moves the capital to Daulatabad in 1327 and employs Ibn Battuta as a court judge. The largest the Delhi Sultanate ever was.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1350": "By 1350, the peak empire has unravelled: Vijayanagara emerges (1336), Mabar declares independence (1338), Bengal revolts (1339), and the Bahmani Sultanate permanently severs the Deccan (1347). Muhammad's copper token currency experiment (1329–1332) crashes the economy; a seven-year famine from 1335 devastates Delhi. Firuz Shah takes the throne in 1351 but never attempts to reconquer the south.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1388": "Firuz Shah (r. 1351–1388) stabilised the north but made iqta assignments hereditary, fatally weakening central authority. After his death, terminal civil war erupts; by 1394 two men simultaneously claim the sultanate from different cities. Gujarat, Bengal, Jaunpur, Malwa, and the Deccan are all autonomous. A royal title without an empire — one city, nothing more.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1398": "On 17 December 1398, Timur defeats Sultan Nasir-ud-Din Mahmud outside Delhi, killing 100,000–200,000. The city endures fifteen days of massacre and looting; its artisans are transported to Samarkand, gutting the economy. The sultan fled to Gujarat beforehand. Capital destroyed, treasury emptied, skilled population gone — yet the legal sultanate technically persists, hence score of 2 rather than 0.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1420": "Khizr Khan, Timur's appointed vassal governor, founds the Sayyid dynasty in 1414. He rules as Rayat-i-Ala — vassal of the Timurid empire — and never claims the title of Sultan. The Sayyids contend on equal footing with petty neighbouring principalities, failing to recover Punjab or Malwa. Barely more than a city-state, continuing the post-Timur collapse.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1451": "In 1451, Alam Shah peacefully surrenders the sultanate to Bahlul Khan Lodi, the Punjab's most powerful Afghan chief. Over 38 years Bahlul annexes the Jaunpur Sultanate (1479) and extends control over Gwalior and upper Uttar Pradesh. Delhi functions again, Afghan nobles provide real military strength — a genuine recovery over the Sayyid nadir, though far below former imperial heights.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1500": "Under Sikandar Lodi (r. 1489–1517), the sultanate expands from Punjab to the Ganga valley, expels Husain Sharqi from Bihar (1496), annexes Chanderi and Gwalior, founds Agra (1503/04), abolishes corn duties, and introduces standardised land measurement. A revived, functioning regional power — stronger than anything since Firuz Shah — but structurally incomparable to the Khalji or Tughlaq imperial scale.",
  "Delhi Sultanate|1526": "Ibrahim Lodi's push for royal absolutism alienates Afghan nobles accustomed to near-equal status, triggering multiple rebellions. His own governor Daulat Khan and uncle Alam Khan invite Babur to invade. On 21 April 1526 at Panipat, Babur deploys field artillery — the first effective use of cannon in Indian warfare — and kills Ibrahim on the battlefield. Three centuries of the Delhi Sultanate end; the Mughal Empire begins.",
  "Dutch Empire|1581": "The Dutch Republic's Act of Abjuration (1581) breaks from the Spanish Empire. Commercially sophisticated and politically inventive, the Republic has enormous potential — but no overseas empire. The first expedition to the East Indies won't depart until 1595. Foundations are being imagined, not laid.",
  "Dutch Empire|1595": "Cornelis de Houtman leads the first Dutch fleet to the Spice Islands. One-third of the crew survives, yet the route proves commercially viable. Competing voorcompagniëen race to follow — proving the model while creating the unsustainable chaos that will force consolidation.",
  "Dutch Empire|1602": "The States-General consolidates the voorcompagniëen into the VOC, granting it a 21-year monopoly east of the Cape of Good Hope, plus rights to wage war, govern, and coin money. The institutional architecture of the Dutch colonial empire is now formally in place.",
  "Dutch Empire|1621": "The Dutch West India Company (WIC) extends Dutch power to the Americas and Africa, completing a two-hemisphere structure. Batavia (1619) anchors Asia; New Amsterdam (1626) and northeast Brazil anchor the west. The Dutch Republic expands simultaneously across Asia, the Americas, and Africa — unprecedented in history.",
  "Dutch Empire|1641": "The VOC captures Malacca from Portugal, controlling the strategic chokepoint between the Indian Ocean and South China Sea. The Dutch empire now spans Batavia, the Spice Islands, Taiwan, Ceylon, Japan's Dejima, New Amsterdam, Suriname, and West Africa. Dutch merchant tonnage exceeds England and France combined.",
  "Dutch Empire|1650": "At its 1652 height, the Dutch Republic — population ~1.5 million — controls colonies across North America, the Caribbean, Brazil, Africa, India, Ceylon, Indonesia, Japan, and Taiwan. The VOC is the world's most valuable company. Rembrandt and Vermeer paint. No empire relative to metropolitan population has matched this.",
  "Dutch Empire|1667": "1667 is marked as the Dutch Golden Age zenith, yet cracks are visible. The Treaty of Breda (1667) trades New Amsterdam for Suriname — a deal reversed in 1674. The coming Rampjaar of 1672 will expose the Republic's fatal weakness: too small to fight on every front.",
  "Dutch Empire|1672": "The Rampjaar: Louis XIV invades with 120,000 troops; England attacks by sea; Münster and Cologne open eastern fronts. Amsterdam floods its polders to halt France. The Republic survives but New Amsterdam is permanently ceded to Britain in 1674. Taiwan had already fallen to Koxinga in 1662.",
  "Dutch Empire|1700": "Following the Rampjaar, the Dutch have lost New Amsterdam (1674), Taiwan (1662), and the Japanese silver trade (~1685). Britain overtakes the Dutch as the leading slave-trading nation by 1700. The VOC's intra-Asian growth stalls post-1670. Still the world's second-largest colonial empire, but no longer unchallenged hegemon.",
  "Dutch Empire|1783": "The Fourth Anglo-Dutch War (1780–1784) destroys much of the Dutch fleet — a financial catastrophe for both the VOC and WIC. French revolutionary armies invade in 1794–95. Britain seizes Cape Colony, Ceylon, and Coromandel. Both colonial companies are mortally wounded; the Dutch Republic becomes a French satellite state.",
  "Dutch Empire|1800": "The VOC is dissolved 1 January 1800 after going bankrupt — its 200-year run ended. The Netherlands, now the French-controlled Batavian Republic, cannot defend its colonies. Britain has seized Cape Colony (1795), Ceylon (1796), and temporarily Java (1811–16). The empire is reduced to an Indonesian rump under foreign rule.",
  "Dutch Empire|1816": "The Congress of Vienna (1815) restores Java, the Moluccas, Suriname, and the Caribbean to the new Kingdom of the Netherlands. But Cape Colony and Ceylon remain permanently British. The 1824 Anglo-Dutch Treaty formalises the divide: Dutch take the East Indies; Britain takes the Malay Peninsula and Singapore.",
  "Dutch Empire|1850": "Governor-General Van den Bosch's cultuurstelsel (1830) forces Javanese peasants to devote one-fifth of farmland to Dutch export crops. By the 1860s, Java's revenues constitute ~33% of total Dutch state income, financing Dutch industrialisation. The empire is highly profitable but geographically shrunken — a single-theater colonial dependency.",
  "Dutch Empire|1900": "The Aceh War (1873–1912) — 100,000+ deaths — completes the Dutch 'pacification' of the Indonesian archipelago. The Dutch East Indies now produces most of the world's quinine, over a third of its rubber, a quarter of coconut products, and a fifth of tea, sugar, coffee, and oil.",
  "Dutch Empire|1942": "Eight days after Pearl Harbor, Japan invades the Dutch East Indies. The Battle of the Java Sea (27 February 1942) is a decisive Japanese naval victory; Java falls 9 March. The entire Dutch East Indies — 350 years of colonial enterprise — is occupied in under 90 days. The Netherlands itself has been under Nazi occupation since May 1940.",
  "Dutch Empire|1949": "Indonesia declares independence 17 August 1945. Two Dutch 'Police Actions' (1947, 1948–49) fail to reassert control. US pressure — threatening to cut Marshall Plan aid — forces withdrawal. On 27 December 1949 the Netherlands formally recognises Indonesian independence. Only Suriname and the Netherlands Antilles remain.",
  "Dutch Empire|1975": "Suriname's independence on 25 November 1975 ends the Dutch colonial empire — 380 years after Houtman's first voyage in 1595. Remaining Caribbean territories (Aruba, Curaçao, Sint Maarten) continue as autonomous partners within the Kingdom of the Netherlands, not colonies. The arc from world-dominating republic to post-colonial state is complete.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1550": "Ahmose I (c.1550–1525 BCE) defeats the Hyksos — the Asiatic dynasty controlling Lower Egypt for over a century — and reunites Egypt for the first time since the Middle Kingdom. He recaptures Thebes, reconquers Nubia, and rebuilds state institutions. Empire is unified but not yet dominant.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1510": "Building on Ahmose I's reunified Egypt, Thutmose I (c.1504–1492 BCE) becomes the first pharaoh to cross the Euphrates, winning allegiance from Syrian princes. He campaigns to the 4th Nile cataract in Nubia and begins building at Karnak. The empire's strategic perimeter — Euphrates to 4th cataract — is now defined.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1458": "Hatshepsut (c.1479–1458 BCE) maintains Thutmose I's empire, launching the famous Punt trade expedition and an extraordinary building program at Karnak. She leaves Thutmose III a prosperous kingdom — but having fought no major wars, Egypt's military grip on the Levantine periphery has softened, as the instant Canaanite rebellion at Megiddo proves.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1450": "Responding to the Canaanite rebellion, Thutmose III wins the Battle of Megiddo (1457 BCE) via a daring march through the Aruna pass, defeating 150+ rulers. Over 17 campaigns he destroys Mitanni power, crosses the Euphrates, and installs Pax Egyptica — taking royal children as hostages. Empire spans Nubia's 5th cataract to the Euphrates.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1425": "Thutmose III dies c.1425 BCE after 54 total years of rule. Wikipedia states he brought Egypt to its greatest territorial extent. The Karnak Annals record tribute from Babylon, Assyria, and the Hittites. The empire spans Nubia's 5th cataract to the Euphrates — the territorial maximum, though peak wealth and diplomacy still lie ahead under Amenhotep III.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1370": "Amenhotep III (c.1390–1353 BCE) inherits Thutmose III's territorial empire and transforms it into a civilisation of unmatched wealth and prestige. He builds the Luxor Temple colonnade, the Colossi of Memnon, and Malkata palace. The Amarna Letters show every Near Eastern power — Babylon, Mitanni, Assyria, the Hittites, Cyprus — writing to him in deference. No major war needed.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1336": "Akhenaten (1353–1336 BCE) dismantles Amenhotep III's diplomatic order: he abolishes traditional polytheism, suppresses the Amun cult, and moves the capital to Akhetaten. The Amarna Letters document vassal rulers of Canaan and Syria begging for military help against Hittite-backed rebels — requests Akhenaten ignores. The province of Amurru is lost to the Hittites. The empire retains its core but haemorrhages its periphery.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1323": "Tutankhamun (c.1336–1327 BCE) immediately restores the Amun cult and abandons Akhetaten. His Restoration Stela acknowledges the damage: temples 'gone to pieces.' Horemheb (c.1323–1295 BCE) destroys Akhetaten, erases Akhenaten's name from monuments, and reforms the judiciary and military. Internal stability is recovered — but Kadesh remains in Hittite hands, lost since Akhenaten's neglect.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1279": "Seti I (c.1294–1279 BCE) campaigns into the Levant and retakes Kadesh from the Hittites — recovering the city lost under Akhenaten 70 years earlier. His Karnak reliefs are among the most detailed military records in Egyptian history. Ramesses II inherits a reconsolidated, militarily active empire, but faces a revitalised Hittite Empire under Muwatalli II.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1274": "Ramesses II (c.1279–1213 BCE) leads ~20,000 troops and thousands of chariots against Muwatalli II's Hittites at Kadesh on the Orontes — history's largest chariot battle. The result is tactically indecisive; both sides claim victory. Egypt remains enormous and wealthy, but cannot dislodge the Hittites from northern Syria. The Amenhotep III-era prestige ceiling is not recovered.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1213": "Ramesses II dies after 66 years of rule. His 1258 BCE peace treaty with the Hittites is history's first recorded bilateral peace treaty. Abu Simbel and Pi-Ramesses cement his monumental legacy. But Egypt has failed to recover northern Syria, fought costly Libyan campaigns, and faces mounting succession conflicts — the empire is formidable but past its strategic zenith.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1200": "Merenptah (c.1213–1203 BCE) defeats a Libyan-Sea Peoples coalition in 1209 BCE, naming the Ekwesh, Sherden, and Shekelesh in his Karnak inscription. But after his death the 19th Dynasty fractures in civil war. Simultaneously the Bronze Age Collapse destroys Egypt's neighbours: the Hittites fall, Ugarit burns, and the international trade network that sustained New Kingdom prosperity disintegrates.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1177": "Ramesses III (c.1184–1153 BCE) defeats the Sea Peoples simultaneously at the Battle of Djahy (land) and Battle of the Delta (sea) in Year 8 — uniquely among Bronze Age civilisations, Egypt survives intact. But the treasury is drained, the eastern empire is gone, and the international trade networks that sustained New Kingdom wealth are permanently severed.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1100": "Following Ramesses III's assassination (Harris Papyrus), Ramesses IV through XI rule in rapid decline. The Amun priesthood at Thebes accumulates two-thirds of all temple land and 90% of Egypt's ships. By 1100 BCE Upper Egypt is effectively governed by the High Priest of Amun. The New Kingdom empire — built from Ahmose I's reunification — is institutionally collapsing.",
  "Egyptian Empire New Kingdom|-1070": "Ramesses XI (c.1099–1070 BCE) dies, ending the 20th Dynasty and the New Kingdom. Power had already split: the Amun High Priests govern Upper Egypt from Thebes; Smendes controls Lower Egypt from Tanis, founding the 21st Dynasty. The Third Intermediate Period begins — 450+ years of division and priestly rule. Egypt's five-century run as a Near Eastern great power is over.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2700": "The Sumerian King List records King Enmebaragesi of Kish defeating the Elamites c.2700 BCE. Elam is a loose confederation of principalities — Awan, Anshan, Shimashki — with Susa as a culturally Mesopotamian-influenced node. Political coherence is minimal; Elam is more geographic zone than unified state, known primarily as a target of Sumerian raids.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2550": "Under the early Awan kings, Elam coalesces as a political entity. Elamite raids into Mesopotamia are recorded — 600 Elamites intercepted at Lagash. Active trade runs with Sumerian city-states: food in, cattle, wool, silver, and tin out. The Awan kings contest power with Kish and Lagash, signalling regional significance if not dominance.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2350": "Sargon of Akkad defeats Awan king Luh-ishan and reduces Elam to vassal status. Susa becomes an Akkadian administrative centre with appointed governors; the Akkadian language is imposed. However, the highland capital Anshan is never reached — Elam retains a core of independence in its mountainous interior despite near-total lowland subjugation.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2240": "As Akkad weakens after Naram-Sin's death, governor Kutik-Inshushinak (c.2240 BCE) asserts independence, conquers Anshan, unites Elam, destroys 70 cities, seizes Eshnunna, and takes Akkad itself — declaring himself 'king of the four quarters.' He promotes Linear Elamite script and builds Susa's administrative structure. Elam's first true unification — dramatic but short-lived.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2200": "After Kutik-Inshushinak's death, Linear Elamite script vanishes and Elam is overrun — first by the Gutians, then by Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur (c.2112 BCE). Susa falls under Ur III control; Shulgi builds temples there. The Awan dynasty disappears, but the Shimashki dynasty begins organising highland resistance.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-2004": "Shimashki king Kindattu, allied with Susa, captures Ibbi-Sin — the last king of the Third Dynasty of Ur — c.2004 BCE, ending Ur III permanently. Susa passes into Elamite hands for good. The destruction of Ur is mourned in Sumerian lament literature. Elam regains full independence, though the Shimashki state remains regionally confined.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1900": "Ebarat II (c.1950–1900 BCE) founds the Sukkalmah structure: a tripartite hierarchy of sukkalmah (grand regent), sukkal of Susa, and king of Anshan. Lateral brother-to-brother succession provides remarkable stability. Susa becomes a true Elamite capital with extensive building. The Elamite federation reaches its most stable institutional form yet.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1780": "Under Siwe-Palar-Khuppak (c.1778–1745 BCE), Hammurabi of Babylon and Zimri-Lim of Mari address the sukkalmah as 'father,' denoting superior rank. Elam leads a coalition conquering Eshnunna and intervenes diplomatically to Aleppo. Siwe-Palar-Khuppak calls himself 'Enlarger of the Empire' — briefly the most powerful ruler in the Near East.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1760": "A coalition led by Hammurabi breaks Elamite suzerainty over Mesopotamia c.1764 BCE, driving Elam from its positions. The Sukkalmah state persists but retreats to core territory. Elamite ambitions in Mesopotamia are checked for centuries — though a 12th-century document still lists Siwe-Palar-Khuppak among 'Elam's great men,' suggesting the defeat was significant but not existential.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1600": "Kassite invasions c.1600 BCE likely bring down both Babylonia and the late Sukkalmah dynasty. Britannica records: 'thereafter almost nothing is known of Elam.' The last sukkalmahs leave almost no records. A political vacuum opens before the Middle Elamite resurgence. Institutional survival is probable but unverifiable — the state is in severe political decline.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1500": "The Kidinuid dynasty (c.1500–1400 BCE) introduces the title 'King of Anshan and of Susa,' reunifying highland and lowland under one crown. They begin 'Elamisation' — reasserting Elamite cultural identity over Mesopotamian influence at Susa. But only five known rulers and limited power projection keep this a recovery phase, not a resurgence.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1340": "Untash-Napirisha (c.1340–1300 BCE) builds Dur-Untash (Chogha Zanbil) — a 105m-square, five-storey ziggurat, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site — demonstrating massive resource mobilisation. Religious tolerance is promoted across Elamite deities. The Igihalkids intermarry with the Kassites, confirming diplomatic parity. Elam is powerful, culturally flourishing, and growing in regional influence.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1240": "After Kassite king Kurigalzu II occupied parts of Elam c.1320 BCE, Khiddin-Kutran I decisively repulses Kassite forces c.1240 BCE, restoring full Elamite sovereignty. The military recovery directly sets the stage for the Shutrukid conquests ahead. Elamite independence is fully restored and confidence is at its highest since the Sukkalmah zenith.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1200": "Shutruk-Nahhunte I consolidates Anshan and Susa, restores temples, and prepares the great campaigns. The Shutrukid dynasty is recognised as 'the greatest of the Middle Elamite dynasties' (World History Encyclopedia). Territorial maximum has not yet been reached — that comes under his sons — but the founding conquests already signal a power unlike anything since 1780 BCE.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1158": "Shutruk-Nahhunte and son Kutir-Nahhunte permanently defeat the Kassite dynasty of Babylon, killing king Zababa-shuma-iddin and seizing the Code of Hammurabi and Victory Stele of Naram-Sin. Under Shilhak-Inshushinak (c.1150–1120 BCE), territory extends from Mesopotamia east of the Tigris to near Persepolis — Britannica's 'greatest period of Elamite conquest.' Undisputed peak: maximum territory, military dominance, cultural confidence.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-1100": "Nebuchadnezzar I of Babylon (r.c.1119–1098 BCE) captures Susa and retrieves the statue of Marduk. Last Shutrukid king Hutelutush-Inshushinak flees to Anshan. Britannica: 'for almost 300 years thereafter nothing is known of Elamite history.' The highland core likely persists with residual institutional memory, but the loss of Susa is catastrophic.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-900": "Between c.1100 and c.770 BCE almost nothing is known of Elamite history. No royal inscriptions, building projects, or diplomatic correspondence survive. Neo-Babylonian and Assyrian documents mention Elamite armies in conflicts against Assyria from c.820 BCE — confirming survival of some military capacity. Elam has likely fragmented into small highland polities, leaving almost no footprint.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-800": "Building on the faint military signals from c.820 BCE, Elamite armies are now documented in Mesopotamian conflicts. The Neo-Elamite state is reconstituting but remains a secondary power — no hegemonic capacity, sparse evidence. The long dark age following Nebuchadnezzar I's sack of Susa is slowly ending, but recovery is gradual.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-743": "Humban-nikash I accedes in 743 BCE (recorded in the Babylonian Chronicle). In 721 BCE he defeats Sargon II of Assyria at the Battle of Der, supporting Marduk-apla-iddina II in seizing Babylon. Babylonian sources credit Humban-nikash with the victory, winning roughly a decade of peace — a significant achievement for a state still rebuilding.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-710": "Shutruk-Nahhunte II (18-year reign from 717 BCE) fails to sustain Elamite momentum — defeated by Sargon II in 710 BCE and again in 708 BCE. He is the last Neo-Elamite king to leave native royal inscriptions. After his reign, assassinations, usurpations, and rapid succession accelerate Elam's internal disintegration.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-660": "Between 699–646 BCE, Elam cycles through ~12 kings in 53 years — average reign ~4 years. Hallushu invades Babylon and kills Sennacherib's son (694 BCE), provoking devastating Assyrian reprisals. At Halule (691 BCE), an Elamite-Babylonian coalition fights Sennacherib inconclusively. Internal coups and Assyrian pressure are simultaneously disintegrating the state.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-646": "Ashurbanipal demolishes Susa's ziggurat, loots treasuries, desecrates royal tombs, deports tens of thousands, and sows the earth with salt. Humban-haltash III is captured in 640 BCE and paraded at Nineveh. Score 1 not 0: archaeological evidence from Izeh, Naqsh-e Rustam, Arjan, and Ram Hormuz confirms fragmented Elamite political and cultural survival after the raids.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-600": "After Ashurbanipal's death (627 BCE) and Assyria's collapse, Elamite rump kings — Shuttir-Nahhunte, Hallutush-Inshushinak, Atta-Khumma-Inshushinak — rule fragmented city-states. But Persian Teispes had already conquered Anshan c.650 BCE; his Achaemenid descendants are absorbing Elamite territory from within. Elam is no longer a unitary state — it is being swallowed by Persia.",
  "Elamite Confederation|-539": "Cyrus the Great integrates Elam into the Achaemenid Empire, ending Elamite political independence entirely. Susa becomes one of Persia's three great capitals. The Elamite language survives as an official administrative tongue — appearing on Darius I's Behistun Inscription. A civilisation that began resisting Sumerian raids in 2700 BCE ends absorbed into the empire it culturally shaped.",
  "Empire of Japan|1868": "The Satsuma-Chōshū alliance overthrows the Tokugawa shogunate on 3 January 1868, restoring Emperor Meiji. The Charter Oath commits Japan to seeking knowledge worldwide. Western powers still hold unequal treaties over Japan's trade and legal jurisdiction. The Boshin War continues until May 1869. Enormous modernising energy — but no military power, no colonies, no international standing.",
  "Empire of Japan|1885": "Japan builds a modern conscript army (universal service since 1873), national railways, state-sponsored industry, and a legal system on German and French models. The Meiji Constitution (1889) establishes constitutional monarchy. Japan secures the Ryukyu Islands (1879) and the Japan-Korea Treaty of 1876. The fundamental vulnerability of 1868 is solved — war-testing lies ahead.",
  "Empire of Japan|1895": "The First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895) tests Meiji modernisation: Japan destroys the Chinese Beiyang Fleet and captures Weihaiwei. The Treaty of Shimonoseki awards Taiwan, the Pescadores, and briefly the Liaodong Peninsula — relinquished under Triple Intervention pressure. Japan is now East Asia's dominant power, having extinguished Qing control over Korea. Active imperial participation begins.",
  "Empire of Japan|1905": "Japan defeats Russia in the Russo-Japanese War (1904–1905) — the first Asian nation to defeat a European great power in the modern era. The Treaty of Portsmouth grants southern Sakhalin (Karafuto), the Kwantung Leased Territory including Port Arthur, the South Manchuria Railway zone, and a Korean protectorate (formally annexed 1910). Japan joins Britain as an equal ally.",
  "Empire of Japan|1919": "Japan seizes Germany's Pacific territories north of the equator (South Seas Mandate) and Shandong concessions via the Anglo-Japanese Alliance. At the Paris Peace Conference, Japan joins as one of five permanent League of Nations Council members. The Western rejection of Japan's racial equality clause deepens disillusionment with the Western order. Empire consolidated across Korea, Taiwan, Micronesia, and Karafuto.",
  "Empire of Japan|1931": "The Kwantung Army stages the Mukden Incident (18 September 1931) to justify invading Manchuria. The puppet state Manchukuo is established under last Qing emperor Puyi. Japan withdraws from the League of Nations in 1933 after the Lytton Commission condemns the annexation. Manchukuo delivers vast natural resources, strategic depth against the Soviet Union, and a staging ground for further expansion.",
  "Empire of Japan|1937": "Following the Marco Polo Bridge Incident (July 1937), Japan captures Shanghai, Nanjing (Nanjing Massacre: 100,000–300,000 casualties), and Wuhan, controlling China's major coastal cities and industrial core. The National Mobilization Law (1938) places the entire economy on a war footing. The empire is at its largest yet — full national mobilisation achieved, with the Pacific push still ahead.",
  "Empire of Japan|1941": "Japan attacks Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941 and simultaneously invades Southeast Asia and the Pacific. Hong Kong, Malaya, and the Philippines fall rapidly. Maximum territorial extent has not yet been reached — Singapore falls February 1942, the Dutch East Indies and Burma follow. Pearl Harbor begins the maximum expansion phase; it is not its culmination.",
  "Empire of Japan|1942": "By mid-1942 Japan controls ~7 million km²: the Japanese archipelago, Korea, Manchukuo, eastern China, Taiwan, French Indochina, Burma, Malaya, Singapore, the Dutch East Indies, the Philippines, and Pacific island chains from the Aleutians to the Solomons. The Battle of Midway (June 1942) simultaneously begins the strategic reversal — peak territory and the start of decline coincide almost exactly.",
  "Empire of Japan|1943": "Guadalcanal (August 1942–February 1943) delivers Japan's first major land defeat; surviving forces evacuate. Combined with Midway's loss of four fleet carriers (June 1942), Japan surrenders strategic initiative permanently. Imperial General Headquarters shifts to an 'absolute defense perimeter' strategy. Japan still holds most of its 1942 territory but can no longer defend it — decline is now irreversible.",
  "Empire of Japan|1945": "Atomic bombs destroy Hiroshima (6 August) and Nagasaki (9 August 1945). The Soviet Union declares war on 8 August, overrunning Manchukuo, Karafuto, and the Kurils. Emperor Hirohito announces surrender 15 August; formal signing aboard USS Missouri on 2 September. Japan's 1947 constitution renounces war, strips the emperor to ceremonial role, and extinguishes all colonial claims. The empire is gone.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1270": "Yekuno Amlak defeats the last Zagwe king at the Battle of Ansata (10 August 1270), proclaiming descent from Solomon via Menelik I. The empire consists only of the Amhara highlands and parts of Tigray. No bureaucratic infrastructure exists; the legitimising Kebra Nagast text won't be written until c.1314.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1285": "Yekuno Amlak dies in 1285. His rotational succession scheme collapses immediately into fratricidal conflict. By 1299, son Wedem Arad seizes the throne by force. Fourteen years of chronic instability follow with no territorial expansion. The Kebra Nagast is still unwritten. The Solomonic state survives but barely governs.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1300": "Wedem Arad (r.c.1299–1314) consolidates Solomonic authority and writes to the Mamluk Sultan of Egypt requesting a new abuna — evidence of functioning diplomacy. However, the Sultanate of Ifat is expanding and contesting Ethiopian hegemony over Muslim borderland provinces. The dynasty has survived infancy but military dominance lies ahead.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1314": "Amda Seyon I (r.1314–1344) inherits Wedem Arad's stable realm and immediately expands: subjugating Tigray, incorporating Gojjam and Hadiya by 1316, and restructuring the army into professional chewa regiments — Ethiopia's most effective military force yet. Maximum reach is still a decade away but decisive expansion has begun.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1340": "Amda Seyon crosses the Awash, conquering Dawaro, Bale, and multiple sultanates. He crushes Sultan Sabr ad-Din's Muslim coalition. Arab historian al-Umari records Ethiopian territory 'more than doubled.' Diplomatic contact opens with Byzantine Empire. First written Amharic — royal hymns — dates from his reign. The Solomonic dynasty reaches its first true imperial peak.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1400": "Dawit I (r.1382–1413) defeats the Ifat Sultanate under Sultan Sa'ad ad-Din II, extending Amda Seyon's conquests. The tributary state system functions effectively; Ethiopia dominates the Horn. But sources unanimously identify Zara Yaqob's reign (1434–1468) as the true medieval zenith — this era is strong but subordinate to what follows.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1434": "Zara Yaqob (r.1434–1468) crushes the Hadiya Sultanate (1434–35), marries its converted princess Eleni as a political asset, replaces regional warlords with appointed officials, establishes Debre Birhan as capital, and launches a golden age of Ge'ez literature via a royal scriptorium. Maximum military victories and full institutional florescence are still ahead.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1450": "Zara Yaqob destroys the Adal Sultanate at the Battle of Gomit (1445), killing Sultan Badlay ad-Din. He reoccupies Massawa and extends control to the Dahlak archipelago (1464–65). His Mashafa Berha establishes doctrine for the entire Ethiopian church. Ullendorff: 'unquestionably the greatest ruler Ethiopia had seen since Ezana of Aksum.'",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1500": "After Zara Yaqob's death (1468), successors Baeda Maryam I and then Naod (r.1494–1508) retain territory but lose administrative centralisation. Emir Mahfuz of Harar conducts annual border raids — killed in 1517. Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi won't rise until 1527. The existential crisis is approaching but has not yet arrived.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1529": "Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi (Ahmad Gran) rises in Adal in 1527, invades in 1529, and catastrophically defeats Emperor Dawit II (Lebna Dengel) at Shimbra-Kure. Ottoman firearms and mercenaries burn churches, destroy monasteries, and forcibly convert populations. By the mid-1530s most of Ethiopia is occupied. Dawit II dies in exile in 1540.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1543": "Emperor Galawdewos (r.1540–1559), aided by Portuguese musketeers under Cristóvão da Gama, defeats and kills Ahmad Gran at Wayna Daga (21 February 1543). Adal's occupation forces collapse and withdraw. Ethiopia survives its existential crisis but 14 years of devastation — destroyed churches, displaced populations, permanent Oromo demographic reshaping — leave it severely weakened.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1600": "Sarsa Dengel (r.1563–1597) defeats Adal at the Webi River (1573) and contains the Ottomans to the coast. Emperor Susenyos I converts to Catholicism in 1622, igniting civil war. He abdicates in 1632; son Fasilides expels the Jesuits, restores Orthodoxy, and founds Gondar as permanent imperial capital in 1636.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1636": "Fasilides (r.1632–1667) constructs Fasil Ghebbi — six castles, twenty churches, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Trade flourishes; the Gondarine school of painting develops. Religious peace is restored after the Jesuit civil war. Later Emperor Bakaffa (r.1721–1730) purges corrupt nobles — but conditions for the coming Era of the Princes are forming.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1700": "Emperor Iyasu I (r.1682–1706), 'Iyasu the Great,' expands the empire, promotes commerce, and is an accomplished poet and theologian — the most effective Gondarine ruler. The Zemene Mesafint doesn't formally begin until 1769. Gondarine art and architecture are at or near their height. But the nobility's growing power is the key structural threat.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1769": "Ras Mikael Sehul deposes and assassinates Emperor Iyoas I on 7 May 1769, beginning the Zemene Mesafint. Over 86 years, 23 emperors cycle through the throne — many repeatedly. The Yejju Oromo Wara Sheh dynasty dominates the regency from 1786. Trade routes collapse, the army fragments, and territorial integrity is compromised.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1855": "Kassa Hailu defeats all rival warlords (1852–1855) and is crowned Emperor Tewodros II (11 February 1855). He centralises power, conscripts a national army, and attempts to bring the Ethiopian Church under state control. Eighty-six years of Zemene Mesafint fragmentation ends — but reconstruction is only beginning. He dies by suicide at Magdala, 1868.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1889": "Emperor Yohannes IV dies at Metemma fighting the Mahdists. Menelik II becomes emperor (3 November 1889), systematically incorporating Oromo, Somali, Gurage, Sidama, Welayta, Kaffa, and Harar territories — creating Ethiopia's modern shape. Addis Ababa is established (1886). The 1889 Treaty of Wuchale with Italy contains a disputed protectorate clause that will lead to war.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1896": "Emperor Menelik II's army of ~100,000 destroys three Italian brigades at Adwa (1 March 1896): 6,000+ Italian and colonial troops killed, 3,500–3,800 captured. Prime Minister Crispi resigns. The Treaty of Addis Ababa forces Italy to recognise full Ethiopian sovereignty — the only African state to emerge from the Scramble for Africa with independence intact.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1930": "Haile Selassie I is crowned on 2 November 1930. He introduces Ethiopia's first constitution (1931), formally abolishes slavery (1942), and joined the League of Nations in 1923 during his regency. He builds a professional military and school network. Ethiopia's international standing is high — but Mussolini's Italy is preparing its revenge for Adwa.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1941": "Italy invades in 1935 with poison gas and aerial bombing; Addis Ababa falls May 1936. Haile Selassie's League of Nations appeal produces no intervention. After Italy enters WWII, British forces join Ethiopian patriots. Haile Selassie returns 5 May 1941, reorganises government into 14 provinces, and pursues Eritrean reunification — achieved 1952.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1960": "Haile Selassie sends the Kagnew Battalion to the Korean War (1950–53); Eritrea federates with Ethiopia (1952). He founds the Organisation of African Unity in Addis Ababa (1963). A failed coup (December 1960) is suppressed but reveals educated-elite discontent. The feudal land system remains unreformed — structural poverty is unaddressed beneath the international prestige.",
  "Ethiopian Empire|1974": "The 1973–74 Wollo famine — denied by the imperial government — triggers strikes, mutinies, and the formation of the Derg military committee. Haile Selassie is deposed 12 September 1974. The monarchy is abolished 21 March 1975. Haile Selassie is killed 27 August 1975; his remains are found under Mengistu's office floor in 1994. The Solomonic dynasty, founded by Yekuno Amlak in 1270, is extinguished after 704 years.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|909": "Da'i Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i leads Kutama Berbers to overthrow the Aghlabids. Abdallah al-Mahdi is proclaimed first Fatimid caliph (December 909), rivalling the Abbasids. The power base is narrow, the Sunni-Maliki population hostile, and three Egyptian invasions (913–915, 919–921, 925) all fail.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|934": "Al-Mahdi dies in 934; son al-Qa'im succeeds. Another Egyptian invasion (935) is defeated by Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid. The Fatimids have stabilised Ifriqiya, built al-Mahdiyya, extended nominal authority over Maghribi tribes, and raided Sicily and southern Italy — but Egypt, the fundamental objective, remains entirely unreachable.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|943": "Khariji preacher Abu Yazid leads Zenata Berbers, sweeps through Ifriqiya, captures Kairouan, and besieges al-Mahdiyya by January 945. Al-Qa'im dies during the siege; his son al-Mansur keeps the succession secret until the siege is broken. Abu Yazid is captured August 947. The Fatimids held only one besieged city — their closest moment to total annihilation.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|953": "Al-Mu'izz — the greatest early Fatimid strategist — extinguishes Byzantine Sicily at Rometta (965), and general Jawhar reconquers the western Maghreb (958–960), reaching the Atlantic. Al-Mu'izz assembles a 100,000-man army and dispatches agents throughout Egypt. The caliphate has fully recovered from Abu Yazid and stands poised for the historic eastward conquest.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|969": "General Jawhar al-Siqilli completes the bloodless conquest of Egypt on 6 July 969, replacing the weak Ikhshidid regime. He founds al-Qahira (Cairo) immediately; al-Azhar mosque begins 4 April 970. Al-Mu'izz arrives 10 June 973, transferring his predecessors' coffins from Ifriqiya. The Fatimid prayer is briefly heard in Mecca, Medina, and Damascus.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|975": "Al-Aziz — described by scholars as 'the most capable Fatimid ruler' — captures Damascus (978), wins Yemeni suzerainty (987, 992), and receives Qarmatian recognition (992). He formalises the vizierate under Ya'qub ibn Killis and lavishes patronage on architecture and scholarship. The empire is institutionally excellent and territorially ambitious, but Syria remains contested with Byzantium.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1000": "The Fatimid caliph controls coastal North Africa, Sicily, Egypt, Palestine, southern Syria, the Hejaz including Mecca and Medina, and Yemen. Cairo and al-Azhar dominate Islamic intellectual life. Egypt's Red Sea–Mediterranean transit trade generates vast revenues. The da'wa missionary network reaches Persia and Sind. Britannica: 'the height of Fatimid power at the beginning of the 11th century.'",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1021": "Al-Hakim disappears February 1021 — assumed murdered. His reign produced the Church of the Holy Sepulchre's destruction (1009) and the Druze schism, eroding the da'wa's universal appeal. Al-Zahir (r.1021–1036) stabilises and recovers nominal Aleppan suzerainty. The empire remains near maximum territorial reach but ideological fragmentation and Zirid tensions in North Africa are widening.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1047": "Persian traveller Nasir-i Khusraw (1047–48) documents extraordinary Cairene wealth: 20,000 caliph-owned shops, 50,000–100,000 troops. But Zirid governor al-Mu'izz ibn Badis is already distancing himself from Cairo and will formally defect to the Abbasids c.1048–1052. The western provinces are effectively already lost; Egypt's wealth masks deep structural contraction.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1050": "Zirid governor al-Mu'izz ibn Badis transfers allegiance to the Sunni Abbasid caliphate c.1048–1052. Vizier al-Yazuri retaliates by sending the Banu Hilal and Banu Sulaym Bedouin tribes into Ifriqiya — permanently devastating North Africa's agricultural base. Egypt remains prosperous and the army is intact, but the entire Maghribi hinterland is irreversibly lost.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1057": "Defected Seljuk general al-Basasiri proclaims the Fatimid caliph in Mosul, then Baghdad itself (1057–59). The Fatimids cannot provide military support; al-Basasiri is expelled by the Seljuqs in 1059. Britannica: 'a turning point and the beginning of the decline of both Fatimid power and Isma'ili influence.' Maximum ideological reach, minimum ability to consolidate it.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1062": "Ethnic factionalism between Kutama Berbers, Turkish ghilman, and Black African troops erupts into Cairo street warfare (1062). The Mustansirite Hardship (1064–1071): seven low Nile floods, catastrophic famine, ~40,000 deaths, Cairo's population halved. The caliph sells his treasuries. In 1070, Ibn Hamdan briefly reads the Friday prayer in the Abbasid caliph's name in Cairo itself.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1073": "With famine devastating Egypt, the Seljuk commander Atsiz ibn Uwaq holding Jerusalem (1073) and Damascus (1076), and the army in murderous factional chaos, al-Mustansir secretly summons Badr al-Jamali from Acre. Badr arrives January 1074, eliminates Turkish military leadership through assassination, and is invested with triple power: vizier, army commander, and da'wa director.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1080": "Badr al-Jamali rebuilds Cairo's walls (Bab Zuwayla, Bab al-Futuh, Bab al-Nasr), reforms taxes, raises revenues from 2 to 3 million dinars, reestablishes Hejaz authority, and repels Atsiz ibn Uwaq's invasion of Egypt. But Syria and Jerusalem are permanently lost to the Seljuks; the caliph is a figurehead; Badr rules as military dictator with his own Armenian army.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1094": "Badr al-Jamali dies 1094; al-Mustansir dies January 1095 after 60 years — the longest caliphate in Muslim history. Vizier al-Afdal bypasses heir Nizar and installs the pliable Ahmad (al-Musta'li). Nizar rebels, is crushed and executed (1095). Da'i Hasan-i Sabbah in Iran refuses to recognise al-Musta'li, founding the Nizari branch — the future Order of Assassins. The First Crusade follows in 1097.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1100": "Jerusalem falls to the Crusaders on 15 July 1099, taken from the Fatimids who had recaptured it from the Seljuks only in 1098. Vizier al-Afdal's relief army is routed at Ascalon (12 August 1099), losing 10,000–12,700 men. Campaigns in 1101, 1102, and 1105 all fail. The caliphate retains Egypt, the Hejaz, and the Ascalon coastal strip — all of Palestine proper is gone.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1150": "Between 1130–1150, viziers murder and replace each other relentlessly. The caliphate retains only Egypt; child caliphs are powerless figureheads. The Crusaders besiege Ascalon (which falls 1153). All Levantine territories are lost. Factional conflict between rival army commanders and viziers has hollowed out every institutional, territorial, and ideological function of the caliphate.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1153": "Baldwin III of Jerusalem besieges Ascalon; no relief comes from Cairo. Ascalon surrenders 22 August 1153 — the final elimination of Fatimid military presence outside Egypt. In 1154, vizier Abbas murders caliph al-Zafir and installs 5-year-old al-Fa'iz. Abbas is then expelled by Armenian commander Tala'i ibn Ruzzik. The caliphate is one country, governed by assassins.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1163": "Vizier Shawar is overthrown by Dirgham (1163); Shawar flees to Zengid ruler Nur ad-Din, who sends general Shirkuh (Saladin's uncle) into Egypt. Fatimid viziers alternately invite Crusaders and Zengids, surrendering autonomous governance entirely. The caliph is a powerless child figurehead; Egypt's armies are commanded by foreign generals; the treasury is exhausted.",
  "Fatimid Caliphate|1171": "Saladin enters Egypt as a Zengid general in 1169, replaces Ismaili judges with Sunni ones, and restores Sunni religious education. In September 1171, he orders the Friday prayer read in the Abbasid caliph's name. Caliph al-Adid dies 13 September 1171, bedridden and unaware his caliphate had already been erased. No battle, no ceremony — the Fatimid Caliphate is simply abolished.",
  "French Empire|1534": "Jacques Cartier explores the Gulf of St. Lawrence (1534), claiming New France for Francis I. France's Wars of Religion prevent sustained commitment. Footholds in Brazil (1555) and Florida (1562) are expelled by Spain and Portugal. France has exploratory voyages but no permanent settlement — an empire in name only.",
  "French Empire|1608": "Champlain founds Quebec (1608) as capital of New France and centre of the fur trade, following Port Royal in Acadia (1605). France now has permanent, functional colonial settlement in North America for the first time. The foundation is genuine but fragile — colonisation has begun; consolidation lies ahead.",
  "French Empire|1664": "Louis XIV's finance minister Colbert establishes the French East India Company (1664) and French West Indies Company, builds a powerful navy, and acquires Saint-Domingue in the Caribbean. France controls six fortified Indian trading posts. Saint-Domingue will become the Caribbean's richest sugar colony. The first empire now has real economic weight.",
  "French Empire|1700": "France is the global hegemon: Europe's largest army, second-most powerful navy. La Salle claims the entire Mississippi basin (1682), extending French territory from the Gulf of Mexico to the Great Lakes. The First French Colonial Empire approaches ~10,000,000 km² — the world's second-largest behind Spain. Saint-Domingue is becoming the Caribbean's most profitable plantation colony.",
  "French Empire|1710": "Historians date 1710 as the First French Colonial Empire's peak: 10,000,000+ km², the world's second-largest. The arc runs from Acadia through the Great Lakes to Louisiana and New Orleans; Caribbean islands (Saint-Domingue, Martinique, Guadeloupe) generate sugar and slave revenues; Réunion, Mauritius, and Indian trading posts complete the global footprint. Chronic under-population and British naval rivalry are the structural weaknesses.",
  "French Empire|1763": "The Seven Years' War (1756–1763) devastates French colonial power: defeats at Plassey (1757), Quebec (1759), and Martinique (1762). The Treaty of Paris strips all of Canada and North America east of the Mississippi to Britain; Louisiana goes to Spain. France retains only Martinique, Guadeloupe, Saint-Pierre & Miquelon, and five Indian trading posts.",
  "French Empire|1783": "French support for the American colonies against Britain yields the 1783 Treaty of Paris, returning Saint Lucia, Tobago, and Senegalese stations. Saint-Domingue now produces 40% of Europe's sugar and 50% of its coffee — more revenue than any other colony in the world. The first empire is recovering; its real jewel is now the Caribbean.",
  "French Empire|1804": "The Haitian Revolution (1791–1804), led by Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines, destroys Saint-Domingue and declares Haiti independent. Napoleon sells Louisiana to the United States in 1803. Britain seizes most remaining French colonies by 1803. Napoleon dominates Europe, but the overseas colonial footprint is near its historic low.",
  "French Empire|1812": "Napoleon's continental empire encompasses 130 departments, 44 million direct subjects, and 90 million European dependants. He controls Spain, Italy, the Low Countries, and the Confederation of the Rhine; the Napoleonic Code transforms European law. But overseas colonial holdings are minimal — the navy is wrecked, most colonies are held by Britain, and Louisiana is sold.",
  "French Empire|1815": "After Waterloo (18 June 1815), the Congress of Vienna restores France to its 1792 borders. Overseas France retains only Guadeloupe, Martinique, Saint-Pierre & Miquelon, Réunion, French Guiana, and five Indian trading posts. The First French Colonial Empire is essentially finished — what remains is a rump of sugar islands with no continental hegemony.",
  "French Empire|1830": "Charles X orders the invasion of Algiers (July 1830), ostensibly to suppress piracy — beginning the Second French Colonial Empire. Under Louis-Philippe (1830–1848), France begins the brutal conquest of Algeria; Abd al-Qadir is captured in 1847, though resistance continues. France also acquires Mayotte and Tahiti (both 1843). The second empire is in its earliest, most fragile stages.",
  "French Empire|1870": "Napoleon III (1852–1870) triples French overseas territory to ~1,000,000 km² with 5 million subjects: seizing Cochinchina (1859), annexing Cambodia (1863), establishing New Caledonia (1853), and acquiring Senegal. France's defeat in the Franco-Prussian War (1870–71), losing Alsace-Lorraine to Germany, reshapes colonialism as a tool of national rehabilitation under the Third Republic.",
  "French Empire|1880": "Jules Ferry (PM 1880–81, 1883–85) frames expansion as 'mission civilisatrice' and post-1871 national restoration. He acquires Tunisia (1881), pushes into the Congo basin, establishes Indochina protectorates over Tonkin and Annam (1883–85), and partitions West Africa. France participates in the Berlin Conference (1884–85). The empire is building fast but the African territorial maximum is still ahead.",
  "French Empire|1914": "On the eve of WWI, France holds the world's second-largest colonial empire: ~10,000,000 km² spanning Algeria, Morocco, Tunisia, French West Africa, French Equatorial Africa, Indochina (Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia), the Caribbean, and the Pacific. It supplies rubber, cotton, and phosphates, and provides colonial troops for the coming war. Absolute territorial peak comes after WWI — hence 9, not 10.",
  "French Empire|1931": "The 1931 Paris Colonial Exhibition — 33 million visitors — celebrates the absolute territorial maximum: ~12,347,000–13,500,000 km², covering ~10% of the world's land, with 65–68 million colonial subjects. Post-WWI League of Nations mandates (Syria, Lebanon, Cameroon, Togo) complete the empire. No other period combines greater territorial reach, administrative coherence, and symbolic confidence — though the 1925 Great Syrian Revolt has already been suppressed.",
  "French Empire|1940": "Germany defeats France in six weeks (June 1940). The empire fractures: Vichy France retains nominal control of North Africa, West Africa, and Indochina; de Gaulle's Free French secure Equatorial Africa (Chad rallies August 1940). Japan occupies French Indochina from September 1940, leaving French administrators nominally in place until March 1945. Most territory survives but institutional legitimacy is shattered.",
  "French Empire|1950": "France fights the costly First Indochina War against the Viet Minh (begun December 1946). Syria and Lebanon achieve independence in 1946. Anti-colonial pressure mounts in North Africa. The French Union (1946 Constitution) attempts to reframe empire as partnership but convinces few. The empire is under simultaneous military, political, and moral pressure on multiple fronts.",
  "French Empire|1954": "The French garrison at Dien Bien Phu surrenders to the Viet Minh on 7 May 1954 after a 56-day siege. The parliament votes 471–14 to end the Indochina War; the Geneva Accords partition Vietnam. On 1 November 1954 — six months later — the FLN launches its Algerian uprising, explicitly inspired by Dien Bien Phu. France loses its entire Southeast Asian empire in one year.",
  "French Empire|1960": "Following de Gaulle's 1958 French Community referendum, France grants independence to virtually all of sub-Saharan Africa in 1960: Togo, Mali, Chad, Gabon, Senegal, Mauritania, Madagascar, Ivory Coast, Dahomey, Niger, Upper Volta, Central African Republic, Cameroon, and others. France retains its Caribbean departments and Réunion — but still holds Algeria, where the war intensifies.",
  "French Empire|1962": "The Évian Accords (18 March 1962) grant Algeria independence after eight years of war costing 300,000–1.5 million Algerian lives and the fall of the Fourth Republic (1958). Algeria had been a French department since 1848, with one million pieds-noirs settlers. Historian Robert Aldrich: the empire 'practically had come to an end.' France still holds small territories but the imperial enterprise is finished.",
  "French Empire|1980": "The New Hebrides becomes independent Vanuatu (1980) — the last significant non-departmental territory. French Somaliland became Djibouti in 1977. Remaining overseas territories (Guadeloupe, Martinique, French Guiana, Réunion, Mayotte, French Polynesia) are constitutionally integrated into France — not colonies. The empire that began with Cartier's 1534 voyage to the St. Lawrence ends, absorbed into the French Republic itself.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-1000": "The proto-Garamantes retreat into the Fezzan's four habitable oases — Wadi al-Ajal, Wadi ash-Shati, Wadi Tanezzuft, Wadi Barjuj — following the final drying of the Green Sahara. Shallow shadoof wells, cattle herding, and date farming sustain them. Rock art at Wadi Mathendous documents their culture. No urban centres, no state apparatus, no foggara irrigation yet.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-500": "Herodotus describes the Garamantes as 'a very great nation' deploying four-horse chariots to hunt Troglodyte Ethiopians — likely enslaved as foggara labourers. They trade salt, enslaved persons, and exotic goods with Carthaginians and Egyptians from their hilltop capital at Zinkekra. Basic foggara technology, transmitted via Persian qanat networks westward, is beginning to be adopted.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-400": "Between 400–100 BCE the Garamantes progressively replace shallow wells with foggaras — inclined underground tunnels tapping the fossil sandstone aquifer beneath the Fezzan highlands. Aerial photography reveals 530+ individual foggara canals in Wadi al-Ajal alone, with 100,000 vertical shafts averaging 10 metres deep. Centralised labour mobilisation confirms hierarchical political organisation is now solidifying.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-300": "The expanding foggara network supports permanent agricultural communities. The Garamantes hold an unbreakable trans-Saharan monopoly: sitting astride the only viable desert crossing, they alone move gold, ivory, enslaved persons, ostrich feathers, and exotic animals from the Saharan interior to Carthaginian, Greek, and later Roman coastal markets. No rival can contest this — the Sahara itself is the barrier.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-200": "The Wadi al-Ajal foggara network is now the longest built, enabling agricultural production far beyond what oasis springs alone could support. The royal citadel at Zinkekra governs a centralised tribute and trade system. Garamantian caravans supply Carthaginian markets with sub-Saharan goods. Population estimates reach tens of thousands. The empire is a complex state — but Garama's urban peak is still ahead.",
  "Garamantian Empire|-100": "The Geological Society of America (2023) identifies 100 BCE–100 CE as peak foggara construction: 750 km of tunnels in Wadi al-Ajal alone. Garama's population approaches 4,000 city, 6,000 suburbs. Trade with Rome's North African colonies intensifies: Garamantian caravans deliver elephants, lions, and leopards for Roman arenas, depicted in the Zliten Mosaic from Leptis Magna. Infrastructure is still being built at maximum rate.",
  "Garamantian Empire|0": "Lucius Cornelius Balbus captures 15 Garamantian settlements, including Garama (19 BCE), earning Rome’s last private triumph. He enforces a trade-and-tribute treaty, then withdraws. The Garamantes quickly rebuild. Tacitus (~109 CE) still calls them unconquered. Foggara output is near maximum; even 10,000 legionaries cannot hold the desert.",
  "Garamantian Empire|100": "The GSA (2023) study by Professor Frank Schwartz establishes 100 CE as the true peak: 750 km of Wadi al-Ajal foggaras at maximum output, Garama at ~10,000 population — the largest city ever built in the central Sahara without a river. Trans-Saharan trade monopoly at full height. The GSA paper is unambiguous: 'a decline in the Garamantian kingdom' begins 'after about AD 100.'",
  "Garamantian Empire|200": "Septimius Severus's legate Quintus Anicius Faustus sacks Garama (202 CE); Rome establishes monitoring posts 400 km south of the formal frontier. The Garamantian kingdom recovers politically — as it had from Balbus. But the aquifer decline is real and ongoing: foggaras deliver progressively less water each decade, wells require deeper digging each generation. Trade networks and urban centres remain intact but are past their hydraulic peak.",
  "Garamantian Empire|300": "Groundwater falls below foggara levels in Wadi al-Ajal, forcing costly deepening or abandonment. The Roman Limes Tripolitanus blocks Garamantian raiding routes. Trade rents and raiding income decline. The kingdom remains urbanized and coherent but is structurally weakened.",
  "Garamantian Empire|400": "Rome’s mid-3rd-century crises — usurpations and frontier abandonment — collapse demand for Garamantian goods. Foggara maintenance requires constant, labor-intensive shaft-deepening, postponing but not reversing aquifer decline. The kingdom retains trade routes and urban centers, but structural erosion is now irreversible.",
  "Garamantian Empire|430": "The Vandals under Gaiseric cross from Spain in 429 CE, capturing Carthage and all of Tripolitania by 430–439 CE, severing the Garamantian Mediterranean trade lifeline. Sabratha, Oea, and Leptis Magna fall; demand for Garamantian products collapses. Trade destruction combined with aquifer exhaustion reduces the kingdom from a complex urban state to scattered oasis communities past the point of systemic recovery.",
  "Garamantian Empire|500": "The Garamantian state fragments into isolated oasis communities. Garama remains inhabited but with a drastically reduced population. Large-scale construction and high-value trade vanish. The trans-Saharan monopoly ends. The Garamantes persist as a people but no longer function as an empire.",
  "Garamantian Empire|600": "The aquifer is near-exhausted; most foggaras are dry or require shafts deeper than 40 metres. Arab Muslim armies begin their Maghreb conquest in the 640s–670s CE. Garama is recorded as still standing on the Arabs' arrival but is a fraction of its 2nd-century self. The state, economy, and hydraulic infrastructure that made Saharan civilisation possible are effectively gone.",
  "Garamantian Empire|700": "Arab armies conquer the Fezzan c.643–670 CE. Garama is a small fortified settlement with minimal resistance. Population disperses into the Islamic order; foggaras are abandoned. Libya’s Great Man-Made River later confirms collapse was due to aquifer depletion, not political failure. A civilization built on water ends by its absence.",
  "German Empire|1871": "William I is proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles (18 January 1871) after Prussia's defeat of France. Germany receives Alsace-Lorraine and a 5-billion-franc indemnity. But 67% of Germans remain rural, industrial takeoff is only beginning, and Bismarck faces the Kulturkampf, socialist agitation, and a fragmented parliament. A militarily powerful but institutionally nascent empire.",
  "German Empire|1880": "Protective tariffs (1879) stimulate recovery from the post-1873 depression. Bismarck constructs the Three Emperors' League (1873, renewed 1881), Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary (1879), and the Triple Alliance (1882). Germany acquires German New Guinea (1884). Coal and steel production are rising but Britain has not yet been eclipsed industrially.",
  "German Empire|1884": "The Berlin Conference (1884–85), hosted by Bismarck, sets Scramble for Africa rules and establishes Germany as Europe's diplomatic convening power. Germany acquires German East Africa, South West Africa, Togo, and Cameroon. The Reinsurance Treaty with Russia (1887) — maintaining Russian friendship while allied with Austria-Hungary — represents the apex of Bismarck's diplomatic juggling.",
  "German Empire|1890": "Wilhelm II dismisses Bismarck (March 1890). The Reinsurance Treaty with Russia lapses — destroying Bismarck's anti-encirclement keystone. Admiral Tirpitz's Navy Bills (1898, 1900) launch a naval arms race with Britain. Yet the economy surges: machine-building workers double from 500,000 to over 1 million between 1895 and 1907. Economic trajectory strongly upward; diplomatic foundations quietly demolished.",
  "German Empire|1900": "Germany becomes the largest continental economy and third globally (Wikipedia). It surpasses Britain in steel; BASF, Bayer, and Hoechst control 60% of world synthetic dye supply; Germany produces half of global electrical equipment. Population grows from 35 million (1850) to 67 million (1913). But the Entente Cordiale (1904) and Anglo-Russian Convention (1907) are hardening diplomatic encirclement.",
  "German Empire|1910": "By 1913, German chemical firms produce 'almost 90% of world dyestuffs' (Ritschl, LSE). Steel output triples since 1900; Germany leads in industrial research. But the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) is fully formed; the Morocco crises (1905, 1911) damage German prestige without territorial gain; the naval arms race has hardened British hostility. Industrial power at maximum; strategic net closing.",
  "German Empire|1912": "Germany simultaneously peaks economically and militarily c.1911–1913: leading the world in chemicals, producing more steel than Britain, generating half of global electrical equipment. Ritschl's LSE paper identifies 1911 as Germany's peak pre-war construction boom. The Schlieffen Plan is complete; the army is at maximum peacetime readiness. This is the zenith — every subsequent development is decline.",
  "German Empire|1914": "Archduke Franz Ferdinand is assassinated (28 June 1914). Germany issues its 'blank check' to Austria-Hungary and backs escalation. German leaders feared systematic encirclement and believed delay only worsened the odds. The July Crisis is a decision from fear of relative decline, not peak confidence. Maximum military mobilisation begins — but the trajectory now ends in 0 four years later.",
  "German Empire|1916": "Germany fights the Battle of Verdun (February–December 1916, ~300,000 German dead) and the Battle of the Somme — neither achieves a breakthrough. The British 'Hunger Blockade' causes severe food shortages by 1916–17. Hindenburg and Ludendorff replace Falkenhayn (August 1916), admitting strategic failure. Germany still controls Belgium, northern France, Poland, and Serbia — but resource exhaustion is underway.",
  "German Empire|1918": "The Hundred Days Offensive ends the Western Front. Bulgaria and the Ottomans sign armistices; Austria-Hungary collapses. The Kiel Mutiny sparks the November Revolution. Wilhelm II abdicates 9 November; Scheidemann proclaims the German Republic. The 11 November armistice leads to the 1919 Treaty of Versailles, stripping territory, colonies, and limiting the army.",
  "Ghana Empire|300": "A wet Sahel period and the camel's introduction to the western Sahara catalyse trade-route formation. Wagadou emerges from the Tichitt culture (traceable to c.1600 BCE at Dhar Tichitt, Mauritania). Soninke oral tradition credits founding to Dinga Cissé. Iron-working and goldfield access begin — but no documented territorial reach beyond the core chiefdom.",
  "Ghana Empire|500": "Soninke iron-smelting produces superior weapons, giving systematic military advantage. Radiocarbon dating places Koumbi Saleh's earliest occupation in the 5th century CE. Trans-Saharan trade is growing but irregular. No written documentation exists — all evidence is archaeological. Wagadou is solidifying but lacks the trade monopoly and tributary structure that define the mature empire.",
  "Ghana Empire|700": "Persian astronomer al-Fazārī (d. c.777 CE) describes 'the territory of Ghana, the land of gold' as equal in size to Idrisid Morocco. The camel has transformed the Sahara into a structured trade network; Ghana is already positioned as gatekeeper of gold heading north and salt heading south — a powerful, wealthy trading state noted by outside observers.",
  "Ghana Empire|750": "EBSCO dates Ghana's formal foundation to c.750 CE. Around 770, Arab geographers first formally apply the name 'Ghana.' Oral tradition credits Dinga Cissé (or son Majan Dyabe Cissé) with unifying the Soninke clans and establishing Koumbi Saleh as capital. The transition from proto-state chiefdom to empire with defined succession and systematic trade taxation is now complete.",
  "Ghana Empire|800": "Britannica: 'by 800, Ghana had become rich and powerful.' Al-Khwārizmī maps Ghana in 833 CE. The king monopolises all gold nuggets; merchants pay weight-based duties on imports and exports; multiple sub-kings confirm tributary imperial structure. A direct trade route to Egypt is operating. Ghana's reputation has reached the Islamic heartland — territorial maximum is still decades ahead.",
  "Ghana Empire|872": "Al-Ya'qubi (872 CE) confirms Ghana's king is 'very powerful,' with gold mines and 'several kingdoms' under his authority — one of two dominant Western Sudan powers alongside Gao. Al-Masudi (c.944 CE) later describes the King of Ghana as 'supreme with vassals under him.' Only the Awdaghast territorial conquest remains before the absolute zenith.",
  "Ghana Empire|992": "King Tounka captures Awdaghast (992 CE), seizing the northern trans-Saharan terminus from the Zanata Berbers. Ghana now taxes gold and salt at both ends of the corridor. EBSCO identifies 992–1054 as the empire's zenith. Ibn Hawqal (writing 977 CE) had already declared Ghana's king 'the wealthiest king on the face of the earth.' The territorial climax is reached.",
  "Ghana Empire|1000": "Koumbi Saleh is a double-city six miles apart — the royal El-Ghaba with stone palace and sacred grove; a Muslim merchants' town with twelve mosques. Al-Bakri (1067/68) records 200,000 warriors under the king. All gold nuggets are royal property. Tribute flows from vassal kingdoms across the western Sudan. Maximum territory, revenue, and diplomatic prestige.",
  "Ghana Empire|1050": "In 1050 Ghana retains all territory including Awdaghast. Al-Bakri's celebrated description — 10 gold-embroidered horses, gold-collared guards, scholars and jurists — reflects this era. Tunka Manin ascends in 1062/63 to a militarily and economically robust throne. The Almoravid movement is nascent in the 1040s but has not yet struck Ghana's territory.",
  "Ghana Empire|1054": "Almoravid commander Abū Bakr ibn ʿUmar captures Awdaghast (1054 CE), ending 62 years of Ghana's northern trade monopoly. Berber merchants can now bypass Ghana's tax regime. The empire's core — Koumbi Saleh, Bambuk goldfields, southern tributary kingdoms — remains intact; 200,000 warriors still under command. A significant wound, but not yet fatal.",
  "Ghana Empire|1076": "Almoravids sack Koumbi Saleh (1076); Tunka Manin continues as vassal, paying tribute; Ghana formally converts to Islam. Almoravid control of both Sijilmasa and Awdaghast severs Ghana's trans-Saharan stranglehold. Conrad and Fisher (1982) dispute the 'conquest' narrative — archaeology shows no rapid destruction. Ghana formally exists but has lost autonomy, tribute income, and trade dominance simultaneously.",
  "Ghana Empire|1087": "Abu Bakr ibn Umar dies November 1087; the southern Almoravid confederation fragments; the Soninke regain nominal independence. But recovery is structural: Almoravid overgrazing initiates desertification, trade routes permanently shift east to the Niger corridor, and the Wangara merchant diaspora has dispersed. Ghana is alive but functions as a reduced regional power, not a trans-Saharan hegemon.",
  "Ghana Empire|1100": "Al-Idrisi (1154 CE) confirms Ghana's royal city still functions; a new palace is built in 1116–17 CE. But World History Encyclopedia notes Ghana 'really began to collapse in the 12th century': eastern trade routes open, prolonged Sahel drought sets in, and civil war erupts between Islamised elites and animist subjects. Territorial extent is perhaps one-third of its peak.",
  "Ghana Empire|1140": "The Sosso Kaniaga kingdom — a former Ghana vassal — begins conquering Wagadou c.1140 CE. By c.1180, Diara Kante of Sosso subjugates Wagadou, forcing the Soninke to pay tribute. Ghana's former subjects now extract revenue from the once-supreme empire. The political reversal is complete; Ghana retains nominal existence but has lost all military independence.",
  "Ghana Empire|1200": "Diara Kante establishes the Diarisso dynasty at Koumbi Saleh c.1180; son Soumaoro Kante formally conquers Ghana in 1203 CE. By 1200, Ghana is a rump tributary state — no longer an empire. Residual Soninke actors later ally with Sundiata Keita against Sosso. The terminal score of 0 comes in 1240 when Sundiata destroys the capital.",
  "Ghana Empire|1240": "Sundiata Keita defeats Soumaoro Kante at the Battle of Kirina (1235); remnant Wagadou fights alongside Sundiata. Sundiata destroys Koumbi Saleh in 1240; Britannica: 'what was left of the empire of Ghana was incorporated into his new empire of Mali.' The capital is not rebuilt. In 1957, Kwame Nkrumah names independent Ghana after this empire — dead for seven centuries.",
  "Gupta Empire|240": "Sri Gupta (c.240–280 CE) controls iron-rich Bihar, probably as a Kushan vassal. The Allahabad Pillar later records him merely as 'Maharaja' — a lesser title. Chinese monk Yijing refers to 'Che-li-ki-to' building a temple for Buddhist pilgrims. No imperial reach; the dynasty is indistinguishable from surrounding chiefdoms.",
  "Gupta Empire|319": "Chandragupta I (c.319–335 CE) marries Licchavi princess Kumaradevi of Magadha, dramatically expanding political resources. He adopts the imperial title Maharajadhiraja ('King of Great Kings') and establishes Pataliputra as capital. The Gupta Samvat era dates from his 319–320 CE accession. Foundations are being laid; territorial reach remains modest.",
  "Gupta Empire|335": "Samudragupta (c.335–375 CE) begins systematic expansion: defeats Achyuta and Nagasena of the Naga dynasty in the upper Gangetic valley. Court poet Harisena records his campaigns on the Allahabad Pillar — 'the most important historical document of the classical Gupta age.' Rome receives an Indian embassy c.351 CE. A powerful kingdom in active expansion, midway to imperial zenith.",
  "Gupta Empire|375": "Samudragupta uproots 8 Āryāvarta kings, subjugates all central Indian forest kingdoms, and defeats 12 kings as far south as the Pallava kingdom at Kanchipuram. He performs the Ashwamedha, issues gold coinage, and takes the title 'Exterminator of All Kings.' The Indo-Gangetic plain from Punjab to Bengal is under direct control. Only Chandragupta II's Saka defeat and cultural florescence remain ahead.",
  "Gupta Empire|380": "Chandragupta II (c.375–415 CE) begins the Saka Western Kshatrapa campaign to seize Malwa, Gujarat, and the Arabian Sea ports. Chief opponent Rudrasimha III is defeated c.395 CE. The Mehrauli Iron Pillar records his victories. Daughter Prabhavatigupta is married to the Vakataka king — giving Guptas indirect Deccan influence. Empire still expanding; full territorial and cultural peak lies just ahead.",
  "Gupta Empire|400": "Chandragupta II completes the Saka defeat, stretching the empire from the eastern Gangetic delta to the Arabian Sea. Fa-Hien (c.399–414 CE) describes Magadha as prosperous with no capital punishment. The Ujjain court hosts the Navaratnas: Kalidasa, Aryabhata, Varahamihira, and Vatsyayana. Gold dinara coinage at its purest. Trade with Rome and Southeast Asia at maximum.",
  "Gupta Empire|415": "Kumaragupta I (c.415–455 CE) maintains Chandragupta II's full empire with skill and stability. He founds Nalanda Mahavihara (UNESCO World Heritage Site, 2016) — the ancient world's greatest centre of learning. He issues peacock-type coins and performs the Ashwamedha. The Mandsor inscription records active guild life. Trade with the Eastern Roman Empire continues. Early structural pressures are beginning to form.",
  "Gupta Empire|450": "Late Kumaragupta faces Pushyamitra rebellion and first Huna incursions from the northwest — both successfully repelled, celebrated with a final Ashwamedha. No Gupta territory is lost during his reign; Nalanda is newly founded; gold coinage remains sound. 'Faltering' more accurately begins with Skandagupta's strained inheritance — this is still a broadly stable, commanding empire.",
  "Gupta Empire|455": "Skandagupta (c.455–467 CE) defeats Pushyamitra rebellion and repels the Hephthalite Huna invasion c.455–457 CE — the Bhitari Pillar records near-annihilation and dramatic rescue. He rebuilds the Sudarshana Lake dam at Junagadh. But war costs debase the gold coinage, and no Gupta coins or inscriptions appear in western Malwa and Saurashtra after 467 CE — those economically vital regions are lost.",
  "Gupta Empire|467": "Skandagupta dies c.467 CE; Purugupta (467–473 CE), Kumaragupta II (473–476 CE), and Budhagupta (476–495 CE) follow in rapid succession. Malwa, Gujarat, and Saurashtra — controlling Arabian Sea trade revenues — are effectively lost: no Gupta coin or inscription found there after 467 CE. Gujarat silk weavers migrate to Malwa by 473 CE. Provincial governors assert hereditary rights; feudalism is replacing centralised empire.",
  "Gupta Empire|485": "During Budhagupta's reign (c.476–495 CE), the Hephthalites breach northwest defences in the 480s CE; Gupta general Goparaja dies in battle. By 500 CE, 'much of the empire in northwest was overrun by the Huna' (Lumenlearning). The Gupta state retains Magadha, Bengal, and eastern India — Punjab, Rajasthan, Malwa, and Gujarat are permanently lost. Mihirakula later destroys Nalanda.",
  "Gupta Empire|500": "The eastern Gupta court under Narasimhagupta (c.495–530 CE) patronises Buddhism and sponsors Nalanda construction before Huna destruction. The dynasty still issues inscriptions and land grants. But it governs only a fraction of the former empire; western commercial revenues are entirely gone. Real residual authority exists in the east — but this is not a recoverable position.",
  "Gupta Empire|532": "Yashodharman of Aulikara seizes most of northern and central India c.532 CE — the final demonstration that the Guptas have lost all pan-northern hegemony. The last imperial Gupta inscription is the Damudarpur copper-plate of Vishnugupta — a land grant in Kotivarsha (West Bengal) dated 542/543 CE. Imperial authority now barely exceeds a single district.",
  "Gupta Empire|550": "Vishnugupta (c.540–550 CE), the last Gupta main-line ruler, reigns from a Bengal rump. The Damudarpur copper-plate (542/543 CE) is the final known imperial inscription — after this, silence. No further coins or records survive c.550 CE. The empire dissolves not into a single successor but into dozens of regional powers. The imperial line that produced Kalidasa and Aryabhata ends here.",
  "Gokturk Empire|546": "Bumin of the Ashina clan — vassals of the Rouran Khaganate — crushes a Uyghur and Tiele revolt. The Rouran refuse his marriage request, insulting him as a 'blacksmith slave.' Bumin allies with Western Wei, marries princess Changle, and prepares to revolt. The Göktürk empire does not yet exist.",
  "Gokturk Empire|552": "Bumin Qaghan crushes the Rouran (552 CE), declares the Göktürk Khaganate, and becomes the first ruler to use 'Türk' as a political identity. He dies months later; sons Issik briefly, then Muqan Qaghan succeed. The empire exists on paper — institutions and territory are not yet consolidated.",
  "Gokturk Empire|570": "Muqan Qaghan (553–572) eliminates the last Rouran, defeats the Khitans, and subjugates the Yenisei Kyrgyz. Brother Istämi reaches the Caspian by 570, pressing into former Hephthalite territory. A Göktürk embassy reaches Constantinople (568), received by Emperor Justin II. The Eastern Khaganate extracts 100,000 silk rolls annually from Northern Zhou China.",
  "Gokturk Empire|576": "Göktürk forces cross the Kerch Strait into Crimea (576 CE); cavalry later besiege Chersonesus. The empire spans 8,000+ km from Manchuria to the Black Sea, extracting tribute from Northern Zhou, Qi, and Sui dynasties simultaneously. The Silk Road, managed by Sogdian merchant partners, runs entirely under Göktürk protection. Absolute territorial maximum of the First Khaganate.",
  "Gokturk Empire|600": "Taspar Qaghan dies (581) without clear succession; four simultaneous claimants trigger civil war. By 584 the Eastern and Western halves separate; by 603 the split is formal. Sui Chancellor Su Wei's strategy — 'set the barbarians against each other' — works: the Eastern Khaganate pays tribute to Sui while the Western Khaganate fights internal rebellion.",
  "Gokturk Empire|603": "The First Turkic Khaganate formally dissolves in 603 CE. The Western Khaganate under Tardu (d. c.603) is in terminal crisis; the Eastern Khaganate is a Sui tributary. Both retain the Ashina dynasty and Göktürk name but are separate competing polities. The unified tribute relationship with China is severed; internecine conflict continues.",
  "Gokturk Empire|630": "Emperor Taizong's general Li Jing leads 10,000 cavalry in a surprise night attack (630 CE), capturing Eastern Qaghan Illig — weakened by Tang manipulation, a 627–628 CE snowstorm killing livestock, and Uyghur and Xueyantuo revolts. Taizong is then proclaimed Great Qaghan by surviving Turkic nobles. The Eastern Khaganate is extinguished; the Western Khaganate survives until 657.",
  "Gokturk Empire|657": "Tang general Su Dingfang defeats Western Qaghan Ashina Helu (657 CE). Emperor Gaozong claims indirect rule 'over the entire Silk Road as far as Iran.' Göktürk nobles bear Chinese titles and serve in Tang armies. The Ashina dynasty persists as a Tang-client lineage — maintaining just enough continuity for the 25-year recovery that produces the Second Khaganate.",
  "Gokturk Empire|680": "Ashina Nishufu declares himself qaghan (679 CE), revolts against Tang, and is killed by his own men (680). Successor Ashina Funian's revolt also fails. On 5 December 681, 54 Göktürks including Ashide Wenfu and Ashina Funian are publicly executed in Chang'an's eastern market. Only a handful of Ashina exiles and Göktürk cultural memory prevent a score of 0.",
  "Gokturk Empire|682": "Ilterish Qaghan (Ashina Qutlugh) and strategist Tonyukuk revolt against Tang with 17 followers (682 CE), growing to 700 through guerrilla success. By 687–691 they reconquer the Mongolian heartland, re-establishing the capital at Ötüken. The Second Khaganate is genuinely re-established but remains a fragile insurgent state without the First Khaganate's institutional depth.",
  "Gokturk Empire|710": "Qapaghan Qaghan (691–716) crosses the Yellow River six times, defeating an 80,000-man Tang army at Ming Sha (705–710). He conquers Siberian tribes, kills the Yenisei Kyrgyz qaghan (710), raids Sogdia, and extends the khaganate from Manchuria to the Syr Darya. Tang is reduced to tribute-payer. The Orkhon inscriptions (c.732–735) explicitly identify this era as Göktürk peak.",
  "Gokturk Empire|716": "Qapaghan is killed in ambush (716 CE); his head is sent to Tang. Khitans, Qays, and Turkic tribes immediately defect to Tang. His son Inel Qaghan illegitimately seizes the throne. Kül Tigin stages a coup, kills Inel, and installs elder brother Bilge Qaghan. Within a year Bilge subdues the Karluks, re-subjugates the Toquz Oghuz, and defeats Tang probes.",
  "Gokturk Empire|730": "Bilge Qaghan rules until November 734. In 727 CE, 30 horses sent to Tang Emperor Xuanzong yields 100,000 silk bolts in return — nominal submission masking Chinese wealth extraction. He defeats the Khitans again (733). The Orkhon steles (732–735 CE) demonstrate a regime confident enough to invest in permanent stone monuments. Decline only becomes structural after Bilge's death.",
  "Gokturk Empire|734": "Bilge Qaghan is poisoned by chancellor Buyruk Chor (25 November 734); Bilge executes Buyruk Chor's family before dying. After Tonyukuk (d.726) and Kül Tigin (d.731), the entire governing cohort is gone. Successors Yollıg and Tengri Qaghan are killed by their own uncle Kutlug Yabghu. The Uyghur, Basmyl, and Karluk tribes coalesce into an opposition coalition.",
  "Gokturk Empire|744": "Uyghur leader Kutlug I Bilge Qaghan, allied with Karluks and Basmyls, seizes Ötüken (744 CE) and beheads the last Göktürk qaghan Ozmish. His head is sent to Tang. No rump state, no exile court. The Ashina dynasty is extinguished — but Göktürk cultural, linguistic, and institutional legacy flows into the Uyghur, Khazar, Bulgar, Seljuk, and Ottoman traditions.",
  "Han Dynasty|-206": "Liu Bang (Gaozu) is designated King of Han in remote Bashu by rival Xiang Yu of Chu. China is fragmented into eighteen kingdoms. Granaries are empty, armies undemobilised. The Han dynasty does not functionally exist — the true founding comes only at the Battle of Gaixia (202 BCE).",
  "Han Dynasty|-202": "Gaozu defeats Xiang Yu at Gaixia (202 BCE); Xiang Yu commits suicide. Liu Bang is proclaimed Emperor of reunified China. The state is devastated: granaries empty, populations displaced, armies exhausted. The land tax is cut to 1/15 of crop yield. Gaozu also faces powerful, disloyal vassal kings he must systematically eliminate.",
  "Han Dynasty|-200": "Gaozu leads 320,000 troops north; Xiongnu Chanyu Modu encircles him at Baideng Mountain for seven days (200 BCE). Escape comes through secret bribery of Modu's consort. Gaozu initiates the heqin policy: Han princesses and annual tribute of silk, grain, and wine flow to the Xiongnu. The Han emperor must address the Chanyu as an equal.",
  "Han Dynasty|-180": "Emperor Wen (r.180–157 BCE) introduces Huang-Lao Taoist governance: minimal interference, taxes cut to 1/30 of harvest, prisoners released, agriculture encouraged. State granaries gradually fill. But Xiongnu cavalry raid to within 100 miles of Chang'an during Wen's reign; the heqin tribute still flows. Real institutional progress — but strategic weakness persists.",
  "Han Dynasty|-140": "Emperor Wu (r.141–87 BCE) inherits a treasury and granaries full after four generations of Huang-Lao fiscal restraint. Confucianism is elevated as state ideology; the Imperial Academy (Taixue) is established. However, Grand Empress Dowager Dou still controls court decisions, and the first Xiongnu offensive doesn't launch until the Mayi ambush (133 BCE).",
  "Han Dynasty|-119": "Generals Wei Qing and Huo Qubing cross the Gobi, reaching Lake Baikal — inflicting catastrophic Xiongnu losses. The Hexi Corridor is secured; five new commanderies are established and colonised by 700,000 soldier-settlers. Han territory roughly doubles since Gaozu's reign. The military peak is absolute — but the treasury is being dangerously depleted.",
  "Han Dynasty|-100": "Han reaches maximum geographic extent: Korea east, Ferghana west (campaigns 104–101 BCE), Tarim Basin northwest, northern Vietnam south. The Silk Road is open. Sima Qian (145–86 BCE) writes the Shiji. But Emperor Wu's campaigns impose grain, salt, and iron monopolies and new taxes — fiscal exhaustion is now structurally embedded.",
  "Han Dynasty|-87": "Emperor Wu dies (87 BCE) after 54 years — a record among ethnic Han emperors. Regent Huo Guang reduces taxes, lightens conscription, and convenes the Discourses on Salt and Iron (81 BCE) rolling back Wu's monopolies. The empire retains all Wu-era territorial gains; no external power threatens it. A competent managed recovery is underway.",
  "Han Dynasty|-50": "Emperor Xuandi (r.74–48 BCE) presides over Han's peak. Southern Xiongnu Chanyu Huhanye submits in person to the Han court (51 BCE) — the first time in the dynasty's history the confederation formally acknowledges Chinese suzerainty. The heqin tribute policy is reversed: Huhanye receives Han gifts without returning tribute. Fiscal health restored; Silk Road flourishing; governing system at finest calibration.",
  "Han Dynasty|9": "Wang Mang (45 BCE–23 CE) usurps the throne from infant Liu Ying (9 CE). His reforms — nationalising land as 'royal land,' abolishing private land ownership, prohibiting slave trading, repeatedly changing coinage — cause immediate economic chaos. The noble class, merchants, and peasants are all alienated simultaneously. Yellow River floods, famines, and plagues compound the political collapse.",
  "Han Dynasty|23": "Wang Mang's reforms trigger civil war killing ~25 million — roughly half of China's population. His 430,000-man army is annihilated at Kunyang (23 CE). Chang'an is sacked 7 October 23 CE; Wang Mang is killed and dismembered. The Red Eyebrows and multiple Han restorationist claimants fight on. Western Han's institutional state is reduced to rubble.",
  "Han Dynasty|50": "Liu Xiu proclaims himself Emperor Guangwu (25 CE); by 37 CE all rivals and warlords are eliminated. Land surveys rationalise taxation; agricultural production and population recover. The empire is stable and functional. But Britannica notes: 'The Dong Han was never as powerful as the Xi Han' — structural dependence on powerful aristocratic land-holding families limits institutional depth.",
  "Han Dynasty|75": "Emperors Ming (r.57–75 CE) and Zhang (r.75–88 CE) reduce taxes, promote water conservancy, and limit consort-family political power. General Ban Chao (32–102 CE) reasserts Central Asian control (73–102 CE), re-opening the Silk Road; by 94 CE, fifty Central Asian envoys arrive in Luoyang monthly. Buddhism enters China via the Silk Road under Emperor Ming.",
  "Han Dynasty|150": "By c.130 CE eunuchs exercise more real power than the emperors. Emperor Huan (r.146–168 CE) reigns but does not govern. The 'Partisan Prohibitions' (166–184 CE) — factional warfare between eunuchs and Confucian scholar-officials — paralyse court governance. Frontier generals are increasingly autonomous. The empire remains territorially intact but central authority is visibly collapsing.",
  "Han Dynasty|184": "Zhang Jue leads the Yellow Turban Rebellion across eight provinces (184 CE). Emperor Ling suppresses it by authorising provincial Inspectors to become Governors and raise their own armies. Cao Cao, Sun Jian, and others retain these armies after the rebellion. Central authority is irreversibly shattered by the very act of suppressing the uprising.",
  "Han Dynasty|189": "Emperor Ling dies (189 CE). General He Jin is assassinated by eunuchs; Yuan Shao retaliates with a eunuch massacre. Warlord Dong Zhuo deposes Emperor Shao, installs puppet Emperor Xian (aged 8), and burns Luoyang (190–191 CE), relocating the court to Chang'an. The Han emperor is a hostage; effective authority has dissolved into competing warlord armies.",
  "Han Dynasty|220": "Cao Cao (155–220 CE) dies; son Cao Pi forces Emperor Xian's abdication (December 220 CE), proclaiming Cao Wei. Liu Bei establishes Shu Han (221 CE); Sun Quan establishes Eastern Wu (222/229 CE). The Three Kingdoms period begins. Census data show China's population collapses from ~50 million to 16 million. The Han dynasty — 426 years — is extinguished.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1650": "Labarnas I (c.1680–1650 BCE) unites Anatolian polities around Hattusa. Hattusili I (c.1650–1620 BCE) campaigns across Anatolia into northern Syria, claiming the sea as his border. He rebuilds Hattusa as capital but ends his reign lamenting noble betrayals and choosing grandson Mursili I over his own sons. Militarily active; institutionally immature.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1600": "Mursili I operates across northern Syria and Upper Mesopotamia, crossing the Euphrates at will — building on Hattusili I's foundations. The Old Kingdom is at its fullest pre-Imperial extent: a functional, expansionist 50-year-old state about to conduct one of the Bronze Age's most audacious raids.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1595": "Mursili I raids down the Euphrates, sacks Mari and Babylon (1595 BCE), ending the First Babylonian Dynasty of Hammurabi. He transfers Babylonia to Kassite allies and returns with plunder and Marduk's cult statue. He is then assassinated by brother-in-law Hantili — triggering the dynastic catastrophe recorded in the Edict of Telipinu.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1540": "The Edict of Telipinu documents the sequence: Hantili I killed by son-in-law Zidanta I; Zidanta I killed by son Ammuna. Under Ammuna, campaigns to Arzawa, Adaniya, and elsewhere 'returned unsuccessful'; all peripheral territories are lost to rebellion and drought. The Hittite kingdom contracts to the Hattusa heartland — on the edge of extinction.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1500": "Telipinu (c.1525–1500 BCE) seizes power and issues his famous Edict: banning royal killings, establishing the Pankus nobles' assembly as constitutional court, and codifying succession rules. He recovers some territory by allying with Hurrian Kizzuwatna. But the kingdom 'had long since lost all of its conquests' (Wikipedia) — stable, but not yet expanding.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1450": "After Telipinu, the Middle Kingdom (c.1500–1400 BCE) leaves almost no records. Kings Alluwamna, Hantili II, Zidanta II, Muwatalli I, and Huzziya II are nearly unknown — Study.com suggests scarce documents reflect 'constant attack.' Egypt's Mitanni alliance places the Hittites in a strategic vice. The empire survives as a reduced principality in the Hattusa heartland.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1400": "Tudhaliya I allies with Kizzuwatna, vanquishes Aleppo and Mitanni, and expands west against Arzawa — a genuine imperial revival from the Middle Kingdom nadir. But weak successors follow; enemies advance from all directions; Tudhaliya III (~1360–1350 BCE) is forced to abandon Hattusa. Amenhotep III of Egypt declares: 'The Land of Hatti is finished!'",
  "Hittite Empire|-1360": "Tudhaliya III faces simultaneous attacks from Arzawa (west), Kaska tribes (north), and Isuwan forces (east), abandoning Hattusa and ruling in exile at Samuha. Amenhotep III of Egypt exults that 'The Land of Hatti is finished!' Hattusa is physically destroyed. Yet Tudhaliya III is already reconquering from exile — his son Suppiluliuma I will reverse everything.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1344": "Suppiluliuma I (c.1350–1322 BCE) rebuilds Hattusa on a grander scale and begins systematic reconquest: pacifying the Kaska north, conquering Arzawa west, and launching exploratory raids toward Mitanni. Building on Tudhaliya III's partial recovery from exile, Suppiluliuma now has the military and diplomatic infrastructure in place. The defining Mitanni campaign is still ahead.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1322": "Suppiluliuma I crosses the Euphrates, sacks Washukanni (Mitanni capital), installs a Mitanni puppet, then conquers Aleppo, Alalakh, Amurru, and Carchemish — installing his sons as sub-kings. He becomes the supreme Near Eastern power broker alongside Assyria and Egypt. Egypt's 'Zannanza Affair' request confirms his prestige. He dies of plague (tularemia) from Egyptian prisoners; Arnuwanda II dies of the same plague within a year.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1300": "Mursili II (c.1321–1295 BCE) defeats the Arzawan coalition, campaigns 9 times against the Kaska, and destroys the Hayasa-Azzi confederation. Wikipedia explicitly records the maximum extent under Mursili II: from Arzawa west to Mitanni east, Kaskian territories north, and Canaan south near Lebanon's border. His annals are the most detailed surviving Hittite military record. This is the territorial zenith.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1274": "Muwatalli II deploys 2,500 chariots and 37,000 troops against Ramesses II at Kadesh on the Orontes — the largest chariot battle in history. Tactically indecisive but strategically successful: Syria remains Hittite. However, Adad-Nirari I of Assyria had already annexed Carchemish and northeast Syria. The empire fights strategic two-front containment — powerful, but past its Mursili II zenith.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1250": "Hattusili III (c.1267–1237 BCE) seizes the throne from nephew Mursili III. He concludes the Eternal Treaty with Ramesses II (1258 BCE) — history's first recorded peace treaty — and gives his daughter to the pharaoh. But the treaty reflects mutual exhaustion: Assyria has taken the east, and the peace with Egypt compensates for what has been permanently lost.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1245": "Tudhaliya IV loses the Battle of Nihriya to Tukulti-Ninurta I of Assyria (c.1245 BCE) — the definitive end of Hittite resistance in Upper Mesopotamia. Despite this, Tudhaliya temporarily annexes Cyprus and continues building at Hattusa and Yazilikaya sanctuary. The Anatolian and Syrian cores remain intact; the eastern empire lost to Assyria is not recovered.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1207": "Suppiluliuma II (c.1207–1178 BCE) faces open defiance from vassal states and elite subjects. The Sea Peoples destabilise the eastern Mediterranean. Pollen and tree-ring data records 'declining annual rainfall beginning around 1250 BCE, intensifying after 1200 BCE.' He wins naval victories off Cyprus — but governs a contracting, defiant system under simultaneous environmental and military collapse.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1200": "Hattusa texts record grain shortages; letters to Ugarit beg for food. Tree-ring data confirms a catastrophic three-year drought beginning 1198–1196 BCE — the worst in 700 years of the sample. The Sea Peoples have severed Cilicia and Cyprus. Elite Hittite families are in open rebellion. The collapse has already begun; the original score of 5 drastically overstates what remains.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1190": "Archaeological evidence shows Hattusa abandoned by its elite c.1190 BCE — the palace rooms eerily intact, scribal archives left behind. Suppiluliuma II begs Ugarit for grain; Ugarit itself is destroyed c.1185 BCE. Building on the 1200 BCE collapse already underway: the capital is now emptied, the scribal tradition ended, vassals gone, the king fleeing.",
  "Hittite Empire|-1178": "Hattusa is burned c.1180 BCE by Kaskians from the north, Phrygians and Bryges from Thrace, and possibly Arzawan forces. Suppiluliuma II vanishes entirely from the record. Ramesses III's Medinet Habu inscriptions (c.1177 BCE) confirm 'Khatte' destroyed by the Sea Peoples. No successor king, no administrative continuity — a 500-year empire extinguished without a trace.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|800": "Pope Leo III crowns Charlemagne Imperator Romanorum on Christmas Day 800 — traditional HRE starting point, though most modern scholarship takes 962 as the true founding. The Carolingian Empire forms the political and conceptual substrate. Score reflects institutional precedent, not the later German-based medieval polity.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|850": "The Treaty of Verdun (843) partitions the Carolingian Empire into three kingdoms. Emperor Lothair I holds a title but 'little authority outside his own kingdom.' Viking raids devastate the Rhine delta. No unified administration, no coherent army, no clear succession. The empire nominally exists as a concept but is institutionally dissolving.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|900": "The Carolingian imperial title passes to contested minor Italian rulers; Wikipedia records it 'lapsed in 924.' The Magyars raid deep into Germany and Italy annually. Germany is governed by elected tribal dukes. This is the nadir of the HRE concept between Charlemagne and Otto I — the empire barely exists even on paper.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|946": "Otto I (r.936–973) builds the 'Ottonian church system' tying bishops and abbots to imperial service. In 951 he rescues and marries Queen Adelaide of Italy, extending power south. The Battle of Lechfeld (955) — ending devastating Magyar raids — and imperial coronation are imminent. Otto is the dominant power in Germany and increasingly in Italy.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|962": "Pope John XII crowns Otto I emperor in Rome (2 February 962). Wikipedia: 'From 962 until the 12th century, the empire was one of the most powerful monarchies in Europe.' Otto controls Germany and Italy; the Ottonian church system gives him direct control over bishops as administrative agents. The Ottonian Renaissance begins.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1000": "Otto III (r.983–1002) moves his capital to Rome, attempting to revive the Roman Empire from its ancient seat. He controls papal elections directly — Britannica notes popes 'were subject to him and his successors down to Henry III.' He integrates Kievan Rus, Poland, Bohemia, and Hungary into a Christian European order centred on the emperor.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1050": "Henry III (r.1039–1056) deposes three competing popes simultaneously at the Synod of Sutri (1046) and installs his own candidate, Clement II. He controls every bishop and abbot appointment in Germany. Wikipedia describes him as 'the last emperor to successfully dominate the selection process.' The Investiture Controversy is about to permanently destroy this model.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1076": "Pope Gregory VII excommunicates Emperor Henry IV (1076), releasing Christians from oaths of loyalty. German princes declare him dethroned; Henry IV stands barefoot in snow at Canossa for three days to receive absolution. Civil war breaks out in Germany. The controversy causes 'nearly fifty years of civil war' and the bishops can no longer serve as reliable imperial administrators.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1122": "The Concordat of Worms (23 September 1122) between Henry V and Pope Calixtus II ends the 46-year Investiture Controversy. The emperor renounces spiritual investiture (ring and crosier) but retains temporal investiture (sceptre) in Germany. Historians call this 'the defeat of the Empire' on church control — but the civil war ends and the Hohenstaufen rise is imminent.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1150": "Conrad III (r.1138–1152) begins the Hohenstaufen revival but returns from the disastrous Second Crusade (1147–1149) with damaged prestige. Barbarossa is elected king only in March 1152 and crowned emperor in 1155. The Roncaglia Decrees (1158), Italian campaigns, Peace of Venice (1177), and Peace of Constance (1183) defining the Hohenstaufen zenith all lie ahead.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1180": "Frederick I Barbarossa (r.1152–1190) consolidates Germany after exiling Henry the Lion (1180), governs Italy via the Peace of Constance (1183), is crowned King of Burgundy (1178), and receives feudal homage from Richard I of England (1189). Wikipedia calls him 'among the HRE's greatest medieval emperors.' The term 'sacrum imperium' first appears under Barbarossa (1157).",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1230": "Frederick II (r.1220–1250), Stupor Mundi, founds the University of Naples (1224), recovers Jerusalem diplomatically (1229), and makes Palermo the cultural hub of 13th-century Europe. But the Confoederatio cum principibus ecclesiasticis (1220) and Statutum in favorem principum (1232) surrender imperial governance of Germany to the princes — an irreversible institutional hollowing.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1250": "Frederick II dies (December 1250), triggering the Great Interregnum (1250–1273) — 23 years without a crowned emperor. Princes govern with near-total autonomy: minting coins, setting tolls, legislating independently. The massive privileges granted by Frederick II's Confoederatio (1220) and Statutum (1232) are now fully in effect. Imperial authority over Germany has essentially collapsed.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1350": "Charles IV (r.1346–1378) issues the Golden Bull (1356), formalising election of the emperor by seven prince-electors and confirming near-full princely sovereignty. Charles concentrates personal power in Bohemia rather than governing Germany. The Black Death (1347–1351) devastates central Europe simultaneously. The empire is a functioning but profoundly decentralised confederation.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1500": "Maximilian I (r.1493–1519) establishes the Reichskammergericht (Imperial Chamber Court, 1495), a permanent Reichstag, and the Ewiger Landfriede banning private warfare. Habsburg dynastic marriages assemble Europe's most powerful ruling family. But ~300 semi-sovereign German princes retain legislative, judicial, and coinage powers. Genuine institutional improvement — not a return to Hohenstaufen-era imperial authority.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1555": "The Peace of Augsburg (1555) legally recognises Lutheranism and establishes cuius regio eius religio — each prince determines his territory's religion. Emperor Charles V abdicates the same year. The empire's core ideological claim as defender of unified Christendom is formally compromised; princely autonomy is now grounded in both the Statutum in favorem principum (1232) and the Peace of Augsburg.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1618": "The Defenestration of Prague (May 1618) triggers the Thirty Years' War (1618–1648), killing ~8 million and reducing some German regions by 15–40%. The Habsburgs under Ferdinand II win the early phases. But the war will confirm the empire's transformation into a loose confederation under the Peace of Westphalia (1648). Religious, constitutional, and dynastic fragmentation make the conflict inevitable.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia (October 1648) grants princes full sovereignty, the right to foreign alliances, and their own armies. France and Sweden become legal guarantors of the imperial constitution — able to intervene in the empire's internal affairs. Britannica: the HRE 'had become little more than a loose confederation.' The emperor is definitively primus inter pares, not a sovereign.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1700": "Emperor Leopold I fights Louis XIV of France over the Spanish Succession — but the empire contributes through Habsburg personal power in Austria, Bohemia, and Hungary, not a unified imperial war machine. The Elector of Bavaria sides with France against the emperor. The Reichstag and courts still function, but the empire is effectively the Habsburg family's tool.",
  "Holy Roman Empire|1806": "Napoleon establishes the Confederation of the Rhine, making the HRE redundant. Austria's defeat at Austerlitz (December 1805) proves the Habsburgs cannot defend the imperial framework. Francis II abdicates and dissolves the empire on 6 August 1806, taking the title Emperor of Austria (established 1804). The HRE's institutional continuity from Charlemagne — or Otto I — ends.",
  "Ilkhanate|1256": "Hulagu Khan commands two-tenths of the entire Mongol army — 'perhaps the largest ever assembled' (Wikipedia) — charged by Great Khan Möngke to conquer western Asia to Egypt's borders. He destroys the Nizari Ismaili ('Assassin') state in 1256; Alamut surrenders without a fight. The Ilkhanate barely exists institutionally — but its military power is at an absolute apex.",
  "Ilkhanate|1258": "Hulagu sacks Baghdad (February 1258), executing the last Abbasid caliph and ending the Islamic Golden Age. Up to 800,000 die; the centre of Islamic civilisation shifts permanently to Mamluk Cairo. Hulagu conquers Muslim Syria with Armenian and Crusader assistance, takes Damascus, and appears on the verge of Egypt. The next year will establish permanent limits.",
  "Ilkhanate|1260": "Mamluk Sultan Qutuz, with Baybars commanding, defeats Hulagu's general Kitbuqa at Ain Jalut (3 September 1260) — the first major Mongol defeat by a non-Mongol army. The Ilkhanate's western border is permanently fixed. Simultaneously, Muslim Golden Horde Khan Berke declares jihad against the Ilkhanate, initiating the first Mongol civil war in the Caucasus.",
  "Ilkhanate|1270": "Abaqa Khan (r.1265–1282) defeats the Chagatai Khanate invasion (1270) and repels repeated Golden Horde incursions. He pursues a Franco-Mongol alliance with Louis IX of France, Pope Gregory X, and Edward I of England — none materialise. Persian bureaucratic practices administer the Silk Road effectively. The empire is stable and competent, but still unable to defeat the Mamluks.",
  "Ilkhanate|1282": "Abaqa dies (February 1282); brother Ahmad Tekuder, a Muslim convert, becomes the first Muslim Ilkhan and attempts peace with the Mamluks. His Islamisation of Mongol traditions loses army support. Nephew Arghun, backed by non-Muslim military elite, overthrows and executes Tekuder in 1284. The succession crisis reveals the Ilkhanate's fundamental Muslim vs. non-Muslim political fault line.",
  "Ilkhanate|1291": "Arghun Khan (r.1284–1291) achieves no strategic Mamluk breakthrough. Brother Gaykhatu (r.1291–1295) introduces paper money (chao, Yuan dynasty model) in 1294 — the Middle East's first. The population refuses; commerce collapses; Tabriz descends into anarchy. Gaykhatu is killed by cousin Baydu (1295), who reigns months before being overthrown by Ghazan. State finances are wrecked.",
  "Ilkhanate|1295": "Ghazan converts to Sunni Islam (16 June 1295), defeats and executes Baydu (4 October 1295), and is enthroned (3 November 1295). He suppresses five princely revolts 'with utmost severity' (Britannica) in his first year. Ally Nawruz is deposed and executed in 1297 after growing too powerful. Ghazan's celebrated fiscal reforms are still ahead — this is the start, not the summit.",
  "Ilkhanate|1300": "Ghazan defeats the Mamluks at Wadi al-Khaznadar (December 1299) — the only significant Mongol military victory over the Mamluks — and briefly occupies Damascus. Fiscal reforms reverse Gaykhatu's paper money disaster. Vizier Rashid al-Din Hamadani composes the Jami' al-Tawarikh — the world's first universal history. Ghazan omits the Yuan Great Khan from his coins, asserting full sovereignty. Britannica: 'the most prominent of the Il-Khans.'",
  "Ilkhanate|1305": "Öljeitü (r.1304–1316) inherits Ghazan's well-organised empire. He builds the domed mausoleum at Sultaniyya (completed c.1313, UNESCO World Heritage Site) and attempts a Mamluk campaign (1312–13) that fails. Rashid al-Din's Jami' al-Tawarikh is completed c.1315. Still powerful and culturally productive — but the absolute peak belonged to Ghazan, now dead.",
  "Ilkhanate|1310": "Öljeitü converts to Twelver Shia Islam (1309–10) under al-Allama al-Hilli and al-Bahrani, declaring it state religion and renaming his capital after Imam Ali ibn Abi Talib. The Sunni population and Mongol military elite resist. Britannica: 'civil war was imminent when he died in 1316.' Son and successor Abu Sa'id promptly reconverts to Sunni Islam.",
  "Ilkhanate|1320": "Abu Sa'id (r.1317–1335) is enthroned as a child of ~12; Amir Chupan governs as regent. Chupan repels the Golden Horde invasion (1319), crushes a Chagatayid revolt (1320), and concludes peace with the Mamluk Sultanate (1322) — enabling free Silk Road trade for the first time. Ibn Battuta marvels at prosperity before the sudden collapse. Regent-vs.-khan tension is already building.",
  "Ilkhanate|1330": "Abu Sa'id eliminates regent Chupan in 1327; replaces him with 'Big' Hasan, who is exiled in 1332. Rashid al-Din has been executed on false poisoning charges (1318), depriving the state of its greatest administrator. Non-Mongol emirs receive unprecedented military authority. Britannica: factional disputes 'became rampant.' The Black Death is approaching. Dissolution is five years away.",
  "Ilkhanate|1335": "Abu Sa'id dies 30 November 1335 without a successor. Arpa Ke'un is elevated but executed at the Battle of Jaghatu (May 1336). Shadow khans — Musa, Muhammad, Sati Beg, Jahan Temür, Sulayman, Anushirvan — are manipulated by competing Jalayirid, Chupanid, Muzaffarid, and Sarbadar emirs. Ibn Battuta: astonished 'the realm which had seemed so mighty had dissolved so quickly.'",
  "Ilkhanate|1350": "Successor states stabilise: Jalayirids hold Iraq and western Persia; Muzaffarids hold Fars and Kirman; Sarbadars govern Khorasan; Chupanids hold Azerbaijan. Anushirvan (r.1344–1356), a Mongol great-grandson, serves as Jalayirid puppet 'Ilkhan.' The Black Death devastates Persia's population. The dynastic name barely survives in puppet form, attached to no real state.",
  "Ilkhanate|1357": "Anushirvan, last nominal Ilkhan, disappears from sources after 1356; the last Ilkhanid pretender is assassinated c.1353 (New World Encyclopedia). The Jalayirid Sultanate dominates Iraq and western Persia. The state that sacked Baghdad and destroyed the Abbasid Caliphate is gone — leaving behind Persian culture, Rashid al-Din's historiography, and proto-Safavid Shia traditions introduced under the Ilkhanids.",
  "Italian Empire|1861": "Italy unifies in 1861 as a latecomer to colonialism — Portugal, Spain, Britain, and France have centuries of empire-building behind them. The Scramble for Africa is about to close the window of opportunity. Italy's first colonial foothold, Assab Bay in Eritrea, comes only in 1869–1882. Score reflects potential, not achievement.",
  "Italian Empire|1889": "Italy formally establishes Eritrea as its first colony and develops Italian Somaliland. The Treaty of Wuchale (1889) with Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia gives Italy a claimed protectorate over Ethiopia — the basis for the coming war. Italy is a functioning colonial power, but its ambitions have not yet been tested militarily.",
  "Italian Empire|1896": "Emperor Menelik II destroys the Italian force at Adwa (1 March 1896): 6,000–7,000 Italian and Eritrean soldiers killed. The Treaty of Addis Ababa forces Italy to recognise Ethiopian independence and abandon its claimed protectorate. The worst European colonial defeat of the era — Mussolini explicitly cites revenge for Adwa as motivation for the 1935 invasion.",
  "Italian Empire|1911": "Prime Minister Giolitti declares war on the Ottoman Empire (October 1911), annexing Libya under full Italian sovereignty and seizing the Dodecanese Islands. Following Italian fears of French consolidation in Morocco, 150,000 settlers arrive rapidly. Adwa is symbolically revenged — but Senussi resistance in Libya continues until 1931.",
  "Italian Empire|1922": "Mussolini appointed Prime Minister (October 1922); full dictator by 1926. Imperial expansion becomes central to Fascist identity — Italy framed as heir to ancient Rome. Libya's pacification (1923–1932) under Graziani employs concentration camps, mass deportations, and the execution of resistance leader Omar Mukhtar (1931). Pre-war colonial possessions — Libya, Eritrea, Somaliland, Dodecanese — are intact.",
  "Italian Empire|1936": "Mussolini announces the Italian Empire (9 May 1936). Eritrea, Somalia, and conquered Ethiopia merge into Africa Orientale Italiana (AOI); Victor Emmanuel III is proclaimed Emperor of Ethiopia. Chemical weapons and aerial bombardment kill ~275,000 Ethiopians. Britain and France tacitly approve, prioritising Italy as a counterweight against Germany. The formal African colonial empire is now complete.",
  "Italian Empire|1939": "Italy invades Albania (April 1939); Victor Emmanuel III adds the title King of Albania. The empire — Libya, Eritrea, Somalia, Ethiopia (AOI), Dodecanese, Albania — covers ~3.7 million km² with ~15 million subjects. Italy enters WWII only in June 1940. This is Mussolini's 'New Roman Empire' at its pre-war maximum.",
  "Italian Empire|1941": "Italy's wartime maximum combines formal empire with WWII occupations: British Somaliland (1940), southeast France (1940), Greece including Athens and Crete, Yugoslav Dalmatia, Slovenia, Montenegro, Kosovo, and Corsica (1942). Maximum-ever territorial footprint — but already shadowed: British forces begin recapturing East Africa in early 1941; Haile Selassie returns to Addis Ababa on 5 May 1941.",
  "Italian Empire|1942": "East Africa (Eritrea, Somalia, Ethiopia — AOI) is entirely lost by May 1941; Haile Selassie returns to his throne. The Second Battle of El Alamein (October–November 1942) halts Rommel's Afrika Korps. Italy still nominally holds Libya, Albania, and occupation zones — but all are German-dependent and indefensible. Axis forces in Tunisia surrender entirely by May 1943.",
  "Italian Empire|1943": "Allied invasion of Sicily (July 1943) triggers Mussolini's deposition by the Fascist Grand Council (25 July). Marshal Badoglio signs an armistice (September 1943). Germany occupies northern Italy, establishes the puppet Salò Republic, and seizes Albania, Dalmatia, Greece, and Slovenia. The Treaty of Paris (1947) strips Italy of all colonial possessions. The imperial project is completely extinguished.",
  "Jolof Empire|1300": "In 1300 CE, the Wolof existed as a nascent proto-state and vassal of the Mali Empire. Scattered chieftaincies under Malian suzerainty lacked political coherence or independent power projection, marking a period of consolidation against neighboring Serer and Fula populations before the formal founding of the empire.",
  "Jolof Empire|1350": "The empire was founded by the legendary Ndiadiane Ndiaye, who united the region through supernatural authority and diplomatic recognition. While still embryonic and acknowledging Malian dominance, this period marks the transition from scattered groups to a newly confederated polity gaining its initial foothold in Senegambia.",
  "Jolof Empire|1360": "Jolof achieved permanent independence by exploiting internal Mali succession disputes. Transitioning to a sovereign empire, it established a confederal structure and an electoral monarchy. The core states of Cayor, Baol, and Waalo joined through diplomatic prestige, creating a functioning but still fragile imperial framework.",
  "Jolof Empire|1380": "By 1380, the core confederacy was firmly established, and the \"Wolofization\" of regional ruling classes proceeded. The empire controlled vital trade between Saharan networks and coastal rivers, funded by a tribute system and a standing army. Power projection increased, although deep political integration remained shallow.",
  "Jolof Empire|1400": "The empire extended its influence north to the Senegal River and east toward the Sudan. Filling the vacuum left by the declining Ghana and Mali empires, Jolof commanded tribute from a vast federation and dominated trade routes involving slaves, gold, and ivory, reaching mid-sized imperial status.",
  "Jolof Empire|1444": "Portuguese arrival initiated trade that initially bolstered the empire. Exchanging captives for horses significantly enhanced Jolof’s cavalry and military reach. However, this maritime contact began shifting economic gravity toward coastal vassal states with direct port access, subtly planting the seeds for future structural instability.",
  "Jolof Empire|1450": "Jolof approached its territorial zenith during this period of aggressive expansion. With the Portuguese trade established and conquests of Gambian states underway, the empire maintained five vassal states in tribute. Its cavalry-based military reached near-peak strength, positioning the state just below its absolute historical climax.",
  "Jolof Empire|1460": "Under Buurba Biram Njeme Eler, the empire reached its absolute peak, controlling all territory from the Senegal to the Gambia rivers. With the capital moved to Thieung to reflect its expanded dominion, the empire achieved maximum revenue, military capacity, and confederal cohesion as a regional hegemon.",
  "Jolof Empire|1489": "A severe succession crisis saw the murder of the Buurba and a failed Portuguese intervention to install a client king. While the military and tributary systems survived, the political instability and alienating shifts toward coastal interests caused significant structural damage, weakening the central authority of the state.",
  "Jolof Empire|1500": "Although maintaining a formidable army of 110,000 men, the empire faced irreversible structural decline. The Atlantic trade disproportionately enriched coastal vassals like Cayor while Jolof remained landlocked. This economic shift hollowed out the imperial center’s leverage, leaving the sovereign state militarily massive but politically compromised.",
  "Jolof Empire|1513": "The loss of Futa Toro to Koli Tengella’s Fulani and Mandinka forces breached the empire’s northern frontier. This defeat signaled the Buurba’s inability to defend his periphery, further emboldening coastal states to seek independence and highlighting the diminishing reach of Jolof’s once-dominant military power.",
  "Jolof Empire|1530": "By 1530, the empire entered terminal decline. Rich from Atlantic trade, coastal states no longer required the Buurba’s protection or patronage. Landlocked and lacking modern military technology compared to its vassals, the imperial center became a hollowed-out entity with its constituent states on the verge of secession.",
  "Jolof Empire|1549": "The empire dissolved following the Battle of Danki, where the Buurba was killed in an ambush by the rebellious Cayor army. This decisive defeat triggered a total cascade of secession by all vassal states, ending Jolof’s imperial hegemony and reducing the state to a diminished local kingdom.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1537": "Under Sami Koli, Kaabu achieved independence from the declining Mali Empire, transitioning from a province to a sovereign Mandinka federation. Though institutions were still forming and the economic engine of the slave trade was not yet integrated, this era marked the formal birth of the empire.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1580": "Kaabu integrated into the Atlantic slave trade, supplying captives to Portuguese merchants to fund territorial consolidation. The Nyancho warrior aristocracy matured, establishing a rotational succession system while subjugating local principalities, signaling the empire’s transition into a sophisticated, predatory regional state with growing military dominance.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1600": "Now the undisputed power of southern Senegambia, Kaabu expanded to over a dozen provinces with Mandinka as the lingua franca. The slave trade became the primary engine of aristocratic wealth, fueling a self-reinforcing cycle of raids that solidified the federation's robust functionality and regional hegemony.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1650": "At its absolute peak, Kaabu controlled major river trade routes and half the regional slave trade. With a stable 32-province federal structure and a unique cloth-tax system funding perpetual military campaigns, the Nyancho hegemony remained unchallenged, maintaining diverse ethnic enclaves under a sophisticated constitutional framework.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1700": "Although still the unquestioned hegemon, internal cohesion began to fray due to the violence inherent in the slave-raiding cycle. The Nyancho aristocracy remained wealthy and military outcomes appeared stable, but scholarly analysis identifies this period as the germination of a long-term downward structural trajectory.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1750": "Structural strain became visible as the Atlantic slave trade declined and pastoralist Fula populations grew within Kaabu’s borders. While still militarily dominant, the emergence of Islamic reform movements and accumulating foundational vulnerabilities indicated that the empire's plateau of maximum strength had officially passed.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1776": "The establishment of the Futa Jallon Imamate created a permanent existential threat and a shift in the strategic landscape. Continuous jihadist pressure and the internal oppression of Fula immigrants fractured the empire along ethnic and religious lines, despite Kaabu successfully repulsing early military incursions.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1800": "Kaabu faced dual crises: relentless raids from Futa Jallon and the collapse of the slave trade due to British abolitionism. To replace lost revenues, elites increased peasant taxation, sparking internal class conflict and territorial contraction while the state struggled to maintain its social and economic foundations.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1840": "Internal feuds among Nyancho nobles and their slave armies further fractured the state as Fula raids intensified. While the fort of Berekolong remained a defensive bulwark, the loss of its economic engine and total surrounding by hostile Islamic states left the empire fundamentally weakened.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1860": "The fall of Berekolong signaled the final phase of collapse, leaving the empire reduced to its capital, Kansala. Besieged and stripped of meaningful territorial control, the state entered a period of military certainty where the last Mansaba, Janke Waali Sanneh, ruled over a dying polity.",
  "Kaabu Empire|1867": "The empire was obliterated during the Battle of Kansala when the Mansaba deliberately detonated the city’s gunpowder stores. This dramatic act of self-destruction killed most defenders and attackers alike, ending 807 years of Mandinka hegemony and permanently dissolving Kaabu as a political entity.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|700": "The Kanem Empire emerged as a decentralized pastoral confederation under the Duguwa dynasty near Lake Chad. Transitioning from nomadic roots to settled governance, this embryonic proto-state established its initial foothold, though it lacked the Islamic institutions and trade dominance that would define its later power.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|872": "By 872 CE, Kanem gained external recognition as a structured kingdom, documented by Arab geographers. Controlling vital trans-Saharan trade routes between Tripoli and Lake Chad, the state utilized formidable cavalry, marking a significant developmental leap from its pastoral origins into a recognized regional commercial anchor.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|900": "By 900 CE, Kanem’s integration into Saharan trade deepened, accumulating wealth from ivory and slaves. The Duguwa dynasty developed formalized court structures and expanded its military capacity. While a prosperous regional power, it still lacked the literate administrative and legal frameworks of its later era.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1068": "Around 1068 CE, Mai Hummay’s conversion to Islam launched an institutional revolution and the long-ruling Sayfawa dynasty. This transition introduced Arabic literacy, Quranic legal frameworks, and new diplomatic ties to the Mediterranean, transforming Kanem from a regional confederation into a sophisticated and literate Islamic state.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1100": "By 1100 CE, the Sayfawa dynasty successfully consolidated its Islamic transformation. With expanding Arabic literacy and established pilgrimage traditions, the empire leveraged trade wealth to project regional authority. This period marks a steady ascent toward its first peak, characterized by institutional depth and military capability.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1200": "By 1200 CE, Kanem was a confident, expanding empire with deep Islamic institutions and significant cavalry forces. While controlling vital trade routes and maintaining diplomatic connections to Cairo, it had not yet reached the territorial and military zenith that would define the upcoming reign of Dunama II.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1240": "Under Mai Dunama II Dibalemi, the Kanem phase reached its zenith. Commanding 40,000 cavalry, the empire annexed the Fezzan to control central Saharan trade. This power projected influence from modern-day Libya to Cameroon, establishing international prestige through diplomatic hostels in Cairo and widespread regional conquest.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1300": "Following Dunama II’s death, the empire spiraled into a catastrophic succession crisis and civil war. This internal fragmentation, coupled with relentless Bulala invasions, severely weakened the state. By 1300, Kanem had lost control of the Fezzan and faced existential threats, marking a sharp decline.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1380": "In 1380 CE, the empire reached its nadir when Bulala forces seized the capital, Njimi. The Sayfawa were forced to abandon their heartland and relocate to Bornu. This near-extinction event saw the loss of ancestral territories and trade routes, reducing the mighty empire to a displaced polity.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1400": "By 1400 CE, the Sayfawa began a fragile reconstitution in Bornu. Lacking a permanent capital and suffering from extreme dynastic instability—with fifteen rulers in seventy-five years—the state remained weak. While the move provided an agricultural base, the empire had yet to reclaim its former dominance.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1460": "Around 1460, Mai Ali I Gaji restored dynastic stability and founded the fortified capital, Ngazargamu. This strategic consolidation ended a century of chaos, fostering trade revival and agricultural development. This pivotal era marked the beginning of a sustained recovery toward a second imperial peak.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1500": "By 1500, the Bornu revival accelerated as Mai Idris III Katakarmabe recaptured the ancestral capital, Njimi. Dominating both Kanem and Bornu, the Sayfawa dynasty grew more powerful than ever. The empire re-established trade dominance over Saharan routes, signaling a robust return to regional hegemony.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1590": "Under Idris Alooma, Kanem-Bornu achieved its absolute zenith. Alooma revolutionized the military with Ottoman firearms and musketeers while implementing groundbreaking constitutional reforms, including independent qadi courts. Diplomatically prestigious and territorially expansive, the empire became the most formidable power in the central Sahel.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1600": "At the close of the 16th century, the empire maintained its peak under Alooma’s statesmanlike rule. Controlling the Lake Chad basin, Hausaland, and trans-Saharan routes, Bornu enjoyed absolute regional hegemony. Its sophisticated administrative, legal, and military systems represented the highest achievement of the 1,200-year polity.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1650": "Following Alooma’s death, the empire initially sustained its power through institutional reforms and trade revenues. However, by 1650, a slow decline began as military technology regressed. While still a wealthy, functioning state with intense diplomatic activity, the empire started drifting away from its peak.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1700": "By 1700, the rise of Atlantic trade began eroding the economic foundation of trans-Saharan commerce. Coupled with increasing Tuareg invasions and internal famines, the empire’s power visibly faded. Though it remained a functioning regional entity, its structural vulnerabilities were becoming more pronounced.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1780": "The late 18th century saw a sharp acceleration in decline, highlighted by a humiliating military defeat by the Mandara Kingdom in 1781. As Hausa states drifted toward independence and external pressures mounted, the empire shed much of its periphery, leaving the core increasingly isolated and vulnerable.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1800": "By 1800, Kanem-Bornu faced an existential threat from the looming Fulani jihad. Under incapacitated leadership, the state’s coherence deteriorated despite maintaining a large cavalry. Territorially present but institutionally weakened at every level, the empire was ill-equipped to survive the approaching political upheavals.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1808": "In 1808, Fulani jihadists captured and destroyed the long-standing capital, Ngazargamu. The empire survived only through the intervention of the warlord al-Kanemi, who defeated the invaders but became the de facto ruler. Displaced and dependent on new leadership, the state was reduced to a shell.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1846": "The millennium-old Sayfawa dynasty ended in 1846 following a failed attempt to reclaim power from the al-Kanemi line. Though the institutional shell survived under the new Shehus, the empire remained a shadow of its former self, having lost vast territories and trade revenues to its neighbors.",
  "Kanem-Bornu Empire|1893": "In 1893, the Sudanese warlord Rabih az-Zubayr conquered Bornu, decisively ending the 1,200-year-old polity. After the final Shehu fled, the territory was partitioned among European colonial powers. This event marked the absolute extinction of the empire as a sovereign entity, completing its historical cycle.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|650": "Following the collapse of the Western Turkic Khaganate, the newly-constituted Khazars successfully repelled the first Arab invasion near Balanjar. This victory established the Khaganate as an emerging steppe power capable of defending its territory against the expanding Islamic caliphate during its formative and expansionary period.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|653": "Despite losing the strategic Derbent fortress, the Khazars defeated a major Umayyad siege at Balanjar. This conflict established the Caucasus as a permanent front line, demonstrating the Khaganate’s military resilience while facing constant strategic pressure from the south as it consolidated its northern heartland.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|700": "Khazaria emerged as the dominant western steppe power by scattering the Bulgar confederation and seizing control of Crimea. Establishing a long-term geopolitical alliance with the Byzantine Empire, the mature Khaganate anchored its regional authority before facing the full military weight of the Umayyad Caliphate.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|722": "The Khaganate suffered a significant tactical setback when the Umayyad governor al-Jarrah sacked the city of Balanjar. Although pushed back, the Khazar military capacity remained largely intact, allowing the empire to prepare a massive counter-offensive to reclaim its territorial standing within the following decade.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|730": "At its military zenith, the Khazar army destroyed a 25,000-strong Arab force at the Battle of Marj Ardabil. This victory reversed decades of Umayyad penetration, though the empire had yet to translate this battlefield dominance into the commercial stability characterizing its later golden era.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|737": "The deepest Arab penetration, led by Marwan II, reached the Volga and forced the Khagan to flee. Although a severe psychological shock, the Khazars survived intact as the Arabs could not maintain occupation. This humiliation likely accelerated the ruling class’s definitive conversion to Judaism.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|750": "The Abbasid Revolution ended the Arab-Khazar wars, removing the existential southern threat. Simultaneously, the ruling elite converted to Judaism to leverage international Radhanite merchant networks. These geopolitical shifts launched the Khaganate into its golden age, combining military security with unprecedented commercial expansion.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|800": "The Pax Khazarica represented the empire’s absolute peak as a cosmopolitan commercial hub. Controlling the Silk Road and the Volga, the Khaganate institutionalized Judaism and maintained a vital Byzantine alliance. During this era, irrigation and urban life flourished, making Atil a premier medieval city.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|832": "Utilizing Byzantine engineering, the Khazars constructed the Sarkel fortress to defend against nomadic incursions. While reflecting the depth of the Byzantine-Khazar alliance and current prosperity, the need for permanent fortifications signaled a shifting strategic environment and emerging pressure from Pecheneg and Rus forces.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|850": "The Pax Khazarica entered a late phase marked by internal fissures and external threats. The Kabar rebellion revealed religious tensions among subordinate tribes, while Pecheneg pressure began disrupting northern steppe control. Despite remaining commercially dominant, these fractures indicated the start of a gradual structural erosion.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|900": "The displacement of Magyar vassals by the Pechenegs cost the Khaganate vital military manpower and revenue. Furthermore, the Byzantine Empire shifted from an ally to an antagonist, encouraging regional attacks. Although the commercial core remained functional, these structural blows significantly weakened the empire's peripheral control.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|932": "A victory in the Alan War demonstrated that the Khaganate retained functional military capacity, yet its tributary network continued to fray. Increasing Varangian raids down the Volga highlighted a deteriorating strategic situation as the empire struggled to maintain authority over its once-vast and loyal northern periphery.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|950": "The Khaganate entered its terminal phase, contracting to its Volga-Caspian core under increasing pressure from Kievan Rus. While still legally extant and commercially active, correspondence from this era reveals an empire on the defensive, with no realistic path to recovery against its encroaching and organized enemies.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|965": "Sviatoslav I of Kievan Rus delivered a death blow by capturing the western defensive anchor, Sarkel. This campaign dismantled the Khaganate’s frontier defenses and collapsed its western tributary network. The empire’s total destruction became inevitable, as it no longer possessed the means to resist the final assault.",
  "Khazar Khaganate|969": "The Khaganate was entirely destroyed when Sviatoslav I captured and leveled the capital, Atil. The political entity disintegrated immediately, and the surviving Khazar populations were assimilated into neighboring cultures. This total collapse ended a three-century arc, leaving no trace of the once-mighty commercial power.",
  "Khmer Empire|802": "Jayavarman II founded the empire on Phnom Kulen, performing the devaraja ritual to declare independence from Java. This marked the birth of a fragile federation held together by personal authority rather than infrastructure. While historically significant, the lack of permanent administrative centers reflects a nascent imperial stage.",
  "Khmer Empire|835": "Following Jayavarman II’s death, his son Jayavarman III maintained the kingdom's modest extent without major expansion. The empire remained a loose confederation of chieftains organized around the royal cult. Lacking large-scale hydraulic works or a centralized capital, the state’s institutional power remained steady but largely undeveloped.",
  "Khmer Empire|889": "Yasovarman I founded the first city of Angkor, Yasodharapura, and completed the massive East Baray reservoir. This qualitative leap established a permanent capital and a functional hydraulic network. These developments provided the agricultural surplus and infrastructure necessary to support a growing population and connect provincial centers.",
  "Khmer Empire|950": "Rajendravarman II restored stability after a period of fragmentation, returning the capital to Angkor. Resuming major temple construction like Pre Rup, he solidified the empire's institutional coherence. Economically productive through rice agriculture and trade, the state was mid-trajectory, approaching its mature phase of imperial development.",
  "Khmer Empire|1040": "Known as the \"King of the Just Laws,\" Suryavarman I centralized governance by requiring personal oaths from thousands of officials. He expanded territory significantly and began the West Baray, the empire’s largest reservoir. This era represents a mature peak, characterized by sophisticated hydraulics and wide territorial reach.",
  "Khmer Empire|1100": "Following Suryavarman I’s death, the empire faced political instability and succession crises, including the rise of the usurper Jayavarman VI. While the institutional foundations remained strong, the political legitimacy crisis prevented further expansion. This period represents an inter-peak plateau before the true 12th-century zeniths.",
  "Khmer Empire|1130": "Suryavarman II launched an unprecedented building program, including the massive Angkor Wat temple. He expanded the empire’s borders into modern Thailand, Burma, and the Malay Peninsula while engaging in diplomacy with China. This era represents a near-maximum territorial extent and a high point for Khmer architecture.",
  "Khmer Empire|1177": "A surprise Cham naval invasion sacked the capital, resulting in the king's death and a four-year occupation. The central government collapsed and the treasury was looted. While hydraulic infrastructure and provincial nobility survived, the empire’s institutional coherence was shattered in a single engagement, marking a catastrophic nadir.",
  "Khmer Empire|1200": "Jayavarman VII achieved the absolute zenith of Khmer power, rebuilding Angkor as the walled city of Angkor Thom. He established a welfare state with over 100 hospitals and rest houses while conquering Champa. At one million square kilometers, the empire reached its maximum territorial and institutional extent.",
  "Khmer Empire|1250": "Post-Jayavarman VII contraction saw withdrawal from Champa and aggressive religious iconoclasm under Jayavarman VIII. The empire also acknowledged external suzerainty for the first time by paying tribute to Mongol-led Yuan forces. While still wealthy, internal religious polarization and territorial losses signaled the start of decline.",
  "Khmer Empire|1300": "The conversion to Theravada Buddhism under Srindravarman fundamentally altered the state's legitimating ideology. Although Chinese diplomat Zhou Daguan described a still-rich empire, institutional decline was visible. The state lost its offensive military capacity and significant territory, transitioning from a divine-king model to a pious monarchy.",
  "Khmer Empire|1350": "The newly founded Ayutthaya Kingdom besieged and captured Angkor in 1353, installing Siamese princes to rule. Although the Khmer eventually retook the throne, losing the capital for several years demonstrated a critical loss of strategic initiative. This era marked a new level of existential crisis for the empire.",
  "Khmer Empire|1393": "Ayutthayan forces captured Angkor for a second time, installing a Siamese ruler. Repeated sackings and deforestation caused the vital hydraulic systems to silt up, while erratic monsoons disrupted agriculture. Although the dynasty briefly reasserted control, the empire faced a terminal trajectory due to structural and environmental decline.",
  "Khmer Empire|1431": "Following a final Ayutthayan siege, King Ponhea Yat permanently abandoned Angkor as indefensible. The court relocated to the Phnom Penh region, shifting the state's focus from hydraulic agriculture to maritime trade. This marked the absolute end of the empire’s traditional institutional, territorial, and civilizational character.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1077": "Malik-Shah I appoints the Turkic commander Anushtegin Gharchai as governor of Khwarazm, a peripheral Seljuk province. While establishing a hereditary household, the state possesses no independent power, sovereign territory, or imperial status. It functions entirely as a provincial governorship under the authority of Seljuk masters.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1097": "Upon Anushtegin’s death, the Seljuks appoint his son, Qutb al-Din Muhammad I, as the first hereditary Khwarazmshah. This move secures the Anushteginid lineage while maintaining firm Seljuk suzerainty. The province remains a loyal vassal with no autonomous military or diplomatic standing during this consolidation phase.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1127": "Ala ad-Din Atsiz ascends, marking the first genuine assertion of imperial ambition by rebelling against the Seljuks. He briefly occupies Khurasan before being forced back into vassalage. This era reflects a state beginning to assert an autonomous identity despite being constrained by both Seljuk and Qara Khitai suzerainty.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1150": "Atsiz achieves de facto independence by expanding across northwest Central Asia and annexing territories like Jand. Although the Seljuk threat diminished due to internal chaos, the state remains a double-tributary to both the Seljuks and Qara Khitai. This status prevents it from reaching full sovereign imperial standing.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1156": "Il-Arslan inherits the throne and effectively ends Seljuk suzerainty following the death of Sultan Ahmad Sanjar. Khwarazmian military power projects into Transoxiana, demonstrated by the invasion of the Karakhanid emirate and the taking of Bukhara. The state maintains only one remaining overlord, the Qara Khitai.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1172": "Tekish emerges from a succession crisis, initially enlisting Qara Khitai aid before turning against them to assert full independence. By cultivating Turkic tribal allies for his cavalry, he consolidates the state internally. However, the empire has yet to achieve the major southern conquests of a great power.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1190": "Tekish establishes a genuine imperial power, controlling Khorasan, western Iran, and the Syr Darya basin. The state stretches from the Aral Sea to Iraq, having destroyed remnant Seljuk power structures. While dominant, it still faces potential threats from the Ghurids and the Abbasid Caliphate.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1194": "Tekish kills the last Great Seljuk sultan, Toghrul III, ending Seljuk power and earning formal recognition as Sultan from the Abbasid Caliph. Although recognized as the dominant eastern Islamic power, the empire’s territorial limits remain short of the maximum extent reached by his successor.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1200": "Ala ad-Din Muhammad II succeeds his father, inheriting a recognized sultanate despite early invasions by the Ghurids. While momentarily on the defensive, the state possesses secure central territory and sufficient resources. It retains the momentum necessary to eventually become the most powerful empire in the region.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1210": "Muhammad II becomes the undisputed master of Central Asia by absorbing the Qara Khitai and destroying the Western Karakhanids. The empire expands from the Aral Sea to the Afghan highlands. Full dominance over Persia proper continues to be consolidated as the Atabegs of Azerbaijan become vassals.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1215": "The destruction of the Ghurid dynasty and the dismantling of their rule in Afghanistan brings the empire to near-peak territorial breadth. It now spans Central Asia, Persia, and Afghanistan. However, the total consolidation of western Persian territories and the final march on Baghdad are still pending.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1217": "The empire reaches its absolute zenith, spanning 2.3 to 3.6 million km² from the Syr Darya to the Persian Gulf. As the undisputed dominant power of the Islamic world, Muhammad II subordinates the Abbasid Caliph. This moment represents the empire's maximum territorial, military, and ambitious peak.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1218": "The Otrar Massacre and the execution of Mongol diplomatic envoys seal the empire's doom. While physically intact and prosperous, this fatal blunder provokes Genghis Khan to launch a full-scale invasion. Internal fractures and a fragmented command structure are now dangerously exposed to imminent external assault.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1219": "The Mongol invasion begins, and the empire crumbles with shocking speed as major cities like Bukhara and Samarkand fall. Muhammad II’s disastrous strategy of dispersing his army into city garrisons allows the Mongols to seize the initiative. The Shah flees westward, abandoning the front entirely.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1220": "Muhammad II dies in exile on a Caspian island while the heartland and capital are systematically destroyed. The Mongols massacre the populations of Merv, Nishapur, and Herat. Jalal ad-Din succeeds as claimant Shah but lacks controlled territory, marking a state whose central institutions have collapsed.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1221": "Jalal ad-Din scores a brief victory at Parwan before his army is destroyed by Genghis Khan at the Battle of the Indus. He flees to India after a daring escape. The state exists only as a charismatic leader with borrowed forces and no home territory or future prospects.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1225": "Jalal ad-Din reconstitutes a temporary base in Azerbaijan and Georgia, operating essentially as a roving mercenary warband. He lacks a stable tax base, administrative apparatus, or core territory. This phase reflects the last embers of a state conducting raids rather than practicing sustainable governance.",
  "Khwarazmian Empire|1231": "The empire is extinguished when Jalal ad-Din is murdered by highwaymen following a decisive defeat by the Seljuks of Rum. His remaining followers disperse as mercenaries across the Islamic world. The political entity ceases to exist, completing its rapid and catastrophic historical cycle.",
  "Kongo Empire|1390": "Lukeni lua Nimi founded the Kingdom of Kongo by merging the Mpemba Kasi and Mbata polities through dynastic marriage. This nascent confederacy established Mbanza Kongo as its capital, leveraging regional trade in copper and raffia to build initial administrative and military foundations for future expansion.",
  "Kongo Empire|1420": "Successors consolidated power by integrating neighboring groups through conquest and treaty. The state developed institutional depth by appointing provincial governors to collect tribute in exchange for protection. This governance structure provided the military and administrative framework necessary for the kingdom to evolve beyond its original core.",
  "Kongo Empire|1450": "The kingdom achieved regional dominance, extending its reach from the Atlantic to the Kwango River. Managing a population of over half a million, its sophisticated tax system integrated diverse ecological resources. A formidable army maintained hegemony, reflecting a strong power approaching its maximum expansionary phase.",
  "Kongo Empire|1483": "Portuguese explorer Diogo Cão initiated first contact, establishing a transformative relationship. While initially elevating the kingdom through cultural exchange and trade, this era simultaneously introduced the demand for slave markets. This pivotal moment provided the tools for the empire's peak while introducing long-term structural vulnerabilities.",
  "Kongo Empire|1491": "Manikongo Nzinga a Nkuwu’s baptism established formal diplomatic relations with Europe. Portuguese missionaries and craftsmen introduced new goods and technologies, bringing the kingdom to near-peak prestige. However, internal tensions emerged between pro-European court factions and traditionalists, signaling future succession conflicts and ideological shifts.",
  "Kongo Empire|1500": "Following a brief succession civil war, Afonso I ascended to the throne. This transitional period saw the continued absorption of European technical knowledge and the growth of the slave trade. Mbanza Kongo began its transformation into a stone-built capital, setting the stage for Afonso's absolute zenith.",
  "Kongo Empire|1512": "The Regimento treaty with Portugal acknowledged Kongo’s equality with European monarchies. Afonso I expanded territorial reach and institutional sophistication, sending ambassadors to Rome and Lisbon. The appointment of the first African bishop and the reconstruction of the capital in stone marked the immediate cusp of peak power.",
  "Kongo Empire|1520": "At its absolute peak, the kingdom achieved maximum territorial, diplomatic, and administrative reach. Afonso I maintained a sophisticated correspondence with European monarchs and governed a major urban capital with elaborate architecture. This era represents the pinnacle of Kongo's institutional strength and cultural prestige in west-central Africa.",
  "Kongo Empire|1526": "Strains appeared as Portuguese merchants engaged in illegal slave trading, undermining sovereign authority. Afonso I established administrative committees to regulate trade, but his protests were largely ignored by the Portuguese crown. While still near its peak, the state faced the beginning of structural erosion and internal instability.",
  "Kongo Empire|1542": "Afonso I’s death triggered succession disputes that fragmented noble loyalty and diverted military resources. Although institutions and trade networks remained functional, the kingdom lost the centralizing force of its greatest ruler. This transition marked the beginning of a post-peak decline driven by internal factionalism and maneuvering.",
  "Kongo Empire|1568": "The Jaga invasion devastated the heartland, forcing the Manikongo to flee the capital. This existential crisis necessitated a desperate appeal for Portuguese military intervention. While the invaders were eventually repelled, the occupation shattered institutional coherence and left the kingdom vulnerable to increasing external and internal pressures.",
  "Kongo Empire|1580": "Álvaro I restored control over the capital, but the price of Portuguese assistance was the permanent loss of Luanda. This strategic foothold allowed for the expansion of Portuguese Angola. The kingdom survived a near-death experience but now operated under the constant shadow of an aggressive colonial neighbor.",
  "Kongo Empire|1600": "The kingdom remained intact but grew structurally dependent on the slave trade, which began eroding sovereign capacity. Although the Catholic Church was entrenched, internal fractures deepened as the supply of foreign captives dwindled. This stability was precarious, faced with both internal social shifts and external Angolan aggression.",
  "Kongo Empire|1641": "Garcia II skillfully asserted independent foreign policy by allying with the Dutch to expel the Portuguese. This moment of strategic agency temporarily restored imperial prestige. However, the subsequent Dutch withdrawal left the kingdom isolated, failing to provide the long-term leverage necessary to secure its sovereignty permanently.",
  "Kongo Empire|1665": "Defeat at the Battle of Mbwila and the death of António I permanently destroyed the kingdom's unitary character. The capital was eventually abandoned as rival factions launched a century of civil war. The state fragmented into competing enclaves, ending its role as a unified regional power.",
  "Kongo Empire|1700": "During the kingdom’s darkest period, the capital remained abandoned while rival claimants engaged in relentless civil war. The population became the primary commodity of the slave trade, with even Christian nobles sold into Atlantic captivity. This era reflects a state reduced to factional fragments and institutional ruin.",
  "Kongo Empire|1709": "Pedro IV reunified core territories and reoccupied the capital, establishing a rotational succession system to manage rival royal houses. While this provided relative stability and a genuine recovery of central institutions, the restored state remained significantly smaller, weaker, and more internally divided than the pre-1665 monarchy.",
  "Kongo Empire|1800": "The rotational kingship system broke down, leading to resumed factional fighting and the loss of political relevance. The kings of Kongo possessed no more wealth than common merchants, overshadowed by the adjacent Portuguese colony and neighboring successor states. The dynasty entered a long twilight of institutional insignificance.",
  "Kongo Empire|1885": "The Berlin Conference partitioned Kongo's sphere among European powers without local consultation. By 1888, Pedro V signed a treaty of vassalage, formally ceding sovereignty to Portugal. Although the royal title remained, all actual power transitioned to the colonial administration, leaving only the final vestiges of nominal independence.",
  "Kongo Empire|1914": "Following the suppression of the Buta revolt, Portugal formally abolished the monarchy and integrated the remaining territories into colonial Angola. This administrative decree extinguished a political entity that had maintained continuity for 524 years. The deposition of the last king marked the absolute end of the kingdom.",
  "Kushite Empire|-1070": "With the collapse of the Egyptian New Kingdom and the withdrawal of Egyptian administration from Nubia, the region of Upper Nubia around Napata emerged as a nascent independent polity. Local rulers began asserting authority over the Nile corridor, laying the earliest foundations of what would become the Kushite state.",
  "Kushite Empire|-1069": "Kush emerged as an independent polity centered at Napata following the Egyptian New Kingdom's withdrawal from Nubia. Though culturally influenced by centuries of Egyptian rule, this nascent state began defining its own political identity while confined territorially to Upper Nubia, marking its initial sovereign stage.",
  "Kushite Empire|-800": "King Alara unified the scattered polities of Upper Nubia, establishing Napata as a centralized political and religious capital. He initiated the reconstruction of significant temples and formalized high religious offices, creating a unified regional power that laid the foundations for future expansion beyond Nubian borders.",
  "Kushite Empire|-750": "King Kashta peacefully extended Kushite influence into Upper Egypt, declaring himself Pharaoh at Thebes. By appointing his daughter to a high religious office, he secured ideological authority over Egypt’s sacred centers. This established a critical foothold in Egypt without military conflict, signaling major imperial growth.",
  "Kushite Empire|-727": "Piye launched a successful military campaign to conquer Lower Egypt, reaching the Mediterranean. However, because he returned to Napata rather than directly occupying the north, the empire lacked the administrative consolidation required for its absolute peak, despite its massive territorial extent from the Sixth Cataract.",
  "Kushite Empire|-712": "Pharaoh Shabaka fully consolidated Kushite rule, moving the capital to Thebes and governing a unified Egyptian and Nubian state. He promoted Egyptian cultural preservation through the Shabaka Stone. This era represents the full coherence of the 25th Dynasty as the largest unified state of its time.",
  "Kushite Empire|-690": "Under Taharqa, the empire reached its absolute zenith, characterized by prosperity, a massive cultural renaissance, and extensive temple construction throughout the Nile Valley. Taharqa’s formidable military even intervened in Levantine affairs against Assyria, marking the maximum territorial and institutional reach of the Kushite Pharaonic state.",
  "Kushite Empire|-670": "The empire suffered a significant blow when the Assyrian King Esarhaddon conquered Memphis, forcing Taharqa to flee to Napata. Although the Kushites briefly recaptured territory, the imperial structure was visibly cracked, with the loss of Lower Egypt and the enslavement of the royal heir by Assyria.",
  "Kushite Empire|-661": "The 25th Dynasty ended definitively after Ashurbanipal’s punitive army sacked Thebes, the empire’s spiritual heart. Tantamani fled to Napata, unable to threaten Assyrian dominance again. This ideological catastrophe stripped the Kushites of their Egyptian throne, reducing them once more to a purely Nubian power.",
  "Kushite Empire|-640": "Retaining their Nubian heartland at Napata, Kushite kings maintained pharaonic titles but lost imperial prestige. Though politically diminished, the state remained economically functional, leveraging its vital gold trade with Saite Egypt. This period represents a transition from a world-class empire to a stable, regionally focused kingdom.",
  "Kushite Empire|-600": "Under Aspelta, the Napatan rump state remained an economically viable regional power, primarily through its gold trade. The kingdom’s continued significance was demonstrated by its ability to resist external threats, including a failed Persian invasion attempt, though its territorial reach remained confined compared to its imperial peak.",
  "Kushite Empire|-591": "Following the Egyptian sack of Napata by Psamtik II, the dynasty relocated its capital south to Meroë. This strategic move to a resource-rich location further from the Egyptian border saved the kingdom from extinction but reflected the lowest point of the Napatan era's political and religious stability.",
  "Kushite Empire|-350": "Reconstituted at Meroë, the kingdom entered a new phase of regional prosperity. Capitalizing on its iron-ore deposits and southern trade routes, Meroë developed into a vibrant civilizational center. This era marked the beginning of an upward trajectory toward a second golden age defined by unique African institutions.",
  "Kushite Empire|-295": "King Ergamenes launched a Meroitic renaissance by breaking the traditional political power of the priesthood. He asserted a distinct indigenous identity, abandoning Egyptian hieroglyphs for the Meroitic script and moving royal burials to Meroë. This institutional reform created a sophisticated and culturally independent African civilization.",
  "Kushite Empire|-100": "Meroë reached its second-life peak during the golden age of the Candaces. Queen Amanirenas successfully repelled a Roman invasion, securing a favorable peace treaty. The empire thrived as a major industrial and commercial hub, exporting iron, gold, and luxury goods across Mediterranean and Indian Ocean networks.",
  "Kushite Empire|100": "The empire began a gradual decline as the rising Aksumite Kingdom provided aggressive competition for Red Sea trade. Simultaneously, systemic iron production led to massive deforestation, and agricultural productivity began to erode. While still functional, the state faced emerging structural vulnerabilities that compromised its long-term position.",
  "Kushite Empire|300": "Kush entered terminal systemic failure due to Noba incursions, total ecological exhaustion, and the loss of trade routes to Aksum. Agricultural land was depleted, and internal political coherence fragmented. The state reached a point of disintegration where it could no longer resist foreign invasion or internal collapse.",
  "Kushite Empire|350": "The Kingdom of Kush was extinguished when the Aksumite King Ezana sacked Meroë. The city was deserted, and the political entity dissolved into successor kingdoms. This absolute trough marked the end of a millennium-long polity and the disappearance of its unique script and unified governance.",
  "Luba Empire|1500": "Around 1500, the Upemba Depression saw the coalescence of proto-Luba chiefdoms. While archaeological evidence shows ironworking and social stratification, the state lacked the sacred kingship and tribute mechanisms of the later empire. This period represents an institutionally complex but pre-imperial formative stage.",
  "Luba Empire|1585": "Kalala Ilunga founded the Second Luba Dynasty after defeating Nkongolo Mwamba. This era introduced the sacred balopwe kingship and the Bambudye secret society, providing institutional memory and political legitimacy. These foundational changes allowed for the empire’s first significant territorial expansion and lasting statecraft.",
  "Luba Empire|1600": "Following Kalala’s death, succession disputes slowed growth, yet Luba cultural prestige surged. The Lunda Kingdom began adopting Luba statecraft through dynastic marriage, demonstrating the empire's regional influence. Despite internal fragility, the state remained an influential, trade-connected polity poised for future consolidation and expansion.",
  "Luba Empire|1650": "The Luba governance model expanded significantly via trade networks, circulating copper, salt, and iron. While economically dynamic, the empire’s core territory remained modest. Frequent court relocations and persistent succession vulnerabilities meant direct territorial control was limited, preventing the state from achieving full imperial maturity.",
  "Luba Empire|1700": "Under Kings Ndaye Mwine Nkombe and Kadilo, serious territorial consolidation began. Enhanced trade links with Portuguese and East African networks enriched the aristocracy through monopolies. While this period represents a strong pre-peak growth phase, the empire’s greatest territorial expansions remained decades away.",
  "Luba Empire|1750": "King Kekenya led the empire to its strongest pre-peak position, establishing functioning tribute systems and expansive trade networks. While formidable, placing the absolute peak here is historically inaccurate. Kekenya laid the vital groundwork for the coming 19th-century zenith, but his reach was ultimately eclipsed.",
  "Luba Empire|1790": "Ilunga Sungu initiated a transformational eastward expansion toward Lake Tanganyika, opening vital trade routes to the East African coast. Through military campaigns and strategic marriage alliances, he significantly increased the empire's frontier. This era marks the beginning of the \"Three-King Peak\".",
  "Luba Empire|1800": "The empire maintained maximum expansion under Ilunga Sungu and early Kumwimbe Ngombe. Trade reached its height, utilizing copper and raffia currencies to exchange ivory and hides across vast networks. This phase represents a state at its territorial limit, with its regional presence documented by visiting foreign envoys.",
  "Luba Empire|1825": "Under Kumwimbe Ngombe, the Luba Empire reached its absolute apogee. Achieving maximum territorial extent from the Lualaba to Lake Tanganyika, Ngombe established sophisticated provincial administration and received tribute from vast populations. This documented peak represents the highest degree of Luba military, economic, and administrative power.",
  "Luba Empire|1850": "During Ilunga Kabale’s reign, the empire sustained its near-maximum extent through final expansions northwest and northeast. Despite looming threats from Arab-Swahili slave traders, the Luba heartland remained unbreached. This period reflects the final chapter of the empire’s peak before the structural fractures of succession took hold.",
  "Luba Empire|1870": "The death of Ilunga Kabale triggered a catastrophic succession crisis, as the empire lacked a formalized means of transfer. Simultaneously, external raiders breached the heartland, exploiting internal fractures. While institutions remained, the state was politically decapitated, locking in a terminal trajectory toward collapse.",
  "Luba Empire|1880": "The empire effectively collapsed as economic monopolies were broken by external ivory and slave hunters. Rival succession factions sought outside backing, while the Luba themselves turned to internal slave raiding. This desperation destroyed social cohesion and territorial sovereignty, leaving only a remnant of former unity.",
  "Luba Empire|1889": "A final civil war permanently fractured the empire into two lineage-based rump states, ending the unified Luba polity. Deprived of its centralized tribute system and sovereignty, the territory was soon absorbed by Belgian colonial forces. This dissolution marks the absolute end of the Luba Empire’s cycle.",
  "Lunda Empire|1600": "Luba prince Ilunga Tshibinda marries Queen Lueji, fusing Luba statecraft with indigenous Lunda culture. At this stage, the polity remains a hamlet-scale chiefdom called N'Gaange, relying on hunting and raiding. It lacks territorial reach or complex institutional structures, representing a pre-state foundational phase in formation.",
  "Lunda Empire|1650": "Under Yaav II a Nawej, the Lunda establish their first commercial links with the Portuguese via intermediaries. Trading ivory and slaves for cloth and firearms, the kingdom expands to approximately 150,000 km². Despite growing commerce, the state remains fragile and lacks the full institutional architecture of later eras.",
  "Lunda Empire|1665": "The formal establishment of the \"Mwata Yamvo\" title marks the genesis of the Lunda Empire proper. While developing an institutional identity and maintaining Atlantic trade contacts, the polity remains a frontier chiefdom struggling for survival against neighboring groups like the Kanyok in western DRC and northeastern Angola.",
  "Lunda Empire|1695": "Nawej I, the first well-attested historical ruler, introduces constitutional innovations including the offices of Queen Mother and heir apparent. These semi-constitutional mechanisms stabilize succession. However, the empire remains vulnerable; Nawej is killed during a military campaign against the Kanyok, highlighting the state's continued structural fragility.",
  "Lunda Empire|1700": "The empire expands west past the Kasai River, becoming a key inland supplier for the Atlantic slave trade. Institutional complexity grows with the formation of four standing armies and a tributary bureaucracy. Having survived its early existential crises, the state transitions from a small chiefdom into a regional power.",
  "Lunda Empire|1720": "The establishment of the Mwata Kazembe satellite kingdom transforms the Lunda into a genuine multi-state confederation. This \"commonwealth\" model secures trade routes to the Indian Ocean, granting the empire intercontinental commercial reach across both African coasts. This strategic expansion significantly enhances the state's institutional and geopolitical complexity.",
  "Lunda Empire|1750": "Under Muteba I, the empire matures into a Luba-derived political model with a centralized core and autonomous tributary rings. Trade caravans cross the continent regularly, and Lunda culture influences neighboring regions. The state is now a substantial, expanding power with deep and established transcontinental commercial relevance,.",
  "Lunda Empire|1770": "Asserting commercial sovereignty, the Lunda attack intermediary kingdoms to deal directly with the Portuguese. The capital, Musumba, grows into a sophisticated urban center with paved roads and a large population. Four standing armies secure tribute across Central Africa, bringing the empire near its institutional and military zenith.",
  "Lunda Empire|1800": "Approaching its territorial maximum, the empire is documented by Portuguese traders as a prosperous, secure, and well-organized state. Although expansion continues under Nawej II Ditend, the polity has not yet reached its 300,000 km² peak or the full height of its intercontinental trade prestige and diplomatic influence.",
  "Lunda Empire|1820": "Under Nawej II Ditend, the empire reaches its absolute peak, doubling its earlier size to 300,000 km². Monopolizing transcontinental trade and maintaining four standing armies, the Mwata Yamvo becomes Central Africa's preeminent political figure, receiving formal diplomatic missions from the King of Portugal at his zenith.",
  "Lunda Empire|1850": "The empire maintains its maximum extent and institutional vigor into the mid-19th century. Although the abolition of the Atlantic slave trade begins to threaten revenue, the state remains powerful. This period marks a late peak before the rise of the armed Chokwe creates irreversible structural stress.",
  "Lunda Empire|1860": "The empire enters structural decline as the Chokwe—armed with European firearms—begin resisting tribute. Simultaneously, the abolition of the Atlantic slave trade undermines the economy. While still territorially intact and politically coherent, the state faces mounting pressure from internal rebellion and shifting trade routes.",
  "Lunda Empire|1870": "Existential crisis intensifies as Chokwe incursions dismantle the empire's peripheral provinces. Under Mulaji II, the Lunda lose their tributary network and military edge. Although the capital and central administration remain functional for now, the state has lost its imperial reach and is spiraling toward terminal collapse.",
  "Lunda Empire|1884": "The Berlin Conference partitions Lunda territory between colonial powers while the Chokwe devastate the heartland. The Mwata Yamvo retains only nominal control over the capital as better-armed invaders overrun the former empire. The polity effectively ceases to function as a sovereign or coherent political entity.",
  "Lunda Empire|1887": "The Lunda Empire ends as the Chokwe kill Mwata Yamvo Mulaji II, the last paramount ruler of a functioning sovereign state. A humiliating defeat leads to the total disintegration of the political entity, which is subsequently absorbed by Portuguese, Belgian, and British colonial administrations",
  "Macedonian Empire|-336": "Alexander ascends at twenty following Philip II’s assassination, inheriting a peerless military machine. Despite immediate revolts in Thessaly and Thrace, the state possesses immense latent power. This volatile start marks the beginning of a rapid trajectory from regional power to a world-conquering imperial state.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-335": "Moving with legendary speed, Alexander secures his northern frontiers before crushing the Theban revolt. The total destruction of Thebes cowers Greece into submission and provides the funds necessary to clear inherited debts. With external threats suppressed, Alexander prepares his kingdom for the upcoming invasion of the Persian Empire.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-334": "Crossing the Hellespont with nearly 55,000 troops, Alexander wins his first pitched battle at the Granicus River. This victory shatters Persian resistance in Anatolia, allowing Greek coastal cities to declare for him. The campaign demonstrates that Persian satraps cannot halt the Macedonian phalanx's advance.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-333": "At Issus, Alexander defeats Darius III in person, forcing the Great King to flee and abandon his family and treasury. By breaking the myth of Persian invincibility, Alexander secures Syria and begins the systematic siege of the Phoenician coast, signaling the transition from a regional power to an empire.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-332": "Following the fall of Tyre and Gaza, Alexander enters Egypt and is welcomed as Pharaoh and divine \"son of Ammon\". He founds Alexandria, establishing a strategic maritime hub. This era marks the consolidation of the Levant and Egypt, providing the logistical and ideological foundation for the final assault.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-331": "At Gaugamela, Alexander decisively routes Darius III's superior forces, effectively ending Achaemenid field resistance. Occupying Babylon, Susa, and Persepolis, he is acclaimed \"King of Asia\". The fall of the Persian heartland places the vast wealth and territory of the former empire entirely at the mercy of the king.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-330": "The destruction of Persepolis and the assassination of Darius III by his own satraps finalize the Persian collapse. Alexander assumes the role of legitimate successor, dismissing his Greek allies to pursue a personal war of conquest. His authority is absolute, though the territorial boundary remains largely unchanged.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-328": "Subduing Central Asia proves exceptionally difficult, characterized by brutal guerrilla warfare and internal dissent over Persian court rituals. Despite marrying Roxana to secure local alliances, the Macedonian officer corps grows restless. The empire is vast, but Alexander is forced to fight desperately to maintain control.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-326": "At the Hydaspes, Alexander defeats King Porus in a tactical masterpiece, reaching his absolute territorial zenith from the Adriatic to the Indus. However, the subsequent army mutiny at the Beas River marks a decisive reversal. This hinge point represents the maximum reach allowed by his exhausted veterans.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-325": "The return journey through the Gedrosian Desert becomes a catastrophic self-inflicted disaster, claiming three-quarters of the army. While Alexander remains the undefeated sovereign of a vast domain, the severe human losses and damaged morale reveal the physical and psychological limits of his unprecedented and unsustainable imperial overextension.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-324": "At Susa and Opis, Alexander attempts to fuse Greco-Persian cultures, sparking a second major army mutiny. The death of Hephaestion further erodes the king’s emotional stability and judgment. While the empire remains territorially intact, institutional integration is failing and the central authority of the monarch is strained.",
  "Macedonian Empire|-323": "Alexander’s sudden death in Babylon collapses the empire's only organizing principle: his personal authority. Lacking a functional successor, his generals immediately began maneuvering for power. Within years, the unified project fragmented into competing successor kingdoms, marking the absolute termination of the Macedonian Empire as a unified political project.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1293": "Raden Wijaya founded Majapahit after executing a brilliant double-cross against Mongol invaders following the fall of Singhasari. Though a successful start, the nascent state relied on personal loyalties rather than deep institutions, facing immediate internal rebellions from disgruntled generals that limited its initial territorial reach.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1309": "Upon Raden Wijaya’s death, his son Jayanagara inherited a functionally dominant power in eastern Java. While the succession demonstrated established dynastic foundations, the realm entered a turbulent era marked by royal unpopularity, despite the early career of the future legendary Grand Prime Minister Gajah Mada.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1320": "Jayanagara’s weak and despised reign faced relentless noble rebellions, culminating in Ra Kuti’s seizure of the Trowulan palace. Only Gajah Mada’s tactical brilliance saved the crown, reflecting near-institutional collapse where the empire survived solely through the loyalty of exceptional subordinates rather than royal competence.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1330": "Following Jayanagara’s assassination, Queen Tribhuwana assumed authority as regent, placing the empire on an upward trajectory. With Gajah Mada as the dominant military commander and internal traumas resolved, Majapahit moved into an ascending phase, stabilizing the Javanese core before launching massive territorial maritime conquests.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1334": "Gajah Mada’s inauguration as Grand Prime Minister was marked by the Palapa Oath, a vow to bring the entire Nusantara archipelago under Majapahit suzerainty. This initiated the empire’s most consequential era, shifting from regional stabilization to active conquest, beginning with strategic gains in Bali and Sumatra.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1343": "Systematic archipelago expansion transformed Majapahit into a genuine maritime empire. By conquering Bali and bringing Sumatra’s Srivijayan remnants and early Islamic sultanates under tribute, Gajah Mada secured strategically vital trade routes and waterways, encoding Javanese hegemony across the diverse islands of the Nusantara.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1357": "The Bubat Incident, involving the massacre of a Sundanese royal wedding party, permanently alienated Sunda and ended Gajah Mada's career. While the empire remained enormously powerful, this diplomatic disaster set a ceiling on westward expansion and signaled the loss of the empire’s primary architect.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1360": "Under Hayam Wuruk and a demoted Gajah Mada, the empire neared its maximum extent, stretching from Sumatra to New Guinea. Trade revenues from spice routes funded a refined, cosmopolitan court at Trowulan, though the shadow of decline loomed following the earlier diplomatic failures in Sunda.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1365": "The absolute peak was documented in the Nagarakretagama, listing 98 tributary territories. Majapahit achieved maximum territorial reach, institutional sophistication, and economic vitality as a global maritime hub. Hayam Wuruk successfully consolidated authority following Gajah Mada’s death, marking the empire's greatest cultural and political achievement.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1380": "Though expansion ceased, the empire remained powerful and unchallenged, conducting its final major offensive against Palembang. However, the absolute peak had passed; institutional entropy began to take hold as the personal authority that managed the vast archipelago-wide mandala started its inevitable slow decline.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1389": "Hayam Wuruk’s death without a direct male heir sparked a structural succession crisis. The competing claims of his daughter and a son by a concubine created the conditions for civil war, weakening the personal authority required to maintain control over the empire's diverse overseas vassals.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1406": "The Regreg civil war ended with the eastern court’s defeat but resulted in the permanent loss of the maritime empire. Overseas vassals like Malacca declared independence, and the empire was essentially confined to Java, further burdened by heavy reparations owed to the Ming dynasty.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1420": "Under Wikramawardhana’s diminished reign, the empire’s economic and ideological monopoly was undermined by Ming-backed Islamic expansion in northern Javanese ports. A major famine further crippled the state's foundations, as Malacca’s commercial network grew at Majapahit’s expense, marking a period of profound structural weakening.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1430": "Queen Suhita governed a realm in passive decline, facing an inexorable cultural shift as Muslim communities became permanent internal opposition. While the central court maintained control over the interior, it lost the ability to reverse the Islamization of economically vital coastal trading cities.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1450": "A three-year interregnum following succession chaos signaled deep institutional disintegration. The Javanese nobility grew accustomed to operating without central authority, while the overseas empire vanished entirely. The state drifted toward fragmentation as its Hindu-Buddhist cultural foundations continued to erode under coastal Islamization.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1468": "Prince Kertabhumi’s rebellion forced the legitimate court into exile at Daha, splitting the empire into two competing, fragmented courts. This dual-governance era reflected a state that had lost all territorial coherence and maritime reach, struggling merely to control a shrinking heartland in interior Java.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1478": "Traditionally marked as the empire's end, the fall of Trowulan left the Hindu-Buddhist court as a minor power in the Javanese interior. While Ranawijaya notionally reunified the realm, actual power resided with Islamized coastal sultanates, reducing Majapahit to a rump state with prestige but minimal substance.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1510": "A coup by Patih Udara confirmed the royal line’s total loss of internal legitimacy. A desperate, failed attempt to secure a Portuguese military alliance against the Sultanate of Demak backfired, provoking preemptive attacks that left the state a mere ghost of its former imperial name.",
  "Majapahit Empire|1527": "The Sultanate of Demak launched a final assault, sacking the last stronghold and extinguishing the Hindu-Buddhist court. Courtiers fled to Bali, the last refuge of Majapahit culture, marking the definitive end of Indianized civilization as the dominant political force in the Indonesian archipelago.",
  "Mali Empire|1235": "Sundiata Keita founded the empire after defeating the Sosso at the Battle of Kirina. He established a governing charter and initiated expansion into vital goldfields. Although a defining victory, the state remained embryonic, with major trade nodes and northern salt deposits yet to be integrated.",
  "Mali Empire|1255": "Sundiata’s death triggered a succession crisis, though Mansa Uli successfully expanded toward the Senegal River and initiated diplomatic ties through the hajj. While maintaining territorial gains, the ensuing dynastic instability during the 1270s-1280s slowed further growth and concentrated administrative energy on internal power struggles.",
  "Mali Empire|1285": "General Sakura, a former slave, seized power and restored military momentum. His effective rule expanded Mali’s reach further into the western Sahel before his murder returning from Mecca. Under Sakura, the empire recovered its administrative energy and territorial size, though it remained well below its eventual peak.",
  "Mali Empire|1300": "Returning to the Keita dynasty, Mansa Gao inherited a stable state controlling major Niger trade corridors and goldfields. The empire hosted international scholars and explored Atlantic maritime expeditions. While prosperous and well-connected to trans-Saharan networks, it had not yet achieved the institutional zenith of the Musa era.",
  "Mali Empire|1312": "Mansa Musa initiated the empire's golden age, dramatically expanding territorial reach from the Atlantic to the Saharan salt deposits. Implementing a sophisticated provincial governor system, he prioritized Islamic scholarship and monumental architecture. This era marked Mali’s transition into a globally recognized power of immense wealth and prestige.",
  "Mali Empire|1337": "At its absolute zenith, Mali became the medieval world’s largest gold producer and a global center for Islamic learning. Mansa Musa’s 1324 hajj cemented its international reputation for staggering wealth. With fourteen centralized provinces and control over Timbuktu, the empire reached its maximum territorial and institutional extent.",
  "Mali Empire|1350": "Mansa Suleyman maintained a stable, functioning court witnessed by Ibn Battuta, yet structural erosion began. Songhai asserted independence, and Mossi raids signaled peripheral vulnerability. Although the empire remained wealthy and organized, the loss of eastern nodes and the rising power of palace viziers indicated a post-peak decline.",
  "Mali Empire|1390": "Decades of incompetent rule and the rise of non-royal viziers hollowed out central authority. Provincial governors treated territories as personal fiefods while Songhai formalized independence. Despite nominally ruling a vast area, the empire suffered from structural damage, shrinking effective control, and persistent raids from the Mossi emperor.",
  "Mali Empire|1400": "Mali transitioned from a declining empire to a contracting regional power as Gao formally rebelled. Strategic trans-Saharan connections weakened against rising Songhai dominance and emerging Portuguese coastal trade. The mansa became increasingly dependent on military commanders, losing the centralized control that had characterized earlier prosperity.",
  "Mali Empire|1433": "The loss of Timbuktu and Walata to Tuareg nomads severed Mali's access to vital trans-Saharan trade routes. This economically devastating event stripped the empire of its role as a \"middle trader\" of gold and salt. Without northern revenues, the state was reduced to its original Mandinka heartland.",
  "Mali Empire|1450": "Although significantly reduced, Mali survived as a functioning state under intensifying pressure from Songhai and the Mossi. Portuguese records confirm the mansa still commanded tribute in Senegambia. While possessing genuine authority over a contracted core, the empire lacked the capacity to project power over its former broader domain.",
  "Mali Empire|1468": "Sunni Ali’s conquest of Timbuktu and Djenne stripped Mali of its remaining commercial influence over the Niger bend. While the Mandinka heartland repulsed a Songhai invasion, the strategic loss was irreversible. The empire became a remnant rump state with legitimate continuity but no significant regional power or reach.",
  "Mali Empire|1500": "Contracted to its original Kangaba core, the empire maintained diplomatic relations with Portugal, seeking alliances against regional rivals. Despite field armies and a continuous dynasty, the state controlled only a tiny fraction of its former height. External pressure from the Songhai and Denianke dynasties further compromised its survival.",
  "Mali Empire|1600": "The empire was effectively extinguished following humiliating military defeats and the sack of its capital by Songhai. A final attempt to restore preeminence in 1599 ended in catastrophe. Upon the last mansa's death around 1610, the polity fragmented definitively, marking the absolute end of Mali as an empire.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1250": "Bahri Mamluk commanders founded the sultanate by assassinating the last effective Ayyubid ruler following victory against the Seventh Crusade. Although the state demonstrated immense military potential, it remained politically fragile as control was contested between the new regime in Egypt and exiled factions in Syria.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1255": "The sultanate entered a state of internal collapse as key military commanders fled to Syria. Following the assassination of Sultan Aybak and his wife Shajar al-Durr, the kingdom became militarily divided and politically chaotic. The polity existed as a rump sultanate with no clear central authority.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1260": "The Battle of Ain Jalut marked a macro-historical turning point where Mamluk forces halted Mongol westward expansion. Sultan Baybars seized power, unified Egypt and Syria, and restored the Abbasid Caliphate. He established a massive standing army and a sophisticated postal system, creating a dominant regional power.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1268": "At the height of his campaigns, Baybars captured Antioch and Krak des Chevaliers, dealing a devastating blow to Crusader presence. He institutionalized the empire through advanced spy networks and commercial treaties with European powers. The sultanate became the dominant regional force, though it awaited future economic maturity.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1277": "The death of Baybars triggered a brief succession crisis, eventually leading to the election of Sultan Qalawun. Although the loss of the founder introduced political uncertainty, the military machine remained intact and dominant against the Mongols. This period reflects a powerful state in transition during an institutional shift.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1281": "Sultan Qalawun decisively defeated a massive Mongol-Armenian coalition at the Battle of Homs, ending immediate external threats. Simultaneously, he reformed the iqta' land system and initiated major charitable building projects. These achievements confirmed Mamluk dominance over Syria while the state emerged stronger under a capable builder-sultan.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1290": "By Qalawun’s final year, the sultanate had organized a diplomatic network stretching to Europe and captured most Crusader strongholds. The founding of the Qalawunid dynasty ensured long-term political continuity. Although territorial expansion approached its maximum, the empire had yet to reach its absolute economic and cultural zenith.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1291": "The fall of Acre marked the definitive end of the Crusader States and established the Mamluks' maximum territorial extent. Rule stretched across Egypt, Syria, the Hejaz, and parts of Anatolia. This military zenith preceded the administrative and cultural peak that would follow under al-Nasir Muhammad's stable governance.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1299": "During al-Nasir Muhammad’s troubled second reign, the empire suffered a serious military setback against the Ilkhanid Mongols at Wadi al-Khaznadar. Internal factional instability further weakened the state. While the institutional foundations remained strong, they were actively contested by competing emirs, resulting in a temporary loss of coherence.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1310": "Al-Nasir Muhammad seized autocratic power for his third reign, eliminating rival factions and initiating a decade of sustained peace. He launched massive building projects and centralized fiscal administration. This era marked the launch of the sultanate's zenith, though institutional consolidation was still maturing toward its absolute maximum.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1320": "The sultanate reached its absolute peak of civilization, characterized by unprecedented cultural programs and economic prosperity. Cairo became the wealthiest city west of China. Under a single long-reigning sultan, the empire achieved maximum territorial control, institutional sophistication, and cultural output that commanded respect from all regional kings.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1341": "Following al-Nasir Muhammad’s death, a rapid succession of twelve figurehead sultans revealed the systemic weakness of the autocracy. While the empire remained wealthy and the military capacity was physically intact, the damage from political instability was prospective. The state began a decline from its peak as factions vied for control.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1347": "The arrival of the Black Death caused a catastrophic demographic collapse, killing a third of Cairo’s population. Irrigation systems failed as the agricultural labor force evaporated, leading to a permanent loss of revenue. The empire entered a compound crisis where physical structures remained functional but foundational bases were undermined.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1360": "Political chaos persisted as short-lived sultans were controlled by senior emirs. Agricultural output collapsed and Bedouin infiltration increased, though the state could still mount major projects like the Sultan Hasan Mosque. The empire visibly declined from its peak, losing the administrative coherence that once defined its prosperity.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1382": "The transition to the Burji period under Circassian sultans signaled declining institutional coherence and shorter reign lengths. The empire faced chronic plague, fiscal crises, and intensifying factional conflict among the military elite. These underlying structural problems remained unresolved despite the establishment of a new dynasty in the citadel.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1400": "Timur’s devastating invasion of Syria resulted in the sacking of Aleppo and Damascus, massacring populations and enslaving artisans. The sultanate suffered an immense demographic shock on top of existing plague legacies. This compound crisis left the state profoundly weakened, characterized by fiscal collapse and a loss of territorial initiative.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1426": "Sultan Barsbay achieved a brief resurgence by conquering Cyprus and forcing it to pay tribute. He attempted to stabilize the state by monopolizing the spice trade and curbing tax abuses. However, this military success was not a structural recovery, as the underlying economic decline from depopulation continued unabated.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1450": "The mid-Burji period was defined by chronic instability and an agricultural sector that never recovered from depopulation. Irrigation infrastructure visibly decayed as the peasantry halved, and the state lacked major military achievements. While still functional, the sultanate was structurally weakened with no realistic prospect of reversing its downward trajectory.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1468": "Sultan Qaitbay’s long reign brought relative stability and a flowering of administrative competence. He successfully stalemated the Ottomans through extensive fortification projects across Syria and Egypt. However, he could not solve the deep structural problems of a plague-depleted population and failing irrigation that continued to stress the state.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1500": "The discovery of the Cape route by the Portuguese destroyed the Mamluk economic model by redirecting the lucrative spice trade. This existential crisis coincided with military defeats in the Indian Ocean and escalating tensions with the Ottomans. While still controlling territory, the state's economic foundations were hollowed out.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1516": "The Mamluk army was destroyed at the Battle of Marj Dabiq, where the failure to adopt field artillery proved fatal against the Ottomans. Syria was lost immediately as officials defected. Although a final defense was attempted in Cairo, the state technically controlled only a fragment of Egypt with certain defeat looming.",
  "Mamluk Sultanate|1517": "The Mamluk Sultanate was destroyed following the capture and execution of its last sultan by Ottoman forces. Egypt became a province, ending 267 years of sovereign Mamluk rule. The political entity was entirely extinguished, though its military caste survived as a subordinate social group under Ottoman suzerainty for centuries.",
  "Maratha Empire|1674": "Shivaji was crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad, formally founding the Maratha kingdom as a sovereign entity. This political statement challenged Mughal and Deccan powers, supported by a strong network of 300 forts. Though regionally limited, the state established a formidable defensive and naval foundation in the Konkan.",
  "Maratha Empire|1680": "Following Shivaji’s death, Sambhaji ascended the throne just as Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb launched a massive military mobilization to crush the Marathas. Despite Sambhaji’s tactical brilliance in defending his forts, the sheer scale of the Mughal campaign created an existential threat that prevented the kingdom from expanding further.,",
  "Maratha Empire|1689": "The empire reached its absolute nadir when Sambhaji was captured, tortured, and executed by Aurangzeb. With the capital fallen and the heir imprisoned, the central government collapsed. The polity survived solely as a decentralized network of guerrilla commanders raiding Mughal supply lines across the Deccan highlands.",
  "Maratha Empire|1700": "Following Rajaram’s death, Queen Tarabai assumed regency, masterfully organizing dispersed commanders into a coherent resistance against Aurangzeb. While the state lacked a stable capital or formal administration, her aggressive guerrilla strategy took the fight into Mughal-held Malwa, proving the Maratha movement could not be militarily extinguished.",
  "Maratha Empire|1707": "Aurangzeb’s death after a futile 27-year campaign signaled a strategic Maratha victory and the structural decline of the Mughal Empire. The release of Prince Shahu initiated a brief civil war, ultimately resulting in institutional renewal. The Marathas emerged as the subcontinent's rising power, surviving an unprecedented military onslaught.",
  "Maratha Empire|1720": "The appointment of military genius Bajirao I as Peshwa launched a period of extraordinary northward expansion. Bajirao unified Maratha command and transformed the regional kingdom into a subcontinent-spanning confederacy. His \"tenfold expansion\" strategy exploited Mughal weakness, establishing Maratha authority over the Deccan through brilliant maneuver warfare at Palkhed.",
  "Maratha Empire|1737": "Bajirao’s audacious raid on Delhi and victory at Bhopal forced the Mughals to cede Malwa and pay massive reparations. This established the Marathas as the effective arbiters of Mughal politics. The strategic balance of the subcontinent shifted decisively, positioning the Peshwa as the most powerful figure in India.",
  "Maratha Empire|1750": "Under Peshwa Balaji Bajirao, the empire reached near its maximum extent, conducting successful campaigns as far as Bengal and Mysore. The capital shifted to Pune as the confederacy extracted tribute from vast territories. While highly prosperous and militarily dominant, the final push into the northwest frontier was still pending.",
  "Maratha Empire|1758": "The empire achieved its absolute zenith as Maratha forces captured Peshawar, extending their reach to the northwest frontier of modern Pakistan. Controlling over 2,500,000 square kilometers, the confederacy became the largest Indian power since the Mughals, effectively dictating imperial politics in Delhi from a position of undisputed hegemony.",
  "Maratha Empire|1761": "The catastrophic defeat at the Third Battle of Panipat annihilated the Maratha army and decimated its leadership. This generational disaster resulted in the loss of Delhi and the northwest frontier overnight. While the institutional core survived, the empire was structurally broken, ending the era of unified, northward expansion.",
  "Maratha Empire|1770": "In a remarkable \"phoenix\" recovery, the young Peshwa Madhavrao I rebuilt the army and reasserted control over lost territories, including Delhi. His administrative brilliance restored the empire’s status as a major power. However, he granted significant autonomy to great military families, permanently shifting the state toward a fragmented confederacy model.",
  "Maratha Empire|1780": "Madhavrao’s early death triggered a violent succession crisis and the First Anglo-Maratha War. Although the Marathas achieved a military victory over the British, the conflict exposed deep internal fractures. The era of the \"power behind the throne\" began, with central authority increasingly contested by semi-independent military families.",
  "Maratha Empire|1800": "The death of Nana Fadnavis removed the last capable centralizing force, leading to the fatal fracturing of the confederacy. Individual Maratha families began competing with each other, falling prey to the British subsidiary alliance system. Significant territorial losses to the East India Company signaled the end of true sovereignty.",
  "Maratha Empire|1818": "The Third Anglo-Maratha War resulted in the final defeat and exile of the Peshwa, leading to the formal dissolution of the empire. The British annexed core territories, reducing remaining Maratha states to subordinate subsidiary status. This marked the absolute termination of the Maratha Empire as a sovereign political entity.",
  "Maurya Empire|-322": "Chandragupta Maurya, guided by Kautilya, overthrows the Nanda dynasty to seize Pataliputra. Exploiting the power vacuum left by Alexander the Great, he consolidates northwestern satrapies and inherits a massive military. This newly founded empire establishes a significant power base while still actively consolidating its western frontiers.",
  "Maurya Empire|-305": "Chandragupta defeats Seleucus I Nicator, securing northwestern satrapies and extending the empire into modern Afghanistan and Iran. In exchange for 500 war elephants, the resulting peace treaty and diplomatic ties establish the Maurya Empire as a genuine great power ruling from Bengal to the Hindu Kush.",
  "Maurya Empire|-297": "Chandragupta abdicates to son Bindusara, retiring for religious fasting. This peaceful transfer of a vast, consolidated state stretching from Afghanistan to the Narmada River demonstrates extraordinary institutional confidence. While territory is maintained, Bindusara must now prove his personal leadership capacity beyond his father’s military momentum.",
  "Maurya Empire|-290": "Known as the \"Slayer of Enemies,\" Bindusara conquers sixteen kingdoms across the Deccan plateau, extending Mauryan authority deep into south-central India. He maintains sophisticated diplomatic ties with Greek kingdoms while leaving friendly Tamil states untouched, representing significant growth toward the Indian subcontinent’s near-complete political integration.",
  "Maurya Empire|-273": "Bindusara’s death triggers a violent four-year succession war among his sons. Ashoka eventually defeats his rivals to claim the throne. Despite preserving the vast territorial inheritance, the interregnum causes frontier instability and revolts, reflecting a state temporarily weakened by political turbulence before a new ruler consolidates power.",
  "Maurya Empire|-268": "Formally ascending the throne, Ashoka reasserts imperial authority with military vigor, earning the epithet \"Ashoka the Cruel\". Ruling the largest domain in Indian history, he governs through a functioning state machinery and the world's largest standing army, though the empire’s absolute territorial and cultural zeniths remain ahead.",
  "Maurya Empire|-261": "The bloody Kalinga War completes the empire’s territorial map of the subcontinent. Witnessing the horrific slaughter, Ashoka undergoes a profound moral transformation, embracing Buddhism and public remorse. This represents the territorial maximum, shifting the state from a military engine toward a philosopher-king model of governance.",
  "Maurya Empire|-250": "The empire reaches its absolute zenith, converging territorial, institutional, and cultural power. Ashoka’s edict network and Dhamma officials enforce moral governance, while missionary dispatches establish Buddhism as a world religion. This unique peak represents the simultaneous apex of South Asian administrative reach and international influence.",
  "Maurya Empire|-232": "Ashoka’s death removes the empire's central organizing engine. While territorially intact and militarily formidable, the succession is compromised as no single heir possesses Ashoka’s authority. Border states immediately begin asserting independence, signaling a structural decline despite the administrative machinery remaining temporarily functional at transition.",
  "Maurya Empire|-230": "Under weak successors like Dasharatha, the empire begins a rapid disintegration. Central authority collapses as the edict network falls silent and provincial governors accumulate local power. The empire splits into eastern and western fragments, losing the unified command structure and territorial coherence established previously.",
  "Maurya Empire|-215": "Samprati partially reconquers lost territories, yet the empire remains structurally fractured. Administrative systems designed for a strong emperor fail under weak leadership, leading to falling tax revenues and peripheral independence. This military recovery cannot offset the irreversible political decay of authority over distant provinces.",
  "Maurya Empire|-200": "Succession cycles through increasingly weak rulers who lose control over provinces. The once-centralized administration devolves, with governors functioning as autonomous entities. Unable to defend strategic northwestern passes, the empire becomes a rump state in Magadha, surviving through institutional inertia rather than active national coordination.",
  "Maurya Empire|-185": "The Mauryan dynasty ends when the last emperor, Brihadratha, is publicly assassinated by his commander-in-chief, Pushyamitra Shunga. Having shrunk to a few city-states, the remnants of the empire are absorbed into the Shunga domain. This coup marks the physical termination of the dynasty's subcontinent-spanning project.",
  "Median Empire|-678": "The Median dynasty emerged as a tribal confederation in the Zagros highlands, establishing Ecbatana as its capital while revolting against Assyrian vassalage. Though independent, the polity remained an emergent highland power, lacking the capacity to project significant influence beyond its immediate mountainous heartland.",
  "Median Empire|-670": "Under Phraortes, the Medes subjugated the Persians and launched their first major imperial campaigns. While successfully bringing the Persian people into their political orbit, an ambitious attempt to directly challenge the formidable Assyrian military ended in total defeat and the death of the king.",
  "Median Empire|-650": "Following Phraortes' death, the Medes fell under Scythian suzerainty for nearly three decades. This interregnum represents a period of profound humiliation and political subordination, where the Median state existed nominally but lacked any sovereign power to project authority, marking a deep pre-conquest trough.",
  "Median Empire|-625": "Cyaxares regained independence by assassinating Scythian leaders and professionalizing the Median military. By reorganizing chaotic tribal militias into specialized units of spearmen, bowmen, and cavalry, he created a formidable imperial machine, marking a pivotal inflection point for the empire's rapid ascent to regional dominance.",
  "Median Empire|-614": "The reformed Median army struck the Assyrian heartland, capturing and destroying the religious capital of Ashur. This victory secured a formal military alliance with the Babylonians, cemented by a high-level dynastic marriage. This breakthrough shattered Assyrian regional prestige and set the stage for total conquest.",
  "Median Empire|-612": "Combined Median and Babylonian forces successfully besieged and sacked Nineveh, the world's most feared capital, resulting in the Neo-Assyrian Empire's destruction. This extraordinary military achievement signaled the Medes' arrival as a superpower, though their final territorial expansion to the furthest borders was still pending.",
  "Median Empire|-600": "At its absolute zenith, the empire stretched from the Black Sea to Afghanistan, incorporating Urartu, Mannai, and the Iranian plateau. Cyaxares established a massive continental dominion, controlling diverse territories through both direct and suzerain rule, representing the maximum territorial extent and prestige of the state.",
  "Median Empire|-585": "The war with Lydia ended in a stalemate following a total solar eclipse, confirming the Halys River as the border. Cyaxares’ death shortly after marked a strategic turning point; while the empire remained physically vast, the loss of its founding genius initiated a post-peak plateau.",
  "Median Empire|-560": "Under Astyages, the empire suffered from visible structural decay and elite alienation. Despite maintaining territorial breadth, the state faced severe logistical challenges in governing a transcontinental realm. This period was defined by weakening political cohesion and failing central authority, leaving the empire vulnerable to internal dissent.",
  "Median Empire|-553": "The revolt of the Persian vassal Cyrus the Great revealed the empire’s internal fragility. Astyages’ failure to swiftly suppress a peripheral rebellion highlighted a terminal political crisis, as disgruntled Median nobles began defecting. The state remained territorially large but had become a hollowed-out political shell.",
  "Median Empire|-550": "The Median Empire ceased to exist as a sovereign entity when Cyrus captured Ecbatana and defeated Astyages, aided by defecting Median generals. Crucially, the Median nobility and institutions were integrated into the new Achaemenid Persian state, effectively transforming rather than being destroyed by the conquest.",
  "Ming Empire|1368": "Hongwu founded the Ming Dynasty after expelling the Mongol Yuan rulers. A former peasant, he restored Han rule, reinstated civil service exams, and established a massive standing army. However, the war-scarred landscape remained depopulated and politically fragile, with Mongols still posing a threat in the north.",
  "Ming Empire|1380": "Emperor Hongwu abolished the Prime Ministership to centralize absolute power, conducting massive purges that killed over 30,000 people. While he successfully rebuilt the economy and expanded the \"lijia\" welfare system, the state suffered from structural fragility due to extreme over-centralization and persistent northern border pressure.",
  "Ming Empire|1398": "Hongwu’s death triggered a violent succession crisis between the Jianwen Emperor and his uncle, the Prince of Yan. The resulting Jingnan civil war devastated northern and central China for three years. Political legitimacy was undermined, and territorial control was lost as the dynasty reverted to foundational fragility.",
  "Ming Empire|1402": "After a destructive civil war, the Yongle Emperor seized the throne, initiating a \"second founding\". He restored stability, created the Grand Secretariat, and began planning the move to Beijing. This era marked the start of ambitious new leadership and a period that raised Ming power significantly.",
  "Ming Empire|1405": "The Yongle Emperor dispatched Zheng He’s massive \"treasure fleet,\" the largest naval force ever seen, to reach the Persian Gulf. Simultaneously, construction began on the Forbidden City, Vietnam was conquered, and Japan joined the tributary system. The empire reached extraordinary global reach, though its absolute zenith was pending.",
  "Ming Empire|1413": "Zheng He’s fourth voyage reached East Africa, bringing envoys from thirty states to the Ming court. The massive Yongle Dadian encyclopedia was completed, and the Forbidden City was well advanced. The tributary system reached its maximum extent, reflecting a dynasty just below its absolute civilizational and global peak.",
  "Ming Empire|1420": "The Ming reached its absolute zenith with the completion of the Forbidden City and the relocation of the capital to Beijing. China commanded the world’s largest navy, bureaucracy, and continuous land empire. No other moment matched this simultaneous maximization of territory, institutional sophistication, maritime reach, and cultural output.",
  "Ming Empire|1424": "Following the Yongle Emperor's death on campaign, the Hongxi Emperor suspended the expensive treasure voyages to prioritize fiscal reform. Although the maritime policy was reversed, the empire's core institutional strength remained near its peak. However, the emperor’s reign lasted only one year, leaving the state in transition.",
  "Ming Empire|1430": "The Xuande Emperor restored maritime ambition with Zheng He’s final voyage while ensuring domestic fiscal stability and cultural flourishing. This period represents the end of the \"golden age,\" as the empire maintained near-peak conditions despite allowing Vietnam to regain independence after a costly and exhausting regional insurgency.",
  "Ming Empire|1435": "Upon Xuande’s death, the treasure voyages permanently ended, and the imperial navy fell into disrepair. The child Zhengtong Emperor was dominated by eunuchs, leading to intensified factionalism between palace officials and the bureaucracy. While institutionally robust, the era of maximum imperial ambition and global projection was over.",
  "Ming Empire|1450": "The Tumu Crisis saw the emperor captured and his army annihilated by Mongols, marking the dynasty’s greatest military humiliation. Although Beijing was defended, the psychological and institutional damage was permanent. Outward expansion ended as the Ming shifted toward a purely defensive, inward-looking posture on the northern frontier.",
  "Ming Empire|1487": "The Hongzhi Emperor initiated a brief \"restoration\" by reducing eunuch influence and promoting virtuous officials. While he stabilized the state through economic reforms, he could not reverse the deep structural decline or the defensive military posture established after the Tumu Crisis. The recovery was genuine but partial.",
  "Ming Empire|1519": "The erratic Zhengde Emperor’s misrule, characterized by unauthorized frontier tours and palace rebellions, caused significant institutional stress. Factional paralysis intensified after his death without an heir, leading to a succession crisis. Despite these central failures, the empire continued to function largely through its deeply entrenched institutional momentum.",
  "Ming Empire|1550": "During the Jiajing era, Mongol leader Altan Khan raided the outskirts of Beijing, while \"wokou\" piracy ravaged the coast. The emperor became increasingly disengaged from governance, consumed by Daoist alchemy. These concurrent threats and stagnant leadership highlighted the vulnerability of northern defenses and the erosion of central authority.",
  "Ming Empire|1572": "Grand Secretary Zhang Juzheng launched a decade of vital reforms, including the \"Single Whip\" tax reform and military reorganization. This led to a brief resurgence in revenue and governance, allowing for successful military campaigns. However, structural vulnerabilities like silver dependency and demographic pressures remained unaddressed despite this recovery.",
  "Ming Empire|1582": "Following Zhang Juzheng’s death, his reforms were dismantled by political enemies. The Wanli Emperor withdrew entirely from governance, leaving ministerial posts vacant and court affairs paralyzed for decades. This initiated a terminal period of institutional degradation and fiscal stress, accelerated by the costly and exhausting Korean military campaigns.",
  "Ming Empire|1600": "The late Wanli era was defined by total administrative stasis, with the emperor refusing to attend court for years. Factional warfare paralyzed the bureaucracy while Manchu forces consolidated in the northeast. Despite massive silver imports keeping the economy afloat, the state lacked the central leadership to address gathering threats.",
  "Ming Empire|1618": "Nurhaci declared war on the Ming, delivering a catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Sarhu. Simultaneously, the Little Ice Age triggered endemic crop failures and famine. The dynasty entered an existential crisis as Manchu military pressure, fiscal collapse, and climate-induced social unrest converged against a corrupt court.",
  "Ming Empire|1630": "The Chongzhen Emperor attempted desperate reforms but faced a dynasty in freefall due to famine, drought, and Li Zicheng’s growing peasant rebellion. The execution of his most capable general destroyed the last defense against the Manchu advance. The state effectively lost control over its periphery.",
  "Ming Empire|1644": "Rebel forces entered Beijing, leading the Chongzhen Emperor to commit suicide. The Ming general Wu Sangui invited Manchu forces across the Great Wall to expel the rebels, ultimately handing China to the Qing Dynasty. This marked the absolute termination of the Ming Empire as the primary political entity.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1590": "Emerging from the power vacuum left by the Hittite-Kassite collapse, Hurrian tribes began coalescing into a proto-state in the Khabur region. Under King Kirta, this nascent polity lacked institutional structure but occupied a strategic void, setting the foundation for future imperial development amidst regional chaos.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1500": "Under Barattarna I, Mitanni expanded westward, vassalizing commercial hubs like Aleppo and Alalakh. While establishing formal vassal relationships and colliding with Egyptian interests, the state functioned as an empire but still lacked the deep institutional complexity and northern Mesopotamian dominance achieved during its later zenith.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1475": "Mitanni engaged in direct military competition with Thutmose III of Egypt over the Syrian interior. Despite Egyptian raids reaching the Euphrates, Mitanni successfully absorbed Syrian city-states into its confederation as lands reverted to its control. Egypt remained a peer rival, actively checking Mitanni’s western ambitions.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1450": "King Shaushtatar achieved a military zenith by sacking the Assyrian capital, Ashur, and carrying away its golden doors as trophies. Subjugating Assyria as a formal vassal, the empire extended from northern Syria to the Zagros Mountains, marking a significant leap in regional territorial control and military prestige.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1420": "Artatama I transitioned military dominance into durable institutional power by establishing peace with Egypt. Through royal marriages and formal recognition of borders, Mitanni became a charter member of the \"Great Powers Club\". This diplomatic settlement secured a recognized domain stretching from the Mediterranean to the Zagros.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1400": "Maintaining near-maximum territorial extent, the empire reached a plateau of significant prestige. Mitanni’s equestrian expertise, exemplified by the Kikkuli horse-training manual, became highly prized by regional rivals. While a formidable power, the explicit diplomatic and cultural flowering associated with Shuttarna II’s upcoming reign remained pending.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1380": "Under Shuttarna II, Mitanni reached its absolute peak of power and diplomatic supremacy. As a central member of the Great Powers Club, the empire exercised suzerainty over Assyria and maintained intimate alliances with Egypt through royal marriages. It fielded the era's most feared chariot-based military force.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1365": "Tushratta inherited a powerful state, initially defeating a Hittite invasion and maintaining extensive diplomatic correspondence via the Amarna Letters. However, early signs of internal succession crises and the rising ambitions of Assyria and the Hittites introduced structural vulnerabilities, signaling the end of unchallenged regional hegemony.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1350": "Internal fragmentation accelerated as a rival court, backed by Assyria, split the empire’s political unity. While still formally recognized as a member of the \"Great Powers Club\" and maintaining contact with Egypt, the kingdom suffered territory losses to the annexing Assyrians, reflecting a state in decline.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1340": "The Hittite sack of Washukanni and Tushratta’s assassination dealt a shattering blow to the empire. Territory was halved, and the rump state, Hanigalbat, was confined to the Khabur valley as a Hittite vassal. Lack of Egyptian intervention left the decapitated dynasty isolated and fundamentally broken.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1307": "Following a failed rebellion, Shattuara I was defeated and taken to Ashur in chains, formally beginning Assyrian vassalage. Though it remained a nominally autonomous state, Hanigalbat lost its status as a peer to regional great powers, surviving only as a politically subordinate shadow of its former glory.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1300": "Another failed rebellion led to the deportation of populations and their replacement with Assyrian subjects. The political entity technically persisted with a nominal local king, but the state functioned effectively as an Assyrian administrative zone, lacking any meaningful sovereign power or independent strategic agency.",
  "Mitanni Empire|-1250": "Shalmaneser I decisively crushed the final coalition of resistance, blinding thousands and deporting survivors. The territory east of the Euphrates was converted into a formal Assyrian province, and the name \"Mitanni\" disappeared from historical records. The political entity was entirely terminated and absorbed.",
  "Mongol Empire|1206": "Temüjin was proclaimed Genghis Khan, unifying the Mongol and Turkic tribes under a meritocratic code of law. While formidable as a military machine, the empire remained confined to the steppe, lacking control over sedentary urban centers or agricultural provinces during this foundational period of consolidation.",
  "Mongol Empire|1215": "Following the sack of Beijing, the Jin court fled south, marking a surge in Mongol power. After destroying the Kara Khitai in 1218, the empire dominated East and Central Asia. Despite these vast gains, Russia and the Middle East remained untouched by imperial forces.",
  "Mongol Empire|1219": "The Khwarezm campaign initiated a massive westward explosion, resulting in the brutal sacking of major Islamic cities like Samarkand and Merv. Mongol scouting forces reached Russia, defeating a regional coalition. This rapid expansion stretched the empire to Persia, though the systematic conquest of Europe remained ahead.",
  "Mongol Empire|1227": "At the death of Genghis Khan, the empire controlled northern China, Central Asia, and Iran. While staggering in scale, major targets like Russia, Baghdad, and the wealthy Southern Song remained untouched. The current reach represented approximately 60% of the empire's eventual unified potential.",
  "Mongol Empire|1236": "Ögedei Khan authorized the Great Western Campaign, leading to the destruction of Russian principalities and the fall of Kiev. Simultaneously pressing against the Southern Song, the empire conducted coordinated multi-front operations across Eurasia. This era reflected peak organizational coherence and unmatched military dominance.",
  "Mongol Empire|1241": "Reaching its absolute zenith, the empire achieved simultaneous military victories in Poland and Hungary while holding Korea and Central Asia. This represented the maximum unified reach of a single Great Khan, with functioning imperial hierarchies managing armies from the Adriatic Sea to the Pacific coast.",
  "Mongol Empire|1258": "Möngke Khan restored central authority, launching coordinated campaigns to conquer the Islamic world and Southern Song. The catastrophic sack of Baghdad marked a high point of imperial coordination. However, Möngke’s death in 1259 triggered the civil war that would permanently fracture structural unity.",
  "Mongol Empire|1260": "Fragmentation began as a Toluid civil war erupted and the Mongols suffered their first major open-field defeat at Ain Jalut. The empire split into competing khanates, with Mongol factions warring against each other. While still formidable, the functionally unified imperial project was effectively terminated.",
  "Mongol Empire|1279": "The conquest of the Southern Song brought the empire to its territorial maximum, covering 22% of Earth’s land area. Despite this geographic extent, the Mongol world consisted of four independent, often hostile states. Kublai’s authority was largely unrecognized by the western khanates.",
  "Mongol Empire|1280": "While the Yuan dynasty flourished and Silk Road trade peaked under the Pax Mongolica, failed overseas invasions and internal wars in Central Asia drained the treasury. The unified empire was no longer recoverable as institutional fractures deepened and western khanates became de facto independent, anti-Yuan powers.",
  "Mongol Empire|1294": "Kublai Khan’s death ended any meaningful Mongol unity. The four khanates evolved into separate states with divergent religious and cultural identities, from the Islamizing Ilkhanate to the fracturing Chagatai. Collective territory remained vast, but the states functioned independently, acknowledging only nominal sovereignty.",
  "Mongol Empire|1300": "The Mongol world-system entered active self-destruction through institutionalized inter-khanate wars. The Ilkhanate suffered decisive defeats by the Mamluks, while factionalism paralyzed the Yuan court. Unified force projection vanished, leaving a collective of fractious successor states each declining from their respective historical peaks.",
  "Mongol Empire|1351": "The Red Turban Rebellion erupted amidst catastrophic floods, plague, and economic collapse, marking the Yuan’s terminal crisis. Han Chinese resentment toward ethnic discrimination fueled a nationwide insurgency. With other khanates already dissolved, the Mongol imperial project effectively ended everywhere except a failing fragment of China.",
  "Mongol Empire|1368": "The empire reached its absolute trough as Ming forces captured Beijing, forcing the Yuan court to flee into Mongolia as a rump state. With the earlier collapse of the western khanates, the Mongol Empire as a meaningful political entity officially ceased to exist.",
  "Mughal Empire|1526": "In 1526, Babur defeats Ibrahim Lodi at Panipat using artillery and tactics, founding the Mughal Empire. He secures further victories at Khanua and Ghaghara, gaining control of the Ganges-Yamuna region. However, Afghan and Rajput elites remain resistant, leaving the empire strong militarily but weak administratively and reliant on Babur’s personal rule.",
  "Mughal Empire|1530": "After Babur’s death in 1530, Humayun inherits a fragile empire. He faces threats from Sher Shah Suri, Rajput states, and rival brothers. Constant warfare prevents consolidation, leaving the Mughal state weak, lacking institutions, and still dependent on personal rule.",
  "Mughal Empire|1540": "Sher Shah Suri defeats Humayun at Chausa and Kannauj, forcing him into exile. Humayun seeks refuge in Safavid Iran, making concessions for support. The Sur Empire replaces Mughal rule, marking the dynasty’s lowest point, with its survival uncertain.",
  "Mughal Empire|1556": "Humayun briefly restores Mughal rule but dies in 1556. His young son Akbar becomes emperor under Bairam Khan. After defeating Hemu at the Second Battle of Panipat, Mughal control is reestablished, though still developing under regency.",
  "Mughal Empire|1580": "By 1580, Akbar transforms the Mughal Empire through inclusive and institutional reforms. He abolishes jizya, allies with Rajputs, and introduces the mansabdari system. With strong administration and cultural integration, the empire expands widely, reaching its institutional peak.",
  "Mughal Empire|1600": "By 1600, Akbar had ruled 44 years, with the Mughal Empire at its peak, spanning most of India except the deep south. Deccan sultanates were tributary, and Mughal goods dominated the Indian Ocean trade. The Ain-i-Akbari was compiled. Akbar, aging, died in 1605, and his son Salim briefly rebelled.",
  "Mughal Empire|1628": "Shah Jahan (r. 1628–1658) inherited a wealthy, stable empire after Jahangir. He built iconic monuments like the Taj Mahal, Red Fort, and Jama Masjid, reconquered Kandahar in 1638, and ruled an empire whose GDP was about a quarter of the world’s output.",
  "Mughal Empire|1648": "By 1648, the Mughal Empire reached its zenith. The Taj Mahal was complete, the Peacock Throne installed, and the empire, with 100–150 million people, accounted for ~25% of world GDP. The mansabdari system worked efficiently, marking maximum wealth, power, and cultural achievement.",
  "Mughal Empire|1658": "After Shah Jahan fell ill in 1657, a war of succession broke out among his sons. Aurangzeb (r. 1658–1707) emerged victorious, executing Dara Shikoh, imprisoning Murad, and confining Shah Jahan until his death in 1666. Though capable and disciplined, Aurangzeb reversed court culture, reinstated the jizya, destroyed temples, and alienated Rajputs, setting the stage for future rebellions.",
  "Mughal Empire|1690": "By 1687, Aurangzeb had conquered Bijapur and Golconda, giving the empire its largest extent—over 4 million km² and 158 million people. However, prolonged wars and Maratha resistance strained the treasury and administration, foreshadowing structural challenges despite territorial peak.",
  "Mughal Empire|1707": "Aurangzeb died on 3 March 1707 in Ahmednagar after 26 years in the Deccan. The treasury was empty, Marathas undefeated, Rajput alliances broken, and the Sikh Khalsa strong. Within 12 years, four emperors ruled in a single year, marking the empire’s fiscal collapse and rapid fragmentation.",
  "Mughal Empire|1720": "Between 1712–1720, Mughal central authority collapsed. Puppet rulers, Sayyid Brother dominance, and rapid emperor turnover marked the period. By 1720, Muhammad Shah removed the Sayyid Brothers, but independent states had emerged in the Deccan, Awadh, and Bengal, leaving the empire fragmented.",
  "Mughal Empire|1739": "In 1739, Nader Shah defeated the Mughals at Karnal and sacked Delhi, killing 20,000–30,000 civilians in a day. He seized the Peacock Throne, Koh-i-Noor, Darya-i-Noor, and vast treasure, leaving the empire a weakened shell with little prestige or capacity to defend its capital.",
  "Mughal Empire|1750": "After Nader Shah and Afghan invasions, central India came under Maratha control. By 1771, the Mughal emperor survived under Maratha protection, issuing coins and receiving formal obeisance, but effective authority over the subcontinent had ended.",
  "Mughal Empire|1784": "In 1784, the Marathas became official protectors of the Mughal emperor, who retained only ceremonial authority. After their defeat by the British (1803–1805), the East India Company took over as protector, further reducing the emperor to a powerless figurehead.",
  "Mughal Empire|1800": "By 1800, the Mughal emperor (Shah Alam II) controlled only the Red Fort in Delhi. After the East India Company abolished local rule in Bengal-Bihar and won the Battle of Buxar (1764), the emperor retained only ceremonial duties, with no army, revenue, or administrative power.",
  "Mughal Empire|1857": "During the 1857 Indian Rebellion, rebels made Emperor Bahadur Shah II their nominal leader. The British retook Delhi, captured and exiled him to Rangoon, and executed two sons, ending the 331-year Mughal dynasty and establishing direct British Crown rule.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1430": "By the early 15th century, Great Zimbabwe was in decline. Mutota moved north, founded a new capital at Chitako-Changonya, and conquered the Tavara and Tonga. He established the Mutapa state under his personal authority, laying the foundations of a nascent polity with no formal tribute or trade networks.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1450": "Around 1450, Matope inherits the empire, abandoning Great Zimbabwe and expanding over Tavara, Tonga, Uteve, and Manyika. He establishes the kuruva tribute tax and appoints regional governors from the royal household, creating a growing patronage network.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1480": "Under Mukombero (c. 1480–1490), Mutapa reaches full territorial extent, controlling northern Zimbabwe’s gold plateau and ivory trade, and holding suzerainty from the Zambezi to beyond the Limpopo. The Rozvi secession erupts when Changamire I briefly seizes power, foreshadowing future internal challenges.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1500": "Chikuyo (c. 1494–1530) reunifies the empire after defeating Changamire I, consolidating trade routes and the tribute system. The Rozvi retain Guruuswa as semi-independent. In 1506, the Portuguese seize Sofala, creating a commercial intermediary, but Mutapa remains strong and tribute continues.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1530": "By the 1520s–1530s, under Chikuyo, the Mutapa Empire reaches its peak, controlling gold and ivory trade across the Zimbabwean plateau, collecting kuruva from Swahili and Portuguese traders, and holding suzerainty from the Zambezi to beyond the Limpopo. Portuguese military interference is still decades away.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1550": "Under Neshangwe Munembire and Chivere Nyasoro (c. 1530–1560), Mutapa remains strong. Portuguese traders expand inland but operate under Mutapa authority, paying the kuruva tax. Gold trade continues, tribute flows, and the empire faces no military threat, maintaining its mid-16th-century heyday.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1570": "After the 1561 killing of missionary Gonçalo da Silveira, Portugal sends a punitive expedition in 1571. Disease and Mutapa resistance decimate the force, which fails, but Portuguese attention shifts from commercial to adversarial.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1600": "By 1600, under Gatsi Rusere (r. 1589–1623), Mutapa faces decline. Portuguese traders and priests dominate court politics, tributaries resist, and he grants extensive trade and gold concessions for support. Some territories are recovered, but structural decline is evident.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1629": "After Gatsi Rusere’s death, a succession dispute leads to Portuguese retaliation in 1628. Kapararidze is driven out, Mavura Mhande installed as a puppet, and the 1629 treaty makes Mutapa effectively a Portuguese colony, granting them trade, mining, and settlement rights, though local Shona culture persists.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1650": "Following the 1629 treaty, Portuguese prazeros establish fortified settlements and dominate Mutapa politics. Mavura Mhande (r. 1629–1652) and his son Siti Kazurukamusapa (r. 1652–1663) rule under Portuguese influence, who can depose and install kings at will, making the empire a colonial client state.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1693": "Under Kamharapasu Mukombwe (r. 1663–1692), Mutapa recovers some lands and mines from the Portuguese. In 1693, Changamire Dombo expels the Portuguese, but Mutapa becomes a Rozvi vassal in 1694, gaining nominal independence from Portugal but losing full sovereignty.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1700": "In the late 17th and early 18th century, Mutapa’s throne is contested by Rozvi and Portuguese forces. By 1712, Samatambira Nyamhandu I becomes a Rozvi vassal, reducing the kingdom to a minor chiefdom with no independent policy, trade control, or military capacity.",
  "Mutapa Empire|1760": "By 1760, the Portuguese withdraw, and the Rozvi dominate the Zimbabwe plateau. Mutapa survives only nominally in remote borderlands of Mozambique. A later polity (1803–1902) exists as a minor chiefdom with no connection to the original empire’s trade, territory, or significance.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2112": "After the Akkadian collapse, Ur-Nammu of Ur establishes the Third Dynasty of Ur, conquering Lagash, redirecting trade through Ur, and unifying southern Mesopotamia. He takes the title “King of Sumer and Akkad,” marking the dynasty’s early, militarily successful beginnings.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2100": "Ur-Nammu consolidates his rule through major building projects, temple restorations, and the Code of Ur-Nammu, the oldest surviving law code. He defeats Elamites and expands territory, but dies fighting Gutian forces, showing the state is still fragile and confined to southern Mesopotamia.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2094": "Shulgi succeeds Ur-Nammu, focusing on domestic affairs, completing the Great Ziggurat, and building temples. He creates a standing army, defeats the Gutians, secures borders, and taxes temples to fund the military, consolidating and innovating institutions.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2074": "Around his 20th year, Shulgi declares himself a living god, standardises administration, taxes, weights, measures, and the calendar. He creates e-dubba schools for bureaucrats, improves roads and communications, and secures eastern alliances, building a highly centralised and sophisticated state.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2070": "By the late 2070s–2060s BCE, Shulgi expands Ur to its maximum extent, from southeastern Anatolia to the Persian Gulf. Campaigns crush regional cities, and tens of thousands of tablets track a sophisticated bureaucracy. Deitified in life, he is celebrated in temples and hymns, marking peak territorial, administrative, and cultural achievement.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2050": "Shulgi dies c. 2047 BCE after 48 years. Until then, the empire remains strong, with an intact army, loyal administrators, and a smoothly running bureaucracy. Evidence suggests little decline, and the empire operates near its institutional peak under his rule.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2046": "Amar-Sin (r. c. 2046–2038 BCE) inherits a strong empire, annexes Assyria, and campaigns in the Zagros. He maintains administration and building projects, but mounting Amorite pressure reveals growing vulnerabilities. His death from an infected foot marks a less glorious end.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2037": "Shu-Sin (r. c. 2037–2029 BCE) faces severe Amorite incursions and builds the Muriq-Tidnim wall to defend central Mesopotamia. While suppressing revolts, the empire shifts to a defensive posture, though southern provinces and administration continue to function effectively.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2028": "Ibbi-Sin (r. c. 2028–2004 BCE) inherits a stressed empire. Governors, including Ishbi-Erra at Isin, declare independence, cutting off grain and tribute. The bala tax system collapses, and Ibbi-Sin’s control steadily shrinks, leaving him with Ur and nearby territories only.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2020": "By c. 2020 BCE, Ibbi-Sin rules only Ur and its surroundings. Former provinces are controlled by independent rulers, administrative records dwindle, and his limited campaigns fail. Grain shortages and rising prices mark the empire’s collapse into a single besieged city.",
  "Neo-Sumerian Empire|-2004": "Around 2004 BCE, Elamites under Kindattu sack Ur and capture Ibbi-Sin. The city is destroyed, and the event is commemorated in the Lamentations. Elamite occupation lasts ~21 years, marking the end of Ur III as a political entity.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1299": "Osman I, a Turkoman chieftain in Bithynia, declares independence from the Seljuks and raids Byzantine frontier settlements. After winning the Battle of Bapheus (1301/02), he steadily expands his beylik, a small but militarily active frontier state.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1326": "Orhan (r. 1323/4–1362) captures Bursa in 1326 as the first Ottoman capital, moves the court into Europe by 1352, and secures almost all of Thrace. The capital later shifts to Edirne, gradually surrounding Constantinople, marking the rise of a regional empire.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1389": "Murad I wins the Battle of Kosovo (1389), ending Serbian power in the Balkans. Bayezid I succeeds him, defeats the crusade at Nicopolis (1396), begins the first siege of Constantinople, and rules an empire from southern Greece to the Danube and Aegean to central Anatolia.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1402": "On 20 July 1402, Timur defeats Bayezid I at Ankara. Bayezid is captured and later dies in captivity. Western Anatolia is devastated, Anatolian beyliks restored, and an 11-year Ottoman civil war follows. The dynasty survives but the empire fragments.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1413": "After 11 years of civil war, Mehmed I (r. 1413–1421) reunifies the empire, re-subordinating the Anatolian beyliks. He consolidates internally, avoiding European aggression. His son Murad II resumes Balkan expansion, repelling Crusader attacks at Varna (1444) and Kosovo (1448).",
  "Ottoman Empire|1453": "At 21, Mehmed II conquers Constantinople on 29 May 1453, ending the Byzantine Empire and making it the Ottoman capital, Istanbul. He claims the title ‘Caesar of Rome’ and expands into Serbia, Bosnia, the Black Sea coast, and briefly Otranto, establishing the Ottomans as a transcontinental power.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1512": "Selim I (r. 1512–1520) expands the empire dramatically, defeating the Safavids at Chaldiran (1514) and conquering Syria (1516) and Egypt (1517). He becomes Caliph and Custodian of Mecca and Medina, doubling the empire’s territory, revenue, and religious authority",
  "Ottoman Empire|1520": "Suleiman I (r. 1520–1566) rules the Ottoman Empire at its peak, conquering Belgrade, Hungary, Baghdad, and extending influence across the Mediterranean via Barbarossa. The empire spans from the Persian Gulf to Algeria and from Yemen to Hungary. His Kanun-i Osmani legal reforms endure for 300 years. Population reaches 25 million. Score 10: maximum military, legal, cultural, and territorial achievement.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1566": "Suleiman dies at Szigetvár in 1566 during his 13th campaign; succeeded by Selim II. Empire retains full territory, Kanun-i Osmani intact, Janissaries and timar system functional. ‘Sultanate of Women’ begins influencing palace politics. Score 9: peak strength maintained, but ascent ends.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1600": "Lepanto naval defeat (1571) marks limits of Ottoman maritime power, though fleet rebuilt by 1574. Institutional erosion begins: devshirme weakens, Janissaries admit freeborn Muslims, timar system declines. Köprülü viziers (1656–1683) later revive structures, but underlying problems persist. Score 8: empire remains strong and territorial peak intact, but institutional peak passed.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1683": "1683 — Ottoman territorial maximum and turning-point defeat. Empire spans greatest extent, but second siege of Vienna fails: Kara Mustafa Pasha’s 170,000-strong army defeated by Jan Sobieski (12 Sept). Holy League exploits victory for 16 years, ending in Treaty of Karlowitz (1699). Score 7: maximum territory, but pivot to permanent contraction begins.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1699": "1699 — Treaty of Karlowitz; major territorial losses. Ottomans cede Hungary, Transylvania, and most of Slavonia to Austria; Morea to Venice; Podolia to Poland. First permanent surrender of significant European lands. Score 6: core Anatolia, southern Balkans, and Arab provinces remain, but structural blow to empire’s European power.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1700": "1700s — Post-Karlowitz consolidation; central authority weakens. Provincial governors gain autonomy, and coordination across Balkans, Anatolia, Egypt, Levant, Mesopotamia, and Arabia declines. Score 6: core empire intact but institutional fragmentation accelerating.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1800": "1798–1821 — Tanzimat threshold; empire’s fragility exposed. Napoleon’s Egyptian campaign, Janissary revolt against Selim III, and Mehmed Ali’s de facto Egyptian autonomy reveal weakening military and provincial control. Greek independence and Russian protectorate status further stress the state. Score 5: vast territories nominally held, but central authority and military capacity eroding.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1878": "1878 — Congress of Berlin; European losses formalised. Following defeat in the Russo-Turkish War (1877–78), Romania, Serbia, and Montenegro gain full independence, Bulgaria becomes autonomous, and the Ottomans cede territory to Russia and allow Austria-Hungary to occupy Bosnia-Herzegovina. Score 3: Anatolia, the Levant, Mesopotamia, Egypt, and Arabia remain, but the empire is politically weakened and strategically defensive.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1913": "1913 — Balkan Wars; near-total European loss. The First Balkan War (1912–13) strips the Ottomans of Macedonia, Albania, and most of Thrace, while the Italo-Turkish War (1911–12) cedes Libya and the Dodecanese to Italy. The Second Balkan War allows recovery of only Edirne. Score 2: empire largely confined to Anatolia and the Middle East, having lost most European holdings.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1914": "1914 — Pre-WWI; empire at historic low. By 1914, the Ottomans have lost Bulgaria, most of European Turkey, Libya, and the Dodecanese. Economy is in debt; military largely rebuilt under German advisors. Score 2: controlling only Anatolia and Arab provinces, politically weak, economically dependent, and militarily limited.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1920": "1920 — Treaty of Sèvres; empire partitioned on paper. Allies occupy Constantinople; France takes Syria/Lebanon, Britain takes Mesopotamia/Palestine, Greece gets Smyrna/Thrace, Armenia/Kurdistan slated for independence. Mustafa Kemal begins Turkish War of Independence. Score 1: sovereignty exists only on paper; capital occupied, territories divided, but resistance underway.",
  "Ottoman Empire|1922": "1922 — ABSOLUTE TROUGH; abolition of the Sultanate. On 1 Nov, the Grand National Assembly abolishes the Ottoman Sultanate; Mehmed VI flees Istanbul. The Treaty of Lausanne (1923) confirms the Republic of Turkey as successor. Score 0: the Ottoman political entity ceases to exist.",
  "Oyo Empire|1300": "Oranmiyan, son of Oduduwa, establishes Oyo-Ile and becomes the first Alaafin. Early successors include Ajaka, Shango, and Kori. The kingdom survives Bariba attacks but initially controls only the city and surroundings. Score 2: nascent city-state with dynastic legitimacy, not yet an empire.",
  "Oyo Empire|1400": "Oyo steadily conquers neighbouring territories to form the core Yoruba heartland (‘metropolitan Oyo’), with Kori completing the consolidation. Wealth grows from Trans-Saharan trade in salt, leather, horses, and kola nuts. By century’s end, Oyo is a formidable regional power, though not yet an empire beyond Yorubaland. Score 4: regional dominance established, territorial breadth still limited.",
  "Oyo Empire|1500": "By 1500, Oyo’s expansion stalls. The kingdom struggles against northern neighbours Borgu and Nupe and fails to penetrate the tsetse-infested southeastern forests. The Nupe threat grows, and conquest is imminent. Score 3: still a functioning kingdom with a capital, but territorially limited, strategically vulnerable, and facing a major crisis.",
  "Oyo Empire|1535": "The Nupe kingdom under Tsoede conquers Oyo, sacks Oyo-Ile, and forces the Alaafin and chiefs into 80 years of exile in Borgu. Trade networks collapse, and Trans-Saharan connections are severed. Score 1: dynastic lineage survives, but Oyo ceases to function as a governing state.",
  "Oyo Empire|1600": "The Oyo dynasty returns from Borgu exile and reconquers its homeland. Ofinran restores core territory, Eguguojo expands across Yorubaland, and Orompoto campaigns against the Nupe. Under Abipa, Oyo-Ile is repopulated and rebuilt; the first Bere festival celebrates peace. The empire becomes more centralised and militarily sophisticated, incorporating cavalry tactics and cultural elements from the Nupe. Score 7: consolidation underway; near full recovery by 1630–1650.",
  "Oyo Empire|1650": "Oyo expands its influence from the Volta River to the Niger River, conquering 13 rival kingdoms including Owu and Ede. Cavalry becomes the backbone of its military, and control of the Egbado corridor secures access to Atlantic trade. The empire begins funneling slaves from its northern hinterlands to Portuguese, Dutch, and English traders.",
  "Oyo Empire|1700": "By 1700, Oyo is the dominant power in West Africa between the Sahel and the coast, controlling key trade routes and collecting tribute from Dahomey, though Dahomey itself is not yet fully subdued. Its army is strong and well-organized, projecting influence across the region, with full territorial dominance still a few decades away.",
  "Oyo Empire|1748": "After two wars with Dahomey (1724–1730; 1738–1748), Oyo secures full tributary status from King Tegbesu, gaining direct access to the Atlantic coast via Porto-Novo. The empire now spans from the Niger River to the Volta River, controls key coastal trade ports, and dominates all of West Africa south of the Sahel, becoming the region’s wealthiest and most powerful state.",
  "Oyo Empire|1750": "Shortly after reaching its territorial peak, Oyo’s political stability erodes. Basorun Gaha, head of the Oyo Mesi council, dominates the state, forcing four successive Alaafins to commit ritual suicide and diverting tribute revenues to his family. While the empire retains its full territory and Dahomey continues paying tribute, central authority and constitutional balance are severely weakened.",
  "Oyo Empire|1774": "Alaafin Abiodun ends Basorun Gaha’s dominance by executing him in 1774, restoring royal authority. He focuses on Atlantic trade with European merchants, bringing prosperity, but deliberately weakens the military to prevent challenges. Failed campaigns against Borgu and Nupe reveal declining army capacity, signaling the start of military deterioration despite political stability.",
  "Oyo Empire|1800": "By 1800, Oyo is fracturing. Alaafin Awole is deposed and commits suicide after a rebellion by Afonja, the commander at Ilorin, who consolidates the city as an autonomous power. Meanwhile, the Egba provinces expel Oyo’s agents, becoming effectively independent. Three Alaafins rule in rapid succession, and though Dahomey still pays tribute, central authority has collapsed, leaving the empire a loose confederation rather than a unified state.",
  "Oyo Empire|1823": "In 1823, King Ghezo of Dahomey defeats the Oyo army, ending Oyo’s control over the coast and its lucrative Atlantic trade revenues. Simultaneously, Afonja’s former stronghold at Ilorin is taken over by Fulani allies, who kill Afonja in 1824 and establish Ilorin as a Fulani emirate linked to the Sokoto Caliphate. Oyo retains only a diminished core territory around Oyo-Ile, having lost its richest vassal, northern military arm, and major provinces.",
  "Oyo Empire|1830": "Under Alaafin Majotu, Oyo exists as a fragmented state. The Fulani emirate of Ilorin invades Oyo territory, Dahomey remains independent and hostile, and the Egba establish Abeokuta as a self-governing city. Yoruba civil wars escalate, and Oyo-Ile is increasingly besieged. The internal slave trade fuels widespread conflict, leaving the dynasty in its historic capital but controlling very little beyond it.",
  "Oyo Empire|1835": "The Fulani of Ilorin destroy Oyo-Ile, the historic capital founded by Oranmiyan. Thousands flee south, establishing settlements such as Ibadan. The Alaafin moves the court to Ago d’Oyo (‘New Oyo’), a much smaller city. The dynasty survives but controls only a tiny territory, has no vassals, and lacks military power—essentially a king without an empire.",
  "Oyo Empire|1888": "New Oyo becomes a formal British protectorate in 1888, and by 1893 the rest of Yorubaland falls under British control. The Alaafin retains only ceremonial status under indirect rule, with no independent military, fiscal, or foreign authority. The Oyo Empire ceases to exist as a sovereign political entity, though the dynasty and title endure.",
  "Oyo Empire|1896": "By 1896, Oyo ceases to exist as a self-governing political entity. The British consolidate control under the Lagos Protectorate, later forming the Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria (1900). Successor cities like Ibadan and Abeokuta are absorbed into the colonial system. The Oyo state ends completely as a political power.",
  "Parthian Empire|-247": "Arsaces I establishes the Arsacid dynasty by seizing the northeastern Iranian province of Parthia from the distracted Seleucids. The new kingdom is tiny and vulnerable, with its capital at Nisa, and initially acknowledges Seleucid suzerainty while consolidating survival.",
  "Parthian Empire|-209": "Antiochus III defeats Arsaces II, reducing Parthia to a tributary vassal state. Arsaces II retains the title of king but must pay tribute and acknowledge Seleucid overlordship, leaving Parthia politically subservient and far weaker than at its founding.",
  "Parthian Empire|-200": "By 200 BCE, the Arsacid kings remain tributary vassals of Antiochus III, confined to Parthia and Hyrcania. They function as a local polity but cannot expand or act independently until the Seleucid ruler’s death opens a window for recovery.",
  "Parthian Empire|-188": "The death of Antiochus III and the Seleucid defeat at Apamea weaken imperial control over the east. Phraates I exploits this to expand Parthia north of the “Gates of Alexander,” shedding vassal status and establishing the kingdom as an emerging regional power.",
  "Parthian Empire|-150": "Mithridates I seizes eastern territories from the Greco-Bactrians and conquers Media, taking Ecbatana as a capital. Parthia transitions from a minor kingdom to a significant regional power, though Mesopotamia remains unconquered and Seleucid retaliation is still possible.",
  "Parthian Empire|-141": "Mithridates I captures Seleucia-on-Tigris and Babylon, establishing control over Mesopotamia. He defeats and captures the Seleucid king Demetrius II, consolidating Parthian authority across the Iranian plateau, Mesopotamia, and parts of Bactria, marking Parthia’s emergence as a true empire.",
  "Parthian Empire|-129": "Phraates II defeats and kills Antiochus VII Sidetes at Ecbatana, ending Seleucid attempts to reclaim the east. With its western rival removed, Parthia becomes the undisputed power over the Iranian plateau and Mesopotamia, though eastern nomadic pressures remain.",
  "Parthian Empire|-100": "Under Mithridates II, Parthia reaches its greatest territorial, political, and diplomatic height, extending from Syria and the Caucasus to Central Asia and India. He reconquers Babylonia, subdues Armenia and northern Mesopotamian kingdoms, defeats Arab raiders, and seizes Dura-Europos. He adopts the title “King of Kings” and the Iranian tiara on coinage, establishes the first formal contacts with Rome and the Han Dynasty, and solidifies Parthia as a central broker on the Silk Road.",
  "Parthian Empire|-80": "The death of Mithridates II triggers a period of fragmentation and dynastic conflict. Rival claimants vie for the throne, and reliable records are scarce. Taking advantage of the chaos, Tigranes the Great of Armenia seizes large portions of Mesopotamia and Adiabene (c. 88–83 BCE), territories that had belonged to Parthia under Mithridates II. The empire is dramatically weakened, territorially reduced, and politically unstable.",
  "Parthian Empire|-53": "Under Orodes II, General Surena leads about 10,000 cavalry to decisively defeat Marcus Licinius Crassus’s Roman force of roughly 43,000 at Carrhae. Around 20,000 Romans are killed and 10,000 captured, including the legionary standards, marking one of Rome’s worst defeats. While this is a military and prestige high point, Parthia’s territorial and institutional peak had already occurred under Mithridates II.",
  "Parthian Empire|-36": "Between 40–38 BCE, the Parthians exploit Roman civil wars, briefly occupying parts of Syria, Palestine, and Asia Minor. In 36 BCE, Mark Antony invades via Armenia, but his siege of Phraaspa in Media Atropatene fails disastrously, costing him around 35,000 men. While the empire is no longer expanding, it successfully repels major Roman offensives, maintaining strategic security.",
  "Parthian Empire|-20": "In 20 BCE, Augustus and Phraates IV negotiate the return of the legionary standards taken at Carrhae. Parthia retains Armenia, and Rome accepts the Euphrates as the frontier, marking a practical stalemate. The treaty stabilizes the western border, but internal noble faction tensions continue, and Parthia no longer pursues expansionist campaigns.",
  "Parthian Empire|50": "Around 58–63 CE, Vologases I installs his brother Tiridates as king of Armenia, provoking war with Rome. The conflict ends with the Rhandeia settlement: Tiridates keeps the throne but receives his crown from Emperor Nero in Rome. This compromise preserves Parthian influence in Armenia. The empire remains strong, wealthy from Silk Road trade, and diplomatically active, though the Roman threat is now enduring.",
  "Parthian Empire|117": "Between 114–117 CE, Trajan conquers Armenia, northern Mesopotamia, and captures Seleucia and Ctesiphon, reaching the Persian Gulf. Revolts erupt, Trajan dies, and Hadrian reverses all gains, restoring the Euphrates as the border. Parthia survives territorially, but the sacking of its capital marks a permanent blow to its prestige and aura of invincibility.",
  "Parthian Empire|150": "By 150 CE, Parthia maintains peace with Rome and controls Iran, Mesopotamia, and influence over Armenia, benefiting from Silk Road trade. However, powerful noble clans like the Suren and Karin increasingly challenge the Arsacid kings, eroding central authority. The empire is outwardly stable but structurally fragile, with the Antonine War looming (161–166 CE).",
  "Parthian Empire|165": "In 161 CE, Vologases IV invades Roman Armenia and Syria, but a Roman counterattack under Avidius Cassius sacks Ctesiphon in 165 CE, burning the royal palace. The returning army brings the Antonine Plague, likely smallpox or measles, devastating Parthian cities, trade, and population. The empire faces repeated military setbacks while struggling with a catastrophic epidemic.",
  "Parthian Empire|200": "Between 195 and 197 CE, Septimius Severus campaigns in Mesopotamia and sacks Ctesiphon for the third time in under a century, enslaving tens of thousands. Rome permanently annexes northern Mesopotamia, establishing a lasting presence east of the Euphrates. The Parthians retain Iran but lose their northern buffer, while internal dynastic struggles continue and the rise of Ardashir I in Persis begins threatening Arsacid authority.",
  "Parthian Empire|224": "In 224 CE, Ardashir I of Persis defeats and kills the last Parthian king, Artabanus IV, at the Battle of Hormozdgan. Years of internal civil wars had left the Arsacid military weakened. Ardashir establishes the Sasanian Empire, while the Arsacid line survives only in minor cadet branches ruling Armenia and parts of the Caucasus. The Parthian state ceases to exist as a political entity.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1569": "On 1 July 1569, Poland and Lithuania unite to form the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, a federal state with about 7 million people and 815,000 km². It establishes a Sejm representing the politically enfranchised nobility. The union is partly coerced, and the childless Sigismund II Augustus leaves a looming succession crisis, creating immediate instability despite the Commonwealth’s strong foundations.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1573": "After Sigismund II’s death, the Commonwealth holds its first viritim royal election, in which all nobles vote in person. Henry III of Valois is elected but quickly leaves for France. The Henrician Articles establish a constitutional framework guaranteeing religious tolerance, limiting royal authority, and granting nobles the right to refuse military service for unjust wars. Despite early dysfunction, the transfer of power occurs without civil war, showing both resilience and emerging institutional fragility.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1582": "King Stephen Báthory strengthens the Commonwealth’s military, forming elite infantry units and reversing losses in the Livonian War against Ivan the Terrible. By the Peace of Jam Zapolski (1582), the Commonwealth regains Livonia and Polotsk, securing 815,000 km² and about 6.5 million people. Báthory demonstrates the potential of a strong elected monarch, though reforms face ongoing resistance from the nobility.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1600": "By 1600, the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth is Europe’s largest state by territory, with a thriving grain export economy via Baltic ports and a flourishing noble culture. Its parliamentary system functions effectively, the liberum veto is not yet abused, and the state has successfully resisted Sweden, Russia, and Ottoman vassals. Having secured Livonia, the Commonwealth is poised for its most expansionist phase.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1618": "The Truce of Deulino ends the Polish-Muscovite War, granting the Commonwealth Smolensk and Chernihiv. Polish forces had even briefly occupied Moscow, and Władysław was elected Tsar by Russian boyars. With roughly 990,000 km² and 12 million people, the Commonwealth reaches its largest territorial extent, enjoys military prestige, economic strength, and remains untouched by the Thirty Years’ War ravaging Western Europe.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1634": "After the Smolensk War, Russia formally renounces claims to the Polish throne, confirming Commonwealth borders. Władysław IV governs effectively, managing noble factions and maintaining stability. The liberum veto remains unused, and the Commonwealth avoids the devastation of the Thirty Years’ War. While Livonia has been lost to Sweden, the state remains powerful, territorially significant, and economically strong.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1648": "Following Władysław IV’s death, the weak John II Casimir ascends the throne as Bohdan Khmelnytsky leads a massive Cossack-peasant revolt in Ukraine. The rebellion devastates the eastern provinces, defeats multiple Commonwealth armies, and allies with the Crimean Tatars. Central authority falters as the Sejm cannot coordinate an effective response, exposing the vulnerabilities of the Commonwealth’s decentralised military system. While the state still exists, much of Ukraine is effectively lost.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1650": "By 1650, the Khmelnytsky Uprising has weakened the Commonwealth and devastated Ukraine. Although it still controls much of its territory and maintains a sizable army, the state is militarily exhausted and fiscally strained. Structural decay is advanced, leaving the Commonwealth vulnerable to the simultaneous Swedish and Russian invasions that will trigger the catastrophic Deluge.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1660": "Between 1655 and 1660, the Commonwealth suffers simultaneous invasions: Sweden from the west and north, Russia from the east, and Transylvania from the south. Much of the territory is occupied, and the king flees to Silesia. By 1660 the Swedes are expelled (Treaty of Oliva), and Russia eventually cedes Left-Bank Ukraine and Kyiv (Truce of Andrusovo, 1667). Up to 40% of the population is lost to war, plague, and famine. The liberum veto is used for the first time to paralyse the Sejm (1652), revealing institutional weakness. The Commonwealth survives but is permanently weakened—territories lost, population decimated, and constitutional functionality impaired.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1683": "In 1683, John III Sobieski (r. 1674–1696) leads the relief of Vienna, personally commanding the Winged Hussars to break the Ottoman siege. The victory earns the Commonwealth the title Antemurale Christianitatis (“Bulwark of Christianity”) and temporarily restores military prestige. Despite this success, the liberum veto continues to paralyse the Sejm, magnate factionalism is rampant, and the king’s campaigns are costly. The triumph reflects personal leadership rather than effective institutions, marking the Commonwealth’s final major military achievement.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1700": "After John III Sobieski’s death in 1696, the Commonwealth rapidly loses sovereignty. By 1700, Sweden and Russia are fighting on its territory, each supporting rival claimants to the throne. In 1704, Sweden installs Stanisław I Leszczyński, while Russia reinstates Augustus II in 1709. The Sejm is repeatedly paralysed by the liberum veto, leaving the Commonwealth unable to govern itself while foreign armies occupy its lands.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1720": "After the Great Northern War, under Augustus II and Augustus III (1697–1763), the Commonwealth suffers royal incompetence, magnate domination, and Russian control. Between 1717 and 1763, no Sejm session completes legislation. The 1717 ‘Silent Sejm,’ held under Russian military pressure, formalises Russian suzerainty. Prussia and Russia exploit this weakness to keep the Commonwealth politically impotent, with foreign policy dictated from St. Petersburg and the army reduced to a token force.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1764": "Stanisław August Poniatowski (r. 1764–1795), the last king of the Commonwealth, is elected with Russian support. Personally reformist, he initiates major reforms: the Commission for National Education (1773), the first state education ministry in the world, and later the Constitution of 3 May 1791. These efforts face immediate resistance from conservative nobles and Russian forces. The Bar Confederation (1768–1772), a noble uprising claiming to defend traditional freedoms, provokes Russian intervention and leads to the First Partition. The Commonwealth shows brief reform energy but remains structurally weak.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1772": "In 1772, Austria, Prussia, and Russia seize roughly 211,000 km² (~30%) of the Commonwealth: Russia takes the eastern borderlands, Prussia claims Royal Prussia (cutting off Gdańsk and Baltic access), and Austria annexes Galicia. About 4.5 million people are lost. The Commonwealth remains formally independent but is now effectively a Russian protectorate, weakened and territorially diminished.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1791": "The Four-Year Sejm (1788–1792) enacts the Constitution of 3 May 1791, creating a hereditary monarchy, abolishing the liberum veto, expanding rights to burghers, and modernizing government. Reformist but too late, it provokes the Targowica Confederation, leading to Russian invasion in 1792 and nullification of the constitution within weeks. The Second Partition follows in 1793, showing the Commonwealth’s reform potential but fatal vulnerability.",
  "Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth|1795": "After the failed Kościuszko Uprising (1794), Russia, Prussia, and Austria divide the remaining territory. King Stanisław August Poniatowski abdicates on 25 November 1795, and the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth disappears from the map. Poland and Lithuania remain partitioned until 1918, 123 years later.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1415": "On 21 August 1415, King João I of Portugal and his sons (including Prince Henry, later “the Navigator”) capture the Muslim port of Ceuta on the North African coast, a key node in trans-Saharan gold and slave trade routes. This marks Portugal’s first territorial expansion beyond Iberia. Strategically, the gain is limited: Saharan caravans soon bypass Ceuta. Score 2: a tiny, embryonic empire — one city on the African coast, with decades of exploration and expansion still ahead.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1460": "By 1460, Portugal had colonised Madeira, the Azores, and Cape Verde, explored the West African coast to Sierra Leone, and established a gold and slave trade post at Arguim Island. The Sagres maritime school pioneered the caravel and navigation techniques, laying the foundation for future Indian Ocean voyages.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1488": "Bartolomeu Dias successfully sails around Africa’s southern tip, proving that Europe can reach the Indian Ocean by sea without crossing Ottoman or Arab territories. This breakthrough allows King João II to plan direct expeditions to Asia, while Portugal continues to control the West African coast.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1498": "Vasco da Gama completes the first direct European voyage to India, arriving at Calicut and returning with highly profitable spices. Shortly after, Cabral accidentally discovers Brazil. While the route to Asia is now open, Portugal’s empire is still limited to Atlantic islands and West African trading posts, with the Indian Ocean empire yet to be established.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1510": "Under Manuel I, viceroy Afonso de Albuquerque secures key trade chokepoints: conquering Goa as a permanent Indian base (1510), Malacca as Southeast Asia’s spice hub (1511), and Hormuz at the Persian Gulf entrance (1515), plus Socotra (1507). This strategy gives Portugal control over the Red Sea, Persian Gulf, and Strait of Malacca, establishing the foundation of its Indian Ocean empire.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1521": "Manuel I transformed Portugal from a small Atlantic kingdom into the dominant Indian Ocean power. His reign saw Vasco da Gama reach India, Cabral discover Brazil, Albuquerque secure Goa, Malacca, and Hormuz, Magellan begin the first circumnavigation, and Europeans reach China and Japan. Lisbon becomes Europe’s wealthiest city, and his son John III inherits a fully operational global empire from Brazil to Japan.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1550": "By the mid-16th century, Portugal reached the height of its power. John III controlled a network of fortified trading posts from Ceuta and Elmina in Africa to Goa, Ceylon, Malacca, the Moluccas, and Macau. Brazil’s sugar plantations were booming, and the Estado da India dominated the Indian Ocean trade through naval strength and diplomacy, having neutralised both the Mamluk-Gujarati fleet and the Ottoman naval threat. Portuguese naval and commercial supremacy was unmatched, marking the empire’s undisputed zenith.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1578": "King Sebastian I leads a crusade into Morocco and is killed at Alcácer Quibir, along with much of the nobility. With no heir, his granduncle Cardinal Henry rules briefly before dying in 1580, leaving the throne contested. Philip II of Spain claims Portugal, triggering a dynastic crisis. The empire’s overseas trade remains intact, but political instability sets the stage for 60 years of lost independence and strategic weakening.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1600": "By 1600, Portugal is ruled from Madrid under the Iberian Union. The Dutch-Portuguese War begins, with the VOC and Dutch West India Company systematically attacking Portuguese posts in Asia, Africa, and Brazil. Formerly Portugal’s major spice customer, the Dutch now seize key trade centers. While the empire’s infrastructure remains mostly intact, its defenses are weakened by Spanish control, and major losses in Malacca, Ceylon, the Moluccas, the Persian Gulf, and Japanese trade are looming.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1640": "On 1 December 1640, Portuguese nobles stage a bloodless coup, ending the 60-year Iberian Union and proclaiming John IV of Braganza king. Portugal regains independence but suffers major losses: the Dutch capture Malacca, Elmina, and parts of northeast Brazil, while Bombay and Tangier are ceded to England as dowry. The empire is restored but weakened, now relying heavily on Brazil for its economic strength.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1661": "Portugal formally cedes Ceylon and the Moluccas to the Dutch, paying 8 million guilders, and gives Bombay and Tangier to England as Catherine of Braganza’s dowry. Its Asian holdings shrink to Goa, Macau, and East Timor, ending the golden era of Albuquerque’s Indian Ocean empire. The empire survives but is now largely reliant on Brazil for wealth.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1700": "Gold is discovered in Minas Gerais in 1695, sparking a rush that transforms Portugal’s economy. By the 1720s–1740s, Brazilian gold production surpasses Spain’s American colonies, making King John V Europe’s richest monarch. Much of the gold flows to England through the Methuen Treaty, while Portugal’s Asian trade network remains permanently diminished.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1720": "Gold and diamonds from Minas Gerais reach their peak, producing an estimated 10–15 tonnes of gold per year. King John V, known as ‘the Magnanimous,’ uses the wealth for grand architecture, lavish court ceremonies, and arts patronage. Portugal gains international prestige, including the title ‘Fidelissimus’ from the Pope, though its territorial and military power remains far below its 16th-century peak.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1755": "On 1 November 1755, a massive earthquake, tsunami, and fire devastate Lisbon, killing at least 15,000 and destroying much of the commercial district. King Joseph I entrusts recovery to Sebastião José de Carvalho e Melo (later Marquis of Pombal), who leads reconstruction, expels the Jesuits, and implements Enlightenment-inspired reforms in Portugal and Brazil. Brazilian gold revenues begin to decline, but the empire remains economically significant and modernising administratively.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1808": "In November 1807, Napoleon’s forces invade Portugal, prompting the royal family and 10,000 officials to flee to Rio de Janeiro, arriving in March 1808. For the first time, a European empire is ruled from a colony, with Rio as the imperial seat. Prince Regent João opens Brazilian ports to foreign trade, establishes the Bank of Brazil, and founds higher education institutions. In 1815, Brazil is elevated to a kingdom. Meanwhile, Portugal remains under French occupation and later a British protectorate, sowing the seeds for Brazilian independence.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1822": "On 7 September 1822, Dom Pedro I declares Brazil independent, siding with local elites instead of restoring Portuguese control. Portugal formally recognises Brazilian independence in 1825 for a nominal indemnity. The empire loses its most valuable colony, which had generated more revenue than all others combined, leaving Portugal with its remaining African and Asian territories—Angola, Mozambique, Guinea, Cape Verde, São Tomé, Goa, Macau, and East Timor—still extensive but economically weakened.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1890": "Portugal’s plan to link Angola and Mozambique across Central Africa—the “Pink Map”—was halted in January 1890 when Britain issued an ultimatum demanding withdrawal from the Shire Highlands. With no military means to resist, Portugal complied, suffering national humiliation. The crisis sparked public outrage, riots, and political instability, undermining Portugal’s prestige and contributing to the monarchy’s fall in 1910, while the empire retained Angola and Mozambique but abandoned its Central African ambitions.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1900": "By the early 20th century, Portugal retains a substantial African empire, including Angola, Mozambique, Guinea-Bissau, Cape Verde, and São Tomé and Príncipe, alongside small Asian enclaves—Goa, Daman and Diu, Macau, and East Timor. The African colonies are being developed with railways, administrative structures, and plantations. After recovering from the 1890 Pink Map setback, Portugal asserts historical claims at the 1884–85 Berlin Conference, maintaining a territorially significant but economically secondary empire compared to Britain, France, or Belgium.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1961": "In 1961, nationalist insurgencies erupt in Angola, triggering the Angolan War of Independence, followed by conflicts in Guinea-Bissau and Mozambique. Portugal, a NATO member but economically weak, commits up to 40% of its budget to fight on three continents. In the same year, India annexes Goa, Daman, and Diu, ending over four centuries of Portuguese presence in India. The empire faces overextension, international isolation, and growing internal unrest.",
  "Portuguese Empire|1975": "By 1975, following the bloodless Carnation Revolution of 25 April 1974, Portugal withdraws from all African colonies. Angola, Mozambique, Guinea-Bissau, Cape Verde, and São Tomé and Príncipe become independent; East Timor is invaded by Indonesia shortly after declaring independence. Only Macau (returned to China in 1999) and the Atlantic islands remain under Portuguese control. Five and a half centuries of Portuguese empire, from Ceuta in 1415 to African decolonisation in 1975, have come to an end.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-323": "After Alexander the Great’s death in 323 BCE, Ptolemy becomes satrap of Egypt, the empire’s wealthiest and most defensible province. He acts with near-royal independence, securing Alexander’s body for Egypt and repelling Perdiccas in 321 BCE.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-305": "In 305 BCE, Ptolemy assumes the titles of king and pharaoh, controlling Egypt, Cyrene, and Cyprus, while Alexandria grows into a major city.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-300": "By 300 BCE, borders secured, Cyprus regained, and a sophisticated bureaucracy established. Alexandria becomes a cultural hub with the Library, Mouseion, and Pharos lighthouse under construction.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-283": "Ruling 284–246 BCE, Ptolemy II completes the Library and Pharos lighthouse. His fleet dominates the eastern Mediterranean, and scholars like Euclid work under royal patronage.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-280": "By 280 BCE, Ptolemy II expands into Asia Minor, dominating the Aegean and sending embassies to Rome and India.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-260": "Late in his reign, Ptolemy II loses ground after the Battle of Kos and Chremonidean War, retaining economic strength but reduced naval dominance.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-250": "By 250 BCE, Ptolemy II has ended the Second Syrian War and is partially restoring Aegean influence, including reviving the Ptolemaia festival at Delos. Egypt remains economically dominant, but the empire’s absolute peak will come under Ptolemy III after the Third Syrian War (246–241 BCE).",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-246": "In 246 BCE, Ptolemy III reunites Cyrenaica through marriage and launches the Third Syrian War (246–241 BCE). His forces march through Coele-Syria, capture Antioch, advance to Mesopotamia, and reach Bactria, while the navy seizes Aegean islands and pushes to Thrace. An internal revolt forces his return to Egypt, preventing total Seleucid collapse, but the empire reaches its maximum military projection.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-241": "By 241 BCE, after the Third Syrian War, the Ptolemaic Empire controls Egypt, Cyrenaica, Cyprus, Coele-Syria, Seleucia Pieria, the Syrian coast, Ephesus, and parts of Thrace. Ptolemy III is acclaimed as Euergetes, Egyptian priests deify him in the Canopus Decree (238 BCE), and major projects like the Temple of Horus at Edfu begin. The empire combines peak territorial reach, naval dominance, cultural prestige, and administrative stability.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-221": "Ptolemy III dies in 222/221 BCE and is succeeded by his son Ptolemy IV, a weak ruler who delegates power to ministers and indulges in court pleasures. The empire remains vast and institutionally intact, but the capable leadership that secured its peak is gone. Antiochus III of the Seleucids immediately begins planning to reclaim lost territories.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-217": "During the Fourth Syrian War (219–217 BCE), Antiochus III initially gains ground, but at the Battle of Raphia (June 217 BCE), Ptolemy IV decisively defeats him, reclaiming all lost territory. To field enough troops, 30,000 native Egyptians are armed as phalangites, breaking tradition. This empowers Egyptian nationalism and triggers 30 years of internal rebellions, creating lasting challenges to Ptolemaic authority.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-200": "After Ptolemy IV’s death in 205 BCE, his young son Ptolemy V becomes king under regents. Antiochus III seizes the chance to invade, and at the Battle of Panium (200 BCE), he permanently annexes Coele-Syria — the Ptolemaic Empire’s richest overseas province and key link to Asia. The loss is irreversible. Native rebellions in Upper Egypt further weaken internal control, leaving Egypt, Cyprus, and Cyrene as the core of a diminished empire.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-170": "In 170–168 BCE, Antiochus IV invades Egypt twice, briefly capturing Ptolemy VI and threatening Alexandria. Roman intervention — famously via Gaius Popillius Laenas’ ultimatum at the ‘Day of Eleusis’ — forces Antiochus to withdraw. Egypt survives, but only as a Roman-protected client state, territorially reduced, internally divided, and dependent on Roman authority for its continued existence.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-100": "By 100 BCE, decades of dynastic civil wars have left the Ptolemaic Empire confined to Egypt and Cyprus. The court in Alexandria is plagued by factional feuds, and while the Egyptian economy remains strong, political control is weak. The empire is a shadow of its former Ptolemy III-era power, permanently losing its other territories.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-51": "Cleopatra VII (r. 51–30 BCE) is the most capable Ptolemaic ruler since Ptolemy III, fluent in Egyptian and several languages, with strong political skill and charisma. However, the empire is entirely dependent on Rome, with its remaining territory limited to Egypt and Cyprus. Cleopatra’s personal abilities mitigate but cannot overcome the structural weakness and loss of independent power.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-34": "In 34 BCE, Mark Antony ceremonially grants Cleopatra and her children vast Roman eastern territories, making her ruler of Egypt, Cyprus, Libya, and Coele-Syria, with Caesarion as joint king. This temporarily restores the Ptolemaic Empire’s greatest extent since the 3rd century BCE. In reality, the revival is illusory — the lands remain under Roman control and the arrangement is soon exploited by Octavian in his propaganda.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-31": "On 2 September 31 BCE, Octavian’s fleet under Agrippa decisively defeats Antony and Cleopatra off northwestern Greece. Their forces retreat to Egypt, with many troops defecting. Antony commits suicide in July 30 BCE, leaving Cleopatra negotiating with Octavian. The Ptolemaic state is effectively terminal, with its military and political structure collapsing.",
  "Ptolemaic Empire|-30": "Cleopatra VII dies on 12 August 30 BCE. Octavian executes her son Caesarion, the last legitimate heir, and annexes Egypt as a Roman province under his direct control. The Ptolemaic dynasty, founded in 305 BCE, ceases to exist as an independent political entity.",
  "Punic Empire|-814": "Legendary Queen Dido (Elissa) establishes Carthage as a Phoenician trading outpost from Tyre. At this stage, it is a dependent settlement, not a political or military power.",
  "Punic Empire|-650": "Carthage becomes politically independent from Tyre and establishes colonies in western Sicily, controlling trade in silver, grain, copper, and purple dye.",
  "Punic Empire|-600": "Under Mago I, Carthage begins systematic empire-building, using mercenary armies and allying with Etruscans; Greeks expelled from Corsica.",
  "Punic Empire|-509": "Formal recognition by Rome; Carthage consolidates Sardinia, Balearics, western Sicily, and the North African coast.",
  "Punic Empire|-500": "Maximum Magonid power; defeat at Himera halts Sicilian expansion but core territories remain intact.",
  "Punic Empire|-409": "Hannibal Mago captures Selinus and Himera; Carthage reasserts Sicilian dominance.",
  "Punic Empire|-400": "Carthage is “superior power” of the western Mediterranean, controlling North African coast, Sardinia, Malta, Balearics, and western Sicily; exploratory voyages expand influence.",
  "Punic Empire|-332": "Alexander the Great sacks Tyre; wealthy Phoenicians migrate to Carthage, boosting its commercial and financial base",
  "Punic Empire|-300": "Consolidation of western Mediterranean trade routes; Carthage is the wealthiest and most commercially powerful city in the region.",
  "Punic Empire|-265": "Eve of First Punic War; Carthage dominates western Mediterranean, commanding vast commercial networks, richest North African territories, and the strongest navy; Rome is militarily weaker.",
  "Punic Empire|-241": "Sicily ceded, 3,200-talent indemnity paid, naval supremacy lost; Sardinia and Corsica seized by Rome; mercenary mutiny threatens internal stability.",
  "Punic Empire|-220": "Hamilcar Barca establishes Iberian holdings with silver mines; Hannibal and Hasdrubal consolidate a new power base.",
  "Punic Empire|-216": "Hannibal’s campaigns in Italy peak, threatening Rome existentially; strategic brilliance limited by lack of reinforcements and naval weakness.",
  "Punic Empire|-201": "Carthage becomes a Roman client state; overseas territories lost, fleet and military restricted, war elephants banned; political and military autonomy eliminated.",
  "Punic Empire|-155": "Carthage regains commercial prosperity, but Numidia seizes territory unchecked; Rome enforces subordination.",
  "Punic Empire|-146": "Rome destroys Carthage after three-year siege; city demolished, population enslaved, territories annexed; Carthaginian political identity erased.",
  "Qing Empire|1644": "Manchu forces enter via Shanhai Pass; Shunzhi Emperor installed. Vast regions still uncontrolled; Ming loyalists resist in the south. Empire unstable, newly conquered.",
  "Qing Empire|1661": "Regency under Oboi; partial consolidation underway. Southern China partly under Ming loyalists, Taiwan under Zheng Chenggong; institutions begin stabilizing.",
  "Qing Empire|1673": "Wu Sangui and other Han feudatories revolt, threatening Qing survival. Southern China engulfed; score reflects crisis-level instability.",
  "Qing Empire|1680": "Kangxi personally directs suppression; Qing survives existential threat. Taiwan incorporated; consolidation underway.",
  "Qing Empire|1689": "Northern borders secured; Qing recognized diplomatically by Russia; empire stabilizes and expands.",
  "Qing Empire|1700": "Empire secure in north and south, trade flourishing, population rising; New World crops introduced.",
  "Qing Empire|1720": "Empire spans Manchuria to Central Asia; administrative and cultural institutions at peak.",
  "Qing Empire|1735": "Fiscal and institutional peak inherited; economy prosperous, central administration strong.",
  "Qing Empire|1750": "Qianlong expands empire across frontiers, notably Xinjiang; cultural projects flourish.",
  "Qing Empire|1760": "Qing reaches maximum territorial extent (~14.7 million km²).",
  "Qing Empire|1780": ": Qianlong’s cultural, military, demographic, and territorial zenith; Siku Quanshu encyclopaedia compiled; population ~300 million.",
  "Qing Empire|1796": "Peasant revolt exposes Banner army weaknesses; corruption entrenched.",
  "Qing Empire|1804": "White Lotus rebellion defeated at enormous cost; Banner system obsolete; regional Han militias now dominant.",
  "Qing Empire|1820": "Fiscal pressures worsen; population ~400 million; frontier instability grows; structural decline evident.",
  "Qing Empire|1839": "British naval power exposes Qing military obsolescence; dynasty in measurable decline.",
  "Qing Empire|1842": "Hong Kong ceded, treaty ports opened; foreign extraterritoriality imposed; sovereignty compromised.",
  "Qing Empire|1850": "Major internal rebellion and ongoing foreign pressure; dynasty struggling to survive.",
  "Qing Empire|1860": "Anglo-French forces invade; internal rebellions persist; dynasty survives only via delegated regional armies",
  "Qing Empire|1864": "Pyrrhic recovery; immense casualties (20–30 million); structural transformation of dynasty; Tongzhi Restoration begins.",
  "Qing Empire|1875": "Industrial modernization and limited reforms under Guangxu/Cixi; efforts undermined by conservative politics.",
  "Qing Empire|1895": "Taiwan lost; Korea independence effectively ceded; military modernization inadequate; triggers Hundred Days Reform.",
  "Qing Empire|1900": "Beijing occupied by Eight-Nation Alliance; crippling indemnity imposed; dynasty survives only with foreign supervision.",
  "Qing Empire|1908": "Last capable emperor gone; reforms too late; warlordism and revolutionary movements strengthen.",
  "Qing Empire|1911": "Wuchang Uprising sparks provincial secession; Qing dynasty collapses; Puyi abdicates, ending over 2,100 years of imperial China.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|632": "Muhammad dies; Abu Bakr elected first caliph at Saqifah. The nascent caliphate faces widespread revolts across Arabia as many tribes refuse to pay zakat or recognize central authority. Abu Bakr launches the Ridda Wars to reassert control, dividing his forces into eleven corps. The caliphate exists in name but is fighting for survival.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|633": "Victory at the Battle of Yamama and other campaigns fully unites Arabia under Medina. Khalid ibn al-Walid emerges as a brilliant commander. With internal threats neutralized, Abu Bakr begins outward expansion into Syria and Iraq.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|634": "Battle of Ajnadayn secures the Levant against Byzantines. Abu Bakr dies peacefully; Umar ibn al-Khattab becomes caliph, continuing expansion and administrative consolidation.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|636": "Battle of Yarmouk defeats Byzantines; Battle of Qadisiyyah defeats Sassanids. These victories dismantle two superpowers simultaneously, opening the Levant and Mesopotamia to Islamic control.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|637": "Jerusalem surrenders peacefully; Ctesiphon falls after a siege. The Levant and Mesopotamia secured; campaigns into Egypt begin. Administrative systems like the diwan and Bayt al-Mal strengthen governance.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|640": "Egyptian conquest underway; Armenia raided; empire near maximum extent.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|644": "Umar assassinated; Uthman becomes caliph. Expansion continues — Persia fully annexed, Armenia and parts of North Africa added. Standardization of the Quran begins",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|650": "Territorial maximum achieved: Libya, Egypt, Levant, Mesopotamia, Persia, parts of Central Asia and Sindh. Administrative and religious structures fully consolidated.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|656": "Uthman murdered due to nepotism grievances; Ali becomes caliph but authority contested by Muawiya and Aisha’s faction. Civil war erupts.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|657": "Battle of Siffin ends in inconclusive arbitration; Kharijites secede. Muawiya proclaims himself caliph in Syria, controlling Egypt. Ali’s domain shrinks to Iraq.",
  "Rashidun Caliphate|661": "Ali assassinated; Hasan abdicates under Muawiya’s pressure. Umayyad Caliphate (661–750) established; Rashidun Caliphate ends. Sunni-Shia split crystallized",
  "Roman Empire|-27": "Octavian becomes Augustus, founding the Principate while preserving republican forms. Civil wars end, stability returns, and large-scale reforms begin (roads, administration, economy). The empire is strong but still dependent on Augustus personally.",
  "Roman Empire|9": "Three legions destroyed by Germanic forces under Arminius. Rome halts expansion beyond the Rhine. Despite the shock, internal stability and the Pax Romana continue.",
  "Roman Empire|14": "First peaceful succession proves the system works. Economy strong, frontiers stable, infrastructure thriving.",
  "Roman Empire|69": "Civil war erupts after Nero’s death; four emperors rise and fall in one year. Vespasian restores stability and finances.",
  "Roman Empire|117": "Empire reaches maximum extent (~5 million km²), from Britain to Mesopotamia. Population and economic output at global high levels.",
  "Roman Empire|165": "Pandemic spreads across empire, killing millions; Germanic pressure increases. Despite this, institutions remain strong under Marcus Aurelius",
  "Roman Empire|180": "End of Pax Romana; Commodus succeeds. Empire still strong structurally, but decline begins due to poor leadership.",
  "Roman Empire|235": "Severus Alexander assassinated; decades of chaos follow. Civil wars, economic collapse, plagues, and fragmentation weaken the empire.",
  "Roman Empire|284": "Diocletian stabilizes the empire with the Tetrarchy, military expansion, and administrative reforms. Damage from the crisis remains significant.",
  "Roman Empire|312": "Constantine reunifies the West and legalizes Christianity. Currency reforms stabilize the economy.",
  "Roman Empire|330": "New eastern capital strengthens defense and trade. Empire is reorganized and relatively stable again",
  "Roman Empire|378": "Eastern army destroyed by Visigoths; major structural blow. Barbarian groups settle within the empire.",
  "Roman Empire|395": "Empire splits into Eastern and Western halves after Theodosius I’s death. East remains strong; West begins steady decline",
  "Roman Empire|410": "Rome sacked for the first time in centuries; symbolic collapse of authority. Western Empire losing control rapidly.",
  "Roman Empire|455": "Second sack; loss of North Africa cripples economy. Western Empire reduced to fragments.",
  "Roman Empire|476": "Romulus Augustulus deposed by Odoacer. Western Empire formally ends; Eastern (Byzantine) Empire continues.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1684": "Dombo defeats a combined Mutapa–Portuguese force at the Battle of Maungwe, asserting independence and founding the Rozvi state. Backed by a disciplined army built through cattle-based patronage, he demonstrates clear military superiority over both his former suzerain and European allies. He begins expanding toward Manyika and asserting tribute over Portuguese traders.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1696": "Through sustained campaigns in the 1680s–1690s, Dombo drives Portuguese forces out of the interior and unifies the Zimbabwe plateau. Key urban centers like Danangombe, Naletale, and Manyanga come under Rozvi control. Portuguese influence is reduced to the coast.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1700": "Dombo’s successors maintain territorial control and extend influence over the Mutapa state by installing client rulers. Trade in gold and ivory continues to flow through established networks, and the empire faces no serious external threats.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1720": "Chirisamuru inaugurates a long era of peace and administrative stability. Tribute systems function effectively, trade networks expand, and religious unity is reinforced through the Mwari cult centered at the Matopos shrines. Stone-built centers flourish across the plateau.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1750": "Mid-reign of Chirisamuru marks the zenith of Rozvi power. The empire reaches maximum territorial extent and influence, with strong control over trade routes and regional politics. Cultural and architectural achievements peak, and no external threats challenge its dominance.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1788": "After a remarkably long reign, Chirisamuru’s death introduces succession uncertainty. Internal palace struggles emerge, while environmental pressures like drought begin to strain the agrarian economy. External groups also test Rozvi defenses.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1800": "A severe drought (1795–1800) disrupts agriculture and cattle wealth, undermining the patronage system. At the same time, palace revolts and declining demand for gold weaken central authority. Trade networks shift, and external raids increase.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1820": "Nguni groups begin sustained raids into Rozvi territory, while internal divisions deepen. Although the empire still resists and repels some attacks, it suffers losses in manpower and cohesion. A second drought compounds the crisis.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1831": "Multiple Nguni invasions devastate Rozvi centers of power. Leadership weakens significantly, and effective control shrinks to a small core under Chirisamhuru II. Administrative and military systems largely break down.",
  "Rozvi Empire|1836": "Chirisamhuru II is killed, ending centralized Rozvi rule. No effective successor emerges, and the state disintegrates. Subsequent Ndebele expansion definitively replaces Rozvi political authority.",
  "Russian Empire|1721": "Following victory in the Great Northern War, Peter I proclaims the Russian Empire after the Treaty of Nystad. Russia gains key Baltic territories, securing vital access to European trade routes. Major reforms—military modernisation, naval construction, subordination of the Orthodox Church, and founding of St. Petersburg—transform Russia into a recognised European great power.",
  "Russian Empire|1760": "During the Seven Years’ War, Russian forces demonstrate continental strength by briefly occupying Berlin. Cultural and educational growth continues with institutions like Moscow University. However, limited territorial expansion and the abrupt policy reversal under Peter III reveal political fragility at the top.",
  "Russian Empire|1762": "Catherine II begins a transformative reign after overthrowing Peter III. Early successes include victories in the Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774) and the First Partition of Poland. She promotes Enlightenment ideals, patronises culture, and strengthens administration.",
  "Russian Empire|1796": "By Catherine’s death, Russia has massively expanded—annexing Crimea, partitioning Poland, and extending into the Caucasus. The empire now stretches across Europe and into North America (Alaska), with greatly enhanced political and cultural influence.",
  "Russian Empire|1800": "Despite Paul I’s erratic rule and eventual assassination, the empire retains its vast territorial gains and military strength. Administrative structures remain intact, and succession passes to Alexander I.",
  "Russian Empire|1815": "After defeating Napoleon Bonaparte, Russia emerges as a dominant European power at the Congress of Vienna. It gains Poland, influences European politics, and leads the Holy Alliance.",
  "Russian Empire|1856": "The Crimean War exposes Russia’s military and technological weaknesses. Defeat leads to the Treaty of Paris, limiting naval power in the Black Sea and damaging prestige.",
  "Russian Empire|1870": "The Great Reforms modernise Russia: serf emancipation, judicial restructuring, and military reform. Expansion into Central Asia continues, and Russia regains lost prestige after the Crimean setback.",
  "Russian Empire|1885": "The empire reaches its largest territorial size, spanning Eastern Europe, Central Asia, and Siberia. Stability under Alexander III of Russia and early industrialisation efforts strengthen internal power.",
  "Russian Empire|1905": "Defeat in the Russo-Japanese War and domestic unrest lead to the 1905 Revolution. The October Manifesto introduces limited constitutional reforms.",
  "Russian Empire|1914": "Entry into World War I exposes deep structural weaknesses—poor leadership under Nicholas II of Russia, logistical failures, and social unrest.",
  "Russian Empire|1917": "The February Revolution forces Nicholas II’s abdication, ending the Romanov dynasty. Later that year, the October Revolution brings the Bolsheviks to power, replacing the empire with a new political system.",
  "Safavid Empire|1501": "Ismail I captures Tabriz after defeating the Aq Qoyunlu and proclaims himself Shah, founding the Safavid Empire. He enforces Twelver Shi‘ism as the state religion, reshaping Iran’s identity and distinguishing it from Sunni rivals like the Ottomans and Uzbeks. Rapid conquests unify much of Iran and Iraq.",
  "Safavid Empire|1510": "Ismail defeats and kills Muhammad Shaybani Khan, ending major Uzbek threats in Khorasan and securing eastern territories like Herat. This marks his greatest territorial extent before facing the Ottomans",
  "Safavid Empire|1514": "Selim I decisively defeats the Safavids using firearms and artillery. Tabriz is briefly occupied, and key western territories are lost. The myth of Ismail’s invincibility collapses, weakening internal cohesion.",
  "Safavid Empire|1524": "Ismail’s death leaves the throne to the young Tahmasp I. Qizilbash factions dominate politics, leading to internal conflict, while Ottomans and Uzbeks exploit the instability with repeated invasions.",
  "Safavid Empire|1550": "Tahmasp gradually consolidates authority, stabilising the empire during his long reign. He modernises the army with firearms and defends against external threats.",
  "Safavid Empire|1555": "The treaty establishes a long-term border with the Ottomans, reducing conflict. Safavids retain core Persian lands while conceding Iraq and Anatolia.",
  "Safavid Empire|1576": "After Tahmasp’s death, weak rulers and factional conflict trigger rapid decline. Ottoman and Uzbek invasions reclaim lost territories, and central authority weakens significantly.",
  "Safavid Empire|1588": "Abbas inherits a collapsing empire and temporarily concedes territory to the Ottomans to stabilise the state. He begins major military and administrative reforms.",
  "Safavid Empire|1598": "Abbas defeats the Uzbeks, regains Khorasan, and shifts the capital to Isfahan, launching a cultural and economic renaissance. His new standing army reduces reliance on tribal forces.",
  "Safavid Empire|1600": "With reforms complete, Abbas begins campaigns to reclaim western territories. Military strength and administrative centralisation position the empire for its peak.",
  "Safavid Empire|1620": "The empire reaches maximum territorial extent, controlling Iran, Iraq, the Caucasus, and key trade routes. Victories over Ottomans and Portuguese (Hormuz) secure dominance. Isfahan becomes a global cultural center.",
  "Safavid Empire|1629": "Despite peak power, Abbas leaves no capable successor due to purges of heirs. Shah Safi inherits a strong but vulnerable system.",
  "Safavid Empire|1638": "Ottomans retake Baghdad permanently. The treaty fixes borders but marks the start of irreversible territorial contraction.",
  "Safavid Empire|1650": "A capable ruler temporarily restores stability and military effectiveness, even reclaiming Kandahar briefly. However, deeper institutional decline persists.",
  "Safavid Empire|1666": "After Abbas II’s death, weak leadership leads to administrative decay, economic problems, and military stagnation",
  "Safavid Empire|1694": "Religious extremism and weak governance alienate key populations, especially Sunni Afghans. Peripheral regions begin breaking away.",
  "Safavid Empire|1700": "Despite structural weakness, the empire still formally controls its territory. However, military and administrative effectiveness are severely reduced.",
  "Safavid Empire|1722": "Afghan forces under Mahmud Hotak capture Isfahan after a devastating siege. The shah surrenders, effectively ending central authority.",
  "Safavid Empire|1736": "Nader Shah formally deposes the Safavid dynasty after restoring order and defeating foreign enemies. He establishes a new dynasty.",
  "Sasanian Empire|224": "Ardashir I defeats Artabanus IV at the Battle of Hormozdgan and establishes the Sasanian Empire. He centralises authority, promotes Zoroastrianism as the state religion, and expands into eastern regions. However, resistance from Parthian elites and external threats from Rome remain strong.",
  "Sasanian Empire|260": "At the Battle of Edessa, Shapur I captures Valerian—a unique humiliation for Rome. The empire reaches vast territorial extent and demonstrates superpower-level military strength.",
  "Sasanian Empire|300": "A series of weak rulers leads to internal instability and loss of territory to Rome, culminating in the Treaty of Nisibis.",
  "Sasanian Empire|350": "Shapur II restores Sasanian strength, defeating Rome and reclaiming lost territories after Emperor Julian’s failed campaign. Eastern threats are also neutralised.",
  "Sasanian Empire|400": "Under rulers like Yazdegerd I, the empire enjoys stability, economic productivity, and relative peace with Rome. Religious tolerance improves internal cohesion.",
  "Sasanian Empire|500": "Kavad I’s reign sees major upheaval from the Mazdakite movement and external pressure from the Hephthalites. Despite setbacks, he initiates reforms that strengthen the state.",
  "Sasanian Empire|550": "Khosrow I brings administrative, military, and cultural excellence. Tax reforms, army reorganisation, and intellectual centers like Gundishapur flourish. The empire reaches maximum prosperity and stability.",
  "Sasanian Empire|600": "The empire remains strong early in Khosrow II’s reign, benefiting from earlier reforms and stable institutions.",
  "Sasanian Empire|615": "Sasanian forces conquer vast Byzantine territories, including Egypt and the Levant, even threatening Constantinople.",
  "Sasanian Empire|628": "After defeat by Heraclius at the Battle of Nineveh, Khosrow II is overthrown and killed. Rapid succession crises and plague destroy central authority.",
  "Sasanian Empire|636": "Rashidun forces defeat the main Sasanian army and capture Ctesiphon. Leadership and military structure collapse.",
  "Sasanian Empire|651": "Yazdegerd III is killed at Merv, ending the Sasanian dynasty. The empire is absorbed into the expanding Islamic Caliphate.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-312": "In 312 BCE, Seleucus, a former general of Alexander the Great, reclaims Babylon after years in exile, marking the traditional start of the Seleucid Era. Though a significant comeback, his position remains fragile. Rival Antigonus I Monophthalmus quickly attempts to dislodge him but fails. Seleucus consolidates local support and begins rebuilding authority in Mesopotamia. However, this is still an early foothold rather than a full empire, with constant military threats and limited territorial control.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-305": "In 305 BCE, Seleucus formally assumes the royal title (basileus), signaling his transition from satrap to monarch. His campaign against Chandragupta Maurya ends in a strategic treaty: Seleucus cedes eastern territories in exchange for 500 war elephants. These elephants later prove decisive at the Battle of Ipsus. His empire now stretches across vast regions from Anatolia to Central Asia, though still shaped by ongoing wars among the Diadochi and lacking stable administrative depth.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-301": "At the Battle of Ipsus, Seleucus joins a coalition that defeats and kills Antigonus, eliminating the strongest rival to Alexander’s legacy. His war elephants play a decisive role. As a result, Seleucus gains Syria and large eastern territories, forming the largest Hellenistic empire. He establishes key cities like Antioch and Seleucia as administrative centers. Despite this immense expansion, governance structures are still developing, and cohesion across such a vast domain remains a challenge.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-281": "At the Battle of Corupedium, Seleucus defeats Lysimachus, gaining control over most of Anatolia and effectively becoming the dominant successor of Alexander. He is on the verge of reclaiming even Macedon. However, this triumph is immediately undercut when he is assassinated by Ptolemy Ceraunus. His sudden death introduces instability just as the empire reaches peak territorial extent, leaving consolidation incomplete.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-280": "Antiochus I inherits one of the largest empires of the Hellenistic world, stretching from Anatolia to Central Asia. He successfully repels external threats like the Galatian invasion and maintains control over key regions. The empire benefits from a blend of Greek and Persian administrative systems, making it relatively sophisticated. However, early signs of fragmentation appear in distant eastern satrapies, and constant pressure from rivals like Ptolemaic Egypt begins to strain resources.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-250": "Around 250 BCE, the Seleucid Empire begins to visibly weaken. During the Third Syrian War, internal dynastic conflict allows Ptolemy III Euergetes to invade deep into Seleucid territory. More critically, eastern satraps declare independence: Diodotus I forms the Greco-Bactrian Kingdom, and Arsaces I establishes Parthia. These regions are never recovered, marking a permanent loss of wealth and manpower.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-223": "When Antiochus III takes power, the empire is at a low point. Key territories like Coele-Syria are lost, eastern regions are independent, and internal rebellions weaken central authority. His early defeat at the Battle of Raphia against Egypt highlights this weakness. However, Antiochus begins laying the groundwork for recovery through military campaigns and internal consolidation, setting the stage for a later resurgence.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-200": "By 200 BCE, Antiochus III achieves a remarkable revival. His eastern campaigns reassert control over lost territories, including Parthia and Bactria, while victory in the Battle of Panium secures Coele-Syria from Egypt. He even expands into Thrace, bringing the empire to its greatest extent since Seleucus I. His achievements earn him the title “the Great,” placing him among the most successful Hellenistic rulers.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-190": "After intervening in Greece, Antiochus miscalculates Roman strength. In 190 BCE, despite numerical superiority, his army is crushed by Roman forces, marking Rome’s first major campaign into Asia. The resulting Treaty of Apamea forces the Seleucids to surrender all territories north of the Taurus Mountains to Rome’s allies and pay massive indemnities. The empire loses its Anatolian core and strategic depth, becoming confined to Syria and Mesopotamia.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-175": "A decisive and irreversible geopolitical setback that permanently reduces Seleucid power.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-150": "By the mid-2nd century BCE, Mithridates I of Parthia captures Seleucia on the Tigris, the empire’s eastern capital. All territories east of the Euphrates are permanently lost, including Mesopotamia and Babylonia. Attempts at recovery fail: Demetrius II Nicator is captured, and Antiochus VII Sidetes dies in a final failed reconquest.The seleucid Empire is now reduced to a weakened Syrian core,losing its economic and political heartlands.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-100": "By this stage, the Seleucid state is crippled by continuous civil wars and rival claimants such as Seleucus VI Epiphanes, Antiochus XI Epiphanes, and Philip I Philadelphus. Multiple kings rule simultaneously over fragmented territories in northern Syria, with no central authority. The kingdom has shrunk into a minor regional state, unable to project power or maintain stability.A dysfunctional remnant of what was once a vast empire.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-83": "Amid ongoing civil wars, Seleucid factions invite Tigranes II of Armenia, who instead annexes the entire Syrian domain. For the next 14 years, the Seleucid dynasty ceases to function as a ruling power, surviving only through exiled claimants. Syria becomes part of a larger Armenian empire.The Seleucid state effectively disappears, though the dynasty technically survives.",
  "Seleucid Empire|-63": "After defeating Tigranes, Rome restores order in Syria but does not reinstate Seleucid independence. In 64–63 BCE, Pompey the Great reorganizes the region and formally annexes Syria as a Roman province. Final Seleucid claimants are removed, ending the dynasty nearly 250 years after its founding.The Seleucid Empire ceases to exist entirely, absorbed into Roman imperial structure without resistance.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1037": "Tughril and his brother Chaghri Beg, descendants of Seljuk Beg, consolidated power in Transoxiana before seizing Merv and Nishapur (1037–1038), declaring independence from the Ghaznavid Empire. Despite this breakthrough, their rule remained fragile: limited territorial control, no Abbasid recognition, constant Ghaznavid threat, and a largely tribal, nomadic power structure.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1040": "On 23 May 1040, Tughril and Chaghri crushed Mas'ud I of Ghazni, ending Ghaznavid dominance in Khorasan. This victory transformed the Seljuks into a regional power. Crucially, they adopted Persian administrative practices, establishing a functioning bureaucracy. By 1046, al-Qa'im formally recognised their rule.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1046": "Formal recognition by Caliph al-Qa’im elevated Tughril’s legitimacy within the Islamic world. Simultaneously, Seljuk military reach expanded: Ibrahim Yinal defeated a Byzantine-Georgian force at the Battle of Kapetrou, signaling growing influence beyond Muslim territories. Khorasan became economically productive under Persian administration, and the Seljuks began advancing toward Iraq.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1055": "In December 1055, Tughril entered Baghdad, overthrowing the Buyids and restoring Sunni Abbasid authority. Caliph al-Qa’im granted him the title of Sultan, formalising Seljuk political supremacy. This marked a transformation from regional power to dominant force in the Islamic world. However, governance structures were still developing, and succession remained uncertain.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1063": "After Tughril’s death, Alp Arslan secured power with the support of Nizam al-Mulk, whose administrative reforms strengthened the empire. Expansion accelerated: the capture of Ani (1064) demonstrated growing military strength. Diplomatic ties with Georgia further stabilised the frontier.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1071": "Alp Arslan defeated and captured Romanos IV Diogenes, a historic shock to Byzantine power. Though the emperor was released, Anatolia’s defenses collapsed, allowing rapid Turkic settlement across the region. This fundamentally reshaped the demographic and political future of Anatolia.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1090": "Under Malik Shah and Nizam al-Mulk, the empire reached its maximum territorial and institutional strength, spanning from Anatolia to Central Asia. A sophisticated bureaucracy operated from Isfahan, while the Nizamiyya madrasas shaped Islamic scholarship. Scientific and cultural achievements flourished, including the Jalali calendar reform.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1092": "The deaths of Nizam al-Mulk and Malik Shah within weeks shattered central authority. Rival heirs, including Barkiyaruq and Ahmad Sanjar, plunged the empire into civil war. Though territory remained intact, governance disintegrated.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1100": "Internal clash prevented coordinated resistance as Crusaders captured key cities like Jerusalem and Antioch. Regional fragmentation intensified: Syria split among rival rulers, and the Sultanate of Rum emerged independently.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1118": "Sanjar unified much of the remaining empire and reasserted authority in Khorasan and beyond. He achieved military successes against the Ghaznavids and maintained regional stability. However, western losses—especially in Syria and Anatolia—remained permanent.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1135": "Sanjar briefly reasserted influence over western Seljuk territories, restoring a semblance of imperial cohesion. Khorasan remained prosperous, and vassal states acknowledged his authority. Yet structural weaknesses persisted, and key regions like Anatolia and the Levant were irretrievably lost.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1141": "Sanjar’s defeat by the Qara Khitai destroyed Seljuk dominance in Transoxiana. Territories east of the Syr Darya were lost, and vassals rebelled. The empire’s military prestige collapsed, and Khorasan came under pressure.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1150": "By mid-century, Sanjar controlled little beyond Khorasan. Western Seljuk lands functioned independently, while Khwarazm and other forces grew stronger. Internal instability and external raids further weakened the empire.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1153": "Defeated by rebellious Oghuz Turks, Sanjar was taken prisoner, leaving the region leaderless. Major cities like Nishapur and Merv were sacked, and economic infrastructure destroyed.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1157": "Sanjar’s death marked the ending of any unified Seljuk authority. Former territories fragmented among regional powers, including the Ghurids and Khwarazmians. No successor could restore central control.",
  "Seljuk Empire|1194": "Defeated and killed by Ala al-Din Tekish, the last Seljuk ruler in Persia was eliminated. The Khwarazmian Empire annexed remaining territories. Only the Anatolian branch survived temporarily.",
  "Sikh Empire|1799": "At just 19, Ranjit Singh seized Lahore after the retreat of Zaman Shah Durrani, ending the control of the Sikh confederacy factions. Though confirmed as governor, his authority remained limited to his own Sukerchakia Misl and the capital. The wider Punjab was still divided among rival Misls, and no formal monarchy yet existed.",
  "Sikh Empire|1801": "On Baisakhi (12 April 1801), Ranjit Singh was crowned Maharaja by Sahib Singh Bedi, linking his rule to the legacy of Guru Nanak. He began systematically absorbing remaining Misls and centralising authority. The Treaty of Amritsar with the British East India Company fixed the Sutlej boundary, securing the southern frontier while limiting expansion eastward.",
  "Sikh Empire|1810": "By the early 1810s, Ranjit Singh had effectively dismantled the Misl system and unified the Sikh polity. His authority now extended across the Punjab heartland, creating a cohesive state. The acquisition of the Koh-i-Noor diamond symbolised rising prestige. However, frontier regions like Multan, Kashmir, and Peshawar were still under Afghan control and remained key military objectives.",
  "Sikh Empire|1820": "Following the capture of Multan (1818) and Kashmir (1819), the empire gained critical economic and military advantages. Commanders like Hari Singh Nalwa led successful campaigns. The army was modernised along European lines under officers such as Allard and Ventura, forming the disciplined Fauj-i-Ain.",
  "Sikh Empire|1823": "Ranjit Singh’s forces defeated Afghan resistance and took Peshawar, extending control to the empire’s natural western à¤¸à¥à¤®à¤¾ at the Khyber. This marked the strategic completion of northwestern expansion. Remaining gains, such as Ladakh, were still to come, but the empire had achieved its defining territorial identity.",
  "Sikh Empire|1835": "By the mid-1830s, the Sikh Empire stretched from the Khyber Pass to the Sutlej and from Tibet to Sindh. Ladakh and Baltistan had been incorporated. The army, nearly 100,000 strong, was highly modernised with advanced artillery. Ranjit Singh maintained a careful balance with both Afghans and the British.",
  "Sikh Empire|1839": "Ranjit Singh died on 27 June 1839, triggering immediate succession struggles. His son Kharak Singh proved ineffective, while Nau Nihal Singh died under suspicious circumstances. Rival factions like the Dogras and Sindhanwalias began power struggles.",
  "Sikh Empire|1841": "Sher Singh took the throne with support from Dhian Singh Dogra. Although borders remained intact, central authority weakened significantly. The Khalsa army expanded uncontrollably, becoming a à¦°à¦¾à¦à¦¨à§à¦¤à¦¿à¦ force rather than a disciplined institution. Sher Singh’s assassination in 1843 further destabilised governance.",
  "Sikh Empire|1845": "Despite a large army, internal betrayal and weak leadertship led to defeat. The Treaty of Lahore imposed heavy penalties: territorial loss, army reduction, indemnities, and British oversight. The subsequent Treaty of Bhairowal effectively made Punjab a British-controlled state.",
  "Sikh Empire|1849": "After the Second Anglo-Sikh War, British forces secured final victory. Duleep Singh was deposed, and Punjab was annexed by the British Empire. The Koh-i-Noor diamond was taken to Britain.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1804": "Usman dan Fodio declares jihad after Sultan Yunfa attempts assassination. He leads followers in a hijra to Gudu in February 1804, is elected Commander of the Faithful, declares jihad against the Hausa kings, and defeats Gobir at the Battle of Koto. A revolutionary movement at its inception, with military momentum but not yet a functioning state.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1808": "Jihad expansion achieves core territorial victories: Kebbi (1805), Katsina and Daura (1806–07), Kano and Zaria (1807–09). Caliphate organised around a network of 30+ emirates pledging allegiance to Sokoto. Dan Fodio organises administration on the model of Medinan governance. A fully established caliphate still absorbing peripheral territories and stabilising institutions.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1810": "Eastward and southward expansion reaches toward Bornu and Yorubaland. The Adamawa emirate is established under Modibo Adama; Fulani conquest of Zaria completed (1809). Islamic scholarship flourishes — dan Fodio, his son Bello, and daughter Nana Asma'u are all prolific writers in Arabic, Hausa, and Fulfulde. Caliphate powerful and expanding but still maturing.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1815": "Dan Fodio retires from direct governance around 1815, dividing authority between his son Muhammad Bello and his brother Abdullahi dan Fodio (Emir of Gwandu). Division proves administratively stable rather than destabilising. The emirate system is now fully operational, generating zakat revenues, supporting courts, schools, and mosques across the savanna.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1837": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — by the end of Bello's rule (he dies in 1837 after 20 years as Sultan), the Caliphate has reached its maximum: BlackPast confirms it had grown to be the most populous empire in West Africa, with a population of 10–20 million across more than 30 emirates spanning over one million square kilometres from Burkina Faso to northern Cameroon.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1850": "Post-Bello era — a succession of less distinguished sultans governs. The caliphate remains the dominant power of the West African savanna and its institutional structure is intact. The emirate system continues to function, Islamic scholarship remains vibrant, and trans-Saharan trade routes through Hausaland are active. A still-powerful state in a consolidation/plateau phase.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1870": "Caliphate under Sultan Ahmadu Atiku and successors remains the paramount power of northern Nigeria. No European threat has yet materialised; emirates function under Sharia law, the zakat system collects revenues, and the ribats (border fortresses) guard the periphery. Stable and sovereign but no longer expanding, beginning to show centrifugal strains.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1890": "BlackPast confirms: \"The empire built by dan Fodio began to disintegrate by the 1890s. Internal rivalry brought civil war while European colonial armies began encroaching on the periphery.\" France conquers Ségou in 1890; the Royal Niger Company pushes northward. The caliphate is sovereign but under acute dual pressure of internal fragmentation and closing European encirclement.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1900": "Frederick Lugard proclaims the Northern Nigeria Protectorate in January 1900, asserting British sovereignty without caliphal consent. Caliph Abdurrahman dismisses the messenger, declaring war the only terms. Between 1901–02, 300 British troops burn Bida, Kontagora, and Zaria. Kano falls in February 1903. The caliphate loses emirate after emirate to a technologically superior force.",
  "Sokoto Caliphate|1903": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — British forces defeat the Sokoto army on 15 March 1903. Caliph Attahiru I refuses surrender, leads followers on a Mahdist hijra eastward, and is killed at the Battle of Burmi on 27 July 1903. Lugard installs Muhammadu Attahiru II as a puppet sultan. The caliphate is partitioned between Britain, France, and Germany. Sovereign existence ends.",
  "Song Dynasty|960": "Military commander Zhao Kuangyin stages the Chenqiao Mutiny on 3 February 960 and is proclaimed Emperor Taizu. China remains divided among eight other successor kingdoms; the powerful Khitan Liao dynasty holds the Sixteen Prefectures. A dynasty that has just been proclaimed but has not yet conquered a single rival kingdom; all empire-building remains ahead.",
  "Song Dynasty|976": "Taizong continues the reunification campaign: Southern Tang falls in 975, Wu-Yue submits in 978, Northern Han is defeated in 979, reunifying China for the first time in over 70 years. However, Taizong's immediate attempt to retake the Sixteen Prefectures from the Liao ends in complete failure at the Battle of Gaoliang River — the emperor flees in a donkey cart.",
  "Song Dynasty|1000": "Song economy enters what scholars call the most spectacular phase of growth in Chinese history: population grows to 60+ million, iron production reaches levels not seen again until the Industrial Revolution, and paper currency circulates in Sichuan. The Chanyuan Treaty of January 1005 ends twenty-five years of Song-Liao warfare, securing a century of peace in exchange for annual tribute.",
  "Song Dynasty|1044": "Renzong's 41-year reign is considered the apogee of Song stability. The Qingli Reform (1043–45) — a comprehensive institutional programme by Fan Zhongyan — signals the depth of Song intellectual culture. War with the Western Xia kingdom (1038–44) ends with the Treaty of Qingyuan, by which Song pays costly additional annual tribute to the Xia in exchange for peace.",
  "Song Dynasty|1050": "Northern Song at peak stability with the Commercial Revolution in full bloom. Paper currency in widespread use; iron production approaching proto-industrial levels; cities of 50,000+ are common (London at this date has approximately 15,000 people). The Silk Road and maritime trade networks link Song ports from Quanzhou to India, the Persian Gulf, and East Africa.",
  "Song Dynasty|1076": "Emperor Shenzong appoints Wang Anshi as Chancellor in 1069, launching the most ambitious reform programme in Song history. By 1076 state revenues reach their all-time peak — non-agricultural tax revenues from commerce and monopolies now exceed land taxes. But the reform-vs.-conservative political split was tearing the scholar-official class apart, political polarisation proving as damaging as any external enemy.",
  "Song Dynasty|1100": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Northern Song at its greatest territorial extent in 1111. Emperor Huizong is a brilliant artist-emperor but catastrophically poor political ruler. By 1100, Kaifeng has a population exceeding one million (when London has approximately 15,000–20,000); the Song economy is the most sophisticated in the world. Corruption and the catastrophic \"Alliance Conducted at Sea\" with the Jurchen Jin was about to destroy everything.",
  "Song Dynasty|1127": "Jurchen Jin forces sack Kaifeng on 9 January 1127. Emperors Huizong and Qinzong are captured along with approximately 15,000 courtiers, officials, and imperial family members and marched north as prisoners. Zhao Gou — the ninth son of Huizong — fled south and proclaimed himself Emperor Gaozong, pursued by Jin armies as far as the Zhejiang coast and islands.",
  "Song Dynasty|1142": "The Treaty of Shaoxing (ratified 1141, formalised 1142) fixes the border at the Huai River, surrenders all recovered territory, requires Song to accept vassal status vis-à-vis the Jin, and imposes annual tribute of 250,000 taels of silver and 250,000 bolts of silk. General Yue Fei is murdered in prison. Genuine recovery but vassalage to Jin and the shadow of Yue Fei's murder overshadow political culture.",
  "Song Dynasty|1150": "Southern Song adapts remarkably well to loss of the north. The Yangtze delta's irrigation supports intensive wet-rice agriculture; Champa rice allows multiple harvests per year; maritime trade replaces lost Silk Road revenues. Hangzhou grows from a provincial city to a metropolis of perhaps 500,000–1,000,000 by 1200 — Marco Polo would later call it \"the greatest city in the world.\"",
  "Song Dynasty|1200": "Under Ningzong, the Southern Song reaches something approaching its maximum internal development. Population peaks at 80.6 million by 1223 as 5 million northern refugees migrate south. Hangzhou's cultural and economic life is at its zenith; neo-Confucian philosophy has transformed Chinese intellectual life. Military weakness is structural, the north cannot be recovered, and the Mongol threat will become existential within decades.",
  "Song Dynasty|1234": "The Mongol Empire destroys the Jin dynasty with nominal Song assistance in 1234. Song forces briefly reoccupy the former imperial capitals of Kaifeng, Luoyang, and Chang'an. However, when Song generals attempt to hold these cities, the Mongols drive them out, and the Song's unauthorised reoccupation of Jin territory is used as a pretext for war. The Mongol-Song border is now direct.",
  "Song Dynasty|1258": "Great Khan Möngke personally leads two-thirds of the Mongol army into Sichuan in 1258. His death during the siege of Diaoyucheng in Chongqing on 11 August 1259 — one of the only battles in which a Mongol Great Khan died in combat — temporarily halts the invasion. The Mongols begin besieging Xiangyang (controlling the Han River approach to the Yangtze) in 1267, a siege that lasts five years.",
  "Song Dynasty|1276": "Xiangyang falls to the Mongols in February 1273 after a six-year siege, made possible by Muslim counterweight trebuchets supplied by Iraqi engineers which outranged Song defensive artillery. The Yangtze lies open. The Mongol general Bayan sweeps through southern China; Empress Dowager Xie surrenders the underage Emperor Gongzong and the imperial seal in February 1276. A remnant court flees to ships off the coast.",
  "Song Dynasty|1279": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — On 19 March 1279, the Song fleet of approximately 1,000 warships chained together in the Yamen inlet is destroyed by a smaller but more manoeuvrable Mongol-Yuan fleet. The Yuan navy uses fire attacks then direct assault to destroy the immobile formation. As the last resistance collapses, the loyalist official Lu Xiufu embraces the eight-year-old Emperor Bing of Song and leaps into the sea, drowning both.",
  "Songhai Empire|1430": "The Songhai people established themselves in Gao around 800 CE. From 1325 to 1375, Mali conquered Gao and incorporated it into the Mali Empire. By the early 15th century the Songhai under the Sonni dynasty have reasserted nominal independence, but remain hemmed in by the declining Mali Empire, Mossi raiders to the south, and Tuareg pressure from the north (who actually occupy Timbuktu from 1433).",
  "Songhai Empire|1464": "Around 1464 Sunni Ali (Ali Ber) escapes Malian political detention and seizes Gao, inaugurating the expansion phase. He immediately shifts to \"a more sustained campaign of permanent territorial expansion\" (World History Encyclopedia). His army — equipped with armoured cavalry and the only riverine naval fleet in the region — is immediately effective. He repulses Mossi raids, defeats the Dogon and Fulani, and in 1468 evicts the Tuareg from Timbuktu.",
  "Songhai Empire|1468": "Sunni Ali's conquest of Timbuktu in 1468 is the pivotal early milestone — Timbuktu is the intellectual and commercial capital of the entire western Sudan, home to the Sankore mosque-university and the nexus of trans-Saharan trade. Its capture transforms Songhai from a local power into a contender for regional hegemony. Sunni Ali also begins his legendary seven-year siege of Djenné, the major inland market city.",
  "Songhai Empire|1480": "By 1480 Sunni Ali has absorbed the rump of the old Mali Empire, conquered Timbuktu and Djenné, repulsed Mossi attacks on Walata, and established a standing army with both infantry and cavalry. The Tarikh al-Sudan records that he \"waged 32 wars of which he won every one, always the conqueror, never the conquered.\" His Niger River fleet gives him logistical dominance over the 500-mile corridor from Gao to Djenné.",
  "Songhai Empire|1493": "Sunni Ali dies in 1492 reportedly drowned crossing a flooded stream. His son Sonni Baru reigns less than a year before Askia Muhammad — one of Sunni Ali's generals — defeats him at the Battle of Anfao (April 1493) on the grounds that Sonni Baru is \"not a faithful Muslim.\" Askia Muhammad then forms the first fully professional standing army in Songhai history and embarks on extensive campaigns to the south, west, and east.",
  "Songhai Empire|1510": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — by c. 1510 the Songhai Empire reaches its greatest historical size: from the Atlantic coast (Senegal-Gambia) in the west, east to the Hausa states, and north to the Saharan salt mines of Taghaza. Leo Africanus visits and describes Gao's enormously wealthy merchant class. Askia Muhammad has centralised administration, implemented universal weights and measures, appointed qadis to all major trading cities, and made the hajj to Mecca.",
  "Songhai Empire|1528": "In 1528, Askia Muhammad's sons revolt and forcibly depose him, installing his son Musa as king. Askia the Great — now elderly and reportedly blind — is exiled to an island in the Niger River. The institutional system he built is still intact and the territory is still nominally the same, but the moral authority of the state is shattered. The succession crisis that will define the next two decades has just been initiated.",
  "Songhai Empire|1540": "Between 1528 and 1549, five kings succeed one another in rapid, often violent succession: Askia Musa (1528–31), Askia Muhammad II (1531–37), Askia Ismail (1537–39), and Askia Issihak I (1539–49). Britannica notes these \"dynastic squabbles\" but also observes that \"the Muslims in the towns continued to act as middlemen in the profitable gold trade\" — meaning the commercial economy persists despite political instability. Borders broadly maintained; no major territorial losses.",
  "Songhai Empire|1560": "Askia Dawud (r. 1549–1582) is the last great Songhai ruler. Britannica explicitly describes his reign as characterised by \"peace and prosperity\" — a marked contrast to the chaos of the preceding two decades. He rules for 33 years, the longest reign since Sunni Ali, provides political stability, and continues to preside over a flourishing trans-Saharan trade. Timbuktu remains one of the world's great centres of Islamic scholarship.",
  "Songhai Empire|1582": "Dawud's death in 1582 ends the last period of stability. His successor Muhammad Bani (r. 1586–88) faces a new external threat: the Saadian sultan Ahmad al-Mansur of Morocco launches a raid on the Songhai-controlled Saharan salt mines at Taghaza. This is a direct challenge to Songhai's economic foundations — Taghaza produces the salt that is the empire's most valuable trade commodity alongside gold. The population of Taghaza responds by abandoning the mines entirely.",
  "Songhai Empire|1591": "In 1591, Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur of Morocco sends an army of approximately 4,000 soldiers across the Sahara under Judar Pasha, equipped with arquebuses and cannon. At the Battle of Tondibi (March 1591), Issihak II's larger Songhai force — reportedly including a stampede of cattle intended to disrupt the Moroccan formation — is routed. Timbuktu and Gao fall in quick succession. The administrative and commercial infrastructure of the empire disintegrates.",
  "Songhai Empire|1600": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — by 1600 the Moroccan-controlled pashalik of Timbuktu dominates the northern Niger region, though Morocco too finds the territory impossible to hold effectively from across the Sahara. The Songhai successor states — fragmented chiefdoms along the middle Niger — have no capacity for revival. The trans-Saharan gold and salt trade that sustained the empire collapses, shifting trade patterns permanently.",
  "Spanish Empire|1492": "The fall of Granada (2 January 1492) ends 780 years of Muslim rule and unites the Iberian Christian kingdoms under a single crown. Columbus departs in August and reaches the Bahamas and Cuba in October. The Treaty of Tordesillas (1494) formalises the New World split with Portugal. The empire at this moment is embryonic — no significant colonies established, no wealth extracted, no administrative apparatus in place.",
  "Spanish Empire|1516": "The 16-year-old Charles of Habsburg inherits Castile, Aragon, Naples, Sicily, Sardinia, the Canary Islands, and the nascent American territories through a chain of dynastic marriages. The Caribbean colonies are now producing revenue, and Hernán Cortés launches his expedition to Mexico from Cuba in 1519. Charles also inherits the Low Countries and in 1519 is elected Holy Roman Emperor. The empire is growing rapidly but has not yet secured its greatest prize.",
  "Spanish Empire|1520": "Charles I (as Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor since 1519) rules over Spain, the Spanish Americas, the Low Countries, the Franche-Comté, Naples, Sicily, Sardinia, and parts of North Africa. Cortés is mid-confrontation with Tenochtitlan — the Aztec capital falls on 13 August 1521. The Castilian War of the Communities (1520–21) is suppressed, consolidating royal authority. The empire is enormously powerful and on the cusp of its American breakthrough.",
  "Spanish Empire|1521": "Cortés completes the conquest of the Aztec Empire on 13 August 1521, with the capture of Emperor Cuauhtémoc after a 93-day siege. The Aztec capital is razed and Mexico City is built atop its ruins as the capital of the Viceroyalty of New Spain. The enormous wealth of the Aztec state is looted and shipped to Spain. The conquest is transformative but its full economic impact will take decades to materialise; the Inca (far wealthier in silver) are not yet reached.",
  "Spanish Empire|1545": "The discovery of the Cerro Rico silver mountain at Potosí (in modern Bolivia) in 1545 is one of the most consequential economic events in world history. Potosí becomes the largest single source of silver in the world, transforming the global economy and funding wars, courts, and infrastructure across Europe and Asia. Philip II (who inherits the throne in 1556) will build his entire imperial strategy around this revenue stream.",
  "Spanish Empire|1556": "Charles V abdicates in 1555–56, splitting the Habsburg inheritance: his brother Ferdinand receives the Holy Roman Empire and Austrian territories, while his son Philip receives Spain, the Spanish Americas, the Low Countries, Milan, Naples, Sicily, and the Philippines (colonisation begins 1565). Britannica describes Philip II as a king under whom \"the Spanish empire attained its greatest power, extent, and influence.\" Silver from Potosí fills the treasury.",
  "Spanish Empire|1570": "By 1570 Philip II has been king for 14 years and the empire is operating at maximum efficiency. The annual silver fleet from the Americas delivers unprecedented revenues. In 1571 the Holy League — financed and largely organised by Philip II — defeats the Ottoman fleet at the Battle of Lepanto (7 October 1571), ending the myth of Ottoman naval invincibility and cementing Spain's dominance over the western Mediterranean.",
  "Spanish Empire|1580": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Philip II claims the Portuguese throne after the death of Cardinal-King Henry of Portugal and defeats a Portuguese army at the Battle of Alcântara (25 August 1580). He becomes Philip I of Portugal in 1581. For the first and only time in history, a single ruler controls both the Spanish and Portuguese global empires simultaneously — all of the Americas (including Brazil), the entire African coast, the sea route to India and China, the Philippines, and vast European territories.",
  "Spanish Empire|1588": "Philip II dispatches 130 ships (the \"Grande y Felicísima Armada\") to invade England, overthrow Elizabeth I, and end English interference in the Spanish Netherlands. The fleet is repulsed by the English navy and catastrophically scattered by storms; more than a third of the ships are lost, along with approximately 20,000 men. The Armada failure ends the possibility of eliminating England as a naval rival, and Philip declares bankruptcy in 1596.",
  "Spanish Empire|1600": "Philip II died on 13 September 1598. Philip III is 20 years old and far less capable, delegating real power to the Duke of Lerma. Spain declared bankruptcy in 1596 (the fifth time under Philip II: 1557, 1560, 1569, 1575, 1596). The Dutch revolt is not suppressed — the Twelve Years' Truce of 1609 will effectively acknowledge Dutch independence in practice. The empire is still vast and silver revenues remain high, but the trajectory is clearly downward.",
  "Spanish Empire|1621": "Philip IV inherits in 1621, just as the Twelve Years' Truce with the Dutch expires and the Thirty Years' War enters its most brutal phase. The Battle of the Downs (1639) sees Dutch admiral Maarten Tromp destroy a Spanish fleet of 77 ships in a neutral English harbour, ending Spanish naval supremacy in the North Sea permanently. The Count-Duke of Olivares attempts sweeping fiscal and military reforms but overtaxes Catalonia and Portugal to breaking point.",
  "Spanish Empire|1640": "On 1 December 1640, Portugal rises in revolt against the Iberian Union and declares independence under John IV of the House of Braganza — the beginning of a war lasting until 1668. In the same year, Catalonia revolts under French protection (the \"Reapers' War\"). The Portuguese revolt removes the vast Portuguese colonial empire from Spanish control: Brazil, the African coast, the Indian Ocean trade routes, Goa, Macao. An irreversible strategic contraction.",
  "Spanish Empire|1648": "The Treaty of Münster (one of the Westphalian treaties) formally recognises the independence of the Dutch Republic after 80 years of war. The Dutch Republic has used the war to seize many Portuguese colonies now nominally under Spanish rule: parts of Brazil, the Cape of Good Hope, Ceylon, and much of the East Indies. France emerges from Westphalia as the new hegemon of continental Europe, displacing Spain. Spanish dominance in Europe is over.",
  "Spanish Empire|1650": "By 1650 every major negative trend is already underway: the Dutch Republic is independent (1648), Portugal is fighting for independence (recognised 1668), Catalonia has revolted, the Thirty Years' War has exhausted Spain, and France under Mazarin is rapidly becoming the dominant European power. Silver production at Potosí is beginning its long-term decline (output peaks around 1600 and falls throughout the 17th century).",
  "Spanish Empire|1700": "Charles II, the last Habsburg king of Spain, dies on 1 November 1700 without heirs. His death triggers the War of the Spanish Succession (1701–14), involving virtually every major European power and devastating Spain itself. Britannica notes: \"Spain's central problem in the 17th century had been to maintain what remained of its European possessions… At the beginning of the 18th century, both tasks appeared to be beyond the military and economic resources of the monarchy.\"",
  "Spanish Empire|1713": "The Peace of Utrecht (April 1713) confirms Philip V as King of Spain — but at enormous cost. Spain cedes: the Spanish Netherlands, Milan, Naples, Sardinia, and Sicily to Austria; Gibraltar and Minorca to Britain (permanently). Spain also grants Britain the Asiento — a monopoly on supplying enslaved Africans to the Spanish colonies — and trading rights. In New World Encyclopedia's words, Spain becomes \"a second-rate nation in Continental politics.\" The Americas remain.",
  "Spanish Empire|1762": "Spain's alliance with France in the Seven Years' War (1756–63) ends disastrously. Britain captures Havana (July 1762) and Manila in the Philippines. Although both cities are returned at the Treaty of Paris (1763), Spain is forced to cede Florida to Britain. Spain compensates by acquiring Louisiana from France. The episode reveals Spanish military weakness and dependence on alliance with France. The core American empire is intact, but Spain cannot defend it independently against Britain.",
  "Spanish Empire|1778": "Under Charles III (r. 1759–88), the Bourbon Reforms represent the most serious attempt to modernise and rationalise the Spanish Empire since the 16th century. The 1778 \"Decree of Free Trade\" opens direct commerce between most Spanish ports and American colonial ports, dramatically increasing revenues. Spain enters the American Revolutionary War (1779–83) as France's ally against Britain and wins significant returns: Florida is recovered and Minorca is briefly retaken.",
  "Spanish Empire|1800": "By 1800 the Spanish Empire is at its maximum geographic extent — at 13.7 million square kilometres, larger than any previous empire in history (Wikipedia). The Bourbon Reforms have produced a fiscal recovery and the American viceroyalties are generating unprecedented revenues. Charles IV reigns (since 1788) but is dominated by his favourite, Manuel Godoy. At the Battle of Cape St. Vincent (1797) a British fleet defeats the Spanish fleet, again exposing naval weakness.",
  "Spanish Empire|1808": "Napoleon forces Charles IV and Ferdinand VII to Bayonne and compels both to abdicate. He installs his brother Joseph Bonaparte as king of Spain. Virtually all Spaniards reject the new king, and spontaneous popular uprisings — most famously in Madrid on 2 May 1808 (the \"Dos de Mayo\") — mark the start of the Peninsular War. The same political vacuum that empowers juntas in Spain triggers the independence process in Spanish America: Creole elites begin establishing their own governing juntas.",
  "Spanish Empire|1825": "The Battle of Ayacucho (9 December 1824) — won by Bolívar's general Antonio José de Sucre against the last major royalist army in Peru — effectively ends Spanish rule on the American mainland. By 1825, every former viceroyalty and captaincy-general of mainland Spanish America has declared independence: Mexico (1821), Central America (1821), Gran Colombia (1819), Peru (1821), Bolivia (1825), Argentina (1816), Chile (1818), Paraguay (1811). Spain retains only Cuba, Puerto Rico, the Philippines, Guam, and small Pacific islands.",
  "Spanish Empire|1898": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — American popular sentiment, inflamed by reports of Spanish atrocities in Cuba and the sinking of USS Maine (February 1898), pushes the US into declaring war on Spain on 25 April 1898. Spain's Caribbean and Pacific fleets are destroyed at Santiago de Cuba and Manila Bay respectively. The Treaty of Paris (December 1898) cedes Cuba, Puerto Rico, and Guam to the United States; the Philippines are sold for $20 million. The empire that began with Columbus in 1492 — 406 years — effectively ceases to exist.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|650": "Around 650 CE Srivijaya is a proto-state emerging from the legacy of earlier Sumatran polities, trading along the Musi River basin, and beginning to project maritime influence. The Chinese pilgrim Yijing would visit just two decades later (671 CE) and find a settled Buddhist polity, but the Kedukan Bukit inscription — the oldest firm evidence of the empire's existence — is dated 682 or 683 CE. An embryonic state whose formal founding is still 30 years away.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|683": "The Kedukan Bukit inscription, dated June 682 or 683 CE, is \"the oldest known specimen\" of the Malay language (Grokipedia). It records that Dapunta Hyang Sri Jayanasa led an expedition of 20,000 troops from Minanga Tamwan to Jambi and Palembang in a sacred journey of conquest. The Melayu Kingdom (Jambi) — rich in gold and highly prestigious — is integrated as the first subordinate polity. The empire is now formally founded, militarily active, and beginning to accumulate wealth and prestige.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|695": "Before Yijing left Southeast Asia in 695, he wrote that Mo-lo-yu (Melayu/Jambi) \"had become part of what he called Shih-li-fo-shih\" — i.e., Srivijaya (EBSCO). Britannica notes: \"before I-ching left Southeast Asia in 695, Srivijaya had gained control of the Strait of Malacca.\" Control of the Malacca Strait meant every merchant vessel travelling between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea had to pass through Srivijayan-controlled waters. The empire has now secured its defining strategic asset.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|750": "By the mid-8th century Srivijaya had secured the Malacca and Sunda straits, was sending regular tributary missions to Tang China (starting under Śrī Indravarman, c. 702), controlled much of the Malay Peninsula and western Java, and had become the pre-eminent centre of Mahayana Buddhism in Southeast Asia. Wikipedia notes that \"by the end of the eighth century, many Javanese kingdoms, such as Tarumanagara and Holing, were within the Srivijayan sphere of influence.\"",
  "Srivijaya Empire|860": "An inscription dated 860 CE records that the Maharaja of Srivijaya — Balaputra, a Sailendra prince expelled from Java who had seized the Srivijayan throne — dedicated a monastery at the Nalanda university complex in Pala-controlled Bengal, one of the great Buddhist universities of the ancient world. New World Encyclopedia notes this as evidence of Srivijaya's deep integration with the pan-Asian Buddhist world. Commercially dominant, religiously prestigious, diplomatically active with China and India's Pala Empire.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|900": "The 9th and early 10th centuries represent the sustained height of the Pax Srivijaya. The Studocu notes describe 10th-century Srivijaya as \"arguably one of the largest empires in the world\" at its Golden Age. A tributary mission to China in 902 had been successfully dispatched, and diplomatic ties with the Tang (and later Song) dynasty were a cornerstone of Srivijayan legitimacy. The empire is at the upper end of its arc, with maximum prestige short of the full Java inclusion that would come by 1000 CE.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1000": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Britannica states: \"By the year 1000 it controlled most of Java.\" This represents the maximum extent of Srivijayan hegemony — control over both the Strait of Malacca and the Java Sea, meaning Srivijaya effectively dominated all major sea routes linking the Indian Ocean to the South China Sea and the Pacific. EBSCO notes it was \"the most powerful polity to emerge\" in western Maritime Southeast Asia. Relations with China were strong under the Song Dynasty, and the Buddhist monastic network was at peak influence.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1025": "In 1025 CE Rajendra Chola I launched a naval armada across the Bay of Bengal and captured at least 14 Srivijayan ports, including Palembang and Kadaram (Kedah). Wikipedia records that the Maharaja Sangrama Vijayottunggavarman was imprisoned, \"most of its cities destroyed,\" and the Sailendra dynasty at Palembang ended. No Srivijayan envoys came to China between 1028 and 1077 — a 49-year diplomatic silence. The centre of Srivijaya shifts from Palembang to Jambi (Malayu).",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1100": "Britannica (Indonesia) records: \"Toward the end of the 11th century, Srivijaya-Palembang ceased to be the chief estuary kingdom in Sumatra.\" Between 1079 and 1088, Chinese records show separate ambassadors from Jambi and Palembang — indicating political fragmentation. EBSCO states that after the Chola invasion, \"Śrivijaya continued to play a much less influential part in the region than it had in earlier years.\" A functioning but reduced polity, having lost Java, its Malacca monopoly, and its most prestigious Buddhist institutions.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1200": "By around 1200 the name \"Srivijaya\" itself had become ambiguous. Chinese records continued to refer to \"Sanfoqi\" but this term likely referred to whichever polity had the dominant trade position on Sumatra. The last epigraphic evidence mentioning the word \"Sriwijaya\" is the Tanjore Chola inscription of 1030 or 1031. By 1200, multiple Sumatran city-states send separate embassies to China. The Srivijayan commercial tradition persists, but the unified thalassocracy is long gone.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1288": "In 1288, the East Javanese kingdom of Singhasari launched the Pamalayu expedition and conquered Palembang and Jambi — the two surviving centres of Srivijayan culture. New World Encyclopedia records: \"In 1288, Singhasari conquered Palembang, Jambi and much of Srivijaya during the Pamalayu expedition. In the year 1293, Majapahit ruled much of Sumatra as the successor of Singhasari.\" Srivijaya has been conquered by an external power and exists only as a vassal or tributary, with no independent foreign policy or naval capacity.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1300": "After the initial Singhasari conquest, the transition to Majapahit (from 1293) allowed some local Srivijayan/Sumatran autonomy within the Majapahit world-system. New World Encyclopedia states that \"Prince Adityawarman was given responsibilities over Sumatra in 1347 by Hayam Wuruk, the fourth king of Majapahit.\" A polity that formally exists and functions at a local level but has entirely surrendered sovereignty and strategic independence to Majapahit's Javanese empire.",
  "Srivijaya Empire|1377": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — New World Encyclopedia records: \"A rebellion in 1377 was suppressed by Majapahit but it left the area of southern Sumatra in chaos and desolation.\" Following this, sedimentation on the Musi River estuary cut Palembang off from direct sea access — the geographical foundation of Srivijayan power was physically severed. The advance of Islam through northern Sumatra meant the cultural and religious basis of Srivijayan identity — Mahayana Buddhism — was also eroding. By the late 14th century, no political entity claimed the Srivijayan name or legacy.",
  "Swedish Empire|1611": "When Charles IX died in October 1611, his sixteen-year-old son Gustav inherited a kingdom at war on three fronts: the Kalmar War against Denmark (1611–13), the Ingrian War against Russia (1610–17), and the Polish-Swedish War over Livonia and dynastic legitimacy (1600–29). The crown was financially strained, the nobility demanding constitutional concessions, and Sweden's claim contested by Sigismund III Vasa of Poland. The absolute baseline from which the Swedish Empire would be built entirely from scratch.",
  "Swedish Empire|1617": "The Ingrian War concluded 27 February 1617 with Russia ceding the strategically vital provinces of Ingria and Kexholm to Sweden. The result was decisive: \"Russia was denied access to the Baltic Sea and turned back toward Asia, thereby delaying its emergence as a major power in Europe for more than one hundred years\" (EBSCO). Sweden now controlled a continuous strip of territory from Finland through Ingria to Estonia, encircling Russia's only potential Baltic outlets. Meaningful, irreversible strategic progress, but the empire is still embryonic.",
  "Swedish Empire|1629": "The Polish-Swedish War concluded 25 September 1629 with the Truce of Altmark, negotiated with French diplomatic assistance from Cardinal Richelieu. Sweden gained the large province of Livonia and the right to collect customs at key Baltic ports — \"a lucrative commercial prize\" that gave the Swedish crown a revenue stream independent of domestic taxation. Sweden has transformed from a defensive regional power into an aggressive Baltic hegemon on the cusp of projecting power into Central Europe.",
  "Swedish Empire|1630": "Gustav II Adolf lands in Pomerania in July 1630 with approximately 13,000 Swedish and Finnish troops. Initial reception was mixed: \"the Protestant princes resented Swedish interference\" and refused cooperation, while Catholic forces under Tilly sacked Magdeburg in May 1631. At this moment, Sweden has the military capability and French subsidies (Treaty of Bärwalde, January 1631) to operate in Central Europe, but has not yet demonstrated it can dominate continental warfare.",
  "Swedish Empire|1631": "On 17 September 1631, the combined Swedish-Saxon forces under Gustav II Adolf destroyed the Imperial-Catholic League army under Count Tilly at Breitenfeld. Imperial losses reached approximately 7,600 killed or wounded and 6,000 captured. Britannica called the battle \"a landmark in the art of war and a turning point in the history of Germany.\" Swedish forces swept through central Germany in the following months, occupying Würzburg, Frankfurt am Main, and Mainz. Sweden replaced the Netherlands as the military model for Europe.",
  "Swedish Empire|1632": "On 6 November 1632, Gustav II Adolf was shot and killed while leading his cavalry in the fog at Lützen. Sweden won the battle tactically, but the strategic blow was enormous. His six-year-old daughter Christina became queen, and Chancellor Axel Oxenstierna assumed control. In 1634, Sweden suffered a major setback at the Battle of Nördlingen — the only significant Swedish defeat of the Thirty Years' War. However, when France intervened directly from 1635 onward, Swedish-French coordination stabilised the military situation.",
  "Swedish Empire|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia (24 October 1648) ended the Thirty Years' War and formalised Sweden's emergence as a European great power. Sweden received Western Pomerania (including Stettin and the island of Rügen), the island of Wismar, and the bishoprics of Bremen and Verden in northern Germany. More significantly, Sweden became one of two external \"guarantors\" of the peace settlement (alongside France), giving it formal standing to intervene anywhere in German affairs. Sweden has unambiguously arrived as a Continental great power.",
  "Swedish Empire|1650": "Post-Westphalia consolidation under Queen Christina — strong but with the true peak still ahead. Every major source consistently identifies the Treaty of Roskilde in 1658 as \"the climax of stormaktstiden\" and the moment Sweden reached \"its largest territorial extent\" (Wikipedia). In 1650, Sweden still did not control Scania, Blekinge, or Bohuslän — territories critical to controlling the Øresund and effectively surrounding Denmark. Parity with 1648 (Peace of Westphalia) — both represent the same plateau of power.",
  "Swedish Empire|1658": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — on 26 February 1658, King Charles X Gustav concluded the Treaty of Roskilde with Frederick III of Denmark — the culmination of a breathtaking winter campaign in which Swedish forces marched across the frozen straits of the Little Belt and Great Belt to place troops within 22 km of Copenhagen. Denmark surrendered Scania, Halland, Blekinge, Bohuslän, Bornholm, and the Norwegian province of Trøndelag — fully a third of its territory. \"Sweden reached its largest territorial extent under the rule of Charles X after the treaty of Roskilde in 1658\" (Schweden/Liquisearch).",
  "Swedish Empire|1680": "At the Riksdag of 1680, Charles XI established an absolute monarchy, declaring that \"he was not bound by anyone other than himself\" (Wikipedia). The Great Reduction confiscated alienated crown lands from the nobility, increasing crown holdings in Sweden-Finland from less than 1% to over 30% of all land (Britannica). In 1682 he reformed the military into the Carolean allotment system (indelningsverket) — which became \"the best trained and equipped force ever to leave Sweden\" (History Learning Site). The empire's institutional zenith, correctly placed below the 1658 territorial-geopolitical peak.",
  "Swedish Empire|1700": "In 1700, Russia, Saxony-Poland, and Denmark-Norway launched a coordinated attack on Sweden. The eighteen-year-old Charles XII responded with remarkable speed: Denmark was knocked out at Travendal in August 1700. Then, on 19 November 1700, a Swedish force of approximately 8,000–10,000 routed a Russian besieging army of 35,000–40,000 at the Battle of Narva — one of the most lopsided victories in early modern European military history. The empire's territory was fully intact; the institutional peak of 1680 is now being spent rather than replenished.",
  "Swedish Empire|1706": "By September 1706 Charles XII had forced Augustus II of Saxony-Poland to sign the Treaty of Altranstädt — dethroning Augustus and placing Sweden's nominee Stanisław I Leszczyński on the Polish throne. All of Europe sent diplomatic envoys to Charles XII's camp in Saxony. However, Russia had used the years of Charles's Polish distraction to recover from Narva, found Saint Petersburg (1703) on Swedish Ingrian territory, and begin stripping Sweden's Baltic provinces. When Peter I offered in 1707 to return most occupied territory except Saint Petersburg, Charles XII refused — a decision that sealed the empire's fate.",
  "Swedish Empire|1709": "On 8 July (O.S.) 1709, approximately 20,000 Swedish troops — already weakened by the catastrophic winter of 1708/09, Russian scorched-earth tactics, and chronic supply failures — attacked a Russian army of at least 44,500 men with 130 cannons from prepared positions outside Poltava in present-day Ukraine. The Swedes had only four operational field guns. The main Swedish field army was destroyed, and the survivors (some 16,000 men) surrendered at Perevolochna three days later. \"The Battle of Poltava is widely considered to be the greatest defeat in Swedish history\" (Svea Fandom).",
  "Swedish Empire|1718": "On 30 November 1718, Charles XII was killed by a single shot to the head while inspecting trenches at the Siege of Fredriksten Fortress in Fredrikshald (present-day Halden, Norway). He died childless. \"With news of the childless king's death reaching Stockholm, Swedish officials immediately begun struggling to achieve more power and abolish the absolute monarchy\" (Svea Fandom). By 1718, most of Sweden's empire was under foreign military occupation: Russia controlled Estonia, Livonia, Ingria, and Finland. Dynastic survival and physical integrity of the Swedish homeland — but the empire, as a political entity, has already ceased to function.",
  "Swedish Empire|1721": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — the Treaty of Nystad (10 September 1721) concluded the Great Northern War. Sweden ceded Estonia, Livonia, Ingria, and a portion of Karelia to Russia — the entire eastern Baltic empire accumulated over a century of warfare. Russia's Peter I, who promptly took the title of Emperor, replaced Sweden as the dominant Baltic power. The Riksdag shifted Sweden toward parliamentary governance (the \"Age of Liberty,\" 1718–72), abandoning the absolutist war-state model. The Stormaktstiden was definitively over.",
  "Tang Dynasty|618": "Li Yuan declared himself emperor and founded the Tang dynasty in June 618, having already occupied Chang'an in winter 617. The dynasty exists and has a capable ruling family, but is far from consolidated: multiple rival warlords continue contesting China's territory. Britannica notes Gaozu \"overcame various rivals and rebels\" and only controlled the eastern plain by 621. A score of 0 would represent the dynasty's final dissolution — not its inception; a fragile but operational new state still at war for its own territory.",
  "Tang Dynasty|621": "By 621 CE, Gaozu's forces — crucially aided by his brilliant second son Li Shimin — had defeated the major rival claimants, including Wang Shichong (at Luoyang) and Dou Jiande (in Hebei). World History Encyclopedia notes the Tang Legal Code was established in 624 CE, which would be copied by Japan, Korea, and Vietnam. By 623–624 \"China was nearly completely reunified again\" (Chinese History Digest). The dynasty is clearly winning the unification wars but has not yet consolidated bureaucratic control over its vast territory.",
  "Tang Dynasty|626": "In 626 Li Shimin ambushed and killed his brothers (including the crown prince) in the Xuanwu Gate massacre, forcing his father Gaozu to abdicate. Despite this brutal seizure of power, Taizong's reign (626–649) would be celebrated as one of the finest in Chinese imperial history. Taizong immediately reformed the government, promoted meritocracy, reduced taxation, and exhibited a legendarily \"unbiased willingness to follow wise advice\" (ECPH-China). In 627 he launched campaigns against the Eastern Turks, destroying the Eastern Turkic Khaganate by 630.",
  "Tang Dynasty|630": "In 630 Tang general Li Jing crushed the Eastern Turks at Yin Mountain, capturing their ruler Illig Qaghan. The Turks — formerly dominant overlords who had extorted tribute from the Sui — now \"accepted Taizong as their khagan, a title rendered as Tian Kehan\" (Wikipedia), making him ruler of both the Chinese and Turkic worlds. Tang influence extended across the steppe to the Mongolian heartland. Military campaigns were subsequently launched against Gaochang (640), Karasahr (644, 648), and Kucha (648) in Central Asia.",
  "Tang Dynasty|649": "Taizong died in 649 at age 51, after 23 years as emperor. The Zhenguan era (627–649) was later canonised as the model of Chinese good governance — meritocratic appointments, fiscal restraint, military expansion, and genuine responsiveness to ministerial advice. His son Gaozong inherits a richly institutionalised, large empire. Historian Patricia Buckley Ebrey calls Taizong \"the exemplary ruler.\" The empire is at maximum institutional health and near-maximum territorial strength. The cultural or territorial peak (Kaiyuan era) has not yet arrived, but governance quality is at its highest point.",
  "Tang Dynasty|650": "Emperor Gaozong inherits a strong empire; population of Tang grew from less than 3 million households during the Zhenguan era to 3.8 million households by 655 — consistent prosperity, not decline. Emperor Gaozong in 650 captured the Eastern Turkic ruler Qibi Heli and extended Tang protectorates. He would go on to defeat the Western Turks in 657 and conquer Korea by 668, making the Gaozong reign (649–683) the one under which the Tang reached its maximum territorial extent.",
  "Tang Dynasty|668": "In 668, Tang and Silla armies conquered the kingdom of Goguryeo, taking Pyongyang on 22 October and absorbing 200,000 prisoners. World History Encyclopedia confirms: \"By the late 7th century, around 669 CE, the empire controlled vast regions stretching from the Korean Peninsula to Central Asia.\" The Oreate AI blog notes the Tang's historical peak in territorial terms: \"eastward to Pyongyang City in Korea, westward to Central Asia's Caspian Sea and Persia's Khorasan region, encompassing all areas around Lake Baikal up north.\"",
  "Tang Dynasty|690": "On 16 October 690, after years of ruling through puppets, Wu Zetian formally proclaimed a new Zhou dynasty and became China's only empress regnant in history. Despite the constitutional interruption, she was an effective ruler: she reformed the civil service examinations to broaden meritocratic recruitment, expanded the bureaucracy, and oversaw continued military operations. The empire itself remains large, well-administered, and prosperous. Score of 6: an empire still strong but undergoing institutional instability — the Li family's legitimacy is in suspension.",
  "Tang Dynasty|700": "In 700 CE Wu Zetian is in the 10th year of her Zhou dynasty, aged approximately 76. The empire remains large, its Confucian administrative machinery functioning well, and Wu's later years are marked by cultural patronage and relative stability. Her reign is credited with institutionalising the expanded examination system. The borders have stabilised. However, the Tibetan Empire remains a powerful western adversary, and the empress's advanced age and reliance on favourites (the Zhang brothers) is generating court factionalism.",
  "Tang Dynasty|712": "After the turbulent 705–712 period (four emperors and an empress dowager in rapid succession), Li Longji seized power in 712, becoming Emperor Xuanzong. He reigned for 44 years — the longest of any Tang emperor. The Metropolitan Museum of Art identifies the 8th century under Xuanzong as \"the classical period of Chinese art and literature.\" He purged the court of factionalism, restored meritocratic appointments, reformed the tax system, and oversaw remarkable economic growth. Chang'an's population swelled to approximately 1–2 million, making it the world's largest city.",
  "Tang Dynasty|740": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Kaiyuan era zenith. The Kaiyuan era (713–741) represents the undisputed apex of Tang civilisation across every dimension. Economically: revenues and agricultural output are at their highest. Culturally: the \"golden age of Chinese poetry\" — Li Bai (701–762), Du Fu (712–770), Wang Wei (699–759), and the court painter Wu Daozi produced work that set the standard for all subsequent Chinese artistic achievement. Demographically: the census of 754–755 would record 52,919,309 people — the Tang high-water mark.",
  "Tang Dynasty|751": "In July 751 Tang forces under General Gao Xianzhi were defeated by Abbasid armies at the Talas River in Central Asia, after the Karluk mercenaries defected mid-battle. The battle ended Tang westward territorial expansion permanently. EBSCO notes that China \"lost control of its main trade route, the Silk Road, for the next half century.\" However, as War History Online emphasises, it was the An Lushan Rebellion in 755 that truly ended Tang western power. In 751 the empire is still enormous and culturally at its peak.",
  "Tang Dynasty|755": "On 16 December 755, General An Lushan — jiedushi of three northeastern prefectures commanding over 164,000 troops — launched his rebellion from Fanyang. Luoyang fell in January 756; Chang'an fell in June 756 and Emperor Xuanzong fled to Sichuan. An Lushan declared himself emperor of a new \"Great Yan\" dynasty. The census of 755 had just recorded a population of approximately 53 million; by 764 the registered population had collapsed to approximately 17 million. Wikipedia: \"Previously at the height of their power, the An Lushan rebellion (755–763) ultimately destroyed the prosperity of the Tang.\"",
  "Tang Dynasty|763": "The rebel Yan dynasty formally collapsed on 17 February 763. However, in November 763 the Tibetan Empire occupied Chang'an for fifteen days before withdrawing. The Tang had permanently lost the Western Regions (Xinjiang, Gansu, Qinghai) to Tibet. Critically, the jiedushi (regional military governors) in Hebei had gained de facto autonomy the Tang court could never revoke. The Lumen Learning summary: \"the power of the jiedushi increased greatly after imperial troops crushed the rebels, taking administrative power away from scholar-officials.\"",
  "Tang Dynasty|780": "The liang shui (two-tax) reform under Chancellor Yang Yan in 780 CE replaced the collapsing equal-field land system with a twice-annual tax based on actual property holdings — acknowledging that the Tang central government had permanently lost control of land distribution to the jiedushi. In 781–784 Emperor Dezong attempted to reassert central authority over the jiedushi in the \"Jianzhong Wars\" and catastrophically failed — the emperor had to flee the capital. By 784 he acknowledged jiedushi hereditary authority permanently.",
  "Tang Dynasty|800": "Emperor Dezong (r. 779–805) is on the throne in 800 CE, at the end of a long reign defined by failed reassertion of central power. After the Jianzhong Wars humiliation (781–784), Dezong accepted the political reality: the Hebei jiedushi operated as autonomous warlords. However, the court still controls the Yangtze basin, the southeast, and large parts of central China. Economic recovery in the south is real — the canal economy is functioning and the tea trade is flourishing. The empire is stable but the political architecture of the pre-755 Tang is gone permanently.",
  "Tang Dynasty|820": "Emperor Xianzong (r. 805–820) was the most capable Tang ruler since Xuanzong. He undertook systematic reconquest of the autonomous circuits: between 806 and 820 he defeated independent jiedushi in Henan and briefly \"restored the authority of the empire throughout most of the country\" (Britannica). But the \"Yuanhe Restoration\" was built on military coercion rather than structural reform, and in 820 his own court eunuchs murdered him. The restoration died with him. The assassination signals that eunuch power over the palace was now structural and ineradicable.",
  "Tang Dynasty|850": "The Great Anti-Buddhist Persecution under Emperor Wuzong (840–845) — the Huichang Suppression — had just been reversed in 846 by Emperor Xuanzong II. While politically volatile, the empire in 850 still controls its core territories, Chang'an functions as a world-class capital, and the examination system still recruits able officials. However, palace eunuchs had by now dominated court politics for decades: they appointed and dismissed emperors, commanded the palace armies (Shence Armies), and were effectively a parallel government.",
  "Tang Dynasty|875": "In September 874, Wang Xianzhi raised a revolt at Changyi, Shandong, driven by successive famines, crushing taxation, and the government's oppressive salt monopoly. Huang Chao, a failed examination candidate and salt smuggler, joined with his own 5,000 followers. Grokipedia notes the uprising was rooted in \"successive famines, poor harvests, exorbitant taxation, and the government's monopolistic salt trade.\" Unlike the An Lushan rebellion — a military elite revolt — the Huang Chao rebellion was a mass peasant uprising that would traverse nearly the entire empire.",
  "Tang Dynasty|881": "In 881 CE (following the fall of Chang'an in late 880), Huang Chao declared himself emperor of a new \"Great Qi\" dynasty. Emperor Xizong had already fled to Sichuan. New World Encyclopedia records: Huang Chao \"sacked the capital Chang'an, slaughtering its residents and leaving the city in ruins.\" Grokipedia notes that tax revenues \"plummeted to under 10% of pre-rebellion levels by the 890s.\" Chang'an — which had been the world's most populous city for much of the dynasty's history — was comprehensively devastated.",
  "Tang Dynasty|900": "In 900–901, the warlord Zhu Wen (former follower of Huang Chao, now military governor of Xuanwu Circuit) had seized effective control of the Tang court from his power base at Kaifeng. He entered Chang'an in 901, controlled the imperial family, and forced Emperor Zhaozong to move the capital to Luoyang in 903. In 905, Zhu \"executed the brothers of Emperor Ai as well as many officials\" (Wikipedia). The Tang dynasty as a political entity has been reduced to a puppet, its territory fragmented among competing warlords.",
  "Tang Dynasty|907": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — on 4 June 907 CE, Zhu Wen forced Emperor Ai (the 15-year-old puppet installed by Zhu in 904) to abdicate the throne to him. Zhu proclaimed himself Emperor Taizu of the Later Liang, inaugurating the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms period — 53 years of fragmentation, civil war, and rapid dynastic succession. A year later Zhu had the deposed Emperor Ai poisoned to death. The Tang dynasty — 289 years of rule, 21 emperors, and the greatest cultural flowering in Chinese imperial history — ceased to exist.",
  "Timurid Empire|1363": "Born c. 1336 near Samarkand, Timur is a minor noble of the Barlas tribe — Turkicised Mongols who settled in Transoxiana after the conquests of Genghis Khan. Through the 1360s he builds a power base \"by guile and force of arms\" (Britannica), exploiting the collapse of the Chagatai Khanate's unity. He fights both for and against various khans, is wounded in battle (giving him a permanent limp — \"Timur the Lame\" / Tamerlane), and gradually absorbs tribal followings. Score of 1: an embryonic political force with no formal state — powerful potential not yet realised.",
  "Timurid Empire|1370": "Timur takes Samarkand in 1366 and Balkh in 1369, completing his control of Transoxiana by 1370. He becomes the effective ruler of the western Chagatai ulus, though — lacking Genghisid ancestry — he must legitimise his rule indirectly, marrying a Genghisid princess (Saray Mulk Khanum) and styling himself \"Gurkan\" (son-in-law of the Khans). He maintains a puppet Chagatai Khan while holding real power. His capital Samarkand begins its transformation into a showcase of Islamic architecture.",
  "Timurid Empire|1380": "Through the 1370s–1380s Timur launches successive campaigns into surrounding regions. He sacks Herat in 1381, \"reducing it to rubble and massacring most of its citizens\" (Wikipedia); the Kartid dynasty submits. He campaigns in Khorasan, Mazandaran, Sistan, and begins incursions into Persia proper as far as Soltaniyeh by 1384. A characteristic pattern emerges: Timur conquers, withdraws to Samarkand, and must reconquer again when subject regions revolt — reflecting the empire's shallow administrative integration. Samarkand is lavishly rebuilt with artisans and craftsmen looted from conquered cities.",
  "Timurid Empire|1390": "By the late 1380s–early 1390s Timur has subdued most of Persia, conquered Azerbaijan (1386), ravaged the southern Caucasus (1386–88, 1394–96), and launched devastating campaigns against the Golden Horde. In 1395–96 he ravages southern Russia and Ukraine, sacking every major Golden Horde city (Sarai, Astrakhan) — effectively destroying the Horde as a power and permanently opening the Eurasian steppe for Ottoman and Russian expansion. His great monuments — the Mausoleum of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi and the Bibi-Khanym Mosque — mark Samarkand's transformation into a world-class Islamic capital.",
  "Timurid Empire|1398": "In September 1398 Timur crosses the Indus with 90,000 troops and invades the weakened Delhi Sultanate. He massacres approximately 100,000 Indian prisoners he fears might revolt; defeats the Sultan's war elephants by sending flaming camels into their lines. Delhi is sacked for two months; artisans are carried off to Samarkand. In 1400 he captures Sivas from the Ottomans and sacks Aleppo and Damascus. In June 1401 he takes Baghdad. The empire now stretches from Delhi to Damascus and from the Caucasus to the Persian Gulf. The pre-Ankara maximum.",
  "Timurid Empire|1400": "At 1400 the empire is at Damascus and Baghdad, representing an extremely high point — but the absolute peak has been placed at 1398 (9) to capture the Delhi-Damascus-Baghdad arc as a unit. The 1400 score reflects the slightly more complex situation of 1400–1401, when Timur is managing concurrent campaigns in Syria and Iraq while preparing the Ottoman confrontation. The empire is at near-maximum power, with the Ottoman clash and the definitive 10 still ahead.",
  "Timurid Empire|1402": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — on 20 July 1402 Timur defeats the Ottoman Sultan Bayezid I at the Battle of Ankara — the greatest Ottoman military disaster until the 20th century. Bayezid is captured in the field (the only Ottoman sultan ever taken prisoner by an enemy), his army is destroyed, and his empire fractures into a twelve-year interregnum civil war among his sons. Britannica states the defeat \"came close to destroying the Ottoman Empire.\" Timur advances to the Aegean, sacks Smyrna, and receives tribute from Byzantine Constantinople. He is undefeated in battle across forty years and commands the largest army in the world.",
  "Timurid Empire|1405": "On 19 February 1405, Timur dies of illness at Otrar (in modern Kazakhstan) while leading his army toward a planned invasion of Ming China, aged 69. His death triggers an immediate succession crisis: the empire is divided between his sons, with Miranshah receiving Iraq, Azerbaijan, and the western territories, and Shah Rukh receiving Khorasan. Neither initially controls Transoxiana. The fundamental weakness Timur had masked through personal genius — no durable administrative structures, the nomadic tradition of dividing land among heirs, reliance on terror and tribute — is instantly exposed.",
  "Timurid Empire|1415": "After Timur's death, Shah Rukh — his youngest son and most capable successor — spends 1405–1415 methodically reassembling the fractured empire. He defeats or displaces rival claimants, occupies Samarkand (1409), and extends his control to include Transoxiana, Khorasan, Mazandran, Sistan, Fars, and Kerman. He moves the political capital from Samarkand to Herat. His court becomes a celebrated centre of Persian-language art and letters; EBSCO describes Herat as \"a cultural center comparable to Renaissance Florence.\"",
  "Timurid Empire|1420": "In Samarkand, Shah Rukh's son Ulugh Beg (viceroy of Transoxiana from 1409) builds the Ulugh Beg Madrasa at the Registan (1417–20) and his famous astronomical observatory (completed c. 1428–29) — the most advanced observatory of the pre-Copernican world, compiling star catalogues accurate to within one arc minute. In Herat, Shah Rukh's queen Gawhar Shad commissions extraordinary religious and funerary architecture. The Baysunqur workshops produce the finest illuminated manuscripts in the Islamic world. A stable, culturally extraordinary empire whose political power is steady but clearly below Timur's military zenith.",
  "Timurid Empire|1447": "Shah Rukh dies in 1447 after 42 years of rule — the longest and most stabilising reign of any Timurid. His son Ulugh Beg — a brilliant astronomer but politically weak and militarily ineffective — ascends to the throne. Cambridge History of Inner Asia notes that \"Timurid power quickly atrophied after Shah Rukh's death.\" Ulugh Beg cannot control his relatives and faces multiple rebellions. The western Timurid territories are increasingly contested by the Turkmen Kara Koyunlu and Ak Qoyunlu confederations.",
  "Timurid Empire|1450": "On 25–27 October 1449, Ulugh Beg is murdered by his own son Abd al-Latif after being defeated at the battle of Dimashq near Samarkand. He had been ordered to make the pilgrimage to Mecca; he was killed on the road. Abd al-Latif himself is then murdered six months later. The empire enters a chaotic interregnum with no clear sovereign. UNESCO's account records a \"struggle for succession\" among multiple competing Timurid princes, with the Uzbeks already beginning to interfere. A crisis comparable to 1405 but without a Shah Rukh figure to reassemble it.",
  "Timurid Empire|1460": "After the murderous chaos of 1449–1451, Timurid prince Abu Said — supported by the Uzbek leader Abu'l-Khayr Khan — defeats rival claimants and seizes Samarkand in 1451. He spends the 1450s–1460s fighting the Kara Koyunlu and Ak Qoyunlu Turkmen confederations for control of the western Timurid territories. Cambridge notes he managed \"to keep the aggressive steppe warriors at bay\" for more than two decades. The cultural florescence in Herat and Samarkand continues — extraordinary literature, painting, and architecture still flourish in the Timurid courts.",
  "Timurid Empire|1469": "In 1469, Abu Said is defeated and killed at the Battle of Karabakh by the Ak Qoyunlu confederation under Uzun Hasan. EBSCO states: \"After his death at the Battle of Karabakh, the west fell to the Ak Qoyunlu, reducing Timurid power to Khorasan.\" Wikipedia confirms that \"by 1467, the ruling Timurid dynasty had lost most of Persia to the Aq Qoyunlu.\" The once-vast Timurid Empire is now effectively reduced to a single province — Khorasan, centred on Herat. Militarily defeated, territorially contracted, dependent on the cultural prestige of Herat rather than any political or military power.",
  "Timurid Empire|1480": "Husayn Bayqara (r. 1469–1506), the last significant Timurid ruler, unexpectedly rebuilds some of what had been lost. EBSCO notes he \"took Samarkand from Khoja Ahrar, as well as Merv, Khiva, and Herat.\" He makes Herat his capital and presides over what Britannica describes as an \"extraordinary cultural florescence in literature, the arts, and architecture.\" His close friend Mir Ali Shir Nava'i \"single-handedly turned Chaghatay Turkic into a literary language\" (Encyclopedia.com). Score of 3: the bittersweet paradox of the late Timurid era — extraordinary cultural output in a political shell.",
  "Timurid Empire|1500": "In 1499–1500, Muhammad Shaybani Khan capitalises on Timurid dynastic squabbling to seize Transoxiana. He takes Bukhara, then forces the last Timurid ruler of Samarkand (Ali Mirza) to negotiate, then kills him. In 1500 he declares himself ruler of Transoxiana. The Timurid prince Babur (later founder of the Mughal Empire) attempts to retake Samarkand in 1500 and 1501 but fails both times. By 1503–1504, the Uzbeks have seized Samarkand, Bukhara, Tashkent, Ferghana, and Khwarazm. Herat alone remains.",
  "Timurid Empire|1507": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — Husayn Bayqara dies in 1506; leadership falls to his son Badi al-Zaman, who proves wholly unequal to the crisis. In spring 1507, Muhammad Shaybani Khan's Uzbek forces attack Balkh, then advance on Herat. After a defence lasting only three days, the capital surrenders on 27 May 1507. The remarkable library, artisans, and court scholars of Herat are dispersed or absorbed. A Timurid rump survives at Kabul under Babur, who will eventually found the Mughal Empire in India in 1526 — but as a different political entity in a different territory.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1603": "Following his decisive victory at the Battle of Sekigahara (1600), Ieyasu receives the title of shōgun from Emperor Go-Yōzei in 1603. However, the Toyotomi clan — the previous ruling house — survives as a rival power base at Osaka Castle, and many western daimyō remain resentful. Ieyasu immediately abdicates in favour of his son Hidetada in 1605 to demonstrate hereditary succession, but retains actual power. A regime that has won the civil war but not yet secured permanent dominance — with a powerful rival still present and institutions still forming.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1615": "Ieyasu destroys the Toyotomi stronghold at Osaka Castle, killing Toyotomi Hideyori and eliminating the last major rival to Tokugawa authority. Wikipedia confirms: \"After 1615, various policies were implemented to assert the shogunate's control, which severely curtailed the daimyō's independence.\" The Laws for the Military Houses (buke shōhattō) — first issued in 1615 — ban fortification without shogunal approval, restrict daimyō marriages, and begin the architecture of control. A genuinely unified Japan under a single authority for the first time in a century.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1635": "The third shōgun Tokugawa Iemitsu (r. 1623–51) completes the shogunate's two defining control mechanisms. In 1635 he issues the expanded buke shōhattō (21 edicts), institutionalises the sankin-kōtai system requiring daimyō to reside alternately in Edo and their domains (keeping families as permanent Edo hostages), and begins the sakoku seclusion decrees. By 1639 all Westerners except the Dutch are expelled. In 1634 Iemitsu marched 300,000 troops to Kyoto in a show of supreme power.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1650": "By 1650 the shogunate's institutional framework is fully operational: sankin-kōtai keeps daimyō perpetually indebted and geographically constrained; sakoku has sealed the borders; the class hierarchy is frozen; census and land registration systems generate predictable revenue. No military challenge exists anywhere in Japan. Iemitsu dies in 1651 — the same year as the failed Keian Uprising (a small rōnin plot), which is swiftly crushed. The shogunate is not a newly-founded enterprise but a mature, tested, fully operational regime.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1695": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — the Genroku era is \"often considered the height of the Tokugawa era, a period of peace, economic prosperity, and artistic development\" (Japan Experience). History.com similarly identifies the Genroku era as when \"a vibrant urban culture emerged… catering to merchants, samurai and townspeople.\" Kabuki theatre, Bunraku puppet theatre, ukiyo-e woodblock prints, and the haiku of Matsuo Bashō all flourish simultaneously. The merchant class of Osaka has made it the commercial capital of Asia. Edo's population reaches over one million — one of the largest cities on earth.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1700": "The Genroku era runs until approximately 1704 and the early 1700s still exhibit its characteristic prosperity and cultural confidence. However, the currency debasement begun in 1695 under shōgun Tsunayoshi has caused inflation and fiscal instability. Tsunayoshi's controversial \"Edicts on Compassion for Living Things\" (dog protection laws) reflect both the shogunate's confidence in social control and a dawning arbitrariness. The Genroku era has not yet ended and prosperity remains near maximum. The fiscal stress is a warning sign, not yet a structural crisis.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1750": "By 1750 the shogunate has passed its Genroku peak. Shōgun Yoshimune's Kyōhō Reforms (1716–45) attempt to address a deepening fiscal crisis — samurai stipends are cut, sumptuary laws target merchant wealth, and new agricultural land is opened. EBSCO notes that \"tensions grew as the wealthy merchant class began to outpace the traditional ruling lords, resulting in social unrest.\" World History Encyclopedia identifies this period as when \"agricultural production had reached its ecological limits.\" The Kyōhō famine (1732–33) killed tens of thousands in western Japan.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1800": "Matsudaira Sadanobu's Kansei Reforms (1787–93) attempt to arrest fiscal decline through austerity and a return to Confucian orthodoxy — cutting samurai debts, restricting credit, and censoring heterodox scholarship. The reforms buy a generation of stability but do not resolve the underlying structural problem: a warrior-class government dependent on rice taxation in an increasingly monetised commercial economy. Population growth has flatlined. Russian envoys begin probing Japan's northern frontier (1792, 1804), signalling the approaching end of effective sakoku.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1837": "The Tempō era (1830–1844) is the hinge point of Tokugawa decline. A series of catastrophic crop failures (1833–36) produce the worst famine since the shogunate's founding — hundreds of thousands die across Japan. In 1837, Ōshio Heihachirō, a former magistrate's officer and Confucian scholar, leads an uprising in Osaka with 300 followers, citing the shogunate's failure to feed the people. The revolt is crushed within half a day, but its symbolic significance is enormous: an official of the shogunate itself had turned against the regime.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1853": "On 8 July 1853, U.S. Commodore Matthew Perry arrives in Edo Bay with four steam-powered warships, demanding Japan open to foreign trade. The shogunate has no answer — the technological gap between Japan and the Western powers is now manifest and terrifying. Perry returns in February 1854 and Japan is forced to sign the Convention of Kanagawa, opening the ports of Shimoda and Hakodate. History Skills states this demonstrated \"the shogunate's inability to defend Japan against foreign threats.\" The Bakumatsu era has definitively begun.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1863": "By 1863 Japan is in open political crisis. Anti-Tokugawa samurai in Chōshū and Satsuma are attacking Western ships and personnel. Western naval retaliations — the Anglo-Satsuma War (1863) and the bombardment of Shimonoseki (1864) by British, French, Dutch, and American warships — humiliate both the Tokugawa and the anti-foreign sonnō jōi movement simultaneously. Crucially, Satsuma and Chōshū secretly conclude that Japan must modernise rather than expel foreigners — and that the shogunate must be removed to make this possible. They begin forming a military alliance.",
  "Tokugawa Shogunate|1868": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — Emperor Meiji is restored to nominal power in January 1868 after Satsuma and Chōshū forces seize the imperial court in Kyoto. The 15th and last Tokugawa shōgun Yoshinobu resigns and surrenders Edo Castle without a fight (May 1868), averting the destruction of the city. Tokugawa loyalists continue fighting in the Boshin War until the final surrender at the Battle of Hakodate (June 1869), but for practical purposes the shogunate ceases to exist in 1868. The political entity has ended.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|661": "After the First Fitna (656–661), Muʿawiya ibn Abi Sufyan enters Kufa after Ali's assassination and Hasan ibn Ali's abdication, in the so-called \"Year of Unity.\" He transfers the capital from Medina to Damascus, his power base for twenty years. He inherits the vast territorial legacy of the Rashidun Caliphate — from Egypt to Khorasan — along with its Syrian army. Score of 4: the empire's territorial footprint is already enormous and Muʿawiya is a capable administrator, but legitimacy remains contested.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|668": "Muʿawiya uses his Syrian army for near-annual raids against Byzantium, gaining naval supremacy in the eastern Mediterranean (Arab fleets capturing Rhodes and Arwad). His administration is built around experienced Byzantine-model governance. He bribes enemies rather than fighting them where possible. By the early 670s he launches the First Arab Siege of Constantinople (674–678) — ultimately unsuccessful and ending with a 30-year truce obliging the Umayyads to pay the Byzantines annual tribute. A functioning, expanding empire, but the failed Constantinople siege signals the limits of military power.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|680": "The appointment of Yazid I (r. 680–683) as Muʿawiya's hereditary successor — the first dynastic succession in Islamic history — violates traditional consultative norms and generates fierce opposition. In October 680, Husayn ibn Ali, grandson of the Prophet, is intercepted en route to Kufa with approximately 70 men and massacred at the Battle of Karbala by a 4,000-strong Umayyad army. The event is an immediate propaganda catastrophe. Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr simultaneously proclaims a rival caliphate from Mecca, gaining recognition in most provinces outside Syria.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|684": "Yazid I dies in November 683 shortly after besieging Mecca (during which the Kaʿba is damaged by catapult fire — another propaganda catastrophe). His son Muʿawiya II is a sickly 22-year-old who commands virtually no support and dies within months without naming a successor. At peak crisis (~686 CE), the Zubayrids control Mecca, Medina, Iraq, most of Arabia, and Egypt. Pro-Alid Mukhtar al-Thaqafi seizes Kufa in 685, routing a large Umayyad army at the Battle of Khazir. The Umayyad \"dynasty\" is effectively reduced to Syria and parts of Jordan.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|692": "Under the fifth Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan (r. 685–705), the reconquest proceeds systematically. He defeats Ibn al-Zubayr's brother Musʿab at the Battle of Maskin (October 691), then sends his general Hajjaj ibn Yusuf to besiege Mecca. Ibn al-Zubayr is killed fighting at the Kaʿba (October/November 692), ending 12 years of civil war. Abd al-Malik builds the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem (691–92) — the earliest archaeologically-attested Muslim religious monument. He defeats the Byzantines at the Battle of Sebastopolis (692) and captures Carthage (695).",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|700": "By 700 Abd al-Malik has transformed the Umayyad state: Arabisation of the entire bureaucracy (replacing Greek and Coptic officials); Arabic as the sole official state language; a new Arabic Islamic coinage replacing Byzantine and Sasanian imitations; a standardised postal service; and centralisation of caliphal authority. Islamic historian Gerald Hawting identifies \"the combined reigns of Abd al-Malik and al-Walid, tied together by al-Hajjaj [viceroy of the east]\" as representing \"in some ways the high point of Umayyad power.\" At 700, the empire spans from Hispania to Khorasan.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|710": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — al-Walid I's era of great conquests; maximum territorial extent of 11.1 million km². Concurrent conquests on three fronts: (West) Tariq ibn Ziyad crosses the Straits of Gibraltar (711) and conquers most of the Iberian Peninsula; (East) Muhammad ibn Qasim conquers Sindh (711–12), the first Arab-Muslim presence in the Indian subcontinent; (Northeast) Qutayba ibn Muslim secures Bukhara (706–09), then Khwarazm and Samarkand (711–12). Historian Hugh Kennedy calls al-Walid's reign \"perhaps the zenith of Umayyad power.\"",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|715": "Sulayman (r. 715–717) succeeds al-Walid I and immediately mobilises the Caliphate's greatest military effort: an all-out siege of Constantinople, reportedly driven by a prophecy that a caliph bearing the name of a prophet would conquer the city. A massive army of 100,000 under Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik besieges Constantinople from August 717. The empire still holds all its territories from 710; this represents a continuation of maximal military ambition. However, the seeds of overextension are being planted.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|717": "The Second Arab Siege of Constantinople fails catastrophically. The Arab navy is repeatedly destroyed by Greek fire; Christian Egyptian crews desert to the Byzantines; a Bulgarian army attacks the Arab land forces from the rear; and starvation devastates the besieging army. Sulayman dies during the campaign (September 717). His successor Umar II orders the immediate withdrawal and contemplates withdrawing from recent conquests of Hispania and Transoxiana entirely. The Byzantines recover territory in Armenia. The most expensive military gamble in the Caliphate's history has failed.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|720": "A score of 10 at 720 is incorrect: the territorial and political peak was 710–715. By 720 the Caliphate faces the consequences of the failed Constantinople siege, is withdrawing from some frontier positions, and has just lost its most reformist caliph Umar II (died February 720, likely poisoned by dynastic opponents). Umar II had attempted to equalise the status of non-Arab Muslims (mawali). His successor Yazid II (r. 720–724) immediately reverses these policies, reimposing the jizya on the mawali, sparking revolts in Khurasan and \"strong resistance among the Berbers of Ifriqiya\" (Wikipedia).",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|724": "Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik (r. 724–743) accedes after Yazid II's death. Britannica describes him as the caliph \"who reigned during the final period of prosperity and glory of the Umayyads.\" He inherits an empire with multiple problems: Kharijite revolts in Iraq, tribal Qays–Yaman feuds threatening Syria's cohesion, and frontier pressures on all sides. Hisham is a \"careful and frugal administrator\" who reforms agricultural taxation, constructs infrastructure, encourages Arabic translation of scientific literature, and reinstates some of Umar II's equalisation policies.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|732": "Under the Umayyad governor Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi, a large Muslim army invades the Frankish kingdom, reaching as far north as Poitiers before being decisively defeated by Charles Martel at the Battle of Tours (October 732). Abd al-Rahman is killed. While the Umayyads retain al-Andalus (Spain), all further expansion into Western Europe is permanently abandoned. In Central Asia, costly campaigns against the Turgesh Turks yield diminishing returns. Historian Khalid Yahya Blankinship argues that by Hisham's reign the \"jihad state\" model was becoming fiscally unsustainable.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|740": "By 740 the Umayyad Caliphate faces crisis everywhere simultaneously: (North Africa) the Great Berber Revolt (739–743) erupts in Tangier; a Berber force destroys a loyal army at Wadi Sherif; Hisham sends 27,000 Syrian troops under Kulthum ibn Iyad — they are annihilated at the Battle of the River of Nobles; Morocco is permanently lost; (Anatolia) the Battle of Akroinon (740) sees a major Arab force destroyed by the Byzantine emperor Leo III. The Syrian army — the dynasty's irreplaceable military foundation — has been \"uselessly immolated in profitless battles on many frontiers\" (Wikipedia).",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|743": "Hisham dies of diphtheria on 6 February 743, succeeded by his nephew Al-Walid II (r. 743–744) — a figure described by sources as more interested in earthly pleasures than governance (his desert palaces Qusayr Amra and Khirbat al-Mafjar feature explicitly erotic frescoes). Al-Walid II is immediately resented within the Umayyad family for designating his two young sons as heirs, flogging and imprisoning his cousin Sulayman ibn Hisham, and persecuting a religious faction. The state is \"extremely strapped for cash due to the previous catastrophic campaigns\" (Berber Revolt, Wikipedia).",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|744": "In April 744, Yazid III enters Damascus with tribal supporters and proclaims himself caliph, while Al-Walid II is killed — his severed head sent to Damascus. Yazid III dies six months later. His designated brother Ibrahim rules for two months before Marwan II (r. 744–750) marches south and enters Damascus in December 744. The civil war (Third Fitna) has just begun. Marwan II moves the capital north to Harran and razes the walls of Homs and Damascus in 746 in retaliation for rebellion. Four caliphs in one year, the Syrian army fractured along tribal lines, multiple rebel factions active simultaneously.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|747": "In 747, Abu Muslim raises the Abbasid black banners in Khurasan, launching the open phase of the Abbasid Revolution. The movement draws on a coalition united by Umayyad grievances: non-Arab Muslim mawali denied equality, Shiʿa sympathisers still bitter over Karbala, Khorasani Arabs integrated with local Persian populations, and eastern provincials exhausted by decades of Arab-supremacist policy. The Hashimiyya movement sweeps through the east with messianic fervour. A dynasty still militarily present but facing an existential threat it cannot contain — its eastern provinces are gone, its treasury empty, its Syrian army exhausted.",
  "Umayyad Caliphate|750": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — on 16 January 750, the forces of Marwan II and the Abbasid army under Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah meet at the Battle of the Great Zab River. The Umayyads are decisively defeated; nine days later Marwan II's army is completely destroyed. He flees to Egypt, where he is tracked down and killed in August 750. Damascus falls to the Abbasids in April 750. The Abbasids show no mercy: male members of the Umayyad family are systematically hunted and killed; eighty Umayyad princes who gather for an Abbasid \"amnesty banquet\" are clubbed to death. One survivor — Abd al-Rahman I — escapes across North Africa to establish the Emirate of Córdoba in 756.",
  "Wadai Empire|1611": "Abd al-Karim initiates uprising against the Tunjur dynasty. Controls only the Maba heartland around future capital Wara; polity is embryonic with no formal state structure yet established.",
  "Wadai Empire|1635": "Abd al-Karim completes Tunjur overthrow; Sultanate formally established. Abd al-Karim becomes first kolak. Controls compact highland domain; still militarily weak and geographically limited. Score of 2 correctly reflects a newly founded, unproven state.",
  "Wadai Empire|1655": "Yaʿqub Arus begins reign (r. 1655–1681). Refuses to pay tribute to Darfur; repels Darfurian punitive invasion. First real assertion of Wadai independence. Military credibility established; score rises. The empire demonstrates it can survive confrontation with a stronger neighbour.",
  "Wadai Empire|1681": "End of Yaʿqub era; independence consolidated. Wadai is fully sovereign for the first time. Expanded southward into former Tunjur heartland at Mondo. Growing slave-raiding economy and cavalry-based military. A functioning Islamic sultanate with recognised borders and growing regional prestige.",
  "Wadai Empire|1700": "Steady growth under post-Yaʿqub sultans. Wadai expands trade networks and slave-raiding operations; remains regionally significant but not yet dominant in the Chad Basin. Score of 5 correctly reflects a mid-tier regional power approaching but not yet at the ascending phase that will produce the Djawda-era expansion.",
  "Wadai Empire|1747": "Muhammad Djawda begins long reign (r. 1747–1795). Expansion accelerates into Kanem (taken from declining Bornu). Fitri installed as tributary state. Darfur defeated and its sultan Umar Lel captured. Major regional ascent begins under the most capable ruler since Yaʿqub Arus. Score of 6 reflects the opening of the dynasty's greatest era of expansion.",
  "Wadai Empire|1750": "Early Djawda expansion; strong but still building. Score of 9 in the original dataset was premature — Djawda had only just begun his reign in 1747 and the full territorial maximum came in his later decades (1770s–1790s). Score revised to 7 to reflect strong but still-building power at this mid-century point. The empire is clearly ascending but has not yet reached its 18th-century zenith.",
  "Wadai Empire|1790": "Territorial zenith of Djawda era. By end of his reign (1795), Wadai controls nearly one-third of modern Chad including Sahelian grasslands, Saharan fringes, and Kanem. This is the true peak of 18th-century expansion. The empire's cavalry armies dominate the Chad Basin and no regional power can seriously challenge Wadai's authority. Score of 9: the 18th-century peak, with Sabun's structural and commercial improvements still ahead.",
  "Wadai Empire|1800": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Muhammad Sabun era (r. 1804–c.1815). In 1805, Wadai conquers Bagirmi, kills its ruler, enslaves 20,000. By 1809/10 a new direct trans-Saharan trade route to Benghazi is established, freeing Wadai from economic dependence on Bornu and Darfur. Sabun mints his own coinage, imports chain mail and military advisers from North Africa, and transforms the empire from a regional power into an economically self-sufficient and diplomatically assertive state. Score 10 correctly anchored here.",
  "Wadai Empire|1815": "Sabun dies; instability erupts. Sabun's death triggers six successive sultans across the 1820s and early 1830s. Trade routes disrupted, northern merchants imprisoned/expelled. Power erodes sharply but institutional foundation holds. Score of 7: the empire remains large and its institutions functional, but the loss of Sabun's centralising genius triggers immediate fragmentation that will worsen over the next two decades.",
  "Wadai Empire|1820": "Crisis of succession deepens. Chronic civil war; Darfur exploits the power vacuum. Northern Benghazi trade route collapses for extended periods (1820–1835) due to internal conflict and Ottoman-Fezzan commercial rivalry. Score of 5: the empire has returned to the mid-tier regional power level of 1700, its Sabun-era achievements largely squandered in factional warfare.",
  "Wadai Empire|1838": "Darfur installs Muhammad al-Sharif as vassal (1835–1838). Al-Sharif placed on the throne in exchange for tribute to Darfur. Wadai briefly reduced to subordinate status — lowest point since the 17th century. Score of 4 reflects significant but not catastrophic regression: the institutional structure of the sultanate survives, and al-Sharif will rapidly turn the tables on his Darfurian patrons.",
  "Wadai Empire|1846": "Al-Sharif repudiates Darfur, launches offensives. Immediately renounces tribute obligation; sacks Bornu's capital Kukawa; re-imposes tributary status on Bagirmi. Meets Senussi founder at Mecca and begins the strategic religious-commercial alliance that will power Wadai for the next 50 years. Capital shifted to Abéché (c.1850), which grows to approximately 28,000 by 1900. Score of 7: a V-shaped recovery from the Darfur vassalage — as strong as the pre-crisis era.",
  "Wadai Empire|1850": "Al-Sharif at height of reassertion. Score of 6 in the original dataset understated Sharif's success: he defeated Darfur, sacked Bornu, secured Bagirmi as tributary, launched the Senussi alliance, and shifted the capital to Abéché. By 1850 his reign represents a genuine high-water mark of post-Sabun recovery. Score revised to 7.",
  "Wadai Empire|1865": "Sultan Ali ibn Muhammad Sharif (r. 1858–1874) — consolidation peak. Stable reign. Army expands to approximately 5,000 cavalry and 56,000 infantry. Innovates militarily (mines used in 1870 Massenya siege; bronze cannons cast with Ottoman/Egyptian advisers). Senussi trade route to Benghazi increasingly dominant. Score of 8: the empire under Ali represents the strongest post-Sabun recovery, combining military innovation with the Senussi commercial network.",
  "Wadai Empire|1880": "Sultan Yusuf (r. 1874–1898) — height of Senussi partnership. Caravans across the Sahara prefer the Abéché–Benghazi route for its security. Wadai exports slaves, ivory, ostrich feathers; imports firearms and manufactured goods. Expands against former Mahdist vassals in southeast. Peak trade volume: approximately 548 tonnes of merchandise annually by the 1890s. Score of 8: the longest sustained period of high-level power in the empire's history.",
  "Wadai Empire|1900": "Yusuf dies (1898); succession crisis. Darfur-backed Ahmad al-Ghazali takes throne; Senussi-backed Dud Murra launches counter-coup (1901). French destroy Rabih az-Zubayr's empire and establish French Chad, directly bordering Wadai. Two years of unstable rule precede Dud Murra's takeover. Score of 3 correctly captures the near-collapse of autonomy under simultaneous internal succession crisis and external French encirclement.",
  "Wadai Empire|1906": "France launches formal aggression. French back Adam Asil as puppet claimant and begin punitive expeditions into Wadai's border regions. Trade routes disrupted; vassal states peeling away (Bagirmi 1898, Kanem 1901). Dud Murra consolidates Senussi alliance but faces existential threat from French forces armed with modern rifles. Score of 2: empire still fighting but facing overwhelming technological and logistical asymmetry.",
  "Wadai Empire|1909": "Abéché falls (12 June 1909). Captain Fiegenschuh occupies the capital with 180 men. Adam Asil installed as puppet sultan. Dud Murra retreats north into Senussi territory and wages insurgency — defeating French columns at Wadi Kadja (January 1910) and Dorothe (November 1910) — but cannot reverse the conquest. Score of 1: the capital is lost, the legitimate sultan is in exile, but resistance continues with some local military success.",
  "Wadai Empire|1912": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — complete French pacification; Wadai absorbed into French Equatorial Africa. Last effective anti-French resistance suppressed by 1912. Wadai absorbed into French Equatorial Africa. Empire formally dissolved as an independent polity. End of the last great trans-Saharan Sudanic empire. The sultanate survives as a ceremonial institution: reconstituted under French suzerainty in 1935, it survived Chadian independence (1960) and continues as a non-sovereign monarchy today — over 400 years of institutional continuity from Abd al-Karim's founding revolt.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-36": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — Zhizhi Chanyu killed at Battle of Zhizhi; last independent Xiongnu state eliminated. In 36 BCE, Han junior officer Chen Tang assembled 40,000 troops and attacked Zhizhi Chanyu's capital city (near modern Taraz, Kazakhstan). Zhizhi was killed in the battle and his severed head sent to Chang'an. With Zhizhi's death, the last independent Xiongnu chanyu is gone. Huhanye's southern branch survives, but as a Han vassal — functionally an instrument of Han border policy rather than a sovereign power. Score of 0 correctly placed here — not at -50 BCE — marking the definitive end of Xiongnu political independence.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-50": "At 50 BCE, the political situation remains split: Huhanye's southern faction is a Han vassal while Zhizhi continues building an independent western Xiongnu state near present-day Kazakhstan. Zhizhi still commands an army capable of attacking Han's Western Regions allies and executes a Han envoy in 45 BCE. He is a genuine, if declining, sovereign power — not merely a refugee chieftain. Assigning a score of 0 here is premature: 0 marks the extinction of the last independent Xiongnu political entity, which does not occur until 36 BCE. The original terminus at -50 is therefore revised to -36.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-53": "In 53 BCE, Huhanye Chanyu — defeated repeatedly by his brother Zhizhi — submits to Han suzerainty. He sends his son to Chang'an as a hostage, then in 51 BCE personally visits Emperor Xuan to pay homage, formally acknowledging Han overlordship. He is reduced from a \"brotherly state\" (equal diplomatic rank) to an \"outer vassal\" (tributary inferior). Meanwhile Zhizhi Chanyu moves westward, building a new power base near Lake Balkash in Central Asia, but remains isolated and unable to sustain a serious challenge to Han. Score of 1: a confederation split between a vassal faction and an isolated western remnant — not quite 0 (Zhizhi still fights), but clearly in terminal condition.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-60": "When Xulüquanqu Chanyu dies in 60 BCE, the succession crisis triggers a catastrophic civil war. By 57 BCE, five rival claimants have declared themselves chanyu simultaneously. Two main claimants emerge: Zhizhi Chanyu (r. 56–36 BCE) in the west and Huhanye Chanyu (r. 58–31 BCE) in the east, who ultimately seeks Han protection. The Han, recognising the opportunity, backs Huhanye economically while refusing to sanction military action — allowing the Xiongnu to exhaust each other. Score of 2: the confederation is functionally broken, simultaneously fighting a civil war and incapable of projecting power against Han.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-87": "Emperor Wu's death in 87 BCE removes the driving force behind Han's aggressive military posture. However, in 72 BCE Han organises a massive coalition — including the Wusun, Dingling, and Wuhuan — that inflicts a catastrophic multi-front defeat on the Xiongnu. In 67 BCE, Han retakes the Turpan Depression. In 60 BCE, Han formally establishes the Protectorate of the Western Regions, permanently administering the Tarim Basin. The Xiongnu are no longer able to secure even temporary diplomatic equality. Score of 3: a power reduced to holding its Mongolian core, losing the western regions permanently, and failing even in diplomacy.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-100": "After the campaigns of 127–119 BCE, the Xiongnu enter a period of attempted peace overtures and internal consolidation. Wuwei Chanyu (r. 114–105 BCE) halts border raids and seeks diplomatic resolution, but Han demands the chanyu send his heir as a hostage — a demand so humiliating it collapses negotiations. Hulugu Chanyu (r. 95–85 BCE) similarly pursues renewed heqin, offering tribute, but Han demands full tributary submission rather than the older equal-status arrangement. In 103 BCE, the Junjie Campaign shows the Xiongnu can still inflict serious losses. Score of 4: the Xiongnu are badly diminished but not shattered, still capable of offensive action.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-119": "In 119 BCE, Emperor Wu mounted the largest offensive yet: Wei Qing and Huo Qubing each commanded 50,000 cavalry advancing along separate routes deep into the Gobi Desert. Wei Qing's force forced the chanyu to flee north of the Gobi; Huo Qubing's army pursued Xiongnu forces to Lake Baikal, killing approximately 70,000 men. Total Xiongnu losses numbered 80,000–90,000 combatants. The Encyclopaedia Iranica notes the Xiongnu were \"expelled to the north of the Gobi in 121 and 119 BCE.\" Score of 4: the confederation has lost most of its southern territories, suffered enormous manpower and livestock losses, and can no longer threaten Han's core territory.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-121": "In 121 BCE, General Huo Qubing led a rapid cavalry strike that swept through five Xiongnu kingdoms in the Hexi Corridor in just six days. The Xiongnu Hunye King surrendered with 40,000 men. Han established four commanderies in the Hexi Corridor, garrisoned with Chinese settlers. This severed the Xiongnu from their Qiang allies to the south and eliminated their access to the western oases of the Tarim Basin — a critical economic blow. Score of 5: a major strategic retrenchment — the Xiongnu have now lost both the Ordos (127 BCE) and the Hexi Corridor (121 BCE) and are being pushed back toward their Mongolian core.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-127": "In 127 BCE, Han general Wei Qing led a campaign that recaptured the entire Ordos Loop — the fertile territory north of the Yellow River bend that the Xiongnu had held since Modu retook it from the Qin. The Ordos was economically and symbolically vital: it was where the Xiongnu had first established their confederation and from where they had staged generations of raids into China. The Han immediately settled it with Chinese farmers and began fortifying the region. Score of 6: the first concrete strategic setback — loss of the Ordos marks a transition from resilience to retreat.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-130": "By 130 BCE the Han-Xiongnu War has been underway for three years. Wei Qing wins Han's first victories — raids on the sacred Xiongnu meeting-site at Longcheng (129 BCE) and north of Yanmen Pass (128 BCE). The Xiongnu have suffered their first real setbacks in decades but have lost no major territory: the Ordos, Hexi Corridor, and Mongolian plateau remain under their control. The chanyu (now Yizhixie, r. 126–114 BCE) can still muster massive cavalry forces. Score of 7: the Xiongnu remain formidable and Han victories are tactically significant but have not yet translated into strategic loss.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-133": "In 133 BCE, Emperor Wu of Han orchestrated a 300,000-man ambush at Mayi, luring Junchen Chanyu's 100,000-strong army toward a trap. Junchen Chanyu grew suspicious and withdrew before the trap was sprung. The failed ambush permanently ended the heqin peace policy (in place since 198 BCE) and committed both sides to open warfare. Score of 8: the last year in which the Xiongnu can be said to be fully dominant and unchallenged. Han has definitively resolved to destroy them through military means, though the Xiongnu are still undefeated, control vast territory, and are extracting tribute.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-161": "Laoshang is succeeded by Junchen (r. 160–126 BCE), who continues the heqin tribute relationship with Han and maintains the confederation's territorial extent. However, Junchen fails to establish the level of authority Modu and Laoshang commanded: repeated raids by subordinate Xiongnu kings continue despite official peace agreements, suggesting that the chanyu's control over his own tribal leaders is imperfect. The Han meanwhile quietly begin extensive horse-breeding programmes and administrative preparations for eventual military confrontation.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-170": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — Laoshang's reign at maximum territorial extent. The -170 datapoint correctly captures the midpoint of Laoshang's reign (r. 174–161 BCE) — the period of maximum Xiongnu territorial control. Xiongnu hegemony stretches from western Manchuria to the Pamirs, encompassing the Mongolian Plateau, much of Siberia, the Tarim Basin oases, and the Ordos. The Han dynasty continues paying tribute (silk, grain, wine, Chinese princesses) and is diplomatically subordinate. Britannica describes the confederation ruling \"a territory that extended from western Manchuria to the Pamirs,\" making it one of the largest nomadic empires in history to this point.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-174": "Modu dies in 174 BCE and is succeeded by his son Laoshang. Laoshang immediately defeats the Yuezhi decisively, killing their king and driving the entire Yuezhi people westward into Bactria. He also leads a 140,000-strong cavalry raid that penetrated to the imperial retreat at Yong (166 BCE), within striking distance of the Han capital Chang'an. Under his reign, the Xiongnu extend control over the Tarim Basin's urban oases, gaining access to agricultural surpluses without fighting China. EBSCO notes Laoshang \"completed the consolidation of the Xiongnu Empire and reunited all the tribes of Mongolia.\"",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-176": "In 176 BCE, Modu wrote an extraordinary letter to the Han Emperor Wen — preserved in the Shiji — reporting his conquest of 26 nations, the westward expulsion of the Yuezhi, the annexation of the Wusun and Loufan peoples, and control of all territory north of the Great Wall. He dictates terms: increased tribute, renewed marriage alliances, and Han acceptance of Xiongnu supremacy. The Han, unable to match Xiongnu cavalry on the steppe, complied. By this point the Xiongnu confederation extends from Korea in the east to the Aral Sea in the west, and from Lake Baikal in the north to Tibet and the Karakorum in the south.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-200": "In 200 BCE, Emperor Gaozu personally led a campaign against the Xiongnu who were besieging Taiyuan. Modu ambushed him at Baideng (near modern Datong, Shanxi), surrounding the emperor's 300,000-man force with Xiongnu cavalry for seven days. Gaozu escaped only by bribing Modu's wife. The Han emperor agreed to recognise the Xiongnu as an equal state, pay annual tribute in silk, grain, and luxury goods, and provide a Han princess as consort to the chanyu. For two centuries, Han effectively paid tribute to the Xiongnu — a reversal of the traditional Chinese world-order.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-208": "Within a year of seizing power, Modu launches a surprise offensive against the Donghu Confederation to the east — previously the Xiongnu's overlords who had extracted tribute. He annihilates the Donghu military, absorbs their tribes, and seizes their territory from eastern Mongolia to Manchuria. He simultaneously drives the Yuezhi from the Hexi Corridor and retakes the Ordos region from the dissolving Qin state. Within roughly two years of taking power, Modu has created an empire stretching from eastern Manchuria to western Gansu. The Xiongnu are now unambiguously a steppe empire and the dominant power of Inner Asia.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-209": "In 209 BCE, Touman's son Modu engineers a meticulously planned coup — training his followers to fire on command at any target, including Touman himself. He assassinates his father, kills his stepbrother (the intended heir), and purges his rivals. The Qin dynasty is simultaneously collapsing from internal revolt (Qin falls 207 BCE), eliminating the greatest external pressure on the Xiongnu. Modu immediately organises the army on a decimal principle (units of 10, 100, 1,000, 10,000) and creates a dual command structure. Score of 5: the institutional foundation is in place, the armies are reorganising, and the Qin threat has evaporated.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-215": "In 215 BCE, Qin Shi Huang dispatched General Meng Tian with a 300,000-strong army into the Ordos region. The campaign was decisively successful: Touman's forces were routed and expelled from the Ordos Loop — the most strategically valuable Xiongnu grazing territory — and driven approximately 1,000 li northward. The Xiongnu lose not merely territory but their primary economic resource: the Ordos, which allowed them to supplement pastoralism with raiding and trade. This is the lowest point of the pre-Modu Xiongnu — expelled from their homeland, leaderless in the Mongolian north.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-220": "Touman (r. c. 220–209 BCE) is the earliest named chanyu — the title denoting supreme ruler of the Xiongnu. His assumption of this title represents a qualitative leap in Xiongnu political organisation: he creates the foundational structure of left-wing and right-wing kings flanking a central chanyu command. Grokipedia's Chanyu article notes that Touman \"unified disparate clans through martial prowess and strategic retreats, establishing a hierarchical system that subordinated weaker tribes.\" However, Touman's empire is still structurally immature and he is defeated by Qin forces (215 BCE).",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-250": "By 250 BCE the Xiongnu are still a constellation of loosely affiliated clans rather than an organised empire. Touman — the first named chanyu — does not begin his reign until approximately 220 BCE. At 250 BCE no supreme ruler is attested in Chinese sources, and the Xiongnu lack the centralised military hierarchy that will define the Modu era. Score of 3: a group growing in cohesion and threat, but remaining politically fragmented without the institutional framework that Touman and Modu will create. The original score of 5 significantly overstates their condition.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-300": "By 300 BCE, three of the seven Warring States (Zhao, Yan, Qin) share borders with Xiongnu territory and have begun building defensive fortifications — precursors to the Great Wall — specifically in response to Xiongnu raids. By 300 BCE they are a consistent raiding force capable of pressuring multiple Chinese states simultaneously. Score of 2: an emergent but pre-confederation tribal alliance — significant enough to reshape Chinese border policy, but not yet politically unified under a supreme chanyu. A score of 0 is inappropriate here as 0 is reserved for the terminus of the empire.",
  "Xiongnu Empire|-318": "The earliest datable reference to the Xiongnu in Chinese historical sources is a 318 BCE border conflict. At this point the Xiongnu are one of many Hu (nomadic) peoples on the steppe frontier — not yet a unified confederation. They lack a named supreme ruler and operate as loosely coordinated tribal clans in the Mongolian Plateau and Ordos region. Score of 1: a culturally and militarily significant but politically inchoate tribal grouping, recognisable enough to be mentioned in state documents but not yet constituting an empire by any meaningful definition.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1271": "In 1271, Kublai Khan formally claims the Mandate of Heaven and issues the Proclamation of the Dynastic Name, naming the new dynasty \"Great Yuan\" — derived from the I Ching, meaning \"origin of the universe.\" He moves the capital to Dadu (Khanbaliq, modern Beijing), the first Chinese dynasty to make Beijing its primary capital. After the fall of the fortress city of Xiangyang in 1273 (following a five-year siege aided by Iraqi trebuchet engineers from the Ilkhanate), Kublai appoints Bayan of the Baarin as supreme commander for the southern campaign. The Yuan controls all of northern China, Mongolia, Korea, and Tibet.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1279": "On 19 March 1279, the Yuan navy annihilates the remnant Song fleet at the Battle of Yamen. The seven-year-old last Song emperor Zhao Bing drowns when his loyal official Lu Xiufu jumps into the sea carrying the child. The Yuan becomes the first non-Han Chinese dynasty to rule all of China proper — unifying a country divided since the 9th century CE. The Grand Canal is rebuilt and extended to connect Beijing and Hangzhou. The Silk Road trade network is at its peak; a single administrative system (the Pax Mongolica) connects China to Persia, the Black Sea, and Europe for the first time. Marco Polo arrives at Kublai's court around this time.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1285": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — by c. 1283–1287 the Yuan reaches its absolute institutional and commercial zenith. The empire encompasses roughly 4.25 million square miles: all of China, Mongolia, Korea, Tibet, northern Vietnam and Burma (tributaries after campaigns of 1277–1287), and the Ryukyus. The Grand Canal is complete. Paper currency (jiaochao) circulates throughout the empire. The port of Quanzhou is described by contemporaries as one of the greatest ports in the world. Kublai promotes religious tolerance: Tibetan Buddhism, Taoism, Islam, Nestorian Christianity, and Confucianism all receive state patronage. The Yuan establishes 20,166 public schools.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1290": "After 1281, the cumulative drain of failed overseas campaigns becomes visible. The second invasion of Japan (1281) — the largest naval expedition in history to that point, with over 4,400 ships and up to 140,000 troops — is destroyed by a typhoon (the \"kamikaze,\" divine wind). Vietnam defeats Mongol invasions three times (1257, 1284–85, 1287–88). These failures drain the treasury without yielding territory. Paper currency becomes overprinted, causing inflation. Kublai's favourite wife Chabi dies in 1281; his chosen heir Zhenjin dies in 1285; Kublai retreats into food, alcohol, and depression. Score of 7: still the most powerful state on Earth but internal stress is now measurable.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1300": "Temür Khan (r. 1294–1307), Kublai's grandson, proves \"an effective and able emperor\" (Study.com): he suppresses rebellions in southern China and Korea, fights large-scale corruption, maintains Kublai's institutions, concludes peace with the Chagatai Khanate's Kaidu (reducing the western military burden), and conducts diplomatic missions to Southeast Asia. He institutes economic reforms reducing taxation on peasants. Score of 7: a capable but consolidating ruler who has halted expansion and is managing an inherited empire — the last moment before the succession chaos begins.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1310": "Temür Khan dies in February 1307 without a surviving male heir. Two rival factions clash at court until Külüg Khan (Wuzong, r. 1307–11) emerges victorious with military support from Central Asian Mongol forces. His reign is marked by financial mismanagement — he rewards his military backers from the treasury, dramatically increasing state expenditure. His brother Ayurbarwada (Renzong, r. 1311–20) succeeds relatively peacefully and reinstates the civil service examination system in 1313. The original score of 9 — placing 1310 as the Yuan's highest rating — is dramatically wrong. By 1310, Kublai has been dead 16 years and the dynasty has burned through three emperors in a succession crisis.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1320": "Ayurbarwada Buyantu Khan (Renzong, r. 1311–1320) restores the civil service examination system, promotes Confucian scholarship, and translates Chinese classical texts. His reign represents a brief cultural renaissance under Mongol rule. However, his successor Gegeen Khan (Yingzong, r. 1320–1323) is assassinated in the \"Southern Slope Incident\" — killed by a clique of Mongol nobles who resist his pro-Chinese reforms. Five more emperors will reign in the next decade. Score of 6: intact territory and commercial activity but with the succession problem now lethal — emperors are literally being murdered.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1330": "By 1330 the Yuan has had eight emperors since Kublai's death in 1294 — an average reign of under five years. Tugh Temür (Wenzong, r. 1328–1332, reigning twice) is personally cultured: he writes poetry, paints, and sponsors literary compilations. But his reigns involve outright civil war: the \"War of the Two Capitals\" (1328) sees rival factions backing different claimants with mass military violence. The four-class social hierarchy — Mongols at top, Central Asians second, northern Chinese third, southern Chinese lowest — generates chronic ethnic resentment. Tax revenues fall as corrupt officials collect for themselves rather than remitting to the centre.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1340": "Toghon Temür (Huizong, r. 1333–1368) ascends as a teenager; real power rests with ministers and factions. From the late 1330s, cascading crises strike simultaneously: the Black Death kills enormous numbers as it spreads along the Mongol trade routes through Central Asia into China; repeated droughts, locust plagues, and Yellow River floods devastate northern China; crop failures produce famine. The government cannot finance effective disaster relief because the treasury has been plundered by civil wars and corrupt officials. Wikipedia records that \"from the late 1340s onwards, people in the countryside suffered from frequent natural disasters… and the government's lack of effective policy led to a loss of popular support.\"",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1350": "The Red Turban Rebellion, sparked in 1351 when conscripted Yellow River dike labourers under Han Shantong rise in revolt citing Buddhist prophecies of Yuan collapse, explodes into a nationwide uprising within months. By 1350–1352, rebel forces control Kaifeng (where a restored Song dynasty is proclaimed) and ravage the Yangtze basin. Zhu Yuanzhang, the future founder of the Ming dynasty, emerges as a rebel commander from 1352. The Yuan government deploys capable commanders and temporarily suppresses major rebel centres, but Emperor Toghon Temür then dismisses his best general (Chancellor Toghtoʿa, who had stabilised the military situation) in 1354 due to court intrigue, allowing the rebellion to recover and intensify.",
  "Yuan Dynasty|1368": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — Zhu Yuanzhang declares the Ming dynasty (23 January 1368) and dispatches his general Xu Da northward. Toghon Temür flees north to Shangdu in September 1368 without a battle as Ming forces approach; Shangdu itself falls shortly after. The 97-year Yuan dynasty in China is over. The Mongol ruling house retreats to the Mongolian steppe, where it continues as the \"Northern Yuan\" dynasty, but its claim to rule China is permanently broken. Zhu Yuanzhang demolishes the Yuan palaces in Beijing. Score of 0: the Yuan as a Chinese dynasty has ceased to exist.",
  "Zand Empire|1747": "After Nader Shah's assassination in June 1747, the Zand tribe — which Nader had displaced from Luristan to Khorasan — returns home under Karim Khan's leadership. Karim participates in the multi-sided struggle for control of western Iran, allying with Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari and setting up a puppet Safavid claimant, Shah Ismail III, in Isfahan. He is one of many regional strongmen; the Qajars, Durranis, Afsharids, and Bakhtiari are all competing simultaneously. Score of 1: pre-dynasty — there is no \"Zand Empire\" yet, only a tribal warlord among many.",
  "Zand Empire|1751": "In January 1751, Karim Khan defeats and eliminates his co-regent Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari in battle, assumes the title of Wakil al-Dawla (\"Deputy of the State\"), and takes control of western and central Persia including Isfahan. He still faces serious rivals: the Qajar leader Mohammad Hasan Khan in the north, Azad Khan Afghan in Azerbaijan, and Afsharid remnants in the east. Score of 3: a newly consolidated western power with much of Iran still contested.",
  "Zand Empire|1759": "Karim Khan's commander Sheikh-Ali Khan Zand defeats and kills the Qajar leader Mohammad Hassan Khan near Tehran on 14 February 1759, eliminating the most dangerous single rival. Karim Khan holds Agha Mohammad Khan (Mohammad Hassan's son, the future Qajar dynast) as a hostage in Shiraz — a fact with enormous consequences for 1779. With the Qajar threat neutralised, Karim Khan controls all of Iran except Khorasan. He begins consolidating Shiraz as a capital and planning his building programme. Score of 5: a state that has just won its decisive existential contest and is consolidating into peaceful governance.",
  "Zand Empire|1760": "By 1760, Karim Khan has defeated all major rivals and controls all of Iran south of Khorasan. The Encyclopaedia Iranica confirms \"by 1764/1178 AH, he had successfully established rule in all of Iran with the exception of Khorasan.\" Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar sits as a hostage in Shiraz. Trade agreements with the British East India Company at Bushehr are being negotiated. The building programme for Shiraz is being planned. Score of 6: a functioning, consolidated state at near-peak territorial extent — the original score of 5 implies continued uncertainty; at this point the dynasty's security is genuinely established.",
  "Zand Empire|1770": "From 1765–1779, Wikipedia describes this as \"the zenith of Zand rule.\" By 1770, Karim Khan's building programme has transformed Shiraz: the Arg citadel, Vakil Mosque, Vakil Bazaar, Vakil Bathhouse, and several gardens are under construction or complete. He has renovated the tombs of Hafez and Saadi. Trade is reviving; religious tolerance is practised; the country is at peace. Karim Khan does not use the title of Shah — ruling instead as the People's Advocate (Vakil-ol-Roʾaya) — and this genuine humility contributes to his popular legitimacy.",
  "Zand Empire|1775": "In 1775, Karim Khan lays siege to Basra — the Ottoman port city on the Persian Gulf — in response to Ottoman border provocations and in a bid to revive Safavid-era trade routes. The siege takes a full year. It is the Zand dynasty's largest external military operation and the farthest projection of Zand power beyond Iran. Score of 9: the empire at the height of its external ambition — the siege is underway, internal Iran is peaceful and prosperous, and Karim Khan is at the peak of his power and reputation.",
  "Zand Empire|1776": "ABSOLUTE PEAK — in 1776, after a year's siege, Karim Khan's forces capture Basra. This is the maximum territorial extent of the Zand dynasty: all of Iran except Khorasan, plus the Caucasian nominal suzerainty and now occupied Basra. Iran is peaceful and prosperous. Trade with Britain is active. Shiraz is being rebuilt into a beautiful capital. Karim Khan holds the most dangerous potential rival (Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar) safely as a hostage. The Oxford Dictionary of Islam's verdict — \"the most humane Iranian ruler of the Islamic era\" — reflects the genuine popular legitimacy that this period represents.",
  "Zand Empire|1779": "Karim Khan dies of tuberculosis on 1 March 1779. On the same day, Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar — who had been a hostage in Shiraz for sixteen years — escapes north to begin consolidating Qajar power. What follows is among the most chaotic succession collapses in Iranian history: Zaki Khan (Karim's half-brother) seizes power, rules for approximately 100 days with great cruelty, and is murdered by his own court. Abol-Fath Khan then rules for approximately 70 days before being deposed. Sadeq Khan declares himself king. Wikipedia confirms: \"civil war broke out once more, and none of his descendants was able to rule the country as effectively as he had.\"",
  "Zand Empire|1785": "By 1785, five Zand rulers have cycled through in six years (Zaki Khan, Abol-Fath Khan, Sadeq Khan, Ali-Morad Khan). The Encyclopaedia Iranica notes that none of Karim Khan's five successors formally adopted his title; they ruled as nominal khans rather than as legitimate shahs. Karim's careful hostage policy is destroyed, and Agha Mohammad Khan is now building a formidable army in northern Iran. Basra has been abandoned. Score of 3: a dynasty that retains control of its core territory but is visibly bleeding legitimacy and military capability.",
  "Zand Empire|1789": "Jafar Khan Zand (r. 1785–1789), who had made a genuine effort to stabilise the dynasty, is assassinated in 1789. The 17-year-old Lotf Ali Khan — a grand-nephew of Karim Khan — seizes the throne from Shiraz. He is brave, energetic, and personally courageous, but faces the full weight of Agha Mohammad Khan's consolidated Qajar army and constant betrayals within his own court. Haji Ibrahim, the mayor of Shiraz and Lotf Ali Khan's own grand vizier, will defect to the Qajars and surrender Shiraz in 1791–1792. Score of 2: a dynasty in its terminal phase — a young leader fighting desperately against overwhelming odds.",
  "Zand Empire|1794": "ABSOLUTE TROUGH — Lotf Ali Khan continues his resistance against the Qajars despite the fall of Shiraz (1792). He recaptures Kerman in 1794 but holds it for only four months before Agha Mohammad Khan's forces are admitted by treachery. He is captured, blinded, and cruelly tortured before being brought to Tehran for execution in November 1794. With his death, the Zand dynasty is extinguished. Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar goes on to be formally crowned Shah in 1796, founding the Qajar dynasty. Score of 0: the political entity has ended.",
  "Teotihuacan|-100": "In the second and first centuries BCE Teotihuacan was one of several growing settlements in the Basin of Mexico, overshadowed by Cuicuilco in the south. Its springs, fertile land and nearby obsidian sources gave it advantages, but it was still a regional centre, so its power stood low on its own later scale.",
  "Teotihuacan|-50": "During the first century BCE the settlement expanded rapidly, drawing farmers from across the eastern Basin of Mexico. Larger public buildings began to appear, and Teotihuacan started to rival Cuicuilco, though it still lacked the monumental core and planned layout that would define it in the following centuries.",
  "Teotihuacan|1": "At the start of the Tzacualli phase Teotihuacan was becoming the largest settlement in the basin, as volcanic activity, including eruptions of Popocatépetl, displaced people from the south and east. Much of the basin’s population was concentrating in the city, giving it a demographic weight that no rival could match.",
  "Teotihuacan|100": "The Pyramid of the Sun, some 65 metres high, rose during the Tzacualli phase, and early stages of the Pyramid of the Moon and the Avenue of the Dead were laid out. Monumental building on this scale shows a state already able to mobilise very large workforces for years at a time.",
  "Teotihuacan|200": "Work on the Ciudadela and the Feathered Serpent Pyramid began around this time, accompanied by the sacrifice of perhaps 200 people buried in its foundations. The city’s grid was spreading across some 20 square kilometres, and its rulers commanded labour and ideology on a scale unmatched in Mesoamerica.",
  "Teotihuacan|250": "By about 250 CE Teotihuacan held perhaps 100,000 people, who increasingly lived in the walled apartment compounds that would house most of them for centuries. Some scholars see a shift towards more collective government at about this time. The city dominated the Basin of Mexico and the neighbouring valleys.",
  "Teotihuacan|350": "In the fourth century the city stood near its height, with workshops exporting obsidian and pottery, enclaves of migrants from Oaxaca and the Gulf Coast living within it, and painted murals decorating its compounds. Teotihuacan goods and styles circulated widely, reflecting a broad commercial and political reach across Mesoamerica.",
  "Teotihuacan|378": "In January 378 Siyaj K’ak’ arrived at Tikal, the reigning Maya king died the same day, and a new ruler linked to a lord called Spearthrower Owl was installed. Whether or not this was a direct conquest, it marks the furthest documented projection of Teotihuacan’s influence and the height of its prestige.",
  "Teotihuacan|426": "K’inich Yax K’uk’ Mo’ founded the royal dynasty of Copán in 426 and was later portrayed with Teotihuacan-style insignia, showing how Maya rulers drew legitimacy from the great city. Teotihuacan’s influence in the Maya region and its long-distance exchange networks remained near their peak in the early fifth century.",
  "Teotihuacan|475": "In the later fifth century Teotihuacan was still the largest city in Mesoamerica, but signs of change appear. Its presence in the Maya region weakened and the population may have begun a slow decline. Its power remained considerable, though less far-reaching than at the height of its long-distance influence.",
  "Teotihuacan|525": "In the early sixth century the city’s foreign ties were fading and its population was probably shrinking. Strains within the elite, and possibly drought and economic pressure, were growing. Teotihuacan remained the leading centre of central Mexico, but its ability to project influence beyond the region had clearly declined.",
  "Teotihuacan|550": "Around 550 the temples and elite buildings along the Avenue of the Dead and in the Ciudadela were burned and their sculptures smashed, probably in an internal revolt against the ruling elite. The state collapsed, the population fell sharply, and only a reduced settlement survived among the ruins.",
  "Inca Empire|1438": "According to the traditional chronology, Cusi Yupanqui defeated the Chanka attack on Cusco and took power as Pachacuti. The victory turned a regional kingdom into a conquering state, but its rule still extended over only part of the southern Peruvian highlands, so its power stood well below its later peak.",
  "Inca Empire|1450": "Pachacuti rebuilt Cusco as an imperial capital, reorganised religion and the nobility, and extended Inca rule over the central highlands and the wealthy Colla and Lupaca kingdoms around Lake Titicaca. The state was growing quickly, but its institutions and frontiers were still taking shape at this stage of the reign.",
  "Inca Empire|1463": "In the traditional chronology Pachacuti gave command of the armies to his son Túpac Inca Yupanqui, who campaigned northwards through the highlands towards Ecuador, subduing the Cañari and other peoples after hard fighting. The empire now spanned much of the central Andes, but many recent conquests remained unsettled and rebellious.",
  "Inca Empire|1471": "Shortly after conquering the Kingdom of Chimor, the great coastal state of northern Peru, Túpac Inca Yupanqui succeeded Pachacuti as Sapa Inca. The absorption of Chimor’s wealth, craftsmen and irrigated valleys greatly strengthened the empire, which now controlled most of the coast and highlands of present-day Peru.",
  "Inca Empire|1480": "Túpac Inca Yupanqui’s southern campaigns carried Inca rule through Bolivia into north-west Argentina and central Chile, where the advance halted near the Maule River against Mapuche resistance. Roads, storehouses and administrative centres spread across the conquered lands, and the empire approached its final territorial extent.",
  "Inca Empire|1493": "Túpac Inca Yupanqui died and Huayna Capac succeeded him after a contested transition among royal factions. The new ruler spent his first years consolidating control, suppressing revolts and visiting the provinces. The empire’s institutions functioned well, but the succession showed how vulnerable the state was at every change of ruler.",
  "Inca Empire|1500": "Under Huayna Capac the empire was at its height of administrative integration, with the four quarters governed through decimal units, khipu records, labour tribute and resettled colonists. Royal estates were built near Cusco and in the north, and the empire’s population may have numbered around ten million.",
  "Inca Empire|1515": "Huayna Capac’s long northern campaigns subdued the Caranqui and other peoples of highland Ecuador and pushed the frontier into southern Colombia. Ruling largely from Tumebamba and Quito, he presided over the empire at its greatest territorial extent, stretching some 4,000 kilometres along the Andes.",
  "Inca Empire|1527": "Around this date Huayna Capac died in the north of an epidemic, probably smallpox introduced by Europeans, and his designated heir is said to have died as well. The empire remained intact but faced a disputed succession between Huáscar in Cusco and Atahualpa with the northern armies.",
  "Inca Empire|1529": "Rivalry between Huáscar and Atahualpa turned into open civil war. Armies of tens of thousands fought across the highlands, the provinces divided, and royal kin groups in Cusco were split. The empire’s cohesion, built on loyalty to a single Sapa Inca, was gravely weakened.",
  "Inca Empire|1532": "Atahualpa’s generals defeated and captured Huáscar near Cusco, but in November Francisco Pizarro seized Atahualpa at Cajamarca. The empire now had a captive ruler, a shattered royal family and provinces whose lords looked for chances to escape Inca control, leaving it with little power to resist.",
  "Inca Empire|1533": "After receiving an immense ransom of gold and silver, the Spaniards executed Atahualpa in July 1533 and entered Cusco in November with Andean allies, installing Manco Inca as a puppet. The imperial state ended, although resistance continued in the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba until 1572.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1450": "According to the date followed in the register, the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca accepted the Great Law of Peace around this time, ending feuds among themselves. The league was a defensive and ceremonial union of farming nations, with little power yet beyond their own homelands in present-day upstate New York.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1600": "By the end of the sixteenth century the five nations were a well-established league of perhaps 20,000 people, living in large palisaded towns. European goods were beginning to arrive through trade, and the league was emerging as a significant regional power, rivalled by the Huron Confederacy to the north.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1634": "Dutch traders at Fort Orange supplied the Mohawk with metal goods and later firearms in exchange for beaver pelts. But a smallpox epidemic in 1634 probably killed around half of the population, and the league turned to mourning wars to replace its dead, holding its power at a modest level.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1649": "Haudenosaunee war parties, armed with Dutch guns, destroyed the principal towns of the Huron Confederacy in 1649, dispersing its people and killing Jesuit missionaries. Thousands of captives were adopted. The victory removed the main rival in the northern fur trade and made the league the dominant military power of the region.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1656": "After breaking the Neutral Confederacy around 1651, the league defeated the Erie Nation by about 1656. Its warriors raided from the St Lawrence to the Ohio country, and adopted captives swelled its numbers. The league was now feared by New France and by Native peoples across the eastern Great Lakes.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1677": "The Covenant Chain agreed at Albany with Governor Edmund Andros of New York made the league the principal Native ally of the English colonies and gave it a claim to speak for many dependent peoples. Combining military reputation with diplomatic leverage, the league stood at the height of its power.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1689": "French invasions of Seneca country in 1687 and repeated warfare took a heavy toll. Haudenosaunee warriors struck back with the raid on Lachine in 1689, but war with New France in the 1690s, including Frontenac’s attack on Onondaga in 1696, steadily drained the league’s population and resources.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1701": "Exhausted by war, the league made the Great Peace of Montreal with France and its Native allies and, in the same year, an agreement with the English at Albany. Its new policy of neutrality between the empires allowed it to remain a major power through diplomacy rather than warfare.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1722": "Around 1722 the Tuscarora, displaced from North Carolina after the Tuscarora War, were adopted into the league, which became the Six Nations. Playing France and Britain against each other, the Haudenosaunee kept wide influence, though their own numbers were far below those of the colonies around them.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1754": "The Albany Congress met to renew the alliance with the Six Nations as war with France approached. Mohawk warriors fought beside the British at the Battle of Lake George in 1755, but the league’s room to balance between the empires was narrowing as colonial populations grew rapidly.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1768": "After the British conquest of New France removed the French counterweight, the league ceded vast lands south of the Ohio River at the Treaty of Fort Stanwix, much of them hunted by other peoples. It remained a valued British ally, but its bargaining power was clearly declining.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1779": "The American Revolution split the league: the Grand Council covered its fire in 1777, and its nations fought each other at Oriskany. In 1779 the Sullivan Expedition burned some forty towns and their crops, causing famine and flight, and the league’s power collapsed in its homeland.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1784": "The Treaty of Paris ignored Britain’s Native allies, and the Treaty of Fort Stanwix of 1784 forced large land cessions in New York. Joseph Brant led many loyalist Haudenosaunee to the Grand River in Ontario under the Haldimand Proclamation, dividing the league between two countries and two council fires.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1799": "Confined to shrinking reservations in New York and to the Grand River lands, the Haudenosaunee faced poverty and social crisis. The Seneca prophet Handsome Lake began preaching his Code in 1799, founding the Longhouse religion and helping to renew community life, although political power remained very limited.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|1924": "After the Cayuga chief Deskaheh sought recognition of Six Nations sovereignty from the League of Nations, Canada replaced the traditional chiefs at Six Nations of the Grand River with an elected band council in 1924. The confederacy survived as a political and ceremonial institution without state recognition.",
  "Iroquois Confederacy|2025": "The Grand Council still meets at Onondaga, and the traditional chiefs at Six Nations of the Grand River. The Haudenosaunee issue their own passports, field national lacrosse teams and press land claims, giving the confederacy a modest but persistent standing as one of North America’s most assertive Indigenous nations.",
  "New Spain|1521": "Tenochtitlan fell to Hernán Cortés and his indigenous allies on 13 August 1521, and Mexico City began to rise on its ruins. Spanish rule was still a fragile conquest regime held by a few hundred conquistadors and dependent on allied indigenous lords, so the new colony’s power stood low.",
  "New Spain|1535": "The Crown appointed Antonio de Mendoza as first viceroy, establishing royal government over the conquistadors. The audiencia, the mint, friars’ missions and the first printing press followed. New Spain now had a stable administration, though its economy still rested mainly on indigenous tribute and labour.",
  "New Spain|1546": "Silver was discovered at Zacatecas, and Guanajuato followed soon after, opening the mining economy that would sustain New Spain for centuries. The Mixtón War had been won, but a devastating epidemic in 1545–1548 sharply reduced the indigenous population on which the colony depended.",
  "New Spain|1565": "Andrés de Urdaneta found the return route across the Pacific, opening the Manila galleon trade, and the Philippines came under New Spain. The patio process had boosted silver production, and the viceroyalty was becoming the economic centre of the Spanish Empire’s northern half.",
  "New Spain|1576": "A great epidemic of cocoliztli killed a large share of the remaining indigenous population, causing labour shortages across central Mexico. The Chichimeca War still disrupted the silver road. Despite steady mining output, the colony’s demographic base was shrinking, holding back its strength for decades.",
  "New Spain|1629": "Mexico City was flooded for about five years after heavy rains in 1629, and the indigenous population was near its lowest point. Historians debate whether New Spain suffered a seventeenth-century depression, but the colony grew more self-sufficient as haciendas, local trade and criollo society developed.",
  "New Spain|1692": "A food riot in Mexico City burned part of the viceregal palace, and Diego de Vargas began the reconquest of New Mexico after the Pueblo Revolt. Pirates had sacked Veracruz in 1683. The colony remained stable and prosperous overall, but its defences and governance showed clear weaknesses.",
  "New Spain|1765": "José de Gálvez arrived as inspector-general to begin the Bourbon reforms, after the loss of Havana in 1762 had exposed imperial weakness. A regular army and militias were created, the tobacco monopoly was set up, and royal revenues began to rise as silver production expanded.",
  "New Spain|1786": "The intendancy system divided New Spain into twelve intendancies under salaried officials, tightening royal control and increasing tax collection. Missions and presidios had pushed north into Alta California since 1769. Silver output, revenue and population were all rising strongly towards their colonial peak.",
  "New Spain|1800": "Around 1800 New Spain was at its height, with some six million inhabitants, record silver output from mines such as the Valenciana at Guanajuato, and Mexico City the largest city in the Americas. It provided the bulk of the revenue Spain drew from its American empire.",
  "New Spain|1808": "Napoleon’s seizure of the Spanish throne created a crisis of legitimacy. Spanish merchants in Mexico City overthrew Viceroy José de Iturrigaray to block criollo demands for self-government, while the Consolidation of 1804 had already strained landowners. The colony remained rich but politically divided and unstable.",
  "New Spain|1810": "On 16 September 1810 Miguel Hidalgo launched a mass insurrection at Dolores. His followers took Guanajuato and threatened Mexico City before being defeated, and the war damaged mines, haciendas and trade. Royal authority survived, but the colony’s economy and social order were severely shaken.",
  "New Spain|1815": "Royalist forces captured and executed José María Morelos, and the insurgency was reduced to guerrilla bands such as Vicente Guerrero’s. Spanish rule had been restored by military force, but at the cost of a militarised society, heavy debts and a sharply reduced silver output.",
  "New Spain|1821": "Agustín de Iturbide and Vicente Guerrero proclaimed the Plan of Iguala, Juan O’Donojú signed the Treaty of Córdoba, and Iturbide’s army entered Mexico City in September. After three centuries of Spanish rule, New Spain ceased to exist and was succeeded by the First Mexican Empire, with Iturbide soon crowned emperor.",
  "New France|1534": "Jacques Cartier explored the Gulf of St Lawrence and claimed the Gaspé for Francis I. His later voyages reached Stadacona and Hochelaga, but no lasting settlement followed. France’s presence was limited to a claim, a handful of voyages and visiting fishermen, so its power in North America was minimal.",
  "New France|1608": "Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec as a fur-trading post and soon allied with the Montagnais, Algonquin and Huron. The settlement had only a few dozen inhabitants and depended on a trading monopoly, but it gave France its first permanent foothold on the St Lawrence.",
  "New France|1629": "English privateers led by the Kirke brothers forced Champlain to surrender Quebec, cutting off the fragile colony. Although the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye returned it to France in 1632, the episode showed how weak and dependent New France remained, with fewer than a hundred settlers.",
  "New France|1642": "Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve and Jeanne Mance founded Ville-Marie, later Montreal, after Trois-Rivières in 1634. Jesuit missions worked among the Huron. Yet Iroquois raids, which destroyed Huronia in 1649, kept the small colony on the defensive and at times threatened its survival.",
  "New France|1663": "Louis XIV made New France a royal province with a governor, intendant and Sovereign Council. Royal troops, settlers and the filles du roi soon arrived, and Jean Talon promoted growth. With about 3,000 inhabitants, the colony was still small but now had firm royal backing.",
  "New France|1682": "René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle reached the mouth of the Mississippi and claimed Louisiana for Louis XIV, after Jolliet and Marquette’s voyage of 1673. French claims now stretched from the St Lawrence to the Gulf of Mexico, even if they rested on a few posts and Native alliances.",
  "New France|1690": "Governor Frontenac repulsed Sir William Phips’s attack on Quebec, and French and Native raids struck English frontier towns. War with England and the Iroquois strained the colony, but it held its ground and its network of western posts and alliances continued to expand.",
  "New France|1701": "The Great Peace of Montreal ended a century of war with the Iroquois Confederacy, and Antoine de Lamothe Cadillac founded Detroit. With Louisiana established at Biloxi in 1699, New France now controlled a vast network of alliances and posts across the interior of North America.",
  "New France|1713": "The Treaty of Utrecht ceded Acadia, Newfoundland and the Hudson Bay region to Britain. New France kept the St Lawrence, the Great Lakes and Louisiana, but lost important fisheries and fur territories, and Britain gained bases that later threatened the Gulf of St Lawrence.",
  "New France|1720": "France began building the fortress of Louisbourg on Île Royale to protect the Gulf of St Lawrence, and New Orleans had been founded in 1718. Decades of peace allowed the population, agriculture and fur trade of Canada to grow steadily under royal administration.",
  "New France|1745": "New England forces captured Louisbourg in 1745, though it was returned to France in 1748. The La Vérendrye family had pushed posts towards the prairies. New France held a wide sphere of influence, but the war exposed its vulnerability to British sea power.",
  "New France|1754": "The French built Fort Duquesne at the forks of the Ohio and forced George Washington to surrender at Fort Necessity. New France claimed its largest territory, from the Gulf of St Lawrence to the Gulf of Mexico, and with its Native allies won the opening campaigns of the war.",
  "New France|1758": "After victories at Oswego, Fort William Henry and Carillon, the tide turned. Louisbourg, Fort Frontenac and Fort Duquesne fell to the British, cutting off supply lines and the Ohio country. Blockade, poor harvests and inflation weakened the colony as British armies prepared to invade.",
  "New France|1759": "Fort Niagara fell, and on 13 September James Wolfe defeated Montcalm on the Plains of Abraham; both generals were killed and Quebec surrendered five days later. New France kept only Montreal and the interior posts, with little hope of reinforcement from France.",
  "New France|1760": "The Chevalier de Lévis won at Sainte-Foy in April, but British ships relieved Quebec, and three British armies converged on Montreal. On 8 September Governor Vaudreuil surrendered Montreal and all of Canada, ending French rule in the St Lawrence valley.",
  "New France|1763": "By the Treaty of Paris France ceded Canada and the lands east of the Mississippi to Britain, having already given western Louisiana to Spain. Only Saint-Pierre and Miquelon remained French. New France ceased to exist, and Canada became the British Province of Quebec.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1542": "Charles V created the viceroyalty through the New Laws, uniting the former governorates of New Castile and New Toledo under a viceroy at Lima. Royal authority existed mostly on paper: the conquerors held encomiendas, armed followers and the loyalty of the settlers, and the Neo-Inca State still resisted from Vilcabamba.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1546": "Viceroy Blasco Núñez Vela\u0027s attempt to enforce the New Laws provoked a revolt of encomenderos under Gonzalo Pizarro, who defeated and killed him at the Battle of Añaquito. For two years Peru was effectively ruled by rebels, and the Crown\u0027s new viceroyalty reached its lowest point.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1548": "Pedro de la Gasca, armed with royal pardons and a promise to soften the New Laws, won over most rebels and defeated Gonzalo Pizarro at Jaquijahuana. Pizarro\u0027s execution restored royal authority, while the discovery of silver at Potosí in 1545 had begun to give the viceroyalty an enormous source of revenue.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1569": "Francisco de Toledo arrived as viceroy after two decades of encomendero unrest, indigenous resistance and uncertain revenues. Potosí\u0027s richest surface ores were running out, and the colony still lacked a settled system of tribute and labour. His arrival opened the most important reorganisation in the viceroyalty\u0027s history.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1581": "By the end of Toledo\u0027s term the Neo-Inca State had been destroyed, indigenous villages had been resettled into reducciones, and the mita supplied thousands of workers to Potosí. Mercury amalgamation, using mercury from Huancavelica, had multiplied silver output, and the Crown\u0027s grip on Peru was firmer than ever before.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1600": "Around the turn of the century Potosí\u0027s silver output stood near its historic peak, and Peru was the richest part of the Spanish Empire. Lima, with its viceregal court, audiencia, archbishopric, university and Inquisition, governed nearly all of Spanish South America, and silver flowed to Seville and across the Pacific to Manila.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1650": "Silver output had fallen from its peak, Dutch raiders had threatened the coast, and demands for remittances to Spain strained the treasury. More revenue was spent in Peru itself on defence and administration, and creoles bought offices, so the viceroyalty remained wealthy but was less closely controlled from Madrid.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1687": "A severe earthquake struck Lima and Callao, just as a new wall around the capital was being completed against pirate raids. Mining output was low, the highland population had fallen sharply over the previous century, and the viceroyalty, though still Spain\u0027s main South American dominion, was in a prolonged slump.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1739": "After a first attempt in 1717, the Viceroyalty of New Granada was permanently re-established, removing present-day Colombia, Ecuador and Panama from Lima\u0027s authority. The loss reduced Peru\u0027s territory and revenues, although silver mining had begun to recover and Lima remained the commercial centre of the Pacific coast.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1746": "On 28 October a great earthquake devastated Lima, and the tsunami that followed destroyed the port of Callao, killing most of its inhabitants. Viceroy José Antonio Manso de Velasco directed the rebuilding, including the Real Felipe fortress, but the disaster drained resources at a time of growing competition from other ports.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1776": "The creation of the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata transferred Upper Peru and the mines of Potosí to Buenos Aires. Peru lost its greatest source of silver and much of its trade, and the free trade regulation of 1778 soon ended Lima\u0027s privileged control of Pacific commerce.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1781": "The rebellion of Túpac Amaru II, begun in 1780 against the corregidores and the burdens of the reparto and mita, swept the southern Andes before its leader was captured and executed in Cuzco. Its suppression cost tens of thousands of lives and left the viceroyalty secure but deeply divided.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1814": "Under Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal, Peru became the royalist bastion of South America. Its armies crushed revolutionary governments in Upper Peru and Quito and reconquered Chile after the Battle of Rancagua, while Lima stayed loyal. It was a last display of strength by a viceroyalty already reduced in size.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1821": "José de San Martín\u0027s expedition from Chile, landed in 1820, entered Lima, and on 28 July 1821 he proclaimed Peru\u0027s independence. Viceroy José de la Serna withdrew to Cuzco and the highlands, where the royalist army remained strong, but the viceroyalty had lost its capital and its coast.",
  "Viceroyalty of Peru|1824": "Simón Bolívar\u0027s forces defeated the royalists at Junín in August, and on 9 December Antonio José de Sucre won the Battle of Ayacucho, capturing Viceroy La Serna. The capitulation that followed ended the viceroyalty, although the royalist garrison of Callao held out until January 1826.",
  "Suriname|1667": "A fleet from Zeeland under Abraham Crijnssen captured the English colony’s main fort, renaming it Fort Zeelandia, and the Treaty of Breda confirmed Dutch possession. The colony was small and fragile, with a few dozen plantations, a departing English planter class and little capital to develop it.",
  "Suriname|1683": "The West India Company, the city of Amsterdam and Cornelis van Aerssen van Sommelsdijck formed the Society of Suriname. As governor, Van Aerssen made peace with the Indigenous peoples in 1686 and imposed order on planters and soldiers, laying the foundations of the plantation colony before mutineers killed him in 1688.",
  "Suriname|1712": "A French squadron under Jacques Cassard raided the colony and forced the planters to pay a heavy ransom, a blow to a growing economy. Maroon attacks on plantations were also increasing, but sugar exports were rising and new estates were spreading along the Suriname and Commewijne rivers.",
  "Suriname|1740": "Coffee, introduced in the early eighteenth century, had joined sugar as a major export, and hundreds of plantations lined the rivers. Fort Nieuw-Amsterdam was under construction to guard the river junction. Yet constant warfare against maroon communities in the rainforest drained money and soldiers and left planters insecure.",
  "Suriname|1760": "The colonial government signed a peace treaty with the Ndyuka, followed by treaties with the Saramaka in 1762 and the Matawai in 1767, recognising their freedom in the interior. Peace on the frontier, together with abundant credit from Amsterdam, allowed planters to expand production rapidly during the following decade.",
  "Suriname|1770": "Suriname stood at the height of its plantation boom, with several hundred estates producing sugar, coffee, cacao and cotton and tens of thousands of enslaved Africans working them. Amsterdam loans had fuelled heavy investment, making the colony one of the most valuable Dutch possessions, though its prosperity rested on extreme violence.",
  "Suriname|1773": "The Amsterdam credit crisis of 1772–1773 ruined many planters who had borrowed heavily, and plantations passed to creditors in the Netherlands. At the same time maroon groups led by Boni were attacking estates, forcing the colony to call in Dutch troops, form the Black Rangers and build a defensive cordon path.",
  "Suriname|1799": "During the French Revolutionary Wars a British force occupied Suriname, which surrendered without serious resistance. The Society of Suriname had been dissolved in 1795, and the plantation economy, burdened with absentee owners and debt, had long passed its peak, leaving the colony a minor prize in a global war.",
  "Suriname|1816": "Britain returned Suriname to the new Kingdom of the Netherlands after a second occupation from 1804. The colony was now governed from The Hague, and with the slave trade abolished the enslaved population slowly declined, while many plantations, especially coffee estates, were abandoned or merged into larger sugar estates.",
  "Suriname|1863": "Slavery was abolished on 1 July, freeing some 33,000 people, whose owners received compensation while the freed were bound to ten years of supervised contract labour. Many plantations closed in the following decades, and the colony’s economic weight within the Dutch empire fell far below that of the Dutch East Indies.",
  "Suriname|1873": "The first indentured labourers from British India arrived on the Lalla Rookh as the period of state supervision of former slaves ended. Asian contract workers, joined by Javanese from 1890, kept the surviving sugar estates running, but the colony remained poor and of modest importance to the Netherlands.",
  "Suriname|1916": "A bauxite company owned by the American firm Alcoa was founded to mine the deposits at Moengo. Mining gradually became the colony’s main source of export income, giving Suriname new strategic value and tying its economy more closely to the United States than to the declining plantations.",
  "Suriname|1942": "During the Second World War Suriname supplied a large share of the bauxite used by the United States for aircraft aluminium, and American troops were stationed there from late 1941 to protect the mines. The war brought prosperity and political awakening, and demands for self-government grew among the colony’s new parties.",
  "Suriname|1954": "The Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands made Suriname an autonomous country within the kingdom, with its own government and parliament. Politics organised along ethnic lines, with Creole and Hindustani parties in shifting coalitions, while bauxite and the Afobaka hydroelectric dam, completed in the 1960s, powered economic growth.",
  "Suriname|1975": "Suriname became independent on 25 November, with Johan Ferrier as president and Henck Arron as prime minister, backed by a large Dutch aid package. Sovereignty marked a high point of its political standing, but a large share of the population emigrated to the Netherlands around independence, fearing ethnic tension and economic uncertainty.",
  "Suriname|1980": "On 25 February sixteen sergeants overthrew the elected government, and Desi Bouterse emerged as the dominant figure of a military regime. Parliamentary democracy was suspended, and in December 1982 fifteen prominent critics were murdered, after which the Netherlands suspended development aid and the country grew increasingly isolated.",
  "Suriname|1986": "The interior war began between the National Army and the Jungle Commando of Ronnie Brunswijk, a former bodyguard of Bouterse. Fighting devastated Maroon villages in the east, including the Moiwana massacre of November 1986, and drove thousands of refugees into French Guiana while the economy collapsed under inflation and shortages.",
  "Suriname|1992": "A peace accord ended the interior war a year after elections had returned a civilian government under Ronald Venetiaan in 1991. Democratic institutions stabilised, Dutch aid resumed, and the country began a slow recovery, though the economy remained dependent on bauxite, gold and oil and vulnerable to price swings.",
  "Suriname|2010": "Desi Bouterse, despite his past as military ruler, was elected president by the National Assembly and governed until 2020. His presidency saw rising state debt and, after alumina refining ended in the mid-2010s, a severe economic crisis, while his conviction for the December murders came in 2019.",
  "Suriname|2025": "Suriname is a stable multi-party democracy of about 600,000 people, with a debt restructuring behind it and large offshore oil discoveries, made since 2020, under development. In 2025 Jennifer Geerlings-Simons became its first woman president. Its regional influence is modest, but oil revenues could transform the economy within a decade.",
  "United States|1776": "The Second Continental Congress declared the independence of thirteen colonies on 4 July, while British armies prepared to seize New York. The new United States had no national government beyond the Congress, no regular navy and only George Washington’s fragile Continental Army, and it depended on foreign help to survive.",
  "United States|1783": "The Treaty of Paris recognised American independence and drew a western border along the Mississippi, after the victory at Yorktown in 1781 won with French help. Yet under the Articles of Confederation Congress could not tax, the states quarrelled, and the republic was burdened with war debts and weak credit.",
  "United States|1789": "The Constitution took effect, and George Washington became the first president, with Alexander Hamilton as Treasury Secretary establishing national credit and a national bank. The federal government gained the power to tax, regulate commerce and raise armies, turning a loose confederation into a functioning state of some four million people.",
  "United States|1803": "President Thomas Jefferson bought the vast Louisiana Territory from Napoleon’s France for about 15 million dollars, roughly doubling the country’s area. The Lewis and Clark expedition soon explored the route to the Pacific. The United States was still a minor military power, but its territory and population were growing rapidly.",
  "United States|1848": "The Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo ended the Mexican–American War and transferred California and the Southwest, completing a continental territory after the annexation of Texas in 1845 and the Oregon settlement of 1846. The discovery of gold in California followed, but the question of slavery in the new lands deepened sectional conflict.",
  "United States|1861": "The election of Abraham Lincoln led eleven Southern states to secede and form the Confederate States of America, and war began at Fort Sumter in April. The country was split in two, yet the Union kept most of the population, industry and railways, which would decide the coming war.",
  "United States|1865": "Union victory ended the Civil War, and the Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery. Between about 620,000 and 750,000 soldiers had died, and Lincoln was assassinated in April. The federal government emerged far stronger, and Reconstruction began an uneven attempt to secure the rights of four million freed people.",
  "United States|1898": "Victory over Spain brought Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines, and Hawaii was annexed the same year, making the United States a colonial power in the Caribbean and the Pacific. It was already the largest industrial economy in the world, though its army remained small by European standards.",
  "United States|1918": "American troops, arriving in large numbers on the Western Front, helped defeat Germany, and Woodrow Wilson’s Fourteen Points framed the peace. The United States emerged as the world’s leading creditor, but the Senate rejected the Treaty of Versailles and membership of the League of Nations, keeping the country outside the new order.",
  "United States|1933": "The Great Depression had cut output sharply and left about a quarter of the workforce unemployed. Franklin D. Roosevelt took office and launched the New Deal, expanding federal power over banking, labour and welfare. Economic weakness and isolationism limited American influence abroad as dictatorships rose in Europe and Asia.",
  "United States|1945": "Victory over Germany and Japan, the latter after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, left the United States the strongest power on earth. It produced roughly half of the world’s manufacturing output, held a brief monopoly on nuclear weapons and led the founding of the United Nations and the Bretton Woods system.",
  "United States|1957": "The United States led NATO and a worldwide system of alliances and bases, but the Soviet launch of Sputnik exposed a technological challenge and ended its sense of invulnerability. At home the economy boomed, while the civil rights movement, after the Montgomery bus boycott, pressed for an end to segregation.",
  "United States|1973": "The withdrawal of American combat troops from Vietnam after the Paris Peace Accords, the end of the dollar’s convertibility into gold in 1971 and the oil shock of 1973 marked a period of relative decline. The Watergate scandal, which led to Richard Nixon’s resignation in 1974, deepened a crisis of confidence.",
  "United States|1991": "The dissolution of the Soviet Union ended the Cold War and left the United States the only superpower, a status underlined by the swift victory of an American-led coalition over Iraq in the Gulf War. Its economy, military reach and cultural influence were without rival anywhere in the world.",
  "United States|2001": "The terrorist attacks of 11 September killed nearly 3,000 people and led to the invasion of Afghanistan, followed by that of Iraq in 2003. The country still held unrivalled military and economic power, but long wars drained resources and damaged its standing, while China’s economy grew rapidly.",
  "United States|2008": "The financial crisis that began in the American housing market caused the deepest recession since the 1930s and spread worldwide. Barack Obama, elected that year as the first African American president, led the recovery, but the crisis strengthened doubts about American economic leadership and fed growing political polarisation.",
  "United States|2025": "The United States remains the world’s largest economy by nominal output and its leading military power, with about 340 million people. Strategic rivalry with China, sharp domestic polarisation and debates over its alliances and trade policies have raised questions about the durability of the international order it built after 1945.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1806": "Emperor Jacques I was assassinated near Port-au-Prince in October, and leaders of the south and west drafted a republican constitution that made the senate supreme. The new state controlled only part of Haiti, its army was divided, and the powerful northern general Henri Christophe refused to accept its limits.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1807": "After repulsing Christophe’s attack on Port-au-Prince in January, the senate elected Alexandre Pétion president in March. Haiti was now split between his republic in the west and south and Christophe’s northern state, and the two fought an inconclusive war that drained both of men and money.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1810": "General André Rigaud, returned from exile in France, broke away with the south and founded the State of South Haiti, leaving Pétion with little more than the west around Port-au-Prince. The republic had reached its weakest point, threatened both by Christophe in the north and by division within.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1812": "After Rigaud’s death in 1811 the south rejoined the republic, and Christophe’s siege of Port-au-Prince failed. Pétion’s land distributions were winning the loyalty of soldiers and smallholders, and the division of Haiti settled into an uneasy stalemate between the republic and the northern kingdom of Henri I.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1816": "Pétion supplied Simón Bolívar’s expedition from Les Cayes and Jacmel with arms and ships, asking that he free the slaves of the lands he liberated. A revised constitution made Pétion president for life, giving the republic greater stability and a modest standing in the Caribbean.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1818": "Pétion died in March, and the senate chose Jean-Pierre Boyer, commander of the presidential guard, as his successor. The transition was peaceful, the republic’s institutions held, and Boyer inherited a state that controlled the west and south of Haiti, though the northern kingdom remained stronger in arms.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1820": "Henri Christophe, paralysed by a stroke and facing revolt, shot himself in October, and Boyer marched north to reunite Haiti under the republic without major fighting. For the first time since 1806 the whole of the former French colony was governed from Port-au-Prince, greatly increasing the republic’s population and resources.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1822": "Boyer occupied Santo Domingo in February, weeks after its leaders had declared independence from Spain, bringing all of Hispaniola under the republic and abolishing slavery in the east. This was the territorial peak of the Haitian state, which now controlled the entire island with a large standing army.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1825": "Under threat from a French fleet, Boyer accepted the ordinance of Charles X recognising Haitian independence in return for an indemnity of 150 million francs and reduced customs duties. Recognition ended the fear of reconquest, but the debt and the loans raised to pay it crippled state finances for generations.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1838": "New treaties with France reduced the outstanding indemnity to 90 million francs and confirmed recognition of independence. Boyer’s government was stable but stagnant, burdened by debt and a large army, and liberal critics in the legislature increasingly attacked his authoritarian rule and the privileges of the mixed-race elite.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1842": "A great earthquake in May destroyed Cap-Haïtien and other towns in the north, killing thousands. The disaster came on top of years of economic stagnation and political discontent, and it weakened Boyer’s regime just as reformers in the south were preparing to challenge his long hold on power.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1843": "The Reform movement led by Charles Rivière-Hérard rose in the south, and Boyer resigned and sailed into exile in March after twenty-five years in power. The new government promised liberal reforms, but it inherited a divided army, empty finances and growing unrest among Dominicans in the east.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1844": "On 27 February the Dominican Republic proclaimed its independence in Santo Domingo, and Haitian attempts to reconquer it failed. Peasant uprisings in the south demanded land, Rivière-Hérard was overthrown in May, and the republic lost the east and entered a period of short, unstable presidencies under figurehead leaders.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1847": "After the death of President Jean-Baptiste Riché, the senate elected Faustin Soulouque, a little-known Black general it expected to control. Soulouque instead built up his own power base, and in April 1848 his forces massacred mixed-race opponents in Port-au-Prince, breaking the elite that had dominated the republic.",
  "Republic of Haiti (1806-1849)|1849": "On 26 August Soulouque proclaimed himself Emperor Faustin I, ending the republic founded in 1806 and creating the Second Empire of Haiti. The republican state disappeared after forty-three years, having reunited Haiti and ruled all of Hispaniola, but leaving behind a debt-burdened and divided country.",
  "Republic of Chile|1818": "Independence was proclaimed on 12 February, and the victory at Maipú in April secured central Chile for the patriots. The new state under Supreme Director Bernardo O’Higgins depended on San Martín’s Army of the Andes, faced royalist resistance in the south and had to build a navy from scratch.",
  "Republic of Chile|1823": "O’Higgins, whose authoritarian rule had alienated much of the elite, abdicated in January. Chile had helped carry the war of independence to Peru, but at home it entered years of political experiment, with short-lived constitutions, federalist schemes and frequent changes of government that weakened the young state.",
  "Republic of Chile|1830": "Conservative forces defeated the liberals at the Battle of Lircay, opening decades of conservative rule. Diego Portales, as minister, built a strong centralised state, disciplined the army and organised a civic guard, laying the foundations for the Constitution of 1833 and for Chile’s unusual political stability.",
  "Republic of Chile|1839": "General Manuel Bulnes won the Battle of Yungay in January, destroying the Peru–Bolivian Confederation that Chile had regarded as a threat to its security and trade. The victory confirmed Chile as an independent regional power and strengthened the conservative order, and Bulnes became president two years later.",
  "Republic of Chile|1866": "Chile and Peru, allied against a Spanish squadron in the Chincha Islands War, saw Valparaíso bombarded in March. Despite the damage, Chile was prospering from copper, silver and wheat exports, had enjoyed three decades of constitutional government and was building railways and telegraphs under its decennial presidents.",
  "Republic of Chile|1879": "A dispute with Bolivia over taxes on Chilean nitrate companies in Antofagasta led to the War of the Pacific against Bolivia and Peru. Chile occupied Antofagasta in February and, after the naval Battles of Iquique and Angamos, won command of the sea, opening the way to invasion.",
  "Republic of Chile|1884": "With Lima occupied, the Treaty of Ancón of 1883 and a truce with Bolivia in 1884 gave Chile Tarapacá and Antofagasta and control of Tacna and Arica. The Mapuche of Araucanía had also been subdued. Chile stood at the height of its regional power, enriched by nitrate taxes.",
  "Republic of Chile|1891": "A civil war between Congress, backed by the navy, and President José Manuel Balmaceda, backed by most of the army, killed thousands and ended with Balmaceda’s defeat and suicide. The victory of Congress opened the Parliamentary Republic, a period of weaker presidents, while the nitrate economy continued to grow.",
  "Republic of Chile|1914": "Nitrate exports, taxed by the state, still provided the bulk of government revenue, and Chile was among the most prosperous countries of Latin America. But the opening of the Panama Canal reduced the importance of the route around Cape Horn, and synthetic nitrates would soon threaten the economy.",
  "Republic of Chile|1931": "The Great Depression devastated Chile, with exports collapsing and unemployment soaring; it was among the hardest-hit countries in the world. The dictatorship of Carlos Ibáñez del Campo fell in July, and turmoil followed, including the short-lived Socialist Republic of 1932, before constitutional order returned.",
  "Republic of Chile|1952": "Under the Constitution of 1925 Chile had regained stable democratic government, and the Popular Front and its successors had promoted industry through the state agency CORFO. Copper, mined mainly by American companies, now dominated exports. In 1952 Carlos Ibáñez returned to the presidency, this time through election.",
  "Republic of Chile|1970": "Salvador Allende was elected president at the head of the Popular Unity coalition, promising a democratic road to socialism. Chile was a stable democracy with a growing state sector, but deep polarisation over land reform, nationalisation and the Cold War was about to test its institutions as never before.",
  "Republic of Chile|1973": "On 11 September the armed forces overthrew Allende, who died in the presidential palace, and General Augusto Pinochet took power. Congress was closed and parties banned, and thousands of people were killed, disappeared or imprisoned, isolating Chile internationally for much of the following decade.",
  "Republic of Chile|1982": "After several years of rapid growth under free-market reforms, a severe banking and debt crisis caused output to fall sharply and unemployment to rise above a fifth of the workforce. Mass protests against the dictatorship began the following year, and the regime was forced to change economic course.",
  "Republic of Chile|1990": "After losing the 1988 plebiscite, Pinochet handed the presidency to Patricio Aylwin, elected at the head of the Concertación. Democracy returned with the military still influential, while the economy entered a long period of rapid growth that sharply reduced poverty and reintegrated Chile into world affairs.",
  "Republic of Chile|2010": "In the year Chile joined the OECD, a magnitude 8.8 earthquake struck the centre-south of the country. Sebastián Piñera took office as the first right-wing president elected since 1958, confirming the stability of democratic alternation in one of the most prosperous countries of Latin America.",
  "Republic of Chile|2019": "In October mass protests, sparked by a rise in metro fares and fuelled by anger at inequality and the cost of services, shook the country. About thirty people died in the unrest, and the political parties agreed to hold a referendum on writing a new constitution to replace that of 1980.",
  "Republic of Chile|2025": "Chile is a stable presidential democracy of about 20 million people and the world’s largest copper producer, though two proposed constitutions were rejected in 2022 and 2023. It has modest military weight but strong institutions and trade links, and remains one of the most developed countries in Latin America.",
  "Gran Colombia|1819": "After the Battle of Boyacá opened Bogotá to the patriots, the Congress of Angostura proclaimed the Republic of Colombia in December. The new state was largely a union on paper: royalists still held much of Venezuela, Quito, Panama and the Caribbean ports, and its government moved with Bolívar’s army rather than sitting in a settled capital.",
  "Gran Colombia|1821": "The Battle of Carabobo freed most of Venezuela, and the Congress of Cúcuta gave the republic a constitution, with Bolívar as president and Santander as vice-president. Panama joined voluntarily in November. The state now had working institutions in Bogotá, though royalist garrisons held out at Puerto Cabello and the south remained unconquered.",
  "Gran Colombia|1822": "Sucre’s victory at Pichincha brought Quito into the republic, and Bolívar annexed Guayaquil in July. The United States recognised Colombia, and a first loan was raised in London. With the whole of the former Viceroyalty of New Granada under its control, the republic was approaching the height of its territorial power and prestige.",
  "Gran Colombia|1824": "Colombian troops under Bolívar and Sucre won the battles of Junín and Ayacucho in Peru, ending Spanish power in South America. A second large London loan funded the war, and Santander’s administration in Bogotá was at its most effective. Gran Colombia stood at its peak as the leading military power of Spanish America.",
  "Gran Colombia|1826": "La Cosiata, Páez’s revolt in Venezuela, exposed regional hostility to Bogotá, and the republic stopped paying its foreign debt. Bolívar returned from Peru, pardoned Páez and proposed constitutional changes that divided him from Santander. The state still held all its territory, but its finances and political consensus were badly weakened.",
  "Gran Colombia|1828": "The Convention of Ocaña failed to agree on reform, and Bolívar assumed dictatorial powers by the Organic Decree of August. A conspiracy in September nearly killed him, and Santander was exiled. War with Peru broke out over Guayaquil. Authority now rested on Bolívar personally rather than on institutions, leaving the union fragile.",
  "Gran Colombia|1829": "Sucre and Flores defeated Peru at Tarqui in February, saving Guayaquil, but General Córdova’s revolt in Antioquia and talk of a European monarchy alienated liberals. In November Venezuelan assemblies called for separation. The republic kept its borders but was close to breaking point, held together mainly by the army and Bolívar’s fading prestige.",
  "Gran Colombia|1830": "Venezuela and Ecuador went their own ways, Bolívar resigned, Sucre was assassinated at Berruecos, and Bolívar died near Santa Marta in December. Rafael Urdaneta’s seizure of power in Bogotá gave him control only of the central provinces. Gran Colombia survived in name only, reduced to the territory of present-day Colombia and Panama.",
  "Gran Colombia|1831": "Urdaneta’s regime collapsed, and a convention in Bogotá reorganised the remaining provinces as the State, later Republic, of New Granada. Venezuela and Ecuador were by then separate republics. The union founded at Angostura in 1819 had ceased to exist after barely twelve years, its territory and debts divided among three successor states.",
  "Republic of Peru|1821": "San Martín proclaimed independence in Lima on 28 July and became Protector of Peru. The new state controlled Lima and the coast, but royalist armies held the Andean highlands and the viceroy’s government continued from Cuzco. Its survival depended on foreign armies from Chile and Argentina, and soon on Gran Colombia.",
  "Republic of Peru|1824": "Bolívar, granted dictatorial powers, led the decisive campaign: Junín in August and Sucre’s triumph at Ayacucho in December ended Spanish power. Peru was finally free, but it was devastated by war, burdened with debts to its liberators and governed by a foreign dictator, so its own institutions remained weak.",
  "Republic of Peru|1836": "The Bolivian president Andrés de Santa Cruz, invited into a Peruvian civil war, divided Peru into North and South Peruvian states within the Peru-Bolivian Confederation. The arrangement brought order and trade reforms but subordinated Peru to Bolivian leadership and alarmed Chile and Argentina, which soon went to war against it.",
  "Republic of Peru|1839": "Chilean troops and Peruvian exiles defeated Santa Cruz at the Battle of Yungay in January, dissolving the confederation. Peru was restored as a single republic under Agustín Gamarra, but caudillo rivalry, civil war and Gamarra’s death at Ingavi in 1841 left the country fragmented and nearly bankrupt.",
  "Republic of Peru|1845": "Ramón Castilla became president and brought the caudillo wars to an end. Guano exports from the Chincha Islands were beginning to fill the treasury, allowing the state to pay its army and officials regularly, draw up its first budget and settle debts. Peru entered its first period of sustained stability.",
  "Republic of Peru|1854": "Castilla, returning to power through a liberal revolution, abolished slavery and the Indian tribute, compensating slaveholders from guano revenue. The guano boom made Peru one of the richest states in South America, spending on a navy, railways and public works, though its dependence on a single export was growing.",
  "Republic of Peru|1866": "Spain had seized the Chincha Islands in 1864, but Peru, allied with Chile, Ecuador and Bolivia, repelled the Spanish squadron at Callao on 2 May 1866. The victory confirmed Peru’s independence and naval standing on the Pacific coast, even as guano reserves and government finances were beginning to show strain.",
  "Republic of Peru|1872": "Manuel Pardo became the first elected civilian president, promising to use guano revenue for railways, education and productive investment. Henry Meiggs was building the Central Railway into the Andes, and Peru was at the height of its nineteenth-century wealth and regional prestige, though its foreign debt had become very heavy.",
  "Republic of Peru|1879": "Peru entered the War of the Pacific through its secret alliance of 1873 with Bolivia. It had defaulted on its foreign debt in 1876 and guano was running out, but its navy and the nitrate province of Tarapacá still made it a significant regional power at the outbreak of war.",
  "Republic of Peru|1883": "Chile had destroyed the Peruvian navy, occupied Lima and looted the National Library. The Treaty of Ancón ceded Tarapacá and left Tacna and Arica under Chilean control. Peru emerged bankrupt, humiliated and divided by civil war, at the lowest point of its republican history.",
  "Republic of Peru|1895": "Nicolás de Piérola’s coalition overthrew Andrés Avelino Cáceres and opened the Aristocratic Republic. The Grace Contract of 1889 had cancelled the foreign debt in exchange for the railways, and exports of sugar, cotton, copper and wool slowly revived. The state recovered order and credit, though it governed for a narrow elite.",
  "Republic of Peru|1919": "Augusto B. Leguía seized power and began his eleven-year Oncenio, centralising authority and borrowing heavily in the United States to build roads, ports and public works in Lima. The economy expanded with American investment in mining and oil, and the state became stronger and more modern, though increasingly authoritarian.",
  "Republic of Peru|1930": "The Depression ended Leguía’s rule, and Luis Sánchez Cerro’s coup opened years of violence between the army and APRA, including the Trujillo uprising of 1932. Peru also fought Colombia over Leticia. Export revenue fell sharply, but the state survived the crisis without losing territory or its basic institutions.",
  "Republic of Peru|1968": "General Juan Velasco Alvarado’s coup launched a revolutionary military government that nationalised the International Petroleum Company and later the fishmeal industry and large mines, and carried out a sweeping agrarian reform. The state’s economic reach had never been greater, though rising debt and inefficiency soon undermined the experiment.",
  "Republic of Peru|1980": "Civilian government returned with Fernando Belaúnde under the 1979 constitution, which gave illiterate citizens the vote. On the eve of the election the Shining Path began its armed struggle in Ayacucho. Democratic institutions were restored, but the economy was strained by debt and the coming insurgency would test the state severely.",
  "Republic of Peru|1990": "Hyperinflation under Alan García’s first government, the collapse of public services and Shining Path attacks reaching Lima brought Peru close to state failure. Alberto Fujimori, a political outsider, won the presidency and imposed a severe stabilisation programme. Power stood at its lowest level since the War of the Pacific.",
  "Republic of Peru|1992": "Fujimori closed Congress and the courts in his self-coup of April, and in September police captured Abimael Guzmán in Lima, breaking the Shining Path. Inflation fell and foreign investment returned. The state recovered security and economic control, but at the cost of democratic institutions and serious human-rights abuses.",
  "Republic of Peru|2000": "Fujimori’s third term collapsed when videos showed his adviser Vladimiro Montesinos bribing politicians; he fled to Japan and resigned. A transitional government restored democratic institutions. The economy, freed from inflation and opened to trade, was recovering, giving the restored democracy a firmer base than its predecessors had enjoyed.",
  "Republic of Peru|2013": "After a decade of growth averaging around six per cent a year, driven by mining exports and Chinese demand, Peru had roughly halved its poverty rate, won investment-grade credit ratings and co-founded the Pacific Alliance. Its economy, reserves and international standing were the strongest in its history, marking the republic’s peak.",
  "Republic of Peru|2022": "President Pedro Castillo tried to dissolve Congress in December and was removed and arrested within hours; protests against his successor Dina Boluarte left dozens dead. The economy remained stable and the central bank respected, but repeated clashes between presidents and Congress had seriously weakened governance.",
  "Republic of Peru|2025": "Congress removed Boluarte in October, the latest of several presidents removed or forced out since 2018. Peru’s economy kept growing and the new port of Chancay deepened links with Asia, but crime, corruption scandals and political fragmentation held the state below its earlier strength ahead of the 2026 elections.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1823": "A congress convened after the fall of Iturbide declared the United Provinces of Central America independent on 1 July. The new state had no constitution, no treasury and no army of its own, and Chiapas had chosen to stay with Mexico, but it inherited the territory and capital of the colonial Kingdom of Guatemala.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1824": "The constituent assembly abolished slavery and on 22 November adopted a federal constitution, creating the Federal Republic of Central America. Five states organised their own governments. The United States recognised the new republic, and a framework of institutions now existed, though its finances and division of powers remained untested.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1825": "Manuel José Arce took office as the first federal president, and the federal congress and senate began to meet in Guatemala City. For a brief period the federation functioned as a single country at peace, with optimistic plans for a canal, foreign loans and trade, marking the high point of its unity.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1826": "Arce’s arrest of the Guatemalan state chief Juan Barrundia in September and his turn towards the conservatives broke the liberal alliance. Civil war began between federal troops and the liberal states. The federation still existed in full, but its president had become a party leader and its institutions lost legitimacy.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1827": "Federal forces invaded El Salvador and the war spread across the isthmus, while Arce governed without a functioning congress. Morazán’s victory at La Trinidad in Honduras in November marked the start of a liberal counter-offensive. The federation was at war with itself, its trade disrupted and its treasury empty.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1829": "Morazán’s allied army took Guatemala City in April, ending the civil war. The conservative leaders, the archbishop and the religious orders were expelled, and a liberal federal congress reassembled. Order was restored and federal authority revived, though the victory had been won by force and left lasting conservative and clerical hostility.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1830": "Morazán was elected federal president and, with Mariano Gálvez in Guatemala from 1831, began an ambitious liberal programme of education, religious freedom and legal reform. The federation enjoyed its strongest leadership since 1825, though its revenue remained small and the states retained most real power.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1834": "To escape Guatemalan politics, the federal capital moved to San Salvador. José Cecilio del Valle won the presidential election but died before taking office, and Morazán was re-elected in 1835. The federation remained intact and its leader respected, but proposals for constitutional reform to strengthen federal finances failed.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1837": "Cholera swept Guatemala, rumours blamed the government for poisoning wells, and Rafael Carrera led a peasant revolt against the Gálvez administration and its reforms. The federal government kept its institutions, but its most populous state was slipping out of liberal control, undermining the base on which the union rested.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1838": "Carrera’s forces entered Guatemala City, Los Altos broke away, and congress allowed the states to organise as they wished. Nicaragua, Honduras and Costa Rica seceded during the year. The federation had lost three of its five states and existed effectively only as an alliance between El Salvador and Morazán.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1839": "Morazán’s presidential term ended in February with no successor elected, and Guatemala declared its own sovereignty in April. Morazán still defeated Honduran and Nicaraguan forces in El Salvador, but federal institutions no longer functioned, and the union was reduced to a cause defended by one general and one state.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1840": "Morazán’s attempt to retake Guatemala City failed in March against Carrera, and he left for exile in Peru. Carrera had also crushed the State of Los Altos. With its last military champion gone, the federation retained no army, territory or government capable of exercising authority.",
  "Federal Republic of Central America|1841": "El Salvador, the last state still loyal to the union, adopted a constitution as a sovereign republic, completing the dissolution. The five former states went their separate ways. Morazán’s return and execution in Costa Rica in 1842 ended the last serious attempt to restore the federation by arms.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1825": "An assembly in Chuquisaca declared the independence of Upper Peru on 6 August, and the new republic took Bolívar’s name. It had a ruined silver industry, an army largely made up of Colombian troops and claims on it from both Peru and Argentina, but it also held a Pacific coastline and vast lowland territories.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1829": "After Sucre’s departure in 1828 and a period of instability, Andrés de Santa Cruz became president. He balanced the budget, organised the army, founded universities and issued legal codes on the Napoleonic model. Bolivia gained an effective government and became, for a time, one of the best-ordered states in South America.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1836": "Santa Cruz intervened in Peru’s civil war and created the Peru-Bolivian Confederation, with himself as Supreme Protector. Bolivia stood at the centre of the most powerful state on the Pacific coast of South America, a moment of regional influence it never regained, though the union provoked war with Chile and Argentina.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1839": "Defeat at the Battle of Yungay in January destroyed the confederation, and Santa Cruz was overthrown and exiled. Bolivia returned to its own borders amid factional fighting. The state retained its territory and army, but its moment of regional leadership was over and caudillo rivalries began to dominate politics.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1841": "José Ballivián defeated a Peruvian invasion at the Battle of Ingavi in November, killing the Peruvian president Agustín Gamarra and securing Bolivia’s independence from its larger neighbour. Ballivián’s government that followed brought a period of relative order, exploration of the eastern lowlands and administrative reform.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1864": "Mariano Melgarejo seized power and ruled erratically until 1871. He sold communal indigenous lands, provoking revolts, and his government signed a treaty sharing coastal mineral revenues with Chile in 1866 and ceded Amazon territory to Brazil in 1867. The state was poorly governed and losing control of its frontiers.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1879": "Bolivia raised taxes on a Chilean nitrate company in Antofagasta, and Chile occupied the port in February, starting the War of the Pacific. Silver exports were reviving and the state had modest revenue, but its army was small and its coastal province was populated largely by Chileans.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1884": "A truce signed at Valparaíso left Chile in control of the entire Bolivian coast. Bolivia was now landlocked, defeated and politically shaken, although a civilian oligarchy of silver-mining entrepreneurs was beginning to give the state more stable government and to build railways linking the mines to the Pacific.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1899": "The Federal War ended with victory for the Liberals of La Paz, aided by Aymara forces under Pablo Zárate Willka, who were then repressed. The seat of government moved to La Paz. Tin was replacing silver as the leading export, giving the state a new and larger revenue base.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1903": "The Treaty of Petrópolis ceded Acre to Brazil after a rebellion by Brazilian rubber tappers, in return for compensation and a promised railway. A treaty of 1904 confirmed the loss of the coast to Chile. The Liberal state was stable and the tin economy growing, but its territory continued to shrink.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1929": "Tin exports reached a peak at the end of the 1920s, and Simón Patiño had become one of the richest men in the world. The state borrowed abroad for railways and public works. Bolivia enjoyed relative stability and prosperity, though the onset of the Depression would soon devastate tin revenue.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1932": "Amid economic crisis, President Daniel Salamanca led Bolivia into the Chaco War with Paraguay. Bolivia had a larger population and a German-trained army and expected a quick victory over its smaller neighbour, but it fought far from its highland centres, with poor supply lines and conscripts unused to the lowlands.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1935": "The Chaco War ended in defeat, with tens of thousands of Bolivians dead and most of the disputed Chaco in Paraguayan hands. Discredited civilian elites were soon swept aside by reforming officers. The state was at a low point, but the war generated the nationalist movements that would remake it.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1952": "The Revolutionary Nationalist Movement, backed by armed miners and townspeople, defeated the army in the April revolution. Víctor Paz Estenssoro introduced universal suffrage, nationalised the great tin mines and prepared land reform. The state gained new legitimacy and economic power, although falling tin prices and inflation soon strained it.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1964": "General René Barrientos overthrew Paz Estenssoro, beginning eighteen years of mostly military rule. The army, allied with peasant organisations through a military-peasant pact, governed with United States support. Economic growth resumed with oil, tin and later Santa Cruz agriculture, but politics was repressive and unstable.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1982": "After a succession of coups, including García Meza’s cocaine coup, the military handed power to Hernán Siles Zuazo in October. Democracy was restored, but the state inherited a huge foreign debt, falling tin prices and an economy sliding into hyperinflation, which left it weak and close to bankruptcy.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|1985": "Hyperinflation passed 10,000 per cent, tin prices collapsed on world markets and the state mining company was crippled. Paz Estenssoro’s New Economic Policy stabilised the currency but closed most state mines. Bolivia’s economic power stood at its lowest point of the twentieth century, though democracy survived.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|2000": "Privatisation and decentralisation had attracted investment, and large natural gas reserves had been found, but the Cochabamba Water War forced the government to cancel a water concession held by a foreign consortium. The state was more stable than in the 1980s, yet faced mounting protest over control of natural resources.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|2006": "Evo Morales took office as Bolivia’s first indigenous president and nationalised the hydrocarbons industry on 1 May. Rising gas prices brought unprecedented revenue, paying for large social programmes. The state’s economic reach and political confidence grew rapidly, despite bitter conflict with the eastern lowland departments over autonomy.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|2014": "With record gas revenues, years of growth around five per cent, sharply reduced poverty, large reserves and a new plurinational constitution, Bolivia reached the strongest position in its history. Morales won a third term with over sixty per cent of the vote, though dependence on gas exports remained a vulnerability.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|2019": "Morales sought a fourth term despite losing a 2016 referendum on re-election. A disputed October election, mass protests and pressure from the police and army led to his resignation in November. The crisis that followed, with deadly clashes, weakened institutions, though the economy was still functioning.",
  "Republic of Bolivia|2025": "Falling gas production under Luis Arce, and a failed military uprising in 2024, had left Bolivia short of fuel and foreign currency, with rising inflation. The 2025 elections ended nearly two decades of MAS government. The state remained intact and democratic, but economically weaker than at its peak.",
  "Venezuela|1830": "The congress at Valencia proclaimed Venezuela’s separation from Gran Colombia and adopted a constitution, with José Antonio Páez as the dominant figure. The new state was small in population, impoverished by twenty years of war and dependent on cacao, coffee and cattle, but it was independent and soon reasonably orderly under Páez.",
  "Venezuela|1859": "The Federal War broke out between Liberal federalists and Conservative centralists after years of unrest under the Monagas brothers. Fighting spread across the plains, destroying herds and farms. The central government lost control of much of the country, and the state reached one of the weakest points in its history.",
  "Venezuela|1863": "The Treaty of Coche ended the Federal War with a Liberal victory, and Juan Crisóstomo Falcón became president; the 1864 constitution created the United States of Venezuela. Peace returned, but the war had cost many lives and left power dispersed among regional caudillos who governed their states almost independently.",
  "Venezuela|1870": "Antonio Guzmán Blanco took Caracas and began nearly two decades of dominance. He decreed free compulsory primary education, built railways and public buildings, introduced the bolívar and curbed the Church. The state became more centralised and orderly than at any time since independence, though it rested on one man’s authority.",
  "Venezuela|1902": "Cipriano Castro’s refusal to pay foreign debts led Britain, Germany and Italy to blockade Venezuelan ports and shell coastal forts. The crisis, settled through arbitration in 1903, exposed the country’s weakness. Venezuela remained a poor coffee exporter, its finances in disorder and its government dependent on a single caudillo.",
  "Venezuela|1908": "Juan Vicente Gómez seized power from Castro and began a twenty-seven-year dictatorship. He built a national army, roads and a centralised administration, ending regional revolts, and welcomed foreign investors. The state became stronger and more stable, though Venezuela was still an agrarian country with limited revenue.",
  "Venezuela|1922": "The blowout of the Barroso II well near Cabimas revealed the vast oil reserves of the Lake Maracaibo basin, and foreign companies rushed in. Gómez granted generous concessions on terms that enriched his circle. Oil revenue began to transform the treasury, lifting the state above its agrarian limits for the first time.",
  "Venezuela|1928": "Venezuela became the world’s leading oil exporter and second-largest producer after the United States, and oil revenue would soon pay off the foreign debt. Student protests by the Generation of 1928, including Rómulo Betancourt, announced the democratic movement that later reshaped politics, though repression kept the dictatorship firmly in control.",
  "Venezuela|1948": "The Democratic Action government of the Trienio had introduced universal suffrage and fifty-fifty profit sharing with the oil companies, and Rómulo Gallegos had been elected president, but a military coup in November overthrew him. Oil wealth was growing fast, though power passed back to the armed forces for a decade.",
  "Venezuela|1958": "A popular uprising and military revolt overthrew Marcos Pérez Jiménez on 23 January. The Punto Fijo pact committed the main parties to democratic rules, and Rómulo Betancourt was elected president. Venezuela entered four decades of civilian government, its institutions strengthened by oil revenue and broad elite consensus.",
  "Venezuela|1960": "Venezuela joined Iran, Iraq, Kuwait and Saudi Arabia in founding OPEC at Baghdad, an initiative of its minister Juan Pablo Pérez Alfonzo. The government launched an agrarian reform and expanded schools and services while defeating left-wing guerrillas. Venezuela had become a leading voice of oil producers and a model Latin American democracy.",
  "Venezuela|1976": "Carlos Andrés Pérez nationalised the oil industry on 1 January, creating PDVSA, at a time when the quadrupling of oil prices had made Venezuela one of the richest countries per head in Latin America. Grand development projects and rising imports reflected the state’s peak of wealth, confidence and international influence.",
  "Venezuela|1983": "Falling oil prices and heavy borrowing forced a sharp devaluation of the bolívar on Black Friday, 18 February. Capital flight and the debt crisis ended the boom years. The state remained democratic and its institutions intact, but its ability to buy social peace with oil revenue was in decline.",
  "Venezuela|1989": "Austerity measures introduced by Carlos Andrés Pérez in his second term provoked the Caracazo riots in February, suppressed by the army with hundreds of deaths. Confidence in the two-party system collapsed, opening the way to the failed coups of 1992, though elected government and oil exports continued.",
  "Venezuela|1999": "Hugo Chávez, elected in December 1998, introduced a new constitution approved by referendum in December 1999, renaming the state the Bolivarian Republic. The old party system had collapsed, and oil prices were low. The state entered a period of radical transformation, its institutions remade around the presidency.",
  "Venezuela|2006": "Having survived a coup attempt in 2002 and a long oil strike, Chávez controlled PDVSA and enjoyed soaring oil prices. Revenue funded social programmes, nationalisations and regional alliances such as ALBA and Petrocaribe. Venezuela’s influence in Latin America was greater than at any time since the 1970s.",
  "Venezuela|2013": "Chávez died in March, and Nicolás Maduro narrowly won the election to succeed him. Oil production was already falling, shortages and inflation were rising, and debts had grown. The state remained influential in the region, but its economic foundations were weakening just before the collapse of oil prices in 2014.",
  "Venezuela|2019": "After hyperinflation, an economic contraction of roughly three-quarters since 2013 and the emigration of millions, the opposition leader Juan Guaidó claimed the interim presidency with backing from dozens of countries. United States sanctions hit oil exports. Maduro kept control of the army and territory, but the state was gravely weakened.",
  "Venezuela|2026": "After the disputed 2024 election, United States forces captured Maduro in Caracas in January and took him to New York for trial, and Delcy Rodríguez assumed the presidency. Oil output had partly recovered from its lowest point, but the economy, institutions and population remained far below their peak, and the political future was uncertain.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1830": "An assembly in Quito declared the Southern District of Gran Colombia independent on 13 May, and a congress at Riobamba gave the new state a constitution and the name Ecuador. Under Juan José Flores it was poor, indebted and split between Quito and Guayaquil, with an army led largely by foreign-born officers.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1845": "The Marcist Revolution, launched in Guayaquil in March, drove Flores from power after fifteen years of dominance. Civilian liberal governments followed, and slavery was abolished in the early 1850s, but the state remained weak, short of revenue and vulnerable to coups, regional revolts and exile plots by Flores himself.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1859": "In the ‘terrible year’ Ecuador nearly disintegrated: rival governments sat in Quito, Guayaquil, Cuenca and Loja, and Peru blockaded the coast. Some leaders even discussed placing the country under foreign protection. This was the lowest point of the republic’s cohesion, ended only when García Moreno and Flores retook Guayaquil in 1860.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1869": "Gabriel García Moreno returned to power and imposed a centralising Catholic constitution. Roads, schools, the Escuela Politécnica Nacional and a more effective treasury gave Ecuador its most coherent government of the century, though at the cost of harsh repression of opponents. His assassination in 1875 ended the experiment.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1895": "The Liberal Revolution, launched from Guayaquil on 5 June, brought Eloy Alfaro to power. Backed by coastal cacao wealth, the liberals secularised the state, expanded public education and built infrastructure, shifting the balance of power from the clerical Sierra towards the commercial coast.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1908": "The Guayaquil–Quito railway reached the capital in June, linking the coast and the highlands for the first time and fulfilling Alfaro’s central project. Cacao exports were near their height, and the separation of church and state under the constitution of 1906 gave the liberal republic its fullest form.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1922": "Plant disease and falling world prices were wrecking the cacao economy, and on 15 November troops killed striking workers in Guayaquil. Alfaro had been murdered by a Quito mob in 1912, and the banking ‘plutocracy’ of the coast dominated a weakened state, setting the scene for the Julian Revolution of 1925.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1942": "Defeat by Peru in the war of 1941 led to the Rio de Janeiro Protocol of January 1942, which assigned most of Ecuador’s Amazon claim to Peru. The loss was a national trauma; it brought down President Carlos Arroyo del Río in 1944 and marked the low point of Ecuador’s twentieth-century standing.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1952": "Under Galo Plaza, the first president in decades to complete his term, the banana boom transformed the coast and made Ecuador the world’s largest banana exporter. Rising revenues, new roads and relative political calm marked a period of growth that lasted into the late 1950s.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1972": "A military government took power in February just as oil began to flow from the Amazon through the new Trans-Ecuadorian pipeline. Ecuador joined OPEC in 1973, and oil revenue multiplied the state’s budget, funding roads, schools, electricity and an enlarged bureaucracy and middle class.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1982": "Democracy had returned in 1979, but falling oil prices and heavy borrowing brought Ecuador into the Latin American debt crisis. Austerity, inflation and the death of President Jaime Roldós in 1981 weakened the new democratic order, though institutions survived the decade.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1998": "The Brasília Presidential Act of October ended the long border dispute with Peru after the Cenepa War of 1995, removing the main external threat. Yet El Niño floods and collapsing oil prices were already undermining the banks, and the political system had lost a president to removal in 1997.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|1999": "A banking collapse led the government to freeze deposits, the sucre lost much of its value and the economy shrank sharply. Hundreds of thousands of Ecuadorians emigrated, many to Spain. In January 2000 President Jamil Mahuad announced dollarisation and was overthrown days later.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|2008": "Rafael Correa’s Citizens’ Revolution, financed by high oil prices, adopted the Montecristi constitution, which recognised the rights of nature and a plurinational state. The government defaulted on part of the foreign debt and greatly expanded spending on roads, hydroelectric dams, schools and health care.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|2014": "Ecuador stood at its peak of state capacity and regional prominence: poverty had fallen sharply, public investment was at record levels and Correa enjoyed strong majorities. The collapse of oil prices from late 2014 and the earthquake of April 2016 soon brought deficits and new borrowing.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|2020": "The COVID-19 pandemic struck Guayaquil with exceptional severity, oil prices collapsed and the government restructured its foreign debt. Ecuador left OPEC at the start of the year, and the previous October mass protests led by CONAIE against fuel subsidy cuts had forced the government to retreat.",
  "Republic of Ecuador|2025": "Ecuador remains a democratic presidential republic, but cocaine trafficking gangs have made it one of the most violent countries in the region. President Daniel Noboa, re-elected in April, has relied on states of emergency and the army, while dollarisation and oil exports continue to anchor the economy.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1838": "On 30 April Nicaragua became the first member to leave the Federal Republic of Central America. The new state had no settled capital, a tiny revenue and no real army beyond the factional militias of León and Granada, while Britain controlled the Caribbean Mosquito Coast as a protectorate.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1856": "William Walker, invited by the León liberals, made himself president in July and sought recognition from the United States. Nicaragua had effectively lost its sovereignty to a foreign adventurer, its cities were battlefields and Granada was burned by Walker’s men before his retreat, the lowest point in the republic’s history.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1857": "A coalition of Central American armies, led by Costa Rica, forced Walker to surrender at Rivas in May. Exhausted and discredited, the Liberals accepted Conservative leadership, beginning a long period of relative order in which Managua became the settled capital and the war was remembered as a national victory.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1880": "Under the Conservative Thirty Years, presidents succeeded one another peacefully, coffee exports were growing and a railway from the Pacific port of Corinto was under construction. The state was still weak and the Mosquito Coast autonomous, and the indigenous revolt at Matagalpa in 1881 revealed tensions over land.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1894": "José Santos Zelaya, in power since the Liberal revolution of 1893, sent troops into the Mosquito Reserve and incorporated the Caribbean coast into Nicaragua. With a modern army, a secular constitution and expanding coffee exports, Zelaya made Nicaragua briefly a regional power that intervened in neighbouring states.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1909": "Zelaya’s execution of two American mercenaries and Washington’s open hostility forced his resignation in December. His fall ended the strongest Liberal regime and opened a period of civil war, foreign loans and dependence on the United States, though the state retained the territory he had united.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1912": "United States Marines landed in August to save a Conservative government from a Liberal rising, and the nationalist general Benjamín Zeledón was killed. American bankers controlled customs and the national bank, and the Bryan–Chamorro Treaty of 1914 followed, reducing Nicaragua to a near-protectorate.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1933": "The last Marines left in January after six years of guerrilla war with Augusto César Sandino. The country was impoverished by war and the Depression, and the Managua earthquake of 1931 had destroyed much of the capital, but the new National Guard gave the state its first strong instrument of force.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1937": "Anastasio Somoza García, director of the National Guard, took office as president on 1 January after forcing out President Juan Bautista Sacasa. Having ordered Sandino’s murder in 1934, he controlled army, party and administration, giving Nicaragua a centralised if personal government for the first time in decades.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1956": "Somoza García was shot in León in September and died days later, but his sons inherited a consolidated state. Wartime and postwar exports, the cotton boom and close ties with the United States had brought growth, roads and a modernised Guard, while the family amassed vast holdings.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1967": "Nicaragua stood at the height of its economic and administrative strength: the cotton boom and the Central American Common Market produced some of the fastest growth in the region, and Anastasio Somoza Debayle won the presidency. The regime looked secure, though the small FSLN guerrilla movement had been founded in 1961.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1972": "The earthquake of 23 December destroyed central Managua and killed some 10,000 people. The misappropriation of relief aid by the Somoza circle turned business leaders and the church against the regime, while the economy was still growing and the National Guard remained powerful.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1979": "After a national insurrection, Somoza Debayle fled on 17 July and the FSLN entered Managua two days later. The war had killed tens of thousands and left the economy shattered and the state rebuilt from scratch around revolutionary institutions, a new army and broad international sympathy.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1984": "The Sandinista state had built the largest army in Central America and held national elections won by Daniel Ortega. It carried out land reform and literacy and health campaigns, but the US-backed Contra war, the mining of its harbours and falling production were draining its resources.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1990": "Exhausted by war, a US trade embargo and hyperinflation, Nicaraguans elected Violeta Barrios de Chamorro in February, ending the revolution peacefully. The army was cut drastically, the Contras demobilised, and the country began reconstruction as one of the poorest in the Americas.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|1998": "Hurricane Mitch struck in October, killing thousands, including the victims of a mudslide on the Casita volcano, and destroying much of the infrastructure. The governments of the 1990s had stabilised the currency and debt, but corruption under Arnoldo Alemán and heavy dependence on aid kept the state weak.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|2007": "Daniel Ortega returned to the presidency after the 1999 pact with Alemán lowered the threshold for a first-round victory. Venezuelan oil aid under the ALBA alliance, business cooperation and social programmes brought steady growth and the political stability on which his later dominance was built.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|2017": "Nicaragua enjoyed a decade of steady growth, low crime by regional standards and rising tourism, and the Ortega–Murillo government controlled all branches of the state. It was the country’s most prosperous and stable period since the 1970s, though the rule of law and opposition parties were being eroded.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|2018": "Protests that began in April over social security cuts were crushed by police and paramilitaries, with more than 300 dead. Tens of thousands fled, mainly to Costa Rica, tourism and investment collapsed, and the government lost its international partners and turned to open repression.",
  "Republic of Nicaragua|2025": "A constitutional reform made Rosario Murillo co-president with Ortega and placed all branches of the state under the presidency. Opposition leaders, journalists and clergy have been jailed, exiled or stripped of citizenship. The economy has recovered on remittances and gold exports, but Nicaragua is internationally isolated.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1838": "The assembly at Comayagua declared Honduras separate from the collapsing Central American federation on 26 October. The new state was sparsely populated, poor and without a settled capital, and its politics were dominated by local strongmen and by the rivalry between federalists loyal to Francisco Morazán and conservatives allied with Guatemala.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1851": "Rafael Carrera of Guatemala defeated a Honduran and Salvadoran army at the Battle of La Arada in February, ending the last serious liberal attempt to rebuild the federation by force. Honduras remained in the orbit of Guatemalan conservatism, with weak governments and an army of little more than militias.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1860": "Under the Wyke–Cruz Treaty of 1859 Britain agreed to return the Bay Islands, handed over in 1861, and in September William Walker was executed at Trujillo after landing on the north coast. The state gained territory and recognition of its Caribbean claims, though it remained poor and politically unstable.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1872": "British loans raised for an interoceanic railway had produced a vast foreign debt but only a short stretch of track near Puerto Cortés. The scandal crippled Honduran credit for decades, while civil wars and Guatemalan and Salvadoran interventions continued to make and unmake presidents.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1880": "Marco Aurelio Soto, in power since 1876, moved the capital to Tegucigalpa and adopted a liberal constitution. His reforms of finance, schools and law and the arrival of foreign mining companies such as the Rosario Mining Company gave Honduras its first modern administration.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1912": "Manuel Bonilla, restored to power by an invasion financed by Samuel Zemurray, granted generous concessions to banana companies, and United Fruit entered the country. Railways and plantations transformed the north coast, but much of the economy and politics now depended on foreign corporations.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1924": "A civil war over a disputed election brought fighting to Tegucigalpa and the landing of US Marines; American mediation produced a new government. The episode showed how far the state depended on outside powers and on fruit companies that backed rival factions.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1930": "Honduras had become the world’s leading banana exporter, and the companies’ railways, ports and towns made the north coast the most dynamic region of the country. Government revenue and employment rose, though the state’s reach remained limited and the Depression soon cut exports.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1949": "Tiburcio Carías Andino stepped down after sixteen years of dictatorship that had ended civil wars and built roads, an air force and a more professional army. His successor Juan Manuel Gálvez began cautious modernisation, creating a central bank in 1950.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1959": "Following the great banana strike of 1954, Ramón Villeda Morales’s government adopted a labour code and social security system. Honduras had organised unions, rising coffee and cotton exports and a more modern state, though the army had gained constitutional autonomy in 1957.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1969": "The Football War with El Salvador in July, fought over land, migration and the expulsion of Salvadoran settlers, killed thousands. Salvadoran troops advanced into Honduras before an OAS ceasefire, while the Honduran air force struck back; the war damaged the Central American Common Market and strengthened military rule.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1975": "Hurricane Fifi had killed several thousand people in 1974, and in April 1975 Oswaldo López Arellano was ousted after revelations that United Brands had bribed officials over banana taxes. A reformist military government continued agrarian reform while preparing a return to civilian rule.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1982": "A new constitution restored civilian rule under President Roberto Suazo Córdova, and US aid poured in as Honduras became a base against Sandinista Nicaragua. The state gained resources and allies, but the military, led by General Gustavo Álvarez Martínez, held real power and repressed opponents.",
  "Republic of Honduras|1998": "Hurricane Mitch struck in October, killing thousands, destroying bridges, roads and harvests and setting back development by years. Reforms of the 1990s had reduced the power of the military and placed the police under civilian authority, but reconstruction depended heavily on foreign aid and international debt relief.",
  "Republic of Honduras|2008": "Debt relief, remittances and the Central American free trade agreement with the United States brought steady growth. Elected governments had alternated peacefully for a quarter of a century, though President Manuel Zelaya’s turn towards Venezuela was creating tensions that led to the coup of 2009.",
  "Republic of Honduras|2012": "After the coup of 2009 and international isolation, Honduras had one of the highest murder rates in the world, driven by gangs and cocaine trafficking. Institutions were weak and corruption widespread, though the country was readmitted to the OAS in 2011 and economic growth resumed.",
  "Republic of Honduras|2019": "Homicide rates had fallen sharply from their peak, the economy was larger than ever and public investment had grown, leaving the state far stronger than in the crisis years after the coup. Yet the disputed re-election of Juan Orlando Hernández and mass emigration revealed a deep crisis of legitimacy.",
  "Republic of Honduras|2020": "The COVID-19 pandemic and the hurricanes Eta and Iota, which struck within two weeks in November, caused severe economic contraction and displaced hundreds of thousands. The crisis drove a new wave of emigration towards the United States and set back much of the economic and social progress of the previous decade.",
  "Republic of Honduras|2025": "Honduras remains a presidential republic with its largest economy to date, based on remittances, maquilas and agricultural exports, and homicide rates far below their peak of the early 2010s, leaving the state on most measures at its strongest. The disputed election of November, narrowly won by Nasry Asfura, showed that polarisation, gangs and poverty persist.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1841": "A constituent assembly adopted a constitution on 18 February declaring El Salvador a sovereign and independent state after the collapse of the federation. The small, liberal-leaning state depended on indigo exports, had a weak treasury and faced a hostile conservative Guatemala under Rafael Carrera, which repeatedly intervened in its politics.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1851": "At the Battle of La Arada in February, Rafael Carrera routed a Salvadoran and Honduran army that had invaded Guatemala to restore liberal federalism. The defeat left El Salvador under conservative Guatemalan influence, with frequent changes of government and little capacity to shape regional affairs.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1859": "Gerardo Barrios became president and launched the first serious modernisation of the state, promoting coffee cultivation, schools and a reorganised army and administration. As a liberal admirer of Morazán, he also challenged the church and Guatemalan conservatism, raising El Salvador’s standing but provoking Carrera’s hostility.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1863": "Carrera invaded, besieged San Salvador and overthrew Barrios, installing the conservative Francisco Dueñas. Barrios was later captured and executed in 1865. El Salvador again became a client of Guatemala, and liberal reforms were paused, though coffee planting continued to spread on the volcanic highlands.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1871": "Liberals overthrew Dueñas and began a long period of liberal rule that secularised education and civil life and favoured the expansion of coffee estates. The state became more stable and better funded as coffee exports grew, and liberal governments would hold power without interruption into the twentieth century.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1886": "A new constitution, lasting until 1939, gave the coffee republic its framework after the abolition of communal and ejido lands in 1881–1882. In 1885 Salvadoran forces had repelled Justo Rufino Barrios at Chalchuapa, securing independence from Guatemalan domination, and coffee had become the dominant export.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1913": "The assassination of President Manuel Enrique Araujo brought Carlos Meléndez to power, opening fourteen years of rule by the Meléndez and Quiñónez families. High coffee prices funded railways, ports and a professionalised army, and the coffee oligarchy reached the height of its wealth and confidence.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1932": "The Depression had halved coffee prices, and a military coup in December 1931 brought General Maximiliano Hernández Martínez to power. In January 1932 the army crushed a peasant and indigenous uprising, killing thousands in La Matanza, and established half a century of military rule over a frightened society.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1944": "A general strike forced Martínez to resign in May after an abortive military revolt in April. Military rule continued, but from 1948 a new generation of officers promoted state-led modernisation, social security, public works and industrial development under a reformed official party.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1965": "Under the Party of National Conciliation, El Salvador was among the most industrialised countries of Central America, thanks to the Central American Common Market, cotton and coffee exports and foreign investment. Strong growth and a powerful army made this the high point of the old military-oligarchic order.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1969": "In July El Salvador invaded Honduras in the Football War after the expulsion of Salvadoran settlers. The army advanced quickly before an OAS ceasefire, but the return of tens of thousands of migrants and the collapse of trade with Honduras worsened land pressure and damaged the Common Market.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1979": "Electoral fraud in 1972 and 1977, rising repression and growing guerrilla movements led reformist officers to overthrow General Carlos Humberto Romero in October. The juntas that followed announced land and bank reforms, but death squads escalated their killings and the country slid into civil war.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1981": "The FMLN’s ‘final offensive’ in January failed, but the guerrillas held large parts of the north and east. In December the Atlacatl Battalion massacred hundreds of villagers at El Mozote. The state survived only through massive US aid, as the economy contracted and refugees fled abroad.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|1992": "The Chapultepec Peace Accords of 16 January ended twelve years of war that had killed about 75,000 people. The army was cut and removed from internal security, a new civilian police was created and the FMLN became a party, giving El Salvador its first genuinely competitive democracy.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|2001": "El Salvador adopted the US dollar as legal tender in January, and two earthquakes in January and February killed more than a thousand people and destroyed many homes. ARENA governments had privatised banks and utilities, and remittances from the United States had become the main source of foreign currency.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|2015": "Following the collapse of a truce between the major gangs, El Salvador recorded a homicide rate of over 100 per 100,000 people, among the highest in the world. The FMLN government struggled with slow growth and debt, and both traditional parties were weakened by corruption scandals.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|2019": "Nayib Bukele took office in June after defeating both traditional parties, and homicides began to fall. His popularity was immense, though in February 2020 he entered the Legislative Assembly with soldiers to press for security funding, a sign of the concentration of power to come.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|2024": "Bukele was re-elected with an overwhelming majority in February despite the constitutional ban on consecutive terms. After two years of the state of exception, homicides were at historic lows and the state controlled the territory the gangs had dominated, at the cost of mass imprisonment and weakened courts.",
  "Republic of El Salvador|2025": "An agreement with the IMF led the legislature to end the compulsory acceptance of bitcoin, and constitutional reforms removed limits on presidential re-election. El Salvador remains firmly controlled by its government and largely free of gang violence, but with fewer institutional checks than at any time since 1992.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1847": "Rafael Carrera proclaimed the Republic of Guatemala on 21 March, formalising the independence the state had exercised since the federation collapsed in 1840. The new republic was the largest in Central America, but liberal opposition, regional revolts and a poor treasury soon forced Carrera into brief exile.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1851": "Back in power, Carrera routed a Honduran and Salvadoran army at the Battle of La Arada in February, ending liberal hopes of restoring the federation. Guatemala became the dominant power of the isthmus, able to shape the governments of its neighbours, and a concordat with the Holy See followed in 1852.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1865": "Carrera died in April after ruling as president for life since 1854. He left a stable conservative state allied with the church and merchant elite, which had taken San Salvador in 1863, though cochineal exports were collapsing and coffee had only begun to spread.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1871": "The Liberal Revolution, launched from the Mexican border, took Guatemala City on 30 June and overthrew Vicente Cerna. The liberals began dismantling the conservative order, but the transition brought conflict with the church and a regime still consolidating its control over the country.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1879": "Under Justo Rufino Barrios, a new liberal constitution crowned a decade of reforms: the church had lost its property and the Jesuits had been expelled, coffee exports were booming, railways and telegraphs were spreading and the army had been modernised. Guatemala was at its nineteenth-century height.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1885": "Barrios proclaimed the union of Central America and invaded El Salvador, but was killed at the Battle of Chalchuapa on 2 April. The failure ended Guatemalan hopes of leading a reunited isthmus, though the liberal coffee state he built survived intact.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1920": "Manuel Estrada Cabrera, dictator since 1898, was declared insane by the assembly and overthrown after mass protests. His rule had given United Fruit and its railway affiliate great privileges, and his fall opened a decade of unstable governments and economic dependence on coffee and bananas.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1931": "Jorge Ubico took power as the Depression collapsed coffee prices. He balanced the budget, built roads and suppressed opposition, replacing debt peonage with vagrancy laws in 1934, and favoured United Fruit. Order was restored, but at the price of a police state and stagnant social conditions.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1944": "Protests by students, teachers and workers forced Ubico out in July, and the October Revolution overthrew his successor Federico Ponce Vaides. A decade of democracy began with free elections, a new constitution, labour rights and social security, widening the state’s reach and legitimacy.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1952": "Jacobo Árbenz enacted Decree 900, distributing uncultivated land on large estates to some hundred thousand peasant families. The reformed state was at the height of its democratic ambition, though the expropriation of United Fruit reserves brought it into direct conflict with the company and Washington.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1954": "A CIA-organised invasion under Carlos Castillo Armas led the army to abandon Árbenz, who resigned on 27 June. The land reform was reversed, unions and parties were suppressed, and the country entered a long period of military-dominated government and political violence.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1966": "The civilian Julio César Méndez Montenegro took office, but the army ran a brutal counter-insurgency campaign in the east against the guerrillas that had emerged after 1960. Economic growth under the Central American Common Market expanded industry and the state, while political violence and death squads grew.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1976": "The earthquake of 4 February killed about 23,000 people and left over a million homeless. Relief work spread new community organisations in the highlands, while repression of cooperatives and unions intensified, preparing the ground for a wider insurgency among the Maya.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1982": "Efraín Ríos Montt seized power in March and intensified the scorched-earth campaign in the highlands, during which hundreds of villages were destroyed. The state survived as an army-run regime, internationally isolated, with its economy in recession and much of its population displaced or terrorised.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1986": "Vinicio Cerezo took office in January under the constitution of 1985 as the first civilian president since 1970. The army retained great power and the war continued at lower intensity, but democratic institutions began to take shape and the Esquipulas peace process soon opened regional negotiations.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|1996": "The Agreement on a Firm and Lasting Peace of 29 December ended thirty-six years of internal conflict. The URNG demobilised, the army was reduced and the accords promised indigenous rights and fiscal reform, though much of this programme was later left unfulfilled.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|2007": "Guatemala invited the UN-backed CICIG, an international commission with Guatemalan prosecutorial partners, to investigate criminal networks embedded inside the state. Growth, remittances and free trade with the United States strengthened the economy, though drug trafficking organisations and gangs posed serious challenges to state authority.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|2015": "CICIG revealed a customs fraud network known as La Línea, and mass peaceful protests led to the resignation and arrest of President Otto Pérez Molina. The episode showed both the depth of corruption and a new capacity of courts and citizens to hold leaders to account.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|2024": "Bernardo Arévalo took office on 15 January after months of attempts to annul his election, supported by mass indigenous-led protests and international pressure. Guatemala had the largest economy in Central America, low public debt and a stable currency, by economic measures the strongest position in its history.",
  "Republic of Guatemala|2025": "Guatemala remains a presidential republic with sound public finances and growing exports, but its government is constrained by a hostile prosecutor’s office and Congress, while poverty, chronic child malnutrition, organised crime and emigration to the United States continue to weigh on the country.",
  "Argentine Republic|1861": "Bartolomé Mitre’s victory at the Battle of Pavón reunited Buenos Aires with the provinces of the former Argentine Confederation. The new republic had a constitution and a dominant port city, but a small population, a weak national army, provincial caudillos still in arms and Indigenous peoples controlling much of the pampas and Patagonia.",
  "Argentine Republic|1870": "The War of the Triple Alliance ended with Paraguay crushed and Argentina holding Misiones and part of the Chaco. The war had been costly and unpopular in the interior, but it strengthened the national army and state, and the first national census of 1869 counted about 1.9 million inhabitants.",
  "Argentine Republic|1880": "The defeat of the Buenos Aires rising and the federalisation of the city as national capital ended the long contest between port and provinces. Julio Argentino Roca took the presidency after his campaign against the Indigenous peoples of the pampas, opening an era of conservative order, railway building and export growth.",
  "Argentine Republic|1890": "The Baring crisis, caused by speculative borrowing and a collapse in Argentine bonds, nearly bankrupted the London bank Baring Brothers and brought down President Miguel Juárez Celman after the Revolution of the Park. Growth paused, but immigration, railways and the frontier of cultivation resumed their expansion within a few years.",
  "Argentine Republic|1914": "On the eve of the First World War Argentina was among the world’s leading exporters of beef, wheat and maize, with over 30,000 kilometres of railway and a population of nearly eight million, about 30 per cent of it foreign-born. The Sáenz Peña Law of 1912 had opened politics to mass male suffrage.",
  "Argentine Republic|1929": "Under the Radical presidents Hipólito Yrigoyen and Marcelo T. de Alvear the country reached its relative peak, with high incomes by world standards, a large educated middle class, a fast-growing capital and orderly constitutional successions since 1916. The export economy, however, remained dependent on British markets and foreign capital.",
  "Argentine Republic|1930": "The Great Depression cut export prices, and on 6 September 1930 General José Félix Uriburu overthrew Yrigoyen in the first successful coup under the constitution of 1853. The Infamous Decade that followed combined electoral fraud with the Roca–Runciman Treaty of 1933, which secured beef sales to Britain on unfavourable terms.",
  "Argentine Republic|1946": "Juan Perón, who had risen within the military government formed after the coup of 1943, won the presidency with trade union support. Large reserves built up during the Second World War financed nationalisations, industrial promotion and welfare, and Argentina began the post-war era wealthy, confident and pursuing a ‘third position’ between the superpowers.",
  "Argentine Republic|1955": "After economic difficulties from 1949, the death of Eva Perón in 1952 and a conflict with the Catholic Church, the armed forces overthrew Perón in the self-styled Liberating Revolution. Peronism was banned, and the country entered two decades of weak civilian governments, military interventions and recurrent stop-go economic cycles.",
  "Argentine Republic|1976": "After Perón’s return in 1973 and his death in 1974, political violence and inflation spiralled under his widow Isabel Perón. The coup of 24 March 1976 installed a junta led by Jorge Rafael Videla, whose regime abducted and killed thousands of people and whose economic policies brought deindustrialisation and heavy foreign borrowing.",
  "Argentine Republic|1982": "The junta under Leopoldo Galtieri invaded the Falkland Islands in April 1982, hoping to rally the nation during an economic crisis. British forces retook the islands by 14 June. The defeat discredited the military, isolated Argentina internationally and left it with a large foreign debt and runaway inflation.",
  "Argentine Republic|1983": "Raúl Alfonsín won free elections and took office in December 1983, restoring constitutional government. The Nunca más report and the Trial of the Juntas in 1985 gave Argentina international moral standing, but foreign debt, military rebellions and inflation limited the new democracy’s capacity to recover economically.",
  "Argentine Republic|1991": "Carlos Menem’s Convertibility Plan pegged the peso to the US dollar and ended hyperinflation, while privatisations attracted investment and the Treaty of Asunción founded Mercosur with Brazil, Uruguay and Paraguay. Growth returned strongly in the early 1990s, though unemployment and public debt rose later in the decade.",
  "Argentine Republic|2001": "Recession, an overvalued currency and mounting debt culminated in December 2001 in limits on bank withdrawals, riots, the resignation of President Fernando de la Rúa and a sovereign default. Five presidents held office within two weeks, the peso was devalued in early 2002, and more than half the population fell into poverty.",
  "Argentine Republic|2007": "A commodity boom driven by soybean exports to China, a debt restructuring in 2005 and a competitive currency produced several years of fast growth under Néstor Kirchner. Cristina Fernández de Kirchner was elected president in 2007, though inflation was rising again and official statistics were being manipulated.",
  "Argentine Republic|2023": "After years of stagnation, a further default in 2020 and inflation above 100 per cent, voters elected Javier Milei, who promised to cut the state drastically. Argentina remained a large food exporter and a regional middle power, but far below its early twentieth-century standing among the world’s economies.",
  "Argentine Republic|2025": "Milei’s austerity sharply reduced monthly inflation and produced a fiscal surplus in 2024, at the cost of a recession and, at first, higher poverty, and his party won the midterm elections of October 2025. Argentina remains a democratic federal republic of about 46 million people, with strong agricultural, energy and mining sectors and a persistent claim to the Falkland Islands.",
  "Mexico|1867": "Juárez re-entered Mexico City in July 1867 after the execution of Maximilian at Querétaro. The republic had defeated France and the conservatives, but it was bankrupt, scarred by a decade of civil and foreign war and dependent on regional military chiefs, with banditry widespread and very few railways.",
  "Mexico|1876": "Porfirio Díaz overthrew President Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada under the Plan of Tuxtepec after Lerdo sought re-election. The Restored Republic had completed the Mexico City–Veracruz railway in 1873 and enforced the Reform laws, but government finances were weak and foreign investment remained scarce.",
  "Mexico|1884": "Díaz returned to the presidency after the term of his ally Manuel González and stayed in office until 1911. Debt settlements restored Mexico’s credit abroad, foreign capital flowed into railways and mines, and the rurales and the federal army imposed an order that Mexico had rarely known since independence.",
  "Mexico|1900": "The Porfiriato was at its height. Railways linked central Mexico to the United States, mining and export agriculture boomed, and Monterrey grew into an industrial centre. Prosperity was narrowly shared, however: real wages stagnated, villages lost their lands and military campaigns continued against the Yaqui and the Maya.",
  "Mexico|1910": "The centenary of independence was celebrated in September 1910 with great pomp, and Mexico had a stable currency, new oil wells and a modernised capital. But Díaz, now 80, had again been re-elected through fraud, and Francisco I. Madero’s call to revolt in November began the Mexican Revolution.",
  "Mexico|1914": "Victoriano Huerta, who had seized power and had Madero murdered in 1913, was driven out by the Constitutionalists and Zapatistas while United States forces occupied Veracruz. The federal army was dissolved, the revolutionary factions turned on each other, and the national economy, currency and railways were collapsing.",
  "Mexico|1920": "Carranza was killed after the Agua Prieta rebellion, and Álvaro Obregón became president, beginning the rule of the Sonoran generals. A decade of war had cost perhaps a million or more lives, but the Constitution of 1917 offered a new framework and the state began rebuilding schools and finances.",
  "Mexico|1929": "Calles founded the National Revolutionary Party, uniting revolutionary leaders in one organisation after Obregón’s assassination, and an agreement with the Church ended the Cristero War. The institutional basis for seven decades of one-party rule was in place, though the Great Depression soon hit exports and silver prices.",
  "Mexico|1938": "Lázaro Cárdenas expropriated the foreign oil companies, created Pemex and reorganised the ruling party around workers, peasants, the military and the popular sector. Massive land reform and the nationalisation of the railways greatly strengthened the state, though Britain broke off relations and foreign companies boycotted Mexican oil.",
  "Mexico|1958": "Import-substituting industrialisation sustained growth of around six per cent a year with low inflation, and the peso was held stable from 1954. Adolfo López Mateos took office in 1958 as cities, the middle class and universities expanded rapidly, although rural poverty persisted and the ruling party tolerated little dissent.",
  "Mexico|1968": "Mexico hosted the Olympic Games, a symbol of its modernisation, but on 2 October soldiers fired on student protesters at the Plaza de las Tres Culturas in Tlatelolco, killing dozens and possibly hundreds. Growth continued, yet the massacre badly damaged the legitimacy of the PRI state.",
  "Mexico|1981": "Huge oil discoveries in the Gulf of Campeche, including the Cantarell field, financed a boom under José López Portillo, and Mexico hosted the Cancún North–South summit in 1981. Rapid growth and confident diplomacy masked rising foreign borrowing, an overvalued peso and dependence on oil prices.",
  "Mexico|1982": "Falling oil prices and rising interest rates left Mexico unable to service its foreign debt in August 1982, setting off the Latin American debt crisis. López Portillo nationalised the banks, the peso was repeatedly devalued, and the lost decade that followed cut real wages and forced a turn to market reforms.",
  "Mexico|1994": "NAFTA came into force on 1 January, the same day as the Zapatista rising in Chiapas. The murder of the PRI candidate Colosio and a December currency collapse followed, requiring an international rescue of about 50 billion dollars, but the trade agreement turned Mexico into a large manufacturing exporter.",
  "Mexico|2000": "Vicente Fox of the National Action Party won the presidential election, ending 71 years of rule by the PRI and its predecessors through a peaceful transfer of power. Democratic institutions, a freer press and a growing export economy gave Mexico new standing, though inequality and crime remained serious problems.",
  "Mexico|2018": "Andrés Manuel López Obrador won a landslide for Morena, promising to fight corruption and poverty. Mexico was among the world’s fifteen largest economies, a major manufacturing hub and the most populous Spanish-speaking country, yet homicides reached record levels and the power of criminal organisations remained a grave challenge.",
  "Mexico|2025": "Claudia Sheinbaum, elected in 2024 as the first woman president, governs a federal republic of about 130 million people. Mexico has become the largest trading partner of the United States and a centre of nearshored manufacturing, while it faces cartel violence, controversy over judicial reform and pressure over trade and migration.",
  "Canada|1867": "The British North America Act united Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Ontario and Quebec as the Dominion of Canada on 1 July 1867. The federation had about 3.5 million people, no control over its foreign policy, a British garrison for defence and a narrow band of settlement along the St Lawrence and the Great Lakes.",
  "Canada|1870": "Canada acquired Rupert’s Land and the North-Western Territory from the Hudson’s Bay Company, multiplying its territory many times over. The resistance of the Métis at Red River under Louis Riel led to the Manitoba Act, creating a small new province, and a military expedition was sent west to assert federal authority.",
  "Canada|1871": "British Columbia joined Confederation on the promise of a transcontinental railway within ten years, giving Canada a Pacific coast. The Treaty of Washington settled disputes with the United States, and the first numbered treaties with First Nations opened the prairies to settlement, though the railway project soon became mired in scandal.",
  "Canada|1885": "The last spike of the Canadian Pacific Railway was driven at Craigellachie in British Columbia in November 1885, linking the country from coast to coast. The same year troops carried west by rail crushed the North-West Rebellion at Batoche, and Louis Riel’s execution embittered relations between French and English Canada.",
  "Canada|1905": "Under Wilfrid Laurier immigration to the prairies surged and wheat exports boomed, and Alberta and Saskatchewan were created as provinces in 1905. Canada was among the fastest-growing countries in the world, though its foreign relations were still conducted through London and it remained bound by British imperial commitments.",
  "Canada|1917": "Canadian troops captured Vimy Ridge in April 1917, a victory remembered as a moment of national coming of age. The war effort raised Canada’s international standing, but the introduction of conscription and the bitterly contested election of December 1917 deepened the divide between English Canada and Quebec.",
  "Canada|1931": "The Statute of Westminster gave Canada and the other dominions full legislative independence from the British Parliament, confirming the equality declared in 1926. The Great Depression, however, hit the export-dependent economy hard, with drought on the prairies, collapsing wheat prices and unemployment reaching more than a quarter of the workforce at its worst.",
  "Canada|1945": "Canada ended the Second World War with one of the largest navies and air forces among the Allies, a greatly expanded industrial base and a seat at the founding of the United Nations. Wartime growth and the mobilisation of over a million people made it a significant middle power.",
  "Canada|1949": "Newfoundland joined Canada as the tenth province after two referendums, and Canada became a founding member of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization. The end of appeals to the Privy Council made the Supreme Court of Canada the final court of appeal, completing much of the transition to full sovereignty.",
  "Canada|1970": "The October Crisis, in which the Front de libération du Québec kidnapped a British diplomat and a Quebec minister, led Pierre Trudeau to invoke the War Measures Act. Canada was prosperous, officially bilingual since 1969 and an active peacekeeper, but the federation faced a serious nationalist challenge from Quebec.",
  "Canada|1982": "The Constitution Act, 1982, patriated the constitution and added the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms, ending the British Parliament’s remaining legal authority over Canada. Full constitutional independence was achieved, though Quebec’s government refused to sign, and a severe recession and high interest rates strained the economy.",
  "Canada|1995": "The second Quebec referendum on sovereignty was defeated by just 50.6 to 49.4 per cent, the closest the federation has come to breaking up. Federal deficits and debt were also at their height, prompting deep spending cuts, although free trade with the United States and Mexico was expanding exports.",
  "Canada|2025": "Canada is a parliamentary democracy of about 41 million people, a member of the G7 and NATO, and a leading producer of energy, minerals and food. Its standing is the highest in its history, though United States tariffs and annexation talk in 2025, which dominated the election won by Mark Carney’s Liberals, exposed its dependence on a single market.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1901": "Six self-governing colonies federated on 1 January 1901 as the Commonwealth of Australia, with about 3.8 million people. The new nation controlled its own tariffs and immigration, but relied on the Royal Navy for defence, followed Britain in foreign policy and had only a small federal administration based in Melbourne.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1915": "The landing at Gallipoli on 25 April 1915 committed Australia to a costly campaign against the Ottoman Empire and created the Anzac legend. The war expanded federal powers, including the first federal income tax in 1915, and drew the young federation deep into a global imperial war effort.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1918": "The Australian Corps under Sir John Monash played a leading part in the Allied offensives of 1918, beginning at Hamel and Amiens. Prime Minister Billy Hughes then won separate representation for Australia at the Paris Peace Conference and a League of Nations mandate over the former German New Guinea.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1931": "The Great Depression hit Australia severely as wool and wheat prices collapsed; unemployment approached 30 per cent in 1932, and the Labor government of James Scullin split over how to handle debts. Political turmoil included the dismissal of the New South Wales premier Jack Lang by the state governor in 1932.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1942": "The fall of Singapore and the bombing of Darwin exposed Australia to direct attack. John Curtin turned to the United States, General Douglas MacArthur set up his headquarters in Australia, and Australian troops halted the Japanese on the Kokoda Track, while the Commonwealth took control of income tax and wartime planning.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1945": "Australia ended the Second World War with almost a million people having served, a much larger industrial base and greater confidence in its own foreign policy. Arthur Calwell launched a mass immigration programme in 1945 under the slogan ‘populate or perish’, laying the foundation for decades of growth.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1951": "The ANZUS Treaty with the United States and New Zealand made the American alliance the basis of Australian security, while Australian forces fought in Korea. Under Robert Menzies, returned to office in 1949, a wool boom caused by the Korean War brought record export earnings and the start of a long post-war expansion.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1966": "Menzies retired after a record term, and decimal currency was introduced. Australia was prosperous, with full employment and a fast-growing immigrant population, and was sending conscripts to the Vietnam War. The dismantling of White Australia had begun, and trade was shifting from Britain towards Japan.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1975": "The dismissal of the Whitlam government by Governor-General Sir John Kerr on 11 November 1975 caused a constitutional crisis. Whitlam had ended conscription, abolished White Australia and introduced universal health insurance, but inflation and the oil shock had ended the post-war boom. Papua New Guinea became independent the same year.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|1986": "The Australia Act ended the last legal links that allowed British courts and the British Parliament to intervene in Australian affairs. The Hawke government had floated the dollar in 1983 and was opening the economy, while Treasurer Paul Keating warned that without reform Australia risked becoming a banana republic.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|2000": "After the defeat of the republic referendum in 1999, Australia led the international force in East Timor and hosted the Sydney Olympic Games in 2000. Nearly a decade of uninterrupted growth, a multicultural population and expanding ties with Asia gave the country greater standing than ever before.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|2008": "Kevin Rudd delivered the national apology to the Stolen Generations, and Australia was one of the few developed economies to avoid recession during the global financial crisis, helped by Chinese demand for iron ore and coal and by government stimulus. Its prosperity rested increasingly on trade with Asia.",
  "Commonwealth of Australia|2025": "Australia is a federal parliamentary democracy and constitutional monarchy of about 27 million people, among the wealthiest nations per head, and in May 2025 Anthony Albanese’s Labor government was re-elected with an enlarged majority. AUKUS deepens defence ties with the United States and Britain, while relations with China, its largest trading partner, remain delicate.",
  "Republic of Panama|1903": "Panama declared independence from Colombia on 3 November 1903 under the protection of American warships. Within weeks the Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty, signed by a Frenchman on Panama’s behalf, granted the United States a canal zone in perpetuity, so that the new republic was born small, poor and heavily dependent on Washington.",
  "Republic of Panama|1904": "Manuel Amador Guerrero became the first president under a constitution whose Article 136 allowed the United States to intervene to keep order. American construction of the canal began, the army was disbanded after a coup scare, and the US dollar was adopted as legal tender alongside the balboa.",
  "Republic of Panama|1914": "The Panama Canal opened on 15 August 1914, ten years after American work began, at a cost of about 5,600 deaths among the workforce of the American period. The canal made the isthmus central to world trade, but the Canal Zone, its bases and revenues remained under United States control.",
  "Republic of Panama|1936": "The Hull–Alfaro Treaty, ratified by the United States in 1939, ended Panama’s status as a protectorate by renouncing the American right of intervention and raising the canal annuity. Panama gained greater control over its own affairs, while ship registration and commerce in the transit cities slowly broadened the economy.",
  "Republic of Panama|1941": "Arnulfo Arias, elected in 1940, introduced a nationalist constitution with discriminatory provisions against West Indian and other non-Hispanic immigrants, but was overthrown in October 1941 while abroad. During the Second World War the United States garrisoned the canal heavily and leased additional bases outside the zone.",
  "Republic of Panama|1948": "The Colón Free Zone was created to make Panama a hub for re-export trade, after the National Assembly had rejected a new American bases agreement in 1947 following popular protests. The merchant fleet was growing quickly under open registration, though politics remained unstable and dominated by the National Police.",
  "Republic of Panama|1964": "On 9 January 1964 a dispute over flying the Panamanian flag at a Canal Zone high school led to riots in which more than 20 Panamanians and four American soldiers died. Panama briefly broke off relations with the United States, which agreed to negotiate a new canal treaty.",
  "Republic of Panama|1968": "Arnulfo Arias, elected for the third time, was overthrown by the National Guard only eleven days after taking office. Political parties were suspended, and Omar Torrijos emerged as the dominant figure in a military regime that would rule Panama, at times behind civilian presidents, for more than two decades.",
  "Republic of Panama|1977": "Omar Torrijos and President Jimmy Carter signed the Torrijos–Carter Treaties on 7 September 1977, providing for the transfer of the canal by the end of 1999. Approved by Panamanian voters in a plebiscite, the treaties fulfilled the central goal of Panamanian nationalism and brought the country wide international support.",
  "Republic of Panama|1979": "The treaties came into force on 1 October 1979, abolishing the Canal Zone and returning most of its land, the ports and the railway to Panama. Canal administration passed to a binational commission, while the banking centre and the Colón Free Zone made Panama one of the more prosperous countries in Central America.",
  "Republic of Panama|1987": "Accusations by a former military chief that Manuel Noriega had rigged elections and ordered the murder of the critic Hugo Spadafora set off mass protests by the Civic Crusade. The United States imposed sanctions, the banking sector contracted, and the economy shrank sharply as the military regime clung to power.",
  "Republic of Panama|1989": "Noriega annulled the May 1989 election won by Guillermo Endara, and on 20 December the United States invaded with about 26,000 troops. The Panama Defence Forces were destroyed, hundreds of Panamanians died and much of the El Chorrillo district burned, leaving the economy and state institutions in ruins.",
  "Republic of Panama|1994": "A constitutional amendment abolished the army, and Ernesto Pérez Balladares of the Democratic Revolutionary Party won a free election and succeeded Guillermo Endara peacefully, confirming the restoration of democracy. The economy had recovered strongly from the crisis of the late 1980s, and preparations for the transfer of the canal advanced.",
  "Republic of Panama|1999": "At noon on 31 December 1999 Panama took full control of the canal under the autonomous Panama Canal Authority, and the last American bases closed. President Mireya Moscoso, the widow of Arnulfo Arias, presided over the handover, completing the long struggle for sovereignty over the whole national territory.",
  "Republic of Panama|2016": "The expanded canal, with larger locks approved by referendum in 2006, opened in June 2016, allowing much bigger ships to cross. Growth among the fastest in Latin America had made Panama one of the region’s richest countries per head, though the Panama Papers leak that year damaged its financial reputation.",
  "Republic of Panama|2025": "Panama remains a stable democracy and global transit hub of just over four million people, but drought in 2023–2024 cut canal traffic, protests and a Supreme Court ruling closed a large copper mine, and Donald Trump’s demands from December 2024 that the United States take back the canal raised questions about its hard-won sovereignty.",
  "New Zealand|1947": "Parliament adopted the Statute of Westminster under Peter Fraser, giving New Zealand full legislative independence. The country was prosperous, with a welfare state and secure British markets, but its defence and foreign policy still followed Britain closely, so its independent international standing was modest compared with what came later.",
  "New Zealand|1951": "The ANZUS Treaty with Australia and the United States gave New Zealand a security guarantee beyond Britain, while the Korean War wool boom brought record export prices. The same year the Legislative Council disappeared and Sidney Holland\u0027s government broke the long waterfront dispute, consolidating National Party rule.",
  "New Zealand|1953": "Edmund Hillary\u0027s ascent of Everest with Tenzing Norgay, announced on the eve of Queen Elizabeth II\u0027s coronation, brought New Zealand international attention. Living standards ranked among the highest in the world, and the country joined SEATO in 1954, reinforcing its place in Western defence arrangements in Asia.",
  "New Zealand|1965": "The Cook Islands became self-governing in free association, three years after Western Samoa\u0027s independence, and Keith Holyoake\u0027s government sent an artillery battery to Vietnam. Prosperity based on exports to Britain continued, but the war divided opinion and tied New Zealand closely to American strategy in Asia.",
  "New Zealand|1973": "Britain joined the European Economic Community, ending secure access for New Zealand butter, lamb and wool, and the oil shock followed. Norman Kirk\u0027s Labour government, which had recognised the People\u0027s Republic of China, asserted a more independent foreign policy, but the country\u0027s economic security was badly shaken.",
  "New Zealand|1975": "The Treaty of Waitangi Act created the Waitangi Tribunal, and Whina Cooper led the Māori land march to Parliament, opening a new era in race relations. Robert Muldoon won the general election, beginning nine years of interventionist government amid inflation, rising public debt and slow growth.",
  "New Zealand|1984": "A currency crisis followed Muldoon\u0027s defeat in a snap election. David Lange\u0027s Labour government began rapid deregulation under Roger Douglas and banned nuclear-armed and nuclear-powered ships. Power stood at its lowest because the economy was stagnant and heavily indebted, though the country\u0027s international voice was about to grow.",
  "New Zealand|1987": "The Nuclear Free Zone Act turned the anti-nuclear policy into law after the rift with the United States and the Rainbow Warrior bombing of 1985. Te reo Māori became an official language and immigration reform opened the country to Asia, while the October share market crash exposed the costs of rapid liberalisation.",
  "New Zealand|1996": "The first election under mixed-member proportional representation produced a coalition of National and New Zealand First. Treaty settlements with Waikato-Tainui and Ngāi Tahu were under way, and the economy had recovered from the recession of the early 1990s, giving the country a more stable base.",
  "New Zealand|2008": "New Zealand became the first developed country to sign a free trade agreement with China, the culmination of a long shift towards Asian markets. Helen Clark\u0027s nine years in office ended with low unemployment and an active foreign policy, including peacekeeping and support for multilateral institutions.",
  "New Zealand|2019": "After the Christchurch mosque shootings, Jacinda Ardern\u0027s government banned most semi-automatic weapons within weeks and launched the Christchurch Call against online extremism with France. The country\u0027s international profile reached a new height, supported by a growing economy and a population approaching five million.",
  "New Zealand|2020": "Strict border closures and lockdowns eliminated community transmission of COVID-19 for long periods, and deaths stayed among the lowest in the developed world. Ardern\u0027s Labour Party won an outright majority, the first under MMP. This marks the peak of New Zealand\u0027s relative international standing in its independent history.",
  "New Zealand|2025": "New Zealand remains a stable constitutional monarchy of about 5.3 million people under Christopher Luxon\u0027s coalition government. Debates over the Treaty of Waitangi, including the defeated Treaty Principles Bill, high living costs and emigration to Australia continue, and its international profile is slightly lower than in the pandemic years.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1600": "Tradition says Tang of Shang overthrew Jie of the Xia around this date, and archaeologists identify the early Shang with the Erligang culture. The new dynasty controlled a modest core on the eastern plain, and its power, though growing, stood far below that of the later kingdom at Anyang.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1550": "Large rammed-earth walled cities at Zhengzhou and Yanshi show a state able to mobilise thousands of workers for building. Bronze workshops at Zhengzhou produced ritual vessels on a growing scale, and the Shang organised the supply of copper, tin and lead from distant sources.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1450": "The Erligang culture reached its widest extent, with outposts such as Panlongcheng on the Yangtze guarding routes for copper and other resources. Erligang-style bronzes spread over much of northern and central China, marking the first great expansion of Shang influence, although the extent of direct political control is uncertain.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1400": "The Erligang network contracted and Zhengzhou declined. Later tradition speaks of disorder over nine reigns as royal brothers and sons competed for the throne, and the capital moved several times. Archaeology of the Middle Shang, including the walled city at Huanbei, suggests a smaller and less stable state.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1300": "Tradition credits King Pan Geng with moving the capital to Yin near Anyang, ending the wanderings of the court. Settlement around Huanbei and then south of the Huan River grew into a vast capital that would remain the royal seat until the end of the dynasty.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1250": "Wu Ding came to the throne at about this time and began a long reign, the best documented of the dynasty. The earliest oracle-bone inscriptions date from his time, and they record frequent campaigns against the Tufang, Guifang and Qiang, extensive royal hunts and an active ritual calendar.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1200": "Under Wu Ding the Shang reached their peak. Fu Hao and other commanders led campaigns, chariots appeared at Anyang, bronze casting reached its greatest scale, and the royal tombs at Xibeigang were filled with sacrificial victims. Shang power and influence extended across the middle Yellow River plain and beyond.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1150": "After Wu Ding the oracle-bone record shows a narrower range of divination topics and a more routine ritual schedule. The kingdom remained the dominant power of the Yellow River plain, but there are fewer records of large campaigns, and its network of allied lords seems less extensive.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1100": "Late kings such as Di Yi organised the ancestral rites into a strict sacrificial schedule and campaigned against the Renfang or Yi in the east. Meanwhile the Zhou in the Wei River valley grew stronger as Shang allies, creating a western rival the Shang could not fully control.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1060": "Di Xin\u0027s campaigns in the east probably drained Shang resources, while King Wen of Zhou extended his network of alliances in the west. The kingdom was still large and wealthy, but it was overstretched and faced a powerful, organised enemy beyond its western frontier.",
  "Shang dynasty|-1046": "King Wu of Zhou and his allies defeated the Shang army at the Battle of Muye. Di Xin reportedly burned himself to death in his palace, the Shang royal centre fell, and the dynasty came to an end. Its descendants later ruled the small State of Song under the Zhou.",
  "State of Qi|-1046": "After the Zhou conquest Jiang Ziya, Lord Taigong, received a fief in northern Shandong among the Dongyi peoples. Qi began as a frontier outpost with limited territory, its strength resting on the salt and fish of the coast and on its founder\u0027s prestige at the Zhou court.",
  "State of Qi|-690": "Qi annexed the neighbouring state of Ji, one of several small polities absorbed as it expanded across the Shandong plain. Its wealth from salt, fish and textiles was growing, although civil strife followed the murder of Duke Xiang in 686 BCE and a contest for the succession.",
  "State of Qi|-685": "Duke Huan seized the throne after a contest with his brother Prince Jiu and, on the advice of Bao Shuya, appointed his former enemy Guan Zhong as chief minister. Their reorganisation of the population, army and revenues laid the foundation for Qi\u0027s rise to primacy among the states.",
  "State of Qi|-679": "Qi\u0027s position as leader of the Zhou states is traditionally dated to a gathering of lords in 679 BCE, after which Duke Huan was recognised as hegemon. Qi convened meetings of the lords, mediated disputes and coordinated defence against outside peoples in the name of the Zhou king.",
  "State of Qi|-651": "At the covenant of Kuiqiu Duke Huan presided over the assembled lords with the approval of King Xiang of Zhou, who sent him sacrificial meat as an honour. This marked the height of his hegemony after campaigns against the Shanrong, the Di and the State of Chu.",
  "State of Qi|-643": "Duke Huan\u0027s death, two years after Guan Zhong\u0027s, was followed by a violent succession struggle among his sons, during which his body reportedly lay unburied for months. Qi lost its hegemony, and leadership of the northern states passed to the State of Jin under Duke Wen.",
  "State of Qi|-589": "Jin and its allies defeated Qi at the Battle of An, and Duke Qing narrowly escaped capture. Qi remained a large and wealthy state but now ranked below Jin and Chu, and power at court increasingly lay with great ministerial families rather than with the ruler.",
  "State of Qi|-481": "Tian Chang killed Duke Jian and installed a compliant successor, giving his family effective control of Qi. The Tian enlarged their own estates and built popular support, while the old Jiang ducal line became a ceremonial shell. Qi remained strong but was absorbed in internal politics.",
  "State of Qi|-386": "King An of Zhou recognised Tian He as ruler of Qi, legitimising the Tian takeover; the last Jiang ruler died a few years later. Qi entered the Warring States period under a new dynasty, with a capital among the largest cities in China and a reorganised administration.",
  "State of Qi|-342": "Qi forces under Tian Ji, advised by the strategist Sun Bin, destroyed the army of Wei at the Battle of Maling, where Pang Juan died. Following the earlier victory at Guiling, this broke Wei\u0027s dominance and made Qi one of the two or three strongest powers.",
  "State of Qi|-334": "King Wei of Qi and King Hui of Wei met at Xuzhou and recognised each other as kings, a formal break with the Zhou hierarchy. Qi was prosperous, the Jixia Academy flourished under royal patronage, and reformed officials and armies made it the leading power of the east.",
  "State of Qi|-314": "King Xuan exploited a civil war in Yan to occupy that state, and Mencius was among the scholars at his court who debated whether the conquest was justified. The occupation provoked resistance and the hostility of other states, and Qi was forced to withdraw, leaving Yan seeking revenge.",
  "State of Qi|-286": "King Min annexed the wealthy State of Song, bringing Qi to its greatest territorial extent and the height of its power. The conquest alarmed every neighbour, and Qin, Yan, Zhao, Wei and Han began to form the coalition that would strike two years later.",
  "State of Qi|-284": "A coalition led by the Yan general Yue Yi defeated Qi west of the Ji River, sacked Linzi and occupied more than seventy cities. King Min was killed at Ju, and only Ju and Jimo held out. Qi had fallen from its peak to near extinction within two years.",
  "State of Qi|-279": "Tian Dan defended Jimo, sowed discord in Yan, broke the siege with his famous fire-ox attack and then recovered the lost cities for King Xiang. Qi was restored as a state, but its wealth and armies never recovered, and it adopted a cautious policy of avoiding wars.",
  "State of Qi|-221": "After Qin had destroyed all the other states, its general Wang Ben attacked from the north and King Jian surrendered Linzi without a battle. Qi became Qin territory, completing the unification of China under King Zheng, who then proclaimed himself First Emperor.",
  "Western Zhou|-1046": "King Wu of Zhou and his allies defeated Di Xin at the Battle of Muye and took the Shang royal centre. The Zhou now claimed the Mandate of Heaven over the Shang heartland, but they ruled it through Shang princes and royal brothers, and their control of the east was still insecure.",
  "Western Zhou|-1043": "King Wu died within a few years of the conquest, traditionally around this date. The Duke of Zhou became regent for the young King Cheng, and Wu Geng, the Shang heir, joined the royal brothers Guanshu and Caishu in the Rebellion of the Three Guards, threatening the new dynasty.",
  "Western Zhou|-1039": "By about this time the Duke of Zhou had crushed the rebellion in a three-year eastern campaign and begun building Chengzhou at Luoyi. The Zhou planted regional states such as Lu, Qi, Yan and Wey across the conquered east, turning a military victory into a lasting network of kin-ruled territories.",
  "Western Zhou|-1020": "Under King Cheng and King Kang the Western Zhou reached its height. Peace at the centre, the growth of the regional states and campaigns into Shandong and the Huai region extended royal authority over its widest area. Later tradition remembered these reigns as an age when punishments went unused.",
  "Western Zhou|-957": "King Zhao\u0027s expedition against Chu and the south ended in disaster: the king died near the Han River and, according to later texts, the Six Armies were lost. The defeat ended southern expansion and began a long period in which the Zhou were mostly on the defensive.",
  "Western Zhou|-920": "King Mu, remembered in legend for his travels to the west, reigned long and campaigned against the Quanrong and the Xu. Royal administration became more bureaucratic, with formal appointment ceremonies recorded on bronzes, but land grants to officials slowly reduced the royal domain.",
  "Western Zhou|-877": "King Li came to the throne. He tried to restore royal authority and revenue and fought the Huaiyi and the rebellious Marquis of E, but his attempts to monopolise resources and silence critics alienated the people of the capital. Royal power was declining as regional lineages grew more independent.",
  "Western Zhou|-841": "A revolt in the capital drove King Li into exile at Zhi, and the Gonghe regency governed until 828 BCE. This year is the first exactly dated year in Chinese history. The court\u0027s authority was at its lowest point before the final collapse of 771 BCE.",
  "Western Zhou|-827": "King Xuan came to the throne and began a revival. His commanders campaigned against the Xianyun in the north-west and the Huaiyi in the south-east, and he enfeoffed new regional states, among them Zheng in 806 BCE and Shen in the south, restoring some royal prestige.",
  "Western Zhou|-800": "The middle of King Xuan\u0027s long reign marked the high point of the late revival, celebrated in bronze inscriptions and in odes of the Book of Songs. Yet defeats such as the reported loss of his southern armies at Qianmu in 789 BCE showed the limits of royal strength.",
  "Western Zhou|-781": "King You succeeded his father. Natural disasters, including an earthquake in the Wei valley recorded in later sources, and a dispute over the succession weakened the court. You\u0027s favour for Bao Si and the deposition of Queen Shen alienated her powerful father, the Marquis of Shen.",
  "Western Zhou|-771": "The Marquis of Shen, the State of Zeng and the Quanrong attacked the capital, killed King You at Mount Li and sacked Haojing. The court abandoned the Wei valley and moved east to Luoyang under King Ping, and the Western Zhou period came to an end.",
  "State of Chu|-1030": "Tradition holds that King Cheng of Zhou enfeoffed Xiong Yi of the Mi clan as a viscount at Danyang, in the hills of the Han River region. Chu was then a minor polity on the southern margin of the Zhou world, regarded by the northern states as semi-barbarian.",
  "State of Chu|-740": "Xiong Tong came to power, reportedly after killing his elder brother\u0027s son, and began a rapid expansion into the plains of the middle Han and Yangtze, attacking neighbours such as Sui. Chu was emerging from obscurity to become a significant regional power in the south.",
  "State of Chu|-704": "Refused a higher rank by the Zhou king, Xiong Tong proclaimed himself king, taking the name King Wu. The title asserted equality with the Zhou kings and defined Chu as a rival centre of power in the south rather than a subordinate fief of the Zhou order.",
  "State of Chu|-689": "King Wen moved the capital to Ying and soon annexed the states of Shen and Xi, which became counties governed by appointed officials. These conquests opened the route north towards the central plain and gave Chu a solid base of manpower and farmland for further expansion.",
  "State of Chu|-632": "Chu\u0027s advance north was stopped when Jin under Duke Wen defeated its army at the Battle of Chengpu. The defeat began a century of rivalry with Jin, but Chu remained the strongest power in the south and continued absorbing the small states of the Han and Huai regions.",
  "State of Chu|-597": "King Zhuang defeated Jin at the Battle of Bi and was recognised as hegemon, after earlier asking a Zhou envoy about the weight of the royal cauldrons, a symbolic challenge to the dynasty. Chu reached the peak of its influence in the Spring and Autumn period.",
  "State of Chu|-575": "Jin defeated Chu at the Battle of Yanling, ending its ascendancy in the central plain. Chu remained one of the two great powers, and in 546 BCE the two rivals agreed at a peace conference in Song to share the leadership of the other states.",
  "State of Chu|-506": "The State of Wu, guided by the Chu exile Wu Zixu, defeated Chu at Boju and occupied Ying. King Zhao fled, and the state survived only after the minister Shen Baoxu persuaded Qin to send help. This was the lowest point of Chu\u0027s fortunes in the Spring and Autumn period.",
  "State of Chu|-479": "King Hui annexed the State of Chen, beginning a renewed advance after the crisis caused by Wu. Wu, exhausted by its wars with Yue and its northern ambitions, was no longer able to threaten Chu, and the Huai valley lay open to Chu expansion once more.",
  "State of Chu|-447": "King Hui annexed the State of Cai, and Chu continued to absorb the small states of the Huai valley. By now it was the largest state of the Zhou world, controlling much of the middle Yangtze and the Han, and its culture of lacquer, silk and bronze was flourishing.",
  "State of Chu|-381": "King Dao died, and the aristocracy killed the reformer Wu Qi, who had cut noble privileges and strengthened the army. Chu kept its vast territory and resources, but the failure of the reforms left it less centralised than Qin, a weakness that would later prove decisive.",
  "State of Chu|-333": "King Wei of Chu is traditionally said to have defeated the State of Yue at about this time and taken its lands, extending Chu to the eastern sea, although the date is debated. Chu was the largest of the Warring States and a leader of alliances against Qin.",
  "State of Chu|-312": "Deceived by the Qin diplomat Zhang Yi into breaking with Qi, King Huai attacked Qin and was defeated at Danyang and Lantian, losing Hanzhong. Chu\u0027s armies were still large, but the defeats exposed its diplomatic isolation and its weakness against the reformed Qin state.",
  "State of Chu|-299": "King Huai was lured to Qin for a meeting and held captive when he refused to cede territory; he died there in 296 BCE. The humiliation weakened the monarchy, and the poet Qu Yuan, who opposed the pro-Qin faction at court, was later sent into exile.",
  "State of Chu|-278": "The Qin general Bai Qi captured the capital Ying and burned the royal tombs at Yiling. Chu lost its western heartland and moved its capital to Chen. Qu Yuan is traditionally said to have drowned himself in the Miluo River at about this time in despair.",
  "State of Chu|-256": "Under King Kaolie and his minister Lord Chunshen, Chu annexed the State of Lu, the homeland of Confucius, and continued to expand in the east. Despite its losses in the west, Chu remained one of the largest states, though it could no longer challenge Qin directly.",
  "State of Chu|-241": "After a failed allied attack on Qin, Chu moved its capital to Shouchun in modern Anhui. Lord Chunshen\u0027s dominance bred factional rivalry at court, while Qin was steadily growing stronger, and Chu\u0027s capacity to resist was shrinking with each passing decade.",
  "State of Chu|-225": "Chu forces under Xiang Yan routed a Qin army of about 200,000 men under Li Xin, the last great Chu victory. Qin had already destroyed Han and Zhao and was completing the conquest of Wei, and Chu\u0027s resistance only delayed the outcome.",
  "State of Chu|-223": "Wang Jian\u0027s large Qin army defeated Chu, captured King Fuchu and killed the last pretender, Lord Changping. Chu was annexed to Qin, but resentment there remained strong, and Chu rebels would rise against the Qin dynasty in 209 BCE and bring it down.",
  "State of Qin|-897": "Tradition dates to about 897 BCE the grant of a small fief at Qin, in eastern Gansu, to Feizi, a horse breeder serving King Xiao of Zhou. The Ying clan held only a minor dependency of the Zhou on a frontier exposed to Rong attacks, and its power stood near its lowest point.",
  "State of Qin|-770": "After the fall of the western Zhou capital, Duke Xiang of Qin escorted King Ping of Zhou eastward and was raised to full ducal rank, with a promise of the old Zhou heartland if he could win it from the Rong. Qin gained status and a claim, but still held little territory.",
  "State of Qin|-677": "Qin moved its capital to Yong, near modern Fengxiang, in the Wei valley that its dukes had won from the Rong over the previous century. Holding the western part of the old Zhou heartland, Qin was now a solid regional power, though still peripheral to the states of the central plain.",
  "State of Qin|-645": "Duke Mu of Qin defeated Jin at the Battle of Han and captured its ruler, Duke Hui, before releasing him. Guided by able advisers such as Baili Xi, Qin briefly shaped the politics of its powerful eastern neighbour, the high point of its power in the Spring and Autumn period.",
  "State of Qin|-621": "Duke Mu died after decades in which, blocked in the east by defeat at Xiao in 627 BCE, he subdued many Rong peoples and dominated the west. The 177 people who followed him into death reflect the wealth of his court, but Qin remained a secondary power behind Jin and Chu.",
  "State of Qin|-408": "In the late fifth century BCE the State of Wei, led by able ministers and the general Wu Qi, seized Qin\u0027s Hexi lands west of the Yellow River. Weakened by disputed successions and court intrigue, Qin was pushed back towards the Luo River and stood at its weakest among the major states.",
  "State of Qin|-361": "Duke Xiao of Qin came to the throne determined to recover the lost lands and called for able men from other states to serve him. Shang Yang answered the call. Duke Xian\u0027s earlier measures, including the end of human sacrifice and a forward capital at Yueyang, had already begun a recovery.",
  "State of Qin|-340": "With Shang Yang\u0027s reforms in force, Qin defeated Wei, and Shang Yang captured the Wei commander, Prince Ang, by a ruse. Wei began ceding the Hexi lands, a process completed by 330 BCE. Qin\u0027s registered, rank-driven farmer-soldiers had made it the rising power of the west.",
  "State of Qin|-325": "Duke Huiwen took the title of king, as the rulers of Wei and Qi had already done. Advised by Zhang Yi, Qin played the eastern states against each other, and a coalition of eastern states that attacked Qin in 318 BCE failed to check its growth.",
  "State of Qin|-316": "Qin\u0027s general Sima Cuo conquered the kingdoms of Ba and Shu in the Sichuan basin, greatly enlarging Qin\u0027s territory and giving it a secure, fertile rear base. Its grain, timber and manpower, later increased by the Dujiangyan irrigation system, sustained Qin\u0027s long wars against the eastern states.",
  "State of Qin|-293": "Bai Qi crushed the combined armies of Han and Wei at Yique, the sources claiming 240,000 enemy dead. Under King Zhaoxiang of Qin (r. 306–251 BCE) Qin pressed steadily east, taking cities from Han and Wei and establishing itself as the strongest of the Warring States.",
  "State of Qin|-278": "Bai Qi captured Ying, the capital of Chu, and Qin turned the Chu heartland on the middle Yangtze into the commandery of Nan. The Chu court moved east to Chen. Qin now held a vast arc of territory from the Wei valley to the Yangtze, far larger than any rival\u0027s.",
  "State of Qin|-260": "At the Battle of Changping Bai Qi encircled and destroyed the army of Zhao, the last state able to field forces comparable to Qin\u0027s. The traditional figure of 400,000 executed prisoners is probably inflated, but Zhao never recovered, and Qin\u0027s dominance of northern China was secured.",
  "State of Qin|-257": "Qin\u0027s siege of the Zhao capital Handan failed when relief armies from Wei and Chu arrived, and Bai Qi, who had opposed the campaign, was forced to take his own life. The setback showed that a coalition could still check Qin, although Qin absorbed the Zhou royal domain in 256 BCE.",
  "State of Qin|-246": "Zheng, aged about thirteen, became king, with the chancellor Lü Buwei directing government. Qin had annexed the last Zhou territory in 249 BCE and continued taking land from Han, Wei and Zhao, while the Zheng Guo Canal, begun this year, would add irrigated farmland north of the Wei.",
  "State of Qin|-230": "Having taken personal control in 238 BCE, King Zheng began the final campaigns with the annexation of Han, the weakest of the six rivals. Advised by Li Si, Qin combined military pressure with bribery and diplomacy to divide its remaining enemies before attacking each in turn.",
  "State of Qin|-221": "The conquest of Qi completed the unification of China. Zheng took the title of First Emperor and turned the kingdom into the Qin dynasty, ruling the whole Chinese world from Xianyang. The State of Qin ended at the summit of its power, transformed into the first imperial state.",
  "Saka|-700": "By about 700 BCE fully mounted pastoral nomadism had spread across the eastern steppe, and early Saka cultures were forming in Kazakhstan, Zhetysu and neighbouring regions. Groups were scattered and their power local, though elite burials of the same steppe horizon, such as Arzhan in Tuva, already show chiefs of considerable wealth.",
  "Saka|-600": "Through the seventh and sixth centuries BCE Saka tribes spread across the steppe from the Aral Sea towards the Altai. Rich burials of the period show an aristocracy able to command wealth and labour, while no outside power yet threatened their pastures or forced them into tributary relationships.",
  "Saka|-530": "According to Herodotus, Cyrus the Great was killed fighting the Massagetae under Queen Tomyris, a people many scholars count within the Saka world. Whatever the literary embellishment, the steppe peoples east of the Caspian had defeated the founder of the largest empire yet seen, a high point of their military strength.",
  "Saka|-519": "Darius I campaigned against the pointed-cap Saka, captured their chief Skunkha and installed a ruler loyal to Persia; Skunkha\u0027s figure was added to the Behistun relief. Several Saka groups now paid tribute and supplied soldiers to the Achaemenid Empire, reducing their independence, though Persian control of the deep steppe remained loose.",
  "Saka|-480": "Saka troops served in the great Persian invasions of Greece, having already fought in the centre of the line at Marathon in 490 BCE. Their prominence in the Persian army shows their military value, but it also reflects a period in which many Saka depended on the Achaemenid king.",
  "Saka|-400": "In the fifth and fourth centuries BCE the steppe Saka flourished. Burial mounds in Zhetysu, the Altai and central Kazakhstan grew larger and richer, and finds in the Pazyryk tombs reveal exchange reaching Persia and China. Achaemenid authority in Central Asia weakened, leaving most Saka groups effectively independent.",
  "Saka|-329": "Alexander the Great defeated a Saka army at the Battle of the Jaxartes after founding Alexandria Eschate on the Syr Darya, but made peace rather than pursue them into the steppe. The Saka remained a powerful, unconquered force on the northern frontier of the new Hellenistic east.",
  "Saka|-300": "After Alexander\u0027s death the Saka faced only the distant Seleucids. Nomad raids appear to have damaged settlements in Margiana and Bactria in the early third century BCE, prompting Seleucid rebuilding of frontier cities. The Saka held the steppe from the Aral Sea to the borderlands of the Tarim Basin.",
  "Saka|-250": "Around the third century BCE the Saka world reached its height in wealth and extent. Elite burials such as the Issyk kurgan near Almaty, with its gold-clad \u0027Golden Man\u0027, probably belong to this period, and Saka groups dominated the Ili valley, Zhetysu, Fergana and the edges of the Tarim Basin.",
  "Saka|-176": "The Xiongnu defeated the Yuezhi in the Gansu corridor, setting off a chain of migrations across Inner Asia. The Saka still held their homelands in the Ili valley and Zhetysu, but the displaced Yuezhi would soon move west into their pastures, ending centuries of Saka dominance there.",
  "Saka|-160": "The Yuezhi, driven west by the Xiongnu, occupied the Ili valley and Zhetysu around the 160s BCE. The Sai king fled south across the Pamirs towards Jibin, while many Saka remained under Yuezhi rule. The core of the Saka steppe world was lost and its peoples scattered.",
  "Saka|-140": "Displaced Saka groups pushed south and west into Fergana, Bactria and the borderlands of Parthia. Nomadic invaders destroyed the Greek city at Ai-Khanoum around 145 BCE, whether Saka or Yuezhi is debated, and the Greco-Bactrian kingdom north of the Hindu Kush collapsed under nomad pressure.",
  "Saka|-127": "Saka warriors, first hired by Parthia and then turned against it, defeated and killed the Parthian king Phraates II in about 127 BCE and ravaged parts of the Iranian plateau. For a brief period the migrating Saka were again a formidable power, threatening the strongest state in the Iranian world.",
  "Saka|-100": "Mithridates II of Parthia subdued the Saka, and many settled in Drangiana, which became Sakastan. The free steppe Saka had disappeared as a political force; their descendants lived on as Parthian subjects, as rulers of Indo-Scythian kingdoms in Gandhara and India, or as oasis peoples in the Tarim Basin.",
  "Yuezhi|-300": "By around 300 BCE the Yuezhi controlled the pastures of the Gansu corridor between Dunhuang and the Qilian Mountains. Their confederation was growing in strength and wealth, profiting from its position on the routes between China and the west, though it faced competition from the Xiongnu and other nomads.",
  "Yuezhi|-230": "In the later third century BCE the Yuezhi were among the strongest peoples on China\u0027s north-western frontier. Chinese sources say they looked down on the still weak Xiongnu, and their control of the corridor gave them access to trade in horses and, possibly, jade from the Tarim Basin.",
  "Yuezhi|-209": "The Yuezhi were at their height when the Xiongnu ruler Touman sent his son Modu to them as a hostage. Modu escaped, killed his father and seized power among the Xiongnu in 209 BCE. The Yuezhi remained dominant in the west, but a formidable rival had now emerged.",
  "Yuezhi|-176": "Modu\u0027s armies crushed the Yuezhi, and a Xiongnu letter to the Han court around this time claimed their defeat and subjection. The Yuezhi lost control of the Gansu corridor and its peoples, and their confederation, though still able to field large forces, began to break apart under Xiongnu pressure.",
  "Yuezhi|-162": "During the reign of the chanyu Laoshang (c. 174–161 BCE) the Xiongnu killed the Yuezhi king and made his skull into a drinking cup. Around this time the main body of the Yuezhi abandoned the Gansu corridor and moved west, leaving the Lesser Yuezhi behind. Their power stood at a low point.",
  "Yuezhi|-150": "Settled in the Ili valley and Zhetysu after defeating the Saka and driving their king south, the Greater Yuezhi rebuilt their strength on the rich pastures of the western Tianshan. Many Saka remained under their rule, and for a generation they were again the dominant power of the region.",
  "Yuezhi|-132": "The Wusun, backed by the Xiongnu, attacked and defeated the Yuezhi in the Ili valley, probably in the 130s BCE. Uprooted for a second time, the Yuezhi set out south-west through Fergana towards the Amu Darya, their future dependent on finding new lands to occupy.",
  "Yuezhi|-128": "The Yuezhi had settled north of the Amu Darya and made Bactria, whose Greek kingdom had collapsed, their tributary. When the Han envoy Zhang Qian reached them about this time, he found them prosperous and content, unwilling to return east for an alliance with Han against the Xiongnu.",
  "Yuezhi|-100": "By about 100 BCE the Greater Yuezhi had consolidated their hold on Bactria, probably extending their rule south of the Amu Darya. Ruling a wealthy urban population and the routes through the Pamirs and Hindu Kush, they were richer than ever, though divided among five princely chiefdoms.",
  "Yuezhi|-50": "The five xihou of the Yuezhi each ruled their own territories in Bactria and its mountain borderlands. Nomad nobles were adopting the urban, Hellenistic culture of their subjects, but the lack of a strong central ruler kept the confederation politically fragmented and limited its power abroad.",
  "Yuezhi|30": "Kujula Kadphises, xihou of the Guishuang, destroyed the other four chiefdoms and made himself king, probably in the first half of the first century CE. Uniting the Yuezhi and expanding towards Kabul and India, he transformed the confederation into the Kushan Empire, near the summit of Yuezhi power.",
  "Xianbei|-206": "Modu, chanyu of the Xiongnu, destroyed the Donghu confederation. Survivors fled into the mountains of the north-east, where one group took the name Xianbei after the Xianbei mountains. They were a small, defeated remnant living under Xiongnu domination, with no independent power of their own.",
  "Xianbei|-100": "Through the second and first centuries BCE the Xianbei lived in the Greater Khingan region and western Manchuria as minor tribes subject to the Xiongnu, appearing hardly at all in Chinese records. They had no unified leadership and no direct relations with the Han court.",
  "Xianbei|45": "The Xianbei first appeared on the Han frontier as raiders alongside the Xiongnu and Wuhuan, and around this time they were defeated in Liaodong by its governor Ji Tong. They were still a secondary people following the lead of the Xiongnu, though now a recognised factor in frontier politics.",
  "Xianbei|49": "Ji Tong won the Xianbei chiefs over with gifts and paid bounties for Xiongnu heads, turning them against their former overlords. The split of the Xiongnu in 48 CE weakened their old masters, and the Xianbei began trading and receiving Han rewards, growing in wealth and confidence.",
  "Xianbei|87": "Xianbei warriors attacked the Northern Xiongnu and killed their chanyu Youliu, a dramatic reversal of the old relationship. Serving Han interests, the Xianbei were now a significant military force on the eastern steppe, though still divided among many independent chiefs.",
  "Xianbei|91": "After Han armies under Dou Xian broke the Northern Xiongnu in 89–91 CE, the Xianbei moved west into the Mongolian heartland. More than 100,000 Xiongnu tents joined them and took the Xianbei name, and they became the largest nomadic population on the steppe, though not yet united.",
  "Xianbei|156": "Tanshihuai was chosen as leader for his courage and fairness and established his court at Mount Danhan, north of the Han frontier. According to the Hou Han shu he repelled the Dingling to the north and the Fuyu to the east and attacked the Wusun to the west, uniting the Xianbei tribes.",
  "Xianbei|166": "Around this time Tanshihuai\u0027s confederation stretched from Manchuria to the borders of the Wusun, divided into eastern, central and western sections. Emperor Huan of Han offered him a royal title and a marriage alliance, which he refused, while his horsemen raided the northern commanderies almost every year.",
  "Xianbei|177": "Tanshihuai routed three Han armies, each said to number 10,000 cavalry, sent deep into the steppe under Xia Yu, Tian Yan and Zang Min; most of their men failed to return. The victory confirmed the Xianbei as the dominant power of the steppe and marked the peak of the confederation.",
  "Xianbei|181": "Tanshihuai died, aged about 45, and his son Helian lacked his authority and was later killed raiding the Han frontier. Without a fixed succession or central institutions, the confederation quickly split into rival groups, and Xianbei power fell sharply from its peak.",
  "Xianbei|190": "With the Han empire sliding into civil war after the Yellow Turban rebellion of 184, the Xianbei were divided among competing chiefs, including members of Tanshihuai\u0027s family such as Budugen. Raids continued, but no leader could command the whole people, and their collective power remained low.",
  "Xianbei|220": "Kebineng, a chief from a minor Xianbei group, had built a large confederation by the time the Cao Wei dynasty was founded in 220. Strengthened by Chinese refugees who taught weapon-making and writing, he controlled much of the eastern and central steppe and dealt with Wei from a position of strength.",
  "Xianbei|231": "Kebineng was at the height of his power, said to command more than 100,000 mounted archers. He answered Zhuge Liang\u0027s call by marching to Beidi in support of the Shu Han offensive against Cao Wei, and in 233 drew the rival chief Budugen into alliance, alarming the Wei court.",
  "Xianbei|235": "Wang Xiong, the Wei inspector of You province, had Kebineng assassinated by an agent named Han Long. The last great Xianbei confederation dissolved, and the Xianbei broke into separate peoples, the Murong, Tuoba, Duan, Yuwen and others, who later founded states in northern China.",
  "Silla|-57": "The Samguk sagi dates to 57 BCE the choice of Bak Hyeokgeose as ruler by the six villages of the Gyeongju basin. The date is traditional and probably too early; Saro was then at most a small chiefdom among the twelve Jinhan statelets, with very limited power.",
  "Silla|262": "Michu became the first king of the Kim lineage, according to the traditional chronology, as the Bak, Seok and Kim clans shared the throne. Saro was gradually extending its influence over neighbouring Jinhan communities, but it remained a modest regional chiefdom in south-eastern Korea.",
  "Silla|356": "Naemul began a reign in which the Kim clan made the throne hereditary and the title maripgan came into use. Saro absorbed most of the Jinhan polities and sent envoys to the Former Qin court in China in 377 and 382, marking its emergence as a recognisable kingdom.",
  "Silla|400": "Under attack from Wa raiders and Gaya, Silla depended on Goguryeo, whose King Gwanggaeto sent an army south in 400, according to his stele. The rescue preserved Silla but placed it under Goguryeo influence, with Goguryeo troops present in its territory for much of the fifth century.",
  "Silla|503": "King Jijeung adopted the dynastic name Silla and the Chinese royal title wang, and encouraged ox-drawn ploughing and stronger central control. Having shaken off Goguryeo tutelage, Silla was growing into a consolidated kingdom, and in 512 its general Isabu subdued the island of Usan.",
  "Silla|527": "King Beopheung, who had promulgated a law code in 520, recognised Buddhism after the martyrdom of Ichadon. The new faith and a centralised system of official ranks gave the monarchy ideological and administrative tools that strengthened it against the old aristocracy of the six districts.",
  "Silla|532": "Geumgwan Gaya at Gimhae surrendered to Silla, and its royal family was admitted to the Silla aristocracy; Kim Yusin was among its descendants. Silla gained the lower Nakdong River and access to the iron and sea trade of the south coast, reinforcing its rise among the Korean kingdoms.",
  "Silla|553": "Having joined Baekje to drive Goguryeo from the Han River valley in 551, King Jinheung seized Baekje\u0027s share of it. Silla now reached the Yellow Sea and could deal directly with Chinese courts, and in 554 its army killed Baekje\u0027s King Seong at the Battle of Gwansan Fortress.",
  "Silla|562": "Silla conquered Daegaya, the last independent Gaya state, completing its control of the Nakdong basin. Jinheung\u0027s stelae marked frontiers far to the north-east and north-west, and the hwarang trained a new military elite. Silla had become a power equal to Baekje and Goguryeo.",
  "Silla|642": "Baekje captured some forty Silla fortresses, including Daeya, where Kim Chunchu\u0027s daughter and son-in-law died, while Goguryeo also pressed on the northern frontier. Under Queen Seondeok Silla was on the defensive, and Kim Chunchu sought help first from Goguryeo and then from Tang China.",
  "Silla|660": "With Tang forces landing under Su Dingfang, Silla\u0027s army under Kim Yusin overcame Gyebaek\u0027s last stand at Hwangsanbeol, and the Baekje capital Sabi fell. Silla, now ruled by Kim Chunchu as King Muyeol, had eliminated one of its two great rivals, though Baekje loyalists fought on.",
  "Silla|668": "Silla and Tang armies took Pyongyang and destroyed Goguryeo. Silla controlled the south of the peninsula, but Tang set up the Protectorate General to Pacify the East to rule the conquered lands, making clear that it intended to dominate Korea rather than share it with Silla.",
  "Silla|676": "After six years of war, Silla\u0027s victories at Maesoseong in 675 and Gibeolpo in 676 led Tang to withdraw its protectorate to Liaodong. Silla ruled the peninsula south of the Taedong River, reaching its greatest territorial extent and security under King Munmu.",
  "Silla|687": "King Sinmun completed the reorganisation of the unified kingdom into nine provinces and five secondary capitals, created the nine seodang divisions and founded the National Confucian Academy. Royal authority over the aristocracy stood at its height, and the state enjoyed peace and prosperity.",
  "Silla|751": "Under King Gyeongdeok the minister Kim Daeseong began building Bulguksa and the Seokguram grotto. Unified Silla\u0027s culture, Buddhist patronage and trade with Tang China and Japan flourished, though the restoration of stipend villages to officials in 757 showed the aristocracy regaining ground against the throne.",
  "Silla|780": "King Hyegong was killed during an aristocratic revolt, ending the direct line of King Muyeol on the throne. Twenty kings reigned over the next 155 years, many after violent disputes, and the central government\u0027s grip on the provinces and the aristocracy began to loosen.",
  "Silla|822": "Kim Heonchang, governor of Ungju, rebelled and proclaimed a state called Jangan, winning support in several provinces before he was crushed. Silla survived, but the revolt and the later rise of maritime magnates such as Jang Bogo revealed growing regional independence.",
  "Silla|889": "Under Queen Jinseong, attempts to collect taxes from impoverished provinces provoked peasant uprisings, beginning at Sabeolju. Local strongmen and bandit leaders took control of much of the countryside, and the court in Gyeongju lost effective authority over most of the kingdom.",
  "Silla|900": "Gyeon Hwon proclaimed Later Baekje in the south-west in 900, and Gung Ye founded Later Goguryeo in the north in 901. Silla was reduced to the Gyeongju region and a few loyal districts, one of three rival states in the Later Three Kingdoms period.",
  "Silla|927": "Gyeon Hwon\u0027s forces sacked Gyeongju and forced King Gyeongae to take his own life, placing Gyeongsun on the throne. Silla survived only as a dependent remnant, its kings looking to Wang Geon of Goryeo for protection against Later Baekje until the end came.",
  "Silla|935": "King Gyeongsun surrendered his kingdom peacefully to Goryeo. Wang Geon received him with honour, and the Silla aristocracy was absorbed into the new state, ending a kingdom that had lasted, by traditional reckoning, nearly a thousand years. Goryeo completed the reunification of Korea in 936.",
  "Goguryeo|-37": "According to the Samguk sagi, Jumong, a refugee prince from Buyeo, founded Goguryeo at Jolbon in the middle Yalu region. The state was then a small federation of Yemaek clans in poor mountain country, overshadowed by Buyeo and by the Han commanderies of Xuantu and Liaodong, so its power was minimal.",
  "Goguryeo|3": "Tradition dates the transfer of the capital to Gungnae, modern Ji\u0027an on the Yalu, to the reign of King Yuri. The new centre gave the federation a defensible base with river access, but Goguryeo remained a minor polity on the Han frontier, still contending with Buyeo and Chinese pressure.",
  "Goguryeo|53": "The accession of King Taejo, traditionally dated 53, opened a period of expansion in which Goguryeo subjugated the Okjeo and Dongye peoples of the east coast and raided Liaodong and Xuantu. Tribute from these subjects strengthened a state that was growing from a clan federation into a monarchy.",
  "Goguryeo|244": "The Cao Wei general Guanqiu Jian invaded in 244–245, defeated King Dongcheon and sacked the capital Hwando, and the king fled to the Okjeo country. The disaster showed the limits of Goguryeo\u0027s strength against a unified northern Chinese state, though the kingdom recovered once the Wei forces withdrew.",
  "Goguryeo|313": "King Micheon captured the Lelang commandery in 313 and Daifang soon after, ending more than four centuries of Chinese administration in north-western Korea. The collapse of the Western Jin left a vacuum that Goguryeo exploited, gaining fertile land and the old commandery centre near Pyongyang.",
  "Goguryeo|342": "Former Yan forces under Murong Huang sacked the capital, seized the queen mother and carried off the body of King Micheon to force King Gogugwon\u0027s submission. Goguryeo was humiliated and sent tribute to the Yan, and its westward expansion into Liaodong was blocked for the time being.",
  "Goguryeo|371": "King Gogugwon was killed defending Pyongyang against the Baekje king Geunchogo. Weakened on two fronts, Goguryeo turned to internal reform under King Sosurim, who received Buddhism, founded the Taehak academy and issued a law code, building the institutions that made later expansion possible.",
  "Goguryeo|391": "Gwanggaeto the Great came to the throne and began two decades of campaigns recorded on his stele: against Baekje, which lost dozens of fortresses in 396, against the Khitan and the Later Yan, and in support of Silla. Goguryeo became the leading power of the peninsula and southern Manchuria.",
  "Goguryeo|413": "At Gwanggaeto\u0027s death Goguryeo controlled Liaodong, much of Manchuria and the northern peninsula, had subdued eastern Buyeo and treated Silla as a dependent ally. His son Jangsu inherited a powerful state, and in 414 erected the great stele at Ji\u0027an recording his father\u0027s conquests.",
  "Goguryeo|427": "King Jangsu moved the capital from Gungnae to Pyongyang, shifting the kingdom\u0027s centre to the richer Taedong basin and signalling his ambitions in the south. He kept stable relations with both the Northern Wei and the southern Chinese courts, securing the west while he pressed Baekje and Silla.",
  "Goguryeo|475": "Jangsu\u0027s armies took the Baekje capital Hanseong and executed King Gaero, forcing Baekje to move south to Ungjin. Goguryeo now held the Han River basin and approached its greatest extent, from the Liao and Sungari regions to the centre of the peninsula, the peak of its territorial and political power.",
  "Goguryeo|494": "The last ruler of Buyeo surrendered to King Munja, completing Goguryeo\u0027s absorption of the kingdom from which its founder was said to have come. Goguryeo remained at its peak as the dominant state of north-east Asia, though Baekje and Silla were now allied against it.",
  "Goguryeo|551": "A Silla and Baekje alliance drove Goguryeo from the Han River basin, which it had held since 475, and Silla seized the whole region two years later. Goguryeo was also troubled by succession strife and by Turkic attacks in the north, and its southern frontier receded sharply.",
  "Goguryeo|598": "Sui Wendi sent a large army and fleet after Goguryeo raided Liaoxi; disease, storms and supply failures destroyed the expedition without a major battle. Goguryeo had survived, but now faced a reunified China determined to control Liaodong, and the long contest that followed would consume both states.",
  "Goguryeo|612": "Sui Yangdi led one of the largest armies of the pre-modern world against Goguryeo. Liaodong fortress held, and a column sent towards Pyongyang was routed by Eulji Mundeok at the Salsu River. Victory raised Goguryeo\u0027s prestige, and further Sui campaigns in 613 and 614 also failed.",
  "Goguryeo|645": "Tang Taizong invaded in person, took Liaodong and several other fortresses and defeated a relief army, but failed to take Ansi and withdrew as winter approached. Goguryeo again survived, now under the dictatorship of Yeon Gaesomun, but its frontier was weakened and the Tang prepared repeated raids.",
  "Goguryeo|666": "After Yeon Gaesomun\u0027s death his sons fought for power. The eldest, Yeon Namsaeng, fled to the Tang and offered to guide their armies, and Yeon Gaesomun\u0027s brother surrendered a group of fortresses to Silla. With Baekje already destroyed, a divided Goguryeo faced a combined Tang and Silla offensive.",
  "Goguryeo|668": "Tang armies under Li Ji, joined by Silla, took Pyongyang after a siege and captured King Bojang, ending the kingdom. The Tang established the Protectorate General to Pacify the East and deported many families to China, while restoration attempts continued and Goguryeo traditions later passed into Balhae.",
  "Baekje|-18": "According to the Samguk sagi, Onjo, a son of Goguryeo\u0027s founder Jumong, founded Baekje at Wiryeseong on the Han River. In reality Baekje probably began as one of the fifty or so Mahan statelets of south-western Korea, small and overshadowed by its neighbours, so its power stood near its minimum.",
  "Baekje|250": "In the 3rd century, traditionally under King Goi (234–286), Baekje grew into the leading Mahan polity of the Han basin, building large earthen-walled centres such as Pungnap Toseong. Tradition credits Goi with official ranks and robes, marking a transition from chiefdom to kingdom, though Baekje still controlled only modest territory.",
  "Baekje|346": "King Geunchogo\u0027s accession began Baekje\u0027s greatest age. He extended royal control over the remaining Mahan polities towards the south-west coast, consolidated the Han basin heartland and built ties with the Wa of Japan, giving Baekje the manpower and resources to challenge Goguryeo in the north.",
  "Baekje|371": "Geunchogo\u0027s army attacked Pyongyang and killed the Goguryeo king Gogugwon, and in 372 Baekje opened relations with the Eastern Jin. Controlling the richest farmland of the peninsula, with diplomatic reach to China and Japan, Baekje stood at the height of its relative power among the Korean kingdoms.",
  "Baekje|396": "Gwanggaeto the Great of Goguryeo captured dozens of Baekje fortresses and, according to his stele, forced King Asin to submit. Baekje lost ground north of the Han and turned increasingly to its Wa allies, but it remained a substantial kingdom with its heartland around Hanseong intact.",
  "Baekje|433": "Baekje and Silla, both threatened by Goguryeo\u0027s southward expansion under King Jangsu, formed an alliance that lasted more than a century. Baekje\u0027s position was steady but defensive: it relied on allies and on Chinese recognition, while Goguryeo\u0027s move to Pyongyang in 427 had brought the main threat closer.",
  "Baekje|475": "Jangsu of Goguryeo stormed Hanseong and executed King Gaero. The court fled south to Ungjin, modern Gongju, losing the Han basin that had been Baekje\u0027s core for centuries. Short reigns and assassinations followed, and the kingdom was at its weakest point before its final fall.",
  "Baekje|501": "King Muryeong restored stability after the turmoil at Ungjin, strengthened royal authority over the aristocracy, repelled Goguryeo attacks and sent envoys to the Liang dynasty. His tomb at Gongju, with its Chinese-style brick chamber, reflects a revived and cosmopolitan court. Baekje recovered to a middling position among the Three Kingdoms.",
  "Baekje|538": "King Seong moved the capital to Sabi, modern Buyeo, renamed the state Nambuyeo and reorganised government into departments and regions. Buddhism flourished and links with the Liang and Yamato were close. The kingdom was better organised than at any time since the loss of Hanseong sixty years earlier.",
  "Baekje|554": "After Silla seized the Han basin, which the allies had recovered together in 551, King Seong attacked Silla and was killed at the Battle of Gwansanseong with much of his army. The defeat ended the Silla alliance and left Baekje facing two hostile neighbours with reduced manpower.",
  "Baekje|600": "King Mu, who reigned 600–641, campaigned repeatedly against Silla and sponsored great Buddhist building, including the vast Mireuksa temple at Iksan. Relations with the Sui and later the Tang were maintained, and Baekje had regained confidence, though the strategic balance on the peninsula was shifting towards Silla.",
  "Baekje|642": "King Uija took about forty Silla fortresses and the stronghold of Daeya, killing the son-in-law of Kim Chunchu. These were Baekje\u0027s greatest gains in generations, but they drove Silla into a close alliance with the Tang, who saw Baekje as part of Goguryeo\u0027s defensive system.",
  "Baekje|660": "A Tang fleet under Su Dingfang and a Silla army under Kim Yu-sin converged on Sabi; Gyebaek fell at Hwangsanbeol, the capital was taken and King Uija surrendered. Restoration forces backed by Yamato fought on until their defeat at Baekgang in 663, but the kingdom itself had ended.",
  "Kushan Empire|30": "Around this time Kujula Kadphises, chief of the Kushan branch of the Yuezhi, defeated the four other Yuezhi chiefdoms of Bactria and made himself king, though the exact date is uncertain. His realm was still confined to the lands north of the Hindu Kush, one regional power among several.",
  "Kushan Empire|60": "Kujula crossed the Hindu Kush and, according to the Hou Hanshu, attacked the Parthians and took the Kabul region and Gandhara, destroying the Indo-Parthian principalities there. Dates are approximate, but by the later 1st century the Kushans held both sides of the mountains and controlled key routes between Central Asia and India.",
  "Kushan Empire|90": "Under Vima Takto the Kushans pressed into the Punjab and beyond. A Kushan army of reportedly 70,000 men crossed the Pamirs against the Han general Ban Chao in the Tarim Basin but was forced to withdraw by supply failure. The setback ended northern ambitions, though the Indian conquests continued.",
  "Kushan Empire|113": "Vima Kadphises, son of Vima Takto, ruled from about this date, though estimates vary. He extended Kushan rule deeper into northern India and introduced a large gold coinage, much of it showing the god Oesho. The empire\u0027s wealth from trade between Rome, Parthia, India and China was growing rapidly.",
  "Kushan Empire|127": "Kanishka the Great acceded, on the widely accepted dating that places the first year of his era in 127. He made Bactrian the language of state, issued coins with a broad pantheon, and, according to the Rabatak inscription, held authority across the Ganges plain as far as Pataliputra and Champa.",
  "Kushan Empire|150": "The later years of Kanishka and the reign of Huvishka mark the height of the empire, stretching from the Amu Darya to the middle Ganges. Gandharan and Mathuran art flourished, Buddhist monasteries multiplied and Kushan gold circulated widely. Stability allowed the dynasty to profit fully from Silk Road commerce.",
  "Kushan Empire|190": "Vasudeva I, the last great Kushan king, reigned from about 190. His inscriptions are concentrated in the Mathura region and his coins favour Oesho, often identified with Shiva. The empire remained large and wealthy, but there are signs that the northern provinces were less firmly held than before.",
  "Kushan Empire|229": "An embassy from Bodiao, king of the Da Yuezhi, probably Vasudeva I, reached the Wei court in China and received a royal title. It was the last major Kushan contact with China, sent just as the new Sasanian dynasty in Persia was beginning to threaten the empire\u0027s western flank.",
  "Kushan Empire|240": "The Sasanians, who had overthrown the Parthians in 224, pushed east under Ardashir I and Shapur I. Kushan rulers after Vasudeva I faced Persian advances into Bactria and the Kabul region, while the Indian provinces began slipping into the hands of local dynasties such as the Nagas.",
  "Kushan Empire|262": "Shapur I\u0027s inscription at Naqsh-e Rostam, from around this time, claims Kushanshahr as far as Peshawar for the Sasanians. Bactria became the Kushano-Sasanian Kingdom under Persian princes, and the Kushan kings were reduced to lands in Gandhara, the Punjab and Kashmir, possibly under Sasanian overlordship.",
  "Kushan Empire|300": "Later Kushan kings such as Vasishka and Vasudeva II ruled a much smaller realm around the Indus and the Punjab, issuing increasingly debased coins. Mathura and the Ganges region were held by the Nagas and other local powers. The dynasty survived only as one regional kingdom among several in north-western India.",
  "Kushan Empire|350": "The Gupta king Samudragupta\u0027s Allahabad pillar inscription lists a Daivaputra-Shahi-Shahanushahi among rulers who paid him homage, generally identified with a late Kushan king. At the same time the Kidarites, a new power from the north, were moving into Bactria and towards Gandhara, threatening the last Kushan principalities.",
  "Kushan Empire|375": "By the later 4th century the Kidarites under their founder Kidara had taken Gandhara and the remaining Kushan lands, adopting Kushan-style coinage and titles. The Kushan dynasty disappears from the record around this time, although its name and prestige lived on in the titles of its successors.",
  "Eastern Jin|317": "Sima Rui proclaimed himself King of Jin at Jiankang after the fall of Chang\u0027an, taking the imperial title the next year. The new regime held the south but depended on the Wang clan and on the southern gentry, and faced northern states that had just destroyed the Western Jin.",
  "Eastern Jin|322": "Wang Dun, commander of the middle Yangtze, rebelled and marched on Jiankang, humiliating Emperor Yuan, who died soon after. A second rising in 324 ended with Wang Dun\u0027s death, and in 328 Su Jun\u0027s rebels sacked the capital. Internal conflict kept the regime weak for a decade.",
  "Eastern Jin|347": "Huan Wen, governor of Jing province, conquered the Cheng Han state in Sichuan, adding the rich Shu basin to the Eastern Jin. The victory, won with a small army, made Huan Wen the dominant figure of the state and greatly expanded the territory and resources at the court\u0027s disposal.",
  "Eastern Jin|356": "Huan Wen retook Luoyang, the old Western Jin capital, two years after advancing to the outskirts of Chang\u0027an against the Former Qin. Although he could not hold the north for long, the recovery of Luoyang showed that the southern regime could project power well beyond the Huai River.",
  "Eastern Jin|369": "Huan Wen\u0027s expedition against the Former Yan ended in a heavy defeat at Fangtou, damaging his prestige. In the following years the Former Qin under Fu Jian conquered the Former Yan and much of the north, and in 373 took Sichuan from the Jin, creating a far stronger northern enemy.",
  "Eastern Jin|373": "Huan Wen died before he could usurp the throne, and leadership passed to Xie An, who balanced the Huan family and the court. The Former Qin was unifying northern China and had annexed Sichuan, and the Eastern Jin prepared its defences, including the Beifu army formed at Jingkou in 377.",
  "Eastern Jin|383": "At the Battle of Fei River the Beifu army under Xie Shi and Xie Xuan routed the vanguard of Fu Jian\u0027s huge Former Qin invasion, and the northern army disintegrated in retreat. The victory saved the southern dynasty from conquest and set off the collapse of the Former Qin state.",
  "Eastern Jin|385": "Following up the victory, Xie Xuan\u0027s northern campaigns of 384–385 recovered lands as far as the Yellow River, including much of Henan and Shandong, as the Former Qin broke apart. Xie An died in 385, but the Eastern Jin briefly stood at its greatest territorial extent and security.",
  "Eastern Jin|396": "Emperor Xiaowu was murdered in his palace, leaving the disabled Emperor An on the throne under the control of Sima Daozi and his son Sima Yuanxian. Northern gains were slipping away to new states such as the Later Yan, and quarrels between the court and provincial governors split the regime.",
  "Eastern Jin|399": "Sun En\u0027s Daoist rebellion swept the coastal commanderies south of the Yangtze, killing officials and devastating the region that supplied much of the court\u0027s revenue. Beifu troops under Liu Laozhi suppressed it brutally, while the governors upstream in Jing province grew increasingly independent of Jiankang.",
  "Eastern Jin|403": "Huan Xuan, who had taken Jiankang the previous year, forced Emperor An to abdicate and proclaimed his own Chu dynasty. The Jin dynasty was formally interrupted, and the state, ravaged by the Sun En revolt and divided among warlords, had reached its lowest point.",
  "Eastern Jin|404": "Liu Yu and other Beifu officers rose against Huan Xuan, drove him from Jiankang and killed him, and Emperor An was restored. The dynasty survived, but real authority now lay with Liu Yu, whose army had become the main source of power in the state.",
  "Eastern Jin|410": "Liu Yu conquered the Southern Yan, taking its capital Guanggu in Shandong, and then turned back to defeat Lu Xun, whose rebels had nearly reached Jiankang. These victories restored control over the south and extended Jin territory northward, greatly strengthening the state under Liu Yu\u0027s leadership.",
  "Eastern Jin|417": "Liu Yu\u0027s armies conquered the Later Qin and entered Chang\u0027an, the first southern recovery of both old capitals in a century; Western Shu had fallen in 413. Militarily the state was at a new height, though Chang\u0027an was lost to the Xia in 418 after Liu Yu returned south.",
  "Eastern Jin|420": "Emperor Gong, enthroned after Liu Yu had Emperor An killed, abdicated in Liu Yu\u0027s favour, and the Liu Song dynasty began. The Eastern Jin ended not through conquest but by a transfer of power, and its territory, capital and institutions passed intact to its successor.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|330": "In the early 4th century the Rouran were only a band of fugitives and herders north of the Gobi, said to descend from Mugulü, a runaway slave of the Tuoba. Under his son Cheluhui they became a tribe paying tribute to the Tuoba state of Dai, with no independent power to speak of.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|391": "The Northern Wei founder Tuoba Gui campaigned against the Rouran and defeated them, driving them away from the Wei frontier. The tribe was divided between rival chiefs and suffered repeated losses, but the setback pushed them north into Mongolia, where Shelun would rebuild them into a much larger confederation.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|402": "Shelun conquered the Gaoche and other tribes of Mongolia, organised his warriors in units of thousands and hundreds, and took the title khagan. In a few years the Rouran grew from a fugitive band into a confederation stretching across the steppe, strong enough to challenge the Northern Wei.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|410": "By Shelun\u0027s death the khaganate reached, according to the Wei shu, from the borders of Korea in the east to Karashahr in the west, with its centre in the Mongolian steppe. His brother Hulü succeeded, but succession disputes soon followed, a recurring weakness of Rouran rule.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|424": "Under the khagan Datan the Rouran raided Shengle, an old Northern Wei centre, with a large cavalry army soon after the death of Emperor Mingyuan, threatening the Wei heartland. This marked the height of Rouran pressure on northern China, before the new emperor Taiwu turned to the offensive.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|429": "Emperor Taiwu\u0027s surprise invasion of Mongolia caught the Rouran scattered; Datan fled and died soon after, and many Gaoche subjects surrendered to the Wei with their herds. Further Wei campaigns in the following decades kept the Rouran on the defensive, sharply reducing their power and reach.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|460": "Rouran forces took Gaochang in the Turfan basin from the last Northern Liang ruler and installed Kan Bozhou as a dependent king. Rouran influence over the western oases was at its height, and the khaganate had largely recovered from the Wei offensives of earlier decades.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|487": "Afuzhiluo led the Gaoche tribes west and founded his own kingdom north-west of Gaochang, refusing to join a Rouran attack on the Wei. The loss of these numerous herders and warriors weakened the khaganate and created a lasting enemy on its western flank, while Wei pressure continued in the south.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|508": "The Gaoche king Mi\u0027etu defeated and killed the khagan Futu. Rouran fortunes were at a low ebb, with western subjects lost and power contested within the ruling clan. Futu\u0027s son Chounu later defeated and killed Mi\u0027etu, briefly restoring Rouran prestige, but internal intrigue continued.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|520": "Chounu was killed in a palace conspiracy, and his brother Anagui, soon driven out by a rival, fled to the Northern Wei court at Luoyang. The Rouran were in civil war, with the khaganate split between claimants and dependent on Chinese support for the survival of its royal line.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|525": "Restored with Wei backing, Anagui rebuilt his power and helped the Northern Wei against the Six Garrisons rebellion, receiving rewards and freedom of action. With northern China sliding into civil war, the Rouran regained the initiative on the frontier, and Anagui consolidated his rule over the steppe.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|541": "Anagui crushed the Gaoche kingdom in the west and played the rival Eastern Wei and Western Wei against each other through marriage alliances, both courts sending princesses and gifts. The khaganate had recovered much of its former extent and stood again as the leading power of the steppe.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|546": "Bumin, leader of the Turks who served the Rouran as ironworkers in the Altai, defeated a Tiele force marching against the khaganate and asked for a Rouran princess. Anagui refused with an insult, a decision that turned his most capable vassal into an enemy at the height of his own power.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|552": "Bumin, now allied with the Western Wei by marriage, defeated the Rouran north of Huaihuang, and Anagui killed himself. The Turks proclaimed their own khaganate, and the Rouran broke into fragments fleeing east and south, some seeking refuge in northern China.",
  "Rouran Khaganate|555": "The Turkic khagan Muqan destroyed the last Rouran groups in the steppe, and the Western Wei, seeking Turkic friendship, surrendered about 3,000 Rouran refugees, who were executed outside Chang\u0027an. The khaganate ceased to exist, though some scholars connect fleeing Rouran with the Avars of Europe.",
  "Tibetan Empire|618": "Namri Songtsen had extended Yarlung rule over much of central Tibet, and his son Songtsen Gampo succeeded him around this time, possibly somewhat later. The young king faced revolts by newly conquered clans and the former lords of central Tibet, so the new state was still fragile and confined to the heartland of the plateau.",
  "Tibetan Empire|634": "Songtsen Gampo had crushed the revolts, made Lhasa his seat and subjected the Sumpa of the north-east. His first embassy reached Tang China in 634, and in 638 a Tibetan army attacked the Tang frontier at Songzhou. Tibet was emerging as a regional power, though Zhangzhung in the west remained independent.",
  "Tibetan Empire|641": "Princess Wencheng arrived in Tibet as a bride for the royal house, sealing peace with the Tang emperor Taizong. In the following years Zhangzhung was absorbed, uniting central and western Tibet. When Songtsen Gampo died around 649, the kingdom covered much of the plateau and commanded the respect of its neighbours.",
  "Tibetan Empire|663": "Under the minister Gar Tongtsen, who governed after Songtsen Gampo\u0027s death, Tibet conquered the Tuyuhun around Lake Kokonor, gaining rich pastures and a gateway to the Tang frontier in Gansu. The Gar clan gave the young empire capable leadership and turned its army outwards, beginning decades of expansion at China\u0027s expense.",
  "Tibetan Empire|670": "Gar Trinring Tsendro routed a Tang army under Xue Rengui at the Dafei River, and Tibetan forces with Western Turkic allies drove the Tang from the Four Garrisons of the Tarim Basin. Tibet now contested the Silk Road oases, a remarkable reach for a state only about half a century old.",
  "Tibetan Empire|692": "A Tang counter-offensive retook the Four Garrisons of the Tarim Basin, and in 698 the emperor Tridu Songtsen destroyed the overmighty Gar clan, whose leaders were killed or fled to China. The empire lost its hold on the oases and went through internal upheaval, although its core on the plateau remained secure.",
  "Tibetan Empire|704": "Tridu Songtsen died while campaigning in the south-east, and the throne passed to the infant Me Agtsom under the regency of his powerful grandmother Thrimalö. Revolts in the south-east and the absence of an adult ruler limited Tibetan initiative, although the empire kept its gains around Lake Kokonor.",
  "Tibetan Empire|710": "Princess Jincheng of Tang married into the Tibetan royal house, and the Tang granted lands in the Jiuqu district on the upper Yellow River. Tibet then turned westwards, at times allying with the Turgesh. Its power was steady rather than growing, sustained more by alliances and diplomacy than by new conquests.",
  "Tibetan Empire|747": "The Tang general Gao Xianzhi crossed the Pamirs and seized Little Balur, cutting Tibet\u0027s route to its western allies, while Tang armies pressed on the Kokonor frontier. Tibet was on the defensive in the decade before the An Lushan Rebellion, and Me Agtsom\u0027s long reign was ending amid court intrigue.",
  "Tibetan Empire|755": "Me Agtsom was killed in a conspiracy, and his son Trisong Detsen came to the throne just as the An Lushan Rebellion broke out in China. With Tang frontier armies withdrawn to fight the rebels, and Nanzhao now a Tibetan ally, the empire was poised for its greatest expansion.",
  "Tibetan Empire|763": "A Tibetan army entered Chang\u0027an, the Tang capital, and installed a puppet emperor before withdrawing after about two weeks. In the following years Tibet seized Liangzhou and the eastern Hexi Corridor. The raid showed that Tibet had become the dominant military power on China\u0027s western frontier.",
  "Tibetan Empire|786": "Dunhuang, the last Tang stronghold in the Hexi Corridor, surrendered around this time, completing Tibetan control of the corridor. Trisong Detsen had founded Samye and made Buddhism the religion of the court. With tribute from Nanzhao and the oases, the empire stood at its greatest extent and prestige.",
  "Tibetan Empire|794": "Nanzhao abandoned its Tibetan alliance, rejoined the Tang and defeated Tibetan forces in Yunnan, while Tang and Uyghur armies pressed in the north. Trisong Detsen\u0027s reign was nearing its end, and disputes over Buddhism divided the court. The empire remained vast but faced enemies on several fronts.",
  "Tibetan Empire|821": "Ralpachen concluded a sworn treaty with Tang China, recorded on a pillar in Lhasa, and settled with the Uyghurs. Peace preserved Tibet\u0027s hold on the Hexi Corridor, but lavish patronage of Buddhism and the influence of clerics at court bred resentment among the nobility, setting the stage for crisis.",
  "Tibetan Empire|842": "Udumtsen, called Langdarma by later tradition, was assassinated, and rival factions backed two claimants to the throne. Central authority dissolved, commanders in the north-east fought one another and Dunhuang was lost in 848. The Tibetan Empire ceased to exist as a unified state, giving way to the Era of Fragmentation.",
  "Balhae|698": "Dae Joyeong defeated a Tang army at Tianmenling and founded the state of Jin near Mount Dongmo, gathering Goguryeo refugees and Mohe tribesmen. The new kingdom was small and insecure, threatened by Tang and surrounded by independent Mohe groups, but it held a defensible base in the mountains of eastern Manchuria.",
  "Balhae|713": "Tang recognised Dae Joyeong as Commandery Prince of Bohai, ending hostilities and giving the state the name by which it became known. Recognition secured the frontier and allowed the kingdom to absorb neighbouring Goguryeo remnants and Mohe tribes, so that by Dae Joyeong\u0027s death in 719 it held much of eastern Manchuria.",
  "Balhae|719": "King Mu succeeded his father and pursued an assertive policy of expansion, using his own reign title. He extended Balhae rule over neighbouring Mohe groups to the north and east, and in 727 sent the first Balhae embassy to Japan, looking for partners against Tang and Silla.",
  "Balhae|732": "After a quarrel with Tang over the Heishui Mohe, a Balhae fleet under Jang Mun-hyu raided Dengzhou in Shandong. A Tang request for Silla to attack Balhae in 733 failed when the Silla army turned back in snow. Balhae had shown it could defy Tang, though at the cost of open war.",
  "Balhae|737": "King Mun came to the throne and began a long reign of consolidation. Relations with Tang turned peaceful, and Balhae adopted Tang-style institutions: three departments, six ministries and a network of prefectures. The kingdom\u0027s strength now rested on administration, trade and diplomacy rather than on war with its powerful neighbour.",
  "Balhae|756": "Around this time King Mun moved the court to Sanggyeong, a new capital in the Mudan River valley laid out on the plan of Chang\u0027an. While Tang China was convulsed by the An Lushan Rebellion, Balhae stayed out of the conflict and continued to build up its institutions and cities.",
  "Balhae|762": "Tang raised the Balhae ruler\u0027s title from commandery prince to king of the Bohai state, recognising its standing as a full kingdom. Embassies to Tang and Japan were regular, and Balhae controlled most of eastern Manchuria and the north of the Korean peninsula under a settled administration.",
  "Balhae|793": "King Mun died after a reign of more than half a century, and a series of brief reigns followed amid disputes over the succession. The court returned to Sanggyeong in 794. The kingdom\u0027s institutions held, but central leadership remained weaker for a quarter of a century.",
  "Balhae|818": "King Seon, from a collateral branch of the royal house descended from Dae Joyeong\u0027s brother, came to the throne and restored strong rule. He campaigned against the Mohe tribes of the north, bringing many under Balhae control and extending the kingdom towards the Amur and Ussuri rivers.",
  "Balhae|830": "At King Seon\u0027s death Balhae was at its greatest extent, organised into five capitals, fifteen prefectures and sixty-two districts. In this period Tang called it the flourishing land of the east. Trade with Tang and Japan was active, and Sanggyeong was one of the largest cities of north-east Asia.",
  "Balhae|857": "The long reign of Dae Ijin (830–857) kept the kingdom stable and prosperous, with regular embassies to Tang and Japan. Detailed records of the later ninth century are scarce, but Balhae appears to have maintained its institutions, although its hold on remote Mohe groups gradually weakened.",
  "Balhae|907": "The fall of the Tang dynasty removed the regional order that had sheltered Balhae, while the Khitan under Yelü Abaoji grew rapidly in strength on its western frontier. Balhae still held its territories, but it now faced an expansionist neighbour with no Chinese power to balance it.",
  "Balhae|916": "Abaoji proclaimed himself emperor of the Khitan and turned his armies against neighbours on every side. Balhae and the Khitan contested the Liaodong region. The kingdom remained intact, but internal divisions left it poorly prepared for the full-scale war that came less than a decade later.",
  "Balhae|925": "Late in the year Abaoji invaded with a large army; early in 926 he captured the western fortress of Buyeo and advanced on the capital. Balhae\u0027s defences collapsed within weeks, showing how far the kingdom\u0027s military strength had declined during the long peace of the ninth century.",
  "Balhae|926": "The last king, Dae Inseon, surrendered at Sanggyeong, and Abaoji converted the kingdom into the client state of Dongdan under his son Yelü Bei. Balhae ceased to exist as an independent state, although revolts and restoration movements continued for decades and many nobles fled to Goryeo.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|840": "The fall of the Uyghur Khaganate to the Kyrgyz left a vacuum in the eastern steppe, and Karluk-led tribes in the Chu and Talas valleys probably formed the core of the new khanate around this time. Its rulers held Balasagun and Kashgar, but little is securely known of them, and their power was regional.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|893": "The Samanid amir Ismail ibn Ahmad captured Talas, turning its church into a mosque and pushing back the Turkic frontier. The defeat showed that the early khanate, still pagan and loosely organised, was no match for the Muslim states of Transoxiana, though it kept its core in Semirechye and Kashgar.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|934": "Satuq Bughra Khan, according to tradition, converted to Islam around this time with the help of Muslim merchants and scholars, and seized power in Kashgar from his uncle. His conversion gave the dynasty new legitimacy and ties with the Muslim world, although his realm was still a regional power.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|960": "Muslim chroniclers report that some 200,000 tents of Turks converted to Islam in 960, an event usually linked to the Kara-Khanids under Satuq\u0027s son Musa. Islam became the religion of the dynasty and its tribes, binding them together and preparing them for expansion against Khotan and the Samanids.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|992": "Hasan Bughra Khan exploited the decline of the Samanid Empire and briefly occupied Bukhara, the Samanid capital, before falling ill and dying on his way home. The campaign revealed how weak the Samanids had become and made the Kara-Khanids the leading power on Transoxiana\u0027s north-eastern frontier.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|999": "Nasr ibn Ali entered Bukhara and captured the last Samanid amir, Abd al-Malik II, bringing Transoxiana under Turkic rule. The Kara-Khanids now held the rich cities of Bukhara and Samarqand as well as the steppe, and agreed with Mahmud of Ghazni to make the Amu Darya their frontier.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1006": "The khanate completed the conquest of the Buddhist kingdom of Khotan, probably around this year, and invaded Ghaznavid Khurasan while Mahmud was campaigning in India. Kara-Khanid rule stretched from the Tarim Basin to the Amu Darya, the greatest extent the united dynasty ever achieved.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1008": "Mahmud of Ghazni defeated the armies of Nasr ibn Ali and Yusuf Qadir Khan near Balkh, ending Kara-Khanid ambitions south of the Amu Darya. The khanate kept its territories, but the defeat fixed its southern frontier and turned its princes\u0027 energies towards rivalry with one another.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1020": "Ali Tegin seized Bukhara, beginning a period of open conflict between branches of the dynasty. Rival princes allied with outsiders, including the Seljuk Turkmen, and the authority of the senior khan weakened. The khanate remained large, but its unity was becoming increasingly nominal.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1025": "Mahmud of Ghazni crossed the Amu Darya and met Yusuf Qadir Khan, the eastern khan, near Samarqand, agreeing to act together against Ali Tegin. The meeting showed that outside powers now arbitrated Kara-Khanid quarrels, although Yusuf Qadir Khan himself still ruled a large and secure eastern realm.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1032": "Yusuf Qadir Khan died, and Ali Tegin followed in 1034. Their heirs inherited divided lands at a time when the Seljuk Turkmen were growing powerful in the region. Without a dominant senior khan, the princes of the two branches pursued separate interests, and the khanate drifted towards partition.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1040": "As the Seljuks defeated the Ghaznavids at Dandanaqan and took Khurasan, Ibrahim ibn Nasr, known as Böritegin, seized Bukhara and Samarqand. He made Transoxiana the base of a separate western realm, and the authority of the eastern khans at Balasagun no longer reached the western oases.",
  "Kara-Khanid Khanate|1042": "By about this year the khanate had divided into two independent states, the Western Kara-Khanid Khanate at Samarqand and the Eastern Kara-Khanid Khanate at Balasagun and Kashgar. The united khanate ceased to exist, although both successor states survived, later as vassals of the Seljuks and the Qara Khitai.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|879": "This conventional start date precedes any reliable record of the Shansabani family. Ghor was a remote, fragmented mountain region, only partly Muslim, divided among local chiefs in fortified villages. Its rulers, if they already formed a single line, held no power beyond their own valleys, which is why the curve begins near zero.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1011": "Mahmud of Ghazni invaded Ghor and captured its chief, Muhammad ibn Suri, the first Shansabani known from near-contemporary sources. Ghor became a Ghaznavid tributary and was opened to stronger Islamic influence. Its chiefs remained minor vassals, valued mainly as a source of soldiers, slaves and iron weapons.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1146": "Izz al-Din Husayn, who had ruled Ghor as a vassal of the Seljuk sultan Sanjar and the Ghaznavids, died, leaving seven sons. Their ambitions soon clashed with Ghazni: the poisoning of Qutb al-Din Muhammad by Bahram Shah began a blood feud that turned the Ghurids from vassals into rivals of their overlords.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1150": "Ala al-Din Husayn defeated Bahram Shah and sacked Ghazni, burning it for seven days to avenge his brothers. The deed made the Ghurids a feared regional power and earned him the name World Burner, but his realm was still limited to Ghor and its borderlands, and he could not hold Ghazni.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1152": "Ala al-Din Husayn challenged the Seljuk sultan Sanjar, was defeated at Nab near Herat and taken prisoner. Released as a vassal, he returned to Ghor, while Bahram Shah recovered Ghazni. The Ghurids remained a mountain kingdom whose first bid for wider power had been checked.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1163": "Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad came to the throne after the death in battle of his cousin Sayf al-Din Muhammad, and shared power with his brother Mu\u0027izz al-Din. The collapse of Seljuk authority in the east after Sanjar\u0027s death in 1157 opened Khurasan and Ghazni to the brothers\u0027 ambitions.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1173": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din took Ghazni from the Ghuzz and made it the base for campaigns into India, while Ghiyath al-Din expanded towards Herat, which fell around 1175. The brothers\u0027 partnership was turning a mountain principality into an expanding empire with two capitals and two fronts.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1178": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din, having taken Multan and Uch, attempted to invade Gujarat but was defeated by the Chaulukyas at Kasahrada near Mount Abu. The setback turned his attention north to the Punjab, still held by the last Ghaznavids, while in the west the Ghurids consolidated their hold on Herat.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1186": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din took Lahore and captured Khusrau Malik, ending the Ghaznavid dynasty. The fall of Lahore gave the Ghurids a wealthy Indian province and a forward base for the Ganges plain, and made them the leading power between Khurasan and the Indus.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1191": "At the First Battle of Tarain a Rajput coalition led by Prithviraj Chauhan defeated Mu\u0027izz al-Din, who was wounded and carried from the field. The Ghurids still held the Punjab and their western lands, but their advance into the Ganges plain was halted while the sultan raised a larger army.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1192": "At the Second Battle of Tarain Mu\u0027izz al-Din\u0027s cavalry defeated Prithviraj Chauhan, who was captured and executed. The victory opened northern India to the Ghurids: Ajmer and Delhi soon fell, and Qutb al-Din Aybak became the sultan\u0027s commander in India, governing the newly conquered lands.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1194": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din defeated Jayachandra of the Gahadavala dynasty at the Battle of Chandawar, gaining access to Varanasi and the middle Ganges. Over the following decade his commanders raided Gujarat, Gwalior, Bihar and Bengal, and Ghurid power stretched from Herat deep into the Ganges plain.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1200": "After the death of the Khwarazmshah Tekish, the Ghurids overran much of Khurasan and briefly occupied Nishapur. In the same years Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji raided Bihar and advanced towards Bengal. The empire stood at its greatest extent and prestige, although its western gains proved short-lived.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1204": "A year after Ghiyath al-Din\u0027s death, Mu\u0027izz al-Din, now sole sultan, invaded Khwarazm but was crushed at Andkhud by the Khwarazmshah Muhammad II and the Qara Khitai. The defeat destroyed Ghurid prestige in the west and provoked revolts, including a rising of the Khokhars in the Punjab.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1206": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din was assassinated at Damyak after crushing the Khokhars, leaving no son. His slave commanders took over their provinces, with Aybak at Lahore founding what became the Delhi Sultanate. The Ghurid house kept Ghor and Firozkoh, but the Indian conquests passed out of its control.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1212": "Ghiyath al-Din Mahmud, ruler of Firozkoh, was murdered after years of conflict with rival kinsmen and with Yildiz of Ghazni. The Khwarazmshah Muhammad II was absorbing Khurasan and pressing on Ghor itself. Ghurid power was reduced to a shrinking mountain realm torn by succession disputes.",
  "Ghurid Sultanate|1215": "The Khwarazmshah Muhammad II took Ghazni and completed his conquest of the Ghurid lands, ending the dynasty\u0027s rule. Yildiz fled to India, where Iltutmish defeated him in 1216. Within a few years the Mongol invasion devastated Ghor, while the Delhi Sultanate carried on the Ghurid legacy in India.",
  "Liao dynasty|916": "Abaoji, khagan of the Khitan since about 907, proclaimed himself emperor after crushing revolts by his brothers, and named his eldest son as heir. The new state united the Khitan tribes and their Chinese captives in the Liao river basin, but it was still a steppe confederation surrounded by powerful neighbours.",
  "Liao dynasty|926": "Abaoji destroyed Balhae and set up the client kingdom of Dongdan, adding the farmlands and towns of Manchuria. He died on the way home, but his widow secured the throne for Yelü Deguang. The Khitan state now ruled a large empire stretching from Mongolia to the Sea of Japan.",
  "Liao dynasty|938": "In return for helping Shi Jingtang found the Later Jin, Yelü Deguang received the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing and Datong, formally handed over in 938. The Khitan now held the passes of the Great Wall and a large Chinese farming population, with Later Jin as a vassal.",
  "Liao dynasty|947": "Yelü Deguang overthrew the Later Jin, entered Kaifeng and adopted the dynastic name Liao, briefly claiming rule over north China. Chinese uprisings forced him to withdraw, and he died on the march north. The episode marked the height of Khitan ambition in China, though the conquest could not be held.",
  "Liao dynasty|951": "After the brief reign of Shizong, murdered in a court conspiracy, Muzong came to the throne. His long reign was marked by neglect of government and drunken violence, and the Liao lost two southern prefectures to the Later Zhou in 959, although the steppe and most of the Sixteen Prefectures remained secure.",
  "Liao dynasty|979": "The Song emperor Taizong, having conquered the Northern Han, a Liao ally, marched on Nanjing, but Liao cavalry routed him at the Gaoliang River. The victory secured the Sixteen Prefectures and restored Liao confidence, a decade after the troubled reign of Muzong had ended in his murder.",
  "Liao dynasty|986": "A three-pronged Song invasion to recover the Sixteen Prefectures was defeated with heavy Song losses. Under the regency of the Empress Dowager Chengtian, the young emperor Shengzong presided over a capable government that reformed administration, opened examinations to Chinese candidates and strengthened the army.",
  "Liao dynasty|1005": "After a deep invasion to Chanyuan near the Yellow River, the Liao and Song concluded the Treaty of Chanyuan, which brought annual payments of silver and silk and recognised the Liao emperor as an equal. The Liao stood at the height of its power, wealth and prestige in East Asia.",
  "Liao dynasty|1019": "A Liao army invading Goryeo was destroyed at the Battle of Gwiju by Gang Gam-chan, and peace followed with Goryeo acknowledging Liao overlordship. The defeat showed the limits of Liao power, but the empire remained secure, prosperous and dominant across the steppe and north China.",
  "Liao dynasty|1031": "Shengzong died after a reign of nearly fifty years, during which the Liao reached its greatest stability and cultural flowering. His successor Xingzong faced court struggles involving the empress dowager, but inherited a strong administration, a full treasury and peace with the Song.",
  "Liao dynasty|1042": "Exploiting Song difficulties in its war with the Western Xia, Xingzong pressed demands on the Song and obtained an increase in the annual payments of 100,000 taels of silver and 100,000 bolts of silk. Liao diplomatic leverage remained considerable, although a campaign against the Western Xia in 1044 failed.",
  "Liao dynasty|1055": "Daozong began a long reign marked by devotion to Buddhism and a flourishing of Liao culture, including the Yingxian pagoda of 1056. Factional struggles soon grew, however, and the costs of religious patronage mounted, so that the empire\u0027s outward stability concealed a slowly weakening court.",
  "Liao dynasty|1077": "After the forced suicide of the empress in 1075, the crown prince was murdered in a plot attributed to the minister Yelü Yixin. The purges damaged the dynasty\u0027s leadership and left the succession to a grandson, Tianzuo, while frontier peoples such as the Jurchen grew stronger.",
  "Liao dynasty|1101": "Tianzuo came to the throne and devoted himself to hunting, while heavy demands for tribute, including gyrfalcons and pearls, alienated the Jurchen of Manchuria. The Liao was still a large and wealthy empire, but its leadership was weak and its subject peoples increasingly resentful.",
  "Liao dynasty|1114": "The Jurchen chieftain Wanyan Aguda rebelled and defeated Liao forces at Ningjiang. In 1115 he proclaimed the Jin dynasty and routed Tianzuo\u0027s much larger army at Hubudagang. Liao military prestige collapsed, and Khitan, Balhae and Chinese subjects began to defect to the Jurchen.",
  "Liao dynasty|1116": "The Jin captured Dongjing at Liaoyang and with it the Liao lands of Manchuria, including the former Balhae territories. Rebellions broke out within the empire, and the Liao could no longer protect its north-eastern lands against the fast-moving Jurchen armies.",
  "Liao dynasty|1120": "The Song concluded an alliance with the Jin against the Liao, and Jurchen forces took Shangjing, the Supreme Capital in the Khitan homeland. The Liao now held only its southern and western regions, and the emperor Tianzuo was losing the confidence of his own nobles.",
  "Liao dynasty|1122": "The Jin took Zhongjing and Xijing, and Tianzuo fled into the western mountains. At Nanjing princes set up the rival Northern Liao, but the Jin captured the city at the end of the year. The Liao survived only as a fugitive court with a dwindling band of followers.",
  "Liao dynasty|1125": "Jin troops captured Tianzuo, ending the Liao dynasty. The prince Yelü Dashi had already gone west, and in Central Asia he founded the Qara Khitai, or Western Liao. The Jin inherited the Liao territories and many of its institutions, including its dual system of government.",
  "Goryeo|918": "Generals overthrew Gung Ye of Taebong and raised Wang Geon to the throne, proclaiming the kingdom of Goryeo as heir to Goguryeo. The new state held the central and western peninsula but competed with Later Baekje and a weakened Silla, and depended on alliances with regional strongmen for its survival.",
  "Goryeo|936": "Silla\u0027s last king surrendered in 935, and in 936 Taejo defeated Later Baekje at the Battle of Illicheon, reuniting the peninsula. Goryeo now ruled most of Korea and had absorbed Balhae refugees, but royal power still rested on marriage alliances with local families whose loyalty remained uncertain.",
  "Goryeo|958": "Gwangjong curbed the great families that had dominated the court after Taejo\u0027s death. His Slave Review Act of 956 reduced their manpower, and in 958 he introduced civil service examinations on the Chinese model, laying the groundwork for a bureaucratic monarchy and strengthening royal authority over the aristocracy.",
  "Goryeo|983": "Seongjong adopted the Confucian principles of government urged by Choe Seung-ro and in 983 created twelve provincial governorships, extending central control into the regions for the first time. The main structures of the Goryeo state were now in place, and the kingdom was stable enough to face the rising Khitan.",
  "Goryeo|993": "A Khitan Liao army invaded the north. The official Seo Hui negotiated its withdrawal, and Goryeo gained land east of the Yalu that became six garrison districts, at the price of accepting Liao suzerainty. The kingdom had avoided defeat, but the northern threat now dominated its policy.",
  "Goryeo|1010": "The Khitan invaded again, citing the deposition of King Mokjong. They burned Kaegyong, and King Hyeonjong fled south to Naju. The invaders withdrew, but the destruction of the capital and the flight of the court revealed how exposed the kingdom was to a determined northern power.",
  "Goryeo|1019": "Gang Gam-chan destroyed the third Khitan invasion at the Battle of Gwiju, one of the greatest victories in Korean history. Peace with Liao followed, and Goryeo began a defensive wall across its northern frontier, completed in 1044. The kingdom entered a long period of security and growth.",
  "Goryeo|1046": "Munjong came to the throne at the start of a thirty-seven-year reign often seen as the height of Goryeo. Government institutions, the stipend land system and the legal codes were refined, Buddhist and Confucian learning flourished, and private academies such as Choe Chung\u0027s prepared candidates for the examinations.",
  "Goryeo|1076": "Munjong revised the stipend land system in 1076, completing the institutional framework of the state. Official relations with Song China had resumed, foreign merchants visited Byeongnando, and the capital was rich in monasteries. With peace on the frontier and a working bureaucracy, Goryeo stood at the peak of its power.",
  "Goryeo|1107": "Yun Gwan led the Byeolmuban, a special army created after earlier defeats by the Jurchen, into the northeast and built nine fortresses. The campaign showed that Goryeo could still project force, but the forts proved costly to hold and were returned to the Jurchen in 1109.",
  "Goryeo|1126": "The Jurchen had founded the Jin dynasty and destroyed Liao, and in 1126 Goryeo accepted Jin suzerainty. In the same year the over-mighty royal in-law Yi Ja-gyeom rebelled and much of the palace was burned. Aristocratic feuding was now weakening the crown from within.",
  "Goryeo|1135": "The monk Myocheong, who urged moving the capital to Pyongyang and defying the Jin, led a rebellion there. Kim Bu-sik suppressed it in 1136 after a long siege. The victory of the conservative capital aristocracy deepened the dominance of civil officials and the resentment of the military.",
  "Goryeo|1170": "Military officers led by Jeong Jung-bu, angered by their humiliation at the hands of civil officials, massacred courtiers and deposed King Uijong. A quarter of a century of violent struggles among generals followed, with peasant and slave revolts, and kings became figureheads in a weakened state.",
  "Goryeo|1196": "Choe Chung-heon seized power and founded a military house that ruled through its own directorate and private guards for four generations. The Choe regime restored a measure of order and suppressed revolts, but royal government was hollowed out and the state served the interests of one family.",
  "Goryeo|1231": "Mongol forces invaded, citing the killing of a Mongol envoy in 1225. The Choe regime moved the court to Ganghwa Island in 1232 and resisted from behind its straits, while the mainland suffered repeated devastating campaigns. Goryeo survived as a state, but its population and economy were gravely damaged.",
  "Goryeo|1259": "After the last Choe dictator was killed in 1258, the court made peace, and the crown prince, the future Wonjong, went to submit to the Mongols. Goryeo kept its dynasty and its own institutions, a rare concession, but became a vassal, and part of the northeast had been annexed.",
  "Goryeo|1270": "The court returned to Kaegyong under Mongol protection, ending a century of military rule. The Sambyeolcho, the elite units of the old regime, rebelled and fought on from Jindo and Jeju until 1273. Goryeo now depended on Mongol support for internal order and the survival of its crown.",
  "Goryeo|1274": "The crown prince, married to a daughter of Kublai Khan, became King Chungnyeol, making Goryeo kings imperial sons-in-law. Goryeo built ships and supplied troops for the Mongol invasion of Japan that year and again in 1281, both failures. The dynasty\u0027s standing within the empire rose, but its autonomy was limited.",
  "Goryeo|1356": "King Gongmin, exploiting the decline of the Yuan dynasty, purged the powerful pro-Yuan Gi family, ended Yuan supervision of the court and recovered the northeastern Ssangseong territory by force. Goryeo regained its independence and sought reform, but great landholding families and new external threats limited what the crown could achieve.",
  "Goryeo|1361": "Red Turban rebels from China, who had first invaded in 1359, returned with a large army and took Kaegyong, forcing Gongmin to flee to Andong. The capital was recovered in 1362, but the war, together with mounting raids by Japanese pirates along the coasts, exhausted the kingdom\u0027s resources.",
  "Goryeo|1388": "King U and General Choe Yeong ordered an attack on Ming forces in Liaodong. Yi Seong-gye, who opposed the campaign, turned his army back at Wihwa Island on the Yalu and seized the capital. Choe Yeong was executed, and real power passed to Yi and his reformist allies.",
  "Goryeo|1392": "After a sweeping land reform in 1391 destroyed the economic base of the old aristocracy, Yi Seong-gye\u0027s supporters assassinated the loyalist Jeong Mong-ju. In 1392 Yi deposed King Gongyang, the last Goryeo ruler, and took the throne himself, founding the Joseon dynasty and ending Goryeo after 474 years.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|977": "The Turkic garrison of Ghazni chose Sabuktigin, a former slave soldier, as its leader. Nominally a Samanid governor, he controlled only a small mountain principality around Ghazni, but its army of Turkic slave cavalry gave him a strong base from which to expand towards Kabul and the Indus borderlands.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|994": "Sabuktigin had defeated the Hindu Shahi king Jayapala, pushing his frontier towards Peshawar, and in 994 he and Mahmud helped the Samanid emir against rebels, Mahmud receiving command of the army of Khurasan. The Ghaznavids were now the strongest power within the crumbling Samanid state.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|999": "Having overthrown his brother Ismail in 998, Mahmud took Khurasan in 999 as the Samanid state collapsed, and the Abbasid caliph al-Qadir recognised him with honorific titles. Ruling an independent realm from the Oxus to the Indus borderlands, Mahmud was ready to turn his army towards India.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1001": "Mahmud defeated the Hindu Shahi king Jayapala near Peshawar, opening the way into the Punjab. The victory began a quarter of a century of campaigns in India that brought enormous plunder to Ghazni and made the Ghaznavid army, with its slave cavalry and war elephants, the most feared in the region.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1008": "Mahmud defeated the Qarakhanid invaders of Khurasan near Balkh and the Hindu Shahi king Anandapala at Chach. With both his northern frontier and the road into India secured, he could raid the Punjab and beyond at will, and the treasure of Nagarkot followed in 1009.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1018": "Mahmud\u0027s most ambitious campaign so far reached the Ganges plain, plundering Mathura and taking Kanauj, the seat of the Gurjara-Pratihara kings. Khwarazm had been annexed the previous year. The empire now stretched from the lower Oxus to the Punjab, and Ghazni was filled with captured wealth.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1026": "After a long march across the Thar desert, Mahmud sacked the temple of Somnath on the Gujarat coast early in 1026 and carried off great treasure. The raid crowned his reputation as a holy warrior in the Muslim world, though it brought no lasting conquest beyond the Punjab.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1029": "Mahmud took Rayy and the Buyid lands of Jibal in 1029, bringing the empire to its greatest extent, from western Iran and Khwarazm to the Punjab. Ghazni was among the richest capitals of the Islamic world, though Turkmen bands were already a growing danger in Khurasan.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1030": "Mahmud died in April 1030. His son Mas\u0027ud I deposed and blinded his brother Muhammad and took the throne. The empire was still intact and wealthy, but Mas\u0027ud faced growing Seljuk Turkmen raids in Khurasan while dividing his attention between campaigns in India and purges at court.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1038": "Seljuk Turkmen under Tughril entered Nishapur, the chief city of Khurasan, whose people had been alienated by heavy Ghaznavid taxation. Mas\u0027ud\u0027s large army, slowed by baggage and elephants, struggled to bring the mobile nomads to battle, and Ghaznavid authority in the west was crumbling.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1040": "The Seljuks destroyed Mas\u0027ud\u0027s exhausted and thirsty army at the Battle of Dandanaqan near Merv. Khurasan was lost permanently, and the Seljuks went on to conquer Iran. Mas\u0027ud retreated towards India with his treasure, and the empire was reduced to its eastern half.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1041": "Mas\u0027ud was deposed and killed by his own troops early in 1041, and his blinded brother Muhammad was briefly restored. Mas\u0027ud\u0027s son Mawdud soon avenged his father and took the throne, but the dynasty had lost its western provinces and spent years fighting the Seljuks and rival claimants.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1059": "Sultan Ibrahim made peace with the Seljuks around 1059, accepting the loss of Khurasan. A long, stable reign followed, with renewed campaigns in India and a prosperous court at Ghazni. The later Ghaznavid state, smaller but rich, ruled eastern Afghanistan and the Punjab.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1099": "Ibrahim died after a reign of some forty years, and his son Mas\u0027ud III succeeded him. The state remained stable and prosperous, and Mas\u0027ud III\u0027s minaret at Ghazni still partly stands. But the dynasty depended on Seljuk goodwill, and a succession struggle after his death in 1115 invited intervention.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1117": "The Seljuk sultan Sanjar invaded, took Ghazni and installed Bahram Shah as his vassal and tributary. Bahram Shah reigned for some forty years, and his court remained a centre of Persian letters, but the Ghaznavid state no longer acted as an independent power.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1150": "Avenging two brothers put to death by Bahram Shah, Ala al-Din Husayn of Ghur sacked Ghazni in 1150 or 1151 and burned it, earning the name \u0027world burner\u0027. Bahram Shah later returned, but the dynasty\u0027s heartland was ruined and the Ghurids were now the rising power.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1160": "Khusrau Malik succeeded his father Khusrau Shah, who had withdrawn to Lahore; Ghazni itself fell to Oghuz Turkmen. The Ghaznavids now ruled only the Punjab from Lahore, while the Ghurid brothers Ghiyath al-Din and Mu\u0027izz al-Din built a rival empire in Afghanistan.",
  "Ghaznavid Empire|1186": "Mu\u0027izz al-Din Muhammad of Ghor, ruling at Ghazni since 1173, besieged Lahore and forced Khusrau Malik to submit. The last Ghaznavid sultan was imprisoned and later put to death, and the Punjab became the Ghurid base for the conquest of northern India that led to the Delhi Sultanate.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1115": "Wanyan Aguda proclaimed the Great Jin after defeating Liao forces at Ningjiang and Chuhedian. The new state, ruling the Jurchen tribes of northern Manchuria from Huining, was small, but its army of armoured mounted archers was already beating the much larger Liao empire in open battle.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1120": "Jin concluded the Alliance Conducted at Sea with the Song against Liao and took the Liao Supreme Capital. Having captured the Eastern Capital at Liaoyang in 1116, the Jurchen now controlled most of Manchuria and were breaking up the Khitan empire piece by piece.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1125": "Jin forces captured the Liao emperor Tianzuo, extinguishing the Liao state, and Western Xia had accepted Jin overlordship. Quarrels with the Song over the former Liao lands led Taizong to invade the Song at the end of the year. Jin was now the strongest military power in East Asia.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1127": "In the Jingkang Incident, Jin armies took Kaifeng and carried off the emperors Huizong and Qinzong with much of the court. The Northern Song collapsed, and Jin was master of most of northern China, though it had not yet secured the vast lands its armies had overrun.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1130": "After a campaign south of the Yangtze in 1129–1130 failed to destroy the new Southern Song court, Jin set up the puppet state of Qi under Liu Yu to govern the lands south of the Yellow River, while consolidating its control over the north.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1141": "The Treaty of Shaoxing fixed the border along the Huai River; the Song accepted vassal status and paid an annual tribute of silver and silk. Jin rule over northern China was now recognised, and the court of Xizong turned to building Chinese-style institutions of government.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1153": "Prince Hailing, who had murdered Xizong, moved the capital from Huining to Zhongdu, the site of Beijing, and centralised government on Chinese lines. The empire was strong and prosperous, but his ruthless purges of the Wanyan clan and his ambition to conquer the Song created dangerous tensions.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1161": "Hailing invaded the Southern Song, but his fleet was beaten at the Battle of Caishi on the Yangtze and he was murdered by his own officers. Meanwhile Shizong had been proclaimed emperor at Liaoyang, and a great Khitan rebellion added to the crisis.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1165": "Shizong made peace with the Song on terms more favourable to them. His long reign brought stability, tax reform and prosperity, and he sought to preserve Jurchen language and customs while governing through Confucian institutions. Chinese historians later remembered his era as the golden age of the Jin.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1189": "At Shizong\u0027s death and the accession of his grandson Zhangzong, the Jin empire stood at its peak in population, wealth and cultural achievement. Confucian learning, literature and the arts flourished at court, and work began on the Lugou Bridge outside Zhongdu.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1194": "The Yellow River broke its banks and shifted its course, causing massive flooding and long-term damage to agriculture in northern China. Rising military spending against the Mongol and Tatar tribes of the steppe also strained the treasury, though the empire\u0027s institutions remained strong.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1208": "Jin armies had defeated a Song offensive launched by the chief minister Han Tuozhou in 1206, and the peace of 1208 brought increased tribute and Han\u0027s head. But the war, inflation of the paper currency and Zhangzong\u0027s death left the dynasty weaker than its victory suggested.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1211": "Genghis Khan invaded and destroyed a large Jin army at the Battle of Yehuling. Mongol forces ravaged the north, exposing the weakness of Jin frontier defences and the poor leadership of Weishao Wang, who was murdered in a coup in 1213.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1215": "Xuanzong had bought a truce and moved the court to Kaifeng in 1214. The Mongols took Zhongdu in 1215, and Jin lost Manchuria and much of the north to the Mongols, Khitan rebels and local warlords. The empire was reduced to the Yellow River plain.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1224": "Aizong came to the throne and made peace with the Song and Western Xia, ending costly wars on those fronts. During a pause in Mongol attacks around Genghis Khan\u0027s death in 1227 the Jin held their ground and even won some battles, but their resources were steadily shrinking.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1232": "The Mongols under Tolui destroyed the last major Jin field army at the Battle of Sanfengshan and besieged Kaifeng, where the defenders used gunpowder bombs. Aizong fled the city, which surrendered in 1233. The dynasty had lost almost all capacity to resist.",
  "Jin dynasty (1115-1234)|1234": "Mongol and Southern Song armies stormed Caizhou in February 1234. Emperor Aizong abdicated and took his own life, and his successor Wanyan Chenglin was killed the same day, ending the Jin dynasty after 119 years and leaving northern China to the Mongols.",
  "Golden Horde|1242": "Batu withdrew from Central Europe to the lower Volga after the great western campaign, ruling the Kipchak steppe, Volga Bulgaria and the conquered Rus\u0027 principalities. The khanate was still part of the united Mongol Empire, and its control over the Rus\u0027 princes was only beginning to be organised.",
  "Golden Horde|1257": "Berke, Batu\u0027s brother and the first Muslim khan, came to power. Censuses of the Rus\u0027 lands from the mid-1250s, extended to Novgorod in 1259, organised regular tribute and recruitment. With Sarai built and the princes obedient, the khanate had a firm grip on its territories.",
  "Golden Horde|1262": "War broke out with the Ilkhanate of Persia over Azerbaijan and the Caucasus, and Berke allied with the Mamluk sultan Baybars against their common enemy. The Mongol Empire was splitting into rival khanates, and the Jochid ulus was acting as an independent great power.",
  "Golden Horde|1266": "Mengu-Timur became khan and struck coins in his own name, marking effective independence from the Great Khan. Around this time the Genoese obtained a trading base at Caffa in the Crimea, opening the Black Sea trade that would enrich the khanate for a century.",
  "Golden Horde|1280": "Power was increasingly shared between the khans and the general Nogai, who dominated the western steppe and the lower Danube. Raids on Hungary and Poland in the 1280s showed continuing military strength, but the rivalry between Nogai and the khans threatened to split the Horde.",
  "Golden Horde|1300": "Khan Tokhta defeated and killed Nogai around 1299–1300, restoring the unity of the khanate. Order returned to the steppe, trade revived and the Rus\u0027 principalities remained firmly tributary. The foundations were laid for the khanate\u0027s greatest era under Tokhta\u0027s successor.",
  "Golden Horde|1313": "Uzbek Khan came to the throne and during his reign made Islam the religion of the state. He kept the Rus\u0027 princes under tight control, playing Moscow against Tver, and presided over a period of prosperity in which Sarai and the trading cities of the Volga and Crimea flourished.",
  "Golden Horde|1340": "Near the end of Uzbek Khan\u0027s reign the Golden Horde was at the height of its power and wealth. Ibn Battuta had admired its capital, caravans linked Sarai to Central Asia, Italian ships traded at Caffa and Tana, and the princes of Moscow collected tribute for the khan.",
  "Golden Horde|1346": "Janibeg was besieging Caffa when plague broke out in his army, and the Black Death spread through the khanate\u0027s cities. The epidemic reduced the population and trade, but the khanate remained powerful; Janibeg briefly took Tabriz in 1357, shortly before he was murdered.",
  "Golden Horde|1359": "With the murder of Berdibeg in 1359 the line of Batu died out and the Great Troubles began. More than twenty khans followed in two decades, while emirs such as Mamai ruled through puppets. Lithuania defeated Horde forces at Blue Waters around 1362, and Moscow grew bolder.",
  "Golden Horde|1380": "Grand Prince Dmitry of Moscow defeated Mamai at the Battle of Kulikovo, the first major Russian victory over the Horde. Mamai was soon overthrown by Tokhtamysh, a Jochid prince from the east backed by Timur, who had begun to reunite the khanate.",
  "Golden Horde|1382": "Tokhtamysh sacked Moscow and forced its princes back into obedience. Having reunited the eastern and western parts of the Jochid lands, he restored much of the khanate\u0027s strength, but his ambition soon led him into conflict with his former patron, Timur.",
  "Golden Horde|1395": "Having already beaten him at the Kondurcha River in 1391, Timur defeated Tokhtamysh on the Terek River and went on to sack Sarai, Astrakhan, Tana and other cities. The destruction of these trading centres crippled the economy, and the khanate never recovered its former prosperity or its unity.",
  "Golden Horde|1399": "The emir Edigu, ruling through puppet khans, crushed Vytautas of Lithuania and the exiled Tokhtamysh at the Battle of the Vorskla River. Edigu restored some authority over the steppe and besieged Moscow in 1408, but real power lay with him rather than with the khans.",
  "Golden Horde|1419": "Edigu\u0027s death in 1419 removed the last strong figure of the united Horde. Rival Jochid princes fought for the throne, and regional groups in the Crimea, on the middle Volga and in the eastern steppe began to go their own way. Tribute from Moscow became irregular.",
  "Golden Horde|1441": "The Crimean Khanate under Hacı Giray broke away around 1441, following the emergence of the Khanate of Kazan around 1438. The rump of the old state, increasingly called the Great Horde, held the lower Volga steppe but faced hostile Jochid neighbours on several sides.",
  "Golden Horde|1480": "Ivan III of Moscow had stopped paying tribute, and Khan Ahmed\u0027s attempt to restore it ended in the Great Stand on the Ugra River, where the two armies faced each other until Ahmed withdrew without battle. Moscow\u0027s subordination to the Horde was over.",
  "Golden Horde|1502": "The Crimean khan Meñli I Giray, allied with Moscow, defeated Sheikh Ahmed, the last khan of the Great Horde, and sacked Sarai. The Horde\u0027s remaining people were absorbed by the Crimean Khanate and its neighbours, ending the line of khans that went back to Batu.",
  "Joseon|1392": "Yi Seong-gye deposed the last Goryeo king and took the throne, backed by Neo-Confucian reformers and the land reform of 1391. The new dynasty inherited a unified kingdom and a working bureaucracy, but it still needed Ming recognition and faced rivalries within the ruling house.",
  "Joseon|1398": "Yi Bang-won killed the chief minister Jeong Do-jeon and two half-brothers in the first Strife of Princes, and Taejo abdicated. The violence threatened the young dynasty, but it cleared the way for Yi Bang-won, who took the throne as Taejong in 1400 and strengthened the crown.",
  "Joseon|1418": "Taejong had abolished private armies, created eight provinces and registered the population, and in 1418 he handed the throne to his son Sejong. Joseon now had a strong monarchy, secure relations with the Ming, and a treasury and army able to support ambitious policies.",
  "Joseon|1446": "Sejong promulgated the new Korean alphabet, created in 1443. His reign also brought scientific instruments, agricultural manuals, the expedition to Tsushima and the fixing of the northern frontier on the Yalu and Tumen. Joseon was at the peak of its strength, prosperity and cultural creativity.",
  "Joseon|1455": "Sejo seized the throne from his young nephew Danjong, and officials who plotted Danjong\u0027s restoration were executed. Sejo\u0027s forceful rule centralised power and began the National Code, but the usurpation divided the elite and left a lasting legacy of distrust between kings and scholars.",
  "Joseon|1485": "The Gyeongguk daejeon was promulgated under Seongjong, giving the state a complete legal code. Seongjong promoted Neo-Confucian scholars and the censorate, and the bureaucratic state reached its classic form, supported by a stable population and expanding agriculture. Joseon was secure on its frontiers and at peace with its neighbours.",
  "Joseon|1498": "The first of four literati purges, under the tyrannical Yeonsangun, began half a century of violent conflict between kings, entrenched officials and Neo-Confucian scholars. Yeonsangun was deposed in 1506, but repeated purges weakened government, and the neglected army began to decay.",
  "Joseon|1545": "The last great literati purge accompanied a struggle between royal in-law families. Private academies spread and Neo-Confucian learning flourished under Yi Hwang and Yi I, but the military system decayed, and from the 1570s the bureaucracy split into rival factions.",
  "Joseon|1592": "Toyotomi Hideyoshi\u0027s armies invaded and took Seoul within three weeks, and King Seonjo fled to the Yalu. Admiral Yi Sun-sin\u0027s naval victories, volunteer righteous armies and Ming intervention saved the kingdom, but the country was devastated and the state brought close to collapse.",
  "Joseon|1598": "The death of Hideyoshi ended the war, and Japanese forces withdrew after the Battle of Noryang, where Yi Sun-sin was killed. Joseon survived, but much farmland lay waste, population and tax registers were lost, and the palaces of Seoul had been burned.",
  "Joseon|1608": "Gwanghaegun introduced the Uniform Land Tax Law in Gyeonggi province, rebuilt the state and steered a cautious course between the declining Ming and the rising Manchus. Recovery was under way, though Gwanghaegun was overthrown in 1623 by a faction committed to the Ming.",
  "Joseon|1637": "After the Manchu invasions of 1627 and 1636, King Injo surrendered to the Qing emperor Hong Taiji following a siege at Namhansanseong. Joseon became a Qing tributary, sent princes as hostages and paid heavy tribute, a deep humiliation for a court loyal to the Ming.",
  "Joseon|1724": "Yeongjo came to the throne after decades of bitter factional struggle and began his policy of impartiality to balance the factions. Agriculture, commerce and population had recovered, and the Uniform Land Tax now covered the whole country, giving the state a firmer fiscal base.",
  "Joseon|1776": "Jeongjo, grandson of Yeongjo, established the Gyujanggak royal library, promoted scholars from all factions and men of illegitimate birth, and built Hwaseong Fortress at Suwon. His reign marked an eighteenth-century revival of royal authority, scholarship and culture, though the in-law families and factions remained powerful beneath the surface.",
  "Joseon|1800": "Jeongjo\u0027s sudden death brought a child king and the rule of royal in-law clans, above all the Andong Kim. The persecution of Catholics began in 1801, offices were sold, tax collection grew corrupt, and the state\u0027s capacity to respond to famine and unrest declined.",
  "Joseon|1811": "The Hong Gyeong-nae rebellion in the northwest, fed by regional discrimination and famine, held out until 1812. It was the first of many nineteenth-century risings that revealed the decay of local government and the growing gulf between the state and the peasantry.",
  "Joseon|1866": "The regent Heungseon Daewongun had restored royal authority and was rebuilding Gyeongbokgung. He executed French missionaries and thousands of Korean Catholics and repelled a French punitive expedition, as he would an American one in 1871, keeping Joseon closed at great cost to the treasury.",
  "Joseon|1876": "Japan forced Joseon to sign the Treaty of Ganghwa, opening ports and granting extraterritorial rights. Treaties with the United States and European powers followed in the 1880s, and the kingdom became an arena of rivalry between China, Japan and Russia.",
  "Joseon|1894": "The Donghak Peasant Revolution led both China and Japan to send troops, and the First Sino-Japanese War followed. Japanese forces occupied the royal palace and backed the Gabo Reforms, which abolished the examinations and legal slavery but left Joseon under foreign control.",
  "Joseon|1897": "After Japan\u0027s victory ended Chinese suzerainty, Queen Min was murdered by Japanese agents in 1895, and Gojong sheltered in the Russian legation. Returning to his palace, he proclaimed the Korean Empire in October 1897, ending the kingdom of Joseon after 505 years.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1429": "Sho Hashi of Chuzan defeated Nanzan and completed the unification of Okinawa, having already taken Hokuzan\u0027s castle at Nakijin. Recognised by the Ming as king of Chuzan, he ruled from Shuri over a single island state whose tribute missions gave it privileged access to Chinese trade, a solid but still fragile foundation.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1439": "Sho Hashi\u0027s death opened a period of short reigns in the First Sho dynasty. The kingdom\u0027s tribute trade with China and its voyages to Southeast Asia continued, but royal authority over the powerful aji lords in their castles remained weak, and the throne passed rapidly between members of the royal family.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1458": "King Sho Taikyu had the great bell cast for Shuri Castle that called Ryukyu a \u0027bridge of all nations\u0027, reflecting booming trade. In the same year the Gosamaru–Amawari conflict pitted two great lords against each other and the crown, showing that the court\u0027s control of the aji was still incomplete.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1470": "After the death of Sho Toku, the royal treasurer Kanemaru took power and became king as Sho En, founding the Second Sho dynasty. The change of dynasty was accepted by the Ming, the trade networks remained intact, and the new house laid the base for a far stronger monarchy.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1500": "Sho Shin\u0027s forces defeated Oyake Akahachi in the Yaeyama Islands and extended Shuri\u0027s rule to the southern islands. At home he was concentrating the aji at Shuri and building a ranked gentry and royal priesthood, while Ryukyuan ships traded from Fujian and Japan to Siam, Malacca and Java.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1520": "Late in Sho Shin\u0027s long reign the kingdom stood at its height: centralised under the king at Shuri, ruling from the Amami fringes to Yaeyama, and still a major carrier between China, Japan and Southeast Asia. Monuments such as Tamaudun and the Sonohyan-utaki stone gate date from these decades.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1537": "Sho Sei campaigned in the Amami Islands to enforce royal authority in the north. The state remained strong and the Omoro Soshi song collection was being compiled, but Portuguese Malacca and Chinese and Japanese private traders were beginning to take over the sea routes on which Ryukyu\u0027s prosperity depended.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1570": "The last royal voyage to Siam, usually dated to this year, marked the end of Ryukyu\u0027s Southeast Asian trade after the Ming relaxed its maritime ban in 1567. The kingdom still traded with China and Japan and kept its independence, but its role as a regional entrepot was fading.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1609": "Satsuma\u0027s invasion, launched with Tokugawa Ieyasu\u0027s consent, overwhelmed the kingdom within weeks; King Sho Nei surrendered Shuri and was taken to Japan. Ryukyu lost the Amami Islands, accepted Satsuma\u0027s oversight and tribute demands, and kept only the outward forms of an independent kingdom subordinate to China.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1634": "The first of the Ryukyuan embassies to the shoguns at Edo set out, confirming the kingdom\u0027s place within the Tokugawa order. Satsuma now controlled appointments and finances behind the scenes, while tribute missions to China continued, so the kingdom settled into a stable but closely supervised double subordination.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1666": "Sho Shoken (Haneji Choshu) became regent and began reforms that cut court spending and reorganised government. His official history Chuzan Seikan of 1650 had already given the dynasty a new narrative. The kingdom adapted to its new position, and Qing investiture of its kings had resumed after the Ming fall.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1728": "Sai On joined the council of three and directed a programme of forestry, river control and agricultural improvement that stabilised the kingdom\u0027s finances. Court arts such as kumi odori flourished, and Ryukyu reached its strongest condition under Satsuma rule, though still far below its independent commercial peak.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1800": "The kingdom remained stable as a dual tributary of the Qing and Satsuma, but debts to Satsuma, poor harvests and the heavy cost of Chinese investiture missions weighed on its finances. Its institutions functioned, yet it had little capacity to act beyond what its two overlords allowed.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1853": "Commodore Matthew Perry used Naha as a base on his way to Japan and in 1854 signed a compact with the kingdom. Western warships exposed how little power Ryukyu had to defend itself, and its ambiguous position between China and Japan became a problem for the emerging modern state system.",
  "Ryukyu Kingdom|1872": "Meiji Japan declared the kingdom the Ryukyu Domain and made Sho Tai a domain king, ending its standing as a separate kingdom. The tribute to China was banned in 1875, and in 1879 the domain was abolished and replaced by Okinawa Prefecture, with Sho Tai removed to Tokyo.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1465": "Kerei and Janibek, sultans of the line of Urus Khan, had broken with the Uzbek ruler Abu\u0027l-Khayr and settled their followers in the western Chu and Talas valleys under the protection of the Moghul khan Esen Buqa. Their people, the Kazakhs, were still a small breakaway group on the edge of the steppe.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1469": "The death of Abu\u0027l-Khayr Khan around 1468–1469 broke up the Uzbek confederation. Many nomad clans joined Kerei and Janibek, who returned to the Dasht-i Qipchaq and began to take over the steppe north of the Syr Darya, turning the breakaway band into a rising power.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1480": "Burunduk Khan, Kerei\u0027s son, led the Kazakhs in long wars against Muhammad Shaybani for the towns of the middle Syr Darya, such as Sauran, Sygnak and Turkistan. The khanate controlled much of the steppe, but the oasis towns changed hands repeatedly and Shaybani remained a dangerous rival.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1511": "Qasim Khan, son of Janibek, became the leading khan as Muhammad Shaybani\u0027s death in 1510 removed the Uzbek threat to the steppe. He brought together most of the Kazakh clans, extended his reach west towards the Nogais and turned the khanate into a feared power in Central Asia.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1520": "Late in Qasim Khan\u0027s reign the khanate reached its peak: Muhammad Haidar Dughlat credits him with a following of about a million people, and his authority stretched over most of the steppe from the Syr Darya to the Ural region. His customary rules were later remembered as \u0027Qasim Khan\u0027s bright road\u0027.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1530": "After Qasim\u0027s death the khanate fell into crisis. His successors quarrelled, the Nogais and Moghuls inflicted defeats, and khans such as Tahir lost the support of many clans. Haidar Dughlat describes a sharp fall in Kazakh numbers, and much of the steppe slipped out of the khans\u0027 control.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1538": "Haqnazar Khan, a son of Qasim, began a long reign that restored Kazakh power. He reunited the clans, fought the Nogais and the Moghuls, and pressed on the Shaybanid towns of the Syr Darya, laying the base for renewed expansion later in the sixteenth century.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1570": "Haqnazar\u0027s khanate again dominated the steppe and pushed west into former Nogai pastures. Contacts with Muscovy began as Russia expanded to the Volga after taking Kazan and Astrakhan, and the khans became important players in the politics of Bukhara and the Syr Darya towns.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1598": "Tauekel Khan took Tashkent, Turkistan and other towns from the Shaybanids of Bukhara, and his successor Esim Khan kept most of them. Control of the Syr Darya oases gave the khans markets, revenues and a political centre at Turkistan, near the peak of their territorial reach.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1628": "Esim Khan\u0027s death closed a strong reign remembered for his body of customary rules. Disputes with Tashkent\u0027s rulers and the rise of the Oirats to the east began to strain the khanate, while the three juzes were emerging as separate political groupings within it.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1643": "Jahangir Khan, with help from Samarkand, is said to have checked the Dzungar ruler Erdeni Batur at the Battle of Orbulaq. The victory saved Zhetysu for a time, but the Dzungars were now a permanent and growing threat on the khanate\u0027s eastern frontier.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1680": "Tauke Khan came to power and, with the leading biys of the three juzes, is credited with the Zhety Zhargy law code. His reign brought internal order, but Galdan\u0027s Dzungar raids on Sayram and the Syr Darya towns in the 1680s showed the khanate\u0027s military weakness.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1715": "Tauke Khan died around this time, and no successor held authority over all three juzes. Each juz followed its own khans, rivalries among Genghisid sultans intensified, and the Dzungars under Tsewang Rabtan prepared for a major offensive against the divided Kazakhs.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1723": "The Dzungar invasion began the \u0027Years of the Great Disaster\u0027. Kazakh clans fled west and south, losing herds and people, and the Dzungars took Turkistan, Sayram and Tashkent. It was the lowest point of the khanate before its final dissolution, and remains a central episode in Kazakh national memory.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1730": "Uniting under Abulkhair Khan and batyrs such as Kabanbai and Bogenbai, the Kazakhs won victories at Bulanty and Anyrakai and recovered part of their pastures. The revival was short-lived, as rivalry over the supreme khanship broke up the alliance soon after the fighting.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1731": "Abulkhair Khan of the Junior Juz swore allegiance to Empress Anna of Russia, seeking protection and support for his own position. Parts of the Middle Juz followed, and the khanate\u0027s separate juzes began to slide into Russian dependency, though the khans long kept practical autonomy on the steppe.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1771": "After the Qing destroyed the Dzungars, Abylai Khan of the Middle Juz, who had balanced skilfully between Russia and the Qing, was proclaimed khan of all three juzes at Turkistan. He restored some Kazakh prestige and recovered pastures, but his authority rested on his own person.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1781": "Abylai Khan\u0027s death ended the last attempt to reunite the Kazakhs under a single strong khan. His successors in the Middle Juz and the khans of the Junior Juz depended increasingly on Russian recognition, subsidies and frontier officials based in Orenburg and Omsk.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1822": "The Regulations on the Siberian Kirgiz, drafted by Mikhail Speransky, abolished the khanship in the Middle Juz and placed the steppe under Russian districts; the Junior Juz khan was removed in 1824. The khanate survived only as a memory and in the authority of individual sultans.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1841": "Kenesary Kasymov, a grandson of Abylai, who had rebelled against Russian rule in 1837, was proclaimed khan by his followers. His movement was the last attempt to restore an independent Kazakh khanate, but it controlled only shifting groups of nomads under constant Russian pressure.",
  "Kazakh Khanate|1847": "Kenesary Kasymov was killed while fighting the Kyrgyz in the Tian Shan, ending the last Kazakh khanate. Russia completed its control over the Middle and Junior Juz, and the Senior Juz passed under Russian rule as Russian forts advanced into Zhetysu in the following years.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1634": "On the death of Khara Khula, chief of the Choros, his son Erdeni Batur took the title khong taiji and began uniting the Oirat tribes that had not migrated to the Volga or Kokonor. The new khanate controlled the pastures between the Altai and the Ili but was still one steppe power among several.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1640": "Erdeni Batur joined Oirat and Khalkha princes at the assembly that drew up the Mongol-Oirat Code, regulating relations among princes and the status of the Buddhist church. The code strengthened the khanate\u0027s institutions and its standing in the Mongol world, while Erdeni Batur encouraged farming and building.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1653": "Erdeni Batur died and was succeeded by his son Sengge, who maintained Dzungar power but faced hostility from his half-brothers. The khanate was consolidated around its Ili and Dzungarian pastures, traded with Russian Siberia and kept close ties to Tibet, but had not yet begun major conquests.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1671": "After Sengge\u0027s murder in 1670, his brother Galdan returned from his religious studies in Tibet, avenged him and took control of the khanate. With the support of the Fifth Dalai Lama, who soon granted him the title Boshugtu Khan, he set about making the Dzungars the dominant power of the western steppe.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1680": "Galdan conquered the Yarkent Khanate, intervening on behalf of the Afaqi khojas, and made the oasis cities of the Tarim Basin pay tribute. Control of Kashgar, Yarkand and the other oases gave the nomad state grain, revenue and craftsmen, sharply increasing its strength.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1688": "Galdan invaded the lands of the Khalkha Mongols, scattering their princes, who fled to the Qing. Dzungar power now reached from the Kazakh steppe and the Tarim Basin to eastern Mongolia, the khanate\u0027s greatest extent under Galdan, but the invasion brought it into direct conflict with the Kangxi Emperor.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1690": "At Ulan Butung, a few hundred kilometres north of Beijing, Galdan fought a Qing army behind a wall of camels and withdrew. Meanwhile his nephew Tsewang Rabtan had taken control of the Ili homeland, so Galdan was cut off from his base and his power was beginning to erode.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1696": "The Kangxi Emperor led a massive expedition into Mongolia, and a Qing army under Fiyanggu destroyed Galdan\u0027s forces at Jao Modo. Galdan was left a fugitive with a few followers, and the Khalkha lands passed firmly under Qing control, a severe setback for Dzungar power.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1697": "Galdan died, and Tsewang Rabtan became undisputed ruler of the Dzungars. Holding the Ili valley and still receiving tribute from the Tarim Basin, he kept peace with the Qing for a time and set about rebuilding the khanate\u0027s strength, its herds and its trade with Russia and Central Asia.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1717": "Tsewang Rabtan\u0027s commander Tsering Dondub crossed the Tibetan plateau, killed the Khoshut ruler Lha-bzang Khan and sacked Lhasa. The Dzungars had also repelled a Russian expedition up the Irtysh in 1716, and now dominated the region from the Kazakh steppe to Tibet.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1720": "A Qing expedition drove the Dzungars out of Tibet and installed a new Dalai Lama under Qing protection, ending Dzungar influence in Lhasa. The defeat was a setback, but the khanate\u0027s heartland, its army and its hold over the Tarim Basin were untouched.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1723": "Dzungar armies invaded the Kazakh steppe, taking Turkistan, Sayram and Tashkent and driving the Kazakh clans west in the \u0027Years of the Great Disaster\u0027. With the Kazakhs crushed, the Tarim oases paying tribute and Swedish-made cannon at its disposal, the khanate stood at its height.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1727": "Tsewang Rabtan died, and his son Galdan Tseren succeeded peacefully. The khanate remained the strongest power of Central Asia, but the Yongzheng Emperor was preparing renewed war, and the Kazakhs were regrouping in the west, so Dzungar power faced pressure on two fronts.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1731": "Galdan Tseren\u0027s army destroyed a Qing force at Hoton Nor in western Mongolia, one of the heaviest defeats the Qing suffered in the steppe. The victory showed that the khanate could still defeat the largest empire in Asia, although its raid into Khalkha was repulsed at Erdene Zuu in 1732.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1739": "Exhausted by war, the Qing and the Dzungars agreed a peace fixing the boundary along the Altai. Galdan Tseren gained access to Qing frontier markets and tribute trade and turned to rebuilding the economy, a stable and prosperous period with fewer military ambitions than before.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1745": "Galdan Tseren died, leaving a prosperous khanate but no secure rule of succession. His young son Tsewang Dorji Namjal came to the throne amid rivalries among his relatives and the great nobles, the start of a decade of instability that would open the khanate to Qing intervention.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1750": "Tsewang Dorji Namjal was overthrown and blinded by his half-brother Lama Dorji, whose legitimacy was contested. Nobles and princes formed rival factions, and the civil war that followed drained the khanate\u0027s strength, scattered its herds and gave its Qing and Kazakh neighbours openings to intervene.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1755": "After Dawachi seized power with Amursana\u0027s help and the two fell out, Amursana went over to the Qing. Qing armies reached the Ili valley almost unopposed and Dawachi was captured. Amursana\u0027s revolt later that year provoked the Qianlong Emperor to order the Dzungars\u0027 destruction.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1757": "Qing armies and their allies carried out mass killings across Dzungaria, while smallpox spread among the Oirats. Amursana fled to Russia and died of smallpox at Tobolsk. Organised Dzungar resistance had collapsed and most of the population had been killed, had died of disease or had fled.",
  "Dzungar Khanate|1758": "Qing campaigns ended the last resistance, and the Dzungars as a political people had been destroyed. Dzungaria was resettled with bannermen, Han and Hui colonists and Turkic farmers, and in the following year the Qing conquered the Tarim Basin, forming the region later called Xinjiang.",
  "Republic of China|1912": "Sun Yat-sen proclaimed the republic at Nanjing on 1 January, the Xuantong Emperor abdicated in February and Yuan Shikai became provisional president in Beijing. The new state inherited the Qing’s vast territory but little real authority over the provinces, an empty treasury and foreign privileges in the treaty ports, so its power was modest.",
  "Republic of China|1916": "Yuan Shikai’s failed attempt to make himself emperor provoked provincial revolts, and his death in June left the Beiyang government in Beijing without a strong leader. Real power passed to rival militarists who fought over the capital and its revenues, and the republic was internationally recognised but internally fragmented, near the lowest point of its mainland history.",
  "Republic of China|1924": "China was still divided among warlords, while Sun Yat-sen’s rival government in Guangzhou reorganised the Kuomintang with Soviet help, formed an alliance with the Communists and founded the Whampoa Military Academy. These steps laid the foundations of later recovery, but the republic as a whole remained weak, poor and divided at this point.",
  "Republic of China|1928": "The Northern Expedition took Beijing, the Northeast accepted Nationalist authority at the end of the year, and the Nationalist government at Nanjing won international recognition as the government of a nominally reunified China. Regional militarists still kept much autonomy and the Communists survived in rural bases, but central power was far greater than in the warlord years.",
  "Republic of China|1936": "After the ‘Nanjing decade’ of road and railway building, currency reform and army modernisation, the government was at its strongest before the war, although Japan had seized Manchuria in 1931. The Xi’an Incident in December forced Chiang Kai-shek to end the civil war and prepare a united front against Japanese aggression.",
  "Republic of China|1938": "Japan’s invasion cost the republic Beijing, Shanghai, Nanjing, Wuhan and Guangzhou, with most of its industry, revenue and coastal cities. The government withdrew to Chongqing in the interior and fought on, with growing Soviet and later American aid, but it now controlled only the poorer western and south-western provinces.",
  "Republic of China|1943": "After Pearl Harbor the republic became one of the Allies. In 1943 the United States and Britain gave up extraterritoriality, and at the Cairo Conference Chiang Kai-shek met Roosevelt and Churchill, who promised the return of Manchuria and Taiwan. Its international standing rose sharply even though its armies remained hard pressed.",
  "Republic of China|1945": "Japan’s surrender brought victory after eight years of war. The republic became a founding member and permanent Security Council member of the United Nations, recovered Manchuria and took over Taiwan in October. Its territorial reach and international prestige were at their peak, although the economy was exhausted and the Communists were much stronger.",
  "Republic of China|1948": "The resumed civil war turned against the government. Communist armies took Manchuria in the Liaoshen Campaign, the Huaihai Campaign began, and hyperinflation destroyed the currency reform of August 1948 and much public confidence. The constitution of 1947 was overridden by emergency powers, and the government’s grip on the mainland was collapsing.",
  "Republic of China|1949": "The Communists took Nanjing, Shanghai and Guangzhou, and in December the government moved to Taipei. The republic was reduced to Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen, Matsu and some smaller islands, with a large army of refugees, little support from Washington until the Korean War, and a real threat of invasion.",
  "Republic of China|1954": "After the outbreak of the Korean War in 1950 the United States protected the Taiwan Strait, and in December 1954 it signed a mutual defence treaty with the republic. American aid and land reform stabilised the island, but Chiang Kai-shek ruled it under martial law while still claiming to govern all China.",
  "Republic of China|1971": "General Assembly Resolution 2758 gave China’s seat at the United Nations to the People’s Republic, and the republic withdrew from the organisation. Its claim to represent all China lost international backing, and Japan and many other states soon switched recognition, even as Taiwan’s export economy was growing quickly.",
  "Republic of China|1979": "The United States switched recognition to Beijing on 1 January and ended the mutual defence treaty, but Congress passed the Taiwan Relations Act to maintain unofficial ties and arms sales. Diplomatically isolated, the republic relied on rapid industrial growth and American goodwill under President Chiang Ching-kuo.",
  "Republic of China|1987": "Martial law was lifted after 38 years, the year after the opposition Democratic Progressive Party had been founded, and travel to the mainland was permitted. The Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company was founded. Political liberalisation and a strong export economy improved the republic’s standing despite its limited recognition.",
  "Republic of China|1996": "In the first direct presidential election Lee Teng-hui won, despite Chinese missile tests that triggered the Third Taiwan Strait Crisis and the dispatch of two American carrier groups. Democratic institutions were now complete, and Taiwan was a prosperous high-technology economy, though its diplomatic status remained unresolved.",
  "Republic of China|2025": "The republic governs Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu, with about 23 million people, a consolidated democracy and a leading role in advanced semiconductor manufacturing. It is recognised by only a few states and faces growing military pressure from the People’s Republic, so its power stands well below its 1945 peak.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1949": "Mao Zedong proclaimed the People’s Republic on 1 October after the Communist victory in the civil war. The new state controlled almost the whole mainland and a huge army, but the country was impoverished by decades of war, its industry was small and damaged, and inflation and banditry still had to be brought under control.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1953": "The Korean War armistice in July left China with prestige as the power that had fought the United States to a stalemate. Land reform was complete, Soviet aid was flowing and the First Five-Year Plan began building heavy industry, giving the state far firmer control than any Chinese government for a century.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1957": "The First Five-Year Plan had raised industrial output quickly and agriculture had been collectivised. China was a recognised partner of the Soviet Union and had played a prominent part at the Bandung Conference, but the Anti-Rightist Campaign silenced critics just before the radical experiment of the Great Leap Forward.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1960": "The Great Leap Forward had collapsed into the worst famine in modern history, which killed tens of millions between 1959 and 1961. The Soviet Union withdrew its advisers in 1960, ending aid to many projects. Economic output fell steeply and the state’s capacity was badly damaged, although its political control held.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1964": "The economy had recovered under the adjustment policies of Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping, China had defeated India in the 1962 border war, and on 16 October 1964 it tested its first atomic bomb at Lop Nur. Isolated from both superpowers, it nevertheless stood stronger than at any time since 1949.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1967": "The Cultural Revolution threw the country into chaos. Red Guards attacked party officials and each other, schools and universities closed, and factional fighting drew in parts of the army. Although China tested a hydrogen bomb this year, administration and the economy were severely disrupted and the state was internally weakened.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1971": "In October the People’s Republic took China’s seat at the United Nations, including permanent membership of the Security Council, and secret contacts with the United States prepared Nixon’s visit of 1972. Order had largely been restored by the army, giving China a new diplomatic standing despite continuing political turmoil at home.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1976": "Zhou Enlai and Mao Zedong both died, the Tangshan earthquake killed some 240,000 people by official count, and the Gang of Four was arrested in October. After a decade of the Cultural Revolution the economy was stagnant and the leadership divided, leaving the state’s future direction uncertain.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1978": "The Third Plenum in December confirmed Deng Xiaoping’s leadership and launched reform and opening. Diplomatic relations with the United States were agreed for 1 January 1979. China remained poor, but the turn to market incentives and foreign trade began four decades of rapid growth.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1989": "A decade of reform had more than doubled output, opened Special Economic Zones and freed farmers to sell surplus produce. Inflation and corruption fed the protests in Tiananmen Square, crushed by the army on 4 June, which brought Western sanctions and a brief pause in reform, though the economy was far stronger than in 1978.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|1997": "Deng Xiaoping died in February and Hong Kong returned to Chinese sovereignty on 1 July. After Deng’s southern tour of 1992 reform had accelerated, the economy was growing at around ten per cent a year, and China had become a major destination for foreign investment and a growing military power.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|2001": "China joined the World Trade Organization in December after fifteen years of negotiations, opening world markets to its exports. Macau had returned in 1999. Membership accelerated the transformation of the coastal provinces into the world’s main manufacturing base and raised China’s economic weight sharply over the following decade.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|2010": "After hosting the 2008 Beijing Olympics and weathering the global financial crisis with a large stimulus, China passed Japan as the world’s second-largest economy and became the largest exporter and manufacturer. Military modernisation and diplomatic reach grew with its wealth, making it a recognised great power.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|2015": "Under Xi Jinping, leader since 2012, China launched the Belt and Road Initiative and the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank, built artificial islands in the South China Sea and began a major reorganisation of the armed forces. Its economy, trade and military spending had reached a scale second only to the United States.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|2020": "COVID-19, first identified in Wuhan, spread around the world, and China imposed strict zero-COVID controls. A National Security Law was imposed on Hong Kong and a deadly clash with India took place in the Galwan Valley. Rivalry with the United States sharpened, while the economy kept growing and the navy became the largest by ship numbers.",
  "People\u0027s Republic of China|2025": "The People’s Republic is the world’s second-largest economy, the leading manufacturer and trading nation in goods, a nuclear power with the largest navy by number of ships and a global lender. Slower growth, a property-sector crisis and a shrinking population are challenges, but its overall power stands at its peak.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-2055": "Mentuhotep II succeeded to the Theban throne at a time when Egypt was still divided between the Eleventh Dynasty in the south and the Herakleopolitan kings in the north. Thebes controlled Upper Egypt only, so its power was regional, though its rulers already claimed the kingship of the whole country.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-2040": "By about this time, though the exact date is uncertain, Mentuhotep II had defeated Herakleopolis and its allies in Middle Egypt and reunited the Two Lands, taking the name Sematawy. A single court again controlled the whole Nile valley, its revenues and its expeditions, and work began on his great temple at Deir el-Bahri.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-2004": "Mentuhotep III inherited a united and stable kingdom. His reign is known for building in the Theban region and for an expedition under the official Henenu that crossed the Eastern Desert to the Red Sea to sail for Punt. The state was secure but still governed from Thebes with strong provincial nomarchs.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1985": "The vizier Amenemhat took the throne as Amenemhat I, probably after a period of unrest, and founded the Twelfth Dynasty. He built the new residence of Itjtawy near el-Lisht, reorganised provincial boundaries and began fortification of the north-eastern frontier, laying the institutional base of the dynasty\u0027s later strength.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1956": "Amenemhat I died, apparently after a palace conspiracy recalled in the Instruction of Amenemhat and the Tale of Sinuhe, and Senusret I took sole power. His long reign brought conquest of Lower Nubia to the Second Cataract, the fortress at Buhen, and major building at Heliopolis, Karnak and many provincial temples.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1911": "Amenemhat II\u0027s reign was prosperous and largely peaceful. An annals inscription found at Memphis records expeditions and gifts from the Levant, and the treasure deposited in the temple at Tod held silver and lapis lazuli from abroad. Egypt\u0027s power rested on stable administration and wide contacts rather than new conquest.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1877": "Senusret II began the large-scale development of the Faiyum, extending cultivation through water management along the Bahr Yusuf, and built his pyramid and planned town at el-Lahun. The great nomarch families of Middle Egypt, such as that of Khnumhotep II at Beni Hasan, still flourished alongside a confident crown.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1870": "Senusret III came to the throne and launched repeated campaigns into Nubia, fixed the frontier at Semna and completed the chain of Second Cataract fortresses. The disappearance of the great nomarch tombs in his reign points to a stronger central administration. The kingdom approached the height of its military and administrative power.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1831": "Under Amenemhat III, who reigned for some forty-five years, the Middle Kingdom reached its peak. The Faiyum was intensively farmed, the Sinai turquoise mines were worked on an unprecedented scale, the frontier forts held Nubia, and the king built pyramids at Dahshur and Hawara with the vast mortuary temple later called the Labyrinth.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1786": "Amenemhat IV completed his predecessor\u0027s projects, including the temple at Medinet Madi in the Faiyum, and expeditions to Sinai continued. The machinery of state still worked well, but the reign was short and the royal line was running out of male heirs, so the dynasty\u0027s position was weaker than at its height.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1773": "After the short reign of Sobekneferu the Twelfth Dynasty ended and the Thirteenth began. The new kings kept Itjtawy, the bureaucracy and the Nubian forts, so continuity was strong, but many reigned only briefly, and authority began to shift towards viziers and leading official families at court.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1740": "Around the middle of the eighteenth century BCE, under Neferhotep I and Sobekhotep IV, the Thirteenth Dynasty enjoyed a period of relative strength. Building continued at Abydos and Karnak, and the ruler of Byblos acknowledged Egyptian overlordship. Yet the eastern Delta was already slipping away from direct control by the crown.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1725": "By this time, and perhaps much earlier, a separate line, the Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt, ruled the eastern Delta from Avaris, where a population of Levantine origin had grown over the previous century. The kingdom at Itjtawy thus lost the gateway to the Levant and much of the Delta\u0027s wealth, and its kings were increasingly short-lived.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1690": "The later Thirteenth Dynasty kings are little more than names in the Turin King List. Royal monuments became rare, the Second Cataract forts were losing contact with the north, and Kerma grew stronger in Upper Nubia. The state survived as a reduced kingdom of Middle and Upper Egypt with weak central authority.",
  "Middle Kingdom of Egypt|-1650": "Around this date the Hyksos rulers of the Fifteenth Dynasty, based at Avaris, took Memphis and the north, and the residence at Itjtawy was abandoned. Egypt split among the Hyksos, a Theban kingdom and probably an Abydos Dynasty, ending the Middle Kingdom and opening the Second Intermediate Period.",
  "Numidia|-202": "After the Battle of Zama, in which his cavalry helped Rome defeat Hannibal, Masinissa was recognised as king of both the Massylii and the Masaesyli. Numidia was united for the first time, with Cirta as capital, but the new kingdom still had to absorb Syphax\u0027s former lands and build a royal administration.",
  "Numidia|-161": "Around this time Masinissa seized the Emporia, the rich coastal towns of the Lesser Syrtis such as Leptis Minor, and Roman envoys let him keep them. His kingdom now controlled valuable ports and farmland taken from Carthage, and its grain exports and diplomatic standing in the Mediterranean were growing.",
  "Numidia|-152": "In the 150s Masinissa took the Great Plains and the district of Tusca with some fifty towns, cutting deeper into Carthage\u0027s African hinterland. Numidia reached nearly its greatest extent, with a strong army of cavalry, infantry and elephants, a wealthy court and towns open to Punic and Hellenistic influence.",
  "Numidia|-150": "Carthage, provoked beyond endurance, raised an army against Masinissa and was decisively defeated near Oroscopa, with the nearly ninety-year-old king still leading his troops. Numidia stood at its height as the dominant power of North Africa, and Rome used Carthage\u0027s breach of treaty to declare the Third Punic War.",
  "Numidia|-148": "Masinissa died after a reign of over fifty years, and Scipio Aemilianus divided royal powers among his sons Micipsa, Gulussa and Mastanabal. The kingdom stayed intact and Gulussa\u0027s cavalry served Rome at Carthage, but Rome now took over Carthage\u0027s territory itself, ending Numidia\u0027s prospects of further expansion.",
  "Numidia|-118": "Micipsa, who had ruled alone after his brothers\u0027 deaths, died after a long and peaceful reign of building and urban growth. He left the kingdom jointly to his sons Adherbal and Hiempsal and his adopted son Jugurtha, a fragile arrangement that soon turned into rivalry and murder among the heirs.",
  "Numidia|-116": "After Jugurtha had Hiempsal killed and defeated Adherbal, a Roman commission divided Numidia, giving the richer, more urban east with Cirta to Adherbal and the west to Jugurtha. The partition showed how far the Roman Senate now arbitrated Numidian affairs and weakened the kingdom as a single power.",
  "Numidia|-112": "Jugurtha besieged Cirta, forced Adherbal to surrender and killed him together with the Italian traders who had helped defend the city. He reunited the kingdom by force, but the massacre of Italians provoked outrage in Rome and led to a declaration of war the following year.",
  "Numidia|-109": "Jugurtha had humiliated a Roman army near Suthul and now fought Metellus at the Battle of the Muthul. Though he lost ground and towns, his mobile cavalry warfare and his control of the interior kept the Romans from a decisive victory, and the kingdom still resisted Rome in arms.",
  "Numidia|-105": "Bocchus I of Mauretania betrayed his son-in-law Jugurtha to Sulla, ending the war. Rome gave much of western Numidia to Bocchus and set up Gauda as a client king over the rest. The kingdom survived only as a reduced Roman dependency with no independent foreign policy.",
  "Numidia|-81": "During Rome\u0027s civil wars Pompey invaded Africa, defeated and killed the Marian-backed king Hiarbas and restored Hiempsal II. Numidia\u0027s rulers were now made and unmade by Roman factions. The kingdom kept its own court, coinage and towns but its political fate depended entirely on Rome.",
  "Numidia|-60": "Around this time Juba I succeeded his father Hiempsal II. He was ambitious and hostile to Julius Caesar, who had publicly insulted him in Rome, and built up a substantial army with elephants. Numidia regained some confidence, though it remained a client kingdom whose independence depended on Roman politics.",
  "Numidia|-49": "Juba I destroyed the Caesarian army of Curio at the Battle of the Bagradas River, killing Curio. The victory made Juba the strongest supporter of the Pompeian cause in Africa and briefly gave Numidia a decisive part in Rome\u0027s civil war, but it also tied its fate to the losing side.",
  "Numidia|-46": "Caesar defeated the Pompeians and Juba at the Battle of Thapsus. Juba died in a suicide pact with Petreius, and Caesar annexed the core of the kingdom as the province of Africa Nova, giving western lands to Mauretanian kings and the Cirta district to Sittius. The Numidian kingdom ceased to exist.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|-146": "Rome destroyed Carthage and made its remaining territory the province of Africa, governed from Utica. The province was small, limited by the Fossa Regia against Numidia, its land partly confiscated as Roman public land. Its importance lay mainly in denying the region to any rival power rather than in its own wealth.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|-122": "Gaius Gracchus founded the colony of Junonia on the site of Carthage, one of the first Roman colonies planned outside Italy, but it was abandoned after his fall, though some settlers kept their land. The province grew slowly, with Roman and Italian traders and landholders settling among Punic and Berber communities.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|-46": "After defeating the Pompeians and Juba I at Thapsus, Caesar annexed much of the Numidian kingdom as Africa Nova, governed first by the historian Sallust. Roman territory in North Africa more than doubled, bringing the fertile plains of the Medjerda valley and many Punic-speaking towns under direct Roman rule.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|-27": "Probably around this time Augustus joined Africa Vetus and Africa Nova into one province, Africa Proconsularis, under a senatorial proconsul. Carthage was refounded as a Roman colony and became the capital, and veteran colonies and new settlers spread Roman law and land division across the countryside.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|17": "The Numidian Tacfarinas began a revolt among the Musulamii and other peoples of the interior that lasted until 24 CE. Rome needed extra troops and several campaigns to end it, but the settled heart of the province was not seriously threatened and continued to grow in wealth and population.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|39": "Around this year Caligula transferred command of the Third Legion Augusta from the proconsul to an imperial legate, creating a separate military zone in Numidia. The civil province lost its army but kept its wealth and prestige, and its grain shipments to Rome were by now essential to the capital\u0027s food supply.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|98": "Trajan\u0027s accession opened a century of growth. Many native towns were promoted to Roman municipia or colonies, olive farming spread onto drier land, and African oil and pottery began to reach markets across the Mediterranean. Leading Africans entered the Roman Senate in rising numbers.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|193": "Septimius Severus of Leptis Magna became emperor, the first from Africa. His dynasty lavished buildings on African cities, promoted towns to colonial status and favoured Africans at court. The province stood at the height of its prosperity, urban building and political weight within the Roman Empire.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|238": "A revolt at Thysdrus proclaimed the proconsul Gordian I emperor. Within weeks Capelianus, legate of Numidia, defeated the rebels at Carthage, killing Gordian II and prompting his father\u0027s suicide. Reprisals hit Carthage and its elite, and the episode exposed the province to the instability of the third-century crisis.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|258": "Bishop Cyprian of Carthage was executed in the persecution of Valerian. Plague and political crisis troubled the empire, and some African building slowed, though the province suffered less than frontier regions. Its economy remained strong and its Christian community kept growing despite persecution.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|303": "Around this time Diocletian divided the old province, separating Byzacena and Tripolitania and leaving Africa Proconsularis as the northern core, Zeugitana, within a new diocese of Africa. Persecution of Christians began the same year. The province was smaller but still the richest and most prestigious in the African diocese.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|312": "After the usurpation of Domitius Alexander, Constantine gained control of Africa. A disputed election to the see of Carthage was starting the Donatist schism, which would divide African Christians for a century. Economic life remained strong, with rural prosperity and new building in many towns.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|398": "The general Gildo, who had cut off the grain supply to Rome, was defeated and killed. His revolt showed how vital African grain was to Rome and how exposed the province was to ambitious commanders. Church councils against the Donatists, led by Augustine, filled the following years.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|429": "Gaiseric led the Vandals and Alans across the Strait of Gibraltar and moved east. Roman forces under Boniface were defeated and Hippo Regius was besieged in 430. The province\u0027s frontier and hinterland were overrun, and Roman control shrank towards Carthage and the coast.",
  "Africa Proconsularis|439": "On 19 October Gaiseric seized Carthage by surprise, ending Roman rule over the province. The Vandal Kingdom took over its estates, ports and fleet, and the western Roman Empire lost its richest source of grain and tax revenue. Roman rule returned only with Justinian\u0027s reconquest in 533–534.",
  "Roman Egypt|-30": "Octavian took Alexandria, Antony and Cleopatra died, and the Ptolemaic kingdom became a Roman province under an equestrian prefect. Egypt brought immense wealth in grain and taxes to the new regime, but the country had to be pacified, and a revolt in the Thebaid soon tested Roman control.",
  "Roman Egypt|-24": "Roman forces were active on every frontier: Aelius Gallus had campaigned in Arabia, and the prefect Petronius drove back a Kushite raid and sacked Napata. A treaty concluded at Samos a few years later fixed a stable southern border, and the province settled into regular grain shipments to Rome.",
  "Roman Egypt|38": "Violence between Greeks and Jews broke out in Alexandria, and embassies, including one led by the philosopher Philo, went to the emperor Gaius. The disorders exposed social tensions in the great city, but the province as a whole was prosperous, well administered and central to feeding Rome.",
  "Roman Egypt|117": "The Jewish revolt of 115–117 ended with the destruction of the Jewish community of Alexandria and heavy losses in the countryside. Trajan\u0027s forces restored order, but the province had suffered devastation, and parts of the land and population took time to recover under his successor Hadrian.",
  "Roman Egypt|130": "Hadrian toured Egypt and founded the city of Antinoopolis in memory of his favourite Antinous, who drowned in the Nile. The province stood at the height of its prosperity, with flourishing nome capitals, intense Red Sea trade and Alexandrian scholarship that included Ptolemy\u0027s astronomical work.",
  "Roman Egypt|175": "The Antonine Plague had struck from the 160s, the Boukoloi rose in the Delta in the early 170s, and in 175 the general Avidius Cassius proclaimed himself emperor with Egyptian support. Population and revenue fell in many villages, and Egypt\u0027s loyalty and stability were briefly in doubt.",
  "Roman Egypt|212": "The Constitutio Antoniniana of Caracalla made almost all free inhabitants Roman citizens, following Septimius Severus\u0027s grant of town councils to Alexandria and the nome capitals. Egypt\u0027s cities gained new institutions and new burdens, while the province remained wealthy and central to the empire\u0027s food supply.",
  "Roman Egypt|215": "Caracalla, angered by Alexandrian mockery, ordered a massacre in the city during his visit. The episode harmed the city\u0027s population and elite, and the wider crisis of the third century, with inflation and political instability, began to weigh on Egypt\u0027s economy and administration.",
  "Roman Egypt|270": "Forces of Palmyra under Queen Zenobia occupied Egypt, cutting it off from Rome\u0027s government until Aurelian restored control around 272. The loss of the province showed the depth of the third-century crisis, with debased coinage, civil wars and disrupted grain shipments.",
  "Roman Egypt|298": "Diocletian crushed the revolt of Domitius Domitianus and Achilleus after a siege of Alexandria, pulled the southern frontier back to Philae and began to reform taxation and provinces. Egypt was divided into smaller provinces and integrated into the common imperial system, restoring order and revenue.",
  "Roman Egypt|330": "Constantinople was dedicated as the new imperial capital, and Egyptian grain was now shipped there. Christianity was spreading quickly with imperial support, monasticism was flourishing in the deserts under Anthony and Pachomius, and Athanasius led the Alexandrian church. Egypt was again prosperous and central to the empire.",
  "Roman Egypt|391": "The Serapeum of Alexandria was destroyed under Patriarch Theophilus, marking the triumph of Christianity over public pagan cult. Egypt remained wealthy, with large estates and busy towns, but the patriarchs of Alexandria became powerful rivals to imperial officials and religious conflict grew more intense.",
  "Roman Egypt|451": "The Council of Chalcedon deposed Patriarch Dioscorus, and most Egyptian Christians rejected its doctrine. The resulting split between Chalcedonian and Miaphysite churches undermined loyalty to Constantinople for two centuries, though Egypt\u0027s grain and taxes still flowed to the capital and its economy remained strong.",
  "Roman Egypt|539": "Around this year Justinian\u0027s Edict 13 reorganised Egypt\u0027s administration to secure the grain supply, joining civil and military powers in regional governors. The province was still prosperous and productive, but plague arrived in 541, and church divisions and powerful landowners limited imperial authority.",
  "Roman Egypt|619": "In the course of the great war with Persia, Sasanian forces took Alexandria and occupied Egypt. The grain supply to Constantinople was cut off and Roman administration ceased for a decade, leaving the province\u0027s loyalty and institutions badly weakened just before the Arab conquest.",
  "Roman Egypt|629": "After Heraclius\u0027s victories over Persia, the Sasanians withdrew and Roman rule returned. But the province was exhausted, its garrison weak, and Heraclius\u0027s patriarch Cyrus soon tried to force religious union on the Miaphysite majority, which deepened resentment against the imperial government.",
  "Roman Egypt|641": "Amr ibn al-As had invaded in 639, won at Heliopolis and taken Babylon. In November 641 Cyrus agreed by treaty to surrender Alexandria to the Rashidun Caliphate, and imperial troops left the following year. Nearly seven centuries of Roman rule over Egypt came to an end.",
  "Makuria|450": "Makuria was emerging as one of several successor polities in the Middle Nile valley after the fall of Meroë. Large royal tumuli at el-Zuma and Tanqasi point to a powerful elite around Dongola, but the kingdom was still small, pagan and largely undocumented, so its power stood low.",
  "Makuria|569": "According to John of Biclaro, the Makurians accepted Christianity around this time and soon sent ivory and a giraffe to Justin II. Conversion linked the court to the Byzantine world and gave the kings a new ideology, but Makuria was still one of three Nubian kingdoms of modest reach.",
  "Makuria|642": "A raiding force sent from newly conquered Egypt, probably under Uqba ibn Nafi, met fierce resistance from Makurian archers, remembered by the Arabs as pupil-smiters. Repelling an army that had just overrun Byzantine Egypt showed that Makuria was a serious military power on the Middle Nile.",
  "Makuria|652": "Abdallah ibn Sa\u0027d ibn Abi Sarh besieged Old Dongola and damaged its church, but could not conquer the kingdom. The resulting Baqt treaty exchanged slaves for Egyptian grain, wine and cloth and guaranteed Makurian independence, laying the foundation for six centuries of stable relations with Muslim Egypt.",
  "Makuria|700": "By about this time, and perhaps much earlier, Makuria had absorbed Nobadia, ruling the Nile from the First Cataract far upriver. King Merkurios, praised by Coptic sources as a new Constantine, presided over a united kingdom that had turned to the Miaphysite Coptic Church, greatly increasing its reach and coherence.",
  "Makuria|747": "According to the History of the Patriarchs of Alexandria, King Kyriakos marched into Upper Egypt to demand the release of the imprisoned Coptic patriarch. Whatever its scale, the episode shows a confident kingdom that saw itself as protector of Egyptian Christians and could project force beyond its borders.",
  "Makuria|836": "Georgios I, then heir to the throne, travelled to Baghdad and negotiated with the Abbasid caliph al-Mu\u0027tasim over unpaid Baqt arrears, returning with favourable terms. The embassy reflected Makuria\u0027s diplomatic standing, and the ninth century saw extensive church building and a prosperous, stable kingdom.",
  "Makuria|969": "When the Fatimids took Egypt, Jawhar sent Ibn Salim al-Aswani as envoy to the Makurian king, whose account later described a prosperous, populous Nile kingdom. Arab historians also report Nubian raids into Upper Egypt in the 960s. This was the height of Makurian power, wealth and confidence.",
  "Makuria|1100": "Under the Fatimids relations with Egypt remained largely peaceful and trade flowed through Aswan. Makuria stayed prosperous and devout, with cathedrals at Faras, Qasr Ibrim and Old Dongola and a literate church using Greek, Coptic and Old Nubian, though there are signs of slowly growing pressure from the north.",
  "Makuria|1172": "After a Nubian attack on Aswan, Shams al-Dawla Turan-Shah, brother of Saladin, led an Ayyubid army south and sacked Qasr Ibrim in 1172–1173. The raid ended the friendly Fatimid era and exposed the northern province, though Makuria itself remained independent and the Ayyubids soon withdrew.",
  "Makuria|1250": "As the Mamluks seized power in Egypt, Makuria was still an independent Christian kingdom, but Arab nomads were moving into Nubia and the northern borderlands, and the Banu Kanz of the Aswan region were growing in influence. The kingdom held its position, but its margin of security was narrowing.",
  "Makuria|1276": "After King David raided Aidhab and Aswan, Baybars sent an army that defeated him near Dongola and installed his relative Shakanda as a tributary king. Makuria lost its military independence; from now on Mamluk armies repeatedly intervened in royal successions, and its power fell sharply.",
  "Makuria|1317": "The Mamluks placed a Muslim prince, Abdallah Barshambu, on the throne, and the Throne Hall at Old Dongola was converted into a mosque. Rival claimants, including Kanz al-Dawla of the Banu Kanz, fought over the crown while Arab migration and internal division left the monarchy weak and dependent.",
  "Makuria|1365": "Arab sources indicate that Old Dongola was sacked around this time amid civil war between royal rivals and their Arab allies, and the surviving court withdrew north to Gebel Adda. Makuria ceased to exist as a kingdom ruling from Dongola, though a reduced Christian successor, Dotawo, survived in Lower Nubia.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1229": "Abu Zakariya Yahya, Almohad governor of Ifriqiya, broke with Marrakesh after the caliph al-Ma’mun abandoned the doctrine of Ibn Tumart. Ruling from Tunis as emir, he controlled the old province of Ifriqiya and a seasoned Almohad elite, but his independence was new and his western frontier still open.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1235": "Abu Zakariya had taken Constantine and Bejaia in 1230 and now added Algiers, extending Hafsid rule across the eastern and central Maghreb. Revenues from the coastal trade of Tunis and the arrival of Andalusi administrators fleeing the Christian conquests in Spain strengthened the new state’s finances and government.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1242": "The Zayyanid ruler of Tlemcen, Yaghmurasan, submitted to Hafsid overlordship, and several Andalusi cities and the rising Marinids gave Abu Zakariya nominal allegiance. Tunis was now the leading Muslim capital of the western Mediterranean, although much of this wider supremacy rested on recognition rather than direct rule.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1253": "Abu Zakariya’s son al-Mustansir, who had succeeded in 1249, claimed the caliphal title of commander of the faithful. The claim reflected genuine strength: secure frontiers, profitable treaties with Italian and Aragonese merchants and a well-funded army, although rebellious relatives and the Arab tribes required constant management.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1260": "After the Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258, the Sharif of Mecca briefly recognised al-Mustansir as caliph, the high point of Hafsid prestige. Tunis was wealthy, its court attracted Andalusi scholars and poets, and an embassy from Kanem had arrived with a giraffe, a sign of the dynasty’s reach.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1270": "Louis IX of France landed at Carthage on the Eighth Crusade. Disease devastated the crusader camp and Louis died near Tunis. Al-Mustansir survived the invasion intact but agreed to pay an indemnity and renew tribute to Sicily, a costly settlement that nonetheless confirmed the dynasty’s resilience.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1277": "The death of al-Mustansir opened decades of succession disputes among Hafsid princes. Rival claimants sought support from the Arab tribes, Aragon and the Marinids, and the unity that Abu Zakariya and al-Mustansir had built began to fragment, reducing the dynasty’s military and financial strength.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1285": "A Hafsid prince established a separate emirate at Bejaia, splitting the realm into eastern and western halves. Roger of Lauria had seized Jerba for Aragon and Sicily the year before, and the Arab tribes were extracting ever larger concessions, leaving the court at Tunis noticeably weaker than in the previous generation.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1309": "The Bejaia branch reunited with Tunis, restoring the dynasty’s territorial unity. The recovery was partial: rulers remained dependent on powerful ministers and tribal allies, and Christian fleets still held Jerba, but the reunification allowed the state to rebuild its revenues and authority in the following decades.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1347": "The Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan of Morocco occupied Tunis, temporarily ending effective Hafsid rule. Although Arab tribes defeated him near Kairouan the next year, and his occupation collapsed, the episode showed how far Hafsid power had declined under rulers dominated by their chamberlains.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1370": "Abu al-Abbas Ahmad II, ruler of Constantine and Bejaia, took Tunis and began reuniting the dynasty’s lands. He curbed the independence of provincial governors and tribal chiefs and restored central finances, inaugurating a long recovery that would carry the sultanate to a second peak.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1394": "Abu Faris Abd al-Aziz II succeeded his father and ruled for forty years. He brought the outlying towns and tribes firmly under control, extended influence over Tlemcen and Tripoli, and strengthened Tunis as a commercial and religious centre, returning the state close to its thirteenth-century strength.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1434": "At the death of Abu Faris the sultanate was again among the strongest states of the Maghreb, with secure frontiers, healthy trade with Italy and Aragon, endowed madrasas and a respected court. His grandson Abu Amr Uthman, who succeeded in 1435 after the brief reign of his brother al-Muntasir, maintained this position for half a century.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1488": "Abu Amr Uthman died after a reign of over fifty years. His successors faced renewed tribal unrest, court intrigues and falling revenues, while Spanish and Portuguese expansion in North Africa threatened the coast. The sultanate entered a lasting decline from which it never recovered.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1510": "Spain captured Bejaia and Tripoli, stripping the Hafsids of major ports. Corsair captains, notably the Barbarossa brothers, operated from Tunisian harbours with the sultan’s permission but soon built their own power at Algiers. Hafsid control of the interior was also slipping as local leaders asserted independence.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1535": "Charles V captured La Goulette and Tunis, expelling Hayreddin Barbarossa, who had seized the city the year before. He restored Mulay Hasan as a Spanish vassal, leaving a Spanish garrison at La Goulette. The dynasty survived only through Habsburg protection, with little real authority beyond the capital.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1569": "Uluj Ali, the Ottoman governor of Algiers, took Tunis, while the Spanish held on at La Goulette. Don John of Austria recaptured the city in 1573 and installed a Hafsid prince, but by now the dynasty was a pawn in the contest between the Habsburg and Ottoman empires.",
  "Hafsid Sultanate|1574": "An Ottoman expedition under Sinan Pasha and Uluj Ali captured Tunis and the fortress of La Goulette. The last Hafsid ruler was taken to Istanbul, ending the dynasty after three and a half centuries, and Ifriqiya was organised as the Ottoman province of Tunis.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1504": "According to tradition, the Funj leader Amara Dunqas founded Sennar on the Blue Nile. Allied with the Arab leader Abdallah Jamma, the Funj took the place of the collapsing Christian kingdom of Alodia, whose capital Soba was destroyed. The new state was powerful locally but its institutions were still taking shape.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1530": "The Funj had consolidated their hold on the Gezira between the Blue and White Niles, with the Abdallab ruling the northern Nile valley as their vassals. Sennar’s rulers adopted Islam and began to attract Muslim scholars, while trade in gold and slaves with Egypt began to fill the royal treasury.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1585": "After the Ottoman advance into Lower Nubia, a clash near Hannik at the Third Cataract, probably in this period, fixed the northern frontier. The Funj secured control of the middle Nile, and the arrival of Sufi teachers of the Qadiriyya order deepened the spread of Islam across the sultanate.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1612": "Sultan Adlan I defeated and killed the rebellious Abdallab ruler Ajib at Karkoj, reasserting Sennar’s dominance over its strongest vassal. The compromise that followed confirmed the Abdallab as subordinate rulers of the north and strengthened the sultan’s position at the head of the Funj hierarchy.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1644": "Badi II Abu Diqn came to the throne and began a long reign of expansion. His campaigns against the Shilluk on the White Nile and towards the Nuba Mountains brought in tribute and captives, giving the sultan new resources independent of the old Funj nobility.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1670": "Under Badi II the sultanate stood near its height. Taqali in the Nuba Mountains paid tribute, a slave army settled around Sennar strengthened royal authority, and the capital flourished as a market for the trade in gold, ivory and slaves with Egypt and the Red Sea.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1700": "Sennar remained a wealthy capital, described by the French physician Charles-Jacques Poncet, who passed through in 1699. Tensions were rising, however, between the sultans and their slave soldiers on one side and the old nobility on the other, and revolts on the northern Nile, notably among the Shaigiya, weakened control.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1718": "The nobility deposed Sultan Unsa III, ending the old royal line and raising a new one. The coup reflected deep conflict over the sultans’ reliance on slave troops and officials, and it left the monarchy more dependent on noble support, temporarily reducing the sultanate’s strength.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1744": "Sultan Badi IV’s forces defeated an invasion by the Ethiopian emperor Iyasu II on the Dinder River. The victory demonstrated that the Funj state could still mobilise effectively, and it restored the sultanate’s prestige in the region between Egypt and Ethiopia.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1748": "In the late 1740s the general Muhammad Abu Likaylik conquered Kordofan from the Musabba’at, extending Funj rule west of the White Nile. The sultanate reached its greatest territorial extent, though the victorious commander and his Hamaj followers were becoming a power in their own right.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1762": "Muhammad Abu Likaylik deposed Badi IV, and the Hamaj regents took over the government, ruling through puppet sultans. The regency kept the state together for a time, but it marked the end of effective royal power and opened decades of rivalry among regents and princes.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1785": "The Darfur Sultanate seized Kordofan, reversing the conquest of the 1740s. Civil wars among the Hamaj regents and royal claimants weakened Sennar further, and the northern Nile groups, such as the Shaigiya, acted with growing independence, leaving the sultanate much reduced.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1800": "By the turn of the century the sultan was a figurehead and Hamaj regents fought among themselves. Sennar’s authority barely extended beyond the Gezira and its immediate surroundings, while merchants in towns such as Shendi conducted the long-distance trade independently of the court.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1820": "Muhammad Ali of Egypt sent an army under his son Ismail Kamil Pasha to conquer the Sudan. Equipped with firearms and artillery, it defeated the Shaigiya at Korti and advanced up the Nile towards Sennar, meeting little organised resistance from the divided sultanate.",
  "Funj Sultanate|1821": "In June 1821 Sultan Badi VII submitted to Ismail Kamil Pasha at Sennar without a fight. The sultanate came to an end after more than three centuries, and its lands were absorbed into the Turco-Egyptian Sudan, which later made Khartoum its capital.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1510": "The tribes of the Sus proclaimed the Saadi sharif Muhammad al-Qa’im bi-amr Allah leader of the holy war against the Portuguese enclaves. The movement had religious prestige and tribal support but no cities, no regular army and no administration, and Morocco was divided among the Wattasids, local lords and Portugal.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1525": "The sons of al-Qa’im, Ahmad al-A’raj and Muhammad al-Shaykh, took Marrakesh, probably in 1524 or 1525, giving the Saadis a capital and control of southern Morocco. They now rivalled the Wattasid sultans of Fez, while continuing the war against the Portuguese coastal fortresses.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1541": "Muhammad al-Shaykh captured the Portuguese fortress of Agadir, and Portugal soon evacuated Safi and Azemmour. The victory made him the leading figure of the dynasty, allowing him to push aside his brother Ahmad al-A’raj and prepare the conquest of the Wattasid north.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1549": "Muhammad al-Shaykh took Fez from the Wattasids, uniting most of Morocco under Saadi rule for the first time. The conquest alarmed the Ottomans in Algiers, who now regarded the new dynasty as a rival, and resistance in the north had not yet been fully broken.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1554": "The Wattasid Abu Hassun briefly recaptured Fez with Ottoman troops, but Muhammad al-Shaykh defeated and killed him at the Battle of Tadla later in the year. The Saadis were now the unchallenged rulers of Morocco, with an army equipped with firearms and a growing administration.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1557": "Muhammad al-Shaykh was assassinated by members of his Turkish guard, reportedly on Ottoman instigation. His son Abdallah al-Ghalib succeeded and in 1558 defeated an army from Algiers at Wadi al-Laban, preserving Moroccan independence, though the loss of the founder was a setback.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1576": "Abd al-Malik, backed by Ottoman troops, ousted his nephew Muhammad al-Mutawakkil and took the throne. The deposed sultan turned to Portugal for help, setting the stage for a confrontation that would decide Morocco’s place between the Iberian and Ottoman powers.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1578": "At the Battle of Alcácer Quibir the Moroccan army destroyed the Portuguese invasion force. King Sebastian, al-Mutawakkil and Abd al-Malik all died, and Ahmad al-Mansur became sultan. Ransoms for captured Portuguese nobles enriched the treasury, and the victory gave Morocco great prestige across the Mediterranean.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1591": "Al-Mansur’s expedition under Judar Pasha defeated Songhai at the Battle of Tondibi and occupied Gao and Timbuktu. Morocco reached its greatest extent, from the Mediterranean to the Niger, with revenues from sugar and Saharan gold and a well-organised court and army at Marrakesh.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1603": "Ahmad al-Mansur died of plague, and his sons Zidan, Abu Faris and al-Ma’mun began fighting for the succession. The state was still large and wealthy, but without an agreed heir its army, treasury and administration were drawn into a civil war that would last for decades.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1610": "Al-Ma’mun ceded the port of Larache to Spain in return for support, a deeply unpopular concession. Morocco was divided between rival Saadi rulers at Fez and Marrakesh, control over the Niger valley was slipping, and regional religious leaders were gaining power at the dynasty’s expense.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1627": "Morisco corsairs founded the independent Republic of Salé, while the marabout al-Ayyashi, the Dila’iyya brotherhood and the Sharifate of Tazeroualt controlled much of the country. Mulay Zidan died in this year, and his successors in Marrakesh ruled only a fraction of the former kingdom.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1641": "The Dila’iyya brotherhood, having defeated al-Ayyashi, dominated much of northern and central Morocco and occupied Fez. The Saadi sultans were confined to Marrakesh and its region, unable to raise armies or collect taxes across the country, and survived mainly because no rival could yet unite Morocco.",
  "Saadi Sultanate|1659": "The last Saadi sultan, Ahmad al-Abbas, was killed in Marrakesh by the family of his mother, ending the dynasty. Morocco remained fragmented until the Alaouites, another sharifian family from Tafilalt, reunited it, taking Fez in 1666 and Marrakesh soon afterwards.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1516": "Oruç Barbarossa seized Algiers at the invitation of its notables, who wanted help against the Spanish fort on the Peñón. His rule was precarious: the Spanish garrison still commanded the harbour, local rivals resented the corsairs, and Oruç’s own push inland to Tlemcen overstretched his small force.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1519": "After Oruç was killed near Tlemcen in 1518, Hayreddin Barbarossa placed Algiers under the Ottoman Sultan Selim I. The title of beylerbey, about 2,000 Janissaries and the right to recruit in Anatolia gave the corsair base imperial backing, though Spanish attacks and local revolts still threatened it.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1529": "Hayreddin captured the Spanish fort on the Peñón and linked the islets to the shore with a mole, giving Algiers a protected harbour. With the last Spanish foothold at the city removed, the corsair fleet could grow, and Algiers became the main Ottoman naval base in the western Mediterranean.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1541": "Emperor Charles V led a great expedition against Algiers, but a storm wrecked much of his fleet and the defenders under Hasan Agha drove off the landing force. The victory secured the city for almost three centuries and greatly raised the prestige of the young Ottoman province.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1556": "Salah Rais had taken Bejaia from Spain in 1555, and Algerian forces ended the Zayyanid kingdom of Tlemcen in these years. The regency now controlled most of the coast and the Tell between Morocco and Tunisia, leaving Spain only Oran and Mers el-Kébir in the region.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1569": "Uluj Ali, beylerbey since 1568, captured Tunis from its Hafsid ruler and two years later led the Ottoman left wing at Lepanto. Algiers stood at the height of its importance within Ottoman strategy, a regional power projecting force both eastward along the coast and across the sea.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1587": "After Uluj Ali’s death the Ottomans ended the rule of beylerbeys and sent pashas for three-year terms. Imperial control was meant to tighten, but in practice power drifted to the Janissary divan and the corsair captains, while the corsair economy continued to expand rapidly.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1627": "The corsair fleet was at its peak, its sailing ships ranging into the Atlantic; raiders from Algiers and Salé reached Iceland in 1627 and Baltimore in Ireland in 1631. Captives, prizes and ransoms made Algiers one of the richest and most feared ports of the Mediterranean.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1659": "The Janissaries rose against the appointed pasha and gave power to their own aghas, each of whom was murdered in office. Treaties with northern European naval powers were beginning to limit corsair prizes, and the political instability reflected strains within the ruling military caste.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1671": "The corsair captains overthrew the last agha and imposed a dey, elected in principle by the ojaq. The new system gave Algiers a ruler chosen locally and largely independent of Istanbul, but frequent assassinations of deys kept government unstable for decades.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1683": "The French admiral Abraham Duquesne bombarded Algiers in 1682 and 1683 with new bomb vessels, destroying much of the lower town. The attacks showed that European navies could now strike the city directly, and peace treaties with France, England and the Netherlands gradually narrowed corsairing.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1708": "The regency captured Oran and Mers el-Kébir from Spain, removing the last major Spanish outpost on its coast. Shortly afterwards the dey also took the title of pasha, completing the regency’s practical independence from Istanbul and marking a phase of recovery and territorial consolidation.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1732": "A large Spanish expedition retook Oran and Mers el-Kébir, restoring Spain’s foothold on the western coast. The loss was a serious setback for the beylik of the West, though the regency still controlled the interior and its corsairs and treaties continued to bring revenue.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1756": "Algerian armies took Tunis and imposed tribute on its beys, a dependence that lasted until 1807. The episode showed that the regency remained the strongest military power of the central Maghreb, even as its corsair fleet was smaller than in the seventeenth century.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1792": "Spain, after an earthquake devastated Oran in 1790, ceded the city to the regency, and the beylik of the West moved its seat there. The gain came as corsairing and grain exports revived during the French Revolutionary Wars, though plague and political murders weakened Algiers itself.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1815": "After the Napoleonic Wars an American squadron under Stephen Decatur forced Algiers to end tribute from the United States and release captives. The European powers, now at peace with one another, no longer needed to tolerate corsairing, and the regency’s main source of revenue began to collapse.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1816": "An Anglo-Dutch fleet under Lord Exmouth bombarded Algiers, destroying much of its fleet and forcing the release of more than a thousand Christian captives. Corsairing never recovered, and the regency entered its last years financially weakened and diplomatically isolated from the European powers.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1827": "In a quarrel over French debts for grain, Hussein Dey struck the French consul Pierre Deval with a fly-whisk. France answered with a naval blockade that strangled Algerian trade for three years, while the dey refused to apologise and the regency had no allies willing to help.",
  "Regency of Algiers|1830": "A French army landed at Sidi Ferruch in June and Algiers capitulated on 5 July. Hussein Dey went into exile and the Ottoman regime in Algiers ended; only the bey of Constantine held out, keeping his capital until 1837, and the territory became French Algeria.",
  "French Algeria|1830": "French troops landed at Sidi Ferruch and took Algiers on 5 July, ending the Ottoman regency. France held only the capital and a few coastal points, the July Revolution soon toppled Charles X, and the new regime was unsure whether to keep, limit or abandon the costly conquest.",
  "French Algeria|1837": "After a failed attempt in 1836, French troops stormed Constantine and ended Ahmed Bey’s rule in the east. In the west, however, the Treaty of Tafna recognised Abdelkader’s authority over much of the interior, so France controlled only part of the country and the coastal enclaves around it.",
  "French Algeria|1847": "Abdelkader surrendered in December after years of war against Bugeaud’s mobile columns, which had devastated the countryside. His defeat gave France control of most of the Tell, though the cost in Algerian lives was immense and Kabylia and the Saharan fringes still lay beyond French control.",
  "French Algeria|1848": "The Second Republic declared Algeria French territory and divided the civilian north into the departments of Algiers, Oran and Constantine. Settlement accelerated with state-sponsored colonies of migrants, and the institutional model of governing Algeria as part of France itself was established for over a century.",
  "French Algeria|1857": "Marshal Randon’s campaign subdued Great Kabylia, completing the conquest of the northern mountains. Under Napoleon III the colony expanded its settlement and infrastructure, though the emperor’s talk of an “Arab kingdom” and military rule over the interior frustrated settlers who wanted full civilian government.",
  "French Algeria|1871": "The fall of the Second Empire brought civilian rule and the Crémieux Decree, while the Mokrani revolt in Kabylia and the east was crushed. Heavy collective fines and land confiscations followed, opening vast areas to new settlers and strengthening colonial domination over a weakened rural society.",
  "French Algeria|1881": "The Code de l’indigénat was systematised and many Algerian services were attached directly to Paris ministries. Settlement, vineyards and railways expanded quickly as phylloxera hit French wine, and the settler population grew, though the colony depended heavily on metropolitan money and troops.",
  "French Algeria|1900": "Algeria received financial autonomy under a budget voted by settler-dominated Financial Delegations, and the Southern Territories were organised two years later. The colony was now territorially complete, economically productive and politically controlled by its European minority, with Muslim Algerians largely excluded from power.",
  "French Algeria|1914": "Algeria sent large numbers of soldiers and workers to France during the First World War, and the colonial economy kept growing on wine and cereal exports. Wartime service, however, encouraged demands for political rights that would feed the first organised Algerian nationalist movements in the 1920s.",
  "French Algeria|1930": "France celebrated the centenary of the conquest with great ceremony, at the height of settler prosperity and confidence. Colonial power, the European population and wine exports stood near their maximum, yet the reformist ulama, founded the next year, and Messali Hadj’s nationalists were already organising.",
  "French Algeria|1940": "After France’s defeat the colony came under the Vichy regime, which abolished the Crémieux Decree and applied antisemitic laws to Algerian Jews. Algeria escaped occupation but was cut off from much of its metropolitan trade, and Vichy’s authority rested on a narrow settler base.",
  "French Algeria|1943": "Following the Allied landings of Operation Torch in November 1942, Algiers became the capital of Free France and the seat of de Gaulle’s French Committee of National Liberation. Algeria’s strategic importance peaked, though Ferhat Abbas’s Manifesto of the Algerian People showed growing demands for autonomy.",
  "French Algeria|1945": "Demonstrations at Sétif on 8 May led to riots and to massive reprisals that killed thousands of Algerians around Sétif and Guelma. French control was restored by force, but the massacres convinced many nationalists that reform within the colonial system was impossible.",
  "French Algeria|1954": "The FLN launched its insurrection on 1 November with coordinated attacks across the country. France still held Algeria firmly, its economy was growing and oil exploration in the Sahara had begun, but a long war against a determined guerrilla movement had now started.",
  "French Algeria|1957": "Paratroopers under General Massu won the Battle of Algiers by breaking the FLN’s urban network through mass arrests and torture. Militarily France was gaining ground, but the methods used destroyed support for the war in France and abroad and pushed many Algerians towards the FLN.",
  "French Algeria|1958": "Settler crowds and army officers in Algiers rebelled against Paris in May, bringing down the Fourth Republic and returning de Gaulle to power. The colony’s future now depended on a leader who gradually concluded that French Algeria could not be preserved by force.",
  "French Algeria|1961": "Four generals attempted a putsch in Algiers in April, but conscripts and the public in France rallied to de Gaulle and it collapsed within days. The settler OAS turned to terrorism, while negotiations with the FLN’s provisional government moved towards independence.",
  "French Algeria|1962": "The Évian Accords of 18 March ended the war, a referendum approved independence on 1 July, and Algeria became independent on 5 July. Around 800,000 pieds-noirs left for France within months, and French Algeria ceased to exist after 132 years.",
  "Ephesus|-1000": "By tradition Ionian settlers under Androclus founded Greek Ephesus near the mouth of the Cayster, beside older Carian and Lelegian communities and a native goddess cult. The new town was small, ruled by kings and dependent on farming and local exchange, so its standing was modest compared with what followed.",
  "Ephesus|-750": "The first temple buildings rose at the Artemision in the marshy plain, and the sanctuary began to attract offerings from a wide area. Ephesus was by now an established member of the Ionian League, trading with the Lydian interior, though still a modest town among the Ionian cities.",
  "Ephesus|-600": "Ephesus grew rich on the road to Sardis and the wealth of its goddess. Electrum coins buried beneath the Artemision belong to the earliest coinage in the world, and the temple was already acting as a treasury, giving the city an economic standing out of proportion to its size.",
  "Ephesus|-560": "Croesus of Lydia besieged Ephesus and the city submitted after tying itself symbolically to the temple. The tyrant Pindarus withdrew, and Ephesus became a Lydian tributary. Its independence was reduced, but royal patronage paid for the great marble temple, so its wealth and prestige held steady.",
  "Ephesus|-546": "After Cyrus the Great defeated Croesus, Ephesus passed under the Achaemenid Empire. It paid tribute through the satrap at Sardis and lost control of its foreign affairs, but trade along the royal routes and the sanctuary\u0027s income continued, so the city remained prosperous though politically subordinate.",
  "Ephesus|-498": "During the Ionian Revolt the rebel army gathered at Ephesus before burning Sardis, then was beaten near the city on its return. Ephesus kept largely out of the later fighting and escaped the destruction that fell on Miletus in 494 BCE, preserving its wealth under renewed Persian rule.",
  "Ephesus|-478": "After the Persian defeats at Salamis, Plataea and Mycale, Ephesus joined the Delian League led by Athens. It paid tribute in money rather than providing ships, and benefited from Aegean trade under Athenian protection, though its political choices were now limited by Athenian supervision.",
  "Ephesus|-412": "Ephesus went over to Sparta and Persia in the Ionian War and became Lysander\u0027s naval base; the Spartan victory at Notium in 406 BCE was fought nearby. Its strategic importance and the money flowing through its harbour lifted its standing among the Ionian cities.",
  "Ephesus|-356": "Herostratus set fire to the Artemision, destroying the archaic temple. Ephesus was again under Persian sovereignty after the King\u0027s Peace of 387/386 BCE, ruled by oligarchs friendly to the satraps. The city began rebuilding the temple on a grander scale, showing resources and pride despite political dependence.",
  "Ephesus|-334": "Alexander the Great entered Ephesus after the Battle of the Granicus, restored democracy and redirected the old Persian tribute to Artemis. Freed from Persian rule, the city rebuilt its temple as one of the Seven Wonders and regained a measure of self-government under Macedonian protection.",
  "Ephesus|-290": "Lysimachus moved Ephesus to a new site nearer the sea, girded it with long walls and renamed it Arsinoeia. The planned city, with its new harbour and gerousia, became one of the largest in Asia Minor, marking the high point of its size and strategic weight in this period.",
  "Ephesus|-281": "Lysimachus fell at the Battle of Corupedium and the city reverted to the name Ephesus under Seleucus I. It kept its walls, harbour and institutions, but became a prize between the Seleucid and Ptolemaic kingdoms rather than the favoured capital of a single king.",
  "Ephesus|-246": "During the Third Syrian War Ephesus came under the Ptolemaic Empire and probably remained a Ptolemaic base for much of the next half-century. Commerce and the temple economy flourished, but the city\u0027s role was that of a garrisoned naval station in a wider rivalry between dynasties.",
  "Ephesus|-196": "Antiochus III of the Seleucid Empire captured Ephesus and made it a headquarters for his campaigns in Asia Minor and Greece. The city gained importance as a royal base but was drawn into his war with Rome, which ended with his defeat at Magnesia in 190 BCE.",
  "Ephesus|-188": "The Treaty of Apamea gave Ephesus to Eumenes II of Pergamon. Under Attalid rule it was the kingdom\u0027s chief port, and Attalus II rebuilt its harbour. The city prospered greatly, though the narrowed harbour mouth reportedly made the silting of the Cayster worse.",
  "Ephesus|-133": "Attalus III left his kingdom to Rome. Ephesus backed Rome against the pretender Aristonicus and became part of the province of Asia, organised by 129 BCE. It lost its last independent political role, while its wealth and size made it the chief city of Roman Asia.",
  "Chios|-1000": "Ionian Greeks settled Chios alongside earlier inhabitants and formed a community centred on the east-coast harbour facing Asia. The island had good farmland and a sheltered position, but its early state was a small kingdom of farmers and seafarers with little influence beyond its own coasts.",
  "Chios|-750": "Chios was now an established member of the Ionian League. Its farmers had begun producing the wine for which the island became famous, and Chian pottery and settlements such as the hilltop village at Emporio show a growing population and expanding contacts across the Aegean.",
  "Chios|-600": "Chios had become one of the richest Greek states. Its wine and mastic were exported widely, its merchants shared the Hellenion at Naucratis in Egypt, and a law of about 575–550 BCE records a popular council. Its craftsmen, sculptors and fleet gave it real standing in Ionia.",
  "Chios|-546": "After Cyrus the Great defeated Lydia, Chios submitted to the Achaemenid Empire and received Atarneus on the mainland for surrendering the rebel Pactyes. It kept its prosperity but now paid tribute and was ruled by a Persian-backed tyrant, Strattis, which limited its independence.",
  "Chios|-494": "In the Ionian Revolt Chios sent 100 ships to the Battle of Lade, the largest contingent of any rebel state, and its crews fought on after other squadrons fled. The size of this fleet shows Chios near the height of its naval strength, even though the battle was lost.",
  "Chios|-493": "In the aftermath of Lade the Persian fleet took the Ionian islands and ravaged Chios, according to Herodotus netting the population and burning settlements and sanctuaries. The island\u0027s manpower and fleet were badly reduced, and it returned to Persian rule under a restored tyranny.",
  "Chios|-479": "After the Greek victory at Mycale, Chios joined the Hellenic alliance and soon the Delian League as a ship-providing ally. Freed from Persian tribute, it rebuilt its fleet and economy, keeping full autonomy within the Athenian-led system and resuming its export trade in wine.",
  "Chios|-440": "Chios helped Athens crush the revolt of Samos in 440–439 BCE. With Samos disarmed, Chios and Lesbos were the only allies that still had their own fleets. Its stable government and great wealth won the praise of Thucydides, and its navy made it valuable to Athens.",
  "Chios|-430": "At the start of the Peloponnesian War Chios stood at its height: autonomous, rich in slaves, wine and silver, and contributing warships to Athenian expeditions. After Lesbos lost its fleet in 427 BCE, Chios was the last major ally with independent naval forces, giving it unique standing.",
  "Chios|-412": "After the Athenian disaster in Sicily, Chios revolted with Spartan backing. It brought its fleet and wealth to the Spartan side, but Athens landed troops, won several battles on the island and ravaged its rich countryside for the first time since the Persian Wars.",
  "Chios|-404": "The war ended with Spartan victory, but Chios had suffered heavy losses, slave desertions and occupation by Spartan commanders. Like other former allies of Athens it was probably ruled by a narrow pro-Spartan regime, its fleet much reduced and its independence constrained by its new hegemon.",
  "Chios|-378": "Chios, which had allied with Athens in 384 BCE, became a founding member of the Second Athenian League. It recovered much of its earlier prosperity and naval importance, joining a defensive league against Sparta on terms designed to protect members from Athenian interference.",
  "Chios|-357": "With Rhodes, Cos and Byzantium, and backed by Mausolus of Caria, Chios left the Second Athenian League. Its fleet repelled an Athenian assault on its harbour, killing Chabrias, and the Social War ended in 355 BCE with Chian independence secured, though Carian influence grew.",
  "Chios|-333": "Persian commanders seized Chios during their counter-offensive in the Aegean, installing a pro-Persian oligarchy. The island became a base against Alexander\u0027s forces, and its own citizens had little say in its fate as the great powers fought over the eastern Aegean.",
  "Chios|-332": "Macedonian forces retook Chios. A letter of Alexander the Great, probably from these years, ordered the return of exiles, a new democratic constitution and a garrison until the settlement was complete. Chios regained self-government, but under the clear supremacy of the Macedonian king.",
  "Chios|-200": "As a Hellenistic free city Chios balanced between the Antigonid, Ptolemaic, Seleucid and Attalid kingdoms. A great naval battle was fought in the strait off the island in 201 BCE. It remained prosperous through trade and kept its institutions, but was now a minor power.",
  "Chios|-88": "Mithridates VI of Pontus overran Roman Asia and Chios submitted to him. The king, who suspected the Chians of Roman sympathies, held them in distrust; according to Appian, a Chian ship collided with his flagship during the attack on Rhodes, deepening his hostility.",
  "Chios|-86": "Mithridates fined Chios heavily and then had his general Zenobius deport its citizens towards the Black Sea. Sulla later restored the survivors and Rome confirmed the island\u0027s freedom, but its existence as an independent state was effectively over within Roman Asia.",
  "Miletus|-1000": "Ionian Greeks settled Miletus near the mouth of the Maeander, on a site with a long Bronze Age past, traditionally under Neleus, son of Codrus. The new community mixed Greek and Carian elements and was still a small town with a sheltered harbour and limited farmland, but well placed for trade.",
  "Miletus|-750": "Miletus had become a leading member of the Ionian League and controlled the oracle of Apollo at Didyma. Its harbours, wool trade and growing population gave it a commercial lead over its neighbours, and its sailors were beginning to explore the Hellespont and the approaches to the Black Sea.",
  "Miletus|-650": "The great age of Milesian colonisation was under way. Settlements such as Cyzicus, Sinope and Histria opened the Black Sea to Greek trade, sending grain, fish and timber home. Miletus was now the largest and richest city of Ionia, and its merchants were also active in Egypt.",
  "Miletus|-605": "Under the tyrant Thrasybulus Miletus withstood eleven years of Lydian invasions by Sadyattes and Alyattes, holding out because it controlled the sea. A negotiated peace with Alyattes confirmed its independence and alliance with Lydia, a rare success against the strongest power in western Anatolia.",
  "Miletus|-585": "Miletus was the intellectual and commercial centre of Ionia. Thales was said to have predicted the eclipse of this year, and Anaximander and Anaximenes followed him. Despite civil strife between rich and poor, the city\u0027s trade and colonies continued to expand across the Black Sea.",
  "Miletus|-546": "When Cyrus the Great conquered Lydia, Miletus alone among the Ionian cities secured a treaty on the terms it had held with Croesus. It escaped the sieges suffered by its neighbours and kept its wealth, though it now lived under Persian supremacy and paid tribute.",
  "Miletus|-513": "Histiaeus, tyrant of Miletus, guarded the Danube bridge during the Scythian campaign of Darius I and was rewarded with land at Myrcinus in Thrace. The city stood in high favour with Persia and was, as Herodotus put it, at the height of its prosperity, the ornament of Ionia.",
  "Miletus|-500": "Miletus was at its peak: the leading city of Ionia, mother of dozens of colonies, home of the first Greek philosophers and geographers, and a trusted Persian client. Aristagoras governed it while Histiaeus remained at Susa, and he planned an expedition against Naxos with Persian support.",
  "Miletus|-499": "The failure at Naxos led Aristagoras to renounce tyranny and raise the Ionian Revolt. Miletus led the rebellion and won Athenian and Eretrian help, and Sardis was burned in 498 BCE. The city remained powerful but had staked its future on defeating the Persian Empire.",
  "Miletus|-494": "The Ionian fleet was beaten at the Battle of Lade, and Persia stormed Miletus. Most men were killed, women and children enslaved, and captives deported to Ampe on the Persian Gulf. Didyma was burned. The city effectively ceased to exist as a power for a generation.",
  "Miletus|-479": "After the Greek victory at Mycale, Miletus was freed from Persia and refounded on a new regular grid of streets. Its population and resources were much reduced, and it rebuilt slowly as one of many allies in the new Athenian-led alliance against Persia.",
  "Miletus|-450": "Miletus was a tribute-paying member of the Delian League. Internal unrest, probably an oligarchic revolt, brought Athenian intervention and a decree regulating its government. In 440 BCE a war with Samos over Priene drew Athens in on its side. The city was prosperous again but not independent.",
  "Miletus|-412": "After the Athenian disaster in Sicily Miletus revolted with Spartan support and became a key Spartan and Persian base in Ionia; the first Spartan-Persian treaty was concluded there. The city regained freedom from Athens but at the cost of renewed Persian influence.",
  "Miletus|-386": "The King\u0027s Peace handed the cities of Asia back to Persia. Miletus came under Persian satraps and later the Hecatomnid dynasts of Caria. It remained a sizeable trading city with its harbours and Black Sea links, but had no independent foreign policy.",
  "Miletus|-334": "After the Battle of the Granicus, Alexander the Great besieged Miletus, whose garrison hoped for help from the Persian fleet. His ships blocked the harbour and the city fell to assault. Miletus was freed from Persia but was now subject to Macedonian power.",
  "Miletus|-300": "Under the successors of Alexander, Miletus recovered as a nominally free city. Work began on the vast new temple of Apollo at Didyma, and the city attracted royal patronage. It changed hands between Antigonus, Lysimachus and the Ptolemies, which limited its real independence.",
  "Miletus|-259": "Antiochus II removed the tyrant Timarchus, who had seized power in the city, and the grateful Milesians gave him the title Theos, god. Miletus prospered under Seleucid and Ptolemaic patronage, with new public buildings, but its freedom depended on the goodwill of rival kings.",
  "Miletus|-200": "Miletus absorbed neighbouring Myus, whose site was suffering from the silting of the Maeander, and extended its territory. It remained an important trading city and a centre of the Didyma cult, balancing between Rhodes, Pergamon, Macedon and the rising power of Rome.",
  "Miletus|-133": "Attalus III bequeathed the Pergamene kingdom to Rome, and after the revolt of Aristonicus the province of Asia was organised by 129 BCE. Miletus remained a prosperous city under Roman supremacy, but its history as a sovereign Ionian state was over.",
  "Samos|-1000": "Ionian Greeks were established on Samos beside earlier inhabitants and an old cult of Hera. The island had fertile land and a natural harbour facing Asia, but its community was still a small kingdom of farmers and seafarers with little influence beyond the strait of Mycale.",
  "Samos|-800": "Samos was a member of the Ionian League and was building one of the earliest monumental Greek temples at the Heraion. Imports from Egypt, Cyprus and the Near East at the sanctuary show growing overseas contacts, though the island was still one Ionian state among many.",
  "Samos|-704": "According to Thucydides the Corinthian shipwright Ameinocles built four ships for the Samians around this time. Samos was developing one of the earliest Greek war fleets and was drawn into wider conflicts such as the Lelantine War, siding with Chalcis against its rival Miletus.",
  "Samos|-638": "The voyage of Colaeus to Tartessos in Spain brought back a fortune in silver and became a famous story of Samian enterprise. The island\u0027s merchants were reaching Egypt and the western Mediterranean, and its wealth was reflected in rich dedications at the Heraion.",
  "Samos|-600": "Samos was founding colonies such as Perinthus on the Propontis and settlements in Cilicia, and its traders had a sanctuary at Naucratis in Egypt. The rule of the landowning geomoroi was probably ending, and Samian craftsmen were renowned for bronze-working and architecture.",
  "Samos|-538": "Polycrates seized power with his brothers and soon ruled alone. He built a fleet of a hundred penteconters, hired archers, raided widely and allied with Amasis of Egypt. Samos rapidly became the most aggressive naval power in the Aegean and the richest court in Greece.",
  "Samos|-525": "Samos was at its peak. Polycrates beat off a Spartan and Corinthian siege of forty days, controlled many islands, and attracted poets and craftsmen. His great building works, including the harbour mole, the temple of Hera and the Tunnel of Eupalinos, were under way or complete.",
  "Samos|-522": "The Persian satrap Oroetes lured Polycrates to the mainland and had him killed. Samos still had its fleet and fortifications, but it lost the ruler who had held its power together, and his secretary Maeandrius struggled to keep control against rival factions and Persian ambitions.",
  "Samos|-517": "The Persian general Otanes conquered Samos to install Syloson as tyrant. According to Herodotus the Persians killed so many Samians that the island had to be repopulated. Samos became a Persian client state paying tribute, its naval supremacy and independent foreign policy gone.",
  "Samos|-494": "In the Ionian Revolt Samos sent sixty ships to the Battle of Lade, but forty-nine deserted before the fighting. The Persians spared the island and restored the tyrant Aeaces, while discontented Samians sailed west and seized Zancle in Sicily. Samos remained a minor Persian subject.",
  "Samos|-479": "The Greek fleet came to Samos at the invitation of its people, and the Battle of Mycale was fought on the mainland opposite. Samos regained its freedom and joined the new alliance against Persia, keeping its own fleet as a privileged ship-providing member.",
  "Samos|-441": "Samos was one of the strongest members of the Delian League, with its own fleet and territory on the mainland. A dispute with Miletus over Priene led Athens to intervene, and the Samian oligarchs, backed by the Persian satrap Pissuthnes, rose in revolt.",
  "Samos|-439": "After a siege of nine months Samos surrendered to Pericles. It lost its walls and fleet, gave hostages and agreed to pay a heavy indemnity. The island was reduced from an autonomous naval ally to a closely controlled subject of Athens.",
  "Samos|-411": "A democratic revolution in 412 BCE made Samos Athens\u0027 most loyal ally, and the Athenian fleet was based there. Its sailors rejected the oligarchy of the Four Hundred and recalled Alcibiades. Samos had recovered autonomy and importance, but as the base of another state\u0027s navy.",
  "Samos|-404": "After the Athenian defeat Lysander besieged and took Samos, restored the oligarchs and was honoured with a festival in his name. The democrats who had received Athenian citizenship in 405 BCE were driven out, and Samos came under Spartan domination.",
  "Samos|-365": "The Athenian general Timotheus took Samos from a Persian garrison after a siege of ten months. Athens settled the island with its own citizens, and the Samians were expelled. For more than forty years the Samian state existed only in exile.",
  "Samos|-322": "After the death of Alexander, Perdiccas returned the Samians to their island under the Exiles Decree and expelled the Athenian cleruchs. Samos was restored as a self-governing city, but it was small and dependent on the favour of Macedonian rulers.",
  "Samos|-281": "In the third century BCE Samos became a major Ptolemaic naval base in the Aegean. The city prospered, produced figures such as Aristarchus and the historian Duris, and kept its own assembly and magistrates, but its foreign policy followed the Ptolemaic kings and their fleet.",
  "Samos|-188": "After the Peace of Apamea ended Seleucid power in western Asia Minor, Samos came within the orbit of Pergamon and Rome. It remained a prosperous island community with a famous sanctuary of Hera and its own civic institutions, but it had no independent military or naval strength.",
  "Samos|-129": "After Attalus III bequeathed his kingdom to Rome and the revolt of Aristonicus was crushed, Samos became part of the province of Asia. It remained prosperous under Roman rule, and its sanctuary still drew visitors, but its history as an independent state was over.",
  "Sparta|-900": "Dorian Greeks had settled villages in the Eurotas valley in the tenth century BCE, and four of them were growing together to form Sparta. The community was small and poor, one of many in a thinly populated Laconia recovering from the end of the Mycenaean world.",
  "Sparta|-800": "The four villages of Sparta had united under two royal houses, and Amyclae was probably absorbed soon afterwards. Sparta was extending its control over the Eurotas valley and the rest of Laconia, turning neighbouring communities into perioikoi subject to Spartan leadership.",
  "Sparta|-725": "In the First Messenian War, traditionally dated c. 743–724 BCE, Sparta conquered Messenia across Mount Taygetus. Its people became helots working the land for Spartan masters, doubling Spartan territory and creating the agricultural base that would sustain a full-time citizen army.",
  "Sparta|-669": "According to the traditional date, Argos defeated Sparta at the Battle of Hysiae. The setback showed the limits of Spartan power in the Peloponnese, and tensions within Sparta and in conquered Messenia were growing, foreshadowing a dangerous revolt that would test the Spartan state to its limits.",
  "Sparta|-650": "In the Second Messenian War, remembered through the poems of Tyrtaeus, the Messenians rose against Sparta. Sparta crushed the revolt after a long struggle. The ordeal probably encouraged the reforms that created the classical Spartan system of training, messes and citizen equality.",
  "Sparta|-550": "After repeated failures Sparta defeated Tegea and turned it into an ally, the start of the Peloponnesian League. Sparta was now the strongest state in the Peloponnese, and around 546 BCE it won the Battle of the Champions against Argos over the Thyreatis.",
  "Sparta|-494": "King Cleomenes I destroyed the Argive army at the Battle of Sepeia, removing Argos as a rival for a generation. Sparta had already helped expel the tyrant Hippias from Athens in 510 BCE and was recognised as the leading land power of Greece.",
  "Sparta|-480": "Sparta led the Hellenic Alliance against the invasion of Xerxes. Leonidas I and his 300 Spartiates died at Thermopylae, delaying the Persian advance, while Spartan commanders held overall command of the allied fleet that fought at Artemisium and then at Salamis, a sign of their unrivalled prestige.",
  "Sparta|-479": "The regent Pausanias led the largest Greek army yet assembled to victory at the Battle of Plataea, ending the Persian invasion; King Leotychidas shared in the victory at Mycale. Spartan military prestige stood at its height, though Athens soon took over the war at sea.",
  "Sparta|-464": "A great earthquake devastated Sparta, killing many citizens, and the helots of Messenia revolted, holding out on Mount Ithome for years. Sparta dismissed an Athenian relief force, and relations with Athens broke down. The disaster weakened Spartan manpower permanently and made fear of the helots even more central to policy.",
  "Sparta|-431": "Sparta and its Peloponnesian League allies went to war with Athens, invading Attica in an attempt to bring the Athenians to battle. Sparta remained the leading land power of Greece, but it lacked the fleet and money to defeat the Athenian empire quickly.",
  "Sparta|-425": "The Athenians captured some 120 Spartiates on the island of Sphacteria near Pylos, shattering Sparta\u0027s aura of invincibility. Athens occupied Pylos and encouraged helot desertions, and Sparta sought peace, which was concluded in 421 BCE as the Peace of Nicias.",
  "Sparta|-404": "With Persian money and Lysander\u0027s fleet, Sparta destroyed the Athenian navy at Aegospotami and starved Athens into surrender. Sparta now dominated the whole Greek world, imposing oligarchies and governors, and it stood at the peak of its power, though its citizen numbers were already shrinking.",
  "Sparta|-386": "Despite naval defeat at Cnidus and the costly Corinthian War, the King\u0027s Peace confirmed Spartan supremacy in mainland Greece as guarantor of the settlement. Under Agesilaus II Sparta broke up hostile leagues, crushed the Chalcidian League around Olynthus, and seized the citadel of Thebes in 382 BCE.",
  "Sparta|-371": "At the Battle of Leuctra the Thebans under Epaminondas killed King Cleombrotus I and some 400 of the 700 Spartiates present. The myth of Spartan invincibility collapsed, and Sparta\u0027s allies in the Peloponnese began to desert it, while Theban armies prepared to invade Laconia itself.",
  "Sparta|-362": "Thebes had invaded Laconia, freed Messenia and founded Messene, depriving Sparta of half its land and helots. The Battle of Mantinea left Greece without a leader, but Sparta was reduced to a second-rank Peloponnesian state with a dwindling citizen body.",
  "Sparta|-331": "Sparta refused to join the League of Corinth under Macedon. When Alexander was in Asia, King Agis III led a revolt, but Antipater crushed it at Megalopolis, where Agis was killed. Sparta was now a minor power with little influence outside Laconia.",
  "Sparta|-227": "Cleomenes III killed the ephors, cancelled debts, redistributed land and enlarged the citizen body to about 4,000, reviving the agoge. Sparta briefly became a serious power again, challenging the Achaean League for leadership of the Peloponnese and winning a series of victories against Aratus of Sicyon.",
  "Sparta|-222": "At the Battle of Sellasia Antigonus III Doson of Macedon and the Achaean League defeated Cleomenes III, who fled to Egypt. Macedonian forces occupied Sparta for the first time, and many of the reforms were undone, ending the last attempt to restore Sparta as a great power.",
  "Sparta|-192": "After the death of Nabis, who had defied Rome and the Achaeans, Philopoemen forced Sparta into the Achaean League. Its independence was lost, and in 188 BCE Philopoemen abolished the traditional Lycurgan laws and the agoge, though both were later revived in some form.",
  "Sparta|-146": "Sparta\u0027s disputes with the Achaean League helped provoke the Achaean War. After Rome destroyed Corinth, Sparta became a free city allied to Rome, keeping its own institutions and a revived agoge under Roman supremacy, but it was no longer a sovereign power.",
  "Athens|-900": "In the early Iron Age Athens was one of the larger communities of a depopulated Greece, a cluster of settlements around the Acropolis whose Kerameikos cemetery produced fine Protogeometric and Geometric pottery. Attica was not yet fully unified under the city, and Athens had no influence beyond its own region.",
  "Athens|-683": "The list of annual archons begins, marking the transition from kingship to rule by aristocratic magistrates. Attica was now largely united under Athens, but the city remained a modest agrarian state, less prominent in trade and colonisation than Corinth, Chalcis or Miletus, and divided by rivalries between noble families.",
  "Athens|-621": "Draco\u0027s written laws were the first attempt to replace private vengeance with public justice after the failed coup of Cylon. Athens remained troubled by debt, land hunger and aristocratic feuds, yet its pottery and trade were growing and its control of Attica was secure, giving a stronger base than most Greek cities.",
  "Athens|-594": "Solon, appointed archon to settle the crisis, cancelled debts on the person, ended debt bondage and created four property classes as the basis of political rights. The reforms averted civil war but did not end faction, and Athens was still a regional power, contesting Salamis with Megara.",
  "Athens|-546": "Peisistratus established a lasting tyranny after the Battle of Pallene. He and his sons promoted festivals, public building and Attic pottery exports, and extended Athenian interests to the Hellespont at Sigeion. Stable rule and growing prosperity raised Athens\u0027 standing, though Sparta and Corinth remained stronger in mainland Greece.",
  "Athens|-508": "Cleisthenes reorganised Attica into demes and ten tribes and created the Council of 500, founding the democracy. The new state soon defeated Boeotia and Chalcis in 506 BCE, settling colonists on Chalcidian land. Athens had become a confident, cohesive state, though still overshadowed militarily by Sparta.",
  "Athens|-490": "Athenian and Plataean hoplites under Miltiades defeated the Persian expeditionary force at Marathon. The victory gave the young democracy enormous prestige and confidence, but Athens was still primarily a land power with a modest fleet, and Persia\u0027s resources remained vastly greater than those of any Greek city.",
  "Athens|-480": "Laurion silver had financed some 200 triremes, and Athens provided the largest contingent at Salamis, where Xerxes\u0027 fleet was defeated. The city itself had been evacuated and sacked by the Persians, so power rested on the fleet and on prestige rather than on intact territory or wealth.",
  "Athens|-478": "After Sparta withdrew from leadership against Persia, the Ionian and island Greeks formed the Delian League under Athenian command, with tribute assessed by Aristides. Athens now led a naval alliance across the Aegean and began rebuilding its walls, becoming the rival of Sparta for leadership of Greece.",
  "Athens|-454": "The League treasury was moved from Delos to Athens, and the alliance was turning into an empire of tribute-paying subjects. Despite the disaster of the Egyptian expedition, Athens controlled the Aegean, held allies on the mainland and had the Long Walls linking the city to Piraeus.",
  "Athens|-431": "At the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War Athens stood at its height, with some 300 triremes, a reserve of 6,000 talents on the Acropolis, about 150 tribute-paying allies and the Parthenon complete. Pericles\u0027 strategy relied on the walls and the fleet to outlast Sparta\u0027s superior army.",
  "Athens|-413": "The Sicilian Expedition ended in the destruction of two fleets and thousands of men, Sparta occupied Decelea in Attica, and allies began to revolt. Athens retained a fleet and much of its empire and recovered remarkably in the following years, but its strength was permanently reduced.",
  "Athens|-404": "After defeat at Aegospotami and a winter of siege, Athens surrendered to Sparta. It lost its fleet except twelve ships, its empire and the Long Walls, and the Thirty Tyrants ruled with a Spartan garrison. This was the lowest point of the Classical city, though democracy returned within a year.",
  "Athens|-378": "With the Long Walls rebuilt and a new fleet, Athens founded the Second Athenian League as a voluntary alliance against Spartan aggression, promising not to repeat the abuses of the fifth century. It regained naval leadership in the Aegean, though with fewer resources and less control than before.",
  "Athens|-338": "Athens and Thebes were defeated by Philip II at Chaeronea. Philip spared the city and its fleet but dissolved the Second Athenian League, and Athens had to join the League of Corinth under Macedonian leadership. It remained prosperous and well governed under Lycurgus, but no longer independent in foreign policy.",
  "Athens|-322": "Defeat in the Lamian War brought a Macedonian garrison to Munychia in Piraeus, a property qualification that disenfranchised poorer citizens, and the end of the Athenian navy after its losses at Amorgos. Demosthenes took his own life. Athens was now a dependent city within the Macedonian sphere.",
  "Athens|-307": "Demetrius Poliorcetes expelled Cassander\u0027s regent Demetrius of Phalerum and restored the democracy, earning divine honours. Athens regained a measure of self-government and prestige as an ally of the Antigonids, but its freedom depended on the fortunes of rival Macedonian kings rather than on its own strength.",
  "Athens|-262": "After the Chremonidean War, in which Athens allied with Sparta and Ptolemy II, Antigonus II Gonatas took the city and garrisoned it, controlling its magistrates for decades. This was the low point of Hellenistic Athens, politically subordinate though still the leading centre of philosophy.",
  "Athens|-229": "Athens paid the Macedonian commander to withdraw the garrisons from Piraeus and Attica, regaining independence under the leadership of Eurykleides and Mikion. The city followed a cautious policy of neutrality and friendship with the Ptolemies and later with Rome, a minor but free state.",
  "Athens|-167": "As a loyal ally after the Third Macedonian War, Athens received Delos, Lemnos and other territory from Rome. Delos became a free port where Athenian, Italian and eastern merchants grew rich, and Athens enjoyed renewed prosperity, though it was by now a client of Roman power.",
  "Athens|-86": "Having sided with Mithridates VI in 88 BCE, Athens was besieged by Sulla, who stormed the city on 1 March 86 BCE and plundered Piraeus. The independent polis ended; Athens survived as a free city under Rome, valued for its schools and monuments rather than its power.",
  "Knossos|-900": "Two or three centuries after the fall of the palace, Knossos was a sizeable early Iron Age town around the old ruins. Rich burials in the North Cemetery already contained imported goods, showing that the community kept wider contacts than most Aegean settlements, though it controlled only its immediate surroundings.",
  "Knossos|-800": "Knossos flourished as one of the wealthiest communities of Geometric Greece. Tombs of the North Cemetery held gold jewellery, Near Eastern bronzes and Cypriot and Phoenician imports, perhaps linked to craftsmen from the Levant settling in the area. Its standing rested on wealth and contacts rather than territorial power.",
  "Knossos|-650": "Knossos had become a Dorian-speaking polis with its own territory and institutions, one of the larger cities of a densely settled Crete. Cretan art and law were influential in the seventh century, but the island was divided among dozens of competing poleis, which limited any one city\u0027s reach.",
  "Knossos|-550": "Like most of Crete, Knossos is much less visible archaeologically in the sixth century BCE, with fewer burials and imports. Whether this reflects decline, social restrictions on display or gaps in research is debated, but the city seems to have played a modest role in a period of Cretan isolation.",
  "Knossos|-450": "An inscription records a treaty between Knossos and Tylissos concluded under the arbitration of Argos, regulating territory and the sharing of spoils. Knossos was again an active regional power in central Crete, extending influence over smaller neighbours, though it remained a city of local rather than Aegean importance.",
  "Knossos|-400": "Knossos began striking silver coins with the labyrinth and the Minotaur, proclaiming its Minoan heritage. With Gortyn it was now one of the two strongest cities of Crete, and Cretan archers and mercenaries were increasingly hired abroad, but the island remained outside the main conflicts of Classical Greece.",
  "Knossos|-300": "In the early Hellenistic period Knossos competed with Gortyn for leadership of Crete, building networks of alliance and shared citizenship with smaller cities. Hellenistic kings recruited Cretan mercenaries, and Knossos\u0027 standing rose with the island\u0027s importance in eastern Mediterranean warfare, while its own wars remained local.",
  "Knossos|-221": "Polybius reports that Knossos and Gortyn, acting together, had brought nearly all of Crete under their control except Lyttos. Knossos, backed by Aetolian troops, opened the Lyttian War, and the alliance with Gortyn made it the strongest city on the island.",
  "Knossos|-220": "While the Lyttian army was absent, the Knossians seized and destroyed Lyttos, eliminating their most stubborn rival. This marked the height of Knossian power, as the city led a coalition that dominated central and eastern Crete, though the war split Gortyn and provoked a hostile coalition backed by Philip V.",
  "Knossos|-216": "Philip V of Macedon, called in by the opponents of Knossos, was recognised as protector of the Cretan Koinon. Knossos remained a leading city but had to share influence with Gortyn and others under Macedonian patronage, and Lyttos was gradually refounded, reducing the gains of the war.",
  "Knossos|-200": "The Cretan War against Rhodes, in which many Cretan cities took part, ended around 200 BCE, and Rome began to intervene in the Aegean. Knossos was still one of the two great powers of Crete, but the island\u0027s divisions and piracy left it exposed to stronger outside states.",
  "Knossos|-100": "Through the second century Knossos and Gortyn alternately allied and fought, with foreign envoys repeatedly arbitrating. Around 100 BCE Rome passed measures against piracy in the east, and Crete\u0027s reputation as a pirate haven drew growing Roman hostility, while Knossos itself remained locally strong.",
  "Knossos|-71": "The Cretans defeated the Roman fleet of Marcus Antonius near Kydonia and forced a humiliating treaty, which the Roman Senate refused to ratify. The victory was a last show of Cretan strength, but it made war with Rome inevitable, and Knossos faced a far stronger enemy.",
  "Knossos|-69": "Quintus Caecilius Metellus landed in Crete with three legions, defeated the Cretans near Kydonia and began reducing their cities one by one. Knossos, defended for a time by the Cretan commander Lasthenes, came under siege, and its ability to resist depended on walls rather than allies.",
  "Knossos|-67": "Metellus completed the conquest of Crete, earning the surname Creticus. Knossos lost its independence, and the new province was governed from Gortyn. The city was later refounded as a Roman colony, but the free polis of Knossos came to an end.",
  "Delphi|-800": "Offerings of bronze tripods and figurines began to accumulate at the shrine of Apollo on Parnassus, where a small settlement had existed since Mycenaean times. The sanctuary was still of regional importance, used mainly by the peoples of central Greece, and the community of Delphians that served it was tiny.",
  "Delphi|-733": "Tradition dates the founding of Syracuse to about this time, one of many colonies whose founders were said to have consulted the Pythia. Whatever the truth of individual stories, the colonising movement spread Delphi\u0027s reputation across the Mediterranean, and the oracle began to attract inquirers from all over the Greek world.",
  "Delphi|-650": "Delphi had become the leading oracle of Greece, consulted on colonies, cults and laws. Offerings from Greek cities and foreign rulers, such as the Phrygian king Midas and Gyges of Lydia mentioned by Herodotus, filled the sanctuary, but the Delphians still depended on their neighbours, especially Kirrha, which controlled the approach from the sea.",
  "Delphi|-595": "The First Sacred War began, traditionally around this date, when the Amphictyons, with Cleisthenes of Sicyon and the Thessalians, moved against Kirrha. The war placed the sanctuary under the protection of the Amphictyonic League and freed it from local domination, though many details of the ancient account are doubted.",
  "Delphi|-586": "With Kirrha destroyed and its plain consecrated to Apollo, the Pythian Games were reorganised as a panhellenic festival with athletic as well as musical contests. Delphi gained a recurring gathering that brought visitors from the whole Greek world and placed the sanctuary on a level with Olympia.",
  "Delphi|-548": "The temple of Apollo burned down. The disaster was serious, but rebuilding was financed by subscriptions from Greek cities and individuals and even from the Egyptian pharaoh Amasis, showing how widely the shrine\u0027s importance was recognised. Croesus of Lydia had shortly before sent some of the richest offerings it ever received.",
  "Delphi|-510": "The new temple was completed around this time, with the exiled Athenian Alcmaeonid family as contractors who, Herodotus says, gave it a marble front. Treasuries such as that of Siphnos lined the Sacred Way. Delphi was at the height of its prestige as the arbiter consulted by Greek cities and foreign kings.",
  "Delphi|-480": "During Xerxes\u0027 invasion the oracle told the Athenians to trust in their wooden walls, and Herodotus reports that a Persian force sent against Delphi was driven off by falling rocks. After the victories of 480 and 479 BCE the Greeks dedicated the Serpent Column there, the sanctuary\u0027s moment of greatest panhellenic significance.",
  "Delphi|-449": "In the Second Sacred War Sparta gave the sanctuary to the Delphians, and Athens then returned it to the Phocians. Delphi remained the leading oracle, but rivalry between the great powers over who controlled it showed that its independence depended on the balance of power around it.",
  "Delphi|-373": "The temple of Apollo was destroyed, probably by an earthquake. Rebuilding was again financed by contributions from across Greece and supervised by boards whose accounts survive. The oracle remained widely consulted, but it was less often the arbiter of great political decisions than in the Archaic period.",
  "Delphi|-356": "After the Amphictyony imposed heavy fines on Phocis, the Phocians under Philomelus seized Delphi, opening the Third Sacred War. Onomarchus and his successors spent the temple treasures on mercenaries. The sanctuary lost much of its accumulated wealth, and its government was in the hands of an occupying power.",
  "Delphi|-346": "Philip II of Macedon ended the Third Sacred War, the Phocians were expelled from the Amphictyony and their votes passed to Philip. Delphi regained its sanctuary and the Phocians had to repay the stolen treasure in instalments, but the Amphictyonic council was now an instrument of Macedonian policy.",
  "Delphi|-279": "A Gallic army under Brennus attacked Delphi and was driven back by Aetolian and Phocian forces, helped, as the Greeks believed, by Apollo himself. The victory, celebrated in the festival of the Soteria, restored the sanctuary\u0027s prestige and gave its defenders, the Aetolians, a strong claim to protect it.",
  "Delphi|-250": "The Aetolian League dominated the Amphictyonic council and treated Delphi as part of its sphere. Hellenistic kings and cities still sent dedications and honoured the god, but the oracle was consulted mainly on religious matters, and the city\u0027s policy followed that of its Aetolian protectors.",
  "Delphi|-191": "After defeating Antiochus III and the Aetolians at Thermopylae, the consul Manius Acilius Glabrio restored the property of the sanctuary and the autonomy of the Delphians, which the Senate confirmed. Delphi was free of the Aetolians but now depended on the goodwill of Rome.",
  "Delphi|-168": "After the Battle of Pydna, Lucius Aemilius Paullus took over a pillar prepared for Perseus of Macedon and set his own monument on it. The gesture showed that Rome had replaced the Hellenistic kings as the power that used Delphi to display its victories.",
  "Delphi|-146": "After the Achaean War and the destruction of Corinth, Greece came under the oversight of Roman governors of Macedonia. Delphi kept its sanctuary and its local institutions, but its freedom was now a privilege granted by Rome, and its independent political role came to an end.",
  "Byzantium|-667": "Colonists from Megara founded Byzantium on the promontory between the Golden Horn and the Sea of Marmara, traditionally in this year. The new settlement was small, surrounded by Thracian peoples and overshadowed by older Greek cities, but it occupied an unrivalled site commanding the passage to the Black Sea.",
  "Byzantium|-600": "Byzantium grew as Greek colonies multiplied around the Black Sea and traffic through the Bosporus increased. Fishing and trade made it prosperous, but it remained one of several modest Megarian colonies around the straits, with limited territory and constant pressure from its Thracian neighbours.",
  "Byzantium|-513": "Darius I crossed the Bosporus near Byzantium on a bridge of boats for his campaign against the Scythians, and the city came under Persian rule. It kept its local institutions but paid tribute and served Persian interests, its control of the straits now an asset of the Achaemenid Empire.",
  "Byzantium|-493": "Byzantium had joined the Ionian Revolt against Persia. When the revolt collapsed, its inhabitants fled to Mesembria on the Black Sea and a Phoenician fleet burned the city. Byzantium was at its lowest point, temporarily abandoned and back under Persian control.",
  "Byzantium|-478": "The Greek fleet under Pausanias captured Byzantium from the Persians. After his high-handed conduct, the city and other allies turned to Athens and joined the Delian League. Restored and freed from Persia, Byzantium regained its role as the gatekeeper of the Black Sea trade.",
  "Byzantium|-440": "Byzantium revolted against Athens alongside Samos but was soon brought back into the Delian League. Its large tribute assessment shows that it was among the wealthiest cities in the Hellespontine district, and its trade flourished under Athenian naval protection, though its foreign policy and finances were controlled by Athens.",
  "Byzantium|-408": "Byzantium had defected to Sparta in 411 BCE, but Alcibiades besieged it and recovered it for Athens after a party inside opened the gates. The city\u0027s wealth and position made it a prize in the last phase of the Peloponnesian War, but it had little control over its own fate.",
  "Byzantium|-405": "After the Spartan victory at Aegospotami, Lysander took Byzantium, and the Spartan governor Clearchus later ruled it so harshly that Sparta removed him. The city was now subject to a Spartan garrison and a narrow regime, with its revenues serving Spartan purposes.",
  "Byzantium|-390": "The Athenian general Thrasybulus brought Byzantium back to the Athenian side, restored democracy and levied a tithe on ships leaving the Black Sea. Free of the Spartan garrison, the city recovered its prosperity and influence over the straits, though part of the revenue from its position was still diverted to Athens.",
  "Byzantium|-378": "Byzantium became one of the first members of the Second Athenian League, joining as an ally on more equal terms than in the Delian League. Its fleet and wealth made it one of the League\u0027s most important members, and it took a growing part in the politics of the straits.",
  "Byzantium|-357": "Byzantium, with Chios, Rhodes and Kos, broke away from Athens in the Social War of 357–355 BCE. The rebels won, and Byzantium emerged fully independent, controlling the Bosporus on its own account and extending its influence over neighbouring Greek cities on the Asian and Thracian shores.",
  "Byzantium|-340": "Philip II of Macedon besieged Byzantium, but the city, aided by Athenian fleets and its former Social War allies, held out, and he withdrew in 339 BCE. Its successful defence against the greatest military power of the age marked the height of the city\u0027s standing.",
  "Byzantium|-300": "After Alexander\u0027s conquests Byzantium kept its independence amid the wars of his successors. Its control of the straits and its fleet made it a valued ally, and it prospered from the expanding trade of the Hellenistic world, though it could not match the resources of the new kingdoms.",
  "Byzantium|-277": "Gallic invaders who had crossed into Thrace founded the Kingdom of Tylis close to Byzantium\u0027s territory. The Gauls ravaged its lands and forced it to pay tribute that Polybius says eventually reached 80 talents a year, a heavy and lasting drain on the city\u0027s wealth.",
  "Byzantium|-220": "To pay the Gauls, Byzantium imposed tolls on Black Sea shipping. Rhodes, leading the trading cities, went to war with Byzantium and allied with Prusias I of Bithynia. The war was short, and the Byzantines abandoned the tolls, showing that the city could no longer act alone against stronger neighbours.",
  "Byzantium|-146": "Byzantium had sided with Rome in its wars against the Macedonian kings and remained a free and allied city after Rome\u0027s settlement of Greece and Macedonia. It kept its laws and harbour revenues, but its foreign policy now followed that of Rome, the dominant power in the east.",
  "Byzantium|53": "Byzantine envoys asked the Senate for relief, recalling their help to Rome in wars from Macedon to Mithridates. Claudius remitted their tribute for five years. The city was still prosperous and formally free, but its welfare depended on imperial favour rather than its own power.",
  "Byzantium|73": "Vespasian deprived Byzantium of its freedom, as Suetonius records, and around this year it was incorporated into a Roman province. The independent city-state came to an end, though Byzantium remained an important port until Constantine refounded it as Constantinople in 330 CE.",
  "Celtiberians|-600": "Across the eastern Meseta, cemeteries of cremation burials with iron weapons and small hilltop settlements mark the emergence of a distinct culture later called Celtiberian. Communities were small and scattered, with warrior elites controlling flocks and farmland, and there was no wider political organisation beyond local kin groups and chiefs.",
  "Celtiberians|-500": "Celtiberian communities multiplied and adopted more ironworking, with rich warrior burials showing growing social differences. Fortified hilltop settlements, the castros, controlled valleys and grazing land. Contacts with the Iberian peoples of the coast brought new goods and techniques, but power remained divided among many small groups.",
  "Celtiberians|-400": "Larger fortified settlements developed and territories became better defined, forming the basis of the later peoples such as the Arevaci and Belli. Celtiberian ironworking and horse-breeding prospered, and warrior elites built networks of clients and allies, though the region was still far from any form of unified authority.",
  "Celtiberians|-300": "Fortified towns, or oppida, such as Numantia, Termes and Segeda grew into regional centres governing wide territories. The Celtiberians were now a substantial population of farmers, herders and warriors, and their soldiers began to be known to the Mediterranean powers, in particular Carthage, as formidable mercenaries.",
  "Celtiberians|-211": "During the Second Punic War both Rome and Carthage hired Celtiberian warriors. Some 20,000 recruited by the Scipio brothers deserted them, contributing to the destruction of both Roman armies. The episode showed that Celtiberian manpower could tip the balance in Spain, though the peoples acted for pay rather than as a bloc.",
  "Celtiberians|-181": "As Rome expanded inland after taking over Carthaginian Spain, the Celtiberians resisted in the First Celtiberian War. Their towns fielded large armies against the praetors Quintus Fulvius Flaccus and Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, and fighting was hard on both sides, marking the first full trial of strength with Rome.",
  "Celtiberians|-179": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus ended the war with treaties requiring tribute and auxiliary troops and forbidding new fortified towns. He founded Gracchurris on the Ebro. The Celtiberians kept their institutions and land, and the settlement brought a generation of relative peace, but their independence was now bounded by Roman terms.",
  "Celtiberians|-154": "Segeda, the chief town of the Belli, began enlarging its walls and gathering neighbours inside them. Rome declared this a breach of the Gracchan treaties and prepared for war. The Celtiberians were populous and confident, and the Arevaci, led from Numantia, were ready to support the Belli.",
  "Celtiberians|-153": "The Belli under Carus of Segeda ambushed the consul Quintus Fulvius Nobilior, killing about 6,000 Romans according to Appian, and Nobilior\u0027s later attack on Numantia failed. The Celtiberians had inflicted one of Rome\u0027s worst defeats in Spain, though Carus himself was killed in the fighting.",
  "Celtiberians|-151": "The consul Marcus Claudius Marcellus negotiated a peace with the Celtiberians after further campaigns. The war, which Polybius called a fiery war, had shown the strength of the Celtiberian towns, but it ended without a decisive victory for either side.",
  "Celtiberians|-143": "Inspired by Viriathus\u0027 war in Lusitania, the Arevaci rose again, beginning the Numantine War. Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus subdued much of Celtiberia, but Numantia and Termantia held out, and his successor Quintus Pompeius failed to take them and agreed a peace the Senate later repudiated.",
  "Celtiberians|-137": "The Numantines trapped the consul Gaius Hostilius Mancinus and forced him to surrender with about 20,000 men, accepting a treaty negotiated by Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus. A small town had humbled a consular army; Rome rejected the treaty and handed Mancinus over, but the Celtiberians were at the height of their reputation.",
  "Celtiberians|-134": "Rome elected Scipio Aemilianus, the conqueror of Carthage, as consul to finish the war. He restored discipline in the army, ravaged the surrounding land and surrounded Numantia with a ring of camps and walls. Celtiberian power was now concentrated in a single besieged town cut off from its allies.",
  "Celtiberians|-133": "After months of blockade and famine, Numantia surrendered. Many defenders killed themselves, the survivors were enslaved and the town was razed. Celtiberian independence was broken and Numantia\u0027s land was divided among its neighbours, although the other communities retained their local institutions under Roman supervision and tribute obligations.",
  "Celtiberians|-98": "Celtiberian communities revolted again, and the governor Titus Didius campaigned against them over several years, forcing the people of Termes to move to an unfortified site. Resistance was local and uncoordinated, and Roman commanders were able to defeat the towns one by one.",
  "Celtiberians|-92": "Gaius Valerius Flaccus suppressed further revolts, and at Belgeda the people burned their council house with the councillors inside in a dispute over rebellion. The Celtiberian towns were weakened, divided, and increasingly integrated into Roman provincial administration under local senates.",
  "Celtiberians|-77": "The Roman rebel Quintus Sertorius had won broad support among Celtiberians and Lusitanians, and Celtiberian towns supplied him with troops in his war against Metellus Pius and Pompey. The war briefly revived their military importance, though now in the service of a Roman faction.",
  "Celtiberians|-72": "After the murder of Sertorius, Pompey defeated Marcus Perperna and reduced the last resisting towns, including Uxama and Clunia. The Celtiberians were fully absorbed into the province of Hispania Citerior, and the independent political life of their peoples came to an end.",
  "Boeotian League|-550": "In the mid sixth century BCE the cities of Boeotia shared a dialect, cults and festivals but were independent of one another. Thebes, the largest, was beginning to draw its neighbours into a federation, and a shared coinage with the Boeotian shield soon followed, but the union was still loose and contested.",
  "Boeotian League|-519": "Plataea, unwilling to accept Theban leadership, placed itself under Athenian protection, traditionally in 519 BCE. The episode shows a federation forming around Thebes but meeting resistance, and it began the long hostility between Thebes and Athens. Power stood modestly, as the league was still incomplete and untested.",
  "Boeotian League|-480": "When Xerxes invaded Greece, Thebes and most Boeotian cities took the Persian side, while Plataea and Thespiae fought with the Greeks. Boeotia supplied troops and a base to the Persian army, giving the Theban-led cities a strong but temporary position under Persian patronage before the decisive campaign of 479 BCE.",
  "Boeotian League|-479": "At the Battle of Plataea Boeotian troops fought for Mardonius and shared the Persian defeat. The Greek allies then besieged Thebes and executed its pro-Persian leaders. The federation lost its cohesion and its prestige, and for some decades Boeotia had little weight in Greek affairs.",
  "Boeotian League|-457": "After the Spartans won at Tanagra, the Athenians defeated the Boeotians at the Battle of Oenophyta and for about ten years dominated Boeotia, supporting friendly regimes in its cities. With no effective federal government and Athens controlling the region, Boeotian power stood at one of its lowest points.",
  "Boeotian League|-447": "Boeotian exiles defeated the Athenian general Tolmides at the Battle of Coronea, ending Athenian control. The federation was refounded with a formal constitution of districts, boeotarchs and a federal council, creating a stable framework that allowed Boeotia to act again as a single military power in central Greece.",
  "Boeotian League|-424": "During the Peloponnesian War Boeotia fought as a Spartan ally. Thebes had destroyed Plataea in 427 BCE and absorbed its district, and at the Battle of Delium the Theban commander Pagondas defeated a major Athenian army with a deep phalanx and cavalry. The league was now a significant military power.",
  "Boeotian League|-404": "Athens surrendered in 404 BCE and Boeotia, enriched by plunder from Attica during the Decelean War, stood among the victors. Thebes urged the destruction of Athens but soon fell out with Sparta, sheltering Athenian democratic exiles. The league was strong and confident, though now overshadowed by Spartan supremacy.",
  "Boeotian League|-395": "Thebes led the coalition with Athens, Corinth and Argos against Sparta in the Corinthian War. Boeotians killed Lysander at the Battle of Haliartus but were beaten by Agesilaus at Coronea in 394 BCE. The league remained a serious power, but the war showed its limits against Sparta’s full strength.",
  "Boeotian League|-386": "The King’s Peace, imposed by Persia and enforced by Sparta, required the autonomy of all Greek cities, and Sparta used it to dissolve the Boeotian League. In 382 BCE the Spartan Phoebidas seized the Cadmea of Thebes, leaving Boeotia divided and under Spartan control, with no federal state at all.",
  "Boeotian League|-379": "In the winter of 379–378 BCE Theban exiles led by Pelopidas killed the pro-Spartan rulers and expelled the garrison. A more democratic league, led by Thebes, was rebuilt, the Sacred Band was formed, and Thebes joined Athens against Sparta. Power was recovering quickly but the state was still fighting for survival.",
  "Boeotian League|-371": "At the Battle of Leuctra Epaminondas defeated the Spartan army and killed King Cleombrotus I, destroying the belief that Spartan hoplites were invincible. The Theban-led league became the strongest land power in Greece, and Boeotian cities that had resisted Thebes were now firmly subordinated to the federal state.",
  "Boeotian League|-369": "Epaminondas invaded Laconia, the first enemy invasion of Spartan territory in centuries, freed the Messenians and founded the city of Messene. With allies in Arcadia, Argos and Thessaly and influence in Macedonia, the Theban hegemony reached its greatest extent, marking the high point of the league’s power.",
  "Boeotian League|-364": "Epaminondas launched a naval programme of 100 triremes to challenge Athens at sea, and Thebes destroyed Orchomenus. Pelopidas, however, was killed at Cynoscephalae in Thessaly, though his army won. Thebes remained the leading power in Greece, but its allies were growing restless and its leadership was thinning.",
  "Boeotian League|-362": "At the Battle of Mantinea the Thebans defeated a coalition of Sparta, Athens and Mantinea, but Epaminondas was mortally wounded. The victory brought no settlement, and without its great commander the league could no longer impose its will on Greece. Its supremacy now began to fade.",
  "Boeotian League|-346": "The Third Sacred War against Phocis, which had seized the treasures of Delphi, exhausted Boeotia for ten years. Thebes needed Philip II of Macedon to end it, and the peace of 346 BCE made Philip the arbiter of central Greece. The league was intact but clearly declining in independence.",
  "Boeotian League|-338": "At the Battle of Chaeronea Philip II defeated Thebes and Athens, and the Sacred Band died almost to a man. A Macedonian garrison occupied the Cadmea, and Thebes lost control of the league, whose other cities were freed from its domination. Boeotia was now subject to Macedonian power.",
  "Boeotian League|-335": "When Thebes revolted on a false report of Alexander’s death, Alexander the Great stormed and destroyed the city, selling its people into slavery. Boeotian cities hostile to Thebes supported the decision. The league survived only as a small federation of lesser towns under Macedonian control.",
  "Boeotian League|-287": "Cassander had rebuilt Thebes in 316 BCE, and by about the 280s BCE it had rejoined the league, which again acted as a regional federal state. Its institutions of boeotarchs, assembly and federal archon functioned, but it remained a second-rank power among the Hellenistic kingdoms and leagues.",
  "Boeotian League|-245": "The Aetolian League defeated the Boeotians near Chaeronea around 245 BCE, and Boeotia was drawn into the Aetolian orbit for a time. The episode showed how weak the league had become, shifting between Macedon, Aetolia and the Achaean League rather than shaping events.",
  "Boeotian League|-192": "Boeotia leaned towards Antiochus III when he invaded Greece in 192 BCE, and after his defeat it was exposed to Roman displeasure. Polybius described the Boeotians of this period as burdened by debt and public feasting. The league continued but carried little weight beside Rome.",
  "Boeotian League|-171": "As war with Perseus of Macedon began, Roman envoys led by Quintus Marcius Philippus persuaded the Boeotian cities to submit to Rome separately. Thebes broke with the pro-Macedonian cities, Haliartus was destroyed, and the league was dissolved, ending Boeotian federal independence.",
  "Roman Republic|-509": "Tradition dates the expulsion of Tarquinius Superbus and the first consuls to 509 BCE. The new Republic was a substantial city in Latium with a treaty with Carthage, but it was soon threatened by Lars Porsena and its Latin neighbours, and its power reached little beyond the lower Tiber valley.",
  "Roman Republic|-493": "After the Battle of Lake Regillus Rome made the foedus Cassianum with the Latin cities, a defensive alliance that pooled their forces against the Aequi and Volsci. In 494 BCE the first secession of the plebs created the tribunate. Rome was a leading Latin state but faced constant border wars.",
  "Roman Republic|-396": "Marcus Furius Camillus captured Veii after a long siege, removing Rome’s main Etruscan rival and roughly doubling its territory. The plunder and new land strengthened the citizen body. Rome was now the strongest power in Latium and southern Etruria, though still a regional state.",
  "Roman Republic|-390": "A Gallic war band defeated the Romans at the Battle of the Allia and sacked the city, around 390 BCE in Roman reckoning or 387 BCE in Greek sources. The blow was severe but brief. Rome rebuilt quickly, raised the Servian Wall and reasserted control over its allies within a few decades.",
  "Roman Republic|-338": "Victory in the Latin War ended the Latin League. Rome annexed some cities, gave others partial citizenship and bound the rest by separate treaties, a flexible settlement that became the model for its control of Italy. With Campania also tied to Rome, it was now the leading power in central Italy.",
  "Roman Republic|-295": "At the Battle of Sentinum Rome defeated a coalition of Samnites, Gauls, Etruscans and Umbrians, and the Third Samnite War ended in 290 BCE. Rome now dominated the Apennines from Etruria to Samnium, with a network of colonies and roads, including the Via Appia, holding the conquered lands together.",
  "Roman Republic|-272": "After Pyrrhus of Epirus withdrew following the Battle of Beneventum, Tarentum surrendered in 272 BCE. Rome controlled the Italian peninsula south of the Po valley through alliances and colonies, giving it manpower that no Mediterranean rival could match. It was now a major power in the western Mediterranean.",
  "Roman Republic|-241": "Rome won the First Punic War after the naval victory at the Aegates Islands. Carthage surrendered Sicily, which became Rome’s first province, and paid a large indemnity; Sardinia and Corsica followed soon after. Having built a navy from almost nothing, Rome was now a leading sea power as well.",
  "Roman Republic|-216": "Hannibal destroyed a huge Roman army at Cannae, after earlier victories at the Trebia and Lake Trasimene, and Capua and much of southern Italy defected. Rome lost perhaps tens of thousands of citizens, but it refused to negotiate and its central Italian alliance held, so its power dipped without collapsing.",
  "Roman Republic|-202": "Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal at the Battle of Zama. Carthage surrendered its fleet and Spain and became a dependent state. Rome now controlled the western Mediterranean, with new provinces in Spain, and turned towards the Hellenistic kingdoms of the East as the strongest military power in the Mediterranean.",
  "Roman Republic|-168": "Lucius Aemilius Paullus defeated Perseus of Macedon at the Battle of Pydna, ending the Antigonid monarchy, after earlier victories over Philip V and Antiochus III. No Mediterranean state could now resist Rome, and plunder from Macedon allowed direct taxation of Roman citizens in Italy to be suspended from 167 BCE.",
  "Roman Republic|-146": "Rome destroyed both Carthage and Corinth in 146 BCE, creating the provinces of Africa and Macedonia. Its hegemony over the Mediterranean was complete. Yet the wealth of conquest was concentrating land and slaves in the hands of the elite, laying the ground for the social conflicts of the next century.",
  "Roman Republic|-133": "Numantia in Spain fell to Scipio Aemilianus and Attalus III bequeathed Pergamon to Rome, which became the province of Asia. Externally Rome stood very strong, but the murder of the tribune Tiberius Gracchus that year opened a century of political violence that would eventually destroy the Republic.",
  "Roman Republic|-101": "Gaius Marius, consul for several years in a row, destroyed the Teutones at Aquae Sextiae in 102 BCE and the Cimbri at Vercellae in 101 BCE, after the disaster of Arausio. His enlistment of landless volunteers saved Italy but created armies whose loyalty went to their generals rather than to the state.",
  "Roman Republic|-88": "The Social War forced Rome to grant citizenship to its Italian allies, while Mithridates VI of Pontus overran Asia and massacred Roman and Italian residents. In 88 BCE Sulla marched his army on Rome. Internal war and the loss of the East left Roman power badly shaken.",
  "Roman Republic|-82": "Sulla returned from the East after defeating Mithridates, won the civil war at the Colline Gate and became dictator. His proscriptions killed perhaps several thousand opponents, and his reforms strengthened the Senate. Roman control of the provinces was restored, though the precedent of a general seizing Rome remained.",
  "Roman Republic|-63": "Pompey ended the Third Mithridatic War, annexed Syria and reorganised the East with a ring of provinces and client kings, greatly increasing Rome’s revenues. In the same year Cicero suppressed the conspiracy of Catiline. Rome’s territory, wealth and prestige stood at their greatest before the final civil wars.",
  "Roman Republic|-49": "Caesar, fresh from conquering Gaul, crossed the Rubicon and civil war engulfed the Mediterranean from Spain to Egypt. Pompey and most of the Senate fled to Greece. Roman armies were now fighting each other rather than foreign enemies, and the government of the Republic broke down.",
  "Roman Republic|-44": "After victories at Pharsalus, Thapsus and Munda, Caesar was dictator for life and planned a great war against Parthia. Rome was again united under one man, but his murder on the Ides of March by senators led by Brutus and Cassius plunged the state back into civil war.",
  "Roman Republic|-31": "Octavian’s fleet under Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium, and Egypt, the last great Hellenistic kingdom, was annexed in 30 BCE. The whole Mediterranean was now under Roman rule and Octavian was the sole master of the Roman world and its many legions.",
  "Roman Republic|-27": "In January 27 BCE Octavian formally handed the state back to the Senate and people and received the name Augustus. The republican institutions survived in name, but real power lay with the princeps. The Republic ended as a political system while its empire passed intact to the Principate.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-480": "After the failed Persian invasion of Greece, Persian power withdrew from Europe and left the Thracian tribes free. Teres I, chief of the Odrysians in the Hebrus valley, began to bring his neighbours under his rule. The kingdom was still a tribal hegemony of uncertain extent, and its beginnings are only roughly dated.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-450": "By the middle of the fifth century Teres had extended Odrysian authority over much of inner Thrace and made marriage alliances abroad, including one with the Scythian king Ariapeithes. His son Sitalces succeeded around the 440s, inheriting a growing kingdom that already drew tribute from neighbouring tribes and traded with the Greek coast.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-431": "At the start of the Peloponnesian War Athens made Sitalces an ally and gave his son Sadocus Athenian citizenship. The Odrysian kingdom now reached from Abdera on the Aegean to the Danube mouth, collecting tribute from Thracian tribes and Greek cities, and was courted as a major power in the north Aegean.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-429": "Sitalces invaded Macedonia with an army that Thucydides says was reported at about 150,000 men, a third of them cavalry. The campaign alarmed Greece as far as Thermopylae, though Sitalces withdrew after about a month without Athenian naval support. It marked the military and territorial peak of the kingdom.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-424": "Sitalces was killed fighting the Triballi and his nephew Seuthes I succeeded. Thucydides records that under Seuthes the yearly tribute reached about 400 talents, with gifts of equal value. The kingdom remained at the height of its wealth, though its unity depended on the king’s personal authority over subordinate princes.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-405": "After Seuthes I, power was divided. Medocus, often identified with Amadocus I, ruled the interior, while Seuthes II held lands towards the Propontis. Athens dealt with both. The kingdom was still large and wealthy, but divided rule weakened its ability to act as a single power.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-400": "Seuthes II hired the survivors of Xenophon’s Ten Thousand to subdue tribes near the Propontis, as Xenophon’s Anabasis describes. The episode shows Odrysian princes still able to field armies and pay mercenaries, but also the dependence of a divided royal house on outside soldiers and alliances.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-384": "Cotys I reunited the Odrysian lands and strengthened the crown, issuing coins and building ties with Athens through the marriage of a daughter to the general Iphicrates. He later fought Athens for the Thracian Chersonese. This was the last period of strong, unified Odrysian rule before Macedonian expansion.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-360": "Cotys I was murdered and his kingdom was divided between Cersobleptes in the east, Amadocus II in the centre and Berisades in the west. Athens and the rising Macedon of Philip II exploited the division, playing the three rulers against each other and weakening Odrysian power.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-342": "In campaigns of 342–340 BCE Philip II of Macedon defeated Cersobleptes, imposed a tithe on the Thracians and founded garrison towns such as Philippopolis and Cabyle. Most of the Odrysian lands came under Macedonian rule, and the kingdom lost its independence, surviving only through local princes.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-323": "After the death of Alexander the Great, Seuthes III fought Lysimachus, the new Macedonian governor of Thrace, in an indecisive battle. Ruling from his new city of Seuthopolis near Kazanlak, he restored a measure of Odrysian autonomy in the Tundzha valley, though Macedonian overlordship remained a constant threat.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-313": "Seuthes III sided with Antigonus against Lysimachus and held the passes of the Haemus, but Lysimachus defeated him. Seuthes kept his throne as a dependent ruler, and Seuthopolis flourished as a royal town, but the Odrysians now lived within the sphere of a Hellenistic king.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-279": "After the death of Lysimachus, Celtic invaders swept through the Balkans and founded the kingdom of Tylis in eastern Thrace. Odrysian authority shrank to scattered princes in inner Thrace, surviving between Celts, Macedonians and the Greek cities, with little ability to act beyond their own districts.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-171": "An Odrysian king named Cotys fought for Perseus of Macedon at the start of the Third Macedonian War against Rome. Macedon’s defeat in 168 BCE left the Thracian princes facing Rome alone. Odrysian rule continued, but only as a minor regional power dependent on stronger neighbours.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|-11": "Roman forces under Lucius Calpurnius Piso crushed a rising of the Bessi, and Augustus supported the Sapaean prince Rhoemetalces I as king of a Thracian client kingdom that claimed the Odrysian inheritance. The realm was larger and more united than for centuries, but existed entirely at Rome’s pleasure.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|19": "Augustus had divided the kingdom between Rhescuporis II and Cotys, son of Rhoemetalces I. In 19 CE Rhescuporis seized and killed Cotys, and Tiberius had him deposed. The quarrel showed how far Thracian kingship now depended on Roman decisions, and the realm was again split.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|26": "A revolt of Thracian tribes against conscription into the Roman army was suppressed by Gaius Poppaeus Sabinus, as Tacitus describes. The client kings sided with Rome against their own subjects, leaving the dynasty with little authority of its own and heavily dependent on Roman military support.",
  "Odrysian Kingdom|46": "After the murder of Rhoemetalces III, the emperor Claudius annexed the kingdom and made it the Roman province of Thracia. Native kingship in Thrace came to an end, about five centuries after Teres I first united the Odrysian tribes, and Thrace was governed by Roman officials.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-438": "Spartocus I took power at Panticapaeum from the Archaeanactids, founding the Spartocid dynasty. The kingdom was still a union of Greek towns around the Kerch Strait, dependent on good relations with the Scythians and with limited control over the native peoples of the hinterland.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-389": "Satyrus I died besieging Theodosia and Leucon I succeeded him. Satyrus had built ties with Athens and extended Bosporan influence over the Sindi, but Theodosia, backed by Heraclea Pontica, still resisted. The kingdom was growing in wealth and territory but had not yet reached its full extent.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-360": "Leucon I captured Theodosia and extended his rule over the Sindi, Maeotians and other peoples of the Kuban, taking the title of archon of the Greek cities and king of the native peoples. Grain exports to Athens, with tax privileges for Athenian buyers, made the kingdom rich and its alliance valuable.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-349": "Paerisades I succeeded Leucon, at first sharing power with his brothers, and Athens honoured the sons of Leucon in 347/346 BCE. The kingdom controlled both shores of the strait and a large native hinterland, and its grain trade with the Aegean stood at its height. This was the peak of Spartocid power.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-310": "The death of Paerisades I led to civil war between his sons. Satyrus II won at the river Thatis but died besieging his brother’s stronghold, and Eumelus took the throne. The war cost lives and resources, but the kingdom remained strong, and Eumelus’ fleet suppressed Black Sea piracy.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-284": "Under Spartocus III and his successors the kingdom remained a wealthy grain exporter, and Athens continued to honour its kings with decrees. Power stood a little below its fourth-century height, as the Hellenistic kingdoms and new grain sources reduced the special importance of the Bosporus to Athens.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-250": "During the third century BCE Sarmatian peoples moved westwards and pressed on the Scythians and the Bosporan hinterland. Some historians argue that competition from Egyptian grain also hurt Bosporan exports. The kingdom survived intact but with reduced income and growing insecurity on its steppe frontiers.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-150": "The revived Scythian kingdom of Scilurus in the Crimea threatened Greek cities on the peninsula, and the Bosporan kings paid subsidies or tribute to steppe neighbours. The Spartocid state continued, but it was clearly weaker than in earlier centuries and increasingly unable to defend itself unaided.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-108": "The childless Paerisades V ceded his kingdom to Mithridates VI of Pontus. A revolt led by the Scythian Saumacus killed Paerisades, but Mithridates’ general Diophantus suppressed it. Spartocid rule ended, and the Bosporus became a province of the Pontic kingdom, valued for its grain, men and money.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-63": "After defeat by Pompey, Mithridates VI retreated to Panticapaeum, planned new wars from there, and died during a revolt led by his son Pharnaces II. Pompey recognised Pharnaces as king of the Bosporus, making the kingdom a Roman client but once again a separate state under its own ruler.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|-47": "Pharnaces II tried to recover his father’s Pontic lands and was defeated by Julius Caesar at Zela. He was then killed fighting his rebel governor Asander, who seized the Bosporus, later ruling with Pharnaces’ daughter Dynamis. Power was low amid usurpation and civil conflict, though Asander restored order and fortified the kingdom.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|14": "Aspurgus, probably the son of Asander and Dynamis, became king around this time and secured Roman recognition, styling himself a friend of the Romans. His dynasty, later bearing the names Tiberius Julius, gave the kingdom new stability and ruled with Roman backing for over three centuries.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|49": "In the war of 45–49 CE Rome deposed Mithridates, son of Aspurgus, after he turned against Roman interests, and installed his brother Cotys I. Roman troops and allied Sarmatians took the town of Uspe. The client kingdom emerged firmly tied to Rome but internally stable and capable of defending its frontiers.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|193": "Sauromates II, who ruled from about 174 to 210 CE, is credited in inscriptions with victories over Scythians, Siraces and Tauri and with protecting shipping. The late second century was a period of relative prosperity, active towns and secure trade, the high point of the Roman-era kingdom.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|255": "Gothic and Boranoi raiders used Bosporan ships to attack the coasts of Asia Minor, apparently after the Bosporan dynasty lost control. The crisis of the Roman Empire left the kingdom exposed, its coinage debased and its trade disrupted. It survived, but as a much weakened client state.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|341": "The last regular Bosporan coins were struck in the early 340s CE. Thereafter the kingdom’s history is almost unknown. It still existed on the edge of the Roman world, but its economy and institutions were in long decline and its rulers had little power beyond the cities of the strait.",
  "Bosporan Kingdom|370": "The arrival of the Huns in the region during the 370s appears to have destroyed what remained of royal authority. Many settlements were damaged or abandoned. The city of Bosporus survived and was later garrisoned by the Byzantine Empire under Justinian I, but the ancient kingdom had ended.",
  "Rhodes|-408": "Ialysos, Kamiros and Lindos, which had broken away from Athens in 411 BCE, joined to found the single city of Rhodes at the northern tip of the island. The new state had good harbours and a growing population, but it was still a middling Greek power within the Spartan sphere.",
  "Rhodes|-378": "Rhodes joined the Second Athenian League, an alliance of Aegean states against Spartan domination. The city was growing as a trading centre, but its foreign policy was tied to Athens, and its power was still that of a prosperous but secondary member of a larger alliance.",
  "Rhodes|-357": "Rhodes, Chios, Kos and Byzantium revolted against Athens in the Social War and won independence by 355 BCE, with support from the Carian dynast Mausolus. Freedom from Athens soon gave way to Carian influence, as an oligarchy friendly to the Hecatomnid rulers took power in the city.",
  "Rhodes|-332": "After Alexander the Great’s victories in Asia Minor and Phoenicia, Rhodes submitted to him and received a Macedonian garrison. The city prospered from the new trade opened by the conquest of Egypt and the east, but its political freedom was limited under Macedonian control.",
  "Rhodes|-323": "On the news of Alexander’s death the Rhodians expelled the Macedonian garrison. Independent again, Rhodes built close ties with Ptolemy I in Egypt and became a centre of the grain trade, developing the policy of neutrality and naval strength that would define it for the next century and a half.",
  "Rhodes|-305": "Demetrius Poliorcetes besieged Rhodes with a great fleet and massive siege engines to force it to abandon its friendship with Ptolemy. The city faced the most powerful siege of its time, and its survival depended on its walls, its navy and supplies from Egypt.",
  "Rhodes|-304": "After about a year the siege ended in a settlement that left Rhodes free and allied with Demetrius except against Ptolemy. The successful defence made Rhodes famous across the Greek world, and the sale of the abandoned siege engines is said to have funded the Colossus.",
  "Rhodes|-280": "Around this time the Colossus of Helios by Chares of Lindos was completed at the harbour. Rhodes was a leading port of the eastern Mediterranean, prospering from Egyptian grain, its wine trade and its harbour dues, and its navy policed the sea against pirates.",
  "Rhodes|-227": "An earthquake around 227/226 BCE ruined much of the city and toppled the Colossus. Kings including Ptolemy III, Seleucus II and Hiero II of Syracuse sent money, grain and materials, showing the standing of Rhodes, which recovered quickly although the statue was never rebuilt.",
  "Rhodes|-220": "Rhodes went to war with Byzantium to force it to abolish tolls on ships passing through the Bosporus, and won. The episode showed Rhodes acting as the protector of free navigation in the eastern Mediterranean, able to impose its will on another major trading city.",
  "Rhodes|-201": "With Pergamon, Rhodes fought Philip V of Macedon at Chios and Lade and appealed to Rome for help. The appeal helped bring about the Second Macedonian War. Rhodes stood among the leading powers of the Aegean, with a respected fleet and wide influence among the islands.",
  "Rhodes|-190": "In the war against Antiochus III, the Rhodian fleet under Eudamus defeated a Seleucid squadron commanded by Hannibal off Side, and Rhodian ships fought beside the Romans at Myonessus. Rhodes was at the height of its naval reputation as a valued ally of Rome.",
  "Rhodes|-188": "The Treaty of Apamea rewarded Rhodes with Lycia and much of Caria south of the Maeander. With a mainland empire, a strong fleet and rich harbour revenues, the island republic reached the peak of its territory, wealth and influence, although the Lycians resisted Rhodian rule.",
  "Rhodes|-167": "After Pydna, Rome punished Rhodes for trying to mediate with Perseus by freeing Lycia and Caria and making Delos a free port. Polybius reports that Rhodian harbour revenues fell from a million drachmas to 150,000. The loss of territory and trade halved Rhodian power within a few years.",
  "Rhodes|-164": "Rhodes obtained a formal treaty of alliance with Rome, which bound it to Roman foreign policy. The city remained rich and influential at sea, fighting a war against Cretan pirates in the 150s BCE, but it no longer acted as an independent great power.",
  "Rhodes|-88": "When Mithridates VI of Pontus overran Asia Minor, Rhodes sheltered Romans and withstood his siege by land and sea. Rome rewarded its loyalty. The episode briefly restored Rhodian prestige, though the city now acted as a loyal Roman ally rather than an independent power.",
  "Rhodes|-43": "After Rhodes refused to support Caesar’s assassins, Gaius Cassius defeated its fleet, captured the city and seized its ships and treasures. The sack ended Rhodes as a naval power. It remained a free city and centre of learning, but with little military or political weight.",
  "Rhodes|44": "Claudius withdrew the freedom of Rhodes after Roman citizens were executed there. Although it was restored in 53 CE, it was lost for good under Vespasian, and Rhodes became part of the Roman provincial system, ending its long history as a self-governing state.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-306": "After Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy\u0027s fleet off Salamis in Cyprus, Antigonus I took the title of king and shared it with his son. The curve starts high because the dynasty began as the leading Successor realm, ruling Asia Minor and Syria, commanding the strongest fleet and controlling Athens, long before it held Macedon.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-302": "Demetrius revived the League of Corinth as a Hellenic alliance under Antigonid leadership and advanced into Thessaly against Cassander, while Antigonus held the lands from the Aegean to Syria. This was the widest reach the family ever achieved, and Cassander, Lysimachus, Seleucus and Ptolemy formed a coalition to stop them.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-301": "At the Battle of Ipsus in Phrygia the allied armies, strengthened by Seleucus\u0027 war elephants, defeated the Antigonids, and Antigonus, then over eighty, was killed. His Asian empire was divided among the victors. Demetrius escaped with his fleet, some Greek cities and coastal bases, a powerful but landless remnant of his father\u0027s realm.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-294": "Exploiting a quarrel between the sons of Cassander, Demetrius had Alexander V killed and was acclaimed king by the Macedonian army. For the first time the family held Macedon itself as well as much of Greece, and Demetrius began building a vast fleet for a renewed attempt to reconquer Asia.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-288": "Demetrius\u0027 heavy demands and grandiose preparations alienated the Macedonians. When Pyrrhus of Epirus and Lysimachus invaded from two sides, his army deserted and he fled. He kept some Greek possessions, but the Antigonids had lost Macedon only six years after winning it, and their power fell sharply.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-285": "After a failed campaign in Asia Minor, Demetrius surrendered to Seleucus I and died in captivity in 283 BCE. His son Antigonus Gonatas held only a fleet and a chain of Greek fortresses, including Corinth and Demetrias. This was the lowest point of the dynasty before its recovery under Gonatas.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-277": "Celtic invaders had killed King Ptolemy Keraunos in 279 BCE and plunged Macedon into anarchy. Gonatas lured a Gallic army into an ambush near Lysimachia in Thrace and destroyed it, a victory that gave him the prestige to claim the Macedonian throne, which he secured over the following year.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-272": "Pyrrhus of Epirus invaded in 274 BCE and drove Gonatas from much of Macedon, but he turned south into the Peloponnese and was killed in street fighting at Argos in 272 BCE. Gonatas recovered his kingdom, strengthened his position in Greece and ruled a stable state from Pella.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-261": "The Chremonidean War, in which Athens and Sparta, backed by Ptolemy II, tried to break Macedonian control, ended with the surrender of Athens after a long siege, probably in 262 or 261 BCE. Gonatas garrisoned Athens and its harbours, and Macedonian dominance of southern Greece reached a new high.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-245": "Gonatas recovered Corinth by a ruse from Nicaea, widow of his rebellious nephew Alexander, and Antigonid fleets had defeated the Ptolemies off Cos and probably off Andros, though both dates are debated. Macedon was the leading power in mainland Greece and the Aegean, with garrisons in the main fortresses.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-243": "In a night attack Aratus of Sicyon seized Acrocorinth for the Achaean League and expelled its Macedonian garrison. Losing the strongest of the ‘fetters of Greece’ was a heavy blow, and the Achaean League began to absorb the cities of the Peloponnese at Macedonian expense.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-229": "Demetrius II died after a defeat by the Dardanians, leaving the boy Philip V. Thessaly wavered, Athens paid off its Macedonian garrison and regained its freedom, and the Aetolians pressed on Macedonian allies. Antigonus Doson took over as guardian in one of the kingdom\u0027s most dangerous crises.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-224": "Desperate in the war against Cleomenes III of Sparta, the Achaean League under Aratus turned to Doson, who received Acrocorinth back as the price of his help. He organised a Hellenic alliance of leagues under his leadership, restoring Macedonian supremacy in Greece by diplomacy as well as garrisons.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-222": "At the Battle of Sellasia Doson\u0027s army of Macedonians and allies defeated Cleomenes III, who fled to Egypt, and Doson entered Sparta, a city never before taken by a foreign army. Macedon stood once again as the unchallenged master of mainland Greece, its strongest position since Gonatas.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-217": "Philip V, king since 221 BCE, led the Hellenic alliance to victory over the Aetolian League in the Social War, concluded by the Peace of Naupactus in 217 BCE. The young king was energetic and popular, and Macedon\u0027s position in Greece was secure, though his ambitions were turning towards the Adriatic.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-215": "After Rome\u0027s defeat at Cannae, Philip V made a treaty of alliance with Hannibal, and the First Macedonian War with Rome and its Aetolian allies followed from 214 BCE. Macedon was still strong, but the alliance made Rome a lasting enemy and drew Roman power into Greek affairs.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-205": "The Peace of Phoenice ended the First Macedonian War on terms broadly favourable to Philip, who kept most of his gains in Illyria. Rome had been distracted by Hannibal and Macedon lost little territory, but the war had shown how readily Greek enemies of Macedon would turn to Rome.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-197": "At the Battle of Cynoscephalae in Thessaly, Flamininus\u0027 legions routed Philip V\u0027s phalanx. The peace of 196 BCE stripped Macedon of its Greek possessions, including the ‘fetters’, most of its fleet and 1,000 talents. The kingdom was reduced to Macedon proper and became a second-rank power watched by Rome.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-179": "Philip V had spent his last years rebuilding revenues, mines and population, and Perseus inherited a wealthy kingdom with a large army. His diplomacy won sympathy among Greeks discontented with Rome, a partial recovery that alarmed Eumenes II of Pergamon and the Roman Senate.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-171": "Rome declared war on Perseus. He won a cavalry victory at Callinicus in Thessaly in 171 BCE and held the passes into Macedon for three years, but he failed to win the allies he needed while Roman commanders gradually tightened their grip. Antigonid strength was real but isolated.",
  "Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia|-168": "Lucius Aemilius Paullus destroyed Perseus\u0027 army at the Battle of Pydna in June 168 BCE. Perseus surrendered on Samothrace and died in Roman captivity. In 167 BCE Rome abolished the Antigonid monarchy and divided Macedon into four republics, ending the dynasty and the kingdom\u0027s independence.",
  "Sarmatians|-300": "Sarmatian groups from the lower Volga and southern Urals, heirs of Herodotus\u0027 Sauromatae, were moving west towards the Don. Still a collection of pastoral tribes on the eastern edge of the Scythian world, they had begun to raid across the river, but the Scythians remained the dominant power on the Pontic steppe.",
  "Sarmatians|-250": "During the third century BCE Sarmatian tribes crossed the Don in force and, as Diodorus records, laid waste much of Scythia. The Scythian kingdom fell back towards the Crimea, and the open steppe between the Don and the Dnieper passed to Sarmatian chiefs. The exact dates of this advance are uncertain.",
  "Sarmatians|-179": "Polybius names the Sarmatian ruler Gatalos among the parties to the treaty that ended the war of Pharnaces I of Pontus, showing that Sarmatian kings now counted in the diplomacy of the Black Sea. Their tribes held the steppe north of the sea, pressing on the Greek cities and the Crimean Scythians.",
  "Sarmatians|-107": "The Roxolani under Tasius fought for the Scythian king Palacus against Diophantus, general of Mithridates VI of Pontus, and Strabo reports that their large army was routed by a much smaller Pontic force. The defeat showed the limits of loosely organised nomad armies against disciplined Hellenistic infantry.",
  "Sarmatians|-60": "By the mid-first century BCE the Aorsi and Siraces dominated the steppe between the Don, the Caucasus and the Caspian. Strabo says Spadines of the Aorsi could field 200,000 horsemen, surely an exaggeration, and that the Aorsi carried Indian and Babylonian goods by camel. Other Sarmatian groups pressed towards the lower Danube.",
  "Sarmatians|20": "In the early first century CE the Iazyges probably moved into the plain between the Danube and the Tisza, while the Roxolani settled north of the lower Danube. Sarmatian peoples now stretched from the Volga to the edge of the Roman provinces, and the exiled poet Ovid complained at Tomis of their raids across the frozen Danube.",
  "Sarmatians|49": "In the Bosporan war of 49 CE Rome and Eunones, king of the Aorsi, defeated the deposed Bosporan king Mithridates and his ally Zorsines of the Siraces, as Tacitus describes. Sarmatian kings were now partners and clients in Roman frontier diplomacy, and their cavalry decided the balance of power north of the Black Sea.",
  "Sarmatians|69": "During the Roman civil wars some 9,000 Roxolani horsemen raided Moesia but were cut down by the Legio III Gallica on ground softened by a thaw. The defeat was costly, yet Sarmatian pressure on the Danube continued, and in the east the rising Alans were becoming the strongest of the steppe peoples.",
  "Sarmatians|92": "Under Domitian a Sarmatian war, fought chiefly against the Iazyges and their Suebian allies, cost Rome an entire legion together with its commander. The Sarmatians were near the height of their military power on the middle Danube, able to destroy regular Roman forces and to draw emperors into costly campaigns.",
  "Sarmatians|135": "The Alans, now the leading Sarmatian people of the eastern steppe, raided through the Caucasus into Albania, Media and Armenia and threatened Cappadocia, where the governor Arrian marched against them. Sarmatian peoples controlled the whole steppe from the Volga to the Danube, the greatest reach and influence they ever achieved.",
  "Sarmatians|175": "After years of fighting in the Marcomannic Wars, the Iazyges under King Zanticus made peace with Marcus Aurelius, returning reportedly 100,000 captives and providing 8,000 cavalry, of whom 5,500 were sent to Britain. The western Sarmatians were checked and tied to Rome, though the eastern groups remained strong.",
  "Sarmatians|238": "Goths and other peoples from the north-west reached the Black Sea in the early third century and joined raids on the Roman lower Danube, including the sack of Histria around 238. The Sarmatians of the Pontic steppe began to lose their dominance to these newcomers, sometimes as their allies and sometimes as their subjects.",
  "Sarmatians|270": "By the later third century the Goths controlled much of the Pontic steppe and forest-steppe, and many Sarmatians were absorbed into the mixed Chernyakhov culture. Sarmatian groups survived on the Danube and further east, where the Alans remained independent, but the confederation no longer dominated the region it had ruled for centuries.",
  "Sarmatians|334": "Constantine aided the Danubian Sarmatians against the Goths in 332, but in 334 their subject Limigantes rose and drove out their masters. Constantine admitted many of the refugees into the empire, reportedly 300,000. The Sarmatians of the Hungarian Plain survived only as Roman clients, divided against themselves.",
  "Sarmatians|358": "Constantius II campaigned against the Sarmatians and Limigantes across the middle Danube in 358 and 359, installing a client king and taking the title Sarmaticus. The western Sarmatians were reduced to dependent tribes on the Roman frontier, while the Alans on the Don faced a new threat from the east.",
  "Sarmatians|375": "In the 370s the Huns crossed the Volga, defeated the Alans on the Don and overthrew the Gothic realm of Ermanaric around 375. Sarmatian groups were absorbed by the Huns, fled into Roman lands or joined the westward migration of the Alans, ending the Sarmatians\u0027 long dominance of the Pontic steppe.",
  "Achaean League|-280": "Four small cities of western Achaea, Dyme, Patrae, Tritaia and Pharai, renewed the old Achaean League while the Macedonian kingdoms were in turmoil after the deaths of Lysimachus and Seleucus I. The new league was tiny and poor, a defensive association of farming towns on the edge of the Greek world.",
  "Achaean League|-275": "Aigion expelled its Macedonian garrison and joined the league, followed by Bura and Keryneia, whose tyrant Iseas stepped down. With Aigion came the federal sanctuary of Zeus Homarios, and the league again included most of the old Achaean cities, though it remained a minor regional power.",
  "Achaean League|-255": "The league reformed its leadership, replacing two generals with a single annual strategos, the first being Margus of Keryneia. The change gave the federation a clearer command structure, but it still consisted only of the Achaean towns and had little influence beyond the southern shore of the Gulf of Corinth.",
  "Achaean League|-251": "Aratus, a young exile, overthrew the tyrant Nicocles of Sicyon and brought his city into the league, its first non-Achaean member. Sicyon was wealthy and well placed near the Isthmus, and Aratus\u0027 energy and diplomacy, backed by subsidies from Ptolemy II, turned the league into an expanding force.",
  "Achaean League|-243": "Aratus captured Acrocorinth from its Macedonian garrison in a daring night attack, and Corinth, Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus joined the league. Controlling the Isthmus, the league became the leading opponent of Macedon in southern Greece and named Ptolemy III its honorary commander by land and sea.",
  "Achaean League|-235": "Lydiadas, tyrant of Megalopolis, voluntarily laid down his power and brought the great Arcadian city into the league, where he soon became Aratus\u0027 rival for the generalship. With Megalopolis the federation reached deep into Arcadia and bordered Sparta, setting the stage for future conflict.",
  "Achaean League|-229": "After the death of Demetrius II of Macedon, Argos, Hermione and Phlius joined the league. The federation now held most of the northern and central Peloponnese and stood at the height of Aratus\u0027 independent power, but its expansion alarmed Sparta, where Cleomenes III was preparing to challenge it.",
  "Achaean League|-226": "Cleomenes III of Sparta defeated the league\u0027s armies in successive battles, including at the Hekatombaion near Dyme. His promises of debt cancellation and land redistribution won support in several member cities, and Argos and others defected. The league seemed close to collapse, forcing Aratus to seek help from Macedon.",
  "Achaean League|-224": "Aratus surrendered Acrocorinth to Antigonus III Doson in return for Macedonian intervention. The league became part of Doson\u0027s Hellenic alliance, regaining security at the cost of independence. Corinth, the prize of 243 BCE, was again a Macedonian fortress, and Achaean policy now followed the Macedonian king.",
  "Achaean League|-222": "Doson\u0027s victory over Cleomenes at the Battle of Sellasia, with Achaean contingents including the young Philopoemen, ended the Spartan threat. The league recovered its lost cities, Mantinea having been sacked and refounded as Antigoneia the year before, but it remained a junior partner of Macedon.",
  "Achaean League|-217": "The Social War against the Aetolian League, fought alongside Philip V, ended with the Peace of Naupactus. Aetolian raids had exposed the weakness of Achaean forces, and dependence on Macedon deepened. Aratus, increasingly at odds with Philip, died a few years later in 213 BCE.",
  "Achaean League|-207": "Philopoemen, general from 208 BCE, had reformed the army into a Macedonian-style phalanx and retrained the cavalry. At the Battle of Mantinea he defeated and killed the Spartan ruler Machanidas, giving the league its greatest military victory and the confidence to act as an independent power once more.",
  "Achaean League|-198": "During the Second Macedonian War the league\u0027s assembly at Sicyon voted, after bitter debate, to abandon Philip V and join Rome. The decision put the league on the winning side; after Cynoscephalae it regained Corinth and became Rome\u0027s main partner in the Peloponnese.",
  "Achaean League|-192": "After Nabis of Sparta was assassinated by the Aetolians, Philopoemen hurried to Sparta and brought it into the league. For the first time Achaea\u0027s historic enemy was a member of the federation, a triumph for Philopoemen, though Spartan loyalty was doubtful from the start.",
  "Achaean League|-191": "With Messene and Elis joining, the Achaean League controlled the entire Peloponnese, the first time a single Greek government had achieved this. Backed by Rome and led by Philopoemen, it was at the height of its territory, prestige and military strength, the strongest power in southern Greece.",
  "Achaean League|-183": "Messene rebelled, and Philopoemen, now about seventy, was captured and forced to drink poison. Lycortas recovered Messene the next year, but repeated Spartan and Messenian appeals to the Senate showed how readily Rome could interfere in the league\u0027s affairs and limit its independence.",
  "Achaean League|-167": "After Rome\u0027s victory at Pydna, the pro-Roman leader Callicrates denounced his rivals, and about 1,000 leading Achaeans, Polybius among them, were deported to Italy without trial. The league survived intact, but its political class was purged and its policy now followed Roman wishes.",
  "Achaean League|-150": "The surviving Achaean hostages, about 300 men, were allowed to return home after seventeen years. Their bitterness, renewed quarrels with Sparta and economic strains fed anti-Roman feeling, and leaders such as Diaeus and Critolaus gained support for a firmer stand against Roman demands.",
  "Achaean League|-146": "When Rome demanded that Sparta, Corinth, Argos and other cities leave the league, the Achaeans went to war. Metellus defeated Critolaus at Scarpheia and Mummius crushed Diaeus on the Isthmus, then sacked Corinth. The league was dissolved, ending the last major independent power of mainland Greece.",
  "Lusitania|-27": "Augustus took charge of the Iberian provinces and began dividing Hispania Ulterior, separating the western lands of the Lusitanians and their neighbours from Baetica. The new imperial province, recently pacified and lightly urbanised, had few Roman towns and probably still included the unconquered north-west, where the Cantabrian Wars were under way.",
  "Lusitania|-25": "The legate Publius Carisius founded Emerita Augusta on the Guadiana for veterans discharged after the Cantabrian campaigns. The colony, planned on a grand scale with a long bridge over the river, became the capital of Lusitania and the main instrument for spreading Roman urban life in the west of the peninsula.",
  "Lusitania|-16": "During Augustus\u0027 second stay in Hispania (16–13 BCE) the provinces were probably given their final boundaries, with Gallaecia and Asturia passing to Tarraconensis. At Emerita the theatre was dedicated under Agrippa, and colonies and towns such as Pax Iulia and Olisipo anchored the organised province between the Douro and the Algarve.",
  "Lusitania|58": "Nero sent Marcus Salvius Otho to govern Lusitania, removing a rival for Poppaea Sabina. Otho\u0027s decade of competent rule coincided with the steady growth of towns, roads and mining. When Galba revolted in neighbouring Tarraconensis in 68, Otho joined him, and the governor of Lusitania briefly became emperor in 69.",
  "Lusitania|74": "Vespasian granted Latin rights to all the communities of Hispania around 73–74, allowing local magistrates to become Roman citizens. Many Lusitanian towns were reorganised as municipalities with Roman-style constitutions, forums and public buildings, deepening Romanisation in the interior and strengthening the province\u0027s urban network.",
  "Lusitania|106": "Under Trajan, the first emperor from Hispania, Lusitania flourished. The great bridge at Alcántara over the Tagus, paid for by Lusitanian communities, was completed around 104–106, and the province\u0027s mines, estates and fisheries supplied markets across the empire. Towns enlarged their forums and temples in a long period of prosperity and peace.",
  "Lusitania|138": "By the end of Hadrian\u0027s reign Lusitania was at its height. Imperial regulations inscribed at Vipasca organised one of its major mining districts, Emerita was a wealthy city of monuments, and fish-salting at Tróia and other coastal sites exported across the Mediterranean. The province enjoyed security, growth and full integration into the empire.",
  "Lusitania|212": "The Constitutio Antoniniana of Caracalla made all free inhabitants Roman citizens. Lusitania remained prosperous, though the Moorish raids of the 170s on southern Hispania had shown that its security could not be taken for granted. Its towns and villas continued to grow, with Emerita among the leading cities of the West.",
  "Lusitania|260": "During the third-century crisis invasions and civil wars disrupted the empire, and Frankish raiders reached Tarraconensis. Lusitania escaped major invasion, but trade weakened, inflation eroded wealth, and in the following decades towns such as Conimbriga built tighter defensive walls, a sign of reduced security and confidence.",
  "Lusitania|300": "Diocletian\u0027s reforms made Lusitania one of the provinces of the Diocese of the Spains, and Emerita probably became the seat of the diocesan vicar. In 304, during the Great Persecution, the young Christian Eulalia was martyred at Emerita. The province entered the fourth century with renewed administrative importance.",
  "Lusitania|337": "Under Constantine and his sons Emerita was the leading city of Roman Iberia; its circus was restored around 337–340, and large villas with fine mosaics flourished in the countryside. The church grew, and the bishops of Emerita and Ossonoba later took leading parts in Hispanic church affairs, including the Priscillianist controversy.",
  "Lusitania|409": "Vandals, Alans and Suebi crossed the Pyrenees in the autumn of 409 and spent two years plundering the Iberian provinces. Roman forces in Hispania were weakened by the civil wars around the usurper Constantine III, and Lusitania\u0027s towns, lacking garrisons, were left to defend themselves behind their walls.",
  "Lusitania|411": "The invaders divided Hispania by lot, and the Alans took Lusitania and Carthaginiensis. Roman administration survived in some towns, but the province was effectively under barbarian occupation, its countryside exposed to plunder and its links with the rest of the empire disrupted.",
  "Lusitania|418": "Visigoths under Wallia, fighting for Rome, defeated the Alans and killed their king Addax; the survivors joined the Hasding Vandals. Roman authority was restored in much of Lusitania, though the Vandals and Suebi remained strong elsewhere in Hispania and the imperial government had few forces of its own there.",
  "Lusitania|429": "As the Vandals under Gaiseric prepared to leave for Africa, they defeated the Suebic king Hermigar, who had been plundering the region, near Emerita, and Hermigar drowned in the Guadiana. The Vandal departure left the Suebi as the main barbarian power in the west, while Roman control of the province remained precarious.",
  "Lusitania|439": "The Suebic king Rechila entered Emerita, the provincial capital, and in the following year forced the Roman commander Censorius to surrender at Myrtilis. Imperial rule over Lusitania effectively ended, and the province passed under Suebic and later Visigothic control, though its name survived as a church province.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|-27": "Augustus took Hispania Citerior into his own provinces and prepared the conquest of the north. The province already held the Roman cities of the Mediterranean coast and the Ebro valley, but the Cantabri and Astures remained unconquered, and Augustus himself would soon campaign against them and winter at Tarraco.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|-19": "Agrippa ended the Cantabrian Wars, completing the conquest of the peninsula after two centuries. The north-western lands, with their rich gold deposits, were attached to the province, probably by 13 BCE, creating one of the largest provinces of the empire, from the Pyrenees to the Atlantic coast of Galicia.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|15": "Tiberius permitted the province to build a temple to the deified Augustus at Tarraco, an example soon followed elsewhere. Roman colonies and towns such as Caesaraugusta, Barcino and Asturica Augusta were growing, roads were extending inland, and the gold mines of the north-west were coming into large-scale production.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|68": "The governor Servius Sulpicius Galba rebelled against Nero and was proclaimed emperor at Carthago Nova, raising a new legion in the province. His brief reign showed the province\u0027s political weight, though the civil wars of 68–69 caused disruption and drew its forces into conflicts far from Hispania.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|74": "Vespasian granted Latin rights to the whole of Hispania and stationed the Legio VII Gemina in the north-west at the camp that became León. Hundreds of communities became self-governing municipalities, and the monumental rebuilding of upper Tarraco as a provincial centre went ahead under the Flavian emperors.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|122": "Hadrian spent the winter of 122–123 at Tarraco, restored the temple of Augustus and held an assembly of the Hispanic provinces. The province was at its height: wealthy from wine, oil, metals and trade, densely urbanised and fully integrated into imperial administration and Latin culture.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|170": "Under the Antonine emperors the province remained prosperous, its cities embellished with forums, theatres and baths. Yet the Antonine Plague and Moorish raids on southern Hispania in the 170s were early signs of strain, and the gold mines of the north-west were probably already past their peak output.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|214": "Caracalla briefly separated the north-west as a new province, Hispania Nova Citerior Antoniniana, and his edict of 212 had made all free inhabitants Roman citizens. The province remained wealthy and stable, but the army and administration were being reorganised in ways that foreshadowed later division.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|260": "During the third-century crisis Frankish raiders crossed Gaul and, according to later writers, reached and damaged Tarraco, and Hispania for a time recognised the Gallic emperor Postumus. Insecurity, inflation and disrupted trade led many towns to build strong walls in the following decades, as at Lugo, León and Barcelona.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|293": "Diocletian\u0027s reforms split the old province into three: Gallaecia in the north-west, Carthaginiensis in the south and centre, and a much smaller Tarraconensis in the Ebro valley and the north-east. Tarraco remained a provincial capital, but its governor now ruled only a fraction of the former territory.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|350": "In the fourth century the reduced province enjoyed renewed stability. Large villas flourished in the countryside, among them Centcelles near Tarraco, whose domed hall with mosaics may have been a mausoleum, possibly for the emperor Constans, who died in 350. Tarraco became a leading Christian centre under its metropolitan bishops.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|409": "Vandals, Alans and Suebi crossed the Pyrenees in the autumn of 409, amid the civil war between the usurper Constantine III and his rebellious general Gerontius in Hispania. The invaders settled in the west and south, while Tarraconensis remained in Roman hands, though now isolated and exposed.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|418": "Visigoths under Wallia, fighting as Roman allies, destroyed the Siling Vandals and many Alans before being settled in Aquitaine. Tarraconensis was secure as Rome\u0027s base in Hispania and imperial authority there was restored, but the province depended on Gothic and imperial armies from Gaul for its defence.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|441": "The Bagaudae, rebels against the burdens of late Roman society, rose in Tarraconensis. Asturius and then his son-in-law Merobaudes were sent to suppress them, with some success, but the revolts showed that the province\u0027s social order and Roman control were breaking down even without foreign invasion.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|449": "The Bagaudic leader Basilius killed federate soldiers and the bishop of Tarazona, then joined the Suebic king Rechiar in plundering the lands around Caesaraugusta and seizing Ilerda by trickery. Roman authority in the province had become fragile, dependent on walled cities and help from outside.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|456": "The Visigothic king Theodoric II, acting for the emperor Avitus, destroyed the Suebic army near Astorga and later executed Rechiar. Tarraconensis was still nominally Roman, but real power in Hispania now lay with the Visigoths, and after Avitus\u0027 fall the Western emperors could no longer protect the province.",
  "Hispania Tarraconensis|472": "King Euric of the Visigoths, breaking with Rome, sent armies across the Pyrenees that took Pamplona and Caesaraugusta and then Tarraco and the coastal cities, probably around 472–475. The last Roman province in Iberia passed to the Visigothic Kingdom, ending nearly seven centuries of Roman rule.",
  "Roman Britain|43": "Claudius sent Aulus Plautius with four legions and entered Camulodunum in person, receiving the surrender of eleven kings. Roman control covered only the south-east and a ring of client kingdoms, so the new province was still small, insecure and dependent on the goodwill of allied British rulers.",
  "Roman Britain|51": "The Romans held a frontier from Exeter to Lincoln and pushed into Wales. Caratacus, the leader of British resistance, was defeated and then handed over by Cartimandua of the Brigantes. A veteran colony now stood at Camulodunum, and the province was more secure, though the western uplands remained unconquered.",
  "Roman Britain|61": "Boudica\u0027s revolt destroyed Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium and killed many thousands before Suetonius Paulinus defeated her. The province survived, but its towns lay in ruins, the Iceni and Trinovantes were punished harshly, and Rome briefly weighed the cost of holding Britain at all.",
  "Roman Britain|84": "Agricola completed the conquest of Wales and northern England and defeated the Caledonians at Mons Graupius in 83 or 84. Roman forts reached far into Scotland and the towns of the south were being rebuilt with fora and baths, giving the province its widest reach so far.",
  "Roman Britain|122": "Hadrian visited Britain and ordered the wall from the Tyne to the Solway. The army had withdrawn from Scotland, but the frontier was now fixed and fortified, three legions held permanent fortresses, and towns and villas spread in a stable, increasingly prosperous lowland zone.",
  "Roman Britain|142": "Under Antoninus Pius the governor Quintus Lollius Urbicus advanced again and built the Antonine Wall between the Forth and the Clyde. The province reached its greatest northward extent, though the new wall was held for only about twenty years before the army fell back to Hadrian\u0027s Wall.",
  "Roman Britain|211": "Septimius Severus campaigned against the Caledonians and Maeatae from 208 and died at York in 211. His sons made peace and the northern frontier then stayed quiet for most of the 3rd century. The province was divided in two, reducing the power of any one governor.",
  "Roman Britain|260": "During the crisis of the 3rd century Britain joined the breakaway Gallic Empire of Postumus and his successors until 274. The province itself was spared the worst invasions, but it was cut off from the central government and drawn into the empire\u0027s civil conflicts.",
  "Roman Britain|286": "Carausius, commander of the fleet against Frankish and Saxon pirates, declared himself emperor and ruled Britain independently. He was murdered by his minister Allectus in 293. Separation from Rome and growing seaborne raids show the province\u0027s weakness, though its economy remained sound.",
  "Roman Britain|296": "Constantius Chlorus recovered Britain after his praetorian prefect Asclepiodotus defeated Allectus. Diocletian\u0027s reforms divided the island into four provinces under a vicarius, and the Saxon Shore forts were strengthened. Restored to the empire, the province entered its most prosperous period.",
  "Roman Britain|306": "Constantius died at York and his army proclaimed his son Constantine emperor there. Britain\u0027s armies thus launched one of the most important reigns in Roman history, and the province shared the stability and growing Christianity of the Constantinian empire. Its bishops attended the Council of Arles in 314.",
  "Roman Britain|330": "The early 4th century was the high point of rural Britain: the largest villas, such as Chedworth and Bignor, were enlarged with fine mosaics, grain was exported to the Rhine armies, and Christianity spread among the elite. Defences held, and the province was rich and secure.",
  "Roman Britain|353": "After the usurper Magnentius was defeated, the imperial agent Paul \u0027the Chain\u0027 carried out harsh purges of his supporters in Britain. Prosperity continued, but raids by Picts and Scotti grew, and in 360 Julian had to send troops to deal with them.",
  "Roman Britain|367": "The so-called barbarian conspiracy of Picts, Scotti, Attacotti and Saxons overran the province, killing the Count of the Saxon Shore. Count Theodosius restored order in 368–369, but the crisis revealed how thinly the province was now defended and how vulnerable it was to raids from several directions at once.",
  "Roman Britain|383": "Magnus Maximus, a commander in Britain, was proclaimed emperor and took troops to Gaul. He was defeated and killed in 388, and the garrison he removed was probably never fully replaced. Towns were shrinking, public buildings decaying and the money economy weakening, even though many villas still flourished.",
  "Roman Britain|407": "After several short-lived usurpers, the army in Britain proclaimed Constantine III, who crossed to Gaul with the remaining field forces. New coinage had ceased to arrive, and the province was left with little defence against raiders. Constantine III\u0027s struggle for the West drew Britain into a civil war it could not influence.",
  "Roman Britain|410": "Around 409 the Britons expelled Roman officials, and in 410 Honorius reportedly told the cities of Britain to defend themselves. Roman rule ended without formal withdrawal, and within a generation towns and villas were largely abandoned as the island split into successor kingdoms.",
  "Alemanni|213": "Caracalla campaigned on the Main frontier, and Cassius Dio names the Alemanni among his enemies, though the reference may be a later insertion. The confederation was only beginning to form from Elbe Germanic newcomers and local groups, so it was a loose and modest threat to the well-defended Roman Limes.",
  "Alemanni|233": "Germanic raiders, probably Alemanni, crossed the Limes into the Agri Decumates and Raetia while Severus Alexander was fighting Persia, destroying forts and villas. The emperor came to Mainz to fight them but was murdered by his troops in 235. The raiders were growing bolder, but they still held no Roman land.",
  "Alemanni|260": "Amid the crisis of the 3rd century Rome abandoned the Upper German and Raetian Limes, and Alemannic bands raided deep into Gaul and as far as Italy, where Gallienus defeated a great army near Milan. The Agri Decumates lay open for settlement, giving the Alemanni a new homeland east of the Rhine.",
  "Alemanni|268": "Claudius II defeated a large Alemannic army near Lake Garda. The confederation continued to settle the former Roman lands between the Rhine and the Danube, but the defeat showed that raids into Italy were costly, and Alemannic power remained that of many separate war bands rather than a united state.",
  "Alemanni|271": "Juthungi and Alemanni invaded Italy, defeated Aurelian near Placentia and caused alarm in Rome, which began its great new walls. Aurelian then destroyed the invaders at Fano and Pavia. The raid marked the height of Alemannic aggression in the 3rd century, though it ended in heavy losses.",
  "Alemanni|298": "Constantius Chlorus defeated Alemannic armies near Langres and at Vindonissa in the late 290s, and the Tetrarchs rebuilt the frontier along the Rhine, the Iller and the Danube with new forts. The Alemanni kept their new lands, but for half a century they were contained and largely bound by treaties.",
  "Alemanni|352": "During the civil war between Magnentius and Constantius II, who reportedly encouraged them, Chnodomar defeated Magnentius’s brother Decentius and Alemannic kings seized a wide strip west of the Rhine, sacking towns across Alsace and beyond. It was the largest Alemannic seizure of Roman land in the 4th century.",
  "Alemanni|357": "At the Battle of Strasbourg Julian’s army of about 13,000 defeated Chnodomar’s coalition, which Ammianus Marcellinus put at 35,000 men. Chnodomar was captured and sent to Rome, where he died. Julian followed up with campaigns across the Rhine, and the Alemanni lost their conquests on the west bank.",
  "Alemanni|361": "Julian’s agents arrested King Vadomarius of the Breisgau at a dinner, suspecting him of plotting with Constantius II, and sent him to Spain; he later served the empire in the East. Roman campaigns across the Rhine had forced several kings to return captives and supply materials for rebuilding forts, leaving the Alemanni divided and on the defensive.",
  "Alemanni|368": "Exploiting the emperor’s absence, the king Rando surprised Mainz during a Christian festival and carried off its people. Valentinian I answered with a major invasion and a costly victory at Solicinium. The Alemanni could still strike hard, but Rome rebuilt the frontier with a new chain of forts and watchtowers.",
  "Alemanni|374": "Unable to capture Macrianus of the Bucinobantes, Valentinian I met him on the Rhine near Mainz and made peace. Macrianus remained a loyal ally until he was killed fighting the Franks. Rome’s acceptance of a powerful king by treaty shows the confederation’s strength, though its kings still acted separately.",
  "Alemanni|378": "The Lentienses crossed the frozen Rhine but were crushed near Argentaria in Alsace by Gratian’s generals Nannienus and the Frankish king Mallobaudes, and their king Priarius was killed. Gratian then crossed into their lands and forced their submission. It was among the last great Roman victories on the upper Rhine.",
  "Alemanni|406": "In the winter of 406–407 Vandals, Alans and Suebi crossed the Rhine, and the western frontier collapsed. Alemannic groups probably took part in or profited from the upheaval, and in the following decades they spread across the river into Alsace and the Palatinate as Roman control of the frontier weakened.",
  "Alemanni|455": "The murders of Aetius in 454 and Valentinian III in 455 removed the last strong Roman authority in the north-west. Alemannic settlement spread across Alsace and into northern Switzerland, and in 457 a band of some 900 Alemanni crossed the Alps into Italy before Majorian’s troops destroyed it.",
  "Alemanni|476": "When the last western emperor was deposed, the Alemanni held a wide territory reaching roughly from the Vosges to the Lech and from the Main to the Alps. Without Roman opposition they were free to expand, but they now bordered the rising Frankish kingdoms to the north and west and the Burgundians to the south-west.",
  "Alemanni|496": "Around this date, traditionally at Tolbiac near Cologne, the Franks under Clovis I defeated the Alemanni and killed their king. Gregory of Tours made the battle the occasion of Clovis’s vow to become a Christian. Many historians date the decisive defeat later, but Alemannic expansion to the north was halted.",
  "Alemanni|506": "A second, decisive Frankish victory, probably around 506, broke Alemannic independence. Theodoric the Great asked Clovis to spare refugees who had fled into Ostrogothic territory, and the northern Alemanni came under Frankish rule. By 536–537 all of them were subjects of the Merovingian kings, organised as the Duchy of Alamannia.",
  "Franks|250": "Frankish groups appear on the lower Rhine as a new confederation formed from older frontier peoples such as the Chamavi, Bructeri and Chattuarii. Roman forces still held the Rhine, and the Franks were only one of several small groupings beyond it, so their power was modest and mostly local.",
  "Franks|260": "During the crisis of the 3rd century Frankish bands broke into Gaul, and some reportedly reached Spain and sacked Tarraco. Postumus, ruler of the breakaway Gallic Empire, fought them on the Rhine. The Franks were now a recognised danger, though they raided for plunder rather than for land.",
  "Franks|288": "Maximian’s campaigns restored the Rhine frontier, and the Frankish king Gennobaudes submitted, receiving his kingdom back as a Roman client. Captured Franks were settled in Gaul as laeti. Frankish pirates remained active in the Channel, but on land the confederation was contained by revived Roman power.",
  "Franks|306": "Constantine the Great, newly proclaimed in Britain, defeated the Franks and had their kings Ascaric and Merogaisus thrown to wild beasts in the amphitheatre at Trier. He soon built a bridge over the Rhine at Cologne and a fort at Deutz. Frankish power stood at a low point under his firm frontier policy.",
  "Franks|342": "Constans campaigned against the Franks in 341–342 and imposed a treaty on them. Frankish groups had recovered from Constantine’s campaigns and were pressing on the frontier again, while growing numbers of Franks served in the Roman army, where some rose to senior commands.",
  "Franks|355": "The Frankish-born general Silvanus was proclaimed emperor at Cologne and murdered within a month, and soon afterwards Franks sacked Cologne itself. With the frontier in disorder after the civil war against Magnentius, Frankish groups occupied land west of the lower Rhine.",
  "Franks|358": "Julian, having recovered Cologne in 356, attacked the Franks. He allowed the Salian Franks to remain in Toxandria as subjects of Rome and defeated the Chamavi. The confederation was contained again, but Franks now lived permanently on Roman soil west of the Rhine.",
  "Franks|378": "Frankish leaders reached the top of the Roman army. The Frankish king Mallobaudes, also a commander of the imperial guard, helped Gratian defeat the Lentienses at Argentaria, and Merobaudes, probably a Frank, was one of the most powerful generals in the West. Frankish influence in the empire rose sharply.",
  "Franks|392": "Arbogast, a Frank who commanded the western army, made the rhetorician Eugenius emperor after Valentinian II was found dead. Arbogast also campaigned across the Rhine against the Frankish kings Marcomer and Sunno. Franks now held decisive influence over the government of the western empire.",
  "Franks|394": "Theodosius I defeated Eugenius and Arbogast at the Battle of the Frigidus, and Arbogast took his own life. Frankish influence at the imperial court fell sharply, and in the following years the western general Stilicho dealt firmly with the Frankish kings, sending Marcomer into exile in Italy.",
  "Franks|406": "When the Vandals, Alans and Suebi approached the Rhine, Frankish federates fought them and killed the Vandal king Godegisel before the Alans turned the battle. The invaders crossed into Gaul, Roman authority on the frontier weakened, and the Franks were left freer to expand into neighbouring lands.",
  "Franks|445": "Chlodio, king of the Salian Franks, took Cambrai and occupied the land up to the Somme, although Aetius defeated him at Vicus Helena around this time. The Rhineland Franks were pressing on Cologne and had sacked Trier more than once. Frankish power in northern Gaul was growing steadily.",
  "Franks|451": "When Attila invaded Gaul, Frankish contingents fought with Aetius at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains, while a rival Frankish prince is said to have sought Hunnic support. The defeat of Attila preserved the balance in northern Gaul, and the Franks emerged as allies whom Roman commanders needed.",
  "Franks|463": "Childeric I probably fought alongside the Roman commander Aegidius against the Visigoths near Orléans in 463 and later fought Saxon raiders on the Loire. The Rhineland Franks had taken Cologne by about this time. Frankish kings were now major powers within the fragmenting Roman order in Gaul.",
  "Franks|476": "The deposition of the last western emperor ended imperial authority in Gaul. Childeric ruled at Tournai over Franks and Roman provincials of Belgica Secunda, the Rhineland Franks held Cologne, and only Syagrius’s enclave around Soissons separated Frankish lands from the Visigoths and Burgundians.",
  "Franks|481": "Childeric died and was buried at Tournai, and his son Clovis I, then about fifteen, became king. The Franks were stronger than ever but still divided among several kings. Within thirty years Clovis would unite them, ending the confederation and founding a single Frankish kingdom.",
  "Saxons|280": "Saxon raiders began to appear on the coasts of Gaul and Britain alongside Frankish pirates. The Saxons were then a loose grouping of coastal peoples between the Elbe and the Weser, dangerous at sea but small in number and with no territory beyond their homeland, so their power was modest.",
  "Saxons|286": "Carausius, commander of the Channel fleet against Frankish and Saxon pirates, was accused of letting raiders strike before seizing their plunder, and declared himself emperor. Saxon raiding was now serious enough for Rome to organise naval defence and, in time, the chain of forts later called the Saxon Shore.",
  "Saxons|367": "Saxons joined Picts, Scotti and Attacotti in the barbarian conspiracy that overran Roman Britain, while other raiders struck the Gallic coast. Count Theodosius needed two years to restore order. Saxon sea power was at an early height, able to strike alongside other enemies against a weakened province.",
  "Saxons|370": "A large Saxon force raided northern Gaul. Ammianus Marcellinus reports that the Romans agreed a truce, then ambushed and destroyed the Saxons as they withdrew. The raid shows Saxon reach and boldness, but also the continuing strength of Roman defences on the Channel coasts.",
  "Saxons|410": "Roman rule in Britain ended, and a Gallic chronicle reports a Saxon devastation of Britain around this time. With the Saxon Shore no longer manned, the way lay open to raiding and later settlement, and Saxon groups began to establish themselves in eastern Britain and along the coasts of Gaul.",
  "Saxons|450": "Saxon settlement in Britain was gathering pace; Bede later dated the arrival of Hengest and Horsa to around 449. Saxon communities also settled on the Channel coast of Gaul. Saxon power was spreading across the North Sea, though it was exercised by many independent groups rather than one people.",
  "Saxons|469": "Saxons under Adovacrius fought on the lower Loire around Angers, but Childeric I and the Franks took the city and drove them back. Frankish power now blocked Saxon expansion in Gaul, and Saxon activity turned to Britain and to the lands of the continental homeland.",
  "Saxons|531": "The Franks destroyed the kingdom of the Thuringians, and later Saxon tradition, recorded by Widukind of Corvey, says that the Saxons fought as Frankish allies. Saxons then took land in northern Thuringia, extending their territory inland. On the continent they were becoming a large land power rather than mainly sea raiders.",
  "Saxons|556": "Saxons rebelled against Frankish tribute and inflicted a heavy defeat on Chlothar I, as Gregory of Tours records. Frankish kings claimed overlordship and an annual tribute, which Fredegar describes as 500 cows, but in practice Saxon independence remained intact and Frankish armies seldom ventured far into Saxon territory.",
  "Saxons|632": "According to Fredegar, Dagobert I released the Saxons from their tribute of 500 cows in return for their help in guarding the frontier against the Wends. The weakness of later Merovingian kings left the Saxons effectively free, and they began to expand westwards towards the Rhine.",
  "Saxons|695": "Around the 690s the Saxons overran the lands of the Bructeri between the Lippe and the Ruhr, and English missionaries working among them, such as the two Hewalds, were killed. Saxony reached its greatest extent, from the North Sea and the Elbe to the borders of the Rhineland.",
  "Saxons|738": "Charles Martel campaigned into Saxony and imposed tribute, the first of many Carolingian expeditions. The Saxons were still strong and independent, raiding Frankish territory on the Rhine, but the revival of Frankish power under the Carolingian mayors of the palace began to press them hard.",
  "Saxons|758": "Pippin the Short stormed Saxon ramparts and forced the Saxons to promise an annual tribute of 300 horses. Repeated Frankish campaigns since 743 had shown that the Saxons could be defeated in the field, although they continued to break their oaths when the Franks withdrew.",
  "Saxons|772": "Charlemagne opened the Saxon Wars by taking the Eresburg and destroying the Irminsul, the Saxons’ chief sanctuary. His aim was now permanent conquest and conversion, not tribute. The Saxons still controlled their land and fought back fiercely, but they faced a far more determined enemy.",
  "Saxons|782": "After Charlemagne held an assembly at Lippspringe, Widukind led a revolt and a Saxon army destroyed a Frankish force in the Süntel hills. Charlemagne responded by having, according to the Royal Frankish Annals, some 4,500 Saxons beheaded at Verden, and harsh laws imposed Christianity.",
  "Saxons|785": "Widukind submitted and was baptised at Attigny, with Charlemagne as his godfather. Organised resistance collapsed, Frankish counts and churches spread across Saxony, and for several years the country seemed subdued. Saxon independence had effectively ended, though resentment of tithes and forced conversion remained.",
  "Saxons|793": "Saxons rose again while the Franks were fighting the Avars, destroying a Frankish force near the Weser in 793. The revolt was strongest in the north and in Nordalbingia. Charlemagne answered with yearly campaigns and large deportations, and the conflict became a war of attrition.",
  "Saxons|799": "Repeated campaigns, deportations and the settlement of Franks had broken resistance in most of Saxony. Charlemagne held court at Paderborn, where he received Pope Leo III in 799, and only Nordalbingia, north of the Elbe, remained in arms against the Franks.",
  "Saxons|804": "Charlemagne’s final campaign removed the Saxons of Nordalbingia, deporting them into Francia and giving their land to his Obodrite allies. The Saxon Wars were over: Saxony was Christian, divided into counties and bishoprics, and fully part of the Frankish realm, later to become the Duchy of Saxony.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|300": "The Picts had just appeared in Roman sources, first named in a panegyric of 297 as enemies of Britain, and Constantius Chlorus campaigned against them in 305–306. They were still a collection of tribal groups north of the Forth, capable of raiding but far weaker than the Roman province to the south.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|367": "Picts joined Scotti, Attacotti and Saxons in the barbarian conspiracy that overran Roman Britain, killing senior Roman commanders. Count Theodosius restored order only in 368–369. The raid showed that the Picts, now grouped into the Dicalydones and Verturiones, had become a serious military threat.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|382": "Magnus Maximus, a Roman commander in Britain, defeated an invasion of Picts and Scots before claiming the throne the following year. Roman defences in the north still worked, but his removal of troops to the continent soon weakened them, opening the way to renewed Pictish raids.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|410": "Roman rule in Britain ended, and the Picts were free to raid the former province; the 6th-century writer Gildas later described their attacks across the Wall. Their society was consolidating into larger lordships, and wealth from raiding, including Roman silver, strengthened their elite.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|560": "Bridei son of Maelchon was the dominant king in the north, and the Irish annals record a flight of the Gaels of Dál Riata before him around 560. Columba visited his fortress near the River Ness in the 560s. Pictish power had grown into a large over-kingship.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|658": "After the death of the Pictish king Talorgan son of Eanfrith in 657, himself the son of a Northumbrian prince, the Northumbrian king Oswiu extended his overlordship over much of Pictland. For nearly thirty years many Picts paid tribute to Northumbria, the low point of Pictish independence.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|685": "Bridei son of Beli defeated and killed Ecgfrith of Northumbria at Dun Nechtain on 20 May 685, ending Northumbrian overlordship. He had already attacked the Orkneys in 682. The victory restored Pictish independence and made Fortriu the leading power of northern Britain.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|717": "Nechtan son of Der-Ilei aligned the Pictish church with Rome, adopting the Roman Easter after consulting Abbot Ceolfrith of Wearmouth and Jarrow, and expelled the clergy of Iona in 717. The kingdom was stable and confident, building closer ties with Northumbria and the wider Latin church.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|729": "Civil war followed Nechtan’s retirement to a monastery as rival kings fought for power, and Óengus son of Fergus emerged as victor after battles in 728–729. In the same year 150 Pictish ships were wrecked, according to the annals. Internal conflict weakened the kingdom but produced a formidable new ruler.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|741": "Óengus son of Fergus, having taken Dunadd in 736, inflicted what the annals call the smiting of Dál Riata in 741. He dominated the Gaels, fought the Britons of Alt Clut and was among the most powerful rulers in Britain. The Pictish kingdom stood at its height.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|750": "A battle against the Britons in which Óengus’s brother Talorgan was killed led the Irish annals to record the ebbing of Óengus’s sovereignty. Pictish power remained great, and in 756 Óengus joined Eadberht of Northumbria in forcing the submission of Dumbarton, but its expansion had peaked.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|761": "Óengus son of Fergus died after some thirty years as king. His successors, among them his brother Bridei, maintained Pictish dominance in the north, but the succession was again contested, and Dál Riata seems to have recovered some independence in the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|789": "Constantín son of Fergus won the Pictish kingship by defeating his rival Conall son of Tadg. He would rule for over thirty years and may also have controlled Dál Riata, bringing Picts and Gaels under one dynasty. The first Viking raids on Iona and the isles began in the 790s.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|820": "Constantín son of Fergus died after a long and stable reign, commemorated on the Dupplin Cross near Forteviot. His dynasty held both the Pictish and probably the Dál Riatan kingships, and the church flourished. The kingdom was strong and increasingly Gaelic in culture.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|839": "The Vikings won a great victory over the men of Fortriu, killing King Eóganán son of Óengus, his brother Bran, Áed son of Boanta and many others. The ruling dynasty was destroyed, Pictish noble families were devastated, and rival claimants fought over the kingship.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|843": "Cináed mac Ailpín, probably of Gaelic descent, became king of the Picts around 842–843 after overcoming rival claimants. The annals still called him king of the Picts, but Gaelic speech and customs spread among the ruling class, and the kingdom’s Pictish identity began to fade.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|866": "Olaf and Auisle, Viking kings based in Dublin, plundered the whole of Pictland and took hostages. Repeated Viking attacks, and Norse settlement in Orkney, Shetland and Caithness, cut off the north and weakened the kingdom’s resources. Pictish kingship survived largely in name.",
  "Kingdom of the Picts|900": "Domnall son of Constantín died, and the Irish annals called him king of Alba, the first use of the new name. The Pictish kingdom had become the Gaelic kingdom of Alba under Cináed’s descendants, and the Picts as a distinct people vanish from the record soon after.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|410": "Roman government in Britain ended and authority in the territory of the Cornovii passed to local British leaders. Wroxeter, the old civitas capital, stayed occupied, but there was not yet a clearly defined kingdom. Power was local and fragmented, which is why the curve begins low.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|500": "By about 500 a British kingdom was probably taking shape on the upper Severn and in the lowlands to the east, ruled by a dynasty that later traced itself to Cadell Ddyrnllug. Its history is known only from later genealogies, but it held good farmland and faced little English pressure yet.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|600": "Around 600 Powys was probably at its greatest extent, holding the uplands of mid Wales together with much of the plain of later Shropshire and Cheshire. The English kingdoms had not yet reached its eastern lowlands, and its kings could field war bands against Northumbria. Evidence is thin, so the peak is approximate.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|615": "Æthelfrith of Northumbria crushed the Britons at the Battle of Chester around 615. King Selyf ap Cynan of Powys was killed, and Bede reports the slaughter of about 1,200 monks of Bangor-on-Dee. The defeat exposed the eastern lowlands and opened a long period of English advance.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|656": "By the mid seventh century the lowlands of Shropshire were passing to the English, and later poems mourn Cynddylan and his hall at Pengwern. The date of these losses is uncertain, but Powys was pushed back towards the Welsh hills and its power fell to a middling level.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|750": "In the eighth century Elisedd ap Gwylog won back land from the English, as the Pillar of Eliseg later recorded. Powys remained a serious opponent of Mercia, strong enough that Offa soon built his great dyke along the frontier. This recovery lifts the curve somewhat.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|822": "According to the Annales Cambriae, the Mercians took the region of Powys into their power in 822, after destroying the fortress of Deganwy. Even if their control was brief, the kingdom was gravely weakened, and its king Cyngen ap Cadell ruled a reduced and threatened realm.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|854": "Cyngen ap Cadell died on pilgrimage at Rome in 854 or 855, the last king of the old line of Powys. The kingdom passed, it is usually said, to Rhodri the Great of Gwynedd, and Powys lost its independent dynasty, leaving its power as a separate state very low.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|878": "Rhodri the Great was killed fighting the English in 878, and Powys remained in the hands of his descendants, ruled from outside as part of wider Welsh dominions. For almost two centuries it existed as a region rather than an independent kingdom, which is why the curve stands at its lowest.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1063": "Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, ruler of all Wales, was killed in 1063 after Harold Godwinson’s campaign. Bleddyn ap Cynfyn and his brother Rhiwallon were installed in Gwynedd and Powys under English overlordship, and a native Powysian dynasty returned to power, restoring Powys as a recognisable kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1075": "Bleddyn ap Cynfyn was killed in 1075 by Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth. At the same time Norman lords based at Shrewsbury were pressing into the Welsh borderlands, so the restored kingdom faced both leadership losses and a dangerous new neighbour, holding its power at a modest level.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1102": "Iorwerth ap Bleddyn’s support for Henry I helped to bring down Robert of Bellême, earl of Shrewsbury, in 1102. The removal of the Montgomery earls relieved Norman pressure on Powys, although Henry I soon turned against Iorwerth and the sons of Bleddyn continued to quarrel among themselves.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1132": "Maredudd ap Bleddyn died in 1132 after restoring a degree of unity, and his son Madog ap Maredudd succeeded him. The civil war in England after 1135 weakened royal control of the March, giving the rulers of Powys room to recover territory and influence in the borderlands.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1149": "Madog ap Maredudd stood at the height of his power, ruling a reunited Powys and building a castle at Oswestry in about 1149. He had led Welsh troops at the Battle of Lincoln in 1141 and was a leading figure in the politics of the March.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1157": "Madog allied with Henry II during the English king’s campaign against Owain Gwynedd in 1157. The alliance protected Powys from Gwynedd’s expansion but also made it dependent on English support, and Owain Gwynedd’s pressure on its northern lands limited its independence.",
  "Kingdom of Powys|1160": "Madog ap Maredudd died in 1160, and his son and heir Llywelyn was killed soon afterwards. The kingdom was divided among Madog’s kin into Powys Fadog in the north and Powys Wenwynwyn in the south, ending Powys as a single kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|450": "After Roman rule ended, north-west Wales was held by British rulers whose descendants later claimed as founder Cunedda, said to have come from Manaw Gododdin. The tradition is late and doubtful, and Irish settlers were also present in the region, so the kingdom’s beginnings are obscure and its power was limited.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|547": "Maelgwn Gwynedd, denounced by Gildas as Maglocunus, the dragon of the island, had made Gwynedd one of the strongest British kingdoms before he died of plague in 547, according to the Annales Cambriae. Snowdonia and Anglesey formed a secure and productive core, giving the kingdom a solid regional standing.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|633": "Cadwallon ap Cadfan, allied with Penda of Mercia, defeated and killed Edwin of Northumbria at Hatfield Chase and ravaged Northumbria. For a short time the king of Gwynedd was among the most formidable rulers in Britain, although the victory rested on his alliance and on raiding rather than on lasting conquest.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|634": "Oswald of Northumbria defeated and killed Cadwallon at the Battle of Heavenfield near Hexham in 634. Gwynedd’s brief role as a power in northern Britain ended, and the English kingdoms consolidated their hold over the lowlands, leaving the Britons of Wales increasingly confined to the west.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|825": "Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog died in 825, and Merfyn Frych, a ruler possibly from the Isle of Man, took the throne. Mercian forces had destroyed the fortress of Deganwy in 822, and Gwynedd was a modest kingdom under pressure, but Merfyn founded a dynasty that would soon expand.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|855": "Rhodri ap Merfyn, Rhodri the Great, who had succeeded his father in 844, acquired Powys after its last native king, Cyngen ap Cadell, died in Rome in 854 or 855. The union of Gwynedd and Powys made him the most powerful ruler in Wales.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|872": "Rhodri had married Angharad, sister of the last king of Seisyllwg, and after that king drowned around 872 the south-western kingdom passed to Rhodri’s family. He had also killed the Danish leader Horm in 856. Gwynedd’s ruler now dominated most of Wales outside the south-east.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|878": "Rhodri the Great was killed fighting the English in 878, and his lands were divided among his sons. Anarawd ap Rhodri held Gwynedd and avenged his father by defeating the Mercians at Conwy in 881, but he later submitted to Alfred the Great, and Gwynedd’s reach contracted.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|942": "Idwal Foel, king of Gwynedd, was killed fighting the English in 942, and Hywel Dda of Deheubarth seized the kingdom, excluding Idwal’s sons. For much of the tenth century Gwynedd was contested between branches of Rhodri’s line and exposed to Viking raids, leaving its power at a low ebb.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1039": "Gruffudd ap Llywelyn seized Gwynedd and Powys in 1039 and in the same year defeated a Mercian army at Rhyd-y-groes on the Severn. From his base in Gwynedd he began a career of conquest that would bring the rest of Wales under his rule, restoring the kingdom’s strength.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1055": "By 1055 Gruffudd ap Llywelyn had taken Deheubarth, and in alliance with Ælfgar of Mercia he sacked Hereford. He was now king of almost all Wales, a unity no Welsh ruler before him had achieved. Gwynedd, the centre of his power, stood at an early medieval high point.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1063": "Harold Godwinson’s land and sea campaign of 1063 drove Gruffudd ap Llywelyn into Snowdonia, where he was killed, according to English sources by his own men. His realm fell apart, and Gwynedd and Powys passed to Bleddyn ap Cynfyn and Rhiwallon under English overlordship.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1100": "Norman lords based at Chester had occupied much of Gwynedd after capturing Gruffudd ap Cynan around 1081. A Welsh revolt from 1094 and the death of Hugh of Montgomery on Anglesey in 1098 at the hands of Magnus Barefoot’s Norwegians allowed Gruffudd to return and rebuild the kingdom from Anglesey.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1137": "Gruffudd ap Cynan died in 1137 after a long reign in which he and his sons had restored Gwynedd and extended its reach. His son Owain Gwynedd succeeded, inheriting a strong kingdom just as civil war in England weakened royal control of the Welsh border.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1165": "Owain Gwynedd led a coalition of Welsh rulers against Henry II’s great invasion of 1165, which collapsed in heavy rain on the Berwyn mountains. Owain then pushed his frontier east, taking Rhuddlan in 1167, and styled himself prince of the Welsh. Gwynedd was the clear leader of native Wales.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1170": "Owain Gwynedd died in 1170, and his sons fought over the inheritance; Hywel ab Owain, himself a poet, was killed within months. For a generation Gwynedd was divided among rival princes such as Dafydd ab Owain, and the leadership of native Wales passed to the Lord Rhys of Deheubarth.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1218": "Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, having reunited Gwynedd by about 1200 and survived King John’s invasion of 1211, exploited civil war in England to extend his authority over most of native Wales. The Treaty of Worcester in 1218 confirmed his gains, including custody of the royal castles of Cardigan and Carmarthen.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1240": "Llywelyn the Great died in 1240, having arranged for his son Dafydd, born of Joan, to succeed alone rather than share the land with his elder half-brother Gruffudd. Henry III moved quickly to reduce Dafydd’s inheritance, and the dominance Llywelyn had built over the other Welsh lords began to dissolve.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1247": "After Dafydd ap Llywelyn died in 1246, his nephews Owain and Llywelyn ap Gruffudd accepted the Treaty of Woodstock in 1247. It confined them to Gwynedd west of the River Conwy and ceded the eastern district, the Perfeddwlad, to the English crown, reducing the kingdom to its mountain core.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1267": "Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, sole ruler of Gwynedd since his victory at Bryn Derwin in 1255, recovered the Perfeddwlad and won the homage of most Welsh lords. Exploiting the baronial wars in England, he obtained the Treaty of Montgomery in 1267, by which Henry III recognised him as Prince of Wales.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1277": "Edward I, angered by Llywelyn’s refusal of homage, invaded with forces supplied by sea. Llywelyn submitted in the Treaty of Aberconwy, which took away the homage of most Welsh lords and confined him to Gwynedd west of the Conwy. He kept the title Prince of Wales, but his power was much reduced.",
  "Kingdom of Gwynedd|1283": "After the rising of 1282, Llywelyn was killed near Cilmeri in December, and his brother Dafydd, the last leader of the resistance, was captured in June 1283 and executed at Shrewsbury. Gwynedd ceased to exist as an independent state and was organised into English shires by the Statute of Rhuddlan in 1284.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|481": "Clovis I succeeded his father Childeric I as king of a group of Salian Franks based at Tournai, traditionally in 481. His was one of several small Frankish realms in northern Gaul, alongside a surviving Roman authority at Soissons, so the state began small and largely regional.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|486": "Clovis defeated Syagrius, ruler of the Roman Domain of Soissons, and took over northern Gaul as far as the Loire. Control of the old Roman administrative centres and estates of the region gave the young kingdom a much larger base of land, revenues and followers.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|507": "Clovis, now a Catholic Christian supported by the Gallo-Roman bishops, defeated and killed the Visigothic king Alaric II at the Battle of Vouillé. Most of Aquitaine passed to the Franks, and the emperor Anastasius soon honoured Clovis with an honorary consulship, confirming him as the dominant ruler in Gaul.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|511": "Clovis died in 511 after eliminating the other Frankish kings and holding a church council at Orléans. His kingdom was divided among his four sons, Theuderic, Chlodomer, Childebert and Chlothar, but the brothers often cooperated in war, so the Frankish realm as a whole remained strong.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|534": "Clovis’s sons and grandson conquered the Kingdom of the Burgundians in 534, after destroying the Thuringian kingdom in 531, and soon obtained Provence from the Ostrogoths. The Franks now controlled almost all of Gaul and much of central Germany, the widest extent of Merovingian power.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|561": "Chlothar I, who had reunited the whole kingdom in 558, died in 561, and the realm was divided again among his four sons. The rivalry that followed, including the long feud of the queens Brunhild and Fredegund described by Gregory of Tours, weakened royal power for half a century.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|613": "Chlothar II captured and executed Queen Brunhild in 613 and reunited the Frankish kingdoms under a single king. In the Edict of Paris of 614 he confirmed the rights of the aristocracy and the church, restoring stability at the price of conceding influence to the great landholders.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|629": "Dagobert I became sole king in 629, the last Merovingian to exercise strong personal authority. He governed from Paris, intervened in Visigothic Spain, fought the Slavs under Samo with little success and was a generous patron of Saint-Denis. The kingdom stood at its Merovingian height.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|639": "After Dagobert’s death in 639 the throne passed to young kings, and power shifted to the aristocracy and the mayors of the palace in Austrasia and Neustria. Outlying regions such as Aquitaine, Thuringia and Bavaria became increasingly independent under their own dukes.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|687": "Pippin of Herstal, mayor of the palace of Austrasia, defeated the Neustrians at the Battle of Tertry in 687 and became the effective ruler of the Frankish kingdoms. The Merovingian kings remained on the throne, but real authority now lay with the Pippinid family.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|714": "Pippin of Herstal died in 714, leaving young grandsons as heirs, and civil war broke out between Neustrian and Austrasian factions while Frisians and Saxons attacked. His son Charles Martel, excluded from the succession, had to fight his way to power, which he achieved by about 718.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|732": "Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad army under Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi between Poitiers and Tours, usually dated 732. He also reasserted Frankish authority over Burgundy, Frisia and Alamannia, and from 737 ruled without any Merovingian king on the throne, rebuilding a strong central power.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|751": "Pippin the Short, with the approval of Pope Zachary, deposed the last Merovingian, Childeric III, and was anointed king of the Franks. The Carolingian dynasty now held both real power and the crown, and Pippin soon allied with the papacy against the Lombards and subdued Aquitaine.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|774": "Charlemagne, sole king since the death of his brother Carloman in 771, besieged Pavia and conquered the Lombard Kingdom in 774, taking the title king of the Lombards. Together with the long campaigns against the Saxons begun in 772, this made the Frankish kingdom the dominant power in western Europe.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|800": "Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne emperor in Rome on 25 December 800. By then the Saxons had largely been subdued, the Avar realm broken and the Spanish March established. Charlemagne ruled from Aachen over most of western Christendom, the peak of Frankish power and prestige.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|814": "Charlemagne died in 814, and his only surviving legitimate son, Louis the Pious, became sole ruler of an intact empire. Louis reformed the church and the palace, and in 817 issued the Ordinatio imperii to keep the realm united under his eldest son Lothar, so power remained near its peak.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|830": "The elder sons of Louis the Pious rebelled in 830 and again in 833, when Louis was briefly deposed and made to do public penance. The quarrels, provoked by plans to provide for his youngest son Charles the Bald, divided the aristocracy and weakened imperial authority.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|841": "Louis the Pious died in 840, and his sons fought for the inheritance. At the Battle of Fontenoy in June 841 Louis the German and Charles the Bald defeated their brother Lothar I in a costly battle, and in 842 they sealed their alliance with the Strasbourg Oaths.",
  "Kingdom of the Franks|843": "The Treaty of Verdun divided the Frankish realm into West Francia, East Francia and Middle Francia under the three brothers. The single Frankish kingdom ceased to exist as a political unit, although the idea of Frankish unity survived and Charles the Fat briefly reunited the kingdoms in the 880s.",
  "Dal Riata|500": "By about 500 the rulers of Dál Riata, based in north-east Antrim, held lands across the North Channel in Argyll. Later tradition credited Fergus Mór mac Eirc with taking part of Britain around this time, but the evidence is thin, and the kingdom was still a small coastal lordship.",
  "Dal Riata|563": "Columba founded his monastery on Iona in 563, reportedly with the favour of King Conall mac Comgaill, whom the annals credit with granting the island. The new foundation gave Dál Riata a centre of learning and prestige, while its kings strengthened their hold over the kindreds of Kintyre, Lorn and Islay.",
  "Dal Riata|574": "Áedán mac Gabráin became king in 574 and, according to Adomnán, was ordained by Columba. A vigorous war leader, he soon began campaigns beyond the kingdom’s borders, and his close relationship with Iona and with Irish kings raised the standing of the Dál Riata kingship.",
  "Dal Riata|590": "In the 580s and 590s Áedán mac Gabráin led an expedition to Orkney, fought a battle in Manann, either the Isle of Man or Manaw, and fought the Miathi. Dál Riata was a significant power in northern Britain, able to raise large armies and respected in Ireland. This marks its peak.",
  "Dal Riata|603": "Æthelfrith of Northumbria crushed Áedán’s army at the Battle of Degsastan in 603. Bede wrote that afterwards no king of the Scots in Britain dared to make war on the English, and Dál Riata’s ambitions to the south-east ended, though the kingdom itself remained intact.",
  "Dal Riata|637": "Domnall Brecc joined Congal Cáech of the Ulaid against the high king Domnall mac Áedo and was defeated at the Battle of Mag Rath in 637. The defeat damaged Dál Riata’s standing in Ireland and began the loss of its Irish territories, sharply reducing its power.",
  "Dal Riata|642": "Domnall Brecc was killed at Strathcarron in 642 by Owen, king of the Britons of Strathclyde. Dál Riata entered a period of weakness, apparently subject for a time to Northumbrian overlordship under Oswiu, and its leading kindreds increasingly contested the kingship among themselves.",
  "Dal Riata|719": "Rivalry between Cenél nGabráin and Cenél Loairn culminated in a sea battle in 719 in which Dúnchad Becc of Cenél nGabráin defeated Selbach of Cenél Loairn. The kingdom still had considerable naval strength, but its divisions left it exposed to its powerful Pictish neighbour.",
  "Dal Riata|736": "Óengus son of Fergus, king of the Picts, invaded Dál Riata in 736, captured Dunadd and took prisoner two sons of Selbach. The fall of the royal centre showed that the kingdom could no longer defend itself against the Picts, now the dominant power in northern Britain.",
  "Dal Riata|741": "The annals record the smiting of Dál Riata by Óengus son of Fergus in 741. For a generation the kingdom appears to have been under Pictish overlordship, its kings either subject to the Picts or absent from the record, leaving its power at a low point.",
  "Dal Riata|768": "Áed Find, a king of Cenél nGabráin, fought a battle in Fortriu, the Pictish heartland, against the Pictish king Ciniod in 768. His reign marked a recovery of Dál Riata’s independence, and later tradition remembered the laws of Áed as a standard for the Gaels of Alba.",
  "Dal Riata|778": "Áed Find died in 778, and the following decades are poorly recorded. The kings who followed are little more than names in later lists, and from the late eighth century the Pictish kings of the line of Fergus extended their influence over Dál Riata, limiting its separate power.",
  "Dal Riata|795": "Viking raiders attacked the Hebrides from 795, and Iona was raided that year and again in 802 and 806, when sixty-eight members of the community were killed. The sea routes that had been the kingdom’s strength became routes of attack, exposing its coasts and monasteries.",
  "Dal Riata|843": "Cináed mac Ailpín, Kenneth MacAlpin, became king of the Picts around 842 or 843. Later tradition made him a king of Dál Riata who conquered the Picts, but his origins are uncertain. By now Dál Riata had little existence apart from the wider kingdom of the Picts.",
  "Dal Riata|850": "Around 849 Cináed moved relics of Columba to Dunkeld, a sign that the centre of the Gaelic church had shifted east into Pictland. Dál Riata ceased to exist as a separate kingdom, merging into the realm that by about 900 was called Alba, the Kingdom of Scotland.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|519": "The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle dates Cerdic and Cynric’s seizure of the kingdom to 519, after their victory at Cerdicesford. The chronology is doubtful, and the early West Saxons, or Gewisse, were probably a modest group of communities in the upper Thames valley and the Solent region.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|577": "The Chronicle records that Ceawlin and Cuthwine won the Battle of Dyrham in 577, killing three British kings and taking Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath. Bede counted Ceawlin among the kings who held authority over the southern English, a sign that the West Saxons had become a leading power.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|592": "In 592, according to the Chronicle, Ceawlin was driven out after a great slaughter at Woden’s Barrow, and he died soon afterwards. The kingdom fell into a period of rivalry between branches of the royal family, and Mercian pressure in the Thames valley soon checked its expansion.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|635": "King Cynegils was baptised in 635 by Bishop Birinus, with Oswald of Northumbria as godfather, and a bishopric was founded at Dorchester-on-Thames. Christian kingship strengthened the dynasty, but the kingdom still lay in the shadow of Mercia and Northumbria and was losing ground in the Thames valley.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|685": "Cædwalla seized the West Saxon throne in 685 and in a short, violent reign conquered the Isle of Wight and Sussex and briefly held Kent. He abdicated in 688 to be baptised in Rome, where he died, leaving a much enlarged kingdom to his successor Ine.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|694": "Ine, king since 688, issued his law code around 694, the earliest from Wessex, and in that year received compensation from Kent for the killing of Cædwalla’s brother Mul. He fought Dumnonia, and the emporium of Hamwic probably developed under him, making Wessex one of the strongest English kingdoms.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|726": "Ine abdicated in 726 and went to Rome, where he died. His departure was followed by disputed successions, and for much of the eighth century Wessex lay under the overlordship of the Mercian kings Æthelbald and Offa, who controlled the lands north of the Thames.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|752": "Cuthred of Wessex defeated Æthelbald of Mercia at Burford in 752, according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, briefly throwing off Mercian domination. The success did not last: under Offa Wessex again accepted a subordinate role, and in 779 Offa took Bensington from the West Saxon king Cynewulf.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|802": "Ecgberht, who had spent three years in exile among the Franks, became king of Wessex in 802 on the death of Beorhtric, Offa’s son-in-law. On the day of his accession the men of Wiltshire defeated a Mercian force from the Hwicce, and he set about rebuilding West Saxon strength.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|825": "Ecgberht defeated Beornwulf of Mercia at the Battle of Ellendun in 825. As a result Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex submitted to Wessex, and the East Angles, with West Saxon support, killed Beornwulf. The balance of power among the English kingdoms shifted decisively to the south.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|829": "Ecgberht conquered Mercia in 829 and received the submission of the Northumbrians, and the Chronicle called him the eighth king to rule over all the southern English. Mercia regained its independence within a year, but Wessex was now the leading English kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|839": "Ecgberht died in 839, a year after defeating Cornish and Danish forces at Hingston Down. His son Æthelwulf succeeded and placed Kent and the south-east under his eldest son. Viking raids were increasing, but the enlarged kingdom remained the strongest in southern England.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|851": "Æthelwulf and his son Æthelbald defeated a large Viking army at the battle of Aclea in 851, which the Chronicle described as the greatest slaughter of a heathen army ever heard of. Wessex was holding its own, and in 853 it helped Mercia against the Welsh.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|871": "The Great Heathen Army, having conquered Northumbria and East Anglia, invaded Wessex in 871, the year of battles. Alfred’s brother King Æthelred died after a series of engagements, including the victory at Ashdown, and Alfred became king of a hard-pressed kingdom that had to buy peace from the Danes.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|878": "In January 878 Guthrum’s surprise attack on Chippenham drove Alfred into the marshes of Athelney, and Wessex came close to conquest. In May Alfred rallied the men of Somerset, Wiltshire and Hampshire and defeated Guthrum at Edington; Guthrum accepted baptism and withdrew to East Anglia.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|886": "Alfred occupied London in 886 and entrusted it to Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians. All the English not under Danish rule submitted to him, and he was styled king of the Anglo-Saxons. His network of burhs and reorganised army made Wessex secure against renewed attack.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|899": "Alfred died in 899 after defeating the Viking campaigns of 892–896, and his son Edward the Elder succeeded, overcoming a revolt by his cousin Æthelwold. With the burhs, a reformed army and the Mercian alliance, Wessex was the dominant power in southern Britain.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|918": "By 918 Edward the Elder and his sister Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, had conquered the Danish territories south of the Humber. On Æthelflæd’s death that year Edward took direct control of Mercia, and within two years the rulers of the north acknowledged his overlordship.",
  "Kingdom of Wessex|927": "Æthelstan took York in 927 and received the submission of the northern rulers at Eamont on 12 July. He now ruled as king of the English, and Wessex was absorbed into the new Kingdom of England, ending at the height of its power, transformed rather than destroyed.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|527": "The traditional starting date rests on late genealogies rather than contemporary records. Anglian groups were settling the upper Trent valley under leaders later remembered as Icel and Creoda, but they formed one frontier people among many in the Midlands, with no clear kingdom yet visible to writers of the period.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|600": "Around 600 the Mercians had kings, among them the shadowy Pybba and Cearl, whose daughter Bede says married Edwin of Northumbria. The people held the Trent valley and its borderlands, but they remained overshadowed by Northumbria and East Anglia and had not yet absorbed the smaller Midland peoples around them.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|633": "Penda, allied with Cadwallon of Gwynedd, defeated and killed Edwin of Northumbria at the Battle of Hatfield Chase. The victory broke Northumbrian dominance south of the Humber for a time and made Penda the most feared warlord in the Midlands, though his authority still depended heavily on alliances and plunder.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|642": "At the Battle of Maserfield Penda killed Oswald of Northumbria, the leading Christian king of the day. Over the following years he killed kings of East Anglia, drove Cenwalh of Wessex into exile and set his son Peada over the Middle Angles, making Mercia the strongest power in southern Britain.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|655": "Penda was defeated and killed by Oswiu of Northumbria at the Battle of the Winwaed. Oswiu took control of Mercia, allowing Peada to rule the south briefly until his murder in 656. For about three years the Mercians lived under Northumbrian rule, the lowest point of the kingdom\u0027s early history.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|658": "Mercian nobles rose against Northumbrian rule and placed Penda\u0027s son Wulfhere on the throne. A Christian king, Wulfhere rebuilt Mercian power, extended influence into the Thames valley, Essex and Surrey, and established the Mercian church under Chad, who set the see at Lichfield in 669.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|679": "Æthelred, Wulfhere\u0027s brother, defeated Ecgfrith of Northumbria at the Battle of the Trent, recovering Lindsey and ending Northumbrian claims south of the Humber. Mercia was now the largest English kingdom, with client peoples such as the Hwicce and Magonsaete increasingly absorbed into it, though its southern overlordship was contested by Wessex.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|716": "Æthelbald became king after years of exile, following the short reign of Ceolred. Inheriting a large but loosely held kingdom, he began four decades of rule in which he would bring the kings south of the Humber under his lordship and turn Mercia into the leading power of England.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|736": "A charter of this year styles Æthelbald \u0027king of Britain\u0027, reflecting his lordship over the southern English kingdoms. He controlled London and its trade, granted toll exemptions to churches and presided over church councils. Mercia was close to its peak, although Wessex and Kent retained some independence and Æthelbald faced criticism from Boniface.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|757": "Æthelbald was murdered by his own bodyguard at Seckington. A brief succession struggle followed, in which Beornred was driven out by Offa, a distant kinsman. Mercian lordship over the south loosened during this crisis, and Offa needed several years to restore control over Kent, Sussex and the other client kingdoms.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|787": "Offa stood at the height of his power, ruling Kent and Sussex directly, dominating Wessex and East Anglia and corresponding with Charlemagne. In this year a papal legation helped create an archbishopric at Lichfield, and Offa\u0027s son Ecgfrith was consecrated king, the first recorded royal anointing in England.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|796": "Offa died in July, and his son Ecgfrith survived him by only a few months. In the same year Offa exchanged letters with Charlemagne about trade and merchants. The succession passed to the distant kinsman Coenwulf, while Kent rose in revolt, but Mercia still held the strongest position among the English kingdoms.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|821": "Coenwulf died after a reign in which he crushed the Kentish revolt of 798, kept East Anglia and the southeast under Mercian control and attacked the Welsh. He had however quarrelled bitterly with Archbishop Wulfred of Canterbury, and his death opened a period of rapid royal turnover and weakening authority.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|825": "Ecgberht of Wessex defeated King Beornwulf at the Battle of Ellendun in Wiltshire. Wessex then took Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex, and the East Angles rebelled and killed Beornwulf. Mercia lost its southern empire in a single campaign season and was reduced to its Midland heartland.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|829": "Ecgberht of Wessex conquered Mercia and briefly ruled it, being recorded as \u0027bretwalda\u0027 by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. The kingdom was at its lowest point since 655. Wiglaf recovered the throne in 830, but Mercia never regained its former supremacy and increasingly acted as a partner of Wessex.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|874": "The Viking Great Heathen Army wintered at Repton, the royal burial church, and drove King Burgred into exile; he went to Rome and died there. The Vikings installed Ceolwulf II as a dependent king. Independent Mercian royal power had effectively ended, and the kingdom lay at the mercy of the invaders.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|877": "The Vikings divided Mercia, settling the eastern part, which later formed the Five Boroughs and part of the Danelaw, and leaving the west to Ceolwulf II. Western Mercia, centred on the Severn valley and Worcester, was a much reduced territory whose survival depended on resisting further Viking settlement.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|886": "Alfred the Great occupied London and entrusted it to Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians, who acknowledged Alfred\u0027s overlordship and married his daughter Æthelflæd. Western Mercia kept its own ruler, council and identity while being bound to Wessex, a partnership that steadied it against Viking attack.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|911": "Æthelred died and his widow Æthelflæd, the Lady of the Mercians, took sole charge. She was among the few women to rule an Anglo-Saxon realm in her own right and governed in close alliance with her brother Edward the Elder, preparing a programme of fortified burhs against the Danes.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|917": "Æthelflæd\u0027s burhs at Bridgnorth, Tamworth, Stafford, Warwick and elsewhere allowed her to go on the offensive. In 917 she stormed Derby, one of the Five Boroughs, and early in 918 Leicester submitted peacefully. Western Mercia briefly regained an expansive role, and York\u0027s leaders offered her their submission.",
  "Kingdom of Mercia|918": "Æthelflæd died at Tamworth in June. Her daughter Ælfwynn succeeded briefly, but in December Edward the Elder removed her to Wessex and took direct control of Mercia. The kingdom ceased to exist as a separate realm and became part of the emerging kingdom of the English.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|555": "The Frankish kings recognised Garibald I of the Agilolfing family as duke of the Bavarians, a people recently formed between the Danube, the Lech and the Alps. The duchy was a frontier territory under Frankish overlordship, with modest resources, Roman towns in decay and powerful neighbours in the Lombards and the Avars.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|591": "Tassilo I became duke with Frankish approval, while Theodelinda, probably his sister, was queen of the Lombards in Italy. The Agilolfings were now a dynasty of international standing. Tassilo fought the Slavs and Avars pressing into the eastern Alps, but the duchy remained a dependent frontier principality of the Frankish kingdom.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|739": "Boniface, acting with papal authority and the support of Duke Odilo, organised the four Bavarian bishoprics of Salzburg, Regensburg, Freising and Passau. The duchy gained a full church structure of its own, strengthening ducal government and preparing Bavaria to become a base for missions into the Alpine and Danubian lands.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|743": "Duke Odilo, who had sought greater independence, was defeated on the Lech by the Frankish mayors Pippin and Carloman. He kept his duchy but had to accept Frankish supremacy. The setback showed the limits of Agilolfing power in the face of the rising Carolingian family, though Bavarian institutions remained intact.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|772": "Tassilo III subdued Carantania, extending Bavarian rule over the Slavs of the eastern Alps, and founded monasteries such as Kremsmünster in 777. Ruling almost as an independent king, allied by marriage with the Lombard royal house, he brought the Agilolfing duchy to its greatest extent and prestige.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|788": "Charlemagne summoned Tassilo III to Ingelheim, where an assembly condemned him for desertion committed decades earlier, and sent him to a monastery. Bavaria lost its native dynasty and its autonomy and was placed under Frankish prefects, ending two centuries of Agilolfing rule and absorbing the duchy into the Carolingian Empire.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|817": "Under Louis the Pious\u0027s settlement, Bavaria became the kingdom of his son Louis the German, who later ruled from Regensburg. Bavaria was now the political centre of the eastern Frankish lands and the base for campaigns against Moravians and Slavs, regaining importance though not as an autonomous duchy.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|907": "A Bavarian army under Margrave Luitpold was destroyed by the Magyars at the Battle of Pressburg. Luitpold and many nobles and bishops died, and the eastern lands were lost. His son Arnulf the Bad became duke, restoring Bavaria as a stem duchy with wide powers but under constant threat of raids.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|955": "Bavarian forces shared in Otto I\u0027s victory over the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld, ending the raids. Duke Henry I, Otto\u0027s brother, had added the March of Verona and Friuli in 952. The duchy now stretched from the Lech to the Adriatic hinterland, its largest territorial extent within the Empire.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|976": "After the revolt of Duke Henry the Wrangler, Emperor Otto II separated Carinthia, with the March of Verona, from Bavaria as a new duchy and granted the eastern march to the Babenberg Leopold. Bavaria lost much of its southern and eastern territory, though it remained one of the largest duchies.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1002": "Duke Henry IV of Bavaria was elected king as Henry II, later emperor. A Bavarian duke stood at the head of the Empire and Bavaria was closely tied to the crown, which held or granted the duchy directly for much of the 11th century, giving it influence but limiting its independent dynastic development.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1156": "With the Privilegium Minus, Frederick Barbarossa raised the Bavarian eastern march to the separate Duchy of Austria under the Babenbergs, compensating them for returning Bavaria to the Welf Henry the Lion. Bavaria lost its eastern march for good, while Henry the Lion founded Munich two years later.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1180": "Barbarossa deposed Henry the Lion and granted a reduced Bavaria to Otto of Wittelsbach; Styria was detached as a duchy. The new dynasty began with a smaller territory and many powerful rivals among bishops and counts, but it would build a compact territorial state over the following century.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1255": "The Wittelsbach brothers Louis II and Henry XIII partitioned the duchy into Upper Bavaria, with the Rhenish Palatinate, and Lower Bavaria. The division was the first of many and weakened Bavaria\u0027s weight within the Empire, though each part continued to consolidate lands and rights through purchase and inheritance.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1314": "Duke Louis of Upper Bavaria was elected king of the Romans in a double election against Frederick the Fair of Habsburg. His victory at the Battle of Mühldorf in 1322 secured the throne, and he was crowned emperor in Rome in 1328, raising the Wittelsbachs to the first rank of German dynasties.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1340": "On the extinction of the Lower Bavarian line, Emperor Louis IV reunited Bavaria. His family also held Brandenburg and soon Tyrol and the counties of Holland, Zeeland and Hainaut. Bavaria stood at the centre of the most extensive power the Wittelsbachs ever assembled, its peak of influence in the Empire.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1349": "Two years after Louis IV\u0027s death, his six sons divided their inheritance, and Bavaria was again partitioned. The wider Wittelsbach holdings soon slipped away: Brandenburg and Tyrol were lost within a generation. The duchy returned to the status of a divided regional power overshadowed by Luxembourgs and Habsburgs.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1392": "Bavaria was divided into three duchies based at Munich, Landshut and Ingolstadt, alongside Straubing. Fraternal rivalries and wars between the lines followed throughout the 15th century. The Landshut dukes grew rich, founding the University of Ingolstadt in 1472, but Bavaria as a whole remained fragmented and politically weak.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1505": "The Cologne arbitration ended the Landshut War of Succession and reunited most Bavarian lands under Albert IV of Munich, though the Palatinate-Neuburg was created for his rivals. In 1506 Albert decreed primogeniture, ending partitions and laying the foundations of an undivided, centralised Bavarian state.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1609": "Maximilian I, having reformed finances and administration, founded the Catholic League of German princes in response to the Protestant Union. Having seized Donauwörth in 1607, he led the most effective Catholic military organisation in the Empire, making Bavaria the principal Catholic power after the Habsburgs.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1620": "The army of the Catholic League under Count Tilly, with Maximilian present, shared in the victory over the Bohemian rebels at the Battle of White Mountain. Maximilian occupied Upper Austria as a pledge for his costs and became the emperor\u0027s indispensable ally at the start of the Thirty Years\u0027 War.",
  "Duchy of Bavaria|1623": "At Regensburg Emperor Ferdinand II transferred the electoral dignity of the Palatinate to Maximilian I. The Duchy of Bavaria became the Electorate of Bavaria, joining the small circle of princes who elected the emperor. The duchy ended at a high point of power and was transformed rather than destroyed.",
  "Avar Khaganate|567": "Bayan I, allied with the Lombard king Alboin, destroyed the Kingdom of the Gepids. Having already subdued steppe peoples such as the Kutrigurs and fought the Franks, the Avars now gained a foothold in the eastern Carpathian basin, though they still shared the region with the Lombards and depended on Byzantine payments.",
  "Avar Khaganate|568": "The Lombards departed for Italy, leaving the whole Carpathian basin to the Avars. Bayan now controlled a defensible plain with pasture for his horsemen and a large subject population of Gepids, Slavs and Romanised provincials, the territorial foundation of the khaganate for the next two and a half centuries.",
  "Avar Khaganate|582": "After a blockade lasting about three years, the Byzantine city of Sirmium surrendered to Bayan. Its fall opened the Danube frontier, and the Avars soon took Singidunum and raided deep into the Balkans. Byzantium, engaged in war with Persia, could only buy peace with rising annual payments of gold.",
  "Avar Khaganate|599": "Byzantine generals under the emperor Maurice, freed from the Persian war, campaigned north of the Danube. Priscus defeated the Avars near Viminacium and on the Tisza, inflicting heavy losses and capturing many prisoners. The khaganate was put on the defensive for the first time, though its core remained intact.",
  "Avar Khaganate|602": "Maurice was overthrown and killed after his army mutinied rather than winter north of the Danube. The Byzantine counter-offensive collapsed, and the long war with Persia that followed drew imperial forces east. The Avars regained the initiative, and Slav settlement south of the Danube accelerated in the following decades.",
  "Avar Khaganate|623": "The khagan nearly captured the emperor Heraclius near Heraclea in Thrace during a meeting, probably in 623 (some date it to 617), and Byzantium agreed to pay reportedly 200,000 solidi a year. With the empire fighting for its survival against Persia, the khaganate stood at the height of its power, dominating the Balkans and the Slavs of central Europe.",
  "Avar Khaganate|626": "The khagan besieged Constantinople in coordination with a Persian army on the Asian shore. The land walls held, and the Slav boat fleet was destroyed in the Golden Horn. The failure shattered Avar prestige, and many Slav subjects and allies broke away, ending the khaganate\u0027s period of great-power expansion.",
  "Avar Khaganate|631": "A struggle for the khaganate broke out between an Avar and a Bulgar candidate. The Avar prevailed, and according to Fredegar thousands of defeated Bulgar families fled to Frankish territory. The khaganate survived the crisis but had lost much of its cohesion and the confidence of its subject peoples.",
  "Avar Khaganate|635": "Around this time Kubrat of the Onogur Bulgars threw off Avar overlordship and founded Great Bulgaria north of the Black Sea, while Samo\u0027s Slav realm to the northwest remained independent. Cut off from the steppe and from Byzantine tribute, the khaganate contracted to the Carpathian basin.",
  "Avar Khaganate|680": "A Late Avar culture took shape, marked by cast bronze belt fittings with griffins and tendrils, and possibly by the arrival of new steppe groups. Around this time Kuver led a mixed population south towards Thessalonica. The khaganate was now a settled regional power, secure within the Carpathian basin.",
  "Avar Khaganate|720": "The 8th century was a period of internal stability, about which written sources say little. Cemeteries show a dense, increasingly agricultural population across the basin, and the khaganate kept its borders against Franks, Bavarians and Bulgars. It remained a respected regional power, though no longer a threat to Constantinople.",
  "Avar Khaganate|788": "After Charlemagne deposed Tassilo III of Bavaria, Avar armies attacked Bavaria and Friuli, perhaps in alliance with the deposed duke. Both attacks were defeated. The khaganate now faced the full power of the Frankish kingdom on its western border, and Charlemagne began preparing a war of conquest.",
  "Avar Khaganate|791": "Charlemagne led a great campaign down both banks of the Danube as far as the Raab. The Avars avoided battle and withdrew, but the expedition revealed their weakness. A horse epidemic forced the Franks to return, and internal tension among Avar leaders grew in the following years.",
  "Avar Khaganate|796": "After a civil war in which the khagan and the jugurrus were killed, Frankish forces under Eric of Friuli and Pippin of Italy captured and destroyed the Ring in 795–796, carrying off its treasure. Avar leaders submitted to Frankish lordship and accepted Christianity, ending the khaganate as an independent power.",
  "Avar Khaganate|805": "A Christian khagan named Theodore, pressed by Slav attacks, asked Charlemagne for land between Savaria and Carnuntum. The Avars survived as a small dependent group within the Frankish sphere, while the eastern parts of their old territory were probably taken by the First Bulgarian Empire.",
  "Avar Khaganate|822": "Avar envoys appeared at the assembly of Louis the Pious at Frankfurt, the last mention of the Avars in written sources. The remaining population merged with Slavs and others in the Frankish frontier marches of Pannonia, and the name disappeared from history.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|654": "Oswiu of Bernicia, after killing his Deiran rival Oswine in 651, was working to bring both northern kingdoms permanently under his house, though Deira was held by his nephew Œthelwald and both faced Penda of Mercia. The union already commanded the north from the Humber to the Forth, but it remained fragile.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|655": "Oswiu defeated and killed Penda of Mercia at the Battle of the Winwaed, despite Œthelwald of Deira deserting him. He then ruled Mercia directly, held lordship over the Picts and Scots, and installed his son Alhfrith in Deira. Northumbria was the dominant kingdom of Britain, its greatest moment of power.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|658": "Mercian nobles rose against Northumbrian rule and placed Wulfhere on the throne, ending Oswiu\u0027s control of Mercia. Northumbria lost its southern empire but kept its overlordship over the northern peoples and its large territory, remaining the strongest kingdom north of the Humber and one of the leading powers of Britain.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|664": "Oswiu presided over the Synod of Whitby, which adopted Roman practice for calculating Easter. The decision aligned the Northumbrian church with Canterbury and Rome. Under Oswiu and then Ecgfrith from 670, the kingdom remained large and powerful, with a flourishing church and overlordship over the Picts to the north.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|679": "Ecgfrith was defeated by Æthelred of Mercia at the Battle of the Trent, where his brother Ælfwine was killed. Through the mediation of Archbishop Theodore, peace was made, but Lindsey was lost and Northumbrian claims south of the Humber ended. The kingdom still dominated the north, including much of Pictland.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|685": "Ecgfrith invaded Pictland and was killed with most of his army by Bridei son of Beli at the Battle of Nechtansmere. Bede writes that the kingdom\u0027s power began to ebb from that day. The Picts, Scots and Britons regained independence, and the bishopric at Abercorn was abandoned.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|705": "Aldfrith, a scholar king educated in Ireland, died after twenty years of peaceful rule in which Wearmouth-Jarrow and Lindisfarne flourished. His death brought a disputed succession and his young son Osred to the throne. Northumbria was a smaller power, but its monasteries were at the height of their cultural achievement.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|735": "Ecgberht, brother of the future king Eadberht, secured the pallium and York became the seat of an archbishop, the second in England. Bede died in the same year. Under the two brothers, ruling from 737, the kingdom regained stability, and York\u0027s cathedral school became a major centre of learning.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|756": "Eadberht, who had taken Kyle in Ayrshire in 750, joined the Pictish king Óengus in forcing the Britons of Dumbarton to submit. Days later, according to a northern annal, most of his army was destroyed. The campaign marked the last major Northumbrian expansion and showed the limits of its strength.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|758": "Eadberht abdicated to become a monk at York, and his son Oswulf was murdered within a year. The kingdom entered a period of constant dynastic violence, with rival families contesting the throne. Northumbria\u0027s capacity to act beyond its borders declined sharply, although its church and scholarship remained strong.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|774": "King Alhred was deposed by his nobles and fled to the Picts. Over the following decades almost every Northumbrian king was deposed, exiled or killed, including Æthelred I, murdered in 796. The kingdom was politically weak, with royal authority dependent on aristocratic factions and frequent violent changes of ruler.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|793": "Vikings sacked the monastery of Lindisfarne in June, an attack that shocked western Christendom and was lamented by Alcuin. Jarrow was raided the following year. Northumbria remained politically divided, and the raids opened a period in which its wealthy coastal monasteries were exposed to seaborne attack.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|829": "The Northumbrians submitted to Ecgberht of Wessex at Dore, near the Mercian border, after he had conquered Mercia. The submission was brief and largely symbolic, and Northumbria soon regained its independence, but it showed the kingdom was no longer a match for the southern English powers.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|867": "The Great Heathen Army seized York in late 866. In March 867 the rival kings Osberht and Ælla, reconciled, attacked the city and were both killed. The Vikings installed a dependent English king, and Northumbria lost control of its southern heartland and its principal city.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|876": "Halfdan, a leader of the Great Heathen Army, shared out the lands of southern Northumbria among his followers, who settled and farmed them. The Viking Kingdom of York took shape, while English rulers held only the north from Bamburgh. The old Northumbrian kingdom survived only as a divided remnant.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|927": "Æthelstan of England took York and received the submission of the northern rulers, including Ealdred of Bamburgh, at Eamont. Northumbria came under the English king for the first time, though Norse kings from Dublin would repeatedly reclaim York in the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|948": "Eric Bloodaxe, a Norse king, was accepted at York, and the English king Eadred ravaged Northumbria in response, burning Ripon. York changed hands between Eric, Olaf Sihtricson and the English several times, a sign that the northern kingdom now existed only as a prize contested by outsiders.",
  "Kingdom of Northumbria|954": "The Northumbrians expelled Eric Bloodaxe, who was traditionally killed at Stainmore. Eadred took control of York, and Northumbria was absorbed into the Kingdom of England, governed thereafter by earls, notably of the house of Bamburgh. The last remnant of an independent Northumbrian kingdom disappeared.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|681": "After routing the army of Constantine IV in 680, Asparuh\u0027s Bulgars crossed the Danube, and Byzantium made peace, agreeing to pay tribute. The new state, with its centre at Pliska, joined a Bulgar warrior elite with Slav groups in Moesia. It was small and untested but had won recognition from the empire.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|705": "Khan Tervel helped the deposed emperor Justinian II regain his throne and received the title of caesar and territory south of the Balkan mountains. Bulgaria had become a recognised power in Byzantine politics, and in 717–718 Tervel\u0027s forces assisted against the Arab siege of Constantinople.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|763": "During a long internal crisis, with rapid changes of ruler and conflict between noble factions, the emperor Constantine V waged repeated campaigns against Bulgaria and won a major victory at the Battle of Anchialus. The state survived, but it was weakened and on the defensive against Byzantine power.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|792": "Khan Kardam defeated the emperor Constantine VI at the Battle of Markellai, forcing Byzantium to resume tribute payments. The victory ended decades of crisis and restored stability to the khanate, preparing the ground for the expansion that followed under Krum early in the next century.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|809": "Krum, who had probably extended Bulgar rule into the former Avar lands in the northwest, captured the Byzantine city of Serdica, modern Sofia, and destroyed its garrison. Bulgaria now reached deep into the central Balkans and was a direct threat to Byzantine control of Thrace and Macedonia.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|811": "The emperor Nikephoros I invaded and burned Pliska, but Krum trapped his army in a mountain pass and destroyed it. The emperor was killed, the first Roman emperor to die in battle since 378, and Krum had his skull made into a drinking cup. Bulgarian prestige rose sharply.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|813": "Krum defeated Michael I at the Battle of Versinikia, took Adrianople and appeared before the walls of Constantinople. He died in 814 while preparing a further attack. Bulgaria had become the most dangerous enemy of Byzantium in Europe, controlling much of Thrace and the central Balkans.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|816": "Omurtag concluded a thirty-year peace with Byzantium that fixed the frontier in Thrace. He turned to building palaces and fortifications at Pliska and on the Danube, and to campaigns against the Franks in the northwest. Bulgaria consolidated its gains and remained a strong, stable power.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|864": "Boris I accepted baptism from Byzantium, taking the name Michael, and imposed Christianity on his people, crushing a revolt of pagan nobles in 866. The conversion integrated Bulgaria into Christian Europe and strengthened royal power, though it opened a contest between Rome and Constantinople over its church.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|893": "The Council of Preslav confirmed the deposition of Vladimir-Rasate, who had tried to restore paganism, and enthroned Simeon I. The capital moved to Preslav, and Slavonic letters taught by Methodius\u0027s disciples were promoted. Bulgaria entered its most powerful and culturally creative period.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|896": "After a trade dispute, Simeon I fought Byzantium and its Magyar allies. Bringing in the Pechenegs, who drove the Magyars west towards Pannonia, he defeated a Byzantine army at Bulgarophygon. The peace that followed renewed Byzantine payments and confirmed Bulgaria as the leading Balkan power.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|917": "Simeon destroyed a large Byzantine army at the Battle of Achelous near Anchialus, one of the heaviest defeats in Byzantine history, and soon after won again at Katasyrtai near Constantinople. Bulgaria dominated most of the Balkan peninsula, and Simeon claimed the imperial title of tsar.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|924": "Simeon conquered Serbia, bringing the Bulgarian realm to its greatest extent, from the Danube to the Aegean and the Adriatic. He met the emperor Romanos I before Constantinople but could not take the city, which lacked a Bulgarian fleet. The empire stood at its territorial and political peak.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|927": "Simeon died, and his son Peter I made peace with Byzantium, married Maria Lekapene and received recognition as tsar and a patriarchate for his church. Serbia soon regained independence. Bulgaria remained large and respected, but the peace began a long period without major expansion.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|968": "Encouraged by Byzantium, Prince Sviatoslav of Kiev invaded Bulgaria and took much of the north. Magyar raids and the rise of Bogomilism had already weakened the state. Peter I died in 969, and Sviatoslav returned to occupy Preslav, leaving Bulgarian authority in the east in collapse.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|971": "John I Tzimiskes captured Preslav and forced Sviatoslav out after the siege of Dorostolon. Tsar Boris II was taken to Constantinople and stripped of his regalia, and eastern Bulgaria was annexed. Bulgarian independence survived only in the western lands, beyond direct Byzantine control.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|976": "On the death of John I Tzimiskes, the four Cometopuli brothers rose in the west. Their revolt revived Bulgarian rule in Macedonia and the western Balkans, though the state now lacked its old capitals and depended on the leadership of the youngest brother, Samuel, who soon became its sole ruler.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|986": "Samuel defeated Basil II at the Gates of Trajan, a pass near Serdica, nearly capturing the emperor. He went on to extend his power to Thessaly, Dyrrhachium and Duklja. Ruling from Prespa and Ohrid, he rebuilt a substantial western Bulgarian realm, though it never equalled Simeon\u0027s.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|997": "Samuel took the title of tsar after the death of Roman, son of Peter I, while still in captivity. In the same year his army was badly defeated by the Byzantines on the Spercheios river. His state remained extensive, but from 1001 Basil II began a sustained war of attrition against it.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|1014": "Basil II destroyed Samuel\u0027s army at the Battle of Kleidion. A later chronicler claims some 15,000 prisoners were blinded, a figure treated with caution. Samuel died in October. The Bulgarian state survived only as a collection of fortresses defended by quarrelling successors.",
  "First Bulgarian Empire|1018": "After Tsar Ivan Vladislav was killed besieging Dyrrhachium, the Bulgarian nobility and Tsaritsa Maria surrendered to Basil II at Ohrid. The First Bulgarian Empire was absorbed into Byzantium, though the archbishopric of Ohrid kept its autonomy. Bulgaria regained independence only in 1185.",
  "Republic of Venice|697": "Venetian tradition dates the election of the first doge, Paolo Lucio Anafesto, to this year, though he may be legendary. The lagoon communities were a small, poor frontier district of the Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna, living from fishing, salt and coastal shipping, with no independent foreign policy of their own.",
  "Republic of Venice|810": "Pepin of Italy attacked the lagoon and took Chioggia and Pellestrina but could not conquer the central islands. The Peace of Aachen of 812 left Venice under distant Byzantine sovereignty, and the government moved to the Rialto, the core of the future city. Venice was surviving between two empires rather than shaping events.",
  "Republic of Venice|840": "With Saint Mark’s relics brought from Alexandria in 828 and the Pactum Lotharii of 840 regulating relations with the Carolingian kingdom of Italy, Venice behaved increasingly as an independent state. Its merchants traded between the Frankish world and Byzantium, but its power was still modest and local.",
  "Republic of Venice|1000": "Doge Pietro II Orseolo led a fleet down the Dalmatian coast and won the submission of its towns, taking the title Duke of the Dalmatians. Venice now policed the Adriatic against Narentine pirates and held trading privileges in Byzantium, becoming the leading sea power of the northern Adriatic.",
  "Republic of Venice|1082": "In return for naval help against the Normans of Robert Guiscard, Emperor Alexios I Komnenos granted Venetians tax-free trade throughout the Byzantine Empire and a quarter in Constantinople. The privilege made Venice the chief western trader in the East and laid the basis of its commercial wealth.",
  "Republic of Venice|1204": "The Fourth Crusade, transported by the Venetian fleet under Doge Enrico Dandolo, captured Constantinople. Venice claimed three-eighths of the Byzantine Empire, acquired Crete, Modon and Coron, and dominated trade in the Aegean and Black Sea. It had become a colonial power with a true maritime empire.",
  "Republic of Venice|1381": "The War of Chioggia ended with the Peace of Turin after Venice had narrowly survived a Genoese fleet inside its own lagoon. The cost was heavy, and Dalmatia had already passed to Hungary in 1358, but Genoa never again threatened it seriously, leaving Venice the strongest trading power in the eastern Mediterranean.",
  "Republic of Venice|1428": "Expansion on the Italian mainland reached its widest extent with the conquest of Brescia in 1426 and Bergamo in 1428, under Doge Francesco Foscari. Venice now ruled a Terraferma stretching to the Adda, alongside a recovered Dalmatia, and ranked among the leading states of Italy as well as a sea power.",
  "Republic of Venice|1489": "Caterina Cornaro, the Venetian-born queen of Cyprus, ceded her kingdom to the republic. Venice held its largest combination of mainland territory, Aegean and Ionian islands, Dalmatian ports and Levantine trade, and its commerce, industry and printing were at their height. This was the peak of Venetian power, just before the Portuguese reached India.",
  "Republic of Venice|1509": "The League of Cambrai, uniting the pope, France, the emperor and Spain, routed the Venetian army at the Battle of Agnadello and overran the Terraferma. Diplomacy and the divisions of its enemies allowed Venice to recover most of its mainland by 1517, but its ambitions in Italy were permanently checked.",
  "Republic of Venice|1571": "Venice lost Cyprus to the Ottomans after the fall of Famagusta, even as its galleys formed about half of the Holy League fleet that won the Battle of Lepanto. The republic remained rich, with a strong navy and a flourishing culture, but it was now clearly on the defensive in the East.",
  "Republic of Venice|1669": "Candia, the capital of Crete, surrendered to the Ottomans after a siege of more than twenty years. The Cretan War exhausted Venetian finances and cost the republic its largest and most valuable colony. Venice survived as a respected second-rank power with the Ionian Islands and Dalmatia.",
  "Republic of Venice|1699": "Under Francesco Morosini Venice had conquered the Morea, and the Treaty of Karlowitz confirmed its possession. The gain briefly restored prestige, but holding the peninsula strained resources, and the republic depended on its alliance with Austria and the Holy League rather than on its own strength.",
  "Republic of Venice|1718": "The Ottomans retook the Morea in 1715, and the Peace of Passarowitz confirmed the loss. Venice kept Corfu and the Ionian Islands, whose defence in 1716 was its last great feat of arms, and parts of Dalmatia. Thereafter it followed a policy of neutrality as a minor power.",
  "Republic of Venice|1797": "Napoleon’s invasion of northern Italy left neutral Venice defenceless. On 12 May the Great Council voted to dissolve the republic and Doge Ludovico Manin abdicated. By the Treaty of Campo Formio France handed Venice to the Habsburg Monarchy, ending eleven centuries of independent statehood.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|700": "Bulgar groups that had left Old Great Bulgaria after the Khazar conquest were moving north towards the middle Volga. The date is a convenient approximation for a gradual migration known mainly from archaeology. The newcomers were still pastoral clans under Khazar supremacy, not yet an organised state.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|790": "By the late eighth century Bulgar clans were settling around the confluence of the Volga and the Kama and imposing their rule on Finnic and other local peoples. They remained tributaries of the Khazar Khaganate, and their power was regional, based on control of land, people and the first river trade.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|900": "Islamic silver poured through the middle Volga as the fur trade with Central Asia boomed under the Samanids. Bolghar was becoming a commercial centre where northern furs met Muslim merchants, and the ruler had begun striking imitation dirhams, but the Bulgars still paid tribute and gave hostages to the Khazars.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|922": "The ruler Almish received the embassy of the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir, described by Ibn Fadlan, publicly adopted Islam and took the name Jafar. Recognition by the caliph gave the Bulgar ruler new legitimacy and links to the Muslim world, though he still sought help against Khazar overlordship.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|965": "Sviatoslav I of Kiev destroyed the power of the Khazar Khaganate. Freed from Khazar tribute, the Bulgars became fully independent and controlled the middle Volga trade on their own account. This marked the beginning of Volga Bulgaria as a sovereign regional power, though Rus pressure now grew.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|985": "The Russian Primary Chronicle records a campaign by Vladimir of Kiev against the Bulgars, ending in a peace treaty. Bulgar power was checked rather than broken, and trade with the Rus continued, but the episode showed the new strength of the Kievan state to the west.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1050": "The middle decades of the eleventh century appear to have been a period of consolidation and prosperity, although sources are thin. Islam was spreading in the towns, crafts flourished in Bolghar and Suvar, and the Bulgars remained the main intermediaries between the northern forests and Muslim Asia.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1107": "A Bulgar army besieged Suzdal, showing that Volga Bulgaria could carry war into the heart of the north-eastern Rus lands. The state was at the height of its regional strength, though the growing principality of Vladimir-Suzdal was becoming a serious rival for control of the upper and middle Volga.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1164": "Andrei Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal led a major campaign against the Bulgars and took the town of Bryakhimov. The defeat cost prestige but not territory or prosperity, and the core of the country, with its growing city of Bilyar, remained intact and wealthy.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1200": "Around this time Volga Bulgaria was probably at its height. Bilyar had become one of the largest fortified cities of eastern Europe, Bolghar remained a great market, and the state controlled a wide network of tributary peoples and trade routes reaching the far north and Central Asia.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1220": "After the Bulgars took Ustyug around 1218, a Vladimir-Suzdal army sacked the Bulgar town of Oshel in 1220, and in 1221 Yuri II founded Nizhny Novgorod as a base against them. Bulgar expansion up the Volga was blocked, and the balance of power on the river was shifting.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1223": "Bulgar forces ambushed and defeated part of the Mongol army of Subutai and Jebe near the Samara Bend as it returned from the Battle of the Kalka River. The victory showed real military capacity, but it also made Volga Bulgaria a target for Mongol revenge.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1229": "From about this time Mongol forces campaigned on the lower Volga and the Yaik, defeating the Bulgars’ steppe neighbours and harrying their frontier posts. The Bulgars took in refugees and strengthened their defences, but they were now exposed to an enemy of overwhelming strength.",
  "Volga Bulgaria|1236": "A large Mongol army under Batu and Subutai invaded in the autumn, burned Bilyar and other towns and killed or enslaved much of the population. Volga Bulgaria ceased to exist as an independent state and was absorbed into the Ulus of Jochi, the later Golden Horde.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|718": "Pelagius led the Asturians in revolt against Muslim rule and won a skirmish at Covadonga, dated traditionally to 718 though many historians prefer 722. His realm was a tiny mountain lordship centred on Cangas de Onís, surviving mainly because the Muslim conquerors had little interest in the poor and remote north.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|739": "After Favila’s death, Alfonso I, son of Duke Peter of Cantabria and husband of Pelagius’s daughter, became king and united Asturias with Cantabria. The kingdom was still small, but the union doubled its territory and gave it a dynasty that would rule for most of its history.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|750": "The Berber revolt of the 740s and a famine led Muslim garrisons to abandon the north-west. Alfonso I raided the Douro valley, took towns such as Lugo, León and Astorga, and brought their Christian inhabitants north. The kingdom extended its reach into Galicia, gained people, and left a thinly settled buffer zone to the south.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|768": "Fruela I, founder of Oviedo, was murdered by members of his own court, and several weak reigns followed in which the court moved to Pravia and the kingdom avoided war with Córdoba, possibly paying tribute. Asturias survived but was politically unstable and on the defensive.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|791": "Alfonso II the Chaste became king, beginning a reign of more than fifty years. He made Oviedo the royal capital, built churches and a palace, and gave the monarchy a more stable and Gothic-inspired form, though the kingdom remained exposed to Córdoban raids.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|795": "Armies of the emir Hisham I sacked Oviedo in 794 and again in 795, despite Alfonso II’s victory at Lutos. The raids showed how vulnerable the young kingdom still was to the far stronger Emirate of Córdoba, though the mountains again prevented any lasting occupation.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|798": "Alfonso II raided as far as Lisbon and sent captured spoils to Charlemagne, according to Frankish annals. Contacts with the Carolingian court gave the kingdom international recognition, and the discovery of the tomb of Saint James at Compostela in the following decades added religious prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|842": "Alfonso II died after a long and successful reign, and Ramiro I secured the throne after a contest with a rival. Ramiro repelled a Viking raid in 844 and built the halls and churches of Monte Naranco. The kingdom was stable, but its frontiers had changed little.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|859": "Ordoño I defeated Musa ibn Musa of the Banu Qasi near Albelda, according to the chronicles, and resettled León, Astorga and Tui. The kingdom was beginning to move out of the mountains and onto the plains of the Douro basin, gaining land and people.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|866": "Alfonso III succeeded his father Ordoño I and, after overcoming a rebellion, began the most expansive reign in Asturian history. The kingdom had a secure core and a growing network of frontier counts in Galicia and Castile ready to push south.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|878": "Hermenegildo Guterres took Coimbra, ten years after Vímara Peres had taken Porto, and Alfonso III’s forces defeated a Córdoban army at Polvoraria. The western frontier now reached the Mondego, and the County of Portugal had been created. Asturias had become a real regional power.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|899": "With fortified towns at Zamora, Simancas, Toro and Burgos, the frontier ran along the Douro, while the emirate of Córdoba was weakened by rebellions. The new basilica of Saint James at Compostela was consecrated in 899. The kingdom was at its greatest extent and prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|910": "Alfonso III was forced to give up power by his sons and died at Zamora. The kingdom was divided: García I took León, Ordoño II Galicia and Fruela II Asturias. The Asturian core was now only one of three linked realms, and the political centre had moved south.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|914": "On García I’s death Ordoño II took León, keeping Galicia, and León became the principal kingdom. Fruela II’s Asturias was reduced to a mountain realm overshadowed by its southern neighbour, which now led the war against Córdoba and held the resettled lands of the Douro. Oviedo kept its bishop and royal tombs but no longer ruled.",
  "Kingdom of Asturias|924": "Ordoño II died, and Fruela II of Asturias succeeded him as king of León, reuniting the inheritance under the crown of León. Asturias ceased to exist as a separate kingdom and became a region of the Kingdom of León, though Oviedo remained an important religious centre.",
  "Papal States|754": "Pope Stephen II crossed the Alps and won the protection of Pepin the Short against the Lombards, who had taken Ravenna. Papal territory was limited to the Duchy of Rome and church estates, and the pope depended entirely on Frankish arms, but the alliance laid the legal basis of the papal state.",
  "Papal States|774": "Charlemagne conquered the Lombard kingdom and confirmed to Pope Hadrian I the donation of his father, covering the former Exarchate of Ravenna and the Duchy of Rome. Papal government was real in Rome and its region, though the Franks claimed supervision and the Ravenna lands were hard to control.",
  "Papal States|800": "Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne emperor in Saint Peter’s. The ceremony sealed the partnership between the papacy and the Frankish empire and enhanced papal prestige, though the Constitutio Romana of 824 soon placed papal elections and Roman government under imperial oversight.",
  "Papal States|880": "With the Carolingian empire disintegrating, Arab raiders active in Italy and Roman aristocratic factions fighting over the papal throne, the popes lost effective control of most of their lands. The coming decades, when the house of Theophylact dominated Rome, mark the lowest point of papal temporal power.",
  "Papal States|962": "Otto I was crowned emperor in Rome and issued the Diploma Ottonianum confirming papal territories, but he also claimed the right to approve papal elections. The pope’s lands were protected, yet emperors and Roman nobles continued to make and unmake popes for decades.",
  "Papal States|1198": "Innocent III became pope and quickly recovered the Duchy of Spoleto and the March of Ancona from imperial officials after the death of Emperor Henry VI. He organised the lands into provinces under rectors, giving the papal state a much broader territorial base than before.",
  "Papal States|1216": "At Innocent III’s death the papal state stretched from Rome across central Italy to the Adriatic, and the papacy was at the height of its medieval political influence. Communes and local lords still enjoyed wide autonomy, but papal claims of sovereignty were widely recognised.",
  "Papal States|1309": "Clement V settled at Avignon, where the popes lived until 1377. In their absence Rome declined, Roman nobles and local tyrants took control of much of the state, and papal authority in Italy almost collapsed, despite large revenues collected from the wider church.",
  "Papal States|1357": "Cardinal Albornoz, sent from Avignon, reconquered much of the state with mercenary armies, built fortresses and issued the Egidian Constitutions at Fano. These laws gave the papal state a lasting legal framework and prepared the way for the return of the popes to Rome.",
  "Papal States|1378": "The return of Gregory XI to Rome was followed by the Great Western Schism, with rival popes in Rome and Avignon. Divided obedience, war with Florence and the ambitions of neighbouring rulers again weakened papal control over the provinces and drained its revenues.",
  "Papal States|1420": "Martin V, elected at the Council of Constance, entered Rome and began rebuilding papal government and the city itself. Recovery was slow and many cities remained under local lords as papal vicars, but the papacy was once more a resident Italian territorial power.",
  "Papal States|1503": "Cesare Borgia, backed by his father Alexander VI and by France, had overthrown the lords of the Romagna and created a strong territorial block in the north-east of the state. On Alexander’s death these conquests passed to the papacy itself.",
  "Papal States|1512": "Julius II had taken Perugia and Bologna in 1506 and, after the wars of the League of Cambrai and the Holy League, added Parma and Piacenza. The papal state was at its greatest effective power, a major player in Italian wars and the patron of Michelangelo and Raphael.",
  "Papal States|1527": "The mutinous army of Charles V sacked Rome, imprisoning Clement VII in Castel Sant’Angelo. The disaster ended the papacy’s ambitions as an independent military power in Italy; thereafter it survived as a smaller state within a Spanish-dominated peninsula, though its administration grew stronger.",
  "Papal States|1598": "Clement VIII absorbed the Duchy of Ferrara after the extinction of the main Este line, and Urbino would follow in 1631. The state reached its fullest territorial extent, its central government had been reorganised by Sixtus V, and Baroque Rome was the capital of Catholic Europe.",
  "Papal States|1700": "The papal state remained intact but had become a minor power, unable to defend its interests in the wars of the great Catholic monarchies and burdened by debt. Its role in European diplomacy shrank, though Rome remained a great centre of art, pilgrimage and learning.",
  "Papal States|1797": "Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, Ferrara and Romagna at the Treaty of Tolentino and pay a large indemnity, after France had annexed Avignon in 1791. The following year a Roman Republic was proclaimed and the pope taken into exile.",
  "Papal States|1809": "Napoleon annexed the remaining Papal States to the French Empire, and Pius VII, who excommunicated him, was arrested and held at Savona and Fontainebleau. For the first time since the eighth century the pope ruled no territory at all, and Rome became the second city of the French Empire.",
  "Papal States|1815": "The Congress of Vienna restored almost all the Papal States, except Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin, and Cardinal Consalvi introduced administrative reforms. The restored state was intact but dependent on Austrian support and faced growing liberal and national opposition from its own subjects.",
  "Papal States|1849": "After the murder of the minister Pellegrino Rossi, Pius IX fled to Gaeta and a Roman Republic was proclaimed under Giuseppe Mazzini. French troops crushed it and restored the pope, who now ruled with the help of a French garrison in Rome and Austrian forces in the north.",
  "Papal States|1860": "The Romagna joined Piedmont, the papal army was defeated at Castelfidardo, and the Marche and Umbria voted to join the new Kingdom of Italy. The pope was left with only Rome and Lazio, protected by French troops, a remnant of his former state.",
  "Papal States|1870": "After France withdrew its garrison during the Franco-Prussian War, Italian troops entered Rome through the breach at Porta Pia on 20 September. The Papal States ceased to exist. Pius IX refused to accept the loss, and the dispute lasted until the Lateran Treaty of 1929.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|756": "Abd al-Rahman I defeated the governor Yusuf al-Fihri at al-Musara and became emir at Córdoba, independent of the Abbasids. His power rested on Umayyad clients and part of the Syrian army, and much of al-Andalus, including Toledo and the north-east, did not yet obey him.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|763": "The emir destroyed an army raised in the name of the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur by al-Ala ibn Mughith. The victory ended any serious Abbasid attempt to recover al-Andalus and secured the dynasty, though revolts by Arab chiefs and Berbers continued for years.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|778": "Rebels in the north-east invited Charlemagne to Zaragoza, but the Frankish expedition failed and its rearguard was destroyed at Roncesvalles. Abd al-Rahman I then campaigned in the Ebro valley to restore his authority, emerging stronger from a dangerous combination of internal revolt and foreign intervention.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|788": "Abd al-Rahman I died, leaving a consolidated state with a professional army and the first phase of the Great Mosque, begun around 785. His son Hisham I succeeded and turned the emirate’s strength against the Christian north, sacking Oviedo in 794 and 795.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|797": "Al-Hakam I faced unrest in Toledo, where according to tradition he had leading citizens massacred in the so-called Day of the Ditch. The emirate remained strong abroad, but tensions between the ruler, the jurists and the urban population were growing, and in 801 the Franks took Barcelona.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|818": "Al-Hakam I crushed the Revolt of the Arrabal in Córdoba, destroyed the suburb and expelled thousands of its inhabitants. The brutal repression ended open resistance in the capital and showed the strength of his new standing army, though it left lasting resentment.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|822": "Abd al-Rahman II came to the throne and the musician Ziryab arrived at his court from Iraq. The new emir inherited a pacified capital and a strong army, and began developing a more elaborate administration, court ceremonial and urban culture on Eastern models.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|844": "Vikings sacked Seville, but Abd al-Rahman II defeated them at Tablada and then built a fleet and shipyards to protect the coasts. The emirate was prosperous, centralised and respected abroad, exchanging embassies with Byzantium, and was approaching the height of its power.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|852": "Abd al-Rahman II died after a thirty-year reign generally regarded as the high point of the emirate. Córdoba was a flourishing capital, the Great Mosque had been enlarged, and the state’s administration, army, fleet and coinage were at their strongest, before the revolts of the next reign.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|880": "Revolts multiplied under Muhammad I: Ibn Marwan established himself at Badajoz in 875, the Banu Qasi dominated the Ebro valley and Toledo was almost independent. Around 880 Umar ibn Hafsun began his rebellion from Bobastro, opening a long civil war in the south.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|888": "The emir al-Mundhir died while besieging Bobastro, and his brother Abd Allah succeeded. The emirate was now in deep crisis, with Arab, Berber and muwallad lords throwing off its authority in many provinces, and revenues falling as taxes went unpaid.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|900": "Under Abd Allah the emirs controlled little beyond Córdoba and its surroundings. Seville and other cities were ruled by independent Arab lords, and Ibn Hafsun held much of the south, although his conversion to Christianity around 899 began to cost him Muslim support.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|913": "Abd al-Rahman III, emir since 912, recovered Seville and launched yearly campaigns against the rebels. His methodical reconquest, combining force and negotiation, steadily restored the authority of Córdoba over the provinces and the revenues that came with them, while Ibn Hafsun’s death in 917 weakened the rebels.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|928": "The fall of Bobastro, the stronghold of Ibn Hafsun’s sons, ended the great southern rebellion. Abd al-Rahman III had restored control over almost all of al-Andalus and stood ready to challenge the Fatimids in North Africa and the Christian kingdoms in the north.",
  "Emirate of Córdoba|929": "In January Abd al-Rahman III proclaimed himself caliph and Commander of the Faithful, answering the Fatimid claim and asserting his supremacy in al-Andalus. The emirate ended not in defeat but by transformation into the Caliphate of Córdoba, which reached far greater power.",
  "County of Toulouse|778": "Charlemagne appointed the Frankish noble Chorson as count of Toulouse, part of his reorganisation of Aquitaine, which soon became a subkingdom for his son Louis. The county was a royal office rather than a principality, its count an agent of the crown on a frontier exposed to Basque and Umayyad attack, so its power was modest.",
  "County of Toulouse|801": "William of Gellone, count since 790, helped Louis of Aquitaine capture Barcelona from the Umayyads. Toulouse served as a base for the Spanish March, and its count was a leading commander on the frontier, though the office still depended on royal favour and could be granted to another family at any time.",
  "County of Toulouse|852": "Raymond I succeeded his brother Fredelo, beginning the line later called the Raymondines. Civil wars among the heirs of Louis the Pious and Viking raids weakened the crown, allowing the family to turn office into inheritance, but rivals still contested the county and its resources and reach remained limited.",
  "County of Toulouse|936": "In the time of Raymond III Pons the counts held Toulouse, Rouergue and wide rights in Languedoc, and some documents call him duke of Aquitaine. Royal power in the south had almost vanished, so the counts acted as independent princes, though their authority over local castellans and viscounts was loose.",
  "County of Toulouse|1094": "Raymond IV, already lord of Saint-Gilles, Rouergue and much of Languedoc, succeeded his brother William IV as count of Toulouse, uniting the family’s lands. He was the richest lord of the south, and his wealth soon allowed him to raise one of the largest armies of the First Crusade.",
  "County of Toulouse|1105": "Raymond IV died outside Tripoli, but his crusade had made the house of Toulouse famous across Christendom, and his son Bertrand would take the city in 1109. At home the county faced the claims of William IX of Aquitaine, who occupied Toulouse more than once, so its power was high but contested.",
  "County of Toulouse|1159": "Henry II of England, claiming Toulouse through his wife Eleanor of Aquitaine, besieged the city, but Raymond V held out when Louis VII entered Toulouse to support him, and Henry withdrew. The county had withstood the greatest power of the age and still dominated much of Languedoc and lands in Provence.",
  "County of Toulouse|1196": "Raymond VI, count since 1194, married Joan of England, who brought the Agenais as her dowry, ending decades of conflict with the Angevins. The house of Toulouse stood at its widest reach and highest prestige, though the spread of Catharism in its lands was already drawing papal hostility.",
  "County of Toulouse|1209": "Pope Innocent III’s crusade reached the south: crusaders massacred Béziers, took Carcassonne and made Simon de Montfort their leader. Raymond VI submitted and briefly joined the crusaders to protect his lands, but he would soon be excommunicated again, and the county’s independence was now openly threatened.",
  "County of Toulouse|1213": "Simon de Montfort routed Peter II of Aragon and Raymond VI at the Battle of Muret, where the Aragonese king was killed. The southern coalition collapsed, Raymond went into exile, and in 1215 the Fourth Lateran Council assigned most of his lands to Simon, leaving the house of Toulouse close to destruction.",
  "County of Toulouse|1218": "Raymond VI and his son had recovered Beaucaire and Toulouse, and Simon de Montfort was killed by a stone from a siege engine while besieging the city. Southern lords returned to the Raymondines, and Simon’s son Amaury could not hold the conquests, so the county regained much of its strength.",
  "County of Toulouse|1226": "Louis VIII of France took command of a new crusade, besieged and took Avignon, and received the submission of most of Languedoc before dying on his way home. The French crown had replaced the faltering Montforts as the county’s main enemy, a far heavier threat to Raymond VII.",
  "County of Toulouse|1229": "Exhausted, Raymond VII accepted the Treaty of Paris: he did penance, ceded his eastern lands, kept a reduced county for life and married his daughter Joan to Alphonse of Poitiers, brother of Louis IX. The settlement made eventual absorption by the French crown almost certain.",
  "County of Toulouse|1243": "Raymond VII’s revolt, allied with Henry III of England and the Lusignans, collapsed after Louis IX’s victories at Taillebourg and Saintes in 1242, and the Peace of Lorris confirmed the settlement of 1229. The fall of Montségur in 1244 ended organised Cathar resistance, and the county was politically subordinate.",
  "County of Toulouse|1249": "Raymond VII died at Millau, and his daughter Joan and her husband Alphonse of Poitiers took the county. Alphonse governed largely from afar through officials on the royal model, so Toulouse had become in effect a Capetian appanage rather than an independent principality.",
  "County of Toulouse|1271": "Alphonse of Poitiers and Joan died within days of each other in Italy, returning from Louis IX’s crusade to Tunis, without children. Under the Treaty of Paris the county passed to King Philip III and was absorbed into the royal domain of the Kingdom of France, ending its separate existence.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|824": "After a Frankish expedition was destroyed in the Pyrenees around 824, Íñigo Arista emerged as ruler of Pamplona, independent of the Carolingians and allied with his Muslim kinsmen the Banu Qasi. His realm was a small mountain lordship around Pamplona, at times obliged to pay tribute to Córdoba.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|905": "Sancho Garcés I took power, founding the Jimena dynasty, broke with the Banu Qasi and pushed south towards the Ebro and La Rioja. The kingdom gained territory and prestige, but Córdoba under Abd al-Rahman III would soon show its superiority by defeating the Christians at Valdejunquera and sacking Pamplona.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|970": "Sancho II Garcés Abarca succeeded his father García Sánchez I. The kingdom also held Aragon but faced the caliphate and then the military regent al-Mansur, to whom Sancho paid tribute and gave a daughter in marriage. Pamplona survived as a minor Christian kingdom under heavy Muslim pressure.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1004": "Sancho III Garcés, later called the Great, came to the throne as a boy. The collapse of the Caliphate of Córdoba after 1009 removed the main threat from the south, and Sancho began extending his authority over the Pyrenean counties of Sobrarbe and Ribagorza, raising Pamplona to a regional power.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1029": "After the murder of Count García Sánchez of Castile in 1029, Sancho III took control of Castile through his wife Muniadona, the count’s sister, and installed their son Ferdinand. Pamplona now dominated the Christian north, ruling or overseeing lands from the Pyrenean counties to the Duero.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1034": "Sancho III occupied León, forcing Bermudo III to withdraw to the west, and some documents give him a quasi-imperial style. Ruling directly or through his sons from Ribagorza to Castile and León, he was the most powerful ruler in Christian Iberia, and the kingdom stood at its absolute peak.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1054": "Sancho III’s death in 1035 divided his lands among his sons. García Sánchez III kept Pamplona but was killed at the Battle of Atapuerca in 1054 fighting his brother Ferdinand I of Castile, who now overshadowed Navarre. The kingdom remained substantial but was no longer the leading Christian power.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1076": "Sancho IV was murdered at Peñalén, pushed from a cliff in a plot involving his own siblings. Alfonso VI of Castile seized La Rioja and the western lands, and Sancho Ramírez of Aragon became king of Pamplona. Navarre lost its own king and became the junior partner in a union with Aragon.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1134": "When Alfonso I the Battler of Aragon died without children, the Navarrese nobles chose García Ramírez the Restorer as their own king, ending the union with Aragon. The revived kingdom was independent again but hemmed in by Castile and Aragon, with no frontier left for southern expansion.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1200": "While Sancho VII was away in North Africa, Alfonso VIII of Castile conquered Álava and Gipuzkoa, including the town of Vitoria. Navarre lost its access to the Bay of Biscay and much of its western territory, reduced to the compact Pyrenean kingdom that survived into the early modern period.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1212": "Sancho VII the Strong joined Alfonso VIII of Castile and Peter II of Aragon at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa, which broke Almohad power. Navarre shared the prestige of the victory, and later tradition linked the chains on its coat of arms to the battle, but it gained no new lands.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1234": "Sancho VII died childless and the crown passed to his nephew Theobald I, count of Champagne. The kingdom entered the orbit of France, its rulers often absent but rich and well connected, and the fueros were compiled into the Fuero General to secure the rights of the Navarrese under foreign kings.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1328": "After decades of union with France following Joan I’s marriage to Philip IV, the Navarrese recognised Joan II, daughter of Louis X, who had been excluded from the French throne. With her husband Philip of Évreux she restored a separate monarchy, giving Navarre its own court again.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1387": "Charles III the Noble began a long and peaceful reign after the turbulent rule of his father Charles II the Bad. He settled disputes with France and Castile, built the palace of Olite and united Pamplona’s boroughs, giving the kingdom its last long period of stability and prosperity as an independent state.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1451": "Open war broke out between John II and his son Charles, Prince of Viana, and the nobility split into Agramontese and Beaumontese factions. Decades of conflict drained the kingdom and drew in Castile, Aragon and France, leaving Navarre’s independence dependent on the balance between its neighbours.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1512": "Ferdinand the Catholic sent the Duke of Alba to conquer Pamplona, and the Albret monarchs John III and Catherine fled north. Upper Navarre was incorporated into the Crown of Castile in 1515, keeping its laws and cortes, while only Lower Navarre beyond the Pyrenees stayed with the Albrets.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1521": "A Franco-Navarrese army briefly retook Pamplona, where the young Ignatius of Loyola was wounded defending the citadel, but it was defeated at Noáin. The failure ended realistic hopes of restoring an independent kingdom south of the Pyrenees; Navarre survived as a separate realm within the Spanish monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1589": "Henry III of Navarre became Henry IV of France, and the small Lower Navarre was joined to France in personal union, formally united in 1620. Spanish Navarre remained a kingdom with its own cortes and viceroy under the Habsburgs and later the Bourbons, autonomous but without independent power.",
  "Kingdom of Navarre|1841": "After the First Carlist War, the law of 16 August 1841, the Ley Paccionada, ended Navarre’s status as a kingdom and made it a Spanish province. It kept a special fiscal regime and its own provincial council, the root of the modern Foral Community of Navarre, but the kingdom itself ended.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|843": "Kenneth MacAlpin, a king of Gaelic descent, became ruler of the Picts around this time, the traditional starting point of the kingdom. The realm north of the Forth was still exposed to Viking attack and divided among regional powers, so its strength was limited despite the union of Pictish and Gaelic lordship.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|900": "By the reign of Constantine II, which began in 900, the kingdom was known as Alba, with its centre at Scone. His predecessor Donald II had probably been killed by Vikings, but the Gaelic realm was consolidating its church and royal institutions, though it was still confined largely to the lands north of the Forth.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1018": "Malcolm II’s victory over the Northumbrians at the Battle of Carham, around 1018, secured Lothian and the Tweed frontier, while Strathclyde came increasingly under Scottish kings. The kingdom now reached south of the Forth into English-speaking lands, enlarging its resources, though the north and west remained largely outside royal control.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1124": "David I, raised at the English court, became king and began a programme of reform: royal burghs, sheriffdoms, coinage, Anglo-Norman settlers and new monasteries. These changes gave the crown new resources and administrative reach, transforming the Gaelic kingdom of Alba into a recognisably European feudal monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1141": "Taking advantage of the English civil war between Stephen and Matilda, David I held Cumberland and, through his son Henry, Northumberland, often residing at Carlisle. Scottish royal power extended well into northern England, and the kingdom stood at its medieval territorial height under a respected and reforming king.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1174": "William the Lion was captured at Alnwick while invading England and, by the Treaty of Falaise, had to accept Henry II as his overlord and hand over key castles. Scotland was formally subject to England until 1189, when Richard I released it for a payment of 10,000 marks.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1266": "After the Battle of Largs in 1263, the Treaty of Perth transferred the Western Isles and the Isle of Man from Norway to Scotland. Under Alexander III the kingdom enjoyed peace with England, growing trade and expanded borders, a period later remembered as a golden age.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1296": "After the death of the Maid of Norway, Edward I of England chose John Balliol as king and then invaded when the Scots allied with France. He sacked Berwick, defeated the Scots at Dunbar, deposed Balliol and removed the Stone of Scone: Scottish independence was all but extinguished.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1314": "Robert the Bruce, king since 1306, had retaken most English-held castles, and at Bannockburn he routed Edward II’s much larger army. The victory secured his rule in Scotland and allowed raids deep into northern England, though England did not yet recognise Scottish independence.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1328": "By the Treaty of Edinburgh–Northampton, England recognised Robert I as king of an independent Scotland and abandoned its claim to overlordship. The kingdom had survived three decades of war and stood united under a strong and legitimate monarch, though Robert died the following year leaving a five-year-old heir.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1346": "Invading England in support of France, David II was defeated and captured at the Battle of Neville’s Cross and held in England for eleven years. Coming after Halidon Hill in 1333 and the return of Edward Balliol, the defeat left Scotland weakened, divided and later burdened with a heavy ransom.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1371": "Robert II, grandson of Robert the Bruce through his mother, became the first Stewart king. Royal government was weak and great magnates such as the Douglases dominated, but Scotland had recovered most of its territory from English occupation and maintained its independence through the alliance with France.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1488": "James IV became king after his father James III was killed during a rebellion. Over the next quarter-century he forfeited the Lordship of the Isles, built a navy, encouraged printing and learning and married Margaret Tudor, presiding over a strong and prosperous Renaissance court.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1513": "James IV invaded England in support of France and was killed at the Battle of Flodden with many nobles and churchmen. The disaster left an infant king, James V, and a long minority, setting back royal authority and leaving the kingdom open to factional struggle and English pressure.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1560": "The Reformation Parliament rejected papal authority and adopted a Protestant confession, after English intervention ended French influence by the Treaty of Edinburgh. Though Mary, Queen of Scots, returned in 1561 and was later deposed, Scotland emerged with a new national church and closer ties to England.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1603": "James VI inherited the thrones of England and Ireland on the death of Elizabeth I, becoming the first king to rule all of Britain. Scotland’s dynasty now ruled the larger kingdom, giving Scots unprecedented influence, though the king’s move to London began to draw power away from Edinburgh.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1640": "Opposing Charles I’s religious policies, the Covenanters raised a large, well-led army that defeated the English at Newburn and occupied Newcastle in 1640. The Covenanting regime controlled Scotland and forced concessions from the king, giving the kingdom more weight in British affairs than ever before.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1651": "Having crowned Charles II, the Scots were defeated by Oliver Cromwell at Dunbar in 1650 and Worcester in 1651. English troops occupied the whole country, and Scotland was incorporated into the Commonwealth, losing its parliament and independence, its lowest point since the Wars of Independence.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1660": "The Restoration of Charles II revived the Scottish Parliament, Privy Council and separate institutions. Scotland was again a kingdom in its own right, though governed from London through commissioners, and conflict between the crown and Presbyterian Covenanters led to repression and armed risings in the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1700": "The collapse of the Darien colony on the Isthmus of Panama, after the famines of the 1690s, destroyed a large share of Scotland’s capital and left the kingdom economically fragile. Disputes with England over trade and the succession made union or separation the central question of Scottish politics.",
  "Kingdom of Scotland|1707": "The Acts of Union came into force on 1 May 1707, ending the separate Kingdom of Scotland and creating the Kingdom of Great Britain with a single parliament at Westminster. Scotland retained its law, church and universities, which kept a distinct national identity alive within the union.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|850": "Around the middle of the ninth century Count Liudolf held lands in eastern Saxony and a leading position among the Saxon nobility, founding Gandersheim in 852. Saxony was part of East Francia under Louis the German, and Liudolf’s power was that of a great noble rather than a duke.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|880": "Liudolf’s son Bruno, leader of the Saxons, was killed with many nobles in a battle against Viking raiders in northern Saxony. The defeat exposed the region’s vulnerability, but it also confirmed the family’s role as the natural military leaders of the Saxons in the absence of effective Carolingian protection.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|912": "Henry, son of Otto the Illustrious, succeeded as the leading prince of Saxony. The Liudolfings now controlled Saxony and much of Thuringia, and the weakness of King Conrad I allowed Henry to resist royal interference, making the duchy one of the strongest powers in East Francia.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|919": "Henry was elected king of East Francia at Fritzlar, the first Saxon to hold the throne. Saxony became the royal heartland and its duke now ruled the kingdom; Henry built fortifications against the Magyars and prepared the cavalry that defeated them at Riade in 933.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|936": "Otto I succeeded his father as king and kept Saxony under royal control, entrusting the lower Elbe frontier to Hermann Billung and the middle Elbe to Gero. Saxon kingship and its eastern marches gave the duchy great weight, although it was now subordinated to the crown.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|973": "Otto I died, and Hermann Billung, who had acted as the king’s deputy in Saxony, died the same year, leaving his position to his son Bernard. The Billung dukes held a narrower power in the north, and the Slav revolt of 983 soon swept away much of the eastern frontier.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1002": "The death of Otto III without heirs ended the direct Ottonian line, and the Bavarian Henry II became king. Saxony lost its privileged place as the royal homeland, though Saxon nobles secured recognition of their law, and the Billung dukes remained important in the north.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1075": "The Saxon War against the Salian king Henry IV brought the defeat of the Saxon rebels at Langensalza, and their leaders were imprisoned. Royal castles such as the Harzburg had provoked the rising, and the defeat showed that the Saxon nobility could not yet resist the crown on its own.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1115": "Saxon princes led by Lothair of Supplinburg, duke since 1106, defeated Emperor Henry V at the Battle of Welfesholz, ending Salian control in Saxony. The duke was again the leading figure of the Saxon nobility and a rival to the emperor himself.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1125": "Lothair of Supplinburg was elected king, and in 1133 crowned emperor, once again making the duke of Saxony the ruler of Germany. He arranged for his son-in-law Henry the Proud of the Welf family, already duke of Bavaria, to succeed him in Saxony.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1138": "Conrad III of the Staufer family, elected king instead of Henry the Proud, stripped him of Saxony and gave it to Albert the Bear. War followed in Saxony, Henry the Proud died in 1139, and the duchy was divided and weakened by the struggle between Welfs and Staufer.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1142": "A settlement restored Saxony to Henry the Proud’s young son, Henry the Lion, while Albert the Bear kept his own lands. The duchy regained unity under the Welfs, but Henry was still a minor and his authority had to be built up against powerful Saxon nobles.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1159": "Henry the Lion, also duke of Bavaria since 1156, refounded Lübeck around this time and was extending his power over the Slav lands east of the Elbe. In alliance with Frederick Barbarossa he was the most powerful prince of the empire, ruling Saxony as a near-royal territory.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1176": "Henry stood at the summit of his power, ruling from Brunswick lands from the Weser to Mecklenburg, married since 1168 to Matilda of England. His refusal to help Frederick Barbarossa in Italy that year, however, began the breach that would lead to his fall.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1180": "Barbarossa condemned Henry the Lion, and the Gelnhausen charter split Saxony: Westphalia went to the archbishop of Cologne and the reduced ducal title to Bernhard of Anhalt. The great stem duchy ceased to exist, leaving a small Ascanian duchy with scattered lands on the Elbe.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1212": "Bernhard of Anhalt died and his son Albert I became duke. The Ascanian duchy held lands around Lauenburg and Wittenberg but little of the old ducal power, while the Welfs kept Brunswick and Lüneburg and the bishops and counts of Saxony governed their own territories.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1260": "Albert I died, and his sons John I and Albert II ruled jointly, with the lands in practice divided between them. The duchy was a minor principality, notable mainly for its claim to a vote in royal elections, which its two branches would later dispute.",
  "Duchy of Saxony|1296": "Around this time the heirs of John I and Albert II completed the division into the Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg and the Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg, ending the single Duchy of Saxony. Saxe-Wittenberg later secured the electoral title, which carried the name Saxony up the Elbe to the Wettin lands.",
  "County of Flanders|862": "Baldwin I, called Iron Arm, eloped with Judith, daughter of Charles the Bald. After a reconciliation the king gave him lands around Bruges to defend the coast against Viking raids. Flanders was then a modest coastal command within West Francia rather than a principality, so its power was small.",
  "County of Flanders|918": "Baldwin II died after a long reign in which he had used the Viking crisis to build fortresses and absorb neighbouring counties and abbey lands. His marriage to Ælfthryth, daughter of Alfred the Great, linked Flanders to Wessex, and the county had become a hereditary principality.",
  "County of Flanders|965": "Arnulf I died after extending Flemish rule south to Artois and Arras and supporting monastic reform at houses such as St Peter’s in Ghent. Flanders was now one of the great principalities of West Francia, though his death brought a brief royal intervention in the south of the county.",
  "County of Flanders|1060": "Baldwin V became regent of France for the young Philip I, son of Baldwin’s brother-in-law Henry I. Holding Imperial Flanders east of the Scheldt as well, and with his daughter married to William of Normandy, he was among the most powerful princes of western Europe.",
  "County of Flanders|1127": "Count Charles the Good was murdered in Bruges, setting off a succession war in which King Louis VI imposed William Clito, who was soon resisted by the towns. Thierry of Alsace emerged as count in 1128, but the crisis revealed the new political weight of the Flemish towns.",
  "County of Flanders|1180": "Philip of Alsace, count since 1168, ruled Flanders together with Vermandois through his wife and acted as guardian of the young Philip II of France. With rich towns, efficient administration and influence at the French court, the county reached the height of its political power.",
  "County of Flanders|1191": "Philip of Alsace died at the siege of Acre, and Philip II of France secured Artois, the dowry of his wife Isabella of Hainaut, Philip’s niece. Flanders passed to Baldwin VIII, husband of Philip’s sister, smaller in extent and facing a stronger French crown.",
  "County of Flanders|1204": "Count Baldwin IX, a leader of the Fourth Crusade, was crowned the first Latin emperor of Constantinople. The county’s prestige rose sharply, but Baldwin was captured by the Bulgarians at Adrianople in 1205 and died in captivity, leaving his young daughters Joan and Margaret as heirs.",
  "County of Flanders|1214": "Count Ferrand of Portugal, husband of Countess Joan, joined the coalition against Philip II Augustus and was captured at the Battle of Bouvines. He remained a prisoner until 1227, and Flanders fell under strong French influence, governed by a countess in the shadow of the French crown.",
  "County of Flanders|1302": "Flemish town militias destroyed a French knightly army at the Battle of the Golden Spurs near Courtrai, throwing off the French occupation begun in 1300. The victory made the cloth towns the decisive power in Flanders, which stood near the height of its economic wealth.",
  "County of Flanders|1305": "After the French victory at Mons-en-Pévèle in 1304, the Treaty of Athis-sur-Orge imposed a heavy indemnity on Flanders and led to the transfer of Lille, Douai and Orchies to France in 1312. The county kept its autonomy but was financially burdened and divided between counts, towns and factions.",
  "County of Flanders|1340": "Under Jacob van Artevelde’s leadership Ghent allied with England, Edward III was proclaimed king of France in Ghent, and the English fleet won the Battle of Sluys off the Flemish coast. The towns secured their wool supply and dominated Flemish politics, though at the cost of continuing conflict with the count.",
  "County of Flanders|1384": "Count Louis of Male died, and Flanders passed to his son-in-law Philip the Bold, duke of Burgundy, after the defeat of Ghent’s rebels at Roosebeke in 1382. The county lost its separate dynasty but became the richest part of a powerful new Burgundian state.",
  "County of Flanders|1500": "Charles of Habsburg, the future Emperor Charles V, was born in Ghent. Flanders, now part of the Habsburg Netherlands, had lost much of its cloth trade and Bruges was declining as trade moved to Antwerp, but the county remained populous, prosperous and politically important within the Habsburg lands.",
  "County of Flanders|1529": "By the Treaty of Madrid of 1526 and the Peace of Cambrai of 1529, Francis I of France renounced French suzerainty over Flanders. The county ceased to be a French fief, ending centuries of divided allegiance, but it was now a province of the Habsburg Netherlands with limited independence.",
  "County of Flanders|1584": "Spanish forces under Alexander Farnese recaptured Ghent, ending its Calvinist republic, while the Dutch Revolt left Zeelandic Flanders in rebel hands. Flanders emerged ravaged by war, with many Protestants emigrating to the north, its population and economy diminished within the Spanish Netherlands.",
  "County of Flanders|1678": "By the Treaty of Nijmegen France secured further Flemish towns, after earlier gains including Lille in 1668. Louis XIV’s conquests had removed the southern part of Flanders, and the remaining county was a frontier province of the Spanish Netherlands, repeatedly a battlefield of European wars.",
  "County of Flanders|1714": "After the War of the Spanish Succession, the Treaty of Rastatt confirmed the transfer of the Spanish Netherlands, including Flanders, to the Austrian Habsburgs. The county kept its estates and privileges as a province of the Austrian Netherlands, but it had no independent policy of its own.",
  "County of Flanders|1795": "Following the Brabant Revolution and the French revolutionary conquest, the Austrian Netherlands were annexed to France on 1 October 1795. The County of Flanders was abolished and divided into the departments of Lys and Escaut, ending more than nine centuries of existence.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|872": "By tradition Harald Fairhair defeated rival kings at Hafrsfjord around this date, beginning the unification of Norway. His real authority probably covered only the western and southern coast, while the earls of Lade and the inland chieftains remained largely independent, so the new kingdom was a loose overlordship rather than a state.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|950": "Haakon the Good, raised in England, ruled much of Norway in the mid-tenth century and is credited with organising the coastal levy and the law assemblies of the Gulating and Frostating. His attempt to introduce Christianity failed, and the sons of Erik Bloodaxe, backed by Denmark, contested his rule until his death around 961.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1000": "Olaf Tryggvason had spent five years forcing conversion on the coastal districts, but in 1000 he was killed at the Battle of Svolder by a coalition of the Danish and Swedish kings and the earls of Lade. Norway was divided again, largely under Danish overlordship exercised through the Lade earls.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1030": "Olaf Haraldsson, driven out by Cnut the Great in 1028, returned and was killed at Stiklestad. Norway was ruled by Cnut\u0027s son Svein and his mother Ælfgifu, but the unpopular Danish regime and the growing cult of Saint Olaf soon prepared the restoration of a native king in 1035.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1066": "Magnus the Good, restored in 1035, had also held Denmark, and Harald Hardrada built a strong, wealthy kingship funded partly by Byzantine gold. His death at Stamford Bridge in 1066, with much of his army, ended Norwegian ambitions in England, though the kingdom itself remained stable under his son Olaf Kyrre.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1103": "Magnus Barefoot had tightened Norwegian control over Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, confirmed by an agreement with Scotland in 1098, before he was killed in Ulster in 1103. His sons then shared the throne peacefully, and Sigurd the Crusader\u0027s expedition to the Holy Land in 1107–1111 showed the reach of the monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1130": "The death of Sigurd the Crusader opened more than a century of civil wars, as any son of a king, legitimate or not, could claim the throne. Rival kings and their followers fought across the country, and royal authority weakened even as the towns and the Church grew stronger.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1152": "A papal legate, Nicholas Breakspear, founded the archbishopric of Nidaros in 1152–1153, giving Norway its own church province, which included Iceland, Greenland and the western isles. The Church became a powerful ally of the crown, though the civil wars continued between competing royal factions for decades.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1240": "Haakon IV\u0027s victory over the rebel duke Skule Bårdsson in 1240 ended the civil war era. The king governed through royal officials, gained papal recognition and was crowned in 1247; Norway entered its period of greatest stability, prosperity and international prestige, with Bergen as the chief royal seat.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1263": "At its height under Haakon IV the Norwegian crown claimed lands from Greenland and Iceland, which submitted in 1261–1264, to the Hebrides, Man, Orkney, Shetland and the Faroes. Haakon\u0027s death in Orkney after his expedition to defend the Hebrides marked the high point of this North Atlantic realm.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1274": "Magnus VI ceded the Hebrides and Man to Scotland by the Treaty of Perth in 1266 but strengthened the kingdom internally, issuing the national law code of 1274 and a town law. Norway was a well-governed, prosperous realm, though increasingly dependent on German merchants for its grain.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1319": "Haakon V had built Akershus and moved the centre of government to Oslo, but his death without a son passed the crown to his three-year-old grandson Magnus Eriksson, also elected king of Sweden. Personal union and long regencies drew royal attention away from Norway and weakened its separate government.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1349": "The Black Death reached Bergen in 1349 and killed perhaps half the population. Deserted farms, shrinking rents and collapsing tax income gravely weakened the crown, the nobility and the Church, while the Hanseatic merchants in Bergen gained an even firmer hold over Norway\u0027s foreign trade.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1380": "The death of Haakon VI left the throne to his young son Olaf, already king of Denmark, under the guidance of his mother Margaret. Norway entered a union with Denmark that would last until 1814, with the Danish court and nobility increasingly dominant within it.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1537": "Christian III imposed the Lutheran Reformation, Archbishop Olav Engelbrektsson fled abroad, and the Norwegian council of the realm ceased to exist. Norway kept the title of kingdom and its own laws but was governed from Copenhagen as part of the composite monarchy of Denmark-Norway.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1718": "Norway had lost Jämtland, Härjedalen and Bohuslän to Sweden in 1645 and 1658, but its mines, timber and shipping were growing. Charles XII of Sweden was killed while besieging the border fortress of Fredriksten in 1718, ending the last Swedish invasion of the Great Northern War.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1814": "Ceded to Sweden by Denmark at the Treaty of Kiel, Norway declared independence, adopted the Eidsvoll constitution on 17 May and elected the Danish prince Christian Frederik king. After a short war it accepted union with Sweden in November, keeping its own constitution, parliament and government.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1905": "On 7 June 1905 the Storting declared the union with Sweden dissolved, a decision confirmed by an overwhelming referendum. After negotiations at Karlstad, Sweden accepted, and the Danish prince Carl was elected King Haakon VII, making Norway a fully independent constitutional monarchy for the first time in over five centuries.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1940": "Germany invaded on 9 April 1940. Despite the sinking of the cruiser Blücher at Oscarsborg, the country was occupied within two months; King Haakon VII and the government continued the war from London with the merchant fleet, while Vidkun Quisling headed a collaborationist regime at home.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1945": "Liberation in May 1945 restored the king and the democratic government. Norway abandoned its neutrality to become a founding member of NATO in 1949 and began a long period of Labour-led reconstruction and the building of a comprehensive welfare state with high employment.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|1969": "The discovery of the Ekofisk field in the North Sea in 1969 transformed the economy. Production began in 1971, and state ownership and taxation channelled the revenue into public finances and later into a sovereign wealth fund that became one of the largest in the world.",
  "Kingdom of Norway|2025": "Norway is a prosperous constitutional monarchy under King Harald V, with a population of about five and a half million. Outside the European Union but within the European Economic Area and NATO, it is a leading energy exporter and one of the richest countries in the world per head.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|882": "According to the Primary Chronicle, Oleg came south from Novgorod, killed Askold and Dir and made Kiev his seat, joining the northern and southern parts of the Dnieper route. The young state was a network of tribute-collecting strongholds controlled by a Scandinavian-led warrior elite rather than a territorial kingdom.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|911": "Oleg had extended tribute over the Drevlians, Severians and Radimichians and now concluded a trade treaty with Byzantium, preserved in the Primary Chronicle, regulating the rights of Rus\u0027 merchants in Constantinople. Commerce in furs, slaves and wax gave Kiev\u0027s rulers wealth and a recognised place in Byzantine diplomacy.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|945": "Igor\u0027s attack on Constantinople in 941 had been destroyed by Greek fire, and a new treaty in 944 was less favourable. In 945 he was killed by the Drevlians while collecting excessive tribute, and his widow Olga, regent for their young son Sviatoslav, crushed the revolt and regulated tribute.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|965": "Sviatoslav I destroyed the Khazar Khaganate around 965, taking Sarkel and sacking Itil, and subdued the Vyatichians. With the main power of the western steppe gone, Rus\u0027 dominated a vast area from the Baltic to the Black Sea, though Sviatoslav cared more for conquest than for administration.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|972": "Sviatoslav\u0027s attempt to move his centre to the Danube ended in defeat by John I Tzimiskes at Dorostolon in 971, and in 972 the Pechenegs killed him at the Dnieper rapids. His sons then fought each other for Kiev until Vladimir emerged as the victor around 978–980.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|988": "Vladimir the Great accepted baptism, married the emperor\u0027s sister Anna and sent warriors to help Basil II, while imposing Christianity on Kiev. The conversion tied Rus\u0027 to Byzantium, brought churches, clergy and writing, and gave the dynasty a new ideology of Christian rulership.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1015": "Vladimir\u0027s death set off a war between his sons. Sviatopolk had his brothers Boris and Gleb murdered, later venerated as the first saints of Rus\u0027, and Yaroslav of Novgorod fought him and his Polish ally Bolesław I for Kiev until his final victory in 1019.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1036": "After the death of his brother Mstislav of Chernigov, Yaroslav the Wise became sole ruler of Rus\u0027 and decisively defeated the Pechenegs outside Kiev. He began a great building programme, including Saint Sophia and the Golden Gate, and is credited with the first written law code.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1051": "Rus\u0027 was at the height of its power and prestige. Yaroslav appointed the native Hilarion metropolitan of Kiev, the Monastery of the Caves was founded, and his children married into the royal houses of France, Norway, Hungary and Byzantium. Kiev was among the largest and richest cities in Europe.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1054": "Yaroslav\u0027s death left the realm to his sons according to seniority, with Iziaslav in Kiev, Sviatoslav in Chernigov and Vsevolod in Pereyaslavl. The triumvirate kept order for a time, but the principle of shared rule within a growing dynasty contained the seeds of later conflict.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1068": "The Cumans routed the sons of Yaroslav on the Alta River, and the people of Kiev rose against Iziaslav, who fled to Poland. Decades of conflict between princes followed, with brothers and nephews competing for Kiev and calling in Poles and Cumans as allies.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1097": "Weakened by feuds and Cuman raids, the princes met at Liubech and agreed that each should keep his father\u0027s patrimony. The agreement was broken almost at once by the blinding of Prince Vasilko of Terebovl, and it confirmed the transformation of Rus\u0027 into hereditary principalities.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1113": "After a riot in Kiev, the townspeople invited Vladimir Monomakh to take the throne. A respected warrior who had led successful campaigns against the Cumans, he restored the authority of the grand prince, and his sons governed key principalities, giving Rus\u0027 its last period of real unity.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1132": "Mstislav the Great, Monomakh\u0027s son, had kept the princes in obedience and campaigned against Polotsk and the Lithuanian tribes. His death in 1132 is conventionally taken as the end of united Rus\u0027, as his brothers and the other branches of the dynasty began to compete for Kiev.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1169": "Andrei Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal sacked Kiev with a coalition of princes and installed a junior relative there instead of ruling it himself. Political power had moved to the regional principalities, and Kiev, though still the seat of the metropolitan, was now one prize among many.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1185": "Prince Igor Sviatoslavich of Novgorod-Seversk was defeated and captured by the Cumans, an episode retold in the Tale of Igor\u0027s Campaign. The poem laments the disunity of the princes, which left the southern lands exposed to steppe raids while each principality pursued its own interests.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1223": "A Mongol army under Jebe and Subutai crushed the combined forces of several Rus\u0027 princes and their Cuman allies at the Kalka River. The Mongols then withdrew, and the princes made no common preparation for their return, continuing their quarrels over Kiev and Galicia.",
  "Kievan Rus\u0027|1240": "Batu Khan\u0027s campaign of 1237–1240 destroyed the principalities one by one, and in December 1240 Kiev fell after a siege and was sacked. Most surviving principalities became tributaries of the Golden Horde, and the political world of Kievan Rus\u0027 came to an end.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|893": "William the Pious, count of Auvergne, took the title of duke of the Aquitanians as Carolingian authority in the south collapsed. His power rested on his own counties and on loose homage from other nobles, giving the new duchy prestige but only limited control over the vast region it claimed.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|910": "William the Pious founded the abbey of Cluny and stood among the leading princes of the West Frankish kingdom, holding Auvergne and wide lands in central Gaul. His authority was personal, however, and much of Aquitaine, including Poitou and Gascony, lay outside his effective control.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|927": "The death of Duke Acfred ended the Auvergnat line, and the ducal title was disputed for decades between the counts of Toulouse and the counts of Poitou, with the West Frankish kings intervening. The duchy existed more as a claim than as a functioning principality during this long rivalry.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1000": "William V the Great now ruled as count of Poitou and duke of Aquitaine. His Poitevin house had won the ducal title under William III Towhead in the mid-tenth century, and William V restored ducal prestige through his piety, his patronage of churches and abbeys and his wide diplomatic contacts.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1025": "William V the Great was offered the Italian crown after the death of Emperor Henry II in 1024, a measure of his European standing, though he eventually declined. He kept order among his vassals, supported the Peace of God and monastic reform, and made Poitiers one of the great princely courts of France.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1064": "Guy-Geoffrey, ruling as William VIII, had secured the Duchy of Gascony, extending ducal lordship from the Loire to the Pyrenees. In 1064 he is said to have led Aquitainian knights at the siege of Barbastro in Aragon, an early expedition by French nobles against Muslim Spain.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1137": "William X died on pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, leaving his daughter Eleanor heiress to the largest principality in France. Her marriage to the future Louis VII the same year brought Aquitaine under the French crown, a union that promised royal power in the south but rested on a single marriage.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1152": "After the annulment of her marriage to Louis VII, Eleanor married Henry Plantagenet, count of Anjou and duke of Normandy, who became King Henry II of England in 1154. Aquitaine became part of the Angevin Empire, gaining a powerful protector but losing its separate princely court and political independence.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1204": "Eleanor\u0027s death and King John\u0027s loss of Normandy and Anjou to Philip II Augustus left Aquitaine exposed. The French crown advanced into Poitou, while Gascony stayed loyal to the English king, largely because its wine trade depended on English markets and Bordeaux valued a distant overlord.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1224": "Louis VIII of France captured La Rochelle and completed the conquest of Poitou, the duchy\u0027s historic heartland and the seat of its dukes. English-held Aquitaine was reduced essentially to Gascony and parts of Saintonge in the south-west, governed from Bordeaux by a royal seneschal.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1259": "In the Treaty of Paris Henry III gave up his claims to Normandy, Anjou and Poitou and did homage to Louis IX for Gascony, in return for promises of lands in the Limousin, Périgord, Quercy and Agenais. The settlement brought peace but made the English king a French vassal.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1294": "Philip IV of France summoned Edward I to answer before the Parlement of Paris and confiscated Gascony, which French troops largely occupied in the war of 1294–1303. The duchy was restored by treaty in 1303, but the episode showed how vulnerable it was to French legal and military pressure.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1337": "Philip VI\u0027s confiscation of Gascony started the Hundred Years\u0027 War. Bordeaux and Bayonne remained loyal to Edward III, and the duchy became the main English base in south-western France, defended by local lords, castles and fortified towns and supplied by sea from England.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1362": "After the English victories at Crécy and Poitiers, the Treaty of Brétigny gave Edward III an enlarged Aquitaine in full sovereignty, free of French homage. In 1362 he made the Black Prince prince of Aquitaine, ruling lands from Poitou to the Pyrenees from a brilliant court at Bordeaux.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1369": "The Black Prince\u0027s Castilian campaign, victorious at Nájera in 1367 but ruinously expensive, led him to impose a hearth tax. The count of Armagnac and other lords appealed to Charles V, who accepted their appeals and renewed the war in 1369, as towns and nobles began to defect.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1376": "French armies under Bertrand du Guesclin reconquered Poitou, Saintonge, the Limousin and much of Gascony within a few years; the sick Black Prince surrendered the principality in 1372 and died in 1376. English Aquitaine shrank to a coastal strip around Bordeaux and Bayonne.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1451": "With the English cause collapsing after the Treaty of Arras and the loss of Normandy in 1450, French armies overran Gascony, and Bordeaux and Bayonne surrendered in 1451. Three centuries of English rule appeared to be over, though many Gascons resented the new French administration and taxes.",
  "Duchy of Aquitaine|1453": "Bordeaux rose in 1452 and welcomed an English army under John Talbot, but the French victory at Castillon in July 1453, where Talbot was killed, ended the revolt. Bordeaux capitulated in October, and Guyenne was annexed to the Kingdom of France, ending the duchy as a separate principality.",
  "County of Hainaut|898": "Reginar Longneck, the leading noble of Lotharingia, held comital authority in the Hainaut district between the Scheldt and the Sambre. The county was one of several Reginarid holdings in a contested borderland, and its rulers depended on the shifting favour of the East and West Frankish kings.",
  "County of Hainaut|958": "Archbishop Bruno of Cologne, governing Lotharingia for Otto I, exiled Reginar III and divided Hainaut into the counties of Mons and Valenciennes under loyal appointees. The Reginarids lost their inheritance, and the county existed for decades only as an imperial administrative district under outsiders.",
  "County of Hainaut|998": "Reginar IV, helped by marriage ties with the new French royal house, had recovered his family\u0027s county of Mons by the end of the tenth century. Reginarid rule over Hainaut was restored, though the counts remained second-rank princes among powerful neighbours in Flanders, Brabant and Liège.",
  "County of Hainaut|1051": "Richilde, widow of Count Hermann of Mons, married Baldwin of Flanders, who ruled both counties as Baldwin VI of Flanders from 1067. The union placed Hainaut in the orbit of the most powerful principality of the Low Countries and brought it new wealth, connections and prestige.",
  "County of Hainaut|1071": "After Baldwin VI\u0027s death, his brother Robert the Frisian defeated Richilde and killed her son Arnulf III at the Battle of Cassel, taking Flanders. Richilde kept Hainaut for her younger son Baldwin II, placing it under the feudal protection of the Bishop of Liège.",
  "County of Hainaut|1171": "Baldwin V began an energetic reign, strengthening comital government, building alliances with France and pursuing the inheritance of Namur. His chancellor Gislebert of Mons recorded these years in detail, and the county gained influence far beyond its size through his diplomacy and marriages.",
  "County of Hainaut|1191": "Baldwin V\u0027s wife Margaret inherited Flanders, and he ruled it with Hainaut, having also obtained the margraviate of Namur by imperial grant. His daughter Isabella had married Philip II Augustus, so the house of Hainaut now stood among the most powerful families of north-western Europe.",
  "County of Hainaut|1204": "Baldwin VI, count of Hainaut and, as Baldwin IX, count of Flanders, led the Fourth Crusade and was elected the first Latin emperor of Constantinople. The dynasty reached the height of its prestige, ruling two rich counties in the Low Countries and a new empire in the East.",
  "County of Hainaut|1214": "Baldwin\u0027s young daughters Joan and Margaret had been left as heiresses, and Joan\u0027s husband Ferdinand of Portugal joined the coalition defeated by Philip II Augustus at Bouvines. Ferdinand was imprisoned in Paris, and Hainaut and Flanders came under strong French influence for years.",
  "County of Hainaut|1256": "The long feud between the children of Countess Margaret\u0027s two marriages, the Avesnes and the Dampierres, was settled by the arbitration of Louis IX in the Dit of Péronne. Hainaut went to the Avesnes and Flanders to the Dampierres, separating the two counties for good.",
  "County of Hainaut|1299": "John II of Avesnes inherited the counties of Holland and Zeeland and united them with Hainaut. The Avesnes counts now ruled a scattered group of principalities from the North Sea coast to the Sambre, giving Hainaut a larger political role than at any time since 1204.",
  "County of Hainaut|1345": "William II was killed fighting the Frisians at Warns, ending the Avesnes line. His sister Margaret, wife of Emperor Louis IV, inherited Hainaut, Holland and Zeeland, which passed to the Wittelsbachs; Holland soon fell into the Hook and Cod wars, while Hainaut remained more stable.",
  "County of Hainaut|1433": "After a decade of conflict with Philip the Good of Burgundy, Jacqueline of Bavaria surrendered Hainaut, Holland and Zeeland to him. The county became one province among many in the Burgundian Netherlands, keeping its institutions and Estates but losing any separate princely policy.",
  "County of Hainaut|1482": "The death of Mary of Burgundy brought Hainaut to the Habsburgs through her husband Maximilian of Austria. The county remained a prosperous province of the Netherlands, with its own Estates and courts, but its fate was now tied to the wars and dynastic ambitions of the Habsburgs.",
  "County of Hainaut|1579": "Shaken by the Dutch Revolt, Hainaut joined Artois and Walloon Flanders in the Union of Arras and returned to obedience to Philip II of Spain. It remained Catholic and Spanish, but its frontier position exposed it to repeated French invasions over the following century.",
  "County of Hainaut|1659": "The Treaty of the Pyrenees gave France Landrecies, Le Quesnoy and Avesnes, the first step in the dismemberment of the county. Spanish power was declining, and Hainaut\u0027s fortress towns became prime targets in Louis XIV\u0027s drive to push the French frontier northwards.",
  "County of Hainaut|1678": "The Treaty of Nijmegen confirmed French possession of Valenciennes, Maubeuge, Condé and Bouchain, which Vauban fortified as part of the northern frontier. The county was now split permanently between French Hainaut and a smaller Spanish Hainaut centred on its old capital, Mons.",
  "County of Hainaut|1714": "Under the treaties ending the War of the Spanish Succession, the Spanish Netherlands, including Hainaut, passed to the Austrian Habsburgs; the Battle of Malplaquet in 1709 had been fought on its soil. Under Austrian rule the county recovered and its coal industry expanded, though it remained a minor province.",
  "County of Hainaut|1795": "French revolutionary armies, victorious at Jemappes in 1792 and Fleurus in 1794, occupied the Austrian Netherlands. On 1 October 1795 France annexed them, and Hainaut was abolished as a county, its territory forming the department of Jemmapes, later the Belgian province of Hainaut.",
  "Kingdom of León|910": "Alfonso III was deposed by his sons, and García I took the southern lands centred on León, while his brothers held Galicia and Asturias. The new kingdom inherited a long tradition of expansion to the Douro but began as only one of three shares of a divided realm.",
  "Kingdom of León|914": "On García\u0027s death Ordoño II, already ruling Galicia, took León and made the city his royal seat. Reuniting most of his father\u0027s lands, he campaigned south of the Douro and in 917 won a victory over a Córdoban army at San Esteban de Gormaz.",
  "Kingdom of León|920": "Abd al-Rahman III answered Leonese raids with a great campaign, defeating Ordoño II and his Navarrese allies at Valdejunquera. The defeat halted the southward advance, and the deaths of Ordoño II in 924 and Fruela II in 925 were followed by succession disputes among their sons.",
  "Kingdom of León|939": "Ramiro II, allied with Count Fernán González of Castile, defeated the caliph Abd al-Rahman III at Simancas, one of the greatest Christian victories of the tenth century. León resettled the Tormes valley around Salamanca and stood as the leading Christian power of the peninsula.",
  "Kingdom of León|958": "Sancho I, called the Fat, was deposed by the nobility and fled to Córdoba to seek the caliph\u0027s help. Restored with caliphal and Navarrese support, he became a client of Córdoba, while Castile under Fernán González acted increasingly as an independent principality.",
  "Kingdom of León|997": "Almanzor\u0027s campaigns devastated the kingdom: León itself had been sacked in the late 980s, and in 997 his army destroyed the town of Santiago de Compostela, carrying the bells of its church to Córdoba. Bermudo II\u0027s kingdom was reduced to submission and tribute.",
  "Kingdom of León|1017": "After the death of Almanzor in 1002 and the collapse of the Caliphate of Córdoba, Alfonso V rebuilt León, issued the city\u0027s fuero at a royal council and restored churches and monasteries. The kingdom recovered, though it now faced the rising power of Navarre under Sancho III.",
  "Kingdom of León|1037": "Ferdinand I of Castile defeated and killed Bermudo III, the last of the old Leonese dynasty, at Tamarón and became king of León by right of his wife Sancha. Under the new dynasty León was joined with Castile, gaining resources but losing its separate royal line.",
  "Kingdom of León|1065": "Ferdinand I had taken Coimbra in 1064 and collected gold tribute from the taifa kingdoms. At his death in 1065 he divided his lands among his sons, with Alfonso VI receiving León, which soon led to war between the brothers.",
  "Kingdom of León|1072": "After the murder of Sancho II at the siege of Zamora, Alfonso VI took Castile and Galicia as well as León. The reunited kingdom collected rich tribute from the taifa kingdoms of al-Andalus, and Alfonso, styling himself emperor, became the dominant ruler in Iberia.",
  "Kingdom of León|1085": "Alfonso VI captured Toledo, the former Visigothic capital, extending the kingdom to the Tagus. León-Castile was at the height of its power and prestige, with Cluniac reform, French settlers and growing pilgrim traffic to Santiago linking it closely to Latin Europe.",
  "Kingdom of León|1086": "The taifa kings called in the Almoravids from North Africa, who defeated Alfonso VI at Sagrajas. Toledo held, but the Christian advance was halted, and the Almoravids absorbed most of the taifas within a few years, ending the flow of tribute gold to León.",
  "Kingdom of León|1109": "The death of Alfonso VI\u0027s heir Sancho at Uclés in 1108 and of Alfonso himself in 1109 left the throne to Urraca. Her quarrelsome marriage to Alfonso I of Aragon, civil war and Galician unrest weakened the kingdom, while the County of Portugal drifted towards independence.",
  "Kingdom of León|1135": "Alfonso VII, Urraca\u0027s son, was crowned emperor in León in the presence of García Ramírez of Navarre, the count of Barcelona and other princes who acknowledged his overlordship. Recognised as the senior ruler of Christian Iberia, he campaigned deep into al-Andalus and briefly took Almería in 1147.",
  "Kingdom of León|1157": "Alfonso VII\u0027s death led to the separation of his kingdoms: Ferdinand II took León and Galicia, and Sancho III took Castile. León was now the smaller of the two, hemmed in by Castile to the east and by the new Kingdom of Portugal to the west.",
  "Kingdom of León|1188": "Alfonso IX succeeded his father and summoned the Cortes at León, with bishops, nobles and representatives of the towns. Facing a powerful Castile and a disputed succession, he secured internal support by promising to respect the laws and to consult his subjects on war and peace.",
  "Kingdom of León|1218": "Alfonso IX founded the University of Salamanca, later one of the great universities of Europe, around this time. After years of conflict with Castile and Portugal he turned his efforts south, where the weakening of the Almohads after Las Navas de Tolosa opened Extremadura to Leonese conquest.",
  "Kingdom of León|1229": "Alfonso IX captured Cáceres and in 1230 took Mérida and Badajoz, carrying the Leonese frontier to the Guadiana. The kingdom ended its separate history at a moment of territorial success, with its conquests helping to open the way to the great Castilian advances of the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of León|1230": "Alfonso IX died in September 1230. Although he had named his daughters Sancha and Dulce as heirs, the Treaty of Benavente gave León to their half-brother Ferdinand III of Castile. León and Castile were united permanently, and the kingdom became part of the Crown of Castile.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|911": "Charles the Simple granted Rollo’s Vikings lands around Rouen, traditionally by the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, in return for defending the Seine and accepting baptism. The new lordship was small, ruled by a foreign warlord with relatively few settlers and no secure standing among the Frankish princes, so its power stood far below its later peak.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|933": "King Rudolph of West Francia granted William Longsword further lands in the west, probably the Cotentin and the Avranchin, after an earlier grant that included the Bessin in 924. Normandy now approached its lasting borders, but its Scandinavian rulers still depended on personal leadership and war bands rather than settled institutions.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|942": "William Longsword was murdered at Picquigny during a meeting with men of Count Arnulf I of Flanders, leaving his young son Richard I as heir. King Louis IV of West Francia and Hugh the Great both intervened in Normandy, and for several years the survival of the principality was in real doubt.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|996": "Richard I died after a reign of more than fifty years that had secured the dynasty, restored monasteries such as Fécamp and Mont-Saint-Michel and allied Normandy with the family of Hugh the Great. His son Richard II, soon styled duke, crushed a peasant revolt and inherited a stable, Christian and largely French-speaking principality.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1035": "Robert I died at Nicaea while returning from pilgrimage to Jerusalem, leaving the duchy to his illegitimate son William, aged about seven or eight. During the long minority several guardians were murdered, barons built castles and fought private wars, and ducal authority fell to one of its lowest points.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1047": "With the help of King Henry I of France, the young William II defeated rebel barons of western Normandy at the Battle of Val-ès-Dunes. The victory, followed by the Truce of God proclaimed at a council near Caen, began the restoration of ducal control over castles and the aristocracy.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1054": "When Henry I of France and Geoffrey Martel of Anjou turned against William, he defeated one French army at the Battle of Mortemer in 1054 and another at Varaville in 1057. Normandy was now secure against its own overlord and ranked among the strongest principalities of the French kingdom.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1063": "William completed the conquest of the county of Maine, extending Norman power south towards Anjou. The duchy now had a disciplined aristocracy, a reformed Church, flourishing abbeys and wealthy towns, giving William the men, money and prestige to pursue his claim to the English throne within a few years.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1066": "After the death of Edward the Confessor, William crossed the Channel, defeated Harold Godwinson at the Battle of Hastings on 14 October and was crowned in Westminster Abbey on Christmas Day. The duke of Normandy was now also King of England, the highest point of the duchy’s power and prestige.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1087": "William the Conqueror died after a campaign in the French Vexin, and his lands were divided: Robert Curthose took Normandy and William Rufus England. Under Robert’s lax rule barons built castles and fought one another without restraint, and the duchy lost much of the order William had imposed.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1106": "Henry I of England defeated and captured his brother Robert Curthose at the Battle of Tinchebray, reuniting Normandy and England. Henry restored firm government through his justices and the Exchequer and defended the duchy against Louis VI of France, whom he defeated at the Battle of Brémule in 1119.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1135": "Henry I died without a surviving legitimate son, his heir William Adelin having drowned in the White Ship in 1120. Stephen of Blois seized the crown, but the Empress Matilda and her husband Geoffrey of Anjou attacked Normandy, and the duchy slipped into almost a decade of war.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1144": "Geoffrey Plantagenet, count of Anjou, completed his conquest of Normandy, entered Rouen and was recognised as duke. Orderly government returned, but Normandy was now separated from England and tied to Anjou, its power deriving from an Angevin ruler rather than from its own dynasty.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1154": "Henry II, duke since 1150, became King of England, and Normandy formed a core province of an Angevin Empire stretching from the Scottish border to the Pyrenees. Norman government reached its most developed form, with an active Exchequer at Caen, sworn inquests and royal justices.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1198": "Richard the Lionheart completed Château Gaillard above the Seine to guard Normandy against Philip II of France and in 1198 defeated the French near Gisors. Richard’s wars recovered much lost ground, but the defence of the duchy had become a costly struggle against an increasingly powerful French king.",
  "Duchy of Normandy|1204": "Philip II, having declared King John’s fiefs forfeit, took Château Gaillard in March 1204, and Rouen surrendered in June. Normandy was annexed to the French royal domain and ceased to exist as a separate principality; only the Channel Islands remained with the English crown.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|918": "Richard the Justiciar, count of Autun, had gathered several counties of western Burgundy and, after defeating Vikings near Saint-Florentin in 898, was recognised as the region’s leading prince, with the ducal title attested by about 918. The principality was still a loose grouping of counties held together by his personal power.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|923": "Richard’s son Rudolph, duke since 921, was elected King of West Francia in 923, a sign of Burgundy’s weight among the Frankish principalities. Royal duties drew him away, however, and after his death in 936 the duchy was contested among powerful neighbours, above all Hugh the Great.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1002": "Duke Henry the Great died without a legitimate son, and his stepson Otto-William and King Robert II of France fought over the succession. Years of war followed before the king secured the duchy, and Burgundy lost much of its independence to the growing power of the Capetian crown.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1032": "King Henry I granted the duchy to his younger brother Robert, founding the Capetian House of Burgundy. Robert I ruled from Dijon with wide autonomy, but his reign was violent and his authority over the counts and bishops of the region remained limited, so the duchy’s power stood modest.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1098": "Robert of Molesme founded Cîteaux south of Dijon, and the Cistercian order soon spread across Europe. Under Duke Odo I the duchy was stable and its abbeys flourishing, while his brother Henry of Burgundy had established himself as count of Portugal, where his son would found a royal dynasty.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1191": "Duke Hugh III took command of the French crusaders at Acre after Philip II returned home, and died there in 1192. The duchy was prosperous and well defended, and its dukes ranked among the great peers of the French kingdom, though they rarely acted independently of the crown.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1237": "Hugh IV acquired the county of Chalon by exchange, extending ducal power along the Saône and its trade routes. Burgundy’s vineyards, fairs and monasteries prospered, and in 1248 the duke joined Louis IX’s crusade to Egypt as an established great lord within the Capetian kingdom.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1330": "Through Odo IV’s marriage to Joan of France, the duke gained the Free County of Burgundy and Artois when Joan inherited them in 1330. The two Burgundies were united under one ruler, giving the Capetian duchy its greatest extent and resources before the Valois period.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1361": "The fifteen-year-old Duke Philip of Rouvres died of plague, ending the Capetian line. King John II of France took over the duchy as nearest heir, and for two years Burgundy was ruled by the crown, its separate existence in doubt amid the disorder of the Hundred Years’ War.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1363": "John II granted the duchy as an appanage to his youngest son, Philip the Bold, who had fought beside him at Poitiers. The new Valois duke had royal backing and a firm base at Dijon, and his marriage in 1369 to Margaret of Male promised a far greater inheritance.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1384": "On the death of Louis of Male, Philip the Bold and Margaret inherited Flanders, Artois, the Free County of Burgundy and other lands. The duchy became the core of a wealthy composite state; Philip was building the Charterhouse of Champmol and in 1386 set up a Chambre des comptes at Dijon.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1419": "John the Fearless was murdered on the bridge at Montereau by followers of the Dauphin. His son Philip the Good allied with England, and Burgundian power, now resting on the Low Countries as much as on the duchy, became decisive in the French civil war and the Hundred Years’ War.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1435": "By the Treaty of Arras Philip the Good made peace with Charles VII of France, gaining the counties of Mâcon and Auxerre and exemption from personal homage. Founder of the Order of the Golden Fleece, he ruled one of Europe’s richest states, and the duchy stood at the height of its power and prestige.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1467": "Charles the Bold succeeded his father as one of the wealthiest princes in Europe, ruling lands from Burgundy to Holland. He reformed the army and administration and sought a royal crown, but his ambitions set him against Louis XI of France, the Swiss Confederates and the Duke of Lorraine.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1476": "The Swiss Confederates routed Charles the Bold’s army at Grandson in March and at Morat in June 1476, capturing much of his artillery and treasure. Burgundian power, still formidable on paper, was overstretched, and Lorraine, which Charles had occupied in 1475, rose against him.",
  "Duchy of Burgundy|1477": "Charles the Bold was killed at the Battle of Nancy on 5 January 1477. Louis XI occupied the duchy, claiming that it reverted to the crown in the absence of a male heir, and Burgundy was annexed to the Kingdom of France, while Mary of Burgundy kept most of the Low Countries.",
  "Kingdom of England|927": "Æthelstan took York from its Viking rulers and received the submission of the northern rulers at Eamont Bridge, creating a single English kingdom for the first time. It was a West Saxon conquest state, still dependent on the king’s personal power, and on its own later scale its strength stood low.",
  "Kingdom of England|937": "At the Battle of Brunanburh Æthelstan defeated a coalition of Scots, Strathclyde Britons and the Dublin Vikings under Olaf Guthfrithson, confirming English dominance in Britain. Yet after his death in 939 York slipped back under Viking kings, and unity was not secure until 954.",
  "Kingdom of England|973": "King Edgar was crowned at Bath in a ceremony that shaped later English coronations, and at about this time reformed the coinage into a uniform royal currency. Late Anglo-Saxon England, with its shires, writs and monastic revival, was one of the best-governed kingdoms of Europe.",
  "Kingdom of England|1016": "After years of Danish attacks under Æthelred the Unready and heavy payments of tribute, Cnut won the throne following the death of Edmund Ironside. England became part of a North Sea empire with Denmark and later Norway, prosperous and orderly but ruled by a foreign dynasty.",
  "Kingdom of England|1066": "Edward the Confessor’s death led to the coronation of Harold Godwinson, who defeated a Norwegian invasion at Stamford Bridge but fell at Hastings to William of Normandy. The conquest replaced the English elite and brought upheaval, though it preserved and strengthened royal administration.",
  "Kingdom of England|1154": "Henry II ended the civil war known as the Anarchy and ruled England as part of an Angevin Empire reaching the Pyrenees. His reforms created the common law, with itinerant justices, writs and juries, and royal government in England became among the most effective in Europe.",
  "Kingdom of England|1215": "King John, having lost Normandy in 1204 and facing baronial revolt, accepted Magna Carta at Runnymede. Civil war and a French invasion followed, and royal authority was at a low ebb, though the charter became a lasting statement that the king was bound by law.",
  "Kingdom of England|1283": "Edward I completed the conquest of Wales, securing it with great castles such as Caernarfon, Conwy and Harlech, and in 1295 he summoned a Parliament that included knights and burgesses. The crown was strong and well organised, and Edward soon turned to the subjugation of Scotland.",
  "Kingdom of England|1314": "Edward II was decisively defeated by Robert the Bruce at the Battle of Bannockburn, ending English hopes of conquering Scotland. Baronial opposition, the Great Famine of 1315–1317 and the king’s favourites weakened the kingdom until Edward was deposed in 1327.",
  "Kingdom of England|1346": "Edward III’s army of longbowmen and dismounted men-at-arms defeated the French at the Battle of Crécy and took Calais in 1347. Despite the Black Death of 1348–1349, English arms dominated the early Hundred Years’ War, culminating in the capture of King John II of France at Poitiers in 1356.",
  "Kingdom of England|1415": "Henry V won the Battle of Agincourt and went on to conquer Normandy. By the Treaty of Troyes in 1420 he was recognised as heir to the French throne, bringing English power in France to its medieval height, though it rested on a fragile alliance with Burgundy.",
  "Kingdom of England|1453": "Defeat at the Battle of Castillon ended the Hundred Years’ War and left Calais as England’s only French possession. The weak rule of Henry VI and the rivalry of the houses of Lancaster and York soon plunged the kingdom into the Wars of the Roses.",
  "Kingdom of England|1485": "Henry Tudor defeated and killed Richard III at the Battle of Bosworth and took the throne as Henry VII. He restored royal finances and order after decades of intermittent civil war, but England remained a second-rank power beside France and Spain.",
  "Kingdom of England|1536": "Henry VIII, declared head of the English Church in 1534, began dissolving the monasteries and transferring their vast lands to the crown. Under Thomas Cromwell royal government and Parliament extended their reach, and Wales was formally incorporated into the English kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of England|1588": "English ships helped defeat the Spanish Armada, a victory that secured Elizabeth I’s Protestant regime and encouraged English maritime ambitions. The kingdom was flourishing culturally and expanding its trade, though it remained smaller, poorer and less populous than Spain or France.",
  "Kingdom of England|1649": "After civil war between Charles I and Parliament, the king was tried and executed, and England became a republic, the Commonwealth. The New Model Army made England militarily formidable and conquered Ireland and Scotland, but the regime rested on force and lacked broad support.",
  "Kingdom of England|1660": "The monarchy was restored under Charles II. England fought the Dutch for maritime supremacy, acquired Bombay in 1661 and New York in 1664, and its trade, colonies and science grew rapidly, though conflicts over religion and royal power remained unresolved.",
  "Kingdom of England|1689": "After the Glorious Revolution, William III and Mary II accepted the Bill of Rights, establishing parliamentary supremacy. England joined the Nine Years’ War against Louis XIV, and new institutions such as the Bank of England, founded in 1694, allowed the state to borrow and fight on an unprecedented scale.",
  "Kingdom of England|1704": "John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, led an allied army to victory over France and Bavaria at the Battle of Blenheim, and English and Dutch forces captured Gibraltar. England stood at the height of its power as a leading naval, financial and military state of Europe.",
  "Kingdom of England|1707": "The Acts of Union joined England and Scotland in the Kingdom of Great Britain on 1 May 1707. England ended not through decline but by merging into a larger state at the height of its power, and its law, church and institutions continued within the new kingdom.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|929": "Abd al-Rahman III proclaimed himself caliph after nearly two decades spent subduing rebels across al-Andalus, culminating in the fall of Bobastro in 928. The new caliphate inherited a reunified emirate with a strong army and treasury, but Toledo still held out and the Fatimids threatened from North Africa.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|931": "The occupation of Ceuta, following Melilla in 927, gave the caliph a bridgehead across the Strait of Gibraltar, and Toledo submitted in 932. Al-Andalus was united under Córdoba for the first time in decades, and client Berber chiefs in Morocco helped check Fatimid expansion.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|939": "At the Battle of Simancas Ramiro II of León inflicted a heavy defeat on Abd al-Rahman III, who narrowly escaped. The caliph rarely led armies in person afterwards, and the Duero frontier stabilised, though the setback did little lasting harm to the caliphate’s internal strength.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|951": "The caliphate occupied Tangier, consolidating its hold on the Strait, while Byzantine embassies and a mission from Otto I came to Córdoba in these years. Madinat al-Zahra was rising west of the capital, and the divided Christian kingdoms increasingly sought the caliph’s arbitration.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|961": "Abd al-Rahman III died after a reign of nearly half a century, leaving his son al-Hakam II a wealthy, centralised and respected state. Shortly before, Sancho I of León had regained his throne with caliphal help, a sign of Córdoba’s dominance over the Christian north.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|974": "Al-Hakam II’s general Ghalib defeated the Idrisid prince al-Hasan ibn Qannun, restoring caliphal authority in northern Morocco. At home the caliph had enlarged the Great Mosque, gathered his famous library and presided over a flourishing court culture, keeping the caliphate near the height of its power.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|976": "Al-Hakam II died, and his son Hisham II, aged about eleven, became caliph. Real power passed to a group of courtiers among whom the ambitious administrator Muhammad ibn Abi Amir quickly rose, and the caliph himself was soon excluded from government, a structural weakness for the future.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|981": "Ibn Abi Amir defeated his former ally Ghalib and took the title al-Mansur, becoming master of the state. He reorganised the army around Berber and Saqaliba regiments and built his own administrative city, Madinat al-Zahira, keeping the caliphate militarily powerful while sidelining the caliph.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|997": "Almanzor sacked Santiago de Compostela, the great pilgrimage centre of Christian Spain, and carried its bells to Córdoba. His annual campaigns, which had already struck Barcelona and León, made the caliphate the dominant power of the peninsula and brought its military reach to its peak.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1002": "Almanzor died at Medinaceli on his return from a campaign, and his son Abd al-Malik al-Muzaffar succeeded him as hajib. The state remained strong and the campaigns continued, but power now rested on a family of Amirid regents rather than on the Umayyad caliph himself.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1008": "Abd al-Malik’s death brought his brother Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo to power. When he persuaded Hisham II to name him heir to the caliphate, he alienated the Umayyad family, the religious scholars and the people of Córdoba, and the regime’s support quickly collapsed.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1009": "A revolt in Córdoba deposed Hisham II and installed Muhammad II al-Mahdi, and Sanchuelo was killed. The Berber regiments chose a rival caliph, Sulayman, and civil war began, with both sides hiring Christian troops; central authority collapsed and provinces drifted away.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1013": "Berber troops under Sulayman took and plundered Córdoba, Madinat al-Zahra lay in ruins, and Hisham II disappeared, probably killed. Governors and commanders in Seville, Granada, Zaragoza, Valencia and elsewhere were already ruling independently, and the caliphate retained little more than its title.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1016": "Ali ibn Hammud, a commander of Idrisid descent, seized Córdoba and proclaimed himself caliph, interrupting Umayyad rule. Hammudid and Umayyad claimants alternated in the following years, each controlling little beyond the capital and its surroundings, while the taifa kingdoms consolidated their independence.",
  "Caliphate of Córdoba|1031": "The notables of Córdoba deposed the last caliph, Hisham III, and abolished the caliphate, setting up a council led by Abu l-Hazm ibn Jahwar. Al-Andalus was now fully divided among the taifa kingdoms, and the Caliphate of Córdoba had ceased to exist.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|933": "Rudolf II of Upper Burgundy, having renounced his Italian ambitions, acquired the rights of Hugh of Arles in Lower Burgundy, according to the usual reading of Liutprand of Cremona. The united kingdom was large on paper, but royal authority in Provence was slight and the two halves had little in common.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|937": "Rudolf II died, leaving a young son, Conrad. Hugh of Arles married the widowed Queen Bertha and sought to control the kingdom, but Otto I of Germany took Conrad to his court. The kingdom survived, but as a dependant of the Ottonian king rather than an independent power.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|951": "Conrad’s sister Adelaide, widow of King Lothair of Italy, married Otto I, binding the Burgundian royal house to the Ottonians. Conrad, now ruling in his own right, enjoyed powerful protection, and the kingdom’s security and prestige rose, although its kings still governed mainly in Upper Burgundy.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|973": "After Muslim raiders from Fraxinetum captured Abbot Majolus of Cluny in 972, Count William of Provence and his allies destroyed their base on the Provençal coast. The kingdom was freed from the raids, Conrad’s long and peaceful reign was at its height, and royal standing reached its peak.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|993": "Conrad the Peaceful died after a reign of fifty-six years, and his son Rudolf III succeeded. The new king faced resistance from powerful lay lords and turned increasingly to bishops, granting them counties, as royal authority began a long contraction from the level it had reached under his father.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1016": "Rudolf III, childless and struggling against his nobles, confirmed his nephew Emperor Henry II as his heir in agreements around 1016, after an earlier pact of 1006. The kingdom’s future was now tied to the German crown, and its own king held little power beyond his domains around Lake Geneva.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1032": "Rudolf III died childless in September 1032, and under the earlier agreements the kingdom passed to Emperor Conrad II. Count Odo II of Blois, Rudolf’s nephew, disputed the succession and invaded, while many Burgundian lords hesitated between the two claimants and the kingdom’s future hung in the balance.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1034": "Conrad II, crowned king of Burgundy at Payerne in 1033, campaigned in the kingdom, forced Odo II to withdraw and received the submission of its lords at Geneva. Burgundy became the third kingdom of the Holy Roman Empire, gaining imperial protection but losing a resident monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1127": "Emperor Lothair III named Conrad of Zähringen rector of Burgundy, delegating imperial authority to a German ducal family. Real power lay with counts, bishops and rising dynasties such as the counts of Savoy and Provence, and the kingdom existed mainly as an imperial title and claim.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1156": "Frederick Barbarossa married Beatrice, heiress of the Free County of Burgundy, giving the emperor a strong personal base in the kingdom. Imperial assemblies and charters followed, and Barbarossa sought to revive imperial authority throughout the Burgundian lands after decades of neglect.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1178": "Frederick Barbarossa was crowned king at Arles in the cathedral of Saint-Trophime, the most impressive assertion of imperial rule in the kingdom for more than a century. The coronation renewed imperial claims over Provence, but it did not create lasting institutions of royal government.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1246": "Charles of Anjou, brother of Louis IX of France, married Beatrice, heiress of the County of Provence, bringing the south of the kingdom into the orbit of the French royal family. With imperial power collapsing in the conflict between Frederick II and the papacy, the kingdom counted for little.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1312": "Philip IV of France formally annexed Lyon, an imperial city on the Rhône, and the popes had settled at Avignon from 1309 under strong French influence. French power was advancing steadily along the river, while the emperors, distracted by German and Italian affairs, offered no effective resistance.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1349": "Humbert II, the last independent dauphin of Viennois, transferred his lands to the heir of the French throne. The Dauphiné passed into French hands, and only Provence, Savoy and the northern Burgundian lands remained even nominally within the kingdom, under fragmented local rulers.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1365": "Emperor Charles IV was crowned king at Arles, the last coronation of the kingdom. The ceremony asserted an ancient claim but brought no real authority, since the Angevins held Provence, France held the Dauphiné and Lyon, and Savoy acted as an independent Alpine power.",
  "Kingdom of Arles|1378": "During his visit to Paris, Charles IV appointed the French dauphin, the future Charles VI, imperial vicar of the kingdom for life. This effectively surrendered imperial authority in the Rhône valley, and the Kingdom of Arles ceased to function as a political entity.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|936": "Gorm the Old established his rule at Jelling in central Jutland. His authority covered Jutland and probably the Danish islands, but the realm was still a loose association of regional chieftains with a pagan cult and few royal institutions, so its power stood low despite a strategic position between the North Sea and the Baltic.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|965": "Harald Bluetooth accepted baptism, traditionally around 965, and raised the great Jelling stone claiming all Denmark and Norway. Christianisation tied the kingdom to the German church while easing Ottonian pressure, and the ring fortresses and Ravning Enge bridge built around 980 show a king able to command labour and soldiers across the realm.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1013": "Sweyn Forkbeard completed the conquest of England in 1013, driving King Æthelred into exile in Normandy. Years of raiding and Danegeld had already enriched the Danish crown. Sweyn died in February 1014, but his campaign showed that a Danish king could now raise forces able to overthrow a major western kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1018": "Cnut the Great, king of England since 1016, succeeded to Denmark around 1018 and later added Norway in 1028. Ruling a North Sea realm largely from England, he made Denmark part of the strongest power in northern Europe, with fleets, silver and prestige that only the Valdemars and Margaret I would later approach.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1035": "Cnut’s death in 1035 broke up the North Sea empire. England and Norway went their own ways, Harthacnut died in 1042, and Magnus the Good of Norway ruled Denmark until 1047. Sweyn Estridsen then founded a lasting dynasty, but the kingdom had shrunk back to its Scandinavian core and its power fell sharply.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1157": "After a quarter-century of civil wars, Valdemar I the Great became sole king following the Battle of Grathe Heath in 1157. With Absalon, later archbishop of Lund, he rebuilt royal power, fortified the coasts and began campaigns against the Wendish pirates that restored Danish authority over the western Baltic.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1219": "Valdemar II the Victorious led a crusade to northern Estonia and founded Danish rule at Reval after the Battle of Lyndanisse. Holding Holstein, lands along the southern Baltic coast and overlordship of Rügen, Denmark reached its widest medieval extent and dominated the western Baltic at the height of the Valdemarian age.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1227": "Count Henry of Schwerin kidnapped Valdemar II in 1223, and after his release the king was defeated by a north German coalition at the Battle of Bornhöved in 1227. Denmark lost Holstein and most southern conquests, keeping Estonia and Rügen, although the Jutland Code of 1241 still shows a capable monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1332": "Christopher II had pawned almost the whole kingdom to the counts of Holstein and other creditors. After his death in 1332 Denmark had no king for eight years, while Scania placed itself under the Swedish king. This was the lowest point of the medieval state, with royal authority almost extinct.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1340": "Valdemar IV Atterdag became king in 1340 and spent decades redeeming pawned provinces, financed partly by selling Estonia to the Teutonic Order in 1346. He recovered Scania in 1360 and sacked Visby in 1361, rebuilding the crown’s power but provoking a war with the Hanseatic League that ended in the Treaty of Stralsund.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1397": "Margaret I, already ruler of Denmark and Norway and accepted in Sweden after the defeat of Albert of Mecklenburg in 1389, had her grandnephew Eric of Pomerania crowned king of all three realms at Kalmar in 1397. The Kalmar Union made Denmark the leading power of a Scandinavia stretching from Greenland to Finland.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1523": "Christian II’s attempt to crush Swedish opposition, above all the Stockholm Bloodbath of 1520, provoked a revolt that made Gustav Vasa king of Sweden in 1523. The same year Danish nobles deposed Christian II. Denmark kept Norway, Iceland and the duchies, but the union of all Scandinavia was finished.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1536": "After the civil war known as the Count’s Feud, Christian III introduced the Lutheran Reformation in 1536, confiscating church estates and greatly enlarging crown lands. Norway lost its own council and became a dependency. The richer crown and the Sound Toll gave Denmark–Norway a firm base for the coming century.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1625": "Christian IV, wealthy from the Sound Toll and crown estates, entered the Thirty Years’ War as leader of the Protestant cause in Lower Saxony. Having won the Kalmar War against Sweden in 1613, Denmark still looked like the leading Nordic power, though defeat at Lutter am Barenberge in 1626 soon exposed its limits.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1645": "The Torstenson War ended with the Treaty of Brömsebro in 1645. Denmark ceded Gotland, Saaremaa, Jämtland and Härjedalen to Sweden, pledged Halland for thirty years and exempted Swedish ships from the Sound Toll. The balance of power in the north had shifted decisively from Copenhagen to Stockholm.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1660": "Charles X Gustav’s march across the frozen belts forced the Treaty of Roskilde in 1658, costing Scania, Halland, Blekinge, Bornholm and the Norwegian provinces of Bohuslän and Trøndelag. Copenhagen survived a Swedish siege, the Treaty of Copenhagen of 1660 returned Bornholm and Trøndelag, and Frederick III then made the crown hereditary and absolute.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1814": "Allied with Napoleon after the British seizure of its fleet in 1807, Denmark went bankrupt in 1813 and by the Treaty of Kiel in 1814 ceded Norway to Sweden. Keeping only Iceland, the Faroe Islands and Greenland of the old Norwegian realm, it became a small state dependent on the goodwill of greater powers.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1849": "Frederick VII signed the Constitution of 5 June 1849, ending absolutism and creating a constitutional monarchy with a broad male franchise. The First Schleswig War of 1848–1851 against the Schleswig-Holstein movement and its German supporters ended in a restoration of the old arrangements, leaving the national question of the duchies unresolved.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1864": "Defeat by Prussia and Austria in the Second Schleswig War, marked by the storming of Dybbøl on 18 April, led to the Treaty of Vienna, which surrendered Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg. Denmark lost a large share of its territory and population and was reduced to a small, nationally homogeneous kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1940": "Germany occupied Denmark on 9 April 1940 against little resistance. The Danish government continued under German supervision until August 1943, when cooperation broke down. Although the state survived and most Danish Jews were rescued to Sweden in October 1943, real sovereignty was suspended until liberation in May 1945.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|1973": "Denmark joined the European Community on 1 January 1973, together with the United Kingdom and Ireland, having been a founding member of NATO in 1949. A prosperous welfare state that had granted home rule to the Faroe Islands, it combined Nordic cooperation with membership of the main Western institutions.",
  "Kingdom of Denmark|2025": "Denmark is a prosperous constitutional monarchy of about six million people under King Frederik X, who succeeded on Queen Margrethe II’s abdication in January 2024. The Faroe Islands and Greenland remain self-governing parts of the realm. Its standing is modest beside its medieval Baltic dominance but secure and stable.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|939": "Alan II Barbetorte, back from exile at the English court, completed the expulsion of the Vikings with his victory at Trans in 939. He ruled from Nantes as duke, but the country was devastated, many monasteries lay empty and his authority in the north and west depended on uneasy alliances with regional counts.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|952": "Alan II died in 952 leaving a young heir, and influence slipped to the counts of Blois and Anjou and to rival Breton houses. The counts of Rennes and Nantes contested leadership, and the duchy was weak, divided and exposed to stronger neighbours in the Loire valley and Normandy.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|992": "Conan I of Rennes, who had taken the ducal title, was killed by Fulk Nerra of Anjou at the Battle of Conquereuil in 992. The house of Rennes nonetheless kept the duchy, and under Geoffrey I and Alan III ducal authority slowly recovered, although the dukes of Normandy increasingly claimed overlordship.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1066": "Breton lords formed a large part of William of Normandy’s army at Hastings and gained great estates in England, among them the honour of Richmond. Duke Conan II, who had resisted Norman pressure, died in 1066, and the house of Cornouaille that followed bound the west of the peninsula more closely to the duchy.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1166": "Henry II of England forced Conan IV to give up power in 1166 and betrothed Conan’s daughter Constance to his son Geoffrey. Brittany became part of the Angevin dominions, governed for years by Henry’s officials, and its dukes lost real independence for more than a generation.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1213": "After Arthur of Brittany vanished in King John’s custody around 1203, Philip II of France married the heiress Alix to Peter Mauclerc of Dreux in 1213. Peter imposed firmer ducal control on the nobility and the church, founding the Dreux line of dukes that built a more coherent duchy under Capetian protection.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1297": "Philip IV raised Duke John II to the dignity of peer of France in 1297, recognising Brittany as one of the great fiefs of the kingdom. The Dreux dukes governed a stable and increasingly prosperous duchy, though the honour also bound them more closely to the French crown and its wars.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1341": "John III died childless in 1341, and the succession was disputed between his half-brother John of Montfort, backed by England, and his niece Joan of Penthièvre, wife of Charles of Blois, backed by France. Over twenty years of war devastated the duchy and made it a battlefield of the Hundred Years’ War.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1364": "John of Montfort’s son won the Battle of Auray in 1364, where Charles of Blois was killed, and the First Treaty of Guérande of 1365 recognised him as Duke John IV. Despite his exile and return in 1379, the Montfort dynasty began a century of institutional growth and relative independence.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1399": "John V succeeded in 1399 and ruled until 1442, keeping Brittany largely neutral between England and France during the later Hundred Years’ War. Trade, ducal finance and the institutions of council, chancery, chamber of accounts and Estates developed, and the duchy behaved increasingly like a sovereign principality.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1442": "By John V’s death in 1442 Brittany was among the most prosperous and best-governed principalities of the French kingdom. His brother Arthur de Richemont, constable of France, shared in the victory at Formigny in 1450, and the dukes held a respected position between the French crown and its rivals.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1465": "Francis II, duke from 1458, founded the University of Nantes in 1460, rebuilt the castle of Nantes and in 1465 joined the League of the Public Weal against Louis XI. The duchy’s wealth, diplomacy and near-sovereign claims stood at their height, in alliance with Burgundy and England.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1488": "Francis II’s army was destroyed at Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier in July 1488, and the Treaty of Le Verger subjected his daughters’ marriages to the king’s consent. Francis died in September, leaving the eleven-year-old Anne as duchess of a partly occupied duchy divided between rival factions of nobles.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1491": "French armies overran the duchy after Anne’s proxy marriage to Maximilian of Austria. Besieged in Rennes, she agreed to marry Charles VIII, and the wedding took place in December 1491. Brittany entered a personal union with the French crown, and its independent foreign policy came to an end.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1499": "After Charles VIII’s death Anne resumed government of her duchy and in 1499 married Louis XII under a contract intended to keep Brittany separate and pass it to a younger child. The duchy kept its own institutions and chancellor, a brief and partial recovery of autonomy.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1514": "Anne died in January 1514. Her daughter Claude, married to Francis of Angoulême, who became Francis I in 1515, granted the administration of the duchy to her husband, and the separation promised in 1499 was not honoured. Brittany was governed in practice from the French court.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1532": "Petitioned by the Estates meeting at Vannes, Francis I issued the edict of union in 1532, joining Brittany perpetually to France while guaranteeing its privileges, Estates and consent to taxation. The Dauphin Francis was crowned duke at Rennes the same year, but the duchy was no longer a separate state.",
  "Duchy of Brittany|1547": "The Dauphin Henry, duke since his brother’s death in 1536, became King Henry II in 1547, and the ducal title merged with the crown. Brittany lived on as a privileged province with its own Estates and, from 1554, its own Parlement, seated at Rennes from 1561, until the Revolution.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|959": "Archbishop Bruno of Cologne divided Lotharingia in 959 and entrusted Upper Lorraine to Frederick, count of Bar. The new duchy along the Moselle and upper Meuse was a frontier command of the German kingdom, its duke one lord among powerful bishops and counts rather than a true territorial ruler.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1033": "Gothelo I, already duke of Lower Lorraine, received Upper Lorraine in 1033 and reunited the old Lotharingian duchy, becoming one of the greatest princes of the Empire. The union did not outlast his death in 1044, when the revolt of his son Godfrey the Bearded led Henry III to break it up.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1048": "Henry III granted Upper Lorraine to Gerard of Alsace in 1048 after the death of his kinsman Adalbert. Gerard’s line would rule for almost seven centuries, but the early dukes held scattered domains among strong bishops of Metz, Toul and Verdun and counts of Bar, and their power stayed limited for generations.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1346": "By the fourteenth century the dukes had settled at Nancy and gathered a more compact domain, but they were drawn into French wars. Duke Raoul was killed at Crécy in 1346 fighting for Philip VI, leaving a child heir, and the duchy suffered from regency, plague and the passage of armies.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1390": "Charles II, duke from 1390 to 1431, was an energetic ruler who allied with Burgundy, campaigned widely and governed a prosperous duchy; Joan of Arc visited him at Nancy in 1429. His long reign marked a high point of the medieval duchy, but he left only daughters, inviting a disputed succession.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1431": "Charles II’s death in 1431 led to a succession war between his son-in-law René of Anjou and his nephew Antoine de Vaudémont, who captured René at Bulgnéville with Burgundian help. René kept Lorraine but spent years in captivity and paying ransom, and the duchy was weakened under largely absentee Angevin dukes.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1477": "René II, of the Vaudémont line, defeated and killed Charles the Bold of Burgundy at the Battle of Nancy in January 1477 after recovering his capital from Burgundian occupation. The victory removed the greatest threat to the duchy’s survival and gave the dynasty lasting prestige among the powers of the region.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1480": "After the death of his grandfather René of Anjou in 1480, René II secured the Duchy of Bar, uniting it with Lorraine in a lasting personal union. The enlarged state, its frontiers safer after Burgundy’s collapse, entered a period of rebuilding in which work on the ducal palace at Nancy began.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1525": "Duke Antoine crushed a peasant army of the German Peasants’ War near Saverne in May 1525, keeping the revolt and the Reformation out of Lorraine. The duchy stood as a Catholic bulwark on the western edge of the Empire, stable, enriched by salt and mines, and courted by both France and the Habsburgs.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1542": "The Treaty of Nuremberg with Charles V in 1542 recognised Lorraine as a free and non-incorporable duchy with only limited obligations to the Empire. Under Antoine the duchy practised neutrality between France and the Habsburgs, and its autonomy reached a high point in law, if not in military strength.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1552": "Henry II of France seized the Three Bishoprics of Metz, Toul and Verdun in 1552 and took the young Duke Charles III to be raised at his court. French garrisons now surrounded the duchy, a serious strategic blow, though the regency kept Lorraine intact and the dynasty’s standing undiminished.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1600": "Charles III, ruling in person since 1559, gave Lorraine its greatest age: he founded the University of Pont-à-Mousson, built the Ville-Neuve of Nancy, codified customary law and strengthened the administration. His court rivalled those of larger states, and the duchy stood at the peak of its wealth and stability.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1624": "Charles IV became duke in 1624, inheriting a prosperous and well-fortified duchy. Ambitious and a talented soldier, he aligned with the Habsburgs and conspired with Louis XIII’s enemies, staking the duchy’s independence on the anti-French side just as Richelieu was preparing to intervene in the Thirty Years’ War.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1633": "French armies took Nancy in September 1633, and Charles IV left to fight for the emperor and Spain. Occupation, war, plague and famine devastated Lorraine for decades and killed a large share of its people. The ducal state effectively ceased to function while France administered the duchy.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1661": "The Treaty of Vincennes of 1661 returned Lorraine to Charles IV on harsh terms, including the dismantling of Nancy’s fortifications and the cession of a corridor for French troops. The ruined and depopulated duchy began to recover, but its duke remained at the mercy of Louis XIV.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1670": "Louis XIV reoccupied Lorraine in 1670, and Charles IV died in exile in 1675. His nephew Charles V of Lorraine became a celebrated imperial general who commanded at the relief of Vienna in 1683, but he never ruled his duchy, which remained under French control until 1697.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1697": "The Treaty of Ryswick of 1697 restored Duke Leopold, son of Charles V, to Lorraine and Bar. He repopulated the countryside, encouraged crafts and industry and built a court at Lunéville, and the duchy recovered much of its prosperity during his long and largely peaceful reign until 1729.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1729": "Francis III Stephen succeeded his father in 1729. Educated in Vienna and the intended husband of Maria Theresa, he tied the duchy to the Habsburgs, which made its future a matter of great-power diplomacy. Lorraine itself was prosperous and well governed but militarily helpless against France.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1737": "Under the peace ending the War of the Polish Succession, Francis Stephen exchanged Lorraine for Tuscany, and in 1737 the duchy passed to Stanisław Leszczyński for life. Real power lay with the French intendant La Galaizière, who governed for France under the secret Declaration of Meudon of 1736.",
  "Duchy of Lorraine|1766": "Stanisław Leszczyński died at Lunéville in February 1766, and Lorraine and Bar were annexed to the Kingdom of France as agreed in the 1730s. The duchy ceased to exist, though its old ruling family continued as the house of Habsburg-Lorraine in Vienna and Florence.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|970": "The kings of the Svear around Uppsala were the leading power of central Sweden, enriched by Viking-age trade through Birka and the eastern river routes. Eric the Victorious consolidated rule over the Svear, but the Geats, the islands and the north lay largely outside royal control and the realm was still pagan.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|995": "Olof Skötkonung, who succeeded around 995, was probably the first king to rule both Svear and Geats and the first Christian king. He struck coins at Sigtuna modelled on English pennies, but royal power remained limited and the conversion of the country proceeded slowly over the following century.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1250": "Birger Jarl, who governed for his son Valdemar after his election in 1250, strengthened the crown, issued peace laws protecting women, homes and churches, founded Stockholm and extended rule over Tavastia in Finland. After two centuries of rivalry between the houses of Sverker and Eric, Sweden was becoming a coherent kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1319": "Magnus Eriksson, a child, became king of both Sweden and Norway in 1319, and the Treaty of Nöteborg followed in 1323. His reign brought the national Land Law and control of Scania from 1332, but also the Black Death of 1350 and aristocratic revolts that eventually brought down his rule.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1397": "Under the Kalmar Union, crowned in 1397 for Eric of Pomerania, Sweden became a junior partner in a Scandinavian union ruled from Denmark. Swedish nobles and peasants resented Danish bailiffs and taxes, and Engelbrekt’s revolt of 1434 began a century of conflict between union kings and Swedish regents.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1471": "The regent Sten Sture the Elder defeated Christian I of Denmark at the Battle of Brunkeberg outside Stockholm in 1471. Sweden remained formally in the union, but the Sture regents governed with the support of peasants and miners, Uppsala University was founded in 1477, and a separate Swedish polity took shape.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1523": "Gustav Vasa, leading a rising after the Stockholm Bloodbath of 1520, was elected king on 6 June 1523 and took Stockholm. Sweden left the Kalmar Union for good, and the confiscation of church wealth after 1527 gave the crown the means to build a strong, centralised Lutheran monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1561": "Eric XIV took Reval and northern Estonia in 1561, beginning Sweden’s expansion across the Baltic. The costly Northern Seven Years’ War against Denmark and Lübeck followed, along with long wars against Russia and Poland in Livonia, but the Vasa state now had the means to sustain commitments overseas.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1617": "Gustav II Adolf won Ingria and Kexholm from Russia by the Treaty of Stolbovo in 1617, cutting Russia off from the Baltic. With Axel Oxenstierna he reformed administration, finances and the army, and Swedish arms soon turned against Poland in Livonia, where Riga fell in 1621.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1632": "Gustav II Adolf’s intervention in the Thirty Years’ War, his victory at Breitenfeld in 1631 and his death at Lützen in November 1632 made Sweden the leading Protestant power in Europe. Oxenstierna continued the war as regent for Queen Christina, and Swedish armies occupied much of Germany.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia gave Sweden Western Pomerania, Bremen-Verden and Wismar, with seats in the imperial diet. Together with Finland, Ingria, Estonia, Livonia and the gains from Denmark at Brömsebro in 1645, these lands made Sweden the dominant power of the Baltic at the height of its European weight.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1658": "Charles X Gustav marched across the frozen belts and forced the Treaty of Roskilde on Denmark in 1658, winning Scania, Halland, Blekinge and Bohuslän. Sweden reached its greatest extent and its modern southern frontier, though constant war placed a heavy strain on a small population and limited treasury.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1700": "Charles XII opened the Great Northern War with brilliant victories, knocking Denmark out of the war and routing Peter the Great’s army at Narva in November 1700. Sweden’s absolutist state and allotment army stood at full strength, but it faced a coalition of Russia, Denmark and Saxony-Poland.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1709": "Charles XII’s invasion of Russia ended in catastrophe at the Battle of Poltava in 1709, and most of the surviving army surrendered at Perevolochna days later. The king spent five years in the Ottoman Empire while Russia overran the Baltic provinces and Finland, and Sweden’s great-power position collapsed.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1721": "The Treaty of Nystad of 1721 ceded Livonia, Estonia, Ingria and part of Karelia to Russia, ending Sweden’s empire. At home the Age of Liberty shifted power from the crown to the Riksdag, and Sweden became a middling power known for parliamentary politics, science and iron exports.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1772": "Gustav III’s coup of 1772 ended the Age of Liberty and restored strong royal government. A patron of the arts who founded the Swedish Academy in 1786, he fought Russia in 1788–1790 without gain and was assassinated in 1792. Sweden was culturally lively but militarily secondary.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1809": "Defeat in the Finnish War cost Sweden Finland, about a third of its territory, ceded by the Treaty of Fredrikshamn in 1809. Gustav IV Adolf was deposed, and a new Instrument of Government created a constitutional monarchy. The loss ended Sweden’s role as a power in the eastern Baltic.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1814": "Under Crown Prince Charles John, formerly Marshal Bernadotte, Sweden joined the coalition against Napoleon and gained Norway from Denmark by the Treaty of Kiel in 1814. After a short campaign Norway accepted a personal union under its own constitution. This was Sweden’s last war, opening two centuries of peace.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1905": "The union with Norway was dissolved peacefully in 1905 after a Norwegian referendum. Sweden was industrialising rapidly through timber, iron ore and engineering firms as mass emigration to America slowed, and reforms between 1909 and 1921 brought universal suffrage and parliamentary government.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1945": "Having stayed neutral in the Second World War, Sweden emerged with its industry intact and grew into one of the world’s richest countries. The Social Democrats, in power almost continuously from 1932 to 1976, built a comprehensive welfare state, while armed neutrality sustained a strong air force and defence industry.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|1995": "Sweden joined the European Union in 1995 after a referendum, following a severe banking and economic crisis in the early 1990s that prompted reforms of its welfare model. It remained militarily non-aligned and kept the krona, rejecting the euro in a referendum in 2003.",
  "Kingdom of Sweden|2025": "Sweden is a prosperous constitutional monarchy of about ten and a half million people under King Carl XVI Gustaf. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022 it applied to join NATO and became a member in March 2024, ending more than two centuries of military non-alignment.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|980": "Otto II confirmed the immunity of the church of Liège in 980, removing its lands from the jurisdiction of royal counts. Bishop Notger, appointed in 972, was a trusted servant of the Ottonian dynasty, and the see began its transformation from a diocese with scattered estates into a territorial principality.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|985": "Otto III granted Notger the county of Huy in 985, the first full county acquired by the see and a strategic town on the Meuse. Notger walled the city of Liège, rebuilt its cathedral and expanded its schools, laying the institutional base of a principality loyal to the emperor.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1008": "At Notger’s death in 1008 Liège was a walled episcopal city with flourishing schools and a growing domain along the Meuse. His successors continued to gather counties and rights from the emperors, and the church of Liège became one of the most important ecclesiastical lordships in Lotharingia.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1096": "Bishop Otbert bought the castle and lordship of Bouillon from Godfrey of Bouillon in 1096, providing the money Godfrey needed for the First Crusade. The principality extended its reach into the Ardennes, while Liège’s schools and the Mosan metalworkers were at the height of their reputation.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1213": "The militia of Liège and its allies defeated Duke Henry I of Brabant at the Battle of Steppes in 1213, checking the ambitions of the principality’s most dangerous neighbour. The victory strengthened both prince-bishop and city, which was growing rich from trade and the early working of coal.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1316": "The Peace of Fexhe of 1316 required the consent of the prince and the estates for changes in the law, establishing a framework of shared government that lasted until 1789. Internal conflict continued, but the principality now had stable institutions and a strong sense of its own rights.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1366": "The prince-bishop annexed the County of Loon in 1366 after its ruling line died out, adding a large Dutch-speaking territory around Hasselt. The principality reached its greatest extent, and the Tribunal of the Twenty-Two, created in 1373, completed its constitutional order.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1408": "The Liègeois rebelled against the bishop-elect John of Bavaria and were crushed by John the Fearless of Burgundy at the Battle of Othée in 1408. The city’s privileges were revoked for a time, and the principality fell increasingly under the influence of the Burgundian dukes who surrounded it.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1468": "After defeat at Brustem in 1467 and a final rising, Charles the Bold took Liège in 1468, sacked and burned the city and tore down its walls. The principality was reduced to a Burgundian protectorate under Bishop Louis de Bourbon, and its civic institutions were suspended.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1477": "Charles the Bold’s death at Nancy in 1477 freed Liège from Burgundian control, and Mary of Burgundy restored its liberties. Yet the principality remained unstable: William de la Marck, the “Boar of the Ardennes”, killed Bishop Louis de Bourbon in 1482, and factional war continued for years.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1505": "Erard de la Marck was elected prince-bishop in 1505. He restored order, balanced between France and the Habsburgs and began to rebuild Liège after decades of destruction. The principality’s economy, based on coal, iron and the Meuse trade, recovered quickly under his long rule.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1538": "At Erard de la Marck’s death in 1538 Liège was rebuilt, prosperous and neutral, with a new Palace of the Prince-Bishops under construction since 1526. The principality held a secure position between the Habsburg Netherlands and France, and Liège was becoming a major centre of metalworking and arms making.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1581": "Ernest of Bavaria became prince-bishop in 1581, beginning a century of Wittelsbach rule linked to the archbishopric of Cologne. The principality stayed neutral during the Dutch Revolt and remained prosperous, though conflicts between the princes and the city of Liège, divided into rival parties, intensified.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1649": "Ferdinand of Bavaria imposed his authority on the city of Liège with troops in 1649 after years of conflict with the popular Grignoux faction, and strengthened the citadel to overawe it. The arms industry flourished, but the principality’s politics were embittered and its neutrality increasingly fragile.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1684": "Maximilian Henry of Bavaria’s Regulation of 1684 abolished the political power of the guilds and placed the city firmly under princely control. The principality entered a period of firmer authority and economic strength, but the wars of Louis XIV brought foreign armies across its territory.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1702": "During the War of the Spanish Succession Marlborough captured Liège and its citadel in 1702. Repeated occupation and the presence of foreign garrisons showed that neutrality could not protect the principality from the great powers fighting over the Low Countries, and its political weight was slight.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1772": "François-Charles de Velbruck, prince-bishop from 1772 to 1784, promoted education, public health and the arts and supported the Société d’Émulation founded in 1779. With thriving coal mines and arms workshops, Liège was prosperous and open to the ideas of the Enlightenment.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1789": "The Liège Revolution of August 1789 drove out Prince-Bishop Hoensbroeck, and the citizens installed new magistrates and proclaimed a republic. The old order of the principality was overturned, and its fate became bound up with the rivalries of the Empire, Prussia and revolutionary France.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1791": "Austrian troops restored Hoensbroeck in January 1791 under an imperial mandate. The restored prince-bishopric was weak and depended on foreign arms, and French revolutionary armies entered Liège after their victory at Jemappes in November 1792, though Austria returned briefly in 1793.",
  "Prince-Bishopric of Liège|1795": "After the French victory at Fleurus in 1794 Liège was reoccupied, and on 1 October 1795 the National Convention annexed the principality to France. The cathedral of Saint Lambert was demolished, and more than eight centuries of ecclesiastical rule on the Meuse came to an end.",
  "Kingdom of France|987": "Hugh Capet was elected king after the death of Louis V, the last Carolingian ruler of West Francia. His direct power was limited to a small domain around Paris and Orléans, while great dukes and counts governed their lands almost independently, so the crown\u0027s prestige far exceeded its real strength.",
  "Kingdom of France|1108": "Louis VI the Fat came to the throne with royal authority weak even in the Île-de-France, where castellans defied the king. Over his reign he subdued them with Abbot Suger\u0027s help, laying the base for later growth, but the monarchy was still only one territorial power among several in the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of France|1180": "Philip II Augustus succeeded his father Louis VII. Henry II of England held Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine, far more of France than the king himself, but the Capetian domain was secure, its revenues were growing, and the king\u0027s position as overlord gave him legal tools to challenge his great vassals.",
  "Kingdom of France|1214": "Having taken Normandy, Anjou and Maine from King John in 1202–1204, Philip II crushed the coalition of Emperor Otto IV, the count of Flanders and their English allies at the Battle of Bouvines. The royal domain had grown several times over, and the Capetian king was now the strongest ruler in France.",
  "Kingdom of France|1270": "Louis IX died on crusade at Tunis after a long reign of peace and prestige at home. The Albigensian Crusade had brought much of Languedoc under royal control, the Treaty of Paris of 1259 settled relations with England, and the Parlement of Paris made royal justice a court of appeal for the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of France|1314": "Philip IV the Fair died after a reign that had humbled Pope Boniface VIII, seen the papacy settle at Avignon, destroyed the Knights Templar and summoned the first Estates-General. The monarchy stood at a medieval high point of authority, though defeat by Flemish townsmen at Courtrai in 1302 had shown its limits.",
  "Kingdom of France|1360": "The Treaty of Brétigny ended the first phase of the Hundred Years\u0027 War on English terms. After defeats at Crécy and Poitiers, King John II was a prisoner, and the treaty ceded an enlarged Aquitaine in full sovereignty and set a ransom of three million gold crowns. The Black Death had also cut the population deeply.",
  "Kingdom of France|1380": "At the death of Charles V the Wise, French armies under Bertrand du Guesclin had reconquered most of the lands ceded in 1360 by avoiding pitched battles and besieging towns. Permanent taxes had restored royal power, but Charles VI, who succeeded as a child, would later suffer repeated bouts of madness.",
  "Kingdom of France|1420": "By the Treaty of Troyes the mentally ill Charles VI disinherited his son and named Henry V of England his heir. After the English victory at Agincourt in 1415 and civil war between Armagnacs and Burgundians, the kingdom was divided, Paris was in Burgundian and English hands and the Valois cause seemed lost.",
  "Kingdom of France|1453": "The Battle of Castillon ended the Hundred Years\u0027 War, with the English expelled from all of France except Calais. Charles VII, crowned at Reims in 1429 after Joan of Arc relieved Orléans, had created a permanent army and a permanent taille, resources few other European monarchies then matched.",
  "Kingdom of France|1483": "Louis XI died after defeating the coalitions of great nobles. The death of Charles the Bold at Nancy in 1477 allowed him to seize the duchy of Burgundy and Picardy, and in 1481 he inherited Anjou, Maine and Provence. Of the great fiefs only Brittany remained outside royal control.",
  "Kingdom of France|1515": "Francis I began his reign by defeating the Swiss at the Battle of Marignano and taking Milan. Brittany was tied to the crown by royal marriages, the Concordat of Bologna of 1516 would give the king control of church appointments, and France was a rich, populous kingdom contesting Italy with the Habsburgs.",
  "Kingdom of France|1559": "The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis ended the Italian Wars. France gave up its Italian ambitions but kept Calais, taken in 1558, and the Three Bishoprics of Metz, Toul and Verdun. Henry II died later that year after a jousting accident, leaving young sons and a kingdom divided by the spread of Protestantism.",
  "Kingdom of France|1589": "Henry III was assassinated after nearly three decades of the Wars of Religion, which included the St Bartholomew\u0027s Day massacre of 1572. The Protestant Henry of Navarre inherited a kingdom where the Catholic League held Paris and Spain intervened, and royal authority had collapsed in much of the country.",
  "Kingdom of France|1610": "Henry IV was assassinated in Paris by François Ravaillac. Since converting to Catholicism in 1593 he had won Paris, made peace with Spain at Vervins and granted Protestants toleration by the Edict of Nantes in 1598. With his minister Sully he had restored order and royal finances, though his heir was a child.",
  "Kingdom of France|1643": "Louis XIII died a few months after Cardinal Richelieu. Together they had broken Huguenot military power at La Rochelle, extended the use of intendants and entered the Thirty Years\u0027 War against the Habsburgs in 1635. Days after the king\u0027s death the victory at Rocroi announced France\u0027s military rise, though the Fronde lay ahead.",
  "Kingdom of France|1661": "After Mazarin\u0027s death Louis XIV took personal control of government. The Fronde had been defeated, the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 had given France rights in Alsace, and the Peace of the Pyrenees in 1659 brought Roussillon and Artois. Spain was declining and France was becoming the leading power in Europe.",
  "Kingdom of France|1678": "The Peace of Nijmegen ended the Dutch War, with France gaining Franche-Comté and a line of fortress towns in the Spanish Netherlands. Louis XIV commanded Europe\u0027s largest army, Colbert\u0027s navy and finances were at their height, and the kingdom was the dominant power of the continent and the model of monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of France|1713": "The Treaties of Utrecht ended the War of the Spanish Succession. A Bourbon, Philip V, kept the Spanish throne, but France had suffered defeats such as Blenheim, ceded Newfoundland, Acadia and the Hudson Bay lands to Britain, and carried crushing debts. It remained a great power, but its hegemony was over.",
  "Kingdom of France|1763": "The Treaty of Paris ended the Seven Years\u0027 War with heavy losses: Canada and the lands east of the Mississippi went to Britain, Louisiana west of the river to Spain, and France kept only trading posts in India. The kingdom remained populous and rich, and soon acquired Lorraine in 1766 and Corsica in 1768.",
  "Kingdom of France|1789": "Bankruptcy, worsened by the cost of the American War of Independence, forced Louis XVI to summon the Estates-General. Its third estate proclaimed itself the National Assembly, Parisians stormed the Bastille on 14 July, and feudal privileges were abolished in August. Absolute monarchy was effectively over, though France itself remained populous and strong.",
  "Kingdom of France|1792": "War with Austria and Prussia, suspicion of the king after his flight to Varennes and the storming of the Tuileries on 10 August ended the constitutional monarchy. On 21 September the National Convention abolished the kingship, and the republic was proclaimed the next day. Louis XVI was tried and executed in January 1793.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1000": "Stephen I was crowned king, turning his father Géza\u0027s principality into a Christian kingdom with Latin bishoprics. He still had to subdue semi-independent chiefs such as Gyula in Transylvania, but he controlled the core of the Carpathian basin and enjoyed close ties with the Ottonian emperors.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1030": "Emperor Conrad II invaded Hungary and was forced to retreat, and peace followed in 1031. Stephen had by now overthrown Gyula, organised royal counties and founded the archbishoprics of Esztergom and Kalocsa, and the kingdom controlled nearly all of the Carpathian basin, its high point under the first king.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1038": "Stephen I died without a surviving son, having named his nephew Peter Orseolo as heir. The institutions he built were intact, but the succession pitted Peter against Samuel Aba and other kin, inviting German intervention and opening a period of instability that weakened the young kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1046": "After Henry III restored Peter at Ménfő in 1044 and received his homage, a pagan revolt led by Vata overthrew Peter and killed Bishop Gerard. Andrew I took the throne and restored Christianity, but the kingdom had briefly been an imperial vassal and was torn by civil and religious conflict.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1052": "Andrew I\u0027s resistance forced Henry III to abandon the siege of Pressburg, ending the threat of imperial overlordship. Hungary\u0027s independence was secure, though rivalry between Andrew and his brother Béla, who held a third of the kingdom as a duchy, would soon bring renewed civil war.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1077": "Ladislaus I became king after years of conflict between King Solomon and his cousins Géza and Ladislaus. He restored order with harsh laws against theft, strengthened the church and arranged the canonisation of Stephen I in 1083, giving the kingdom renewed stability and a stronger sense of identity.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1091": "Ladislaus I occupied Slavonia after the death of the last native Croatian king, and the bishopric of Zagreb was founded around this time. He also repelled Cuman raiders. Hungary was becoming a regional power reaching towards the Adriatic, though control of Croatia itself was not yet secure.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1102": "Coloman was crowned king of Croatia, creating a union that lasted, in changing forms, until 1918, and in 1105 he took the Dalmatian towns. With a learned king, new law codes and access to the Adriatic, the kingdom reached a new level of power and international standing.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1167": "The Byzantine army of Manuel I Komnenos defeated the Hungarians at the Battle of Sirmium. Byzantium kept Syrmia and Dalmatia for a time, and Hungary had been drawn into succession disputes promoted by Constantinople. The kingdom was still large and stable, but it had lost ground in the south.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1172": "Béla III, raised at the Byzantine court as Manuel I\u0027s intended heir, returned to become king. After Manuel\u0027s death in 1180 he recovered Dalmatia and Syrmia, and his reign brought a royal chancery, written charters and close ties with both France and Byzantium.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1196": "Béla III died at the height of Árpádian power. A list of his revenues suggested an income rivalling that of western kings, Hungary held Croatia and Dalmatia and exerted influence in Bosnia, Serbia and Galicia, and the royal domain was still intact before Andrew II began giving it away.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1222": "Andrew II\u0027s large grants of royal estates and costly campaigns in Galicia and on the Fifth Crusade provoked the royal servants to force the Golden Bull on him. The kingdom remained large and wealthy, but royal power was weakening as land passed to great lords and the king accepted written limits.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1241": "Batu\u0027s Mongol army destroyed Béla IV\u0027s forces at the Battle of Mohi and ravaged the kingdom for a year. The king fled to the Dalmatian coast, much of the lowland population was killed or starved, and Hungary fell to its lowest point since the kingdom\u0027s foundation.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1260": "Rebuilding was well advanced: new stone castles, new towns such as Buda and fresh settlers had restored the kingdom\u0027s strength. Béla IV contested Styria with Ottokar II of Bohemia and lost the Battle of Kressenbrunn in 1260, but Hungary was again a substantial central European power.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1278": "Hungarian and Cuman cavalry under Ladislaus IV helped Rudolf of Habsburg defeat and kill Ottokar II of Bohemia at the Battle on the Marchfeld. The victory showed the kingdom\u0027s military weight, but at home baronial factions were dividing the realm and the king\u0027s authority was declining.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1290": "Ladislaus IV was murdered by Cumans after years of conflict with the church and nobles. Andrew III took the throne but faced the claims of the Angevins of Naples, and great barons controlled whole regions with their own castles, courts and armies, leaving royal power badly weakened.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301)|1301": "Andrew III died, ending the male line of the Árpád dynasty after three centuries of royal rule. Claimants from Naples, Bohemia and Bavaria contested the throne while oligarchs such as Matthew Csák ruled their provinces, until Charles I of Anjou established a new dynasty.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1035": "On the death of Sancho III the Great of Navarre, his son Ramiro I received the small Pyrenean county of Aragon around Jaca. He ruled independently but without the formal royal title, controlling a few mountain valleys squeezed between Navarre and the powerful Muslim taifa of Zaragoza.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1063": "Ramiro I was killed at Graus fighting the taifa of Zaragoza, which was supported by Castile. He had absorbed Sobrarbe and Ribagorza, enlarging the kingdom, but Aragon remained a small mountain state, and his son Sancho Ramírez inherited a frontier still far from the rich lowlands of the Ebro.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1076": "After the murder of Sancho IV of Navarre at Peñalén, Sancho Ramírez was accepted as king of Pamplona, uniting Navarre with Aragon. Under papal protection since 1068, he now led a larger realm and pressed the siege war against the Muslim towns of the Ebro foothills.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1096": "Peter I defeated an army of Zaragoza and its Castilian allies at the Battle of Alcoraz and took Huesca, the first major Muslim city conquered by Aragon, which became the royal seat. Barbastro followed in 1100, and the kingdom now reached the edge of the Ebro plain.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1118": "Alfonso I the Battler captured Zaragoza after a siege of about seven months, with help from French crusaders. The conquest of the great Muslim city of the Ebro valley, followed by Tudela, Tarazona and Calatayud, more than doubled the kingdom and gave it a rich capital.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1134": "Alfonso I died after his defeat at the Battle of Fraga, leaving his kingdom to the military orders. The Aragonese nobles rejected the will and chose his brother Ramiro II, a monk, while Navarre broke away under its own king and Castile occupied Zaragoza for a time.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1137": "Ramiro II betrothed his infant daughter Petronila to Ramon Berenguer IV, count of Barcelona, who took over the government. The union with Catalonia secured Aragon against Castile and Navarre, recovered Zaragoza from Castilian control and joined the inland kingdom to a Mediterranean power.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1162": "Ramon Berenguer IV died and his son Alfonso II became count of Barcelona, and from 1164 king of Aragon, the first ruler of the Crown of Aragon. The joint realm had taken Tortosa and Lleida in 1148–1149, and Alfonso II soon founded Teruel to secure Aragon\u0027s southern frontier.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1213": "Peter II, who had fought with Castile at Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212, was killed at the Battle of Muret defending his vassals in Languedoc against Simon de Montfort. The Crown lost its influence north of the Pyrenees, and the child king James I faced years of noble faction.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1238": "James I the Conqueror took the city of Valencia, having conquered Majorca from 1229. Aragonese knights shared in these conquests, though Valencia became a separate kingdom with its own laws. The Crown was now a leading power of the western Mediterranean, and the kingdom of Aragon its royal core.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1282": "Peter III took Sicily after the Sicilian Vespers, bringing the Crown of Aragon to its height of power and prestige. The kingdom of Aragon stood at its strongest within the Crown, and its nobles and towns used the crisis to win the General Privilege from the king in 1283.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1348": "Peter IV crushed the Union of Aragon at the Battle of Épila, ending noble attempts to dominate the crown, while the Black Death struck the kingdom heavily. Royal authority was restored, but population losses and long wars with Castile weakened Aragon in the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1412": "The Compromise of Caspe, in which nine delegates from Aragon, Catalonia and Valencia chose among rival claimants, gave the throne to Ferdinand of Antequera of the Castilian house of Trastámara. The peaceful settlement preserved the union of the realms but drew the Crown towards Castile.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1479": "Ferdinand II became king of Aragon, ten years after his marriage to Isabella I of Castile. The union of the two crowns made Aragon part of the new Spanish monarchy, which conquered Granada in 1492 and Naples in 1504, but the kingdom kept its own Cortes, laws and Justicia.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1516": "Ferdinand II died and the crown passed to his grandson Charles I, the future Emperor Charles V. Aragon became one of many Habsburg realms ruled by an absent king through a viceroy, keeping its institutions but losing political weight as Castile and its American empire dominated the monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1591": "Riots in Zaragoza over Antonio Pérez, Philip II\u0027s fugitive secretary, led a royal army to enter the kingdom. The Justicia Juan de Lanuza was executed, and the Cortes of Tarazona in 1592 curtailed the fueros, showing that the kingdom\u0027s privileges could no longer stand against the crown.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1610": "Aragon expelled its Moriscos, the descendants of the Muslim farmers who had worked its irrigated lands for centuries. The loss of tens of thousands of skilled cultivators and taxpayers impoverished noble estates and the countryside, deepening the economic stagnation of the seventeenth century.",
  "Kingdom of Aragon|1707": "After the Bourbon victory at the Battle of Almansa, Philip V abolished the fueros and institutions of Aragon by the Nueva Planta decree of 29 June 1707, as punishment for supporting Archduke Charles. The kingdom became a province of Bourbon Spain, though its civil law was restored in 1711.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1065": "Ferdinand I died and divided his realms, leaving Castile to his eldest son Sancho II. Castile was a vigorous frontier kingdom drawing tribute from the Muslim taifas, but it was smaller than León, and the division at once set Ferdinand\u0027s sons against each other in a struggle for the whole inheritance.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1072": "Sancho II defeated his brother Alfonso VI at Golpejera and seized León, only to be murdered while besieging Zamora. Alfonso VI then reunited Castile, León and Galicia, gaining a much larger realm and a dominant position over the divided Muslim taifa kingdoms of al-Andalus.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1085": "Alfonso VI took Toledo, the old Visigothic capital and one of the great cities of al-Andalus, without a pitched battle. With tributes flowing from the other taifas and the frontier advanced to the Tagus, Castile-León stood at a height of power it would not reach again for over a century.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1086": "The taifa kings called in the Almoravid emir Yusuf ibn Tashfin, who defeated Alfonso VI at the Battle of Sagrajas. Toledo held, but the flow of tribute soon ended, the Almoravids absorbed the taifas, and Castile was thrown on the defensive along its new southern frontier.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1108": "The Almoravids defeated a Castilian army at the Battle of Uclés, killing Alfonso VI\u0027s only son Sancho and taking a line of frontier fortresses. Toledo itself was threatened, and the succession to the aged king now rested on his daughter Urraca and her contested marriages and alliances.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1126": "Alfonso VII succeeded his mother Urraca after years of civil war between her, her husband Alfonso I of Aragon and the Galician nobles. Castile had lost border lands to Aragon, but the new king soon recovered them and restored royal authority across both his kingdoms.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1135": "Alfonso VII was crowned emperor in León, receiving homage from the king of Navarre, the count of Barcelona and other lords. With Aragon weakened after Alfonso I\u0027s death, Castile-León was the leading Christian power of Iberia and was raiding deep into Almoravid Andalusia.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1147": "Alfonso VII captured Almería, a great Mediterranean port, with Genoese and Catalan help, as Almoravid rule collapsed into a second taifa period. Castilian power reached far into the south, though the arrival of the Almohads from North Africa would soon reverse much of this advance.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1157": "Alfonso VII died returning from a failed campaign to save Almería, and his realms were divided again: Castile to Sancho III and León to Ferdinand II. Castile was now a separate kingdom facing both the rising Almohads and a rival Christian neighbour in León.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1158": "Sancho III died after a year, leaving the two-year-old Alfonso VIII. The Lara and Castro families fought over the regency, Navarre seized lands in the Rioja and León intervened, and the kingdom was weakened by a decade of civil war and foreign encroachment.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1177": "Alfonso VIII, ruling in his own right and married to Eleanor of England, took Cuenca after a long siege with Aragonese help. The kingdom had recovered from the minority, extended its frontier towards La Mancha and relied on the military orders of Calatrava and Santiago to hold it.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1195": "The Almohad caliph Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur crushed Alfonso VIII at the Battle of Alarcos. Calatrava and other frontier castles were lost, Leonese and Navarrese forces attacked Castile, and the kingdom spent the following years on the defensive against Muslims and Christians alike.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1212": "Alfonso VIII, with Peter II of Aragon, Sancho VII of Navarre and crusaders, destroyed the Almohad army at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa. The victory broke Almohad power in Iberia and made Castile the unchallenged leader of the reconquest, the high point of the kingdom\u0027s history.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1214": "Alfonso VIII died and his ten-year-old son Henry I succeeded under regents, soon dominated by the Lara family. The kingdom kept the advantage won at Las Navas, but a new minority again set noble factions against each other and halted the advance against the weakened Almohads.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1217": "Henry I was killed by a falling roof tile, and his sister Berenguela inherited the crown and handed it to her son Ferdinand III. The young king defeated the Lara faction and an invasion by his father Alfonso IX of León, and secured his authority in Castile.",
  "Kingdom of Castile|1230": "Ferdinand III inherited León on his father\u0027s death, and the agreement of Benavente made the union permanent. The separate Kingdom of Castile ended, absorbed into the Crown of Castile, a far stronger state that went on to take Córdoba in 1236 and Seville in 1248.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1085": "Henry of Laach appears for the first time as count palatine of the Rhine, as the office moved from the Lower Rhine to the middle Rhine under pressure from the archbishops of Cologne. The counts held scattered lands and rights, but the title was still more an office than a territory.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1156": "Frederick Barbarossa gave the palatinate to his half-brother Conrad of Hohenstaufen, who built a compact base around Heidelberg, Alzey and the lower Neckar. Backed by the emperor, the count palatine became a leading prince of the Rhineland, and the title gained a lasting territorial core.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1214": "Emperor Frederick II granted the palatinate to Louis I of Bavaria, beginning almost six centuries of Wittelsbach rule. Held together with Bavaria, the Rhineland lands were at first governed from a distance, but Heidelberg and its castle grew in importance as the family\u0027s western seat.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1329": "By the Treaty of Pavia, Emperor Louis IV the Bavarian gave the Rhenish Palatinate and the Upper Palatinate to his nephews Rudolf II and Rupert I. The Palatinate became a separate principality again under its own Wittelsbach line, with a claim to the electoral vote shared with Bavaria.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1356": "The Golden Bull of Charles IV fixed the count palatine as one of the seven electors, with the office of arch-steward and the imperial vicariate. Rich from the Rhine tolls and ruling the Upper Palatinate as well, Rupert I was one of the most powerful princes of the Empire.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1400": "Elector Rupert III was elected King of the Romans after the electors deposed Wenceslaus. The electorate reached its greatest prestige, with a king, the University of Heidelberg, large toll revenues and lands from the Rhine to the Upper Palatinate, though Rupert lacked the means to rule the Empire effectively.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1410": "Rupert III died and his lands were divided among four sons, creating the branches of Neumarkt, Simmern-Zweibrücken and Mosbach beside the electoral line. The electorate kept its vote and Heidelberg, but the partition reduced its territory and resources for generations.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1462": "Frederick I the Victorious defeated and captured the margrave of Baden, the count of Württemberg and the bishop of Metz at the Battle of Seckenheim. He enlarged the electorate at his neighbours\u0027 expense and made it the dominant power of the upper Rhine for the rest of his reign.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1505": "The Landshut War of Succession, fought for Elector Philip\u0027s son Rupert over the Bavarian inheritance, ended in defeat. The Palatinate lost lands to Bavaria and Württemberg, and only the small new principality of Palatinate-Neuburg was saved for Rupert\u0027s young sons.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1563": "Frederick III, who had inherited the electorate in 1559 when the senior line died out, issued the Heidelberg Catechism and made the Palatinate the leading Reformed state of the Empire. Its university attracted Calvinist scholars from across Europe, and its electors became leaders of international Protestantism.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1608": "Frederick IV became head of the Protestant Union, a defensive alliance of Protestant princes and cities, two years after founding Mannheim as a fortress town. The Palatinate led Protestant politics in the Empire and was linked to the Dutch Republic, the French Huguenots and soon England.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1619": "The Bohemian estates elected Frederick V, son-in-law of James VI and I, as their king, and he accepted the crown. For a few months the elector ruled Bohemia as well as the Palatinate, the high point of Palatine ambition, but he lacked allies able to defend his new kingdom.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1623": "After the Battle of White Mountain and the occupation of the Palatinate by Spanish and Catholic League troops, Frederick V was outlawed and his electoral title transferred to Maximilian of Bavaria. Heidelberg had fallen and its library was sent to Rome, while the elector lived in exile in The Hague.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia restored Charles I Louis to the Lower Palatinate with a new, eighth electoral title, while the Upper Palatinate stayed with Bavaria. The country had lost a large share of its population in the war, and the elector began a long work of resettlement and rebuilding.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1685": "The Simmern line died out with Elector Charles II, and the Catholic Philip William of Neuburg inherited. The recovering electorate now had Catholic rulers over a largely Protestant population, and Louis XIV claimed lands for his sister-in-law Elizabeth Charlotte, setting the stage for war.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1689": "In the Nine Years\u0027 War French troops systematically devastated the Palatinate, burning Heidelberg, Mannheim, Speyer and Worms and blowing up much of Heidelberg Castle. The electors resided in Düsseldorf, and the land again lost much of its population, wealth and buildings.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1720": "Charles III Philip moved the residence from Heidelberg to Mannheim after a dispute with the Protestants over Heidelberg\u0027s Church of the Holy Spirit. The rebuilt city, laid out on a grid around one of the largest palaces in Europe, signalled the electorate\u0027s recovery as a prosperous middle-sized state.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1742": "Charles Theodore of the Sulzbach line became elector. His Mannheim court, its famous orchestra and theatre and the Academy of Sciences founded in 1763 made the Palatinate a cultural centre of the German Enlightenment, though it remained a modest power in military and political terms.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1777": "Charles Theodore inherited Bavaria on the extinction of the Bavarian Wittelsbachs and moved his court to Munich, uniting the two electorates. The combined state was larger, but the Palatinate became a province, and Austria\u0027s claims led to the War of the Bavarian Succession in 1778–1779.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1797": "By the Treaty of Campo Formio Austria recognised the French annexation of the left bank of the Rhine, including a large part of the Palatinate, which French armies had occupied since 1794. The remaining right-bank lands around Heidelberg and Mannheim were exposed and their future uncertain.",
  "Electoral Palatinate|1803": "The Imperial Recess divided the right-bank Palatinate, giving Heidelberg and Mannheim to Baden and smaller districts to Hesse-Darmstadt, Leiningen and others. The Electoral Palatinate ceased to exist, its elector was compensated in Franconia and Swabia, and its name survived in the later Bavarian Rhenish Palatinate.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1099": "Leading Genoese citizens swore the compagna communis and elected consuls, while Genoese ships and engineers such as Guglielmo Embriaco took part in the capture of Jerusalem. The new commune was a modest port with crusading ambitions, its power still small but its fleets already reaching the eastern Mediterranean.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1101": "Genoese forces helped capture Caesarea and brought home rich booty, including the Sacro Catino. Grants of quarters and tax privileges in the crusader ports gave Genoa its first overseas trading bases and the profits that funded further expansion, lifting it above most rival Italian ports except Pisa and Venice.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1148": "Genoa joined Castile in taking Almería in 1147 and Barcelona in taking Tortosa in 1148. The campaigns showed its naval reach in the western Mediterranean but left the commune deeply indebted, forcing it to pledge revenues to creditors, a pattern of privately financed public enterprise that lasted for centuries.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1191": "Feuds among the consular families led Genoa to appoint an outside podestà to govern for fixed terms. Trade with Sicily, North Africa and the Levant was growing, and Genoa was a major Mediterranean port, but internal strife and rivalry with Pisa held its power at a middling level.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1261": "By the Treaty of Nymphaeum Genoa allied with Michael VIII Palaiologos, who recovered Constantinople the same year. Genoa displaced Venice in Byzantine favour, gained access to the Black Sea and soon received Pera, opening the trade in grain, slaves and furs that underpinned its greatest age.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1284": "Oberto Doria destroyed the Pisan fleet at Meloria, ending Pisa’s career as a naval power and leaving Genoa dominant in the Tyrrhenian Sea. With colonies at Pera and Caffa and a growing network in the Aegean, the republic was close to the height of its maritime strength.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1298": "Lamba Doria’s victory over Venice at Curzola marked the high point of Genoese naval power. Genoa held a chain of colonies from Chios to the Crimea, its merchants traded from Flanders to Persia, and its population and wealth placed it among the leading cities of Europe.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1339": "A popular revolt made Simone Boccanegra the first doge. The office gave the republic a new head, but feuds between noble clans and popular factions remained intense, and repeated exiles and coups sapped the state even as Genoese trade and colonies in the east remained strong.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1347": "Galleys returning from Caffa, besieged by the Golden Horde, are widely thought to have helped bring the Black Death to the Mediterranean. Plague cut Genoa’s population sharply, while the new Maona of Chios, founded in 1346, showed that private companies were now taking over much of colonial enterprise.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1381": "The Peace of Turin ended the War of Chioggia. Genoa had come close to taking Venice in 1379 but lost its fleet at Chioggia in 1380. The defeat ended its hopes of supremacy in the eastern trade and left the republic exhausted and divided at home.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1396": "Unable to end its civil strife, Genoa placed itself under Charles VI of France. Foreign lordship brought governors from outside and periodic revolts, and for much of the next century the republic was alternately ruled by France and by Milan, its independence and power much reduced.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1407": "The Bank of Saint George was founded to consolidate the public debt. Under the French governor Boucicaut Genoa regained some order, and the bank, which collected taxes and later governed colonies, gave the republic a durable financial institution that outlasted its many changes of regime.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1453": "Mehmed II captured Constantinople despite the Genoese Giovanni Giustiniani Longo’s defence of its walls, and Pera surrendered to the Ottomans. Genoa’s Black Sea colonies were now cut off, and the Bank of Saint George took over Caffa and Corsica, which the weakened state could no longer manage.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1475": "The Ottomans took Caffa, ending two centuries of Genoese trade in the Black Sea. Genoa was under Milanese lordship and its eastern empire was reduced to Chios. Its merchants increasingly turned west to Spain, Portugal and the Atlantic, where Genoese capital would find new opportunities.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1528": "Andrea Doria abandoned Francis I for Charles V, freed Genoa from French control and gave it an aristocratic constitution with 28 alberghi and biennial doges. Alliance with Spain brought stability, protection and access to Spanish finance, beginning a strong recovery of the republic’s wealth and standing.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1576": "After brief civil war between old and new nobility, the new laws of 1576 settled the constitution. Genoese bankers were at the centre of Spanish finance, handling American silver through the Piacenza fairs, and the city built its great palaces; in wealth, if not in arms, Genoa had recovered remarkably.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1627": "The Spanish crown’s bankruptcy of 1627 damaged Genoese bankers and ended what Braudel called the age of the Genoese, as Portuguese financiers gained ground in Madrid. Genoa remained rich and independent, but its dependence on Spain and its small military force were increasingly exposed.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1684": "Louis XIV’s fleet bombarded Genoa for its ties to Spain, and in 1685 the doge was forced to apologise at Versailles. The plague of 1656–1657 had already cut the population heavily, and the republic was now a minor state at the mercy of the great powers.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1746": "Austrian forces occupied Genoa during the War of the Austrian Succession until a popular rising drove them out in December. The republic survived, but its weakness was plain, and the Corsican revolt that began in 1729 continued to drain its limited resources.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1768": "Unable to subdue the Corsicans under Pasquale Paoli, Genoa ceded its rights over Corsica to France by the Treaty of Versailles. Reduced to Liguria and a few small possessions, the republic remained a commercial and banking centre but had almost no weight in European politics.",
  "Republic of Genoa|1797": "Under pressure from Napoleon Bonaparte, the aristocratic regime gave way to the French-sponsored Ligurian Republic. Nearly seven centuries of the Genoese republic ended; Genoa was annexed to France in 1805 and in 1815 the Congress of Vienna awarded it to the Kingdom of Sardinia.",
  "Republic of Florence|1115": "Matilda of Canossa’s death left the March of Tuscany without effective rule, and Florentine citizens began to govern their own city. Florence was a moderate town of the Arno valley, smaller than Pisa or Lucca, with little territory beyond its walls and no institutions yet recorded.",
  "Republic of Florence|1138": "Consuls are first recorded governing Florence, showing an organised commune. The city had destroyed Fiesole in 1125 and was subduing the rural nobility of its contado, building the territorial base and population that would make it a growing force in Tuscan politics.",
  "Republic of Florence|1252": "Under the popular regime of the Primo Popolo, Florence minted the gold florin. Guild merchants shared power, the cloth trade and banking were expanding fast, and the city was becoming one of the largest in Italy, though Guelph–Ghibelline feuds made its politics violent and unstable.",
  "Republic of Florence|1260": "A Ghibelline army of Siena and Florentine exiles crushed the Guelph forces of Florence at Montaperti. The Ghibellines took over the city, and only the opposition of Farinata degli Uberti, according to the chroniclers, prevented its destruction. The setback was temporary; the Guelphs returned after 1266.",
  "Republic of Florence|1289": "Florence defeated Ghibelline Arezzo at Campaldino, confirming the Guelph alliance as the leading power in Tuscany. The guild-based priorate created in 1282 was taking root, and Florentine banks were collecting papal revenues and lending to kings across Europe.",
  "Republic of Florence|1293": "The Ordinances of Justice excluded the magnate families from office and created the Gonfaloniere of Justice. The guild republic was now firmly established, the cathedral and Palazzo Vecchio were soon begun, and Florence was among the richest and most populous cities in Europe.",
  "Republic of Florence|1338": "According to Giovanni Villani, Florence had around 90,000 people and some 200 wool workshops, and its banks were the greatest in Europe. This was the height of the medieval commune’s economic power, built on cloth, finance and the florin, before the crises of the following decade.",
  "Republic of Florence|1343": "The brief lordship of Walter of Brienne ended in revolt, and defaults by Edward III of England and other debtors helped bring down the Bardi and Peruzzi banks in the mid-1340s. Florence’s financial dominance was badly damaged, though its wool industry remained large.",
  "Republic of Florence|1348": "The Black Death killed perhaps half the population, which never regained its earlier size during the republic. Labour shortages and social tensions followed, but the commune kept its institutions and its territorial ambitions, and commerce recovered in the following decades.",
  "Republic of Florence|1378": "After the War of the Eight Saints against the papacy, the wool workers, the Ciompi, rose and briefly took power. Their regime fell within weeks, and by 1382 an oligarchy of wealthy families led by the Albizzi was in control, giving the republic more stable leadership.",
  "Republic of Florence|1406": "Florence conquered Pisa after a long siege, having survived the threat of Giangaleazzo Visconti of Milan, who died in 1402. With Arezzo, Pisa and access to the sea, the republic now ruled most of Tuscany and was one of the five leading powers of Italy.",
  "Republic of Florence|1434": "Cosimo de’ Medici returned from exile and began three generations of informal Medici leadership. The Medici bank was the richest in Europe, Brunelleschi’s dome was nearing completion, and Florence combined territorial strength, financial power and unmatched artistic patronage.",
  "Republic of Florence|1454": "The Peace of Lodi, which Cosimo de’ Medici helped bring about with Francesco Sforza of Milan, created a balance of power among the Italian states. Florence enjoyed decades of relative peace, prosperity and prestige, its influence resting on diplomacy and wealth more than arms.",
  "Republic of Florence|1478": "The Pazzi conspiracy, backed by Pope Sixtus IV, killed Giuliano de’ Medici but failed against Lorenzo. A war with the papacy and Naples followed, which Lorenzo ended by a daring personal mission to Naples in 1479–1480, strengthening his authority at home and abroad.",
  "Republic of Florence|1490": "Lorenzo de’ Medici was the arbiter of the Italian balance of power, his son Giovanni was made a cardinal, and Florence was the cultural capital of Europe, home to Ficino, Botticelli and the young Michelangelo. This marked the summit of the republic’s prestige and influence.",
  "Republic of Florence|1494": "Charles VIII of France invaded Italy; Piero de’ Medici yielded to him and was expelled. Pisa rebelled, and the restored republic under Savonarola’s influence created a Great Council. Florence kept its independence, but it had lost territory and its place as a balancing power.",
  "Republic of Florence|1512": "A Spanish army sacked Prato, Piero Soderini fled and the Medici returned. The republic now depended on the shifting fortunes of France, Spain and the papacy, and from 1513, with a Medici pope in Rome, Florence was effectively governed as a Medici possession.",
  "Republic of Florence|1527": "The Sack of Rome weakened Clement VII, and the Florentines expelled the Medici and restored a republic. It was isolated diplomatically and militarily, relying on France, which abandoned it in 1529 when Charles V and Clement VII made peace.",
  "Republic of Florence|1530": "After a ten-month siege by imperial and papal troops and the defeat of Francesco Ferrucci at Gavinana, Florence capitulated in August. The republic survived only in name while Charles V and Clement VII arranged a Medici government for the city.",
  "Republic of Florence|1532": "The Signoria was abolished and Alessandro de’ Medici became hereditary Duke of the Florentine Republic. Four centuries of communal and republican government ended, and the Medici state became the Duchy of Florence and, from 1569, the Grand Duchy of Tuscany.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1121": "Bolesław III of Poland conquered the lands of the lower Oder around 1121–1122, and Wartislaw I accepted Polish overlordship and tribute. The Griffin principality began as a dependent Slavic realm of strongholds and trading towns, with a pagan population and limited central control.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1128": "Otto of Bamberg’s second mission persuaded the Pomeranian nobles at Usedom to accept Christianity. Conversion integrated the duchy into Latin Christendom, strengthened the duke’s authority over the tribes and opened the way for a bishopric, monasteries and closer ties with German and Polish princes.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1164": "After Wartislaw I’s murder and Polish fragmentation, the dukes faced Danish raids and Saxon expansion. Defeat at Verchen by forces of Henry the Lion and Denmark forced submission to the Saxon duke, leaving Pomerania weak and exposed to its neighbours on land and sea.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1181": "Bogislaw I received Pomerania as a fief of the Holy Roman Empire from Frederick Barbarossa. Imperial status gave the duke higher standing among German princes, though from 1185 Danish power under Canute VI forced the duchy into Danish vassalage for four decades.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1227": "The Danish defeat at Bornhöved ended Denmark’s overlordship. Under Barnim I and his cousin Wartislaw III the duchy recovered its freedom of action and began large-scale German settlement and town foundation that would transform its economy and society.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1250": "By the Treaty of Landin Barnim I ceded the Uckermark to Brandenburg, whose margraves claimed overlordship by an imperial grant of 1231. Despite the loss, town foundations such as Szczecin in 1243 and growing trade were making Pomerania more populous and prosperous.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1295": "The duchy was divided into Pomerania-Stettin and Pomerania-Wolgast. Partition reduced its unity, but both duchies were growing economically as their towns joined the Hanseatic network, and the dukes of both lines kept their position as imperial princes against Brandenburg’s persistent claims of overlordship.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1325": "Pomerania-Wolgast inherited the Principality of Rügen with Stralsund when its native dynasty died out. The acquisition, secured after war with Mecklenburg, added rich lands and a leading Hanseatic port, and in 1338 Pomerania-Stettin was confirmed as free of Brandenburg overlordship.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1370": "The Peace of Stralsund, concluded in a Pomeranian town, marked the Hanseatic League’s victory over Denmark. Pomeranian towns shared in the League’s commercial height, bringing wealth to the duchy, although the towns were powerful and often independent of their dukes.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1397": "Eric of Pomerania, a Griffin prince, was crowned king of Denmark, Norway and Sweden at Kalmar. The dynasty’s prestige reached a new height, though the duchy itself remained divided among several lines and its own resources were modest.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1456": "The University of Greifswald was founded with the support of Duke Wartislaw IX. Pomerania was culturally and economically established among the north German territories, but its partitions among different lines continued to limit the dukes’ power against Brandenburg.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1478": "Bogislaw X reunited all Pomerania under one ruler for the first time since 1295. He reorganised finances and administration, curbed noble independence and confronted Brandenburg, bringing the duchy close to the greatest strength it would ever reach, with revenues, courts and officials organised on a single territorial basis.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1493": "By the Treaty of Pyritz Brandenburg renounced overlordship over Pomerania in exchange for a right of succession. Bogislaw X, married to a Polish princess and soon to make a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, ruled a united, reformed duchy at the height of its power and standing.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1523": "Bogislaw X died after nearly fifty years of rule. His sons inherited a well-ordered state, and the Treaty of Grimnitz in 1529 confirmed Pomerania’s status as an imperial duchy, though Brandenburg’s right of succession remained a long-term danger.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1534": "The diet at Treptow adopted the Reformation, and Johannes Bugenhagen drew up the Pomeranian church order. The dukes gained church property and authority, but in 1532 the duchy had again been divided between Stettin and Wolgast, weakening its unity.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1618": "Under Philip II of Pomerania-Stettin the court patronised art and learning, commissioning the Pomeranian Art Cabinet and Lubinus’s map of the duchy. The duchy was at peace and prosperous, but the dynasty had few heirs, and the Thirty Years’ War was beginning.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1627": "Bogislaw XIV, sole ruler since 1625, had to accept the occupation of Pomerania by Wallenstein’s imperial army. The duke lost control of his territory, while in 1628 Stralsund successfully resisted Wallenstein’s siege with Danish and Swedish support, foreshadowing Swedish intervention.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1630": "Gustavus Adolphus landed on Usedom, occupied Szczecin and imposed an alliance on Bogislaw XIV. Pomerania became a Swedish base and battleground, its government subordinate to Swedish commanders and its population devastated by war, plague and requisitions, and its ageing duke left with almost no real authority.",
  "Duchy of Pomerania|1637": "Bogislaw XIV died childless in March, ending the Griffin dynasty and the duchy. Sweden kept its occupation against Brandenburg’s claim, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 divided the land between Swedish Western Pomerania and Brandenburg’s Farther Pomerania.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1130": "Roger II was crowned in Palermo on Christmas Day, uniting Sicily with Apulia and Calabria. The new kingdom was rich and well organised from the start, but its legitimacy rested on an antipope, and the pope, the emperor and mainland rebels contested it for nearly a decade.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1139": "Roger’s forces captured Pope Innocent II at Galluccio, and by the Treaty of Mignano the pope recognised Roger as king. With Capua, Naples and Bari subdued, the kingdom controlled all southern Italy, and Roger began building its centralised administration and law.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1148": "George of Antioch’s fleets had taken Tripoli and Mahdia, creating a Sicilian dominion in North Africa, and raided Byzantine Greece. Roger’s kingdom was the leading naval power of the central Mediterranean, its wealth and fleet making it a match for Byzantium and the German emperors.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1154": "Roger II died the year al-Idrisi completed the Book of Roger. The kingdom stood at its height: united, centralised, militarily strong and culturally brilliant, with its African possessions still held and a court that drew on Latin, Greek and Arabic traditions.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1166": "William I’s reign had seen baronial revolts, the murder of his minister Maio of Bari in 1160 and the loss of the African territories to the Almohads. William II succeeded as a child under regency, the kingdom still rich and intact but weaker abroad.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1189": "William II, builder of Monreale, died childless after a reign of peace at home and costly expeditions abroad. The kingdom remained wealthy and stable, but the succession was disputed between Tancred of Lecce and Roger II’s daughter Constance, wife of Henry VI.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1194": "Henry VI conquered the kingdom and was crowned in Palermo, the day before his son Frederick was born. Sicily now passed to the Hohenstaufen and was tied to the Holy Roman Empire, its resources drawn into imperial politics and its barons resentful of German rule.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1231": "Frederick II, after restoring royal authority in the 1220s, issued the Constitutions of Melfi. The kingdom was again centralised, its finances strong and its court a centre of learning and poetry, though Frederick’s wars with the papacy placed heavy demands on it.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1266": "Charles of Anjou, invited by the pope, defeated and killed Manfred at Benevento and took the kingdom. Angevin rule brought French barons and heavy taxation, and the court moved towards Naples, while Sicily itself was increasingly treated as a source of revenue.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1282": "The Sicilian Vespers drove the Angevins from the island, and Peter III of Aragon became king. The mainland remained Angevin, so the realm was split in two, and the island kingdom was left with a fraction of its former territory, population and power.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1302": "The Peace of Caltabellotta ended the War of the Vespers and left Frederick III king of the island, called Trinacria in the treaty. Sicily was independent under a cadet Aragonese line, but small, war-weary and increasingly dominated by its barons.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1409": "Martin the Younger died, ending the separate Sicilian line, and the island passed to the Crown of Aragon. The Compromise of Caspe in 1412 confirmed Aragonese rule, and Sicily became a viceroyalty governed from abroad, keeping its parliament but losing its own king.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1516": "With the accession of Charles of Habsburg, Sicily became part of the Spanish monarchy. As a frontier against the Ottomans and a granary for Spain, it gained strategic importance, fortifications and a role in the naval wars of the sixteenth century.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1647": "Bad harvests and taxation set off a revolt in Palermo, part of the same wave as Masaniello’s rising in Naples. Spanish authority was restored, but the kingdom was burdened by taxes, baronial power and economic stagnation in a declining Spanish empire.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1676": "Messina’s revolt brought French intervention, and French fleets fought the Spanish and their Dutch allies, whose admiral de Ruyter was mortally wounded off Augusta. Spain regained Messina in 1678 and punished it severely, and the island remained a province fought over by greater powers.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1713": "The Treaty of Utrecht gave Sicily to Victor Amadeus II of Savoy, crowned in Palermo. The island kingdom passed between dynasties as part of great-power bargaining, and in 1718 Spain invaded it before a British fleet destroyed the Spanish navy off Cape Passaro.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1720": "By the Treaty of The Hague Savoy exchanged Sicily for Sardinia, and the island passed to the Austrian Habsburg Charles VI. Sicily was now a distant possession of Vienna, with little say in its own fate, the low point of its standing as a kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1735": "Charles of Bourbon conquered Sicily and was crowned in Palermo, beginning Bourbon rule. Sicily shared a king with Naples but remained a separate kingdom with its own parliament, and its nobility kept considerable power under largely absentee rule from Naples.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1812": "Ferdinand, sheltering in Palermo under British protection during the Napoleonic wars, accepted a constitution modelled partly on Britain’s and the abolition of feudalism. For a few years Sicily was the seat of the court and had a parliament with real powers.",
  "Kingdom of Sicily|1816": "After returning to Naples, Ferdinand merged Naples and Sicily into the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies and abolished the Sicilian constitution. The separate Kingdom of Sicily, founded in 1130, ended, and Sicilian resentment fed revolts in 1820 and 1848.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1136": "Novgorodians arrested and expelled Prince Vsevolod Mstislavich, beginning the republic. The city was already the second centre of Rus’ with a large northern hinterland, but its new institutions were still taking shape and its princes were still drawn from rival Rurikid branches.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1156": "The townspeople elected Arkady as their bishop, adding church leadership to the posadnik and tysyatsky chosen in the city. With its own bishop, elected officials and contracted princes, Novgorod had established the distinctive institutions that made it a self-governing state.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1170": "Novgorod repelled the army of Andrey Bogolyubsky of Vladimir-Suzdal, a victory celebrated in the legend of the Icon of the Sign. The city showed it could defend its independence against the strongest Russian prince, though it still depended on grain from the Suzdal lands.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1238": "The Mongol army that destroyed Vladimir and other Russian cities turned back before reaching Novgorod. The city escaped devastation but now faced a new overlord in the steppe, while Swedish and German crusaders pressed on its north-western frontier.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1242": "Prince Alexander Nevsky defeated the Livonian knights on the ice of Lake Peipus, two years after his victory over the Swedes on the Neva. Novgorod secured its western borders and its trade, avoiding the fate of the conquered Baltic peoples.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1259": "Under pressure from Alexander Nevsky, Novgorod accepted a Mongol census and agreed to pay tribute to the Golden Horde. The city kept its institutions and trade, but submission to the Horde and dependence on the grand princes limited its independence.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1268": "Novgorod and its allies fought a bloody battle against Danish and Livonian forces at Rakvere. The outcome was claimed by both sides, but the Livonians made peace, and the city’s western frontier and Hanseatic trade remained secure for decades to come.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1323": "The Treaty of Nöteborg drew the first border between Novgorod and Sweden, after the city built the fortress of Oreshek. Novgorod controlled a huge territory from the Gulf of Finland to the White Sea and the Urals, and its fur trade was flourishing.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1348": "By the Treaty of Bolotovo, traditionally dated to this year, Novgorod recognised the independence of Pskov, its former dependency. The loss was offset by the city’s wealth and the expansion of its northern territories, and Novgorod remained the dominant power of the Russian north-west.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1375": "Novgorodian river raiders, the ushkuiniki, sacked Kostroma and other Volga towns, showing the reach of the city’s adventurers. Trade with the Hanseatic Peterhof was at its height, and the republic’s wealth paid for new churches and their frescoes.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1386": "Dmitry Donskoy of Moscow marched on Novgorod and forced it to pay a large indemnity. The episode showed the growing pressure of Moscow, though Novgorod still balanced between Moscow and Lithuania and kept full control of its institutions and hinterland.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1433": "Under Archbishop Evfimy II the Palace of Facets was built and much building and icon painting was commissioned. Novgorod was at the height of its wealth and self-confidence, its territory and trade intact, before Moscow’s civil war ended and its power turned on the city.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1456": "Vasily II defeated Novgorod’s army, and the Treaty of Yazhelbitsy restricted the city’s right to conduct foreign policy and to issue laws without the grand prince. The republic survived, but its independence had been sharply curtailed and its leaders were now bound to Moscow.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1471": "After a boyar party sought alliance with Casimir IV of Lithuania, Ivan III’s army crushed Novgorod at the Shelon River. The Treaty of Korostyn forbade ties with Lithuania, and the republic, its army broken, now existed only by Moscow’s tolerance.",
  "Novgorod Republic|1478": "Ivan III besieged Novgorod and received its surrender in January. The veche was abolished, its bell taken to Moscow and leading families later deported. Novgorod’s independence ended after nearly three and a half centuries, and its vast lands became part of the Muscovite state.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1139": "Afonso Henriques, ruler of the County of Portugal since 1128, began to style himself king after his victory at Ourique. His realm was a small frontier lordship between the Minho and the Mondego, still under the shadow of León and of the Almoravid powers to the south.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1147": "With the help of northern European crusaders sailing to the Second Crusade, Afonso I captured Lisbon after a long siege, and Santarém fell the same year. León had recognised his title at Zamora in 1143, and the kingdom now held the Tagus, its best harbour and future capital.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1249": "Afonso III took Faro and completed the conquest of the Algarve, ending the Portuguese Reconquista a century before Castile’s. The kingdom had reached its European limits, and the king soon summoned town representatives to the Cortes of Leiria in 1254, strengthening royal authority after years of civil strife.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1297": "By the Treaty of Alcañices King Denis fixed the border with Castile, which has hardly changed since. His long reign brought agricultural growth, a university, the Order of Christ and Portuguese as the written language of government, giving the small kingdom a settled and prosperous footing.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1385": "After the crisis that followed the death of Ferdinand I, the Cortes of Coimbra chose John of Aviz as king, and his army defeated Castile at Aljubarrota. Independence was secured and the English alliance sealed at Windsor in 1386, but the kingdom was exhausted by war and plague.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1415": "John I’s capture of Ceuta in Morocco began Portuguese expansion overseas. Over the following decades Prince Henry sponsored voyages that settled Madeira and the Azores and advanced down the West African coast, bringing gold, sugar and enslaved people and raising the kingdom’s wealth and ambitions.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1488": "Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope, proving that a sea route to the Indian Ocean existed. John II had made African exploration a systematic royal project, with the fortress of Elmina founded in 1482, and the crown now held a commanding lead over its rivals.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1498": "Vasco da Gama reached Calicut on the Malabar Coast, opening a direct sea route from Europe to India. With the Treaty of Tordesillas confirming its half of the newly found world, Portugal under Manuel I was poised to redirect the spice trade through Lisbon.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1515": "Afonso de Albuquerque had taken Goa, Malacca and finally Hormuz, giving Portugal control of key points of the Indian Ocean trade. Spices filled the royal Casa da Índia, Brazil had been claimed, and Manuel I was among the richest monarchs in Europe. This was the kingdom’s peak.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1557": "At the death of John III the empire stretched from Brazil to Macau, but costs were rising. The crown abandoned several Moroccan forts, the Inquisition had been established, and royal debts in Antwerp were heavy, so the kingdom remained powerful but past the height of its strength.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1578": "King Sebastian led an ill-prepared crusade into Morocco and was killed at the Battle of Alcácer Quibir, together with much of the nobility. Thousands were captured and ransomed at great cost. The aged Cardinal Henry succeeded, leaving the kingdom without an heir and badly weakened.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1580": "After Cardinal Henry’s death, Philip II of Spain invaded, defeated the pretender António, Prior of Crato, at Alcântara and was accepted as king at the Cortes of Tomar in 1581. Portugal kept its laws and offices but lost its independent foreign policy within the Iberian Union.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1640": "A conspiracy of nobles in Lisbon on 1 December proclaimed the Duke of Braganza as John IV, ending the union with Spain. The restored kingdom faced Spanish armies at home and the Dutch overseas, where Hormuz had been lost in 1622 and Malacca would fall in 1641.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1668": "Victories at Ameixial and Montes Claros, and English support sealed by the marriage of Catherine of Braganza to Charles II, forced Spain to recognise Portuguese independence by the Treaty of Lisbon. Brazil had been recovered from the Dutch in 1654, and the kingdom again stood secure.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1720": "Gold from Minas Gerais in Brazil made John V one of the wealthiest monarchs in Europe. He built the vast palace-convent of Mafra and the Águas Livres aqueduct and ruled without summoning the Cortes, though the gold flowed largely to England in payment for imports.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1755": "The earthquake, tsunami and fires of 1 November destroyed much of Lisbon and killed tens of thousands. Under the Marquis of Pombal the city was rebuilt on a new plan, and the state was centralised, but the gold supply from Brazil was already starting to decline.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1807": "As Junot’s French army invaded, the royal family and court sailed to Rio de Janeiro under British protection. Portugal became a battlefield of the Peninsular War and a virtual British protectorate, while the centre of the monarchy moved across the Atlantic to its largest colony.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1822": "The Liberal Revolution of 1820 had brought John VI back from Brazil and a constitution was adopted, but on 7 September 1822 his son Pedro declared Brazil independent. The kingdom lost its richest possession and much of its revenue, and politics was split between liberals and absolutists.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1834": "The Liberal Wars between the absolutist Miguel and the liberal supporters of Pedro IV and his daughter Maria II ended at the Convention of Évora-Monte. Constitutional monarchy was restored, church and monastic property was sold off, but the kingdom was poor and deeply in debt.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1890": "Britain’s ultimatum forced Portugal to abandon its claim to the lands between Angola and Mozambique. The humiliation, a financial crisis and the failure of rotating governments strengthened republicans, who staged an unsuccessful uprising in Porto in January 1891. The monarchy’s prestige was badly damaged.",
  "Kingdom of Portugal|1910": "Two years after the assassination of Carlos I and his heir, a revolution in Lisbon on 5 October deposed the young Manuel II, who left for exile in England. The First Portuguese Republic was proclaimed, ending a monarchy that had lasted more than 770 years.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1157": "On the death of Yuri Dolgoruky in Kiev, the towns of the north-east accepted his son Andrei Bogolyubsky as prince. Andrei made Vladimir his capital, inheriting a populous, grain-producing land that was already one of the strongest principalities of Rus’ but still formally junior to Kiev.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1164": "Andrei campaigned successfully against Volga Bulgaria, taking the town of Bryakhimov, and Vladimir’s Golden Gate and Dormition Cathedral now stood as symbols of a new capital. The principality was expanding its trade and prestige along the Volga and challenging the older centres of Rus’.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1169": "A coalition army sent by Andrei Bogolyubsky stormed and plundered Kiev, and he gave the city to his brother Gleb while remaining in Vladimir. The episode showed that the senior power in Rus’ now lay in the north-east, though an attack on Novgorod the next year failed.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1174": "Andrei was murdered by members of his own household at Bogolyubovo. His autocratic style had alienated the boyars, and his death opened a two-year succession war between the boyars of Rostov and Suzdal and the townsmen of Vladimir, temporarily paralysing the principality.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1176": "Andrei’s half-brother Vsevolod, backed by the people of Vladimir, won the succession struggle and became prince. In 1177 he defeated Gleb of Ryazan on the Koloksha, restoring the authority of the Vladimir throne over the neighbouring principalities and over its own boyars.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1183": "Vsevolod led a large expedition of Rus’ princes against Volga Bulgaria, besieging its capital. The campaign underlined his leadership of the north-eastern princes, and the author of the Tale of Igor’s Campaign later praised a ruler whose warriors could splash the Volga dry with their oars.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1200": "Vsevolod the Big Nest, now regularly styled grand prince, placed his sons in Novgorod and Pereslavl and was courted by the princes of Kiev and Chernigov. Vladimir was adorned with the Cathedral of St Demetrius, and the principality stood among the leading powers of the Rus’ lands.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1208": "After crushing the princes of Ryazan and burning their capital, Vsevolod dominated north-eastern Rus’ and influenced Novgorod and the south. This was the principality’s peak: a united territory under one strong ruler, with a capital whose churches matched the ambition of its grand prince.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1212": "Vsevolod died after a reign of thirty-six years, leaving many sons and a disputed succession, since he had passed over his eldest son Konstantin in favour of Yuri. The land was divided into appanages for his sons, beginning the fragmentation that marked the rest of its history.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1216": "At the Battle of Lipitsa, Mstislav the Bold of Novgorod and Konstantin defeated Yuri and Yaroslav, inflicting heavy losses on the Suzdalian forces. Konstantin became grand prince, but the war had exposed the division of the dynasty and weakened the principality’s armies and prestige.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1221": "Yuri II, grand prince again since Konstantin’s death in 1218, founded Nizhny Novgorod at the confluence of the Oka and the Volga, pushing the frontier east against the Mordvins and Volga Bulgars. The principality had partly recovered and remained the strongest power in north-eastern Rus’.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1238": "Batu’s Mongol army stormed Vladimir in February, killing the grand prince’s family, sacked Suzdal, Rostov and many other towns, and destroyed Yuri II’s army on the Sit River on 4 March. The principality’s military power and much of its urban population were wiped out.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1243": "Yaroslav II, Yuri’s brother, travelled to Batu and received the patent for the grand principality, accepting Mongol overlordship. Vladimir survived as the senior title of north-eastern Rus’, but its holder now ruled at the khan’s pleasure, and Yaroslav died in 1246 after a journey to Karakorum.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1252": "After the Mongol general Nevryuy drove out Grand Prince Andrei Yaroslavich, his brother Alexander Nevsky received the title. Famous for victories over the Swedes and the Teutonic Knights, Alexander chose cooperation with the Horde, which brought a measure of stability to a much weakened land.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1257": "Mongol officials carried out a census of north-eastern Rus’ to assess tribute and recruits, and Alexander Nevsky enforced it, including against resistance in Novgorod in 1259. Regular tribute to the Golden Horde drained the principality’s wealth and confirmed its subordinate position.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1263": "Alexander Nevsky died on his way back from the Horde. The title then passed among his brothers, Yaroslav of Tver and Vasily of Kostroma, who governed mainly from their own appanages, so that Vladimir became a seniority title rather than the seat of a working state.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1293": "The rivalry between Alexander’s sons Dmitry of Pereslavl and Andrei of Gorodets, each backed by a different Mongol power, led to Dudenev’s raid, in which a Horde army sacked Vladimir, Moscow, Suzdal and other towns. The grand principality reached its lowest point.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1305": "Mikhail of Tver received the patent from Khan Tokhta and became grand prince, but Yuri of Moscow contested the title. The grand principality had become the prize in a struggle between the appanage principalities of Tver and Moscow, decided by the khans of the Golden Horde.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1318": "Mikhail of Tver was executed at the Horde after Yuri of Moscow, now married to Khan Uzbek’s sister, accused him. Yuri held the title until 1322, and Moscow and Tver continued to fight over it, while the metropolitan’s growing ties with Moscow changed the balance.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1327": "A rising in Tver killed the khan’s envoy Chol Khan and his escort. Ivan Kalita of Moscow joined the punitive expedition of 1328 that devastated Tver, and Khan Uzbek divided the grand principality between Ivan and Alexander of Suzdal, ending Tver’s hold on the title.",
  "Vladimir-Suzdal|1331": "With the death of Alexander of Suzdal, Ivan I Kalita held the title of grand prince of Vladimir alone. The grand principality’s lands and dignity merged in practice with Moscow, whose princes kept the title and later treated it as their hereditary possession.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1157": "Albert the Bear recaptured Brandenburg an der Havel from Jaxa of Köpenick and took the title of margrave. His new march was a small foothold among Slavic lands east of the Elbe, held by an energetic prince with lands in Saxony but with few settlers and little revenue.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1230": "Under Albert’s grandsons John I and Otto III, ruling jointly, the Ascanians expanded into the Teltow and Barnim, founding towns and monasteries and attracting German settlers. Berlin and Cölln appeared on the Spree, and the march was becoming a substantial principality of the eastern Empire.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1290": "The Ascanian margraves had extended their rule beyond the Oder into the Neumark and towards the Baltic, and Brandenburg was among the stronger principalities of northern Germany. Margrave Waldemar, who ruled from 1308, defended these gains against a coalition of northern neighbours.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1320": "The Ascanian line died out with the young Henry II, a year after Waldemar’s death. Neighbouring princes seized border districts, and the leaderless Mark lost much of its strength until Emperor Louis IV granted it to his son Louis of Wittelsbach in 1323.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1356": "The Golden Bull of Charles IV confirmed the margrave of Brandenburg as one of the seven electors of the Empire. The title gave Brandenburg lasting rank, although Wittelsbach rule had been shaken by the impostor known as the False Waldemar, and the Mark remained poor and disorderly.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1373": "By the Treaty of Fürstenwalde Emperor Charles IV acquired Brandenburg for the House of Luxembourg. His death in 1378 left it to absentee rulers who pawned it, notably to Jobst of Moravia, while robber barons in the countryside defied any central authority.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1415": "Emperor Sigismund granted the Mark and the electoral dignity to Frederick VI of Nuremberg, who became Elector Frederick I, founding five centuries of Hohenzollern rule. He had already broken the castles of the Quitzows, but his electorate was small, poor and still unruly.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1448": "Frederick II Irontooth suppressed the revolt of Berlin-Cölln, ending the towns’ independence, and built a castle on the Spree. He also bought back the Neumark from the Teutonic Order in 1455, restoring the Mark’s eastern territory and strengthening the elector’s authority over towns and nobles.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1473": "Albert Achilles issued the Dispositio Achillea, ensuring that the Mark would pass undivided to the eldest son while the Franconian lands went to younger sons. Under his son John Cicero the electors settled permanently in Berlin, giving the state a fixed centre.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1539": "Joachim II introduced the Lutheran Reformation, taking church lands and bishoprics under electoral control. The electorate was settled and modestly prosperous, but its rulers depended on the estates for taxes and on the Habsburg emperors for protection, and its army counted for little.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1618": "John Sigismund inherited the Duchy of Prussia on the death of his father-in-law Albert Frederick, after the Treaty of Xanten had brought Cleves, Mark and Ravensberg in 1614. The Hohenzollern lands now stretched from the Rhine to the Baltic, though widely scattered and hard to defend.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1637": "During the Thirty Years’ War Swedish and imperial armies fought over the Mark, and towns and villages were plundered, burned or depopulated by war, famine and plague. The elector George William, unable to protect his lands, would withdraw to Königsberg in 1638, and Brandenburg reached its lowest point.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1648": "At the Peace of Westphalia the young Great Elector gained Farther Pomerania, the bishoprics of Halberstadt and Minden and the reversion of Magdeburg. He began rebuilding the shattered Mark, and in 1653 bargained with the estates for taxes to pay a permanent army.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1675": "Frederick William defeated an invading Swedish army at the Battle of Fehrbellin, Brandenburg’s first great victory on its own account, and went on to conquer Swedish Pomerania, though he had to return it in 1679. His sovereignty over Prussia had been won in 1657–1660.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1688": "When the Great Elector died, he left a standing army of some 30,000 men, a central administration and a state enriched by Huguenot immigrants welcomed under the Edict of Potsdam. Brandenburg-Prussia had become a leading Protestant power of northern Germany and a sought-after ally: the height of the electorate’s own power and independence.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1701": "Elector Frederick III crowned himself King Frederick I in Prussia at Königsberg. The royal title rested on Prussia, outside the Empire, and Brandenburg, though still an electorate and the dynasty’s heartland with Berlin as royal capital, now became one province of a larger monarchy, its own government increasingly merged into that of the kingdom.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1740": "Frederick William I had doubled the army to about 80,000 and centralised government in the General Directory of 1723, which ran Brandenburg like the other provinces. When Frederick II invaded Silesia, the Mark was the kingdom’s central province and a main recruiting ground, but policy was Prussian, and the electorate’s separate institutions counted for less and less.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1763": "The Seven Years’ War brought invasion to the Mark itself: Austrian raiders held Berlin to ransom in 1757 and Russian and Austrian troops occupied it in 1760. The Peace of Hubertusburg confirmed Prussia’s hold on Silesia, but Brandenburg emerged plundered and depleted, a war-damaged province whose weight within the enlarged monarchy had fallen.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1786": "At Frederick the Great’s death the Mark had been rebuilt: the Oderbruch marshes were drained and settled with colonists, and Berlin had grown to some 150,000 inhabitants. Yet with Silesia and West Prussia gained, Brandenburg was one province among several in a kingdom of some five million, its electoral vote its chief separate role.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1795": "Prussia had gained large Polish lands in the Second and Third Partitions, shifting the monarchy’s centre of gravity further east, and by the Peace of Basel it withdrew from the war against revolutionary France. The imperial structures in which Brandenburg’s electoral rank mattered were weakening, and the Mark was now a minor share of the kingdom.",
  "Margraviate of Brandenburg|1806": "The Holy Roman Empire was dissolved in August, ending the electorate, and in October Napoleon crushed the Prussian army at Jena and Auerstedt and entered Berlin. Brandenburg lost its formal existence as a state and became, from 1815, a province of Prussia.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1162": "Alfonso II inherited the County of Barcelona and, through his mother Petronila, the Kingdom of Aragon. The Catalan counties, already enlarged by the conquest of Tortosa and Lleida, became one of the two founding parts of the Crown of Aragon, with Barcelona as its leading port.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1213": "Peter II was killed at the Battle of Muret fighting Simon de Montfort’s crusaders in Languedoc, ending hopes of a great Catalan-Occitan realm north of the Pyrenees. His son James I was a child, and the Principality endured years of noble disorder under a regency.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1229": "James I, with heavy Catalan support, conquered Majorca, opening the Mediterranean to Barcelona’s merchants. The conquest of Valencia followed in 1238, and Catalan settlers spread their language and customs into the new kingdoms, increasing the Principality’s wealth and commercial reach.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1258": "By the Treaty of Corbeil, Louis IX of France renounced his claims over the Catalan counties, while James I gave up most claims in Occitania. The Principality’s independence from France was formally recognised, and Barcelona’s new city government was taking shape.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1285": "After Peter III took Sicily in 1282, Philip III of France invaded Catalonia in the Aragonese Crusade. Roger of Lauria’s fleet destroyed the French ships, forcing a disastrous retreat. Peter had promised annual Corts in 1283, and the Principality emerged stronger.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1311": "The Catalan Company, veterans of the Sicilian wars, destroyed the army of the Duke of Athens at the Battle of Halmyros and took over his duchy. Catalan merchants traded across the Mediterranean, and the Principality’s ships and money sustained the Crown’s expansion towards Sardinia.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1340": "On the eve of the Black Death, Catalonia was at its demographic and commercial peak. Barcelona’s trade reached from Flanders to Alexandria, Sardinia had been invaded from 1323, and Peter IV was reasserting royal power over Majorca, which he reannexed in 1343–1344.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1348": "The Black Death killed a large part of the population, followed by further epidemics in the 1360s and 1370s. Labour shortages sharpened conflict between lords and remença peasants, and the costly wars of Peter IV in Sardinia and against Castile burdened the Principality.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1359": "At the Corts of Cervera, the Diputació del General, later called the Generalitat, was established as a permanent body to collect taxes voted by the Corts. The Principality’s institutions grew stronger even as its economy strained under war with Castile.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1412": "When Martin the Humane died without a legitimate heir, the Compromise of Caspe chose Ferdinand of Antequera of the Castilian Trastámara dynasty. Catalonia kept its institutions, but the bank failures and commercial troubles of the following decades exposed a weakening economy.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1462": "Conflict between John II and the Generalitat, the death of his son Charles of Viana and the remença revolt led to civil war. The Principality rejected John and offered its crown in turn to Henry IV of Castile, Peter of Portugal and René of Anjou.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1472": "Barcelona surrendered to John II after a long siege, and the Capitulation of Pedralbes ended the civil war on relatively lenient terms. Ten years of fighting had devastated the economy, emptied the treasury and left Catalonia weaker within a Crown soon to be joined with Castile.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1486": "By the Sentence of Guadalupe, Ferdinand II allowed remença peasants to redeem the evil customs, ending a century of rural conflict. Catalonia slowly recovered, but its institutions now faced a king whose interests increasingly lay in Castile and a growing Atlantic world.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1599": "Philip III held Corts in Barcelona, confirming the Principality’s laws. Catalonia enjoyed relative peace within the Spanish Monarchy, though banditry was widespread in the countryside and Barcelona’s trade had fallen far behind Genoa and Seville in the sixteenth century. The Principality’s weight within the monarchy was modest.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1626": "The Count-Duke of Olivares asked the Catalan Corts to contribute men and money under his Union of Arms. The Corts refused, and relations between the Principality and the royal government deteriorated, setting the stage for the crisis that followed in 1640.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1640": "The billeting of royal troops for the war with France sparked a rural revolt, and on 7 June the Corpus de Sang saw the killing of the viceroy in Barcelona. The Generalitat broke with Philip IV and turned to France for protection.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1652": "After a decade of war and French occupation, plague and famine, Barcelona surrendered to the forces of John of Austria the Younger. Philip IV pardoned the rebels and confirmed the Principality’s constitutions, but Catalonia was exhausted and divided, its population reduced by the plague of the early 1650s and its trade disrupted.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1659": "By the Treaty of the Pyrenees, Spain ceded Roussillon and the northern part of Cerdanya to France. The Principality lost its lands north of the Pyrenees, including the city of Perpignan, and in 1660 France suppressed the Catalan institutions there, leaving a reduced and weakened Catalonia.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1705": "Catalonia sided with the Habsburg claimant, Archduke Charles, who entered Barcelona and held Corts there in 1705–1706, confirming Catalan rights. The Principality’s economy had recovered since the 1680s, and it now staked its institutions and laws on an Allied victory over the Bourbon king Philip V.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1714": "Abandoned by Britain after the Treaty of Utrecht, Barcelona withstood a siege of more than a year before falling to the Duke of Berwick on 11 September. Philip V’s forces occupied the whole Principality, Cardona and the last strongholds surrendered, and its institutions were suppressed at once.",
  "Principality of Catalonia|1716": "The Nueva Planta decree of 16 January abolished the Corts, the Generalitat and the Consell de Cent and placed Catalonia under a captain-general and a royal court. The Principality ceased to exist as a self-governing state within Bourbon Spain, and its universities were soon concentrated at Cervera.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1162": "Alfonso II succeeded to the County of Barcelona and two years later to the Kingdom of Aragon, uniting an inland kingdom with the Catalan ports. The new composite monarchy was modest in size, but Roussillon, Provence and lordships in Languedoc soon added influence north of the Pyrenees.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1213": "Peter II, crowned by the pope in 1204 and a victor at Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212, was killed at Muret defending his Occitan vassals against the Albigensian Crusade. The Crown lost its position north of the Pyrenees, and his son James I succeeded as a child.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1229": "James I began the conquest of Majorca with a fleet from Catalan ports, taking Madina Mayurqa by the end of the year. The Crown gained a strategic base in the western Mediterranean and turned decisively from Occitania towards the sea and the Muslim south.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1238": "The city of Valencia surrendered to James I, and over the next decade the conquest of the kingdom was completed. The Crown doubled its territory, gained rich irrigated lands and a large Muslim subject population, and created the Kingdom of Valencia with its own laws.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1276": "At James I’s death the realms were divided: Peter III received Aragon, Catalonia and Valencia, while his brother James II took Majorca, Roussillon and Montpellier as a separate kingdom. The partition weakened the Crown, though Peter remained the senior and stronger ruler.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1282": "After the Sicilian Vespers, Peter III, husband of the Hohenstaufen heiress Constance, landed in Sicily and was accepted as king. Roger of Lauria’s fleet then defeated the Angevins, and the Crown became a leading naval power, though Peter was excommunicated by the pope.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1323": "James II, who had secured Alicante and Elche from Castile by the Treaty of Torrellas in 1304 and declared the realms inseparable in 1319, sent his son Alfonso to invade Sardinia. The Crown was now among the strongest powers of the western Mediterranean.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1343": "Peter IV the Ceremonious invaded the Kingdom of Majorca and reannexed it in 1343–1344, reuniting the realms of James I. The Crown’s commerce, led by Barcelona and Majorca, reached its height just before the Black Death struck in 1348 and cut the population of the eastern realms.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1356": "The War of the Two Peters with Castile began, bringing repeated invasions of Aragon and Valencia until 1369. Sardinian rebellions under the judges of Arborea and the cost of war strained the realms, though Peter IV had already broken the Aragonese Unión at Épila.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1388": "Nerio Acciaioli took the Acropolis of Athens, ending the Aragonese rule in Greece that Peter IV had accepted in 1379, while Sardinia remained largely in rebel hands. Under John I the court was costly and the economy, especially in Catalonia, was suffering from plague, bankruptcies and the pogroms of 1391.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1412": "After Martin the Humane died without a legitimate heir, the Compromise of Caspe chose Ferdinand I of the Castilian Trastámara dynasty. The Crown remained united and held Sicily, reunited with it in 1409, and most of Sardinia, but Castilian influence grew.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1442": "Alfonso V the Magnanimous entered Naples after a decade of war, and ruled Aragon, Catalonia, Valencia, Majorca, Sicily, Sardinia and Naples from his Italian court, a patron of humanist scholars. This was the greatest territorial extent and prestige of the Crown in the Mediterranean.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1458": "On Alfonso V’s death Naples passed to his illegitimate son Ferrante, while John II inherited the Crown with Sicily and Sardinia. The Crown lost its Italian centre of gravity and its richest conquest, and tensions between the new king and the Catalan institutions were rising towards civil war.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1472": "Barcelona surrendered to John II, ending ten years of civil war in Catalonia. The fighting had wrecked the Catalan economy, and France held Roussillon and Cerdanya, so the Crown was weaker, though John had married his son Ferdinand to Isabella of Castile in 1469.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1504": "Ferdinand II, ruling jointly with Isabella since 1479, had recovered Roussillon in 1493 and now secured Naples after Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba’s victories. The Crown again held Sicily, Sardinia and Naples, though within a dynastic union increasingly centred on Castile.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1516": "On Ferdinand’s death his grandson Charles of Habsburg inherited both crowns. The Crown of Aragon kept its laws and Cortes but became a secondary part of a vast monarchy governed from Castile and the Netherlands, and its realms rarely saw their king.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1591": "When Aragon sheltered Philip II’s fugitive secretary Antonio Pérez, Philip sent an army into Zaragoza and executed the Justicia, Juan de Lanuza. The Aragonese fueros survived with modifications, but the episode showed the limits of the realms’ liberties against the crown.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1609": "Philip III ordered the expulsion of the Moriscos, carried out in 1609–1614. Valencia lost perhaps a third of its people and Aragon much of its rural labour, causing lasting economic damage to the eastern realms and reducing their weight within the Spanish Monarchy.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1640": "The Catalan revolt broke out against Olivares’s policies and led to French intervention and twelve years of war. The Crown of Aragon’s most populous and commercial realm was lost to the monarchy until 1652, and Roussillon was ceded to France for good in 1659.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1707": "After the Bourbon victory at Almansa, Philip V abolished the laws and institutions of Aragon and Valencia by the Nueva Planta decrees and dissolved the Council of Aragon. The Crown of Aragon effectively ceased to function as a separate constitutional entity.",
  "Crown of Aragon|1716": "With Sicily and Sardinia lost at Utrecht and Naples at Rastatt, Majorca’s institutions abolished in 1715 and Catalonia’s by the Nueva Planta decree of 1716, the last realms of the Crown of Aragon were absorbed into the centralised Bourbon state.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1182": "Emperor Frederick Barbarossa made Conrad III Otto of Znojmo margrave of Moravia, briefly a fief held directly from the emperor. The new margraviate was still a patchwork of Přemyslid appanages around Olomouc, Brno and Znojmo, with little common administration and its status contested by the dukes of Bohemia.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1197": "Vladislaus Henry, brother of Ottokar I, became margrave under the Bohemian crown and ruled until 1222. He absorbed the old appanage principalities into one land, and the Golden Bull of Sicily of 1212 fixed Moravia\u0027s place within a Bohemian kingdom whose royal title was now hereditary.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1250": "German-speaking colonists, encouraged by Bishop Bruno of Schauenburg of Olomouc and by the crown, were clearing forest and founding villages and chartered towns. Silver from Jihlava and new royal towns such as Brno raised revenues, and Ottokar II used Moravia as a base for wider ambitions.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1278": "Ottokar II was killed fighting Rudolf of Habsburg at the Battle on the Marchfeld just beyond Moravia\u0027s border, and Rudolf\u0027s troops briefly held parts of the land. The defeat ended Bohemian expansion into Austria, though Moravia\u0027s towns and estates continued to grow in wealth and organisation.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1306": "The murder of Wenceslaus III at Olomouc ended the male Přemyslid line, opening years of disputed succession in which Habsburg and other candidates contested the Bohemian crown. Moravian nobles used the turmoil to seize royal estates, weakening the margrave\u0027s authority until the Luxembourgs took control.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1334": "Charles of Luxembourg, the future Emperor Charles IV, was given the title of margrave and began recovering pledged royal castles and estates. Order returned to the land, and in 1348 Charles defined Moravia as a fief of the Crown of Bohemia with its own identity.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1349": "Charles IV granted Moravia to his brother John Henry, founding a Moravian branch of the Luxembourg dynasty. For the first time in generations the margraviate had a resident ruling house, a court at Brno and a ruler whose interests centred on the land itself.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1410": "Margrave Jobst, who had also acquired Brandenburg and fought his brother Prokop and cousin Wenceslaus IV for decades, was elected King of the Romans. A Moravian ruler stood briefly at the head of the Empire, the high point of the margraviate\u0027s political weight, before Jobst died in 1411.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1423": "Amid the Hussite Wars, King Sigismund granted Moravia to his son-in-law Albert of Austria. Most royal towns and many lords stayed Catholic and loyal, but Hussite armies ravaged the land, and the margraviate became a contested frontier between the Hussites and their enemies.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1469": "Matthias Corvinus of Hungary, at war with George of Poděbrady, was proclaimed king of Bohemia by Catholic lords at Olomouc and occupied Moravia. The land was separated from Bohemia proper and governed from Hungary, a sign of how far its fate depended on stronger neighbours.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1490": "On Matthias Corvinus\u0027s death Moravia returned to the Bohemian crown under Vladislaus II Jagiellon. With weak kings in Prague and Buda, the Moravian estates gained in power, governing through their diet, land court and governor with growing confidence and a strong sense of separate identity.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1526": "After Louis II died at Mohács, Ferdinand I of Habsburg became king of Bohemia and margrave of Moravia. The estates kept wide autonomy and religious freedom in practice, and towns and noble estates prospered from grain, wine, fish ponds and cloth in a long period of stability.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1579": "The Unity of the Brethren began printing the Kralice Bible on a Moravian noble estate, while Utraquists, Catholics and Hutterite communities lived side by side. Moravia\u0027s tolerance, prosperity and estate liberties made it one of the most self-confident lands of the Habsburg Monarchy.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1608": "Led by Karel the Elder of Žerotín, the Moravian estates sided with Archduke Matthias against Emperor Rudolf II and had their liberties confirmed. Moravia acted for a time as an almost independent political community, the summit of estate power in its history.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1620": "Moravia had joined the Bohemian Revolt hesitantly, and after the Battle of White Mountain its Protestant estates faced Habsburg reprisals. Confiscations of noble property, expulsion of Protestant clergy and the flight of Brethren such as Comenius broke the political class that had governed the land.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1628": "The Renewed Land Ordinance made the crown hereditary in the Habsburg line, subordinated the diet and imposed Catholicism as the only permitted faith. Moravia kept its name and institutions, but real authority now lay with the court in Vienna and a reshaped Catholic nobility.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1749": "Maria Theresa\u0027s reforms centralised taxation and administration, removing much of what remained of Moravian self-government. The land recovered from the wars of the previous century and later absorbed Austrian Silesia into its administration, but it had become a province of a centralising monarchy.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1805": "Napoleon defeated the Russian and Austrian armies at the Battle of Austerlitz near Brno, the most famous battle fought on Moravian soil. The land, now a crown land of the new Austrian Empire, had no political voice of its own in the war fought across it.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1848": "Serfdom was abolished and the Austrian Reichstag sat at Kroměříž, drafting a federal constitution. Brno\u0027s textile mills and Ostrava\u0027s coal and iron had made Moravia one of the most industrial regions of the monarchy, and Czech and German national movements began to organise.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1905": "The Moravian Compromise divided the diet into Czech and German electorates and eased national conflict. Revived after 1861, the diet gave the crown land real autonomy in education and local affairs, a modest recovery of political life within Austria-Hungary on the eve of its collapse.",
  "Margraviate of Moravia|1918": "With the collapse of Austria-Hungary, Moravia became a land of the new Czechoslovakia proclaimed on 28 October 1918. German-speaking districts that tried to join German Austria were occupied, and the margraviate as a constitutional unit came to an end after 736 years.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1183": "Henry I began to share power with his father Godfrey III, count of Louvain and titular duke of Lower Lorraine. The family\u0027s real base was a modest cluster of lands around Leuven and Brussels, and its ducal title still meant little beyond them, so the future duchy started small.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1190": "At the Diet of Schwäbisch Hall Emperor Henry VI confirmed Henry I\u0027s ducal rank over his own territories, creating the Duchy of Brabant as a principality of the Empire. Henry now ruled alone and began founding towns and castles to bind the countryside to the dynasty.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1213": "Henry I\u0027s attempt to expand towards the prince-bishopric of Liège ended in a heavy defeat at the Battle of Steppes. The setback checked Brabant\u0027s eastward ambitions for a time, although its towns continued to grow and the duke\u0027s position at home remained secure.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1235": "On Henry I\u0027s death Brabant was a consolidated duchy stretching from Brussels and Leuven to Antwerp and \u0027s-Hertogenbosch, with growing cloth towns and a ruling house respected in imperial politics. It had become one of the leading principalities of the Low Countries, ready for further expansion.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1288": "John I defeated the archbishop of Cologne and the count of Guelders at the Battle of Worringen and won the Duchy of Limburg. Brabant now controlled the overland route from the Rhine to the sea and stood at the height of its medieval military power.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1312": "Burdened by debts, the dying John II granted the Charter of Kortenberg, setting up a council of knights and townsmen to supervise government. Brabant remained wealthy and large, but ducal power was now shared with towns that held the purse strings and claimed a right of resistance.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1356": "Duchess Joanna and Wenceslaus swore the Joyous Entry, then lost Brussels to Louis of Male of Flanders after the Battle of Scheut until Everard t\u0027Serclaes retook it. The Peace of Ath of 1357 cost Brabant Antwerp for decades and showed the duchy\u0027s military weakness.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1371": "Duke Wenceslaus was defeated and captured by the duke of Jülich at the Battle of Baesweiler and had to be ransomed. The defeat strained ducal finances and increased the dependence of the rulers on the estates and the towns, though the duchy\u0027s territory stayed intact.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1406": "On Joanna\u0027s death Antoine of Burgundy, a younger son of Philip the Bold, became duke and Antwerp, held by the counts of Flanders since 1357, returned to the duchy. Burgundian backing restored stability, though Brabant was increasingly drawn into the orbit of its powerful Burgundian neighbours.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1430": "Philip the Good inherited Brabant, joining it to the Burgundian State. Brussels became a favoured residence of the Burgundian court, the Council of Brabant was organised and Leuven\u0027s new university flourished, making the duchy a political centre of the Low Countries.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1477": "The death of Charles the Bold at Nancy plunged the Burgundian Netherlands into crisis. Mary of Burgundy had to concede the Great Privilege to the States General, French armies attacked, and the towns of Brabant rebelled repeatedly against Maximilian of Austria in the following years.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1531": "Antwerp opened its new exchange, the symbol of a city that had become the commercial and financial hub of northern Europe. English cloth, Portuguese spices and South German silver and credit all passed through Brabant, whose towns were among the richest in Europe.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1555": "Charles V abdicated in Brussels, the capital of the Habsburg Netherlands, in favour of Philip II. Antwerp\u0027s population approached 100,000, Brabant\u0027s printing, art and finance led northern Europe, and the duchy stood at the peak of its wealth and political importance.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1576": "Unpaid Spanish soldiers sacked Antwerp in the Spanish Fury, killing thousands of its inhabitants, and Brabant joined the general revolt of the provinces against Philip II. Warfare, iconoclasm and religious division tore the duchy apart, and much of its trade and population began to leave.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1585": "Alexander Farnese took Antwerp after a siege of more than a year, restoring Spanish rule over southern Brabant and its capital, Brussels. Protestants were given time to leave, many merchants moved to Amsterdam, and the Dutch blockade of the Scheldt began the city\u0027s long commercial decline.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1648": "The Peace of Münster confirmed the partition of Brabant: the north became States Brabant under the Dutch States General, and the Scheldt stayed closed. The southern duchy, a battleground of the wars of Louis XIV, kept its estates but lost much of its earlier prosperity.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1706": "The Duke of Marlborough\u0027s victory at the Battle of Ramillies drove French forces out of Brabant during the War of the Spanish Succession. The duchy was governed by an Anglo-Dutch condominium, a sign that its fate was now decided by the great powers fighting on its soil.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1714": "By the peace settlements of 1713–1714 the Spanish Netherlands, including southern Brabant, passed to Austria. Brussels remained the seat of the governors-general, and the estates and the Joyous Entry survived, but the duchy was a distant province of the Habsburg Monarchy.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1789": "When Joseph II tried to override the Joyous Entry, the Brabant Revolution expelled the Austrians and led to the United Belgian States of 1790. The brief revolt showed the continued strength of Brabant\u0027s estates and traditions, but Austrian troops returned within a year.",
  "Duchy of Brabant|1795": "After French victories at Jemappes and Fleurus, the French Republic annexed the southern Netherlands on 1 October 1795. Brabant was divided into the departments of the Dyle and the Two Nèthes, and the duchy, its estates and the Joyous Entry ceased to exist.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1185": "Peter and Asen raised a revolt against Isaac II Angelos\u0027s taxes at Tarnovo, proclaiming the restoration of Bulgarian independence. The rebels held only the hill country of northern Bulgaria and depended on Cuman allies, but they had a cause, a capital and a growing following among Bulgarians and Vlachs.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1190": "The brothers ambushed and routed Isaac II\u0027s army in the Tryavna pass, ending Byzantine attempts to crush the revolt by force. The new state controlled the lands between the Danube and the Balkan range and began raiding Thrace, though it still lacked recognition and secure frontiers.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1197": "After the murders of Asen and Peter, their younger brother Kaloyan became ruler. Energetic and ruthless, he took Varna in 1201 and opened negotiations with Pope Innocent III, seeking a crown and recognition that Byzantium refused, while expanding at the expense of a weakening empire.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1205": "Crowned by a papal legate in 1204, Kaloyan destroyed the Latin crusader army at the Battle of Adrianople and captured Emperor Baldwin I. Bulgaria emerged as the strongest power in the Balkans, able to challenge the new Latin Empire for control of Thrace.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1207": "Kaloyan died during the siege of Thessalonica, possibly murdered, and his nephew Boril seized the throne. The new ruler faced rival claimants, revolts by provincial lords and defeats by the Latins, so Bulgaria lost some of the momentum of Kaloyan\u0027s reign.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1218": "Ivan Asen II, son of Asen, returned from exile, deposed Boril and took the throne. He secured his position through marriage to a Hungarian princess and cautious diplomacy, building the strength that allowed him to exploit the rivalries among the Latin, Epirote and Nicaean successor states.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1230": "At the Battle of Klokotnitsa Ivan Asen II captured Theodore Komnenos Doukas of Epirus and took much of Thrace, Macedonia and Albania. Bulgaria reached its greatest extent and prestige, with its tsar claiming lands from Adrianople to the Adriatic and holding Thessalonica as a dependency.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1241": "Ivan Asen II died at the height of his power, having restored the Bulgarian patriarchate in 1235 and allied with Nicaea. The realm was still large and prosperous, but its unity rested on his personal authority and his heirs were children, leaving it exposed to regents and rivals.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1242": "Mongol armies returning from Hungary devastated Bulgaria and forced it to pay tribute to the Golden Horde. Under the boy tsars Kaliman I and Michael II Asen, the Empire of Nicaea soon seized Thrace and much of Macedonia, and Bulgaria lost its position as the leading Balkan power.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1257": "After Michael II Asen\u0027s murder and the end of the Asen line, the boyars chose Constantine Tikh as tsar. The realm was reduced to its northern core, exposed to Hungarian attacks from the north and to Byzantine pressure after the Greek recovery of Constantinople in 1261.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1277": "The swineherd Ivailo led a peasant rising against Mongol raids, killed Tsar Constantine Tikh and briefly took the throne. Civil war, Byzantine intervention and Tatar raids followed, and Bulgaria sank close to collapse under rulers who depended on the warlord Nogai.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1300": "Theodore Svetoslav, son of George I Terter, took the throne after Nogai\u0027s fall and ended Tatar domination. He punished those blamed for collaborating with the Tatars, recovered the Black Sea ports from Byzantium and gave Bulgaria two decades of relative stability and modest recovery.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1323": "The boyars elected Michael Shishman of Vidin as tsar, founding the last ruling dynasty. Ambitious and active, he intervened in Byzantine civil wars and sought to check the rising power of Serbia, showing that Bulgaria could still act as a regional power.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1330": "At the Battle of Velbazhd the Serbs under Stefan Dečanski and the young Stefan Dušan defeated the Bulgarian army and Michael Shishman was killed. Serbia replaced Bulgaria as the dominant power in the central Balkans and influenced the choice of the next tsar.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1332": "Ivan Alexander defeated Andronikos III at the Battle of Rusokastro and regained towns in Thrace. His long reign brought internal peace, silver coinage and an artistic and literary flowering at Tarnovo, the last high point of the medieval Bulgarian state.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1356": "Ivan Alexander\u0027s son Ivan Sratsimir established himself as tsar at Vidin, while Dobruja under its own lords went its own way. Bulgaria was now divided into rival realms just as the Ottomans, established at Gallipoli in 1354, began advancing through Thrace.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1371": "After the Ottoman victory over Serbian lords at the Battle of the Maritsa and Ivan Alexander\u0027s death, Ivan Shishman of Tarnovo became a vassal of Sultan Murad I. Bulgaria had to pay tribute and send troops, and Ottoman forces took towns south of the Balkan range.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1393": "Bayezid I\u0027s army captured Tarnovo on 17 July after a three-month siege, ending the Tsardom of Tarnovo. Patriarch Euthymius was exiled and the patriarchate abolished. Only Ivan Sratsimir\u0027s Tsardom of Vidin remained, as an Ottoman vassal in the north-west of the country.",
  "Second Bulgarian Empire|1396": "After the crusade of Sigismund of Hungary was destroyed at the Battle of Nicopolis, the Ottomans annexed Vidin and deposed Ivan Sratsimir. The last remnant of the medieval Bulgarian state disappeared, and Bulgaria remained under Ottoman rule for almost five centuries, until 1878.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1186": "Frederick Barbarossa\u0027s Gelnhausen Privilege confirmed the rights of Bremen\u0027s citizens and limited the archbishop\u0027s power over them. The city was still an archiepiscopal town with a modest merchant community, but it now had a legal foundation on which to build its later self-government.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1260": "Bremen took part in the network of German merchant towns that became the Hanseatic League, traditionally from around this date. Its Weser trade in grain, timber, beer and cloth was growing, and the council was steadily taking over rights once held by the archbishops.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1358": "Expelled from the Hanse in 1285 for breaking a blockade of Norway, Bremen was readmitted after long negotiations. Restored access to Hanseatic privileges in Flanders, England and Scandinavia strengthened its merchants, and the council was now the real master of the city.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1404": "The citizens set up the stone Roland in the market square and began building the town hall, completed in 1410, as symbols of their rights against the archbishop. Bremen was a prosperous, largely self-governing Hanseatic city, though its legal status remained disputed.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1522": "Heinrich von Zütphen\u0027s preaching brought the Reformation to Bremen, which soon broke with the Catholic archbishops. Religious change was followed by the guild rising of the 104 men in 1530–1532, which the council survived, keeping its oligarchic rule largely intact.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1595": "Bremen had adopted the Reformed confession and had begun remodelling its town hall, which received its Weser Renaissance facade in 1608–1612. Trade with the Netherlands, Norway and the Atlantic coasts flourished, and the city was at the height of its early modern prosperity and confidence.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1646": "Emperor Ferdinand III\u0027s Linz Diploma confirmed Bremen as a free imperial city, just before the Peace of Westphalia gave the surrounding archbishopric to Sweden. The city had come through the Thirty Years\u0027 War unoccupied, and its legal independence was now formally recognised.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1666": "Sweden, which claimed Bremen as part of its Duchy of Bremen-Verden, attacked the city for the second time. The Peace of Habenhausen preserved Bremen\u0027s independence on unfavourable terms, but the costly wars showed how exposed a small city-state was among larger powers.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1803": "The Imperial Recess left Bremen as one of only six free imperial cities to survive the reorganisation of the Empire, and it gained former church property within its walls. Its trade in tobacco, coffee and other colonial goods with the Americas was expanding fast.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1811": "After years of French occupation, Napoleon annexed Bremen to France as chief town of the department of Bouches-du-Weser. The Continental System had already crippled its overseas trade, and the city lost its independence, its institutions and much of its commerce.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1815": "Liberated in 1813, Bremen was confirmed as one of four free cities of the German Confederation at the Congress of Vienna, where Johann Smidt represented it. Its sovereignty restored, it began rebuilding the trade links with the Americas broken by war and blockade.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1827": "Johann Smidt bought land from Hanover at the mouth of the Weser and founded Bremerhaven, giving Bremen a deep-water port for large ships. The same year a commercial treaty with the United States opened the way for the transatlantic trade and emigration that made the city rich.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1857": "Norddeutscher Lloyd was founded in Bremen and soon ran regular steamship services to New York. Hundreds of thousands of emigrants passed through Bremerhaven, and the city\u0027s tobacco, cotton and coffee trades made it one of the wealthiest ports of the German states.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1888": "Bremen joined the German customs union while keeping a free port, completing its integration into the German Empire\u0027s economy. Shipbuilding, the cotton exchange and the Lloyd\u0027s growing fleet made the city a major industrial and commercial centre within the Empire.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1913": "On the eve of the First World War Norddeutscher Lloyd was one of the largest shipping companies in the world, Bremen\u0027s yards were busy and emigration through Bremerhaven was at its height. The city-state had reached the peak of its wealth and international reach.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1919": "Defeat in the First World War cost Germany its merchant fleet, and the short-lived Bremen Soviet Republic was crushed by government troops in February. The city kept its status as a state in the Weimar Republic but its shipping and trade had to be rebuilt.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1933": "The Nazi regime abolished the independence of the German states, placing Bremen under a Reich governor. Its Senate and Bürgerschaft lost all real power, and shipbuilding was turned to rearmament, so the city\u0027s centuries-old self-government existed in name only until 1945.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1945": "Allied bombing had destroyed much of the city and port, and British troops captured Bremen in April. The city-state had no government of its own, its harbour was wrecked, and its future as a separate state was uncertain under military occupation.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1947": "The American military government re-established the Land of Bremen, including Bremerhaven, and a new constitution was adopted in October. As the main supply port of the United States forces in Germany, the city began recovering from wartime destruction under Mayor Wilhelm Kaisen.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1949": "Bremen became a founding state of the Federal Republic of Germany, the smallest in area and population. Its shipyards, ports and coffee and tobacco trades recovered rapidly during the economic boom of the 1950s, restoring its prosperity within a federal system.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|1983": "The closure of the AG Weser shipyard marked the decline of Bremen\u0027s shipbuilding industry, followed by the collapse of Bremer Vulkan in 1996–1997. Unemployment and public debt rose sharply, and the small state came to depend on federal financial support.",
  "Free Hanseatic City of Bremen|2025": "Bremen remains Germany\u0027s smallest federal state, with a strong port at Bremerhaven, car-making, aerospace and science. Its debts and economic weight limit its influence, but its eight centuries of self-government make it one of the oldest continuous city-states in Europe.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1189": "The merchant new town founded by Count Adolf III of Schauenburg beside the old cathedral settlement was taking shape, later backed by a charter dated to this year. Hamburg was a small port under the counts of Holstein, with trading rights on the Elbe but little self-government yet.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1241": "Hamburg allied with Lübeck to protect the road between them, linking North Sea and Baltic trade. The towns had merged in 1216, the council was gaining power at the expense of the Holstein counts, and Hamburg was becoming a leading member of the emerging Hanseatic network.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1401": "A Hamburg fleet captured leaders of the Victual Brothers pirates, and Klaus Störtebeker was executed in the city. Hamburg was the Hanse\u0027s North Sea gateway and a great brewing centre, with hundreds of breweries exporting beer and a fleet able to police the sea routes.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1460": "The Treaty of Ribe made the Danish king Christian I ruler of Holstein, and Danish monarchs began to claim Hamburg as a Holstein town. The city remained prosperous and effectively self-governing, but its legal independence was now contested by a powerful neighbouring kingdom.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1510": "Emperor Maximilian I summoned Hamburg to the imperial diet as a free imperial city, strengthening its claim against Denmark. Its trade was growing as the Hanse\u0027s Baltic monopoly weakened and Atlantic commerce opened new opportunities for North Sea ports such as Hamburg.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1529": "Johannes Bugenhagen\u0027s church order made Hamburg Lutheran, and the Long Recess gave propertied citizens a share in government beside the council. The Reformation settled the city\u0027s constitution and confession for centuries, giving it stable institutions on which later prosperity rested.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1558": "The Hamburg Exchange was founded, and Dutch, Flemish and Portuguese newcomers were bringing capital and skills. Within a decade the English Merchant Adventurers arrived, making Hamburg a hub of the cloth trade and one of the most dynamic ports of northern Europe.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1619": "The Hamburger Bank was founded and new bastioned fortifications were under construction. Behind them Hamburg remained neutral and unoccupied throughout the Thirty Years\u0027 War, profiting from trade with all sides and emerging as the largest and richest city of the Empire\u0027s north.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1768": "In the Gottorp Settlement Denmark finally recognised Hamburg as a free imperial city. The city prospered from Atlantic and French trade, sugar refining and marine insurance, and its music, theatre and press made it one of the cultural centres of the German Enlightenment.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1806": "French troops occupied Hamburg after Prussia\u0027s defeat, and the Continental Blockade against Britain cut off its main trade. Merchants were ruined, shipping lay idle and the city\u0027s neutrality, the basis of its prosperity for two centuries, was ended by Napoleon\u0027s wars.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1811": "Napoleon annexed Hamburg to France as chief town of the department of Bouches-de-l\u0027Elbe. The free city disappeared, and in 1813–1814 Marshal Davout\u0027s siege defence brought hunger and the expulsion of thousands of inhabitants, the lowest point in its modern history.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1815": "Freed in 1814, Hamburg was confirmed as one of four free cities of the German Confederation. It restored its council and institutions and quickly rebuilt its trade with Britain and the Americas, regaining its place as the leading port of the German states.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1842": "The Great Fire destroyed about a quarter of the old town, including the town hall, and left some 20,000 people homeless. Rebuilding modernised the city with new streets, drains and public buildings, and its trade continued to grow despite the disaster.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1847": "HAPAG was founded to run sailing packets to New York, soon followed by steamships. Hamburg became one of Europe\u0027s main emigration ports and a centre of world trade, while new railways linked it ever more closely to the industrial German interior.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1888": "Hamburg joined the German customs union while keeping a free port, and the Speicherstadt warehouse district rose on cleared land. Integrated into the economy of the German Empire, the port grew rapidly into one of the largest in the world.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1892": "A cholera epidemic killed some 8,600 people, exposing the failures of a government dominated by property owners who had delayed water filtration. The disaster forced major reforms in public health and city administration, though the port\u0027s commercial growth continued almost unchecked.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1913": "HAPAG under Albert Ballin was the largest shipping company in the world, and Hamburg was the busiest port on the European continent. The city-state stood at the height of its wealth and global reach on the eve of the First World War.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1919": "Defeat in the First World War cost Hamburg most of its merchant fleet, and the revolution swept away the old propertied franchise. The Bürgerschaft was now elected by universal suffrage, but the city faced unemployment, hyperinflation and political violence in the following years.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1933": "The Nazi regime placed Hamburg under a Reich governor, ending real self-government; in 1937 the Greater Hamburg Act enlarged it with Altona, Wandsbek and Harburg-Wilhelmsburg. The port and shipyards were turned towards rearmament and the city\u0027s Jewish community was persecuted.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1943": "The Allied raids of Operation Gomorrah created a firestorm that killed around 35,000 people and destroyed much of the city. Hamburg ended the war in ruins, surrendering to British troops in May 1945 with its port wrecked and its population scattered.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|1949": "Re-established by the British as a state in 1946, Hamburg became a founding state of the Federal Republic of Germany. Reconstruction of the port and the growth of the press and media industries soon restored its position as West Germany\u0027s gateway to the world.",
  "Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg|2025": "Hamburg is Germany\u0027s second-largest city and largest port, a self-governing city-state with around 1.9 million inhabitants and a strong economy in shipping, logistics, aircraft manufacturing and media. As a modern federal state it stands close to its historic peak. It remains one of Europe\u0027s busiest ports.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1198": "Duke Ottokar I won a royal title by backing Philip of Swabia in the disputed German succession after Henry VI’s death. Bohemia was already a solid Christian duchy ruled from Prague, but the title still depended on imperial politics and could be withdrawn, so its standing was real yet unsecured.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1212": "Frederick II’s Golden Bull of Sicily made the royal title hereditary and limited Bohemia’s obligations to the empire. The kingdom gained a secure, privileged place as the only kingdom inside the Holy Roman Empire, with its king among the electors, and the Přemyslids could now plan for the long term.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1253": "Ottokar II succeeded his father Wenceslaus I, having already taken over the Duchy of Austria in 1251. German settlement, new chartered towns and early silver mining were raising royal revenues, and a strong knightly army gave the young king the means to pursue ambitious expansion southwards.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1269": "Ottokar II added Carinthia and Carniola to Austria and Styria, which he had won after defeating Hungary at Kressenbrunn in 1260. His lands stretched from the Sudetes to the Adriatic, making him the most powerful prince in the empire and a candidate for the German crown itself.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1278": "Having refused to recognise Rudolf of Habsburg, Ottokar II lost Austria and its neighbouring lands in 1276 and was killed at the Battle on the Marchfeld in 1278. Bohemia shrank back to its core, and a period of regency under Otto of Brandenburg brought disorder and foreign occupation.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1300": "Wenceslaus II, enriched by the Kutná Hora silver and the new Prague groschen, was crowned King of Poland in 1300, and his son soon took the Hungarian crown. For a few years the Přemyslids dominated Central Europe, though their hold on Poland and Hungary was contested and fragile.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1306": "The murder of the young Wenceslaus III at Olomouc ended the Přemyslid male line. The Polish and Hungarian connections collapsed, and rival claimants contested the throne until the estates chose John of Luxembourg in 1310. The kingdom remained rich but was politically weakened by the succession crisis.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1335": "In the treaties of Trenčín and Visegrád, Casimir III of Poland renounced Silesia while John of Luxembourg dropped his claim to the Polish crown. Most Silesian duchies and Upper Lusatia were now Bohemian fiefs, greatly enlarging the lands of the crown under a king active across European politics.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1356": "Charles IV, crowned emperor in 1355, issued the Golden Bull confirming the king of Bohemia as the first secular elector. Prague was an imperial residence with a new archbishopric, university and New Town, and the Lands of the Bohemian Crown were at their greatest prestige and cohesion.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1378": "At Charles IV’s death the Luxembourgs held Bohemia, Lower Lusatia and Brandenburg, and his son Wenceslaus IV succeeded as king and King of the Romans. Prague still stood at the centre of the empire, but the new king lacked his father’s authority, and conflict with the nobility and clergy was growing.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1419": "Wenceslaus IV died weeks after the first Defenestration of Prague, as the Hussite movement, inflamed by Jan Hus’s execution in 1415, rejected his brother Sigismund. The kingdom slid into revolution and civil war, and royal authority and central institutions largely dissolved, though Hussite armies proved formidable.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1434": "Moderate Utraquists and Catholics destroyed the radical Hussite field armies at Lipany, opening the way to the Compacts of Basel in 1436 and Sigismund’s recognition. After fifteen years of war the land was exhausted, crown property dispersed to nobles and towns, and royal power badly weakened.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1458": "The estates elected George of Poděbrady, a Utraquist noble who had governed as regent, as king. He restored order and royal finances and proposed a league of Christian princes, but papal hostility and a war with Matthias Corvinus after 1468 limited the recovery of the kingdom’s power.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1526": "Louis II, last Jagiellonian king, died at Mohács, and the estates elected Ferdinand I of Habsburg. Bohemia was still a wealthy kingdom with strong estates, but its future was now tied to a dynasty whose interests centred on Vienna, Hungary and the struggle with the Ottomans.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1609": "Rudolf II, who had made Prague his imperial residence, granted the Letter of Majesty guaranteeing religious freedom. Prague was a brilliant court centre of art and science, but the kingdom’s autonomy rested on the estates’ leverage over a weak ruler, and confessional tensions were sharpening.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1620": "The Protestant estates, having deposed Ferdinand II and elected Frederick V, were crushed at the Battle of White Mountain. Executions, confiscations and emigration followed, the elective monarchy was ended by the Renewed Land Ordinance of 1627, and Bohemia lost most of its political autonomy to Vienna.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia confirmed Habsburg rule over a Bohemia devastated by thirty years of war, with heavy population losses after repeated Swedish campaigns, the last of which took Prague’s Lesser Town in 1648. The Lusatias had already passed to Saxony in 1635, and the kingdom survived as a Catholic Habsburg crown land.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1742": "Frederick II of Prussia seized most of Silesia during the War of the Austrian Succession, and the Treaty of Breslau confirmed the loss. Charles Albert of Bavaria had even been proclaimed king in Prague in 1741. Maria Theresa’s reforms soon merged Bohemian administration with that of Austria.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1848": "Prague hosted the Slav Congress, and František Palacký championed Czech rights within a federal Austria, but the June uprising in Prague was crushed by Prince Windischgrätz. Bohemia was the industrial heart of the monarchy, yet as a kingdom it had little political power of its own.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1867": "The Austro-Hungarian Compromise gave Hungary autonomy but left Bohemia as a crown land of the Austrian half. Czech politicians, demanding recognition of Bohemian state rights, boycotted the Vienna parliament for years, and Czech–German rivalry increasingly paralysed the Bohemian diet. A planned settlement of 1871 recognising Bohemian rights was abandoned under German and Hungarian opposition.",
  "Kingdom of Bohemia|1918": "As Austria-Hungary collapsed at the end of the First World War, Czech leaders proclaimed the Czechoslovak state in Prague on 28 October 1918. The Kingdom of Bohemia ceased to exist, and its lands, together with Moravia and Czech Silesia, became the core of the new republic.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1204": "King Philip II of France conquered continental Normandy, but the Channel Islands stayed with King John. Jersey became a small, exposed frontier of the English crown, governed by royal wardens and dependent on English naval support, with its Norman law and local courts its main assets.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1259": "In the Treaty of Paris Henry III renounced his claim to Normandy but kept the Channel Islands, giving Jersey’s separation from the mainland a lasting legal basis. The island remained poor and agricultural, governed by a warden, with a bailiff and jurats emerging as the core of local administration.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1338": "In the early Hundred Years War French fleets ravaged Jersey in 1338–1339, burning parishes and besieging Mont Orgueil Castle, which held out. The island’s survival under the English crown depended on its castle and on relief from England, and its farms and fisheries suffered heavily from the raids.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1461": "During the Wars of the Roses Margaret of Anjou’s agreement with Pierre de Brézé handed Mont Orgueil and much of Jersey to French control. For seven years the island was effectively lost to the English crown, the lowest point in its political history before the German occupation.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1468": "Sir Richard Harliston, with English ships and island support, besieged Mont Orgueil and recovered Jersey for Edward IV. The episode confirmed the islanders’ attachment to the English crown and to their privileges, which the Yorkist king soon reinforced, restoring the bailiwick’s autonomy under English sovereignty.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1481": "At Edward IV’s request Pope Sixtus IV issued a bull of neutrality protecting the Channel Islands and their waters in wartime. This privilege, together with royal charters, let Jersey trade more securely while England and France fought, and it lasted until 1689.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1600": "Elizabeth Castle was being built off Saint Helier, and Sir Walter Raleigh became governor in 1600. Knitting for export and growing trade with France and England supported a modest prosperity, while the States of Jersey were emerging as a legislative assembly alongside the Royal Court.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1649": "Under Sir George Carteret Jersey was a royalist stronghold and privateering base. Prince Charles, who had sheltered there in 1646, was proclaimed king in Saint Helier in February 1649 and returned later that year, briefly making the island a seat of the exiled Stuart court.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1651": "Parliamentary forces under Robert Blake and James Heane landed and took the island, and Elizabeth Castle surrendered at the end of the year. Jersey lost its royalist independence of action and was garrisoned by the Commonwealth until the Restoration of 1660.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1771": "An Order in Council confirmed that only the States could pass laws, and a code of the island’s laws was issued. Newfoundland cod and privateering brought wealth, and Jersey’s institutions were taking the form of a self-governing legislature under the Crown.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1781": "Baron de Rullecourt’s French force seized Saint Helier, but British troops and militia under Major Francis Peirson defeated it in the Battle of Jersey. The victory ended the last French attempt to take the island and confirmed its value as a fortified British outpost.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1815": "The Napoleonic Wars had brought a large garrison, Fort Regent, coastal towers and profitable privateering. With peace, the cod trade on the Gaspé and in Newfoundland flourished, and Jersey entered a prosperous century of shipping, shipbuilding and growing links with England.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1856": "Elected deputies were added to the States, the first step towards a democratic legislature. The island was prospering from shipbuilding, the cod trade and early potatoes, while English settlers and steamship links were changing Saint Helier and the island’s language and culture.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1940": "After Britain demilitarised the Channel Islands, German forces occupied Jersey on 1 July 1940. The island’s government, headed by the Bailiff Alexander Coutanche, continued under German authority, and its autonomy and links with Britain were suspended in the most serious crisis in its history.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1945": "Jersey was liberated on 9 May 1945, when British forces landed, after five years of occupation, deportations and, from 1944, near-starvation. The island emerged impoverished but with its institutions intact, and the reforms of 1948 soon modernised the States and introduced elected senators.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|1973": "When the United Kingdom joined the European Economic Community, Jersey remained outside it under Protocol 3 of the Accession Treaty, keeping its own tax system. Low taxes and autonomy were drawing banks and trust companies, and finance was overtaking farming and tourism.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|2005": "Ministerial government under a Chief Minister replaced the committee system. Jersey was now a major offshore financial centre, among the wealthiest places in Europe per head, and a self-governing Crown Dependency with growing responsibility for its own external economic relations.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|2007": "On the eve of the global financial crisis Jersey’s finance industry was at its height, with very large bank deposits and fund assets under administration, and public finances were strong. The island’s prosperity and autonomy marked the peak of its standing.",
  "Bailiwick of Jersey|2025": "Jersey remains a prosperous self-governing Crown Dependency with its own States Assembly, courts and tax system. Its finance sector, though under international pressure over tax transparency since 2008, still dominates the economy, and the island’s standing remains close to its peak.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1204": "Burgrave Frederick I of Nuremberg, born a count of Zollern, died around this time, and his sons soon divided the inheritance. The Swabian county kept the ancestral castle and lands around Hechingen, a modest but respectable lordship whose counts were linked to the Staufer court and to their Franconian cousins.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1288": "A further division created a separate line based on the Schalksburg near Balingen, splitting the already small Swabian possessions. Partible inheritance reduced the main county’s lands and revenues, and the counts of Zollern remained minor figures among the many lords of south-western Germany.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1403": "The Schalksburg line sold its lands, including Balingen, to the counts of Württemberg, a growing neighbour that would long overshadow the Zollern territory. The loss showed how fragmentation and debt could strip a small dynasty of its inheritance, leaving the county further reduced.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1415": "The Franconian cousins rose dramatically when Burgrave Frederick VI of Nuremberg was invested with Brandenburg as an elector. In Swabia, by contrast, the county was torn by the feud between Count Frederick the Öttinger and his brother Eitel Friedrich I, which drew in the Swabian imperial cities.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1423": "After a long siege by the Swabian imperial cities, Zollern castle surrendered and was razed. The county lost its principal fortress and the dynasty its ancestral seat, the lowest point in its history, and the county was left divided, indebted and dependent on the goodwill of powerful neighbours.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1454": "Count Jos Niklas I began rebuilding Zollern castle with support from the family’s Franconian relatives, now electors of Brandenburg. The new fortress, larger than the old, restored the dynasty’s seat and prestige, and the county slowly recovered from decades of feuding and debt.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1495": "Eitel Friedrich II, a close adviser of Maximilian I, became the first presiding judge of the new Imperial Chamber Court. Imperial service brought the counts unprecedented influence, rewards and connections, and in 1497 he acquired the Austrian lordship of Haigerloch in exchange for Rhäzüns.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1512": "At the death of Eitel Friedrich II the county was larger and more secure than at any earlier time, enlarged by Haigerloch and backed by Habsburg favour. His son Eitel Friedrich III continued the family tradition of service to the emperor in war and at court.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1525": "Eitel Friedrich III died at Pavia during the imperial campaign in Italy, leaving a young son, Charles I. A minority weakened the county briefly, but the family’s loyalty to the Habsburgs was well rewarded, and the boy was raised at the court of Charles V.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1534": "After the counts of Werdenberg died out, the Habsburgs granted the counties of Sigmaringen and Veringen to Charles I as Austrian fiefs. With Hechingen, Haigerloch and these new lands, the house of Hohenzollern held its largest territory in Swabia, the high point of the county’s power.",
  "County of Hohenzollern|1576": "Charles I died and his lands were divided among his three sons into Hohenzollern-Hechingen, Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen and Hohenzollern-Haigerloch. The single county ceased to exist, replaced by three small territories linked by family agreements, two of which were raised to principalities in 1623.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1205": "William of Champlitte and Geoffrey of Villehardouin defeated the local Greeks at the Olive Grove of Koundouros and took the western Peloponnese. The new principality was a small conquest state held by a few hundred knights and soldiers, still facing Greek strongholds such as Acrocorinth, Argos and Monemvasia.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1209": "After Champlitte’s departure, Geoffrey I of Villehardouin ruled the principality and was recognised as a vassal of the Latin Emperor Henry at the Parliament of Ravennika. Treaties with Venice settled the ports of Modon and Coron, and the conquest of the remaining Greek strongholds was under way.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1223": "With Corinth, Argos and Nauplia taken and the castle of Chlemoutsi completed, Geoffrey I had consolidated a stable feudal principality. The baronies were organised, the Greek archons reconciled, and Achaea was becoming the strongest of the Latin states in Greece, though Monemvasia still held out.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1248": "William II took Monemvasia after a long siege, completing the conquest of the Peloponnese, and the Latin emperor granted him suzerainty over the Duchy of the Archipelago. He was building Mistras and other fortresses, and his court at Andravida was renowned throughout Latin Greece.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1258": "William II defeated Guy I de la Roche of Athens at Karydi in the War of the Euboeote Succession, confirming his hegemony over Latin Greece. Achaea controlled the whole Peloponnese apart from the Venetian ports and stood at the height of its power and prestige.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1259": "Allied with Epirus and Manfred of Sicily, William II was defeated and captured by the forces of Nicaea at the Battle of Pelagonia. With its prince and many barons in captivity, the principality was left leaderless and exposed, and its power collapsed almost overnight.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1262": "William II was released after ceding Monemvasia, Mistras and Grand Magne to Michael VIII Palaiologos, who had recovered Constantinople in 1261. The principality survived, but a Byzantine base now stood inside the Peloponnese, and open war with the Byzantines soon followed.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1264": "The Franks defeated Byzantine armies at Prinitza in 1263 and Makryplagi in 1264, halting the first Byzantine offensive. These victories saved the principality’s core, but the war was costly, and William II looked for a powerful Western protector to secure the future of his realm.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1267": "In the Treaty of Viterbo William II accepted Charles of Anjou as suzerain and agreed that his daughter Isabella would marry Charles’s son Philip. The arrangement brought Angevin support against Byzantium but tied the principality’s future to the distant rulers of Naples.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1278": "William II died without a son, and the principality passed to Charles I of Naples, who governed through bailiffs. Absentee rule weakened the prince’s authority over the barons, while the Byzantines continued to push forward from Mistras into Arcadia and Laconia.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1289": "Isabella of Villehardouin was invested as princess with her husband Florent of Hainaut, who made a truce with the Byzantines. Their resident rule restored some stability and prosperity, and Andravida again had a princely court, though Angevin suzerainty limited their freedom.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1316": "Ferdinand of Majorca, claiming the principality for his son, was defeated and killed by Louis of Burgundy at the Battle of Manolada. The war showed how disputed successions now divided the barons and drained the principality’s strength, while Byzantine advances continued.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1377": "Queen Joanna I of Naples leased the principality to the Knights Hospitaller in return for an annual payment. Princely power was weak, claims were disputed among Angevin heirs, and Byzantine and Turkish pressure was growing, so control passed increasingly to whoever could pay soldiers.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1381": "The Navarrese Company, mercenaries brought in under the Hospitaller lease, seized much of the principality and governed it in their own interest. The legitimate princes in Italy had little real authority, and the principality was reduced to a mercenary-run territory.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1396": "King Ladislaus of Naples recognised the Navarrese captain Pedro de San Superano as Prince of Achaea. His rule gave the remnant some cohesion, but Ottoman raids, including the sack of Argos in 1397, and Byzantine pressure kept the principality on the defensive.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1404": "Centurione II Zaccaria, a Genoese baron and nephew of Pedro’s widow, became prince. He held the north-western Peloponnese and the central baronies but faced the Despotate of the Morea, the Tocco of Epirus, Venice and the Ottomans with very limited resources.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1428": "Constantine Palaiologos gained Glarentza through marriage to a niece of Carlo I Tocco and moved against Patras. The principality had lost its main port and was reduced to a handful of castles, with Byzantine forces closing in from every side.",
  "Principality of Achaea|1432": "Centurione II, who had married his daughter Catherine to Thomas Palaiologos in 1430 with the principality as dowry, died in his castle of Arcadia (Kyparissia) in Messenia. Thomas annexed the last Frankish lands, ending the Principality of Achaea after more than two centuries.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1218": "The dukes of Zähringen died out, and Zürich came directly under the empire as an imperial city. It was a prosperous market town governed by a council of knights and merchants, with the Fraumünster abbess as nominal lady, but it had little territory beyond its walls.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1336": "Rudolf Brun led a revolt that overthrew the old council and introduced a guild constitution, ruling as burgomaster for life. The coup gave craftsmen a share of power and a strong leader, but exiled councillors and their noble allies remained a serious threat to the city.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1351": "After foiling the exiles’ coup of 1350 and destroying Rapperswil, Brun allied Zürich with Lucerne, Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden in a perpetual league. Membership of the Confederacy gave the city powerful allies, though it soon brought war with Duke Albert II of Austria.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1355": "Zürich had withstood Austrian attacks and a siege, and the Peace of Regensburg ended the war. The city remained in the Confederacy while Brun also came to terms with the Habsburgs, keeping its independence but balancing uneasily between its confederates and Austria.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1424": "Zürich acquired the county of Kyburg as a pledge, the largest of a series of purchases and pledges that turned the city into the ruler of an extensive rural territory. With Greifensee, Grüningen and Regensberg already held, it was becoming a territorial state.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1443": "In the Old Zürich War, allied with Frederick III of Habsburg against the other confederates, Zürich was defeated at St. Jakob an der Sihl, where the burgomaster Rudolf Stüssi died. The city was besieged the next year, and its countryside was ravaged.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1450": "The peace settlement forced Zürich to abandon its Habsburg alliance but let it keep most of its territory. Reconciled with the Confederacy, the city resumed its leading role among the cantons, though the war had shown the limits of its independent ambitions.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1476": "Zürich troops under Hans Waldmann fought at the Battle of Morat, part of the confederate victories over Charles the Bold of Burgundy. The Confederacy’s military reputation was at its height, and Zürich, having bought Winterthur in 1467, was prosperous and confident.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1489": "Hans Waldmann, burgomaster since 1483, was overthrown and executed after rural protests and guild opposition to his authoritarian rule. Zürich remained strong, but the crisis forced concessions to the countryside and showed the tensions between the city and its subjects.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1519": "Huldrych Zwingli began preaching at the Grossmünster, starting the Reformation in Zürich. The city was wealthy and secure, the leading canton of a Confederacy at the peak of its military fame, and Zwingli’s reforms would soon make it a European centre of religious change.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1525": "The council abolished the Mass, dissolved the monasteries and founded the Prophezei, establishing a Reformed church under civic control. Zürich had become the head of a new Protestant movement, though the Reformation also divided it from the Catholic cantons and from Anabaptists.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1531": "Defeat at the Battle of Kappel and the death of Zwingli halted the spread of the Reformation in the Confederacy. The Second Peace of Kappel protected Catholic territories, and Zürich lost political leadership in religious affairs for a time, though its own church survived.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1549": "Heinrich Bullinger and John Calvin agreed the Consensus Tigurinus, uniting Zürich and Geneva on the Lord’s Supper. Under Bullinger Zürich was one of the main centres of Reformed Protestantism, with correspondents and influence across Europe, while its economy recovered from the disruption of the 1530s.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia recognised the Swiss Confederacy’s independence from the empire. Zürich had stayed neutral through the Thirty Years War, built new fortifications and prospered from trade, emerging as a wealthy, securely governed city-state and the leading Protestant canton alongside Bern.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1712": "Zürich and Bern defeated the Catholic cantons in the Second War of Villmergen, and the Peace of Aarau gave them the upper hand in the common lordships. Politically dominant within the Confederacy and economically thriving, Zürich stood at the peak of its power.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1780": "The putting-out trade in cotton and silk was at its height, and Zürich was a centre of the German Enlightenment. The city’s wealth and cultural standing were great, but its rural subjects, now numerous and prosperous, increasingly resented their lack of political rights.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1795": "The government crushed the Stäfa affair, occupying the village and punishing the authors of a petition for rural rights. Order was restored, but the repression exposed how narrow and brittle the old regime had become as revolutionary ideas spread from France.",
  "Canton of Zürich|1798": "As French armies invaded the Confederacy, Zürich granted equality to its rural subjects and its old government gave way. The city-republic was absorbed into the Helvetic Republic, ending nearly six centuries of independent rule by the city and of its citizens’ privileges over their territory.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1226": "Duke Konrad I of Masovia invited the Teutonic Order against the pagan Prussians, and the Golden Bull of Rimini, dated 1226, granted Hermann von Salza any lands the Order conquered. The Order was still a modest brotherhood with estates in the Holy Land and the Empire and no foothold yet in Prussia.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1231": "Hermann Balk crossed the Vistula and founded Thorn, followed by Kulm in 1232 and the Kulm charter of 1233. The Order held only a narrow bridgehead in the Kulmerland, dependent on crusaders arriving each season from Germany and Poland to fight the Prussian tribes.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1237": "The Order founded Elbing and absorbed the Livonian Brothers of the Sword after their defeat at Saule, gaining a northern branch in Livonia. Pomesania and Pogesania had largely been subdued, and the knights now controlled the lower Vistula and the shore of the Vistula Lagoon.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1255": "A crusade led by King Ottokar II of Bohemia overran Sambia, and Königsberg was founded and named in his honour. With most western Prussian tribes subdued and four bishoprics created in 1243, the Order now held a chain of castles along the Baltic coast.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1260": "The defeat of the Order\u0027s Livonian branch by the Samogitians at Durbe set off the Great Prussian Uprising. Rebels under leaders such as Herkus Monte destroyed castles across Prussia, and for several years the Order held little beyond its strongest fortresses, relying on crusaders for survival.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1283": "With the subjugation of the Sudovians, the last of the Prussian tribes, the conquest begun more than fifty years earlier was complete. The Order now ruled all of Prussia, began large-scale German settlement and turned its attention to its pagan Lithuanian neighbours beyond the Neman.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1309": "Having seized Danzig and Pomerelia in 1308 and bought out Brandenburg\u0027s claims by the Treaty of Soldin, the Order moved its headquarters from Venice to Marienburg. Prussia became the centre of the whole Order, controlling the mouth of the Vistula and a land link towards the Empire.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1343": "The Treaty of Kalisz with Casimir III of Poland ended decades of conflict: Poland accepted the Order\u0027s rule in Pomerelia and the Kulmerland, and the Order returned Kuyavia and Dobrzyń. Secure in the west, the knights could concentrate their crusading effort against Lithuania.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1370": "Under Grand Master Winrich von Kniprode the knights defeated the Lithuanians at Rudau. Prosperous towns, rising grain exports and a stream of noble crusaders from England, France and the Empire made the Order one of the strongest powers on the Baltic.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1386": "Jogaila of Lithuania was baptised, married Queen Jadwiga and became king of Poland, uniting the Order\u0027s two great enemies and undermining its crusading purpose. The Order nonetheless remained strong in men and money and kept raiding Samogitia while exploiting rivalries within the Lithuanian ruling house.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1404": "At its territorial height the Order held Prussia, Pomerelia, Gotland, the Neumark bought in 1402 and, confirmed by the Treaty of Raciąż, Samogitia, which linked Prussia with Livonia. Its revenues, castles and trade were unmatched among the powers of the eastern Baltic.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1410": "The Polish-Lithuanian victory at Grunwald killed Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen and most senior officers, and many towns and bishops submitted to Władysław II Jagiełło. Heinrich von Plauen\u0027s defence of Marienburg saved the state, but its military reputation and confidence were broken.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1411": "The First Peace of Thorn left the Order\u0027s territory largely intact but surrendered Samogitia to Lithuania for the lifetimes of Jagiełło and Vytautas and imposed a heavy indemnity. Paying it required heavy taxation, the root of lasting friction between the Order and its towns and gentry.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1422": "After the brief Gollub War the Treaty of Melno fixed the border with Lithuania and gave up Samogitia permanently. The land link with Livonia was lost, the Order\u0027s finances remained strained, and its subjects increasingly resented government by a corporation of foreign celibate knights.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1454": "The Prussian Confederation of towns and gentry renounced the Order and submitted to Casimir IV of Poland, starting the Thirteen Years\u0027 War. Danzig, Thorn, Elbing and most other towns rose against the knights, who had to rely on expensive mercenaries they could not pay.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1466": "The Second Peace of Thorn gave Pomerelia, the Kulmerland, Marienburg, Elbing and Ermland to Poland as Royal Prussia. The Order kept only eastern Prussia, ruled from Königsberg, and its Grand Master became a vassal of the Polish king, a sharp fall in status and resources.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1511": "The election of Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach, a nephew of King Sigismund I, reflected the hope that a prince with powerful German relatives could free the Order from Polish overlordship. The truncated state remained poor, depending on its towns and a shrinking body of knights.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1521": "The Polish-Teutonic War of 1519–1521 brought no help from the Empire, and Albert accepted a four-year truce at Thorn. Ravaged lands, unpaid troops and the spread of Lutheran ideas in the towns left the Order\u0027s government in Prussia close to collapse.",
  "State of the Teutonic Order|1525": "By the Treaty of Kraków Albert secularised the Order\u0027s Prussian lands and became the first hereditary Duke of Prussia, paying homage to Sigismund I. The State of the Teutonic Order ceased to exist, though the Order itself survived in the Empire and in Livonia.",
  "Crown of Castile|1230": "Ferdinand III inherited León from his father Alfonso IX, and the Treaty of Benavente bought off his half-sisters\u0027 claims. The permanent union of Castile, León and Galicia created the largest Christian kingdom in the peninsula, ready to exploit the collapse of Almohad power in al-Andalus.",
  "Crown of Castile|1248": "Seville surrendered after a siege of more than a year, following the conquest of Córdoba in 1236 and Jaén in 1246. With Murcia and Granada as vassals, the crown now held the Guadalquivir valley, its rich farmland and the great river port of Seville.",
  "Crown of Castile|1284": "Alfonso X died after a civil war against his son, the future Sancho IV, that split the nobility and towns. His reign brought the Siete Partidas and a flowering of learning, but a failed imperial candidacy, revolts and Marinid invasions left the crown weaker than in 1252.",
  "Crown of Castile|1340": "Alfonso XI defeated the Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan and Yusuf I of Granada at Río Salado and took Algeciras four years later. After decades of regencies and noble disorder, royal authority recovered and the threat of North African invasion receded for good.",
  "Crown of Castile|1369": "Henry of Trastámara killed his half-brother Peter I at Montiel, ending a civil war that had drawn in English and French armies. The new dynasty bought noble support with large grants of land and lordships, but its alliance with France made Castile\u0027s fleet a force in the Hundred Years\u0027 War.",
  "Crown of Castile|1385": "Juan I\u0027s attempt to take the throne of Portugal ended in defeat at Aljubarrota, a costly blow to Castilian arms and prestige. The Royal Council was reorganised the same year, but the early fifteenth century brought royal minorities and bitter struggles among noble factions.",
  "Crown of Castile|1474": "Isabella I took the throne on the death of her half-brother Henry IV, whose reign had seen noble revolts and a disputed succession. Her claim was challenged by Henry\u0027s daughter Juana, backed by Portugal, and the kingdom slid into a succession war with its authority divided.",
  "Crown of Castile|1479": "The Treaty of Alcáçovas ended the War of the Castilian Succession, and Isabella\u0027s husband Ferdinand II inherited the Crown of Aragon. The dynastic union of the two crowns, the new Santa Hermandad and the coming Inquisition gave the monarchs unprecedented authority within Castile.",
  "Crown of Castile|1492": "The fall of Granada completed the conquest of al-Andalus, the Jews were expelled, and Columbus, sailing for Castile, reached the Caribbean. The crown now shared a united peninsular monarchy with Aragon and gained an Atlantic outlet that became the beginning of an empire.",
  "Crown of Castile|1521": "Charles I defeated the Comuneros at Villalar, ending urban resistance to royal taxation, while Cortés completed the conquest of Tenochtitlan. Castile was the heart of a monarchy that also included Aragon, the Netherlands and southern Italy, and its king was Holy Roman Emperor.",
  "Crown of Castile|1556": "Philip II succeeded Charles V as ruler of Castile, the Americas, the Netherlands and Italy. Silver from Potosí, discovered in 1545, and from Mexico flowed through Seville, yet the crown suspended payments to its bankers in 1557, an early sign of the cost of empire.",
  "Crown of Castile|1580": "Philip II inherited Portugal and its overseas empire after King Sebastian fell at Alcácer Quibir and his successor, Cardinal Henry, died childless. Castile, the administrative and fiscal core of a monarchy spanning every continent then known to Europeans, reached its greatest power, with American silver revenues near their height.",
  "Crown of Castile|1598": "Philip II died after the failure of the Armada of 1588, a renewed bankruptcy in 1596 and the outbreak of a devastating plague. Castile still led Europe\u0027s strongest monarchy, but its people and finances were overstrained by war in the Netherlands, France and the Atlantic.",
  "Crown of Castile|1609": "The Twelve Years\u0027 Truce with the Dutch and the start of the expulsion of the Moriscos marked the government of Philip III and the Duke of Lerma. Peace brought relief, but debased copper coinage and the loss of Morisco farmers and artisans weakened the economy of the Spanish kingdoms.",
  "Crown of Castile|1640": "Olivares\u0027s attempt to make the other kingdoms share Castile\u0027s burden in the Thirty Years\u0027 War provoked the revolts of Catalonia and Portugal. Castile, exhausted by taxes and recurrent epidemics, faced war on several fronts and the end of its unquestioned dominance in Europe.",
  "Crown of Castile|1665": "Defeat by Portugal at Villaviciosa and the death of Philip IV left the throne to the sickly three-year-old Charles II. After the Peace of the Pyrenees of 1659 with France, Castile was a declining power whose population and economy had contracted since 1600.",
  "Crown of Castile|1700": "Charles II died childless, leaving his crowns to Philip of Anjou, grandson of Louis XIV of France. Castile had begun a modest recovery, but the will provoked the War of the Spanish Succession, in which European coalitions fought over the whole Spanish monarchy.",
  "Crown of Castile|1715": "Philip V had secured his throne by the treaties of Utrecht and Rastatt but lost the Netherlands and his Italian lands. The Nueva Planta decrees, from 1707, imposed Castilian law and institutions on Aragon, Valencia, Majorca and in 1716 Catalonia, merging the Crown of Castile into a centralised Bourbon Spain.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1232": "Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Nasr proclaimed himself ruler at Arjona as Almohad power collapsed in al-Andalus. He was one of several local strongmen, controlling a handful of towns around Jaén while Ibn Hud of Murcia claimed the loyalty of most of Muslim Spain.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1238": "Muhammad I entered Granada, and after the death of Ibn Hud he gained Almería and Málaga. The Nasrid realm now held the mountains and coast of the south-east, and work began on turning the fortress of the Alhambra into a royal residence above the capital.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1246": "Besieged at Jaén by Ferdinand III of Castile, Muhammad I surrendered the city and became a tributary vassal. The submission cost territory and prestige but secured the emirate\u0027s survival while Castile went on to conquer Seville and the Guadalquivir valley.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1275": "Muhammad II, who succeeded in 1273, invited the Marinid sultan of Morocco to cross the Strait, and Marinid victories over Castile relieved pressure on Granada. The emirate now had to balance a powerful and sometimes dangerous North African ally against its Christian neighbours.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1309": "Castile and Aragon launched a joint crusade, taking Gibraltar while an Aragonese siege of Almería failed. The emirate survived through diplomacy and tribute, but the deposition of Muhammad III the same year showed the dynastic instability that would recur throughout its history.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1333": "Marinid forces, with Granadan support, recaptured Gibraltar from Castile, though Muhammad IV was assassinated shortly afterwards and his brother Yusuf I took the throne. Under Yusuf the court flourished, and work began on the great palaces of the Alhambra and the city\u0027s madrasa.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1340": "The defeat of Yusuf I and the Marinid sultan Abu al-Hasan at Río Salado ended large-scale North African intervention, and Algeciras fell to Castile in 1344. Granada now had to rely on its own forces, on Castilian quarrels and on the strength of its mountain frontier.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1354": "Yusuf I was murdered in the Great Mosque of Granada and succeeded by Muhammad V. The emirate enjoyed peace with Castile, which was distracted by the conflicts of Peter I, and the court of Ibn al-Khatib and Ibn Zamrak made Granada a centre of Arabic letters.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1359": "A palace coup deposed Muhammad V, who fled to Morocco while rivals, Ismail II and then Muhammad VI, seized power. Political turmoil and Castilian hostility to the usurper weakened the state and drew it into the civil conflicts of Castile.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1362": "Muhammad V returned to the throne with the support of Peter I of Castile, who had the usurper Muhammad VI killed in Seville. The restored ruler began the second and most brilliant period of his reign, building the Court of the Lions and strengthening the emirate\u0027s defences.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1370": "Exploiting Castile\u0027s civil war, Muhammad V had taken and razed Algeciras in 1369. Granada was at peace, prosperous from silk and sugar exports and adorned with the Alhambra\u0027s finest palaces, and it stood at the height of its security and prestige.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1391": "Muhammad V died after a long and stable reign, leaving a wealthy and respected state. His successors faced a Castile recovering from the Trastámara civil wars, and the long peace that had protected the emirate began to give way to renewed frontier warfare.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1410": "Ferdinand of Antequera, regent of Castile, captured the town of Antequera after a siege of about five months, the first major loss of territory in generations. The frontier began to move south, though Granada\u0027s defences and Castilian distractions still prevented larger conquests.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1431": "Juan II of Castile won the Battle of La Higueruela outside Granada and backed a rival claimant, Yusuf IV. Recurring civil wars between Muhammad IX and his rivals, with the Abencerraje clan as kingmakers, now weakened the emirate as much as Castilian armies did.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1462": "Castilian nobles took Gibraltar, and Archidona fell the same year, as Granada\u0027s internal conflicts continued. The emirate kept its core territories but lost key frontier strongholds and relied increasingly on Castile\u0027s own troubles under King Henry IV for its continued survival.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1482": "Castile seized Alhama in retaliation for the Nasrid capture of Zahara, beginning the final war. At the same moment Boabdil rebelled against his father, Abu al-Hasan Ali, splitting the emirate into rival factions that Isabella I and Ferdinand II exploited.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1487": "After the fall of Ronda in 1485 and Loja in 1486, the great port of Málaga surrendered after a long siege, and most of its population was enslaved. Granada lost its western half and its chief link with North Africa and with Genoese trade.",
  "Emirate of Granada|1492": "Baza had fallen and al-Zaghal had surrendered Almería and Guadix in 1489. Isolated and besieged, Boabdil agreed the Treaty of Granada and handed over the city on 2 January 1492, ending the last Muslim state in the Iberian Peninsula after 260 years.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1236": "Lithuanian and Samogitian warriors annihilated the Livonian Brothers of the Sword at Saule, halting the crusaders\u0027 advance. The Lithuanians were still a loose alliance of pagan dukes, but the common danger from the crusading orders was pushing them towards unification under a single ruler.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1253": "Mindaugas, who had united the Lithuanian lands by eliminating rivals, was crowned king after accepting baptism. The crown gave him papal recognition and a truce with the Livonian Order, but his authority over the other dukes and the pagan population remained fragile.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1263": "Mindaugas and two of his sons were assassinated by rival dukes, and the kingdom collapsed amid a struggle for power. The state survived, but years of internal conflict and continued war with the crusading orders kept it weak and divided until a new dynasty emerged.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1316": "Gediminas succeeded his brother Vytenis, inheriting a consolidated state that already held Black Ruthenia and influence over Polotsk. He made Vilnius his capital, invited foreign merchants and craftsmen, and pursued marriage alliances and expansion into the fragmented principalities of western Rus\u0027.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1341": "Gediminas died, leaving a realm that stretched from the Baltic coast to the Pripet marshes, including Minsk, Pinsk and parts of Volhynia. Power passed after a brief struggle to his sons Algirdas, who ruled the east, and Kęstutis, who defended the west against the Teutonic Order.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1362": "Algirdas defeated the Golden Horde at Blue Waters, around 1362, and brought Kiev and Podolia under Lithuanian control. The grand duchy now reached deep into Ukraine and rivalled Moscow as a gatherer of the lands of Rus\u0027, while Kęstutis held the Teutonic Order at bay.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1386": "Jogaila was baptised, married Queen Jadwiga and became King Władysław II of Poland under the Union of Krewo. The personal union added a powerful ally against the Teutonic Order, but it provoked civil war with his cousin Vytautas, who resisted subordination to Poland.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1399": "Vytautas, ruling Lithuania since 1392, led a great army against the Tatars but was crushingly defeated at the Vorskla River. Many princes died and his plans for dominance over the Golden Horde collapsed, though the grand duchy itself kept its vast territory.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1410": "Lithuanian forces under Vytautas fought alongside the Poles at Grunwald, destroying the military power of the Teutonic Order. The victory secured Samogitia and the western frontier, leaving Vytautas free to extend his influence over Novgorod, Pskov and the Tatar khanates.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1430": "Vytautas died shortly before a planned coronation as king, at the height of his power. The grand duchy, perhaps the largest state in Europe, stretched from the Baltic almost to the Black Sea, and his grandson, Grand Prince Vasily II of Moscow, had been placed under his protection.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1500": "Muscovy under Ivan III defeated the Lithuanians at Vedrosha and seized wide lands on the upper Oka and Desna, following earlier defections of border princes. The grand duchy, ruled by the Jagiellonian Alexander, remained huge, but its eastern frontier was now under constant pressure.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1514": "Muscovy captured Smolensk, a major loss, although Konstanty Ostrogski defeated a larger Muscovite army at Orsha the same year. The grand duchy held most of its territory and in 1529 issued the First Statute of Lithuania, but it could no longer match Moscow alone.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1569": "Threatened by Muscovy in the Livonian War, the grand duchy accepted the Union of Lublin and entered the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. It lost Podlasie, Volhynia and its Ukrainian lands to the Polish Crown but kept its own army, treasury, offices and law within the new union.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1579": "King Stephen Báthory recovered Polotsk from Ivan IV and founded the Jesuit academy that became Vilnius University. Within the Commonwealth the grand duchy enjoyed relative security and cultural growth, and in 1588 it issued the Third Statute of Lithuania, its definitive legal code.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1600": "The grand duchy was prosperous from grain and timber exports and confident within the Commonwealth, which would soon intervene in Muscovy\u0027s Time of Troubles. The Union of Brest of 1596 had split the Orthodox population, however, sowing religious tensions that later fed Cossack revolts.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1655": "Russian armies overran most of the grand duchy and took Vilnius, while Janusz Radziwiłł signed the Union of Kėdainiai placing Lithuania under Swedish protection. Invasion, epidemics and famine during the Deluge killed a large share of the population and devastated the towns.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1667": "The Truce of Andrusovo ended the war with Russia, confirming the recovery of Vilnius, retaken in 1661, and returning Polotsk and Vitebsk, but ceding Smolensk, while the Commonwealth also lost left-bank Ukraine. The grand duchy survived, impoverished and depopulated, its politics increasingly dominated by magnate families such as the Sapiehas.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1772": "The First Partition gave Russia the grand duchy\u0027s eastern lands around Polotsk, Vitebsk and Mogilev. Weakened by the Great Northern War, magnate feuds and Russian interference in royal elections, the grand duchy, like the whole Commonwealth, had become in effect a Russian protectorate.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1793": "After Russia crushed the reforms of the Constitution of 3 May 1791, the Second Partition took Minsk and the central Belarusian lands. The grand duchy was reduced to its Lithuanian core and the west of present-day Belarus, unable to resist further dismemberment.",
  "Grand Duchy of Lithuania|1795": "After the defeat of the Kościuszko Uprising, the Third Partition divided the remaining lands of the grand duchy between Russia and Prussia, with almost all going to Russia. The Grand Duchy of Lithuania ceased to exist after more than five and a half centuries.",
  "Reval|1248": "Eric IV of Denmark granted Lübeck law to the merchant settlement below the Danish castle on Toompea. The young town gained its own council and courts but remained small and dependent on the Danish crown, which ruled northern Estonia through a captain and its vassal nobility.",
  "Reval|1285": "By the late thirteenth century Reval had joined the network of German trading towns that became the Hanseatic League, its merchants trading with Novgorod and Lübeck. It was walling its lower town and gathering privileges from Danish kings, though it remained one of the smaller Hanseatic ports.",
  "Reval|1343": "The St George\u0027s Night Uprising saw Estonian peasants kill many Germans in the countryside and besiege Reval. The town held out until the Livonian Order crushed the rebels, but the crisis exposed its vulnerability and the weakness of distant Danish rule over northern Estonia.",
  "Reval|1346": "Valdemar IV of Denmark sold northern Estonia, including Reval, to the Teutonic Order, which entrusted it to its Livonian branch. The town kept its privileges under a stronger and closer overlord, and its place in the Livonian group of Hanseatic towns grew in importance.",
  "Reval|1370": "The Peace of Stralsund marked the Hanseatic League\u0027s victory over Denmark and the height of its power in the Baltic. Reval shared in the League\u0027s commercial dominance, carrying furs and wax from Novgorod westwards and salt and cloth eastwards through the Gulf of Finland.",
  "Reval|1404": "Completion of the Gothic town hall on the market square reflected the council\u0027s wealth and confidence. Reval\u0027s merchants were by now among the leading intermediaries in the Russian trade, and a new hall for the Great Guild was built in the following decade.",
  "Reval|1475": "Construction of the artillery tower Kiek in de Kök signalled a town wealthy enough to modernise its defences. In the later fifteenth century Reval was at its height, with prosperous guilds, rich church commissions from artists such as Bernt Notke and a firm hold on the Russian transit trade.",
  "Reval|1494": "Ivan III closed the Hanseatic Peterhof at Novgorod and arrested its merchants, a severe shock to the trade that sustained Reval. The Livonian towns adapted by dealing with Russian merchants in their own markets, but commercial uncertainty and the threat from Moscow grew.",
  "Reval|1503": "Livonia concluded a truce with Moscow after Master Wolter von Plettenberg\u0027s campaigns, including the Battle of Lake Smolino in 1502. A long period of peace followed, in which Reval\u0027s Russian trade recovered and the great artillery tower Fat Margaret rose beside the harbour gate.",
  "Reval|1524": "Lutheran preaching and iconoclastic riots brought the Reformation to Reval, and the council took control of church affairs. The town remained prosperous, but the religious break widened the gap between the Lutheran burghers and their Catholic overlord, the Livonian Order.",
  "Reval|1558": "Ivan IV invaded Livonia, opening the Livonian War. The Russian capture of Narva and Dorpat diverted Russian trade to Narva and cut Reval off from much of its hinterland, while the Livonian Order proved unable to defend its towns and countryside.",
  "Reval|1561": "With the Livonian Confederation collapsing, Reval and the knighthood of Harria and Vironia submitted to Eric XIV of Sweden in exchange for confirmation of their privileges. The town became the capital of Swedish Estonia, ending its existence as a self-governing Hanseatic town of Livonia.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1263": "Traditionally, Alexander Nevsky\u0027s youngest son Daniel received Moscow on his father\u0027s death in 1263, while still a child. The town was a minor stronghold on the Moskva River within Vladimir-Suzdal, itself a tributary of the Golden Horde, and its prince ranked well below the rulers of Tver, Vladimir and Rostov.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1301": "Daniel seized Kolomna from the Principality of Ryazan around 1301 and in 1302 inherited Pereslavl-Zalessky, roughly doubling his small territory and gaining control of the Moskva River down to the Oka. Moscow was now a respectable principality, but it still lacked the grand princely title held by its rivals in Tver.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1331": "After helping the Horde crush the Tver uprising of 1327, Ivan I Kalita became sole grand prince of Vladimir in 1331. He collected the Tatar tribute from other princes, bought up villages and towns, and gained prestige from the metropolitan\u0027s residence in Moscow, making his house the leading dynasty of north-eastern Rus\u0027.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1380": "Dmitry Donskoy, behind the new white-stone walls of the Kremlin, had resisted Lithuanian sieges and outmanoeuvred Tver. On 8 September 1380 he led a coalition of Rus\u0027 princes to victory over Mamai at the Battle of Kulikovo, the first major Rus\u0027 victory over a Tatar army and a great boost to Moscow\u0027s standing.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1382": "Khan Tokhtamysh, having reunited the Golden Horde, marched on Moscow in 1382, took the city by deceit and burned it, killing many inhabitants. Dmitry Donskoy had to resume paying tribute, and the disaster showed that Kulikovo had not ended Tatar overlordship. Moscow\u0027s power fell back sharply, though its institutions survived.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1392": "Dmitry Donskoy\u0027s will of 1389 passed the grand principality to Vasily I as family property, and in 1392 Vasily obtained Nizhny Novgorod from Tokhtamysh, gaining a key position on the Volga. Moscow had recovered from the sack of 1382 and was again clearly the strongest of the Rus\u0027 principalities under Tatar suzerainty.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1408": "The emir Edigu, the real power in the Horde, invaded in 1408 to punish Vasily I for withholding tribute, ravaging the countryside and besieging Moscow for weeks before accepting a ransom. Pressure from Lithuania under Vytautas in the west added to Moscow\u0027s difficulties, and its expansion paused.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1425": "Vasily I\u0027s death in 1425 left his ten-year-old son Vasily II on the throne. His uncle Yury of Zvenigorod claimed the succession by the old rule of seniority among brothers, opening a dynastic civil war. The state remained large, but its resources were increasingly divided between rival branches of the dynasty.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1446": "The low point of the civil war. In 1445 Vasily II was captured by the Tatars of Ulugh Muhammad and released only for a heavy ransom; in 1446 his cousin Dmitry Shemyaka seized and blinded him. Moscow was divided, impoverished and exposed to Tatar raids, while Novgorod and Tver acted independently.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1453": "Vasily II, backed by the church and most boyars, regained Moscow and drove Dmitry Shemyaka into exile, where he died in 1453. Collateral claims ended, the grand prince\u0027s sons received most of the land, and a Russian metropolitan chosen in 1448 gave Moscow ecclesiastical independence. Recovery was well under way.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1471": "Ivan III, ruling since 1462, had absorbed Yaroslavl and part of Rostov. When Novgorod sought Lithuanian protection, his army routed the Novgorodians at the Battle of the Shelon River in 1471 and imposed the Treaty of Korostyn. Moscow now dominated nearly all the Russian lands outside Lithuanian rule.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1478": "Ivan III annexed the Novgorod Republic outright in 1478, abolishing its assembly and removing its veche bell to Moscow. The vast northern territories and fur trade of Novgorod passed to the grand prince, and confiscated estates were redistributed as service lands, greatly increasing the resources at his disposal.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1480": "In the Great Stand on the Ugra River, Ivan III faced Akhmat Khan of the Great Horde, who withdrew without a battle in November 1480. The episode is traditionally taken as the end of Tatar overlordship over the Russian lands, leaving Muscovy a fully sovereign state with growing military strength.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1485": "Ivan III annexed the Grand Principality of Tver, Moscow\u0027s oldest rival, in 1485, while Italian engineers rebuilt the Kremlin in brick. In 1487 his troops took Kazan and installed a client khan. Muscovy was now the dominant power of north-eastern Europe, though Lithuania still held the western Rus\u0027 lands.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1503": "The war with Lithuania of 1500–1503, opened by the victory at the Battle of Vedrosha, won the Chernigov and Seversk lands, roughly a third of Lithuania\u0027s eastern territory. With the Sudebnik of 1497, service landholding and a centralised court, Ivan III left his son a much larger and stronger state.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1514": "Vasily III ended Pskov\u0027s independence in 1510 and captured Smolensk in 1514 after several sieges, a major strategic gain. The same year a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated his forces at the Battle of Orsha, halting further advance westwards, but Smolensk remained in Muscovite hands.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1521": "With the absorption of the last Ryazan principality around 1521, Vasily III completed the gathering of the north-eastern Russian lands. Muscovy reached its greatest extent and cohesion as a grand duchy, even though a Crimean raid that year reached the outskirts of Moscow and Kazan slipped out of its control.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1533": "Vasily III died in 1533, leaving the three-year-old Ivan IV. The regency of Elena Glinskaya kept order and reformed the coinage, but after her death in 1538 rival boyar clans struggled for control of the court. Administration continued to function, yet central direction weakened for over a decade.",
  "Grand Duchy of Moscow|1547": "On 16 January 1547 Ivan IV was crowned tsar in the Cathedral of the Dormition. The grand duchy, still at near-peak strength, ended as a political form and became the Tsardom of Russia, which went on to conquer Kazan and Astrakhan and eventually grew into the Russian Empire.",
  "Andorra|1278": "Bishop Pere d\u0027Urtx of Urgell and Count Roger-Bernard III of Foix signed the first Pareatge, ending decades of conflict by agreeing to share lordship over the Andorran valleys. Andorra was a poor pastoral community of a few thousand people, protected mainly by the balance between its two lords.",
  "Andorra|1288": "A second Pareatge settled questions left open in 1278 about the respective rights of the two lords over justice, tribute and service. The co-principality\u0027s basic legal order was now fixed, giving the valleys a stable, if subordinate, existence between the County of Foix and the Catalan lands of the Crown of Aragon.",
  "Andorra|1419": "The co-princes allowed the Andorrans to form the Consell de la Terra, an assembly of representatives from the parishes. This gave the valleys their own institution for common affairs and the beginnings of self-government, which strengthened their identity while sovereignty remained with the bishop and the counts of Foix.",
  "Andorra|1607": "Henry IV of France, who had inherited the Foix rights through the kings of Navarre, joined the co-princedom to the French crown by edict. Andorra now shared lordship between a Catalan bishop and the king of France, a combination that discouraged either Spain or France from absorbing it.",
  "Andorra|1793": "Revolutionary France refused the Andorran tribute, rejecting the French co-sovereignty as a feudal relic. For more than a decade the bishop of Urgell was the sole co-prince, leaving the valleys exposed to Spanish influence and without the balance between two protectors on which their autonomy had rested.",
  "Andorra|1806": "At the Andorrans\u0027 request, Napoleon restored the French co-princedom by decree in 1806. The traditional balance returned, and the valleys largely stayed outside the Peninsular War that devastated Catalonia. Andorra remained poor and isolated, living from herding, iron forges, tobacco and smuggling.",
  "Andorra|1866": "The Nova Reforma extended the vote for the Consell General to all heads of household, a first step in modernising the medieval constitution. Andorra was still remote and impoverished, with no paved road links, but its institutions were becoming more representative of the population.",
  "Andorra|1933": "A political crisis over voting rights led the French co-prince to send gendarmes into the valleys, and universal male suffrage followed. In 1934 the adventurer Boris Skossyreff briefly proclaimed himself king. Andorra\u0027s neutrality and institutions survived, but the episodes showed how fragile its position was.",
  "Andorra|1950": "After the Second World War, new roads, duty-free trade and the beginnings of tourism started to transform the economy. Andorra had kept out of both the Spanish Civil War and the world war, and its low taxes now began to attract shoppers and businesses from Spain and France.",
  "Andorra|1970": "Women gained the vote in 1970, while ski resorts, retail trade and banking brought rapid growth and immigration. The valleys, once among the poorest corners of the Pyrenees, were becoming a prosperous service economy, though their medieval constitution still left power largely with the co-princes.",
  "Andorra|1982": "Reforms of 1981 created an executive council, and in 1982 Òscar Ribas Reig became the first head of government. For the first time Andorra had a government separate from the Consell General and the co-princes\u0027 representatives, an important step towards full statehood.",
  "Andorra|1993": "The constitution approved by referendum on 14 March 1993 made Andorra a sovereign parliamentary co-principality. It joined the United Nations in July 1993 and the Council of Europe in 1994, gaining full international recognition while keeping its two co-princes as constitutional heads of state.",
  "Andorra|2011": "Andorra signed a monetary agreement with the European Union, making the euro its official currency, and began reforms to its tax system and banking secrecy under international pressure. These changes ended its old role as a tax haven but secured its place in European economic arrangements.",
  "Andorra|2025": "Andorra is a prosperous microstate of around 80,000 people, with a modern parliamentary system, an economy based on tourism, retail and finance, and close ties to the European Union. Its international standing and wealth are the highest in its history, while the co-princes still embody its medieval origins.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1282": "The Sicilian Vespers drove Charles of Anjou from Sicily, which passed to Peter III of Aragon, leaving him only the mainland. Charles still ruled a large, populous realm from Naples with papal and French backing, but the costly War of the Sicilian Vespers began twenty years of fighting.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1309": "Robert the Wise succeeded his father Charles II. The Peace of Caltabellotta of 1302 had ended the war with Sicily, and the kingdom, closely allied with the papacy, was recovering. Robert would become the leading Guelf prince of Italy and the main opponent of the emperor Henry VII.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1330": "Under Robert the Wise Naples was the strongest power in Italy, lord of Genoa and other cities, protector of the Guelf cause and a brilliant cultural centre where Giotto worked. Royal administration was effective, though repeated attempts to reconquer Sicily drained the treasury.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1343": "Robert\u0027s death left the throne to his young granddaughter Joanna I. Rivalries between branches of the Angevin family and the claims of her husband Andrew of Hungary soon undermined royal authority, and the kingdom\u0027s leading role in Italian politics began to fade.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1348": "After Andrew of Hungary was murdered in 1345, his brother Louis I of Hungary invaded to avenge him and Joanna fled to Provence. The Black Death struck the same year. The kingdom was divided, devastated and briefly occupied, and its power fell sharply.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1382": "Joanna I, who had backed the Avignon pope in the Western Schism, was deposed and killed by her cousin Charles of Durazzo. A long struggle followed between the Durazzo line and the Angevins of Provence, and civil war left the kingdom weak and disputed.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1408": "King Ladislaus, having defeated his Angevin rivals, built a strong army of condottieri, occupied Rome in 1408 and threatened Florence and central Italy. For a few years Naples was again the most aggressive power in the peninsula, until his sudden death in 1414.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1435": "The death of Joanna II without children opened a war of succession between René of Anjou and Alfonso V of Aragon. In 1435 Alfonso was defeated and captured by the Genoese at the Battle of Ponza, and the kingdom was divided between rival factions.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1442": "Alfonso V of Aragon captured Naples in 1442 and made it the capital of his Mediterranean empire. He reformed finances and justice, founded the Sacro Regio Consiglio and brought the barons to heel, restoring royal authority after decades of disorder.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1455": "Naples joined the Italian League that followed the Peace of Lodi, as one of the five great powers of Italy. Alfonso\u0027s court was a leading centre of humanism, his fleet and treasury were strong, and the kingdom stood at the height of its independent power and prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1480": "Ferrante, Alfonso\u0027s son, ruled effectively but faced the Ottoman capture of Otranto in 1480, recovered only in 1481, and growing baronial discontent that erupted in the Conspiracy of the Barons of 1485–1486. Naples remained a major Italian power, though under strain.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1495": "Charles VIII of France invaded and entered Naples in February 1495, beginning the Italian Wars. Although the French soon withdrew and the Aragonese dynasty returned, the kingdom had been shown to be vulnerable, and its independence was now at the mercy of France and Spain.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1504": "After Spanish victories at Cerignola and the Garigliano in 1503, the Treaty of Lyon ended the war, leaving Ferdinand II of Aragon king of Naples. The kingdom became a Spanish dominion ruled by viceroys, losing its independent foreign policy while keeping its own laws and institutions.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1532": "Pedro de Toledo became viceroy and over two decades strengthened royal justice against the barons, rebuilt the capital and fortified the coasts against Ottoman and Barbary raids. Within the Spanish monarchy the kingdom was well governed, populous and a valued source of revenue and soldiers.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1600": "Naples was one of Europe\u0027s largest cities and the kingdom a pillar of the Spanish Habsburg system, supplying troops, galleys and taxes. Baroque culture flourished, but growing fiscal pressure, debt and the power of the barons over the countryside were storing up tensions.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1647": "A new tax on fruit sparked the revolt of Masaniello in July 1647, and in October rebels proclaimed a Neapolitan Republic under Henry II of Guise. Spanish authority was restored in 1648, but the revolt exposed deep social and fiscal strains in the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1656": "The plague of 1656 killed around half the population of Naples and devastated the provinces. Combined with heavy taxation and economic stagnation, the epidemic left the kingdom much weakened during the last decades of Spanish rule, and the capital took generations to recover its former population.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1707": "During the War of the Spanish Succession, Austrian troops occupied Naples in 1707, and the peace settlements of 1713–1714 gave the kingdom to Emperor Charles VI. It remained a dependent province of a distant monarchy, governed by Austrian viceroys and taxed for imperial wars, with little independent weight.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1734": "Charles of Bourbon conquered the kingdom, defeating the Austrians at the Battle of Bitonto in 1734, and made Naples an independent monarchy again. He reformed government and built the Teatro di San Carlo and the palace of Caserta, restoring the kingdom\u0027s standing in Italy.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1799": "After Ferdinand IV\u0027s failed attack on the French in Rome, the royal family fled to Sicily and the Parthenopean Republic was proclaimed in Naples. Cardinal Ruffo\u0027s Sanfedisti restored the king within months, but the repression that followed deeply divided the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1806": "Napoleon\u0027s armies drove Ferdinand IV to Sicily, and the mainland became a French satellite under Joseph Bonaparte and then Joachim Murat. The Bourbon Kingdom of Naples survived only as a claim, kept alive by the British fleet and army that protected the king\u0027s Sicilian refuge.",
  "Kingdom of Naples|1816": "After Murat\u0027s defeat and the Congress of Vienna, Ferdinand returned to Naples and by a law of 8 December 1816 merged his realms into the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies, reigning as Ferdinand I. The separate Kingdom of Naples ceased to exist.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1291": "Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden concluded the Federal Charter in early August 1291, a defensive pact against outside lords, above all the Habsburgs. The alliance was small and rural, with no common institutions, and its members still recognised the authority of the Holy Roman Empire.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1315": "The three communities ambushed and defeated a Habsburg army under Duke Leopold I at the Battle of Morgarten, then renewed their alliance at Brunnen. The victory secured their autonomy and made the league attractive to neighbouring towns seeking protection against the Habsburgs.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1353": "Lucerne (1332), Zürich (1351), Glarus and Zug (1352) and Bern (1353) had joined through separate treaties, creating the league of eight places. The addition of powerful cities with their own territories and trade gave the Confederacy far greater resources and weight in the region.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1386": "At the Battle of Sempach the Confederates defeated and killed Duke Leopold III of Austria, and at Näfels in 1388 Glarus routed another Habsburg army. Habsburg power in the region was broken, and the Sempach Charter of 1393 gave the allies their first common military rules.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1415": "When Duke Frederick IV of Austria was outlawed after the Council of Constance, the Confederates conquered the Aargau, the Habsburgs\u0027 ancestral lands, and ruled it jointly. The league was now a significant territorial power between the Alps and the Rhine.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1444": "The Old Zürich War of 1440–1446, in which Zürich allied with the Habsburgs against the other cantons, nearly broke the Confederacy. At St. Jakob an der Birs in 1444 a small Swiss force was annihilated by French Armagnac troops, though it won lasting renown for its resistance.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1477": "Victories over Charles the Bold of Burgundy at Grandson and Murten in 1476 and Nancy in 1477, where he was killed, made Swiss infantry the most feared in Europe. Booty and foreign subsidies flowed in, and in 1481 Fribourg and Solothurn joined the league.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1499": "The Swabian War against Maximilian I and the Swabian League ended in Swiss victory. The Peace of Basel effectively freed the Confederates from imperial courts and taxes, and Basel and Schaffhausen joined in 1501, extending the league to the Rhine.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1513": "With Appenzell\u0027s admission the Confederacy reached thirteen members. Swiss troops controlled the Duchy of Milan and defeated the French at the Battle of Novara. For a brief moment the Confederates acted as a great power in Italian and European politics.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1515": "Francis I of France defeated the Swiss at the Battle of Marignano, ending their ambitions in Italy. The Perpetual Peace of 1516 left them the Italian-speaking bailiwicks south of the Alps and made them France\u0027s main mercenary suppliers, but the Confederacy now turned inwards.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1531": "The Reformation split the cantons. In the Second Kappel War of 1531 the Catholic cantons defeated Zürich, killing Huldrych Zwingli. The peace preserved both confessions but left the Confederacy deeply divided, with common action often paralysed by religious rivalry for the next two centuries.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1536": "Bern conquered Vaud from the Duchy of Savoy and helped secure the independence of Geneva, where Calvin began working that year. The Confederacy and its associates now reached their full extent, from Lake Geneva to Lake Constance and south of the Alps.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1648": "Having stayed out of the Thirty Years\u0027 War, the Confederacy obtained formal exemption from the Holy Roman Empire at the Peace of Westphalia, through the diplomacy of Basel\u0027s mayor Johann Rudolf Wettstein. Its independence was now recognised internationally, and its prosperity contrasted with the devastation of neighbouring Germany.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1653": "The Peasants\u0027 War, a revolt of rural subjects in Lucerne, Bern, Solothurn and Basel against taxes and currency devaluation, was crushed by the city governments. Oligarchic rule hardened, its leaders were executed, and the First Villmergen War of 1656 soon renewed confessional conflict between the cantons.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1712": "In the Second Villmergen War Zürich and Bern defeated the Catholic cantons, and the Peace of Aarau shifted the balance towards the Reformed side. The Confederacy remained prosperous and neutral, but its institutions were rigid and dominated by narrow ruling elites.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1792": "Swiss Guards died defending the Tuileries in Paris in August 1792, and revolutionary ideas spread among subjects in Vaud and elsewhere. The cantons, divided and without common institutions or a federal army, were poorly placed to face the threat from revolutionary France.",
  "Old Swiss Confederacy|1798": "French armies invaded; after the Bernese defeat at the Battle of Grauholz on 5 March, Bern surrendered. On 12 April the Helvetic Republic was proclaimed, abolishing cantonal sovereignty and the subject territories and ending the Old Swiss Confederacy after more than five centuries.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1301": "The death of Andrew III ended the Árpád dynasty. Rival claimants, Charles Robert of Anjou, Wenceslaus of Bohemia and later Otto of Bavaria, fought for the crown, while oligarchs such as Matthew Csák ruled their regions as independent princes. Royal power was at a low ebb.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1310": "Charles Robert was crowned with the Holy Crown at Székesfehérvár, ending the dispute over the throne. Much of the kingdom was still controlled by oligarchs, but in 1312 he defeated the sons of Amadeus Aba at the Battle of Rozgony and began reclaiming royal lands.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1321": "The death of Matthew Csák allowed Charles I to occupy his lands in the north-west, completing the defeat of the oligarchs. Royal authority was restored across the kingdom for the first time in decades, and the king moved his court to Visegrád and began reforming finances, coinage and mining.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1342": "Louis I inherited a strong, solvent kingdom from Charles I, whose gold florin and mining reforms had filled the treasury. Hungary was now a respected central European power with dynastic links to Naples and Poland, an obedient baronage and the resources for an ambitious foreign policy.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1358": "After campaigns in Naples and a war with Venice, Louis I won Dalmatia in the Treaty of Zadar. Hungarian suzerainty extended over Bosnia, parts of Serbia and the Romanian principalities, and the kingdom dominated the north-western Balkans and the Adriatic coast from Kvarner to Dubrovnik.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1370": "Louis I also became king of Poland on the death of his uncle Casimir III, ruling one of the largest realms in Europe. Hungary was rich in gold, culturally active with a new university at Pécs, and at the height of its Angevin power.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1382": "Louis died without sons. His daughter Mary inherited Hungary, but Poland passed to her sister Jadwiga, ending the union. Rival factions, the brief usurpation and murder of Charles of Durazzo in 1385–1386 and revolts in the south weakened the crown.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1396": "Sigismund of Luxembourg, Mary\u0027s husband and king from 1387, led a great crusade that was destroyed by the Ottomans at the Battle of Nicopolis. Baronial rebellions followed, and in 1401 the barons even imprisoned the king for some months. The Ottoman threat to the southern frontier became permanent.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1412": "Sigismund had restored control through alliances with leading barons and the Order of the Dragon. Elected king of the Romans in 1411, he became a leading figure in European politics, though Dalmatia was lost to Venice and the Spiš towns were pledged to Poland.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1437": "Sigismund\u0027s death was followed by the short reign of his son-in-law Albert of Habsburg, who died in 1439. A succession struggle between supporters of the infant Ladislaus V and of Vladislaus of Poland began, dividing the barons into armed camps while Ottoman pressure on the south grew.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1444": "King Vladislaus I was killed at the Battle of Varna, and the kingdom was left without an adult ruler. Rival barons controlled different regions, and the Ottomans threatened the frontier. The young Ladislaus V was held by Frederick III of Habsburg.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1456": "John Hunyadi, governor and captain-general, broke the Ottoman siege of Belgrade in July 1456 with the help of John of Capistrano, securing the southern frontier for decades. He died of plague weeks later, leaving a powerful family and a stabilised frontier.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1458": "After the death of Ladislaus V, the diet elected Hunyadi\u0027s son Matthias Corvinus king. He faced opposition from barons and Emperor Frederick III, who held the Holy Crown, but quickly built up royal revenues and a standing army, recovering the crown in 1463 and being crowned in 1464.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1479": "Matthias\u0027s commanders defeated an Ottoman raid at the Battle of Breadfield, and the Peace of Olomouc gave him Moravia, Silesia and Lusatia. Funded by high taxes and served by the Black Army, Hungary was again a leading central European power, able to fight the emperor and the Ottomans at once.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1485": "Matthias captured Vienna and made it one of his residences, ruling Lower Austria as well as Hungary and the Bohemian lands. His Renaissance court and library made Buda famous. This was the peak of the kingdom\u0027s territorial reach and prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1490": "Matthias died without a legitimate heir. The magnates elected the weak Vladislaus II of Bohemia, and the Treaty of Pressburg of 1491 promised the Habsburgs the succession if his line failed. Maximilian of Habsburg recovered Vienna and Lower Austria, and Matthias\u0027s conquests were lost.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1492": "The unpaid Black Army was disbanded in the early 1490s, removing the kingdom\u0027s main professional fighting force. Royal revenues collapsed as the magnates took control of government and cut taxes, and the southern fortress line against the Ottomans began to decay for lack of money.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1514": "A crusade against the Ottomans turned into the peasant revolt of György Dózsa, crushed with great brutality. The diet bound peasants permanently to the land, and Werbőczy\u0027s Tripartitum entrenched noble privilege. The kingdom was deeply divided, its peasantry embittered and its defences neglected.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1521": "Suleiman the Magnificent captured Belgrade and Šabac in 1521, breaking the southern defences. The boy-king Louis II had little money, the nobles were split into factions, and appeals for help from the Habsburgs and other Christian powers brought little practical support.",
  "Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526)|1526": "Suleiman destroyed the Hungarian army at the Battle of Mohács on 29 August, and Louis II drowned in flight. Two rival kings, John Zápolya and Ferdinand I, were elected, and the late medieval kingdom came to an end, soon divided into three parts.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1324": "Pisa ceded its Sardinian territories after the Aragonese victory at Lucocisterna, turning the papal grant of 1297 into a real kingdom within the Crown of Aragon. It held only the south and parts of the north, and depended on its Arborean ally and on reinforcements from Catalonia.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1354": "Peter IV of Aragon came to Sardinia in person, took rebellious Alghero and resettled it with Catalans. The kingdom had expanded from its southern base, but the war with the Judicate of Arborea under Marianus IV had already begun and would tie down Aragonese forces for decades.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1368": "Arborean armies under Marianus IV overran most of the island, leaving the Aragonese crown with little more than the fortified towns of Cagliari and Alghero. The kingdom survived only because it could be supplied by sea from Catalonia, and its authority outside the walls was minimal.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1409": "Martin the Younger, King of Sicily and heir of Aragon, crushed the Arborean army at the Battle of Sanluri. His death from fever soon afterwards slowed the victory, but the Judicate’s power was broken and royal control began to spread across the island again.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1420": "Alfonso V of Aragon bought the remaining Arborean rights from William III of Narbonne, and the whole island, except a few holdouts, came under the crown. The kingdom finally covered Sardinia, organised in fiefs held largely by Catalan and Aragonese nobles under a viceroy.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1478": "Leonardo Alagon, Marquis of Oristano, the last great feudal rebel, was defeated at the Battle of Macomer. Royal authority was now unchallenged, but the island remained a poor, feudal frontier of the Crown of Aragon, valued chiefly for grain, salt and its strategic position.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1516": "With Charles of Habsburg’s succession the kingdom became part of the Spanish monarchy. Viceroys and the Stamenti parliament governed a stable but marginal island, exposed to Barbary raids and defended by a chain of coastal towers rather than by any significant force of its own.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1652": "A severe plague epidemic swept the island in 1652–1656 and killed a large part of the population. Combined with famines and the general difficulties of Spain, it left the kingdom weaker and poorer than at any time since the end of the Arborean wars.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1708": "During the War of the Spanish Succession an Anglo-Austrian fleet took Cagliari, and Sardinia passed to the Habsburg Monarchy. The island was now a bargaining chip between great powers, briefly reconquered by Spain in 1717 before the Quadruple Alliance reassigned it.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1720": "Under the Treaty of The Hague Victor Amadeus II of Savoy exchanged Sicily for Sardinia and took its royal title. The kingdom now combined the island with Piedmont, Savoy and Nice, a well-administered state with a capable army, and its power rose sharply.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1747": "Charles Emmanuel III’s army defeated the French at the Battle of Assietta during the War of the Austrian Succession, and the peace of 1748 brought gains in Lombardy. The Savoyard kingdom had become a respected middle power, its army and fortresses guarding the Alpine gateway to Italy.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1796": "Napoleon’s Italian campaign defeated the Piedmontese army in a few weeks, forcing the Armistice of Cherasco and the cession of Savoy and Nice to France. The kingdom survived only as a weakened client caught between France and Austria, its army beaten and its western provinces lost.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1799": "French pressure drove Charles Emmanuel IV out of Turin, and the court took refuge in Cagliari. For fifteen years the kingdom was reduced to the island, protected by the British navy, while Piedmont was annexed to France. This was its lowest point since the Arborean wars.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1815": "The Congress of Vienna restored Piedmont, Savoy and Nice to Victor Emmanuel I and added the former Republic of Genoa, giving the kingdom a major port. It was now the strongest independent Italian state, intended by the powers as a buffer between France and Austria.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1848": "Charles Albert granted the Statuto Albertino and declared war on Austria during the revolutions of 1848, presenting Piedmont as the champion of Italian independence. The Perfect Fusion of 1847 had unified the kingdom’s institutions, and its prestige among Italian liberals reached a new height.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1849": "Defeat by Radetzky’s Austrian army at Novara forced Charles Albert to abdicate. His son Victor Emmanuel II made peace but kept the constitution, so the kingdom, though beaten, remained the only liberal state in Italy and a refuge for exiles.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1855": "Cavour sent some 15,000 troops to the Crimean War on the side of Britain and France. The contingent fought at the Chernaya, and the kingdom gained a seat at the Congress of Paris in 1856, where Cavour raised the Italian question before the great powers.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1859": "Allied with Napoleon III after the Plombières agreement, the kingdom fought Austria; Franco-Piedmontese victories at Magenta and Solferino won Lombardy. Revolts in the central duchies opened the way for further annexations, and the kingdom stood on the edge of dominating the peninsula.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1860": "Plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, Garibaldi conquered Sicily and Naples, and Piedmontese troops occupied the Marche and Umbria. Despite ceding Savoy and Nice to France, the kingdom now held almost the whole peninsula except Rome and Venetia: its absolute peak.",
  "Kingdom of Sardinia|1861": "On 17 March the parliament in Turin proclaimed Victor Emmanuel II King of Italy. The Kingdom of Sardinia ended as a name rather than as a state: its constitution, army, administration and dynasty continued in the new Kingdom of Italy.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1346": "According to tradition, Dragoș came from Maramureș around this time as a Hungarian vassal to guard the eastern Carpathian passes against the Golden Horde. The march on the Moldova River was a small frontier lordship, dependent on Hungary, with no independent institutions of its own.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1359": "Bogdan I, a rebel Maramureș noble, drove out Dragoș’s heirs and defeated Hungarian efforts to restore control. Moldavia became an independent principality, though still small, thinly settled and centred on the upper valleys east of the Carpathians, with Siret and later Suceava as its main seats.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1387": "Petru I Mușat did homage to Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland at Lviv, balancing Hungarian pressure. He minted coins, moved the court to Suceava and built stone fortresses, and the principality now extended towards the Dniester and the Black Sea, with a clearer administration around the prince.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1400": "Alexander the Good began a reign of over thirty years that gave Moldavia stable institutions, a recognised Orthodox metropolis from 1401 and control of the ports of Chilia and Cetatea Albă. The principality was prosperous from Black Sea transit trade and respected by Poland and Hungary.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1432": "Alexander’s death opened a quarter-century of civil war among his sons and grandsons, with rivals backed by Poland and Hungary. Princes followed one another rapidly, the treasury was drained and the principality lost much of the authority it had built under Alexander, though it kept its territory.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1456": "Petru Aron agreed to pay tribute to Sultan Mehmed II, three years after the fall of Constantinople. The principality’s first submission to the Ottomans reflected its weakness after decades of internal conflict, with a divided boyar class and a rapidly changing throne.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1457": "Stephen, grandson of Alexander the Good, took the throne with Wallachian help. He began to rebuild princely authority over the boyars, restore the fortresses and organise the peasant levy, laying the foundations for the military successes of the following decades.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1465": "Stephen captured the Danube port of Chilia, and two years later defeated the Hungarian king Matthias Corvinus at Baia. Moldavia now held both its Black Sea ports and had shown it could resist its larger neighbours, its power rising rapidly.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1475": "At the Battle of Vaslui Stephen destroyed a large Ottoman army under Hadım Suleiman Pasha, one of the most notable Christian victories over the Ottomans of the century. Moldavia stood at the peak of its military reputation and political independence.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1484": "Sultan Bayezid II captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the Black Sea and its most profitable transit trade. Stephen kept his throne and his inland fortresses, but the principality had lost its coast for good, and with it much of its revenue.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1497": "Stephen defeated the Polish king John I Albert in the Cosmin Forest, ending Polish attempts to control Moldavia. The aged prince still commanded respect, but he accepted a settlement with the Ottomans that preserved the principality’s autonomy in exchange for tribute.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1504": "Stephen the Great died after a reign of 47 years, leaving a strong state, a network of fortresses and traditionally some forty churches and monasteries. His son Bogdan III kept the peace with the Ottomans, accepting tribute as the price of autonomy.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1538": "Suleiman the Magnificent invaded, deposed Petru Rareș and annexed Tighina and the southern steppe. From now on princes were confirmed and often chosen by the Porte, and Moldavia was firmly an Ottoman tributary with limited room for independent policy, though it kept its own laws and Church.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1564": "Alexandru Lăpușneanu moved the capital from Suceava to Iași, nearer the main trade routes and the Ottoman frontier. The principality remained autonomous in its internal affairs, but it paid rising tribute and was racked by struggles among boyar factions and rival claimants backed by Istanbul or Poland.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1600": "Michael the Brave of Wallachia occupied Moldavia and briefly ruled all three Romanian lands. His rule collapsed within months, and the principality was contested among Polish-backed Movilă princes, Ottoman candidates and Transylvanian interests, its autonomy and finances at a low ebb after years of war.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1634": "Vasile Lupu began a twenty-year reign of relative prosperity and cultural ambition. He built the Three Hierarchs Church at Iași, set up a printing press and issued a printed Romanian law code in 1646, and his court was known for its wealth.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1653": "Vasile Lupu was overthrown by a coalition of Wallachian and Transylvanian forces, and Moldavia became caught up in the wars of the Cossacks, Poles and Ottomans. Repeated invasions, Tatar raids and rapid changes of prince left it impoverished and weakened for decades.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1711": "Dimitrie Cantemir’s alliance with Peter the Great ended in Russian defeat on the Pruth. The Ottomans responded by appointing Phanariot Greek princes, ending the election of native rulers and reducing Moldavia to a closely controlled tributary whose princes often held office for only a few years.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1775": "Austria annexed the north-western part of Moldavia, including Suceava and Putna, which became Bukovina. The loss of the old heartland and its monasteries showed how little the Phanariot principality could defend itself amid the wars of Russia, Austria and the Ottoman Empire.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1812": "By the Treaty of Bucharest Russia annexed the land between the Pruth and the Dniester, nearly half the principality, as Bessarabia. Moldavia was reduced to its western part, between the Carpathians and the Pruth, weaker and smaller than at any time since its foundation.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1832": "The Organic Regulation, introduced under Russian protection after the Treaty of Adrianople, gave Moldavia a modern administration, budget and assembly. Native princes had returned after 1821, trade opened to the world, and Iași grew as a political and cultural centre.",
  "Principality of Moldavia|1859": "After the Treaty of Paris returned southern Bessarabia, both Moldavia and Wallachia elected Alexandru Ioan Cuza as prince. The personal union ended Moldavia as a separate principality and began the modern Romanian state, formally united under the name Romania in 1862, with its capital at Bucharest.",
  "Hanseatic League|1356": "Envoys of the towns met in Lübeck in the first general Hanseatic diet, called to deal with difficulties at the Bruges office. The merchant association was turning into a league of towns, already dominant in Baltic trade but still loosely organised and without any common institutions.",
  "Hanseatic League|1360": "A Hanseatic trade embargo against Flanders, begun in 1358, forced Bruges and the Count of Flanders to restore and extend the German merchants’ privileges. The success showed the effectiveness of collective economic pressure and strengthened the towns’ habit of acting together through the diet.",
  "Hanseatic League|1362": "Valdemar IV of Denmark had sacked Visby in 1361, and in 1362 he defeated a Hanseatic fleet at Helsingborg. The towns had to accept an unfavourable truce, and Danish control of the Sound and the Scanian herring markets threatened the heart of their trade.",
  "Hanseatic League|1367": "Meeting in Cologne, the towns formed the Confederation of Cologne with Holstein, Mecklenburg and Swedish allies against Denmark. They agreed to raise ships, men and a special toll to fund the war, the most ambitious common action the Hansa ever undertook.",
  "Hanseatic League|1370": "After the confederates had ravaged the Danish coasts and taken Copenhagen, the Peace of Stralsund gave the towns the Scanian fortresses for fifteen years, confirmed their trading privileges and a say in the Danish succession. It was the high point of Hanseatic power.",
  "Hanseatic League|1401": "The Victual Brothers, pirates who had plagued Baltic and North Sea shipping, were broken, and their leader Klaus Störtebeker was executed in Hamburg. The Hansa still dominated northern trade, with its four great Kontore at Novgorod, Bergen, Bruges and London in full operation.",
  "Hanseatic League|1441": "A naval war with Holland ended in the Peace of Copenhagen, which effectively admitted Dutch merchants to the Baltic. Dutch competition, together with internal disputes and the growing strength of territorial princes, began to erode the Hansa’s position, though its trade remained very large.",
  "Hanseatic League|1474": "The Treaty of Utrecht ended the Anglo-Hanseatic War on favourable terms, confirming the Hansa’s privileges in England and giving it possession of the London Steelyard. This late success showed that the league could still use war and embargo effectively against a kingdom.",
  "Hanseatic League|1494": "Ivan III of Moscow closed the Hanseatic office at Novgorod and arrested its merchants, cutting off one of the four great Kontore. The rise of strong territorial monarchies around the Baltic was beginning to undermine the privileges on which the Hansa’s trade rested.",
  "Hanseatic League|1523": "With Lübeck’s ships and money, Gustav Vasa drove out the Danes and became King of Sweden, and Christian II of Denmark was deposed. Lübeck seemed able to make kings, but Gustav soon reduced the privileges he had granted it, showing the limits of this influence.",
  "Hanseatic League|1536": "Lübeck’s intervention in the Danish civil war known as the Count’s Feud, led by the burgomaster Jürgen Wullenwever, ended in defeat. The failure destroyed Lübeck’s political influence in Scandinavia and left the Hansa unable to shape the policy of the northern kingdoms.",
  "Hanseatic League|1570": "The Peace of Stettin ended the Northern Seven Years’ War, in which Lübeck had fought on Denmark’s side against Sweden. The Hansa gained little, Dutch shipping now dominated the Sound, and the Livonian towns had been lost in the wars over Livonia after 1558.",
  "Hanseatic League|1598": "Elizabeth I of England revoked the privileges of the London Steelyard and expelled the Hanseatic merchants. The loss of the oldest western office symbolised the league’s decline, as fewer towns attended diets and many pursued their own interests without regard for the others.",
  "Hanseatic League|1648": "The Thirty Years’ War had devastated much of northern Germany, and the Peace of Westphalia confirmed Swedish control over several German Baltic ports. The Hansa survived only as a loose understanding among Lübeck, Hamburg, Bremen and a few others, without any real collective power.",
  "Hanseatic League|1669": "The last Hanseatic diet met in Lübeck, attended by only a handful of towns, and failed to agree on a renewal of the league. No further diet was summoned. Lübeck, Hamburg and Bremen kept a looser alliance and the Hanseatic name into modern times.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1356": "The Golden Bull confirmed Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg as one of the seven prince-electors and Arch-Marshal of the Empire. The title was great, but the Ascanian territory around Wittenberg was small and poor, giving the electorate high rank and little real power.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1423": "Emperor Sigismund granted the vacant electorate to Frederick the Warlike, Margrave of Meissen, rewarding his help against the Hussites. The Wettin dynasty joined the electoral dignity to its large and silver-rich lands of Meissen, creating a far stronger state in central Germany.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1485": "In the Division of Leipzig the brothers Ernest and Albert split the Wettin lands. The Ernestine electorate kept the title, Wittenberg and most of Thuringia, while the Albertines took Meissen, Dresden and Leipzig, leaving each half weaker than the whole.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1517": "Under Frederick the Wise, rich from the Erzgebirge silver boom, Wittenberg had a new university, and there Martin Luther published his theses against indulgences. Frederick’s protection of Luther made the electorate the political centre of the Reformation and a leading voice among the princes.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1547": "Emperor Charles V defeated and captured John Frederick I at the Battle of Mühlberg. The Capitulation of Wittenberg transferred the electoral title and much territory to Maurice of the Albertine line, so the electorate passed to a new branch rather than losing its strength.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1552": "Maurice turned against Charles V in the Princes’ Revolt and forced the Peace of Passau, securing Protestant interests. Holding the electorate with the rich Albertine lands and Dresden as capital, Saxony was now the strongest Protestant principality of the Empire.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1572": "Elector Augustus issued the Saxon Constitutions, reorganised the finances and administration and built up Dresden’s collections. Saxony was rich, well governed and at peace, the leading Lutheran power in the Empire and a mediator between the emperor and the Protestant princes.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1631": "John George I joined Sweden after Tilly’s troops invaded Saxony, but the Saxon army fled at the Battle of Breitenfeld. The Thirty Years’ War brought repeated invasions and plunder, and the electorate was too weak to pursue an independent course.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1635": "By the Peace of Prague with Emperor Ferdinand II, John George I gained Upper and Lower Lusatia, the largest territorial gain in Saxony’s history. The war continued, however, and Swedish armies ravaged the country in revenge for its change of sides.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia confirmed Saxony’s possession of the Lusatias, but the war had cost it a large part of its population and wealth. Soon afterwards John George I’s will created junior duchies for his younger sons, weakening the electorate further.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1697": "Augustus the Strong converted to Catholicism and was elected King of Poland, uniting the electorate with the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. The royal crown raised Saxony’s prestige to its height and opened ambitious plans, but it also drew the electorate into costly eastern wars.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1706": "Charles XII of Sweden invaded Saxony and forced Augustus, by the Treaty of Altranstädt, to renounce the Polish crown and pay heavy contributions. The Swedish army lived off the country for a year, and Saxony’s prestige and finances suffered a severe blow.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1709": "After Peter the Great destroyed the Swedish army at Poltava, Augustus returned to Poland and regained his throne. Saxony recovered its position, and the following decades brought the building of the Zwinger and the founding of the Meissen porcelain manufactory.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1730": "Augustus the Strong displayed his army and court at the great camp of Zeithain, attended by Frederick William I of Prussia. Dresden was a Baroque capital, Meissen porcelain was famous, and Saxony stood at the peak of its wealth, culture and dynastic prestige.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1745": "In the Second Silesian War Prussia defeated the Saxon and Austrian armies at Kesselsdorf and occupied Dresden. The Peace of Dresden forced Saxony to pay an indemnity, showing that it could no longer match its Prussian neighbour on the battlefield.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1756": "Frederick the Great invaded Saxony without a declaration of war, and the Saxon army capitulated at Pirna. Its soldiers were forced into Prussian service, and the electorate was occupied and exploited for the rest of the Seven Years’ War, its power at a low point.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1763": "The Peace of Hubertusburg restored Saxony, but the war had left it devastated and deeply in debt, and the death of Augustus III ended the Polish union. The Rétablissement began a slow recovery based on sound finances and economic reform.",
  "Electorate of Saxony|1806": "After sharing Prussia’s defeat at Jena, Frederick Augustus III made peace with Napoleon by the Treaty of Posen, joined the Confederation of the Rhine and became King of Saxony. The electorate ended as a respected but secondary German state, soon to lose much territory.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1358": "By the Treaty of Zadar Venice gave up Dalmatia to Louis I of Hungary, and Ragusa became a self-governing republic under loose Hungarian protection. It already controlled Pelješac, Ston, Lastovo and Mljet, and its merchants traded actively in the Serbian and Bosnian interior.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1399": "Ragusa acquired the coastal strip known as the Primorje from the Bosnian king, linking its territories along the coast. Its merchants dominated the export of silver and lead from the Balkan mines, and the republic’s wealth and institutions grew steadily.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1416": "The Great Council voted to ban the slave trade, and Ragusan commerce flourished between the Balkan mines and the Italian ports. The republic was secure under Hungarian protection, independent in practice, and increasingly prosperous from its role as an intermediary between east and west.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1427": "Having bought Konavle from Bosnian nobles in two stages from 1419, the republic gained a fertile hinterland and its final territorial extent from Neum to the Bay of Kotor. With this, Ragusa’s small state was complete, protected by walls and diplomacy.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1458": "Facing the Ottoman advance into the Balkans, Ragusa agreed to pay tribute to Sultan Mehmed II. The arrangement secured its trading privileges in the expanding Ottoman lands, though the loss of independent Balkan states disrupted the mining trade on which its wealth had rested.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1481": "The Ottoman tribute was fixed at 12,500 ducats a year. In return the Ragusans enjoyed low customs duties and freedom of trade across the Ottoman Empire, which allowed the republic to prosper as the main commercial link between the Balkans and Italy.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1526": "After the Hungarian defeat at the Battle of Mohács, Hungarian protection lapsed and the republic relied on Ottoman suzerainty and its own diplomacy. Its merchant fleet was growing rapidly, carrying grain, wool and other goods across the Mediterranean, and its trade with the Ottoman lands was secure.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1570": "Ragusa’s merchant fleet, numbering well over a hundred large ships, was among the biggest in the Mediterranean, and its neutrality allowed trade with both Christian and Ottoman ports. This was the height of the republic’s prosperity, cultural output and international standing.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1600": "The republic remained wealthy, but English and Dutch competition was growing and Balkan trade was changing. Ragusa still enjoyed a large fleet, active diplomacy and a flourishing literature, with writers such as Ivan Gundulić beginning their careers, but its relative position was slowly slipping.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1645": "The long war between Venice and the Ottomans over Crete diverted Balkan trade through Ragusa, bringing a period of renewed prosperity. Its institutions remained stable and its diplomacy skilful, though its maritime trade was smaller than in the sixteenth century.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1667": "A great earthquake destroyed much of the city and killed several thousand people, including the Rector. Venice and Ottoman officials sought to exploit the catastrophe, and the republic’s survival seemed uncertain as it struggled to rebuild with greatly reduced resources.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1699": "The Treaty of Karlowitz created Ottoman corridors at Neum and Sutorina, separating Ragusa from Venetian Dalmatia and securing it against Venetian expansion. Rebuilt in Baroque style after the earthquake, the republic recovered its stability and its trade, though not its earlier wealth.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1790": "Ragusan neutral shipping prospered during the wars of the eighteenth century, and the merchant fleet grew again to a substantial size. The republic was stable, solvent and diplomatically active, though politically weak and dependent on the goodwill of the great powers.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1806": "French troops under General Lauriston occupied the city, and Russian and Montenegrin forces besieged it, devastating its territory. The republic’s neutrality had failed, its shipping was ruined by the British blockade and the Napoleonic wars, and its independence now existed only on paper.",
  "Republic of Ragusa|1808": "On 31 January Marshal Marmont proclaimed the abolition of the republic and dissolved its councils, ending more than four centuries of independence. Its territory was absorbed into the Illyrian Provinces of the French Empire and, after 1815, into Austria; Dubrovnik is today part of Croatia.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1395": "Gian Galeazzo Visconti bought the ducal title from Wenceslas, turning his family’s lordship into a hereditary imperial fief. He already held most of Lombardy, with Verona and Vicenza, and commanded the wealth of Milan and Pavia, making the new duchy the strongest power in northern Italy from its first day.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1402": "Gian Galeazzo’s expansion reached its height with Pisa, Siena, Perugia and Bologna under Milanese control and Florence encircled. His death from plague in September 1402 ended the campaign. The duchy never again controlled so much of Italy, so this year marks the peak of its independent power.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1412": "Gian Galeazzo’s young sons could not hold his conquests: captains and local lords seized the outlying cities, and Duke Giovanni Maria was murdered in 1412. His brother Filippo Maria inherited a reduced and divided state, but the Lombard core around Milan and Pavia survived, leaving a basis for recovery.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1427": "Filippo Maria Visconti had reconquered much of Lombardy and ruled Genoa, but his former captain Carmagnola, now serving Venice, defeated Milan at Maclodio. Brescia and Bergamo passed to Venice, fixing a frontier on the Adda. Milan remained a major power, but no longer one that could dominate Italy.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1447": "Filippo Maria died without a legitimate son, and Milanese citizens proclaimed the Golden Ambrosian Republic. Subject cities broke away, Venice advanced, and the republic depended on Francesco Sforza’s army. Without a ruler and with its territory fragmenting, the state reached one of the lowest points of its independent existence.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1450": "Francesco Sforza, husband of Bianca Maria Visconti and the leading condottiere of his age, starved Milan into submission and entered the city as duke. He reunited most of the Visconti core and restored central government, beginning half a century in which Sforza Milan stood among Italy’s leading powers.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1454": "The Peace of Lodi, followed by the Italic League of 1455, recognised Sforza rule and ended decades of war with Venice. Milan gained stability, alliance with Medici Florence and room to invest in building, irrigation and the court, enjoying a strong and secure position in the Italian state system.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1476": "Galeazzo Maria Sforza, who had run a brilliant and costly court, was assassinated in the church of Santo Stefano. The succession of his young son Gian Galeazzo opened a period of regency struggles, but the duchy’s wealth, army and diplomatic standing remained largely intact for the moment.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1494": "Ludovico Sforza, regent since 1480, became duke. His court, with Leonardo and Bramante, was among the most splendid in Europe, and Milan’s wealth stood at its height. Yet his encouragement of Charles VIII’s invasion of Italy that year opened the wars that would destroy Sforza independence.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1499": "Louis XII of France, claiming Milan through Valentina Visconti, conquered the duchy in weeks. Ludovico fled and was captured at Novara in 1500, dying a prisoner in France. Milan became a French possession, ending its standing as an independent power and turning it into an object of rivalry between foreign kings.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1515": "The Sforza had been restored by Swiss arms in 1512, but Francis I of France crushed the Swiss at Marignano and retook the duchy. Milan was now a battlefield and a pawn, its revenues claimed by occupying armies and its dukes dependent on French, Swiss or imperial protection.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1525": "Imperial and Spanish troops defeated and captured Francis I at Pavia. Francesco II Sforza held the ducal title under Charles V’s protection, but the duchy was devastated by war, billeting and requisitions, and real power lay with the emperor’s commanders. Independent Milan was effectively at an end.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1535": "Francesco II Sforza died without heirs, and Charles V kept the duchy as an imperial fief, later investing his son Philip. Milan became a Habsburg province governed from Madrid. Peace brought some recovery of trade and finances, but the state no longer pursued a policy of its own.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1559": "The Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis confirmed Spanish possession and ended the Italian Wars. Under Spanish governors, the Senate and Archbishop Charles Borromeo, Lombardy recovered; it was the strategic hinge of the Spanish Road and a prosperous province, though subordinate to the wider interests of the monarchy.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1630": "The plague of 1630, which killed perhaps half the city’s inhabitants, together with the War of the Mantuan Succession and Spain’s heavy war taxation, devastated Lombardy. Manufacturing contracted and population fell sharply, leaving the duchy at its weakest point as a province of an overstretched monarchy.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1706": "During the War of the Spanish Succession Austrian forces occupied Milan after Prince Eugene’s victory at Turin. Spanish rule ended and the duchy passed to the Austrian Habsburgs, confirmed at Rastatt in 1714. It remained a rich but dependent province, now losing western districts to Savoy.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1748": "The Peace of Aix-la-Chapelle ended the War of the Austrian Succession, completing the transfer of western districts to Savoy. Reduced Austrian Lombardy entered a long peace, and Maria Theresa’s government began the reforms, including the cadastre completed in 1760, that renewed its agriculture and finances.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1786": "Joseph II’s reforms abolished the old Senate and reorganised Lombard administration on rational lines. Milan was prosperous and intellectually lively, a centre of the Italian Enlightenment, but its institutions were being absorbed into the centralised Habsburg monarchy rather than strengthened as those of a separate state.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1796": "Napoleon Bonaparte defeated the Austrians at Lodi and entered Milan on 15 May. Austrian rule collapsed, French armies requisitioned money and art, and in November the Transpadane Republic replaced the old institutions. The duchy survived only as a legal claim of the Habsburgs.",
  "Duchy of Milan|1797": "The Transpadane Republic merged into the Cisalpine Republic, and in October Austria renounced Lombardy in the Treaty of Campo Formio. The Duchy of Milan ceased to exist after four centuries; when Austria returned in 1815, it ruled Lombardy as part of the Kingdom of Lombardy–Venetia.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1416": "Emperor Sigismund raised Amadeus VIII from count to duke at Chambéry. The new duchy held Savoy, Bresse, the Vaud, Nice and the recently bought Genevois, and controlled key Alpine passes. It was a middling power, respected for its position between France, the Swiss and Italy rather than for its wealth.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1418": "The extinction of the Savoy-Achaea branch brought Piedmont, with Turin, under the main line. The duchy now joined French-speaking lands north of the Alps with a fertile Italian plain, a combination that would define it. Amadeus VIII was among the most respected princes of his time.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1439": "Having retired to Ripaille in 1434, Amadeus VIII was elected antipope as Felix V by the Council of Basel. The duchy’s prestige was high and its territory intact, though the papal adventure cost money and drew the dynasty into the conflicts of the conciliar movement until he resigned in 1449.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1465": "After the death of Duke Louis the duchy entered decades of weak rule, short reigns and regencies. French influence grew at court, factions of nobles competed for power, and the Swiss Confederation pressed on the Vaud. The territory survived, but the state lost cohesion and direction.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1504": "Charles III succeeded to a duchy caught between France and the Habsburgs during the Italian Wars. Savoy’s passes made it strategically vital to both, but its army and finances were too weak to keep it neutral, and Geneva was already slipping from ducal control.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1536": "Francis I of France occupied Savoy and most of Piedmont, Bern conquered the Vaud and Geneva became independent. Duke Charles III kept only a few strongholds such as Nice and Vercelli. For more than twenty years the duchy barely existed as a functioning state.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1559": "Emmanuel Philibert, victor of Saint-Quentin for Spain, recovered most of his lands at Cateau-Cambrésis. He returned to a ravaged duchy, with French and Spanish garrisons still in some fortresses, and began rebuilding its army, finances and administration on absolutist lines.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1563": "Emmanuel Philibert moved the capital from Chambéry to Turin, turning the state’s centre of gravity towards Italy. With a militia, new fortresses and Italian as the administrative language of Piedmont, Savoy became a compact, well-governed principality, though Bern kept the Vaud under the Treaty of Lausanne of 1564.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1601": "Charles Emmanuel I’s Treaty of Lyon secured the Marquisate of Saluzzo in exchange for Bresse and Bugey. Savoy lost its last lands west of the Rhône but gained a compact Italian territory and closed a French gateway into Piedmont, reflecting a strategic shift towards Italy.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1639": "After the deaths of Victor Amadeus I and his eldest son, the regency of Christine of France was challenged by her brothers-in-law, backed by Spain. The Piedmontese Civil War of 1639–1642 brought French and Spanish armies into the duchy, leaving it dependent on French protection.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1660": "Under Charles Emmanuel II, with his mother Christine still influential until her death in 1663, the duchy recovered from civil war. Turin was enlarged and embellished, and the state’s finances improved, but Savoy remained a client of Louis XIV’s France, with a French garrison still at Pinerolo.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1693": "Victor Amadeus II had broken with France and joined the Grand Alliance, but defeats at Staffarda in 1690 and Marsaglia in 1693 left Piedmont occupied and plundered. Savoy’s army was still small, yet its alliance had become valuable, and in 1696 a separate peace recovered Pinerolo.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1706": "Victor Amadeus II and Prince Eugene of Savoy broke the French siege of Turin, one of the decisive battles of the War of the Spanish Succession. The victory secured the duchy’s survival and made it a sought-after ally, opening the way to territorial gains and a royal crown.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1713": "The Treaty of Utrecht gave Victor Amadeus II the island of Sicily with the title of king, and added Alessandria and other districts of the Duchy of Milan. The Savoyard state had become a recognised European power, its military and fiscal reforms well advanced.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1720": "Sicily was exchanged for the poorer island of Sardinia, and the dukes became kings of Sardinia. Victor Amadeus II pressed on with his cadastre, university reform and the Royal Constitutions. The mainland duchy and Piedmont formed the strong core of an expanding composite monarchy.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1748": "Charles Emmanuel III had won the Battle of the Assietta in 1747, and the Peace of Aix-la-Chapelle confirmed further gains from Lombardy. The Savoyard state stood at its peak of territory, military reputation and administrative efficiency, one of the best-run middle-sized monarchies of eighteenth-century Europe.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1792": "French Revolutionary armies overran Savoy and Nice, and Savoy was annexed as the department of Mont-Blanc. The ancestral duchy was lost, but Piedmont and its army still held the Alpine passes, and the monarchy joined the First Coalition against Revolutionary France.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1798": "After Bonaparte’s victories and the Armistice of Cherasco, Charles Emmanuel IV was forced in December 1798 to give up his mainland states and withdraw to Sardinia. Piedmont was later annexed by France. The Savoyard mainland state temporarily ceased to exist.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1815": "The Congress of Vienna restored Savoy, Nice and Piedmont to Victor Emmanuel I and added the former Republic of Genoa with its port. The restored monarchy, conservative at first, was larger and stronger than before the Revolution and controlled the gateway between France and Italy.",
  "Duchy of Savoy|1847": "King Charles Albert’s ‘perfect fusion’ merged the separate administrations of Sardinia and the mainland states into one kingdom. The Duchy of Savoy ended as a distinct state; its institutions were absorbed into the Kingdom of Sardinia, which would lead Italian unification, while Savoy itself passed to France in 1860.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1441": "Haci I Giray, a Chinggisid prince backed by Lithuania and the Crimean clans, established an independent khanate as the Golden Horde disintegrated. It held the steppe and the interior of the peninsula, while Genoese Caffa and Theodoro held the coast. It was one of several rival Tatar khanates.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1466": "Haci Giray’s death opened a struggle among his sons for the throne. The Great Horde and the clan beys intervened in Crimean politics, and the young khanate’s authority was contested. It remained a modest regional power, dependent on alliances with Lithuania and Muscovy against the Great Horde.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1478": "After the Ottoman conquest of Caffa and Theodoro in 1475, Mengli I Giray was restored as khan under Ottoman suzerainty. The alliance with Istanbul gave the khanate security, a share of Black Sea trade and a powerful protector, while it allied with Ivan III of Muscovy against Lithuania and the Great Horde.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1502": "Mengli Giray destroyed the Great Horde, the last direct remnant of the Golden Horde, and claimed its legacy. Crimea now led the Tatar world of the western steppe, raiding Lithuania and Poland and conducting diplomacy with Muscovy as an equal, at the head of a growing steppe power.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1521": "Mehmed I Giray led a great campaign to the outskirts of Moscow and extracted a promise of tribute from Grand Prince Vasily III. In 1523 he briefly took Astrakhan before being killed by Nogai allies. The khanate was a dominant force over the steppe and its northern neighbours.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1532": "Sahib I Giray, installed with Ottoman backing, built up a new capital at Bakhchysarai with its palace and strengthened the khan’s authority over the clans with Ottoman troops and firearms. Ottoman supervision grew, but the khanate remained a strong steppe state despite internal feuds.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1571": "Devlet I Giray led a great raid that burned Moscow, killing many inhabitants and taking a large number of captives, and demanded the return of Kazan and Astrakhan. The khanate’s military reputation and its leverage over Muscovy stood at their highest point.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1572": "Seeking to follow up his success, Devlet Giray invaded Muscovy again but was defeated at the Battle of Molodi near Moscow. The defeat ended hopes of reversing Russian conquests on the Volga, though the khanate remained powerful and raiding continued for another century.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1648": "Islam III Giray allied with Bohdan Khmelnytsky’s Cossack uprising against Poland-Lithuania, helping win the battles of Zhovti Vody and Korsun. The khanate became a kingmaker in the struggle for Ukraine, gaining captives, payments and political influence across Eastern Europe, though the khan’s withdrawal at Berestechko in 1651 showed his independent aims.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1659": "Mehmed IV Giray’s cavalry helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky crush a Russian army at the Battle of Konotop. The khanate remained a major military power, playing Poland-Lithuania, Muscovy and the Cossacks against one another in the long wars for Ukraine known as the Ruin.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1683": "Crimean cavalry under Murad Giray took part in the failed Ottoman Siege of Vienna. The ensuing war with the Holy League turned against the Ottomans and their allies, and the khanate faced combined pressure from Poland and Russia, with Russian armies marching towards Perekop in 1687 and 1689.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1700": "Peter the Great’s capture of Azov in 1696 and the Treaty of Constantinople of 1700 ended Muscovy’s payments to the khan. The khanate lost a source of income and prestige, a sign that the balance of power on the steppe was shifting towards Russia.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1711": "Ottoman and Crimean forces encircled Peter the Great’s army on the Pruth, forcing Russia to return Azov. The success restored the khanate’s security for a time, and it briefly gained authority over the Zaporozhian Cossacks, but Russia’s long-term advance continued.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1736": "During the Russo-Turkish War Field Marshal Münnich stormed the Perekop lines and burned Bakhchysarai. Russian invasions ravaged the peninsula in successive campaigns, exposing the khanate’s vulnerability to modern armies with artillery and fortified supply lines. Disease and supply problems forced the Russians to withdraw, but slave raiding declined and the khanate never recovered its earlier strength.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1771": "In the Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774 Russian forces under Vasily Dolgorukov overran the Crimea. Ottoman protection collapsed, and Tatar factions divided between pro-Ottoman and pro-Russian candidates for the throne, leaving the khanate under foreign occupation. Khan Selim III Giray fled to Istanbul, and the khanate could no longer defend itself.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1774": "The Treaty of Kuchuk Kainarji declared the khanate independent of the Ottoman Empire. In reality it became a Russian protectorate with Russian garrisons, and Şahin Giray’s reforms after 1777 provoked revolts. The khanate was a state only in name, its throne contested between Russian and Ottoman candidates.",
  "Crimean Khanate|1783": "Catherine II proclaimed the annexation of Crimea to the Russian Empire, and Şahin Giray abdicated. The khanate ceased to exist after more than three centuries, and many Crimean Tatars emigrated to Ottoman lands in the following decades. The Ottomans recognised the annexation in 1784.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1516": "On Ferdinand II’s death Charles of Ghent inherited the crowns of Castile and Aragon with Naples, Sicily and Sardinia, adding them to the Burgundian Netherlands. The union was personal and fragile, the new king a foreigner, but the combined realms already formed one of Europe’s strongest monarchies.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1519": "Charles was elected Holy Roman Emperor, and Cortés began the conquest of the Aztec Empire. Spain now stood at the centre of a vast dynastic complex, though the cost of the imperial election and the king’s absence soon provoked unrest in Castile.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1521": "The revolt of the Comuneros was crushed at Villalar, securing royal authority in Castile, while Cortés completed the conquest of Tenochtitlan. Charles won the loyalty of the Castilian elites, and New Spain began to send its wealth to the crown, strengthening the monarchy’s base.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1525": "Imperial and Spanish troops defeated and captured Francis I of France at Pavia, confirming Habsburg control of Milan and dominance in Italy. Spanish infantry gained a reputation that would last a century, and Charles V stood as the most powerful ruler in Christendom.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1547": "Charles V defeated the German Protestant princes at Mühlberg, and silver from the newly discovered mines of Potosí and Zacatecas began to flow to Seville. Pizarro’s conquest of Peru had been completed, and the monarchy commanded unmatched resources, though wars were already outrunning revenue.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1556": "Charles V abdicated, leaving Spain, Italy, the Netherlands and the Americas to his son Philip II, while the imperial title passed to his brother Ferdinand. Spain became the centre of the monarchy, and the following year Saint-Quentin and Philip’s first bankruptcy showed both its strength and its financial strain.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1571": "The Holy League fleet under Don John of Austria destroyed the Ottoman navy at Lepanto, and Manila was founded in the Philippines. Spain led the Catholic world and spanned the globe, though the Dutch Revolt and the Morisco rising of 1568–1571 showed the costs of uniformity.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1580": "After the death of King Sebastian’s successor, Philip II made good his claim to Portugal, uniting the whole Iberian Peninsula and the Portuguese empire in Africa, Asia and Brazil with his own. Habsburg Spain reached its greatest territorial extent and global reach.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1588": "The Spanish Armada sent against England was defeated by the English fleet and storms, with heavy losses of ships and men. Spain rebuilt its navy and remained the strongest European power, but the failure ended hopes of overthrowing Protestant England and settling the Dutch war by force.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1598": "Philip II died after a fourth suspension of payments and peace with France at Vervins. Spain was still the greatest power in Europe, yet exhausted by decades of war, its finances mortgaged to foreign bankers and Castile hit by plague at the turn of the century.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1609": "Under Philip III and the Duke of Lerma Spain signed the Twelve Years’ Truce with the Dutch, effectively recognising their independence for a time, and began expelling the Moriscos. Peace eased the treasury, but the expulsions damaged Valencia and Aragon, and government drifted under a royal favourite.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1625": "Under Philip IV and Olivares Spain won a series of successes, including the capture of Breda, the recapture of Bahia in Brazil and the defence of Cádiz against the English. The monarchy seemed to have regained its strength in the renewed wars, though at mounting financial cost.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1640": "Catalonia and Portugal revolted against Olivares’s demands for men and money, with Catalonia turning to France. The monarchy faced war on its own soil while fighting France and the Dutch, and Olivares fell in 1643. Spain’s power was shaken at its core.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1643": "The French under the Duke of Enghien destroyed the Army of Flanders at Rocroi, breaking the aura of the Spanish infantry. Spain remained a great power with vast resources, but it was now clearly on the defensive against France on every front.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1648": "By the Peace of Münster Spain recognised the independence of the Dutch Republic after eighty years of war, while revolts in Naples and Sicily had been suppressed. The war with France continued, and Spain now fought to keep, rather than expand, its inheritance.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1659": "The Peace of the Pyrenees ended the war with France, ceding Roussillon and Artois and arranging the marriage of Maria Theresa to Louis XIV. Catalonia had been recovered in 1652, but France had replaced Spain as the leading power of Europe.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1668": "Spain recognised Portuguese independence after defeats at Ameixial and Montes Claros, and lost towns in Flanders to France in the War of Devolution. The young Charles II reigned under a regency, and the Iberian Union was definitively over, leaving a smaller and weaker monarchy.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1678": "The Treaty of Nijmegen gave Franche-Comté and more Flemish towns to France. Spain kept Italy, Belgium and the Americas, and parts of its economy were recovering, but it was now a defensive power whose fate depended on allies and on the childless king’s succession.",
  "Habsburg Spain|1700": "Charles II died childless on 1 November, leaving the entire monarchy to Philip of Anjou, grandson of Louis XIV. Habsburg rule in Spain ended, and the War of the Spanish Succession followed, after which Bourbon Spain kept Spain and the Americas while losing its European possessions.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1523": "Sweden left the Kalmar Union and the Danish nobles deposed Christian II, electing Frederick I. Denmark and Norway remained under one king with Iceland, the Faroes and the duchies, but the realm was shaken by revolt, the loss of Sweden and the threat of Christian II’s return.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1536": "Christian III won the Count’s Feud, introduced the Lutheran Reformation and seized the wealth of the Catholic Church. Norway was reduced to a part of the Danish realm. The crown emerged far richer and stronger, with a united Protestant church under royal control.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1570": "The Treaty of Stettin ended the Northern Seven Years’ War with Sweden without losses. Frederick II enjoyed rising Sound Toll income and would soon rebuild Kronborg at Helsingør and give Tycho Brahe the island of Hven. Denmark-Norway was the dominant power of Scandinavia and the gatekeeper of the Baltic.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1613": "Christian IV’s victory in the Kalmar War forced Sweden to pay a large ransom for Älvsborg at Knäred. With full coffers, a strong fleet and new towns and mines, the realm stood at the height of its power and prestige, the leading state of the Nordic world.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1626": "Christian IV entered the Thirty Years’ War as a Protestant champion and was defeated by Tilly at Lutter am Barenberge. Imperial armies occupied Jutland until the Peace of Lübeck in 1629. The realm kept its lands, but its military reputation and finances were badly damaged.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1645": "In the Torstenson War Swedish forces overran Jutland and the eastern provinces. The Treaty of Brömsebro cost Gotland, Ösel, Jämtland and Härjedalen and freed Swedish ships from the Sound Toll. Sweden had replaced Denmark-Norway as the leading power of Scandinavia.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1658": "Charles X Gustav marched across the frozen Belts and imposed the Treaty of Roskilde, which took Scania, Halland, Blekinge, Bohuslän, Trøndelag and Bornholm. The realm lost a third of its Danish territory and its control of both shores of the Sound, its lowest point before 1807.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1660": "Copenhagen had withstood a Swedish assault, and the Treaty of Copenhagen returned Trøndelag and Bornholm. Frederick III used the crisis to make the monarchy hereditary and absolute. The realm was smaller but now had a centralised government able to rebuild its army and finances.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1679": "In the Scanian War Niels Juel won at Køge Bay, but French pressure forced Denmark-Norway to accept peace in 1679 without regaining Scania. The absolutist state was nevertheless stronger and better organised, and the Danish Code of 1683 soon followed.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1720": "The Treaty of Frederiksborg ended the Great Northern War. Sweden lost its exemption from the Sound Toll, and the king gained the Gottorp part of Schleswig. Tordenskjold’s naval victories had restored prestige, and Sweden’s fall left Denmark-Norway secure in a long peace.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1733": "The purchase of St Croix from France and the founding of the Asiatic Company in 1732 extended Danish trade to the Caribbean and China. Peace, Pietism and a growing navy and merchant fleet marked a stable middle-sized monarchy, though the stavnsbånd bound Danish peasants to the land.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1772": "Struensee, who had ruled in the name of the mentally ill Christian VII, was overthrown and executed. The episode exposed the weaknesses of absolutism, though government passed to capable ministers and the realm remained at peace, its overseas trade beginning to expand.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1797": "Neutral during the wars of the French Revolution, Denmark-Norway enjoyed a trading boom later called the flourishing period. Agrarian reforms, the abolition of the stavnsbånd and the ban on the slave trade made it a model of enlightened government, its merchant fleet among the largest in Europe.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1801": "Britain attacked to break the League of Armed Neutrality, and Nelson’s fleet defeated the Danish navy at the Battle of Copenhagen. Denmark-Norway withdrew from the League. The realm kept its fleet and trade for the moment, but its neutrality had become dangerous.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1807": "Britain bombarded Copenhagen and seized the Danish fleet, driving the realm into alliance with Napoleon. The blockade cut Norway off from Danish grain, causing famine, while trade collapsed. The realm was isolated, bankrupt by 1813 and on the losing side.",
  "Denmark-Norway|1814": "By the Treaty of Kiel Frederick VI ceded Norway to Sweden, keeping Iceland, the Faroes and Greenland. Norway declared independence with the Eidsvoll constitution before entering a union with Sweden. Denmark-Norway ended after nearly three centuries, leaving two separate states.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1526": "After Louis II died at the Battle of Mohács, Ferdinand of Austria was elected king of Bohemia and, by a rival diet, of Hungary. The union joined three very different realms, but Ferdinand faced a rival Hungarian king, John Zápolya, backed by the Ottomans, and controlled only part of Hungary.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1529": "Suleiman the Magnificent besieged Vienna in autumn 1529. The city held and the Ottomans withdrew before winter, but the siege showed how exposed the new monarchy was. Ferdinand’s resources were limited, and he depended heavily on help from his brother Charles V and the German princes of the empire.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1541": "The Ottomans occupied Buda and turned central Hungary into an Ottoman province, while Transylvania became a separate principality under Ottoman protection. Ferdinand was left with a narrow strip of Royal Hungary and paid tribute to the sultan under later truces, a low point for the young monarchy.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1564": "Ferdinand I died, having built central councils and the beginnings of the Military Frontier. His lands were divided among three sons: Maximilian II took Austria, Bohemia and Hungary, while Tyrol and Inner Austria went to cadet lines, dispersing resources even as the frontier with the Ottomans stabilised.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1606": "The Long Turkish War ended with the Peace of Zsitvatorok, in which the sultan treated the Habsburg ruler as an equal and tribute ceased. Yet the war had exhausted the treasury, and the Bocskai revolt in Hungary and the quarrel between Rudolf II and his brother Matthias weakened the dynasty.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1620": "Imperial and Catholic League forces crushed the Bohemian estates at the Battle of White Mountain. Ferdinand II executed rebel leaders, confiscated estates and imposed Catholicism, turning Bohemia into a hereditary Habsburg kingdom. The victory greatly strengthened the dynasty’s authority in its core lands, though the wider Thirty Years’ War continued.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1648": "The Peace of Westphalia ended the Thirty Years’ War. The Habsburgs failed to impose their will on the Holy Roman Empire and ceded Alsatian rights to France, but they kept firm control over Austria and Bohemia, where Catholicism and their hereditary rule were now secure, laying a base for later growth.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1683": "The Ottoman army under Kara Mustafa Pasha besieged Vienna, but a relief army led by John III Sobieski won the Battle of Kahlenberg. The victory began the great counter-offensive of the Holy League, and the monarchy moved from defence to expansion in the Danube basin.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1699": "After victories at Buda in 1686 and Zenta in 1697, the Treaty of Karlowitz gave the Habsburgs almost all of Hungary, Transylvania and most of Slavonia and Croatia. The monarchy had roughly doubled in size and was now one of the great powers of Europe.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1714": "The Treaty of Rastatt ended the War of the Spanish Succession for the Habsburgs. Charles VI did not gain Spain, but he received the Spanish Netherlands, Milan, Naples and Sardinia, giving the monarchy a large stake in Italy and the Low Countries alongside its Danubian core.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1718": "Prince Eugene’s victories at Peterwardein and Belgrade led to the Treaty of Passarowitz, which added the Banat of Temeswar, northern Serbia with Belgrade and Oltenia. With Sicily soon exchanged for Sardinia, the monarchy reached its greatest territorial extent before the nineteenth century, the peak of its power.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1739": "A badly managed war against the Ottomans ended with the Treaty of Belgrade, which returned Belgrade, northern Serbia and Oltenia. Earlier, in the War of the Polish Succession, Naples and Sicily had been lost to the Spanish Bourbons. Charles VI left an empty treasury and a weakened army.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1748": "Maria Theresa survived the War of the Austrian Succession, in which Prussia, Bavaria, France and Saxony challenged her inheritance. The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle confirmed her rule and her husband’s imperial title, but Silesia, one of the richest provinces, remained Prussian, a lasting loss of revenue and prestige.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1763": "The Seven Years’ War ended with the Treaty of Hubertusburg, leaving Silesia with Prussia. Yet the reforms of Haugwitz and Kaunitz had produced a far stronger state and army than in 1740, and the monarchy emerged as a stable great power with improved finances and administration.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1780": "At Maria Theresa’s death the monarchy was prosperous and consolidated, having gained Galicia in the First Partition of Poland in 1772 and Bukovina in 1775. Its reformed administration, schools and army made this one of the high points of Habsburg strength under enlightened absolutism.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1790": "Joseph II died amid revolt in the Austrian Netherlands, unrest in Hungary and an unsuccessful war against the Ottomans. His brother Leopold II withdrew many reforms to restore order. The monarchy remained a great power, but the limits of centralisation had been clearly exposed.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1797": "Defeated in Italy by Napoleon Bonaparte, Austria signed the Treaty of Campo Formio, giving up the Austrian Netherlands and Lombardy in exchange for Venice, Istria and Dalmatia. The monarchy was still large, but revolutionary France had overturned its position in western Europe.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1801": "After defeats at Marengo and Hohenlinden, the Treaty of Lunéville confirmed French gains and began the reorganisation of Germany that would destroy the Holy Roman Empire. Austria kept its core lands, but its leadership of the empire, the source of much Habsburg prestige, was crumbling.",
  "Habsburg Monarchy|1804": "In response to Napoleon’s imperial title, Francis II proclaimed himself hereditary Emperor of Austria. The Habsburg Monarchy thus continued under a new name, still one of Europe’s largest states, though weakened by defeat and soon to face further losses at Austerlitz and Wagram.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1542": "Henry VIII’s new title of King of Ireland took effect, and surrender and regrant drew Gaelic lords such as Conn O’Neill into the royal system as earls. The new kingdom had a parliament and administration in Dublin, but effective royal authority still covered only part of the island.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1556": "Under Mary I the plantation of Laois and Offaly began, creating Queen’s County and King’s County and settling English tenants on confiscated Gaelic land. Royal government was expanding by force, but resistance in the midlands continued for decades and the crown’s hold beyond the Pale remained limited.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1583": "The second Desmond rebellion ended with the death of Gerald FitzGerald, 14th Earl of Desmond. Munster had been devastated by war and famine, and the confiscation of his vast estates opened the way for the Munster plantation, extending English control over the south.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1598": "Hugh O’Neill’s victory at the Battle of the Yellow Ford was the worst English defeat in Ireland in the period. Rebellion spread across the island, the Munster plantation collapsed, and royal government in Dublin was reduced to a precarious position during the Nine Years’ War.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1603": "Following the defeat at Kinsale, Hugh O’Neill submitted under the Treaty of Mellifont, days after Elizabeth I had died. For the first time the whole island acknowledged the authority of the crown, and James I inherited Ireland together with England and Scotland.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1609": "After the Flight of the Earls in 1607, the government confiscated much of Ulster and began the Plantation of Ulster with English and Scottish settlers. The extension of shires, courts and Protestant settlement gave the Dublin administration more control over Ireland than ever before.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1641": "The Irish Catholic rising broke out in Ulster in October 1641, killing many Protestant settlers and spreading across the country. Royal government in Dublin was reduced to a few strongholds, and the Catholic Confederation of Kilkenny soon formed a rival government controlling most of Ireland.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1649": "After the execution of Charles I, Oliver Cromwell landed at Dublin and stormed Drogheda and Wexford. The kingdom’s institutions were in ruins: the monarchy had been abolished in England and Ireland was conquered by an English parliamentary army, the lowest point in its existence.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1653": "The Cromwellian conquest was completed and the Act of Settlement of 1652 provided for mass confiscation of Catholic land. Ireland was absorbed into the Commonwealth with representation at Westminster. The kingdom as a separate realm was in abeyance, though the land settlement created a new Protestant ruling class.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1660": "The restoration of Charles II revived the Kingdom of Ireland with its own parliament and lord lieutenant. The Acts of Settlement and Explanation largely confirmed Cromwellian land transfers, and a period of relative peace allowed economic recovery under the Duke of Ormond’s administration.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1690": "James II, deposed in England, fought for his throne in Ireland with French support. William III defeated him at the Battle of the Boyne, but the kingdom was divided between rival kings and armies, and the war continued for another year.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1691": "The Battle of Aughrim and the Treaty of Limerick ended the Williamite War. Jacobite soldiers left for France, and the Protestant victors secured control of land, parliament and government, establishing the Protestant order that would last through the eighteenth century.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1720": "The Declaratory Act affirmed the Westminster Parliament’s right to legislate for Ireland, subordinating the Irish Parliament. Yet the kingdom was internally peaceful under the Protestant Ascendancy, with regular parliaments, a growing linen trade in Ulster and an expanding capital in Dublin.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1760": "Mid-century Ireland was more prosperous and stable than at any earlier time. Dublin was among the largest cities in Europe, with new public buildings and squares, the Irish Parliament grew more assertive under patriot politicians, and population and trade were expanding.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1782": "Backed by the armed Volunteers, Henry Grattan’s patriots won repeal of the Declaratory Act and amendment of Poynings’ Law. The Irish Parliament gained legislative independence, and the kingdom stood at the height of its autonomy and self-confidence, though still under a London-appointed executive.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1793": "The Catholic Relief Act gave Catholics the vote on the same terms as Protestants, though they still could not sit in Parliament. Ireland was relatively prosperous, but war with revolutionary France and the growth of the United Irishmen created new tensions.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1798": "The United Irishmen rebellion broke out in Leinster and Ulster and was crushed with great loss of life; a French landing at Killala failed. The rising convinced the British government that Ireland’s separate parliament and kingdom could not be relied on in wartime.",
  "Kingdom of Ireland|1800": "The Act of Union passed both the Irish and British parliaments. From 1 January 1801 the Kingdom of Ireland merged into the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland, and the Irish Parliament ceased to exist after almost five centuries.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1545": "Pope Paul III created the duchy for his son Pier Luigi Farnese out of territory taken from the Papal States. The new state included two wealthy cities and a fertile countryside, but it was contested by the Emperor Charles V, who claimed the cities for Milan.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1547": "Pier Luigi Farnese was murdered in Piacenza by nobles backed by Ferrante Gonzaga, the imperial governor of Milan, whose troops seized the city. His son Ottavio kept only Parma, and the dynasty’s survival depended on French and papal support during several insecure years.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1556": "Philip II of Spain returned Piacenza to Ottavio Farnese, who had married Charles V’s daughter Margaret. The reconciliation with the Habsburgs secured the duchy, though a Spanish garrison held Piacenza’s citadel until 1585. The dynasty now ruled both cities in relative security.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1586": "Alessandro Farnese succeeded as duke while commanding Spain’s Army of Flanders, fresh from capturing Antwerp in 1585. The duchy’s prestige was at its highest, its ruler one of the most celebrated generals in Europe, and the Spanish garrison had left Piacenza, giving the Farnese full control.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1592": "Alessandro Farnese died at Arras after campaigning in France, and his son Ranuccio I took power in Parma. Ranuccio built the Pilotta palace and strengthened ducal authority over the nobility, but the duchy no longer enjoyed the European prominence of the previous reign.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1612": "Ranuccio I executed leading nobles, including the Countess of Sala, for an alleged conspiracy and confiscated their estates. The purge enlarged the ducal domain and ended noble resistance, leaving the Farnese with firm internal control and a grand court soon marked by the Teatro Farnese.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1637": "Duke Odoardo Farnese’s alliance with France had brought Spanish invasion and the devastation of the Piacenza countryside. He made peace with Spain in 1637, but the war had damaged the duchy’s finances and shown the danger of independent ambitions for a small state.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1649": "In the second War of Castro, Pope Innocent X captured and razed the Farnese town of Castro, ending the family’s fief in the Papal States. The duchy itself was intact, but heavy debts and the loss of Castro marked the limits of Farnese power.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1700": "Under Francesco Farnese the duchy was stable and its court remained splendid, but it was a minor state whose fate depended on the great powers. With no Farnese heirs in sight, the succession became a question of European diplomacy after Elisabeth Farnese married Philip V of Spain in 1714.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1731": "Antonio, the last Farnese duke, died without children. The duchy passed to the young Charles of Bourbon, son of Elisabeth Farnese, under the terms of the Quadruple Alliance. Parma had become an object of dynastic bargaining, with its independence dependent on Spanish power.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1735": "During the War of the Polish Succession, Charles of Bourbon became king of Naples and Sicily, and the peace preliminaries assigned Parma to the Habsburgs. He took the Farnese art collections to Naples. The duchy lost its own ruler and became an Austrian possession.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1748": "The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle gave Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla to Philip of Bourbon, younger son of Elisabeth Farnese. The duchy regained its own dynasty, the Bourbon-Parma line, and with French and Spanish backing began a period of recovery and reform.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1765": "Ferdinand succeeded his father Philip, and the minister Guillaume du Tillot’s reforms were at their height, with new institutions, the Palatine Library and restrictions on church privileges. Parma was regarded as a model small enlightened state, though du Tillot was dismissed in 1771.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1796": "Napoleon Bonaparte’s army entered northern Italy, and Duke Ferdinand bought an armistice with money, supplies and paintings sent to Paris. The duchy kept its ruler, but it now survived only at French discretion, its neutrality and resources at the mercy of the invading armies.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1802": "Under the Treaty of Aranjuez the Bourbon-Parma family exchanged the duchy for the Kingdom of Etruria. After Duke Ferdinand died in October 1802, France took over the administration, and in 1808 the territory became the French department of Taro. The independent duchy ceased to exist.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1814": "After Napoleon’s defeat, the duchy was restored and assigned by the Congress of Vienna to his wife, Marie Louise of Austria, for life. It regained its separate existence under Austrian protection, with a moderate administration and new public works in Parma.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1831": "Revolutionary unrest in central Italy reached Parma, and Marie Louise briefly left the city until Austrian troops restored her authority. The duchy remained calm by Italian standards, but its government clearly depended on Austrian military support to survive, a weakness that grew more obvious after 1848.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1848": "After Marie Louise died, the Bourbons returned under Charles II, who fled during the revolutions of 1848 while Piacenza voted to join Piedmont. Austrian troops restored the dynasty, but the duchy was now an unpopular regime facing a growing Italian national movement.",
  "Duchy of Parma and Piacenza|1859": "During the Second Italian War of Independence the regent Louise Marie left Parma. A provisional government joined the United Provinces of Central Italy, and in March 1860 a plebiscite approved annexation to the Kingdom of Sardinia, ending the duchy after more than three centuries.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1547": "Ivan IV was crowned tsar in the Dormition Cathedral, claiming equality with emperors. Moscow had already united most Russian lands, but royal authority was limited by boyar factions after a turbulent regency, and the eastern frontier was still threatened by the Tatar khanates of Kazan and Crimea.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1552": "Ivan IV’s army stormed Kazan after a long siege, destroying the khanate that had raided Russian lands for a century. The conquest opened the middle Volga to Russian control and settlement, and it greatly increased the tsar’s prestige, commemorated by the building of St Basil’s Cathedral.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1556": "The annexation of the Khanate of Astrakhan gave Russia the entire course of the Volga down to the Caspian Sea. With reforms in law, administration and the army under way, the young tsardom stood at a high point of confidence and expansion early in Ivan IV’s reign.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1565": "With the Livonian War going on, Ivan IV created the oprichnina, a separate domain in which his servants persecuted boyars and towns. Russia still held conquests in Livonia, but the terror disrupted government and the economy and turned the state’s strength against its own elite.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1571": "The Crimean khan Devlet Giray burned Moscow, killing many of its inhabitants, while the Livonian War was turning against Russia and the oprichnina had devastated Novgorod. The tsardom was overstretched and weakened, though it defeated the Crimeans at Molodi the following year.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1582": "The Truce of Yam-Zapolsky with Poland-Lithuania ended the Livonian War with the loss of Russia’s gains, and a truce with Sweden followed in 1583. In the east, however, Yermak’s cossacks attacked the Khanate of Sibir, opening the way to Siberia and an empire of furs.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1598": "The death of Feodor I ended the Rurikid dynasty, and Boris Godunov was elected tsar. The new patriarchate and the gradual advance into Siberia gave the state strength, but famine from 1601 and doubts about Godunov’s legitimacy soon opened the Time of Troubles.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1610": "In the depths of the Time of Troubles, Tsar Vasily Shuisky was deposed, Polish troops occupied Moscow and Swedish forces soon seized Novgorod. Rival pretenders, cossack bands and foreign armies fought over the country, and the tsardom effectively had no functioning central government.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1613": "After the national militia of Minin and Pozharsky expelled the Poles from Moscow, an assembly of the land elected the sixteen-year-old Michael Romanov tsar. The state was restored, but it was impoverished, war continued, and Smolensk and the Baltic coast were soon lost by treaty.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1649": "Tsar Alexei’s law code, the Sobornoe Ulozhenie, completed the enserfment of the peasantry and codified Russian law, following the Salt Riot of 1648. The state had recovered its strength, with a growing bureaucracy, expanding Siberian possessions and new-order regiments trained by foreign officers.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1654": "The Pereiaslav agreement brought the Ukrainian Cossack Hetmanate under the tsar’s protection, and Russia went to war with Poland-Lithuania, capturing Smolensk. The tsardom was now on the offensive in the west for the first time since Ivan IV, though the war also brought plague and heavy costs.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1667": "The Truce of Andrusovo gave Russia Smolensk, Kyiv and left-bank Ukraine, a lasting shift of power in eastern Europe. Despite the church schism, the Copper Riot and soon Razin’s revolt, the tsardom under Alexei was strong and had become the leading state of the region.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1689": "Peter I took power from the regent Sophia after failed campaigns against the Crimea. The Treaty of Nerchinsk fixed the border with Qing China. Russia was vast and stable, but its army and economy lagged behind western Europe, as the Crimean defeats had shown.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1696": "Peter I captured the Ottoman fortress of Azov with the help of a newly built river fleet, Russia’s first major success against the Ottomans. The victory encouraged his plans for a navy and his Grand Embassy to the west, beginning an era of rapid military reform.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1700": "At the start of the Great Northern War, Charles XII of Sweden routed Peter’s army at Narva. The defeat exposed the weakness of the old army, but Charles turned against Poland, giving Peter time to rebuild his forces, artillery and administration.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1709": "Peter I crushed the Swedish army at the Battle of Poltava, and Charles XII fled to the Ottoman Empire. The victory established Russia as the dominant power of northern and eastern Europe, though the Pruth campaign of 1711 against the Ottomans would end badly.",
  "Tsardom of Russia|1721": "The Treaty of Nystad confirmed Russia’s conquest of the Baltic provinces, and the Senate proclaimed Peter emperor. The tsardom ended at the height of its power, transformed into the Russian Empire with a new Baltic capital, a navy, a modern army and a reformed administration.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1569": "Pope Pius V raised Cosimo I de’ Medici to Grand Duke of Tuscany. Cosimo had defeated the republican exiles, conquered Siena and built a centralised state, and Tuscany was one of the strongest of the Italian states, though Spain still held coastal fortresses and influence.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1574": "Cosimo I died, and Francesco I inherited a consolidated state. The emperor recognised the grand-ducal title in 1576, removing doubts about its legitimacy, though Francesco was more interested in science, alchemy and art than in government, and taxes weighed heavily on the economy.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1591": "Ferdinando I, a former cardinal and effective ruler, issued the first of the Livornine laws inviting foreign merchants and Jews to Livorno. He balanced French and Spanish influence through his marriage to Christina of Lorraine and strengthened the state’s finances, fortifications and trade.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1609": "At Ferdinando I’s death Tuscany was prosperous, well administered and diplomatically respected, with a flourishing free port at Livorno, an active navy of the Order of Saint Stephen, and a court that attracted artists and scientists. This marked the peak of the grand duchy’s power.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1621": "Cosimo II died young after a reign best remembered for his patronage of Galileo. The state remained stable and prosperous, but a long regency followed for Ferdinando II, and the textile industries were beginning their long decline as northern European competition grew.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1633": "Plague had killed many people in Florence and the countryside in 1630–1633, and Galileo, the grand duke’s court mathematician, was condemned in Rome without effective Tuscan protection. The state was weakened economically and showed its dependence on the papacy and the great powers.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1670": "Ferdinando II died after a long reign that saw the founding of the Accademia del Cimento but also economic stagnation. His son Cosimo III began a 53-year reign of religious conservatism and heavy taxation, during which Tuscany declined further in wealth and political weight.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1723": "Gian Gastone, the last Medici grand duke, succeeded his father. Neither he nor his sister had children, and the great powers, not the Tuscans, were deciding the succession. Tuscany was a declining state, its economy stagnant and its independence increasingly nominal.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1737": "Gian Gastone died and the Medici line ended. Under the Treaty of Vienna, Francis Stephen of Lorraine became grand duke, but he lived in Vienna and governed through a regency council. Tuscany had become a secondary possession of the Habsburg family.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1765": "Peter Leopold arrived in Florence as resident grand duke, restoring a ruling court to Tuscany. He began a wide programme of reforms in trade, agriculture, local government and administration that revived the economy and made Tuscany a model of enlightened government.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1786": "Peter Leopold’s criminal code abolished torture and the death penalty. Tuscany had free trade in grain, reformed finances and local government, and a reputation across Europe as the most progressive state of the age, a second high point after the Medici peak.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1790": "Peter Leopold left to become Emperor Leopold II, and his son Ferdinand III succeeded. Some reforms were reversed amid popular discontent, but Tuscany remained well governed, though neutral and almost without an army as the French revolutionary wars spread into Italy.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1799": "French troops occupied Tuscany and Ferdinand III went into exile. An anti-French rising, the Viva Maria, drove them out briefly, but the state had no means to defend itself and its fate depended entirely on the outcome of the war between France and Austria.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1801": "Under the Treaty of Lunéville the grand duchy was abolished and replaced by the Kingdom of Etruria under Louis of Bourbon-Parma, a French client state. Tuscany later became part of France, and the grand duchy ceased to exist for thirteen years.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1814": "After Napoleon’s fall, Ferdinand III returned to Florence and the grand duchy was restored. Tuscany regained its independence under the Habsburg-Lorraine dynasty, with a relatively mild government that kept much of the previous administration, but it relied on Austria for protection.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1847": "The grand duchy absorbed the Duchy of Lucca, completing its territorial unity. Under Leopold II Tuscany was relatively tolerant and prosperous, but liberal and national movements were growing, and the revolutions of the following year would test the dynasty’s authority.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1849": "Leopold II, who had granted a constitution in 1848, fled to Gaeta as democrats took over Florence. He returned with Austrian troops, who occupied Tuscany until 1855. The dynasty had survived, but it lost much of its popularity and legitimacy.",
  "Grand Duchy of Tuscany|1859": "When war broke out between Austria and France and Piedmont-Sardinia, Leopold II left Florence on 27 April. A provisional government joined the United Provinces of Central Italy, and the plebiscite of March 1860 brought Tuscany into the Kingdom of Sardinia, ending the grand duchy.",
  "Dutch Republic|1581": "By the Act of Abjuration the States General renounced Philip II of Spain. The rebel provinces held Holland, Zeeland and parts of the north, but Spanish armies under Alexander Farnese, Duke of Parma, were advancing, and the new state was still looking for a foreign prince as sovereign, so its position was fragile.",
  "Dutch Republic|1585": "William the Silent had been assassinated at Delft in 1584, and Parma’s capture of Antwerp in August 1585 completed the Spanish reconquest of the southern Netherlands. The Treaty of Nonsuch brought English troops under the Earl of Leicester, but the Republic held a shrinking territory centred on Holland and Zeeland and was at its most vulnerable.",
  "Dutch Republic|1588": "Leicester had returned to England and the States General ceased looking for a sovereign, governing as a republic guided by Johan van Oldenbarnevelt. The failure of the Spanish Armada diverted Spanish resources, giving the provinces breathing space to reorganise their finances and rebuild the army under Maurice of Nassau.",
  "Dutch Republic|1600": "Maurice of Nassau’s reformed army had recaptured Breda, Groningen and towns along the eastern rivers during the 1590s, securing a defensible territory. At Nieuwpoort in 1600 it defeated a Spanish field army. Dutch ships had begun sailing to Asia, and the trade with the Baltic was booming.",
  "Dutch Republic|1609": "The Twelve Years’ Truce with Spain treated the provinces as free in all but formal recognition. The Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, was building its Asian network, the Bank of Amsterdam opened in 1609, and Amsterdam was replacing Antwerp as the commercial centre of northern Europe.",
  "Dutch Republic|1621": "War with Spain resumed when the truce expired, after an internal crisis in which Oldenbarnevelt was executed in 1619. The Dutch West India Company was founded to attack Iberian interests in the Atlantic. Trade remained strong, but Spanish embargoes and the cost of the war held the Republic below its later strength.",
  "Dutch Republic|1632": "Frederick Henry’s capture of ’s-Hertogenbosch in 1629 and Maastricht in 1632 pushed the frontier south. Piet Heyn’s seizure of the Spanish silver fleet in 1628 helped fund the West India Company’s conquest of north-eastern Brazil, and the East India Company, based at Batavia since 1619, was dominating the spice trade.",
  "Dutch Republic|1648": "In the Peace of Münster Spain recognised the Republic’s full independence and accepted the closure of the Scheldt, which protected Amsterdam against the revival of Antwerp. The Republic was Europe’s leading commercial and shipping power, with a merchant fleet larger than its rivals’, and its arts and sciences flourished.",
  "Dutch Republic|1652": "The First Anglo-Dutch War broke out over trade and England’s Navigation Act of 1651. Under Johan de Witt, Grand Pensionary from 1653, the stadtholderless Republic weathered heavy losses at sea and built a navy of purpose-built warships, keeping its commercial lead essentially intact.",
  "Dutch Republic|1667": "The Raid on the Medway, in which Michiel de Ruyter burned English ships and towed away the Royal Charles, led to the favourable Peace of Breda. The Republic stood at the height of its naval, commercial and diplomatic power and in 1668 joined England and Sweden in the Triple Alliance to check France.",
  "Dutch Republic|1672": "In the Disaster Year France, England, Münster and Cologne attacked together. French armies overran Gelderland, Overijssel and Utrecht, the de Witt brothers were murdered, and William III became stadtholder. Only the flooded Holland Water Line and de Ruyter’s victories at sea saved the Republic from conquest.",
  "Dutch Republic|1678": "The Treaty of Nijmegen ended the Franco-Dutch War with the Republic’s territory intact. William III had built an alliance with Spain, the Emperor and Brandenburg, and the Republic re-emerged as the centre of opposition to Louis XIV, though the war had added greatly to its debts.",
  "Dutch Republic|1688": "William III’s invasion of England in the Glorious Revolution, carried by a large Dutch fleet, made him king of England alongside Mary II. The Republic’s money and troops now underpinned a grand alliance against France in the Nine Years’ War, the high point of its weight in European politics.",
  "Dutch Republic|1702": "William III died without children, opening the Second Stadtholderless Period. Under Grand Pensionary Anthonie Heinsius the Republic entered the War of the Spanish Succession alongside Britain and Austria, fielding large armies in the southern Netherlands, but the war strained finances already burdened by decades of conflict.",
  "Dutch Republic|1713": "The Peace of Utrecht and the Barrier Treaty of 1715 gave the Republic the right to garrison a barrier of fortresses in the Southern Netherlands but few other gains. Holland’s debt was enormous, the navy was allowed to decline, and the Republic increasingly followed British leadership, a turning point in its descent from great-power status.",
  "Dutch Republic|1747": "A French invasion of Dutch Flanders during the War of the Austrian Succession provoked an Orangist revolt, and William IV became hereditary stadtholder of all seven provinces. The Republic was militarily weak, and the barrier fortresses had fallen easily, though Amsterdam remained a leading financial centre.",
  "Dutch Republic|1780": "Britain declared war over Dutch trade with the American rebels and the Republic’s move towards the League of Armed Neutrality. The Fourth Anglo-Dutch War exposed a neglected navy; British ships seized large numbers of Dutch merchantmen and attacked colonial posts, notably Sint Eustatius in 1781.",
  "Dutch Republic|1784": "Peace with Britain cost the Republic Negapatnam in India and granted British ships free navigation in the East Indies. Defeat discredited the stadtholder William V, and the Patriot movement, drawing on burghers and some regents, organised armed free corps to reform the Republic.",
  "Dutch Republic|1787": "When Patriots stopped Princess Wilhelmina near Goejanverwellesluis, her brother Frederick William II of Prussia sent an army that restored William V with little resistance. Thousands of Patriots fled to France, leaving the Republic dependent on Prussia and Britain, with which it formed an alliance in 1788.",
  "Dutch Republic|1795": "French revolutionary armies, joined by Dutch Patriots, crossed the frozen rivers in the winter of 1794–1795. William V fled to England, and the Batavian Republic was proclaimed in January 1795, ending the old federation of sovereign provinces after more than two centuries.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1701": "Elector Frederick III crowned himself Frederick I, King in Prussia, at Königsberg. His lands were scattered from the Rhine to the Baltic and thinly populated, with a modest army; the crown brought prestige, but Prussia was still a second-rank German state dependent on alliances and foreign subsidies.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1713": "Frederick William I succeeded his father and gained Upper Guelders under the Peace of Utrecht. He cut court spending and began building a large, well-drilled army and a centralised administration, laying the foundations of Prussian power while avoiding major wars for most of his reign.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1740": "Frederick II inherited an army of about 80,000 men and a full treasury. Within months of his accession he invaded the Habsburg province of Silesia, exploiting the disputed succession of Maria Theresa and launching Prussia’s bid for great-power status in Europe.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1745": "The Treaty of Dresden ended the Second Silesian War and confirmed Prussian possession of Silesia, a rich province with roughly a million inhabitants and important textile and mining industries. Prussia was now recognised as a power able to defy the Habsburgs.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1757": "In the Seven Years’ War Prussia faced Austria, France, Russia and Sweden at once. Frederick’s victories over the French at Rossbach and the Austrians at Leuthen made his reputation, but they were won against far larger coalitions with very limited resources.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1759": "Defeat at Kunersdorf by Russian and Austrian forces nearly destroyed the Prussian army, and Berlin was briefly occupied by enemy troops in 1760. Prussia’s survival seemed doubtful, as men and money ran short despite British subsidies, and Frederick himself wrote of despair after the battle.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1763": "Russia’s change of course after Empress Elizabeth died allowed Prussia to make peace at Hubertusburg and keep Silesia. Exhausted but undefeated, Prussia was accepted as one of Europe’s great powers, and Frederick turned to rebuilding the war-ravaged provinces with seed, horses and tax relief.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1772": "In the First Partition of Poland Prussia took Royal Prussia, except Danzig and Thorn, linking East Prussia with Brandenburg and Pomerania. Frederick II now styled himself King of Prussia, ruling a more compact and efficiently administered state that controlled the lower Vistula and much of Poland’s river trade.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1795": "The Second and Third Partitions of Poland added Danzig, Thorn, Posen and Warsaw, greatly enlarging Prussia’s territory and population. Yet Prussia had withdrawn from the war against revolutionary France at the Peace of Basel in 1795, and its army and institutions were ageing.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1806": "Napoleon crushed the Prussian army at Jena and Auerstedt in October 1806, fortresses surrendered almost without resistance, and the court fled to East Prussia. The collapse revealed how far the Frederician state had fallen behind the mass armies of revolutionary France.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1807": "The Treaty of Tilsit stripped Prussia of about half its territory, including its Polish gains and its lands west of the Elbe, and imposed an indemnity and French occupation. This was the kingdom’s lowest point, but it set off the Stein and Hardenberg reforms.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1813": "Prussia changed sides after Napoleon’s defeat in Russia, allied with Russia in the Treaty of Kalisch and mobilised a reformed army with the Landwehr. Its troops fought in the decisive Battle of Leipzig in October 1813, restoring Prussia as a military power.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1815": "The Congress of Vienna gave Prussia the Rhineland, Westphalia, northern Saxony and Swedish Pomerania, while Blücher’s army helped win at Waterloo. Prussia now spanned northern Germany, though in two separate blocks, and held the coal and iron that would later fuel its industrial growth.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1834": "The Zollverein came into force under Prussian leadership, creating a customs union with most German states but excluding Austria. Prussia gained economic leadership in Germany while remaining politically second to Austria in the German Confederation, and its finances gained from the shared customs revenue.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1848": "Revolution in Berlin forced Frederick William IV to concede a constitution and parliament. In 1849 he refused the imperial crown offered by the Frankfurt Parliament, and in 1850 Austrian pressure at Olmütz forced Prussia to abandon its own plan for a union of German states.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1866": "Prussia defeated Austria and its German allies at Königgrätz in the Austro-Prussian War. It annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel, Nassau, Frankfurt and Schleswig-Holstein and organised the North German Confederation under its king, ending Austrian influence in German affairs and at last linking its eastern and western provinces.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1871": "Victory over France led to the proclamation of King William I as German Emperor at Versailles on 18 January 1871. Prussia, with about three-fifths of the new empire’s population and most of its territory, became the dominant state of the German Empire.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1890": "William II dismissed Bismarck, ending an era, but Prussia stood at the heart of Europe’s leading military and industrial power. Its Landtag, elected by the three-class franchise, remained a stronghold of the conservative landowning nobility, even as the Social Democrats grew strong in the industrial cities.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1914": "Prussia entered the First World War as the core of the German Empire, supplying most of its army and officer corps. The Prussian general staff tradition shaped German strategy, including the plan to defeat France quickly by marching through Belgium, while the unreformed franchise was increasingly contested at home.",
  "Kingdom of Prussia|1918": "Defeat in the First World War and the November Revolution brought the abdication of William II as King of Prussia and German Emperor. The monarchy ended, and Prussia continued as the Free State of Prussia within the Weimar Republic until its abolition in 1947.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1707": "The Acts of Union merged England and Scotland on 1 May 1707 under Queen Anne. The new kingdom was already a major power, fighting France in the War of the Spanish Succession with Marlborough’s armies and a strong navy, and it inherited England’s colonies in North America and the Caribbean.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1713": "The Treaty of Utrecht ended the War of the Spanish Succession. Britain gained Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland, Nova Scotia and the Hudson Bay territory, together with the asiento slave-trading contract with Spanish America, and emerged as a leading maritime and commercial power in Europe.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1715": "Queen Anne’s death in 1714 brought George I of Hanover to the throne. The Jacobite rising of 1715 in Scotland, which aimed to restore James Francis Edward Stuart, faltered after the inconclusive Battle of Sheriffmuir, and the Whigs entrenched their hold on government for decades.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1721": "After the South Sea Bubble collapsed in 1720, Robert Walpole became First Lord of the Treasury and restored public credit. His long ministry kept Britain out of most wars, kept taxes on land low and allowed trade and the colonies to grow steadily, strengthening the state’s finances.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1739": "Commercial disputes with Spain led to the War of Jenkins’ Ear, which merged into the War of the Austrian Succession. Admiral Edward Vernon captured Porto Bello, but the costly attack on Cartagena failed in 1741, and Walpole fell from power in 1742 amid criticism of the war.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1745": "Charles Edward Stuart landed in Scotland, took Edinburgh and advanced as far as Derby before retreating. The Jacobite army was destroyed at Culloden in April 1746, ending the threat of a Stuart restoration and leading to harsh measures against the clan system in the Highlands.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1748": "The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle ended the War of the Austrian Succession largely by restoring conquests on both sides. Britain’s naval strength and overseas trade had grown, but its rivalry with France in North America and India remained unresolved and soon led to renewed war.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1756": "The Seven Years’ War began with setbacks, including the loss of Minorca. William Pitt the Elder’s strategy from 1757 concentrated on naval power and colonial conquest while subsidising Prussia, and Robert Clive’s victory at Plassey gave the East India Company control of Bengal.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1759": "In the ‘year of victories’ British forces took Quebec after James Wolfe’s victory on the Plains of Abraham, won at Minden with their German allies, captured Guadeloupe and destroyed French fleets at Lagos and Quiberon Bay. French naval power and invasion plans were broken for the rest of the war.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1763": "The Treaty of Paris gave Britain Canada, Florida and the lands east of the Mississippi and confirmed its dominance in India. Great Britain was the world’s leading colonial and naval power, though the war had nearly doubled the national debt and left the question of who would pay for imperial defence.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1775": "Attempts to tax the American colonies, beginning with the Stamp Act of 1765, provoked mounting resistance, and fighting broke out at Lexington and Concord in April 1775. Britain remained a great power at sea and in trade, but its North American empire was entering open rebellion.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1781": "France, Spain and the Dutch Republic had joined the American cause, and the surrender of Charles Cornwallis at Yorktown in October 1781 ended British hopes of victory in America. Britain faced the other major navies without allies, a low point in its eighteenth-century fortunes.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1783": "The Treaty of Paris recognised the independence of the United States. Britain kept Canada, Gibraltar and, after Admiral Rodney’s victory at the Saintes in 1782, its main Caribbean islands. William Pitt the Younger became prime minister at the end of the year and began restoring public finances.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1793": "Revolutionary France declared war on Britain in February 1793. Pitt organised coalitions and subsidies against France, while the Industrial Revolution was rapidly expanding British manufacturing and the East India Company’s territories in India continued to grow, giving Britain great financial staying power.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1798": "Nelson’s victory at the Battle of the Nile destroyed the French fleet in Egypt and confirmed British naval supremacy in the Mediterranean. In Ireland the United Irishmen’s rebellion was crushed, and the government resolved to pursue a legislative union of the two kingdoms to secure the island.",
  "Kingdom of Great Britain|1800": "The Acts of Union passed the British and Irish parliaments, and from 1 January 1801 Great Britain merged with Ireland into the United Kingdom. The kingdom ended not in defeat but in enlargement, still at war with France and still the world’s leading naval power.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1719": "Emperor Charles VI united the Lordship of Schellenberg and the County of Vaduz as the Imperial Principality of Liechtenstein for Prince Anton Florian. The tiny territory qualified the family for a princely seat in the Imperial Diet, but the princes lived in Vienna and the principality was a poor, remote possession.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1806": "Napoleon included Liechtenstein in the Confederation of the Rhine, and with the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire it became nominally sovereign. Its survival as a state probably owed more to Prince Johann I’s standing as an Austrian field marshal than to any strength of its own.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1815": "The Congress of Vienna settlement confirmed Liechtenstein’s sovereignty, and it joined the German Confederation, which obliged it to keep a small military contingent. Johann I issued an estates constitution in 1818, but the country remained an agrarian backwater governed by officials on behalf of princes living in Vienna.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1852": "A customs treaty with the Austrian Empire tied Liechtenstein’s economy to the large Habsburg market, which offered outlets for trade but made the principality heavily dependent on its neighbour. Government revenues improved, and the first textile factories followed in the next decade.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1866": "The German Confederation dissolved after the Austro-Prussian War, in which Liechtenstein’s contingent of about 80 men guarded an Alpine pass without fighting. Liechtenstein emerged as a fully independent state, though bound to Austria economically, and its constitution of 1862 had created an elected Landtag.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1868": "Prince Johann II disbanded the army, which had lost its purpose with the end of the German Confederation and weighed heavily on a small budget. Liechtenstein has had no armed forces since, relying on neutrality and the goodwill of its larger neighbours for its security.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1918": "The collapse of Austria-Hungary at the end of the First World War destroyed Liechtenstein’s customs and currency link, as the Austrian krone lost its value. Poverty and political unrest followed, and in 1919 Liechtenstein ended the customs treaty with Austria and began turning towards Switzerland.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1921": "The constitution of 5 October 1921 made Liechtenstein a constitutional hereditary monarchy on a democratic and parliamentary basis, sharing power between the prince and the people and introducing referendums and initiatives on the Swiss model. Much amended, it remains the basis of the state today.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1923": "The customs union treaty with Switzerland, in force from 1924, and the adoption of the Swiss franc gave Liechtenstein a stable currency and a secure market. The Persons and Companies Act of 1926 began attracting holding companies, laying the foundations of its later financial sector.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1938": "Franz Joseph II became prince and moved to Vaduz, the first ruler to live in the country, shortly after Germany annexed Austria. His presence and a coalition government helped Liechtenstein resist local Nazi sympathisers, whose attempted putsch in March 1939 failed.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1945": "Liechtenstein emerged from the Second World War neutral and unoccupied. It sheltered about 500 Russian soldiers who had fought on the German side and refused Soviet demands for their extradition, while the princely family lost its large estates in Czechoslovakia under the Beneš decrees.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1960": "Rapid industrialisation was transforming the country: firms such as Hilti expanded into world markets, agriculture declined and foreign workers arrived. Through Switzerland, Liechtenstein was associated with the new European Free Trade Association, and its financial services were growing strongly, making it one of Europe’s richest countries per head.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1978": "Liechtenstein joined the Council of Europe, its first major step towards membership of international organisations in its own right. A modern economy built on industry and finance gave it high incomes, though women still lacked the vote at national level until 1984.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1990": "Liechtenstein joined the United Nations, followed by full membership of the European Free Trade Association in 1991. Under Prince Hans-Adam II, who had succeeded his father in 1989, the country pursued a more active foreign policy, distinct from that of Switzerland.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|1995": "Liechtenstein joined the European Economic Area, gaining access to the European single market while keeping its customs union with Switzerland, whose voters had rejected the EEA. Membership required adjustments, such as limits on the free movement of people agreed for so small a country, but its economy, international standing and prosperity reached a high point.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|2003": "A referendum approved Hans-Adam II’s constitutional amendments, confirming the prince’s veto and giving him power to dismiss the government, after he had said he would leave for Vienna if they were rejected. Critics, including the Council of Europe’s Venice Commission, questioned the balance of powers.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|2008": "Leaked data from the LGT bank exposed German tax evaders using Liechtenstein foundations, causing a diplomatic dispute and international pressure. Liechtenstein responded by signing agreements on the exchange of tax information and accepting international transparency standards, at some cost to its financial sector’s image.",
  "Principality of Liechtenstein|2025": "Liechtenstein is a stable, prosperous microstate of about 40,000 people with one of the world’s highest incomes per head and membership of the United Nations, EFTA, the EEA and the Schengen area. Its constitutional monarchy under Prince Hans-Adam II, with Hereditary Prince Alois as his representative, remains unusually strong.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1801": "The union took effect on 1 January 1801, joining Great Britain and Ireland under one Parliament at Westminster. The new state was at war with France, already the leading naval and commercial power and growing through the early Industrial Revolution, but the promised Catholic emancipation was refused by George III.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1805": "Nelson’s victory at Trafalgar destroyed the combined French and Spanish fleets and ended the threat of invasion, establishing a naval supremacy that would last a century. On land, however, Napoleon dominated the continent after Austerlitz, and Britain fought on through coalitions, subsidies and blockade.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1815": "Wellington and Blücher defeated Napoleon at Waterloo, and the Vienna settlement confirmed British possession of strategic colonies such as the Cape and Ceylon. With France beaten and no naval rival, the United Kingdom entered a century of unmatched global influence, though burdened by a large war debt.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1832": "The Great Reform Act broadened the franchise and redistributed seats, following Catholic emancipation in 1829. Reform eased revolutionary pressure, the abolition of slavery in most colonies followed in 1833, and railways and factories were rapidly expanding the industrial economy of Great Britain.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1846": "The repeal of the Corn Laws committed Britain to free trade and split the Conservative Party. In Ireland the Great Famine had begun the year before, eventually killing about a million people and driving mass emigration, a catastrophe that permanently embittered relations within the union.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1851": "The Great Exhibition at the Crystal Palace displayed Britain as the ‘workshop of the world’, producing a large share of the world’s manufactured goods, iron and coal. Railways now linked the country, steamships served its trade, and London was the world’s financial centre.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1857": "The Indian Rebellion was suppressed, and in 1858 the Crown took over the government of India from the East India Company. The Crimean War had exposed military weaknesses, but British industrial, financial and imperial power remained unrivalled in the world.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1870": "The Education Act began state elementary schooling in England and Wales, while the Irish Land Act and the disestablishment of the Church of Ireland marked Gladstone’s attempt to reconcile Ireland. The United Kingdom was at its peak as the leading industrial, financial and naval power.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1885": "The Third Reform Act of 1884 widened the vote, and the Berlin Conference opened the Scramble for Africa, in which Britain gained large territories. In 1886 Gladstone’s first Home Rule Bill failed and split the Liberal Party, while Germany and the United States began to rival British industry.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1899": "The Second Boer War against the South African Republic and the Orange Free State proved long and costly, revealing military weaknesses and drawing international criticism, including over concentration camps. The empire was near its widest extent, but Britain’s relative industrial decline was evident.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1906": "A Liberal landslide brought social reforms such as old-age pensions and national insurance, while HMS Dreadnought opened a naval race with Germany. Agreements with Japan in 1902, France in 1904 and Russia in 1907 ended Britain’s isolation and prepared the alliances of the coming war.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1914": "Britain entered the First World War against Germany in August 1914. The Government of Ireland Act granting Home Rule was passed but suspended, after Unionists and Nationalists had formed rival armed volunteer forces that brought Ireland close to civil conflict.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1916": "The Easter Rising in Dublin was crushed within a week, but the execution of its leaders turned Irish opinion towards separatism. On the Western Front the Battle of the Somme brought heavy casualties, and conscription was introduced in Great Britain but not in Ireland.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1918": "Victory in the First World War came at the cost of more than 700,000 dead and large debts to the United States. The Representation of the People Act gave most women over 30 the vote, and in the December election Sinn Féin won most Irish seats.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1921": "After two years of guerrilla war, a truce and the Anglo-Irish Treaty of 6 December 1921 conceded dominion status to most of Ireland. Northern Ireland had already received its own parliament under the Government of Ireland Act 1920, and the union of 1801 was effectively ending.",
  "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|1922": "The Irish Free State came into being on 6 December 1922, and Northern Ireland chose to remain in the United Kingdom. The union of 1801 thus ended, though Britain kept its empire and great-power status as the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1803": "Napoleon\u0027s Act of Mediation replaced the failed Helvetic Republic with a confederation of nineteen cantons, six of them newly created from former subject lands. Switzerland regained internal self-government, but it remained a French protectorate obliged to supply regiments to Napoleon, so its real independence and standing were modest.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1813": "After Napoleon\u0027s defeat at Leipzig, Allied armies marched through Swiss territory and the Mediation regime collapsed. Conservative patricians tried to restore the old order and some cantons threatened to reclaim their former subject lands, leaving the country divided and without a settled constitution at its lowest point.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1815": "The Federal Treaty bound twenty-two cantons, including Valais, Neuchâtel and Geneva, and the great powers at Vienna and Paris recognised Swiss perpetual neutrality and territorial integrity. The confederation gained secure frontiers and international guarantees, though its central institutions remained weak and the cantons were largely sovereign.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1830": "The Regeneration began as liberal movements won new constitutions in Zurich, Bern and many other cantons. Political life became more representative, but the conflict between liberal and conservative cantons, sharpened by religion, left the confederation unable to reform its own weak federal structure for nearly two decades.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1847": "The seven Catholic cantons of the Sonderbund defied the Diet, which ordered the league dissolved. Federal troops under General Dufour won the Sonderbund War in under a month with roughly a hundred dead. The quick, limited victory cleared the way for a stronger federal state.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1848": "The Federal Constitution of 12 September 1848 created a federal state with a two-chamber Federal Assembly, a seven-member Federal Council and Bern as federal city. A single currency, a customs area and federal posts followed, giving Switzerland its first effective central government while leaving wide powers to the cantons.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1857": "The Neuchâtel crisis, in which royalists tried to restore the rights of the King of Prussia, ended when Frederick William IV renounced his claims in the Treaty of Paris of 1857. The young federal state had held firm against a great power and completed its territorial unity.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1874": "A total revision of the constitution strengthened the federal government, centralised military organisation, added the optional referendum on federal laws and established a permanent Federal Supreme Court in Lausanne. The same year the Universal Postal Union was founded in Bern, reflecting Switzerland\u0027s growing role in international cooperation.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1882": "The Gotthard Railway opened, with a tunnel of about fifteen kilometres beneath the Alps, linking northern Europe with Italy through Switzerland. Industry, banking and tourism were expanding rapidly, and the country was turning from a land of emigration into a prosperous industrial and transit economy.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1914": "On the outbreak of the First World War Switzerland mobilised its army under General Ulrich Wille to defend its neutrality. It was not invaded, but sympathies divided between German- and French-speaking regions, and shortages and inflation strained society during the long years of mobilisation.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1918": "Social tension culminated in the general strike of November 1918, which the government ended by deploying troops. The crisis exposed deep class divisions, though it hastened reforms such as proportional representation and later the 48-hour week, and the state emerged intact if shaken.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1920": "Voters approved membership of the League of Nations, which established its seat in Geneva. Switzerland obtained recognition of a differential neutrality exempting it from military sanctions, and Geneva became the capital of interwar diplomacy, raising the country\u0027s international profile beyond its size.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1940": "After the fall of France Switzerland was almost encircled by the Axis. General Henri Guisan rallied the army at the Rütli and adopted the National Redoubt strategy. The country survived as a neutral, but economic dependence on Germany and restrictive refugee policy later drew strong criticism.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1945": "Switzerland emerged from the Second World War undamaged, with its industry and financial system intact while much of Europe lay in ruins. It faced Allied pressure over gold and German assets, settled by the Washington Agreement of 1946, but was well placed for postwar growth.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1959": "The four largest parties agreed to share the Federal Council by the magic formula, an arrangement that lasted until 2003. Sustained economic growth, rising immigration and stable consensus government made Switzerland one of the wealthiest and most politically stable countries in Europe.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1971": "Male voters approved women\u0027s suffrage in federal elections and votes, ending one of the most conspicuous gaps in Swiss democracy. The country was by then a major centre of banking, pharmaceuticals and precision industry, and Geneva hosted a growing number of international organisations.",
  "Swiss Confederation|1992": "In the referendum of 6 December 1992 voters narrowly rejected joining the European Economic Area. Switzerland turned instead to negotiating bilateral treaties with the European Union, keeping its independence and neutrality while securing access to the European market on its own terms.",
  "Swiss Confederation|2002": "Switzerland joined the United Nations after a popular vote, and the first package of bilateral agreements with the European Union, including the free movement of persons, came into force. The final Bergier report on the Second World War era also appeared, closing a period of painful reassessment.",
  "Swiss Confederation|2008": "The global financial crisis forced a federal rescue of UBS, and American and international pressure soon forced Switzerland to begin giving up bank secrecy towards foreign tax authorities. The economy nonetheless proved resilient, and the country joined the Schengen area the same year.",
  "Swiss Confederation|2025": "Switzerland remains a stable federal democracy of twenty-six cantons and about nine million people, with one of the world\u0027s highest incomes per head, strong pharmaceutical and financial sectors and a central place in international diplomacy, despite the 2023 collapse of Credit Suisse and unresolved questions over its relationship with the European Union.",
  "Austrian Empire|1804": "Francis II proclaimed himself hereditary Emperor of Austria, uniting the Habsburg hereditary lands, Bohemia and Hungary under one title. The monarchy was still a great power with a large army and population, but it had already lost territory to France in two wars and faced Napoleon\u0027s growing domination.",
  "Austrian Empire|1805": "Napoleon captured an Austrian army at Ulm and defeated the Austrians and Russians at Austerlitz. By the Peace of Pressburg Austria lost Tyrol, Vorarlberg and Venetia, and in 1806 Francis dissolved the Holy Roman Empire. The new empire had been humiliated within a year of its proclamation.",
  "Austrian Empire|1809": "Austria went to war again and Archduke Charles defeated Napoleon at Aspern-Essling, but defeat at Wagram followed. The Treaty of Schönbrunn took Salzburg, West Galicia and the Illyrian lands, cutting Austria off from the sea and costing several million subjects. Its power stood at its lowest point.",
  "Austrian Empire|1813": "After Napoleon\u0027s disaster in Russia, Metternich brought Austria into the Sixth Coalition in August 1813. Field Marshal Schwarzenberg commanded the Allied armies that won the Battle of Leipzig in October, and Austria regained its standing as one of the decisive powers in the defeat of Napoleon.",
  "Austrian Empire|1815": "The Congress of Vienna, held in the Austrian capital, restored Austria\u0027s losses and added Lombardy-Venetia and Dalmatia. Austria presided over the new German Confederation and dominated Italy, and Metternich was the leading statesman of Europe. The empire stood at the height of its international influence.",
  "Austrian Empire|1821": "Metternich became state chancellor, and Austrian armies crushed liberal revolutions in Naples and Piedmont with the backing of the Holy Alliance powers. The Metternich system of conservative intervention, censorship and police control was at its most effective in Germany and Italy.",
  "Austrian Empire|1835": "Francis I died and was succeeded by Ferdinand I, who was unable to govern. A State Conference including Metternich and Count Kolowrat ruled in his name, and rivalry within it paralysed decision-making. The empire remained powerful abroad, but reform at home stalled while national movements grew.",
  "Austrian Empire|1846": "A Polish rising in Galicia collapsed when peasants killed large numbers of their noble landlords and officials, and Austria annexed the Free City of Kraków with the consent of Russia and Prussia. The territorial gain was small, while the violence showed the depth of social and national tension within the empire.",
  "Austrian Empire|1848": "Revolutions in Vienna, Prague, Milan, Venice and Budapest drove Metternich into exile, Hungary obtained its own government, and Ferdinand fled the capital before abdicating in December. Piedmont invaded Lombardy. For several months the survival of the monarchy itself was in doubt.",
  "Austrian Empire|1849": "Radetzky defeated Piedmont at Novara, Venice surrendered and, with the help of a Russian army, the Hungarian forces capitulated at Világos in August. Thirteen Hungarian generals were executed at Arad. The young Franz Joseph had restored his rule throughout the empire, though at a high political cost.",
  "Austrian Empire|1850": "Prince Felix zu Schwarzenberg forced Prussia to abandon its plans for a union of German states at Olmütz and restored the German Confederation under Austrian leadership. Austria seemed again the dominant power in central Europe, governing through a centralised and absolutist administration.",
  "Austrian Empire|1855": "Austria stayed out of the Crimean War but pressed Russia to evacuate the Danubian principalities, losing Russian goodwill without gaining Britain and France as allies. A concordat with the papacy alienated liberals. The empire faced the coming crises diplomatically isolated and financially strained.",
  "Austrian Empire|1859": "France and Piedmont-Sardinia defeated Austria at Magenta and Solferino, and the empire lost Lombardy. The defeat discredited neo-absolutism, exposed weaknesses in command and finance, and forced Franz Joseph to begin constitutional experiments in order to win the support of his subjects.",
  "Austrian Empire|1861": "The February Patent created an imperial parliament with a centralist, German-liberal bias. Hungarian leaders boycotted it and demanded the restoration of their 1848 laws, and Czech and Polish politicians were dissatisfied. The empire remained a great power but lacked an accepted constitutional settlement.",
  "Austrian Empire|1866": "Prussia defeated Austria at Königgrätz on 3 July, and the Peace of Prague dissolved the German Confederation and excluded Austria from Germany. Victories over Italy at Custoza and Lissa could not prevent the loss of Venetia. The defeat made a compromise with Hungary unavoidable.",
  "Austrian Empire|1867": "The Compromise with Hungary made the Kingdom of Hungary an equal partner, and Franz Joseph was crowned King of Hungary in June. The December Constitution reorganised the western lands. The unitary Austrian Empire ceased to exist, replaced by the dual monarchy of Austria-Hungary.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1806": "Elector Frederick, Napoleon\u0027s ally, proclaimed himself King of Württemberg on 1 January 1806 and joined the Confederation of the Rhine. Mediatised counties, church lands and imperial cities were added to his realm, and as a sovereign kingdom it stood far above the old duchy, though dependent on French favour.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1810": "The acquisition of Ulm and other districts from Bavaria completed the kingdom\u0027s territory, roughly double the size of the old duchy. Frederick ruled autocratically, having abolished the traditional estates, and built a centralised administration that unified Lutheran and Catholic regions under the crown.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1812": "Württemberg sent roughly 15,000 men with Napoleon into Russia, and only a few hundred are said to have returned. The catastrophe drained the small kingdom\u0027s manpower and showed the costs of its dependence on France, while its territorial gains still depended on Napoleon\u0027s survival.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1813": "After the Battle of Leipzig Frederick changed sides, and the Treaty of Fulda guaranteed his sovereignty and possessions. Württemberg troops fought against France in 1814, and the kingdom passed through the end of the Napoleonic era with its enlarged territory and royal title intact.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1819": "William I and the estates agreed a constitution creating a two-chamber parliament, guaranteeing civil rights and an independent judiciary. Recovery from the famine of 1816–1817 was under way, with new agricultural and charitable institutions, and Württemberg became a leading constitutional state of the German Confederation.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1834": "Württemberg entered the Zollverein, gaining free access to a large German market. Under William I\u0027s long reign the kingdom enjoyed stable constitutional government, sound finances and gradual economic improvement, and as a sovereign member of the German Confederation it stood at the height of its standing.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1848": "Revolution spread to Württemberg in March, and William I appointed a liberal ministry under Friedrich Römer. In 1849 Württemberg troops dispersed the rump of the Frankfurt Parliament in Stuttgart. The kingdom weathered the crisis, though conservative government and political frustration followed the revolution.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1864": "When William I died after a reign of nearly fifty years, Württemberg was a prosperous, well-governed middle state with expanding railways and industry. Its sovereignty was still intact, but the rivalry between Austria and Prussia threatened the independence of all the smaller German states.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1866": "Württemberg fought on Austria\u0027s side and was defeated at Tauberbischofsheim. The German Confederation was dissolved, and the kingdom had to pay an indemnity and conclude a secret alliance with Prussia. Its independent foreign policy was effectively at an end for good.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1871": "After fighting alongside Prussia against France, Württemberg joined the German Empire under the November Treaties of 1870. It kept its king, army administration, posts and railways, but lost sovereignty in foreign and military affairs and became a federal state of the new empire.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1891": "William II, the last king, succeeded his uncle Charles. Within the empire, Württemberg prospered as Daimler, Bosch and other firms made the Stuttgart region a centre of engineering, but its political weight depended on its place in the federal system rather than on independent power.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1906": "Constitutional reform made the lower chamber of parliament wholly elected, removing privileged members to the upper chamber. Württemberg had a reputation as one of the most liberal and orderly German states, with a popular king, but its role remained that of a member state of the empire.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1914": "The First World War brought the Württemberg army corps to both fronts, with heavy casualties and growing shortages at home. The monarchy was respected, but the war\u0027s strain undermined the political order of all German states and prepared the way for revolution.",
  "Kingdom of Württemberg|1918": "Revolution reached Stuttgart in November 1918. King William II left the capital on 9 November and renounced the throne on 30 November, ending the monarchy. The kingdom became the Free People\u0027s State of Württemberg, a republic within Germany, and the Kingdom of Württemberg ceased to exist.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1806": "Landgrave Louis X joined the Confederation of the Rhine and became Grand Duke Louis I, gaining sovereignty and mediatised neighbouring lands. With the Duchy of Westphalia, acquired in 1803, his scattered state was much larger than the old landgraviate, though its existence depended on Napoleon\u0027s favour.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1812": "Hessian troops served Napoleon in Spain and marched with the Grand Army into Russia, where the contingent suffered heavy losses. The grand duchy\u0027s administration was being modernised and its new territories integrated, but its military obligations to France were a heavy burden for so small a state.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1813": "After the Battle of Leipzig Louis I abandoned Napoleon and joined the Allies, saving his throne and title. His territorial gains were not yet secure, however, since the coming peace settlement would decide which of his lands he could keep and which neighbours would be restored.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1816": "The Congress of Vienna settlement gave Hesse Rhenish Hesse with Mainz and Worms in exchange for Westphalia, and the state took the name Grand Duchy of Hesse and by Rhine. It now had a compact Rhine-Main core and a secure place among the middle states of the German Confederation.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1820": "Louis I issued a constitution creating a two-chamber parliament with rights over taxation and legislation, after a first restrictive draft provoked protest. The grand duchy joined the group of constitutional states in southern and western Germany, giving its varied provinces common institutions.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1828": "Hesse concluded a customs union with Prussia, a step towards the German Customs Union of 1834. Its finances and administration were sound, and as a sovereign member of the German Confederation with a working constitution, the grand duchy stood at the height of its standing.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1848": "Revolution forced the appointment of the liberal Heinrich von Gagern as chief minister, and he soon became president of the Frankfurt National Assembly. Louis III succeeded his father. The established order was shaken, and for a time the future of the small German states seemed uncertain.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1850": "Reinhard von Dalwigk became chief minister and restored conservative government, curbing the gains of 1848. The German Confederation was revived, and Hesse resumed its place as a sovereign middle state, following a pro-Austrian, anti-Prussian course that would shape its fate in 1866.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1866": "Hesse fought on Austria\u0027s side and its division was defeated in the Main campaign. The peace cost it Hesse-Homburg and districts of Upper Hesse, and Upper Hesse had to enter the North German Confederation. The state was divided between two political systems and its sovereignty curtailed.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1871": "After fighting with Prussia against France, the grand duchy joined the German Empire. It kept its dynasty, parliament and administration, but foreign policy, war and its army passed under imperial and Prussian control, reducing it to a member state of modest weight.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1892": "Ernest Louis became grand duke. Within the empire Hesse prospered, with growing industry at Darmstadt, Mainz, Offenbach and Rüsselsheim, and its dynasty was linked to Britain and Russia, but its political power was limited to the affairs of a federal state.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1899": "Ernest Louis founded the Darmstadt Artists\u0027 Colony on the Mathildenhöhe, making his capital a centre of Jugendstil and modern design. The grand duchy\u0027s cultural prestige rose, though in political terms it remained one of the smaller states of the German Empire.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1914": "The First World War brought heavy casualties, food shortages and a painful conflict for a dynasty whose members included the Empress of Russia. Like other German monarchies, the grand duchy\u0027s government lost legitimacy as the war dragged on and defeat approached.",
  "Grand Duchy of Hesse|1918": "On 9 November 1918 the workers\u0027 and soldiers\u0027 council in Darmstadt declared Ernest Louis deposed. He never abdicated, but the monarchy was at an end, and the grand duchy was replaced by the People\u0027s State of Hesse, a republic within Germany.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1806": "Elector Maximilian IV Joseph became King Maximilian I Joseph of Bavaria and joined the Confederation of the Rhine. Tyrol, Vorarlberg, Franconian church lands and imperial cities had been added to his state, which was now fully sovereign and the strongest of Napoleon\u0027s German allies.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1809": "Austria attacked Bavaria, and in Tyrol Andreas Hofer led a popular rising against Bavarian rule that Bavarian troops could only put down with French help. Napoleon\u0027s victory saved the kingdom, but the revolt revealed the weakness of Bavarian authority in its newly acquired lands.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1810": "The settlement after Napoleon\u0027s victory over Austria brought Bavaria Salzburg, the Innviertel, Bayreuth and Regensburg, though it ceded South Tyrol and Ulm. The kingdom reached its greatest territorial extent, and Montgelas\u0027s centralising reforms were transforming the administration of its varied old and new provinces.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1812": "Bavaria sent a corps of some 30,000 men with Napoleon into Russia, and very few returned. The disaster cost the kingdom most of its army and turned public opinion against the French alliance, leaving Bavaria exposed as Napoleon\u0027s empire began to collapse.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1813": "By the Treaty of Ried Bavaria joined the Allies in October 1813, securing a guarantee of its sovereignty. General Wrede\u0027s attempt to block Napoleon\u0027s retreat ended in defeat at Hanau, but Bavaria had placed itself on the winning side before the final campaigns.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1816": "The Treaty of Munich returned Salzburg and the Innviertel to Austria, after Tyrol and Vorarlberg, but confirmed Würzburg and Aschaffenburg, gained in 1814, and added the Rhenish Palatinate. The kingdom\u0027s lasting shape was fixed: the second largest German state, a leading member of the German Confederation.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1818": "The constitution of 26 May 1818 created a two-chamber parliament and guaranteed basic rights, following a concordat with the papacy in 1817. Bavaria became one of the first constitutional monarchies in Germany, combining the reformed Montgelas administration with representative institutions.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1825": "Ludwig I succeeded his father and began to make Munich a capital of art and learning, moving the university there in 1826. Sovereign within the German Confederation, financially sound and culturally ambitious, the kingdom stood at the height of its independent standing.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1832": "Ludwig I\u0027s son Otto became King of Greece, extending Wittelsbach influence to south-eastern Europe. In the same year the Hambach Festival in the Bavarian Palatinate called for German unity and liberty, and Ludwig turned to repression, revealing tensions between the monarchy and its liberal subjects.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1848": "Revolution and the scandal over Lola Montez forced Ludwig I to abdicate in March in favour of Maximilian II, who granted liberal reforms. Bavaria remained intact and sovereign, but the debate over German unification under Prussian or Austrian leadership threatened its independent position.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1864": "Ludwig II became king at eighteen. Bavaria was a prosperous, sovereign middle state that sought to balance between Austria and Prussia, but the growing conflict between the two great powers left the southern German states with diminishing room for an independent policy.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1866": "Bavaria fought on Austria\u0027s side and its army was defeated at Kissingen. The peace imposed an indemnity, minor territorial losses and a secret alliance with Prussia. The German Confederation was dissolved, and Bavaria\u0027s freedom of action in foreign affairs was largely lost.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1871": "After fighting with Prussia against France, Bavaria joined the German Empire with extensive reserved rights, including command of its army in peacetime, its own railways, posts and legations. It became the second state of the empire but no longer a sovereign power.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1886": "Ludwig II was declared unfit to rule and died days later in Lake Starnberg. Prince Regent Luitpold governed for the insane King Otto. The regency brought stability and growth, and Munich flourished as an art city, but political weight lay in Berlin.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1913": "A constitutional change allowed the regent Ludwig to become King Ludwig III, ending the long regency. Bavaria was prosperous and influential within the empire, but the coming war would expose the limits of its autonomy and the fragility of the German monarchies.",
  "Kingdom of Bavaria|1918": "After four years of war, Kurt Eisner proclaimed a republic in Munich on 7 November 1918 and Ludwig III fled. The king released his officials from their oath at Anif, ending more than seven centuries of Wittelsbach rule, and Bavaria became a free state.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1815": "The Congress of Vienna created the grand duchy and gave it to William I of the Netherlands in personal union. It was also a member of the German Confederation, with a Prussian garrison in its capital, so it had little independent weight: a fortress and a dynastic possession more than a sovereign state.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1839": "The Treaty of London ended the Belgian crisis by partitioning the country. The larger western half went to Belgium, leaving a small, poor and largely agricultural grand duchy of about 2,600 square kilometres, still ruled from The Hague and garrisoned by Prussia, which joined the German Zollverein three years later.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1867": "Napoleon III’s attempt to buy Luxembourg triggered the Luxembourg Crisis. The Treaty of London of 1867 made the grand duchy perpetually neutral under a great-power guarantee, removed the Prussian garrison and ordered the fortress demolished, giving the country a clearer international identity although it remained tied to the Dutch crown and the German customs union.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1890": "The death of William III ended the personal union with the Netherlands, and Adolphe of Nassau-Weilburg became grand duke, giving Luxembourg its own dynasty. Steel production on southern iron ore was growing quickly after 1879, so the country was more prosperous and more fully sovereign than at any earlier point.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1914": "Germany occupied neutral Luxembourg in August 1914 and held it until the end of the First World War. The government and Grand Duchess Marie-Adélaïde stayed in place, but the occupation exposed the emptiness of the 1867 guarantee and compromised the dynasty, leaving the state’s standing at a low point.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1921": "After the war a referendum kept the monarchy under Grand Duchess Charlotte, universal suffrage was introduced, and Luxembourg left the Zollverein. The Belgium–Luxembourg Economic Union of 1921 gave it a new economic partner, while the ARBED steel group made it one of Europe’s significant steel producers in proportion to its size.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1940": "Germany invaded on 10 May 1940. The grand ducal family and the government fled into exile, and the Nazi regime annexed the country in all but name, banned French, imposed German law and later conscripted Luxembourgers into the Wehrmacht. The state survived only as a government in exile in London.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1944": "American troops liberated most of Luxembourg in September 1944, though the north was devastated in the Battle of the Bulge that winter. The returning government had already signed the Benelux customs convention in exile and was preparing to abandon neutrality, laying the basis for a new international role.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1952": "Luxembourg, a founding member of NATO in 1949, became the seat of the High Authority of the European Coal and Steel Community in 1952. Hosting a European institution gave the small country unusual diplomatic weight, while its steel industry flourished within the new common market for coal and steel.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1985": "The Schengen Agreement on open borders was signed at the Luxembourg village of Schengen, and the satellite operator SES was founded. Luxembourg had absorbed the steel crisis through tripartite negotiation and was turning into a major centre for banking and investment funds, with Gaston Thorn just finishing his term as Commission president.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|1999": "Luxembourg adopted the euro, the realisation of the monetary union first sketched in the Werner Report of 1970, and Jacques Santer had presided over the European Commission until that year. The financial sector now dominated the economy, and the country ranked among the richest in the world per head.",
  "Grand Duchy of Luxembourg|2025": "Luxembourg stands at its peak of prosperity and influence: a wealthy EU founding member hosting the Court of Justice and other institutions, a leading investment-fund centre and home of ArcelorMittal and SES. Grand Duke Guillaume succeeded Henri in 2025 at the head of a stable parliamentary monarchy of about 680,000 people.",
  "German Confederation|1815": "The Federal Act of June 1815 created a league of 39 states and free cities under Austrian presidency, with a permanent Diet in Frankfurt. It had no executive and limited powers, but it had the backing of the great powers and of both Austria and Prussia, which gave the new order a solid start.",
  "German Confederation|1819": "After the murder of August von Kotzebue, Metternich used the Diet to pass the Carlsbad Decrees, imposing censorship, university supervision and a central investigating commission at Mainz. The league showed it could act decisively when Austria and Prussia agreed, though its strength was used chiefly to repress liberal and national movements.",
  "German Confederation|1820": "The Vienna Final Act completed the federal constitution, defining the Diet’s powers, the rules for federal executions against disobedient members and the monarchic principle. The military constitution followed in 1821, organising the federal army in ten corps. The Confederation now had its full institutional form and the united support of its leading members.",
  "German Confederation|1832": "After the Hambach Festival, where some 20,000 or more people demanded national unity and freedom, the Diet passed the Six Articles and further repressive measures. The Metternich system was at its height, and the two great powers still acted together, though the Prussian-led Zollverein, founded in 1834, was about to shift economic weight towards Berlin.",
  "German Confederation|1840": "The Rhine Crisis, when France seemed to threaten the left bank of the Rhine, produced a surge of German patriotism and agreement to build new federal fortresses at Ulm and Rastatt. With Austria and Prussia cooperating and the public rallying to common defence, the Confederation stood at the peak of its cohesion and purpose.",
  "German Confederation|1848": "The March revolutions swept the member states. The Diet adopted the black, red and gold colours, but a national parliament met in St Paul’s Church in Frankfurt, and in July the Diet handed its authority to the Imperial Regent, Archduke John. The Confederation’s institutions were effectively suspended in favour of a would-be national state.",
  "German Confederation|1849": "The Frankfurt Parliament adopted a national constitution in March, but Frederick William IV of Prussia refused the imperial crown, and the remnant parliament was dispersed. Revolutionary uprisings in Saxony, the Palatinate and Baden were crushed by Prussian troops. The old league remained in abeyance while Prussia pursued its own Erfurt Union.",
  "German Confederation|1850": "Austria convened a rump Diet in Frankfurt, and the rivalry with Prussia over the Erfurt Union and Hesse-Kassel brought the two powers to the brink of war. Prussia backed down in the Punctation of Olmütz in November, opening the way to restore the Confederation, but its revival was not yet complete.",
  "German Confederation|1851": "After the Dresden conferences failed to reform the league, the Confederation was restored in its old form and the Diet repealed the basic rights of 1848. It functioned again as an instrument of conservative order, but Prussia’s new envoy, Otto von Bismarck, now openly challenged Austrian leadership in Frankfurt.",
  "German Confederation|1859": "Austria’s defeat by France and Piedmont in Italy weakened the presiding power, while Prussia mobilised but refused to place its forces under federal command on Austria’s terms. The episode revealed the federal military system’s dependence on the two great powers and revived national agitation through the new National Association.",
  "German Confederation|1863": "Austria’s plan for federal reform failed at the Frankfurt Congress of Princes, which Prussia under Bismarck boycotted. In December Saxon and Hanoverian troops carried out a federal execution in Holstein against Denmark, one of the league’s few military actions, but Austria and Prussia were preparing to act independently of it.",
  "German Confederation|1864": "Austria and Prussia fought the Second Schleswig War against Denmark on their own account, bypassing the Diet, and took Schleswig, Holstein and Lauenburg into joint possession by the Treaty of Vienna. The Confederation was reduced to a bystander in the decisive German question of the day.",
  "German Confederation|1866": "When the Diet voted to mobilise against Prussia over Holstein, Prussia declared the Confederation dissolved and went to war. Its victory at Königgrätz decided the conflict, and the Peace of Prague confirmed the end of the league. The Diet held its last session in Augsburg in August 1866.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1830": "Revolt broke out in Brussels in August, Dutch troops were driven from the city in the September Days, and a provisional government declared independence on 4 October. The new state had no recognised frontiers, no king and no army able to resist the Netherlands without help, so its position depended on the great powers.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1831": "A National Congress adopted a liberal constitution in February and Leopold of Saxe-Coburg took the oath as king in July. When the Dutch invaded in the Ten Days’ Campaign, Belgium survived only through French intervention, but the London Conference had recognised its independence and the new monarchy gave it diplomatic standing.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1839": "William I finally accepted the Treaty of London, which recognised Belgium, fixed its borders and guaranteed its neutrality, at the cost of half of Limburg and Luxembourg. Belgium was now secure and was industrialising fast, with the first continental steam passenger railway and growing coal and iron industries in Wallonia.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1885": "The great powers recognised Leopold II’s Congo Free State, a vast African territory ruled by the king personally. At home Belgium was one of the most industrialised countries in the world, with Antwerp a great port, although the strikes and riots of 1886 were about to expose the misery of its workers.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1908": "Belgium annexed the Congo from Leopold II after international outrage at the Free State’s atrocities, becoming a colonial power with a territory nearly eighty times its own size. Its industry, banks and railway companies were active across Europe, Russia and China, and universal male suffrage, though plural, had broadened its politics.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1913": "On the eve of the First World War Belgium was among the leading industrial and trading economies of Europe in proportion to its size, with a large colony, a busy port at Antwerp and a stable constitutional monarchy under Albert I. Its neutrality still appeared guaranteed by the great powers.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1914": "Germany invaded on 4 August after Belgium refused passage to its armies. Liège fell to siege artillery, civilians were massacred at Dinant and Leuven, and by October only a strip of land behind the Yser remained free. Most of the country endured four years of occupation, requisitions and deportations of workers.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1919": "Victorious and celebrated for its resistance, Belgium gained Eupen and Malmedy and the mandate of Ruanda-Urundi under the Treaty of Versailles, introduced equal male suffrage and abandoned neutrality for an alliance with France. Reconstruction was rapid, though Walloon heavy industry was beginning to age.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1940": "Germany invaded again on 10 May and Leopold III surrendered the army after eighteen days, against his ministers’ advice. The country was occupied and placed under a German military administration, while the government continued the war from London with the resources of the Belgian Congo, leaving the state divided and weakened.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1944": "Allied forces liberated most of Belgium in September 1944, though Antwerp was struck by V-weapons and the Ardennes was devastated in the Battle of the Bulge. Belgium emerged relatively intact economically, with its Congo resources, including uranium, valuable to the Allies, but the Royal Question already divided the country.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1958": "Belgium hosted the Brussels World’s Fair, Expo 58, with its Atomium, as Brussels became the provisional seat of the new European Economic Community. With a prosperous economy, a large colony and a central role in NATO and European integration, the kingdom stood near the height of its international standing.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1960": "The Congo became independent on 30 June after a hurried transition and fell at once into mutiny, secession and United Nations intervention. The loss of the colony, followed by Ruanda-Urundi in 1962, and the great strike of 1960–61 in Wallonia marked the end of Belgium’s colonial power and of its old industrial balance.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|1993": "The constitution declared Belgium a federal state of communities and regions, the outcome of decades of conflict between Dutch and French speakers. Flanders had become the wealthier region, Brussels hosted the European Union and NATO, and the Maastricht Treaty deepened the integration Belgium had promoted since the 1950s.",
  "Kingdom of Belgium|2025": "Belgium remains a prosperous federal monarchy under King Philippe, with about 11.8 million people, Brussels the principal seat of the European Union and NATO, and Antwerp one of Europe’s largest ports. Its influence rests on European institutions rather than colonial or industrial weight, and government formation remains slow and complex.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1832": "The great powers made Otto of Bavaria king of a small, impoverished state of the Peloponnese, Central Greece and the Cyclades, bounded by the Arta–Volos line. Devastated by a decade of war and dependent on foreign loans and protection, it held only a minority of the Greek people.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1864": "Otto had been deposed in 1862, and the new king, George I, brought a gift from Britain: the cession of the Ionian Islands. A new constitution introduced universal male suffrage, and the kingdom gained territory, population and a more stable dynasty, though it remained poor and politically turbulent.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1881": "Under pressure from the great powers after the Congress of Berlin, the Ottoman Empire ceded Thessaly and the district of Arta. The fertile plain of Thessaly enlarged the kingdom considerably, and Charilaos Trikoupis’s governments were building roads, railways and the Corinth Canal with foreign loans.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1897": "The brief war with the Ottoman Empire over Crete ended in rapid defeat in Thessaly. Already bankrupt since 1893, Greece had to pay an indemnity and accept an International Financial Commission controlling part of its revenues, a low point for the army, the dynasty and national confidence.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1913": "Victory in the Balkan Wars brought Macedonia with Thessaloniki, Epirus, Crete and the eastern Aegean islands, increasing the kingdom’s area and population by roughly two-thirds. George I was assassinated in Thessaloniki, but under Venizelos Greece had become a significant regional power.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1920": "The Treaty of Sèvres assigned Eastern Thrace to Greece and the administration of Smyrna, with Western Thrace already Greek. The Great Idea seemed close to fulfilment, and the kingdom reached its greatest extent and international standing, though its army was overstretched in Anatolia and Venizelos lost the November election.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1922": "The Turkish nationalist offensive routed the Greek army in Asia Minor, Smyrna burned, and over a million refugees fled to Greece. A military revolution forced Constantine I to abdicate and executed six leading figures. The Great Idea was dead, and the kingdom faced economic and social catastrophe.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1924": "The Second Hellenic Republic was proclaimed in March and confirmed by plebiscite, ending the monarchy for eleven years. Greece kept its post-Lausanne frontiers and was absorbing the refugees, but the kingdom as such had ceased to exist, its dynasty discredited and in exile.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1935": "After repeated coups and an unsuccessful Venizelist revolt, General Kondylis abolished the republic and a dubious plebiscite recalled George II. The restored monarchy ruled a country weakened by the Depression and deeply divided, and in 1936 the king allowed Ioannis Metaxas to establish a dictatorship.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1940": "Greece rejected the Italian ultimatum of 28 October and its army drove the invaders back deep into Albania, widely hailed as the first Allied land victory against the Axis. The kingdom enjoyed great prestige, but it was a small country fighting at the limit of its resources.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1941": "Germany invaded in April and took Athens and, in May, Crete. Greece was divided into German, Italian and Bulgarian zones, the king and government fled into exile, and the famine of the following winter killed tens of thousands. The kingdom survived only as a government in exile.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1949": "The Civil War ended with the defeat of the communist Democratic Army in the Grammos and Vitsi mountains. The monarchy, restored by plebiscite in 1946, now ruled a devastated and bitterly divided country dependent on American aid under the Truman Doctrine, but it had gained the Dodecanese in 1947.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1962": "Under Konstantinos Karamanlis Greece had joined NATO in 1952, enjoyed rapid growth and in 1962 its association agreement with the European Economic Community came into force. The monarchy appeared stable and the country more prosperous than ever before, though the political system excluded the left.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1967": "Constantine II’s clash with George Papandreou had produced two years of instability when colonels seized power on 21 April. The king’s failed counter-coup in December sent him into exile, leaving the monarchy a hollow form under a military regime isolated in Europe.",
  "Kingdom of Greece|1973": "With the king in exile, Georgios Papadopoulos abolished the monarchy on 1 June 1973 and proclaimed a republic, confirmed by a controlled referendum. The kingdom ended, and the free referendum of December 1974, after the junta’s collapse, confirmed the republic by a clear majority.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1839": "William I accepted Belgian independence in the Treaty of London after nine years of costly mobilisation. The kingdom was reduced to the old northern provinces and half of Limburg, burdened with debt and politically stagnant, but it retained the profitable East Indies, where the cultivation system was swelling state revenues.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1848": "Amid the revolutions of 1848 William II accepted Thorbecke’s constitutional revision, introducing ministerial responsibility, direct elections and civil liberties. The Netherlands gained a stable parliamentary order without revolution, while colonial profits from Java under the cultivation system helped to restore the state’s finances.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1870": "The cultivation system was being dismantled and Java opened to private plantations under the Agrarian Law of 1870. New canals and railways were modernising the economy, Rotterdam and Amsterdam were gaining direct access to the sea, and parliament had established that cabinets needed its confidence.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1890": "William III died, ending the personal union with Luxembourg, and the ten-year-old Wilhelmina succeeded under the regency of Queen Emma. Industry was finally growing, Royal Dutch had just been founded to exploit oil in Sumatra, and Dutch rule was spreading across the outer islands of the Indies.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1914": "The Netherlands stayed neutral in the First World War, keeping its army mobilised for four years. Trade suffered from blockade and shortages, but the country avoided invasion, its Indies empire was intact and largely pacified after the Aceh War, and the Peace Palace had just opened in The Hague.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1929": "The kingdom stood at the height of its colonial wealth, with the Indies a major producer of oil, rubber, sugar and tin and the home economy prosperous and internationally invested. Universal suffrage and the Pacification of 1917 had settled domestic conflicts, though the Depression was about to strike hard.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1940": "Germany invaded on 10 May, bombed Rotterdam and forced capitulation within five days. Queen Wilhelmina and the government went to London, and the country was ruled by Reichskommissar Arthur Seyss-Inquart. Persecution of the Jews followed, while the Indies, cut off from the homeland, faced the growing threat of Japan.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1945": "Liberation came on 5 May after the Hunger Winter had killed some 20,000 people in the occupied west. Japan had held the East Indies since 1942, and Indonesian nationalists had declared independence. The country was impoverished, its infrastructure damaged and its empire on the point of slipping away.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1949": "After two military campaigns the Netherlands transferred sovereignty to Indonesia in December, losing the core of its empire. But reconstruction aided by the Marshall Plan was advancing, Benelux was in operation and the country was a founding member of NATO, laying the basis for a new European role.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1957": "The Netherlands signed the Treaty of Rome as a founding member of the European Economic Community, introduced a state old-age pension and was rebuilding its economy rapidly. Rotterdam was becoming the world’s busiest port, and the Delta Works were under way after the flood of 1953.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|1975": "Suriname became independent, ending the colonial empire except for the Caribbean islands. The Groningen gas field had made the Netherlands a major energy exporter, the welfare state was among the most generous in the world, and society had been transformed by secularisation and the end of pillarisation.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|2000": "The polder model had produced strong growth and low unemployment, the euro had been introduced and The Hague hosted international courts and tribunals. The Netherlands was among the most prosperous and internationally connected countries in the world, with Rotterdam Europe’s largest port and Dutch multinationals operating worldwide.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|2010": "The Netherlands Antilles were dissolved, leaving Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten as countries within the Kingdom. The financial crisis had forced the rescue of major banks, and political fragmentation over immigration and Europe made coalitions harder to build, though the economy remained one of Europe’s strongest.",
  "Kingdom of the Netherlands|2025": "The Netherlands remains a wealthy constitutional monarchy of about 18 million people under King Willem-Alexander, a leading exporter and logistics hub, a founding EU and NATO member and host of international courts. Its political landscape is fragmented, but its economic and diplomatic weight remains close to its highest level.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1861": "The parliament in Turin proclaimed Victor Emmanuel II king of Italy after the conquests and plebiscites of 1859–1860. The new state already ruled most of the peninsula and some 22 million people, but it lacked Venetia and Rome, faced brigandage in the south and was poor, largely illiterate and administratively improvised.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1866": "Alliance with Prussia in the Austro-Prussian War brought Venetia into the kingdom, although Italian forces were beaten at Custoza and at Lissa. The territorial gain raised Italy’s standing, but the defeats exposed military weaknesses, and the capital had moved to Florence while Rome remained under papal rule with French protection.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1871": "Rome, taken from the Papal States in September 1870, became the capital. Unification of the peninsula was largely complete, and Italy began to count as a European power. The Law of Guarantees regulated relations with the pope, who nonetheless refused to recognise the kingdom, leaving a lasting rift with the Church.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1882": "Italy joined Germany and Austria-Hungary in the Triple Alliance, ending its diplomatic isolation after France seized Tunisia. Under Agostino Depretis the franchise was widened and railways and the navy expanded. Italy stood securely among the lesser great powers, with colonial ambitions beginning at Assab on the Red Sea coast, which the state took over that year.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1896": "The defeat of an Italian army by Emperor Menelik II at the Battle of Adwa ended Francesco Crispi’s attempt to conquer Ethiopia and brought down his government. The humiliation, followed by social unrest and the bloody repression in Milan in 1898, marked a low point of the liberal state’s prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1912": "Victory in the Italo-Turkish War gave Italy Libya and the Dodecanese islands, while the Giolittian era had brought industrial growth in the north and near-universal male suffrage. The kingdom was more prosperous and confident than ever, although the colonial war was costly and Libyan resistance continued in the interior.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1915": "Having declared neutrality in 1914, Italy signed the Treaty of London with the Entente and entered the First World War in May 1915, hoping to gain Trentino, South Tyrol, Trieste and Dalmatia. The army began a long series of costly offensives against Austro-Hungarian positions along the Isonzo River.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1917": "The Austro-German breakthrough at Caporetto in October–November 1917 drove the Italian army back to the Piave with huge losses in prisoners and material. Luigi Cadorna was replaced by Armando Diaz. The front held, but the defeat shook the state and deepened social discontent after years of heavy casualties.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1918": "Victory at Vittorio Veneto in October–November 1918 completed the collapse of Austria-Hungary on the Italian front. Italy gained Trentino, South Tyrol, Trieste and Istria and sat with the victors in Paris, but the price was some 600,000 military dead, heavy debts and resentment that its gains fell short of promises.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1922": "After two years of strikes, factory occupations and Fascist violence, the March on Rome led Victor Emmanuel III to appoint Benito Mussolini prime minister. The state remained a recognised great power and its borders included Fiume from 1924, but parliamentary government was about to be dismantled.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1929": "The Lateran Treaty with the Holy See ended the conflict over Rome, created the Vatican City State and won the regime Catholic support. Mussolini’s dictatorship was consolidated, opposition parties had been abolished, and the regime enjoyed a high point of domestic acceptance and international respectability.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1936": "The conquest of Ethiopia, completed with the capture of Addis Ababa in May 1936, allowed Mussolini to proclaim an Italian empire. League of Nations sanctions had failed, and the regime reached the summit of its prestige and territorial extent, while intervention in Spain and the Rome–Berlin Axis followed.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1939": "Italy occupied Albania in April 1939, united it with the Italian crown and signed the Pact of Steel with Germany in May. The kingdom ruled a colonial empire in Libya and East Africa, but its armed forces and economy were poorly prepared for a major war, and it declared non-belligerence in September.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1940": "Italy entered the Second World War in June 1940, expecting a quick victory after the fall of France. Its invasion of Greece in October failed, the British air attack at Taranto crippled the battle fleet, and in December British forces began destroying the Italian army in Libya.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1941": "Italy lost Italian East Africa to British and Commonwealth forces and depended on German intervention in North Africa and the Balkans. The defeat at Cape Matapan weakened the navy. Although Italy annexed parts of Yugoslavia and occupied much of Greece under German protection, it had become a junior partner within the Axis.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1943": "The loss of North Africa, defeat on the Don and the Allied invasion of Sicily led to Mussolini’s fall in July. The armistice of September left the kingdom divided: Germany occupied the centre and north, Mussolini’s puppet republic ruled there, and the royal government survived only in the Allied-held south.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1945": "Northern Italy was liberated in April 1945 by the Allied offensive and partisan insurrections, and Mussolini was executed. The kingdom had lost its empire and its army, was occupied and devastated, and the monarchy was compromised by its long association with Fascism and the flight from Rome in 1943.",
  "Kingdom of Italy|1946": "Victor Emmanuel III abdicated in May in favour of Umberto II, but the referendum of 2 June 1946 produced a majority of about 54 per cent for a republic. Umberto II left for exile in Portugal, and the Kingdom of Italy came to an end after 85 years.",
  "Russian SFSR|1917": "The Bolsheviks seized power in Petrograd in the October Revolution and formed the Council of People’s Commissars under Lenin. The new regime controlled the capital, Moscow and parts of central Russia, but the army was disintegrating, the war with Germany continued and much of the former empire rejected Soviet rule.",
  "Russian SFSR|1918": "The Treaty of Brest-Litovsk surrendered vast western territories, and the Civil War began as the Czechoslovak Legion revolted and White armies formed. The government moved to Moscow and adopted its first constitution, but at times controlled little more than the central Russian heartland around Moscow.",
  "Russian SFSR|1920": "The Red Army had defeated Kolchak and Denikin and, in November, drove Wrangel’s last White army from Crimea. Soviet rule was restored in much of Ukraine, the Caucasus and Central Asia, although the advance into Poland was repulsed at the Battle of Warsaw in August.",
  "Russian SFSR|1921": "The Civil War was essentially won and the Treaty of Riga fixed the western border. The RSFSR was a sovereign state, dominant among the Soviet republics, but War Communism had wrecked the economy: the Kronstadt revolt and peasant risings forced the New Economic Policy, and famine struck the Volga.",
  "Russian SFSR|1922": "On 30 December 1922 the RSFSR joined Ukraine, Byelorussia and the Transcaucasian Federation in founding the Soviet Union. It remained by far the largest member, but foreign affairs, defence and key economic powers passed to Union institutions, reducing the republic’s own standing as a state.",
  "Russian SFSR|1924": "Lenin’s death and the first Union constitution confirmed the RSFSR as a constituent republic. The national delimitation of Central Asia removed the Turkestan autonomous republic, which was divided among new Union republics. The New Economic Policy brought recovery in agriculture and trade.",
  "Russian SFSR|1928": "The first five-year plan began a crash programme of industrialisation, much of it on the republic’s territory in the Urals, the Volga region and Siberia. The RSFSR’s economic weight within the Union grew rapidly, at the cost of forced collectivisation and famine from 1929 onwards.",
  "Russian SFSR|1936": "The new Soviet constitution raised the Kazakh and Kirghiz autonomous republics to Union republics, removing large territories from the RSFSR, which adopted its own new constitution and present name in 1937. Industrial growth had made it a major industrial region, but the Great Terror was about to begin.",
  "Russian SFSR|1941": "Germany invaded on 22 June 1941. By the end of the year the Wehrmacht occupied much of western Russia, Leningrad was besieged and Moscow threatened. Industry was evacuated eastwards, and the deportations of the Volga Germans and later other peoples began.",
  "Russian SFSR|1945": "Victory over Germany left the RSFSR devastated in the west but with a greatly expanded industrial base in the Urals and Siberia. Tuva had been annexed in 1944 and the Kaliningrad region followed in 1946, and the republic was the core of a superpower controlling Eastern Europe.",
  "Russian SFSR|1954": "Post-war reconstruction was complete and the economy grew quickly. Crimea was transferred from the RSFSR to the Ukrainian SSR to mark the 300th anniversary of the Pereiaslav agreement, and Nikita Khrushchev’s reforms began to ease the Gulag system and Stalinist terror.",
  "Russian SFSR|1961": "Yuri Gagarin’s spaceflight, launched from Baikonur in the Kazakh SSR, crowned a period of rapid growth and scientific prestige, with research centres such as Akademgorodok near Novosibirsk on the republic’s territory. The RSFSR stood at a high point of its economic and demographic weight within the Union.",
  "Russian SFSR|1985": "When Mikhail Gorbachev came to power, the economy was stagnating, oil revenues were falling and the war in Afghanistan continued. The RSFSR still had about half the Soviet population and most of its resources, but its own institutions remained subordinate and largely symbolic.",
  "Russian SFSR|1990": "Competitive elections created a Congress of People’s Deputies, which chose Boris Yeltsin as chairman and on 12 June 1990 declared state sovereignty, asserting the supremacy of Russian laws over Union legislation. For the first time since 1922 the republic acted as a political force in its own right.",
  "Russian SFSR|1991": "Yeltsin was elected president in June and led resistance to the August coup, after which the RSFSR took over Union ministries and assets. The Belovezha Accords dissolved the Soviet Union, and on 25 December the republic was renamed the Russian Federation, continuing the Soviet Union’s international status.",
  "Republic of Finland|1917": "Parliament declared independence on 6 December 1917 and Soviet Russia recognised it at the end of the year. The new state had a parliament, a civil service and courts inherited from the Grand Duchy, but no army, and Russian troops and Red Guards remained on its soil as tensions rose.",
  "Republic of Finland|1918": "The Civil War between Reds and Whites killed some 36,000 people, many of them in executions and prison camps after the White victory. German troops took Helsinki, and Finland came under German influence, electing a German prince as king, until Germany’s defeat left the country isolated and divided.",
  "Republic of Finland|1920": "The republican constitution of 1919 was in force, and the Treaty of Tartu with Soviet Russia fixed the eastern border and gave Finland Petsamo on the Arctic Ocean. Membership of the League of Nations followed, and in 1921 the League confirmed Finnish sovereignty over the Åland Islands.",
  "Republic of Finland|1932": "The failed Mäntsälä rebellion ended the threat from the far-right Lapua Movement, and democracy survived when much of Europe turned authoritarian. The Depression had hit the export-dependent forest industry, but land reform had created a stable smallholder society and the economy soon recovered.",
  "Republic of Finland|1939": "Finland was a stable democracy with a growing economy and a well-trained conscript army, preparing to host the Olympic Games. In October the Soviet Union demanded territory on the Karelian Isthmus and bases; Finland refused, and on 30 November the Red Army attacked, beginning the Winter War.",
  "Republic of Finland|1940": "The Moscow Peace Treaty of March 1940 ended the Winter War. Finland kept its independence and won international admiration, but ceded about a tenth of its territory, including Viipuri, its second city, and had to resettle over 400,000 Karelians in the rest of the country.",
  "Republic of Finland|1944": "The Soviet offensive of June 1944 was halted, but the armistice of September restored the 1940 border, ceded Petsamo, leased Porkkala near Helsinki to the Soviet Union and required heavy reparations. Finland then had to fight its former German allies in Lapland, which retreating troops devastated.",
  "Republic of Finland|1948": "The Treaty of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance with the Soviet Union defined Finland’s postwar position. Finland remained a democracy with a market economy outside the Soviet bloc, though its foreign policy was constrained. Reparations, completed in 1952, were driving the growth of engineering industries.",
  "Republic of Finland|1956": "The Soviet Union returned Porkkala, Finland had joined the United Nations and the Nordic Council in 1955, and Urho Kekkonen began his 26-year presidency. The country was industrialising quickly, its population was moving from farms to towns, and the Olympic Games of 1952 had marked its recovery from the war.",
  "Republic of Finland|1975": "Helsinki hosted the Conference on Security and Co-operation in Europe, whose Final Act was a high point of Kekkonen’s neutral diplomacy. Finland had become an industrial welfare state, trading both with the West through free-trade agreements and with the Soviet Union, though Soviet influence on domestic politics remained.",
  "Republic of Finland|1991": "The collapse of the Soviet Union removed a major trading partner and, with a domestic banking crisis, threw Finland into its deepest peacetime depression. Output fell sharply and unemployment soared, but the end of Soviet power also freed Finland to orient itself fully towards Western Europe.",
  "Republic of Finland|1995": "Finland joined the European Union on 1 January 1995 after a referendum in 1994. The economy was recovering strongly from the depression, led by exports of electronics, and membership anchored the country politically and economically in the West for the first time since independence.",
  "Republic of Finland|2000": "Finland had adopted the euro, and Nokia was the world’s largest maker of mobile phones, driving rapid growth and a large share of exports. A new constitution modernised government, Tarja Halonen became the first female president, and Finnish schools topped the first PISA comparisons published in 2001.",
  "Republic of Finland|2009": "The global financial crisis hit Finland hard, with output falling by about eight per cent in 2009. Nokia was losing its lead in the smartphone era, and the paper industry was shrinking. A long period of weak growth and strained public finances followed, though living standards remained high.",
  "Republic of Finland|2023": "After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Finland abandoned military non-alignment and joined NATO on 4 April 2023, securing the strongest guarantees of its security in its history. The economy remained sluggish, but the country was wealthy, stable and politically united on defence.",
  "Republic of Finland|2025": "Finland is a prosperous parliamentary democracy and member of the European Union and NATO, with President Alexander Stubb in office since 2024. Its security position is stronger than ever, while slow growth, an ageing population and tense relations with Russia along its long eastern border remain challenges.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1918": "The Taryba proclaimed independence on 16 February 1918, but the country remained under German military occupation until November. When the Germans withdrew, the new government had almost no army, money or administration, and the Red Army advanced into Lithuania, taking Vilnius in January 1919.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1920": "Lithuanian forces had defeated the Bermontians and, with the Soviet peace treaty of July, gained recognition of their claims to Vilnius. A Constituent Assembly was elected. In October, however, Polish troops under Żeligowski seized Vilnius, leaving Kaunas as temporary capital and a lasting conflict with Poland.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1923": "The seizure of the Klaipėda Region in January gave Lithuania a seaport, confirmed by the Klaipėda Convention of 1924. With a democratic constitution, the new litas currency, land reform and international recognition, the republic stood at its strongest point of the interwar period.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1926": "The military coup of December 1926 overthrew President Kazys Grinius and brought Antanas Smetona back to power. The state remained stable and its agriculture-based economy grew, but parliamentary democracy ended and the country entered fourteen years of authoritarian nationalist rule.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1938": "A Polish ultimatum forced Lithuania to establish diplomatic relations with Poland, abandoning its formal refusal to accept the loss of Vilnius. The economy and cultural life had developed considerably, with Kaunas a modern capital, but the country was diplomatically isolated between Poland, Germany and the Soviet Union.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1939": "Germany seized Klaipėda by ultimatum in March, depriving Lithuania of its port. In October the Soviet Union transferred Vilnius to Lithuania, but in exchange stationed troops in the country under a mutual assistance treaty, which left independence at Moscow’s mercy.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1940": "Following a Soviet ultimatum in June, the Red Army occupied Lithuania without resistance. After rigged elections the country was proclaimed a Soviet republic and annexed by the Soviet Union in August. President Smetona fled abroad, and the independent state ceased to exist in practice.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1944": "The Red Army reconquered Lithuania after three years of German occupation, during which the Holocaust destroyed almost all of Lithuanian Jewry. Soviet rule was restored, and the independent republic survived only as a legal claim upheld by diplomats in exile and by Western non-recognition.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1949": "The mass deportation of March 1949, part of a wave that sent well over 100,000 Lithuanians to Siberia and Central Asia, broke resistance to collectivisation. The Forest Brothers’ armed struggle was nearing its end, and no independent Lithuanian state existed within Lithuania itself.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1988": "Gorbachev’s reforms allowed the foundation of the Sąjūdis reform movement in June 1988. Mass rallies revived national symbols and demanded sovereignty. Lithuania was still a Soviet republic, but a movement capable of restoring the state had emerged with broad popular support.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1990": "On 11 March 1990 the newly elected Supreme Council under Vytautas Landsbergis declared the restoration of independence. Moscow answered with an economic blockade, and almost no foreign state recognised the declaration, so the restored republic existed largely in law rather than in fact.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1991": "Soviet troops killed fourteen civilians in Vilnius in January, but popular resistance held. After the failed August coup, recognition followed from Western states and, on 6 September, from the Soviet Union itself, and Lithuania joined the United Nations, becoming a fully independent state once more.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|1993": "The last Russian troops left Lithuania on 31 August 1993. A new constitution had been adopted in 1992, the litas was reintroduced, and the country was building institutions, a market economy and armed forces, though the economic transition was painful.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|2004": "Lithuania joined NATO on 29 March and the European Union on 1 May 2004, securing its independence through membership of the main Western institutions. The economy was growing rapidly, although emigration to other EU countries began to reduce the population.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|2015": "Lithuania adopted the euro on 1 January 2015, completing its integration into the European Union’s core institutions. After Russia’s annexation of Crimea in 2014 it reintroduced conscription, opened an LNG terminal at Klaipėda to end dependence on Russian gas, and expanded allied military presence.",
  "Republic of Lithuania|2025": "Lithuania is a member of the European Union, NATO and the eurozone with a high-income economy. After the NATO summit in Vilnius in 2023 and growing allied deployments, its security and prosperity stand at their highest, although emigration and an ageing population remain serious concerns.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1918": "The Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes was proclaimed on 1 December 1918. Serbia emerged among the victors of the First World War, but the new state had no agreed borders, faced Italian occupation along the Adriatic and resistance in Montenegro and Croatia, and joined war-ravaged regions with different laws and currencies.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1920": "The peace treaties of Saint-Germain, Neuilly and Trianon and the Treaty of Rapallo with Italy fixed most borders, although the Carinthian plebiscite went to Austria. The Little Entente with Czechoslovakia was concluded, agrarian reform was underway, and elections were held for a constituent assembly.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1921": "The Vidovdan Constitution created a centralised parliamentary monarchy, and Alexander became king on Peter I’s death in August. A single currency and administration were being built. The state was internationally secure, but Croatian opposition to centralism and the banning of the Communist Party showed the narrowness of its base.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1925": "Stjepan Radić’s Croatian Peasant Party recognised the monarchy and joined Pašić’s government, raising hopes of reconciliation between Serbs and Croats. The economy was recovering from the war, and the kingdom stood as one of the larger states of south-eastern Europe, backed by France and the Little Entente.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1928": "The shooting of Croatian deputies in parliament in June, and Radić’s death in August, provoked a deep crisis. Croatian representatives withdrew from Belgrade and demanded a federal reorganisation, and parliamentary government in the kingdom effectively broke down, leaving the state more divided than at any time since 1918.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1929": "King Alexander proclaimed a personal dictatorship on 6 January, banned national parties and in October renamed the state the Kingdom of Yugoslavia with nine new banovinas. Central authority was restored and order imposed, though at the price of driving opposition underground and abroad.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1931": "Alexander’s imposed constitution restored a parliament dominated by a single government party. The regime appeared stable, the administration had been unified and the king was a leading figure in Balkan diplomacy, though the Great Depression was collapsing farm prices and ruining many peasants, and Croatian opposition persisted.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1934": "Yugoslavia joined the Balkan Entente in February, but in October King Alexander was assassinated in Marseille by a Macedonian revolutionary working with the Ustaše. A regency under Prince Paul took over for the eleven-year-old Peter II, and the state lost its strong central figure.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1937": "Under Prime Minister Milan Stojadinović the economy recovered strongly, helped by trade with Germany, and treaties with Bulgaria and Italy eased old tensions. The kingdom enjoyed its most secure international position and fastest growth, although the concordat crisis with the Orthodox Church exposed internal divisions.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1939": "The Cvetković-Maček Agreement created the autonomous Banovina of Croatia, partly resolving the Croatian question, though Serbs and Slovenes resented it. But Germany had absorbed Austria and Czechoslovakia, Italy had seized Albania, and the outbreak of war in September left Yugoslavia neutral but dangerously exposed.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1940": "The fall of France removed Yugoslavia’s main ally, and Hungary, Romania and Bulgaria were drawn into the Axis camp. Italy’s attack on Greece brought war to the kingdom’s southern border, and the government came under mounting German pressure to align itself with the Axis.",
  "Kingdom of Yugoslavia|1941": "The signing of the Tripartite Pact in March was followed by the coup of 27 March and the Axis invasion of 6 April. The army surrendered on 17 April, King Peter II went into exile and the kingdom was partitioned among Germany, Italy, Hungary, Bulgaria and the Independent State of Croatia.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1918": "Independence was proclaimed in Prague on 28 October and Masaryk was elected president, but the borders were not yet secure. Troops were still occupying the German-speaking borderlands, Slovakia was contested with Hungary and the new state had no settled constitution, so its power was real but unconsolidated.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1920": "The constitution of February 1920 established a parliamentary republic, and the Treaties of Saint-Germain and Trianon fixed its borders, adding Subcarpathian Ruthenia. The dispute over Teschen with Poland was settled by partition. Czechoslovakia now controlled much of the former Habsburg industry and joined Yugoslavia and Romania in the Little Entente.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1924": "A treaty of alliance with France in 1924 anchored Czechoslovakia in the French security system, and Beneš was a leading figure at the League of Nations. Currency reform had stabilised the crown, and coalition government under the Pětka gave political continuity. The republic was becoming a respected middle power.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1929": "Before the Great Depression the First Republic reached its economic high point, with industrial output at its interwar peak and strong exports of machinery, textiles, glass and shoes. German parties had entered government in 1926, easing national tensions, and the democracy appeared more stable than any other in the region.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1933": "The Depression hit Czechoslovakia\u0027s export industries hard, especially in the German-speaking borderlands, where unemployment soared. Hitler\u0027s rise to power in neighbouring Germany created a new threat, and discontent among Sudeten Germans grew. The economy and political cohesion of the state were both weakened, lowering its standing.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1937": "With the economy recovering, rearmament well advanced, border fortifications under construction and alliances with France and the Soviet Union in place, Czechoslovakia stood at its greatest strength. Its arms industry was among the best in Europe. Yet the Sudeten German Party had become the largest party in the 1935 elections.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1938": "The Munich Agreement of 29–30 September forced the cession of the Sudetenland to Germany, without Czechoslovak participation, and Poland and Hungary seized further territory. The state lost its fortifications, much of its industry and about a third of its population, and Beneš resigned. A weakened, federalised Second Republic remained.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1939": "On 14 March Slovakia declared independence under German pressure, and on 15 March German troops occupied Bohemia and Moravia, which became a protectorate, while Hungary took Subcarpathian Ruthenia. Czechoslovakia ceased to exist as a functioning state, surviving only in the claims of Beneš\u0027s exiles in London.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1945": "Liberated mainly by the Red Army, with the Americans in western Bohemia, Czechoslovakia was restored within roughly its pre-Munich borders, except Subcarpathian Ruthenia, ceded to the Soviet Union. The Košice Programme set up a National Front government, and the expulsion of the German minority began. Power was partly recovered but heavily dependent on Moscow.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1948": "The Communist Party seized full power in February 1948, and Gottwald became president after Beneš resigned. Czechoslovakia had a functioning industrial economy and a working state, but its foreign policy was now subordinated to the Soviet Union, and purges and nationalisation began to reshape society.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1955": "Czechoslovakia became a founding member of the Warsaw Pact and a major arms supplier to the Soviet bloc and the developing world, including the 1955 arms deal with Egypt. Heavy industry expanded rapidly under central planning, giving the state considerable economic and military weight within the bloc.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1960": "A new constitution proclaimed the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic, claiming that socialism had been achieved. Industrial production had grown strongly, and the country was among the most developed in the Soviet bloc. Growth slowed sharply in the early 1960s, however, prompting the economic debates that led to the reform movement.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1968": "The Prague Spring under Alexander Dubček relaxed censorship and promised reform, but on 20–21 August Warsaw Pact armies invaded. Soviet troops were stationed in the country permanently, and the reformers were removed. The invasion demonstrated the limits of Czechoslovak sovereignty and began a long decline in prestige.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1977": "Under Gustáv Husák\u0027s normalisation the regime purged hundreds of thousands of party members and imposed conformity. Charter 77 publicly criticised human rights abuses and was persecuted. The economy remained relatively prosperous for the bloc but technologically stagnant, and the state\u0027s international standing was low.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1989": "The Velvet Revolution, begun after police attacked students on 17 November, ended communist rule within weeks. Václav Havel was elected president on 29 December. The state regained full sovereignty and democratic legitimacy, but its economy needed thorough reconstruction and Czech-Slovak tensions were beginning to surface.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1990": "Free elections were held in June, the state was renamed the Czech and Slovak Federative Republic after the \u0027hyphen war\u0027, and plans for the withdrawal of Soviet troops were agreed. Market reforms were prepared, but disputes over the balance of power between Prague and Bratislava increasingly dominated politics.",
  "Czechoslovakia|1992": "The June elections produced opposed majorities under Václav Klaus and Vladimír Mečiar, who negotiated a separation. Slovakia declared sovereignty, Havel resigned and the Federal Assembly passed the dissolution law in November. Czechoslovakia ended peacefully on 31 December, replaced by the Czech Republic and Slovakia.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1918": "The Salvation Committee proclaimed independence on 24 February, but German troops occupied the country the next day. Only after Germany\u0027s collapse in November could the Provisional Government under Päts take power, and the Red Army invaded within weeks. The republic existed largely on paper and in its small, newly raised army.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1920": "Victory in the War of Independence and the Treaty of Tartu of 2 February 1920 secured Soviet recognition of Estonia\u0027s independence. The land reform of 1919 had broken the Baltic German estates and won peasant loyalty, and a democratic constitution was adopted in June. The state was sovereign but small and economically fragile.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1924": "Estonia had joined the League of Nations in 1921 and allied with Latvia in 1923. A communist coup attempt in Tallinn on 1 December 1924 was quickly defeated, confirming the state\u0027s stability. Stabilisation of the economy and new trade links with Britain and Germany were under way.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1929": "Agricultural exports, especially butter and bacon, oil shale and timber gave the economy a modest prosperity, and the Cultural Autonomy Act of 1925 had eased relations with minorities. Estonia was a functioning democracy with a growing education system, although frequent government changes weakened executive authority.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1934": "The Depression and the rise of the Vaps movement led Konstantin Päts to declare a state of emergency on 12 March 1934 and rule by decree. The authoritarian Era of Silence brought stability and economic recovery but ended parliamentary democracy. The state\u0027s capacity was intact, though its legitimacy narrowed.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1938": "A new constitution made Päts president and restored a limited parliament. The economy had recovered strongly, with oil-shale processing expanding, and the twentieth anniversary of independence was celebrated with confidence. This was the high point of the interwar republic, though it remained exposed between Germany and the Soviet Union.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1939": "The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact placed Estonia in the Soviet sphere, and in September the government was forced to sign a mutual assistance pact allowing Soviet bases on Estonian soil. Most Baltic Germans left for German-occupied Poland. Independence continued in form, but the state could no longer act freely.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1940": "On 17 June Soviet forces occupied Estonia. Staged elections produced a parliament that requested admission to the Soviet Union, and annexation followed in August. Päts and Laidoner were deported. The republic ceased to function, surviving only in the diplomatic missions abroad and in Western non-recognition of the annexation.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1944": "As the Red Army returned, Otto Tief formed a government in Tallinn in September 1944 in an attempt to restore independence, but it was swept away within days. Tens of thousands fled to Sweden and Germany. Estonia remained a Soviet republic, with no independent state power for almost five decades.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1988": "Protests against phosphorite mining had grown into a national movement. The Popular Front was founded, the blue, black and white flag reappeared, and on 16 November the Estonian Supreme Soviet declared sovereignty. The republic was not yet restored, but the foundations of its revival had been laid.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1991": "During the failed August coup in Moscow the Supreme Council declared full independence on 20 August, and recognition followed from most states, including the Soviet Union in September. Estonia joined the United Nations that month, but it had no currency or army of its own, and Soviet troops remained on its territory.",
  "Republic of Estonia|1994": "A new constitution, the kroon and radical market reforms under Mart Laar\u0027s government had transformed the economy, and the last Russian troops left at the end of August 1994. The restored state was now fully in control of its territory and was beginning to seek membership of Western institutions.",
  "Republic of Estonia|2004": "Estonia joined NATO in March and the European Union in May 2004, achieving the security and economic integration its leaders had sought since 1991. Rapid growth, digital public services and a flat tax had made it one of the most dynamic economies in Central and Eastern Europe.",
  "Republic of Estonia|2011": "Despite a deep recession in 2008–2009, Estonia met the criteria for adopting the euro on 1 January 2011, weeks after joining the OECD in December 2010. Its e-government services, internet voting and technology companies had given it an international profile far beyond its size.",
  "Republic of Estonia|2017": "After Russia\u0027s annexation of Crimea in 2014, NATO deployed a British-led battlegroup to Tapa in 2017, and Estonia increased defence spending. Its e-Residency programme, launched in 2014, was attracting foreign entrepreneurs, and it held its first presidency of the Council of the European Union in the second half of 2017.",
  "Republic of Estonia|2025": "Estonia is a prosperous parliamentary democracy of about 1.4 million people, a member of the EU, NATO and the eurozone, with high living standards and advanced digital government. After Russia\u0027s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022 it became a leading supporter of Ukraine, and its security standing is the strongest in its history.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1918": "The People\u0027s Council proclaimed the Republic of Latvia in Riga on 18 November, with Kārlis Ulmanis heading the Provisional Government. The new state had almost no army, money or administration, and German troops were still present. Within weeks the Red Army invaded, so the republic\u0027s authority was more a claim than a reality.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1919": "In January the Latvian Soviet republic took Riga, and in April a German coup in Liepāja installed the Niedra puppet government. The national government nearly disappeared. After the Battle of Cēsis in June it returned to Riga and in November repelled the Bermontians, but much of the country remained a battlefield.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1920": "Latgale was cleared of the Red Army with Polish help, a Constituent Assembly was elected in April and the peace treaty with Soviet Russia signed on 11 August recognised Latvian independence. The agrarian reform began breaking up the German estates. The state was now secure, though impoverished by six years of war.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1922": "The Satversme came into force and Jānis Čakste became the first president, completing the constitutional order. Latvia had been recognised de jure by the Allied powers in 1921 and admitted to the League of Nations. Reconstruction of agriculture and the port of Riga was under way.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1929": "Agricultural exports of butter, bacon and flax had restored a modest prosperity, and a stable currency, the lats, had been introduced in 1922. The parliamentary system was fragmented, with frequent cabinet changes, but the state functioned and its international position was secure.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1934": "Hit by the Depression and frustrated by parliamentary deadlock, Kārlis Ulmanis staged a coup on 15 May 1934, suspending the Saeima and banning parties. Authoritarian rule brought stability and state-led economic policies but ended democracy, leaving the state\u0027s overall capacity broadly unchanged.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1938": "Under Ulmanis, who had become president in 1936, the economy recovered, exports grew and state companies such as VEF prospered, launching the Minox camera. Living standards were among the highest the country had known. This marked the interwar peak, though Latvia was isolated between Germany and the Soviet Union.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1939": "The Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact assigned Latvia to the Soviet sphere. In October Latvia was forced to sign a treaty allowing Soviet military bases, and most Baltic Germans left for the Reich. The country remained formally independent but had lost the ability to determine its own foreign and security policy.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1940": "After an ultimatum on 16 June, Soviet forces occupied Latvia the next day. Rigged elections were followed by annexation on 5 August. Ulmanis was deported. The republic ceased to function, its existence preserved only by diplomats abroad and the Western refusal to recognise the annexation.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1990": "After the Popular Front\u0027s victory in the Supreme Council elections, the council adopted the declaration on the restoration of independence on 4 May 1990, reviving the Satversme in principle. Moscow rejected the move and Soviet troops remained, so the restored republic held legitimacy but little real power.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1991": "Latvians defended the barricades in Riga against Soviet forces in January, voted for independence in March, and declared full independence on 21 August during the failed Moscow coup. Recognition followed from the Soviet Union in September and from most of the world, and Latvia joined the United Nations.",
  "Republic of Latvia|1994": "The fifth Saeima had restored the Satversme in 1993, the lats had returned, and the last Russian troops left on 31 August 1994, apart from the Skrunda radar station. Latvia now controlled its territory and economy and began seeking membership of the European Union and NATO.",
  "Republic of Latvia|2004": "Latvia joined NATO in March and the European Union in May 2004 under President Vaira Vīķe-Freiberga, securing its place in Western institutions. Rapid growth followed, fuelled by foreign credit and property lending, making Latvia one of the fastest-growing economies in the European Union.",
  "Republic of Latvia|2008": "The credit boom ended abruptly. The collapse of Parex Bank and capital flight forced the government to seek an international rescue loan from the International Monetary Fund and the European Union in December 2008, opening one of the deepest recessions in Europe.",
  "Republic of Latvia|2010": "Severe austerity, wage cuts and emigration had cut output by about a fifth from its peak, and unemployment had risen sharply. Latvia nevertheless kept its currency peg to the euro, and growth returned by 2011, but the crisis had cost the country many of its gains.",
  "Republic of Latvia|2014": "Latvia adopted the euro on 1 January 2014 and Riga was a European Capital of Culture. The economy had recovered, and Latvia held the presidency of the Council of the European Union in the first half of 2015. Russia\u0027s annexation of Crimea in 2014 had sharpened its security concerns.",
  "Republic of Latvia|2025": "Latvia is a parliamentary republic of under two million people, a member of the EU, NATO, the OECD and the eurozone. NATO forces led by Canada are stationed at Ādaži, and conscription was reintroduced in 2023. Its security, prosperity and international standing are the strongest in its history.",
  "United Kingdom|1922": "With the Irish Free State gone, the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland still governed the largest empire in history, held League of Nations mandates in the Middle East and shared naval supremacy with the United States. War debts and a weakened economy, however, limited its real strength.",
  "United Kingdom|1926": "The Locarno treaties of 1925, negotiated with British participation, eased European tensions, and the Balfour Declaration of 1926 defined the dominions as equal partners with Britain. At home the General Strike of May 1926 was defeated. The UK remained the world\u0027s leading imperial and financial power, though its staple industries were weakening.",
  "United Kingdom|1931": "The Depression drove the UK off the gold standard in September 1931 and brought a National Government to power. The Statute of Westminster gave legislative independence to the dominions. Unemployment approached three million, and the Japanese invasion of Manchuria showed the limits of British power in Asia.",
  "United Kingdom|1936": "Economic recovery was under way in the South and Midlands, and rearmament had begun, but the abdication crisis of Edward VIII shook the monarchy, and Germany remilitarised the Rhineland unopposed. Britain remained a great power that preferred to avoid confrontation, relying on diplomacy and the empire.",
  "United Kingdom|1939": "After the Munich Agreement of 1938 the government accelerated rearmament and guaranteed Poland. When Germany invaded, the UK declared war on 3 September 1939 with the empire and dominions behind it. Its navy was still the strongest in Europe, but its army was small compared with Germany\u0027s.",
  "United Kingdom|1940": "The fall of France and the evacuation from Dunkirk left the UK, led by Winston Churchill, as the only major power fighting Germany. Victory in the Battle of Britain prevented invasion, but the Blitz, the U-boat war and the loss of continental allies placed the country under extreme strain.",
  "United Kingdom|1944": "Fully mobilised and allied with the United States and the Soviet Union, the UK reached its peak of military power. British and Commonwealth troops landed in Normandy on 6 June, fought in Italy and won decisive victories in Burma at Imphal and Kohima. It was one of the Big Three shaping the post-war world.",
  "United Kingdom|1947": "Victorious but exhausted, the UK depended on American loans and faced a sterling crisis. India and Pakistan became independent in August 1947, and Britain ended its aid to Greece and announced its withdrawal from Palestine. At home the Attlee government built the welfare state, while the UK remained a great power with worldwide commitments.",
  "United Kingdom|1952": "The UK tested its first atomic bomb in October 1952, becoming the third nuclear power, and Elizabeth II acceded to the throne. A founding member of NATO, it fought in Korea and still governed a large colonial empire, though its economy was recovering more slowly than those of its rivals.",
  "United Kingdom|1956": "The Anglo-French intervention at Suez, halted by American financial pressure, exposed the UK\u0027s dependence on the United States and its inability to act as an independent world power. The crisis accelerated decolonisation and a reorientation of British foreign policy towards partnership with Washington and, later, Europe.",
  "United Kingdom|1968": "Most of the African and Caribbean colonies had become independent, the pound had been devalued in November 1967, and in January 1968 the government announced the withdrawal of forces from east of Suez. The UK was now a regional power with global interests rather than an imperial one.",
  "United Kingdom|1973": "The UK joined the European Economic Community on 1 January 1973 after two failed applications. The oil shock and industrial unrest that year, including the miners\u0027 overtime ban that led to the three-day week of early 1974, revealed the weaknesses of an economy growing more slowly than its new partners.",
  "United Kingdom|1976": "With inflation having exceeded 20 per cent and sterling falling, the government applied to the International Monetary Fund for a loan in 1976 and accepted spending cuts. The episode was widely seen as the low point of post-war British economic decline, followed by the Winter of Discontent in 1978–1979.",
  "United Kingdom|1982": "Victory in the Falklands War restored national confidence and the prestige of the armed forces, while the Thatcher government pursued monetarism, privatisation and trade union reform. North Sea oil eased the balance of payments, though national unemployment had risen above three million, hitting the industrial regions hardest.",
  "United Kingdom|1997": "Hong Kong was handed over to China on 1 July 1997, ending Britain\u0027s last major colony. A Labour government under Tony Blair began devolution and advanced the Northern Ireland peace process, and the economy, centred on finance and services, was growing steadily.",
  "United Kingdom|2008": "The global financial crisis hit the UK\u0027s large banking sector hard, requiring the rescue of major banks and a deep recession. The UK had fought in Afghanistan and Iraq alongside the United States, campaigns that strained its armed forces and divided public opinion.",
  "United Kingdom|2016": "The UK remained one of the world\u0027s largest economies, a nuclear power and a permanent member of the Security Council. On 23 June 2016 voters chose to leave the European Union by 51.9 per cent to 48.1 per cent, opening a long period of political uncertainty.",
  "United Kingdom|2020": "The UK left the European Union on 31 January 2020 and its single market at the end of the year, while the Covid-19 pandemic caused a sharp economic contraction. Its influence in European affairs declined, and pressure for Scottish independence revived.",
  "United Kingdom|2025": "The United Kingdom is a constitutional monarchy under Charles III, with about 68 million people, one of the world\u0027s largest economies, a nuclear deterrent and a permanent seat on the Security Council. It is a leading supporter of Ukraine within NATO, though far less powerful than in its imperial era.",
  "Soviet Union|1922": "The union was founded on 30 December by four Soviet republics after the Bolsheviks\u0027 victory in the Civil War. The country was devastated by war and the famine of 1921–1922, its industry produced a fraction of pre-war output, and it was diplomatically isolated, though its Red Army controlled most of the former Russian Empire.",
  "Soviet Union|1924": "The first constitution was adopted in January and Lenin died soon after. The New Economic Policy was restoring agriculture and trade, and Britain, Italy and France recognised the Soviet government in 1924. The union was stabilising but remained economically weak and internally divided by the struggle over Lenin\u0027s succession.",
  "Soviet Union|1928": "Stalin had defeated Trotsky and was turning against Nikolai Bukharin. Pre-war levels of production had been roughly restored, and the First Five-Year Plan was launched to industrialise rapidly. The state was more secure but still far behind the Western powers in industry and military technology.",
  "Soviet Union|1933": "The First Five-Year Plan had created new steelworks, tractor plants and power stations, greatly increasing heavy industry. But forced collectivisation had caused a devastating famine in 1932–1933. The state\u0027s industrial and coercive capacity had grown sharply even as the countryside suffered catastrophic losses.",
  "Soviet Union|1938": "Industrial output continued to grow under the Second Five-Year Plan, and a large, mechanised army was being built. Yet the Great Terror of 1937–1938 had executed hundreds of thousands, including much of the officer corps, damaging the state\u0027s competence and leaving its military leadership badly weakened.",
  "Soviet Union|1940": "Under the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact the Soviet Union had taken eastern Poland, the Baltic states and Bessarabia and forced territorial concessions from Finland after the Winter War. It controlled more territory than at any point since 1917, though the Winter War had exposed serious military weaknesses.",
  "Soviet Union|1941": "The German invasion of 22 June destroyed much of the Red Army in huge encirclements and overran Belarus, the Baltic states and most of Ukraine. Leningrad was besieged and Moscow threatened. Industry was evacuated eastwards, and the counter-offensive at Moscow in December saved the capital from capture.",
  "Soviet Union|1943": "The destruction of the German Sixth Army at Stalingrad and victory at Kursk in July turned the tide of the war. Soviet industry, relocated to the Urals and supplied in part by Lend-Lease, now outproduced Germany in tanks and aircraft, and the Red Army had seized the strategic initiative.",
  "Soviet Union|1945": "The Red Army captured Berlin in May, and the Soviet Union defeated Japan in Manchuria in August. It occupied Eastern Europe, took a permanent seat on the UN Security Council and was a superpower in all but nuclear weapons, though about 27 million citizens had died and much of the west lay in ruins.",
  "Soviet Union|1949": "The first Soviet atomic bomb was tested in August 1949, ending the American nuclear monopoly. Communist regimes were in place across Eastern Europe, and the communist victory in China created a powerful ally. Post-war reconstruction had largely restored industrial output, though living standards remained low.",
  "Soviet Union|1957": "Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, was launched in October 1957, following the first intercontinental ballistic missile test. Khrushchev had denounced Stalin and survived a challenge from his rivals. The Soviet Union seemed to many observers to be catching up with the United States technologically.",
  "Soviet Union|1962": "Yuri Gagarin had become the first person in space in 1961, but the Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 ended in a Soviet withdrawal that exposed American nuclear superiority. The split with China was becoming open. The Soviet Union remained a superpower, determined to close the strategic gap.",
  "Soviet Union|1968": "A massive build-up of intercontinental missiles was narrowing the gap with the United States, and the Soviet navy was expanding worldwide. The invasion of Czechoslovakia in August reasserted control over Eastern Europe, though at a cost to the Soviet Union\u0027s prestige among communists in the West.",
  "Soviet Union|1975": "The Soviet Union reached its peak of power and influence. Strategic parity with the United States had been recognised in the SALT I treaty of 1972, the Helsinki Final Act of 1975 confirmed the post-war borders in Europe, and Soviet allies were advancing in Vietnam, Angola and elsewhere.",
  "Soviet Union|1979": "Soviet influence in the developing world was at its widest, from Ethiopia to South Yemen and Nicaragua, and its armed forces matched NATO in Europe. In December 1979 Soviet troops invaded Afghanistan, beginning a costly war that, combined with economic stagnation, would sap the state\u0027s strength.",
  "Soviet Union|1985": "Three ageing General Secretaries had died in under three years, the economy was stagnating, and the war in Afghanistan continued. A renewed arms race with the United States under Ronald Reagan strained resources. Mikhail Gorbachev became General Secretary in March, promising to revive the system through reform.",
  "Soviet Union|1986": "The explosion at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in April 1986 exposed official secrecy and incompetence and cost billions of roubles. The fall of world oil prices cut export revenues sharply. Gorbachev responded by expanding glasnost and preparing more radical economic and political reforms.",
  "Soviet Union|1989": "Soviet troops left Afghanistan in February, and the communist regimes of Eastern Europe fell during the autumn without Soviet intervention. Contested elections to the Congress of People\u0027s Deputies energised opposition, and national movements in the Baltic states and the Caucasus demanded sovereignty. Soviet power was contracting rapidly.",
  "Soviet Union|1991": "The failed August coup destroyed the authority of the Communist Party and the union government. The republics declared independence, the Belovezha Accords of 8 December announced the end of the union, and Gorbachev resigned on 25 December. The Soviet Union was formally dissolved on 26 December 1991.",
  "Nazi Germany|1933": "Hitler became chancellor on 30 January and within months used the Reichstag Fire Decree and the Enabling Act to destroy parliamentary government, ban other parties and dissolve the trade unions. Germany was a large industrial state but still bound by the military limits of Versailles, with a small army and millions unemployed.",
  "Nazi Germany|1934": "The Night of the Long Knives eliminated the SA leadership and other rivals, and after Hindenburg’s death in August Hitler became Führer, with the army swearing a personal oath to him. Internal power was consolidated, but Germany remained diplomatically isolated after leaving the League of Nations and militarily weak.",
  "Nazi Germany|1935": "The Saar voted to return to Germany in January, and in March Hitler announced conscription and the existence of the Luftwaffe in open breach of Versailles. The Anglo-German Naval Agreement and the Nuremberg Laws followed. Rearmament gathered pace, but the new Wehrmacht was still far from ready for war.",
  "Nazi Germany|1936": "German troops reoccupied the demilitarised Rhineland in March without resistance, the Four Year Plan geared the economy to war, and the Berlin Olympics showcased the regime. Agreements with Italy and Japan broke Germany’s isolation, and the Condor Legion gained combat experience in Spain, raising the regime’s weight in Europe.",
  "Nazi Germany|1938": "Germany annexed Austria in March and, through the Munich Agreement of September, the Sudetenland, adding about ten million people and Czech border fortifications without firing a shot. Hitler took personal command over the armed forces through the new OKW, and the November pogrom marked a sharp radicalisation of anti-Jewish policy.",
  "Nazi Germany|1939": "The occupation of Prague in March, the seizure of Memel and the pact with the Soviet Union in August prepared the invasion of Poland on 1 September. Poland was overrun within weeks and partitioned with the Soviets, but Britain and France declared war, committing Germany to a general European conflict.",
  "Nazi Germany|1940": "In a few months Germany conquered Denmark, Norway, the Netherlands, Belgium, Luxembourg and France, whose armistice in June left the Wehrmacht master of western Europe. Failure to defeat the Royal Air Force in the Battle of Britain, however, left Britain undefeated and able to draw on its empire and American support.",
  "Nazi Germany|1941": "The conquest of Yugoslavia and Greece and the invasion of the Soviet Union in June carried German armies deep into Russia, while the systematic murder of Jews began in the east. The halt before Moscow in December and the declaration of war on the United States exposed Germany to overwhelming enemy resources.",
  "Nazi Germany|1942": "German control reached its greatest extent, from the Atlantic coast to the Volga and the Caucasus and from Norway to Egypt, and Speer’s reorganisation raised armaments output. The extermination camps of occupied Poland reached full operation. By November, defeats at El Alamein and the encirclement at Stalingrad marked the turning of the tide.",
  "Nazi Germany|1943": "The surrender of the Sixth Army at Stalingrad, the loss of North Africa, defeat at Kursk and the surrender of Italy cost Germany the initiative on every front. Allied bombing intensified and the U-boats were beaten in the Atlantic, although the Reich still occupied most of continental Europe.",
  "Nazi Germany|1944": "The Normandy landings and the Soviet Operation Bagration destroyed whole German armies in the summer, France and much of eastern Europe were lost, and the 20 July plot revealed opposition within the officer corps. Despite record armaments output, Germany was fighting on its own borders by the autumn.",
  "Nazi Germany|1945": "Allied armies crossed the Rhine and the Oder, the Red Army stormed Berlin and Hitler killed himself on 30 April. The Wehrmacht surrendered unconditionally on 7–8 May, the Dönitz government was arrested and the Allies assumed supreme authority in June, ending the Nazi state completely.",
  "Uruk|-3500": "Kullaba and Eanna had merged into a growing town on a branch of the Euphrates, supported by irrigated barley, herds and marsh resources. Temple precincts for Inanna and An were already prominent, but Uruk was still one of several large settlements in southern Mesopotamia rather than a dominant city.",
  "Uruk|-3300": "In the Late Uruk phase the city expanded rapidly and Eanna was rebuilt with monumental temples and halls decorated with cone mosaics. Administrators began to record goods on clay tablets, the first steps towards writing, and Uruk-style colonies appeared on the upper Euphrates in Syria and Anatolia.",
  "Uruk|-3200": "Uruk was at its relative height as the largest city in the world, perhaps 250 hectares, with tens of thousands of inhabitants. Proto-cuneiform tablets, cylinder seals, the Warka Vase and a network of outposts reaching Syria and Iran show a centre without equal in the Near East.",
  "Uruk|-3100": "The Uruk expansion collapsed: outposts such as Habuba Kabira were abandoned and Uruk’s cultural reach shrank to southern Mesopotamia. The city itself remained very large and writing continued to develop in the Jemdet Nasr period, but its unique primacy over the region was over.",
  "Uruk|-2900": "At the start of the Early Dynastic period Uruk was enclosed by a wall about 9 kilometres long, later credited to Gilgamesh, around one of the largest urban areas of the age. It was now one of several competing Sumerian city-states, though still among the most populous and prestigious.",
  "Uruk|-2700": "This is roughly the time to which Gilgamesh, perhaps a historical king, is often dated. Sumerian tradition remembered Uruk’s First Dynasty contesting supremacy with Kish, but contemporary inscriptions are lacking, and Uruk was one power among several rival cities such as Kish, Ur and Lagash.",
  "Uruk|-2450": "Rulers such as Enshakushanna claimed victories over Kish, and Lugal-kinishe-dudu ruled Uruk together with Ur, but Lagash and Umma were fighting their own long border war. Uruk remained important without dominating southern Mesopotamia, its power resting on alliances and the prestige of Eanna.",
  "Uruk|-2350": "Lugal-zage-si of Umma, having defeated Lagash, made Uruk his capital and claimed kingship over all Sumer from the Gulf to the Mediterranean. For a brief period Uruk was the centre of the largest political unit yet seen in southern Mesopotamia, a hegemony that would not survive Sargon.",
  "Uruk|-2334": "Sargon of Akkad defeated Lugal-zage-si, led him captive to Nippur and tore down Uruk’s walls. The date is approximate. Uruk lost its independence and its role as capital, becoming a subject city within the new Akkadian Empire, which governed southern Mesopotamia from the north.",
  "Uruk|-2250": "Uruk joined the great revolt of southern cities against Naram-Sin of Akkad under its king Amar-girid, and was defeated. Governed by Akkadian appointees and stripped of military power, the city kept its temples and prestige but carried little political weight in this period.",
  "Uruk|-2119": "Utu-hengal of Uruk defeated the Gutian king Tirigan, whose people had dominated parts of Sumer since the fall of Akkad, restoring native rule over the south. This last great victory briefly made Uruk the leading city of southern Mesopotamia again, more than a century after the Akkadian conquest.",
  "Uruk|-2112": "Utu-hengal’s reign ended within a few years and Ur-Nammu of Ur, probably his brother or former governor, founded the Third Dynasty of Ur. Uruk became a province of the new empire, honoured as the city of Gilgamesh, whom the kings of Ur called their brother, but ruled from Ur.",
  "Uruk|-2004": "The Third Dynasty of Ur fell to Elamite attack and Uruk passed under the rival kings of Isin and later Larsa. It remained a large city and a major religious centre, but for much of the next century and a half it had no independent ruler of its own.",
  "Uruk|-1865": "Sin-kashid, an Amorite chief, established an independent dynasty at Uruk, built a large palace and restored temples. The new kingdom was modest in size, a small state holding its own between Larsa, Isin and Babylon, yet it revived Uruk as a royal capital after long subordination.",
  "Uruk|-1820": "Under Sin-kashid’s successors, including Anam and Irdanene, Uruk kept its independence by alliance with Babylon and others, but Rim-Sin of Larsa was steadily absorbing the neighbouring cities of the south. Irdanene’s coalition was defeated by Rim-Sin, leaving the small kingdom isolated, weakened and exposed to conquest.",
  "Uruk|-1802": "Rim-Sin of Larsa took Uruk around this date, ending its last independent dynasty. The city became part of Larsa and, after 1763 BCE, of Hammurabi’s Babylonian kingdom. It survived for two more millennia as a temple and scholarly centre, but never again as a state.",
  "Ebla|-3000": "A modest settlement existed at Tell Mardikh on the plain south-west of modern Aleppo, one of many farming villages in northern Syria at the start of the Early Bronze Age. Nothing yet distinguished it from its neighbours, and it had no known political weight beyond its immediate surroundings.",
  "Ebla|-2700": "Ebla was growing into a town as urbanisation spread across Syria, supported by cereal farming, olives and sheep herding on the surrounding plain. Its early levels are poorly known because later buildings cover them, but the foundations of the later city and kingdom were probably laid in this period.",
  "Ebla|-2500": "Ebla had become a walled city with an acropolis and lower town, controlling a territory of villages and pasture. Its wool and textile production and its position between the Mediterranean and the Euphrates made it a significant regional centre, though Mari was the stronger power on the river.",
  "Ebla|-2400": "Under kings such as Igrish-Halam, Ebla was an important kingdom but under pressure from Mari, whose kings, among them Iblul-Il, conquered towns in its sphere and received large payments of silver and gold. Ebla was wealthy and literate, but in this rivalry it was the weaker side.",
  "Ebla|-2380": "Under Irkab-Damu, Ebla began to recover from Mari’s pressure, building diplomatic ties and possibly concluding the treaty with Abarsal recorded in its archive. Powerful officials such as the vizier Arrukum managed an expanding administration, and the palace’s textile and metal trade reached across northern Syria and beyond.",
  "Ebla|-2340": "In the reign of Ishar-Damu, with the viziers Ibrium and Ibbi-Zikir, Ebla allied with Nagar and Kish and defeated Mari, apparently near Terqa. The palace controlled a large territory, exchanged envoys with distant courts and kept the great archive. This was the kingdom’s height; all dates are approximate.",
  "Ebla|-2300": "Palace G was destroyed by fire, probably by Sargon or Naram-Sin of Akkad or possibly by Mari. The kingdom of the archive period ended abruptly, its administration and wealth lost. The same fire baked thousands of tablets hard and preserved them for discovery more than four thousand years later.",
  "Ebla|-2200": "The city was reoccupied and a second Ebla existed in the late third millennium. It was smaller and left few written records, but it was still known in Mesopotamia, where texts from the Third Dynasty of Ur mention messengers from Ebla, showing it remained a recognised local power.",
  "Ebla|-2000": "The second Ebla was destroyed around the end of the third millennium, during a period of upheaval across Syria often linked to the spread of Amorite groups. Occupation was interrupted, leaving the city at its weakest point between the destruction of Palace G and the Amorite revival.",
  "Ebla|-1950": "Amorite settlers rebuilt Ebla on a large scale, beginning the third city of the Middle Bronze Age. Massive earthen ramparts and new public buildings were begun, and the city recovered as a regional centre, although it was now surrounded by powerful Amorite kingdoms in Syria.",
  "Ebla|-1800": "Middle Bronze Age Ebla prospered behind its ramparts, with a royal palace, the great temple of Ishtar and rich royal tombs. Kings such as Ibbit-Lim dedicated statues to Ishtar, but by the eighteenth century the city had become a vassal of Yamhad, the dominant kingdom of northern Syria.",
  "Ebla|-1700": "Ebla remained a wealthy city with palaces and temples and contacts reaching Egypt, as shown by a ceremonial mace bearing a pharaoh’s name in its royal tombs. Politically it remained subordinate to Aleppo, and the rise of the Hittite kingdom in Anatolia was beginning to threaten northern Syria.",
  "Ebla|-1600": "Around this date Ebla was destroyed, probably by the Hittite king Mursili I or his predecessor Hattusili I, during their campaigns against Yamhad. The city was never rebuilt as a centre of power; only villages later occupied the site, and Ebla vanished from history until its rediscovery.",
  "Mari|-2900": "Mari was founded on the middle Euphrates as a new settlement, interpreted by its excavator as a planned circular city with flood defences and a canal. It controlled river traffic and worked metals, but as a young foundation it was a local centre with little reach beyond its own stretch of the valley.",
  "Mari|-2550": "After the first city declined, a second city was built with a palace and temples to Ishtar, Shamash and other deities. Mari was becoming a wealthy Early Dynastic kingdom, open to Sumerian culture and writing, and positioned to tax the growing trade along the Euphrates between Sumer and Syria.",
  "Mari|-2450": "Mari’s kings were among the leading rulers of the Euphrates, as the Sumerian King List’s dynasty of Mari suggests. The temples were filled with statues of worshippers such as the official Ebih-Il, and Mari was extending its influence upriver into the region contested with Ebla.",
  "Mari|-2400": "Under kings such as Iblul-Il, Mari campaigned against towns in Ebla’s sphere and received large payments of silver and gold from Ebla, as texts in the Ebla archive record. This dominance over its main western rival marks the height of Mari’s power in the third millennium; dates are approximate.",
  "Mari|-2340": "Ebla, in alliance with Nagar and Kish, defeated Mari in a battle apparently fought near Terqa, as recorded in the Ebla tablets. Mari lost its ascendancy over the western trade routes, though it remained a significant kingdom and may have struck back at Ebla shortly afterwards.",
  "Mari|-2300": "The second city of Mari was destroyed around this time, probably by Sargon of Akkad or his successors, who claimed the god Dagan had given them the Upper Land including Mari and Ebla. The kingdom lost its independence and the old palace and temples were ruined.",
  "Mari|-2266": "Mari was rebuilt and governed by military governors, the shakkanakku, under the authority of the Akkadian Empire. The city recovered as a provincial centre controlling the middle Euphrates for its Akkadian overlords, but it no longer pursued an independent policy. The date of this phase is approximate.",
  "Mari|-2150": "As Akkadian power collapsed, the shakkanakku became hereditary rulers of an effectively independent Mari. They rebuilt palaces and temples and kept control of the river route, and Mari regained a position as a respected regional power during the troubled period after the fall of Akkad.",
  "Mari|-2050": "During the Third Dynasty of Ur, Mari remained independent under its shakkanakku rulers and had close relations with Ur, including a marriage between a princess of Mari and the royal house of Ur. The city prospered from trade between Babylonia and Syria in this period of relative stability.",
  "Mari|-1900": "The shakkanakku dynasty came to an end and Mari passed through an obscure period. Amorite tribes and new dynasties were taking power across Syria and Mesopotamia, and Mari’s position on the river made it a prize for rival groups rather than a power in its own right.",
  "Mari|-1810": "Yahdun-Lim of the Amorite Lim dynasty built up Mari’s power, subdued the tribes of the steppe, founded new fortresses and led an expedition to the Mediterranean. Mari was again a significant kingdom on the Euphrates, though it soon faced the expanding power of Shamshi-Adad to the east.",
  "Mari|-1796": "Soon after Yahdun-Lim’s death Mari fell to Shamshi-Adad, who installed his son Yasmah-Addu as king. For about two decades Mari was a subordinate part of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia, administered from Shubat-Enlil, and lost its independence and its own royal line.",
  "Mari|-1775": "After Shamshi-Adad’s death, Zimri-Lim, who claimed descent from Yahdun-Lim, took the throne with the help of Yarim-Lim of Yamhad, whose daughter Shibtu he married. He rebuilt Mari’s independence, subdued the tribes and made vassals of kings in the Khabur region.",
  "Mari|-1765": "Zimri-Lim was at the height of his power, visiting Ugarit and joining Hammurabi of Babylon and other kings to resist the Elamite invasion of Mesopotamia. His palace was among the most famous in the Near East, but Mari was a middle-ranking power within a system dominated by Babylon, Yamhad and Larsa.",
  "Mari|-1761": "Having defeated Elam, Eshnunna and Larsa, Hammurabi turned on his former ally and defeated Zimri-Lim. Mari submitted to Babylon, and two years later its walls were demolished and the palace burned. The kingdom ended, and power on the middle Euphrates passed to the Kingdom of Hana at Terqa.",
  "Byblos|-2600": "Byblos was a walled city with temples and a busy harbour, already sending cedar and resin to the pharaohs of the Old Kingdom. Its ties with Egypt brought gold, stone vessels and linen, but it was still growing into the role of chief timber port of the Levant.",
  "Byblos|-2450": "During the Fifth Dynasty, Egyptian expeditions to Byblos for timber were routine, and Egyptian objects accumulated in the temple of the Baalat Gebal. The city prospered as the main link between Egypt and the eastern Mediterranean coast, with rebuilt temples and fortifications, though its territory remained small.",
  "Byblos|-2300": "Under the Sixth Dynasty of Egypt, with pharaohs such as Pepi I whose objects were dedicated in its temples, Byblos was at the height of its Early Bronze Age wealth. Its monopoly of the cedar trade to Egypt gave it an importance far beyond its size.",
  "Byblos|-2150": "The Early Bronze Age city was destroyed by fire, by an unknown hand, at a time when the Old Kingdom of Egypt had collapsed and its trade had dried up. Byblos lost both its prosperity and its main partner, and the town shrank in the following generations.",
  "Byblos|-2000": "Byblos was recovering as Egypt was reunited under the Middle Kingdom and trade resumed. New settlers, probably including Amorites, rebuilt the town, and the cedar trade with Egypt began to revive, but the city had not yet regained its former wealth or standing.",
  "Byblos|-1850": "Under rulers such as Abishemu and Ipshemuabi, who used Egyptian titles and received gifts from Amenemhat III and Amenemhat IV, Byblos flourished again. Rich royal tombs and the Temple of the Obelisks with its gilded figurines show a wealthy city closely tied to Middle Kingdom Egypt.",
  "Byblos|-1700": "As the Middle Kingdom gave way to divided rule in Egypt, Byblos lost some of its privileged position but remained a prosperous trading city. Local scribes may have developed the undeciphered Byblos syllabic script, and contacts with Syria and the Aegean continued alongside the Egyptian connection.",
  "Byblos|-1550": "Egypt was reunited under the Eighteenth Dynasty, while Mitanni and the Syrian kingdoms competed for the Levant. Byblos remained an important port but lay between rival powers, and its independence and trade depended on the outcome of the struggle for control of the coast.",
  "Byblos|-1450": "After the campaigns of Thutmose III, Byblos became a loyal Egyptian vassal and a supply base for the pharaoh’s armies in the Levant. It kept its kings and its trade, but its foreign policy was now determined in Egypt, and it served Egyptian strategic needs.",
  "Byblos|-1340": "Rib-Hadda’s letters in the Amarna archive show Byblos isolated as Abdi-Ashirta and Aziru of Amurru seized its neighbours and cut off its grain. Egypt sent little help, the townspeople turned against their king, and Rib-Hadda was driven out, leaving the city at a low point.",
  "Byblos|-1150": "Egyptian rule in the Levant ended amid the crisis of the late Bronze Age. Byblos appears to have escaped the destruction that ended Ugarit and other cities, and as Egyptian control faded it regained independence as one of the cities of the Phoenician coast.",
  "Byblos|-1075": "The Report of Wenamun shows King Zakar-Baal treating an envoy from Karnak as a trading partner, demanding payment for cedar and consulting his ancestors’ records. Byblos was independent and self-confident, but its trade was now with a weakened Egypt and with other Phoenician cities.",
  "Byblos|-1000": "Ahiram’s sarcophagus and the inscriptions of his successors show Byblos as an early centre of the Phoenician alphabet. Its kings built and restored temples, and the city enjoyed a period of independence and prosperity in the early Iron Age before Tyre’s rise to leadership.",
  "Byblos|-925": "Kings Abibaal and Elibaal dedicated statues of the pharaohs Shoshenq I and Osorkon I to the Baalat Gebal, showing renewed relations with Egypt. Byblos remained prosperous and independent, though Tyre and Sidon were becoming the leading Phoenician cities through their seaborne trade and colonies.",
  "Byblos|-870": "Ashurnasirpal II’s march to the Mediterranean brought tribute from Byblos, Tyre, Sidon and other coastal cities. Byblos began paying tribute to the Assyrian Empire, keeping its kings and trade but losing its freedom of action, and it was overshadowed by Tyre in commerce and colonisation.",
  "Byblos|-738": "Under Tiglath-Pileser III, Assyria tightened its control of the Levant, and the king of Byblos appears among the rulers who paid tribute. Byblos was a small subordinate city, valued by the Assyrians for timber and harbour but without independent political weight.",
  "Byblos|-701": "During Sennacherib’s campaign in the Levant, King Urumilki of Byblos was among the rulers who submitted and brought tribute. Byblos avoided the punishment inflicted on rebellious cities such as Sidon but remained a minor tributary, its status dependent on loyalty to Assyria.",
  "Byblos|-539": "With Cyrus the Great’s conquest of Babylon, Byblos passed peacefully under the Achaemenid Empire. The Persians gave the Phoenician cities considerable autonomy in return for tribute and ships, and Byblos entered a period of renewed stability under its own kings.",
  "Byblos|-400": "Under Persian rule Byblos minted silver coins showing warships, its king Yehawmilk was among the rulers who dedicated monuments to the Baalat Gebal, and a Persian fortress guarded the town. It was prosperous and autonomous, though still secondary to Sidon, Tyre and Arwad.",
  "Byblos|-332": "Byblos submitted peacefully to Alexander the Great, and King Enylus brought his ships to join the siege of Tyre. The city was spared, but its kings soon disappear from the record under Macedonian rule, and Byblos continued only as a town within the Hellenistic and later Roman world.",
  "Dilmun|-2200": "Settlement at Qal’at al-Bahrain on Bahrain’s north coast began to expand as Gulf trade grew. Dilmun was already known to Mesopotamian scribes, but the name probably still covered parts of the Arabian mainland. The island’s springs made it a natural port, though it was not yet a fully organised trading state.",
  "Dilmun|-2050": "Qal’at al-Bahrain became a walled town with paved streets and storerooms, and temples were built at Barbar. Under the Third Dynasty of Ur, merchants from Ur traded with Dilmun for copper and luxuries, and the island emerged as the main intermediary of Gulf commerce, with power rising quickly.",
  "Dilmun|-2000": "The Early Dilmun culture flourished. Round stamp seals, a weight standard related to that of the Indus, and growing burial-mound cemeteries show an organised society. The fall of Ur around 2004 BCE did not halt trade; merchants of Isin and Larsa continued to buy copper through Dilmun’s harbour.",
  "Dilmun|-1900": "Dilmun stood at its peak as the clearing house of Gulf trade between Mesopotamia, Magan and Meluhha. The town at Qal’at al-Bahrain was at its largest, the Saar village and temple flourished, and the royal mounds at A’ali indicate wealthy rulers. Its influence reached Failaka and the Arabian coast.",
  "Dilmun|-1800": "Trade remained strong under the kingdoms of Isin and Larsa, and texts from Ur record Dilmun voyagers dealing in copper, ivory and beads. The island’s elite continued to build large mounds. Signs of strain were appearing, however, as the Indus cities weakened and Mesopotamian demand shifted.",
  "Dilmun|-1750": "The merchant Ea-nasir of Ur still traded copper through Dilmun, but the southern Mesopotamian cities lost ground to Babylon under Hammurabi and his successors. With the Indus civilisation in decline, the long-distance trade that sustained Dilmun contracted, and its power fell sharply from the Early Dilmun peak.",
  "Dilmun|-1650": "Building activity at Qal’at al-Bahrain slowed and the temples at Barbar and Saar went out of use. Dilmun survived as a local community of date growers and fishermen, but its role as an independent trading centre had largely ended, and written sources about it almost disappear for a time.",
  "Dilmun|-1450": "Kassite Babylonia took control of Dilmun and installed a governor at Qal’at al-Bahrain. Under foreign rule the island revived economically, exporting dates and serving as a Babylonian outpost in the Gulf. Its power as a dependency remained well below the independent Early Dilmun period, but recovered from its low point.",
  "Dilmun|-1350": "Letters from the governor Ili-ippashra to Enlil-kidinni of Nippur show an active Kassite administration, concerned with the date harvest and raids by nomadic Ahlamu. A large administrative building at Qal’at al-Bahrain testifies to this second prosperity, achieved as a province rather than as a sovereign merchant state.",
  "Dilmun|-1225": "As Kassite Babylonia weakened and the Assyrian king Tukulti-Ninurta I claimed the title king of Dilmun and Meluhha, the island’s importance declined. Gulf trade was limited and Dilmun fell back into obscurity, with little sign of the organised authority that had marked the Kassite governorate a century earlier.",
  "Dilmun|-1000": "Little is known of Dilmun in the early first millennium BCE. Settlement on Bahrain continued at a modest level, and the island probably remained a local trading and date-growing community. Its political organisation is obscure, and it had no significant weight in the affairs of Mesopotamia or the Gulf.",
  "Dilmun|-709": "Uperi, king of Dilmun, sent gifts to the Assyrian king Sargon II, who described him as living like a fish in the midst of the sea. Dilmun had regained a recognised kingship, but as a small tributary state whose survival depended on accommodating the Neo-Assyrian Empire.",
  "Dilmun|-689": "According to an inscription of Sennacherib, after the destruction of Babylon the people of Dilmun sent men with bronze tools, a gesture of submission to Assyria. Dilmun remained a minor kingdom, secure through distance and deference rather than strength, with modest trade in dates and pearls.",
  "Dilmun|-640": "Ashurbanipal received tribute from Hundaru, a later king of Dilmun. The island stayed within the Assyrian sphere as a remote tributary. As Assyria itself approached collapse in the following decades, Dilmun’s kings had little independent weight in the conflicts that reshaped Mesopotamia.",
  "Dilmun|-567": "After the fall of Assyria Dilmun came under Babylonian control, and by the sixth century BCE a Babylonian governor is attested. The kingdom had become a province of the Chaldean Empire, retaining its name and trade but no meaningful political independence.",
  "Dilmun|-538": "After Cyrus the Great captured Babylon in 539 BCE, Dilmun passed with the Babylonian provinces to the Achaemenid Empire and disappears from the record as a political entity. The island survived as a trading post, later known to Greek authors as Tylos.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1800": "A dynasty traditionally founded by Yaqaru established a kingdom centred on Ugarit and its harbour at Minet el-Beida. The city was already prosperous from coastal and overland trade, and contacts with Middle Kingdom Egypt are attested, but it was one of several modest Syrian kingdoms in the shadow of larger powers.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1765": "The archives of Mari record King Zimri-Lim’s journey to Ugarit, showing that the city was a recognised destination for rulers and merchants seeking access to the Mediterranean. Ugarit stood within the sphere of the great kingdom of Yamhad centred on Aleppo, prosperous but politically subordinate.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1650": "Ugarit continued as a regional trading centre while the Old Hittite kingdom raided northern Syria and Yamhad declined. Sources for this period are scarce, but the city grew and its harbour kept up contact with Cyprus and the wider Mediterranean, maintaining a moderate level of wealth and influence.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1450": "After the campaigns of Thutmose III, Ugarit fell within the sphere of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom while also dealing with Mitanni. Its position between the great powers allowed its trade to expand, and the city’s palace and merchant houses grew in size and wealth.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1380": "In the Amarna age, Ugarit under Ammishtamru I corresponded with Egypt as a loyal vassal and flourished as a commercial hub linking the Aegean, Cyprus, Egypt and inland Syria. A fire around this time damaged the palace, but the kingdom’s prosperity and international standing continued to rise.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1345": "Niqmaddu II accepted the overlordship of the Hittite king Suppiluliuma I, who repelled attacks by Mukish, Niya and Nuhashshe and rewarded Ugarit with territory. The treaty fixed a tribute but secured the kingdom’s borders, and the great mythological poems copied by the scribe Ilimilku are traditionally dated to his reign.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1315": "Under Niqmepa, Ugarit remained a loyal Hittite vassal, but Mursili II detached the district of Siyannu from its territory, reducing the kingdom. Niqmepa’s long reign nonetheless brought stability, and trade continued to grow, leaving Ugarit slightly weaker in land but still wealthy.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1274": "Ugarit sent troops to fight for the Hittite king Muwatalli II against Ramesses II at the Battle of Kadesh. The battle’s inconclusive outcome confirmed Hittite control of northern Syria, and Ugarit benefited from the stability that followed, especially after the Egyptian-Hittite peace treaty of 1259 BCE.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1250": "Under Ammishtamru II, Ugarit reached the height of its prosperity. Merchant archives show trade with Cyprus, Egypt, the Aegean and Mesopotamia, and the city’s scribes worked in many languages. Although its king was a Hittite vassal, the kingdom was at its wealthiest and most widely connected.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1220": "Under Ibiranu and Niqmaddu III, Ugarit remained wealthy, but Hittite demands for troops and tribute grew heavier and Hittite kings complained about Ugarit’s loyalty. Tensions in the eastern Mediterranean were rising, and the kingdom’s position, though still strong, was beginning to weaken.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1200": "Ammurapi faced mounting pressure as famine struck Hatti and the Hittite king demanded urgent grain shipments to Ura. Troops and ships were sent away for Hittite service, while letters warned of enemy ships at sea. The kingdom remained intact but was increasingly exposed.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1190": "Letters from Ammurapi to the king of Alashiya report enemy ships burning his towns while his forces were absent. With the Hittite Empire collapsing and Egypt unable to help, Ugarit stood almost defenceless, its wealth now a target and its alliances useless.",
  "Kingdom of Ugarit|-1185": "Ugarit was sacked and burned, probably by seaborne raiders, and was never rebuilt. Letters in the palace that were apparently never sent suggest how suddenly the end came. The kingdom disappeared entirely, and its archives survived only because the fire that destroyed the city baked many of the clay tablets hard.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-1200": "Greek tradition placed the start of a Heraclid dynasty in Maeonia around this time, as the Hittite Empire collapsed. In reality, western Anatolia was fragmented among small communities, and Sardis was a modest settlement. Any Lydian polity was local and weak, and its early history is essentially legendary.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-1000": "In the early Iron Age the Lydian-speaking communities of the Hermus valley developed slowly, with Sardis as one settlement among several. Larger powers in Anatolia were absent after the Hittite collapse, but there is no evidence of a significant Lydian kingdom, and power remained at a low level.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-800": "Sardis grew as a regional centre, but the leading power of central and western Anatolia was the Kingdom of Phrygia, ruled from Gordion. Lydia, under kings remembered by the Greeks as Heraclids, was a secondary state in the Phrygian shadow, controlling the fertile Hermus valley but little beyond.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-700": "The Cimmerians destroyed Phrygian power, opening western Anatolia to new forces. Lydia under Candaules, the last Heraclid king according to Herodotus, remained a modest kingdom, but the collapse of Phrygia created both a dangerous frontier and an opportunity for expansion.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-680": "Gyges seized the throne by killing Candaules and founded the Mermnad dynasty. He began attacking the Greek cities of the coast and sent lavish gifts to Delphi. Lydia emerged as an ambitious regional power, though Cimmerian raids still threatened it.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-660": "Gyges sought the support of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal against the Cimmerians and later backed the Egyptian king Psamtik I, showing his growing international reach. Lydia had taken Colophon and pressed on Miletus and Smyrna, becoming the leading state of western Anatolia.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-644": "Gyges was killed fighting the Cimmerians, and under his son Ardys the invaders sacked Sardis except for its acropolis. The kingdom survived, but its expansion halted and much of its territory was exposed to raids, a severe setback after the gains of Gyges’ reign.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-630": "Ardys and then Sadyattes struggled to restore Lydian power while Cimmerian bands remained active in Anatolia. War with Miletus continued. Lydia held its core in the Hermus valley but could not resume large-scale expansion while the nomad threat persisted, and its power remained well below the level reached under Gyges.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-610": "Alyattes came to the throne, around this time according to Herodotus’ chronology, and began a vigorous recovery. The first electrum coins stamped with a lion’s head were probably struck in his reign, and the kingdom’s wealth and military strength grew rapidly.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-600": "Alyattes drove the Cimmerians out of Asia Minor, ended the war with Miletus by treaty and captured Smyrna. Lydia now controlled most of western Anatolia and the approaches to the Aegean, its power resting on cavalry, gold and growing control of the Greek coastal cities.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-585": "After a five-year war with Median king Cyaxares, an eclipse during battle led to peace, with the river Halys fixed as the frontier. Lydia was recognised as one of the great powers of the Near East alongside Media, Babylonia and Egypt.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-560": "Croesus succeeded Alyattes, overcame a rival half-brother and attacked Ephesus. He soon brought all the Greek cities of Ionia and Aeolis under tribute and extended Lydian supremacy over the peoples west of the Halys. Lydian wealth and prestige were close to their height.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-550": "Lydia stood at its peak. Croesus ruled western Anatolia from the Aegean to the Halys, was famous throughout the Greek world for his wealth and offerings at Delphi and Ephesus, and was allied with Egypt, Babylon and Sparta. The fall of Media to Cyrus, however, created a dangerous new neighbour.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-547": "Croesus crossed the Halys to attack Persia and fought an indecisive battle at Pteria in Cappadocia. He withdrew to Sardis and dismissed his mercenaries for the winter, expecting help from his allies. The kingdom was still intact but had committed itself to a war it could not win.",
  "Kingdom of Lydia|-546": "Cyrus the Great pursued Croesus, defeated the Lydian cavalry outside Sardis and took the city, traditionally after a fourteen-day siege. Croesus was captured and Lydia became the Achaemenid satrapy of Sparda, ending its existence as an independent kingdom; Sardis became the Persian capital of the west.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-1047": "According to the traditional chronology, Saul of Benjamin was anointed king by Samuel to lead the Israelite tribes against the Philistines. The new kingdom was little more than a tribal confederation under a war leader ruling from Gibeah, with no capital city, administration or standing army, so its power was low.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-1040": "Saul’s early victories, including the relief of Jabesh-Gilead from the Ammonites and successes against the Philistines, rallied the tribes behind him, according to the books of Samuel. The kingdom gained cohesion and military confidence, but the Philistines remained the stronger power on the coastal plain.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-1010": "Saul and his sons fell at the Battle of Mount Gilboa against the Philistines. The kingdom split: David ruled Judah from Hebron while Saul’s son Ish-bosheth held the northern tribes from Mahanaim. Divided and dominated by the Philistines, the monarchy was at a low point.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-1003": "After the murder of Ish-bosheth, the elders of Israel anointed David king at Hebron, reuniting the tribes under one ruler. Traditional chronology places this about seven years after Saul’s death. The kingdom now had a single, capable leader with an experienced band of warriors.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-1000": "David captured the Jebusite city of Jerusalem and made it his capital, a neutral site between Judah and the northern tribes. He brought the Ark of the Covenant there, giving the city religious as well as political importance, and began building a royal court.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-990": "According to the Bible, David broke Philistine power and subjugated Moab, Ammon and Edom. Whatever their real extent, these campaigns made the kingdom the leading power in the southern Levant, with tribute flowing to Jerusalem and a professional guard supporting the king.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-980": "The Bible records David’s defeat of Hadadezer of Aram-Zobah and his Aramean allies, extending Israelite influence towards Syria. This is presented as the furthest reach of David’s power, although archaeologists debate the scale of the conquests, which no contemporary non-biblical source confirms.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-967": "Solomon succeeded David, around 970 BCE by the traditional chronology, after a struggle with his brother Adonijah. He inherited a consolidated kingdom and, according to 1 Kings, organised it into districts, made alliances with Tyre and Egypt and began large building projects in Jerusalem.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-957": "The completion of the Temple of Jerusalem and the royal palace, with Phoenician craftsmen and cedar from Hiram I of Tyre, marked the kingdom’s peak in the biblical account. Trade, diplomacy and royal building reached their height, though the true scale of Solomon’s realm remains debated.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-940": "Solomon’s later years saw growing strain: heavy taxation and forced labour angered the northern tribes, Hadad stirred up Edom and Rezon seized Damascus. Jeroboam rebelled and fled to Egypt. The kingdom remained intact under Solomon but was losing its outer territories and inner cohesion.",
  "United Kingdom of Israel|-930": "After Solomon’s death, the northern tribes rejected his son Rehoboam at Shechem and made Jeroboam king. The realm split into the Kingdom of Israel and the Kingdom of Judah, ending the United Monarchy; the House of David continued to rule only Judah from Jerusalem.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-930": "After Solomon’s death the northern tribes rejected Rehoboam, leaving him only Judah and part of Benjamin. The new kingdom was small and rural, but held Jerusalem with its palace and temple and the prestige of the House of David, which gave it a stable dynastic foundation.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-925": "The pharaoh Shoshenq I, the biblical Shishak, campaigned through the region, and the Bible says Rehoboam paid him with the treasures of the temple and palace. Combined with continuing border wars against the Kingdom of Israel, this left the young kingdom weak and impoverished.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-870": "Under Asa and then Jehoshaphat, Judah stabilised. Jehoshaphat made peace and an alliance with Ahab of Israel, sealed by the marriage of his son Jehoram to Athaliah, and Edom remained subject to Judah. The kingdom was secure, though clearly the junior partner of the northern kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-845": "During the reign of Jehoram, Edom revolted and established its own king, and Libnah in the Shephelah also broke away, according to 2 Kings. The loss of Edom cut Judah off from the routes to the Gulf of Aqaba and reduced its revenues and prestige.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-841": "Jehu’s coup in Israel killed King Ahaziah of Judah, and his mother Athaliah seized the throne in Jerusalem, killing most of the royal family. Her six-year rule broke the Davidic line until the priest Jehoiada crowned the child Joash, a period of internal crisis.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-790": "Amaziah defeated Edom but then challenged Jehoash of Israel, who routed him at Beth-shemesh, broke down part of Jerusalem’s wall and plundered the temple. Judah was humiliated and overshadowed by a resurgent northern kingdom, and Amaziah was later killed in a conspiracy.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-760": "Under Uzziah, also called Azariah, Judah recovered. The Bible credits him with rebuilding Elath on the Red Sea, fortifying Jerusalem and reorganising the army, and archaeology shows growing settlement. With Assyria still distant, the kingdom enjoyed a period of prosperity and confidence.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-734": "Threatened by Israel and Damascus in the Syro-Ephraimite war, Ahaz appealed to Tiglath-pileser III and became an Assyrian vassal. Judah lost its independence of action and paid heavy tribute, but escaped the destruction that soon fell on Damascus and much of Israel.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-720": "After Assyria destroyed the Kingdom of Israel and took Samaria in 722 BCE, refugees flowed south. Jerusalem expanded greatly and Judah’s population and economy grew, making it the main surviving Israelite state. It remained an Assyrian vassal, but stronger than ever before.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-705": "On the death of Sargon II, Hezekiah joined a revolt against Assyria. He fortified Jerusalem, dug the Siloam tunnel and organised royal storage with lmlk-stamped jars. Judah was at its most populous and best organised, its peak relative to its own history.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-701": "Sennacherib invaded, took Lachish and, according to his annals, forty-six walled towns, and shut Hezekiah up in Jerusalem. The city was not taken, but Hezekiah paid a heavy tribute, and much of the Shephelah was devastated and handed to Philistine cities.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-680": "Under Manasseh, a loyal Assyrian vassal named in the inscriptions of Esarhaddon and Ashurbanipal, Judah recovered economically. Settlement expanded in the Negev and the Judaean desert, and trade with Arabia brought revenue, though the kingdom remained subordinate to Assyria and its power stayed below the peak reached before 701 BCE.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-640": "Manasseh’s long reign ended and his son Amon was soon murdered; the people of the land placed the child Josiah on the throne. Judah remained stable and prosperous within the Assyrian system, which was now beginning to weaken, giving its rulers more room to act independently.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-622": "With Assyria in decline, Josiah carried out a religious reform after the discovery of a book of the law in the temple, centralising worship in Jerusalem. The Bible also describes his actions at Bethel, in former Israelite territory, a time of renewed national confidence.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-609": "Josiah was killed at Megiddo confronting the pharaoh Necho II. Egypt deposed his son Jehoahaz and installed Jehoiakim as a vassal, imposing tribute. Judah lost its brief independence and became a pawn between Egypt and the rising power of Babylon.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-597": "After Jehoiakim’s rebellion, Nebuchadnezzar II captured Jerusalem, deported King Jehoiachin with the elite and craftsmen, and installed Zedekiah. Judah survived only as a weakened Babylonian vassal, stripped of its leaders, skilled workers and the treasures of the temple and palace, with little capacity left to resist its overlord.",
  "Kingdom of Judah|-587": "After Zedekiah’s revolt, Nebuchadnezzar besieged and destroyed Jerusalem, burning the temple, blinding Zedekiah and deporting much of the population. The kingdom ended, and Judah became a Babylonian province governed from Mizpah by Gedaliah, who was soon assassinated, after which many survivors fled to Egypt.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-930": "After Solomon’s death the northern tribes rejected Rehoboam at Shechem and crowned Jeroboam I. The new kingdom held the richest farmland of the southern Levant and more people than Judah, but it had no established capital, no royal tradition of its own and a hostile southern neighbour, so it began at modest strength.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-900": "The first half-century brought chronic border war with Judah, an Egyptian raid under Shishak in the region, and violent changes of king: Baasha murdered Jeroboam’s son Nadab, and his own son Elah was killed in turn. Without a stable dynasty or capital, Israel spent its resources on internal struggle rather than expansion.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-885": "Omri, commander of the army, won a short civil war after the seven-day reign of Zimri and founded a new dynasty. He built Samaria as a royal capital, made peace with Judah, allied with Phoenician Tyre and Sidon and subjugated Moab, turning Israel into a stable and ambitious regional state.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-870": "Under Ahab the Omride state reached maturity. Fortified centres at Samaria, Jezreel, Megiddo and Hazor, the marriage alliance with Sidon, control of Moab and a working partnership with Judah gave Israel a strong economy and a large chariot army, though Ahab’s wars with Aram-Damascus over Gilead continued.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-853": "At Qarqar Ahab contributed 2,000 chariots and 10,000 infantry to the coalition that checked Shalmaneser III of Assyria, the largest chariot force named on the Kurkh Monolith. Israel was at its military height, the leading power of the southern Levant and an equal partner of Damascus and Hamath.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-841": "Jehu’s bloody coup destroyed the House of Omri, broke the alliances with Judah and Phoenicia, and was followed by submission to Shalmaneser III, shown on the Black Obelisk. Moab had already rebelled under Mesha. Israel remained a sizeable kingdom but lost much of its regional standing almost overnight.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-815": "Hazael of Damascus overran Gilead and pressed deep into Israel; according to the Bible, King Jehoahaz was left with only fifty horsemen, ten chariots and 10,000 foot soldiers. This was Israel’s low point before the Assyrian age, a reduced state largely dominated by its Aramean neighbour.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-790": "Assyrian pressure on Damascus under Adad-nirari III weakened the Arameans, and King Joash of Israel defeated them three times, recovering lost towns. He also defeated Amaziah of Judah at Beth-shemesh and plundered Jerusalem. Israel was again the stronger of the two Hebrew kingdoms and recovering steadily.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-760": "Jeroboam II, who reigned some forty years, restored Israel’s borders from Lebo-hamath to the Dead Sea according to the Books of Kings. The Samaria ostraca and rich buildings point to prosperity, while Amos and Hosea condemned its social injustice. Israel enjoyed its last period of strength and wealth.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-745": "Jeroboam II died around this time and his son Zechariah was murdered within months, beginning a cycle of assassinations. In the same year Tiglath-pileser III took the Assyrian throne and began reforming its army for permanent conquest, so Israel’s political instability coincided with the rise of a far stronger enemy.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-738": "Menahem, who had seized the throne by force, paid Tiglath-pileser III 1,000 talents of silver, raised by a levy of fifty shekels on each wealthy landowner. Israel kept its territory and king but was now an Assyrian tributary, its resources drained and its court divided over submission.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-732": "Pekah’s alliance with Rezin of Damascus against Assyria ended in disaster. Tiglath-pileser III took Damascus, annexed Galilee, the coast and Gilead, and deported part of their population. Pekah was murdered and Hoshea became an Assyrian vassal, ruling only the hill country around Samaria.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-725": "Hoshea withheld tribute and sought help from an Egyptian king the Bible calls So. Shalmaneser V responded by arresting Hoshea and laying siege to Samaria. The rump kingdom, without a king at liberty and without allies who could help, held out only behind the walls of its capital.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-722": "Samaria fell after a siege the Bible says lasted three years. Shalmaneser V died about the same time, and Sargon II later claimed the conquest. The Kingdom of Israel had effectively ceased to exist as an independent state, its capital in Assyrian hands and its last king gone.",
  "Kingdom of Israel|-720": "Sargon II suppressed a revolt in the Samaria region, deported some 27,000 people to Mesopotamia and Media, brought in settlers from elsewhere and organised the territory as the Assyrian province of Samerina. Nothing of the Israelite state remained, though its population and traditions survived in the land and in Judah.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-860": "Arame ruled the first Urartian kingdom known to history, a union of highland chiefdoms that had long been raided by Assyria. It controlled territory around Lake Van and could field armies against Shalmaneser III, but it lacked fortified centres, writing and a stable administration, so its power was still limited.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-856": "Shalmaneser III invaded Urartu, sacked Arame’s royal city of Arzashkun and carried off plunder and captives. The young kingdom survived but was humiliated, and the episode shows how vulnerable the Urartian chiefdoms remained to a well-organised Assyrian army in the mid-ninth century BCE.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-840": "Sarduri I founded a new dynasty and built the citadel of Tushpa on the rock of Van, leaving the first Urartian royal inscriptions. A permanent fortified capital and a royal ideology borrowed from Assyria gave the state a durable core from which his successors could expand.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-820": "Ishpuini, with his son Menua as co-ruler, introduced inscriptions in the Urartian language, organised the cult of Haldi and brought the sanctuary of Musasir under Urartian influence, as recorded on the Kelishin stele. Expansion towards Lake Urmia made Urartu a growing regional power.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-800": "Menua campaigned north towards the Araxes and west towards the Euphrates, founded fortresses and built the great canal that still waters Van. Dozens of his building inscriptions survive. Urartu now had the territory, water management and administrative network of a substantial kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-782": "Argishti I founded Erebuni, settling 6,600 captives there, and soon afterwards Argishtihinili in the Ararat plain. His long annals record campaigns across Transcaucasia and against Assyrian-controlled lands. Assyria, weakened by internal troubles, could not stop him, and Urartu became the leading power of the northern highlands.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-760": "Sarduri II succeeded his father around this time and continued expansion towards the Lake Sevan basin, north-western Iran and the upper Euphrates. With Assyria still in a period of weakness, Urartu extended its influence over Melid and other states of the Taurus and northern Syria.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-745": "On the eve of Tiglath-pileser III’s reforms, Sarduri II stood at the head of an alliance with Arpad and other Syrian and Anatolian states. Urartu’s territory, tribute and diplomatic reach were at their greatest, and it rivalled Assyria for control of the routes between Mesopotamia and Anatolia.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-743": "Tiglath-pileser III defeated Sarduri II and his allies, and the Urartian king fled from the battlefield. Urartu lost its position in northern Syria, and its allies were absorbed or punished by Assyria. The heartland was untouched, but the kingdom’s ambitions to the south-west were ended.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-735": "Tiglath-pileser III invaded the Urartian heartland and besieged Tushpa, ravaging the surrounding country, but failed to take the citadel. Urartu remained a large and well-fortified kingdom, yet the war showed that the reformed Assyrian army could strike at its centre.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-714": "Rusa I, already defeated by the Cimmerians, was routed by Sargon II, who devastated the country and sacked Musasir and its temple of Haldi. Rusa died soon after, reportedly by his own hand. This was the deepest crisis of the kingdom since its foundation, combining military defeat with a blow to royal legitimacy.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-700": "Argishti II restored royal authority and campaigned to the east, but Urartu no longer challenged Assyria directly. Cimmerian raiders remained a threat on its borders. The kingdom had survived Sargon’s blow and was slowly rebuilding its strength under a more cautious foreign policy.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-675": "Rusa II undertook the last great building programme, founding Teishebaini, Bastam, Ayanis and Rusahinili and resettling captives. Peaceful relations with Assyria and new storehouses and temples point to a strong recovery, although the kingdom did not regain its eighth-century territory or influence in Syria.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-643": "A King Sarduri sent gifts to Ashurbanipal, and Assyrian scribes describe him addressing the Assyrian king with a new deference. Urartu was still a functioning kingdom with major fortresses, but its standing had declined and evidence for its later kings becomes very scarce.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-608": "The Babylonian Chronicle records a campaign towards Urashtu as Assyria collapsed. Fortresses such as Teishebaini, Ayanis and Bastam were burned around this period, probably by Scythians, Medes or others. Whatever remained of Urartian royal authority was fragmented and close to extinction.",
  "Kingdom of Urartu|-590": "By about this date the Median Empire controlled the former Urartian lands, and the kingdom disappears from history, although the exact date and manner of its end remain uncertain. Its territory later formed the Achaemenid satrapy of Armenia, and the Urartian language fell out of use.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-800": "By about this time Sabaean inscriptions record rulers titled mukarrib at Marib and Sirwah, leading a federation of communities devoted to Almaqah. Irrigation of the Marib oasis was well under way, but Saba was still one of several small South Arabian polities, so its power was modest.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-715": "A Sabaean ruler named Itamra, probably the mukarrib Yitha’amar, sent gifts of precious goods to Sargon II of Assyria. The episode shows that Saba already controlled valuable incense and luxury goods and had diplomatic contact with the great powers of the Near East, a sign of growing wealth.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-685": "Karib’il Watar, probably the Karibilu who sent gifts to Sennacherib, destroyed the Kingdom of Awsan, subdued the towns of the Jawf and imposed Sabaean supremacy over much of south-western Arabia. His inscription at Sirwah marks the height of Sabaean territorial power, although its exact date is debated.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-600": "Saba remained the leading power of South Arabia, with Marib’s irrigation works expanding and the incense trade to the Levant and Mesopotamia flourishing. Its neighbours had not yet thrown off Sabaean dominance, though its hold over the more distant regions conquered by Karib’il Watar was probably loosening.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-500": "Sabaean wealth from incense and agriculture stayed high, and monumental temples such as the Awwam and Bar’an at Marib were enlarged. Qataban and Hadramawt, however, were becoming independent kingdoms, so Saba’s supremacy over the whole of South Arabia was giving way to a system of rival states.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-400": "Qataban, Hadramawt and the Minaean Kingdom of Ma’in in the Jawf were now fully independent and often hostile, and Ma’in took over much of the northern caravan trade. Sabaean rulers had adopted the title of king. Saba remained wealthy but was one power among several.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-200": "Saba held its heartland around Marib but faced strong rivals on all sides. Greek shipping from Ptolemaic Egypt was beginning to exploit the Red Sea route, an early threat to the overland caravan trade on which Sabaean prosperity had always depended.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-110": "The Himyarites established a kingdom in the highlands centred on Zafar, beginning centuries of rivalry. Saba’s old ruling families were weakening, and highland tribes would soon provide its kings. Saba was now a declining power squeezed between Himyar, Qataban and Hadramawt.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|-25": "Saba withstood the Roman expedition of Aelius Gallus, whose army besieged Marib for six days before retreating for lack of water. The kingdom remained able to defend its capital, but monsoon sea trade was steadily diverting the incense and eastern goods that had made it rich.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|100": "In the first century CE Saba and Himyar competed for the title of King of Saba and Dhu Raydan, and the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea describes a Himyarite king at Zafar as ruler of Sabaeans and Himyarites. Saba’s independent power had sunk to a low ebb.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|200": "Under kings from the highland lineages, Saba recovered some strength, allying at times with Aksum and fighting Himyar and Hadramawt. Inscriptions at the Awwam temple record campaigns and victories, showing a revived but embattled kingdom in an era of shifting coalitions.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|225": "Sha’irum Awtar, according to his inscriptions, defeated Hadramawt and took its capital Shabwa, while also fighting Himyar and the Aksumites. This was Saba’s last period of military success, briefly restoring it as one of the strongest powers in South Arabia.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|260": "After Sha’irum Awtar, Sabaean power faded quickly. Himyar gained the upper hand in the long struggle for the highlands, and Sabaean kings now controlled little beyond the immediate Marib region. The old kingdom was close to absorption by its Himyarite rival.",
  "Kingdom of Saba|275": "Around this date the Himyarite king Shammar Yuhar’ish brought Saba under his rule and kept the title King of Saba and Dhu Raydan. The independent Sabaean kingdom ended after about a thousand years, though Marib, its dam and the Sabaic language survived under Himyar.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|-302": "According to Georgian tradition, Pharnavaz I united Kartli around this time, after the collapse of Achaemenid power, and organised it into provinces. The kingdom held the Mtkvari valley around Mtskheta and Armaztsikhe but was still a modest regional power among the peoples of the Caucasus.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|-150": "Iberia consolidated its hold on the central Mtkvari valley and the routes through the Caucasus. Towns, fortresses and a structured aristocracy developed, and the kingdom traded with Colchis, the Black Sea Greeks and Armenia. It remained overshadowed by larger neighbours such as Armenia, whose power would soon peak under Tigranes the Great.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|-65": "Pompey invaded Iberia while pursuing Mithridates VI, defeated King Artoces on the Mtkvari and Aragvi rivers and took his children as hostages. Iberia submitted and became a Roman client, losing freedom of action but gaining a powerful patron against Armenia and Parthia.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|35": "Pharasmanes I, encouraged by Rome, seized Armenia for his brother Mithridates and led Iberians and Sarmatian allies to victory over the Parthians. Iberia was now an active military power shaping the politics of the whole Caucasus, rather than a passive client state.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|75": "The Emperor Vespasian had the walls of Mtskheta strengthened for King Mithridates of Iberia, as a Greek inscription records. Roman support for Iberia as guardian of the Caucasus passes against the Alans confirmed its value and kept the kingdom strong and secure.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|140": "Pharasmanes II, who probably encouraged an Alan invasion of Armenia and Media in the 130s, visited Rome and was honoured by the emperor, receiving an enlargement of his territory. Iberia stood at its height as a wealthy, assertive Roman ally with an influential court at Mtskheta.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|260": "The new Sasanian Empire of Iran pressed hard on the Caucasus, and Shapur I listed Iberia among his dominions in his great inscription. The kingdom kept its own kings but lost its independence of action, slipping from Roman alliance into Persian dependence.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|299": "After Rome’s victory over the Persian king Narseh, the Peace of Nisibis gave Rome the right to invest the kings of Iberia. Iberia returned to the Roman sphere, gaining protection and some freedom from Persian control as the Chosroid dynasty took the throne.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|330": "Under King Mirian III Iberia adopted Christianity, traditionally through the preaching of St Nino, and a royal church was built at Mtskheta. The conversion tied the kingdom closely to the Christian Roman Empire and gave the monarchy a new ideology and cultural direction.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|370": "After Rome abandoned its claims in 363, Shapur II imposed his own king, and Iberia was for a time divided between Roman and Persian candidates. The kingdom lost unity and independence, and Sasanian supremacy over the region became the lasting reality.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|470": "Vakhtang I Gorgasali, according to Georgian tradition, strengthened the monarchy, campaigned in the north and raised the status of the Iberian Church under its own catholicos. He is credited with founding Tbilisi. The kingdom enjoyed a revival of royal power within the Persian sphere.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|484": "Vakhtang joined the Armenian revolt against Peroz in 482, but Persian armies defeated the rebels and devastated the country. The rising showed both the ambition and the limits of Iberian power, and Persian supervision of the kingdom resumed after the war.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|523": "King Gurgenes rose against Kavad I and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor Justin I, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica. Iberia’s monarchy survived only as a weakened institution under tight Persian control.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|555": "During the Lazic War between Byzantium and Iran, Iberia was a Persian base and corridor. Its kings, now based at Tbilisi, kept their titles but had little real authority, while Persian officials and the great noble families held effective power in the land.",
  "Kingdom of Iberia|580": "On the death of King Bakur III, Hormizd IV abolished the Iberian monarchy and placed the land under a Persian marzban. Royal rule in Kartli ended after nearly nine centuries, although the nobility soon created the Principality of Iberia under Guaram I.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-281": "Mithridates I Ktistes took the royal title after the Battle of Corupedium, ruling a newly formed kingdom in the Iris and Lycus valleys from Amaseia. He had no coastal cities of note and faced powerful Hellenistic neighbours, so Pontus began as a small inland state built on fortresses and local loyalties.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-250": "Mithridates II ruled a kingdom that had survived the arrival of the Galatians in Anatolia and continued to build ties with the Seleucids. Pontus held its inland core but still lacked the great ports of the coast, and it remained a minor kingdom among the Hellenistic states.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-220": "Mithridates II, married to a Seleucid princess and linked to the Seleucid royal house by further marriages, attempted to take Sinope in 220 BCE but failed when Rhodes helped the city. Pontus was growing in ambition and prestige, though its reach still exceeded its strength.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-183": "Pharnaces I captured Sinope and made it his capital, gaining a great harbour, a fleet and the trade of the southern Black Sea. He also took Kotyora and Kerasous. Pontus became a maritime power for the first time, though his expansion soon provoked a coalition of neighbours.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-179": "After a war against Eumenes II of Pergamon, Bithynia and Cappadocia, Pharnaces I made peace and gave up most of his recent conquests in Galatia and Paphlagonia, keeping Sinope. Pontus had been checked by its neighbours and, behind them, by the growing influence of Rome.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-150": "Mithridates V Euergetes came to the throne around this time and adopted a policy of friendship with Rome, sending ships for the Third Punic War. Pontus was stable and prosperous, with Sinope flourishing, but it remained a middle-ranking power in Anatolia.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-129": "Rewarded for helping Rome against Aristonicus in the war over the Pergamene inheritance, Mithridates V received Greater Phrygia and extended his influence over Cappadocia and Paphlagonia. Pontus was now one of the leading kingdoms of Anatolia, enjoying Roman favour and growing revenues.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-120": "Mithridates V was assassinated at Sinope, leaving his young sons under the regency of their mother Laodice. Rome soon withdrew the grant of Phrygia. The kingdom lost momentum and faced several years of uncertain rule, factional struggle and court intrigue.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-107": "Mithridates VI, sole ruler since about 113 BCE, had through his general Diophantus brought Chersonesus and the Bosporan Kingdom under Pontic rule, and he soon also controlled Colchis and Lesser Armenia. The Black Sea was becoming a Pontic lake, and the kingdom’s resources multiplied.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-95": "Mithridates VI intervened in Paphlagonia and Cappadocia and allied with Tigranes the Great of Armenia, marrying him to his daughter Cleopatra. Rome blocked his ambitions in Cappadocia, but his empire around the Black Sea gave him armies and revenues larger than any Anatolian king had held.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-88": "Mithridates VI overran the Roman province of Asia, ordered the massacre of Romans and Italians, and sent armies and fleets into Greece, where Athens joined him. Ruling from Pergamon, he controlled most of Anatolia and the Aegean islands, the absolute peak of Pontic power.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-85": "Defeated by Sulla at Chaeronea and Orchomenus, Mithridates accepted the Peace of Dardanus, giving up Asia, his Aegean conquests and part of his fleet, and paying an indemnity. He kept his kingdom and its Black Sea empire, but his bid for supremacy had failed.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-75": "After the brief Second Mithridatic War, Mithridates rebuilt his army on Roman lines and strengthened his fleet, and he allied with Roman rebels in Spain under Sertorius. Pontus was again a formidable power, ready for a new war when Rome annexed Bithynia.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-71": "Lucullus relieved Cyzicus, destroyed much of the Pontic army and defeated Mithridates at Cabira. The king fled to his son-in-law Tigranes in Armenia, and Roman forces occupied Pontus and besieged its cities, leaving the kingdom and most of its fortresses largely in Roman hands.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-67": "While Lucullus’ army was exhausted and mutinous, Mithridates returned to Pontus and destroyed a Roman force under Triarius at Zela. He recovered much of his kingdom, but his resources were far smaller than before and Rome was preparing a new commander.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-66": "Pompey, appointed under the Lex Manilia, routed Mithridates on the Lycus river. The king fled with a small following through Colchis to the Bosporus, and Pompey took over Pontus. The kingdom was effectively lost, and its ruler a fugitive at the edge of the Black Sea.",
  "Kingdom of Pontus|-63": "Abandoned by his army and by his son Pharnaces II, Mithridates VI died at Panticapaeum. Pompey annexed western Pontus to the province of Bithynia et Pontus and gave the rest to client rulers. The independent Kingdom of Pontus came to an end after about 220 years.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|-110": "The Himyarite era, later used to date inscriptions, probably began around this time, marking the emergence of Himyar as a kingdom in the southern highlands around Zafar. Saba, Qataban and Hadramawt still overshadowed it, and its power was regional, resting on highland farming and tribal alliances rather than control of the incense trade.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|-25": "The Roman expedition of Aelius Gallus in 26–25 BCE showed outside interest in the incense lands but failed before Marib. Himyar, already claiming the title king of Saba and Dhu Raydan, was gaining ground as Saba weakened and the sea route through the Red Sea began to replace the caravan trade.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|50": "Around the mid-first century the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea describes Charibael, king of the Homerites and Sabaeans, ruling from Saphar and sending embassies to the Roman emperors. Himyar controlled the port of Muza and the south-western coast, a growing share of Red Sea trade, though it still competed with Saba and Hadramawt.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|200": "By about 200 Qataban had disappeared, and the south was divided between Saba, Himyar and Hadramawt. Himyar and Saba fought repeatedly for the highlands, and early in the third century Aksumite forces became involved in the Tihama. Himyar\u0027s power was considerable but contested, and no single kingdom could dominate the region.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|275": "Shammar Yuhar\u0027ish annexed Saba around this time, ending centuries of rivalry and taking the old Sabaean centres, including Marib and its great dam. Himyar now held the highlands and most of western Yemen, and its king assumed a fuller royal title, making the kingdom the leading power of south Arabia.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|300": "Around 300 Shammar Yuhar\u0027ish completed unification by annexing the Kingdom of Hadramawt, with its frankincense lands and the port of Qana. His title became king of Saba, Dhu Raydan, Hadramawt and Yamnat. For the first time one dynasty ruled all of south Arabia, from the Red Sea coast to the eastern wadis.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|380": "Around 380 King Malkikarib Yuha\u0027min and his court abandoned the traditional gods in favour of a single god, the Merciful, Lord of Heaven. The change gave the dynasty a new official religion close to Judaism. The kingdom was stable and united, and its kings began to look towards central Arabia.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|440": "Under Abikarib As\u0027ad, remembered in Arab tradition as a great conquering king, Himyar reached its widest extent, campaigning into central Arabia and recording an expedition at Ma\u0027sal al-Jumh. The royal title now claimed the Arabs of the highlands and the Tihama, and the Kingdom of Kinda served as Himyar\u0027s client among the tribes.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|456": "An inscription of Sharahbil Ya\u0027fur records extensive repairs of the Marib Dam in the 450s, carried out with labour mobilised across the kingdom. Himyar still commanded the resources for great public works and remained the paramount power of south Arabia, with clients in the interior, although its later fifth-century history is thinly documented.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|500": "Around 500 the kingdom remained united but faced growing tension between the Judaising court and Christian communities in Najran and elsewhere, which looked to Aksum and the Byzantine Empire. Little is known of the reigns of this period, but internal divisions and outside interest in the Red Sea route were weakening the monarchy.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|518": "Around 518 Kaleb of Aksum appears to have intervened in Yemen, and a Christian king, Ma\u0027dikarib Ya\u0027fur, probably ruled as Aksum\u0027s client. Himyar had become tributary to its neighbour across the Red Sea, and its independence and authority over central Arabia were sharply reduced. The exact chronology of this intervention is debated.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|523": "Yusuf As\u0027ar Yath\u0027ar seized power, attacked the Aksumite garrison and the Christian communities, and besieged Najran, where many Christians were killed. The campaign briefly restored Himyarite independence, but it alienated the Christian powers and provoked Aksum and the Byzantine Empire to act against the kingdom, leaving it isolated and exposed.",
  "Himyarite Kingdom|525": "Kaleb of Aksum crossed the Red Sea with a large army, defeated the Himyarites and killed Yusuf As\u0027ar Yath\u0027ar. Aksum installed Sumyafa\u0027 Ashwa\u0027 as a Christian client king, and Himyar\u0027s independent history ended. Aksumite Yemen took its place and was soon ruled by the commander Abraha as a separate kingdom.",
  "Caucasian Albania|-100": "By about 100 BCE the tribes of the Kura plain and the southern Caucasus slopes had come under a single king, according to Strabo\u0027s later account. The new kingdom, centred on Kabala, was a loose union of 26 tribes with different languages, small in resources and dependent on the loyalty of its noble families.",
  "Caucasian Albania|-65": "Oroeses attacked Pompey\u0027s winter quarters in 66–65 BCE and was then defeated near the Abas river, after which he made peace. The kingdom had shown that it could field a large army, but defeat drew it into the Roman sphere, where it would remain a client for much of the following centuries.",
  "Caucasian Albania|-36": "Publius Canidius Crassus, a general of Mark Antony, campaigned against the Iberians and Albanians in 36 BCE, again forcing the kingdom to accept Roman terms. Albania survived as a client monarchy, valued as a buffer on the route to the Caspian but with little weight of its own in the wider struggle between Rome and Parthia.",
  "Caucasian Albania|90": "Under Domitian a detachment of Legio XII Fulminata left a Latin inscription at Gobustan near Baku, marking Roman presence on the Caspian. Albania was a stable client of Rome, guarding the passes against steppe raiders and benefiting from the protection of a distant empire, while its kings kept control of internal affairs.",
  "Caucasian Albania|135": "An Alan invasion through the Caucasus struck Albania and Media in 135, showing the kingdom\u0027s vulnerability to the steppe. The raiders were turned back by Roman diplomacy and the pressure of the governor Arrian, but Albania\u0027s own forces could not stop them, and its security depended on the larger empires to the south.",
  "Caucasian Albania|262": "Shapur I listed Albania among the lands of his realm in his inscription at Naqsh-e Rustam, recording its passage into the Sasanian sphere. The kingdom continued under its own kings but now as a Persian client, supplying cavalry for Sasanian wars and guarding the Caspian route against the peoples of the north.",
  "Caucasian Albania|330": "Around this time, according to tradition, King Urnayr adopted Christianity in connection with the mission of Gregory the Illuminator. An Arsacid branch now ruled, linked to the royal houses of Armenia and Iberia. The conversion gave the kingdom a church and new ties to the Christian Caucasus while it remained a Persian client.",
  "Caucasian Albania|359": "Ammianus Marcellinus records Albanians in the army of Shapur II at the siege of Amida in 359. The kingdom was a valued Sasanian ally, its cavalry fighting in Persia\u0027s wars against Rome, and its standing among the client states of the Caucasus was rising as Armenia was weakened by war.",
  "Caucasian Albania|387": "After the partition of Armenia between Rome and Persia around 387, Albania received the former Armenian provinces of Artsakh and Utik south of the Kura. The kingdom\u0027s territory was greatly enlarged and its population grew, bringing fertile valleys and fortresses under the Albanian crown and making it a significant regional power within the Sasanian system.",
  "Caucasian Albania|430": "In the early fifth century an alphabet was created for the Albanian language, credited by Armenian tradition to Mesrop Mashtots, and church literature began to be written. With enlarged territory, its own catholicos and a written language, the kingdom stood at the height of its political and cultural development under Sasanian overlordship.",
  "Caucasian Albania|451": "Albanians joined the Armenian and Iberian rising against Yazdegerd II\u0027s attempt to impose Zoroastrianism, which was crushed at the Battle of Avarayr in 451. The defeat exposed the kingdom to Persian retaliation and strained the loyalty of its nobles, beginning the decline of royal authority under increasing Sasanian supervision.",
  "Caucasian Albania|462": "King Vache II\u0027s revolt against Persia around 459 ended with his abdication, and the kingship lapsed for about a quarter of a century. Persian officials governed the country, the Albanian nobles and church survived without a king, and the kingdom existed only as a province of the Sasanian Empire for a generation.",
  "Caucasian Albania|485": "The Sasanian king Balash restored the Albanian monarchy around 485 in the person of Vachagan III, as part of a wider settlement with the Christian Caucasus. The restored kingdom was again a recognised client state, with a Christian king allowed to support the church, though Persian supervision remained close and royal resources limited.",
  "Caucasian Albania|488": "Vachagan III the Pious issued church canons at the council of Aghuen around 488, regulating church discipline, marriage and relations between clergy and nobles. His reign, remembered as a golden age of church building, marked a final revival of Albanian royal authority within the limits allowed by Sasanian overlordship.",
  "Caucasian Albania|510": "After Vachagan III died around 510, the Sasanians abolished the Albanian kingship and governed the country through marzbans at Partav. The kingdom ended as an independent monarchy, though its church survived, and local rule revived only a century later under the Mihranid princes before the Arab conquest of the 640s.",
  "Alans|50": "Around the mid-first century the Alans appear in Roman sources as a new people between the Don and the Caucasus, emerging from the Sarmatian world. Their confederation was still forming, absorbing neighbouring groups, and its power was regional, though Roman writers already treated the Alans as a formidable force on the steppe frontier.",
  "Alans|72": "In the early 70s, according to Josephus, the Alans crossed the Caucasus passes and raided Media and Armenia, nearly capturing the Armenian king Tiridates. The raid showed the reach of their cavalry and made them a concern for both Rome and Parthia, which competed for influence over the passes they could open or close.",
  "Alans|135": "Around 135 an Alan invasion struck Albania, Media and Armenia, and the governor Arrian prepared Roman forces against them, writing his Order of Battle against the Alans. The confederation could now threaten the eastern provinces of both empires, and its leaders were courted and bribed by the rulers of Rome and Parthia alike.",
  "Alans|250": "In the third century the Alans were a leading power of the steppe north of the Black Sea and the Caucasus, raiding the Roman frontier alongside the Goths and other peoples and controlling the Caucasus passes. Their absorption of other Sarmatian groups continued, extending the Alan name across a broad belt of the steppe.",
  "Alans|350": "By the mid-fourth century, as Ammianus Marcellinus later described, the Alans had imposed their name on many peoples between the Don and the steppes further east. Their confederation was at its widest, dominating the pastures north of the Caucasus and the Caspian, though it remained a loose union of clans rather than a state.",
  "Alans|370": "Around 370 the Huns defeated the Alans near the Don, killing many and forcing others to submit and join them. The confederation was shattered: some groups fought in Hunnic armies, some fled west with the Goths, and others withdrew into the Caucasus foothills, leaving no single Alan power on the steppe.",
  "Alans|378": "Alan cavalry is reported among the Gothic forces that destroyed a Roman army at the Battle of Adrianople in 378. Displaced Alan groups were now active inside the Roman world, as allies of the Goths or as Roman recruits, while others remained under Hunnic rule. Their power was scattered but still militarily significant.",
  "Alans|406": "On the last day of 406, by the traditional date, Alans under Respendial and Goar crossed the Rhine with the Vandals and Suebi. Respendial\u0027s Alans saved the Vandals from the Franks, while Goar joined Rome. The invasion of Gaul gave the western Alans a new prominence in the collapse of Roman authority.",
  "Alans|411": "After entering Hispania in 409, the invaders divided the peninsula in 411, and the Alans received Lusitania and Carthaginiensis, the largest share. Under their king Addac they were the strongest of the invading groups, controlling much of western and central Iberia and dominating their Vandal and Suebic partners for a few years.",
  "Alans|418": "The Visigoths under Wallia, fighting for Rome, crushed the Alans of Hispania in 416–418 and killed their king Addac. The survivors placed themselves under the Vandal king Gunderic, ending the independent Alan kingdom in Iberia. The western Alans lost their separate political identity and were absorbed into the Vandal following.",
  "Alans|429": "In 429 Alans crossed with the Vandals to North Africa, where the Vandal kings styled themselves kings of the Vandals and Alans. The Alans there no longer formed a separate power, and in Gaul other groups were being settled by Aetius as Roman allies. Alan power existed only in fragments within other states.",
  "Alans|451": "Alans under Sangiban fought on the Roman side against Attila at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451, while others served the Huns. The general Aspar, of Alan descent, dominated the Eastern Roman court. Alans remained militarily useful, but as allies and soldiers of others rather than as an independent power.",
  "Alans|558": "In the Caucasus the Alans had regrouped under their own rulers. Around 558 their king Sarosius helped the first Avar envoys reach the Byzantine court, and he was a valued ally of Byzantium against Persia. The Caucasian Alans controlled the Darial Pass and enjoyed a modest revival as a regional power.",
  "Alans|650": "By the mid-seventh century the Alans of the North Caucasus had become tributary to the Khazar Khaganate, which dominated the steppe north of the Caucasus. They retained their chiefs and their hold on the mountain passes, but now served as junior partners of the Khazars in their wars with the Arab caliphate.",
  "Alans|800": "Around 800 the Alan chiefdoms of the North Caucasus were coalescing into the Kingdom of Alania, a more centralised state that would adopt Christianity in the early tenth century. The old Alan confederation had ended, and its history continued in this medieval kingdom and ultimately in the Ossetians.",
  "Shirvanshah State|861": "Haytham ibn Khalid, a descendant of the Abbasid governor Yazid ibn Mazyad, declared himself Shirvanshah as caliphal power collapsed after the murder of al-Mutawakkil. The new hereditary principality, ruled from Shamakhi, held only Shirvan proper and still recognised the caliph, but it had become a self-governing state on the Caspian coast.",
  "Shirvanshah State|917": "In the early tenth century the Shirvanshahs absorbed the neighbouring principality of Layzan, enlarging their territory. The Rus raid on the Caspian coast in 913–914 showed the region\u0027s vulnerability, but the dynasty consolidated its hold on the lands between the Kura and the Caucasus while caliphal authority faded further.",
  "Shirvanshah State|964": "Under Ahmad ibn Muhammad (956–981) the Shirvanshahs held Shirvan and Layzan and pressed their claim to Derbent, the fortress city guarding the Caspian coastal route, which a Shirvanshah prince had briefly ruled in 939–942. Its Hashimid emirs resisted and in the 960s raided Shirvan, but the dynasty stood at the height of its early power.",
  "Shirvanshah State|991": "Muhammad ibn Ahmad, invited into Derbent in 990, garrisoned its citadel but died in 991, and the Hashimid emir soon retook the city. His brother Yazid ibn Ahmad fought for Derbent for decades, holding it only briefly, and gave his sons Persian names, marking the shift to the Kasranid line within a regional principality.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1066": "The Seljuk Empire\u0027s advance into the Caucasus forced Fariburz I to accept Seljuk overlordship and pay tribute. Shirvan kept its dynasty and internal autonomy, but its rulers were now vassals of a great Turkic empire, and Turkic tribes began to settle in the region, adding to the pressures on the small state.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1123": "In the early 1120s David IV the Builder of Georgia campaigned in Shirvan, and the Georgian kingdom became the dominant power in the region. Shirvan lost much of its freedom of action and for about a century lived under strong Georgian influence, its rulers acting as clients of the kings of Georgia.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1150": "Manuchihr III, married to Tamar, daughter of David IV of Georgia, ruled a prosperous Shirvan under Georgian protection. His court at Shamakhi attracted Khaqani and Falaki Shirvani, and silk and Caspian trade brought wealth. Though still a client, the state enjoyed stability and a cultural golden age.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1188": "Nizami Ganjavi completed Layla and Majnun at the request of Akhsitan I in 1188, a sign of the court\u0027s continuing prestige. Shirvan was prosperous and culturally brilliant, though politically modest. A severe earthquake struck Shamakhi in 1191–1192, and according to tradition the court moved to Baku for a time.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1221": "The Mongols under Jebe and Subutai took Shamakhi in 1221, during their great raid around the Caspian. The sack devastated the capital and was followed in the 1230s by the full Mongol conquest of the Caucasus. The Shirvanshahs survived only by submitting to Mongol overlords and paying heavy tribute.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1262": "In the 1260s Shirvan became a battleground in the wars between the Ilkhanate and the Golden Horde, whose armies fought along the Kura and the Terek. The Shirvanshahs, vassals of the Ilkhans, saw their lands repeatedly crossed by armies, and their own power was reduced to a minimum.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1335": "The death of the Ilkhan Abu Sa\u0027id in 1335 led to the collapse of the Ilkhanate, freeing the Shirvanshahs from direct Mongol control. The dynasty regained some independence amid the struggles of the successor states, but its old line was weakening and Shirvan remained exposed to stronger neighbours.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1382": "When the old line ended, the nobles chose Ibrahim I, of a collateral branch from Derbent, as Shirvanshah. He restored order and began the revival of the state. His Derbendi line would rule until 1538 and bring Shirvan its most prosperous period, though his first task was to survive the coming storm of Timur.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1386": "Ibrahim I submitted to Timur in 1386 and was confirmed as ruler, gaining Timurid protection against the Golden Horde under Tokhtamysh. By accepting Timur\u0027s overlordship he kept his throne and lands intact while much of the region was devastated, and Shirvan emerged as a valued Timurid vassal.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1412": "In 1412 Ibrahim I and his ally Constantine I of Georgia were defeated by Qara Yusuf of the Qara Qoyunlu; Constantine was executed, and Ibrahim was captured and later ransomed. The setback showed the limits of Shirvan\u0027s power, but the dynasty kept its lands and recovered after Ibrahim\u0027s release.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1441": "Under Khalilullah I, who ruled from 1417 for over four decades, Shirvan enjoyed its longest period of peace and prosperity. He built the Palace of the Shirvanshahs in Baku, with its mausoleum of 1435 and mosque of 1441. Silk and trade enriched the state, which stood at the height of its power.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1465": "Khalilullah I died in 1465 and was succeeded by his son Farrukh Yasar, who inherited a wealthy and well-ordered state. In 1460 Shirvanshah forces had killed Shaykh Junayd of the Safavid order, removing an immediate threat, but beginning a blood feud that would later prove fatal to the dynasty.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1488": "Farrukh Yasar, aided by the Aq Qoyunlu, defeated and killed Shaykh Haydar of the Safavid order in 1488. Shirvan remained prosperous and secure under Aq Qoyunlu protection, but the killing of Haydar, father of the future Shah Ismail I, made the Safavids determined enemies of the dynasty.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1500": "Ismail I, seeking revenge for his father and grandfather, defeated and killed Farrukh Yasar at the Battle of Jabani in 1500 and took Shamakhi and Baku. The Shirvanshah state was crippled, its army destroyed and its capital sacked, and it survived only because Ismail turned to wider conquests.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1524": "Ibrahim II Shaykhshah and then Khalilullah II ruled as tributary princes under Safavid supremacy. Shirvan kept its dynasty and some autonomy, but real power lay with the Safavid shahs, and the death of Shah Ismail I in 1524 brought no recovery of the state\u0027s former independence.",
  "Shirvanshah State|1538": "After the death of Khalilullah II, Tahmasp I sent an army under his brother Alqas Mirza, which overcame the last claimants and annexed Shirvan as a Safavid province in 1538. The Shirvanshah state ended after nearly seven centuries, and later revolts by members of the dynasty failed to restore it.",
  "Buyid dynasty|934": "Ali ibn Buya, an officer of the Ziyarid Mardavij, defeated the caliph\u0027s governor Yaqut and took Fars, making Shiraz his base. With his brothers serving in the same armies, the family held a single rich province and a band of Daylamite soldiers, but their position was still precarious among rival warlords.",
  "Buyid dynasty|945": "Ahmad ibn Buya entered Baghdad in December 945, and the caliph al-Mustakfi gave the brothers their famous titles before being deposed and replaced by al-Muti. The Buyids now controlled Fars, the Jibal and Iraq, and held the Abbasid caliph, the most important source of legitimacy in the Sunni world, in their hands.",
  "Buyid dynasty|949": "Imad al-Dawla died in 949, having named his nephew, the future Adud al-Dawla, as his heir in Fars. Rukn al-Dawla at Ray became head of the family. The transition passed smoothly, and the three branches in Fars, Ray and Baghdad held their lands against the Samanids, the Hamdanids and other rivals.",
  "Buyid dynasty|963": "In 963 Mu\u0027izz al-Dawla introduced public mourning for Husayn on Ashura and the feast of Ghadir in Baghdad. The Buyid confederation was stable and secure, though Iraq suffered from disorder, riots between Sunni and Shia quarters and the decline of agriculture under iqta grants. Fars prospered under Adud al-Dawla\u0027s careful rule.",
  "Buyid dynasty|976": "Rukn al-Dawla died in 976, leaving Adud al-Dawla as the strongest member of the family. Adud already controlled Fars, Kerman and Oman, and had intervened in Iraq against the mutiny of Sabuktakin. The confederation was powerful, but the weakness of Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar in Baghdad was opening the way for a struggle.",
  "Buyid dynasty|978": "Adud al-Dawla defeated his cousin Bakhtiyar, who was killed at Qasr al-Jiss in 978, and took Iraq. He styled himself shahanshah and had his name added to the Friday prayer in Baghdad. For the first time a single Buyid ruler controlled Iraq, Fars, Kerman, Khuzistan and Oman.",
  "Buyid dynasty|982": "Adud al-Dawla stood at the height of his power, having taken Mosul from the Hamdanids in 979, while his brother Mu\u0027ayyad al-Dawla drove the Ziyarid Qabus from Gurgan. The Adudi hospital opened in Baghdad around 982, and the Band-e Amir dam irrigated Fars. The Buyid realm reached its greatest extent and prosperity.",
  "Buyid dynasty|983": "Adud al-Dawla died in March 983, leaving no system for passing his authority to a single heir. The realm was still vast and wealthy, and his brother Fakhr al-Dawla held Ray and the Jibal, but Adud\u0027s sons were already divided between Baghdad and Fars, preparing the ground for civil war.",
  "Buyid dynasty|987": "Sharaf al-Dawla took Baghdad from his brother Samsam al-Dawla in 987, after years of rivalry. The dynasty\u0027s resources were now spent in fighting among its own members, and the Turkish and Daylamite soldiers grew more turbulent. Sharaf al-Dawla died in 989, and the struggle passed to his brother Baha al-Dawla.",
  "Buyid dynasty|1012": "Baha al-Dawla, who had reunited Iraq and Fars, died in 1012 after a long reign in which he had not restored Adud\u0027s authority. Rival branches held Ray, Hamadan and Isfahan, local dynasties gained ground, and the caliph al-Qadir reasserted his religious authority. The Buyids remained powerful but divided and in slow decline.",
  "Buyid dynasty|1029": "Mahmud of the Ghaznavid Empire took Ray in 1029 and deposed Majd al-Dawla, ending the Buyid line in the Jibal. The loss of the northern Iranian lands left the dynasty confined to Iraq, Khuzistan and Fars, while the Kakuyids of Isfahan and other local rulers asserted their independence.",
  "Buyid dynasty|1048": "Abu Kalijar, who had held Fars and, from 1044, Iraq, died in 1048. His sons divided the remaining lands, and al-Malik al-Rahim in Baghdad was too weak to control his soldiers or resist the advance of the Seljuks, who had already taken much of Iran from the east.",
  "Buyid dynasty|1055": "In December 1055 Tughril Beg of the Seljuk Empire entered Baghdad, was recognised as sultan by the caliph al-Qa\u0027im, and imprisoned al-Malik al-Rahim. Buyid rule in Iraq ended after 110 years, leaving only a branch in Fars, which survived under Seljuk pressure and amid the rise of local chiefs.",
  "Buyid dynasty|1062": "The last Buyid prince in Fars, Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun, was overthrown and killed by Fadluya, chief of the Shabankara Kurds, in 1062. The dynasty that had dominated Iraq and western Iran for over a century came to an end, and the Seljuks became the masters of its former lands.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1008": "Bagrat III, already king of Abkhazia and ruler of Kartli, succeeded his father Gurgen and united western and eastern Georgia under one crown, with Kutaisi as the main royal seat. The new kingdom was promising but fragile, since Tbilisi stayed under a Muslim emir and great nobles kept wide powers.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1021": "Basil II marched against George I over the lands of Tao that Byzantium had annexed, and in 1021–1022 defeated the Georgians and forced a peace that left the border districts in Byzantine hands. The kingdom survived intact in its core, but its expansion southward was checked and its standing reduced.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1080": "The Seljuk invasions known in Georgian sources as the Great Turkish Troubles devastated the countryside, as Turkmen bands settled in pastures and drove peasants from their land. George II submitted to Sultan Malik-Shah and paid tribute. The kingdom reached its lowest point before its golden age, weakened, fragmented and dependent.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1104": "David IV the Builder, king since 1089, had stopped paying tribute to the Seljuks and in 1103 reformed the church at the Council of Ruisi-Urbnisi. In 1104 he annexed Kakheti and Hereti, reuniting eastern Georgia and beginning a programme of military reform that would transform the kingdom’s strength.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1121": "With Kipchak warriors settled as a professional army, David IV routed a large Muslim coalition under the Artuqid Ilghazi at the Battle of Didgori on 12 August. The following year he captured Tbilisi and made it his capital, turning Georgia into the leading power of the Caucasus.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1161": "George III captured the Armenian city of Ani and pressed the neighbouring Muslim emirates, keeping the gains of David IV. In 1177 he crushed a revolt of great nobles led by the Orbeli family. The kingdom remained strong and prosperous, with royal authority firmly established over a wide region.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1184": "Tamar succeeded her father as sole ruler. Though she faced noble demands for a share of power early in her reign, her generals, the Zakarid brothers, soon led the army to victory, and Georgia dominated Shirvan and Armenia while a flowering of literature and church building began.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1204": "After victories at Shamkor and Basiani over Muslim coalitions, Georgia was at its height. In 1204 Tamar backed her kinsmen in founding the Empire of Trebizond, the Zakarids governed Ani, and raids reached northern Iran. Rustaveli’s epic belongs to these years, the high point of Georgian power and culture.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1221": "Tamar died in 1213, and under her son George IV Lasha the kingdom still held its wide sphere of influence. In 1221 the Mongol vanguard under Jebe and Subutai defeated the Georgian army at Khunan, an unexpected blow that exposed the kingdom to the storms coming from the east.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1226": "The Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din, fleeing the Mongols, defeated the Georgians at Garni in 1225 and sacked Tbilisi in 1226, with great loss of life. Queen Rusudan ruled a weakened realm whose army had been destroyed in successive defeats and whose tributaries were slipping away.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1243": "Mongol armies under Chormaqan had overrun most of Georgia from 1236, and Rusudan finally submitted to Mongol overlordship. Georgian nobles and troops now served Mongol campaigns, and the land was taxed by Mongol officials. The kingdom survived as a vassal, deprived of independent foreign policy.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1289": "The kingdom had split in 1259–1260 when David VI Narin set up a separate western kingdom at Kutaisi. In eastern Georgia the Ilkhan Arghun executed King Demetrius II in 1289, and Mongol overlords made and unmade kings. Royal authority was at its weakest.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1330": "George V the Brilliant used turmoil in the Ilkhanate from the late 1320s to end Mongol rule, reunited Imereti with the eastern kingdom in 1330 and restored royal control over Samtskhe. He issued laws and revived trade, making Georgia once again a respectable regional power.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1386": "Timur took Tbilisi in 1386 and captured King Bagrat V, beginning a series of invasions that continued until 1403. Towns, churches and villages were destroyed and much of the population killed or enslaved. The kingdom paid tribute, and Imereti again drifted away from royal control.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1412": "Alexander I the Great began a long reign devoted to reconstruction, rebuilding churches, fortresses and the cathedral of Mtskheta and restoring royal authority over the provinces. The recovery was real but partial, as the kingdom faced Turkmen raids from the Kara Koyunlu and a depleted population.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1463": "After the Kara Koyunlu sack of Tbilisi in 1440 and Alexander’s abdication, regional lords pulled the kingdom apart. In 1463 Bagrat of Imereti defeated George VIII at Chikhori, and in 1465 the atabeg of Samtskhe captured the king. Unity had effectively ended, leaving rival royal centres.",
  "Kingdom of Georgia|1490": "A royal council under Constantine II accepted the division of Georgia into the kingdoms of Kartli, Kakheti and Imereti and the Principality of Samtskhe. The united kingdom ceased to exist, its successors exposed to the rising Ottoman and Safavid empires that would contest the Caucasus for three centuries.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1077": "Suleiman ibn Qutalmish, a Seljuk prince acting independently of the Great Seljuk sultans, used Byzantine civil wars after Manzikert to take control of much of Anatolia and settled at Nicaea between about 1075 and 1081. His state was new, loosely organised and dependent on Turkmen bands.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1086": "Suleiman had captured Antioch in 1084, but in 1086 he was killed in battle near Aleppo against the Great Seljuk prince Tutush. His son was taken captive, and the young state lost its leader, leaving local Turkmen commanders to act on their own for several years.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1097": "Kilij Arslan I, released in 1092, had restored his rule at Nicaea, but the First Crusade besieged and took the city in June 1097 and defeated him at Dorylaeum. The sultanate lost western Anatolia to Byzantium and retreated to the central plateau around Konya.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1107": "Kilij Arslan I destroyed the crusading expeditions of 1101, but in 1107 he drowned in the Khabur river after a defeat by rival Seljuk forces near Mosul. Succession disputes followed, and the sultanate remained weaker than the Danishmendid emirate in north-eastern Anatolia.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1116": "Mesud I took the throne and made Konya the settled capital of a reunited state. He cooperated at first with the Danishmendids and slowly rebuilt the sultanate’s strength on the plateau, establishing the dynasty firmly after two decades of instability.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1147": "During the Second Crusade, Seljuk forces routed the German army of Conrad III near Dorylaeum and harassed the French army of Louis VII. The victory confirmed that the sultanate could defend central Anatolia against Western armies and raised its prestige in the Muslim world.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1176": "Kilij Arslan II defeated the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos at Myriokephalon, ending Byzantine attempts to reconquer the plateau. In 1178 he absorbed the Danishmendid lands, making the sultanate the dominant power in Anatolia, though its coasts remained in Byzantine hands.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1190": "Kilij Arslan II had divided his realm among his sons, who quarrelled while the army of Frederick Barbarossa stormed Konya during the Third Crusade. The sultanate survived, but civil war among the princes weakened it until Kaykhusraw I restored unity.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1207": "Kaykhusraw I, back on the throne since 1205, captured the Mediterranean port of Antalya, giving the sultanate its first outlet to the sea. Control of a major harbour opened Anatolian trade to Italian and Levantine merchants and began the sultanate’s commercial expansion.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1214": "Kaykaus I, who succeeded after Kaykhusraw I fell fighting the Empire of Nicaea in 1211, took the Black Sea port of Sinop and captured the emperor of Trebizond, who became his vassal. The sultanate now controlled routes between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1221": "Kayqubad I took Alanya on the Mediterranean coast, fortifying it with the Red Tower and a shipyard. Caravanserais multiplied along the trade roads, and a Seljuk expedition crossed the Black Sea to Sudak in the Crimea. The sultanate approached the height of its wealth and power.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1237": "Kayqubad I, victor over the Khwarazmian Jalal al-Din at Yassıçemen in 1230, died in 1237 at the height of Seljuk power, ruling from the Mediterranean to the Black Sea and east beyond the Euphrates. Konya was a centre of Persian culture and the capital of the strongest Muslim state in Anatolia.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1243": "The Mongols under Baiju destroyed the army of Kaykhusraw II at Köse Dağ. The sultan fled, and the sultanate became a Mongol vassal paying heavy tribute. Its administration and economy survived largely intact, but it lost its independence and military power in a single battle.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1277": "The Mamluk sultan Baybars invaded and defeated a Mongol and Seljuk army at Elbistan. The Ilkhan Abaqa took revenge, executed the minister known as the Pervane and imposed direct Mongol control, while the Karamanids briefly seized Konya. The sultans were now little more than figureheads.",
  "Sultanate of Rum|1308": "Mesud II, the last Seljuk sultan of Rum, died around 1308 after a reign spent under Mongol supervision. The title lapsed without formal abolition, and Anatolia was divided among Turkmen principalities, among them the Karamanids, the Germiyanids and the early Ottomans.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1099": "The First Crusade took Jerusalem in July and defeated a Fatimid army at Ascalon in August. Godfrey of Bouillon governed a handful of inland towns with only a few hundred knights after most crusaders went home. The new state was precarious, without ports and surrounded by hostile powers.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1104": "Baldwin I, crowned king in 1100, had defeated Fatimid armies at Ramla and, with Genoese ships, captured Arsuf, Caesarea and in 1104 Acre, which became the kingdom’s main port. Access to the sea secured supplies, pilgrims and reinforcements from Europe.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1110": "With Scandinavian, Venetian and other naval help, Baldwin I took Beirut and Sidon, so that almost the whole coast from Beirut to Jaffa was in Frankish hands. The kingdom was establishing lordships, a church hierarchy and a settled Frankish population.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1124": "Tyre, one of the strongest coastal cities, fell to a Frankish army and a Venetian fleet while Baldwin II was a captive. Venice received a third of the city. Only Ascalon remained of the Fatimid coast, and the kingdom stood as the leading crusader state.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1148": "Zengi’s capture of Edessa in 1144 provoked the Second Crusade, but its siege of Damascus in 1148 failed after only a few days, damaging relations with Damascus and Western enthusiasm. The kingdom itself remained intact and prosperous under Baldwin III.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1153": "Baldwin III captured Ascalon, the last Fatimid fortress on the coast, after a long siege. The kingdom now controlled the whole coastline south of Beirut and threatened Egypt. Its confidence and resources were near their peak, though Nur al-Din took Damascus the following year.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1167": "Amalric I invaded Egypt to prevent Nur al-Din from taking it. In 1167 a Frankish army besieged Alexandria and Egypt agreed to pay tribute, with a Frankish garrison in Cairo. The kingdom was briefly the arbiter of Egyptian politics, the high point of its power.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1177": "The Egyptian venture had failed when Saladin took power there in 1169. Yet the young Baldwin IV, though suffering from leprosy, defeated Saladin at Montgisard in 1177. The kingdom still fielded a strong army, but faction and the king’s illness were weakening it.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1187": "Saladin destroyed the Frankish army at the Battle of Hattin on 4 July, capturing King Guy. Almost every town and castle surrendered, and Jerusalem fell in October. Only Tyre and a few fortresses held out, and the kingdom was close to extinction.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1192": "The Third Crusade recaptured Acre in 1191 and won at Arsuf, and the Treaty of Jaffa left the Franks a coastal strip from Tyre to Jaffa, with pilgrim access to Jerusalem. The restored kingdom, ruled from Acre, was smaller but commercially rich.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1229": "Frederick II, king through his marriage to Isabella II, obtained Jerusalem, Bethlehem and Nazareth from al-Kamil by treaty without fighting. The recovery was a diplomatic success, but Jerusalem was unfortified and the kingdom split between imperial officials and the barons.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1244": "Khwarazmian troops sacked Jerusalem, and in October the combined army of the Franks and their Syrian allies was destroyed at La Forbie by the Egyptian Ayyubids and Khwarazmians. The kingdom lost its field army and its last chance of holding the Holy City; Jerusalem never returned to Frankish rule.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1268": "The Mamluk sultan Baybars had taken Caesarea, Arsuf and Safed and in 1268 captured Jaffa and Beaufort. The kingdom was reduced to Acre, Tyre, Sidon, Beirut and a few castles, defended mainly by the military orders, Italian communes and subsidies sent from the West.",
  "Kingdom of Jerusalem|1291": "Al-Ashraf Khalil besieged Acre with a large army and siege engines and stormed it on 18 May. The remaining coastal towns were evacuated within months. The kingdom ended on the mainland after 192 years, though the kings of Cyprus kept the royal title.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1204": "Alexios and David Komnenos seized Trebizond in April with Georgian troops, just before the Fourth Crusade took Constantinople. Alexios proclaimed himself emperor. The new state held a strong coastal city with Georgian backing but had no settled frontiers and faced rival Byzantine claimants.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1208": "David Komnenos had conquered the coast westward through Paphlagonia to Heraclea Pontica, giving the brothers control of most of the southern Black Sea shore. This was the greatest territorial extent the empire ever reached, though it was contested by Theodore Laskaris of Nicaea.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1214": "Theodore Laskaris had recovered Heraclea and Paphlagonia, and in 1214 the Seljuk sultan Kaykaus I took Sinop, capturing Emperor Alexios I and making him a tributary vassal. The empire was cut back to the coastal strip east of Sinop that it would hold for the rest of its history.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1243": "After the Mongols defeated the Seljuks at Köse Dağ, Emperor Manuel I transferred his allegiance to the Mongols. Vassalage brought security, and Manuel’s reign saw the building of Hagia Sophia and wide circulation of his silver coins. The empire was small but stable.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1282": "John II married Eudokia Palaiologina and abandoned the claim to be Emperor of the Romans, settling relations with Constantinople. The empire had become a regional trading power, profiting from the route to Tabriz, the Ilkhanid capital, though Turkmen and Georgian neighbours remained a threat.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1300": "Under Alexios II, Trebizond was a major terminus of the trade between Persia and the Black Sea, with Genoese and Venetian merchants active in the city. The empire was at the height of its prosperity and cultural contacts, despite clashes with the Genoese over trading privileges.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1341": "After the death of Alexios II in 1330 and the collapse of the Ilkhanate, Trebizond fell into civil war between aristocratic factions, with emperors and empresses deposed in quick succession. Turkmen raiders attacked the capital. Trade declined and the state reached a low point.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1349": "Alexios III took the throne with support from Constantinople and, after some years of conflict, suppressed the rebellious aristocratic factions. Order was gradually restored, though the empire had lost wealth and population, and Turkmen chiefdoms now pressed on its frontiers.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1374": "Alexios III had stabilised the empire through marriage alliances with neighbouring Turkmen rulers, and in 1374 he endowed the new Dionysiou monastery on Mount Athos. Trade revived, and the empire enjoyed a modest second prosperity under the longest reign of the dynasty.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1404": "Manuel III had accepted Timur’s overlordship, and after Timur’s victory over the Ottomans in 1402 Trebizond enjoyed a respite from Ottoman pressure. The Castilian envoy Clavijo passed through on his embassy to Samarkand. The empire remained a modest, tribute-paying state, prosperous enough but dependent on the goodwill of stronger neighbours.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1456": "With Constantinople fallen in 1453, Trebizond was the last Byzantine state in Anatolia. Under Ottoman pressure it agreed to pay tribute, while John IV looked to Uzun Hasan of the Aq Qoyunlu and to the West for help. Its independence was clearly precarious.",
  "Empire of Trebizond|1461": "Mehmed II besieged Trebizond by land and sea after securing Sinop and the withdrawal of Uzun Hasan. Emperor David surrendered in August, and the city became Ottoman. David and most of his male relatives were executed in 1463, ending the dynasty.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1260": "David VI Narin, co-king of Georgia, rebelled against the Mongols and withdrew to Kutaisi, founding a separate western kingdom beyond the Likhi Range. Protected by mountains and distance from Mongol power, the new state held all of western Georgia under the authority of a crowned Bagratid king.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1293": "David Narin died after more than three decades of rule in the west and was succeeded by his son Constantine I. The kingdom kept its independence from the Mongols, but disputes within the royal family and the ambitions of regional lords began to weaken royal control.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1330": "George V the Brilliant, who had ended Mongol rule in eastern Georgia, took Kutaisi and reunited the western kingdom with the rest of Georgia. The western Bagratid line was compensated with a regional fief. Imereti ceased to exist as a separate kingdom for more than half a century.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1387": "As Timur’s invasions devastated eastern Georgia, descendants of David Narin revived a separate kingdom in the west. Its rulers were weak and their independence short-lived, and the kings of Georgia later reasserted control, but the episode showed the strength of western separatism.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1463": "Bagrat, a Bagratid prince who governed in the west, had made himself king of Imereti. In 1463 he defeated King George VIII of Georgia at Chikhori, confirming his kingdom’s independence and his position as the strongest ruler in a Georgia that was rapidly falling apart.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1465": "After George VIII was captured by the atabeg of Samtskhe, Bagrat took Kartli as well and ruled from Tbilisi as Bagrat VI. For a few years a king from Imereti held most of Georgia, the high point of the western kingdom’s power before his death in 1478.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1490": "A royal council accepted the division of Georgia into Kartli, Kakheti and Imereti. Under Bagrat’s son Alexander II, Imereti was an established kingdom with the princes of Mingrelia, Guria and Abkhazia as nominal vassals, and the Ottoman threat still remote.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1510": "According to Georgian sources, Ottoman troops raided Imereti, sacking Kutaisi and burning the Gelati monastery. The new king, Bagrat III, rebuilt and fought energetically against the princes of Samtskhe and his own vassals, but the kingdom now faced the full power of the Ottoman Empire on its borders.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1555": "After the Ottoman victory over a Georgian coalition at Sokhoista in 1545, the Treaty of Amasya placed western Georgia in the Ottoman sphere. Imereti became a tributary of the sultans, and its vassal principalities moved further out of royal control.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1660": "Alexander III, who had fought Levan II Dadiani of Mingrelia for years, briefly extended his authority over Mingrelia after Levan’s death in 1657, but he died in 1660. His death opened decades of disputed successions, noble rebellion and foreign intervention.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1700": "Imereti was in chaos. Kings were enthroned and deposed in rapid succession by noble factions, the pasha of Akhaltsikhe and eastern Georgian rulers, Ottoman garrisons held fortresses including Kutaisi, and the slave trade depopulated the countryside. Royal authority had almost collapsed.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1757": "Solomon I, king since 1752, had curbed the nobility and banned the slave trade. In 1757 he defeated an Ottoman army at the Battle of Chikhori, restoring a measure of royal authority and making Imereti a credible partner for Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti and for Russia.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1774": "With Russian help during the Russo-Turkish war, Solomon I had driven Ottoman garrisons from his fortresses, and the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca ended the tribute in captives. Imereti was more secure than for two centuries, though still poor and dependent on outside powers.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1789": "After Solomon I’s death in 1784 a period of disputed succession followed. In 1789 Solomon II took the throne with the support of his grandfather Erekle II, but rival claimants and rebellious vassals in Mingrelia and Guria kept the kingdom weak.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1804": "Russia had annexed Kartli-Kakheti in 1801. Under pressure, Solomon II signed the Treaty of Elaznauri, accepting Russian protection while retaining his throne. The kingdom’s independence was now largely formal, its foreign relations were subject to St Petersburg, and Russian demands for control over its policy and fortresses steadily increased.",
  "Kingdom of Imereti|1810": "Solomon II resisted Russian control and sought Ottoman support. Russian troops occupied Imereti and deposed him, and the kingdom was annexed to the Russian Empire. Solomon escaped to Ottoman territory and died in exile at Trebizond in 1815, the last king of Imereti, ending more than five centuries of western Georgian kingship.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1378": "The Bayandur-led Turkmen tribes around Diyarbakır emerged as a recognisable confederation under Qara Yuluk Osman. They were one of several Turkmen groups competing in eastern Anatolia after the collapse of Mongol authority, with marriage ties to Trebizond but no fixed territory, cities or administration of their own, so their power was modest.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1398": "Qara Yuluk Osman defeated and killed Kadi Burhan al-Din, ruler of a state in central Anatolia, raising his reputation among the Turkmen tribes. The confederation was still a mobile tribal force without a secure urban base, constantly contesting pastures and towns with the Qara Qoyunlu and local Kurdish and Turkmen emirs.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1402": "Osman sided with Timur during his campaigns in Anatolia and was rewarded with Diyarbakır, which became the confederation’s first true capital. Timurid backing gave the Aq Qoyunlu legitimacy against the Qara Qoyunlu, but they remained a regional power dependent on the goodwill of Timur’s successors.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1435": "Qara Yuluk Osman was killed fighting the Qara Qoyunlu near Erzurum. His death opened nearly two decades of rivalry among his sons and grandsons for leadership of the tribes, while the Qara Qoyunlu under Jahan Shah grew into the leading power of Azerbaijan and Iraq, leaving the Aq Qoyunlu on the defensive.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1453": "Uzun Hasan, a grandson of Osman, took Diyarbakır and established himself as head of the confederation. He gradually brought his relatives and the tribal emirs under control and restored unity, although the Aq Qoyunlu still held only a modest territory in south-eastern Anatolia and upper Mesopotamia.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1462": "Uzun Hasan extended his rule by taking Hisn Kayfa from its Ayyubid princes and strengthened his diplomacy through his marriage to the Trebizond princess Theodora. Although he could not prevent Mehmed II from annexing Trebizond in 1461, he now ruled a compact, growing state in eastern Anatolia and upper Mesopotamia.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1467": "In November Uzun Hasan surprised and killed the Qara Qoyunlu ruler Jahan Shah, whose army had marched against him. The Black Sheep realm collapsed rapidly, and Azerbaijan, Iraq and much of western Iran fell to the Aq Qoyunlu, transforming a regional confederation into a major power of western Asia.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1469": "Uzun Hasan defeated and executed the Timurid sultan Abu Sa’id, who had invaded Azerbaijan to claim the Qara Qoyunlu inheritance. Ruling from Tabriz over eastern Anatolia, Iraq and western and central Iran, and courted by Venice, the Aq Qoyunlu stood at the height of their power and prestige.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1473": "Mehmed II defeated Uzun Hasan at the Battle of Otlukbeli, where Ottoman artillery and janissaries overcame the Turkmen cavalry. The Aq Qoyunlu lost no core provinces, but their ambitions in Anatolia ended, the Venetian alliance proved hollow, and the confederation lost the aura of invincibility gained in 1467 and 1469.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1478": "Uzun Hasan died, and a brief succession struggle followed in which his son Khalil was killed and Ya’qub took the throne. The state remained large and wealthy, but the contest showed how every succession depended on the shifting loyalties of the Turkmen emirs and their followers.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1480": "Ya’qub defeated a Mamluk army that had invaded upper Mesopotamia near Urfa, securing his western frontier. His reign brought a period of relative stability and court splendour in Tabriz, with patronage of poets, historians and painters, keeping Aq Qoyunlu power close to its earlier height.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1488": "Aq Qoyunlu forces, allied with the Shirvanshah, defeated and killed the Safavid leader Shaykh Haydar in the Caucasus. The state was still strong, and Qadi Isa Savaji’s attempted fiscal reforms aimed to strengthen central finances, but they also deepened tensions with the emirs and religious notables.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1490": "Ya’qub died suddenly, and his death unleashed a series of succession wars. His young son Baysunghur could not control the emirs, the reforms were abandoned, and rival tribal factions began to back competing princes, sharply reducing the authority of the throne.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1497": "After the brief reign of Rustam, Ahmad, a grandson of Uzun Hasan raised at the Ottoman court, seized power and was killed within months. Civil war, plague and the partition of the realm among rival princes left the confederation divided and exhausted.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1501": "Ismail, leader of the Safavid order, defeated the Aq Qoyunlu prince Alvand at the Battle of Sharur, took Tabriz and was proclaimed shah. The Aq Qoyunlu lost their capital and Azerbaijan, leaving only rival princes in central Iran, Iraq and Diyarbakır.",
  "Aq Qoyunlu|1503": "Ismail defeated Murad, the last significant Aq Qoyunlu ruler, near Hamadan, taking central and southern Iran. Murad fled to Baghdad, and the confederation ceased to function as a state, its tribes and lands absorbed by the Safavids and, in eastern Anatolia, soon by the Ottomans.",
  "Circassia|1400": "After the Mongol conquests and the campaigns of Timur devastated the north-western Caucasus, the Adyghe peoples re-emerged as a recognisable country of principalities and tribal communities. Without a single ruler or capital, Circassia’s strength lay in numerous warrior nobilities and a shared culture, but it was fragmented and exposed to stronger neighbours.",
  "Circassia|1475": "The Ottoman conquest of Caffa and the Genoese colonies ended Italian trade on the Circassian coast and drew Circassia into the Ottoman and Crimean sphere. Circassian Mamluks ruled Egypt, and the princes of Kabarda were consolidating a strong principality, but the country remained divided and its western coast was increasingly subject to Crimean pressure.",
  "Circassia|1557": "Kabardian princes sent envoys to Moscow and allied with Ivan IV against the Crimean Khanate. The alliance strengthened Kabarda against Crimean and Nogai raiders and gave Circassian princes a powerful partner, while the western societies continued to trade through Ottoman and Crimean ports.",
  "Circassia|1561": "Ivan the Terrible married Maria Temryukovna, daughter of the Kabardian prince Temryuk, sealing the Muscovite alliance. Kabardian princes gained prestige and influence in Moscow, and Kabarda stood among the leading powers of the North Caucasus, although Circassia as a whole still lacked common government.",
  "Circassia|1708": "Kabardian forces defeated a large Crimean army at the Battle of Kanzhal, ending Crimean demands for tribute from Kabarda. Circassia was populous and its warrior nobility respected by all its neighbours, and with Russia not yet advancing into the region this was the peak of Circassian independence and military reputation.",
  "Circassia|1739": "The Treaty of Belgrade between Russia and the Ottoman Empire recognised Kabarda as a neutral barrier between the two empires. Circassian societies remained independent and strong, though their status was now being defined by outside powers rather than by Circassians themselves.",
  "Circassia|1763": "Russia founded the fortress of Mozdok on lands claimed by Kabarda, the first step in a chain of fortified lines along the Terek and Kuban. Kabardian protests and wars followed. Circassia remained independent and well armed, but its eastern part now faced a steadily advancing empire.",
  "Circassia|1774": "By the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca the Ottoman Empire recognised Kabarda as belonging to Russia. Over the following decades Russian forts and Cossack settlements multiplied, and Kabardian resistance grew more desperate, while the western Circassians, still independent, drew closer to the Ottomans at Anapa.",
  "Circassia|1796": "Among the western Circassians free communities defeated their princes and nobles at the Battle of Bziyuk, and power passed to assemblies. The struggle reshaped society but deepened internal divisions just as Russian pressure in the east intensified, keeping Circassian power below its earlier level.",
  "Circassia|1822": "Under General Aleksey Yermolov Russia completed the subjugation of Kabarda with a new line of forts and harsh reprisals, while plague and famine cut its population. The strongest eastern Circassian principality was lost, leaving resistance to the western societies beyond the Kuban.",
  "Circassia|1829": "In the Treaty of Adrianople the Ottoman Empire ceded the Circassian coast to Russia. Circassians rejected the transfer, but Russia now claimed their entire country, began building the Black Sea Coastal Line of forts, and set out to cut the coast off from Ottoman trade and supplies.",
  "Circassia|1840": "Western Circassians stormed several Russian forts on the Black Sea coast, including Lazarevsky and Mikhailovsky. The successes showed that resistance was still formidable and attracted British sympathy after the Vixen affair, briefly lifting Circassian fortunes, although Russia soon rebuilt and reinforced its positions.",
  "Circassia|1856": "The Crimean War ended without the Western intervention in Circassia that many had hoped for. Russia had evacuated its coastal forts during the war, but the Treaty of Paris left its claims untouched, and the empire was free to turn its full strength against the Circassians.",
  "Circassia|1859": "Imam Shamil surrendered in Dagestan, and Muhammad Amin, his representative among the western Circassians, submitted soon after. Russia shifted large armies to the north-western Caucasus, and under Count Nikolai Yevdokimov began systematic campaigns to clear the highlands of their inhabitants.",
  "Circassia|1861": "Delegates of the Circassian societies met at Sochi in a united assembly that sought foreign recognition and help from Britain and the Ottoman Empire. The attempt at common government came too late, as Russian columns were already destroying villages and pushing the population towards the coast.",
  "Circassia|1864": "Russian forces crushed the last resistance and held a victory parade at Kbaada in May. Hundreds of thousands of Circassians were deported or fled to the Ottoman Empire, many dying on the way. Circassia ceased to exist as an independent country, its lands absorbed into the Russian Empire.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1727": "The Qawasim are traditionally said to have established their rule at Sharjah in this year. The town was a small fishing and pearling settlement subordinate to the family’s main centre at Ras al-Khaimah, with modest resources and no independent standing among the powers of the Gulf.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1803": "Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi became head of the Qawasim, whose fleets were then the strongest naval force of the lower Gulf. Sharjah shared in this maritime power and the family’s influence on both shores of the Gulf, though Ras al-Khaimah remained the leading Qasimi town.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1809": "A British expedition from Bombay attacked Ras al-Khaimah and other Qasimi ports, and Lingeh on the Persian coast. The Qawasim survived and rebuilt their fleets, and Sharjah’s importance grew as Sultan bin Saqr manoeuvred between the British, Oman and the Wahhabi state in central Arabia.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1819": "A second British expedition captured Ras al-Khaimah and destroyed Qasimi ships and forts along the coast. The disaster ended Qasimi naval power, but it also made Sharjah, under Sultan bin Saqr, the principal seat of the family and the centre of its remaining authority.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1820": "Sultan bin Saqr signed the General Maritime Treaty with Britain. Sharjah lost the option of maritime warfare but gained a recognised status, and its ruler now governed Ras al-Khaimah and dependencies along the coast, making Sharjah the leading Qasimi sheikhdom on the Trucial Coast.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1853": "The Perpetual Maritime Truce ended warfare at sea and allowed pearling to flourish. Sultan bin Saqr, now elderly, ruled Sharjah, Ras al-Khaimah and their dependencies, and the sheikhdom stood at its greatest extent and influence among the Trucial States of the coast.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1866": "Sultan bin Saqr died after a reign of over six decades, and his sons and relatives contested the succession. The sheikhdom lost the authority of its founder figure, and the unity of the Qasimi dominions he had held together began to break down.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1869": "Ras al-Khaimah became separate under its own Qasimi ruler, Humaid bin Abdullah, and was treated as a distinct sheikhdom. Sharjah lost its largest dependency and much of its weight, though it still controlled Khor Fakkan, Kalba and several interior districts.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1892": "Sharjah’s ruler signed the Exclusive Agreement with Britain, surrendering all control of foreign relations in return for British protection. The sheikhdom remained a modest pearling state whose external position was now fixed firmly within the British-protected system of the Trucial States.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1900": "After the death of Humaid bin Abdullah, Ras al-Khaimah returned to the control of Sharjah’s ruler. The reunion briefly restored the extent of Sharjah’s dominions on the coast, although local branches of the family retained considerable autonomy in the dependencies.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1902": "Fujairah, on the Gulf of Oman coast, rebelled and broke away from Sharjah’s authority, although Britain did not recognise it until 1952. Meanwhile Persian merchants leaving Lingeh moved to Dubai rather than Sharjah, helping Dubai overtake its older neighbour commercially.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1921": "Ras al-Khaimah became permanently independent under its own ruler, recognised by Britain. Sharjah’s territory shrank further, and with its creek silting and pearling facing growing problems, it was now one of several comparable sheikhdoms rather than the leading power of the coast.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1932": "Imperial Airways opened a rest house and airfield at Sharjah on the route to India. The airfield brought rents and jobs just as the pearl industry collapsed, partly cushioning the economic crisis, but Sharjah’s overall wealth and standing remained limited.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1936": "Britain recognised Kalba as a separate Trucial sheikhdom, partly to secure an emergency landing ground for the air route. Sharjah lost yet another of its dependencies, and the collapse of pearling during the Depression left its economy at a very low point.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1951": "Kalba was reincorporated into Sharjah after the death of its ruler, and the Trucial Oman Levies were established with their headquarters at Sharjah. Saqr bin Sultan became ruler. Sharjah gained some importance as a British military centre, though it still lacked oil revenue.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1965": "Britain deposed Saqr bin Sultan for his Arab nationalist sympathies and installed Khalid bin Muhammad. The episode showed the limits of the ruler’s independence, while Abu Dhabi and Dubai pulled ahead with oil wealth, leaving Sharjah one of the weaker sheikhdoms.",
  "Sheikhdom of Sharjah|1971": "Sharjah accepted an arrangement with Iran over Abu Musa and on 2 December joined the United Arab Emirates as a founding member. Its existence as a separate British-protected sheikhdom ended, although it continued as a self-governing emirate within the new federation.",
  "Qajar Iran|1789": "Agha Mohammad Khan, ruling from Tehran, had become master of northern Iran, while the Zand dynasty in Shiraz was weakened by internal strife. The Qajar state was still a tribal military power at war for control of the country, without the south, Khorasan or the Caucasus.",
  "Qajar Iran|1794": "Agha Mohammad Khan captured Kerman and the last Zand ruler, Lotf Ali Khan, ending Zand rule. Southern and central Iran now acknowledged the Qajars, who controlled most of the Iranian plateau, although the Caucasus and Khorasan still lay outside their effective authority.",
  "Qajar Iran|1796": "After sacking Tiflis in 1795 and reasserting authority over the Caucasian khanates, Agha Mohammad Khan was crowned shah and took Mashhad from the Afsharid Shahrokh. Iran was reunited under one ruler for the first time since the death of Nader Shah, and Qajar power was near its height.",
  "Qajar Iran|1797": "Agha Mohammad Khan was assassinated at Shusha during a campaign in the Caucasus. His nephew Fath Ali Shah succeeded after defeating rival claimants. The transition was relatively smooth, but the new shah inherited a state still dependent on tribal forces and personal loyalty.",
  "Qajar Iran|1804": "Fath Ali Shah ruled a reunited Iran and claimed the khanates of the Caucasus, while Russia, having annexed eastern Georgia, began the first Russo-Persian War. With its territory at its largest and its court prosperous, the Qajar state stood at its peak before decades of defeat.",
  "Qajar Iran|1813": "The Treaty of Gulistan ended the first war with Russia, ceding Georgia, Dagestan and most of the Caucasian khanates. Iran kept Erivan and Nakhichevan and its core territory, but the defeat exposed the gap between Qajar armies and modern European forces despite Abbas Mirza’s reforms.",
  "Qajar Iran|1828": "The Treaty of Turkmenchay ended the second Russo-Persian War with the loss of Erivan and Nakhichevan, a heavy indemnity and extraterritorial rights for Russian subjects. Iran was confirmed as a second-rank power exposed to Russian pressure, though its core provinces remained intact.",
  "Qajar Iran|1838": "Mohammad Shah’s siege of Herat failed after months under British pressure, including the occupation of Kharg Island. The episode showed that Iran could not recover its eastern lands against British opposition, and its position stabilised as a buffer between the British and Russian empires.",
  "Qajar Iran|1848": "Naser al-Din Shah came to the throne at seventeen amid Babi uprisings and provincial unrest. The state was weak in finances and administration, but the new reign and the appointment of Amir Kabir as chief minister opened a period of reform and consolidation.",
  "Qajar Iran|1851": "Amir Kabir reorganised finances, suppressed revolts and founded the Dar ol-Fonun in Tehran. Central authority and revenue improved and the Qajar state was more effective than at any time since the 1820s, though his dismissal and execution soon cut short the reforms.",
  "Qajar Iran|1857": "Britain went to war after Iran took Herat, and the Treaty of Paris forced Iran to renounce its claims to Herat and Afghanistan. The shah remained secure at home, but Iran’s eastern frontier was now fixed by British power, further limiting its regional influence.",
  "Qajar Iran|1872": "The great famine of 1870–1872 killed a large part of the population, and the shah granted Baron Julius de Reuter a sweeping concession that was soon cancelled after protests. The episode revealed fiscal weakness and growing dependence on foreign interests, lowering the state’s standing.",
  "Qajar Iran|1892": "The Tobacco Protest forced Naser al-Din Shah to cancel a monopoly concession granted to a British company, after a ruling attributed to the leading cleric Mirza Hasan Shirazi. The crown lost prestige and had to pay compensation, financed by Iran’s first foreign loan.",
  "Qajar Iran|1896": "Naser al-Din Shah was assassinated by Mirza Reza Kermani. His son Mozaffar ad-Din Shah inherited empty coffers and turned to Russian loans, granting more concessions in return. Royal authority and state finances declined, while opposition among merchants, clergy and intellectuals grew.",
  "Qajar Iran|1906": "Mass protests and a sit-in at the British legation forced Mozaffar ad-Din Shah to grant a constitution and an elected Majlis. The revolution created new institutions and briefly revived hopes of a stronger, reformed state, giving it renewed vitality despite its weak finances.",
  "Qajar Iran|1908": "Mohammad Ali Shah used the Persian Cossack Brigade to bombard the Majlis and suspend the constitution, plunging Iran into civil war. Combined with the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907 dividing the country into spheres of influence, central authority fell to a new low.",
  "Qajar Iran|1911": "A Russian ultimatum forced the dismissal of the American adviser Morgan Shuster, and the Majlis was closed. Russian troops occupied parts of the north, and the central government had little control over the provinces, the tribes or its own finances.",
  "Qajar Iran|1915": "Although officially neutral in the First World War, Iran became a battlefield for Ottoman, Russian and British forces and German agents. The government in Tehran lost control of large parts of the country, and famine and epidemics soon devastated the population.",
  "Qajar Iran|1921": "Reza Khan and Sayyed Zia al-Din Tabataba’i seized Tehran in a coup. Ahmad Shah remained on the throne, but real power passed to Reza Khan, who began building a national army and restoring central control; the Qajar monarchy survived largely in name.",
  "Qajar Iran|1925": "The Majlis deposed Ahmad Shah, who had been living in Europe since 1923, ending the Qajar dynasty. In December a constituent assembly proclaimed Reza Khan shah, founding the Pahlavi dynasty, and the Qajar state was replaced by a new, strongly centralising monarchy.",
  "South Yemen|1967": "On 30 November the National Liberation Front proclaimed the People’s Republic of South Yemen as British forces left Aden. The new state controlled the whole of the former protectorate, but it was poor, its port economy was collapsing after the closure of the Suez Canal, and its administration was largely improvised.",
  "South Yemen|1969": "The NLF’s left wing removed Qahtan al-Shaabi in the Corrective Move and installed a collective leadership around Salim Rubaya Ali and Abdul Fattah Ismail. The regime became more radical and consolidated its control over the army and the provinces, though the economy remained very weak.",
  "South Yemen|1970": "A new constitution renamed the country the People’s Democratic Republic of Yemen, and the Agrarian Reform Law broke up the large estates. The state was taking firmer shape, with Soviet support increasing, and the regime extended its authority over land, trade and society across the country.",
  "South Yemen|1972": "Border fighting with North Yemen ended in agreements in Cairo and Tripoli that promised eventual unification. South Yemen held its own militarily and gained in international standing, but relations with Saudi Arabia and Oman remained hostile and its economy was still weak.",
  "South Yemen|1975": "The reopening of the Suez Canal helped Aden’s port, while the Dhofar rebellion in Oman, which South Yemen had backed, was defeated. Party consolidation advanced, Soviet aid and advisers increased, and the state was more stable and better organised than at any time since independence.",
  "South Yemen|1978": "Salim Rubaya Ali was overthrown and executed, and the Yemeni Socialist Party was founded on the Soviet model under Abdul Fattah Ismail. The regime emerged more unified ideologically and more closely tied to Moscow, despite the violence of the leadership struggle.",
  "South Yemen|1979": "A short border war with North Yemen ended with Arab mediation and a new unity agreement, and South Yemen signed a treaty of friendship with the Soviet Union. Militarily well equipped and backed by a superpower, it reached a strong regional position.",
  "South Yemen|1980": "Ali Nasir Muhammad replaced Abdul Fattah Ismail as party leader and head of state. His more pragmatic policies improved relations with Gulf neighbours and brought new aid and remittances, while the state apparatus, army and services continued to grow in a period of relative stability.",
  "South Yemen|1984": "Under Ali Nasir Muhammad South Yemen enjoyed its most settled years. Relations with Oman had been normalised in 1982, oil exploration was under way, and social programmes had expanded, giving the state its greatest internal stability and external acceptance, although party rivalries were sharpening.",
  "South Yemen|1986": "The party civil war in Aden in January killed thousands, including Abdul Fattah Ismail, and drove Ali Nasir Muhammad and tens of thousands of supporters into exile in the north. The leadership, army and party were shattered, and the state never recovered its earlier cohesion.",
  "South Yemen|1989": "Soviet aid dwindled under Gorbachev and communist regimes in Eastern Europe collapsed, removing South Yemen’s main supporters. Weakened at home and isolated abroad, the leadership agreed in November to a unity constitution with North Yemen, seeing unification as the best way out of its crisis.",
  "South Yemen|1990": "On 22 May South Yemen united with the Yemen Arab Republic to form the Republic of Yemen. Ali Salim al-Beidh became vice-president under Ali Abdullah Saleh, and the People’s Democratic Republic ceased to exist as a separate state, its institutions merged into the new republic.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1971": "Six sheikhdoms proclaimed the federation on 2 December 1971 as British protection ended. The new state was small, short of institutions and people, and had just seen Iran occupy the Tunbs and part of Abu Musa. Its strength lay in Abu Dhabi\u0027s growing oil revenues and the partnership of Zayed and Rashid.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1972": "Ras al-Khaimah joined in February 1972, completing the seven-member federation. The UAE had already been admitted to the Arab League and the United Nations in December 1971, and federal ministries and the Union Defence Force began to give the young state an institutional form beyond the personal authority of its rulers.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1974": "The oil price increases of 1973–1974 multiplied Abu Dhabi\u0027s revenues and with them the federal budget. The UAE dirham, introduced in 1973, replaced the Qatar-Dubai riyal and the Bahraini dinar, and money flowed into roads, housing, schools and hospitals across all seven emirates, transforming living standards within a few years.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1979": "The deep-water port of Jebel Ali opened, but the federation faced its most serious internal crisis as Abu Dhabi and Dubai disagreed over unifying the armed forces and the extent of federal powers. The dispute ended when Rashid of Dubai became prime minister, preserving the union while leaving the emirates considerable autonomy.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1981": "The Gulf Cooperation Council was founded in Abu Dhabi in May 1981, with the UAE among its six members, as the Iranian Revolution and the Iran–Iraq War threatened the Gulf. Membership gave the federation a collective security framework while high oil income continued to fund rapid development at home.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1986": "The collapse of oil prices in 1986 sharply reduced government revenues and slowed construction, exposing the federation\u0027s dependence on crude exports. Dubai\u0027s new free zone at Jebel Ali and the airline Emirates, both launched in 1985, pointed towards the diversification that would bear fruit in the following decades.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1991": "Iraq\u0027s invasion of Kuwait in August 1990 showed how vulnerable the small Gulf states were. The UAE joined the United States-led coalition that liberated Kuwait in 1991 and afterwards deepened its defence ties with Washington, building the security partnership that has underpinned its stability ever since.",
  "United Arab Emirates|1996": "The Federal Supreme Council made the provisional constitution permanent, settling the federation\u0027s legal form after twenty-five years. Dubai was emerging as a regional centre of trade and tourism, and the federal state was stronger and more cohesive than at independence, though still overshadowed by larger neighbours in regional affairs.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2004": "Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan, president since 1971, died in November and was succeeded by his son Khalifa bin Zayed without upheaval, a sign of the federation\u0027s stability. The founding of the Dubai International Financial Centre the same year marked Dubai\u0027s ambition to become a global financial centre as oil prices rose.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2008": "High oil prices and a property boom brought unprecedented wealth. Abu Dhabi\u0027s sovereign funds invested worldwide, Dubai\u0027s skyline filled with towers and artificial islands, and the population grew rapidly with migrant labour. The UAE had become the Arab world\u0027s leading commercial hub, though its growth rested heavily on debt-financed real estate.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2009": "Dubai World\u0027s request to delay debt repayments in November 2009 shook global markets and revealed the fragility of Dubai\u0027s model. Abu Dhabi\u0027s rescue, including 10 billion dollars in December, saved the emirate but shifted the internal balance decisively towards the capital, whose wealth now underwrote the whole federation.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2011": "While uprisings shook much of the Arab world, the UAE remained stable, helped by high oil prices and generous spending on its citizens. It flew combat missions over Libya and sent police to Bahrain, beginning a decade of more assertive foreign policy directed largely by the Abu Dhabi crown prince Mohamed bin Zayed.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2015": "The UAE entered the Yemen war as Saudi Arabia\u0027s chief partner, deploying ground forces that helped retake Aden in 2015. It now acted as a regional military power with bases and influence around the Red Sea and the Horn of Africa, an unprecedented projection of force for a state of its size.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2017": "With Saudi Arabia, Bahrain and Egypt the UAE cut ties with Qatar in June, beginning a boycott that lasted until January 2021. The opening of the Louvre Abu Dhabi in November underlined the federation\u0027s cultural ambitions alongside its growing diplomatic and military weight in the Middle East and beyond.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2020": "The UAE became the third Arab state to establish full relations with Israel, through the Abraham Accords signed in September. In July the Hope probe was launched towards Mars, and the first reactor at Barakah was connected to the grid, marking scientific and technological ambitions unusual in the region.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2022": "Mohamed bin Zayed became president on the death of Khalifa in May 2022, formalising the leadership he had exercised for years. Despite Houthi drone and missile attacks on Abu Dhabi in January, the federation emerged from the pandemic with strong trade, rising foreign investment and a confident regional diplomacy.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2023": "Dubai hosted the COP28 climate conference, and the UAE, invited to join the BRICS grouping from January 2024, combined close ties with the United States with growing links to China, India and Russia. Its sovereign funds, ports and airlines gave it a global reach far beyond its size.",
  "United Arab Emirates|2025": "The federation stands at the height of its influence, under the same constitution and ruling families as in 1971. It remains dependent on oil revenues and foreign labour and exposed to regional conflict, but its diversified economy, wealth and diplomacy make it one of the most influential small states in the world.",
  "Republic of Yemen|1990": "On 22 May 1990 the Yemen Arab Republic and South Yemen united, with Saleh as head of state and al-Beidh as vice-president. The new republic combined the north\u0027s population with the south\u0027s port of Aden and new oil fields, but its two armies and political elites remained intact and mistrustful of one another.",
  "Republic of Yemen|1991": "Yemen\u0027s opposition, as a member of the UN Security Council, to military action against Iraq led Saudi Arabia and other Gulf states to expel hundreds of thousands of Yemeni workers and cut aid. Remittances collapsed, unemployment soared and the young republic began its life in economic crisis, despite a new democratic constitution.",
  "Republic of Yemen|1993": "The April 1993 elections, contested by many parties, gave the General People\u0027s Congress, Islah and the Yemeni Socialist Party a share of power. Yet the coalition was unstable: al-Beidh withdrew to Aden that summer, and the killing of southern politicians deepened mistrust between the former ruling parties of north and south.",
  "Republic of Yemen|1994": "Northern forces defeated the southern secession, taking Aden on 7 July 1994. The victory secured territorial unity and gave Saleh\u0027s government full control of the southern oil fields and port, raising the state\u0027s power, but at the cost of purging southern officers and planting resentments that would later resurface in the Southern Movement.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2000": "The Treaty of Jeddah settled the long-disputed border with Saudi Arabia, restoring relations with Riyadh, and oil production approached its peak, providing the bulk of state revenue. The bombing of the USS Cole in Aden in October, however, revealed the presence of al-Qaeda militants and drew Yemen into American counterterrorism efforts.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2003": "Yemen stood at the height of its stability and capacity. Parliamentary elections in April returned a large majority for the General People\u0027s Congress, oil revenues remained near their peak, and Saleh\u0027s patronage system held tribal leaders, army commanders and opposition parties within the political order he dominated from Sanaa.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2004": "Fighting broke out in the northern province of Saada between government forces and followers of Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi, who was killed in September. It was the first of six wars that would strengthen rather than destroy the Houthi movement, while declining oil output began to squeeze the state\u0027s finances.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2006": "Saleh won the September 2006 presidential election against the opposition candidate Faisal bin Shamlan, the most competitive contest in Yemen\u0027s history. The vote suggested a functioning, if unequal, pluralism, but the government faced shrinking oil revenues, widespread poverty and continuing conflict in the north.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2009": "The formation of Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula, the spread of southern protests under the Southern Movement and a sixth Houthi war that drew in Saudi Arabia exposed the state\u0027s weakness on several fronts at once. Exports of liquefied natural gas began, but could not offset the fall in oil income.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2011": "Mass protests demanded Saleh\u0027s departure, and the army split when General Ali Mohsen al-Ahmar defected. Saleh was wounded in a bomb attack in June and in November signed the Gulf Cooperation Council initiative, agreeing to hand over power. Central authority weakened as militants seized towns in the southern province of Abyan.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2012": "Abd Rabbuh Mansur Hadi was confirmed as president in February as the sole candidate. A National Dialogue Conference opened in 2013 to draft a new political order, and the army recaptured towns from militants, but Saleh\u0027s network remained powerful and the economy continued to deteriorate.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2014": "The Houthis, tacitly aided by forces loyal to Saleh, exploited anger at fuel price rises to march on Sanaa, which they seized on 21 September. The government lost control of its capital, and the transitional plan for a federal state of six regions collapsed within months amid growing confrontation.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2015": "Hadi fled to Aden and then Riyadh, and on 26 March a Saudi-led coalition began air strikes against the Houthis. Coalition-backed forces retook Aden in July, but the country split into rival zones, with the recognised government largely in exile and the institutions of the state divided.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2017": "War, blockade and unpaid public salaries produced famine conditions and one of the largest cholera outbreaks ever recorded. In December the Houthis killed Saleh after he broke with them, and the Southern Transitional Council, formed earlier in the year, challenged the recognised government in the south.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2019": "Southern Transitional Council forces seized Aden from government troops in August, and the Riyadh Agreement of November only papered over the division of the anti-Houthi camp. The UAE reduced its ground forces, and the recognised state existed in fragments, dependent on Saudi support for its survival.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2022": "A UN-brokered truce in April brought the first nationwide pause in fighting, and Hadi transferred his powers to a Presidential Leadership Council headed by Rashad al-Alimi. Although the truce formally lapsed in October, large-scale fighting largely stopped, giving the divided country a measure of relief.",
  "Republic of Yemen|2025": "Yemen remains formally one state but in practice divided between the Houthi authorities in Sanaa and the recognised government, whose separatist southern partners seized Hadramawt and Mahra in December 2025. Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping from late 2023 brought American and British strikes, while the humanitarian crisis, economic collapse and divided institutions persist.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-600": "Magadha was one of the sixteen great realms of northern India, a monarchy of south Bihar ruled from the hill-ringed fortress of Rajagriha. Its fertile rice lands, iron and elephants gave it great potential, but it was still only one of several rivals, overshadowed by Kosala, Avanti and the Vajji league.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-544": "On the traditional chronology Bimbisara of the Haryanka dynasty came to the throne about 544 BCE, though many scholars place him later. Through marriages with the royal houses of Kosala, the Licchavis and the Madras he secured his borders and gained Kashi revenues, beginning Magadha\u0027s rise above its neighbours.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-530": "Bimbisara conquered the eastern kingdom of Anga and its river port of Champa, at an approximate date. The annexation gave Magadha control of trade on the lower Ganges and greatly increased its resources, marking the first step in an expansion that would continue for almost two centuries.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-492": "Ajatashatru took the throne, by tradition after imprisoning his father Bimbisara, who died in captivity. The new king inherited a prosperous state with Anga already annexed and turned to aggressive expansion against the Vajji league and Kosala, while strengthening Rajagriha against a threatened attack from Avanti.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-484": "War began between Magadha and the Vajji league, the powerful confederation of clans centred on Vaishali north of the Ganges. To prosecute it Ajatashatru fortified Pataligrama on the river, and Jain tradition credits him with new siege weapons. The long struggle strained Magadha\u0027s resources before victory came.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-468": "The Vajji league was finally defeated, after some sixteen years of war according to Jain tradition, and its lands north of the Ganges were absorbed. Magadha now controlled both banks of the middle Ganges, and the oligarchic republics that had resisted royal power in the east lost their independence.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-460": "By around the mid-fifth century BCE Kosala, Magadha\u0027s great western rival, had been absorbed after the struggle over Kashi and the internal troubles of the Kosalan royal house. Magadha now dominated the whole middle Ganges valley, the most populous and productive region of northern India.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-440": "Udayin, Ajatashatru\u0027s son and successor, moved the capital from Rajagriha to Pataliputra on the Ganges, according to tradition, though his dates are uncertain. The river capital was better placed for trade and for controlling the conquered lands, and it would serve as the seat of north Indian empires for centuries.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-413": "The Haryanka line ended in disorder. According to the Mahavamsa, a series of parricides so disgusted the people that they deposed the last king and raised the minister Shishunaga to the throne, around 413 BCE on the traditional chronology. The change of dynasty briefly checked Magadha\u0027s momentum.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-400": "Shishunaga conquered Avanti, the powerful kingdom of the Pradyota dynasty centred on Ujjain in western India, removing Magadha\u0027s last great rival in the north. The date is approximate. The conquest extended Magadhan power towards Malwa and the routes leading to the western coast and the Deccan.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-383": "Kalashoka, Shishunaga\u0027s son, traditionally sponsored the Second Buddhist Council at Vaishali, about a century after the Buddha\u0027s death, and returned the capital to Pataliputra. Magadha was by then the dominant state of northern India, governing the former realms of Anga, Vajji, Kosala, Kashi and Avanti from a single capital.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-366": "Magadha stood at its peak under the later Shishunagas, controlling the Ganges valley from Kashi to Anga as well as lands in Malwa. Its army, wealth and administrative reach were unmatched in India, although the dates for this period are uncertain and the surviving sources say little about its kings.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-350": "After Kalashoka\u0027s death, by later tradition through assassination, his sons ruled amid apparent weakness. The kingdom\u0027s territory and resources remained intact, but royal authority declined, opening the way for an ambitious usurper from outside the established royal line to seize the throne at Pataliputra.",
  "Kingdom of Magadha|-345": "Mahapadma Nanda overthrew the last Shishunaga king around 345 BCE and founded the Nanda Empire. Magadha did not collapse: its army, revenues and capital passed intact to the new dynasty, which expanded them further. The kingdom of the Haryankas and Shishunagas ended as the core of a larger empire.",
  "Pallava dynasty|275": "The Pallavas emerged in the late third century around Kanchipuram as Satavahana and Ikshvaku power waned. Their earliest Prakrit charters show kings granting land in the Andhra country and around Bellary, but the dynasty was still one regional house among several in a politically fragmented south.",
  "Pallava dynasty|350": "The Gupta emperor Samudragupta, campaigning in the south, defeated Vishnugopa of Kanchi, according to his Allahabad pillar inscription, but left him in place. Around the same time the Kadamba dynasty broke away in the western Deccan, limiting Pallava expansion and keeping the dynasty a modest regional power.",
  "Pallava dynasty|500": "Little is known of the Pallavas in the fifth and early sixth centuries beyond Sanskrit land grants. The far south was dominated by the Kalabhras, and the dynasty held Tondaimandalam around Kanchipuram, a stable but limited territory, while patronising Brahmin learning at the Kanchi ghatika.",
  "Pallava dynasty|575": "Simhavishnu defeated the Kalabhras and extended Pallava rule south to the Kaveri delta, according to later charters. His reign began the line of the later Pallavas, turning a regional dynasty into a major South Indian power able to compete with the Pandyas and the Chalukyas of Badami.",
  "Pallava dynasty|600": "Mahendravarman I came to the throne, a king of wide interests who wrote the Sanskrit farce Mattavilasa and excavated the first rock-cut temples of the Tamil country. Pallava power stood high, but the expansion of the Chalukyas under Pulakeshin II was about to threaten the kingdom\u0027s northern frontier.",
  "Pallava dynasty|619": "Pulakeshin II invaded Pallava territory and reached the neighbourhood of Kanchipuram, taking northern provinces, though the Pallavas claimed a victory at Pullalur. The war revealed the kingdom\u0027s vulnerability and began a rivalry with the Chalukyas of Badami that would last for more than a century.",
  "Pallava dynasty|642": "Narasimhavarman I captured and sacked the Chalukya capital Vatapi, where Pulakeshin II died, and took the title Vatapikonda. With naval expeditions to Sri Lanka and the building of Mamallapuram, the Pallavas stood at the height of their power as the leading state of southern India.",
  "Pallava dynasty|668": "After Narasimhavarman I\u0027s death the Chalukyas recovered under Vikramaditya I, who invaded Pallava territory. The brief reign of Mahendravarman II and the accession of Parameshvaravarman I brought renewed war, and the Pallavas lost their supremacy, though they still held their core lands and wealth.",
  "Pallava dynasty|700": "Narasimhavarman II Rajasimha ruled a largely peaceful and prosperous kingdom, building the Shore Temple at Mamallapuram and the Kailasanatha temple at Kanchipuram. Pallava culture, trade and prestige reached their height and links across the Bay of Bengal flourished, although military power no longer matched that of Narasimhavarman I.",
  "Pallava dynasty|731": "The main line ended with the death of Parameshvaravarman II, and leading men chose the young Nandivarman II of a collateral branch as king. The succession crisis coincided with renewed Chalukya pressure, and the kingdom entered a difficult period of invasion and internal uncertainty.",
  "Pallava dynasty|740": "The Chalukya king Vikramaditya II occupied Kanchipuram, though he respected its temples and made gifts to the Kailasanatha temple. Rashtrakuta raids followed, and the Pandyas advanced from the south. The capture of the capital marked the lowest point of Pallava fortunes in the eighth century.",
  "Pallava dynasty|796": "Nandivarman II died after a reign of some sixty-five years in which he survived invasions, fought the Pandyas and built the Vaikuntha Perumal temple. His son Dantivarman inherited a recovered but diminished kingdom, soon facing Rashtrakuta pressure from the north and the Pandyas in the Kaveri delta.",
  "Pallava dynasty|846": "Nandivarman III came to the throne and defeated the Pandyas at the Battle of Tellaru, a victory celebrated in the Tamil poem Nandikkalambakam. Pallava trade reached Southeast Asia, as the Takua Pa inscription shows, and the dynasty enjoyed a last revival of its power and prestige.",
  "Pallava dynasty|869": "Nripatunga succeeded Nandivarman III and defeated the Pandyas on the Arisil river, but rival members of the royal family divided the dynasty. Growing reliance on allies and feudatories, among them the rising Cholas, weakened royal authority in the kingdom\u0027s last decades.",
  "Pallava dynasty|885": "At the Battle of Sripurambiyam Aparajita and his Ganga ally Prithvipati I defeated the Pandya king Varaguna II. The victory ended the Pandya threat but left the Pallavas dependent on their Chola allies, whose king Aditya I gained territory and prestige from the campaign.",
  "Pallava dynasty|897": "Around 897 the Chola king Aditya I turned on his overlord, defeated Aparajita and annexed Tondaimandalam. The Pallava kingdom ceased to exist, its lands absorbed into the expanding Chola Empire, and Pallava descendants survived only as minor local chiefs in the Tamil country.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|-100": "Simuka, founder of the dynasty according to the Puranas, established Satavahana rule in the northern Deccan after Mauryan authority receded. His date is disputed, ranging from the late third to the first century BCE. The new kingdom was one of several regional powers emerging in the post-Mauryan Deccan.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|-70": "Satakarni I, the first great king of the line, extended Satavahana rule over the western Deccan and probably into Malwa, at dates that remain approximate. The Naneghat inscription of his queen Naganika records two horse sacrifices and lavish gifts, proclaiming a confident kingship that controlled the passes between coast and plateau.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|-28": "On a common reading of the Puranas, an Andhra king ended the Kanva dynasty of Magadha about this time, though the identification with the Satavahanas is debated. The Satavahanas were by then established as the leading power of the northern Deccan, prospering from trade between the coast and the interior.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|60": "Satavahana history in the first century CE is obscure. The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea describes goods carried from Paithan and Ter to Barygaza but reports that the port of Kalyan had been obstructed, probably a sign of Saka pressure on the western coast that would soon cost the dynasty its western lands.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|100": "The Kshaharata ruler Nahapana, a Western Satrap, held much of the western Deccan, including Nashik, Junnar and the Konkan ports, where his family\u0027s donations are recorded in cave inscriptions. The Satavahanas had lost their western heartland and the trade routes to the coast, leaving them a fraction of their former power.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|106": "Gautamiputra Satakarni came to the throne, at a date usually placed around 106 CE, though some scholars put it earlier. He inherited a weakened kingdom pressed by the Western Satraps but began rebuilding Satavahana power, preparing the campaigns that would restore the dynasty\u0027s western provinces.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|124": "Gautamiputra destroyed the Kshaharata dynasty and recovered the western Deccan, restriking thousands of Nahapana\u0027s silver coins with his own name. His mother\u0027s Nashik inscription later celebrated him as the destroyer of the Shakas, Yavanas and Pahlavas, whose horses had drunk the waters of three seas.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|130": "Under Gautamiputra\u0027s son Vasishthiputra Pulumavi the kingdom reached its widest extent, from the Konkan coast to the Krishna delta. The geographer Ptolemy names Paithan as the royal seat of Siri Pulumavi, and work on the great stupa at Amaravati flourished under royal and merchant patronage.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|150": "Rudradaman I of the Western Satraps, according to his Junagadh inscription, twice defeated Satakarni, lord of the south, and held western territories including Malwa and the Konkan that Gautamiputra had claimed. A marriage alliance between the two houses limited the damage, but Satavahana power was clearly reduced.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|175": "Yajna Sri Satakarni, the last strong king, recovered parts of the western Deccan from the Western Satraps, as his inscriptions at Nashik and Kanheri show. His abundant coins, some showing a ship, suggest a revived economy and maritime trade, and the kingdom again spanned the peninsula from coast to coast.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|200": "After Yajna Sri\u0027s death his successors, ruling mainly in the east, lost control of the provinces. Feudatory families, among them the Abhiras in the west and the Chutus in the south-west, asserted their independence, and royal authority shrank rapidly as revenues and territories fell to local rulers.",
  "Satavahana dynasty|220": "By the early third century the Satavahana kingdom had broken apart. The Abhira dynasty ruled in the west, the Ikshvaku dynasty in the Krishna valley and the Chutu dynasty around Banavasi, while the last Satavahana kings disappeared from the record. The Deccan entered a period of regional states.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|543": "Pulakeshin I fortified the hill of Vatapi, as recorded in a rock inscription at Badami, and performed a horse sacrifice to proclaim his independence from the Kadambas. The new kingdom was a regional chiefdom in the Malaprabha valley, with a defensible capital but little territory beyond the surrounding districts of northern Karnataka.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|566": "Kirtivarman I succeeded his father and began the wars that made the Chalukyas the leading power of the western Deccan. He defeated the Kadambas of Banavasi, the Nalas and the Mauryas of the Konkan, extending the kingdom towards the coast. Power grew steadily, though the realm was still a young and loosely held state.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|597": "Mangalesha ruled as regent and king after his brother’s death. He defeated the Kalachuris of Mahishmati to the north and took Revatidvipa near modern Goa, while the Vaishnava cave at Badami, excavated under his direction and dated 578, showed growing wealth. The kingdom now controlled much of the western Deccan and part of the coast.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|610": "Mangalesha’s attempt to make his own son heir led to civil war. His nephew Pulakeshin II killed him and seized the throne around 610, but the conflict encouraged feudatories and neighbours to rebel. Power dipped briefly while the new king restored control over the core territories and subordinate chiefs.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|619": "Pulakeshin II defeated Harsha of Kannauj on the Narmada River, stopping the most powerful ruler of northern India; the date is debated, with some scholars placing it in the 630s. The victory established the Chalukyas as the paramount Deccan power and fixed the Narmada as the frontier between north and south.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|624": "Pulakeshin II conquered Vengi in the eastern Deccan and gave it to his brother Kubja Vishnuvardhana, founder of the Eastern Chalukyas. With the Konkan coast taken by a fleet and the Pallavas pressed in the south, the kingdom now stretched across the peninsula from the Arabian Sea to the Bay of Bengal.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|634": "The Aihole inscription composed by the poet Ravikirti celebrated Pulakeshin II’s conquests at the height of his reign. The Chalukyas ruled from sea to sea, held feudatories from Gujarat to the Kaveri borderlands and commanded respect from northern rulers, the summit of the dynasty’s territorial and military power.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|642": "The Pallava king Narasimhavarman I invaded, defeated Pulakeshin II, who apparently died in the fighting, and occupied Vatapi. The capital was lost, the king’s sons quarrelled over the succession and feudatories were left without a centre. For some years the main line of the dynasty barely functioned as a state.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|655": "Vikramaditya I, a son of Pulakeshin II, expelled the Pallava occupiers and reunited the kingdom, with the support of the Western Ganga dynasty. The restoration recovered the core lands and the capital at Vatapi, but the eastern branch at Vengi now ruled independently, and authority over distant feudatories had to be rebuilt.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|670": "Vikramaditya I carried the war back into Pallava territory and is credited with raiding Kanchipuram and campaigning as far as the Kaveri, though exact dates are uncertain. The restored kingdom was again the strongest power in the Deccan, if smaller than under Pulakeshin II, and its southern rivalry continued.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|696": "Vijayaditya began a reign of nearly four decades that was largely peaceful. Temples multiplied at Aihole, Pattadakal and Mahakuta, and grants to Brahmins and temples show a prosperous agrarian kingdom. Power remained stable at a high level, resting on settled administration rather than new conquest.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|733": "Vikramaditya II succeeded and resumed the offensive against the Pallavas. His feudatories in Gujarat repelled an Arab raid from Sindh around 739, protecting the northern frontier. The kingdom combined internal prosperity with renewed military energy, approaching the standing it had held a century earlier.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|740": "Vikramaditya II occupied Kanchipuram but, according to his own inscription there, spared the city and made gifts to the Kailasanatha temple. His queens built the Virupaksha and Mallikarjuna temples at Pattadakal to celebrate the victory. This was the last high point of Chalukya power and prestige.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|744": "Kirtivarman II succeeded his father, the dates of the change being uncertain by a year or two. Decades of war had strained resources, and powerful feudatories in the north, above all the Rashtrakuta Dantidurga, were building independent bases. The kingdom still looked imposing, but its control over its subordinates was weakening.",
  "Chalukyas of Badami|753": "Dantidurga defeated Kirtivarman II and assumed imperial titles, founding the Rashtrakuta dynasty. The Chalukya king apparently held a reduced domain in southern Karnataka for a few more years until Krishna I completed the conquest by about 757, but the dynasty of Vatapi had lost its kingdom.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|669": "Sundanese tradition dates the kingdom’s foundation to 669, when Tarusbawa is said to have taken the western part of the old Tarumanagara lands while Galuh took the east. The account comes from much later manuscripts, and the early state, if it existed in this form, was a small agrarian realm in western Java.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|932": "The Kebon Kopi II inscription near Bogor, generally dated to 932, gives the earliest secure mention of a king of Sunda, apparently restored to power. The kingdom was by then an established polity in western Java, but its size, institutions and relations with the stronger kingdoms of central Java are poorly known.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1030": "The Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions near Sukabumi record Sri Jayabupati declaring a stretch of river sacred and forbidding fishing there. They show a king able to issue formal edicts in the Javanese manner, with an organised religious and administrative order, ruling a modest but stable realm in the western highlands.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1225": "The Chinese official Zhao Rugu noted that Sunda produced pepper of high quality, evidence that the kingdom was linked to Asian maritime trade through its northern ports. Pepper exports brought revenue and foreign contact, and Sunda had become a recognised trading state on the edge of the Javanese world.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1357": "King Linggabuana and his daughter were killed by Majapahit troops in the Battle of Bubat, according to the Pararaton and the Kidung Sunda. The loss of the king and much of the court was a heavy blow, although Sunda appears never to have been brought under Majapahit rule.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1371": "Niskala Wastu Kancana, a son of Linggabuana, began a very long reign remembered at Kawali, where inscriptions praise him as a just and pious ruler; tradition gives his reign as about a century, which cannot be confirmed. The kingdom recovered from Bubat and remained independent alongside a dominant Majapahit.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1482": "Sri Baduga Maharaja united the crowns of Sunda and Galuh permanently and ruled from Pakuan Pajajaran near modern Bogor. He fortified the capital with a moat and endowed religious communities. Although Cirebon broke away around this time, the united kingdom entered its strongest and best-documented period.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1512": "Tomé Pires described Sunda as a prosperous kingdom reaching from the Sunda Strait to the Cimanuk River, with busy ports exporting pepper and rice to Malacca. Under Sri Baduga Maharaja the kingdom controlled the whole of western Java and its trade, the peak of its wealth and territorial reach.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1522": "Facing the rise of Muslim Demak, Sunda concluded a treaty with the Portuguese of Malacca, offering pepper and a fort site at Sunda Kalapa in return for support. The kingdom was still intact, but the alliance reflected growing anxiety about the Muslim states pressing on its coastal provinces.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1527": "Forces from Demak and Cirebon under Fatahillah captured Sunda Kalapa, after taking Banten, and defeated a Portuguese landing party. Sunda lost its main ports, its access to the sea and most of its pepper revenue, a sharp fall in power although the inland kingdom and its capital survived.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1535": "Surawisesa, who had set up the Batutulis inscription in 1533 to honour his father, died around this time after a reign that Sundanese tradition fills with battles against the Muslim states. His successors inherited a landlocked kingdom of reduced income, still defended but unable to recover the coast.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1552": "Hasanuddin of Banten is traditionally regarded as ruler of an independent Banten Sultanate from about this time, while Cirebon held the east. Hemmed in on two sides, the Sunda Kingdom was confined to the interior highlands around Pakuan, with diminishing resources and few allies.",
  "Sunda Kingdom|1579": "Maulana Yusuf of Banten captured and destroyed Pakuan Pajajaran. According to tradition the royal stone throne was taken to Banten and the last king, Raga Mulya, died soon after. The kingdom ceased to exist, its lands divided among Banten, Cirebon and Sumedang Larang.",
  "Udaipur State|728": "Tradition credits Bappa Rawal with taking Chittor in the early eighth century and founding the Guhila kingdom of Mewar. In reality the Guhilas were one of several local Rajput lineages in southern Rajasthan, ruling a small hill kingdom from Nagda and Ahar under the shadow of larger regional powers.",
  "Udaipur State|1213": "Jaitrasimha came to the throne and made Mewar a significant regional power, resisting the Delhi Sultanate in its early decades. Although Iltutmish sacked Nagda in the 1220s, the kingdom survived and shifted its centre to the fortress of Chittor, which became the symbol of Guhila rule.",
  "Udaipur State|1303": "Alauddin Khalji took Chittor after a siege of about eight months, King Ratnasimha was killed and the fortress was handed to the sultan’s son Khizr Khan. The main Guhila line was broken and Mewar was reduced to scattered chiefs in the hills, its lowest point before the twentieth century.",
  "Udaipur State|1326": "Hammir Singh of the Sisodia branch recovered Chittor from the Sultanate’s allies around 1326 and took the title of rana. The restoration re-established an independent kingdom, though at first it was small and still had to contend with the governors and allies of the Delhi Sultanate.",
  "Udaipur State|1433": "Rana Kumbha began a reign of thirty-five years in which Mewar defeated the sultans of Malwa and Gujarat, built Kumbhalgarh and the Victory Tower at Chittor and flourished as a centre of music and learning. Mewar became one of the leading powers of western India.",
  "Udaipur State|1508": "Rana Sanga won the throne after a struggle with his brothers. A celebrated warrior, he rebuilt the Rajput alliance network around Mewar and began expanding against the sultanates of Malwa, Gujarat and Delhi, which were weakened by internal conflict, raising the kingdom close to its height.",
  "Udaipur State|1517": "Rana Sanga defeated Ibrahim Lodi of Delhi at Khatoli and two years later captured Mahmud II of Malwa. Mewar headed a confederacy of Rajput states and was the strongest Hindu power in northern India, with real prospects of challenging for supremacy in the north. This was its peak.",
  "Udaipur State|1527": "Rana Sanga’s coalition was defeated by Babur at the Battle of Khanwa, where Mughal guns and matchlocks broke the Rajput cavalry. Sanga died the next year. The defeat ended Mewar’s bid for northern supremacy and left a weakened kingdom divided by succession disputes.",
  "Udaipur State|1535": "Bahadur Shah of Gujarat besieged and sacked Chittor, an event remembered for the jauhar of Rani Karnavati and the women of the fort. Mewar was in disorder, with a minor king and feuding nobles, until Udai Singh II secured the throne by about 1540.",
  "Udaipur State|1568": "Akbar took Chittor after a siege of several months, followed by the killing of many of its defenders and inhabitants. Udai Singh II held out in the hills and developed his new city of Udaipur, but Mewar had lost its historic capital and much of its fertile land.",
  "Udaipur State|1597": "Maharana Pratap died at Chavand after two decades of resistance. Despite defeat at Haldighati in 1576, his campaigns from the hills had recovered most of Mewar except Chittor and Mandalgarh, leaving a smaller but independent kingdom and an enduring reputation for defiance of the Mughals.",
  "Udaipur State|1615": "Amar Singh I, worn down by renewed Mughal campaigns, accepted Jahangir’s overlordship on honourable terms through Prince Khurram. Mewar lost its independence but kept its dynasty, internal autonomy and prestige, and was spared the marriage alliances made by other Rajput houses.",
  "Udaipur State|1680": "Raj Singh I died during the Rajput war against Aurangzeb, which he had joined in support of Marwar. His reign had seen great building works such as Rajsamand Lake and a reassertion of Mewar’s dignity, but the state remained a subordinate power unable to defy the empire alone.",
  "Udaipur State|1736": "The Peshwa Baji Rao I came to Mewar and the Marathas began to exact regular tribute. Over the following decades Scindia and Holkar forces drained the treasury, while feuding nobles weakened the Maharanas. Mewar was a declining state with little control over its own territory.",
  "Udaipur State|1818": "Exhausted by Maratha and Pindari raids, Maharana Bhim Singh signed a treaty with the East India Company, accepting British protection and paying tribute. Order was restored but external sovereignty was surrendered, and Mewar became a princely state supervised by a British political agent.",
  "Udaipur State|1948": "Maharana Bhupal Singh, who had acceded to India in 1947, brought Udaipur into the United State of Rajasthan on 18 April 1948 and became its Rajpramukh. The state’s separate existence was ending, though its ruler retained a formal role in the new union.",
  "Udaipur State|1949": "On 30 March 1949 the United State of Rajasthan merged with Jaipur, Jodhpur, Bikaner and Jaisalmer to form Greater Rajasthan, with Bhupal Singh as Maharajpramukh. Udaipur State ceased to exist as a political unit after more than a thousand years of Guhila and Sisodia rule.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|730": "Nagabhata I founded the imperial line of the Pratiharas in western India, probably based at Ujjain or in the Jalor region. Arab armies from Sindh were raiding Gujarat, Rajasthan and Malwa, and the new state was one of several regional powers struggling to survive in an unsettled frontier zone.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|738": "Nagabhata I is credited in the Gwalior inscription with destroying the armies of a powerful foreign king, usually linked to the repulse of Arab raids around 738. The victory gave the dynasty prestige and a role as defender of western India, though its territory remained regional.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|778": "Vatsaraja, named as reigning king in the Kuvalayamala completed at Jalor in 778, had extended Pratihara power over much of Rajasthan and central India. He was preparing to contest the Ganges plain with the Palas, and the dynasty was becoming a power of more than regional importance.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|786": "Vatsaraja defeated the Pala king Dharmapala in the Doab but was then routed by the Rashtrakuta king Dhruva, who invaded from the Deccan. The Pratiharas were thrown back into Rajasthan, losing their gains in the Ganges valley, and for a time their imperial ambitions seemed to be over.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|816": "Nagabhata II captured Kannauj from the Ayudha king Chakrayudha, a Pala protégé, and made it his capital; he also defeated Dharmapala near Monghyr. Despite an earlier defeat by the Rashtrakuta Govinda III, the dynasty now held the symbolic centre of northern India.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|836": "Mihira Bhoja came to the throne after the brief reign of Ramabhadra, during which the empire had lost ground. Early in his reign Bhoja faced setbacks against the Palas and Rashtrakutas, and he had to rebuild the authority of Kannauj over the feudatories of Rajasthan and central India.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|860": "Mihira Bhoja had restored and extended the empire across Rajasthan, Malwa, the Doab and into Punjab. In 851 an Arab merchant described the king of the Gurjaras as having the finest cavalry in India and a realm safe from robbers. Pratihara power was rising towards its height.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|885": "At Mihira Bhoja’s death after a reign of almost fifty years, the empire stretched from Punjab and Gujarat to the borders of Bengal and was the strongest state in northern India. Its feudatories were obedient and its western frontier against the Arabs of Sindh and Multan secure.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|910": "Mahendrapala I had maintained the empire at its height, with inscriptions of his reign found as far east as Bihar, while his court supported the poet Rajashekhara. At his death a succession dispute arose, and the coming years would expose the empire to its southern enemies.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|916": "The Rashtrakuta Indra III sacked Kannauj, driving Mahipala I from his capital. Mahipala returned, apparently with help from the Chandela king Harsha, but the blow shattered Pratihara prestige and encouraged the great feudatories to act independently. Power fell sharply within a few years.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|944": "By the end of Mahipala I’s reign the empire was in clear decline. The Chandelas, Paramaras, Chaulukyas and Kalachuris were becoming independent kingdoms, the Chandelas soon taking the fortress of Kalanjara, and Rashtrakuta campaigns under Krishna III further weakened the north.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|1000": "The Pratiharas now ruled little more than the region around Kannauj. Their former feudatories were stronger than they were, and the dynasty survived mainly through the prestige of its ancient capital, unable to defend the north against the rising Ghaznavid power in the north-west.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|1018": "Mahmud of Ghazni marched on Kannauj; King Rajyapala abandoned the city, which was plundered. The Chandela king Vidyadhara then led a coalition that killed Rajyapala for his submission. The dynasty retained only a fragment of territory under the shadow of its former vassals.",
  "Gurjara-Pratihara|1036": "Yashahpala’s inscription of 1036 is the last record of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty, which had shrunk to a minor principality around Kannauj and Allahabad. Soon after, it disappears from the historical record, its lands absorbed by neighbouring kingdoms, and Kannauj passed to the Gahadavala dynasty around 1090.",
  "Pala Empire|750": "After a century of disorder, which the Palas’ own inscriptions call the law of the fish, Gopala was chosen king in Bengal around 750. The new kingdom restored order in part of Bengal, but it was a regional state surrounded by rivals, with no claim yet beyond the lower Ganges.",
  "Pala Empire|770": "Dharmapala succeeded his father and began expanding beyond Bengal into Bihar and the middle Ganges. Founding monasteries such as Vikramashila and Somapura, he combined conquest with lavish Buddhist patronage, and the Palas emerged as one of the main contenders for supremacy in northern India.",
  "Pala Empire|786": "Dharmapala was defeated in the Ganges–Yamuna Doab by the Pratihara Vatsaraja and then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva, who had invaded from the Deccan. Yet both victors soon withdrew, and the Palas lost little territory in Bengal and Bihar, leaving Dharmapala free to renew his ambitions in the west.",
  "Pala Empire|800": "Dharmapala installed Chakrayudha as king of Kannauj at an assembly that, according to the Khalimpur plate, many northern kings attended. The Palas were the leading power of northern India, although their hold on Kannauj was indirect and soon contested by both the Rashtrakutas and the Pratiharas.",
  "Pala Empire|810": "Devapala succeeded his father after Dharmapala and Chakrayudha had suffered reverses: the Rashtrakuta Govinda III had received their submission and the Pratihara Nagabhata II defeated Dharmapala near Monghyr and soon took Kannauj. The Palas lost their position in the west but kept a strong base in the east.",
  "Pala Empire|840": "Devapala’s reign marked the height of Pala power. His inscriptions claim victories from Orissa to Assam and beyond the north-western frontier, and his grant to Nalanda at the request of the Shailendra king Balaputradeva shows the dynasty’s international standing. Bengal and Bihar were firmly held and prosperous.",
  "Pala Empire|860": "After Devapala’s death the Pala kingdom passed to rulers of a collateral line, and under Narayanapala it lost the initiative. The Pratiharas under Mihira Bhoja and the Rashtrakutas pressed from west and south, and Pala power fell back towards its core in Bengal and southern Bihar.",
  "Pala Empire|900": "The Pratiharas under Mahendrapala I had occupied Magadha, the heartland of Pala Buddhism, and the Palas were reduced to Bengal and parts of eastern Bihar. The dynasty still ruled a wealthy agrarian kingdom, but it was a regional state rather than a contender for the north.",
  "Pala Empire|950": "A Kamboja dynasty took control of northern and western Bengal, and feudatories elsewhere acted independently. The Palas held only fragments of their former territory, mainly in Bihar, at the lowest point of their fortunes before the revival under Mahipala I.",
  "Pala Empire|988": "Mahipala I came to the throne, traditionally around 988, and began to recover the lost lands in Bengal from the Kambojas and other local rulers. His reign marked a genuine revival, though the restored kingdom was smaller and less dominant than the empire of Dharmapala and Devapala.",
  "Pala Empire|1020": "Mahipala I had reunited much of Bengal and Bihar and supported the restoration of monasteries and shrines at Nalanda and Bodh Gaya. The Palas were again a significant regional power in eastern India, though Kalachuri and Chola ambitions threatened from the west and south.",
  "Pala Empire|1023": "The Chola king Rajendra I sent an expedition to the Ganges that claims to have defeated Mahipala I. The raid was not followed by occupation, but it exposed the weakness of the Pala army, and Kalachuri attacks in the following decades eroded the western parts of the kingdom.",
  "Pala Empire|1040": "Atisha left Vikramashila around 1040 for Tibet, a sign of the continuing prestige of the Pala monasteries under Nayapala. Politically the kingdom was stable but under pressure from the Kalachuri king Karna, and it remained a regional state of moderate strength.",
  "Pala Empire|1075": "The Kaivarta revolt in northern Bengal, led by Divya, had killed King Mahipala II and taken Varendra, the dynasty’s ancestral homeland. The Palas were divided and weakened, holding only part of their territory, and the rebels’ rule in the north lasted for a generation.",
  "Pala Empire|1085": "Ramapala, with the support of a coalition of feudatory chiefs, defeated the Kaivarta ruler Bhima and recovered Varendra, founding a new capital at Ramavati. The kingdom was restored as the leading power of eastern India, although its dependence on the samantas was now greater than ever.",
  "Pala Empire|1120": "Late in Ramapala’s reign the Palas extended their influence towards Kamarupa and Orissa, according to the Ramacharita. The kingdom was stable and respected, but feudatories such as the Senas were growing stronger, and the restoration depended heavily on the personal authority of the king.",
  "Pala Empire|1130": "After Ramapala’s death his successors faced rebellions and the rise of the Sena dynasty under Vijayasena, who gradually made himself master of Bengal. The Pala kingdom shrank rapidly, losing its Bengali heartland, including Varendra, and retreating towards Bihar, where its last kings held on with little more than local authority.",
  "Pala Empire|1161": "Madanapala, the last significant Pala king, ruled only parts of Bihar and died around 1161. The dynasty came to an end, Bengal passing to the Senas, and within a few decades Turkish campaigns destroyed the great Buddhist monasteries that the Palas had sustained.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|753": "Dantidurga, a Chalukya feudatory based around Ellora, defeated Kirtivarman II and proclaimed himself paramount ruler, as his Samangad grant of this year shows. The new state already held much of the northern Deccan, but the southern Chalukya lands were still unconquered, so its power stood at a promising but limited level.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|756": "Dantidurga died without a son and his uncle Krishna I took the throne. The transition was peaceful, and Krishna immediately pressed on against the remaining Chalukya territory in Karnataka. The kingdom was strong in the north-western Deccan but had not yet absorbed the whole of the former Chalukya empire.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|772": "By the early 770s Krishna I had completed the conquest of the Chalukya lands, defeated the Western Gangas and attacked Vengi. The Kailasa temple at Ellora, attributed to him, displayed new resources and ambition. The Rashtrakutas were now the undisputed masters of the western Deccan, a major regional power.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|780": "Dhruva Dharavarsha displaced his elder brother Govinda II after a struggle for the throne. Although the dispute briefly divided the court, Dhruva quickly restored the kingdom’s grip on its feudatories, punishing the Gangas and Vengi. Power was steady, with a vigorous new ruler preparing the first campaign into northern India.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|790": "Dhruva crossed the Narmada and defeated both the Pratihara Vatsaraja and the Pala Dharmapala in the Ganga–Yamuna doab. The raid did not create lasting northern provinces, but it made the Rashtrakutas a decisive force in the struggle for Kannauj and lifted their prestige far beyond the Deccan.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|793": "Dhruva’s death led to a challenge from his son Stambha and a coalition of feudatories against the chosen heir, Govinda III. Govinda had to fight to secure the throne, and for a short period the empire’s energy was absorbed by this internal war, slightly lowering its effective power.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|800": "Around 800 Govinda III defeated Nagabhata II of the Pratiharas, received the submission of Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and then broke a southern coalition and took tribute from Kanchi. With armies victorious from the Ganges to Tamil country, the Rashtrakuta empire stood at the height of its military reach and prestige.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|814": "Govinda III died and his young son Amoghavarsha I succeeded under the guardianship of his cousin Karka of the Gujarat branch. Revolts by officials and feudatories, and pressure from Vengi, followed the change of reign. The empire survived intact, but its power fell back from the peak of Govinda’s campaigns.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|830": "Amoghavarsha I defeated the Eastern Chalukya king Vijayaditya II at Vingavalli, ending the threat from Vengi, and governed from his new capital, Manyakheta. Control over the core was firm, although the northern campaigns were not repeated. The empire was stable and prosperous rather than expanding.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|851": "When Sulaiman wrote his account in 851, he counted the Balhara among the four great kings of the world. Amoghavarsha’s long reign brought internal peace, strong trade through the western ports and rich patronage of Jain and Kannada learning, keeping the empire powerful and widely respected.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|878": "Krishna II succeeded his father and faced renewed wars with Vengi and the Pratiharas, some of which went badly. The empire still held the Deccan and its feudatories, but its armies were on the defensive on several fronts, and its standing fell slightly from the prosperous years of Amoghavarsha.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|916": "Indra III crossed the Narmada, defeated the Pratihara Mahipala I and sacked Kannauj, the symbolic capital of northern India. Al-Masudi, visiting western India at about this time, described the Balhara as the greatest king in India. The empire regained much of the prestige of Govinda III’s era.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|936": "Govinda IV’s unpopular reign ended in a revolt by nobles and feudatories, who placed his uncle Amoghavarsha III on the throne with the support of the Chalukyas of Vemulavada. Internal discord and the loss of control over some vassals left the empire noticeably weaker than two decades earlier.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|939": "Krishna III, who had effectively led the government during his father’s reign, became king. A capable general, he restored discipline among the feudatories and renewed the alliance with the Western Gangas, preparing a major offensive against the rising Chola Empire in the south.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|949": "At the Battle of Takkolam Krishna III and his Ganga ally Butuga II defeated the Chola army, killing the prince Rajaditya. The Rashtrakutas occupied the region around Kanchi for years, and the empire once again dominated the south, reaching a second peak almost equal to that of Govinda III.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|967": "Krishna III died and his brother Khottiga succeeded. The empire was still extensive and its southern conquests were largely held, but much of its strength had rested on Krishna’s personal leadership. Ambitious feudatories, especially the Paramaras of Malwa, began to test the new king.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|972": "The Paramara ruler Siyaka II invaded the Deccan and sacked the capital Manyakheta. The loss of the capital and its treasure shattered royal prestige and showed the feudatories that the throne could be defied. Khottiga died soon afterwards, leaving a gravely weakened state.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|973": "Tailapa II, a feudatory claiming Chalukya descent, overthrew Karka II and founded the Western Chalukya Empire, which took over most Rashtrakuta lands. The dynasty was reduced to a claimant, Indra IV, supported in the south by the Western Ganga king Marasimha II.",
  "Rashtrakuta dynasty|982": "After Marasimha II died, Indra IV’s cause collapsed, and he ended his life by the Jain fast of sallekhana at Shravanabelagola in 982. With him the imperial Rashtrakuta line ended, its territory having passed to the Western Chalukyas almost a decade earlier.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|849": "According to the chronicles, Pagan was fortified in 849 by Burman settlers who had moved into the middle Irrawaddy after the decline of the Pyu city-states. It was then one small walled town among several in the dry zone, with only a local following, so its power was very modest.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|950": "Evidence for the tenth century is thin, but Pagan appears to have grown into the leading Burman centre of the middle Irrawaddy, drawing on irrigated rice lands nearby. It remained a local principality without control of Kyaukse or the coast, and its later greatness was not yet visible.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1044": "Anawrahta seized the throne and began to build a unified state. He brought the irrigated plains of Kyaukse and Minbu under direct royal control and organised their manpower, giving Pagan for the first time a secure economic base for wider campaigns, though its territory was still regional.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1057": "According to tradition Anawrahta conquered Thaton in 1057, bringing the Mon south, its monks, scriptures and craftsmen under Pagan. With Arakan and the Shan borderlands also submitting, Pagan became master of most of the Irrawaddy basin, a dramatic rise in territory and prestige within a few years.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1077": "Anawrahta died, reportedly in an accident while hunting, leaving a large but loosely integrated realm to his son Sawlu. The frontier forts and tributary ties he had created still held, so Pagan remained powerful, but the new king lacked his father’s authority among the provincial lords.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1084": "Sawlu was killed in a rebellion led by Nga Yaman Kan, the governor of Pegu. The general Kyansittha defeated the rebels and took the throne. The crisis showed how fragile the new state still was, and power stood briefly below Anawrahta’s level while Kyansittha restored order.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1102": "Kyansittha completed the Shwezigon stupa and built the Ananda temple, signs of a secure and wealthy court. He reconciled Mon and Burman elites, kept the south loyal and maintained contacts with Sri Lanka and China. Pagan was a stable regional power at the start of its great building age.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1113": "The Myazedi inscription in four languages marked the end of Kyansittha’s reign and the accession of Alaungsithu. The kingdom was consolidated, its administration more uniform and its religious institutions flourishing. Pagan stood close to its height, with secure borders and a growing temple economy.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1144": "Under Alaungsithu, who built the towering Thatbyinnyu temple, Pagan enjoyed a long period of internal peace. He is credited with standard weights and measures and with expeditions to the margins of the kingdom. Royal revenues were still strong, though donations to the sangha were steadily growing.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1167": "Alaungsithu’s death was followed by violent successions: Narathu, remembered in tradition for building the Dhammayangyi and for his cruelty, ruled only a few years, and his successor was soon killed too. The court’s instability weakened royal authority for a time, though the kingdom’s territory remained intact.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1174": "Narapatisithu came to the throne and restored stability. He reorganised the palace guard, secured the frontiers and supported monks returning from Sri Lanka who introduced the Mahavihara ordination. With a firm king and a prosperous economy, Pagan entered its last and most splendid phase.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1200": "Around 1200 Narapatisithu’s Pagan was at its height. The Sulamani and Gawdawpalin temples showed the wealth of the court, the provinces were quiet and Pagan’s influence reached from the Shan hills to Tenasserim. Burmese literature and law took shape, and the kingdom stood at its peak.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1211": "Htilominlo succeeded Narapatisithu and built the temple named after him. The kingdom remained peaceful and rich, but the transfer of tax-free land to monasteries was accelerating, and royal income from the core was beginning to shrink. Pagan was still near its height, though the underlying strains were growing.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1235": "Under Kyaswa, a learned king devoted to religious study, little new territory or revenue was gained, and the crown increasingly struggled with the scale of religious landholding. Pagan was still the unchallenged power of the region, but its central resources were noticeably weaker than a generation earlier.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1256": "Narathihapate came to the throne in a contested succession. Efforts to reclaim religious lands were limited, Shan chiefs grew stronger on the frontiers and the treasury was strained by costly building. The kingdom remained large on paper, but its capacity to meet a major external threat had declined.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1277": "Having rejected Yuan demands for tribute, Pagan lost the Battle of Ngasaunggyan to Mongol forces from Yunnan. The defeat broke the myth of Pagan’s invincibility and exposed the northern frontier, though the core of the kingdom was not yet occupied and the court continued to function.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1283": "Yuan troops captured the frontier fortress of Kaungsin, and Narathihapate fled south to Lower Burma, abandoning the capital. Royal authority over the provinces collapsed as governors and Shan chiefs acted independently. Negotiations with the Yuan began, but Pagan no longer controlled its own territory as a unified kingdom.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1287": "Narathihapate was murdered at Prome by his son Thihathu, and a new Yuan expedition entered Upper Burma. The Mon south broke away under Wareru, and Arakan went its own way. Pagan survived only as a reduced kingdom of the dry zone, a nominal vassal of the Yuan.",
  "Pagan Kingdom|1297": "Kyawswa, recognised by the Yuan as king of a small rump state, was deposed by the Myinsaing brothers, who held the real military power of central Burma. The Pagan dynasty ended, and the political centre of the Burman lands moved to the successor kingdoms of Myinsaing, Pinya and later Ava.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|973": "Tailapa II, a Rashtrakuta feudatory in the Bijapur region, overthrew Karka II after the Paramara sack of Manyakheta and proclaimed the restoration of Chalukya rule. He held the northern Karnataka core and the old capital, but many Rashtrakuta vassals had not yet accepted him, so power was moderate.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|982": "The Rashtrakuta claimant Indra IV died at Shravanabelagola, and loyalist resistance in the south collapsed. Tailapa II now received the allegiance of most former Rashtrakuta feudatories from the Konkan to the Ganga country, giving the new dynasty uncontested control of the western Deccan.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|995": "Around this time Tailapa II defeated and captured the invading Paramara king Vakpati Munja, who according to tradition was executed. The victory removed the most dangerous northern rival and confirmed the Chalukyas as the leading Deccan power, with authority stretching from the Narmada towards the Kaveri.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1007": "Satyashraya’s kingdom was invaded by the Chola prince Rajendra, whose army ravaged the Donur region, as the Hottur inscription records. The raid revealed the military strength of the rising Chola Empire and forced the Chalukyas onto the defensive in the Raichur doab, reducing their effective power.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1020": "Jayasimha II was defeated by Rajendra I Chola at Maski around 1020, losing ground in the south, though he held off the Paramara Bhoja in the north and kept his feudatories loyal. The empire remained large but was second to the Cholas in military reputation during these years.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1042": "Someshvara I took the throne and moved the capital to Kalyani. An energetic ruler, he campaigned in the north and renewed the struggle for Vengi and the Raichur doab, restoring the empire’s confidence and initiative after the setbacks of his father’s reign.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1054": "At the Battle of Koppam the Chola king Rajadhiraja I was killed. Although Rajendra II rallied the Chola army and claimed victory, the battle showed that the Chalukyas could meet the Cholas on equal terms, and Someshvara I’s empire stood strong across the Deccan.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1064": "Someshvara I’s forces were defeated by Virarajendra Chola at Kudalasangamam in the 1060s. The loss checked Chalukya ambitions in Vengi and the south, but the empire’s core and its feudatory network remained intact, so its power declined only moderately from the level of the 1050s.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1068": "Someshvara I, suffering from an incurable illness, drowned himself in the Tungabhadra. His son Someshvara II faced Chola intervention and the open rivalry of his brother Vikramaditya, who held Banavasi. Divided leadership and Chola meddling weakened the empire during this short reign.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1076": "Vikramaditya VI deposed Someshvara II and began the Chalukya-Vikrama era. A capable soldier with long experience as a viceroy, he quickly secured the loyalty of the great feudatories and opened a fifty-year reign of stability that marked the empire’s most successful period.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1100": "By around 1100 Vikramaditya VI’s Kalyani was the centre of a prosperous and cultured court, with Bilhana and Vijnaneshvara among its luminaries. Trade guilds flourished, temples were built across the Deccan, and the feudatories remained obedient. The empire was close to the height of its power.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1118": "As Chola control of the east coast weakened late in the reign of Kulottunga I, Vikramaditya VI occupied Vengi around 1118, while his generals contained the Hoysala Vishnuvardhana. With both rivals checked and the Deccan at peace, the empire reached the peak of its territory and influence.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1126": "Vikramaditya VI died after a reign of fifty years, and his son Someshvara III succeeded peacefully. The new king was a scholar who compiled the Manasollasa in 1129. The empire remained wealthy and extensive, though its military initiative and grip on Vengi soon began to weaken.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1138": "Jagadhekamalla II took the throne as the Cholas recovered Vengi and the Hoysalas resumed their expansion. The great feudatories, especially the Hoysalas, Kakatiyas and Kalachuris, were growing more powerful and less obedient, and the empire’s strength declined steadily despite its outward prosperity.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1151": "Tailapa III succeeded to a weakened throne. The Kakatiya chief Prola II defeated and captured him, and though he was released, royal prestige suffered badly. Real power in the army and provinces was passing to feudatories, among whom the Kalachuri commander Bijjala was the most dangerous.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1157": "The Kalachuri feudatory Bijjala II took control of Kalyani and the central government, reducing Tailapa III to a figurehead. The Chalukya dynasty survived in name, but the empire as a state was now directed by its former vassal, and its power fell sharply.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1162": "Bijjala II assumed full royal titles, and Tailapa III was left with a small remnant of territory. For the next two decades the Kalachuris ruled at Kalyani, while the Hoysalas, Yadavas and Kakatiyas acted independently. The Chalukya state had almost ceased to exist.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1184": "With Kalachuri rule collapsing, Someshvara IV, aided by the general Brahma, restored the Chalukya dynasty at Kalyani. The revival was modest: the great feudatories were now virtually independent kings, and the restored state controlled only part of the former empire.",
  "Western Chalukya Empire|1189": "The Seuna Yadava Bhillama V seized Kalyani and the north, and the Hoysala Veera Ballala II defeated the Chalukya forces in the south. Someshvara IV disappears from the records around 1189, and the empire’s lands were divided among its former vassals.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1026": "Nripa Kama II, one of the first Hoysala chiefs known from inscriptions, ruled a small territory in the Malnad hills around Sosevur as a subordinate of larger powers. The family was one local lineage among many, with a modest following of hill warriors and little standing beyond the Western Ghats.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1047": "Vinayaditya, Nripa Kama’s son, served the Western Chalukya king Someshvara I as a loyal feudatory and was rewarded with additional territory in the Malnad and the plateau. The Hoysalas remained subordinate chiefs, but their service in Chalukya wars against the Cholas raised their status and resources.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1098": "After Vinayaditya’s long rule, the family’s standing had grown through Ereyanga’s service as a prominent Chalukya commander. The Hoysalas now governed a substantial province on the edge of the Chola-held Gangavadi, although they were still vassals of the Western Chalukya Empire and depended on its favour.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1108": "Vishnuvardhana succeeded his brother Ballala I. Ambitious and energetic, he began expanding into the Chola-held lands of southern Karnataka and built up a strong army and court. The Hoysalas were becoming one of the most important feudatories of the Chalukya empire.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1116": "Vishnuvardhana’s forces captured Talakad from the Cholas, expelling them from southern Karnataka, and he took the title Talakadugonda. The following year he consecrated the Chennakeshava temple at Belur. The Hoysalas had become a significant regional power, though still formally under the Chalukyas.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1152": "Vishnuvardhana died after attempts to break away from Chalukya overlordship had been checked. His son Narasimha I inherited a large and wealthy kingdom but lacked his father’s vigour, and for two decades the Hoysalas stood under the shadow of the Kalachuris who had seized power at Kalyani.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1173": "Veera Ballala II came to the throne, reportedly after rebelling against his father. He set out to profit from the collapse of the Western Chalukya and Kalachuri states, rebuilding the army and preparing to claim the former imperial lands of northern Karnataka.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1187": "Veera Ballala II assumed full imperial titles, ending the Hoysalas’ formal subordination. As the Western Chalukya restoration collapsed, he moved north to compete with the Seuna Yadavas for its territory, and the Hoysala kingdom became a major independent power of the Deccan.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1190": "Around 1190 Veera Ballala II defeated the Seuna Yadava forces at Soratur and secured the lands south of the Malaprabha and Krishna rivers. With the northern frontier fixed and the old Chalukya south in his hands, the Hoysala kingdom stood close to the peak of its strength.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1220": "Narasimha II succeeded Veera Ballala II. The kingdom was rich, its northern frontier stable and its prestige high, and the new king began to intervene in the Tamil country, where the Chola monarchy was collapsing under pressure from the Pandyas and its own feudatories.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1231": "Narasimha II’s forces rescued the Chola king Rajaraja III from captivity by the Kadava chief Kopperunchinga and checked the Pandyas. The Hoysalas now acted as arbiters of south Indian politics, controlling Karnataka and influencing the Tamil lands, the height of their power.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1235": "Vira Someshvara succeeded and built a second capital at Kannanur Kuppam near Srirangam, where he spent much of his reign. Hoysala influence in the Tamil country was strong, but holding two widely separated territories strained resources as the Pandyas grew more powerful.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1254": "Vira Someshvara divided the kingdom between his sons: Narasimha III in Karnataka and Ramanatha in the Tamil lands. The division created two rival courts that soon quarrelled, dispersing Hoysala military strength just as the Later Pandyan Empire reached its height.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1262": "The Pandya king Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I defeated and killed Vira Someshvara, and Hoysala power in the Tamil country was soon rolled back. The kingdom remained secure in its Karnataka homeland, where Somanathapura’s temple was built in 1268, but its regional dominance had passed.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1292": "Veera Ballala III succeeded Narasimha III and over the following years reunited the Karnataka and Tamil branches of the kingdom. He restored a single Hoysala state of considerable size, though it was weaker than in Narasimha II’s time and faced powerful Yadava and Pandya neighbours.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1311": "Malik Kafur, general of the Delhi Sultanate, captured Dwarasamudra. Veera Ballala III submitted, surrendered treasure and sent his son to Delhi as a hostage. The kingdom survived as a tributary, but its wealth, prestige and independence were badly damaged by this first northern invasion.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1327": "Muhammad bin Tughluq’s armies extended Delhi’s direct rule over much of the south, and Dwarasamudra was attacked again. Ballala III retreated to Tiruvannamalai and fortified his northern frontier. The Hoysala state survived, but it was reduced to a frontier kingdom fighting for its existence.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1336": "As the Delhi Sultanate’s hold on the south weakened, Ballala III remained the senior Hindu ruler of the region, but new powers were rising: the Madurai Sultanate in the Tamil country and the brothers who founded Vijayanagara on the Tungabhadra. Hoysala resources were limited and its territory shrinking.",
  "Hoysala Empire|1343": "Veera Ballala III was captured and killed by the Madurai Sultan near Tiruchirappalli, and his body was displayed at Madurai. His son held out only briefly, and within a few years the remaining Hoysala territories were absorbed by the Vijayanagara Empire.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1190": "Jatavarman Kulasekara I began to rule at Madurai as the Pandyas emerged from long subordination to the Chola Empire. The kingdom held only the far southern Tamil country, around the Vaigai and Tamraparni valleys, and its independence was fragile in the face of a still powerful Chola monarchy.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1205": "The Chola king Kulottunga III invaded the Pandya country, captured Madurai and destroyed the Pandya coronation hall. The Pandyas were reduced again to vassal status, and the revival of their power seemed to have failed, leaving the kingdom at the lowest point of its later history.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1216": "Maravarman Sundara Pandya I came to the throne. An able commander, he took advantage of the Chola decline under Rajaraja III and rebuilt the kingdom’s forces. The Pandyas were again independent in the far south and preparing to turn the tables on their former overlords.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1219": "Maravarman Sundara Pandya I invaded the Chola country, burned Thanjavur and Uraiyur and held a victory coronation in the Chola heartland. Hoysala intervention restored Rajaraja III, but the Pandyas had become the leading Tamil power, with Chola rulers forced to acknowledge their strength.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1238": "Maravarman Sundara Pandya I died, and his successor faced the Hoysalas, who had established a base near Srirangam and supported the Cholas. The Pandyas held their own lands securely but were checked in the north, and their power remained regional rather than imperial.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1251": "Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I succeeded and quickly launched campaigns against the Cholas and the Hoysalas in the Kaveri delta. With an expanding army, wealthy ports and capable co-regents, the Pandyas began to move from regional power towards supremacy across south India.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1258": "Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I defeated the Chola king Rajendra III, pushed back the Hoysalas and campaigned in the north against the Telugu Chodas of Nellore and the Kakatiyas. Northern Sri Lanka paid tribute. Pandya armies were now active from the Krishna region to Jaffna.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1264": "Following the defeat and death of the Hoysala king Vira Someshvara around 1262, the Pandyas dominated the Tamil country, with the Cholas reduced to vassals and the Hoysalas pushed back to Karnataka. Gifts of gold to Srirangam and Chidambaram displayed a wealth and reach that marked the dynasty’s peak.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1268": "Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I succeeded Jatavarman Sundara Pandya I and began a reign of about four decades. The empire’s territory, tributaries and trade remained intact, and the system of co-regents allowed it to govern its wide domains, so its power stood close to the peak.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1279": "Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I defeated the last Chola king, Rajendra III, and the Hoysala Ramanatha. After this the Chola dynasty disappears from the record, and the Pandyas were the unchallenged masters of the Tamil country and the strongest power of the far south.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1284": "A Pandya army under Aryacakravarti invaded Sri Lanka, took Yapahuwa and carried off the Tooth Relic, which Parakramabahu III later recovered by negotiation. The expedition showed Pandya military reach across the sea, and the empire remained wealthy and dominant in south India.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1292": "When Marco Polo visited the coast of Ma’bar, he described rich pearl fisheries, a large horse trade and kings of great wealth. The empire was still prosperous and powerful, though the sharing of power among royal brothers and sons was creating tensions that would soon erupt.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1310": "Maravarman Kulasekara Pandya I was killed by his son Sundara Pandya, who resented the favour shown to his half-brother Vira Pandya. Civil war between the brothers divided the army and the nobility just as the Delhi Sultanate’s armies were advancing into the south.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1311": "Malik Kafur, general of Alauddin Khalji, marched into the Pandya country, plundered Madurai and other towns and carried away vast treasure. Delhi did not yet annex the region, but the raid and the continuing civil war left the empire impoverished and badly weakened.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1323": "An army of the Tughluq dynasty under Ulugh Khan conquered Madurai and made the Pandya lands the Delhi Sultanate’s province of Ma’bar. The Pandya dynasty lost its capital and heartland, surviving only as a group of minor princes in the far south of the Tamil country.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1335": "Jalal al-Din Ahsan Shah, Delhi’s governor of Ma’bar, rebelled and founded the Madurai Sultanate. The old Pandya capital remained in foreign hands, and the surviving Pandya princes in the Tirunelveli region held only small territories, paying little more than lip service to their former imperial titles.",
  "Later Pandyan Empire|1345": "By the mid-1340s the Pandya princes of the far south were minor local rulers, overshadowed by the Madurai Sultanate and the rising Vijayanagara Empire. The Later Pandyan Empire had ceased to exist as a significant state, though the dynasty survived later as the Tenkasi Pandyas.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1228": "Sukaphaa and his Tai followers crossed the Patkai hills into the upper Brahmaputra valley. They were a small migrant band among stronger neighbours, without a fixed capital, surviving by alliance and absorption of the local Moran and Borahi peoples. The future kingdom existed only as a warrior community.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1253": "Sukaphaa fixed his seat at Charaideo after years of moving between settlements. The Ahoms now held a compact territory between the Dikhou and Dihing rivers, practised wet-rice farming and had begun assimilating local communities, but they remained a minor power overshadowed by the Chutia and Kachari kingdoms.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1400": "Over the fourteenth century Sukaphaa\u0027s successors slowly extended the kingdom westwards and southwards along the river. Brahmin influence reached the court, and conflicts with the Chutias and Kacharis continued. The state was consolidating its institutions and population, yet still controlled only a modest part of the upper valley.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1497": "Suhungmung, later called the Dihingia Raja, came to the throne. He was the first Ahom king to take a Hindu title, Swarganarayan, and created the office of Borpatrogohain. His accession opened the period of rapid expansion that would make the Ahoms the leading power in the valley.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1523": "Suhungmung annexed the Chutia kingdom to the north-east, roughly doubling Ahom territory and gaining its population, craftsmen and arms. The conquest removed a long-standing rival and gave the kingdom secure control of the upper Brahmaputra valley, lifting its power well above its earlier level.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1536": "After defeating an invasion from the Bengal Sultanate in the early 1530s, Suhungmung\u0027s forces sacked the Kachari capital at Dimapur, pushing the Kacharis south. The Ahoms now dominated most of the Brahmaputra valley east of Kamrup and had proved able to defeat a lowland Muslim army.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1603": "Pratap Singha\u0027s accession began a long reign of institutional reform. The paik system was reorganised into specialised khels, and the offices of Borbarua and Borphukan were created. The kingdom now bordered the expanding Mughal Empire in Kamrup, setting the stage for decades of frontier war.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1639": "After more than twenty years of war with the Mughals, the Treaty of Asurar Ali fixed a frontier between the two powers. The Ahoms had held the Mughal advance and kept their heartland intact, and Pratap Singha\u0027s reforms gave the state a stronger administrative and military structure than ever before.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1662": "Mir Jumla, the Mughal governor of Bengal, invaded with a large army and flotilla, occupied the capital Garhgaon and forced King Jayadhwaj Singha into the hills. The humiliating Treaty of Ghilajharighat in 1663 imposed tribute and territorial loss, the lowest point of Ahom fortunes in the seventeenth century.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1671": "Lachit Borphukan\u0027s victory over Ram Singh I of Amber at the Battle of Saraighat, fought on the Brahmaputra near Guwahati, ended the Mughal attempt to reconquer the lower valley. The Ahoms had recovered from the disaster of 1662 and demonstrated the strength of their river fleet and fortifications.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1682": "After a decade of court instability in which ministers made and deposed kings, Gadadhar Singha restored royal authority and expelled the Mughals from Guwahati for good. The western frontier was fixed at the Manas River, and the kingdom now controlled the whole Brahmaputra valley to that line.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1696": "Rudra Singha succeeded his father Gadadhar Singha and inherited a secure and well-organised kingdom. He extended Ahom influence over the Kachari and Jaintia kingdoms, built at Rangpur and patronised Hindu religion and learning, bringing the state close to the peak of its power and prestige.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1714": "Rudra Singha died at the height of Ahom power, reportedly while preparing a campaign against Mughal Bengal. The kingdom held the whole valley, received the submission of neighbouring hill states and had a functioning paik administration. This was the peak of its territorial control, wealth and regional standing.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1744": "Siva Singha\u0027s long reign, during which his queens exercised real power, ended. The kingdom remained prosperous and stable, with large temples and tanks built around Sivasagar, but royal favour for Shaktism was creating tensions with the Vaishnavite satras that would later undermine the state.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1769": "The Moamoria rebellion broke out after years of official persecution of the Moamaria satra. Its followers seized the capital Rangpur, captured King Lakshmi Singha and held power for several months. Although royal forces eventually recovered it, the revolt exposed deep social grievances and weakened the paik system.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1788": "A second and more destructive wave of the Moamoria rebellion drove King Gaurinath Singha from his capital to Guwahati. Large areas of Upper Assam were lost to rebels and depopulated, and the kingdom\u0027s administrative and military system was close to collapse, forcing the king to seek outside help.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1794": "Captain Thomas Welsh\u0027s East India Company expedition, sent at Gaurinath Singha\u0027s request in 1792, restored royal control over much of the valley before being recalled in 1794. Gaurinath moved the capital to Jorhat. The kingdom survived, but now depended on others for its security.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1810": "Under the minister Purnananda Burhagohain the Ahom state was partly restored and the last Moamoria resistance suppressed. Yet the population had fallen sharply, kings were weak, and power lay with ministerial factions whose rivalries soon led a disgraced Borphukan to seek Burmese intervention.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1817": "Badan Chandra Borphukan returned with a Burmese army sent by the Konbaung court. The invasion overturned the government, and from this point Ahom kings were made and unmade by Burmese commanders and rival ministers. The kingdom retained its name but had lost real independence.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1821": "A further Burmese invasion drove out King Chandrakanta Singha and turned Assam into a Burmese-ruled province. The occupation brought killings, enslavement and mass flight, devastating the population. Ahom claimants survived only as exiles or fugitives seeking help in British territory.",
  "Ahom kingdom|1826": "British forces expelled the Burmese during the First Anglo-Burmese War, and by the Treaty of Yandabo Burma renounced Assam. The valley passed under the East India Company. A tributary Ahom ruler was briefly installed in Upper Assam in 1833, but the kingdom as an independent state had ended.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1238": "Around this conventional date the Tai chiefs Bang Klang Hao and Pha Mueang expelled the Khmer governor, and Bang Klang Hao became king as Si Inthrathit. The new state controlled only Sukhothai and nearby towns and was one of several small Tai principalities emerging as Khmer authority receded.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1260": "Si Inthrathit\u0027s kingdom consolidated its hold on Sukhothai and Si Satchanalai and defended itself against neighbouring rulers such as the lord of Mueang Chot. It remained a small regional power, but its walled twin cities and farming base gave it a secure core for later expansion.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1279": "Ram Khamhaeng probably came to the throne around this year after his brother Ban Mueang. Already famous for defeating Khun Sam Chon on elephant-back, he began extending Sukhothai\u0027s overlordship over neighbouring towns through war, alliance and marriage, lifting the kingdom from a local principality to a regional power.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1287": "Ram Khamhaeng allied with Mangrai of the future Lan Na and Ngam Mueang of Phayao, and according to tradition supported the Mon ruler Wareru in Lower Burma. With the Mongols weakening Pagan and the Khmer in retreat, Sukhothai\u0027s network of tributaries was expanding rapidly in several directions.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1292": "The inscription attributed to Ram Khamhaeng, dated to this year, lists towns reaching to Nakhon Si Thammarat in the south and the Mekong in the east, and around this time Sukhothai began sending embassies to the Yuan court. Though based on loose tribute, this was the peak of its reach and prestige.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1298": "Ram Khamhaeng died around this time, and his overlordship, which rested on personal authority, began to unravel. Distant tributaries stopped sending tribute, and his son Loe Thai inherited a kingdom still respected and wealthy but shrinking towards its core around the Yom valley.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1320": "Under Loe Thai Sukhothai lost most of the dependencies Ram Khamhaeng had gathered, and powers such as the Mon kingdom and the Tai principalities of the north went their own way. The kingdom continued its embassies to China and its Buddhist patronage but carried far less political weight.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1347": "Li Thai seized the throne in Sukhothai after a period of disorder following Loe Thai\u0027s death. He inherited a reduced kingdom surrounded by stronger neighbours, but he was a scholar-king who would use Buddhism and diplomacy to rebuild royal authority and the kingdom\u0027s standing.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1360": "Li Thai\u0027s reign was a time of religious and political revival. He invited a senior monk from Sri Lanka, sponsored temples and Buddha images, and the Traiphum cosmology attributed to him spread a Buddhist ideal of kingship. He also restored control over some northern towns, partly governing from Phitsanulok.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1368": "Li Thai died around this time, and the revival he had led lost momentum. The newly founded Ayutthaya kingdom in the south, rich from maritime trade, was growing quickly and now had the strength to challenge Sukhothai for leadership of the Chao Phraya basin.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1371": "Borommaracha I of Ayutthaya began a series of northern campaigns, attacking the Sukhothai towns and besieging Chakangrao, today Kamphaeng Phet. Sukhothai resisted for several years but was clearly the weaker power, unable to match Ayutthaya\u0027s resources or protect its outlying towns.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1378": "Maha Thammaracha II submitted to Borommaracha I and became a vassal of Ayutthaya. Sukhothai kept its dynasty and its own administration but lost independence in foreign affairs and war. From now on its power was that of a subordinate principality rather than a sovereign kingdom.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1400": "Sukhothai\u0027s kings ruled the northern towns as vassals, sometimes attempting to recover their freedom when Ayutthaya was distracted. Maha Thammaracha III\u0027s reign saw tensions with the overlord, but the kingdom had neither the army nor the allies to break away, and its centre was shifting to Phitsanulok.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1419": "A succession dispute broke out after Maha Thammaracha III\u0027s death, and King Intharacha of Ayutthaya intervened to settle it in favour of his preferred candidate, who became Maha Thammaracha IV. The episode showed that Sukhothai\u0027s throne was now in Ayutthaya\u0027s gift and its autonomy almost gone.",
  "Sukhothai Kingdom|1438": "On the death of Maha Thammaracha IV, Borommaracha II of Ayutthaya sent his son, the future Borommatrailokkanat, to govern Phitsanulok. The Phra Ruang line ended as a ruling house, and Sukhothai was absorbed as Ayutthaya\u0027s northern province, ending its existence as a separate kingdom.",
  "Lan Na|1292": "Mangrai, ruler of Ngoenyang, conquered the Mon kingdom of Hariphunchai at Lamphun, according to the most commonly cited date. Uniting the upper Mekong principalities with the rich Ping valley, he created a new kingdom of moderate size, still consolidating its conquests and its relations with neighbouring Tai rulers.",
  "Lan Na|1296": "Mangrai founded Chiang Mai on the Ping River, traditionally with the advice of his allies Ngam Mueang of Phayao and Ram Khamhaeng of Sukhothai. The new walled capital gave Lan Na a permanent centre, and the kingdom soon withstood Mongol Yuan expeditions sent against it in the early fourteenth century.",
  "Lan Na|1311": "Mangrai died around this time, leaving a kingdom that held the main valleys of what is now northern Thailand. His successors faced succession disputes, but the dynasty he founded would rule for over two centuries, and the institutions and law attributed to him gave the state lasting coherence.",
  "Lan Na|1338": "Lan Na absorbed the neighbouring principality of Phayao, extending its control eastwards. Kings now resided mainly at Chiang Mai, and the kingdom was a stable regional power in the northern hills, though it had not yet developed the religious and cultural prominence of its fifteenth-century golden age.",
  "Lan Na|1369": "King Kue Na invited the monk Sumana from Sukhothai, who established a Sri Lankan-derived ordination lineage at Wat Suan Dok. This marked the beginning of Lan Na\u0027s rise as a centre of Theravada Buddhism and strengthened royal prestige through patronage of the monastic order and relic cults.",
  "Lan Na|1441": "Tilokarat seized the throne from his father, Sam Fang Kaen. An energetic and ambitious ruler, he began a reign of almost half a century that would see the kingdom\u0027s greatest military expansion and the high point of its religious patronage, lifting Lan Na to the peak of its power.",
  "Lan Na|1449": "Tilokarat brought the principalities of Nan and Phrae under the direct control of Chiang Mai, enlarging the kingdom to the south-east. Soon afterwards war broke out with Ayutthaya over the northern borderlands. Lan Na was now strong enough to challenge the leading power of the Chao Phraya basin.",
  "Lan Na|1477": "Tilokarat convened a council of monks at Wat Chet Yot to revise the Pali canon, counted in Thai tradition as the Eighth Buddhist Council. With the war against Ayutthaya held to a standstill, Nan and Phrae secured and the Emerald Buddha in Chiang Mai, Lan Na stood at its peak.",
  "Lan Na|1495": "Phra Mueang Kaeo came to the throne. His reign was the golden age of Lan Na Pali scholarship, during which works such as the Jinakalamali were composed. Politically the kingdom remained strong, though it fought further wars with Ayutthaya and could no longer expand its borders.",
  "Lan Na|1525": "Phra Mueang Kaeo\u0027s death ended the last strong reign of the Mangrai dynasty. His successors faced rebellious nobles and rival factions, and royal authority began to erode. The kingdom remained wealthy and culturally prominent, but its political cohesion was weakening as court intrigue intensified.",
  "Lan Na|1545": "King Ket Klao was killed amid court factionalism, and the nobles turned to the Lan Xang prince Setthathirath, who soon left to claim his father\u0027s throne. With repeated changes of ruler, interventions by neighbours and a divided nobility, Lan Na had lost most of its former strength.",
  "Lan Na|1558": "Bayinnaung of the First Toungoo Empire captured Chiang Mai almost without resistance. Lan Na became a Burmese vassal, its king Mekuti kept on the throne at first but deposed in 1564. The kingdom\u0027s independence was lost, and its resources were drawn into Burmese wars against Ayutthaya and Lan Xang.",
  "Lan Na|1579": "Bayinnaung appointed his son Nawrahta Minsaw to rule Chiang Mai, replacing the native vassal line. Lan Na was now governed as a Burmese dependency, its armies and taxes serving Burmese campaigns, while its nobles kept local authority in the outlying mueang. The kingdom existed only as a province.",
  "Lan Na|1614": "After the collapse of the First Toungoo Empire and a period of instability in which Ayutthaya also intervened, the Restored Toungoo kingdom under Anaukpetlun reasserted Burmese control over Chiang Mai. Lan Na remained a subordinate territory for more than a century, with little independent political weight.",
  "Lan Na|1727": "A revolt in Chiang Mai expelled the Burmese governor as the Restored Toungoo dynasty weakened. Chiang Mai regained independence under local rulers for several decades, although Lan Na was now divided among rival centres such as Lampang and could not recover its former unity or regional influence.",
  "Lan Na|1763": "The new Konbaung dynasty of Burma reconquered Chiang Mai and made Lan Na a base for its invasion of Ayutthaya, which fell in 1767. Harsh Burmese administration and demands for troops and supplies alienated the northern lords, who began to look to the rising Thonburi kingdom for help.",
  "Lan Na|1775": "Thonburi forces under Chao Phraya Chakri, joined by the northern lords Kawila and Phraya Chaban, took Chiang Mai from the Burmese. Lan Na\u0027s principalities passed under Siamese suzerainty as vassals, and the old kingdom gave way to the Siamese-protected states that later formed the Kingdom of Chiang Mai.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1336": "Harihara I and Bukka I of the Sangama family established a kingdom on the Tungabhadra River, traditionally in this year, as Delhi Sultanate authority in the south collapsed. The new state was a small regional power, one of several successor kingdoms competing for the lands left by the Tughluq retreat.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1346": "After the Hoysala king Veera Ballala III was killed fighting the Madurai Sultanate in 1343, the Sangama brothers absorbed the Hoysala lands. Tradition holds that they celebrated their victories at Sringeri around this time. Vijayanagara now controlled most of the Kannada country and was the leading Hindu power of the south.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1378": "Kumara Kampana\u0027s campaigns in the Tamil country, begun in the early 1370s, destroyed the Madurai Sultanate. With the Tamil lands added, Vijayanagara ruled almost all of India south of the Tungabhadra and Krishna, though it remained locked in costly wars with the Bahmani Sultanate over the Raichur Doab.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1406": "Under Harihara II and his successors the empire consolidated its southern conquests, while the war with the Bahmanis continued with varying fortunes. Deva Raya I suffered a defeat by Firuz Shah Bahmani around this time and had to make peace on unfavourable terms, but the empire remained large and wealthy.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1424": "Deva Raya II came to the throne and began reforming the army, recruiting Muslim cavalry and archers to match his Deccani rivals. His reign brought renewed expansion along the coasts and southwards, and the capital grew in size and splendour, attracting merchants and foreign visitors.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1443": "The Persian envoy Abdur Razzaq visited the capital and described its fortifications, markets and court, presenting it as one of the greatest cities he had seen. Deva Raya II\u0027s power, wealth and reach made this the high point of the Sangama dynasty, though the frontier with the Bahmanis was still contested.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1465": "After Deva Raya II\u0027s death in 1446 the Sangama dynasty weakened under a series of less able kings. The Gajapati rulers of Odisha advanced into the eastern coast, and the Bahmani Sultanate pressed on the west, taking the port of Goa in the early 1470s. The empire lost territory and central authority declined.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1485": "The general Saluva Narasimha seized the throne from the last weak Sangama king, founding the Saluva dynasty. He fought to recover lost territory and restore order, but the empire remained weaker than in its fifteenth-century prime, and power soon passed to the Tuluva general Narasa Nayaka as regent.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1509": "Krishnadevaraya of the Tuluva dynasty came to the throne. Inheriting a recovering empire with a strong army and good relations with the Portuguese, newly established on the western coast, he began a series of campaigns that would raise Vijayanagara to the height of its power within a decade.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1520": "Krishnadevaraya defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the Battle of Raichur, securing the Raichur Doab, after earlier campaigns against the Gajapatis had won the eastern coast. The Portuguese visitor Domingo Paes described the capital\u0027s wealth at about this time. This was the empire\u0027s peak.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1529": "Krishnadevaraya died, leaving the empire at its largest extent and its court famous for its literature and building. His half-brother Achyuta Deva Raya succeeded him and preserved most of his conquests, though rivalries among the nobility and pressure from the Deccan sultanates grew.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1542": "After Achyuta Deva Raya\u0027s death the young Sadashiva Raya was placed on the throne, and real power passed to the regent Aliya Rama Raya. He played the Deccan sultanates against one another and kept the empire strong and feared, but his interventions eventually united them against him.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1565": "At the Battle of Talikota the combined armies of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar defeated the Vijayanagara army and killed Rama Raya. The capital was sacked and abandoned. The empire lost its centre, its treasury was moved away, and its power collapsed to a fraction of its former level.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1570": "Rama Raya\u0027s brother Tirumala, who had moved the court to Penukonda, took the throne around this time and founded the Aravidu dynasty. The empire survived as a large but weakened state, with the great nayakas of the south increasingly acting on their own and the Deccan sultanates pressing from the north.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1586": "Venkatapati Raya II came to the throne and governed effectively for nearly three decades, eventually ruling from Chandragiri. He resisted the Deccan sultanates and restored some royal prestige, but he could not recover the lost northern lands or fully control the powerful nayakas of Madurai, Thanjavur and Gingee.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1614": "Venkatapati Raya II\u0027s death led to a disputed succession and civil war among the nobility, in which rival factions massacred much of the royal family. The nayakas and the Wodeyars of Mysore, now ruling from Srirangapatna, behaved as independent princes, and royal authority shrank to the territories around Chandragiri and Vellore.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1630": "The Aravidu kings held only a reduced realm in the south-east, with nominal overlordship over the nayakas. Bijapur and Golconda, freed from Mughal pressure by treaties in 1636, prepared to advance south. The empire survived mainly as a source of legitimacy for the nayakas rather than as a power.",
  "Vijayanagara Empire|1646": "Sriranga III, who had tried to reassert royal authority, was defeated by the armies of Bijapur and Golconda and abandoned by his nayakas. By about this year he had lost his remaining territories and became a fugitive, and the Vijayanagara Empire ceased to exist as a state.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1347": "The Deccan nobles\u0027 revolt against Muhammad bin Tughluq succeeded, and Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah was proclaimed sultan at Daulatabad before moving to Gulbarga. The new kingdom was still defending itself against Delhi and consolidating control over a large but loosely held territory across the northern Deccan.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1358": "Bahman Shah died, having secured independence from Delhi and a kingdom from the Arabian Sea coast to Telangana. His son Muhammad Shah I inherited a stable state and began organising it into provinces, while preparing for the wars against Vijayanagara and the Telugu chiefs that would define his reign.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1375": "Muhammad Shah I died after a reign of administrative organisation and war. He had divided the kingdom into four provinces, built the great mosque at Gulbarga and received Golconda and a turquoise throne from Kapaya Nayaka. The sultanate was firmly established as the leading power of the Deccan.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1397": "Firuz Shah came to the throne after a period of short reigns and court turmoil. A learned ruler who welcomed scholars and recruited foreigners into his service, he campaigned successfully against Vijayanagara in the following years and maintained the sultanate\u0027s strength, though without decisive territorial gains in the south.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1420": "Firuz Shah suffered a heavy defeat by Vijayanagara at Pangal, losing territory and prestige. His brother Ahmad soon forced him to abdicate. The setback showed the limits of Bahmani power in the long contest for the Raichur Doab, although the kingdom\u0027s core remained intact.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1425": "Ahmad Shah I, after avenging the defeat of 1420 with campaigns against Vijayanagara, overran Warangal in the east and extended Bahmani control into Telangana. Within a few years he moved the capital to Bidar. The sultanate was larger and more secure than before, entering a period of strength.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1447": "Factional conflict between Deccani and Afaqi nobles came to a head when Afaqi soldiers were massacred after a defeat at Chakan. Under Alauddin Ahmad Shah II the kingdom remained large and wealthy, but such rivalries would increasingly weaken the court and the loyalty of the army.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1461": "After the brief and harsh reign of Humayun Shah, a child sultan, Nizam Shah, came to the throne. Mahmud Khalji of Malwa invaded and besieged Bidar, and the kingdom survived only with help from Gujarat. The regency of the queen mother and Mahmud Gawan held the state together.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1463": "The young Muhammad Shah III succeeded his brother, and Mahmud Gawan became the dominant minister. The kingdom had recovered from the Malwa invasion and began a new phase of expansion and reform under Gawan\u0027s leadership, aimed at the western coast and at strengthening central control over the provinces.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1472": "Mahmud Gawan\u0027s armies conquered the Konkan coast and took Goa from Vijayanagara, giving the sultanate its widest reach from the Arabian Sea towards the Bay of Bengal. His madrasa at Bidar was completed, and his reforms strengthened the crown. This was the height of Bahmani power and wealth.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1481": "Deccani nobles forged a letter implicating Mahmud Gawan in treason, and Muhammad Shah III had him executed. The kingdom was still large, but it lost its ablest administrator, and the provincial governors, freed from his reforms, began to act independently. The sultan himself died the following year.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1490": "Around this year Yusuf Adil Khan at Bijapur, Ahmad Nizam ul-Mulk at Ahmadnagar and Fathullah Imad ul-Mulk in Berar effectively declared independence. The sultan Mahmud Shah controlled little beyond Bidar, where real power belonged to Qasim Barid, and the kingdom had fragmented into rival states.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1518": "Mahmud Shah died, and Quli Qutb ul-Mulk was ruling Golconda independently. The Bahmani sultans were now puppets of Amir Barid at Bidar, with no army or territory of their own, while Vijayanagara prepared to exploit their weakness by taking the Raichur Doab two years later.",
  "Bahmani Sultanate|1527": "The last Bahmani sultan, Kalimullah, fled Bidar around this year and sought help from Babur and from rival Deccan sultans, dying in exile. The Barid Shahis ruled Bidar in their own name, and the kingdom was fully replaced by its five successor sultanates.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1351": "U Thong, ruling as Ramathibodi I, founded Ayutthaya on its river island. The new kingdom inherited the Khmer-influenced traditions of Lavo and the manpower of Suphannaphum, and controlled rich rice land with access to the sea, but it was still one central-plain state among several rivals.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1378": "Ayutthayan campaigns forced the kingdom of Sukhothai to accept tributary status. Control over Sukhothai\u0027s lands in the north gave Ayutthaya clear leadership among the Tai states of the Chao Phraya basin, although rivalry between the Suphannaphum and Lavo royal houses still unsettled the succession.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1431": "Armies under Borommarachathirat II attacked and took Angkor, traditionally in 1431. Although no lasting rule over Cambodia followed, the blow hastened the Khmer court\u0027s move south and left Ayutthaya the leading power of the region, now drawing Khmer specialists and court practices into its capital.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1438": "On the death of the last ruler of the Sukhothai line, Ayutthaya absorbed Sukhothai, placing it under a prince of its own house. The kingdom now controlled most of the central and lower northern plain, a territorial base far larger than that of any rival Tai state in the region.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1488": "The death of Borommatrailokkanat closed a reign that is credited with the Kalahom and Mahatthai, the four ministries and the sakdina ranks. Long wars with Lan Na had brought little territory, but the kingdom now had durable institutions for mobilising manpower and governing its provinces.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1548": "Tabinshwehti of Toungoo besieged Ayutthaya but failed to take it, a campaign remembered for Queen Suriyothai\u0027s death in combat. The kingdom kept its independence and prosperity, yet the war revealed that a reunited Burma could now strike at the capital itself.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1564": "Bayinnaung\u0027s invasion forced Ayutthaya to submit, surrender royal white elephants and send princes as hostages. The kingdom kept its own kings but lost freedom of action, and its manpower and prestige were sharply reduced by defeat at the hands of Burma.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1569": "After a long siege Bayinnaung took Ayutthaya. The city was plundered, many inhabitants were deported, and Maha Thammarachathirat ruled as a Burmese vassal. Cambodian raids exploited the weakness; this was the lowest point of the kingdom before 1767, with a functioning court but no independence.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1584": "Prince Naresuan renounced allegiance to Burma and began a war of independence. Ayutthaya\u0027s armies were still small and its frontiers exposed, but Burmese attempts to restore control failed, and the kingdom recovered both independence and the confidence of its nobility.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1593": "At the Battle of Nong Sarai Naresuan defeated a major Burmese invasion and killed the crown prince Mingyi Swa. The victory ended the immediate threat from Burma and opened the way for Siamese campaigns against Cambodia and into lower Burma, restoring the kingdom as a regional power.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1605": "Naresuan died on campaign after a reign that had restored and extended Ayutthaya\u0027s power, from Cambodia to the Tenasserim coast. His brother Ekathotsarot inherited a strong kingdom whose ports were beginning to attract Dutch, Japanese and other merchants in growing numbers.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1629": "Prasat Thong seized the throne in a palace coup and later burned the Japanese quarter whose soldiers had backed his rivals. The usurpation showed the violence of succession politics, but trade revenues continued to grow and the new king built Wat Chaiwatthanaram as a statement of royal power.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1656": "Narai took the throne after another succession struggle. He inherited a wealthy commercial kingdom with Dutch, English, Chinese and Persian merchants in its quarters, and he used trade monopolies and foreign advisers to strengthen royal revenue against both the nobility and the Dutch East India Company.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1685": "Under Narai and his minister Constantine Phaulkon Ayutthaya reached its height of wealth and international standing: royal trade spanned Asia, the French embassy of the Chevalier de Chaumont arrived, and Siamese envoys were soon to be received at Versailles. Few Asian courts were better known in Europe.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1688": "As Narai lay dying, Phetracha seized power, executed Phaulkon and expelled the French garrison from Bangkok. The kingdom did not close itself to foreigners, but European influence at court ended and the new dynasty faced revolts, so Ayutthaya\u0027s power stood below its peak under Narai.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1758": "The death of Borommakot ended a long reign of stability, prosperity and Buddhist revival. His succession was disputed between Uthumphon and Ekkathat, and the court was weakened by factions just as the new Konbaung dynasty was uniting Burma for war with Siam.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1766": "Two Konbaung armies sent by Hsinbyushin in 1765 had converged on the capital in January 1766 and kept it under siege through the rainy season. Provincial forces failed to relieve the city, and Ayutthaya was reduced to a besieged capital whose fall was only a matter of time.",
  "Ayutthaya Kingdom|1767": "In April 1767 Burmese troops breached the walls, plundered and burned Ayutthaya and carried off thousands of captives. King Ekkathat died and the kingdom ceased to exist; Siam broke into rival regimes until Taksin reunited it from Thonburi later that year.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1352": "Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah, already master of Lakhnauti and Satgaon, took Sonargaon and united the delta under one ruler. The new state controlled Bengal\u0027s rice lands and ports, but its independence was not yet accepted by the Delhi Sultanate, which was preparing to reconquer the province.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1354": "Firuz Shah Tughluq\u0027s invasion ended when the Delhi army withdrew after besieging Ilyas Shah in the fort of Ekdala. The sultan lost no lasting ground, and having survived the strongest army in India, his new kingdom gained confidence and prestige among its neighbours.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1359": "A second invasion from Delhi against Sikandar Shah ended in a negotiated peace. Delhi thereafter accepted Bengal\u0027s independence, and Sikandar Shah began the great Adina Mosque at Pandua, a statement of the dynasty\u0027s wealth and its claims to sovereign status.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1390": "Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah came to the throne and ruled a prosperous, secure kingdom. He exchanged embassies with the Ming court and is remembered in tradition for inviting the poet Hafez, signs of a sultanate now known across Asia as a wealthy maritime state.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1414": "Around this time the Hindu landholder Raja Ganesha seized power from the Ilyas Shahis. According to the chronicles, Ibrahim Shah of Jaunpur threatened intervention, and the crisis divided the nobility, so Bengal\u0027s power was temporarily reduced by internal conflict over the throne.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1418": "Ganesha\u0027s son Jadu, converted to Islam as Jalaluddin Muhammad Shah, secured the throne. His reign restored stability, extended influence towards Arakan and Chittagong, and combined Islamic kingship with local Bengali traditions, so the sultanate recovered much of its earlier standing.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1435": "The Ilyas Shahi dynasty was restored under Nasiruddin Mahmud Shah after the end of Ganesha\u0027s line. The kingdom enjoyed a long period of peace and building activity, with Gaur again becoming the main capital, though its expansion beyond the delta remained limited.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1487": "Abyssinian palace guards killed Jalaluddin Fath Shah and seized the throne. During the following Habshi period several sultans were murdered in quick succession, provincial control slackened and the court was dominated by faction, leaving Bengal weaker than at any time since unification.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1494": "The nobles raised Alauddin Husain Shah to the throne. He disbanded the Abyssinian guard, restored order at Gaur and began to rebuild the army and administration, giving the sultanate a firm base for the expansion that marked his reign and the twenty-five years of stability that followed.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1498": "Husain Shah conquered the kingdom of Kamata in the north, ending its ruling dynasty. Further campaigns into Orissa, Tripura and Assam followed, and Bengal became the leading power of eastern India, although the attempt to subdue the Ahom kingdom did not succeed.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1512": "At the height of Husain Shah\u0027s reign Bengal controlled the whole delta, Kamata, Chittagong and frontier lands towards Orissa and Bihar. Its trade flourished, its court patronised Bengali and Persian letters, and its ministers included Rupa and Sanatana, marking the sultanate\u0027s peak.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1519": "Husain Shah died and his son Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah succeeded peacefully. The kingdom remained rich and powerful, and Nusrat Shah built the Bara Sona Mosque at Gaur, but the rise of the Mughals in the north was about to change the balance of power.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1529": "Babur defeated a Bengal army at the Battle of the Ghaghra and a peace was made. Bengal kept its territory and independence, but the defeat showed that its elephants and river forces were no match for the new Mughal and Afghan armies of Hindustan.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1534": "Sher Khan Sur defeated the sultan\u0027s forces at Surajgarh. Ghiyasuddin Mahmud Shah, weakened by rebellions and disputes over the succession, could no longer protect his western frontier in Bihar, and the Afghans pressed steadily towards Gaur, despite the help he received from Portuguese mercenaries.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1538": "Sher Khan took Gaur, and the last Husain Shahi sultan fled and died soon after. Bengal briefly passed to the Mughal emperor Humayun and then became a province of the Sur Empire, so the independent sultanate effectively lapsed for about fifteen years under Afghan governors.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1555": "As the Sur Empire broke apart after the death of Islam Shah Sur, its governor of Bengal declared independence as Shamsuddin Muhammad Shah Ghazi. A revived sultanate emerged under him and his successors, but it was smaller, poorer in allies and less secure than the Husain Shahi kingdom had been.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1568": "Sulaiman Khan Karrani, whose family had taken Gaur in 1564, conquered Orissa and governed a large kingdom from Tanda. By outwardly acknowledging Akbar as overlord he avoided war with the Mughals, and the Afghan sultanate briefly regained much of the territory and power of earlier Bengal dynasties.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1575": "Daud Khan Karrani defied Akbar and was defeated by Mughal forces at the Battle of Tukaroi. A treaty left him only part of his lands, and the Afghan state, short of allies and divided among its commanders, was reduced to a fragile remnant.",
  "Bengal Sultanate|1576": "Daud Khan renewed the war and was captured and executed after the Battle of Rajmahal. Bengal became a Mughal province, ending the sultanate, although the Baro-Bhuyans led by Isa Khan continued to resist Mughal rule in the eastern delta for decades.",
  "Lan Xang|1353": "Fa Ngum took Xieng Dong Xieng Thong and was crowned king of Lan Xang, after campaigns that had brought many Lao principalities under his control. The new kingdom was large but loosely held together, depending on the personal authority of its founder and on Khmer support.",
  "Lan Xang|1373": "The nobility deposed Fa Ngum, whose harsh rule had alienated them, and raised his son Oun Heuan to the throne. As Samsenthai he began a long, peaceful reign that consolidated the kingdom, organised its manpower and strengthened Buddhism at court.",
  "Lan Xang|1416": "Samsenthai died after more than four decades of rule, leaving a stable and prosperous kingdom with established relations with Ayutthaya, Lan Na and the Ming court. A disputed succession followed, with several short reigns, but the realm built by Fa Ngum and his son held together.",
  "Lan Xang|1479": "A large Vietnamese army sent by Lê Thánh Tông invaded Lan Xang and sacked its capital. The king fled and the kingdom was reduced to its lowest point since its foundation, although the invaders suffered heavy losses and could not hold the country.",
  "Lan Xang|1485": "Lao forces had recovered the capital and the Vietnamese had withdrawn. The kingdom was rebuilt under new kings, including Visoun, who later installed the Phra Bang image in the capital, but it took some decades to recover its earlier strength.",
  "Lan Xang|1520": "Photisarath came to the throne. He promoted Buddhism, banned spirit cults in 1527, married a princess of Lan Na and spent long periods at Vientiane. His reign strengthened the kingdom and drew it into the politics of Lan Na, raising its regional standing.",
  "Lan Xang|1548": "Setthathirath, who had briefly ruled Lan Na, returned to succeed his father and in 1552 brought the Emerald Buddha from Chiang Mai. He ruled a kingdom with ties to Lan Na and Ayutthaya and a strong Buddhist court, though the Burmese threat was growing to the west.",
  "Lan Xang|1560": "Setthathirath moved the capital to Vientiane, allied with Ayutthaya against Burma and began an ambitious building programme, from Wat Xieng Thong to the Haw Phra Kaew and later That Luang. Lan Xang stood near the height of its power and prestige.",
  "Lan Xang|1565": "Bayinnaung\u0027s army took Vientiane and carried off many captives to Burma. Setthathirath escaped and fought a guerrilla war that eventually forced the Burmese to withdraw, but the kingdom had been badly shaken and its alliance with Ayutthaya could no longer protect it.",
  "Lan Xang|1571": "Setthathirath disappeared during a campaign in the south, probably killed. His death left the kingdom without a strong ruler, and rival factions contested power just as Burma, now master of Ayutthaya, prepared to impose its overlordship on the Lao lands.",
  "Lan Xang|1574": "Burmese armies took Vientiane and installed Voravongsa as a vassal king. For most of the following two decades Lan Xang was a Burmese dependency, paying tribute, supplying troops and suffering from repeated changes of ruler, factional struggles and the disruption of war.",
  "Lan Xang|1591": "Nokeo Koumane, a son of Setthathirath who had been held in Burma, was restored and soon threw off Burmese overlordship. The kingdom regained its independence but remained politically unstable, with short-lived kings, contested successions and noble rivalries in the following decades.",
  "Lan Xang|1638": "Sourigna Vongsa took the throne after a succession struggle and began a reign of more than fifty years. He restored strong royal authority, kept peace with Vietnam and Ayutthaya, and opened Lan Xang\u0027s longest period of internal stability, Buddhist patronage and commercial prosperity.",
  "Lan Xang|1650": "Lan Xang was at its height under Sourigna Vongsa. Vientiane impressed the Dutch visitor van Wuysthoff and the Jesuit Leria, the kingdom traded forest products widely, Buddhist learning flourished, and its frontiers and tributaries were more secure than at any earlier time.",
  "Lan Xang|1695": "Sourigna Vongsa died in 1694 or 1695 without a surviving son, having, according to tradition, executed his heir. The kingdom was still prosperous, but there was no agreed successor, and a high official soon seized the throne, opening a period of usurpations.",
  "Lan Xang|1700": "Sai Ong Hue, a nephew of Sourigna Vongsa, took Vientiane with Vietnamese support, while Sourigna Vongsa\u0027s grandsons gathered followers in the north. The kingdom was effectively divided between rival claimants, and foreign backing, especially from Vietnam and Ayutthaya, had become decisive in Lao politics.",
  "Lan Xang|1707": "Kingkitsarat established himself as king at Luang Prabang, formalising the division into the kingdoms of Luang Phrabang and Vientiane. Lan Xang ceased to exist as a united state, and the southern provinces broke away as the Kingdom of Champasak in 1713, completing the partition.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1368": "Brunei tradition places the conversion of Awang Alak Betatar, who became Sultan Muhammad Shah, in the 1360s. The polity on the Brunei River was a modest trading port that had recently appeared among Majapahit\u0027s claimed dependencies, with influence over nearby coasts but no wider empire.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1408": "A king of Po-ni, Maharaja Karna, travelled to the Ming court at Nanjing and died there; his tomb survives. The Ming connection brought prestige, protection from Javanese claims and access to Chinese trade, raising Brunei\u0027s standing among the ports of the region.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1430": "By about this time, according to tradition, Sharif Ali ruled as third sultan, building a mosque and strengthening Islamic law. Brunei was becoming an established Muslim port linked to Malacca and the Muslim trading world, though its territorial reach remained limited to nearby coasts.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1485": "Sultan Bolkiah, traditionally dated from about this time, began the expeditions remembered in Brunei tradition. Brunei\u0027s fleets extended its influence along the coasts of Borneo and towards Sulu and Luzon, and its wealth grew with the trade in camphor, pepper and Chinese goods.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1511": "The Portuguese conquest of Malacca drove Muslim merchants to look for other ports, and Brunei gained a larger share of regional trade. Its links with Manila and other Philippine ports made it the leading Muslim commercial power in the eastern archipelago.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1521": "Pigafetta\u0027s visit captured Brunei near its peak: a large water city, a wealthy court with elephants and a ruler with allies and dependants from Borneo to Luzon. Brunei\u0027s political and commercial reach over the eastern archipelago was at its greatest extent.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1571": "Spanish forces under Legazpi took Manila from rulers connected to Brunei. The sultanate lost its northern outpost and its influence in Luzon, although it still controlled most of the coast of northern Borneo and remained a rich trading state with a strong fleet.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1578": "In the Castilian War a Spanish expedition captured the capital and the sultan fled inland. Disease forced the Spanish to withdraw within months, and Brunei survived, but the attack ended its ambitions in the Philippines and showed its vulnerability to European arms.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1660": "A civil war broke out between rival claimants to the throne, Abdul Hakkul Mubin and Muhyiddin. The conflict divided the nobility, interrupted trade and invited Sulu intervention, weakening a sultanate whose power already depended on balancing great families and river holdings.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1673": "Muhyiddin won the civil war with Sulu help, and Sulu later claimed northeast Borneo as its reward. Brunei survived as an independent sultanate but with reduced territory and income, while Sulu replaced it as the leading Muslim maritime power of the region.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1800": "Brunei still claimed the coast of northwest Borneo, but its control over distant rivers was weak and its trade had declined. Local chiefs acted independently, piracy and unrest troubled the coasts, and the sultanate had little military strength left to restore its authority.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1841": "Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddin II granted Sarawak to James Brooke, who had helped suppress a rebellion there. The grant began a long process in which Brooke and his successors absorbed Brunei rivers one after another, steadily shrinking the sultanate and its revenues.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1846": "After a British naval force attacked the capital, Brunei ceded the island of Labuan to Britain. The sultanate was now dependent on British goodwill, unable to defend itself, and caught between the expanding Brooke state and later commercial concessions in the north.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1888": "Brunei became a British protectorate after losing the north to the British North Borneo Company and much of the southwest to Sarawak. Reduced to a small territory and soon divided in two when Sarawak took Limbang, the sultanate stood at its lowest point.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1929": "Oil was discovered at Seria by the British Malayan Petroleum Company. Production grew through the 1930s and transformed Brunei\u0027s finances, giving the protectorate under its British Resident a source of wealth and strategic value out of all proportion to its size.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1945": "Allied forces landed in June 1945 to end the Japanese occupation that had begun in December 1941. The occupation and fighting had damaged the oilfields and the capital, and Brunei emerged from the war devastated and fully dependent on British reconstruction.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1962": "A revolt by the Brunei People\u0027s Party and its armed wing was suppressed with British troops. The sultan then ruled by emergency powers and in 1963 declined to join Malaysia, keeping control of oil revenues and Brunei\u0027s separate identity as a monarchy.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|1984": "Brunei became fully independent on 1 January under Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah and joined ASEAN and the United Nations. A small state, it combined great oil and gas wealth with an absolute monarchy and a national ideology of Malay Islamic Monarchy.",
  "Brunei Sultanate|2025": "Brunei remains an independent, wealthy absolute monarchy under Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah, among the world\u0027s longest-reigning monarchs. It controls only a small part of the coast it once dominated, and its economy still depends heavily on exports of oil and natural gas.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1399": "According to dynastic tradition, Yaduraya founded the Wodeyar line at Mysore in this year. The family ruled only a small chiefdom in the southern Karnataka plateau as subordinate chiefs of the Vijayanagara Empire, with no independent policy and very little military strength of its own.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1565": "Vijayanagara\u0027s defeat at the Battle of Talikota weakened imperial control over its southern chiefs. The Wodeyars of Mysore were still minor rulers, but the decline of their overlord opened the way for local chiefs to expand their holdings and act with growing independence.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1610": "Raja Wodeyar I seized Srirangapatna, the island fortress of Vijayanagara\u0027s viceroy, and made it his capital. Mysore became an independent kingdom in practice, and Raja Wodeyar presented himself as heir to Vijayanagara\u0027s kingship by celebrating the Dasara festival at his new court.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1638": "Kanthirava Narasaraja I came to the throne. During his reign Mysore resisted the armies of the Bijapur Sultanate, expanded at the expense of neighbouring chiefs and began issuing its own coinage, steadily strengthening its position as a regional kingdom on the southern plateau.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1673": "Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar began a reign of three decades in which he centralised the administration, expanded the kingdom\u0027s territory and acquired Bangalore from the Mughals in 1689. Mysore became one of the strongest and best-administered states of the southern peninsula.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1704": "Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar died at the height of the Wodeyar kingdom\u0027s power, leaving an organised administration, a full treasury and a well-defined territory. His successors were weaker rulers, however, and power soon began to shift from the kings towards ambitious ministers and generals.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1734": "Krishnaraja Wodeyar II became king, but real power lay with the dalavayi Devaraja and his brother Nanjaraja. Mysore was weakened by factional struggles, Maratha raids and demands for tribute, and its army was drawn into the wars of the Carnatic.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1761": "Hyder Ali took control of the government, sidelining the ministers while keeping the Wodeyar king on the throne. With a growing army and a reputation as a commander, he restored central authority, reorganised the army and prepared Mysore for a period of rapid expansion.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1766": "Hyder Ali conquered Malabar after taking Bednur in 1763, gaining the pepper ports of the west coast and the treasures of Bednur. Mysore had become the leading power of south India, soon to fight the East India Company in the First Anglo-Mysore War.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1771": "After the favourable Treaty of Madras in 1769, Hyder Ali suffered a heavy defeat by Maratha armies sent by Peshwa Madhavrao and had to cede territory and pay tribute. Mysore\u0027s growth was checked, and it remained exposed to pressure from Maratha armies.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1780": "Hyder Ali invaded the Carnatic and destroyed a British detachment at Pollilur, one of the Company\u0027s worst defeats in India. Mysore stood near the height of its military power, although the war would also bring defeats such as Porto Novo in 1781.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1784": "Tipu Sultan, who had succeeded his father in 1782, concluded the Treaty of Mangalore with the Company on the basis of mutual restitution. Mysore emerged from the Second Anglo-Mysore War undefeated, controlling its greatest territory and a powerful, modernised army.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1792": "In the Treaty of Seringapatam, after the Third Anglo-Mysore War, Tipu ceded about half of his territory to the Company and its allies, paid a heavy indemnity and handed over two sons as hostages. Mysore survived, but much reduced and surrounded.",
  "Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799)|1799": "British and Hyderabadi forces stormed Srirangapatna on 4 May and Tipu Sultan was killed. The Company annexed much of his kingdom and restored a child Wodeyar to rule a reduced princely state, ending Mysore\u0027s existence as an independent power in southern India.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1405": "The earliest date given in later chronologies for the founding of the sultanate. Sulu was then a cluster of trading chiefdoms on Jolo and neighbouring islands, recently touched by Islam through Muslim traders and teachers, with modest power that rested on the sea trade with China and the surrounding islands.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1417": "Three Sulu rulers, among them Paduka Batara, led a large mission to the Yongle Emperor\u0027s court, and Sulu briefly became a Ming tributary. Paduka Batara died on the way home and was buried at Dezhou. The mission shows a prosperous trading society, but one still divided among several rulers.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1450": "Around the mid-fifteenth century, by many accounts, Sharif ul-Hashim established the sultanate, introducing Islamic law and institutions and organising the islands into districts under chiefs. Power remained limited to the archipelago, but a single dynasty and an Islamic identity gave Sulu the framework it kept for nearly five centuries.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1578": "The Spanish, settled at Manila since 1571, sent their first expedition against Jolo, opening three centuries of intermittent warfare. Sulu had by then extended its ties to coastal communities in Palawan and Borneo, and its fortified settlements and fleets of datus allowed it to absorb Spanish attacks without losing independence.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1638": "Governor-General Sebastián Hurtado de Corcuera captured Jolo after a siege of several months and left a Spanish garrison there. The court withdrew and resisted from the interior and neighbouring islands. This was the most serious blow Sulu suffered before the nineteenth century, temporarily reducing the sultan\u0027s control of his own capital.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1646": "The Spanish withdrew their garrison from Jolo under a peace agreement, restoring the sultan\u0027s control of his capital. Spain was overstretched by its war with the Dutch, and Sulu emerged from the occupation intact, with its trade and its network of datus and coastal allies largely undamaged.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1662": "Fearing an attack on Manila by the Chinese warlord Koxinga, Spain abandoned its fort at Zamboanga and did not return until 1718. Freed from close Spanish pressure for more than half a century, Sulu consolidated its position in the southern islands and extended its influence along the Borneo coast.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1673": "Brunei\u0027s civil war of 1660–1673 ended around this time. According to Sulu tradition, the Sultan of Brunei granted Sulu the lands of northern Borneo in return for its help, a claim that later underpinned the 1878 North Borneo grant. Sulu\u0027s reach was now at its widest geographically, though control was loose.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1750": "Sultan Azim ud-Din I, seeking Spanish support against his brother Bantilan, was baptised on Luzon and later imprisoned. The episode split the court and exposed Sulu to Spanish manipulation, but Bantilan held Jolo and the sultanate\u0027s trade and independence survived, holding power at a middling level.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1775": "Tausug warriors destroyed the British East India Company\u0027s new post on Balambangan Island, showing that Sulu could defend its sphere against a European power. At the same time British country traders were turning Jolo into a major entrepôt, beginning the commercial boom that would carry Sulu to its peak.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1800": "The Sulu zone boom was well under way. Exports of tripang, birds\u0027 nests and pearls to China paid for firearms, cloth and opium, and Iranun and Balangingi fleets raided widely for captives to supply labour. Jolo\u0027s wealth and its access to imported guns made Sulu the leading power of the region.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1836": "Sulu concluded a treaty of friendship and commerce with Spain as an independent partner, and its trade and the raiding fleets operating from its waters were at their height. With influence from Mindanao to northern and eastern Borneo, this period represents the sultanate\u0027s greatest wealth and regional power.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1848": "Governor-General Narciso Clavería used steam gunboats to destroy the Balangingi strongholds, a heavy blow to the raiding system on which much of Sulu\u0027s labour supply depended. The sultanate itself remained independent and wealthy, but the military balance in the southern seas had begun to turn decisively against it.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1851": "A Spanish expedition stormed Jolo, and Sultan Muhammad Pulalun signed a treaty acknowledging Spanish sovereignty. In practice Sulu still governed itself and traded freely, but its claim to full independence was now compromised and Spanish naval power could reach its capital whenever Manila chose to act.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1876": "A large expedition under José Malcampo occupied Jolo and built a walled Spanish town there, forcing the court inland. Spanish patrols cut into Sulu\u0027s trade with foreign merchants, and the sultan lost his capital, his customs revenues and much of his ability to command the datus.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1878": "Sultan Jamalul Alam accepted Spanish protection and, in the same year, granted North Borneo to Overbeck and Dent in return for an annual payment. Sulu had lost both its capital and its Borneo dependencies, keeping only limited authority over the islands themselves under Spanish oversight.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1899": "After Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States, the Bates Treaty recognised Sultan Jamalul Kiram II under American sovereignty. The sultan retained ceremonial standing and some local authority, but real power lay with the American army, and the treaty was abrogated in 1904.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1906": "American troops killed hundreds of Tausug men, women and children who had taken refuge in the crater of Bud Dajo. Sulu was by now governed directly through the Moro Province, and the sultan\u0027s authority had become largely symbolic, limited to religious and customary matters.",
  "Sulu Sultanate|1915": "Under the Carpenter Agreement, Jamalul Kiram II renounced temporal power while remaining religious head of Sulu\u0027s Muslims. This ended the sultanate as a political state after some five centuries, although the title survived among rival claimants and the Borneo grant kept its diplomatic significance.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1428": "Lê Lợi took the throne at Thăng Long after forcing the Ming army to withdraw, restoring Đại Việt as an independent kingdom. The new state was battle-hardened and popular but exhausted by twenty years of occupation and war, and its first task was resettling refugees and rebuilding the farming economy.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1442": "The young emperor Lê Thái Tông died suddenly, and the scholar Nguyễn Trãi and his family were executed on the charge of causing his death. Court factions and regents dominated the following years under the child Lê Nhân Tông, but the kingdom remained stable, independent and steadily recovering.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1460": "Lê Thánh Tông came to the throne after the brief usurpation of Lê Nghi Dân. He began a long programme of reform, reorganising provinces, ministries and the army and expanding the examination system, which gave the state a stronger administrative base than any earlier Vietnamese monarchy.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1471": "Lê Thánh Tông captured the Cham capital Vijaya and its king and annexed the northern Cham lands. This conquest broke Champa as a rival power and greatly extended Đại Việt to the south, bringing the kingdom close to the height of its military power and territorial reach.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1479": "Vietnamese armies invaded Lan Xang and briefly occupied Luang Prabang, and Ngô Sĩ Liên completed the national chronicle. With a Confucian bureaucracy, a codified law, a large army and neighbours to west and south humbled, the Lê state stood at the peak of its power.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1497": "Lê Thánh Tông died after a reign of thirty-seven years, and his son Lê Hiến Tông maintained his institutions. The kingdom remained strong and well governed, but the long period of vigorous leadership that had driven its expansion and reform was coming to an end.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1505": "The accession of Lê Uy Mục, remembered as a cruel and dissolute ruler, began a period of misrule. Court purges, heavy exactions and conflict among the great families weakened central authority, although the administrative structure built by Lê Thánh Tông still functioned across most of the realm.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1516": "Under Lê Tương Dực, costly palace building and heavy labour demands provoked the rebellion of Trần Cảo, who briefly seized Thăng Long. Factional fighting among generals followed, and the general Mạc Đăng Dung rose to dominate the court as central control collapsed.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1527": "Mạc Đăng Dung forced the reigning Lê emperor to abdicate and founded the Mạc dynasty. For a few years the Lê ceased to rule anywhere, surviving only as a claim maintained by loyalists, which leaves the dynasty\u0027s power at its lowest point before its restoration.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1533": "Loyalists under Nguyễn Kim enthroned the Lê prince Lê Trang Tông in exile in the hills of Laos and Thanh Hóa. The restored court controlled only a mountainous corner of the country and depended entirely on its generals, but it offered a rallying point against the Mạc.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1545": "After Nguyễn Kim was poisoned, his son-in-law Trịnh Kiểm took command of the loyalist cause and the restored Lê court. The Lê now held the southern provinces from Thanh Hóa, but real authority had passed to the Trịnh family, which would control the dynasty for more than two centuries.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1558": "Nguyễn Hoàng, son of Nguyễn Kim, left for the southern frontier as governor of Thuận Hóa, beginning the separate Nguyễn domain. The Lê-Trịnh state was still fighting the Mạc for the north, and the move planted the seeds of a lasting division of the country.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1592": "Trịnh Tùng\u0027s armies retook Thăng Long from the Mạc, and the Lê emperors returned to their capital. The restored state controlled the Red River delta again and was formally reunited, though the Mạc held out at Cao Bằng and the Nguyễn ruled the south increasingly independently.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1627": "War broke out between the Trịnh and the Nguyễn, who refused to pay tribute to the Lê court. The northern state was populous and well armed, and trade with Chinese, Japanese and European merchants was growing, but the Lê emperors themselves had become figureheads behind the Trịnh lords.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1672": "After seven major campaigns over forty-five years, the Trịnh and Nguyễn agreed to a truce, leaving the country divided near the Gianh River. The northern Lê-Trịnh state remained populous and administratively strong, but the failure to reconquer the south fixed its limits for the next century.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1700": "The northern state was at peace, its Confucian bureaucracy and examination system functioning and its silk trade continuing, though the Dutch East India Company closed its Tonkin post that year as trade declined. Power remained with the Trịnh lords, who governed effectively in the Lê emperors\u0027 name.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1740": "A wave of peasant revolts, driven by famine, land concentration and heavy taxation, swept the north, led by figures such as Nguyễn Hữu Cầu and the Lê prince Lê Duy Mật. The Trịnh suppressed them only after years of fighting, and the state was visibly weakened.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1775": "Taking advantage of the Tây Sơn rising in the south, Trịnh forces under Hoàng Ngũ Phúc captured the Nguyễn capital Phú Xuân. For a moment the Lê-Trịnh state again reached deep into the south, but the gain was fragile and overstretched an already strained government.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1786": "The Tây Sơn under Nguyễn Huệ marched north and destroyed the Trịnh lords, restoring nominal power to the Lê. The aged emperor Lê Hiển Tông died, and Lê Chiêu Thống succeeded with no army of his own, wholly dependent on rival strongmen and soon on the Qing.",
  "Later Le dynasty|1789": "Nguyễn Huệ, proclaimed emperor as Quang Trung, routed the Qing army that had restored Lê Chiêu Thống, in battles at Ngọc Hồi and Đống Đa. Lê Chiêu Thống fled to China, ending the dynasty after 361 years; he died in Beijing in 1793.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1490": "Yusuf Adil Khan, Bahmani governor of Bijapur, made himself effectively independent as the Bahmani Sultanate disintegrated. His new state held the western Deccan plateau and part of the coast, including Goa, but it was one of several rival successor states, surrounded by enemies and still building its institutions.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1510": "The Portuguese under Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa, Bijapur\u0027s best port, and held it after a brief Bijapuri recovery; Yusuf Adil Shah died the same year. His young son Ismail succeeded amid factional quarrels, and the loss of Goa cut Bijapur off from much of the profitable sea trade.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1534": "Ibrahim Adil Shah I came to the throne after the short reign of Mallu Adil Shah. He turned to Sunni Islam and to the Deccani nobles, and is credited with using Marathi in revenue records. Bijapur was consolidating its hold on the western Deccan while fighting its neighbours for the Raichur doab.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1558": "Ali Adil Shah I succeeded and restored Shia observance. He began building Bijapur\u0027s walls and major monuments and pursued shifting alliances with Vijayanagara and the other sultanates. The kingdom was now well established and prosperous, a middle-ranking power among the Deccan states.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1565": "Bijapur joined Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar to defeat Vijayanagara at the Battle of Talikota. The destruction of the great southern empire removed Bijapur\u0027s most dangerous rival and opened the Raichur doab and the lands to the south to its expansion, raising its regional power markedly.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1571": "Ali Adil Shah I\u0027s long siege of Portuguese Goa, begun in 1570, ended in failure. The setback showed the limits of Bijapur\u0027s power against a European sea-borne enemy, though on land the sultanate remained strong and continued to absorb former Vijayanagara territory.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1580": "Ali Adil Shah I was killed and the boy Ibrahim Adil Shah II succeeded under the regency of Chand Bibi. Factional struggles between Deccanis, Westerners and Habshis disturbed the early years of the reign, but the kingdom held together and soon recovered under the new sultan.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1619": "Ibrahim Adil Shah II annexed the Bidar Sultanate, the last remnant of the old Bahmani heartland. His long reign brought stability, a flourishing court culture and building in the capital, making Bijapur one of the two leading powers of the Deccan alongside Golconda.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1627": "Muhammad Adil Shah succeeded his father Ibrahim II. Bijapur was wealthy, its court famous for music, painting and architecture, and its army strong, but the Mughal advance into the Deccan was closing in on Ahmadnagar and threatening to reach Bijapur\u0027s frontiers.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1636": "After the Mughals extinguished Ahmadnagar, Muhammad Adil Shah agreed a treaty with Shah Jahan, accepting Mughal overlordship and paying tribute in exchange for part of Ahmadnagar\u0027s lands. The treaty secured the northern frontier and freed Bijapur to turn its armies south.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1649": "Bijapur\u0027s armies under Mustafa Khan, in which Shahaji Bhonsle served until his arrest during the campaign, completed the conquest of much of southern Karnataka and the Tamil country, taking Gingee. The sultanate reached its greatest territorial extent, while the Gol Gumbaz was rising in the capital, marking the height of its power.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1656": "Muhammad Adil Shah died and was buried beneath the Gol Gumbaz. Bijapur was still large and rich, but the accession of the young Ali Adil Shah II, whose legitimacy the Mughals questioned, invited intervention, and its distant southern conquests were loosely held.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1657": "Prince Aurangzeb invaded with Mughal approval and captured Bidar and Kalyani. A peace was arranged only because Shah Jahan\u0027s illness drew Aurangzeb north, but Bijapur lost territory and prestige and was shown to be unable to stand against a full Mughal campaign.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1659": "Shivaji, son of the Bijapur noble Shahaji, killed the Bijapur general Afzal Khan at Pratapgad and destroyed his army. The rise of an independent Maratha power in Bijapur\u0027s western lands deprived the sultanate of territory, revenue and fighting men, and showed that its armies could no longer control its own provinces.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1666": "A Mughal army under Jai Singh, joined by Shivaji, advanced towards Bijapur but withdrew without taking the city. Bijapur survived, but its finances were strained, its southern governors increasingly independent and its position caught between the Mughals and the Marathas.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1672": "Ali Adil Shah II died and the child Sikandar Adil Shah succeeded. Regents and rival factions of Deccani, Afghan and Habshi nobles fought for control of the court, and the sultanate\u0027s ability to raise armies and collect revenue declined steeply.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1680": "Shivaji had died, but Maratha power in the former Bijapur lands of the west and south was entrenched. Bijapur\u0027s court was riven by factions, its treasury depleted and its army reduced, leaving it unable to resist the Mughal emperor once he turned his full attention to the Deccan.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1685": "Aurangzeb, having moved to the Deccan, sent his armies to besiege Bijapur. The city\u0027s great walls and guns delayed the Mughals, but the sultanate had no field army able to relieve it, and famine set in among the defenders during the long siege.",
  "Bijapur Sultanate|1686": "Sikandar Adil Shah surrendered Bijapur to Aurangzeb in September 1686, ending the Adil Shahi dynasty after nearly two centuries. The sultan was held prisoner until his death in 1700, and Bijapur became the seat of a Mughal province, its palaces left to decay.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1496": "The traditional founding date of the sultanate. Aceh was then a small Muslim port at the northern tip of Sumatra, overshadowed by its neighbours Pidie and Samudera Pasai and by the great entrepôt of Malacca across the strait, with little power beyond the Aceh valley.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1524": "Ali Mughayat Syah conquered Pasai and drove out the Portuguese, completing the unification of the north Sumatran coast. With Malacca in Portuguese hands since 1511, Muslim merchants turned to Aceh, which became the strongest power at the northern entrance to the strait.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1539": "Around this time Alauddin al-Kahhar came to the throne. He built up Aceh\u0027s army and fleet, expanded its control along Sumatra\u0027s coasts and developed the pepper route to the Red Sea, launching attacks on Portuguese Malacca and pressing the Batak and other neighbours.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1568": "After embassies to Istanbul seeking guns and gunners, and with some Ottoman military help, Alauddin al-Kahhar besieged Malacca. The siege failed, but Aceh was now a recognised Muslim ally of the Ottomans and a major regional power controlling much of the pepper trade.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1571": "Alauddin al-Kahhar died, and a series of short reigns, depositions and murders followed over the next decades. Aceh\u0027s trade continued, but court instability and the power of the merchant aristocracy reduced the sultanate\u0027s ability to wage war effectively or to hold its outlying ports.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1607": "Iskandar Muda seized the throne and began to curb the orang kaya and build a powerful army and fleet. Aceh\u0027s revenue from pepper and its strategic position gave the young ruler the means for the most ambitious programme of expansion in its history.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1613": "Iskandar Muda conquered Deli and Aru and sacked Johor\u0027s capital Batu Sawar, carrying members of its royal family to Aceh. Aceh now controlled most of the coasts of northern Sumatra and dominated the strait, threatening Portuguese Malacca from both sides and controlling much of its trade.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1620": "After taking Pahang, Kedah and Perak, Iskandar Muda ruled the west and east coasts of northern Sumatra and much of the Malay Peninsula\u0027s west coast. With its pepper monopoly, great fleet and crowded capital, Aceh stood at the peak of its power.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1629": "Iskandar Muda\u0027s great armada besieged Malacca but was destroyed by a Portuguese fleet with help from Johor and Patani. Aceh lost much of its navy and many of its men, and its offensive power never recovered, though it kept its conquests for the time being.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1636": "Iskandar Muda died and was succeeded by his son-in-law Iskandar Thani, a prince of Pahang. The new ruler governed more mildly and patronised Nuruddin ar-Raniri. Aceh remained wealthy and respected as a centre of trade and learning, but its days of conquest were over.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1641": "Taj ul-Alam Safiatuddin Syah, Iskandar Muda\u0027s daughter, became the first of four queens. The same year the Dutch and Johor took Malacca from the Portuguese. Aceh\u0027s court remained rich and stable, but Dutch competition for pepper and the loss of peninsular dependencies began.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1675": "Safiatuddin died after a reign of thirty-four years, and three further queens followed. The orang kaya governed in partnership with the crown, trade and scholarship continued under Abdurrauf of Singkil, but Aceh\u0027s grip on outlying ports and on the Malay Peninsula had largely gone.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1699": "The last queen, Kamalat Syah, was deposed and an Arab sayyid took the throne, opening a period of short reigns and contested successions. Aceh remained an important port, but real power drifted to the uleebalang and the confederations of the Aceh valley.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1727": "A dynasty of Bugis descent took the throne under Alauddin Ahmad Syah and kept it until 1903. The sultanate was now a loose state in which the sultan presided over largely autonomous chiefs, while English and other traders bought Acehnese pepper directly from the coast.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1824": "The Anglo-Dutch Treaty bound the Netherlands to respect Aceh\u0027s independence. A new pepper boom on Aceh\u0027s west coast, worked by local chiefs selling to American, British and other traders, brought the region great wealth, though much of it escaped the sultan\u0027s control.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1850": "Aceh was at the height of its nineteenth-century pepper trade and remained the largest independent state in Sumatra, conducting its own diplomacy with Britain, France, the Ottoman Empire and others. Its central government, however, remained weak and its chiefs largely independent.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1871": "In the Sumatra Treaty, Britain withdrew its objection to Dutch expansion in Sumatra in exchange for concessions elsewhere. Aceh lost the diplomatic protection that had preserved its independence and looked for support from the Ottomans and from Western consuls in Singapore, without success.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1873": "The Netherlands declared war, and the Acehnese repulsed the first Dutch expedition, killing General Köhler. The victory showed Aceh\u0027s capacity to resist, but the full weight of the colonial army of the Dutch East Indies was now turned against it.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1874": "A second Dutch expedition under General van Swieten captured the sultan\u0027s palace, and Sultan Mahmud Syah died of cholera. The court fled inland and the child Muhammad Daud Syah was proclaimed sultan, but the sultanate had lost its capital and much of its coherence.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1898": "General van Heutsz began systematic campaigns with mobile Marechaussee units, aided by allied uleebalang. The ulama and guerrilla leaders kept fighting, but the sultan\u0027s court had become a fugitive centre of resistance with little territory or regular administration left under its control.",
  "Aceh Sultanate|1903": "Sultan Muhammad Daud Syah surrendered to the Dutch in January 1903, ending the sultanate after some four centuries. He was later exiled, while guerrilla resistance continued in the interior for years and Aceh was absorbed into the Dutch East Indies.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1528": "Alauddin Riayat Shah II, son of the last Sultan of Malacca, established a new court on the Johor River. The young state inherited Malacca\u0027s prestige and claims but had lost its great port, and it faced the Portuguese at Malacca with only a modest fleet and a scattered following.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1587": "A Portuguese force destroyed the capital Johor Lama after years of conflict. Johor survived by moving its court upriver, but the attack showed that it could not yet match Portuguese Malacca in war, while Aceh pressed it from across the strait.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1606": "Johor concluded an alliance with the Dutch East India Company, whose fleet under Cornelis Matelief besieged Malacca without success. The alliance gave Johor a powerful European partner against the Portuguese and marked a recovery in its trade and regional standing.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1613": "Aceh\u0027s ruler Iskandar Muda sacked Batu Sawar and carried members of Johor\u0027s royal family to Sumatra. For some years Johor lived in the shadow of Aceh, its court divided and its capital repeatedly moved, at one of the lowest points in its history.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1641": "With Johor\u0027s help, the Dutch took Malacca from the Portuguese in January 1641, removing Johor\u0027s old enemy. Aceh\u0027s power had declined after 1629, and Johor emerged as the leading Malay state of the southern strait, with its ports attracting the trade Malacca had lost.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1673": "Jambi, a Sumatran rival with which Johor had been at war since 1666, sacked Batu Sawar. The blow was severe, but the laksamana Tun Abdul Jamil rebuilt the state in Riau and, with Bugis mercenaries, eventually turned the war in Johor\u0027s favour.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1690": "Under the dominance of the laksamana family, Johor-Riau was the leading entrepôt of the Malay world, controlling the southern strait, the Riau islands and parts of Sumatra\u0027s east coast. Its trade, its Orang Laut fleets and its prestige as Malacca\u0027s heir brought it to its peak.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1699": "Sultan Mahmud Shah II was murdered at Kota Tinggi, ending the Malacca line, and the bendahara became Sultan Abdul Jalil IV. Johor remained wealthy, but the loss of the sacred royal line weakened the loyalty of the Orang Laut and opened the way to rival claimants.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1718": "Raja Kecil of Siak, claiming to be the posthumous son of the murdered sultan, seized the throne with Orang Laut support. Civil war and the defection of dependencies left Johor divided, and the deposed Abdul Jalil IV was killed by Raja Kecil\u0027s followers in 1721.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1722": "Bugis warriors expelled Raja Kecil and installed Sulaiman Badrul Alam Shah as sultan, taking the office of Yang Dipertuan Muda for themselves. The settlement restored order and a single government in Riau, though real power now lay with the Bugis nobles.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1760": "Under Bugis management, Riau prospered on gambier and pepper grown by Chinese planters and on a busy transit trade. The young Mahmud Shah III succeeded in 1761, and Johor-Riau was again one of the most important ports of the region.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1784": "The Bugis viceroy Raja Haji attacked Dutch Malacca and was killed at Teluk Ketapang. The Dutch then occupied Riau and imposed a treaty of vassalage, and the Bugis were expelled for a time, ending Riau\u0027s period of commercial dominance and its independence.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1812": "Sultan Mahmud Shah III died after a reign of more than fifty years, during which the British had occupied Dutch Malacca in the wars of the French Revolution and Napoleon. A disputed succession between his sons Hussein and Abdul Rahman left the realm without an undisputed ruler.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1819": "Stamford Raffles recognised Hussein as Sultan of Johor and, with him and the Temenggong, founded a British settlement at Singapore. The realm now had two rival sultans backed by rival European powers, and the British settlement soon overshadowed both as the region\u0027s main port.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1824": "The Anglo-Dutch Treaty divided the old kingdom along the Singapore Strait, and Singapore was ceded outright to the British. The mainland remained Johor, under Sultan Hussein and the Temenggong, while Riau-Lingga became a separate Dutch-protected sultanate, leaving Johor a small mainland state.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1855": "Sultan Ali, Hussein\u0027s son, ceded the government of Johor to Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim in a treaty arranged by the British. The temenggong\u0027s line, enriched by Singapore\u0027s trade and pepper and gambier planting, became the real rulers of the mainland state.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1885": "Britain recognised Abu Bakar as Sultan of Johor in a treaty that left him in charge of internal affairs. With a modern administration, a new capital at Johor Bahru and a growing plantation economy, Johor was the most developed independent Malay state on the peninsula.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1914": "Sultan Ibrahim accepted a British General Adviser, bringing Johor into the Unfederated Malay States. Johor kept its monarchy, its own civil service and a large measure of autonomy, but its external affairs and much of its policy were now under British direction.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1946": "Johor was included in the short-lived Malayan Union, and Johor Bahru became the birthplace of UMNO, founded under the state\u0027s chief minister Onn Jaafar to oppose it. Johor\u0027s leaders thus took a central part in shaping the Malay nationalism that led to independence.",
  "Johor Sultanate|1963": "Having joined the Federation of Malaya in 1948 and gained independence in 1957, Johor became a state of Malaysia. It kept its sultan and state constitution, but defence, foreign policy and most major powers now belonged to the federal government.",
  "Johor Sultanate|2025": "Johor is one of Malaysia\u0027s most populous and economically important states, closely tied to Singapore. Its sultan, Ibrahim Iskandar, has served as King of Malaysia since January 2024, and the monarchy retains considerable prestige, though Johor is no longer a sovereign state.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1587": "Sutawijaya, son of Ki Ageng Pemanahan, ruled from Kotagede as Panembahan Senapati after throwing off the authority of Pajang. His realm was still a small inland principality in the plain of Central Java, with rivals at Pajang, Demak, Madiun and on the coast, so its power stood far below its later height.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1590": "Senapati subdued Pajang and Demak and, around 1590, campaigned against Madiun, extending Mataram’s authority over much of the interior of Central Java. The coastal states of East Java, led by Surabaya, still resisted, and the young kingdom’s hold on newly won areas depended on the loyalty of local lords.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1601": "At Senapati’s death in 1601 Mataram dominated the interior but not the wealthy north coast. His son Panembahan Hanyakrawati spent much of his reign suppressing revolts by relatives and regional lords and fighting Surabaya, so the kingdom held its ground without decisive new gains.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1613": "Hanyakrawati died in 1613 and was succeeded by his son, later known as Sultan Agung. The new king inherited a militarised inland state with large peasant levies and began preparing the campaigns against the coastal ports that would turn Mataram into the leading power of Java.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1619": "Agung’s armies took Lasem in 1616, Pasuruan in 1617 and in 1619 Tuban, one of the oldest ports of the north coast. Each conquest weakened the coalition led by Surabaya and brought new tribute and manpower, although the fighting devastated the coastal towns.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1625": "After conquering Madura in 1624, Agung forced Surabaya to surrender in 1625 following a long siege. With its great rival gone, Mataram controlled nearly all of Central and East Java apart from Blambangan in the far east, while Banten and Dutch Batavia remained independent in the west.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1629": "Two large expeditions against Batavia in 1628 and 1629 failed with heavy losses, after Dutch ships burned the army’s rice stores. The defeats checked Mataram’s westward ambitions and encouraged revolts, but the kingdom’s grip on Central and East Java remained intact.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1636": "Agung conquered Giri, the holy Islamic centre near Gresik whose spiritual lords had backed Surabaya, removing a rival source of religious authority. Campaigns against Hindu Blambangan followed. Mataram’s authority was now unchallenged across most of Java outside the Dutch and Banten spheres.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1641": "Agung received the title of sultan with the sanction of Mecca, crowning a reign that had united most of Java under one court. His calendar of 1633 and the royal cemetery at Imogiri expressed the prestige of a kingdom at the height of its power and cohesion.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1646": "Amangkurat I succeeded his father and made peace with the Dutch East India Company in 1646. He inherited the full extent of Agung’s realm, but began a harsh centralisation, killing a rival brother and turning against the nobles and Islamic teachers whose support had sustained the dynasty.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1660": "Amangkurat I’s repression, including the reported massacre of thousands of Islamic teachers and restrictions on coastal trade, alienated princes, regional lords and the coast. The kingdom still covered most of Java, but its cohesion was eroding as the crown prince and Madurese nobles began to plot against the king.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1677": "Trunajaya’s rebels, allied with Makassarese fighters, sacked the capital Plered, and Amangkurat I died while fleeing. Mataram had effectively collapsed: the court was scattered, the treasury lost and much of Java in rebel hands, leaving the dynasty’s survival dependent on foreign help.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1680": "With Dutch East India Company troops, Amangkurat II defeated Trunajaya, who was captured in 1679 and killed in 1680, and built a new capital at Kartasura. The dynasty was restored, but at the cost of ports, revenues and monopolies granted to the Company.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1705": "In the First Javanese War of Succession the Company backed Pakubuwana I against his nephew Amangkurat III. By the treaty of 1705 Mataram ceded Cirebon and the Priangan highlands and accepted a Company garrison at Kartasura, a further step towards dependence.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1723": "After the Second War of Succession of 1719–1723, Amangkurat IV ruled a kingdom that was relatively stable but bound by Company treaties and debts. Mataram still controlled the Javanese heartland, yet its army could no longer defeat major rebels without Dutch support.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1742": "During the Chinese War, which spread from the Batavia massacre of 1740, Pakubuwana II first sided with the rebels and then changed sides. Rebel forces sacked Kartasura in 1742 and the king fled; he was restored with Company and Madurese help but lost the north coast.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1749": "Facing the revolt of his brother Mangkubumi, the dying Pakubuwana II surrendered the sovereignty of the kingdom to the Dutch East India Company. The Company installed Pakubuwana III, while Mangkubumi proclaimed himself king and controlled much of the interior, leaving Mataram split in all but name.",
  "Mataram Sultanate|1755": "The Treaty of Giyanti divided the kingdom between Pakubuwana III at Surakarta and Mangkubumi, who became Sultan Hamengkubuwana I of Yogyakarta. Mataram ceased to exist as a single state; its heirs survived as Dutch-dominated principalities whose courts continue in ceremonial form today.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1599": "Pegu fell in 1599 and the First Toungoo Empire disintegrated. At Ava, Nyaungyan Min, a son of Bayinnaung, held a modest base in Upper Burma and the nearby Shan states, one of several rival centres. Lower Burma was devastated and lay outside his control, so power stood low.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1605": "Nyaungyan had reasserted authority over the dry zone and several Shan states before his death in 1605. His son Anaukpetlun inherited a solid core in Upper Burma and an army capable of moving south, though Prome, Toungoo and Syriam remained in other hands.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1610": "Anaukpetlun captured Prome in 1608 and forced Toungoo to submit in 1610, bringing most of central Burma under Ava. The renewed kingdom now controlled the Irrawaddy valley down to the edge of the delta, where the Portuguese at Syriam still blocked access to the sea.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1613": "The capture of Syriam and the execution of Filipe de Brito gave the kingdom the main port of Lower Burma and a corps of captured European gunners. Anaukpetlun made Pegu his capital, reclaiming Bayinnaung’s seat, although an attack on Siamese Tenasserim soon failed.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1615": "Anaukpetlun retook Chiang Mai, restoring Lan Na as a tributary. The kingdom now held Upper and Lower Burma, the Shan states and Lan Na, the core of Bayinnaung’s empire without Siam and Laos, and enjoyed regular foreign trade through Syriam.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1628": "Anaukpetlun was murdered by his own son in 1628, provoking a brief succession crisis. His brother Thalun suppressed the usurper and took the throne in 1629. The territorial structure survived intact, but the killing showed the danger posed by ambitious princes at court.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1635": "Thalun moved the capital from Pegu to Ava, placing the court near the rice lands and service population of Upper Burma. The move, together with the reorganisation of the ahmudan, strengthened central control and gave the kingdom its settled form as an inland, agrarian state.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1640": "Following the revenue inquest of 1638, Thalun’s government held an unusually detailed record of people, land and obligations. The kingdom was at peace, its tributaries were loyal and Buddhist construction flourished, including the Kaunghmudaw Pagoda at Sagaing, making this the height of its power.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1648": "Thalun died in 1648 as the Kaunghmudaw Pagoda was completed. His son Pindale inherited a stable, well-organised kingdom, but one whose strength rested on careful management of manpower rather than expansion, and which soon faced serious instability on its northern border.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1661": "Ming loyalist armies following the fugitive Yongli Emperor raided Upper Burma, and in 1661 Pindale was overthrown and killed by his brother Pye Min. In 1662 a Qing army reached Ava and the court surrendered the emperor, ending the crisis but exposing military weakness.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1698": "The later seventeenth century was a long period of peace under Minyekyawdin, who died in 1698. Administration continued along Thalun’s lines, but crown servicemen increasingly slipped into private or monastic dependence, slowly eroding the manpower and revenue on which royal power depended.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1727": "Chiang Mai rebelled and Lan Na escaped Burmese control. At the same time Manipuri cavalry under Gharib Nawaz were raiding the dry zone. Under Taninganwe the court failed to respond effectively, and the kingdom lost both territory and prestige in quick succession.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1738": "In the late 1730s Manipuri raiders reached Sagaing, across the river from Ava, burning monasteries and pagodas in the heart of the kingdom. The inability of Mahadhammayaza Dipati’s government to defend even the capital region revealed how far the army and administration had decayed.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1740": "The Mon of Lower Burma rose at Pegu and proclaimed their own king, founding the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom. Ava lost the delta, its ports and their revenues, and with them the southern half of the realm, leaving a weakened court confined to Upper Burma.",
  "Restored Toungoo Kingdom|1752": "Hanthawaddy forces captured Ava, and the last Nyaungyan king was taken prisoner to Pegu. The Restored Toungoo Kingdom ended. Resistance in Upper Burma passed to Alaungpaya of Moksobo, whose Konbaung dynasty soon reunited the country on the Restored Toungoo administrative model.",
  "Hyderabad State|1724": "Nizam-ul-Mulk defeated the Mughal court’s candidate Mubariz Khan at Shakar Kheda and took control of the six Deccan provinces as a practically independent ruler. The new state inherited a large Mughal administration and army but faced a powerful and expanding Maratha rival on almost every side.",
  "Hyderabad State|1728": "At Palkhed the Peshwa Baji Rao I outmanoeuvred Nizam-ul-Mulk, who had to recognise Maratha rights to collect chauth and sardeshmukhi in the Deccan. The defeat showed the limits of the new state’s military power and left a lasting division of revenue with the Marathas.",
  "Hyderabad State|1743": "Having survived a second defeat near Bhopal in 1737–1738, Asaf Jah led an army into the Carnatic, took Trichinopoly from the Marathas and in 1744 installed Anwaruddin Khan as nawab of Arcot. His authority over the south was then at its widest, the height of the state’s power.",
  "Hyderabad State|1748": "Asaf Jah I died in 1748, leaving a large but loosely held realm. His dominions still stretched across the Deccan, but rival sons and a grandson immediately claimed the succession, and the French and British companies prepared to exploit the dispute for their own ends.",
  "Hyderabad State|1751": "After Nasir Jung and Muzaffar Jung were killed within months of each other, Salabat Jung took the throne under the protection of the Marquis de Bussy. The Nizam’s government now depended on French troops, and in 1753 the Northern Circars were assigned to the French to pay for them.",
  "Hyderabad State|1760": "At the Battle of Udgir the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau defeated the Nizam’s army and forced the cession of large territories, including Ahmednagar, Daulatabad and Bijapur. The state reached a low point, while Nizam Ali Khan was rising to dominate his brother Salabat Jung.",
  "Hyderabad State|1763": "Nizam Ali Khan, who had deposed Salabat Jung in 1762, moved the capital from Aurangabad to Hyderabad. He tried to exploit Maratha losses at Panipat in 1761 but was defeated at Rakshasbhuvan in 1763; he nonetheless built a more stable government at his new capital.",
  "Hyderabad State|1792": "As an ally of the British East India Company and the Marathas in the Third Anglo-Mysore War, Hyderabad shared in the territories Tipu Sultan ceded by the Treaty of Seringapatam in 1792. Under Nizam Ali Khan the state had recovered stability and some territorial strength.",
  "Hyderabad State|1795": "At Kharda the Maratha confederacy defeated Nizam Ali Khan’s army and imposed heavy territorial and financial concessions. The British did not intervene, showing the Nizam that without a firm alliance his state could not withstand its neighbours. It was among the last campaigns fought by the Marathas together.",
  "Hyderabad State|1798": "Under the subsidiary alliance with the East India Company, the Nizam disbanded his French-trained corps and accepted British troops paid by the state. Hyderabad gained protection against the Marathas and Mysore, but surrendered control of its foreign relations and much of its military independence.",
  "Hyderabad State|1853": "To pay arrears for the Hyderabad Contingent, the Nizam assigned the revenues of Berar to the British, losing one of his richest provinces. In the same year Salar Jung I became chief minister, inheriting a heavily indebted and disordered administration with little effective control of the districts.",
  "Hyderabad State|1883": "By Salar Jung I’s death in 1883 the state had a reformed revenue system, district administration, courts and schools, and its finances were restored. Hyderabad remained under British paramountcy but had become the leading princely state, with its own railway already in operation.",
  "Hyderabad State|1918": "Mir Osman Ali Khan founded Osmania University and received the style His Exalted Highness. The state was near the height of its modern wealth and institutional development, with its own currency, railways and public works, but remained politically subordinate to the British Crown.",
  "Hyderabad State|1947": "With British paramountcy ending, Osman Ali Khan sought independence and signed a Standstill Agreement with India. The state’s army was small, it was surrounded by Indian territory, and internal order was breaking down amid Razakar violence and the Telangana peasant uprising.",
  "Hyderabad State|1948": "Operation Polo ended the state’s independence within five days in September 1948, and the Asaf Jahi dynasty lost its sovereign power. Hyderabad was integrated into India, first under a military governor and from 1950 as a state of the Union, before being divided on linguistic lines in 1956.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1752": "Alaungpaya, headman of Moksobo, proclaimed himself king after Hanthawaddy forces captured Ava and ended the Restored Toungoo dynasty. His power rested on a few dozen villages of the dry zone and their loyal militia, a modest beginning against an enemy that held the capital and the south.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1754": "Alaungpaya’s forces had driven the southern armies out of Upper Burma and retaken Ava, and they repulsed a Hanthawaddy counter-offensive. The whole dry zone now recognised the new dynasty, giving it the manpower and rice lands that had sustained earlier Burmese kingdoms.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1757": "After taking Dagon, which he renamed Yangon, in 1755 and storming Syriam in 1756, Alaungpaya captured and destroyed Pegu in May 1757. The Hanthawaddy kingdom was extinguished, and the whole Irrawaddy basin was reunited under a single Burmese king for the first time in decades.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1760": "Alaungpaya subdued Manipur and invaded Siam, but fell ill during the siege of Ayutthaya and died on the retreat in 1760. His son Naungdawgyi faced rebellions by generals and relatives, so the dynasty paused in its expansion while keeping the reunited kingdom intact.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1767": "Hsinbyushin’s armies captured and destroyed Ayutthaya in April 1767, ending the Siamese kingdom and carrying off thousands of captives. At the same time Burmese forces held off Qing invasions in the north, showing the military strength of a dynasty now dominant in mainland Southeast Asia.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1769": "The defeat of the fourth Qing invasion at Kaungton ended the war with a negotiated peace. Burma had resisted the largest empire in Asia, but the struggle drew troops away from Siam, where Taksin expelled the Burmese garrisons and restored Siamese independence.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1785": "Bodawpaya conquered the kingdom of Arakan and brought the revered Mahamuni Buddha image to his new capital, Amarapura. His great invasion of Siam in 1785–1786 failed, but within its borders the kingdom was large, populous and closely administered after the revenue inquest of the 1780s.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1819": "Burmese armies had occupied Assam and reimposed control over Manipur, giving the kingdom its greatest extent, from the Brahmaputra valley to Tenasserim. Bagyidaw succeeded Bodawpaya in 1819 at the height of Konbaung power, but the new frontier now touched British Bengal.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1824": "Disputes on the Arakan and Assam frontiers led Britain to declare war. The Burmese army under Maha Bandula advanced towards Bengal, but a British expedition landed at Rangoon. The kingdom still possessed its full territory, yet was entering a war it could not win.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1826": "The Treaty of Yandabo ended the First Anglo-Burmese War. Burma ceded Arakan and Tenasserim, abandoned Assam and Manipur and paid an indemnity of one million pounds. The loss of territory, prestige and treasure sharply reduced the kingdom’s standing in the region.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1852": "The Second Anglo-Burmese War ended with British annexation of Pegu province, including Rangoon and the delta. The kingdom lost its seaports and its richest rice lands, while at Amarapura Mindon deposed his half-brother Pagan Min and took the throne early in 1853.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1857": "Mindon founded Mandalay as his new capital and began reforms of taxation, coinage and administration. With its population and revenues reduced, the kingdom recovered some stability, and Mindon used diplomacy with Britain and other European powers to protect its remaining independence.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1871": "Mindon convened the Fifth Buddhist Council at Mandalay, reaffirming the kingdom’s role as protector of Theravada Buddhism. A telegraph network, steamers and royal factories had been introduced, but the rebellion of 1866 and the murder of the heir had weakened the reform programme.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1879": "Thibaw, enthroned in 1878 by a palace faction, saw dozens of royal relatives executed in 1879. Britain withdrew its Resident from Mandalay, and the kingdom became diplomatically isolated as its finances suffered from the loss of trade and revenue to British Burma.",
  "Konbaung dynasty|1885": "After the court fined the Bombay Burmah Trading Corporation and negotiated with France, Britain invaded and took Mandalay in November 1885. Thibaw was exiled to India and the kingdom was annexed on 1 January 1886, ending the Burmese monarchy and the dynasty Alaungpaya had founded.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1768": "Prithvi Narayan Shah’s Gorkhali army entered Kathmandu in September 1768, and Lalitpur and Bhaktapur fell soon after. The new kingdom joined Gorkha to the wealthy Kathmandu Valley, but it was still one hill power among many, surrounded by independent principalities to the east and west.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1775": "At Prithvi Narayan Shah’s death in 1775 the kingdom had expanded east into the Kirat country and controlled the main trade routes to Tibet. Brief reigns and regency disputes followed, but the Gorkhali army remained intact and ready for further conquest in the western hills.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1790": "Under the regent Bahadur Shah, Gorkhali armies conquered many of the Chaubisi and Baisi principalities of the west and took Kumaon in 1790. Nepal had become a major Himalayan power, though its quarrel with Tibet over coinage and trade had already led to war.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1792": "A Qing army under Fuk’anggan marched to within reach of Kathmandu, and Nepal accepted peace and a tributary relationship with Beijing. The setback ended expansion northwards, but the kingdom kept its territory and turned its armies back to the west.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1806": "Bhimsen Thapa took power as chief minister, and Nepali forces, having conquered Garhwal in 1804, were besieging Kangra. Nepal now stretched from Sikkim to beyond the Yamuna, approaching its widest extent, though Sikh intervention would halt its advance at Kangra in 1809.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1814": "At the outbreak of war with the East India Company, Nepal controlled the Himalaya from the Teesta to the Sutlej, with a battle-hardened army and Bhimsen Thapa in firm command. This marks the peak of the kingdom’s territorial and military power.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1816": "The Treaty of Sugauli, ratified in March 1816, ended the Anglo-Nepalese War. Nepal lost Kumaon, Garhwal, its Sikkim conquests and much of the Terai, about a third of its territory, accepted a British Resident in Kathmandu and was confined roughly within its present borders.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1846": "The Kot massacre ended decades of factional struggle at court and brought Jang Bahadur Rana to power. The kingdom was stable but weakened, its kings reduced to figureheads and its government in the hands of one family committed to isolation from the outside world.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1856": "Victory over Tibet brought Nepal trading privileges in Lhasa, and a royal decree made the Rana premiership hereditary. Jang Bahadur’s support for the British in 1857 soon brought the return of part of the western Terai, consolidating the kingdom’s security and revenues.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1923": "Britain formally recognised Nepal’s independence in the treaty of 1923, and Chandra Shumsher Rana abolished slavery the following year. The Rana state was secure and internationally recognised, but economically undeveloped and politically closed, with very few schools, roads or modern institutions.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1951": "King Tribhuvan, who had fled to India in 1950, returned in triumph as Rana rule collapsed. The monarchy recovered its authority and Nepal opened to the outside world, but the new political order was unsettled and the state remained poor and dependent on foreign aid.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1960": "King Mahendra dismissed the elected government of B.P. Koirala and banned political parties, introducing the panchayat system. The crown reached the height of its political power, though the kingdom itself remained a small, aid-dependent state wedged between India and China.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|1990": "The first People’s Movement forced King Birendra to legalise political parties and accept a constitutional monarchy, and the panchayat system was abolished. Democracy brought new freedoms but also unstable governments, and the monarchy lost much of its direct power over the state.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|2001": "The royal massacre at Narayanhiti Palace killed King Birendra and much of his family, and Gyanendra succeeded. With the Maoist insurgency spreading across the countryside, the state’s authority outside the towns was collapsing and the monarchy’s prestige was badly damaged.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|2006": "Gyanendra’s direct rule, begun in 2005, ended when weeks of mass protests forced him to restore parliament. The reinstated parliament removed the king’s powers, and the peace accord with the Maoists in November prepared the way for an elected constituent assembly.",
  "Kingdom of Nepal|2008": "The elected Constituent Assembly abolished the monarchy at its first sitting on 28 May 2008, and Nepal became a federal democratic republic. The Kingdom of Nepal ended after 240 years, though the country it had created continued under a new constitution.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1782": "Chao Phraya Chakri seized power from Taksin, was crowned as Rama I and founded Bangkok on the east bank of the Chao Phraya. The new kingdom inherited Thonburi’s gains but faced a hostile Burma, depleted manpower and a capital still under construction, so its power was real but not yet secure.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1786": "The Nine Armies War of 1785–1786 ended in Siamese victory: Bodawpaya’s invasion was defeated at Lat Ya and repulsed at Thalang. Survival against the strongest Burmese assault since 1767 confirmed the Chakri dynasty, though the kingdom still had to rebuild its population, revenues and hold over its vassals.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1809": "Rama I died after a reign that rebuilt Bangkok, codified the Three Seals Law and secured Chiang Mai, the Lao states and western Cambodia as dependencies. Rama II inherited a stable kingdom, though a last Burmese attack on Thalang in 1809–1810 showed that the western frontier remained exposed.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1827": "Under Rama III Siamese armies defeated Anouvong of Vientiane, sacked his capital and ended the Lao kingdom of Vientiane. Bangkok’s direct control now reached across the Khorat Plateau to the Mekong, and the Burney Treaty of 1826 with the British had settled relations with the expanding power in Burma.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1834": "The Siamese–Vietnamese war of 1831–1834 brought no decisive result in Cambodia, but Siam held its Lao and Malay dependencies and its trade with China flourished. Rama III’s kingdom was richer and larger than at any earlier point, with Chinese immigration feeding both revenue and the labour supply.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1851": "Mongkut came to the throne after the Siamese–Vietnamese conflict ended with shared suzerainty over Cambodia under Ang Duong. Siam stood at the height of its regional hegemony, overlord of Lao, Cambodian and northern Malay rulers, and not yet bound by unequal treaties with the Western powers.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1855": "The Bowring Treaty opened Siam to British trade on fixed low duties and granted extraterritoriality, and similar treaties with other powers followed. Sovereignty was limited and royal monopolies ended, but the treaty averted conflict with Britain and started a rice export boom that enlarged the kingdom’s economy.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1868": "Mongkut died and the fifteen-year-old Chulalongkorn succeeded under a regency dominated by the Bunnag family. France had already taken Cambodia under protection in 1863, cutting into Siam’s sphere, and real power at court lay with great noble houses rather than with the young king.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1874": "Chulalongkorn began the gradual abolition of slavery and created new councils, provoking the Front Palace crisis of 1874–1875 with the viceroy. The crisis was settled with British mediation; the king pulled back from rapid reform, and the kingdom’s strength remained broadly steady while he consolidated his position.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1892": "Chulalongkorn reorganised the government into functional ministries, and Prince Damrong began building a salaried provincial administration. The state was becoming far more centralised and capable, but French pressure on the Mekong was mounting and Siam’s small modern army could not defend its eastern dependencies.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1893": "In the Paknam incident French gunboats forced the Chao Phraya and blockaded Bangkok. Siam ceded the territories east of the Mekong and paid an indemnity. The loss of Laos and the humiliation of the capital marked the sharpest fall in the kingdom’s standing during the Chakri era.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1905": "The Slave Abolition Act and a conscription law completed key reforms, while railways linked Bangkok with the provinces. Siam ceded more Mekong territory to France in 1904, but its internal administration, finances and army were now stronger than ever, giving a compact but well-governed state.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1909": "The Anglo-Siamese Treaty transferred Kedah, Kelantan, Terengganu and Perlis to Britain, following the 1907 cession of Battambang and Siem Reap to France. In return Siam gained concessions on extraterritoriality and a British loan; its borders were now largely fixed, and its independence internationally recognised.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1917": "Vajiravudh brought Siam into the First World War on the Allied side and sent an expeditionary force to France. The decision earned a seat at the peace conference, founding membership of the League of Nations and, in the 1920s, the start of treaty revisions restoring full sovereignty.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1925": "Prajadhipok succeeded Vajiravudh, inheriting heavy debts from his brother’s spending. He cut court expenses and created a Supreme Council of senior princes, but the absolute monarchy’s legitimacy was weakening among educated officials and officers who resented princely domination of the state.",
  "Rattanakosin Kingdom|1932": "The People’s Party seized power in a bloodless coup on 24 June, ending absolute monarchy; Prajadhipok accepted a constitution in December. The Chakri kingdom continued as a constitutional monarchy, renamed Thailand in 1939, but the royal state of 1782 ended as a system of government.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1800": "The Dutch state took over the possessions of the dissolved VOC: Batavia, parts of Java, the Moluccas and scattered posts. Control outside these footholds was weak, finances were in ruins and Britain dominated Asian seas, so the new colony began as a modest and vulnerable territory.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1808": "Herman Willem Daendels arrived as governor-general, centralised Java’s administration, weakened the Javanese courts and built the Great Post Road. His forceful reforms strengthened the colonial government on Java, though the colony still lacked the naval power to defend itself against Britain.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1811": "With the Netherlands annexed by France, a British expedition conquered Java, and Raffles governed it until 1816. Dutch rule in the archipelago was interrupted entirely, leaving the colonial state at its lowest point before the Japanese occupation of the twentieth century.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1816": "Under the Anglo-Dutch Convention of 1814 the Dutch returned to Java and their other possessions. They inherited Raffles’ land rent system and an empty treasury, and their authority outside Java remained thin, though the 1824 treaty would soon secure them a recognised sphere south of Singapore.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1825": "Prince Diponegoro’s rebellion opened the Java War, which spread across central Java and drained Dutch resources. The colonial government faced the most serious challenge to its rule on Java, and its finances, already strained, were pushed close to collapse during five years of fighting.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1830": "Diponegoro was arrested and exiled, ending the Java War, and Johannes van den Bosch introduced the Cultivation System. Forced export cultivation soon made the colony highly profitable, and the KNIL was established, giving the Dutch a firm hold on Java and the means to expand.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1854": "The Government Regulation set out the colony’s constitutional order under the Dutch parliament. The Cultivation System was at its most profitable, remittances were sustaining the Dutch treasury, and expeditions had brought Bali and parts of Sumatra and Borneo under growing Dutch influence.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1870": "The Agrarian Law and Sugar Law opened the colony to private enterprise, and the Suez Canal shortened the route to Europe. Plantations in Java and Sumatra expanded rapidly, broadening the economic base of the colonial state beyond forced cultivation and attracting investment.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1873": "The Dutch invaded Aceh, beginning a war that would last decades and cost tens of thousands of lives. The colony was economically strong and expanding, but the conflict exposed the limits of its military power in the outer islands and became a heavy burden.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1901": "Queen Wilhelmina proclaimed the Ethical Policy, committing the colonial government to welfare, education and irrigation. Plantations, oil and tin were booming, the KNIL was advancing across the outer islands, and the state was taking on new administrative tasks across the archipelago.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1904": "Van Heutsz, victor in Aceh, became governor-general and pressed the final conquest of the outer islands through the Short Declaration and military expeditions. Within a few years almost the whole archipelago, from Aceh to New Guinea, lay under effective Dutch control for the first time in its history.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1918": "The Volksraad met for the first time, and the colony, neutral in the First World War, was largely pacified from Sumatra to New Guinea. Sarekat Islam had become a mass movement, but the colonial state was at the height of its territorial reach and administrative capacity.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1929": "Before the Depression struck, exports of sugar, rubber, oil and tin were at record levels and the communist uprisings of 1926–1927 had been crushed. With the archipelago pacified and nationalist parties closely watched, the colonial state stood at its peak of wealth and control.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1933": "The Great Depression devastated export earnings and the sugar industry, forcing deep budget cuts. A mutiny on the warship De Zeven Provinciën was suppressed by bombing, and the government exiled Sukarno and other nationalist leaders, ruling more by repression than consent.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1940": "Germany occupied the Netherlands in May, cutting the colony off from its home government. The Indies continued under Governor-General Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer and remained economically valuable, but its weak defences now faced an expansionist Japan hungry for its oil.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1942": "Japan invaded, destroyed the Allied fleet in the Battle of the Java Sea and accepted the Dutch surrender on 8 March. Dutch civilians and soldiers were interned, and the colonial state effectively ceased to exist for the remainder of the war.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1945": "After Japan’s surrender Sukarno and Hatta proclaimed independence on 17 August. Dutch authority existed only on paper or in exile, and the returning British and Dutch forces found an armed Republic, with its own government and army, controlling much of Java and Sumatra.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1947": "Dutch forces launched the first ‘police action’, recapturing plantations, ports and much of Java and Sumatra. Military gains partially restored the colonial position, but international criticism, the United Nations and continuing guerrilla resistance limited what force alone could achieve for the Netherlands.",
  "Dutch East Indies|1949": "After the second police action failed to destroy the Republic, the Round Table Conference agreed to a transfer of sovereignty, which took place on 27 December. The Dutch East Indies ended, with only Netherlands New Guinea remaining under Dutch rule until 1962.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1802": "Nguyen Anh proclaimed himself emperor Gia Long and took Thang Long, completing the defeat of the Tay Son. For the first time one court ruled from the Chinese border to the Gulf of Thailand, though the country was exhausted by decades of war and the north remained wary of the new rulers.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1813": "A Vietnamese army escorted the Cambodian king Ang Chan back to his capital, establishing Vietnamese protection over Cambodia. With the Gia Long Code soon to be issued and the Hue citadel rising, the dynasty consolidated its authority and its standing as the leading power of the eastern mainland.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1820": "Gia Long died and his son Minh Mang succeeded. The new emperor inherited a united, largely peaceful kingdom with a strong army and a tributary relationship with the Qing that secured the northern border, and he soon began a programme of centralisation.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1833": "Le Van Khoi rebelled in Saigon and Siam invaded in the south, but Vietnamese forces held the delta, repelled the Siamese and besieged the rebels. Champa’s last remnant had been annexed in 1832, and Minh Mang’s centralising reforms were reshaping the provinces under direct rule from Hue.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1839": "Minh Mang renamed the country Dai Nam. Vietnam governed much of Cambodia as the Tran Tay province, controlled parts of the Lao uplands and ruled through a centralised provincial system. This was the greatest territorial and administrative reach the dynasty ever attained.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1841": "Minh Mang died, and Cambodian rebellion and Siamese intervention forced Vietnam to pull back from Cambodia. Thieu Tri inherited a powerful state, but the overextension in the west and growing tension with Western missionaries and navies had begun to strain it.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1847": "French warships bombarded Da Nang, Thieu Tri died, and Tu Duc came to the throne. The settlement with Siam left Cambodia under joint suzerainty. The dynasty remained intact and sovereign, but it faced a growing technological gap with the European powers.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1858": "A Franco-Spanish expedition attacked Da Nang, beginning the French conquest. The Vietnamese army contained the attackers at first, but the court’s military and naval inferiority was exposed, and the conflict drew resources away from suppressing the rebellions that troubled the north.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1862": "After defeat at Ky Hoa, the Treaty of Saigon ceded three eastern provinces of Cochinchina to France, opened ports and promised freedom for Catholic missions. The loss of the delta’s rice lands and an indemnity weakened the empire’s finances and authority.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1867": "France seized the three western provinces of Cochinchina, giving it the whole south. Dai Nam was reduced to the centre and north, which were troubled by rebellions and by Chinese bandit armies along the northern frontier. The court’s ability to resist further encroachment was now very limited.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1874": "After Francis Garnier’s seizure of the Hanoi citadel in 1873, the Philastre Treaty recognised French sovereignty over Cochinchina and opened the Red River to trade. The Nguyen court kept its formal independence, but France had gained a foothold in Tonkin and the right to intervene further.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1883": "Tu Duc died, Henri Rivière was killed in Tonkin, and France imposed the Harmand Treaty on the court at Hue. With succession crises, rapid changes of emperor and French troops in the north, the dynasty lost its sovereignty and became a protected monarchy.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1885": "The Treaty of Tientsin ended Qing suzerainty, and the regent Ton That Thuyet’s attack on the French at Hue failed. Ham Nghi fled to lead the Can Vuong resistance, while France installed Dong Khanh as a compliant emperor. The dynasty survived only as a protected throne.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1897": "Governor-General Paul Doumer abolished the viceroy’s office in Tonkin and placed a French official at the head of the Council of Ministers in Hue. The emperor kept his ritual role and palace, but real administrative authority passed entirely to French residents.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1916": "Emperor Duy Tan joined a failed anti-French uprising and was deposed and exiled to Réunion, together with his father Thanh Thai, who had been deposed in 1907. Khai Dinh, who cooperated closely with France, succeeded, confirming the throne’s role as an instrument of colonial rule.",
  "Nguyen dynasty|1945": "After Japan’s coup of 9 March, Bao Dai proclaimed the Empire of Vietnam under Japanese patronage. Japan’s surrender left it without support; the Viet Minh took power in August, and Bao Dai abdicated on 30 August, ending the dynasty after 143 years.",
  "British Raj|1858": "The Government of India Act transferred the East India Company’s territories to the Crown, and the rebellion of 1857 was finally suppressed. The new Raj controlled the whole subcontinent through direct rule and princely alliances, but it had to rebuild its army, finances and the trust of Indian elites.",
  "British Raj|1861": "The Indian Councils Act and the Indian Civil Service Act set the framework of Crown government, and the Indian Penal Code of 1860 was coming into force. A reorganised army with more British troops and new recruitment patterns secured the Raj against any repeat of 1857.",
  "British Raj|1877": "Queen Victoria was proclaimed Empress of India at the Delhi Durbar, giving the Raj an imperial monarchy. Railways and telegraphs knitted India together and exports grew, but the Great Famine of 1876–1878, which killed millions in southern and western India, revealed the regime’s harsh limits.",
  "British Raj|1886": "The Third Anglo-Burmese War brought Upper Burma under British rule, completing the conquest of Burma, while the Second Anglo-Afghan War had secured British control of Afghan foreign policy. The Raj approached its widest territorial extent, though the founding of Congress in 1885 announced organised Indian politics.",
  "British Raj|1899": "Lord Curzon became viceroy and pursued vigorous administrative reform, frontier policy and imperial display. Despite severe famines in 1896–1897 and 1899–1900 and an outbreak of plague, British authority was unchallenged, the army was being reorganised, and India was the strategic core of the British Empire.",
  "British Raj|1905": "Curzon partitioned Bengal, provoking the Swadeshi movement, boycotts of British goods and early revolutionary violence. The Raj remained militarily and administratively dominant, but for the first time its decisions faced sustained mass opposition from the educated classes of Bengal and beyond.",
  "British Raj|1911": "At the Delhi Durbar, attended by King George V, the partition of Bengal was reversed and the transfer of the capital to Delhi announced. The Raj was at the height of its prestige, wealth and imperial confidence, with nationalist opposition still largely confined to petitions and limited reforms.",
  "British Raj|1918": "India had sent more than a million men to fight in the First World War and contributed heavily to British war finance. The Raj emerged victorious and loyal in arms, but the Montagu Declaration of 1917 had promised progressive self-government, and wartime inflation and recruitment had stirred unrest.",
  "British Raj|1919": "The Rowlatt Acts and the Jallianwala Bagh massacre at Amritsar turned moderate opinion against British rule. The Government of India Act introduced dyarchy in the provinces, but the moral authority of the Raj suffered a blow from which it never fully recovered.",
  "British Raj|1922": "Gandhi’s Non-Cooperation movement mobilised millions before he called it off after the violence at Chauri Chaura and was imprisoned. The Raj had withstood the challenge, but Congress had become a mass party with nationwide reach, and the government increasingly relied on Indian cooperation to rule.",
  "British Raj|1930": "Gandhi’s Salt March to Dandi launched civil disobedience across India, and tens of thousands were imprisoned. The government responded with repression and the Round Table Conferences in London, showing that the constitutional future of India had become a matter of negotiation rather than imperial decree.",
  "British Raj|1935": "The Government of India Act of 1935, the longest act ever passed by Parliament at the time, granted provincial autonomy and planned an all-India federation that never came into being. Britain still held the centre, the army and foreign policy, but power was steadily devolving.",
  "British Raj|1937": "Burma was separated from British India, and elections brought Congress ministries to power in most provinces. Indian politicians now governed much of the subcontinent’s domestic affairs, and the Raj’s direct authority shrank to the central government, the army and the princely states.",
  "British Raj|1942": "Japanese conquest of Burma brought the war to India’s frontier, and the Cripps Mission failed. The Quit India movement was crushed by mass arrests, but the Raj now depended on force and on wartime emergency powers to keep control of a country increasingly opposed to it.",
  "British Raj|1945": "Victory over Japan, with a huge Indian Army, left Britain in possession of India but financially exhausted, and the Bengal famine of 1943 had discredited the government. Trials of Indian National Army officers provoked mass protest, and Labour’s election victory brought a government committed to withdrawal.",
  "British Raj|1946": "The Royal Indian Navy mutiny, the failure of the Cabinet Mission plan and the communal killings that followed Direct Action Day in Calcutta showed that Britain could no longer keep order. The Raj was preparing its exit amid an interim government led by Jawaharlal Nehru.",
  "British Raj|1947": "Lord Mountbatten brought forward the date of withdrawal, and the Indian Independence Act created India and Pakistan on 14–15 August. The Raj ended amid partition violence and the migration of millions across the new borders drawn by the Radcliffe Boundary Commission.",
  "French Indochina|1887": "France grouped Cochinchina, Annam, Tonkin and Cambodia into the Indochinese Union by decree of 17 October. The federation existed largely on paper: resistance continued in Tonkin and Annam, finances depended on subsidies from Paris, and the governor-general had little control over the separate territories.",
  "French Indochina|1893": "The Franco-Siamese crisis forced Siam to cede the lands east of the Mekong, which France organised as Laos. The Union now covered most of the eastern mainland of Southeast Asia, though large upland areas were barely administered and armed resistance persisted in parts of Tonkin.",
  "French Indochina|1897": "Paul Doumer became governor-general and began turning the Union into a centralised state, with a general budget funded by the opium, salt and alcohol monopolies and an ambitious programme of railways and public works. Laos was formally attached to the Union in 1899.",
  "French Indochina|1902": "The seat of government moved from Saigon to Hanoi, and the Long Bien bridge over the Red River was completed. Doumer left a colony with federal services, a balanced budget and large public works under way, firmly controlled though deeply unpopular because of its taxes and monopolies.",
  "French Indochina|1910": "The Yunnan railway from Haiphong to Kunming was completed, extending French commercial reach into southern China. Armed resistance was nearly over, Cambodia had regained Battambang and Siem Reap from Siam in 1907, and rice exports were rising, making Indochina France’s most valuable Asian possession.",
  "French Indochina|1917": "Albert Sarraut returned as governor-general, promising collaboration and expanding education. Indochina sent tens of thousands of soldiers and workers to France during the war, and the colony remained calm and profitable, though Vietnamese plots, including the Duy Tan conspiracy of 1916, showed persistent opposition.",
  "French Indochina|1929": "The colonial economy reached its peak: rubber plantations boomed, rice exports were near record levels and French investment surged in the 1920s. The federation was fully administered and its revenues were high, just before the Depression and the uprisings of 1930 shook it.",
  "French Indochina|1931": "The Great Depression collapsed rice and rubber prices, and the Yen Bai mutiny and the Nghe-Tinh soviets were crushed with bombing and mass arrests. French rule survived intact, but the colony’s revenues fell sharply and the communist movement emerged as its most determined opponent.",
  "French Indochina|1936": "The Trans-Indochinese Railway from Hanoi to Saigon was completed, and the Popular Front government in France eased repression, briefly allowing open political activity. The economy had recovered, and French control appeared secure, giving the federation its last period of stability under unchallenged French authority.",
  "French Indochina|1940": "After France’s defeat, Japan forced Governor-General Jean Decoux to admit its troops into northern Indochina in September. French administration continued, but sovereignty was compromised and the colony was cut off from France, dependent on Japanese tolerance to keep governing its territories.",
  "French Indochina|1941": "Japanese mediation ended the Franco-Thai War by transferring parts of Cambodia and Laos to Thailand, and Japanese troops moved into the south. Indochina became a base for Japan’s campaigns in Southeast Asia, with French officials administering the territories under Japanese supervision.",
  "French Indochina|1945": "Japan’s coup of 9 March overthrew French rule, and after Japan’s surrender the Viet Minh seized power and proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam on 2 September. French Indochina had effectively ceased to exist, with French authority surviving only in exile and in plans for return.",
  "French Indochina|1946": "French forces reoccupied the south, Cambodia and Laos, and the 6 March accords allowed them into the north. Recast as the Indochinese Federation in the French Union, the colony was partly restored, but war with the Viet Minh broke out in December.",
  "French Indochina|1949": "The Élysée Accords made Vietnam under Bao Dai, with Laos and Cambodia, Associated States within the French Union. France still controlled defence and the main cities, but the federation was being dismantled politically while the war against the Viet Minh continued.",
  "French Indochina|1950": "Communist China and the Soviet Union recognised the Viet Minh, Chinese aid began to flow, and French forces were routed in the frontier battles along Route Coloniale 4. The United States started financing the French war effort, but the military balance had turned against France.",
  "French Indochina|1954": "The fall of Dien Bien Phu on 7 May led to the Geneva Accords in July, which recognised the independence of Laos and Cambodia and divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel. French Indochina came to an end after sixty-seven years.",
  "Indonesia|1945": "Sukarno and Mohammad Hatta proclaimed independence on 17 August 1945, two days after Japan\u0027s surrender. The new republic had a constitution, a president and enthusiastic popular support on Java and Sumatra, but no international recognition, an improvised army and a returning colonial power determined to reclaim the Dutch East Indies.",
  "Indonesia|1949": "After two Dutch military offensives, guerrilla resistance and mounting American and United Nations pressure, the Round Table Conference at The Hague ended the revolution. On 27 December 1949 the Netherlands transferred sovereignty to the federal United States of Indonesia, giving the nationalists recognition but a war-damaged, divided and deeply indebted state.",
  "Indonesia|1955": "The unitary republic held its first free general election and hosted the Asian–African Conference at Bandung, giving Indonesia standing as a leader of newly independent nations. At home, however, coalition cabinets fell quickly, regional commanders defied Jakarta and the economy stagnated, limiting the young state\u0027s real capacity.",
  "Indonesia|1959": "Sukarno restored the 1945 constitution by decree and introduced Guided Democracy after the regional PRRI and Permesta rebellions had been crushed. Central authority was reasserted, but the system rested on a fragile balance between the army and the Communist Party, and the economy was sliding towards hyperinflation.",
  "Indonesia|1963": "With western New Guinea transferred to Indonesian administration after Operation Trikora and United Nations mediation, Sukarno\u0027s state reached its greatest territorial extent before 1975. Its international profile was high, but the launch of the Confrontation against Malaysia overstretched resources and isolated Indonesia from Western partners.",
  "Indonesia|1965": "The failed coup of 30 September 1965 and the mass killings that followed tore the country apart. Indonesia had left the United Nations in January, inflation was running into hundreds of per cent and government authority was split between Sukarno, the army and a collapsing Communist Party.",
  "Indonesia|1968": "Suharto, acting president since 1967, was formally elected president. The New Order ended the Confrontation, rejoined the United Nations, co-founded ASEAN and brought inflation under control with technocratic advisers and Western aid, restoring order and creditworthiness at the cost of political freedom.",
  "Indonesia|1975": "Oil revenues after the 1973 price rise funded roads, schools and rice programmes, and the regime felt strong enough to invade East Timor in December 1975. The near-collapse of the state oil company Pertamina that year exposed corruption and weak oversight within a fast-growing state.",
  "Indonesia|1984": "Indonesia reached self-sufficiency in rice, a landmark of the Green Revolution, and was beginning to shift from oil towards manufactured exports. The New Order was at the height of its domestic control, with Golkar dominant and the army entrenched throughout government, while poverty fell steadily.",
  "Indonesia|1997": "Before the crisis, Indonesia was hailed as an Asian tiger, with three decades of growth averaging around seven per cent a year, a large industrial sector and rising regional influence. Wealth was concentrated in Suharto\u0027s family and allied conglomerates, a weakness the financial crisis would expose.",
  "Indonesia|1998": "The Asian financial crisis wiped out most of the rupiah\u0027s value, the economy contracted by roughly thirteen per cent, riots struck Jakarta and Suharto resigned on 21 May. The state survived intact, but its economy and authority were badly weakened and secessionist pressures rose.",
  "Indonesia|1999": "The first free election since 1955 was held, and East Timor voted for independence in a referendum followed by militia violence. Reformasi amendments and decentralisation laws began reshaping the state, while communal conflicts in Maluku and Kalimantan showed how fragile order remained.",
  "Indonesia|2004": "Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono won the first direct presidential election, completing the democratic transition. Growth had recovered and institutions such as the Constitutional Court and the anti-corruption commission were functioning, though the December tsunami devastated Aceh and killed well over 100,000 people.",
  "Indonesia|2005": "The Helsinki agreement ended the long war in Aceh, granting the province special autonomy. With political stability, steady growth and a reputation as a successful Muslim-majority democracy, Indonesia regained regional leadership and became a regular participant in G20 summits from 2008.",
  "Indonesia|2014": "Joko Widodo, a furniture exporter turned mayor and governor, won the presidency as the first leader from outside the political and military elite. His government invested heavily in roads, ports and dams and expanded health insurance, and during his first term annual output passed one trillion US dollars.",
  "Indonesia|2022": "Indonesia held the G20 presidency and hosted the leaders\u0027 summit on Bali, while construction began on the new capital, Nusantara. Nickel processing after the ban on raw ore exports made it central to battery supply chains, and the economy had recovered from the COVID-19 pandemic.",
  "Indonesia|2025": "Under President Prabowo Subianto, inaugurated in October 2024, Indonesia stands at its greatest economic weight and diplomatic reach, with some 280 million people and the largest economy in Southeast Asia. Concerns persist about democratic backsliding, the military\u0027s growing civilian role and environmental damage.",
  "Myanmar|1948": "The Union of Burma became independent on 4 January 1948 with an elected government under U Nu and a federal constitution. The new state inherited war-damaged infrastructure, a valuable rice economy and an army divided along ethnic lines, and lacked the leadership of the assassinated Aung San.",
  "Myanmar|1949": "The Communist Party of Burma and the Karen National Union were in open revolt, army units had mutinied, and Karen forces fought at Insein on the edge of Rangoon. For several months the government held little more than the capital, the weakest point in the union\u0027s early history.",
  "Myanmar|1955": "The government had pushed the insurgents back from central Burma, and rice exports were funding the Pyidawtha welfare plan. U Nu\u0027s prominence at the Bandung Conference gave the union real international standing, although Kuomintang troops and ethnic rebels still held large areas of the borderlands.",
  "Myanmar|1958": "The ruling AFPFL split and U Nu invited General Ne Win to head a caretaker government. The army restored order in towns, cleaned up Rangoon and held elections in 1960, showing both the military\u0027s capacity and its growing confidence that it, rather than politicians, should govern.",
  "Myanmar|1962": "Ne Win\u0027s coup of 2 March 1962 ended parliamentary government and the federal arrangements. Central control over the state reached its peak under the Revolutionary Council, but nationalisation and isolation soon began to undermine the economy that sustained the state\u0027s power.",
  "Myanmar|1974": "A new constitution created the Socialist Republic of the Union of Burma under the single-party rule of the Burma Socialist Programme Party. The regime was secure in the central plains, but rice exports had collapsed, shortages were common and insurgents held much of the frontier.",
  "Myanmar|1987": "The government\u0027s second demonetisation in two years wiped out most of the currency in circulation, and the United Nations classed Burma as a least developed country. Once Asia\u0027s leading rice exporter, the state was bankrupt and its legitimacy was draining away.",
  "Myanmar|1988": "The nationwide uprising of 8 August 1988 brought down the socialist order, and the army reasserted control through massacres that killed perhaps 3,000 people. Western aid stopped, the economy was in ruins and the new State Law and Order Restoration Council ruled by decree.",
  "Myanmar|1990": "The junta held an election, which the National League for Democracy won overwhelmingly, and then refused to transfer power. Ceasefires with the Wa, Kokang and other armed groups ended some wars, and selective opening to foreign trade began a modest recovery under international sanctions.",
  "Myanmar|1997": "Myanmar joined ASEAN and the junta, renamed the State Peace and Development Council, had expanded the army sharply and signed ceasefires with most major armed groups. Gas exports to Thailand were about to begin, giving the regime its first large source of revenue.",
  "Myanmar|2006": "The new capital at Naypyidaw was formally named, symbolising a regime confident of its permanence. Natural gas revenues and Chinese investment strengthened state finances, though the population remained among the poorest in Asia, Aung San Suu Kyi was again under house arrest and political repression continued.",
  "Myanmar|2008": "Cyclone Nargis killed about 138,000 people in the Irrawaddy delta, and the junta\u0027s initial refusal of foreign aid provoked international outrage. Days later it pushed through a constitution entrenching military power in a referendum widely regarded as neither free nor fair.",
  "Myanmar|2011": "President Thein Sein\u0027s quasi-civilian government released political prisoners, relaxed censorship and opened talks with ethnic armies. Western sanctions began to be lifted, investment flowed in and Myanmar gained international legitimacy it had lacked for decades, symbolised by Barack Obama\u0027s visit in 2012.",
  "Myanmar|2016": "The NLD government took office after its landslide victory in 2015, with Aung San Suu Kyi as State Counsellor. Economic growth, foreign investment, telecommunications expansion and diplomatic standing were at their highest since independence, though the military kept its constitutional powers.",
  "Myanmar|2017": "Army operations in Rakhine State after attacks by the Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army drove over 700,000 Rohingya into Bangladesh. The atrocities destroyed much of Myanmar\u0027s international goodwill, led to new sanctions and later to genocide proceedings at the International Court of Justice.",
  "Myanmar|2021": "The coup of 1 February 2021 overthrew the elected government. Peaceful protests were met with lethal force, the economy contracted by nearly a fifth, and the opposition formed a National Unity Government and People\u0027s Defence Forces, beginning civil war across the country.",
  "Myanmar|2023": "Operation 1027 by the Three Brotherhood Alliance captured towns and border crossings in northern Shan State, and resistance forces advanced in Rakhine, Chin, Kachin and Karenni areas. The junta suffered its worst military losses since independence and introduced conscription in early 2024.",
  "Myanmar|2025": "Myanmar remains fragmented, with the military regime controlling the central cities and much of the Irrawaddy valley while ethnic armies and resistance groups hold large parts of the borderlands. Millions are displaced, the economy is shrunken and the state\u0027s authority is at one of its lowest points.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1948": "On 25 March 1948 nine minor states of south-eastern Rajputana and two chiefships formed the first union, with its capital at Kota. It united territories of modest size, revenue and population, with an improvised administration and little weight beside the great Rajput states that still stood apart.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1948.3": "Udaipur joined on 18 April 1948 and Nehru inaugurated the enlarged United State of Rajasthan, with the Maharana of Mewar as Rajpramukh and Manikya Lal Varma heading the government. The prestige of the oldest Rajput dynasty doubled the union\u0027s standing, though Jaipur, Jodhpur and Bikaner remained outside.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1948.6": "Through the second half of 1948 the union worked to merge the laws, revenue systems and services of its member states, while the States Ministry negotiated with the larger states over capital, privy purses and offices. Its territory and resources remained unchanged while those negotiations continued.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1949.2": "On 30 March 1949 Jaipur, Jodhpur, Bikaner and Jaisalmer joined to form Greater Rajasthan, inaugurated by Vallabhbhai Patel at Jaipur, the new capital. The union now covered most of Rajputana and its desert frontier with Pakistan, several times enlarging its area and greatly increasing its population and revenue.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1949.4": "The Matsya Union of Alwar, Bharatpur, Dholpur and Karauli joined on 15 May 1949, adding the populous and fertile eastern districts. The union now included every major state of Rajputana except Sirohi, and Hiralal Shastri\u0027s government was building a single administration from Jaipur.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1949.7": "The Rajasthan High Court was inaugurated at Jodhpur in August 1949, giving the union a single judiciary, and the State Forces were being transferred to the Indian Army. The union\u0027s administration was consolidating, though jagirdari privileges and divided local loyalties still limited the reach of government.",
  "United State of Rajasthan|1950": "On 26 January 1950, as the Constitution of India came into force, most of Sirohi was merged and the union became the state of Rajasthan. At its moment of completion it was at its greatest extent and institutional strength, ending as a constituent state of the Indian republic.",
  "Republic of India|1950": "The constitution came into force on 26 January 1950, creating a sovereign republic of some 360 million people. India had just absorbed the princely states and survived partition, but it was overwhelmingly rural, mostly illiterate and poor, with weak industry and a hostile neighbour in Pakistan.",
  "Republic of India|1952": "The first general election, held from October 1951 to February 1952 with about 173 million voters, gave Nehru\u0027s Congress a large majority and established the legitimacy of the democratic republic. The first five-year plan concentrated on agriculture and irrigation in a still very poor economy.",
  "Republic of India|1956": "The States Reorganisation Act redrew internal boundaries along linguistic lines, settling a dangerous source of conflict, and the second five-year plan launched state-led heavy industry. Nehru was a leading voice of the newly independent world, giving India influence beyond its military or economic weight.",
  "Republic of India|1961": "Indian troops took Goa from Portugal in December, and India co-founded the Non-Aligned Movement at Belgrade. Steel plants, dams and new universities were rising, though growth remained modest and the country still depended on American food aid in bad years.",
  "Republic of India|1962": "Defeat by China in the Sino-Indian War of October–November 1962 exposed India\u0027s military unpreparedness and damaged Nehru\u0027s prestige. The shock led to a rapid expansion of the armed forces, but in the short term India\u0027s international standing and confidence fell sharply.",
  "Republic of India|1966": "After an inconclusive war with Pakistan in 1965 and two failed monsoons, India faced severe food shortages and was forced to devalue the rupee. Indira Gandhi became prime minister in January 1966 at one of the most difficult moments for the young republic.",
  "Republic of India|1971": "India\u0027s intervention in the Bangladesh Liberation War ended with the surrender of some 93,000 Pakistani soldiers at Dhaka and the creation of Bangladesh. With the Green Revolution reducing dependence on food imports and a treaty with the Soviet Union, India was the undisputed power of South Asia.",
  "Republic of India|1975": "India had tested a nuclear device in 1974, but inflation, strikes and protest movements led Indira Gandhi to declare the Emergency in June 1975, suspending civil liberties and jailing opponents. The state was strong in coercive power but its democratic legitimacy was badly damaged.",
  "Republic of India|1977": "In the March 1977 election voters threw out Indira Gandhi and brought the Janata Party to power, the first non-Congress government. Democracy was restored, but the fractious coalition collapsed within two years and the economy remained slow-growing and heavily regulated.",
  "Republic of India|1984": "Operation Blue Star at the Golden Temple, the assassination of Indira Gandhi, the anti-Sikh massacres in Delhi and the Bhopal gas disaster made 1984 a year of crisis. Insurgency in Punjab continued, though cautious economic reforms were beginning to raise growth.",
  "Republic of India|1991": "A balance-of-payments crisis left India with foreign reserves for only a few weeks of imports, and Rajiv Gandhi was assassinated. The new government launched liberalising reforms that ended the licence system, laying the foundation for the faster growth of the following decades.",
  "Republic of India|1998": "Nuclear tests at Pokhran in May 1998 made India a declared nuclear-weapon state, and growth after the 1991 reforms had raised its economic weight. Sanctions followed briefly, but India\u0027s strategic standing rose, confirmed the next year by victory in the Kargil War.",
  "Republic of India|2004": "Growth of around eight per cent a year, a booming information technology industry and expanding trade made India one of the world\u0027s fastest-growing major economies. A peaceful change of government from Vajpayee to Manmohan Singh confirmed the stability of Indian democracy.",
  "Republic of India|2008": "The civil nuclear agreement with the United States ended India\u0027s nuclear isolation, and India joined the first G20 leaders\u0027 summit. The Mumbai attacks of November 2008 showed continuing vulnerability to terrorism, but the economy came through the global financial crisis relatively well.",
  "Republic of India|2014": "Narendra Modi\u0027s Bharatiya Janata Party won the first single-party majority in thirty years, and the Mars Orbiter Mission reached Mars. India had become one of the ten largest economies, with a more assertive foreign policy and growing ties with the United States, Japan and the Gulf.",
  "Republic of India|2020": "The COVID-19 pandemic and a strict national lockdown caused the sharpest economic contraction since independence, and the Galwan clash in June brought India and China to their worst border confrontation in decades. India nonetheless remained among the world\u0027s largest economies and military powers.",
  "Republic of India|2023": "India overtook China as the most populous country, landed Chandrayaan-3 near the Moon\u0027s south pole and hosted the G20 summit in New Delhi. It was the fastest-growing major economy and the fifth largest by nominal output, at the height of its international standing.",
  "Republic of India|2025": "India stands at the peak of its weight in the world, with over 1.4 billion people, a large and fast-growing economy, nuclear weapons and wide diplomatic reach. The 2024 election returned Modi for a third term, now in coalition, after a vigorous democratic contest.",
  "Malaysia|1963": "Malaysia was proclaimed on 16 September 1963, joining Malaya with Singapore, Sabah and Sarawak. The new federation had a solid rubber and tin economy and British military backing, but faced Indonesian Confrontation, a Philippine claim to Sabah and deep tensions between Kuala Lumpur and Singapore.",
  "Malaysia|1965": "Singapore was separated from the federation on 9 August 1965 after bitter disputes over Malay privileges and the place of the Chinese, while Indonesian raids continued in Borneo. Malaysia lost its main commercial city and much of its political confidence in the space of two years.",
  "Malaysia|1966": "The Confrontation ended with an agreement signed in August 1966, after Suharto replaced Sukarno in Indonesia. Secure borders, steady commodity exports and continuing Commonwealth defence support let the federation consolidate, and it joined ASEAN as a founding member the following year.",
  "Malaysia|1969": "The racial riots of 13 May 1969 in Kuala Lumpur, after an election in which the Alliance lost ground, led to a state of emergency and suspension of parliament. Rule passed to the National Operations Council, and the political settlement of independence had to be rebuilt.",
  "Malaysia|1971": "Parliament returned, and the New Economic Policy was launched to reduce poverty and restructure wealth between ethnic groups. The Rukun Negara ideology and the broader Barisan Nasional coalition formed soon after restored political stability, and free trade zones began to draw in electronics manufacturing.",
  "Malaysia|1981": "Mahathir Mohamad became prime minister. Oil revenues through Petronas, rising manufacturing and the NEP\u0027s expansion of the Malay middle class had made Malaysia one of the faster-growing developing economies, and Mahathir launched the Look East policy, modelled on Japan and South Korea, and heavy-industry ambitions.",
  "Malaysia|1985": "A recession caused by falling commodity prices tested the economy, and the national car Proton Saga entered production. Liberalising reforms that followed, encouraging foreign investment and privatisation, prepared the ground for a decade of rapid industrial growth despite the political turmoil of 1987–1988.",
  "Malaysia|1990": "The NEP period ended with poverty sharply reduced and a large Malay business class created, and the communist insurgency had ended with the peace accord of 1989. Malaysia entered seven years of growth averaging over eight per cent a year, driven by electronics exports.",
  "Malaysia|1997": "Before the Asian financial crisis, Malaysia was at the height of its boom, building the Petronas Twin Towers, Putrajaya and a new international airport. Its economy, diplomatic profile and confidence had never been greater, though rapid credit growth had created vulnerabilities.",
  "Malaysia|1998": "The financial crisis caused an economic contraction of around seven per cent. Mahathir imposed capital controls in September and dismissed his deputy Anwar Ibrahim, whose arrest sparked the Reformasi protests and split the Malay electorate, weakening the governing coalition\u0027s legitimacy.",
  "Malaysia|2003": "The economy had recovered strongly from the crisis, and Mahathir handed power to Abdullah Ahmad Badawi after twenty-two years. Malaysia was a stable, upper-middle-income industrial exporter with a strong position in ASEAN and the Islamic world, and the Petronas Twin Towers and Putrajaya stood as symbols of its rise.",
  "Malaysia|2008": "The opposition denied the Barisan Nasional its two-thirds parliamentary majority for the first time since 1969 and won five states. The economy remained solid through the global financial crisis, but the long-dominant political order had begun to fracture, and Abdullah handed over to Najib Razak the following year.",
  "Malaysia|2015": "Under Najib Razak the economy kept growing, but revelations about the 1MDB state fund, from which billions of dollars had been diverted, damaged Malaysia\u0027s reputation. The losses of flights MH370 and MH17 the previous year had also brought international attention and grief.",
  "Malaysia|2018": "The Pakatan Harapan coalition defeated the Barisan Nasional on 9 May 2018, the first change of government since independence, and Mahathir returned as prime minister. The peaceful transfer of power strengthened democratic legitimacy, while the economy remained steady and investigations into 1MDB were reopened against the former prime minister, Najib Razak.",
  "Malaysia|2020": "The Pakatan government collapsed in the Sheraton Move of February 2020, and COVID-19 brought a severe contraction of the economy. Political instability, with three prime ministers in under three years, weakened government effectiveness during a public health emergency and delayed recovery until the economy reopened in 2021–2022.",
  "Malaysia|2022": "After a hung parliament in November 2022, Anwar Ibrahim formed a unity government. The economy had recovered strongly, with growth of almost nine per cent that year, and Malaysia\u0027s role in global semiconductor supply chains, especially chip assembly and testing in Penang, was expanding.",
  "Malaysia|2025": "Malaysia stands at its greatest economic weight, an upper-middle-income country with a large semiconductor sector, stable federal institutions and an active role in ASEAN, whose chairmanship it held in 2025. Ethnic politics, corruption and federal relations with Sabah and Sarawak remain central questions.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1976": "The National Assembly proclaimed the Socialist Republic on 2 July, uniting north and south under Hanoi. The state commanded a huge, battle-hardened army and real prestige from its victory, but its economy was shattered by decades of war, and the integration of the south was only beginning.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1978": "Vietnam joined Comecon, signed a friendship treaty with the Soviet Union and invaded Cambodia in December to overthrow the Khmer Rouge. Military power was at its height, but nationalisation of southern trade, failing collectives and the first waves of boat people revealed deep economic weakness.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1979": "Phnom Penh fell in January and a client government took over Cambodia, but China struck across the northern border in February. The short Sino-Vietnamese War, Western and ASEAN sanctions and the refugee exodus left Vietnam isolated and heavily dependent on Soviet aid.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1985": "A badly handled price, wage and currency reform triggered inflation of several hundred per cent. Food shortages, the cost of occupying Cambodia and continued border fighting with China pushed the country to its lowest point, among the poorest states in the world despite its large army.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1986": "Le Duan died in July, and in December the Sixth Party Congress under Nguyen Van Linh adopted Doi Moi. The decision to restore family farming, private enterprise and market prices marked the turning point, although the economy was still in crisis and the Cambodian occupation continued.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1989": "Reforms began to work: Vietnam went from importing rice to becoming one of the largest rice exporters, and inflation was brought under control. The last Vietnamese troops left Cambodia in September, removing a heavy burden and opening the way to ending international isolation.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1991": "The collapse of the Soviet Union ended the aid on which Vietnam had depended, while relations with China were normalised in November. The Seventh Congress confirmed the reform course under Do Muoi. Power held steady as the state sought new trading partners in Asia and the West.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|1995": "Vietnam normalised diplomatic relations with the United States in July and joined ASEAN the same month, a year after the American trade embargo was lifted. Foreign investment flowed in, growth ran at high rates and the country moved from Cold War pariah to regional partner.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2001": "The bilateral trade agreement with the United States came into force, opening the American market to Vietnamese textiles, footwear and seafood. Exports surged, and Nong Duc Manh became general secretary. Growth of around seven per cent a year steadily raised the country’s economic weight.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2007": "Vietnam became a member of the World Trade Organization in January, locking in market reforms and deepening integration into global trade. Manufacturing investment accelerated, and the country also prepared for its first term on the United Nations Security Council in 2008–2009.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2011": "Nguyen Phu Trong became general secretary. Vietnam had reached lower-middle-income status, and large electronics plants, led by Samsung, were turning it into a major exporter of phones. Tensions with China in the South China Sea, however, prompted naval modernisation and closer ties with other powers.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2016": "Vietnam took delivery of more Kilo-class submarines, the United States lifted its arms embargo during a visit by President Obama, and exports kept rising. The economy was growing quickly, but the party also launched its sweeping anti-corruption drive, signalling tighter political control.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2020": "Vietnam chaired ASEAN and sat on the Security Council again while containing the first waves of COVID-19 with strict measures. It remained one of the few economies to grow that year, and supply chains moving out of China made it a leading manufacturing destination.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2023": "Relations with the United States were raised to a comprehensive strategic partnership in September, while ties with China were also upgraded. With about 100 million people, record trade and expanding diplomatic reach, Vietnam stood at the height of its economic and international standing.",
  "Socialist Republic of Vietnam|2025": "Vietnam remained a fast-growing, trade-dependent economy and a significant middle power in Southeast Asia, governed by the Communist Party since 1976. After the death of Nguyen Phu Trong in 2024 and To Lam’s rise to party leadership, the state kept its course of reform and balanced diplomacy.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1000": "Hausa-speaking farming communities were building the first walled towns on the savanna. Kano tradition places its first king, Bagauda, around 999, and Daura was remembered as the oldest state. Power was local and fragmented, with small chiefdoms centred on sacred sites, iron-working and markets.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1150": "The early Hausa states were taking shape as rulers extended authority over surrounding villages and built larger walls. Trade with the north through the Sahara and with neighbouring peoples was growing, but the kingdoms remained small, competing chiefdoms overshadowed by the Kanem empire to the east.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1350": "According to the Kano Chronicle, Wangarawa merchants from Mali brought Islam to Kano in the reign of Sarki Yaji. Kano and Katsina were growing commercial centres, and Muslim traders linked them to wider networks, though the kingdoms were still modest in size and power.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1450": "The Hausa states were expanding their trade and their armoured cavalry. Kano is said to have begun paying tribute to Bornu around this time, while Katsina’s first Muslim rulers strengthened its position on the caravan routes. Urban growth and crafts were steadily raising the region’s wealth.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1463": "Muhammad Rumfa became king of Kano. His reign brought a new palace, the Kurmi market, extended city walls and Islamic reforms, and Kano fought a long war with Katsina. Rumfa’s Kano became the model of a centralised Hausa monarchy with a titled bureaucracy.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1499": "At Rumfa’s death Kano and Katsina were among the leading cities of the central Sudan, visited by scholars such as Muhammad al-Maghili. Hausa cloth and leather moved along trans-Saharan routes, and the kingdoms had reached a level of wealth and organisation unmatched in their earlier history.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1513": "Songhai forces under Askia Muhammad probably campaigned in Hausaland around this time, and some states may have paid tribute. The extent of Songhai control is debated, but the episode showed the Hausa kingdoms’ vulnerability to the great Sahelian empires on either side.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1516": "Kanta Kotal of Kebbi revolted against Songhai and made Kebbi the dominant power in the western Hausa region. Other states faced Kebbi’s pressure as well as Bornu’s claims to tribute, leaving the older Hausa kingdoms constrained by stronger neighbours for much of the century.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1570": "Hausa commerce was flourishing. Zazzau expanded southwards, an era associated in tradition with the warrior queen Amina, whose historicity is uncertain. Kano and Katsina grew as manufacturing and trading cities, and their merchants extended their reach along the caravan routes.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1600": "After Morocco destroyed the Songhai Empire in 1591, trans-Saharan trade increasingly ran through Hausaland. Katsina and Kano became the principal markets of the central Sudan, and Islamic scholarship grew in their towns, although the kingdoms still competed and fought among themselves.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1675": "In the later seventeenth century the Kwararafa (Jukun) confederacy from the Benue valley raided Kano and Katsina, and famines struck the region. The cities recovered, but the raids exposed the limits of their military power and the cost of rivalry among the Hausa states.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1700": "Hausaland was again prosperous. Katsina in particular flourished as a centre of trade and Muslim learning, attracting scholars and merchants from across the Sudan. Cowrie currency, textile production and long-distance trade grew, and Hausa merchants spread their language well beyond their homeland.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1750": "The Hausa kingdoms stood at the height of their collective wealth and population. Katsina and Kano dominated the commerce of the central Sudan, Gobir and Zamfara were strong military states, and Hausa had become the leading trade language. Rivalry, however, was growing more destructive.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1762": "Around this time Gobir destroyed Birnin Zamfara, the capital of Zamfara, and became the strongest military power in northern Hausaland. Gobir’s wars with Katsina and Kano followed, raising taxes and slave raiding and worsening tensions with Fulani pastoralists and Muslim scholars.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1790": "Continual warfare and heavy taxation strained the Hausa states. In Gobir the preacher Usman dan Fodio had gathered a large community calling for reform, while Gobir’s rulers tried to restrict his movement. The kingdoms remained wealthy but politically divided and increasingly unpopular.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1804": "Usman dan Fodio’s hijra to Gudu and the victory at Tabkin Kwotto launched the jihad. Fulani leaders across Hausaland raised revolt under his flags, and the Hausa rulers, unable to unite against a shared threat, faced attack from within their own kingdoms.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1807": "Kano, Katsina and other kingdoms had fallen to jihadist forces, and their sarakuna fled. Fulani emirs took over the cities and their courts. Only Gobir, around its capital Alkalawa, and a few exiled royal lines still resisted the expanding Sokoto Caliphate.",
  "Hausa kingdoms|1808": "Jihadist forces captured Alkalawa and killed Sarki Yunfa of Gobir, completing the overthrow of the Hausa dynasties. The Sokoto Caliphate absorbed their territory and institutions, while exiled rulers carried on at Abuja, Maradi and Tsibiri as small successor states that kept Hausa royal traditions alive.",
  "Mombasa|1100": "A Swahili-speaking community of farmers, fishers and traders was living on Mombasa island, with its deep harbour. The town was small and drew modest income from fishing, farming and coastal trade, overshadowed by older and wealthier ports elsewhere on the East African coast.",
  "Mombasa|1154": "The geographer al-Idrisi mentioned Mombasa as a small town on the coast of the Zanj, the first clear notice of it in Arabic geography. It was a modest port, beginning to take part in the monsoon trade with Arabia and the Persian Gulf.",
  "Mombasa|1250": "Islam was spreading among the coastal towns, and Mombasa was growing as a Muslim trading centre. Its harbour drew more dhows, and its merchants dealt in ivory and other goods from the hinterland, though Kilwa to the south still dominated the profitable gold trade.",
  "Mombasa|1331": "Ibn Battuta visited and described a large island town with well-built wooden mosques and pious Sunni inhabitants who imported grain from the mainland. Mombasa was now a respectable Muslim port, though still less wealthy than Kilwa, which Ibn Battuta praised as one of the finest cities.",
  "Mombasa|1400": "The Swahili coast prospered, and Mombasa grew in size and wealth as stone building spread. Its merchants traded ivory, gold and slaves for Indian cloth and Chinese porcelain, and the town began to emerge as a leading power on the northern coast alongside its rival Malindi.",
  "Mombasa|1450": "Kilwa was declining through internal disputes, and Mombasa became one of the strongest towns on the coast. Its stone houses, mosques and busy harbour reflected growing wealth from trade with Sofala, Gujarat and Arabia, and its rulers extended influence over nearby settlements.",
  "Mombasa|1498": "When Vasco da Gama arrived, Mombasa was at the height of its prosperity and among the largest Swahili towns. Its rulers saw the Portuguese as a threat and attempted to seize their ships, beginning a century of hostility, while rival Malindi chose alliance with the newcomers.",
  "Mombasa|1505": "Francisco de Almeida stormed, sacked and burned Mombasa in August after fierce fighting, carrying off rich booty while the ruler fled to the mainland. The attack shattered the town’s wealth and showed that its defenders could not resist Portuguese artillery and armour.",
  "Mombasa|1515": "Mombasa had rebuilt and remained a large trading town, as described by Duarte Barbosa. It refused to accept Portuguese overlordship and kept up its trade with Arabia and India, although Portuguese patrols and demands for tribute disrupted the commerce of the Swahili coast.",
  "Mombasa|1528": "A Portuguese fleet under Nuno da Cunha attacked and burned Mombasa again, punishing its continued hostility. The town lost much of its population and property for a second time, and its rulers withdrew to the mainland until the fleet departed for India.",
  "Mombasa|1550": "Mombasa had recovered once more and stood as the chief independent Swahili town north of the Portuguese bases. It maintained trade with Arabia and the hinterland and resisted paying tribute, while Malindi remained the Portuguese ally and rival on the coast.",
  "Mombasa|1585": "The Ottoman corsair Mir Ali Bey appeared off the coast, and Mombasa and other towns welcomed him as a liberator from the Portuguese. The town’s hopes of independence revived, and it became the centre of the anti-Portuguese movement on the Swahili coast.",
  "Mombasa|1589": "A Portuguese fleet from Goa trapped Mir Ali Bey at Mombasa while the Zimba raiders arrived from the mainland. The Portuguese captured Mir Ali, and the Zimba crossed to the island and killed many inhabitants, leaving the city depopulated and broken.",
  "Mombasa|1592": "Around this time the Segeju, allies of Malindi, are said to have attacked Mombasa and killed its ruler. The weakened town could no longer defend itself, and the Portuguese planned a permanent fortress on its harbour to secure the coast.",
  "Mombasa|1593": "The Portuguese began building Fort Jesus and installed the ruler of Malindi as sultan of Mombasa under their protection. This ended Mombasa’s independence as a Swahili city-state and turned the island into the headquarters of Portuguese power in East Africa.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1250": "Somali clans in the inter-riverine region were adopting Islam and competing for wells and grazing. Traditions place the rise of the Garen lineage, with its claim to holy Arab descent, around this period, though the date is uncertain and the Ajuran was still a local confederation.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1350": "The Ajuran confederation was probably extending its authority over neighbouring pastoral clans and farming communities along the Shabelle. Mogadishu, praised by Ibn Battuta in 1331 for its cloth and trade, flourished on the coast, giving the interior a growing market.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1400": "Traditions suggest that Ajuran control of wells and river water was becoming the basis of a tribute system. Trade between the interior and the Benadir ports grew, and Ming Chinese fleets visited Mogadishu and Barawa in the early fifteenth century, reflecting the coast’s prosperity.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1450": "Ajuran authority had probably spread across much of the Shabelle valley and the plains towards the coast. Governors and tax collectors supervised wells and markets, and the alliance with coastal ports drew Somali livestock products, ivory and gums into Indian Ocean trade.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1499": "The arrival of the Portuguese in the Indian Ocean brought a new threat when Vasco da Gama bombarded Mogadishu. The Ajuran and the coastal towns remained strong, however, and the Portuguese failed to establish control over the Somali ports of the Benadir coast.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1507": "A Portuguese fleet sacked Barawa, disrupting the trade of the Benadir coast. Mogadishu resisted, and the interior was untouched, but the attacks showed the vulnerability of the ports on which the Ajuran depended for access to Indian Ocean commerce and imported goods.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1550": "The Ajuran Sultanate was approaching the height of its power, according to tradition, controlling wells, river valleys and caravan routes across southern Somalia. The Muzaffar sultans of Mogadishu were its allies, and its tribute system supported an unusually centralised government.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1585": "The Ottoman corsair Mir Ali Bey won support on the Benadir coast against the Portuguese. The Ajuran stood near its peak, with wide authority in the interior, though how far it directed coastal resistance is uncertain. Oromo expansion was beginning to press on its southern lands.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1600": "Around this time the Ajuran was probably at its greatest extent and strength, dominating the Shabelle valley and much of the southern Somali plains. Its rulers’ control of water and tribute made it the leading power in the region, but heavy exactions were creating resentment.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1650": "Traditions describe growing oppression by the Ajuran rulers, with heavy taxes and extreme demands on the clans. Rival pastoral groups, especially Hawiye clans, were growing stronger, and revolts began to erode central authority over the wells and grazing lands of the south.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1680": "Widespread revolts were tearing the sultanate apart. The Abgaal and related clans challenged Ajuran control around Mogadishu, and the Geledi rose on the lower Shabelle. The Ajuran lost control of key wells and trade routes, and its tribute system collapsed.",
  "Ajuran Sultanate|1700": "By around this date the Ajuran state had disappeared. The Hiraab Imamate controlled the Mogadishu region and the central plains, while the Geledi later dominated the Shabelle valley. Ajuran clans survived, but their dynasty no longer ruled, though some historians date the end slightly later.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1300": "According to tradition, the founding kings Kintu and Kato Kimera established the royal line around this period, though the dates are uncertain. Buganda was a small cluster of clans on the northern shore of Lake Victoria, overshadowed by the far stronger kingdom of Bunyoro-Kitara to the north-west.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1450": "Buganda was still a minor kingdom limited to a few core counties around modern Kampala. Its kabakas fought Bunyoro and smaller neighbours, often on the defensive, while the clans retained much of their power and the plantain economy steadily supported a growing population.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1600": "The kabakas began to expand beyond the core counties, absorbing neighbouring territories such as Singo and Gomba. Wars with Bunyoro continued, and the throne slowly gained authority over the clan heads by rewarding warriors and supporters with appointed chieftaincies and land.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1700": "In the early eighteenth century Kabaka Mawanda extended Buganda eastwards and strengthened royal control by appointing chiefs loyal to the throne. The kingdom was becoming a serious regional power, though Bunyoro remained the larger state and succession struggles among princes often weakened the monarchy.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1780": "Kabakas Junju and Semakookiro conquered the rich province of Buddu from Bunyoro, extending the kingdom along the western shore of Lake Victoria. Buganda now rivalled and soon surpassed Bunyoro, with a growing fleet of war canoes and a strongly centralised system of appointed chiefs.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1832": "Suna II became kabaka and ruled a dominant lake power. Arab and Swahili traders from Zanzibar reached his court in the 1840s, bringing cloth and guns in exchange for ivory and slaves, and Buganda’s armies and canoe fleets raided widely across the region.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1856": "Mutesa I came to the throne and presided over Buganda at the height of its strength. He received Speke in 1862, controlled a large army and fleet, and skilfully balanced the Zanzibari traders, Egyptian ambitions from the north and the growing interest of European explorers.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1875": "Mutesa I’s Buganda stood at its peak, the most powerful and centralised state of the Great Lakes. After Stanley’s visit, the kabaka invited missionaries, and Protestant and Catholic missions arrived in 1877 and 1879, beginning religious rivalries that would later divide the court.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1886": "Mwanga II, who succeeded in 1884, ordered the killing of Bishop James Hannington and the execution of Christian converts, many burned at Namugongo in June 1886. Buganda remained strong, but deep divisions had opened between the kabaka and armed religious factions among the chiefs.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1888": "Christian and Muslim chiefs deposed Mwanga II, and the Muslim faction then seized power, plunging the kingdom into religious civil war. Rival kabakas were installed in quick succession, and Buganda’s unity, built over centuries, broke down as factions fought for control of the court.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1892": "Mwanga had been restored by the Christians, but Protestants and Catholics now fought each other. At the Battle of Mengo, Lugard’s Maxim gun helped the Protestants win. The kingdom was exhausted, divided by religion and increasingly dependent on British support.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1894": "Britain declared a protectorate over Buganda, ending its full independence. Ganda chiefs kept their positions and helped the British conquer neighbouring Bunyoro, but the kabaka now ruled under foreign supervision, and Mwanga’s attempt to resist in 1897 failed, leading to his deposition and exile.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1900": "The Buganda Agreement made the kingdom a privileged province of the Uganda Protectorate, recognising the kabaka, the Lukiiko and the chiefs and granting them freehold mailo land. Buganda prospered from cotton and coffee and supplied agents to administer much of Uganda.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1953": "Governor Andrew Cohen deported Kabaka Mutesa II after a dispute over Uganda’s constitutional future. The crisis united Baganda behind their king, and he returned in 1955, but Buganda’s privileged position within a future Uganda had become a major political problem.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1962": "Uganda became independent, with Buganda holding federal status and Mutesa II soon elected president. The kingdom’s Kabaka Yekka movement allied with Obote’s Uganda People’s Congress, but disputes over the lost counties and over the powers of the central government soon soured relations between Mengo and Kampala.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1966": "After Obote suspended the constitution and the Lukiiko defied him, troops under Idi Amin stormed the palace at Mengo. Mutesa II fled into exile in Britain, and the kingdom’s government was dismantled and placed under the control of the central government.",
  "Kingdom of Buganda|1967": "The new republican constitution abolished all Ugandan kingdoms, ending Buganda as a political entity. The monarchy was restored only as a cultural institution in 1993, when Ronald Muwenda Mutebi II was crowned, without the governmental powers of the old kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1500": "Court traditions place early Nyiginya kings near Lake Muhazi in this period, but historians regard these reigns as largely legendary. If a kingdom existed, it was one of several small chiefdoms in the hills east of Lake Kivu, overshadowed by stronger neighbours such as Bugesera, Gisaka and Bunyoro, hence its low standing.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1650": "Ruganzu II Ndori, whom Jan Vansina sees as the effective founder of the kingdom, probably seized power around the mid-seventeenth century. Remembered as the restorer of a realm shattered by invasions, he established Nyiginya rule over a core in central Rwanda and laid foundations for later expansion, though the kingdom remained modest in size.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1770": "Traditions credit Cyirima II Rujugira, an eighteenth-century king whose exact dates are uncertain, with holding off Burundi, Gisaka and Ndorwa and with stationing permanent social armies on the frontiers. These garrisons made Rwanda a durable military power and prepared the expansion of the following decades.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1795": "Around the end of the eighteenth century Rwanda and Burundi partitioned the Kingdom of Bugesera, removing an old rival in the south-east. The gain extended Rwandan territory towards the lakes of the Akagera basin and strengthened the kingdom against Burundi, its main competitor for dominance in the highlands.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1800": "Around 1800 Rwandan armies overran Ndorwa in the north-east, a state that had long contested the region with the Nyiginya kings. Although royal control of these peripheral districts stayed loose, the conquest gave the kingdom much of its later shape and added valuable pastures and herds.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1852": "Under Mutara II Rwogera, Rwanda annexed Gisaka, the last large independent kingdom on its eastern side, around the 1850s. The conquest brought fertile land and herds under Nyiginya rule and, with earlier gains, created the compact realm between Lake Kivu and the Akagera River that the colonial powers later found.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1867": "Kigeli IV Rwabugiri came to the throne, around 1867 in Vansina\u0027s chronology, though older king lists give 1853. He began replacing entrenched lineage chiefs with his own favourites, imposing royal administration on autonomous regions and launching near-annual campaigns, raising the kingdom\u0027s power steadily towards its peak.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1885": "At the height of Rwabugiri\u0027s reign the kingdom was more centralised than ever. His armies raided Ijwi Island, Bushi and Nkore, chiefs answered directly to the court, and labour obligations such as uburetwa were extended to Hutu farmers. Rwanda was the dominant power of the highlands between Lake Kivu and the Akagera.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1895": "Rwabugiri died in 1895 while on campaign in the west, leaving his chosen heir Mibambwe IV Rutarindwa on the throne. The kingdom was still at full military strength, but deep factional rivalries at court and the first visits of German explorers foreshadowed the crisis that followed almost at once.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1896": "Rwandan troops were repulsed by Congo Free State rifles at Shangi, and later that year the Coup of Rucunshu killed Rutarindwa and brought Yuhi V Musinga to power under the control of his mother Kanjogera and her kinsmen. Military defeat and civil conflict sharply weakened royal authority.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1897": "Seeking outside support against internal rivals, Musinga\u0027s court accepted German protection, and Rwanda became part of German East Africa. Germany ruled indirectly through the monarchy, which kept much prestige and gained help against the north, but the kingdom had lost its independence and its freedom to make war.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1916": "Belgian troops from the Congo occupied Rwanda during the First World War, and from 1922 it was governed as part of Ruanda-Urundi under a League of Nations mandate. Belgian officials intervened increasingly in appointments and taxation, and a severe famine in the war years added to the strains on the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1931": "Belgium deposed Musinga for opposing Christianity and colonial reform and installed his son Mutara III Rudahigwa. Reforms merged the triple chieftaincy into a single chief, gave posts almost exclusively to Tutsi and introduced identity cards recording ethnicity, reducing the monarchy largely to an instrument of colonial administration.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1954": "Mutara III, baptised in 1943 and widely respected, abolished ubuhake cattle clientage and divided its herds between patrons and clients. The reform modernised the kingdom, but it came as Hutu leaders demanded political rights and as Belgium began to withdraw its support from the Tutsi aristocracy, leaving the monarchy exposed.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1959": "Mutara III died suddenly at Usumbura in July, and his half-brother Kigeli V Ndahindurwa was installed by court conservatives. In November an attack on the Hutu sub-chief Dominique Mbonyumutwa sparked violence against Tutsi chiefs; Belgian authorities replaced many with Hutu, and the revolution stripped the monarchy of most of its power.",
  "Kingdom of Rwanda|1961": "On 28 January Hutu leaders at Gitarama proclaimed a republic, and in September a referendum supervised by the United Nations confirmed the abolition of the monarchy by a large majority. Kigeli V went into exile, and the kingdom ended months before Rwanda became independent as a republic in July 1962.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1500": "Tradition places the founding of the Hinda dynasty by Ruhinda around this time, after the Chwezi kingdom of Kitara dissolved. Nkore was then a small pastoral chiefdom east of Lake Edward, exposed to raids by the new Babito rulers of Bunyoro, and its early history survives only in uncertain oral tradition.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1600": "During the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries Nkore remained small and vulnerable. Bunyoro\u0027s armies raided southwards repeatedly, and traditions recall at least one devastating Nyoro invasion. The kingdom survived through the mobility of its herds and the loyalty of Bahima chiefs, but had little power beyond its own pastures.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1690": "Ntare IV, probably reigning in the late seventeenth century, is remembered for defeating a Nyoro invasion and earning the name Kitabanyoro, \u0027killer of the Banyoro\u0027. The victory secured the kingdom\u0027s survival and is treated in tradition almost as a refoundation, though the dynasty\u0027s power remained essentially local.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1780": "In the eighteenth century Nkore slowly enlarged its territory, absorbing border districts and lands of the Kingdom of Mpororo as that state fragmented. Pressure from Bunyoro eased as the Nyoro turned to conflicts with Buganda, giving Nkore room to grow, though it remained a minor kingdom beside its northern neighbours.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1850": "Under rulers such as Mutambuka in the mid-nineteenth century Nkore became an expanding power, raiding Buhweju, Igara, the Mpororo successor chiefdoms and other neighbours for cattle and drawing them into tributary relations. Royal herds and Bahima followings grew with the plunder, though the lack of a succession rule still threatened every transition.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1875": "Ntare V emerged from a succession struggle as king, probably in the 1870s. He continued his predecessors\u0027 expansion, extending Nkore\u0027s influence over neighbouring chiefdoms, while coastal goods and a few firearms began to arrive through traders from Karagwe and Buganda, bringing the kingdom towards its greatest reach.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1888": "Nkore stood at its pre-colonial peak in the late 1880s, dominating much of the grassland between Lake Edward and the Kagera and receiving tribute from smaller neighbours. Its power still rested on cattle, Bahima warriors and personal loyalty rather than institutions, and Rwandan raids under Kigeli IV Rwabugiri exposed its limits.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1891": "Rinderpest reached the region around 1891 and destroyed the great majority of the cattle on which Nkore\u0027s economy, social order and military strength depended. Smallpox and other epidemics followed. The Bahima elite lost much of its wealth, and the kingdom\u0027s capacity to raid or to defend itself collapsed within a few years.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1895": "Ntare V died in 1895, opening a contested succession among royal kin. The young Kahaya II was installed with the backing of the powerful chief Nuwa Mbaguta. Weakened by epidemics and division, Nkore was in no condition to resist the British officers who were advancing westward from Buganda.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1901": "The Ankole Agreement made the kingdom part of the Uganda Protectorate while adding Buhweju, Igara and much of Mpororo to its territory. Ankole was now larger than ever, but its king had lost sovereignty, his subjects paid colonial taxes, and he governed under British supervision with Mbaguta as chief minister.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1930": "By the interwar years Ankole was a colonial district with appointed county chiefs, a headquarters at Mbarara and a council, the Eishengyero. Kahaya II kept ceremonial prestige, but real authority lay with British officials and the chiefs who depended on them, while mission education was producing a new elite.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1944": "Kahaya II died after a reign of almost fifty years and was succeeded by Charles Gasyonga II. The kingship remained a respected institution, but educated Bairu increasingly resented Bahima dominance of chiefships, and the monarchy was drawn into local factional politics rather than serving as a unifying centre.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1962": "Uganda became independent and Ankole was recognised as a kingdom under the new constitution, with its own government and council. This gave the monarchy formal standing, but Bahima–Bairu tensions and national party rivalries limited its authority, and its powers were far smaller than those of Buganda.",
  "Kingdom of Ankole|1967": "Following the 1966 confrontation between Prime Minister Milton Obote and Buganda, the republican constitution of 1967 abolished all of Uganda\u0027s kingdoms, including Ankole. Gasyonga II lost his throne, and unlike its neighbours the kingdom was not restored as a cultural institution when the others returned from 1993.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1500": "Around this time, according to tradition, Luo-speaking Babito rulers led by Isingoma Mpuga Rukidi took over the lands of the Chwezi kingdom of Kitara. Adopting its regalia and rituals, the new dynasty claimed Kitara\u0027s heritage, but it still had to establish its authority over chiefs and herders east of Lake Albert.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1550": "In the sixteenth century the Babito kingdom consolidated its hold on the country between Lake Albert and the Nile and began raiding southwards. Its salt from Kibiro and its iron gave it economic advantages, and branch dynasties spread Babito rule to Kiziba and parts of Busoga, increasing its regional weight.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1650": "In the seventeenth century Bunyoro stood at the height of its power as the strongest kingdom of the Great Lakes. Its armies raided Nkore, Rwanda and the Buganda region, and neighbouring traditions remember Nyoro invasions as disasters, although its dominance rested on raiding and prestige more than on lasting conquest.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1700": "Around 1700 Bunyoro remained the leading power of the region, but its raids met growing resistance. Nkore remembers Ntare IV\u0027s victory over a Nyoro army in this period, and the kingdom\u0027s loose structure, which gave great autonomy to princes and chiefs, limited its ability to hold conquered territory.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1760": "During the eighteenth century the balance shifted towards Buganda, whose more centralised kingship and lake fleet allowed it to expand at Bunyoro\u0027s expense. Contested successions among Babito princes weakened the centre, and border districts began to slip away, although Bunyoro remained large and respected.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1800": "By the end of the eighteenth century Buganda had taken Buddu and other south-eastern districts, cutting Bunyoro off from the western shore of Lake Victoria. The kingdom was still large and economically strong thanks to salt and iron, but it was no longer the dominant power of the region.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1830": "Around 1830 Prince Kaboyo Olimi, son of Omukama Nyamutukura Kyebambe III, broke away to found the Kingdom of Toro, taking rich pastures and the salt lakes of the south-west. The secession was a severe blow to Bunyoro\u0027s resources and prestige and encouraged other peripheral chiefs to assert autonomy.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1852": "Kamurasi became omukama around the early 1850s, but his reign was troubled by civil war among royal claimants and by the arrival of armed traders from Khartoum in the north. The British explorers John Hanning Speke, James Grant and Samuel Baker visited his court, finding a weakened kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1870": "Kabalega won the throne in 1870 after a succession war against his brother Kabigumire. He began building a standing army, the abarusura, loyal to himself rather than to the hereditary chiefs, and appointed commoners to office, laying the foundations for a revival of royal power.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1872": "Samuel Baker, acting for the Khedive of Egypt, proclaimed the annexation of Bunyoro at Masindi, but fighting broke out and Baker was forced to retreat. The victory confirmed Kabalega\u0027s authority, kept Egyptian rule out of the kingdom and showed the growing effectiveness of his firearm-equipped army.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1876": "Kabalega reconquered Toro, reversing the secession of the 1830s, and reasserted control over peripheral districts. With ivory exports paying for guns, the abarusura made Bunyoro a regional military power again, capable of challenging Buganda, though the kingdom never recovered the territories lost in the eighteenth century.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1886": "In the mid-1880s Kabalega\u0027s Bunyoro was at the height of its revival, centralised, well armed and controlling Toro. It remained, however, surrounded by hostile neighbours, and its strength depended on firearms bought through foreign traders and on the personal authority of a king whom the British increasingly saw as an enemy.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1891": "Frederick Lugard of the Imperial British East Africa Company restored Kasagama to the throne of Toro and stationed troops along the border with Bunyoro. The intervention stripped Kabalega of Toro and placed a hostile garrison on his frontier, signalling that Britain intended to confront him directly.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1894": "British forces under Colonel Henry Colvile, supported by large Ganda armies, invaded Bunyoro from late 1893 and occupied much of the kingdom. Kabalega withdrew north and began a guerrilla war. Britain promised large districts to Buganda, creating the \u0027lost counties\u0027, and the kingdom\u0027s independent power was effectively broken.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1899": "Kabalega and his ally, the deposed Kabaka Mwanga of Buganda, were captured in Lango in April 1899 and exiled to the Seychelles. Bunyoro was absorbed into the Uganda Protectorate as conquered territory under a young puppet king, Kitahimbwa, with British officials and Ganda chiefs exercising real power.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1907": "Resentment of Ganda chiefs appointed over Nyoro districts erupted in the Nyangire (\u0027I refuse\u0027) revolt. The protest was suppressed, but it showed the depth of grievance against the colonial settlement. Bunyoro remained impoverished, depopulated by disease and politically marginal within the protectorate.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1933": "The Bunyoro Agreement finally gave the kingdom a formal status within the protectorate comparable to other agreement kingdoms, under Omukama Tito Winyi IV. It restored a measure of dignity to the monarchy, but real power stayed with colonial officials and the lost counties remained part of Buganda.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1964": "A referendum in the lost counties returned Buyaga and Bugangaizi to Bunyoro, a significant symbolic victory after seventy years. The result also deepened the conflict between Prime Minister Milton Obote and Buganda, a conflict that would soon lead to the destruction of all Uganda\u0027s kingdoms.",
  "Kingdom of Bunyoro|1967": "The republican constitution of 1967 abolished the kingdoms of Uganda, ending Bunyoro\u0027s monarchy under Tito Winyi IV. The kingdom ceased to exist as a political unit, although it was restored in 1993 as a cultural institution without governmental powers, under Solomon Gafabusa Iguru I.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1540": "Tradition credits Andriamanelo, conventionally dated to the mid-sixteenth century, with founding the Merina royal line in the central highlands and subduing the earlier Vazimba inhabitants. Imerina was then a cluster of small rice-growing chiefdoms around Antananarivo, and its kings ruled only a few valleys and hills.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1610": "Ralambo and his son Andrianjaka, remembered as the kings who gave Imerina its name, its royal talismans and the hill of Antananarivo, consolidated the small kingdom around the turn of the seventeenth century. Their dates rest only on genealogical tradition, but the state was growing in people and in control of irrigable land.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1700": "Andriamasinavalona, ruling around 1700, extended Imerina to its largest size before the nineteenth century and controlled much of the central highlands. Before his death, however, he divided the kingdom among four sons, creating rival realms that fought one another for most of the following century.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1720": "After Andriamasinavalona\u0027s partition, four Merina kingdoms, including those based at Antananarivo and Ambohimanga, competed for land, captives and trade. Warfare and raiding between them were frequent, and the divided highlands, exposed to Sakalava pressure from the west, carried little weight across Madagascar for most of the eighteenth century.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1787": "Andrianampoinimerina seized power at Ambohimanga around 1787 and began reuniting Imerina through war, diplomacy and marriage alliances. The sale of captives to French traders supplying Réunion and Mauritius brought him firearms and silver, giving his small kingdom the resources to defeat its rivals one by one.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1794": "By about 1794 Andrianampoinimerina had taken Antananarivo and made it his capital, and he went on to complete the reunification of Imerina. He drained the Betsimitatatra plain for rice, organised markets and labour service, and proclaimed laws in public assemblies, creating a populous, disciplined highland state ready to expand beyond its borders.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1810": "Andrianampoinimerina died in 1810, having extended his rule over neighbouring highland peoples and charged his son Radama I with carrying the kingdom to the sea. The young Radama faced revolts on his accession but inherited a centralised kingdom with a large population and an effective system of labour levies.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1817": "Radama I signed a treaty with Robert Farquhar, the British governor of Mauritius, ending the export of slaves in return for arms, training and recognition as King of Madagascar. The same year his army took Toamasina on the east coast, and the kingdom entered a phase of rapid expansion.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1824": "Radama\u0027s British-trained army captured Mahajanga, the main port of the Sakalava kingdom of Boina, giving the kingdom outlets on both coasts. Missionaries of the London Missionary Society were opening schools and writing Malagasy in the Latin alphabet, and Merina garrisons were spreading across the east and north.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1828": "At Radama I\u0027s death in July 1828 the Kingdom of Madagascar controlled the highlands, much of the east and north and key ports on both coasts, perhaps two-thirds of the island. This was the peak of Merina power, though the Sakalava of Menabe and the far south stayed outside its control.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1845": "Ranavalona I had expelled the missionaries, banned Christianity in 1835 and closed the kingdom to most foreign influence. In 1845 her troops repelled a joint British and French bombardment of Toamasina. The kingdom held its territory, but heavy labour levies, ordeals and warfare took a severe toll on its population.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1861": "Ranavalona I died in 1861 and her son Radama II reopened the kingdom to foreigners and missionaries, granting sweeping concessions to the Frenchman Joseph-François Lambert. His policies alarmed the hova elite and he was killed in 1863, after which real power passed to the prime ministers.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1869": "Ranavalona II and the prime minister Rainilaiarivony were baptised as Protestants in 1869, and the royal talismans were publicly burned. Church and school networks became instruments of government across the highlands, strengthening the state\u0027s reach at home while drawing it deeper into the rivalry of Britain and France.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1881": "Rainilaiarivony\u0027s reforms reached their height with ministries on European lines and the Code of 305 Articles in 1881. Compulsory schooling, registers and provincial governors gave the kingdom one of the most modern administrations in Africa, though its hold on the coasts remained uneven and its army weak.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1885": "The Franco-Hova War of 1883–85 ended in a treaty that kept the monarchy in place but gave France Diego Suarez, a heavy indemnity and a claim to control Madagascar\u0027s foreign relations. Paying the indemnity drove the government into debt, and French pressure grew steadily in the following years.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1890": "Britain recognised a French protectorate over Madagascar in return for French recognition of British rule in Zanzibar. The Merina government refused to accept the protectorate, but it had lost its main diplomatic support and faced France alone, with a weakened economy and growing unrest in the provinces.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1895": "A French expedition under General Jacques Duchesne marched from Mahajanga to Antananarivo, which surrendered on 30 September after being shelled. Ranavalona III accepted a protectorate, Rainilaiarivony was exiled, and the Menalamba rising broke out across the highlands against both the French and the Merina court.",
  "Merina Kingdom|1897": "On 28 February 1897 Governor-General Joseph Gallieni abolished the monarchy and exiled Ranavalona III, months after France had annexed Madagascar as a colony. The kingdom ceased to exist, though its administrative structures, capital and educated elite became foundations of the colonial and later the independent state.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1600": "Early in the seventeenth century, according to tradition, a branch of the royal family of Allada settled on the Abomey plateau among the Fon after a succession dispute. Its leaders were at first only one group of chiefs among several in the region, with no access to the coast and little military strength.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1645": "Houegbadja, traditionally dated c. 1645–1685, is regarded as the true founder of Dahomey. He built the first palace at Abomey and laid down the rules of kingship, creating a small but centralised inland kingdom that began to absorb its neighbours on the plateau.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1708": "Agaja came to the throne, in 1708 or 1718 according to different chronologies, after the reign of Akaba had extended the kingdom on the plateau. Agaja strengthened the army, acquired more firearms and prepared to break through to the coast, where the slave trade had made Allada and Whydah rich.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1724": "Agaja conquered Allada, the old kingdom from which Dahomey\u0027s dynasty claimed descent. The victory brought large numbers of captives and a dominant position in the hinterland of the Slave Coast, transforming Dahomey from an inland state into the main power of the region.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1727": "Dahomey conquered the Kingdom of Whydah, the busiest slave-trading port on the coast, giving the king direct access to European merchants. The conquest brought great wealth and firearms but also provoked resistance from exiled Whydah rulers and the hostility of the Oyo Empire.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1730": "After repeated invasions by Oyo cavalry, which Dahomey\u0027s infantry could not withstand in open country, Agaja accepted tributary status around 1730. Dahomey kept its internal autonomy and its coastal conquests but paid annual tribute to Oyo, a clear sign of the limits of its power.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1740": "Tegbesu succeeded Agaja in 1740 and consolidated royal control over Ouidah and the slave trade. His long reign strengthened the administrative system and royal revenues, though Dahomey remained Oyo\u0027s tributary and the trade rose and fell with European demand and wars.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1797": "Adandozan came to the throne amid falling slave exports and military setbacks. His reign was troubled by quarrels with Brazilian traders and court factions, and after his overthrow in 1818 his name was removed from the official king list, reflecting the kingdom\u0027s weakness in these years.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1818": "Ghezo seized the throne from Adandozan with the support of the Brazilian trader Francisco Félix de Souza and powerful court factions. He reorganised the army, expanded the corps of women soldiers and began a revival of royal power that would make his reign the high point of the kingdom.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1823": "Taking advantage of the Oyo Empire\u0027s slide into civil war, Ghezo stopped paying tribute around 1823 and defeated Oyo forces. Freed from a century of subordination, Dahomey launched campaigns against the Mahi and Yoruba towns and became the leading independent power of its region.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1845": "Under Ghezo in the 1840s Dahomey stood at its peak, with a large standing army including several thousand women soldiers, a centralised administration and revenues from both slaves and the growing palm oil trade. European visitors described the splendour of the Annual Customs at Abomey.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1851": "Dahomey\u0027s attack on the Egba city of Abeokuta was repulsed with heavy losses, and in 1852 British pressure led Ghezo to sign a treaty against the slave trade. The kingdom remained powerful, but its military prestige and its main source of revenue had both been shaken.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1858": "Glele succeeded Ghezo, who died in 1858. He continued the annual campaigns and royal ceremonies and promoted palm oil exports, but the end of the Atlantic slave trade in the following decade deprived the crown of a vital source of income and weapons.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1864": "A second great assault on Abeokuta ended in disastrous defeat, with heavy losses among both men and women soldiers. Dahomey remained a formidable regional power, but it could no longer expand against the Yoruba states, and French and British influence on the coast was steadily growing.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1890": "Behanzin, king since 1889, fought the First Franco-Dahomean War over Cotonou and Porto-Novo. Dahomean forces attacked French positions but were repulsed, and the treaty of October 1890 recognised French control on the coast in return for an annual payment, leaving the kingdom intact but threatened.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1892": "A French column under Colonel Alfred-Amédée Dodds defeated the Dahomean army in a series of hard-fought battles and entered Abomey in November, after Behanzin had burned the palaces and withdrawn. Much of the army, including the women\u0027s corps, had been destroyed, and independence was effectively over.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1894": "Behanzin surrendered in January 1894 and was exiled to Martinique. France installed his brother Agoli-agbo as king of a reduced Abomey under French protection, with no army or foreign policy of its own and with the coastal territories placed under direct colonial rule.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1900": "France deposed Agoli-agbo and sent him into exile, ending even the nominal rule of the dynasty. Royal families kept ceremonial roles and prestige in Abomey, but government passed entirely to the French administrators of the colony of Dahomey, and the kingdom survived mainly as a memory.",
  "Kingdom of Dahomey|1904": "With the reorganisation of French West Africa in 1904, the former kingdom was fully absorbed into the colony of French Dahomey. The territory became independent in 1960 and took the name Benin in 1975, while the royal palaces of Abomey became a museum and later a World Heritage Site.",
  "Barotseland|1600": "Luyi communities were settling the Bulozi floodplain of the upper Zambezi. Traditions name Mbuywamwambwa and her son Mboo, the first Litunga, as founders of the royal line, though the dates are uncertain. Kingship was taking shape, but the young state still competed with rival Luyi chiefs for control of the plain.",
  "Barotseland|1700": "Successors of Mboo, above all Ngalama, had subdued rival chiefs and united the floodplain under one king. The division between a northern court of the Litunga and a southern court at Nalolo was established. The kingdom controlled the richest land in the region but had not yet extended its tribute widely beyond it.",
  "Barotseland|1780": "Mulambwa came to the throne at about this time and began one of the longest and most successful reigns in Lozi tradition. Tribute from neighbouring peoples was growing, and the floodplain economy of mounds, fisheries and cattle supported a strong court, raising the kingdom close to its pre-colonial height.",
  "Barotseland|1810": "Under Mulambwa the Luyana kingdom was the leading power of the upper Zambezi. It drew tribute from surrounding peoples, welcomed Mbunda immigrants from the west, and was remembered for his legal reforms. Its reach and prestige stood near their peak, though its army remained a levy rather than a standing force.",
  "Barotseland|1830": "Mulambwa died around this time and a succession struggle broke out between his sons, dividing the aristocracy and the councils. The kingdom kept its territory and institutions for the moment, but the civil conflict left it exposed just as migrants displaced by the Mfecane were moving north towards the Zambezi.",
  "Barotseland|1840": "The Kololo under Sebitwane conquered the floodplain, defeating a Lozi kingdom weakened by civil war. The Lozi monarchy survived only among exiles in the north, while the Kololo ruled the plain and imposed their Sotho language. As an independent state Barotseland had all but ceased to exist.",
  "Barotseland|1864": "After the deaths of Sebitwane in 1851 and his son Sekeletu around 1863, Kololo rule collapsed. The Lozi rose under Sipopa, who returned from northern exile and killed most of the Kololo men. The restored kingdom regained the floodplain, but its councils were divided and Sipopa’s rule was harsh and insecure.",
  "Barotseland|1878": "Sipopa had been overthrown and killed in 1876, and after a short reign by Mwanawina II the councillors chose Lubosi, later known as Lewanika. He began rebuilding royal authority from Lealui and organised raids on neighbouring peoples, though powerful councillors still contested his rule and the kingdom remained unstable.",
  "Barotseland|1884": "A rebellion led by leading councillors drove Lewanika from the throne and installed Akufuna Tatila. Lewanika fled from the floodplain and gathered loyal supporters for a return. The coup showed how fragile royal power was, and civil strife briefly weakened the kingdom’s grip on its tributary peoples.",
  "Barotseland|1885": "Lewanika returned and defeated his rivals, regaining the throne. He purged opponents, reorganised the titled offices and tightened royal control over the councils. With his authority secured, the kingdom entered its strongest period since Mulambwa, while the missionary François Coillard arrived soon afterwards and opened new contacts with the outside world.",
  "Barotseland|1889": "Lewanika ruled a kingdom whose tribute reached from the Kwando to the Kafue region, with firearms obtained through trade and a strong central court. Its power and reach stood at their height, but Ndebele raids and rumours of European expansion led him to seek British protection, following Khama III’s example.",
  "Barotseland|1890": "Lewanika signed the Lochner Concession with the British South Africa Company, believing he was gaining the protection of Queen Victoria. The kingdom remained internally self-governing, but it had granted mineral and commercial rights to a chartered company, and its sovereignty began to slip from the Litunga’s hands.",
  "Barotseland|1900": "Company administrator Robert Coryndon had arrived, the Order in Council of 1899 had placed the territory under company rule, and a new concession of 1900 widened company authority. The Litunga kept his courts and title, but outside control over tax, land and external affairs steadily reduced his effective power.",
  "Barotseland|1905": "The arbitration of King Victor Emmanuel III of Italy fixed the border with Portuguese Angola, giving Portugal much of the western territory that Lewanika had claimed. The kingdom lost land and subjects at a stroke, while the abolition of slavery and limits on tribute labour soon weakened the old economy.",
  "Barotseland|1911": "North-Western and North-Eastern Rhodesia were merged into Northern Rhodesia, making Barotseland one province of a larger colony. It kept a special status, its own king and councils, but hut tax and labour migration to the mines drained men from the floodplain and the Litunga’s authority became largely local.",
  "Barotseland|1916": "Lewanika died after more than three decades on the throne and was succeeded by his son Yeta III. The kingdom’s institutions survived, but they now operated within a colonial system, and under Colonial Office rule from 1924 Barotseland was a protected native authority rather than a state with real independence.",
  "Barotseland|1964": "The Barotseland Agreement, signed by Litunga Mwanawina III and Kenneth Kaunda, brought Barotseland into independent Zambia with guarantees for its institutions. The kingdom ended as a separate political unit. Zambia abrogated the agreement in 1969 and renamed the area Western Province, leaving the Litunga a traditional ruler only.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1745": "Around this time, by traditional reckoning, the Dlamini under Ngwane III settled near the lower Pongola river, giving the country its Swazi name kaNgwane. The chiefdom was small, one of many Nguni-speaking groups competing for land and cattle in the region, with no standing regiments and a limited territory.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1815": "Sobhuza I succeeded his father Ndvungunye at about this time. The chiefdom remained modest in size and was soon squeezed by the expanding Ndwandwe Kingdom of Zwide, whose pressure in the southern lowlands threatened its survival and set the stage for a decisive migration northwards into the hills.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1820": "Under Ndwandwe pressure Sobhuza I moved his people north to the Ezulwini valley in central Eswatini. There he began absorbing local Sotho and Nguni chiefdoms and refugees, laying the foundations of a larger kingdom while avoiding conflict with Shaka’s Zulu through careful diplomacy and marriage alliances.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1840": "Sobhuza I had died and the young Mswati II came to the throne, with the queen mother Thandile acting as a powerful regent. The kingdom faced rebellion by royal rivals and attacks from the Zulu, but its absorbed chiefdoms gave it a broader base than the old Dlamini chiefdom had possessed.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1865": "Mswati II’s age-regiments had made the Swazi a major regional power, raiding into the eastern Transvaal and southern Mozambique, collecting tribute and intervening in Gaza politics. Diplomacy with the British and the Lydenburg Boers checked the Zulu threat. Territory, military reach and royal authority stood at their highest point in the kingdom’s history.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1868": "Mswati II died, and his death opened a contested succession and a long regency. Large-scale raiding declined, while Boer farmers pressed on the kingdom’s western margins. The kingdom was still strong and united internally, but it had lost the momentum of expansion under its most successful king.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1875": "Mbandzeni was installed as king after the succession dispute. He kept the kingdom intact and in 1879 sent warriors to help the British storm Sekhukhune’s stronghold, but gold discoveries and land hunger brought growing numbers of Europeans to Swaziland seeking grazing and mining rights from the king.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1889": "Mbandzeni died after granting hundreds of concessions that covered nearly the whole country, including land, minerals and state functions such as customs. Although Swazi independence was formally recognised by the conventions of 1881 and 1884, real control of land and revenue had passed largely to European concessionaires and their protectors.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1894": "The third Swaziland Convention, between Britain and the South African Republic, made Swaziland a protectorate of the Transvaal republic, administered from 1895 without full incorporation. The monarchy under Ngwane V, known as Bhunu, kept its ceremonial and local authority, but external affairs and taxation were controlled from Pretoria.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1903": "After the Second Boer War Britain took over Swaziland and administered it through the governor of the Transvaal. Sobhuza II, born in 1899, was an infant, and the regent Labotsibeni led the nation. The kingdom had lost its sovereignty entirely, though its chiefs and councils continued to function.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1907": "The Partition Proclamation divided Swaziland’s land, leaving about one third to the Swazi nation and two thirds to concessionaires and the colonial state. This was the lowest point for the kingdom, which had lost both its independence and most of its land while remaining a British High Commission territory.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1921": "Sobhuza II came of age and took up the kingship from the regent Labotsibeni. He began a long campaign to recover land, appealing to the Privy Council and later buying land back. The monarchy remained subordinate to British administration, but its authority over Swazi society steadily grew.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1968": "Swaziland became independent on 6 September 1968 with Sobhuza II as king. After more than seventy years of foreign control the kingdom regained sovereignty, a seat at the United Nations and control of its own affairs, though its economy remained closely tied to South Africa through the customs union and labour migration.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1973": "Sobhuza II repealed the independence constitution, banned political parties and assumed supreme power, restoring the monarchy’s central role. The state was internally stable and its land base was gradually enlarged, but its small size and economic dependence on South Africa limited its standing in the wider region.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|1986": "After Sobhuza II’s death in 1982 and four years of palace rivalry under two regents, Mswati III was crowned at the age of eighteen. Royal authority was restored, and the kingdom remained one of Africa’s few surviving monarchies, its institutions and territory unchanged since independence.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|2018": "Mswati III renamed the country the Kingdom of Eswatini on the fiftieth anniversary of independence. The monarchy retained executive power under the constitution of 2005, but the kingdom faced high unemployment, a severe HIV epidemic and growing demands for political reform among its roughly one million people.",
  "Swazi Kingdom|2025": "Eswatini remains a sovereign absolute monarchy under Mswati III, with the tinkhundla system in place after the pro-democracy protests of 2021 were suppressed. It is a small, independent member of the African Union and the Southern African Customs Union, stable as a state though modest in regional weight.",
  "Cape Colony|1795": "British forces took the Cape from the Dutch East India Company after the Battle of Muizenberg, to keep the route to India from France. Britain held a strategic port and a thinly settled colony with a disputed eastern frontier, governing it as a wartime military occupation rather than a permanent possession.",
  "Cape Colony|1803": "Under the Peace of Amiens Britain handed the Cape back to the Batavian Republic, whose officials under Governor Janssens introduced reforms. British rule was interrupted entirely for three years, and the colony’s future as a British possession was uncertain, which is why its standing on this curve falls to its lowest early level.",
  "Cape Colony|1806": "A British expedition under General David Baird won the Battle of Blaauwberg and reoccupied Cape Town. Britain now held the colony again, but its status remained provisional until the end of the Napoleonic Wars, and governors ruled with military authority over a small settler and enslaved population.",
  "Cape Colony|1814": "The Netherlands formally ceded the Cape to Britain, and Lord Charles Somerset began his long governorship. The colony was now a permanent British possession. Its eastern border had been pushed to the Fish River in 1812, but its economy remained modest, based on wine, wheat and stock farming.",
  "Cape Colony|1820": "Some 4,000 British settlers arrived in the Albany district to strengthen the eastern frontier. English soon became the language of administration and courts. The colony grew in population and commercial life, though frontier tension with the Xhosa and Somerset’s autocratic rule limited its development.",
  "Cape Colony|1834": "Slavery was abolished on 1 December, and at the end of the year the Sixth Frontier War broke out. Governor D’Urban’s annexation of the Province of Queen Adelaide briefly extended the colony to the Kei. Wool exports were growing, giving the colony a stronger economic base than before.",
  "Cape Colony|1836": "The Colonial Office reversed the annexation of Queen Adelaide, and the Great Trek gathered pace as thousands of Dutch-speaking farmers left the colony. The Cape lost settlers, labour and control over the interior to the new Boer republics, a setback to its reach even as its economy recovered.",
  "Cape Colony|1847": "After the War of the Axe Governor Sir Harry Smith annexed land to the Orange River and created British Kaffraria as a separate crown colony. Britain was committing more troops and money to the Cape, and the colony’s territory and authority in the interior were expanding again.",
  "Cape Colony|1853": "The Cape received a representative parliament elected on a qualified, non-racial franchise, as the long frontier war of 1850–1853 came to an end. The colony gained institutions of self-government and a growing wool economy, putting it ahead of the other settler territories of southern Africa.",
  "Cape Colony|1866": "British Kaffraria was annexed to the Cape after the Xhosa cattle-killing of 1856–1857 had broken Xhosa resistance west of the Kei. The colony’s territory grew, and the discovery of diamonds near the Orange River the following year opened the way to rapid economic change.",
  "Cape Colony|1872": "The Cape received responsible government under Prime Minister John Molteno, with ministers answerable to parliament. Diamonds at Kimberley were transforming the economy, financing railways and attracting labour and capital. The colony was becoming the leading economic and political power among the British territories of southern Africa.",
  "Cape Colony|1880": "Griqualand West, with the Kimberley diamond fields, was incorporated, and the Transkeian annexations were under way, while the Ninth Frontier War had completed the conquest of the Xhosa. The disastrous Gun War with the Basotho, however, showed the limits of the colony’s military strength.",
  "Cape Colony|1890": "Cecil Rhodes became prime minister, combining control of De Beers and the British South Africa Company with power in the Cape. With the alliance of the Afrikaner Bond, the colony dominated southern African politics, and its interests reached north into the newly occupied territories beyond the Limpopo.",
  "Cape Colony|1895": "With the annexation of Pondoland in 1894 and British Bechuanaland in 1895, the Cape reached its greatest territorial extent. Its railways, diamond wealth and parliament made it the most powerful self-governing colony in Africa, while Rhodes stood at the height of his political influence.",
  "Cape Colony|1896": "The failure of the Jameson Raid forced Rhodes to resign and broke his alliance with the Afrikaner Bond. Cape politics split along English and Afrikaner lines, and the colony’s ability to lead southern Africa was weakened as relations with the Transvaal deteriorated towards war.",
  "Cape Colony|1902": "The Second Boer War ended with British victory. The Cape had served as Britain’s base but had seen invasion, sieges at Kimberley and Mafeking, rebellion and martial law. It remained the largest colony in the region, but the war had left it divided and economically strained.",
  "Cape Colony|1910": "On 31 May the Cape Colony joined Natal, the Transvaal and the Orange River Colony in the Union of South Africa, becoming the Cape Province. It ceased to exist as a separate colony, keeping only provincial institutions and its qualified franchise, which later governments removed.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1816": "After the death of his father Senzangakhona, Shaka seized the leadership of the Zulu chiefdom with the backing of Dingiswayo of the Mthethwa. The Zulu were a small chiefdom with perhaps a few thousand people, a vassal of the Mthethwa, but Shaka began reorganising its fighting men into disciplined regiments.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1818": "Dingiswayo was killed by Zwide of the Ndwandwe, and Shaka took over leadership of the former Mthethwa alliance. He withstood Ndwandwe attacks, traditionally at Gqokli Hill, and began incorporating neighbouring chiefdoms. The Zulu were now a regional contender, though the Ndwandwe remained stronger in numbers.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1819": "Shaka defeated the main Ndwandwe army at the Mhlatuze river, and Zwide’s people withdrew to the north. With his most dangerous rival gone, Shaka absorbed the Qwabe and other chiefdoms, and the Zulu became the dominant power between the Phongolo and Thukela rivers, expanding their regimental army rapidly.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1824": "Shaka ruled a centralised kingdom from KwaBulawayo, and his regiments raided widely to the south and west. British traders arrived at Port Natal and recognised his authority. The kingdom’s territory, army and cattle wealth were growing quickly, making it the strongest state in southeastern Africa.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1826": "Shaka’s army defeated a renewed Ndwandwe challenge under Zwide’s son Sikhunyana, removing the last serious rival in the region. The kingdom stood at its height, with tens of thousands of men in its regiments and raids reaching far beyond its borders, though Shaka’s harsh rule was already breeding discontent.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1828": "Shaka was assassinated at Dukuza by his half-brothers Dingane and Mhlangana, and Dingane took the throne. The kingdom remained intact and powerful, but the change of ruler, purges at court and the end of Shaka’s distant campaigns marked the passing of its period of greatest expansion.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1838": "Dingane had the Voortrekker leader Piet Retief killed in February and attacked Boer camps, but on 16 December his army was defeated at Blood River. The defeat cost heavy losses and opened Zulu lands south of the Thukela to Boer settlement, weakening Dingane’s authority within the kingdom.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1840": "Mpande, allied with the Boers, overthrew Dingane in a civil war, and Dingane fled and was killed. The kingdom survived under Mpande but had lost land and men, and soon faced the British Colony of Natal across the Thukela, leaving it smaller and more cautious than before.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1856": "Mpande’s long reign brought relative stability, but a succession war between his sons ended at the Battle of Ndondakusuka, where Cetshwayo defeated and killed his brother Mbuyazi. Cetshwayo became the effective heir, and the kingdom’s regiments again proved formidable, though it now avoided conflict with its European neighbours.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1872": "Cetshwayo succeeded Mpande and was crowned in 1873 in a ceremony attended by the Natal official Theophilus Shepstone. He revived the regimental system and the royal authority that had weakened under his father, building a new capital at Ulundi and restoring much of the kingdom’s military strength.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1878": "Cetshwayo commanded an army of perhaps 40,000 men and ruled a stable kingdom. Yet British plans for confederation in southern Africa and border disputes with the Transvaal turned this strength into a threat in British eyes, and in December Sir Henry Bartle Frere issued an ultimatum demanding the army’s disbandment.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1879": "After the victory at Isandlwana in January, the Zulu army was defeated at Ulundi in July, and Cetshwayo was captured and exiled. Wolseley divided Zululand among thirteen appointed chiefs. The kingdom lost its army, its king and its unity, surviving only as a fragmented territory under British influence.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1883": "Cetshwayo was restored to a central portion of Zululand, but his uSuthu supporters were defeated by Zibhebhu, and the kingdom collapsed into civil war. Cetshwayo died in 1884, and Dinuzulu sought Boer help at the cost of land. Royal power was reduced to a faction in a divided country.",
  "Zulu Kingdom|1887": "Britain annexed the remainder of Zululand as a British colony, ending the Zulu Kingdom as an independent state. Dinuzulu resisted in 1888 and was exiled to St Helena, and Zululand was incorporated into Natal in 1897. The monarchy survived only as a traditional institution under colonial rule.",
  "Liberia|1847": "The Colony of Liberia declared independence on 26 July and became a republic under President Joseph Jenkins Roberts. The new state controlled only a strip of coast with a few thousand settlers, but independence allowed it to levy customs duties and seek recognition from Britain, France and other powers.",
  "Liberia|1857": "The Republic of Maryland at Cape Palmas, weakened by war with the Grebo, was annexed to Liberia, uniting the main settler colonies on the coast. The republic had won recognition from European powers, its coastal trade was growing, and Liberia College, chartered in 1851, was preparing to open in Monrovia.",
  "Liberia|1871": "President Edward James Roye was deposed amid controversy over a costly loan raised in London on harsh terms, and he died soon afterwards. The debt burdened the republic for decades, while the decline of settler sugar and coffee farming weakened its economy and the True Whig Party prepared to dominate politics.",
  "Liberia|1885": "An agreement with Britain ceded the Gallinas region in the northwest to Sierra Leone, the first of several territorial losses. During the European partition of Africa Liberia’s claims to the interior were recognised only partially, and the republic’s weak administration there invited foreign pressure on its borders.",
  "Liberia|1912": "Faced with debt and the threat of foreign intervention, Liberia accepted an international loan secured by a customs receivership under an American general receiver. Further border agreements with France and Britain had cut its territory. The republic remained independent, but its finances were effectively supervised from abroad.",
  "Liberia|1926": "The Firestone concession leased up to a million acres for rubber planting and was tied to a loan that refinanced Liberia’s debt. Plantation wages and revenues brought new money into the economy, but foreign control over finances and the country’s main enterprise deepened Liberia’s dependence on American capital.",
  "Liberia|1930": "A League of Nations commission found that Liberian officials had shipped forced labourers to Fernando Po, and President King and Vice-President Yancy resigned. Liberia faced international condemnation and proposals for trusteeship, and the Depression cut revenues, leaving the republic at one of the low points of its standing.",
  "Liberia|1944": "William Tubman became president and Liberia declared war on the Axis, having hosted American bases and the airfield at Roberts Field. Wartime demand for rubber and closer ties with the United States improved the republic’s finances and standing, opening the way for the Open Door Policy.",
  "Liberia|1960": "Iron ore mining at Bomi Hills and new concessions at Bong and Nimba, together with rubber and the growing ship registry, made Liberia one of the fastest-growing economies in the world in the 1950s. As colonies across Africa gained independence, Liberia’s experience gave it prominence in African diplomacy.",
  "Liberia|1971": "Tubman died after nearly three decades in power, leaving a state with expanded roads, schools and mining revenues and a seat among the founders of the Organisation of African Unity. Measured by economic output, stability and international standing, the republic stood at its height, though wealth remained highly concentrated.",
  "Liberia|1979": "Under President William Tolbert falling commodity prices and a proposed rise in the price of rice led to riots in Monrovia in April, in which dozens were killed. The True Whig order faced open opposition from new political movements, and the state’s legitimacy among the indigenous majority was collapsing.",
  "Liberia|1980": "Samuel Doe seized power in a military coup on 12 April, killing Tolbert and ending more than a century of True Whig rule. Thirteen officials were executed. The state survived, and American aid increased during the Cold War, but the economy shrank and the new government soon turned repressive.",
  "Liberia|1990": "Charles Taylor’s invasion of December 1989 had plunged Liberia into civil war. Doe was captured and killed in September 1990, and factions divided the country while a West African force held Monrovia. The central state had effectively ceased to function outside the capital, its lowest point.",
  "Liberia|1997": "After a series of peace agreements Charles Taylor won the presidential election, with many voters hoping to prevent renewed war. A national government was restored in name, but the economy lay in ruins, institutions were controlled by Taylor’s network, and his support for rebels in Sierra Leone brought international sanctions.",
  "Liberia|2003": "A second civil war waged by the LURD and MODEL rebel groups reached Monrovia, and Taylor resigned in August and went into exile in Nigeria. The Accra peace agreement installed a transitional government and a large United Nations mission arrived. Liberia was again a collapsed state dependent on outside protection.",
  "Liberia|2006": "Ellen Johnson Sirleaf took office as Africa’s first elected female head of state. With United Nations peacekeepers maintaining security, her government rebuilt institutions, attracted investment and later secured debt relief. Liberia’s standing improved markedly, though its economy remained far smaller than before the wars.",
  "Liberia|2014": "The Ebola epidemic of 2014–2015 killed several thousand Liberians, closed schools and halted much economic activity, exposing the weakness of the health system. International assistance helped bring it under control. Peace held, but the crisis set back recovery at a time when commodity prices were also falling.",
  "Liberia|2025": "Liberia is a peaceful, multi-party republic of about five million people that has seen peaceful transfers of power, most recently from George Weah to Joseph Boakai in 2024 after the UN mission left in 2018. It remains poor and dependent on mining, rubber and aid, well below its pre-war standing.",
  "South African Republic|1852": "The Sand River Convention recognised the independence of the emigrant Boers north of the Vaal River. Power stood low: the settlers were few and scattered among rival communities at Potchefstroom, Lydenburg and Zoutpansberg, with no common government, regular revenue or army beyond the part-time commandos of individual districts.",
  "South African Republic|1857": "Marthinus Wessel Pretorius was elected the first president, and the constitution adopted the following year gave the republic a Volksraad, a president and a legal framework. Lydenburg and Zoutpansberg still stood apart, however, and the young state had almost no money, so its standing rose only modestly.",
  "South African Republic|1860": "Pretoria became the seat of government and Lydenburg rejoined the republic, bringing most of the Transvaal Boers under one administration. Pretorius’s simultaneous presidency of the Orange Free State raised hopes of a larger Boer union, but it also stirred factional quarrels that soon turned into armed conflict.",
  "South African Republic|1864": "The civil war of 1861–1864 between rival factions ended with all the communities, including Zoutpansberg, accepting a single government. Unity was a gain, but years of strife had left the treasury empty, officials unpaid and African chiefdoms on the frontiers largely beyond the republic’s control, so power held steady.",
  "South African Republic|1872": "Thomas François Burgers became president with ambitious plans for a railway to Delagoa Bay, modern schools and a national currency. He raised a loan in Europe and gave the state a more modern face, but its revenue remained tiny and its authority over the northern and eastern frontiers weak.",
  "South African Republic|1876": "The commando campaign against the Pedi king Sekhukhune failed before his mountain strongholds, and many burghers simply went home. The defeat, a collapsed railway scheme and an empty treasury exposed the republic’s weakness and encouraged Britain, then pursuing confederation in southern Africa, to intervene.",
  "South African Republic|1877": "Sir Theophilus Shepstone annexed the bankrupt republic to the British Empire on 12 April 1877 without meeting armed resistance. Independent statehood was suspended, which is why power falls to its lowest level, although the burghers kept their political organisation and Paul Kruger led deputations to London seeking its reversal.",
  "South African Republic|1881": "The Boers rose in December 1880 and defeated British forces at Laing’s Nek and Majuba Hill. The Pretoria Convention restored self-government under a vague British suzerainty. The victory gave the restored republic prestige among Afrikaners throughout southern Africa, though it remained poor and thinly populated.",
  "South African Republic|1884": "Under its new president, Paul Kruger, the republic obtained the London Convention, which dropped the word suzerainty and left Britain only a veto over foreign treaties. Its formal name, the South African Republic, was restored, and it pressed claims to territory on its western and south-eastern borders.",
  "South African Republic|1886": "The discovery of the Main Reef gold deposits on the Witwatersrand and the founding of Johannesburg began the transformation of the republic. Revenue, investment and immigration surged within a few years, although the influx of foreign uitlanders soon raised political questions that the Boer state found hard to answer.",
  "South African Republic|1890": "Gold revenue paid for a growing civil service under the State Secretary Willem Leyds, and the republic absorbed the Nieuwe Republiek in 1888. Kruger’s new fourteen-year residence rule for the full franchise kept power in burgher hands but deepened resentment among the uitlanders who now drove the economy.",
  "South African Republic|1895": "The railway from Pretoria to Delagoa Bay opened, giving the republic an outlet to the sea independent of British ports. The Rand had become the world’s largest gold field, and the state was the wealthiest in Africa, though tension with Britain and the mining magnates was approaching crisis.",
  "South African Republic|1899": "Strengthened by Krupp and Creusot artillery, Mauser rifles, new forts and the 1897 alliance with the Orange Free State, the republic stood at its peak of wealth and military strength. It went to war with the British Empire in October and laid siege to Mafeking, Ladysmith and Kimberley.",
  "South African Republic|1900": "Lord Roberts’s offensive took Bloemfontein, Johannesburg and Pretoria, and Britain proclaimed the annexation of the Transvaal on 1 September. Kruger left for Europe. The republic lost its capital, its mines and its railway, surviving only as a government in the field that directed commando operations.",
  "South African Republic|1901": "The guerrilla war continued under Louis Botha and Koos de la Rey, but British blockhouse lines, farm burning and the concentration camps wore down the commandos and their families. The republic still had leaders and fighters in the field, yet it controlled almost no territory or resources.",
  "South African Republic|1902": "With the commandos exhausted and thousands of women and children dead in the camps, Boer delegates accepted the Treaty of Vereeniging on 31 May 1902. The republic ceased to exist and became the Transvaal Colony, ending fifty years of independent Boer statehood north of the Vaal.",
  "Orange Free State|1854": "The Bloemfontein Convention ended British rule over the Orange River Sovereignty and handed power to the burghers, who adopted a constitution in April. The new state had a capital, a president and a Volksraad but little revenue, no army beyond the commandos and contested borders with the Basotho and Griqua.",
  "Orange Free State|1858": "The first war with Moshoeshoe’s Basotho Kingdom ended in stalemate after Basotho raids reached deep into Free State districts, and Sir George Grey of the Cape mediated a settlement. The republic survived intact but had shown that it could not impose its border claims on its powerful neighbour.",
  "Orange Free State|1860": "Marthinus Wessel Pretorius, already president of the South African Republic, was elected president of the Free State, raising the prospect of a union of the Boer states. The purchase of the Philippolis Griqua lands in 1861 enlarged the republic, but the union scheme bred factional quarrels and was soon abandoned.",
  "Orange Free State|1864": "Johannes Brand, a Cape-born lawyer, became president and began a quarter-century of steady government. He inherited unresolved border disputes with the Basotho and a weak treasury, but brought administrative order and a pragmatic policy towards Britain that would give the republic its reputation for stability.",
  "Orange Free State|1866": "After a hard war against the Basotho, the Treaty of Thaba Bosiu forced Moshoeshoe to cede much of the fertile land west of the Caledon River. Although the commandos had failed to take Thaba Bosiu itself, the republic emerged as the stronger power and gained its best farmland.",
  "Orange Free State|1869": "Britain had annexed Basutoland in 1868, ending the third war, and the Second Treaty of Aliwal North fixed the border, confirming Free State possession of the Conquered Territory. With its frontier settled and wool exports rising, the republic was secure and more prosperous than at any earlier point.",
  "Orange Free State|1871": "Britain annexed the diamond fields of Griqualand West, which the Free State claimed, and the republic lacked the strength to resist. The loss of the richest mineral deposits then known in southern Africa was a serious blow, though diamond mines at Jagersfontein and Koffiefontein remained within its borders.",
  "Orange Free State|1876": "President Brand travelled to London and accepted £90,000 in compensation for Griqualand West, settling the dispute peacefully. Diamond-field markets boosted Free State farming, and the republic’s finances, courts and civil service were by now well regarded, so its standing recovered despite the territorial loss.",
  "Orange Free State|1884": "The Free State annexed the Barolong territory of Thaba Nchu after a succession dispute there, completing its control of the land between the rivers. Its neutrality in the First Boer War had preserved good relations with Britain, and Brand’s government enjoyed wide respect as a model republic.",
  "Orange Free State|1889": "A customs union with the Cape Colony was agreed, and the railway from the Cape reached Bloemfontein in 1890. Under President Francis William Reitz the state was more prosperous and better connected than ever, while rising Afrikaner sentiment drew it gradually closer to the gold-rich South African Republic.",
  "Orange Free State|1897": "President Martinus Theunis Steyn concluded a defensive alliance with the South African Republic, giving the Free State a central place in Boer politics. Its institutions, finances and commandos were at their strongest, though the alliance also tied the small republic to the Transvaal’s escalating conflict with Britain.",
  "Orange Free State|1900": "Cronjé’s surrender at Paardeberg and Roberts’s capture of Bloemfontein on 13 March broke organised resistance, and in May Britain proclaimed the annexation of the territory as the Orange River Colony. The republic lost its capital and administration and survived only as a government in the field.",
  "Orange Free State|1901": "Steyn’s government moved with the commandos while Christiaan de Wet led a guerrilla war across the Free State. British farm burning, blockhouse lines and concentration camps devastated the rural society on which the republic rested, leaving it with fighters but almost no territory or resources.",
  "Orange Free State|1902": "With the commandos exhausted and thousands of civilians dead in the camps, the Free State delegates reluctantly accepted the Treaty of Vereeniging on 31 May 1902. The republic ceased to exist, and its territory remained the British Orange River Colony until it joined the Union of South Africa in 1910.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1856": "Sayyid Said died at sea and Majid bin Said took over Zanzibar and the African dominions. The new state inherited a thriving clove economy, the caravan trade of the interior and claims along the coast, but its separation from Oman was contested by Thuwaini in Muscat.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1861": "The Canning Award confirmed Zanzibar’s independence from Oman in return for an annual subsidy. Majid had survived Barghash’s attempted coup of 1859 with British help, and the clove and ivory trades were expanding, though the sultan’s growing dependence on British goodwill was already clear.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1870": "Barghash bin Said succeeded his brother and began an ambitious reign. Caravans reached the great lakes and the Congo basin, customs revenue was rising, and Zanzibar town was the busiest port on the East African coast, making the sultanate the leading commercial power of the region.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1872": "A hurricane in April 1872 destroyed most of the clove trees on Zanzibar island and wrecked shipping in the harbour. In 1873 British pressure forced Barghash to close the slave market and ban the shipping of slaves from the mainland, striking at a central pillar of the economy.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1875": "Recovery was rapid. Pemba’s clove plantations made up for Zanzibar’s losses, the ivory trade boomed, and Barghash extended garrisons and customs posts along the coast. Lloyd Mathews’s regular force, raised from 1877, gave the sultan more reach on the mainland than ever before.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1884": "On the eve of the partition, Barghash claimed the coast from the Benadir ports to Cape Delgado, his traders dominated the interior routes, and the House of Wonders had just been completed. The sultanate stood at the height of its wealth, prestige and influence in East Africa.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1885": "A German squadron anchored off Zanzibar and forced Barghash to recognise Carl Peters’s treaties in the interior. The episode showed that the sultan could not defend his mainland claims against a European power and that Britain would not fight Germany on his behalf.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1886": "An Anglo-German agreement reduced the sultan’s mainland domain to a coastal strip ten miles deep and divided the hinterland into British and German spheres. Zanzibar kept its trade and its ports, but the inland empire on which its prestige rested had been signed away.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1888": "Barghash died, and his successor Khalifa leased the coast to the German and British chartered companies. The German takeover of the southern ports provoked the Abushiri revolt, which Germany crushed, leaving the sultan with little authority on the mainland beyond his name on paper.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1890": "The Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty settled Anglo-German claims, Germany bought the southern coastal strip, and Zanzibar and Pemba became a British protectorate in November. The sultanate kept its throne and court, but foreign policy, finance and much of the administration passed into the hands of British officials and consuls.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1896": "When Khalid bin Barghash seized the palace on the death of Hamad bin Thuwaini, British warships bombarded it in the Anglo-Zanzibar War, which lasted less than an hour. A compliant sultan was installed, and in 1897 the legal status of slavery was abolished on the islands.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1913": "Responsibility for Zanzibar passed from the Foreign Office to the Colonial Office, and a British Resident became the effective head of government. Under Khalifa bin Harub the sultan reigned as a respected figurehead, while the clove economy and the Arab landed elite continued under colonial rule.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1960": "Khalifa bin Harub died after a reign of almost fifty years. Political parties had emerged, and the rivalry between the Zanzibar Nationalist Party, supported by many Arabs, and the Afro-Shirazi Party, backed by most Africans, was turning elections into tense and sometimes violent contests.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1963": "Zanzibar became independent on 10 December 1963 under Sultan Jamshid bin Abdullah, with a government led by the Zanzibar Nationalist Party and its allies. Sovereignty restored some standing, but the Afro-Shirazi Party had won the most votes and the new state rested on a narrow and contested base.",
  "Sultanate of Zanzibar|1964": "On 12 January 1964 a revolution led by John Okello and backed by Afro-Shirazi activists overthrew the sultan, who fled into exile. Thousands of Arabs and South Asians were killed, the People’s Republic of Zanzibar was proclaimed, and in April it united with Tanganyika.",
  "Congo Free State|1885": "Leopold II proclaimed the Congo Free State on 1 July after the powers at the Berlin Conference recognised his claims. The new state existed mainly on paper: a chain of river stations built by Stanley, a handful of European agents and treaties with chiefs who often did not understand what they had signed.",
  "Congo Free State|1886": "The capital moved from Vivi to Boma, and the Force Publique was organised as the state’s army. Revenue came almost entirely from ivory, administration cost far more than it earned, and Leopold was pouring his private fortune into a state that controlled only a narrow corridor along the river.",
  "Congo Free State|1890": "Near bankruptcy, Leopold obtained a loan of 25 million francs from the Belgian state in exchange for its right to annex the Congo later. Construction of the Matadi–Léopoldville railway began, and expeditions pushed inland, while the Brussels Anti-Slavery Conference allowed the state to levy import duties.",
  "Congo Free State|1891": "Secret decrees reserved rubber and ivory in vacant lands to the state, laying the foundations of the forced-labour economy. In Katanga, the Stairs expedition reached the Yeke capital and Msiri was killed, securing the copper-rich south against the claims of Cecil Rhodes’s British South Africa Company.",
  "Congo Free State|1892": "The domain system was extended and concessions were granted to companies such as ABIR and the Société Anversoise. War broke out with the Swahili-Arab traders of the eastern Congo, the most serious challenge yet to the state’s authority, while rubber collection began to grow rapidly.",
  "Congo Free State|1894": "The Force Publique won the Congo Arab war, taking Nyangwe and Kasongo and destroying the power of Tippu Tip’s network in the east. Leopold leased the Lado Enclave on the Nile from Britain. The state now controlled most of the Congo basin, though resistance persisted in many regions.",
  "Congo Free State|1896": "Leopold created the secret Crown Domain, an enormous area whose revenues went to him personally. The rubber boom driven by bicycle and motor-car tyres was transforming the finances of the state, but the Batetela mutinies of 1895 and 1897 showed the fragility of its military control.",
  "Congo Free State|1898": "The Matadi–Léopoldville railway was completed, linking the sea to the navigable river above the cataracts and transforming communications. Rubber exports were climbing steeply, the state had become profitable, and the Force Publique had grown into a large army, though abuses in the rubber districts were spreading.",
  "Congo Free State|1901": "Rubber revenues were at or near their height, and the Free State’s territorial control, army and income were at their peak. The system rested on hostage-taking, quotas and mutilation, and evidence gathered by missionaries and the journalist E. D. Morel was beginning to reach a wide public.",
  "Congo Free State|1904": "Roger Casement’s report confirmed the atrocities, Morel founded the Congo Reform Association, and Leopold appointed a Commission of Inquiry. International pressure grew sharply, the state’s legitimacy was collapsing, and rubber yields were beginning to fall as vines in many districts were exhausted.",
  "Congo Free State|1905": "The Commission of Inquiry’s report confirmed many of the accusations, and Mark Twain’s satire added to the outcry. Leopold issued reform decrees, but the campaign in Britain, the United States and Belgium demanded an end to his personal rule over the Congo.",
  "Congo Free State|1906": "Belgian politicians increasingly accepted that the country must take over the Congo, and negotiations with the king began. Leopold resisted giving up the Crown Domain and his financial interests, while the rubber economy continued to decline and the state lost its earlier profitability.",
  "Congo Free State|1908": "The Belgian parliament voted for annexation, and on 15 November the Free State became the Belgian Congo under a colonial charter. Leopold had much of the archive burned before the handover, and his personal state ceased to exist after twenty-three years.",
  "French West Africa|1895": "A decree of 16 June placed Senegal, French Sudan, Guinea and Ivory Coast under a governor-general at Saint-Louis. The federation was little more than a coordinating office: conquest was unfinished, Samori Ture still resisted in the interior, and each colony kept its own budget and administration.",
  "French West Africa|1898": "The capture of Samori Ture ended the largest African resistance to French expansion in the interior, and the Mossi kingdoms had been occupied. France now held a vast continuous territory from Senegal to the Niger bend, although the federal structure remained loose and many regions were barely administered.",
  "French West Africa|1902": "Ernest Roume became governor-general and the seat of government moved from Saint-Louis to Dakar, whose port and naval base made it the natural centre of the federation. Plans for railways and ports were drawn up, financed by loans that the federation’s customs revenue would repay.",
  "French West Africa|1904": "The decree of 18 October gave the governor-general his own administration and a federal budget based on customs duties, separating him from the government of Senegal. The reorganised federation could now plan railways, health services and schools for the whole region, and its authority rose markedly.",
  "French West Africa|1914": "By the eve of the First World War the federation had a functioning administration, the Dakar–Saint-Louis and Kayes–Bamako railways and growing groundnut exports. The indigénat and the head tax were in force throughout, and conscription, introduced in 1912, gave France a reservoir of African soldiers.",
  "French West Africa|1918": "The federation had supplied between about 160,000 and 200,000 soldiers for the war, many recruited by Blaise Diagne in 1918. The revolts of 1915–1917 had been suppressed, and the federation emerged as France’s most important African possession, though wartime recruitment had caused great hardship.",
  "French West Africa|1923": "The Thiès–Kayes line completed the Dakar–Niger railway, linking the Atlantic to the Niger valley, and groundnut and cocoa exports were growing. Upper Volta had been created in 1919 and Niger became a colony in 1922, giving the federation the shape it kept for most of its existence.",
  "French West Africa|1932": "Despite the Depression, the federation consolidated its economic control: the Office du Niger was founded to develop irrigated cotton and rice, and Upper Volta was divided among its neighbours to save money. The administration reached deeper into rural life than ever, through chiefs, taxes and forced labour.",
  "French West Africa|1938": "On the eve of the Second World War the federation was at the height of its administrative reach and economic output, with groundnut exports from Senegal near record levels and Dakar a major port and naval base. Colonial rule faced little open challenge, though discontent was growing.",
  "French West Africa|1940": "After the fall of France, Governor-General Pierre Boisson kept the federation loyal to Vichy and repelled the British and Free French attack on Dakar in September. Cut off from France and blockaded, the federation suffered economic hardship and harsher labour demands, weakening its position.",
  "French West Africa|1942": "After the Allied landings in North Africa in November, Boisson brought the federation over to the Allied side. Its ports, troops and resources supported the Free French war effort, but the strain of wartime requisitions and the massacre at Thiaroye in 1944 deepened African grievances.",
  "French West Africa|1946": "The French Union abolished the indigénat and forced labour and made all Africans citizens, while new parties such as the Rassemblement Démocratique Africain emerged. The reformed federation, with a Grand Council and large investment funds, remained powerful, but African politics was now a major force.",
  "French West Africa|1956": "The loi-cadre Defferre introduced universal suffrage and gave each territory its own elected assembly and executive council. Power and budgets shifted from Dakar to the territorial capitals, hollowing out the federation even as leaders like Senghor warned against the balkanisation of West Africa.",
  "French West Africa|1958": "In the referendum of 28 September, Guinea voted for independence and left the federation, while the other territories became autonomous republics in the French Community. The government-general in Dakar was wound up in 1958–1959, ending French West Africa as a political unit.",
  "Union of South Africa|1910": "The Union came into being on 31 May 1910, joining the Cape, Natal, Transvaal and Orange River colonies under Louis Botha’s government. It inherited the gold economy of the Witwatersrand and an effective colonial administration, but white unity was fragile and most of the population was excluded from political rights.",
  "Union of South Africa|1914": "Entry into the First World War on Britain’s side provoked the Maritz Rebellion among Afrikaners who opposed fighting Germany and remembered the Boer War. Several thousand rebels took up arms before government forces suppressed them, exposing the deep divisions within the white electorate on which the young state depended.",
  "Union of South Africa|1915": "Botha’s forces conquered German South West Africa, where the German colonial troops surrendered in July 1915. The Union gained a vast territory that it would administer for the rest of its existence, later as a League of Nations mandate, and sent forces to East Africa and the Western Front.",
  "Union of South Africa|1922": "The Rand Revolt, an armed strike by white mineworkers against plans to employ Africans in more semi-skilled jobs, was crushed by troops, artillery and aircraft, with well over a hundred dead. The violence damaged Smuts politically and contributed to his defeat by the Hertzog–Labour Pact in the 1924 election.",
  "Union of South Africa|1926": "At the Imperial Conference the Balfour Declaration recognised the dominions as autonomous communities equal in status to Britain. For Hertzog’s government this answered a central nationalist demand, and Afrikaans had become an official language the year before, strengthening the state’s legitimacy among Afrikaners while the economy grew.",
  "Union of South Africa|1931": "The Statute of Westminster gave legal force to dominion equality, ending the British Parliament’s power to legislate for South Africa without its consent. The Great Depression hurt farmers and exports, but the Union now enjoyed full legislative sovereignty, a high point in its constitutional standing within the Commonwealth.",
  "Union of South Africa|1934": "After South Africa left the gold standard at the end of 1932, the rising gold price brought a mining boom. Hertzog and Smuts fused their parties into the United Party, and the Status of the Union Act confirmed sovereignty, giving the state political stability and strong economic growth through the mid-1930s.",
  "Union of South Africa|1939": "Parliament voted by 80 to 67 to enter the Second World War against Germany; Hertzog resigned and Smuts became prime minister. The decision split white opinion again, but it committed the Union’s industrial and mineral resources to the Allied cause and began a period of military and industrial expansion.",
  "Union of South Africa|1942": "South African divisions fought in North Africa, where the surrender of Tobruk in June cost some 10,000 prisoners. Despite this setback, wartime demand drove rapid growth in manufacturing, and the Union’s gold, ports and dockyards on the sea route around the Cape made it valuable to the Allied war effort.",
  "Union of South Africa|1945": "The Union ended the war on the winning side with an expanded industrial base, a larger army and air force, and international prestige; Smuts helped draft the United Nations Charter at San Francisco. This marks the state’s peak in standing, before its racial policies turned international opinion against it.",
  "Union of South Africa|1948": "D. F. Malan’s Reunited National Party won a majority of seats, though not of votes, and began building apartheid. The economy remained strong and the state firmly in control, but the new government’s programme set South Africa on a course of growing isolation from the Commonwealth, the United Nations and the decolonising world.",
  "Union of South Africa|1956": "After years of constitutional conflict, the government enlarged the Senate and the Appellate Division to remove Coloured voters from the common roll. In December 156 opposition leaders were arrested for the Treason Trial. Economic growth continued, but criticism of apartheid abroad grew sharply as former colonies joined the United Nations.",
  "Union of South Africa|1960": "Police killed 69 people at Sharpeville on 21 March, the ANC and the Pan Africanist Congress were banned, and foreign capital briefly fled the country. In October a narrow majority of white voters approved a republic, setting the stage for the Union’s transformation and its departure from the Commonwealth.",
  "Union of South Africa|1961": "On 31 May 1961 the Union became the Republic of South Africa and left the Commonwealth after Verwoerd withdrew its application to remain a member. The state continued with the same territory, economy and laws, so its strength was little changed even as its diplomatic isolation deepened.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1961": "The republic was proclaimed on 31 May 1961 and left the Commonwealth. The economy was strong but shaken by the Sharpeville crisis and capital flight, the ANC and PAC had been banned, and growing diplomatic isolation limited the new state’s standing despite its dominance in southern Africa.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1964": "The Rivonia Trial ended with life sentences for Nelson Mandela and other ANC leaders, crushing the first phase of armed resistance. Foreign investment returned, the economy grew rapidly, and the government felt secure enough to press ahead with grand apartheid and the homeland policy.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1966": "Verwoerd was assassinated in parliament in September and succeeded by John Vorster. The same year the UN General Assembly declared South Africa’s mandate over South West Africa terminated and SWAPO’s armed struggle began, while the domestic economy continued its long post-war boom.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1970": "At the height of the boom, with growth averaging around 5 to 6 per cent a year in the 1960s, the apartheid state appeared economically strong and militarily secure, shielded by white-ruled Rhodesia and Portuguese Mozambique and Angola. Opposition at home had been largely silenced by bannings and imprisonment.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1976": "The Portuguese collapse in Angola and Mozambique removed the buffer of friendly regimes, and the intervention in Angola in 1975–1976 ended in withdrawal. On 16 June 1976 police fired on students in Soweto protesting against Afrikaans as a language of instruction, setting off revolts that left hundreds dead.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1977": "Steve Biko died in police custody in September, and the United Nations Security Council imposed a mandatory arms embargo through Resolution 418. The state responded by expanding its own arms industry, but its isolation grew and the economy entered a period of slower growth and high inflation.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1984": "The tricameral constitution took effect and P. W. Botha became executive state president, but the exclusion of Africans provoked the township revolt that began in the Vaal Triangle in September 1984. The United Democratic Front led mass protest, and the state faced resistance on a scale it had not seen before.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1986": "A nationwide state of emergency, the debt standstill of 1985 after foreign banks refused to roll over loans, and the United States Comprehensive Anti-Apartheid Act left the economy weak and the government isolated. Thousands were detained without trial, and the pass laws were abolished as the system began to crack.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1990": "F. W. de Klerk unbanned the liberation movements and released Nelson Mandela, and Namibia became independent. Sanctions began to ease and the country’s international standing started to recover, though negotiations were uncertain and political violence in Natal and the townships killed thousands in the early 1990s.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1994": "The first democratic elections on 27 April made Nelson Mandela president at the head of a Government of National Unity. Sanctions ended, South Africa rejoined the Commonwealth and resumed its seat in the UN General Assembly, and the state gained, for the first time, legitimacy among its whole population.",
  "Republic of South Africa|1999": "Thabo Mbeki succeeded Mandela after the ANC’s second election victory. The 1996 constitution was in force, the Truth and Reconciliation Commission had delivered its report, and fiscal discipline had stabilised public finances, although the government’s denial of the link between HIV and AIDS damaged its response to the epidemic.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2007": "Several years of growth around 5 per cent, rising commodity prices, budget surpluses and an expanding middle class brought the republic to its economic and diplomatic peak. South Africa was a leading voice in the African Union, but in December the ANC’s Polokwane conference removed Mbeki as party leader.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2010": "South Africa hosted the FIFA World Cup, the first held in Africa, and was invited to join the BRIC group of emerging economies. Although the global financial crisis had caused a recession in 2009, the country’s international profile and infrastructure investment stood at their height.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2012": "Police shot dead 34 striking miners at Marikana in August, the deadliest police shooting since the end of apartheid. Growth slowed, credit ratings began to fall, and corruption allegations surrounding President Jacob Zuma started to erode confidence in the institutions of the state.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2017": "Revelations of ‘state capture’ by business associates of Jacob Zuma, the downgrading of South Africa’s debt to junk status and stagnant growth weakened the state. In December Cyril Ramaphosa won the ANC leadership, and Zuma resigned as president in February 2018 under pressure from his party.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2021": "The COVID-19 pandemic caused a deep recession, and the jailing of Jacob Zuma for contempt of court was followed in July by riots and looting in KwaZulu-Natal and Gauteng in which more than 300 people died. Load-shedding and unemployment above 30 per cent reflected weakened state capacity.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2024": "In the May election the ANC lost its parliamentary majority for the first time, winning about 40 per cent of the vote. Ramaphosa formed a Government of National Unity with the Democratic Alliance and other parties, a peaceful adjustment that confirmed the strength of the constitutional order.",
  "Republic of South Africa|2025": "South Africa remains a constitutional democracy and one of Africa’s largest and most industrialised economies, with strong courts, a free press and global diplomatic weight. Slow growth, high unemployment, crime and weak public services keep its standing below the peak it reached in the mid-2000s.",
  "Uganda|1962": "Uganda became independent on 9 October with a quasi-federal constitution, a parliamentary government under Milton Obote and a working colonial economy based on coffee and cotton. Its institutions were intact, but the uneasy alliance between Obote and the Buganda monarchy left the state’s foundations politically fragile.",
  "Uganda|1964": "An army mutiny in January was put down with British help, and the referendum on the ‘lost counties’ in November returned two counties to Bunyoro, breaking the alliance between Obote and Kabaka Yekka. The economy still grew steadily on coffee exports, but tension between the government and Buganda mounted.",
  "Uganda|1966": "Obote suspended the constitution and in May sent troops under Idi Amin against the Kabaka’s palace at Mengo, killing many and forcing Mutesa II into exile. Central government grew stronger by force, but the state lost the consent of Buganda, its largest and richest region.",
  "Uganda|1971": "Idi Amin overthrew Obote on 25 January while the president was abroad. Many Ugandans welcomed the coup at first, but Amin soon purged and killed Acholi and Langi soldiers, and the army became an instrument of personal rule rather than of the state.",
  "Uganda|1972": "Amin ordered the expulsion of the Asian community, which ran much of the country’s commerce, and handed their shops and businesses to cronies. Britain cut off aid, and the loss of skills and capital set off a long economic collapse that undermined every institution of the state.",
  "Uganda|1976": "The Israeli rescue of hostages at Entebbe in July exposed Amin’s regime to international humiliation. Coffee prices were high, but smuggling, inflation and state terror had hollowed out the administration, and killings of suspected opponents continued, in February 1977 claiming Archbishop Janani Luwum.",
  "Uganda|1979": "Amin’s invasion of Tanzania brought defeat: Tanzanian forces and Ugandan exiles captured Kampala on 11 April and Amin fled into exile. The state’s institutions had collapsed, the economy lay in ruins, and three governments followed one another within two years.",
  "Uganda|1980": "Obote returned to power through the disputed December election run by the Military Commission. Yoweri Museveni rejected the result and in February 1981 began the guerrilla war of the National Resistance Army, while the economy, under an IMF programme, showed only a fragile recovery.",
  "Uganda|1983": "The Bush War reached its worst intensity in the Luwero Triangle, where government counter-insurgency operations killed large numbers of civilians. The Uganda National Liberation Army was undisciplined and divided, and the state could not control the countryside around its own capital.",
  "Uganda|1986": "The National Resistance Army captured Kampala on 26 January and Museveni was sworn in three days later. The new government restored discipline in the army and security in the south, but it inherited a ruined economy and faced new insurgencies in the north and east.",
  "Uganda|1987": "The Economic Recovery Programme, backed by the IMF and the World Bank, devalued the currency and began the liberalisation of prices and trade. Alice Lakwena’s Holy Spirit Movement was defeated near Jinja, though the conflict in the north continued under Joseph Kony’s Lord’s Resistance Army.",
  "Uganda|1993": "The traditional kingdoms were restored as cultural institutions, and Ronald Muwenda Mutebi II was crowned Kabaka of Buganda, reconciling the state with its largest region. Growth was strong, Asian property was being returned, and a Constituent Assembly was elected the following year to draft a new constitution.",
  "Uganda|1996": "Museveni won the first direct presidential election under the 1995 constitution. Uganda was now one of Africa’s fastest-growing economies and a favourite of Western donors, and its army, which had helped bring the Rwandan Patriotic Front to power in 1994, joined the invasion of Zaire that toppled Mobutu.",
  "Uganda|2001": "Museveni defeated Kizza Besigye in a contested election. Ugandan forces had fought their former Rwandan allies at Kisangani in 1999 and 2000 and were accused of plundering Congolese resources, while the Lord’s Resistance Army kept the north insecure, holding back the state’s gains elsewhere.",
  "Uganda|2006": "Multi-party elections returned, and the Lord’s Resistance Army left northern Uganda during peace talks in Juba, allowing displaced people to begin returning home. Oil was found in the Albertine Graben, and Ugandan troops prepared to deploy to Somalia, while the removal of term limits strengthened Museveni’s personal hold.",
  "Uganda|2011": "Museveni won a fourth elected term, and Ugandan troops in Somalia helped drive al-Shabaab out of Mogadishu in August. Earlier in the year the government had suppressed the ‘walk to work’ protests over rising prices, but the economy and the state’s regional military weight continued to grow.",
  "Uganda|2016": "Uganda had become a regional security partner of Western governments and hosted rapidly growing numbers of refugees from South Sudan. A route for an oil export pipeline through Tanzania was agreed, and Museveni was re-elected, though the opposition leader Besigye was repeatedly arrested during the campaign.",
  "Uganda|2025": "Uganda stands at the height of its economic size and regional influence, with growth of around 5 to 6 per cent, oil production in preparation and an army active in Somalia and eastern Congo. The concentration of power in Museveni, in office since 1986, and the unresolved question of succession remain its main weaknesses.",
  "Tanzania|1964": "The union of Tanganyika and Zanzibar on 26 April created a new state of some eleven million people, still recovering from the army mutiny of January and the Zanzibar revolution. Its institutions were inherited from British rule, its economy depended on sisal, coffee and cotton, and its political direction was still undecided.",
  "Tanzania|1967": "The Arusha Declaration launched ujamaa socialism and self-reliance, nationalising banks and major firms, and the East African Community was formed with Kenya and Uganda the same year. The state was stable and Nyerere’s prestige high, and the new policy gave the government strong control over the economy.",
  "Tanzania|1972": "Abeid Karume was assassinated in Zanzibar in April and succeeded by Aboud Jumbe, while on the mainland administration was decentralised to the regions. Tanzania’s diplomatic standing was rising as the centre of the liberation movements, and the economy was still growing modestly before the shocks of the mid-1970s.",
  "Tanzania|1975": "The Tanzania–Zambia Railway was completed with Chinese loans and engineers, and the villagisation campaign had moved millions of people into planned villages. Mozambique’s independence under FRELIMO, which Tanzania had sheltered, was a triumph for its liberation policy, but harvests fell and drought forced costly food imports.",
  "Tanzania|1977": "TANU and the Afro-Shirazi Party merged into Chama Cha Mapinduzi, tightening party control on both sides of the union. The East African Community collapsed the same year amid disputes with Kenya, and Tanzania closed its border with Kenya, damaging trade and tourism and weakening the economy.",
  "Tanzania|1979": "Tanzania’s victory over Idi Amin and the capture of Kampala in April raised its prestige and regional influence to a high point. The war, however, cost an estimated 500 million US dollars, which Tanzania bore largely alone, and coincided with the second oil shock, draining the economy.",
  "Tanzania|1982": "The economy was in deep crisis: exports had fallen, factories stood idle for lack of imported inputs, and basic goods were scarce. Nyerere’s refusal of IMF conditions left Tanzania short of foreign exchange, and the state struggled to deliver the services that ujamaa had promised its citizens.",
  "Tanzania|1986": "Under Ali Hassan Mwinyi, who succeeded Nyerere in 1985, Tanzania signed an agreement with the IMF and began the Economic Recovery Programme, devaluing the shilling and freeing trade. The state was weak and the economy near its lowest point, but the change of course laid the basis for later growth.",
  "Tanzania|1992": "The constitution was amended to end the one-party state, allowing opposition parties such as the Civic United Front to register. Liberalisation was producing modest growth, but the state faced corruption, weak public services and growing tension over Zanzibar’s place in the union.",
  "Tanzania|1995": "The first multi-party elections brought Benjamin Mkapa to the presidency, and he pursued privatisation and fiscal reform that won donor support. Hundreds of thousands of refugees from Rwanda and Burundi strained western Tanzania, and the disputed Zanzibar election began years of political conflict on the islands.",
  "Tanzania|2001": "Security forces killed dozens of opposition demonstrators in Zanzibar and Pemba in January after the disputed elections of 2000, among the worst political violence in the country’s history. Debt relief under the Heavily Indebted Poor Countries initiative and steady growth were, however, strengthening the economy.",
  "Tanzania|2005": "Jakaya Kikwete won the presidency in a peaceful transfer of power within the ruling party. Growth of around 6 to 7 per cent a year, gold mining, rising tourism and expanded primary education gave the state greater resources and capacity than at any time since the 1960s.",
  "Tanzania|2010": "Kikwete was re-elected, and a referendum in Zanzibar created a government of national unity between CCM and the Civic United Front, easing the union’s main political conflict. Large offshore natural gas discoveries in the south were beginning, and the economy continued to expand rapidly.",
  "Tanzania|2015": "John Magufuli won the presidency promising to fight corruption and waste, and launched large infrastructure projects, among them a standard-gauge railway and, later, the Julius Nyerere hydropower dam. The economy had grown for two decades, though Magufuli’s government soon restricted the press and the opposition.",
  "Tanzania|2021": "Magufuli died in March, and Samia Suluhu Hassan became the first woman president. She eased some restrictions on the opposition and the media, reversed Magufuli’s denial of COVID-19 and courted investors. The World Bank had reclassified Tanzania as a lower-middle-income country in 2020.",
  "Tanzania|2025": "Tanzania stands near the height of its economic weight, with over 60 million people, a growing economy, a standard-gauge railway in service and the Nyerere dam generating power. The October 2025 election, held without the main opposition party and followed by deadly protests and a crackdown, damaged its reputation for political stability.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|1991": "The Somali National Movement declared Somaliland’s independence at Burao on 18 May, after Siad Barre’s fall. The new state controlled a war-ravaged territory with Hargeisa largely in ruins, few working institutions, no regular revenue and many armed clan militias, so its power was minimal.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|1993": "The Borama conference of clan elders adopted a national charter, created the Guurti and chose Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal as president. This settlement gave Somaliland a working government and a framework for demobilising militias, a major step towards building a functioning state.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|1995": "Fighting between government forces and clan militias around Hargeisa and Burao, which lasted from 1994 to 1996, drove tens of thousands of people from their homes. Egal’s government survived, but the conflict showed how fragile the new state remained and halted the recovery of its towns and economy.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|1997": "A national conference in Hargeisa ended the civil conflict, re-elected Egal and adopted an interim constitution. Militias were being absorbed into the army, the Somaliland shilling and customs revenue from Berbera funded the government, and the state now controlled most of its claimed territory.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2001": "A referendum on 31 May approved the constitution and reaffirmed independence by a very large majority, opening the way to multi-party politics. The economy, however, was hurt by a Saudi ban on livestock imports from the Horn of Africa, imposed in 2000 because of Rift Valley fever.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2003": "Dahir Riyale Kahin, who had succeeded Egal on his death in 2002, won the first contested presidential election by about 80 votes, and the opposition accepted the result. The local elections of 2002 and this presidential vote established competitive elections as the basis of Somaliland’s legitimacy.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2008": "Al-Shabaab suicide bombers attacked the presidential palace, the Ethiopian mission and a United Nations office in Hargeisa in October, killing more than twenty people. Repeated postponements of the presidential election strained the political system, but the state held its territory and contained the threat.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2010": "Ahmed Mohamed Mohamoud, known as Silanyo, defeated the incumbent Dahir Riyale Kahin in the presidential election, and power passed peacefully to the opposition. This first electoral transfer of power won Somaliland wide international praise and closer cooperation with donors, though not recognition.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2017": "Muse Bihi Abdi of the ruling Kulmiye party won the presidential election, held with iris-recognition voter registration. DP World was expanding Berbera port under its 2016 concession, and the United Arab Emirates agreed to build a military base there, raising Somaliland’s strategic profile.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2021": "Parliamentary and local elections in May, the first parliamentary vote since 2005, produced an opposition majority in the House of Representatives. The new Berbera container terminal opened, and Taiwan and Somaliland had exchanged representative offices in 2020, bringing Somaliland’s institutions and external ties to their high point.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2023": "Fighting around Las Anod against SSC-Khatumo forces, which reject Somaliland’s rule, ended in August with the Somaliland army driven from its positions near the town. The defeat cost Somaliland control of much of the Sool region and exposed the limits of its authority in the contested east.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2024": "Ethiopia’s memorandum of understanding in January raised the prospect of recognition in exchange for sea access, though it was not put into effect. In November the opposition candidate Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi, known as Irro, defeated Muse Bihi Abdi, who stepped down peacefully, renewing Somaliland’s democratic reputation.",
  "Republic of Somaliland|2025": "Somaliland remains a functioning but largely unrecognised state in control of most of its claimed territory. In December Israel became the first United Nations member state to recognise it, a diplomatic breakthrough, though Somalia and most other governments continue to regard it as part of Somalia."
};
